// GENERATED by `node tools/grim.js build` — do not edit. Sources: content/**/*.grim, locales/**/*.lang
window.LUMENHOLD_SOURCES = [{"path":"content/core/balance.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Numbers (design §8.4). Tier words in effects (`mood +medium`,\n#  `rel npc -small`) take their amount from `sizes`; challenge\n#  difficulty tiers shift the DC from the expected skill at that\n#  point of the run (`curve`, one value per turn, fitted by\n#  `node tools/grim.js curve`). All values are tunable.\n# ============================================================\n\n@balance\ndesc: Size tiers are tiny, small, medium, large and huge; skills use the xp row.\nsizes: health 2 4 8 13 20 | mood 3 6 10 16 25 | gold 3 8 15 30 60 | renown 2 4 8 13 20 | mana 3 6 10 16 25\n  | alignment 3 6 10 16 25 | xp 2 3 6 10 16 | lore 1 1 3 5 8 | rel 2 4 7 11 16 | spark 4 8 13 20 30\ndifficulty: trivial -15 easy -7 medium 0 hard 8 heroic 16\n# Skill XP (design §8.5): the next point costs a + b × level. Fitted so a balanced player averages about 50 at the\n# end of a four-year run; \"focused reaches ~95 by month 24\" needs phase 3's course depth (balance alarm).\nxp_cost: 1 0.02\n# Rank XP needed for each promotion (Member → Officer, Officer → Captain), plus performance ≥ 70.\nrank_xp: 10 25\nweights: health 1 mood 1 gold 0.5 renown 0.8 mana 0.4 alignment 0 xp 1.5 rel 0.6 spark 0.4\n# The combat stub (playtest 2, M39, M5): until the minigame, a fight reads arcana, vigor and guile (weighted), plus\n# condition × (the mean of health, mood and mana − 50) and spells × total spell levels (at most +15). Entering costs mana.\ncombat: arcana 1 vigor 1 guile 0.5 condition 0.2 spells 1.5\ncombat_cost: small\n# How far one skill point moves a check's odds (M14): `wild` checks swing whatever your skill; `steady` ones follow it.\nswing: normal 2.5 wild 1.2 steady 4\n# A shift's pay (M10): `wage` in a job's cards pays the job's `pay:` at your rank × this share.\nwage: short 0.5 full 1 overtime 2\ncurve: 7 9 11 12 14 14 15 16 18 20 21 21 22 24 26 27 29 29 30 31 33 35 36 36 37 39 40 42 43 44 44 46 47 49 50 51 52 53 53 55 56 56 56 57 58 59 60\n\n# ---------------- Presets (design §8.4): named groups of effects ----------------\n\n@preset grind\nparams: skill\ndesc: Hard work on one skill.\ndo:\n  $skill +medium\n  mood -small\n  health -tiny\n\n@preset rest\ndesc: A real rest.\ndo:\n  mood +medium\n  health +small\n\n@preset social\nparams: who\ndesc: Time with someone.\ndo:\n  rel $who +small\n  mood +small\n  gold -tiny\n\n@preset work\ndesc: Paid work.\ndo:\n  gold +medium\n  mood -small\n  health -tiny\n\n@preset splurge\ndesc: Spending to feel better.\ndo:\n  mood +large\n  gold -medium\n\n@preset risk\nparams: res\ndesc: A gamble: a large gain or a medium loss.\ndo:\n  chance 50:\n    $res +large\n  else:\n    $res -medium\n"},{"path":"content/core/rules.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Lumenhold Academy — core rules\n#  Resources, skills, calendar, global hooks.\n#  See docs/grimoire-script.md for the language.\n# ============================================================\n\n@game\ntitle: Lumenhold Academy\nsubtitle: Four years. Forty-eight moons. One apprentice.\n# The content format version (design §12). Frozen: v1 may only grow; see test/golden/schema-v1.json.\nschema: 1\n# Four years (design §4.1). The academic year starts in autumn; the run ends in Starfall of year 4 (turn 48).\n# Year 4's last break is the wrap-up: no plans or duties, board on the house; its last month only resolves the run.\nyears: 4\nlast_month: 12\nwrapup: 11-12\nmonths: Harvestmoon, Emberwane, Mistveil, Frostcall, Deepwinter, Thawmoon, Stormbloom, Greenrise, Blossomtide, Highsun, Goldenhaze, Starfall\ncalendar: term=1-4 break=5-6 term=7-10 break=11-12\nseasons: autumn=1-3 winter=4-6 spring=7-9 summer=10-12\nsummer_months: 5, 6, 11, 12\n# Each month is one day (design §4.2). The two visits are picked on the map.\nday_term: morning=courses noon=social:1-2 afternoon=visit evening=visit night=world:1-2 midnight=special:0-1\nday_break: morning=plan afternoon=visit evening=visit night=world:1\nday_last: morning=special:0\n# Social and world cards are dealt across the whole day (playtest 2, M43), not only at noon and night; `slot:` requires a\n# slot and `time:` favours one.\nspread: social world\n# The origin is picked on its own screen before the game (its options are the origins).\norigins: choose_origin\n# Exams are cards (playtest 2, M4): this one is pushed for each enrolled course in a term's last month.\nexam: course_exam\ncards_per_month: 3\nmax_cards_per_month: 7\nsocial_per_month: 1\nmin_random_per_month: 1\nintro: The sky-barge lurches. Below, the lake glitters; above, the seven towers of Lumenhold Academy pierce the clouds. You have a trunk, a borrowed wand and a letter of acceptance with your name spelled wrong. Four years begin now.\n\n# ---------------- Resources ----------------\n# The top bars (design §8.1) are Health · Mood · Mana · Renown · Alignment, in this order;\n# `display: panel` resources (Crowns, Standing) are shown in the You panel instead.\n\n@resource health\nname: Health\nicon: heart\nmin: 0\nmax: 100\nstart: 70\ncolor: #d9606a\ndesc: Your body. At zero you collapse.\nwarn_below: 20\non_zero:\n  queue crisis_collapse now\n\n@resource mood\nname: Mood\nicon: sun\nmin: 0\nmax: 100\nstart: 60\ncolor: #e6b84a\ndesc: Your spirits. At zero, despair takes hold.\nwarn_below: 20\non_zero:\n  queue crisis_despair now\n\n@resource mana\nname: Mana\nicon: sparkle\nmin: 0\nmax: 40 + arcana / 2\nstart: 30\ncolor: #5fa8e8\ndesc: The magic you can spend on spells. Refills each month, faster the stronger your Arcana.\nwarn_below: 8\n\n@resource renown\nname: Renown\nicon: star\nmin: 0\nmax: 100\nstart: 5\ncolor: #b58ae0\ndesc: How much the academy talks about you — for better or worse. Fades a little every month.\n\n@resource alignment\nname: Alignment\nicon: moon\nmin: -100\nmax: 100\nstart: 0\ncolor: #c9b8ff\ndrag: -50 0.5\ndesc: Lumen or Hollow: the light you serve, or the dark that has noticed you. Deep in the Hollow, the way back is slow.\n\n@resource gold\nname: Crowns\nicon: coin\nmin: 0\nmax: 99999\nstart: 30\ncolor: #d7a948\ndisplay: panel\nwarn_below: 6\ndesc: Crowns in your purse. Rent is due every month.\non_zero:\n  flag broke\n\n# ---------------- Skills ----------------\n\n@skill arcana\nname: Arcana\nicon: wand\nstart: 8\nmax: 100\ndesc: Raw spellcraft — power, control, nerve.\n\n@skill lore\nname: Lore\nicon: book\nstart: 8\nmax: 100\ndesc: Scholarship, theory, history, languages of the old world.\n\n@skill alchemy\nname: Alchemy\nicon: potion\nstart: 6\nmax: 100\ndesc: Potions, herbs, reagents and the patience to stir clockwise.\n\n@skill charm\nname: Charm\nicon: mask\nstart: 8\nmax: 100\ndesc: Wit, warmth, persuasion. Making people want to say yes.\n\n@skill vigor\nname: Vigor\nicon: sword\nstart: 8\nmax: 100\ndesc: Stamina, reflexes, dueling and broom-flight.\n\n@skill guile\nname: Guile\nicon: eye\nstart: 6\nmax: 100\ndesc: Sneaking, lying, noticing what others miss.\n\n# ---------------- Global hooks ----------------\n\n# Unpaid board (design §8.2): a hungry month; twice in a row, Grisk settles it for you (grisk_board_debt).\n@hook rent\non: month_start\nlabel: Dormitory & board\nwhen: turn > 1 and term\ndo:\n  if gold >= 8:\n    gold -8\n    board_missed = 0\n  else:\n    gold = 0\n    gain hungry\n    board_missed += 1\n    if board_missed >= 2 and not has(in_debt) and not flag(grisk_done):\n      board_missed = 0\n      queue grisk_board_debt\n\n@hook break_rent\non: month_start\nlabel: Board at the castle\nwhen: break and (has(plan_castle) or has(plan_work))\ndo:\n  gold -8\n\n# Tuition each term (design §8.2); the scholarship pays half.\n@hook tuition\non: term_start\nlabel: Tuition\ndo:\n  if has(origin_scholar):\n    gold -15\n  else:\n    gold -30\n\n@hook gpa_probation\non: term_end\nlabel: Academic probation\nwhen: gpa > 0 and (year >= 2 or month >= 10)\ndo:\n  if gpa < 1.5 and not has(probation):\n    gain probation\n  if gpa < 1.0:\n    gpa_low += 1\n  else:\n    gpa_low = 0\n  if gpa_low >= 2:\n    gpa_low = 0\n    queue crisis_probation\n\n# Coursework (phase 3): each enrolled course trains its main skill a little every term month.\n# (Replaces the v0.2 elective states' ticks, which enrolment used to grant two at a time.)\n@hook coursework_evocation\non: month_start\nlabel: Coursework\nwhen: term and enrolled(evocation)\ndo:\n  arcana +small\n\n@hook coursework_astromancy\non: month_start\nlabel: Coursework\nwhen: term and enrolled(astromancy)\ndo:\n  lore +small\n\n@hook coursework_alchemy\non: month_start\nlabel: Coursework\nwhen: term and enrolled(alchemy)\ndo:\n  alchemy +small\n\n@hook coursework_illusion\non: month_start\nlabel: Coursework\nwhen: term and enrolled(illusion)\ndo:\n  charm +small\n\n@hook coursework_beastcraft\non: month_start\nlabel: Coursework\nwhen: term and enrolled(beastcraft)\ndo:\n  vigor +small\n\n@hook coursework_umbral\non: month_start\nlabel: Coursework\nwhen: term and enrolled(umbral)\ndo:\n  guile +small\n\n@hook coursework_artifice\non: month_start\nlabel: Coursework\nwhen: term and enrolled(artifice)\ndo:\n  alchemy +small\n\n@hook coursework_starcalling\non: month_start\nlabel: Coursework\nwhen: term and enrolled(starcalling)\ndo:\n  lore +small\n\n@hook coursework_hollow_studies\non: month_start\nlabel: Coursework\nwhen: term and enrolled(hollow_studies)\ndo:\n  guile +small\n\n@hook term_stress\non: month_start\nlabel: Term-time pressure\nwhen: turn > 1 and term\ndo:\n  mood -2\n  if month == 4 or month == 10:\n    mood -5\n\n@hook exhaustion_drain\non: month_start\nlabel: Running on empty\nwhen: health < 25\ndo:\n  mood -5\n\n@hook gloom_drain\non: month_start\nlabel: Gloom\nwhen: mood < 20\ndo:\n  health -4\n\n@hook natural_recovery\non: month_start\nlabel: Rest\nwhen: health < 70 and not has(sick) and not has(injured)\ndo:\n  health +6\n\n@hook fame_fades\non: month_start\nlabel: Fading gossip\nwhen: turn > 1 and renown >= 5\ndo:\n  renown -= floor(renown * 0.2)\n\n@hook mana_regen\non: month_start\nlabel: Mana returns\nwhen: turn > 1\ndo:\n  mana += 10 + arcana / 10\n\n# Ambient drains (design §8.2): exam nerves in the finals months, so mana matters even without casting.\n@hook exam_nerves\non: month_start\nlabel: Exam nerves\nwhen: month == 4 or month == 10\ndo:\n  mana -= 15\n\n# ---------------- Game start ----------------\n\n@hook start\non: game_start\ndo:\n  queue intro_arrival now\n  # Picked on the origin screen: the mirror only reflects it. Otherwise (tools, old callers) the mirror asks.\n  if has(origin_noble) or has(origin_scholar) or has(origin_hedge) or has(origin_street) or has(origin_merchant):\n    queue sorting_mirror now\n  else:\n    queue choose_origin now\n\n@hook love_glow\non: month_start\nlabel: Someone to come home to\nwhen: bonds(partner) > 0\ndo:\n  mood +4\n\n@hook lonely\non: month_start\nlabel: Loneliness\nwhen: turn > 4 and bonds(friend) + bonds(close) + bonds(partner) == 0\ndo:\n  mood -2\n"},{"path":"content/endings.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Endings. `kind: early` endings are only reached via `end <id>`\n#  and stay exclusive (game over).\n#  Graduation builds a yearbook (design §10.5) from `slot:`s, each\n#  checked in descending `priority`:\n#    headline (default)  1: career / academic; `ending_graduate`\n#                           (no `when`) is the fallback\n#    mystery             0-1: the Hollow Star outcome\n#    relationships       0-3: bonds\n#    personal            0-2: alignment, renown\n#  `excludes:` drops other endings once this one is picked.\n# ============================================================\n\n# ---------------- Early endings ----------------\n\n@ending ending_sent_home\nkind: early\ntitle: Sent Home\nart: cross\ntext: The infirmary writes to your family. A carriage comes for you on a grey morning and you watch the towers shrink in the rear window. Your body gave out before your dreams did. Perhaps, one day, you will come back.\n\n@ending ending_quiet_departure\nkind: early\ntitle: The Quiet Departure\nart: rain\ntext: You take the dawn barge without telling anyone. The academy does not notice right away; then, one by one, people do. Some of them write. You keep the letters in a box you don't open for years. When you finally do, you are someone gentler, and you find you are not alone.\n\n@ending ending_expelled\nkind: early\ntitle: Expelled\nart: scales\ntext: The Board's decision is final. Your wand is not taken — Lumenhold is not cruel — but your name is struck from the register in ink that cannot be unwritten. You walk out of the gates with a trunk and a lesson, and the long road ahead is entirely your own.\n\n@ending ending_hollow_claimed\nkind: early\ntitle: Claimed by the Hollow Star\nart: moon\ntext: You open the door beneath the lake. The Hollow Star looks back. For a moment you understand everything, and then there is no \"you\" left to understand it. On clear nights students swear they see a new star, black against black, right above the seventh tower.\n\n@ending ending_runaway\nkind: early\ntitle: Gone With the Wind\nart: map\ntext: You and Corvin leave Lumenhold on a stolen sky-barge at midnight, laughing so hard you can barely steer. The academy will tell the story for years — usually wrong. You never graduate. You don't regret it, mostly.\n\n# ---------------- Final endings (graduation) ----------------\n\n@ending ending_hollow_keeper\nslot: mystery\npriority: 100\ntitle: Keeper of the Hollow Star\nart: moon\nwhen: flag(hollow_bargain)\ntext: You graduate with a black star on your palm and a voice in the back of your mind that is not entirely yours. The Hollow Star gave you power; it will want something back. For now, the towers bow a little when you pass, and even the headmistress looks away first.\n\n@ending ending_hollow_freed\nslot: mystery\npriority: 98\ntitle: Herald of the Hollow Star\nart: moon\nwhen: flag(hollow_freed)\ntext: The spring the lake froze, the Star went free, and you held the door. It did not swallow you; it remembered who let it out. The Northern families write to you as one of their own now, and wherever you walk, candles burn a little lower, as if bowing. Lumenhold still hands you a diploma. Nobody at the ceremony looks you in the eye.\n\n@ending ending_hollow_hero\nslot: mystery\npriority: 95\ntitle: Warden of Lumenhold\nart: shield\nwhen: flag(hollow_sealed)\ntext: The academy will never fully know what you did beneath the lake. The headmistress does. At graduation she gives you a silver key without a lock and a seat among the Wardens, the quiet few who keep the old doors closed. Kael stands in the crowd and, for once, he is smiling.\n\n@ending ending_archmage\npriority: 80\ntitle: The Archmage's Path\nart: wand\nwhen: arcana >= 80 and gpa >= 2.5\ntext: Graduating with highest honours and a power that makes the candles lean toward you, you are offered an apprenticeship with the Conclave of the Seven Towers. Professor Thorne shakes your hand and says, gruffly, \"Don't waste it.\" You won't.\n\n@ending ending_scholar\npriority: 75\ntitle: Scholar of the Spire\nart: book\nwhen: (lore >= 98 or (flag(thesis_distinction) and lore >= 85)) and gpa >= 2.5\ntext: Your thesis is shelved in the Spire Library, in the section students whisper about. The faculty offers you a junior fellowship. You will spend your life among books that bite and secrets that wait — and you could not be happier.\n\n@ending ending_beloved\nslot: relationships\npriority: 70\ntitle: Hand in Hand\nart: heart\nwhen: bonds(partner) > 0 and mood >= 60\ntext: Graduation is a blur of robes and cheers, but what you remember is a hand finding yours as the hats go up. Whatever comes next, you won't face it alone. Lumenhold taught you magic; it also taught you this.\n\n# A partner ending names them (playtest 2, B31); each replaces the generic one above.\n\n@ending ending_beloved_lyra\nslot: relationships\npriority: 72\ntitle: The Second Flame\nart: heart\nwhen: bond(lyra) == \"partner\" and mood >= 60\nexcludes: ending_beloved\ntext: When the hats go up, {lyra} catches yours before it lands and hands it back without a word. Later, in front of her whole family, she takes your hand and doesn't let go. For House Vaneshade, that is a declaration. For her it is simpler: she has finally found someone she doesn't need to beat.\n\n@ending ending_beloved_tobias\nslot: relationships\npriority: 72\ntitle: Home, Wherever\nart: heart\nwhen: bond(tobias) == \"partner\" and mood >= 60\nexcludes: ending_beloved\ntext: {tobias} cries through the whole ceremony and blames the pollen. Afterwards he lifts you off your feet in front of everyone, then shows you a drawing of a farmhouse with two chairs on the porch. \"No pressure,\" he says. \"The goats have already voted.\"\n\n@ending ending_beloved_seraphine\nslot: relationships\npriority: 72\ntitle: Already Foreseen\nart: heart\nwhen: bond(seraphine) == \"partner\" and mood >= 60\nexcludes: ending_beloved\ntext: \"I saw this,\" {seraphine} says as the hats go up. \"Years ago. You were holding my hand.\" You are. For once she smiles at the present, as if it has finally caught up with her, and asks whether you'd like to know what happens next. You tell her you'd rather find out together.\n\n@ending ending_beloved_corvin\nslot: relationships\npriority: 72\ntitle: No More Jokes\nart: heart\nwhen: bond(corvin) == \"partner\" and mood >= 60\nexcludes: ending_beloved\ntext: {corvin} slips a key into your palm during the speeches. It opens nothing at Lumenhold; he has checked. \"It's for wherever we end up,\" he says, and for once he doesn't make a joke of it. Then he does, of course. But his hand stays in yours.\n\n@ending ending_beloved_mira\nslot: relationships\npriority: 72\ntitle: Built to Last\nart: heart\nwhen: bond(mira) == \"partner\" and mood >= 60\nexcludes: ending_beloved\ntext: {mira} has rigged the hats to fly back down. Most of them do. In the cheerful chaos they press a small brass ring into your hand; it ticks faintly, like a second heart. \"It'll run for fifty years,\" they say. \"Probably. We should check.\"\n\n@ending ending_beloved_kael\nslot: relationships\npriority: 72\ntitle: Thaw\nart: heart\nwhen: bond(kael) == \"partner\" and mood >= 60\nexcludes: ending_beloved\ntext: {kael} doesn't throw his hat. He stands beside you in the crowd, and when the cheering is loudest he says, very quietly, \"Come north with me. Or I stay. Either.\" It is the longest sentence about feelings he has ever spoken. His hand, in yours, is warm.\n\n@ending ending_beloved_nessa\nslot: relationships\npriority: 72\ntitle: Front Page\nart: heart\nwhen: bond(nessa) == \"partner\" and mood >= 60\nexcludes: ending_beloved\ntext: The Ledger's graduation issue runs a small notice at the bottom of page six, set in {nessa}'s own type: two names, one line, no comment. All of Lumenhold reads it before breakfast. {nessa} pretends not to watch them read it, and holds your hand under the table the whole time.\n\n@ending ending_alchemist\npriority: 65\ntitle: Master of the Crucible\nart: cauldron\nwhen: alchemy >= 72\ntext: Your potions are already sold under the counter in three cities. On graduation day you sign the lease on a crooked little shop by the harbour. The sign reads your name, and underneath: NO REFUNDS FOR TRANSFORMATIONS.\n\n@ending ending_celebrity\nslot: personal\npriority: 60\ntitle: The Name on Every Lip\nart: star\nwhen: renown >= 45\ntext: The Lumen Ledger runs a special edition about you. First-years queue for your autograph. You graduate as the most talked-about student in a generation, and the world beyond the gates is already waiting to hear what you'll do next.\n\n@ending ending_merchant\npriority: 55\ntitle: Gold in the Vaults\nart: coin\nwhen: gold >= 180\ntext: You leave Lumenhold with a diploma and a fortune. Magic, you've decided, is good, but compound interest is better. Within five years your trading house has offices in every port — and an enchanted ledger that never loses a coin.\n\n@ending ending_shadow\npriority: 50\ntitle: The Quiet Hand\nart: mask\nwhen: guile >= 75\ntext: No one quite knows how you graduated with those grades. No one quite remembers the favours you called in. A discreet letter offers you a position with the Crown's Office of Unseen Affairs. You accept, of course. You'd already read it before it was sent.\n\n@ending ending_beloved_friends\nslot: relationships\npriority: 45\ntitle: The Ones Who Stayed\nart: group\nwhen: bonds(friend) + bonds(close) >= 3\ntext: You may not leave as the brightest or the richest, but you leave surrounded. Your friends throw their hats higher than anyone's, and the photograph from that day — all of you squinting into the sun — will hang in every home you ever have.\n\n@ending ending_barely\npriority: 40\ntitle: By the Skin of Your Teeth\nart: hourglass\nwhen: (turn > 4 and gpa < 2.0) or flag(thesis_resubmit)\ntext: Your name is called last, after a long pause and some whispering between examiners. But it is called. You graduate. You are a mage of Lumenhold, and nobody ever has to know the details.\n\n@ending ending_bystander\nslot: mystery\npriority: 20\ntitle: Someone Else's Lantern\nart: lantern\nwhen: not (flag(hollow_sealed) or flag(hollow_bargain) or flag(hollow_freed))\ntext: There was a spring frost, the year you graduated, and a night when the bell under the lake would not stop ringing. You were somewhere else: asleep, or studying, or holding a door shut for the first-years. Someone else went down with the lantern. You never learn their whole story, but every spring, when the lake thaws, you find yourself grateful to them.\n\n@ending ending_graduate\npriority: 0\ntitle: A Mage of Lumenhold\nart: tower\ntext: Robes, hats, a speech that runs long. You walk out of the gates of Lumenhold a qualified mage — not legendary, not disgraced, simply yourself, with four years of stories and a whole world to spend them in.\n\n@ending ending_teacher\npriority: 72\ntitle: The Teacher's Desk\nart: feather\nwhen: flag(career_teaching) and flag(career_accepted)\ntext: In the autumn you return to Lumenhold — not on the student barge, but on the faculty one. Your classroom has a window over the lake and a desk with somebody else's initials carved in it. On the first day a terrified first-year asks a question you once asked. You know exactly what to say.\n\n@ending ending_wanderer\npriority: 58\ntitle: The Road of Wonders\nart: tent\nwhen: flag(career_circus) and flag(career_accepted)\ntext: You join the Travelling Circus of Wonders as its youngest illusionist. For years you cross the kingdoms in a painted wagon, making dragons out of moonlight for children who have never seen magic. Every winter, a letter arrives from Lumenhold. You always write back.\n\n# ---------------- Relationship lines: Ilse Dumont's chain ----------------\n\n@ending ending_dumonts_reunited\nslot: relationships\npriority: 50\ntitle: Two Dumonts in the Photograph\nart: snow\nwhen: flag(dumont_reunited)\ntext: In the graduation photograph, at the edge of the crowd, two pale heads lean together: Kael, and the sister who was raised to open the door he refused. Neither of them is smiling at the camera. They are smiling at each other, which is rarer, and which you helped to happen.\n\n@ending ending_ilse_taken\nslot: relationships\npriority: 45\ntitle: A Chair Left Empty\nart: rain\nwhen: flag(ilse_taken) and not flag(dumont_reunited)\ntext: The man in grey took Ilse north, and no letter has come. At graduation Kael keeps the seat beside him empty without saying why. You know why. Some evenings, when the lake goes very still, you still look for a girl in white furs on the far shore.\n\n# ---------------- Personal lines (yearbook) ----------------\n\n@ending ending_thesis_hollowstar\nslot: personal\npriority: 35\ntitle: The Thesis They Locked Away\nart: book\nwhen: flag(thesis_hollowstar)\ntext: Your thesis on the founders' seal earned a distinction and then disappeared into the headmistress's tower. Its catalogue card in the Spire Library reads only \"Restricted\", in her handwriting. Three people in the world have read it. One of them still writes to you with questions.\n\n@ending ending_lumen\nslot: personal\npriority: 30\ntitle: A Lantern for Others\nart: lantern\nwhen: alignment >= 60\ntext: In the yearbook, under your portrait, someone has written: \"The one you went to when it mattered.\" First-years you barely remember will name their own lanterns after you. Lumenhold has a word for people like that. It is the same word as its name.\n\n@ending ending_hollow_touched\nslot: personal\npriority: 30\ntitle: The Shadow at Your Heel\nart: mask\nwhen: alignment <= -60\ntext: Your portrait in the yearbook is the only one where the candles behind you seem to have gone out. People step aside when you pass, not quite knowing why. The Hollow has learned your name, and you find, more and more often, that you answer to it.\n"},{"path":"content/events/alignment.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Alignment (design §8.2): Lumen (+) ↔ Hollow (−).\n#  Thresholds ±30 and ±60 open these events; characters tagged\n#  `virtuous` or `ruthless` (content/world/cast.grim) react to\n#  where you stand. Deep in the Hollow the way back is slow\n#  (the resource's drag), so the way-back events cost real effort.\n#  Hollow Star plot scenes that read alignment live in\n#  chain_hollowstar.grim (hs_grey_invitation, hs_grey_tests,\n#  hs_wardens_notice).\n# ============================================================\n\n# ---------------- Hollow (alignment <= -30) ----------------\n\n@event align_cold_candles\ntitle: The Candles Lean Away\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nwhen: alignment <= -30\nrepeat: 12\nweight: 10\nart: candle\ntags: hollow\nscene: A long stone corridor at dusk, every candle flame bending away from one student walking down the middle of it.\ntext: {You notice it on the east stair. As you pass, the candle flames bend away from you, all of them, like grass from a wind. A first-year on the landing notices too.|The candles lean away again. You have started to time it. It is getting faster.}\n> left: Let them lean\n  guile +small\n  alignment -small\n  renown -tiny\n  say: You walk on without looking. Behind you the first-year says nothing at all, which is its own kind of saying.\n> right: Stop and relight one by hand\n  mood -small\n  alignment +tiny\n  say: You cup a flame back to life with your palm. It flickers, considers you, and stays. It takes longer than it should.\n\n@event align_whispered_answer\ntitle: A Voice Over Your Shoulder\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nwhen: alignment <= -30 and term\nrepeat: 12\nweight: 13\nart: eye\ntags: hollow study\nscene: A student bent over a desk by candlelight, revision notes everywhere, and a cold patch of frost forming on the inkwell.\ntext: Revising late, you get stuck on the same question for the third time. Then the answer is simply there, in a voice that is almost yours, from just behind your left ear. The inkwell has frosted over.\n> left: Write it down\n  lore +small\n  alignment -small\n  say: It is right. Of course it is right. You write until dawn and never once get stuck, and in the morning you cannot remember learning any of it.\n> right: Close the book\n  mood -small\n  alignment +small\n  say: You close the book and sit with your hands flat on the cover until the frost on the inkwell melts. The question stays unanswered. It's yours, at least.\n\n@event align_maud_way_back\ntitle: Weeding, Dearie\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nactor: maud\ntrigger: social\nwhen: alignment <= -30 and met(maud)\nrepeat: 8\nweight: 14\nart: leaf\ntags: hollow nature\nscene: An old woman in a straw hat kneeling in a vegetable bed, handing a trowel up to a student who hasn't asked for one.\ntext: Old Maud looks at you for a long moment over her spectacles, the way she looks at a plant that's been kept in the wrong window. \"You've a cold look about you, dearie,\" she says, and holds out a trowel. \"Weeds don't pull themselves. Neither does the rest.\"\n> left: Kneel and start pulling\n  health -small\n  alignment +medium\n  rel maud +small\n  say: You weed until your knees ache and your hands smell of earth instead of frost. Maud hums the whole time. Nothing's fixed. Something has started.\n> right: \"I'm fine.\"\n  rel maud -small\n  mood +tiny\n  say: \"Course you are,\" Maud says, and goes back to her beans. The trowel stays in the soil beside her, handle towards you, all afternoon.\n\n@event align_reproach\ntitle: \"I Don't Know You Lately\"\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\ncast:\n  friend: any where tagged(friend, virtuous) and rel(friend) >= 6\nper: friend\ntrigger: social\nwhen: alignment <= -30\nrepeat: 12\nweight: 12\nart: heartbreak\ntags: social hollow\nscene: Two friends on a cold bench in the cloister, one looking straight ahead, the other looking at the first.\ntext: {friend} finds you in the cloister and sits without saying hello. \"People talk,\" {they} say{s} at last. \"I keep telling them they're wrong about you. I'd like to keep being right.\"\n> left: \"You are. I'll do better.\"\n  alignment +small\n  mood -small\n  rel friend +small\n  say: {friend} nods, slowly, and bumps {their} shoulder against yours. It is an apology and a warning at once.\n> right: \"Mind your own business.\"\n  rel friend -medium\n  alignment -tiny\n  guile +tiny\n  say: {friend} gets up and goes. {They} don't look back. That's what stays with you: {they} always used to look back.\n\n@event align_ruthless_nod\ntitle: Finally, Someone Who Gets It\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\ncast:\n  other: any where tagged(other, ruthless)\nper: other\ntrigger: social\nwhen: alignment <= -30\nrepeat: 12\nweight: 10\nart: mask\ntags: social hollow\nscene: A hard-faced student leaning on a pillar, giving another a slow, approving nod across a busy corridor.\ntext: {other} has always looked straight through you. Today {they} nod{s} as you pass, slow and approving, the way people nod at someone who has finally learned the rules of a game.\n> left: Nod back\n  rel other +medium\n  alignment -tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: The nod comes back to you. It feels like being let into a room. You can't tell whether the room is warm.\n> right: Keep walking\n  rel other -small\n  alignment +tiny\n  say: You keep walking. {other} snorts, as if you'd failed a test you didn't know you were sitting.\n\n@event align_northern_parcel\ntitle: A Parcel for the North\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nwhen: alignment <= -30\nrepeat: 12\nweight: 13\nart: pouch\ntags: hollow money\nscene: A hooded courier in a rain-dark alley behind the village, holding out a small parcel wrapped in black oilcloth and a purse.\ntext: {~A hooded courier stops you behind the bakery.|The hooded courier is waiting again, same alley, same black oilcloth.} \"For the boathouse, after dark,\" {~they say, and hold out the parcel and a purse.|they say. \"The families remember who is reliable.\"} The oilcloth is cold enough to ache.\n> left: Carry it\n  gold +medium\n  alignment -medium\n  health -tiny\n  say: You leave it on the boathouse step at midnight and don't look back. In the morning there's frost on the step in the shape of a hand, and the purse is heavier than it looked.\n> right: Walk away\n  mood -tiny\n  alignment +tiny\n  say: You leave the courier holding it. \"Pity,\" they say, without any pity at all, and the alley is empty when you look back.\n\n@event align_cruel_advantage\ntitle: Something You Could Use\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\ncast:\n  mark: any where tagged(mark, student) and rel(mark) < 12\nper: mark\nwhen: alignment <= -30\nrepeat: 12\nweight: 13\nart: scroll\ntags: social hollow\nscene: A crumpled letter on a library floor, a student's name on the envelope, another student bending to pick it up.\ntext: {mark} has dropped a letter in the library: something private, something {they} would hate the corridor to hear. You could hand it back. You could also keep it, and {they} would owe you, quietly, for a long time.\n> left: Keep it\n  guile +small\n  renown +tiny\n  alignment -small\n  rel mark -small\n  say: You fold it into your book. When you mention it, very lightly, {mark} goes pale and agrees to everything. It works. It keeps working.\n> right: Hand it back\n  rel mark +small\n  mood -tiny\n  alignment +small\n  say: You hand it back unread. {mark} stares at you, then at the letter, then says \"thank you\" as if it costs something. It does, for both of you.\n\n@event align_frost_in_the_spell\ntitle: The Cold That Helps\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nwhen: alignment <= -30 and term\nrepeat: 12\nweight: 13\nart: snow\ntags: hollow study\nscene: A student in an empty practice room, a spell flaring unnaturally bright in one hand, frost creeping up the sleeve to the elbow.\ntext: Practising alone, you notice that your spells come easier when you let the cold in, the cold that has been following you lately. It climbs your sleeve like ivy. The candle beside you leans away.\n> left: Let the cold in\n  arcana +small\n  alignment -small\n  health -tiny\n  say: The spell flares bright and clean, brighter than anything you've managed in daylight. It takes an hour for the feeling to come back into your fingers. You don't mind as much as you should.\n> right: Practise the slow way\n  arcana +tiny\n  mood -small\n  alignment +tiny\n  say: You shake the cold out of your hands and do it the slow way, badly, again and again. It is your own bad spell, at least.\n\n# ---------------- Lumen (alignment >= 30) ----------------\n\n@event align_asked_for_help\ntitle: \"You're the One People Ask\"\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nwhen: alignment >= 30\nrepeat: 12\nweight: 10\nart: lantern\ntags: social lumen\nscene: A nervous first-year with an armful of torn notes hovering at the door of a common room, looking at one particular older student.\ntext: {~A first-year hovers by your table with a torn essay and a face like a wet weekend.|A first-year you've never met asks if you're the one who helps people. Apparently you are.|Someone has written your name on the common-room board under \"ask them\".} \"Somebody said you'd know what to do,\" {~they say, as if that settles it.|they say, and then wait.}\n> left: Sit them down and help\n  health -small\n  renown +small\n  alignment +tiny\n  say: It takes the whole evening. They leave lighter than they came. So, oddly, do you.\n> right: Point them to a tutor\n  mood +tiny\n  alignment -tiny\n  say: You give them a name and a smile and get back to your own work. They look disappointed for a moment, then grateful anyway.\n\n@event align_warm_regard\ntitle: A Good Word\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\ncast:\n  friend: any where tagged(friend, virtuous) and rel(friend) >= 3\nper: friend\ntrigger: social\nwhen: alignment >= 30\nrepeat: 12\nweight: 10\nart: heart\ntags: social lumen\nscene: Two students sharing a pot of tea in a sunny window seat, one pushing the last biscuit towards the other.\ntext: {friend} pushes the last biscuit across to you. \"People talk, you know. About the kind things you do when you think nobody's looking,\" {they} say{s}. \"Don't make a face. It's good. Someone should say so.\"\n> left: Accept the biscuit, and the praise\n  rel friend +small\n  mood +small\n  gold -tiny\n  say: You eat the biscuit. {friend} looks very pleased. You buy the next pot.\n> right: Say anyone would have done the same\n  rel friend +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  alignment +tiny\n  say: \"Anyone would,\" you say. \"Nobody did,\" says {friend}, and lets you change the subject, but the look {they} give{s} you says it has only been postponed.\n\n@event align_saint_jibe\ntitle: \"Saint\"\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\ncast:\n  other: any where tagged(other, ruthless) and tagged(other, peer)\nper: other\ntrigger: social\nwhen: alignment >= 30\nrepeat: 12\nweight: 10\nart: scales\ntags: social lumen\nscene: A smirking student blocking a doorway with one arm, another student standing in front of them with an armful of books.\ntext: {other} blocks the doorway with one arm. \"Here comes the saint,\" {they} say{s}, loud enough for the corridor. \"Tell me, does it get heavy, being better than everyone?\"\n> left: Let it go\n  mood -small\n  alignment +tiny\n  say: You wait. Eventually {other} gets bored and drops the arm. Two first-years behind you look at you like you've done something brave. You're not sure you have.\n> right: Give as good as you get\n  rel other -small\n  mood +small\n  guile +tiny\n  say: Your answer is quick and much funnier than theirs. The corridor laughs. {other} goes red. It feels wonderful for about an hour.\n"},{"path":"content/events/calendar_2b.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  The four-year loop's fixed beats (design §4, §7; phase 2b).\n#  These replace the old calendar menus and exams, which are\n#  retired by tools/migrations/0001-retire-v02-calendar.js.\n# ============================================================\n\n# ---------------- Enrolment (design §7.3): two courses a term ----------------\n\n@event enrolment\ntitle: Enrolment\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: term and (month == 1 or month == 7)\nrepeat: 6\npriority: 80\ntype: menu\noffers: courses\npick: 2\nclass: story\ntone: light\nart: scroll\ntags: class study\nscene: The registrar's hall at the start of term, students queueing at long tables with course lists that rewrite themselves.\ntext: {term_index == 1: Your first term. The registrar slides a list across the desk: four courses open to first-years.|A new term, and a new list. Courses you've passed are gone from it; new levels have appeared.} Choose two. Course fees are eight crowns.\non_show:\n  gold -small\n> none: Take no courses this term\n  hint: Allowed. Your GPA will not thank you.\n  mood +small\n  say: You hand back the list blank. The registrar raises one eyebrow, which is the whole of her opinion.\n\n# ---------------- Club Fair (design §7.4): one club at a time ----------------\n\n@event club_fair_v3\ntitle: The Club Fair\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: month == 2\nrepeat: 12\npriority: 60\ntype: menu\noffers: clubs\nclass: story\ntone: light\nart: group\ntags: club festival\nscene: The courtyard full of recruiting tables, banners snapping, a duelling demonstration drawing a crowd.\ntext: The Club Fair fills the courtyard. Every table wants you; every captain swears theirs is the one that matters. {rel(lyra) >= 6: Lyra is at the Duelling Circle's table, pretending not to watch which way you walk.|}\n> none: {member(club): Stay with your club|Not this year}\n  hint: {member(club): Another year with the same table.|You can join at next year's fair.}\n  say: {member(club): {~You stop by your own club's table to help hand out leaflets. Someone asks you if it's worth joining. You say yes and mean it.|You walk past every other table with the ease of someone already spoken for.}|You collect three leaflets and a free biscuit, and sign nothing.}\n> quit: Resign from your club\n  when: member(club)\n  hint: You can join again at next year's fair, as a new member.\n  leave club\n  say: {~You hand back your club pin at the table where you got it. It feels smaller than it did.|You resign. Your evenings are yours again, and nobody will expect you anywhere.}\n\n# ---------------- The Job Board (design §7.4): visiting it is the change event ----------------\n\n@event job_board_visit\ntitle: The Job Board\nplace: job_board\nrepeat: 1\nweight: 30\ntype: menu\noffers: jobs\nclass: story\ntone: light\nart: paper\ntags: job money\nscene: A cork board crowded with notices, a student running a finger down a column of shift times.\ntext: Notices for work, three layers deep: the library, the Gilded Newt, Pell & Daughters, the courier loft. Each wants someone reliable. Each says so twice.\n> look: Just looking\n  say: You read every notice twice and take none of them down.\n> quit: Hand in your notice\n  when: member(job)\n  hint: You can find work again later.\n  leave job\n  mood +small\n  say: You hand in your notice. Your evenings are yours again; so is your empty purse.\n\n# ---------------- Break plans (design §4.3) ----------------\n\n@event break_plan\ntitle: The Break\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: break and (month == 5 or month == 11) and not wrapup\nrepeat: 6\npriority: 75\ntype: menu\nclass: story\ntone: warm\nart: sun\ntags: home\nscene: The academy gates thrown open, barges loading trunks, a few students lingering on the steps with nowhere they'd rather be.\ntext: {month == 5: The winter break.|The summer break.} Two months without lectures. The castle empties, mostly.\n> castle: Stay at the castle\n  hint: Quiet corridors and secrets. Board 8 crowns a month.\n  gain plan_castle\n  say: The castle in the holidays is a different building: louder echoes, kinder staff, doors you've never seen open.\n> home: Go home\n  hint: Family, old friends, free board.\n  gain plan_home\n  say: The barge, the coach, the last mile on foot. Home is smaller than you remember, and warmer.\n> work: Work on campus\n  hint: A holiday gig that pays well. Board 8 crowns a month.\n  gain plan_work\n  queue holiday_gigs now\n  say: You trade the holidays for wages. Your feet will remember it.\n> intern: Start your internship\n  when: member(internship) and month == 11\n  hint: A stipend, a supervisor and a project.\n  gain plan_internship\n  say: You pack a trunk of notebooks and take the long coach to the capital.\n\n# ---------------- Track notices (pushed by the engine at term end) ----------------\n\n@event course_exam\ntitle: Exam Day\ntrigger: queued\nclass: story\ntone: tense\nart: scroll\ntags: study class\nscene: A silent exam hall in late light, rows of bent heads, an hourglass on the invigilator's desk.\ntext: Exam day for {track}. The hall is silent except for quills and somebody's knee bouncing under a desk. {~The paper is long.|The first question is one you revised.|The first question is one you didn't.|The invigilator turns the hourglass with a click you feel in your teeth.}\n> left: Answer what you know, carefully\n  sit_exam steady\n  say: {~You write slowly: everything you're sure of, and nothing you aren't.|You check every answer twice and hand in at the bell.}\n> right: Go for top marks\n  sit_exam wild\n  say: {~You take the hardest question first and gamble the essay on an idea you had at breakfast.|You write the answer you'd want to read, not the safe one.}\n\n@event track_warning\ntitle: A Word About Your Attendance\ntrigger: queued\nclass: incident\ntone: tense\nart: paper\ntags: job club\nscene: A folded note on official paper, the words \"performance\" and \"concern\" underlined twice.\ntext: A note about {track}: your attendance and your efforts have slipped, and it has been noticed. One more term like this and there will be consequences.\n> left: Promise to do better\n  mood -small\n  say: You write back a promise in your neatest hand. Now you'll have to keep it.\n> right: Let it slide\n  perform track -small\n  mood +tiny\n  say: You put the note in a drawer. It doesn't stop existing in the drawer.\n\n# Every job at term end (playtest 2, M33): stay on for another term, or resign.\n@event job_term_review\ntitle: Another Term?\ntrigger: queued\nclass: milestone\ntone: light\nart: coin\ntags: job money\nscene: A staff rota pinned to a door, last term's names crossed off, a blank column waiting for next term's.\ntext: The term is over, and so is the rota for {track}. A fresh one goes up for next term, and there's a gap where your name usually goes. {~Nobody would blame you either way.|Your feet have an opinion. So does your purse.|Someone has already pencilled a question mark next to it.}\n> left: Stay on next term\n  say: {~You write your name in the gap. Another term of shifts, another term of wages.|Your name goes back on the rota, in ink this time.}\n> right: Resign\n  leave track\n  mood +tiny\n  say: {~You hand in your notice, politely. The evenings are yours again; the purse is lighter for it.|You leave the gap empty. You can always find work again, if you need to.}\n\n@event track_promotion\ntitle: A Step Up\ntrigger: queued\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\nart: trophy\ntags: job club\nscene: A small ceremony in a cramped room, someone pinning a badge to a student's collar while the others applaud.\ntext: {track} wants to promote you. More responsibility, more respect, and in the right places, more pay.\n> left: Accept\n  promote track\n  renown +small\n  mood +small\n  say: The badge is a little crooked. You don't straighten it.\n> right: Not yet\n  mood +tiny\n  say: \"When you're ready, then.\" They mean it. The offer will come again.\n\n# ---------------- Research internship (design §7.5; the Royal Library is the slice host) ----------------\n\n@event intern_application\ntitle: Applications Open\ntrigger: auto\nrepeat: 2\npriority: 50\ntype: menu\npick: 6\nwhen: month == 7 and year >= 2 and year <= 3\nclass: story\ntone: light\nart: letter\ntags: job study\nscene: A cluster of notices with wax seals pinned above all the others: summer research posts, each with its own demands.\ntext: Summer research posts are open. Six notices, six seals, six lists of requirements. Apply to as many as you like: the first letter is an evening's work, and every one after it costs another evening and the postage.\non_show:\n  intern_letters = 0\n> royal: The Royal Library\n  when: level(astromancy) >= 2 and not member(intern_royal_library)\n  locked: {member(intern_royal_library): They've already asked you back.|Needs Astromancy II.}\n  hint: GPA 2.5, an interview on lore, a love of indexes.\n  apply intern_royal_library\n  intern_letters += 1\n  if intern_letters >= 2:\n    gold -small\n    mood -tiny\n    say: {~Another letter, another evening, another stamp. Your hand aches in a very particular place.|You write it once, cross out half, write it again. The postage comes out of your own purse.}\n  else:\n    charm +tiny\n    say: You write the letter three times and send the best one. Probably the best one.\n> conclave: The Conclave Laboratory\n  when: level(evocation) >= 2 and not member(intern_conclave)\n  locked: {member(intern_conclave): They've already asked you back.|Needs Evocation II.}\n  hint: GPA 3.0, an interview on arcana, and a word from Thorne helps.\n  apply intern_conclave\n  intern_letters += 1\n  if intern_letters >= 2:\n    gold -small\n    mood -tiny\n    say: {~Another letter, another evening, another stamp. Your hand aches in a very particular place.|You write it once, cross out half, write it again. The postage comes out of your own purse.}\n  else:\n    charm +tiny\n    say: You write the letter three times and send the best one. Probably the best one.\n> alchemists: The Guild of Alchemists' Works\n  when: level(alchemy) >= 2 and not member(intern_alchemists)\n  locked: {member(intern_alchemists): They've already asked you back.|Needs Alchemy II.}\n  hint: GPA 2.0, an interview on alchemy, and a strong stomach.\n  apply intern_alchemists\n  intern_letters += 1\n  if intern_letters >= 2:\n    gold -small\n    mood -tiny\n    say: {~Another letter, another evening, another stamp. Your hand aches in a very particular place.|You write it once, cross out half, write it again. The postage comes out of your own purse.}\n  else:\n    charm +tiny\n    say: You write the letter three times and send the best one. Probably the best one.\n> expedition: The Observatory Expedition\n  when: level(astromancy) >= 2 and not member(intern_expedition)\n  locked: {member(intern_expedition): They've already asked you back.|Needs Astromancy II.}\n  hint: An interview on vigor, and the legs for a plateau.\n  apply intern_expedition\n  intern_letters += 1\n  if intern_letters >= 2:\n    gold -small\n    mood -tiny\n    say: {~Another letter, another evening, another stamp. Your hand aches in a very particular place.|You write it once, cross out half, write it again. The postage comes out of your own purse.}\n  else:\n    charm +tiny\n    say: You write the letter three times and send the best one. Probably the best one.\n> field: The Beastcraft Field Station\n  when: level(beastcraft) >= 2 and not member(intern_field_station)\n  locked: {member(intern_field_station): They've already asked you back.|Needs Beastcraft II.}\n  hint: An interview on vigor. Boots provided, mostly.\n  apply intern_field_station\n  intern_letters += 1\n  if intern_letters >= 2:\n    gold -small\n    mood -tiny\n    say: {~Another letter, another evening, another stamp. Your hand aches in a very particular place.|You write it once, cross out half, write it again. The postage comes out of your own purse.}\n  else:\n    charm +tiny\n    say: You write the letter three times and send the best one. Probably the best one.\n> thornwick: The Thornwick Exchange\n  when: level(illusion) >= 2 and not member(intern_thornwick)\n  locked: {member(intern_thornwick): They've already asked you back.|Needs Illusion II.}\n  hint: GPA 2.5, an interview on charm. Bring a thick skin.\n  apply intern_thornwick\n  intern_letters += 1\n  if intern_letters >= 2:\n    gold -small\n    mood -tiny\n    say: {~Another letter, another evening, another stamp. Your hand aches in a very particular place.|You write it once, cross out half, write it again. The postage comes out of your own purse.}\n  else:\n    charm +tiny\n    say: You write the letter three times and send the best one. Probably the best one.\n> none: Not this year\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You let the notices flap. There's always next year, until there isn't.\n\n@event intern_offer\ntitle: Letters With Seals\ntrigger: auto\ntype: menu\nwhen: month == 9 and year >= 2 and year <= 3 and (applied(internship) or (guile >= 35 and rel(isolde) >= 6 and not member(intern_unseen_office)))\nrepeat: 20\npriority: 55\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\nart: letter\ntags: study\nscene: Heavy envelopes with wax seals on a dormitory desk, one of them plain grey with no seal at all.\ntext: The spring post brings answers. {applied(internship): There are sealed letters on your desk. Which do you open first?|There are no more sealed letters.} {guile >= 35 and rel(isolde) >= 6 and not member(intern_unseen_office): There is also a plain grey envelope you never asked for.|}\n> royal: Letter from the Royal Library\n  when: applied(intern_royal_library)\n  hint: They asked for a GPA of 2.5. The interview tests lore.\n  if gpa >= 2.5:\n    challenge research using lore medium:\n      win:\n        join intern_royal_library\n        renown +small\n        leave intern_conclave\n        leave intern_alchemists\n        leave intern_expedition\n        leave intern_field_station\n        leave intern_thornwick\n        say: \"We are pleased.\" You read the letter until the seal cracks. A summer in the oldest archives in the realm.\n      lose:\n        leave intern_royal_library\n        say: \"We regret.\" The interview went well, they write, and not quite well enough.\n        if applied(internship):\n          queue intern_offer now\n  else:\n    leave intern_royal_library\n    say: \"We regret.\" They wanted a GPA of 2.5, and they say so twice.\n    if applied(internship):\n      queue intern_offer now\n> conclave: Letter from the Conclave\n  when: applied(intern_conclave)\n  hint: They asked for a GPA of 3.0. The interview tests arcana. A word from Thorne would help.\n  if gpa >= 3.0:\n    if rel(thorne) >= 6:\n      challenge research using arcana easy:\n        win:\n          join intern_conclave\n          renown +small\n          leave intern_royal_library\n          leave intern_alchemists\n          leave intern_expedition\n          leave intern_field_station\n          leave intern_thornwick\n          say: \"We are pleased.\" You read the letter until the seal cracks. A summer in spell research under the Seven Towers.\n        lose:\n          leave intern_conclave\n          say: \"We regret.\" The interview went well, they write, and not quite well enough.\n          if applied(internship):\n            queue intern_offer now\n    else:\n      challenge research using arcana hard:\n        win:\n          join intern_conclave\n          renown +small\n          leave intern_royal_library\n          leave intern_alchemists\n          leave intern_expedition\n          leave intern_field_station\n          leave intern_thornwick\n          say: \"We are pleased.\" You read the letter until the seal cracks. A summer in spell research under the Seven Towers.\n        lose:\n          leave intern_conclave\n          say: \"We regret.\" The interview went well, they write, and not quite well enough.\n          if applied(internship):\n            queue intern_offer now\n  else:\n    leave intern_conclave\n    say: \"We regret.\" They wanted a GPA of 3.0, and they say so twice.\n    if applied(internship):\n      queue intern_offer now\n> alchemists: Letter from the Alchemists' Works\n  when: applied(intern_alchemists)\n  hint: They asked for a GPA of 2.0. The interview tests alchemy.\n  if gpa >= 2.0:\n    challenge research using alchemy medium:\n      win:\n        join intern_alchemists\n        renown +small\n        leave intern_royal_library\n        leave intern_conclave\n        leave intern_expedition\n        leave intern_field_station\n        leave intern_thornwick\n        say: \"We are pleased.\" You read the letter until the seal cracks. A summer in the great vats of the Guild.\n      lose:\n        leave intern_alchemists\n        say: \"We regret.\" The interview went well, they write, and not quite well enough.\n        if applied(internship):\n          queue intern_offer now\n  else:\n    leave intern_alchemists\n    say: \"We regret.\" They wanted a GPA of 2.0, and they say so twice.\n    if applied(internship):\n      queue intern_offer now\n> expedition: Letter from the Expedition\n  when: applied(intern_expedition)\n  hint: The interview tests vigor.\n  challenge research using vigor medium:\n    win:\n      join intern_expedition\n      renown +small\n      leave intern_royal_library\n      leave intern_conclave\n      leave intern_alchemists\n      leave intern_field_station\n      leave intern_thornwick\n      say: \"We are pleased.\" You read the letter until the seal cracks. A summer of sky charts on the high plateau.\n    lose:\n      leave intern_expedition\n      say: \"We regret.\" The interview went well, they write, and not quite well enough.\n      if applied(internship):\n        queue intern_offer now\n> field: Letter from the Field Station\n  when: applied(intern_field_station)\n  hint: The interview tests vigor.\n  challenge research using vigor medium:\n    win:\n      join intern_field_station\n      renown +small\n      leave intern_royal_library\n      leave intern_conclave\n      leave intern_alchemists\n      leave intern_expedition\n      leave intern_thornwick\n      say: \"We are pleased.\" You read the letter until the seal cracks. A summer in the moors, the heather and the creatures in them.\n    lose:\n      leave intern_field_station\n      say: \"We regret.\" The interview went well, they write, and not quite well enough.\n      if applied(internship):\n        queue intern_offer now\n> thornwick: Letter from Thornwick\n  when: applied(intern_thornwick)\n  hint: They asked for a GPA of 2.5. The interview tests charm.\n  if gpa >= 2.5:\n    challenge research using charm medium:\n      win:\n        join intern_thornwick\n        renown +small\n        leave intern_royal_library\n        leave intern_conclave\n        leave intern_alchemists\n        leave intern_expedition\n        leave intern_field_station\n        say: \"We are pleased.\" You read the letter until the seal cracks. A summer in the rival academy's illusion laboratory.\n      lose:\n        leave intern_thornwick\n        say: \"We regret.\" The interview went well, they write, and not quite well enough.\n        if applied(internship):\n          queue intern_offer now\n  else:\n    leave intern_thornwick\n    say: \"We regret.\" They wanted a GPA of 2.5, and they say so twice.\n    if applied(internship):\n      queue intern_offer now\n> unseen: Open the plain envelope\n  when: guile >= 35 and rel(isolde) >= 6 and not member(intern_unseen_office)\n  join intern_unseen_office\n  guile +small\n  leave intern_royal_library\n  leave intern_conclave\n  leave intern_alchemists\n  leave intern_expedition\n  leave intern_field_station\n  leave intern_thornwick\n  say: Inside: a time, an address and no signature. The paper is blank again by morning. You go anyway.\n> decline: Fold the letters away\n  leave intern_royal_library\n  leave intern_conclave\n  leave intern_alchemists\n  leave intern_expedition\n  leave intern_field_station\n  leave intern_thornwick\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You fold them carefully. Next year, perhaps.\n\n# An internship lasts one summer (M16). The host asks you back only after a strong one.\n@event intern_asked_back\ntitle: Asked Back\ntrigger: queued\nwhen: year <= 3\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\nart: letter\ntags: study job\nscene: A letter on good paper with a familiar seal, the words \"next summer\" underlined once, neatly.\ntext: A letter from {member(intern_royal_library): the Royal Library Archives|{member(intern_conclave): the Conclave Laboratory|{member(intern_alchemists): the Guild of Alchemists' Works|{member(intern_expedition): the Observatory Expedition|{member(intern_field_station): the Beastcraft Field Station|{member(intern_thornwick): the Thornwick Exchange|the office with no name}}}}}}. They were pleased with your summer, and the post is yours again next summer if you want it.\n> left: Write back yes\n  renown +tiny\n  say: {~You write back the same evening. Next summer is spoken for, and you find you're glad.|\"Yes,\" you write, and then three more lines to make it sound less eager.}\n> right: Thank them, and decline\n  leave internship\n  mood +tiny\n  say: {~You thank them warmly and say no. Next summer is yours to spend, or to apply for something new.|A polite no. You keep the letter anyway; it's good to be asked.}\n\n# ---------------- Thesis (design §7.6) ----------------\n\n@event thesis_setup\ntitle: Choosing a Thesis\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: year == 4 and month == 1 and not member(thesis)\nrepeat: 12\npriority: 85\ntype: menu\nclass: story\ntone: tense\nart: book\ntags: study\nscene: A professor's study, three topic proposals laid out on the desk like cards.\ntext: The final year opens with a thesis. Before you choose this term's courses, Professor Thorne lays out three topics, at three levels of ambition.\n> safe: A careful survey\n  hint: Easier, and it will read as easier.\n  join thesis\n  flag thesis_safe\n  say: A sensible topic. Thorne says \"Sensible,\" the way other people say \"Disappointing.\"\n> solid: An original study\n  hint: The usual path.\n  join thesis\n  flag thesis_solid\n  say: Thorne nods. \"Good. Bring me something I don't already know.\"\n> ambitious: Something no one has done\n  hint: Harder, and worth more.\n  join thesis\n  flag thesis_ambitious\n  say: Thorne is quiet for a long time. \"Hm,\" he says, and for once it sounds like hope.\n\n@event thesis_crunch\ntitle: The Crunch\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: final_year and month == 9 and member(thesis) and progress(thesis) < 60\nrepeat: 12\npriority: 40\nclass: incident\ntone: tense\nart: hourglass\ntags: study\nscene: A thesis carrel at dawn, a candle burned to a stub, drafts on the floor and a student asleep on the keyboard of a typing-quill.\ntext: The defence is next month and your thesis is not ready. Your advisor's note is three words long: \"Well? Final draft.\"\n> left: Work through the nights\n  progress thesis +large\n  health -medium\n  mood -medium\n  say: You stop seeing daylight. The draft gets better and you get worse, which seems to be how this works.\n> right: Hand in what you have\n  mood +tiny\n  progress thesis +small\n  say: You tidy what's there and accept the gaps. Your advisor sighs, which is not the same as no.\n\n@event thesis_defence\ntitle: The Defence\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: final_year and month == 10 and member(thesis)\nrepeat: 12\npriority: 65\nclass: story\ntone: tense\nart: scroll\ntags: study exam\nscene: A semicircular table with three examiners, a student standing at a lectern with a stack of notes.\ntext: Three examiners, one lectern, and a year of work. {progress(thesis) >= 60: You know this material better than anyone in the room.|There are gaps, and you know where they are.}\n> left: Defend it\n  hint: A safe topic is easier to defend and earns less; an ambitious one is harder and earns more.\n  if not (flag(thesis_safe) or flag(thesis_ambitious)):\n    challenge negotiate using lore hard:\n      win:\n        flag thesis_distinction\n        renown +large\n        say: They stop asking questions and start asking for copies. Distinction.\n      partial:\n        flag thesis_passed\n        renown +small\n        say: A pass, with one examiner's handwriting all over your conclusion.\n      lose:\n        flag thesis_resubmit\n        mood -large\n        say: They send you away to resubmit. It isn't the end. It feels like it.\n  elif flag(thesis_safe):\n    challenge negotiate using lore medium:\n      win:\n        flag thesis_distinction\n        renown +medium\n        say: A careful survey, carefully defended. They give you a distinction and the faint air of people who wished you'd risked more.\n      partial:\n        flag thesis_passed\n        renown +tiny\n        say: A pass. Nobody argues with a careful survey. Nobody remembers it either.\n      lose:\n        flag thesis_resubmit\n        mood -medium\n        say: Even a safe topic has its gaps, and they find every one. Resubmit.\n  else:\n    challenge negotiate using lore heroic:\n      win:\n        flag thesis_distinction\n        renown +huge\n        if clue(seven_locks) + clue(star_key) + clue(ilse_truth) >= 3:\n          flag thesis_hollowstar\n        say: Something no one has done, and now someone has. The committee stands when you finish. One of them is crying, and pretending not to.\n      partial:\n        flag thesis_passed\n        renown +medium\n        say: They pass it, arguing among themselves about whether it is brilliant or mad. Possibly both.\n      lose:\n        flag thesis_resubmit\n        mood -large\n        renown -small\n        say: You reached too far, and they tell you so, at length. Resubmit. The idea was good. The proof wasn't there yet.\n  # The defence closes the thesis: its lab events stop (B30).\n  leave thesis\n\n# ---------------- Festivals that take a visit (design §4.4) ----------------\n\n@event lantern_night_v3\ntitle: Lantern Night\nplace: lakeshore\nreplaces: visit2\nwhen: month == 2\nrepeat: 12\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\nart: lantern\ntags: festival\nscene: The Mirror Lake at night, hundreds of paper lanterns drifting over the water, students crowding the shore.\ntext: Lantern Night. The whole academy gathers on the lakeshore with paper lanterns, each carrying a wish out over the water. {month == 2 and year == 1: Your first one.|Another year, another wish.}\n> left: Wish for something selfish\n  mood +medium\n  renown +tiny\n  say: Your lantern goes out over the water and keeps its secret. You feel lighter for having asked.\n> right: Wish for someone else\n  mood +small\n  alignment +small\n  say: You don't tell anyone who. The lantern seems to know; it flies straight.\n\n# ---------------- Calendar beats ----------------\n\n@event year_four\ntitle: The Final Year\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: turn == 37\npriority: 90\nclass: story\ntone: warm\nart: tower\ntags: story\nscene: The seven towers at dawn from the barge, the same view as the first day, but the student watching it has changed.\ntext: The barge again, the towers again, and a thesis waiting. The first-years on the deck look at you the way you once looked at the fourth-years. At the opening feast the headmistress says, \"Fourth years, this is your last year in these halls. What you become here, you will be for a long time.\" She looks, for a moment, directly at you.\n> ok: Here we go\n  mood +small\n  say: You know which steps creak. You know which doors stick. One more year to learn the rest.\n"},{"path":"content/events/calendar.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Calendar: the academy year's fixed beats.\n#  Most are `trigger: auto` — forced into the month's deck\n#  whenever their `when` is true (and `repeat` allows).\n#  Month 1 Harvestmoon ... 12 Starfall. 11–12 are summer recess.\n# ============================================================\n\n@event intro_arrival\ntitle: The Sky-Barge\nclass: story\ntone: light\ntrigger: queued\nart: tower\ntags: story\nscene: A wooden barge with patched sails drifting between clouds toward seven impossibly tall towers above a mirror-still lake.\ntext: The barge drops through the clouds and there it is: Lumenhold, seven towers stitched together with bridges of light. A first-year beside you is quietly being sick over the side. A prefect with a ledger counts heads and frowns at yours.\n> ok: Step onto the landing\n  say: Your boots touch stone that hums faintly underfoot. Four years. Forty-eight moons.\n\n@event choose_origin\ntitle: Who Were You Before?\nclass: story\ntone: light\ntrigger: queued\ntype: menu\nart: mirror\ntags: story\nscene: A tall enchanted mirror in the entrance hall, showing a different version of the viewer in each pane.\ntext: The Sorting Mirror in the entrance hall does not sort you into anything. It simply asks, in a voice like cold water, who you were before you came here. It waits.\n> noble: The younger child of a minor noble house\n  hint: Gold, charm and a name people recognise.\n  gain origin_noble\n  say: The mirror shows you a crest you have tried to outgrow. It approves, faintly.\n> scholar: A scholarship student from the provinces\n  hint: Lore and study. The stipend depends on good marks.\n  gain origin_scholar\n  say: The mirror shows you a candle burning very late in a very small room.\n> hedge: Raised by a hedge-witch in the marshes\n  hint: Alchemy, good spirits, an eye for herbs.\n  gain origin_hedge\n  say: The mirror smells, briefly, of wet moss and woodsmoke. You feel at home.\n> street: Raised on the streets of Port Calder\n  hint: Guile and grit. Little gold.\n  gain origin_street\n  say: The mirror shows a pickpocket's quick hands. You check your pockets out of habit.\n> merchant: The heir to a trading company\n  hint: Plenty of gold, a nose for bargains.\n  gain origin_merchant\n  say: The mirror shows ledgers, ships and your mother's disappointed face. Still: gold.\n\n@event sorting_mirror\ntitle: The Sorting Mirror\nclass: story\ntone: light\ntrigger: queued\nart: mirror\ntags: story\nscene: A tall enchanted mirror in the entrance hall, showing a different version of the viewer in each pane.\ntext: The Sorting Mirror in the entrance hall does not sort you into anything. It simply asks, in a voice like cold water, who you were before you came here. You tell it.\n> ok: Look into the mirror\n  say: {has(origin_noble): The mirror shows you a crest you have tried to outgrow. It approves, faintly.|{has(origin_scholar): The mirror shows you a candle burning very late in a very small room.|{has(origin_hedge): The mirror smells, briefly, of wet moss and woodsmoke. You feel at home.|{has(origin_street): The mirror shows a pickpocket's quick hands. You check your pockets out of habit.|The mirror shows ledgers, ships and your mother's disappointed face. Still: gold.}}}}\n\n@event wrapup_begins\ntitle: The Last Weeks\nclass: story\ntone: warm\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: wrapup and month == 11\npriority: 70\nart: tower\ntags: story\nscene: An emptying castle in late summer light, trunks stacked in a corridor, a notice pinned to the great hall door.\ntext: A notice on the great hall door: classes are over, duties are done, and final-years may stay on until graduation, board on the house. The castle is yours for one last summer.\n> ok: Make the most of it\n  mood +small\n  say: You walk the long way to breakfast, past every door you ever got lost behind.\n\n@event choose_elective\n# retired in phase 2b (was \"month == 1 or month == 6\"); replaced by enrolment (offers: courses, pick: 2)\ntitle: Choosing an Elective\nclass: story\ntone: light\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: false\nrepeat: 5\npriority: 45\ntype: menu\nart: scroll\ntags: study class retired\nscene: A long noticeboard covered in fluttering enchanted parchment, each sheet trying to shoulder the others aside.\ntext: The registrar's noticeboard flaps with sign-up sheets for this term's electives. Core lectures are compulsory; your elective is yours to choose. The sheets jostle for your attention.\n> pyro: Pyromancy\n  hint: +Arcana each month, small burns. Taught by Professor Thorne.\n  gain class_pyromancy\n> tide: Tidecalling\n  hint: +Arcana, +Vigor, lakeside calm.\n  gain class_tidecalling\n> illusion: Illusion & Glamour\n  hint: +Guile, +Charm.\n  gain class_illusion\n> alchemy: Practical Alchemy\n  hint: +Alchemy.\n  gain class_alchemy\n> divination: Divination\n  hint: +Lore, occasional gloomy omens.\n  gain class_divination\n> artifice: Artifice\n  hint: +Alchemy, +Lore.\n  gain class_artifice\n> beast: Beastcraft\n  hint: +Vigor, +Mood. Smells of hay.\n  gain class_beastcraft\n> astro: Astromancy\n  hint: +Lore, late nights.\n  gain class_astromancy\n> healing: Healing Arts\n  hint: +Alchemy, +Lore, a little health.\n  gain class_healing\n> umbral: Umbral Theory (restricted)\n  hint: Requires Lore 20 or second year. +Arcana, dark moods.\n  when: lore >= 20 or year >= 2\n  locked: Needs Lore 20, or your second year.\n  gain class_umbral\n  flag took_umbral\n\n@event club_fair\n# retired in phase 2b (was \"month == 2\"); replaced by club_fair_v3 (offers: clubs)\ntitle: The Club Fair\nclass: story\ntone: light\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: false\nrepeat: 12\npriority: 40\ntype: menu\nart: group\ntags: social club retired\nscene: The great courtyard filled with banners and stalls: a duelist juggling sparks, a choir floating a foot off the ground, a girl handing out newspapers that shout headlines.\ntext: The courtyard is a riot of banners. Every club wants you, or at least your membership fee. A broom whizzes overhead trailing a SKYRACE TRYOUTS banner; somewhere a choir is harmonising with a cat.\n> duel: Dueling Circle\n  hint: +Vigor +Arcana, bruises. Home of the Spring Tournament.\n  gain club_dueling\n  meet lyra\n> choir: Choir of the Spheres\n  hint: +Charm, +Mood.\n  gain club_choir\n> sky: Skyrace Squad\n  hint: +Vigor, crowd glory, crashes.\n  gain club_skyrace\n> ledger: The Lumen Ledger\n  hint: +Guile +Charm. Nessa Quill's student paper.\n  gain club_ledger\n  meet nessa\n> garden: Verdant Circle\n  hint: +Alchemy +Mood. Greenhouses and gossip.\n  gain club_garden\n  meet tobias\n> stars: Stargazers\n  hint: +Lore +Mood.\n  gain club_stargazers\n> none: Keep your evenings free\n  hint: No club (leaves your current club, if any).\n  lose club_dueling\n  lose club_choir\n  lose club_skyrace\n  lose club_ledger\n  lose club_garden\n  lose club_stargazers\n  mood +tiny\n\n@event job_board\n# retired in phase 2b (was \"month == 3 or month == 9\"); replaced by job_board_visit (the Job Board place)\ntitle: The Job Board\nclass: story\ntone: light\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: false\nrepeat: 5\npriority: 35\ntype: menu\nart: coin\ntags: job money retired\nscene: A cork board outside the bursar's office, papered with notices; one is on fire, one is wriggling.\ntext: Rent is ten crowns a month and your purse is a fragile thing. The board outside the bursar's office offers honest work, and some that is merely work.\n> library: Library Assistant — 10 gold/month\n  hint: +Lore, a little dull.\n  gain job_library\n> tavern: The Gilded Newt — 16 gold/month\n  hint: +Charm, tiring evenings in the village.\n  gain job_tavern\n> apothecary: Apothecary Clerk — 12 gold/month\n  hint: Requires Alchemy 12. +Alchemy.\n  when: alchemy >= 12\n  locked: Needs Alchemy 12.\n  gain job_apothecary\n> stables: Familiar Stables — 11 gold/month\n  hint: +Vigor. Things bite.\n  gain job_stables\n> tutor: First-Year Tutor — 12 gold/month\n  hint: Requires Lore 18. +Standing.\n  when: lore >= 18\n  locked: Needs Lore 18.\n  gain job_tutor\n> courier: Broom Courier — 18 gold/month\n  hint: Requires Vigor 14. Dangerous.\n  when: vigor >= 14\n  locked: Needs Vigor 14.\n  gain job_courier\n> none: No job (quit any current one)\n  lose job_library\n  lose job_tavern\n  lose job_apothecary\n  lose job_stables\n  lose job_tutor\n  lose job_courier\n\n@event lantern_night\n# retired in phase 2b (was \"month == 2\"); replaced by lantern_night_v3 (a festival at the lakeshore)\ntitle: Lantern Night\nclass: story\ntone: light\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: false\nrepeat: 12\nart: lantern\ntags: festival social retired\nscene: Hundreds of paper lanterns rising off the lake at dusk, each with a wish written on it in glowing ink.\ntext: On Lantern Night every student writes a wish on a paper lantern and lets it go over the lake. Rumour says the lanterns that sink were wishes the lake wanted for itself.\n> left: Wish for something selfish\n  chance 50:\n    gold +large\n    say: Your lantern rises high. The next morning you find coins in your shoe. The lake is generous, and a little creepy.\n  else:\n    mood -small\n    say: Your lantern sinks with a hiss. Something under the water now knows what you want.\n    flag lake_knows\n    alignment -small\n> right: Wish for someone else\n  alignment +tiny\n  mood +medium\n  renown +small\n  say: Your lantern rises and catches the wind. Across the shore, someone you have never met laughs out loud for no reason.\n\n@event kael_arrives\ntitle: The Boy From the North\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: turn == 3\npriority: 30\nactor: kael\nart: snow\ntags: mystery hollowstar\nchain: hollowstar\nscene: A pale young man in a fur-lined coat standing at the door of the dining hall, frost spreading from his boots across the floor.\ntext: Mid-term, the dining hall doors open and frost crawls across the floor. A boy in a fur coat walks in, takes the empty seat beside you, and says nothing at all. The headmistress introduces him as Kael Dumont, a transfer. She does not say from where.\n> left: Leave him be\n  meet kael\n  rel kael +tiny\n  say: You eat in silence. When you leave, your cup of tea has frozen solid.\n> right: Offer him the bread\n  meet kael\n  rel kael +medium\n  say: He looks at the bread as if no one has offered him anything in a long time. \"...Thank you.\" His voice sounds like wind over snow.\n  flag kael_kindness\n\n@event winter_trials\n# retired in phase 2b (was \"month == 4\"); replaced by term-end exams (engine, design §7.3)\ntitle: The Winter Trials\nclass: story\ntone: tense\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: false\nrepeat: 12\npriority: 40\nart: hourglass\ntags: study exam retired\nscene: A vast examination hall where floating quills hover over a hundred desks and the windows are thick with frost.\ntext: The Winter Trials. A hundred desks, a hundred floating quills, one very old proctor who may be asleep. The first question asks you to explain the Third Law of Correspondence. The second asks you to demonstrate it.\n> left: Lean on theory\n  challenge exam medium:\n    win:\n      study +huge\n      mood +small\n      say: Your answer is so thorough the quill gets a cramp. Top marks.\n    lose:\n      study -large\n      mood -small\n      say: You write three pages and realise halfway that you have been answering a different question.\n> right: Lean on practice\n  challenge spell medium:\n    win:\n      study +huge\n      renown +small\n      say: Your demonstration is clean and bright. The proctor wakes up and applauds.\n    lose:\n      study -large\n      health -small\n      say: The demonstration backfires. Politely, but with smoke.\n\n@event midwinter_ball\ntitle: The Midwinter Ball\nclass: story\ntone: warm\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: month == 4\nrepeat: 12\npriority: 40\ntype: menu\nart: music\ntags: festival romance social\nscene: The great hall transformed: an ice-crystal chandelier, couples waltzing a foot above the floor, snow falling indoors without ever landing.\ntext: The great hall has been turned to ice and starlight for the Midwinter Ball. Couples float above the floor. Who will you go with? You can only ask someone you know well.\n> lyra: Ask Lyra\n  when: met(lyra) and rel(lyra) >= 9\n  hint: She has had six invitations. She hasn't said yes to any.\n  if spark(lyra) >= 25:\n    challenge negotiate trivial:\n      win:\n        spark lyra +medium\n        rel lyra +medium\n        mood +medium\n        say: \"Finally, someone who doesn't stammer.\" She dances like a duel — and wins.\n      lose:\n        rel lyra -small\n        mood -small\n        say: \"I'm going alone. It's more efficient.\" You watch her dance alone all night, perfectly.\n  else:\n    challenge negotiate easy:\n      win:\n        spark lyra +medium\n        rel lyra +medium\n        mood +medium\n        say: \"Finally, someone who doesn't stammer.\" She dances like a duel — and wins.\n      lose:\n        rel lyra -small\n        mood -small\n        say: \"I'm going alone. It's more efficient.\" You watch her dance alone all night, perfectly.\n> tobias: Ask Tobias\n  when: met(tobias) and rel(tobias) >= 9\n  hint: He has been practising with a broom.\n  spark tobias +medium\n  rel tobias +medium\n  mood +medium\n  say: He steps on your feet nine times and apologises ten. It's the best night of the term.\n> seraphine: Ask Seraphine\n  when: met(seraphine) and rel(seraphine) >= 9\n  hint: She may already know you'll ask.\n  spark seraphine +medium\n  rel seraphine +medium\n  mood +medium\n  say: \"I said yes three weeks ago,\" she says, \"you just hadn't asked yet.\" She dances a beat ahead of the music.\n> corvin: Ask Corvin\n  when: met(corvin) and rel(corvin) >= 9\n  hint: Odds he shows up: unclear.\n  chance 70:\n    spark corvin +medium\n    mood +medium\n    say: He arrives late, in a stolen jacket, and is the best dancer in the room.\n  else:\n    mood -medium\n    say: He never shows. A note arrives at midnight: \"Got arrested. Long story. Save me a dance next year.\"\n> mira: Ask Mira\n  when: met(mira) and rel(mira) >= 9\n  hint: They're bringing a self-playing violin.\n  spark mira +medium\n  rel mira +small\n  mood +medium\n  say: Mira's violin plays itself, then plays the chandelier, then plays the windows. You are asked to leave early. Worth it.\n> kael: Ask Kael\n  when: met(kael) and rel(kael) >= 12\n  hint: He doesn't seem to feel the cold.\n  spark kael +medium\n  rel kael +medium\n  mood +medium\n  say: He holds your hand like it is something fragile. Where your fingers touch, frost-ferns bloom on the glass.\n> nessa: Ask Nessa\n  when: met(nessa) and rel(nessa) >= 9\n  hint: She'll be working. She's always working.\n  spark nessa +medium\n  rel nessa +medium\n  renown +small\n  say: She takes notes between dances. \"For the society column.\" Then she stops taking notes.\n> friends: Go with friends\n  mood +medium\n  charm +tiny\n  say: You go as a pack and dance badly together and it is perfect.\n> skip: Stay in and study\n  lore +small\n  study +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: The music drifts up through the floor while you read. Somewhere, people are happy. You are productive.\n\n@event spring_tournament\ntitle: The Spring Tournament\nclass: story\ntone: tense\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: month == 8 and not member(duelling)\nrepeat: 12\npriority: 40\nart: duel\ntags: festival rival\nchain: tournament\nscene: A ring of white stone on the lawn, crowds in the stands, two duelists bowing as their wands spark.\ntext: The Spring Tournament: dueling on the great lawn, the whole academy watching. Entry is open to anyone foolish enough.\n> left: Watch from the stands\n  mood +small\n  say: You eat candied chestnuts and scream for strangers.\n> right: Enter the tournament\n  challenge combat medium:\n    win:\n      renown +medium\n      say: First round: you win, barely, and the crowd learns your name.\n      queue tournament_semifinal now\n    lose:\n      health -medium\n      renown +tiny\n      say: First round: you are knocked out of the ring in eleven seconds. A record, apparently.\n\n@event spring_tournament_circle\ntitle: The Spring Tournament\nclass: story\ntone: tense\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: month == 8 and member(duelling)\nrepeat: 12\npriority: 40\nchain: tournament\nart: duel\ntags: rival\nscene: The great lawn ringed with banners, a chalk duelling circle at its centre and the Duelling Circle's names on a board.\ntext: The Spring Tournament: duelling on the great lawn, the whole academy watching. The Duelling Circle's names went up on the board a week ago, yours among them. The captain wants results, and says so.\n> left: Fight to make a good showing\n  challenge combat easy:\n    win:\n      renown +small\n      perform duelling +small\n      say: You win your first bout cleanly and lose the second honourably. The captain ticks your name, which from her is a medal.\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      say: You go out in the first round, on your feet at least. The captain says you fought like someone who expected to lose.\n> right: Go for the title\n  challenge combat medium:\n    win:\n      renown +medium\n      say: First round: you win, barely, and the crowd learns your name.\n      queue tournament_semifinal now\n    lose:\n      health -medium\n      renown +tiny\n      perform duelling -tiny\n      say: First round: you are knocked out of the ring in eleven seconds. A record, apparently, and not one the Circle wanted.\n\n@event tournament_semifinal\ntitle: Semifinal\nclass: story\ntone: tense\ntrigger: queued\nart: duel\ntags: rival\nchain: tournament\nscene: The same ring at sunset, scorch marks on the stone. Your opponent is grinning.\ntext: The semifinal. Your opponent is a fourth-year who has never lost. The crowd is louder now; you can hear your own name in it.\n> left: Fight clean\n  challenge combat hard:\n    win:\n      renown +medium\n      say: You trade spells like questions and answers — and get the last word.\n      queue tournament_final now\n    lose:\n      health -medium\n      renown +small\n      say: A good fight. Not good enough. The crowd applauds you off the lawn.\n> right: Fight dirty\n  challenge deceive hard:\n    win:\n      renown +small\n      say: A feint, a trip, a jinx to the shoelaces. Legal. Mostly.\n      queue tournament_final now\n      if met(bram):\n        rel bram -small\n    lose:\n      renown -small\n      say: The referee sees the trick. Disqualified, and booed.\n\n@event tournament_final\ntitle: The Final\nclass: story\ntone: tense\ntrigger: queued\nart: trophy\ntags: rival festival\nchain: tournament\nscene: The dueling ring under floating lanterns, a silver cup on a plinth, and across from you, a familiar face.\ntext: The final. Across the ring stands {lyra}, wand raised, eyes blazing. \"I was hoping it would be you,\" she says, and she is not being friendly.\n> left: Give everything you have\n  meet lyra\n  challenge combat heroic:\n    win:\n      renown +huge\n      mood +large\n      flag tournament_champion\n      gain famous\n      rel lyra -medium\n      if bond(lyra) == \"none\":\n        bond lyra rival\n      say: Your final spell lands like a bell. Silence — then the lawn erupts. Lyra stares at you as if seeing you for the first time.\n    lose:\n      renown +medium\n      rel lyra +small\n      say: She wins, but only just. Afterwards, she offers her hand. \"Next year,\" she says. It is a promise and a threat.\n> right: Yield with a bow\n  meet lyra\n  rel lyra +large\n  renown +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: The crowd boos. Lyra does not cheer her victory. Later she finds you: \"Why?\" You don't have a good answer, and she seems to like that.\n\n@event grand_exams\n# retired in phase 2b (was \"month == 10\"); replaced by term-end exams (engine, design §7.3)\ntitle: Grand Examinations\nclass: story\ntone: tense\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: false\nrepeat: 12\npriority: 40\nart: scroll\ntags: study exam retired\nscene: A circular examination chamber with a domed ceiling of moving constellations; examiners in grey robes sit behind a long table.\ntext: Year-end examinations. Three days, five examiners and a practical in front of a panel that includes the headmistress. Your standing for the year rides on this.\n> left: Prepare a dazzling practical\n  challenge spell hard:\n    win:\n      study +huge\n      renown +small\n      say: You finish your practical and the examiners actually lean forward. Distinction.\n    lose:\n      study -huge\n      mood -medium\n      say: The practical sputters. An examiner writes something very short.\n> right: Write a meticulous thesis\n  challenge exam hard:\n    win:\n      study +huge\n      mood +small\n      say: Your thesis is cited by one examiner in an argument with another. Distinction.\n    lose:\n      study -huge\n      mood -medium\n      say: Your thesis is described, aloud, as \"ambitious\". It is not a compliment.\n\n@event summer_plans\n# retired in phase 2b (was \"month == 11\"); replaced by break_plan\ntitle: Summer Recess\nclass: story\ntone: warm\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: false\nrepeat: 12\npriority: 40\ntype: menu\nart: sun\ntags: home retired\nscene: The academy gates thrown open to green hills; students with trunks waving from departing sky-barges.\ntext: The towers empty for the summer. Two months of freedom. How will you spend them?\n> home: Go home\n  hint: Rest and recover. No rent while away.\n  gain summer_away\n  health +large\n  mood +large\n  if has(origin_noble):\n    say: Your family hosts three dinners in your honour and asks about your marriage prospects at all of them.\n  elif has(origin_street):\n    say: The old crowd in Port Calder treats you like a returning hero, and steals your trunk only once.\n  else:\n    say: Home is smaller than you remember, and warmer. Someone made your favourite pie.\n> work: Take summer work in the village\n  hint: About 50 gold over the summer. Tiring.\n  gain summer_work\n  vigor +small\n  say: You haul crates, serve tables and polish brass until your hands know the shape of every coin.\n> study: Stay and study in the empty library\n  hint: +Lore, +Arcana, +Standing. Lonely.\n  lore +medium\n  arcana +medium\n  study +medium\n  mood -small\n  say: Just you, the books and the dust motes. You learn things the term never had time for.\n> travel: Travel to the coast\n  hint: Costs 40 gold. Great for mood.\n  when: gold >= 40\n  locked: Needs 40 crowns.\n  gold -large\n  gain summer_away\n  mood +huge\n  charm +small\n  say: Salt air, shell markets, a sea serpent at sunset. You come back tanned and ten years younger.\n> expedition: Join Old Maud's herb expedition\n  hint: Requires knowing Maud. Adventure.\n  when: met(maud)\n  gain summer_away\n  alchemy +medium\n  vigor +small\n  rel maud +large\n  health +small\n  say: Two months in the Whisperwood with Maud and her goat. You learn which mushrooms sing and which ones lie.\n\n@event summer_letter\ntitle: A Letter From Home\nclass: story\ntone: warm\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: month == 12 and not has(summer_away) and not wrapup\nrepeat: 12\nart: letter\ntags: home\nscene: A letter sealed with candle wax lying on a sunlit windowsill.\ntext: A letter arrives in the last month of summer, the handwriting so familiar it hurts. They want to know how you are doing. Really doing.\n> left: Write back honestly\n  mood +small\n  if (turn > 4 and gpa < 2.0):\n    mood -medium\n    say: You tell them about your grades. Their reply is kind, which is worse.\n  else:\n    say: You write six pages. It feels like setting down a heavy bag.\n> right: Write back that everything is perfect\n  mood -tiny\n  guile +tiny\n  chance 50:\n    gold +medium\n    say: Proud of you, they write, and enclose a little money you suspect they couldn't spare.\n  else:\n    say: They reply with exclamation marks. The lie sits in your stomach all week.\n\n@event starfall\nslot: night\ntitle: Starfall\nclass: story\ntone: light\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: month == 12 and year < 4\nrepeat: 12\npriority: 30\nart: star\ntags: festival\nscene: Students lying on the observatory roof under a sky streaked with falling stars.\ntext: The last night of the academic year. Everyone climbs onto the observatory roof to watch the stars fall. They say if you catch one in a jar, you get a year of luck.\n> left: Lie back and just watch\n  mood +medium\n  health +small\n  say: You count forty falling stars and then stop counting. It's enough.\n> right: Try to catch a star\n  challenge feat trivial:\n    win:\n      renown +small\n      mood +small\n      gain inspired\n      say: It lands in your jar with a hiss and glows for weeks. Students come to see it.\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      say: You lunge, miss, and nearly roll off the roof. A prefect catches you by the collar.\n\n@event year_two\ntitle: Second Year\nclass: story\ntone: light\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: turn == 13\npriority: 55\nart: tower\ntags: story\nscene: The seven towers in autumn colours, the first-year barge arriving below, tiny and nervous.\ntext: The barge brings new first-years, and you watch them gawp at the towers. Was that really you a year ago? You know which staircases move now, and which professors you can charm.\n> ok: Begin the second year\n  mood +small\n  renown +small\n  say: You unpack faster than last year, and in the right order.\n\n@event year_three\ntitle: Third Year\nclass: story\ntone: light\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: turn == 25\npriority: 55\nart: crown\ntags: story\nscene: The headmistress addressing a hall of third-years, every candle in the room leaning toward her.\ntext: \"Third years,\" the headmistress says, \"you are past the middle of your time here. Next year will ask a great deal of you; spend this one finding out what you want from it.\" She looks, for a moment, directly at you.\n> ok: Begin the third year\n  mood +tiny\n  study +tiny\n  say: You know the castle well enough now to get lost in it on purpose.\n\n@event graduation_eve\nslot: night\ntitle: The Eve of Graduation\nclass: story\ntone: light\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: turn == max_turns\npriority: 60\nart: crown\ntags: story\nscene: Graduation robes laid out on a dormitory bed, a single candle burning, the towers lit gold outside the window.\ntext: Tomorrow you graduate. Your robes are laid out on the bed, slightly too long. Outside, the towers are lit gold. Four years have gone like a single held breath.\n> left: Spend the night with friends\n  mood +medium\n  say: Laughter until dawn. Promises to write. Some of them, you'll keep.\n> right: Walk the halls alone one last time\n  lore +small\n  mood +small\n  say: Every corridor holds a memory. At the end of the last one, the castle sighs, as if saying goodbye.\n"},{"path":"content/events/chain_hollowstar_acts.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  THE HOLLOW STAR: Acts II-IV as arc beats (arc: hollowstar)\n#\n#  Act II (investigation, year 2): the Wardens stir.\n#    beat 4  hs_silver_rings, hs_seraphine_towers\n#    beat 5  hs_frost_ring\n#  Act III (escalation, year 3): a tower goes dark, sides are taken.\n#    beat 7  hs_dark_tower (may advance the clock to 8), hs_maud_locks\n#    beat 8  hs_choosing_sides -> flag sided_wardens | sided_dumonts | sided_self\n#  Act IV (convergence, year 4): preparation before the summons.\n#    beat 10 hs_last_winter, hs_ilse_door\n#  Preparation (key clues, allies at rel >= 30, the side flag) sets\n#  the tier of the seal challenge in hs_beneath.\n# ============================================================\n\n# ---------------- Act II: the Wardens stir ----------------\n\n@event hs_silver_rings\ntitle: Silver at Supper\narc: hollowstar\nbeat: 4\nslot: night\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\nart: ring\ntags: mystery hollowstar\nchain: hollowstar\nscene: The Great Hall at supper, candlelight catching three identical silver rings on three very different hands at the high table.\ntext: At supper you notice three identical silver rings at the high table: on the headmistress, on Professor Thorne and on Old Maud's earthy hand. After curfew, from your window, you see all three walking the lakeshore with lanterns, stopping at the foot of each tower in turn.\n> left: Follow them down\n  challenge deceive medium:\n    win:\n      lore +small\n      say: You shadow them from tower to tower. At each one they lay a palm on the stone and wait. At the last, Maud takes her hand away and blows on her fingers as if the wall were ice.\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      rel thorne -small\n      say: A lantern swings toward you. \"Hm,\" says Professor Thorne. \"Bed.\" He doesn't report you, which somehow feels worse.\n> right: Draw the curtains\n  health +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You sleep well, mostly. In the morning the rings are still there at breakfast, and nobody at the high table looks as if they slept at all.\n\n@event hs_seraphine_towers\ntitle: Seven, Then Six\narc: hollowstar\nbeat: 4\nslot: night\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\nactor: seraphine\nart: tower\ntags: mystery hollowstar\nchain: hollowstar\nscene: A seer on the observatory balcony at night, counting the academy's towers on her fingers, one finger held back.\ntext: Seraphine is on the observatory balcony, counting towers on her fingers. \"Seven,\" she says. \"Seven. Six, and then… no. Seven, for now.\" She looks at you as if you had arrived a little early. \"What month is it? I lose track around the… you know.\"\n> left: \"Which one goes out?\"\n  lore +small\n  mood -small\n  say: She points, then frowns at her own hand. \"That one. Or the one next to it. Next year, I think.\" She doesn't remember saying it the next morning, but you do.\n> right: \"It's {month}. Evening.\"\n  rel seraphine +medium\n  mood +tiny\n  say: She lets out a long breath and sits down on the cold stone. \"Thank you. I was halfway into a winter.\" You stay until she is sure which one she's in.\n\n@event hs_frost_ring\ntitle: A Ring of Frost\narc: hollowstar\nbeat: 5\nslot: night\nclass: story\ntone: tense\nart: snow\ntags: mystery hollowstar\nchain: hollowstar\nscene: The Mirror Lake on a warm spring night, a perfect white ring of frost on the water, a tall woman standing at the waterline with a lantern.\ntext: A warm night, and a ring of frost has formed in the middle of the lake: perfectly round, perfectly white, as wide as the Great Hall. Far below it, very faintly, a bell tolls once. The headmistress is standing alone at the waterline.\n> left: Watch what she does\n  challenge deceive medium:\n    win:\n      find lake_bell\n      lore +small\n      say: She kneels and speaks to the water, slow and patient, the way you would speak to a sleeping animal. The ring shrinks, cracks, and is gone. She stays kneeling a long time afterwards.\n    lose:\n      rel isolde +tiny\n      mood -small\n      say: \"Good evening,\" she says, without turning round. \"You are the third student this week. The other two asked questions.\" She tells you nothing, and remembers your name.\n> right: Stay inside\n  mood -small\n  health +tiny\n  say: You stay inside with the shutters closed. By morning the lake is blue and glittering, and the first-years are already arguing about whether anyone saw anything at all.\n\n# ---------------- Act III: escalation ----------------\n\n@event hs_dark_tower\ntitle: The Tower That Went Dark\narc: hollowstar\nbeat: 7\nclass: story\ntone: grim\nart: candle\ntags: mystery hollowstar danger\nchain: hollowstar\nscene: An academy tower standing black against the night sky among its lit neighbours, and below it a lake half-covered in autumn ice.\ntext: Mid-lecture, every lamp in the Lecture Tower goes out at once, and none of them will light again. Nobody is hurt. By morning, weeks before any frost, half the lake has frozen: a sheet of ice that ends in a clean edge, as if cut. The prefects are already telling everyone to keep off it.\n> left: Walk out onto the ice\n  challenge research medium:\n    win:\n      advance hollowstar +1\n      lore +medium\n      health -small\n      say: Under your boots the ice is clear as glass. Far down, something round and dark sits on the lakebed, and a faint glow runs along its rim. Then the glow turns, slowly, as if to look up.\n    lose:\n      health -medium\n      say: The ice groans and a crack races past your feet. You scramble back to the shore soaked to the knee, and a prefect writes your name down very slowly.\n> right: Carry lamps for the prefects\n  renown +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: You spend the night carrying lamps up the Lecture Tower stairs. Every one goes out at the doorway. From her window across the courtyard, the headmistress watches you try.\n\n@event hs_maud_locks\ntitle: Maud Has Seen Ice Before\narc: hollowstar\nbeat: 7\nwhen: seen(hs_dark_tower) > 0\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\nactor: maud\nart: cup\ntags: mystery hollowstar\nchain: hollowstar\nscene: An old woman wrapped in a shawl on her cottage step, two mugs steaming beside her, looking down at a half-frozen lake.\ntext: Maud is on her cottage step with two mugs of tea, as if she expected you. \"The lake doesn't freeze this time of year, dearie,\" she says. \"Last time it froze at all, I lost a friend to it.\" She pushes a mug toward you. \"Sit. Or ask. You can't really do both.\"\n> left: Sit with her\n  rel maud +medium\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You drink your tea and watch the ice. She hums something old. When the mugs are empty she squeezes your hand. \"That was the right one,\" she says, and you'll only understand why much later.\n> right: Ask what's holding it shut\n  find seven_locks\n  rel maud +small\n  mood -small\n  say: \"Seven towers, seven locks, one in each,\" she says. \"When a tower goes quiet, so does its lock.\" She looks at the dark tower. \"Locks can be mended. They need hands, and they need a key.\"\n\n@event hs_choosing_sides\ntitle: Two Invitations\narc: hollowstar\nbeat: 8\nwhen: clues(hollowstar) >= 2 or flag(kael_told) or flag(warden_trial) or flag(wardens_noticed) or flag(hollow_courted)\ntype: menu\nclass: story\ntone: tense\nart: letter\ntags: mystery hollowstar\nchain: hollowstar\nscene: A student holding two folded notes, one sealed in silver wax and one in frost, standing between a tower and a boathouse.\ntext: Two invitations in one day. A note in the headmistress's neat hand: \"My study, after supper. Come alone. There will be chess.\" And Kael, waiting at the boathouse: \"She wants you in her circle. I know the door from the other side.\" He looks at the dark tower. \"Come with us. If you want.\"\n> wardens: Join the headmistress's circle\n  hint: The Wardens: old rules, old wards.\n  flag sided_wardens\n  rel isolde +large\n  rel kael -small\n  lore +small\n  say: She beats you at chess in eleven moves, then tells you about the seal while she resets the board. \"Rings are for people who have stood on the ice,\" she says. \"Until then, you have my door.\"\n> dumonts: Stand with the Dumonts\n  hint: Kael's way, against his own family.\n  flag sided_dumonts\n  rel kael +large\n  rel isolde -small\n  if met(ilse):\n    rel ilse +small\n  arcana +small\n  say: \"Not my family,\" Kael says. \"Us.\" He teaches you to hold a ward in a wind that wants your fingers. His hands are cold. They are steadier than yours.\n> self: Go my own way\n  hint: Nobody's circle. Nobody's help.\n  flag sided_self\n  guile +small\n  lore +small\n  mood -small\n  say: You thank them both and keep your own notes in your own cipher. It's lonelier than you expected. Both of them, you notice, keep an eye on you anyway.\n> none: Stay out of it\n  hint: It isn't your door.\n  mood +small\n  rel kael -tiny\n  say: You turn both invitations down. Kael nods as if he expected it. The headmistress's note simply says, the next morning, \"Understood. Mind the ice.\"\n\n# ---------------- Act IV: convergence ----------------\n\n@event hs_last_winter\ntitle: The Last Winter Before\narc: hollowstar\nbeat: 10\nwhen: clues(hollowstar) >= 2 or flag(sided_wardens) or flag(sided_dumonts) or flag(sided_self)\ntype: menu\nclass: story\ntone: tense\nart: lantern\ntags: mystery hollowstar\nchain: hollowstar\nscene: Lantern light in a frost-rimed window, the frozen edge of the lake beyond it, a student's desk covered in maps of seven towers.\ntext: The Lecture Tower has been dark for a year, and the lake has started freezing overnight in strange shapes: circles, mostly. Everyone who knows what's under it is getting ready in their own way. You have one winter and not enough evenings. Who do you spend them with?\n> kael: Train with Kael on the ice\n  rel kael +large\n  health -small\n  say: He drills you on the ice until you can hold a ward with numb hands. \"Again,\" he says, and then, quietly, \"Good.\" You will remember that he said it.\n> maud: Mend wards with Maud\n  rel maud +large\n  mood -small\n  say: You walk the towers with her, tracing old wards back into the stone. \"Your hands are steadier than mine were,\" she says, and doesn't say whose hands she means.\n> isolde: Study with the headmistress\n  rel isolde +large\n  lore +small\n  mood -small\n  say: You lose at chess every evening and learn the founders' words between moves. Once you nearly win. Her short, delighted laugh is the warmest sound you hear all winter.\n> ilse: Spar with Ilse at the shore\n  when: met(ilse) and not flag(ilse_taken) and not flag(ilse_went_north)\n  rel ilse +large\n  health -small\n  say: She knocks you into the snow eleven times. The twelfth time you don't fall, and she looks at you as if you had done a trick she has never learned the counter to.\n> alone: Rest while you still can\n  hint: A quiet winter. Nobody else's.\n  rest\n  say: You sleep, you eat, you read novels with no doors in them. It's the kindest winter you've had in years, and you can feel the lake getting colder anyway.\n\n@event hs_ilse_door\ntitle: What Ilse Was Taught\narc: hollowstar\nbeat: 10\nwhen: met(ilse) and not clue(ilse_truth) and not flag(ilse_taken) and not flag(ilse_went_north)\nactor: ilse\nclass: story\ntone: grim\nart: door\ntags: mystery hollowstar\nchain: hollowstar\nscene: A young woman in white furs drawing a round door in the snow with the tip of a wand, a frozen lake behind her.\ntext: Ilse draws a circle in the snow with her wand, then a smaller one inside it. \"This is the door,\" she says. \"I have drawn it every day since I was six. I have never seen it.\" She doesn't look up. \"Ask me what they taught me to do. Or don't. I don't care which.\"\n> left: \"What did they teach you?\"\n  find ilse_truth\n  rel ilse +small\n  mood -small\n  say: She tells you, flatly, like a lesson recited: how to stand, what to say, where to put her hands. \"They raised me to open it,\" she says. \"Nobody asked me if I wanted to.\" Then, sharply: \"Now you know. Don't look at me like that.\"\n> right: \"You don't have to tell me.\"\n  rel ilse +medium\n  mood -tiny\n  say: She rubs the circle out with her boot. \"Kindness,\" she says, as if naming a trick. \"I haven't learned the counter to that one yet.\" She doesn't leave, though.\n"},{"path":"content/events/chain_hollowstar.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  MAIN CHAIN: The Hollow Star\n#\n#  Something sleeps beneath the lake, sealed by Lumenhold's\n#  founders. Kael Dumont's family serve it. Progress follows the\n#  arc (content/world/arcs.grim): the clock advances on schedule,\n#  and what you engaged with is counted by clues(hollowstar).\n#    clock >= 1     dreams begin, the man in grey is seen\n#    clues >= 1     research, Kael confides (if he trusts you)\n#    clues >= 2     grey visitors, sides are taken\n#    clues >= 3     the seventh tower, the headmistress\n#  Alignment (Lumen + / Hollow -) is the moral axis: the Star's\n#  gifts pull toward the Hollow; refusing them, toward the Lumen.\n#  Finale (final year, Blossomtide): hs_the_night -> hs_beneath (menu),\n#  for those who engaged (or whom the Star has claimed, alignment <= -60).\n#  Outcomes fill the yearbook's mystery line: sealed / bargained /\n#  freed (alignment <= -60) / claimed (game over) / bystander.\n# ============================================================\n\n@event hs_dreams_begin\ntime: morning\ntitle: The Black Star Dream\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\nwhen: turn >= 5 and clock(hollowstar) >= 1 and not has(hollow_dreams)\nweight: 14\nart: moon\ntags: mystery hollowstar\nchain: hollowstar\nscene: A dreamscape: a frozen lake seen from beneath, a round door glowing at the bottom, and above it a star that is a hole in the sky.\ntext: You dream of the lake from underneath. There is a round door at the bottom, and above the water a star that is not a light but an absence. A voice says your name like it is tasting it. You wake with frost on your pillow.\n> left: Write the dream down\n  lore +small\n  find lake_bell\n  gain hollow_dreams\n  say: Your notes fill four pages. In the morning you don't remember writing the last line: \"It is not asleep. It is waiting.\"\n> right: Ask Seraphine what it means\n  meet seraphine\n  rel seraphine +small\n  gain hollow_dreams\n  say: Seraphine listens, then hands you a sprig of lavender. \"It won't stop the dreams,\" she says. \"But it'll make them smell nicer.\"\n\n@event hs_grey_stranger\ntime: night\ntitle: A Man in Grey\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\nwhen: turn >= 8 and met(kael) and clock(hollowstar) >= 1\nweight: 12\nart: snow\ntags: mystery hollowstar\nchain: hollowstar\nscene: A frozen fountain in a moonlit courtyard; a tall man in a grey coat facing a younger man, frost spreading from both.\ntext: Late at night in the courtyard, Kael is arguing with a tall man in a grey coat. The fountain between them has frozen mid-splash. \"You will open it,\" the man says calmly, \"or your sister will.\"\n> left: Slip away unseen\n  guile +tiny\n  find second_shadow\n  say: You back into the shadows. The man in grey turns his head and looks straight at where you're hiding, then smiles and walks away.\n> right: Step out and interrupt\n  rel kael +large\n  find second_shadow\n  flag confronted_grey\n  say: \"Everything all right?\" The man in grey studies you, nods politely and leaves. Kael's hands are shaking. \"You shouldn't have done that,\" he says. \"...Thank you.\"\n\n@event hs_founders_records\ntitle: The Founders' Ledger\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\nwhen: clues(hollowstar) >= 1 and not flag(knows_founders_seal)\nweight: 12\nart: book\ntags: mystery hollowstar study\nchain: hollowstar\nscene: A dusty archive vault, a giant ledger open on a lectern, one page carefully razored out.\ntext: In the archive vault you find the Founders' Ledger, three hundred years old. The entry for the academy's founding year has been cut out with a razor. But the page beneath still holds the impression of the pen.\n> left: Leave it — this feels dangerous\n  mood +tiny\n  say: You close the ledger. The dust settles. Some doors are better left alone. Probably.\n> right: Rub charcoal over the page\n  challenge research trivial:\n    win:\n      flag knows_founders_seal\n      find knows_founders_seal\n      find founders_ledger\n      lore +small\n      say: Ghostly letters emerge: \"Seven towers, seven locks. The Star sleeps beneath the water. The key is a star that holds no light.\" The founders built the academy as a seal.\n    lose:\n      lore +tiny\n      say: The impression is too faint. You make out only three words: \"...beneath the water...\"\n\n@event hs_kael_truth\ntitle: What Kael Is Running From\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\nwhen: clues(hollowstar) >= 1 and rel(kael) >= 15 and not flag(kael_told)\nactor: kael\nweight: 16\nart: snow\ntags: mystery hollowstar social\nchain: hollowstar\nscene: Two students sitting on the edge of the frozen boathouse jetty at night, one with frost in his hair, both looking down at the black water.\ntext: Kael sits with you on the boathouse jetty. \"My family serve something under this lake,\" he says at last. \"They sent me here to open its door. I came to stop them. Some nights I'm not sure which I'll actually do.\"\n> left: \"Then we stop them together.\"\n  flag kael_told kael_ally\n  rel kael +large\n  spark kael +tiny\n  alignment +small\n  find kael_family\n  if bond(kael) == \"none\":\n    bond kael friend\n  say: He looks at you for a long moment, and the frost on the jetty melts a little. \"Together,\" he repeats, as if trying out a word from another language.\n> right: Tell the headmistress\n  flag kael_told reported_kael\n  rel kael -huge\n  rel isolde +medium\n  find kael_family\n  say: The headmistress thanks you gravely. The next day Kael is moved to a room in the headmistress's tower. He doesn't look at you anymore.\n\n@event hs_star_mark\ntitle: The Cold Coin Burns\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\nwhen: (flag(star_coin) or flag(bell_fragment)) and not has(star_mark) and not flag(threw_star)\nweight: 12\nart: star\ntags: mystery hollowstar magic\nchain: hollowstar\nscene: A student's open palm, a pale star-shaped mark glowing on it, a blackened coin smoking on the floor beside them.\ntext: The black-starred relic you've been carrying burns suddenly, so cold it feels hot. When you drop it, there's a pale star-shaped mark on your palm. It hums when you're near the lake.\n> left: Throw the relic into the lake\n  flag threw_star\n  unflag star_coin bell_fragment\n  alignment +medium\n  mood +tiny\n  say: It sinks without a ripple. The mark on your hand fades — almost entirely. Something under the water sighs.\n> right: Keep it. Feel the power.\n  gain star_mark\n  arcana +medium\n  alignment -medium\n  say: The mark pulses with every heartbeat. Spells come easier now. Much easier. That should probably worry you more than it does.\n\n@event hs_grey_offer\ntitle: An Offer in Grey\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\nwhen: turn >= 18 and (clues(hollowstar) >= 2 or alignment <= -30) and not flag(grey_offer_seen)\nweight: 12\nart: mask\ntags: mystery hollowstar danger\nchain: hollowstar\nscene: A grey-coated man sitting uninvited at a library table, a black velvet box open in front of him, the candles around him burning blue.\ntext: The man in grey is waiting at your library table. \"You've been asking questions about the Star,\" he says pleasantly. \"It's noticed you. It could make you extraordinary. All it wants is for someone to open a door.\"\n> left: \"Get out.\"\n  alignment +small\n  flag grey_offer_seen\n  mood +tiny\n  say: He inclines his head and leaves. The candles return to yellow. You realise your hands are trembling.\n> right: \"...What would it give me?\"\n  flag grey_offer_seen hollow_tempted\n  arcana +medium\n  alignment -medium\n  say: He shows you, just for a moment. Power, endless and cold. When he's gone, you can still taste it.\n\n@event hs_grey_invitation\ntime: evening night\ntitle: A Seat at the Northern Table\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\nwhen: alignment <= -60 and turn >= 12 and not flag(hollow_courted)\nweight: 16\nart: letter\ntags: mystery hollowstar danger\nchain: hollowstar\nscene: A black-edged letter on a dormitory pillow, its wax seal pressed with a star that has no light in it.\ntext: There is a letter on your pillow, sealed with a black star. The handwriting is beautiful. \"The families have watched you with great interest,\" it says. \"There is a seat for you at our table, when the door is opened. Come to the boathouse at midnight, or burn this. Either is an answer.\"\n> left: Go to the boathouse\n  flag hollow_courted hollow_tempted\n  alignment -small\n  guile +small\n  health -small\n  say: The man in grey is waiting with two cups of something hot. He talks to you as an equal for an hour. You come back with frost in your hair and the feeling of having been measured, and found to fit.\n> right: Burn it\n  alignment +medium\n  mood -small\n  say: The paper burns blue and slow, and smells of the lake. It takes a long time to go out. Climbing back from where you've been is slower still, but this is a step.\n\n@event hs_grey_tests\ntitle: The Good Argument\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\nwhen: alignment >= 30 and turn >= 14 and not flag(grey_tested)\nweight: 12\nart: mask\ntags: mystery hollowstar danger\nchain: hollowstar\nscene: A grey-coated man standing beside a student at the infirmary window, both looking down at a first-year with a bandaged arm.\ntext: The man in grey falls into step beside you outside the infirmary. \"You help people,\" he says pleasantly. \"I admire it. But think how many more you could help with real power. The Star doesn't care what it's used for. Only that someone uses it.\"\n> left: \"No.\"\n  flag grey_tested\n  alignment +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: \"No,\" you say. He smiles as if you'd said something charming, and leaves. The first-year in the window waves at you. You wave back, and your hand is shaking.\n> right: \"...How many more?\"\n  flag grey_tested hollow_tempted\n  alignment -medium\n  arcana +small\n  say: He tells you. The numbers are very large and very cold. That night, you dream of the lake from underneath, and you are not frightened in the dream.\n\n@event hs_wardens_notice\ntitle: The Headmistress Takes Note\nclass: story\ntone: warm\nactor: isolde\nwhen: alignment >= 60 and turn >= 12 and not flag(wardens_noticed)\nweight: 16\nart: crown\ntags: mystery hollowstar\nchain: hollowstar\nscene: A tall woman in dark robes pausing on a stair to look down at one student among many, a silver ring glinting on her hand.\ntext: On the grand stair the headmistress stops beside you. \"Several people have told me about you,\" she says, \"and not one of them was complaining. That is rare.\" Her silver ring catches the light. \"I keep a list of students I would trust with a door. Don't let it go to your head.\"\n> left: \"I'd be honoured.\"\n  meet isolde\n  flag wardens_noticed\n  rel isolde +medium\n  mood -tiny\n  say: \"Good,\" she says. \"Honour is a heavy thing to carry. Practise.\" She walks on. You stand on the stair for some time.\n> right: \"What kind of door?\"\n  meet isolde\n  flag wardens_noticed\n  rel isolde +small\n  lore +small\n  say: Her short, delighted laugh. \"The kind that ought to stay shut,\" she says. \"Keep asking questions like that. Just not on the stairs.\"\n\n@event hs_seventh_tower\ntitle: The Seventh Tower\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\nwhen: clues(hollowstar) >= 3 and not flag(star_key)\nweight: 14\nart: tower\ntags: mystery hollowstar\nchain: hollowstar\nscene: The top of a narrow, abandoned tower, cobwebbed and moonlit, with a wall bearing a star-shaped recess.\ntext: The seventh tower has been locked for a century. You find a way in. At the very top, a single room: dust, moonlight, and a star-shaped hollow in the wall, holding something that does not reflect any light.\n> left: Take it quietly\n  challenge deceive trivial:\n    win:\n      flag star_key\n      find star_key\n      say: A key shaped like a star, black as a hole in the world. The tower shudders as you lift it, then is still.\n    lose:\n      gain detention\n      mood -small\n      say: A ward shrieks. A prefect finds you on the stairs. You get detention and a very long look from the headmistress.\n> right: Take it boldly\n  challenge spell trivial:\n    win:\n      flag star_key\n      find star_key\n      say: You tear through the ward with brute force. The key comes free. Every candle in the academy goes out for one second.\n    lose:\n      health -large\n      say: The ward throws you down two flights of stairs.\n\n@event hs_isolde_summons\ntitle: The Headmistress Knows\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: final_year and month >= 3 and month <= 7 and (clues(hollowstar) >= 3 or flag(kael_ally) or flag(wardens_noticed) or flag(sided_wardens) or flag(sided_dumonts) or flag(sided_self))\npriority: 45\nactor: isolde\nart: crown\ntags: mystery hollowstar story\nchain: hollowstar\nscene: A circular study at the top of a tower, walls lined with sleeping portraits, the headmistress standing by a window that looks down on the lake.\ntext: The headmistress summons you to her tower. \"You have been following the same thread I have,\" she says. \"On the night of the Blossom moon, the seal will weaken. I need people I can trust beneath the lake. Or you can walk away. No one would blame you.\"\n> left: Walk away\n  meet isolde\n  flag hollow_out\n  mood +small\n  say: \"Wise,\" she says, and you can't tell if she means it. \"Then stay above, and keep the first-years indoors.\"\n> right: \"I'll be there.\"\n  alignment +small\n  meet isolde\n  flag warden_trial\n  find seven_locks\n  rel isolde +large\n  arcana +small\n  say: She presses something cold into your hand: a silver ward-ring. \"Then learn quickly. You have until Blossomtide.\"\n\n@event hs_the_night\ntitle: The Night the Lake Froze\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: final_year and month == 9 and (not flag(hollow_out) or flag(hollow_tempted) or flag(hollow_courted)) and (clues(hollowstar) >= 3 or flag(warden_trial) or flag(kael_ally) or flag(sided_wardens) or flag(sided_dumonts) or flag(sided_self) or flag(wardens_noticed) or flag(hollow_courted) or alignment <= -60)\npriority: 70\nart: snow\ntags: mystery hollowstar danger\nchain: hollowstar\nscene: The whole lake frozen white in the middle of spring, cracks glowing from beneath, students crowding the towers' windows.\ntext: In the middle of spring, the lake freezes in a single breath. Cracks glow beneath the ice. The bell under the water is tolling, and every candle in the academy leans toward the shore.\n> left: Go down beneath the lake\n  queue hs_beneath now\n  say: You run for the boathouse. Behind you, someone is shouting your name. You don't stop.\n> right: Stay above and protect the students\n  alignment +small\n  health -large\n  renown +large\n  rel isolde +small\n  flag hollow_held_line\n  say: You hold the doors of the first-year tower all night against things made of frost and whispers. By dawn the lake thaws. Something below is still asleep — for now.\n\n@event hs_beneath\ntitle: The Door Beneath the Lake\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\ntrigger: queued\ntype: menu\npriority: 95\nart: door\ntags: mystery hollowstar danger\nchain: hollowstar\nscene: A vast domed cavern under the lake, a round door of black stone standing open a crack, and through it a star that is pure absence.\ntext: The tunnel ends at the round door. It stands open a crack. Through it, the Hollow Star looks at you — and it is beautiful, and it is hungry, and it knows your name.{=flag(kael_ally) and \" Kael is beside you, wand raised, frost spreading from his feet.\" or \"\"}\n> seal: Seal the door\n  hint: Needs the star key or the founders' secret. Arcana; preparation makes it easier.\n  when: flag(star_key) or flag(knows_founders_seal) or flag(warden_trial)\n  alignment +small\n  hs_prep = clue(seven_locks) + clue(star_key) + clue(ilse_truth) + (rel(kael) >= 18) + (rel(ilse) >= 18) + (rel(maud) >= 18) + (rel(isolde) >= 18) + 2 * (flag(sided_wardens) or flag(sided_dumonts)) + flag(sided_self) + (alignment >= 30) + (alignment >= 60) + (spell_level(starlight) >= 1) + (spell_level(ward) >= 1) + flag(wardens_noticed)\n  if hs_prep >= 6:\n    challenge spell trivial:\n      win:\n        flag hollow_sealed\n        renown +huge\n        mood +large\n        lose star_mark\n        lose hollow_dreams\n        queue hs_aftermath in 1\n        say: You speak the founders' words and the door grinds shut. The Star screams without sound. Above, the ice cracks and the lake breathes again.\n      lose:\n        health -huge\n        flag hollow_sealed\n        lose hollow_dreams\n        queue hs_aftermath in 1\n        if flag(kael_ally):\n          rel kael +large\n          say: Your seal falters — and Kael throws his own strength into it, every ounce of frost in him. The door closes. He doesn't wake for three days.\n        else:\n          say: The seal holds, barely, and takes most of you with it. You wake in the infirmary a week later.\n  elif hs_prep >= 3:\n    challenge spell easy:\n      win:\n        flag hollow_sealed\n        renown +huge\n        mood +large\n        lose star_mark\n        lose hollow_dreams\n        queue hs_aftermath in 1\n        say: You speak the founders' words and the door grinds shut. The Star screams without sound. Above, the ice cracks and the lake breathes again.\n      lose:\n        health -huge\n        flag hollow_sealed\n        lose hollow_dreams\n        queue hs_aftermath in 1\n        if flag(kael_ally):\n          rel kael +large\n          say: Your seal falters — and Kael throws his own strength into it, every ounce of frost in him. The door closes. He doesn't wake for three days.\n        else:\n          say: The seal holds, barely, and takes most of you with it. You wake in the infirmary a week later.\n  else:\n    challenge spell medium:\n      win:\n        flag hollow_sealed\n        renown +huge\n        mood +large\n        lose star_mark\n        lose hollow_dreams\n        queue hs_aftermath in 1\n        say: You speak the founders' words and the door grinds shut. The Star screams without sound. Above, the ice cracks and the lake breathes again.\n      lose:\n        health -huge\n        flag hollow_sealed\n        lose hollow_dreams\n        queue hs_aftermath in 1\n        if flag(kael_ally):\n          rel kael +large\n          say: Your seal falters — and Kael throws his own strength into it, every ounce of frost in him. The door closes. He doesn't wake for three days.\n        else:\n          say: The seal holds, barely, and takes most of you with it. You wake in the infirmary a week later.\n> bargain: Accept the Star's bargain\n  hint: Power beyond measure. A voice in your head forever. Only for those it has already tempted.\n  when: alignment <= -30 or flag(hollow_tempted)\n  flag hollow_bargain\n  alignment -large\n  arcana +huge\n  gain star_mark\n  health -large\n  lose hollow_dreams\n  if alignment <= -30:\n    mood +medium\n    say: You take what it offers, and it feels like coming home. The door closes on its own, for now. The Star settles into the back of your mind like a cat finding a warm lap.\n  else:\n    mood -large\n    say: You take what it offers. The door closes on its own, for now. The Star settles into the back of your mind like a cat finding a warm lap, and you lie awake for a week.\n> open: Open the door\n  hint: It wants out. Only one already deep in the Hollow could walk through beside it.\n  when: alignment <= -60 or flag(hollow_tempted)\n  if alignment <= -60:\n    flag hollow_freed\n    alignment -large\n    arcana +large\n    gain star_mark\n    lose hollow_dreams\n    renown -medium\n    say: You set your palm on the black stone and push. The door swings wide. The Star rises past you like a breath held for three hundred years, and on its way out it touches your shoulder, the way a lord touches a sword. Above, every candle in Lumenhold goes out, and does not come back on until dawn.\n  else:\n    end ending_hollow_claimed\n> flee: Run\n  health -large\n  mood -large\n  flag hollow_fled\n  say: You run until the tunnel spits you out onto the shore. Behind you, the door is still ajar. In the morning, the headmistress's hair has gone white.\n\n@event hs_aftermath\ntitle: After the Thaw\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\ntrigger: queued\nart: sun\ntags: hollowstar story\nchain: hollowstar\nscene: The lake glittering in spring sunshine, students skimming stones, a bandaged figure sitting on the jetty.\ntext: The lake glitters as if nothing happened. Most of the academy believes there was a strange spring frost. The headmistress knows better. So does Kael, who finds you on the jetty with two cups of tea.\n> left: Sit with him in silence\n  rel kael +large\n  mood +medium\n  say: You watch the water. It's just water, now. Kael's tea, for once, doesn't freeze.\n> right: Tell him how you feel\n  if spark(kael) >= 25:\n    spark kael +large\n    bond kael partner\n    say: He kisses you, and for the first time since you've known him, his hands are warm.\n  else:\n    rel kael +large\n    if bond(kael) == \"none\":\n      bond kael close\n    say: \"I owe you everything,\" he says. \"I'm not good at owing people. I'll learn.\"\n\n@event hs_kael_sister\ntitle: The Sister\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\nwhen: flag(kael_ally) and turn >= 22 and not flag(met_sister)\nweight: 10\nart: snow\ntags: mystery hollowstar\nchain: hollowstar\nscene: A girl in white furs standing at the academy gates in a snowstorm that is falling only on her.\ntext: A girl in white furs is waiting at the gates in a private snowstorm. Kael's younger sister, Ilse. \"If my brother won't open it,\" she says, \"I will. Unless you can give me a reason not to.\"\n> left: Convince her\n  flag met_sister\n  find ilse_truth\n  challenge negotiate trivial:\n    win:\n      rel kael +large\n      say: You talk for an hour. Then she's crying, and Kael is holding her, and the snow stops. She stays in the village to be near him.\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      say: She listens politely and leaves. \"We'll see each other again,\" she says. \"Beneath the water.\"\n> right: Warn the headmistress\n  flag met_sister\n  find ilse_truth\n  rel isolde +small\n  rel kael -small\n  say: Ilse is escorted from the grounds. Kael understands. He says he understands. He's quiet for a long time.\n"},{"path":"content/events/chain_ilse.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  CHAIN: Ilse Dumont (expansion)\n#  Kael's younger sister, raised by the Hollow cult. Enters once\n#  the Hollow Star thread is under way (met her at the gates, or\n#  Kael known + two Hollow Star clues).\n#    ilse_village -> ilse_frost_lessons -> ilse_grey_returns\n#      -> (ilse_saved) ilse_kael_reunion / ilse_northern_letter / ilse_farewell\n#      -> (ilse_taken) she is gone north\n# ============================================================\n\n@event ilse_village\ntitle: The Girl at the Bakery\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\nactor: ilse\nwhen: turn >= 16 and not met(ilse) and (flag(met_sister) or (met(kael) and clues(hollowstar) >= 2))\nweight: 16\nart: snow\ntags: mystery hollowstar\nchain: ilse\nscene: A village bakery counter, a pale girl in white furs pushing a coin that is visibly frosted across the wood, a baffled baker.\ntext: In the village bakery a girl in white furs is trying to pay for a bun with a coin so cold it has frozen to the counter. The baker is losing patience. You recognise those eyes: they're Kael's. \"Ilse,\" she says, before you ask. \"Don't.\"\n> left: Pay for her bun\n  meet ilse\n  gold -tiny\n  rel ilse +medium\n  say: She stares at the bun, then at you, as if kindness is a trick she hasn't learned the counter to yet. \"Fine,\" she says. \"Thank you. Fine.\"\n> right: Leave her to it\n  meet ilse\n  rel ilse -tiny\n  say: You leave. Through the window you see the coin crack, and the baker's breath freeze in the air. Ilse walks out with the bun. Nobody stops her.\n\n@event ilse_frost_lessons\ntitle: A Trade in Winter\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\ntrigger: social\nactor: ilse\nwhen: rel(ilse) >= 3\nweight: 14\nart: snow\ntags: magic hollowstar\nchain: ilse\nscene: A frozen pond at the edge of the woods, a girl in white furs drawing a perfect hexagon of frost in the air, a student copying her clumsily.\ntext: Ilse finds you by the frozen pond. \"My brother won't talk to me. You will. Tell me how he really is, and I'll teach you Northern frost-craft. It's better than anything your professors know.\"\n> left: Tell her the truth about Kael\n  rel ilse +large\n  arcana +medium\n  say: You tell her about the cold hands, the roof at three in the morning, the bread. She listens without moving. Then she teaches you to freeze a falling leaf mid-air.\n> right: Protect Kael's privacy\n  rel ilse -small\n  rel kael +small\n  say: \"He can tell you himself,\" you say. Ilse's eyes go flat. But the next day, she's at the pond again. \"Fine. I'll teach you anyway. You'll need it.\"\n\n@event ilse_grey_returns\ntime: night\ntitle: The Man in Grey Comes for Ilse\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\ntrigger: social\nactor: ilse\nwhen: rel(ilse) >= 7 and turn >= 20 and not flag(ilse_saved) and not flag(ilse_taken)\nweight: 18\nart: mask\ntags: danger hollowstar\nchain: ilse\nscene: A moonlit village lane, a man in a grey coat with one hand on a girl's shoulder, frost and shadow coiling around them both.\ntext: Late at night, a lane behind the bakery. The man in grey has one hand on Ilse's shoulder. \"Home,\" he is saying softly. \"Your family is waiting. The door is waiting.\" Ilse sees you. She doesn't call out. She just looks.\n> left: Step between them\n  challenge combat trivial:\n    win:\n      flag ilse_saved\n      rel ilse +large\n      renown +small\n      alignment +small\n      find ilse_truth\n      say: Your spell breaks his grip. He looks at his frosted hand with mild surprise, bows, and walks into the dark. Ilse is shaking. \"Nobody's ever — \" she says, and stops.\n    lose:\n      flag ilse_taken\n      health -large\n      alignment +small\n      find ilse_truth\n      say: He swats your spell aside like a moth. When you get up, the lane is empty except for a single white glove in the snow.\n> right: Run for Kael\n  if met(kael):\n    flag ilse_saved\n    rel kael +large\n    rel ilse +medium\n    find ilse_truth\n    say: Kael arrives like a blizzard. The man in grey takes one look at him and leaves without a word. Brother and sister stand in the snow, not touching, not speaking.\n  else:\n    flag ilse_taken\n    mood -medium\n    find ilse_truth\n    say: By the time you're back with help, the lane is empty. There's frost on the cobbles in the shape of a closed door.\n\n@event ilse_kael_reunion\ntitle: Brother and Sister\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\ntrigger: social\nactor: ilse\nwhen: flag(ilse_saved) and met(kael) and not flag(dumont_reunited)\nweight: 16\nart: snow\ntags: social hollowstar\nchain: ilse\nscene: Two pale siblings on opposite ends of a frozen jetty, a third person between them holding three cups of tea.\ntext: Kael and Ilse have been in the same village for weeks and have spoken eleven words to each other. You have three cups of tea, a frozen jetty and a very bad idea.\n> left: Sit them both down\n  challenge negotiate trivial:\n    win:\n      flag dumont_reunited\n      rel kael +large\n      rel ilse +large\n      say: It takes an hour and two cups of tea are frozen solid. Then Ilse says \"I was scared,\" and Kael says \"So was I,\" and you quietly leave them to it.\n    lose:\n      rel kael +small\n      rel ilse +small\n      say: They argue in a language you don't speak, at a temperature you can't endure. But they argue — which, you suspect, is a start.\n> right: Let them find their own way\n  rel kael +small\n  rel ilse +small\n  say: You leave the tea on the jetty. A week later you see them walking by the lake together, a careful arm's length apart. Then less.\n\n@event ilse_northern_letter\ntitle: A Summons From the North\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\ntrigger: social\nactor: ilse\nwhen: flag(ilse_saved) and final_year and not flag(ilse_decided)\nweight: 14\nart: letter\ntags: hollowstar\nchain: ilse\nscene: A girl holding a letter sealed with a black star, standing at a window that looks north toward far white mountains.\ntext: A letter with a black-star seal. The Dumont family summons Ilse home. \"If I don't go, they'll send someone worse than the man in grey,\" she says. \"If I do go, I could tell them to their faces that it's over. Or they could keep me.\"\n> left: \"Go. Tell them it's over.\"\n  flag ilse_decided ilse_went_north\n  rel ilse +medium\n  say: She leaves on the dawn barge, chin up. Before she goes, she presses her forehead to yours, a Northern farewell. \"If I'm not back by graduation,\" she says, \"come and get me.\"\n> right: \"Stay. Let them come.\"\n  flag ilse_decided\n  rel ilse +large\n  gain frost_ward\n  say: She stays. She sews a sliver of winter into your collar \"in case they do come\". Nobody does. Not yet.\n\n@event ilse_farewell\ntitle: Snow in Blossomtide\nclass: story\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: ilse\nwhen: flag(ilse_saved) and final_year and month >= 9 and not flag(ilse_goodbye)\nweight: 14\nart: snow\ntags: social\nchain: ilse\nscene: A single snowflake falling in a spring orchard full of blossom, a girl in white furs catching it on her glove and holding it out.\ntext: It's spring, the orchard is in full blossom, and it is snowing — gently, over one tree. Ilse is underneath it. \"Graduation's soon,\" she says. \"Everyone's leaving. I wanted to give you something before you forgot me.\"\n> left: \"I won't forget you.\"\n  flag ilse_goodbye\n  rel ilse +large\n  gain frost_ward\n  mood +medium\n  say: She doesn't believe you, but she smiles anyway — the first real smile you've seen on her. She gives you a snowflake that never melts.\n> right: Invite her to the graduation\n  flag ilse_goodbye\n  rel ilse +large\n  rel kael +small\n  say: \"Me? At a graduation?\" She looks horrified, and then, slowly, delighted. On the day, she sits next to Kael in the second row and cries more than anyone.\n"},{"path":"content/events/chains_minor.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Minor chains: short cause-and-effect stories.\n#    forbidden  — the Restricted Section key (from umbral_forbidden_notes)\n#    curse      — curing the Umbral Curse\n#    debt       — Grisk the moneylender (see state in_debt)\n#    familiar   — Ember the fox-spirit\n#    ghost      — the haunted dormitory\n# ============================================================\n\n# ---------------- The Restricted Section ----------------\n\n@event forbidden_section_1\ntitle: The Silver Key\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\ntrigger: queued\nart: key\ntags: mystery\nchain: forbidden\nscene: A heavy iron door at the end of a library aisle, a silver key turning in its lock, a candle held low.\ntext: Midnight. The silver key fits the Restricted Section door perfectly. Beyond it, shelves of black-bound books breathe softly in the dark. Somewhere, a prefect's footsteps.\n> left: Turn back\n  mood -tiny\n  flag forbidden_abandoned\n  say: You lock the door again and hang the key on a nail in your wardrobe. Some nights you hear it rattling.\n> right: Slip inside\n  challenge deceive trivial:\n    win:\n      lore +medium\n      queue forbidden_section_2 in 2\n      say: You spend two hours reading by candle-stub. You leave with a head full of things no first-year should know.\n    lose:\n      gain detention\n      gain watched\n      rel bram -medium\n      say: Bram Hollis is waiting by the door when you come out. He is smiling. It's the worst smile you've ever seen.\n\n@event forbidden_section_2\ntitle: The Codex of Hollow Names\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\ntrigger: queued\nart: book\ntags: mystery hollowstar\nchain: forbidden\nscene: A book bound in something that is not leather, open on a reading stand, its letters slowly rearranging themselves.\ntext: On your third visit, a book finds you. It falls from a high shelf and lands open at your feet: the Codex of Hollow Names. The first page is a list of names. The last one is still being written. It's yours.\n> left: Put it back\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You shelve it. When you look back, it has fallen again, open at the same page.\n> right: Read it\n  lore +medium\n  arcana +medium\n  alignment -tiny\n  find codex_names\n  queue forbidden_section_3 in 2\n  chance 45:\n    gain umbral_curse\n    say: You read until dawn. You learn about the thing under the lake. When you finally close the book, your fingertips are grey.\n  else:\n    say: You read until dawn and close the book with shaking hands. Some things you can't unread.\n\n@event forbidden_section_3\ntitle: Caught\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\ntrigger: queued\nactor: isolde\nart: lock\ntags: mystery\nchain: forbidden\nscene: A tall silhouette in the doorway of the Restricted Section, holding a lantern that casts long shadows over a startled student.\ntext: A lantern blazes in the doorway. Not a prefect this time: the headmistress herself. She looks at the book in your hands, then at you, for a very long time. \"Most students who find that book,\" she says, \"don't put it down.\"\n> left: Apologise and accept punishment\n  alignment +small\n  meet isolde\n  study -medium\n  gain detention\n  rel isolde +small\n  unflag forbidden_key\n  say: She takes the key. \"Detention. And come to my tower on Thursdays. If you're going to learn dangerous things, you'll learn them properly.\"\n> right: Ask her what it means\n  meet isolde\n  rel isolde +medium\n  lore +medium\n  find codex_names\n  unflag forbidden_key\n  say: A long pause. \"It means something is waking, and it is looking for help. Make sure it doesn't find yours.\" She takes the key and leaves you the candle.\n\n# ---------------- The Umbral Curse ----------------\n\n@event curse_cure_1\ntitle: A Cold That Won't Leave\nclass: story\ntone: grim\nwhen: has(umbral_curse)\nweight: 30\nart: skull\ntags: crisis mystery\nchain: curse\nscene: A student wrapped in three blankets beside a roaring fire, breath still fogging the air, fingertips grey.\ntext: The cold under your skin is getting worse. Sister Amaranth's tonics do nothing. She looks troubled. \"This isn't an illness. It's a hunger.\" She lowers her voice. \"Old Maud might know something. Don't tell anyone I said so.\"\n> left: Endure it\n  mood -tiny\n  arcana +tiny\n  say: You pile on blankets and keep going. The cold whispers sometimes. You pretend not to hear.\n> right: Go to Old Maud\n  meet maud\n  rel maud +small\n  flag moonpetal_sought\n  say: Maud takes one look at your fingertips and puts the kettle on. \"Moonpetal,\" she says. \"Grows in the Whisperwood. Only blooms at midnight. You'll fetch it yourself — the cure has to come from the cursed.\"\n\n@event curse_cure_2\ntitle: Moonpetal\nclass: story\ntone: tense\nplace: whisperwood\nwhen: has(umbral_curse) and flag(moonpetal_sought)\nrepeat: 6\nweight: 60\nart: flower\ntags: nature danger\nchain: curse\nscene: A dark forest at midnight, a single silver flower glowing in a clearing, eyes watching from between the trees.\ntext: The Whisperwood after dark. Things move between the trees. In a clearing, a single silver flower is opening, and between you and it stands a stag made of shadow.\n> left: Sneak around it\n  challenge deceive trivial:\n    win:\n      lose umbral_curse\n      rel maud +medium\n      say: You creep through the bracken and pluck the moonpetal. Maud's brew tastes like moonlight and burnt sugar. The cold is gone.\n    lose:\n      health -large\n      say: The stag sees you. You run. No moonpetal tonight; you'll have to come back.\n> right: Face the stag\n  challenge combat trivial:\n    win:\n      lose umbral_curse\n      vigor +small\n      rel maud +medium\n      say: You stand your ground. The stag lowers its head — and steps aside. Maud's brew drives the cold out of your bones.\n    lose:\n      health -large\n      say: The stag charges. You wake up in the bracken at dawn. Maud bandages you. \"Another night,\" she says.\n\n# ---------------- Grisk the Moneylender ----------------\n\n@event grisk_offer\ntitle: A Helpful Goblin\nclass: story\ntone: tense\nwhen: gold < 15 and turn >= 3 and not has(in_debt) and not flag(grisk_done)\nweight: 30\nart: pouch\ntags: money danger\nchain: debt\nscene: A small goblin in a velvet waistcoat and pince-nez, sitting on a crate in an alley, a ledger in one hand and a quill behind his ear.\ntext: A goblin in a velvet waistcoat falls into step beside you. \"Grisk,\" he says, \"friend to the temporarily embarrassed. Sixty crowns, today. Twelve a month for six months. Very reasonable.\"\n> left: No thank you\n  meet grisk\n  mood -tiny\n  say: \"My door is always open,\" Grisk says. You suspect it is actually always locked.\n> right: Take the loan\n  meet grisk\n  gold +huge\n  gain in_debt\n  debt_paid = 0\n  debt_missed = 0\n  flag grisk_done\n  say: He counts out sixty crowns, writes your name in the ledger and smiles with a great many teeth.\n\n@event grisk_board_debt\ntitle: Settled, With Interest\nclass: incident\ntone: tense\ntrigger: queued\npriority: 70\nart: pouch\ntags: money danger\nchain: debt\nscene: A goblin in a velvet waistcoat waiting outside the bursar's office, waving a receipt like a small flag.\ntext: The bursar's office has stopped sending reminders. Instead, Grisk is waiting outside it with a receipt. \"Two months' board, paid in full,\" he beams. \"By me. A loan, naturally. Sixty crowns, twelve a month for six months. You can sign here.\"\n> left: Sign his ledger\n  meet grisk\n  gold +huge\n  gain in_debt\n  debt_paid = 0\n  debt_missed = 0\n  flag grisk_done\n  say: You sign. He counts the change into your palm and writes your name in the ledger with a flourish that has teeth in it.\n> right: Beg the bursar for time\n  meet grisk\n  flag grisk_done\n  mood -small\n  renown -tiny\n  say: You spend an afternoon on a hard bench outside the bursar's office and leave with a month's grace and the feeling of having been looked at over spectacles for a very long time.\n\n@event grisk_favour\ntitle: Grisk's Proposition\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\nwhen: has(in_debt)\nweight: 14\nart: chain\ntags: money mystery\nchain: debt\nscene: The goblin moneylender leaning close in a library alcove, pushing a slip of paper across a table.\ntext: Grisk finds you in the library. \"I could cancel your debt,\" he murmurs. \"A small favour. There's a book in the archive — the Founders' Ledger. A client wants a page. Just one page.\"\n> left: Refuse\n  alignment +tiny\n  say: \"Pity.\" He makes a note in his ledger. It looks expensive.\n> right: Do the favour\n  challenge deceive trivial:\n    win:\n      lose in_debt\n      flag grisk_favour\n      alignment -small\n      say: You razor a page from the oldest ledger in the archive. Grisk tears up your debt. You notice the page had a black star on it.\n    lose:\n      gain detention\n      study -medium\n      say: The archivist catches you with a razor in your hand. Detention. Grisk is nowhere to be found, and the debt stands.\n\n@event grisk_smuggling\ntime: afternoon evening\ntitle: One More Job\nclass: story\ntone: tense\nwhen: flag(grisk_favour) and not has(in_debt)\nweight: 10\nart: pouch\ntags: money danger\nchain: debt\nscene: A crate of clinking bottles hidden under a tarp on a dark boathouse dock, a small goblin silhouetted against the lake.\ntext: Grisk again. \"You were so good last time. A crate needs moving across the lake tonight. Don't open it. Thirty crowns.\"\n> left: You're done with him\n  unflag grisk_favour\n  flag grisk_gone\n  mood +small\n  say: \"Everyone's done with me eventually,\" Grisk says, without rancour. You never see him again.\n> right: Move the crate\n  challenge deceive trivial:\n    win:\n      gold +large\n      say: The crate clinks. It is also, faintly, singing. You deliver it without looking inside. Thirty crowns.\n    lose:\n      gold +small\n      gain detention\n      rel bram -medium\n      say: Bram stops you on the dock. The crate is full of contraband charm-wine. You lose the crate, keep eight crowns and gain a detention.\n\n# ---------------- Ember the Fox-Spirit ----------------\n\n@event fox_injured\ntitle: Smoke in the Woods\nclass: story\ntone: light\nwhen: turn >= 4 and not flag(fox_met)\nweight: 6 + flag(familiar_affinity) * 14\nart: fox\ntags: nature\nchain: familiar\nscene: A small red fox lying in the snow at the forest edge, its tail smouldering like a dying campfire, one paw caught in a rusted snare.\ntext: At the edge of the forest, a thin plume of smoke. A small fox is caught in a rusted snare, its tail smouldering like an ember. It snarls at you, then whimpers.\n> left: Leave it — it's wild\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You walk away. That night you dream of smoke.\n> right: Free it and bandage the paw\n  flag fox_met\n  health -tiny\n  queue fox_returns in 2\n  say: It bites you, of course. But it lets you wrap the paw. Then it limps into the trees, glancing back once with eyes like embers.\n\n@event fox_returns\ntitle: A Visitor at the Window\nclass: story\ntone: light\ntrigger: queued\nart: fox\ntags: nature\nchain: familiar\nscene: A fox with a softly glowing tail sitting on a stone windowsill at dawn, a ruby glinting at its feet.\ntext: Two months later, a fox with a glowing tail is sitting on your windowsill. Its paw has healed. At its feet: a small, dusty ruby.\n> left: Shoo it away\n  gold +medium\n  say: It leaves the ruby and vanishes. You sell the ruby. Somehow you feel you've failed a test.\n> right: Open the window\n  gold +medium\n  mood +small\n  queue fox_bond in 1\n  say: It hops in, inspects your room thoroughly and falls asleep on your pillow. Your pillow is now slightly scorched. You don't mind.\n\n@event fox_bond\ntitle: Ember\nclass: story\ntone: light\ntrigger: queued\nart: fox\ntags: nature magic\nchain: familiar\nscene: A student and a small fire-tailed fox nose to nose, a soft golden light connecting them.\ntext: The fox has decided. {term: It follows you to class|It follows you through the half-empty corridors}, sleeps on your feet and growls at Bram. Students aren't allowed unregistered familiars. Registering one costs fifteen crowns.\n> left: Hide her\n  challenge deceive trivial:\n    win:\n      gain familiar_fox\n      say: You name her Ember. Nobody notices a fox that doesn't want to be noticed.\n    lose:\n      gain familiar_fox\n      gold -medium\n      say: A professor spots her at once. You pay the fee — and Ember is yours, officially.\n> right: Register her properly\n  gold -medium\n  gain familiar_fox\n  rel isolde +tiny\n  say: The registrar stamps a form. \"Name?\" \"Ember,\" you say. The fox looks smug.\n\n@event fox_helps\ntitle: Ember Finds Something\nclass: story\ntone: light\nwhen: has(familiar_fox)\nweight: 10\nrepeat: 8\nart: fox\ntags: nature\nchain: familiar\nscene: A fox nosing through dry leaves under a hedge and emerging with a purse in its jaws.\ntext: Ember drags you, by the sleeve, through three corridors and a hedge. She's found something. She's very proud of it.\n> left: Let her keep it\n  mood +small\n  say: It's a sock. Ember is thrilled. You are thrilled for her.\n> right: See what it is\n  chance 50:\n    gold +medium\n    say: A lost purse. You return it to its owner, who insists you keep the reward.\n  else:\n    lore +small\n    say: A lost page from a professor's lecture notes. Enlightening.\n\n# ---------------- The Haunted Dormitory ----------------\n\n@event ghost_socks\ntime: morning\ntitle: Rearranged Socks\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\nwhen: turn >= 3 and not flag(ghost_met)\nweight: 8\nart: ghost\ntags: mystery\nchain: ghost\nscene: A dormitory drawer open in candlelight, socks arranged in a careful spiral, a faint shimmer in the air above them.\ntext: Every morning, your socks have been rearranged. By colour. Then alphabetically by colour. Last night they spelled out \"HELP\".\n> left: Ignore it\n  gain haunted\n  say: You buy new socks. The next morning they're arranged by mood.\n> right: Stay up and watch\n  flag ghost_met\n  gain haunted\n  queue ghost_reveal now\n  say: At three in the morning, the drawer slides open by itself.\n\n@event ghost_reveal\ntitle: Prudence\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\ntrigger: queued\nart: ghost\ntags: mystery\nchain: ghost\nscene: A translucent girl in old-fashioned school robes, hovering by a window, holding a faded letter.\ntext: A girl in old-fashioned robes, translucent and very polite. \"Prudence Ashworth, class of 1793. Terribly sorry about the socks. I never finished my letter. Could you deliver it? The village. The bakery. Please.\"\n> left: Exorcise her\n  lose haunted\n  arcana +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You speak the banishing rite. She fades with a small, hurt look. Your socks stay put after that. You feel a bit rotten.\n> right: Promise to deliver it\n  rel maud +tiny\n  queue ghost_letter in 1\n  say: She claps her translucent hands. \"Oh, thank you! It's only taken two hundred years.\"\n\n@event ghost_letter\ntitle: A Letter Two Hundred Years Late\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\ntrigger: queued\nart: letter\ntags: mystery social\nscene: A cosy village bakery, an old baker reading a yellowed letter with tears on her cheeks, a ghostly figure smiling in the doorway.\ntext: The bakery has been in the same family for two hundred years. The old baker reads Prudence's letter at the counter. \"My great-great-great-grandmother waited for this,\" she says. \"She never stopped.\"\n> left: Stay and listen to the story\n  lose haunted\n  mood +large\n  lore +small\n  say: Prudence was in love with the baker's daughter. When you return, the ghost is waiting, glowing like a sunrise. Then she's gone, at peace.\n> right: Leave quietly\n  lose haunted\n  mood +medium\n  renown +small\n  say: The baker presses a free loaf into your hands. That night, your socks are arranged into a heart. Then never again.\n"},{"path":"content/events/classes.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Elective events — only drawn while the matching class state\n#  is active (see states/states.grim, group: elective).\n#  Convention: weight 16 so your class shows up most months.\n# ============================================================\n\n# ---------------- Pyromancy ----------------\n\n@event pyro_candle\ntitle: Light the Candle\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrack: evocation\nweight: 16\nart: candle\ntags: class magic\nscene: A dark classroom, a single candle on a stool at the far end, Professor Thorne's silhouette with folded arms.\ntext: Professor Thorne places a candle at the far end of the hall. \"Light it. From there.\" The class holds its breath. Last week a boy lit his own sleeve.\n> left: A careful spark\n  challenge spell easy:\n    win:\n      arcana +tiny\n      rel thorne +small\n      say: The wick catches with a polite little flame. Thorne grunts. From him, that is applause.\n    lose:\n      mood -tiny\n      say: Nothing. Then a wisp of smoke from your fingertips. A few people giggle.\n> right: Show them real fire\n  challenge spell hard:\n    win:\n      arcana +small\n      renown +small\n      rel thorne +tiny\n      say: The candle erupts in a pillar of white flame and then settles, obedient. The room goes quiet.\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      study -small\n      rel thorne -tiny\n      say: The candle lights. So do the curtains. And, briefly, your eyebrows.\n\n@event pyro_ember_heart\ntitle: The Ember Within\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrack: evocation\nweight: 16\nart: flame\ntags: class magic\nscene: Students sitting cross-legged in a stone circle, each with a faint glow behind their ribs.\ntext: Today's exercise: hold a spark inside your chest and let it grow. \"Fire is fed by will,\" says Thorne. \"And by you, if you are careless.\" It burns. It is supposed to burn.\n> left: Stop when it hurts\n  health -tiny\n  arcana +tiny\n  mood +tiny\n  say: You let the spark gutter out. Thorne says nothing. The silence is a grade.\n> right: Hold it longer than anyone\n  arcana +medium\n  health -medium\n  say: Your vision goes white at the edges. When you open your eyes, your breath is steaming and the stones around you are warm.\n\n@event pyro_lyra_spar\ntitle: A Challenge in Class\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nwhen: met(lyra)\ntrack: evocation\nactor: lyra\nweight: 14\nart: flame\ntags: class rival\nscene: Two students facing each other across a scorched practice ring; one is a tall girl with crimson hair, flames curling around her wrists.\ntext: \"Professor, I'd like a demonstration partner.\" Lyra Vaneshade points her wand at you without looking. \"That one.\"\n> left: Politely decline\n  rel lyra -tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: \"As expected,\" Lyra says, and turns away. It stings worse than a burn would.\n> right: Step into the ring\n  challenge combat easy:\n    win:\n      rel lyra +medium\n      renown +small\n      if bond(lyra) == \"none\":\n        bond lyra rival\n      say: Your fire meets hers and holds. When Thorne calls time, Lyra is breathing hard. \"Again. Next week,\" she says.\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      rel lyra +small\n      say: She wins in under a minute — but she looks at you afterwards like she's filing you away for later.\n\n# ---------------- Tidecalling ----------------\n\n@event tide_lake_lesson\ntitle: The Lake Answers\nclass: story\ntone: light\ntrack: evocation\nweight: 16\nart: wave\ntags: class magic nature\nscene: Students standing waist-deep in a silver lake at dawn, ripples spiralling out from their outstretched hands.\ntext: Waist-deep in the freezing lake at dawn, Professor Marisol tells you to ask the water to rise. \"Ask,\" she says. \"Never demand. The lake remembers rudeness.\"\n> left: Command it anyway\n  challenge spell easy:\n    win:\n      arcana +small\n      say: The water rises in a shivering column. Marisol raises an eyebrow. \"Effective. Rude.\"\n    lose:\n      health -tiny\n      say: The lake declines, and expresses its displeasure by knocking you flat.\n> right: Listen first\n  lore +small\n  mood +small\n  say: You stand still until the cold stops hurting. The lake murmurs — and for a moment you could swear it said your name.\n  chance 30:\n    flag lake_knows\n\n@event tide_storm_drill\ntitle: Storm Drill\nclass: windfall\ntone: light\ntrack: evocation\nweight: 16\nart: rain\ntags: class nature\nscene: A rocky shore under black clouds, a few figures in soaked robes holding back a curtain of rain with raised hands.\ntext: A storm rolls over the lake and Marisol is delighted. \"Perfect conditions! Hold back the rain for as long as you can.\" Most of the class has already fled.\n> left: Shelter with the sensible ones\n  mood +tiny\n  say: You watch from the boathouse, dry and slightly ashamed.\n> right: Stay out in it\n  vigor +small\n  arcana +tiny\n  chance 35:\n    gain sick\n    say: You hold the rain back for almost a minute. You also catch Lake Fever.\n  else:\n    say: For a full minute, the rain parts around you. Marisol whoops like a child.\n\n@event tide_drowned_bell\ntitle: The Drowned Bell\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\nwhen: not flag(bell_fragment)\ntrack: evocation\nweight: 12\nart: bell\ntags: class mystery hollowstar\nscene: Murky green water, a shaft of light catching the bronze curve of an enormous bell half-buried in silt.\ntext: During a breathing-charm lesson you hear it: a bell, deep under the lake, tolling slow and soft. Nobody else seems to notice.\n> left: Mention it to Professor Marisol\n  lore +tiny\n  say: Her face goes very still. \"There is no bell,\" she says. \"There hasn't been a bell for three hundred years.\"\n  flag heard_bell\n  find lake_bell\n> right: Dive down to find it\n  challenge feat medium:\n    win:\n      lore +small\n      flag bell_fragment\n      find lake_bell\n      say: Deep in the silt you find a broken piece of bronze, warm to the touch, engraved with a black star.\n    lose:\n      health -medium\n      say: The water closes over you, dark and cold. You surface gasping, empty-handed, with the bell still ringing in your teeth.\n\n# ---------------- Illusion ----------------\n\n@event illusion_mirror_self\ntitle: A Second You\nclass: windfall\ntone: light\ntrack: illusion\nweight: 16\nart: mirror\ntags: class magic\nscene: A student facing a shimmering duplicate of themselves; the duplicate's smile is a little too wide.\ntext: Assignment: conjure a convincing illusion of yourself. Professor Vey circles the room. \"Most students make themselves taller. Interesting, what people choose.\"\n> left: Make it flattering\n  charm +small\n  mood +small\n  say: Your double is radiant, confident, slightly better-looking. Vey writes something down and smiles thinly.\n> right: Make it accurate\n  guile +tiny\n  lore +tiny\n  say: Your double slouches the way you slouch and chews its lip the way you do. It is deeply unsettling. Vey awards top marks.\n  study +tiny\n\n@event illusion_professor_prank\ntitle: The Professor's Face\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrack: illusion\nweight: 14\nart: mask\ntags: class social\nscene: A student wearing a shimmering glamour of a stern old professor, classmates stifling laughter behind a door.\ntext: Your classmates dare you: wear a glamour of Professor Thorne and cancel tomorrow's Arcana lecture. \"Nobody will know,\" says someone who will definitely tell.\n> left: Absolutely not\n  study +tiny\n  say: Wise. Someone else tries it the next day and spends a week scrubbing gargoyles.\n> right: Do it\n  challenge deceive easy:\n    win:\n      renown +medium\n      mood +small\n      say: \"Class is cancelled,\" you announce in Thorne's gravel voice. The cheering is audible from three towers away.\n    lose:\n      gain detention\n      study -small\n      rel thorne -small\n      say: Halfway through, the glamour flickers. The real Thorne is standing in the doorway. He is not laughing.\n\n@event illusion_dragon\ntitle: The Courtyard Dragon\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nwhen: arcana >= 12\ntrack: illusion\nweight: 12\nart: dragon\ntags: class magic\nscene: A vast translucent dragon coiled around the academy's central fountain, students frozen mid-step beneath it.\ntext: For your term project you must create an illusion that fools a crowd. You could make something small and flawless. Or you could put a dragon in the courtyard at lunchtime.\n> left: Small and flawless\n  study +medium\n  say: A single butterfly that nobody can tell is fake. Vey taps it with her wand and it bursts into light. \"Perfect.\"\n> right: Dragon. Obviously.\n  challenge spell trivial:\n    win:\n      renown +medium\n      study +small\n      say: The dragon uncurls around the fountain and yawns. Forty students scream. Vey laughs until she cries.\n    lose:\n      renown +small\n      study -small\n      rel isolde -tiny\n      say: The dragon is magnificent for four seconds and then sneezes into a cloud of glitter. The headmistress was eating lunch below it.\n\n# ---------------- Practical Alchemy ----------------\n\n@event alch_first_brew\ntitle: Draught of Mild Courage\nclass: windfall\ntone: light\ntrack: alchemy\nweight: 16\nart: cauldron\ntags: class\nscene: Rows of bubbling cauldrons in a low stone room, each giving off a different coloured steam.\ntext: The recipe is on the board: Draught of Mild Courage. Seven ingredients, three stirs clockwise, one counter-clockwise, and never — never — add the newt eye early.\n> left: Follow the recipe exactly\n  alchemy +tiny\n  study +tiny\n  say: A clear, honey-coloured brew. The professor sniffs it and nods. Safe, solid, unremarkable.\n> right: Improvise a little\n  challenge brew easy:\n    win:\n      alchemy +medium\n      gold +small\n      say: Your variant tastes like cinnamon and makes people brave enough to ask for things. A fifth-year buys the whole flask.\n    lose:\n      gain frog_voice\n      say: You taste-test. Mistake. For the next month, everything you say comes out as a deep, wet croak.\n\n@event alch_unstable_cauldron\ntitle: Unstable Cauldron\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\ntrack: alchemy\nweight: 14\nart: potion\ntags: class danger\nscene: A cauldron shuddering on its iron legs, violet bubbles swelling over the rim, a student frozen beside it.\ntext: The cauldron next to yours starts to shudder. Its owner, a terrified first-year, has added the newt eye early. It is going to blow.\n> left: Dive for cover\n  health -tiny\n  say: The explosion paints the ceiling violet. Nobody is hurt. Everybody is purple.\n> right: Try to neutralise it\n  challenge brew easy:\n    win:\n      renown +small\n      alchemy +small\n      say: A pinch of salt, a drop of moonwater, a quick counter-stir. The bubbling dies. The first-year bursts into grateful tears.\n    lose:\n      health -medium\n      say: The cauldron goes off in your face. You spend a week with violet eyebrows and a ringing in your ears.\n\n@event alch_corvin_market\ntitle: A Side Business\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nwhen: met(corvin)\ntrack: alchemy\nactor: corvin\nweight: 12\nart: bottle\ntags: class money\nscene: A young man leaning on a cauldron table, holding up a vial against the light with a merchant's grin.\ntext: Corvin turns your leftover potions over in his hands. \"These would sell for real crowns in the village. I know a buyer. I take a third. Nobody needs to know they came from a classroom.\"\n> left: Decline\n  rel corvin -tiny\n  say: \"Your loss, saint.\" He tosses the vial back. You fumble it.\n> right: Shake on it\n  gold +large\n  rel corvin +small\n  chance 30:\n    gain detention\n    study -small\n    say: It goes well until a vial turns up in a professor's tea. You and Corvin share a detention and an alibi.\n  else:\n    say: Thirty crowns, no questions asked. Corvin winks. \"Pleasure doing crime with you.\"\n\n# ---------------- Divination ----------------\n\n@event div_tea_leaves\ntitle: Reading the Leaves\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\ntrack: astromancy\nweight: 16\nart: cup\ntags: class mystery\nscene: A delicate teacup tipped on its saucer, the dregs forming a shape like a skull — or possibly a mushroom.\ntext: You read your partner's tea leaves. The shape at the bottom of the cup is unmistakably a skull. Your partner is looking at you hopefully.\n> left: Tell the truth\n  lore +small\n  chance 50:\n    say: \"A skull?\" They laugh. \"Professor says a skull means change.\" They're relieved. You aren't sure you should be.\n  else:\n    mood -tiny\n    say: They go pale and leave early. The next day they trip down the stairs. Nothing serious. Still.\n> right: Lie kindly\n  mood -tiny\n  charm +small\n  say: \"A mushroom,\" you say. \"Means good fortune.\" They beam. The professor, passing behind you, says nothing at all.\n\n@event div_next_tuesday\ntitle: The Mirror of Next Tuesday\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\ntrack: astromancy\nweight: 14\nart: mirror\ntags: class mystery\nscene: An ornate standing mirror whose reflection shows the room on a different day — the light at a different angle, a different person sitting in your chair.\ntext: The Mirror of Next Tuesday shows exactly what it promises. Students take turns looking. Some come away smiling. One comes away and goes straight to the infirmary.\n> left: Take a look\n  chance 50:\n    study +medium\n    say: Next Tuesday you are sitting an Arcana quiz. You memorise the questions. Is that cheating? It's at least ambiguous.\n  else:\n    mood -medium\n    say: Next Tuesday you are crying in the rain outside the observatory. You don't know why. You spend the week dreading it.\n> right: Decline\n  mood +tiny\n  say: \"I'd rather be surprised.\" The professor smiles as if you have passed a different test entirely.\n\n@event div_seraphine_vision\ntitle: She Grabs Your Hand\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\ntrack: astromancy\nactor: seraphine\nweight: 14\nart: eye\ntags: class mystery hollowstar\nchain: hollowstar\nscene: A dreamy-eyed girl gripping a classmate's wrist across a table of crystal balls, her pupils wide and silver.\ntext: In the middle of crystal-gazing, Seraphine Hale seizes your wrist. Her eyes have gone silver. \"The door under the lake,\" she whispers. \"Don't let him open it. Or do. I can't see which.\"\n> left: Pull away\n  meet seraphine\n  rel seraphine -tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: She blinks and lets go. \"Sorry. That happens.\" She goes back to her crystal as if nothing occurred.\n> right: Ask who she means\n  meet seraphine\n  rel seraphine +medium\n  lore +tiny\n  flag heard_prophecy\n  say: \"Someone cold,\" she says. \"Someone kind.\" Then she shakes her head, smiles vaguely, and asks if you'd like a biscuit.\n\n# ---------------- Artifice ----------------\n\n@event art_clockwork_beetle\ntitle: The Clockwork Beetle\nclass: windfall\ntone: light\ntrack: artifice\nweight: 16\nart: gear\ntags: class\nscene: A brass beetle the size of a fist on a workbench, tiny gears visible through its translucent wing-cases.\ntext: Your project: a brass beetle that walks. The gears are in. The spring is wound. You could make it efficient, or you could give it a little personality.\n> left: Make it efficient\n  study +small\n  lore +tiny\n  say: It walks in a perfect straight line until it hits a wall. Full marks.\n> right: Give it personality\n  mood +small\n  chance 40:\n    renown +small\n    say: It escapes during the presentation and lives in the walls for a month, occasionally stealing biscuits. It becomes a minor legend.\n  else:\n    say: It refuses to walk unless you compliment it. The professor is torn between marking you down and asking how you did it.\n\n@event art_mira_collab\ntitle: A Joint Project\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrack: artifice\nactor: mira\nweight: 14\nart: gear\ntags: class social\nscene: A cluttered workbench covered in tools, sketches and one smoking teapot; a grinning student with goggles pushed up into wild hair.\ntext: Mira Ten drops onto the bench beside you with a sketch of something that is either a self-stirring cauldron or a small war machine. \"Partners? Your hands, my brain. Or the other way round. Both, actually.\"\n> left: Work alone\n  meet mira\n  study +tiny\n  say: \"Fair,\" says Mira, with no hard feelings, and is immediately on fire. Not badly.\n> right: Partner with Mira\n  meet mira\n  challenge brew trivial:\n    win:\n      study +medium\n      rel mira +medium\n      say: The cauldron stirs itself, hums a tune and only threatens the professor once. Mira hugs you with oily hands.\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      rel mira +small\n      say: The prototype explodes. Mira laughs so hard they cry. \"Again! We were so close!\" You find you're laughing too.\n\n@event art_enchanted_quill\ntitle: The Self-Writing Quill\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrack: artifice\nweight: 14\nart: feather\ntags: class\nscene: A quill writing busily on its own over a sheaf of paper while its owner sleeps on folded arms.\ntext: You've enchanted a quill to take dictation. It works beautifully. A thought occurs: what if it wrote your essays, too?\n> left: Just use it for notes\n  lore +small\n  say: Your notes are flawless. Even the professor's jokes are transcribed, with a small marginal comment: \"(not funny).\"\n> right: Let it write your essays\n  chance 50:\n    study +medium\n    mood +small\n    say: Your essays arrive on time, in impeccable penmanship. A little too impeccable. Nobody notices. Yet.\n  else:\n    study -medium\n    gain detention\n    say: The quill develops opinions and writes a scathing review of the professor. It signs your name.\n\n# ---------------- Beastcraft ----------------\n\n@event beast_griffon_chick\ntitle: Hatching Day\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrack: beastcraft\nweight: 16\nart: feather\ntags: class nature\nscene: A straw nest under a heat lamp, a cracked golden egg, and a damp fluffy griffon chick blinking at the world.\ntext: The griffon egg is cracking. Professor Brask whispers that the first face a chick sees, it trusts forever — or attacks forever. Hard to predict.\n> left: Hang back\n  lore +tiny\n  say: The chick sees Brask first and bites her. She seems delighted.\n> right: Offer your hand\n  challenge feat trivial:\n    win:\n      mood +medium\n      vigor +tiny\n      say: The chick peeps, nuzzles your palm and falls asleep. You are, apparently, its mother now.\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      say: It bites. Hard. Griffon beaks are sharper than they look, and they look extremely sharp.\n\n@event beast_manticore\ntitle: The Manticore Is Loose\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\ntrack: beastcraft\nweight: 12\nart: paw\ntags: class danger\nscene: A shadowy lion-shape with a scorpion's tail prowling between the greenhouse benches, students pressed against the glass.\ntext: Someone left the manticore pen open. It's in the greenhouse, tail twitching, between you and the only exit.\n> left: Back away slowly\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You edge out. Professor Brask arrives with a bucket of meat and a lullaby and the crisis is over in minutes.\n> right: Try to calm it\n  challenge negotiate trivial:\n    win:\n      renown +medium\n      vigor +tiny\n      say: You hum the lullaby Brask taught you. The manticore yawns, sits, and lets you scratch behind its ear. The whole class saw.\n    lose:\n      health -large\n      gain injured\n      say: The manticore does not like your humming. The tail is quicker than you are.\n\n@event beast_familiar_call\ntitle: Calling a Familiar\nclass: windfall\ntone: light\nwhen: not has(familiar_fox)\ntrack: beastcraft\nweight: 12\nart: fox\ntags: class magic\nscene: A circle of salt and candles in a forest clearing at dusk, shapes of small animals watching from the edge of the trees.\ntext: Brask teaches the summoning circle. \"It doesn't summon,\" she says. \"It invites. Most of the time, nothing comes. That's fine. Familiars choose you.\"\n> left: Just watch the others\n  lore +tiny\n  say: A first-year is chosen by a very large toad. The toad looks smug about it.\n> right: Sit in the circle\n  mood +small\n  flag familiar_affinity\n  say: Nothing comes. But for a long moment, something at the edge of the trees watches you with eyes like embers. Then it is gone.\n\n# ---------------- Astromancy ----------------\n\n@event astro_comet\ntitle: A New Comet\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrack: astromancy\nweight: 16\nart: star\ntags: class\nscene: An observatory dome open to the night, a long-tailed comet smeared across the sky, students crowded at the telescopes.\ntext: A comet appears that isn't in any of the charts. The observatory is in uproar. Everyone wants to be the one to name it.\n> left: Sketch it carefully\n  lore +small\n  say: Your drawing is lovely. Someone else's name goes on the comet. You keep the drawing.\n> right: Calculate its path\n  challenge research easy:\n    win:\n      renown +medium\n      lore +small\n      say: Your math is right. The comet is named after you, or at least after a slight misspelling of you.\n    lose:\n      study -tiny\n      say: Your calculation suggests the comet will hit the academy on Thursday. It does not. You are teased about it for weeks.\n\n@event astro_kael_roof\ntitle: Three in the Morning\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\nwhen: met(kael)\ntrack: astromancy\nactor: kael\nweight: 14\nart: moon\ntags: class mystery hollowstar\nchain: hollowstar\nscene: A young man in a fur coat sitting alone on the observatory roof, frost on the tiles around him, staring at one spot in the stars.\ntext: Long after the lecture ends you find Kael alone on the observatory roof. Frost has spread across the tiles around him. He is staring at a patch of sky where there are no stars at all.\n> left: Leave him be\n  say: You go down to bed. In the morning there's a perfect circle of frost on the roof where he sat.\n> right: Sit beside him\n  rel kael +medium\n  lore +tiny\n  flag saw_hollow_star\n  say: \"There,\" he says, pointing at the dark. \"Can you see it? A star that eats light.\" You look for a long time. You think you can.\n\n@event astro_sleepless\ntitle: Sleepless Weeks\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrack: astromancy\nweight: 14\nart: bed\ntags: class\nscene: A student asleep on an open star atlas at a library desk, a guttering candle beside them, dawn light in the window.\ntext: Midnight lectures, dawn homework, morning classes. You have started seeing constellations in your porridge.\n> left: Skip a few lectures\n  mood -tiny\n  gain slacking\n  health +small\n  say: You sleep for fourteen hours and wake up feeling like a person.\n> right: Power through\n  health -small\n  lore +small\n  say: You learn the names of forty stars and forget the names of two friends. Temporarily.\n\n# ---------------- Healing Arts ----------------\n\n@event heal_first_patient\ntitle: Your First Patient\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrack: alchemy\nweight: 16\nart: cross\ntags: class\nscene: A pale first-year sitting on an infirmary cot holding a wrist at a strange angle, a nervous student healer kneeling beside them.\ntext: A first-year stumbles into the practice ward with a wrist that bends the wrong way. Sister Amaranth is busy. The first-year looks at you with enormous, trusting eyes.\n> left: Fetch Sister Amaranth\n  study +tiny\n  say: Amaranth fixes it in seconds and nods at you. \"Knowing your limits is the first lesson.\"\n> right: Heal it yourself\n  challenge brew easy:\n    win:\n      alchemy +small\n      renown +small\n      say: The bone slides back with a soft click and a glow of warm light. The first-year tells everyone you're a genius.\n    lose:\n      study -small\n      say: The wrist is now straight. The fingers, however, are pointing backwards. Amaranth sighs and fixes both.\n\n@event heal_fever_ward\ntitle: Fever in the Village\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrack: alchemy\nweight: 12\nart: cross\ntags: class nature\nscene: A crowded village hall lined with cots, lanterns hanging from the rafters, student healers moving between patients.\ntext: Lake Fever is spreading through the village below the academy. Sister Amaranth asks for student volunteers. It's hard, unpaid, and you might catch it.\n> left: Decline\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You watch the volunteers board the barge in the morning. You do your homework. It feels like the wrong homework.\n> right: Volunteer\n  renown +tiny\n  alchemy +tiny\n  mood +tiny\n  rel maud +small\n  chance 40:\n    gain sick\n    say: You save lives, and catch the fever yourself. The village sends a basket of honey cakes to your sickbed.\n  else:\n    say: A week of long nights and cool cloths. When you leave, the baker's daughter gives you her best loaf.\n\n@event heal_tobias_sting\ntitle: Stung\nclass: windfall\ntone: light\nwhen: met(tobias)\ntrack: alchemy\nactor: tobias\nweight: 12\nart: flower\ntags: class social\nscene: A broad-shouldered boy sitting on a greenhouse bench, grimacing, holding up a hand swollen to twice its size.\ntext: Tobias wanders into the practice ward holding up a hand swollen like a pumpkin. \"Mandrake,\" he says. \"She didn't like my singing.\" He looks hopefully at you.\n> left: Send him to the infirmary\n  rel tobias +tiny\n  say: \"Right. Proper healer. Good idea.\" He goes, a little disappointed.\n> right: Treat him yourself\n  alchemy +tiny\n  rel tobias +medium\n  spark tobias +tiny\n  say: Your hands are steady. The swelling goes down. Tobias stares at his hand, then at you. \"You're really good at this,\" he says softly.\n\n# ---------------- Umbral Theory ----------------\n\n@event umbral_whisper\ntitle: The Shadow Speaks\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\ntrack: umbral\nweight: 16\nart: skull\ntags: class mystery danger\nscene: A student at a desk in a dim lecture hall, their shadow on the wall behind them turned the wrong way, leaning close to whisper.\ntext: In the middle of a lecture on the Nature of Absence, your shadow leans away from the lamp, toward your ear, and says your name.\n> left: Banish it\n  arcana +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You speak the warding phrase. Your shadow snaps back into place, sulking. It does not speak again. You think.\n> right: Answer it\n  lore +medium\n  chance 40:\n    gain umbral_curse\n    say: It tells you something about death you can't unlearn. The cold starts the next morning.\n  else:\n    say: It tells you where a lost book is shelved in the library. It is right. You decide not to wonder how it knew.\n\n@event umbral_bone_mouse\ntitle: The Bone Mouse\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrack: umbral\nweight: 14\nart: skull\ntags: class magic\nscene: A tiny mouse skeleton on a silver tray, one bony paw twitching, students leaning away from it.\ntext: Practical exercise: animate the skeleton of a field mouse. \"Purely educational,\" the professor says, with a smile nobody trusts.\n> left: Refuse on principle\n  study -tiny\n  mood +tiny\n  say: You write a short essay on why instead. The professor reads it with surprising attention.\n> right: Do the exercise\n  challenge spell easy:\n    win:\n      arcana +medium\n      study +small\n      say: The little skeleton stands up, washes its non-existent whiskers, and lies down again. Perfect control.\n    lose:\n      gain haunted\n      say: It gets up. It does not lie down again. It follows you back to your dormitory and sits on your pillow every night.\n\n@event umbral_forbidden_notes\ntitle: Forbidden Notes\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\ntrack: umbral\nweight: 12\nart: book\ntags: class mystery\nchain: forbidden\nscene: A battered notebook with a cracked leather cover being slid across a library table, a silver key tucked into its spine.\ntext: A sixth-year slides a battered notebook across the table. \"My notes on the Restricted Section. I'm graduating. You look like you'd use them.\" A silver key is tucked in the spine.\n> left: Decline\n  say: The sixth-year shrugs and slides the notebook to the next student. You watch it go.\n> right: Take the notebook\n  alignment -tiny\n  lore +medium\n  flag forbidden_key\n  queue forbidden_section_1 in 1\n  say: The notes are brilliant, half-mad and annotated in three colours of ink. The key is cold in your pocket.\n"},{"path":"content/events/clubs.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Club events — drawn while the club state is active.\n#  Clubs are joined at the Club Fair (calendar.grim) except the\n#  Midnight Cartographers, who find you.\n# ============================================================\n\n# ---------------- Dueling Circle ----------------\n\n@event duel_practice_bout\ntitle: Practice Bout\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrack: duelling\nweight: 14\nart: duel\ntags: club\nscene: A chalk circle on a stone floor under high windows, two students mid-duel, a ring of members cheering.\ntext: Wednesday practice. The captain pairs you with a fourth-year who has a reputation for ending bouts quickly. \"Defence or attack,\" the captain says. \"Pick one and commit.\"\n> left: Hold your ground\n  challenge combat easy:\n    win:\n      vigor +small\n      renown +small\n      say: You weather everything she throws until she runs out of breath. The captain nods slowly.\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      say: You hold your ground very bravely, right up until you're on the floor.\n> right: Go on the attack\n  challenge combat easy:\n    win:\n      arcana +small\n      renown +small\n      say: Your opening hex catches her off guard. Point to you. The circle whoops.\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      say: She sidesteps and you hex the wall. The wall wins.\n\n@event duel_bram_hazing\ntitle: Initiation, Prefect-Style\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\ntrack: duelling\nactor: bram\nweight: 12\nart: shield\ntags: club rival\nscene: A burly upper-year in a prefect's sash blocking a corridor, wand twirling, a crowd of members watching nervously.\ntext: Bram Hollis, prefect and senior Circle member, has decided new members need \"toughening up\". He's made you hold a shield-charm against him for an hour. The hour is not up.\n> left: Endure it\n  meet bram\n  vigor +small\n  mood -small\n  rel bram +small\n  say: Your arms shake. You don't drop the charm. Bram grunts, almost approvingly, and finds someone else to torment.\n> right: Drop the shield and duel him\n  meet bram\n  challenge combat medium:\n    win:\n      renown +medium\n      rel bram -large\n      bond bram rival\n      say: You disarm him in front of everyone. His wand clatters across the floor. The silence is delicious. He will remember this.\n    lose:\n      health -medium\n      rel bram -small\n      say: Bram flattens you with a single bored flick. \"Toughening up,\" he reminds the room.\n\n@event duel_lyra_training\ntitle: Training Partners\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nwhen: met(lyra) and month >= 6 and month <= 7 and rel(lyra) >= 0\ntrack: duelling\nactor: lyra\nweight: 14\nart: duel\ntags: club rival social\nscene: A misty lawn at dawn, two figures practising wand forms side by side, frost on the grass.\ntext: Lyra finds you after practice. \"The tournament is in two months. You're the only person in this club who doesn't bore me. Train with me. Dawn. Every day. Don't be late.\"\n> left: Decline — you need sleep\n  rel lyra -tiny\n  say: \"Sleep,\" she repeats, as if it's a foreign word. \"Fine.\"\n> right: Agree\n  health -tiny\n  gain training\n  rel lyra +medium\n  say: She's a merciless coach. Somewhere around the third week, you notice she's started bringing two cups of tea.\n\n# ---------------- Choir of the Spheres ----------------\n\n@event choir_solo\ntitle: Solo Auditions\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nplace: great_hall\nweight: 14\nart: music\ntags: club\nscene: A circular choir loft under a painted dome, a single student standing in a pool of light, the rest of the choir floating gently above their seats.\ntext: Auditions for the Midwinter solo. The choirmaster's eyebrows are famously hard to impress. Your throat is dry. Your hands are wet.\n> left: Stay in the back row\n  mood +tiny\n  say: You harmonise from safety, and it's lovely. Somebody else gets the solo and cries with relief.\n> right: Audition\n  challenge negotiate trivial:\n    win:\n      renown +medium\n      mood +small\n      say: You hit the high note and the candles in the loft all flare at once. The choirmaster's eyebrows rise one millimetre. You got it.\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      say: Your voice cracks on the high note and a candle goes out in protest.\n\n@event choir_levitation\ntitle: Off-Key Altitude\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nplace: great_hall\nweight: 14\nart: music\ntags: club magic\nscene: A choir hovering a few feet off the floor, all in harmony — except one singer, who has dropped back to the ground with a thump.\ntext: The Choir of the Spheres literally floats when it harmonises. You keep falling out of the air on the third verse. People are starting to notice the thumps.\n> left: Mime the hard bits\n  guile +tiny\n  say: Nobody notices. You float serenely, mouthing words. It's a little bit like fraud.\n> right: Practise every night\n  charm +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: A week of late nights and you float through the third verse like a feather. The choirmaster gives you a tiny nod.\n\n@event choir_concert\ntitle: The Winter Concert\nclass: windfall\ntone: light\nwhen: (month == 4 or month == 5)\nplace: great_hall\nweight: 16\nart: bell\ntags: club festival\nscene: The great hall at night, a floating choir lit by candles, the audience below with faces tilted up.\ntext: The winter concert. The great hall is packed. Somewhere in the audience, the Ledger's critic is sharpening her quill.\n> left: Sing for the audience\n  renown +small\n  mood +small\n  say: You sing and the hall sways. The review calls the choir \"unearthly\". You cut it out and keep it.\n> right: Sing for yourself\n  mood +medium\n  say: You close your eyes and forget anyone is listening. When you open them, you are higher than everyone else.\n\n# ---------------- Skyrace Squad ----------------\n\n@event sky_tryouts\ntitle: Tryouts\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nwhen: not flag(sky_team)\nplace: flight_field\nweight: 18\nart: broom\ntags: club\nscene: Brooms streaking around the towers, hoops of light hanging in the sky, a captain with a whistle on the battlements.\ntext: Skyrace tryouts: three laps around the towers, through the hoops, without dying. The captain has a stopwatch and no visible sense of humour.\n> left: Fly clean and steady\n  challenge feat trivial:\n    win:\n      flag sky_team\n      renown +small\n      say: You're not the fastest, but you hit every hoop. \"Reserve,\" says the captain. Then: \"...no. Team.\"\n    lose:\n      mood -tiny\n      say: You miss two hoops and nearly a gargoyle. \"Reserves,\" says the captain. \"Keep practising.\"\n> right: Go full speed\n  challenge feat trivial:\n    win:\n      flag sky_team\n      renown +medium\n      say: You scream through the hoops so fast the stopwatch has to be checked. You're on the team. Obviously.\n    lose:\n      health -medium\n      say: You go full speed into the north tower. The tower is fine.\n\n@event sky_crash\ntitle: A Bad Landing\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nplace: flight_field\nweight: 12\nart: broom\ntags: club danger\nscene: A broom snapped in two on the grass, a student sitting up dazed, teammates landing around them.\ntext: Practice. A gust catches you mid-turn, and the ground arrives rather sooner than expected. Your ankle is making an interesting shape.\n> left: Walk it off\n  vigor +tiny\n  chance 50:\n    gain injured\n    say: You do not walk it off. You limp it off, for a month.\n  else:\n    say: Tough as old boots. The team cheers when you get back on your broom.\n> right: Straight to the infirmary\n  gold -tiny\n  health +small\n  say: Sister Amaranth sets it in a minute and charges you three crowns for \"carelessness\". Fair.\n\n@event sky_championship\ntitle: Lumenhold vs. Thornwick\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nwhen: flag(sky_team) and month >= 6\nplace: flight_field\nweight: 16\nart: trophy\ntags: club festival\nscene: Two teams of broom-riders in blue and green above a packed stadium, the golden finishing hoop glowing at the top of the highest tower.\ntext: The championship against Thornwick Academy. Final lap. You're neck and neck with their captain, and the golden hoop is at the top of the tallest tower.\n> left: Play it safe\n  renown +small\n  say: Thornwick takes the hoop by a whisker. Still: second place in the region. The team carries you off the field anyway.\n> right: The suicide dive\n  challenge feat easy:\n    win:\n      renown +medium\n      gain famous\n      mood +large\n      say: You dive straight down the tower face and pull up at the last second, through the hoop. The stadium explodes.\n    lose:\n      health -large\n      gain injured\n      renown +small\n      say: You dive. You miss. The crowd gasps, then cheers you anyway when you stagger up waving.\n\n# ---------------- The Lumen Ledger ----------------\n\n@event ledger_pie_scoop\ntitle: The Case of the Missing Pies\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrack: lumen_ledger\nactor: nessa\nweight: 14\nart: paper\ntags: club social\nscene: A cramped newspaper office in a tower attic, a printing press clanking, a girl with an ink-stained grin pointing at a map pinned with string.\ntext: Nessa slams a file on your desk. \"Pies. Forty-two of them, gone from the kitchens in a single night. I want the culprit, I want a photo and I want it by Friday.\"\n> left: Write a light-hearted piece\n  charm +tiny\n  say: \"Pie-rates Strike Again.\" Nessa groans at the headline and prints it anyway.\n> right: Actually investigate\n  challenge deceive easy:\n    win:\n      renown +small\n      rel nessa +medium\n      say: The culprit: a family of brownies living in the chimney. Your story runs on the front page. Nessa frames it.\n    lose:\n      mood -tiny\n      rel nessa +tiny\n      say: You stake out the kitchens for three nights and catch nothing but a cold. Nessa pats your head.\n\n@event ledger_bram_story\ntitle: The Prefect's Bribes\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nwhen: met(bram)\ntrack: lumen_ledger\nactor: nessa\nweight: 12\nart: scales\ntags: club rival\nscene: An anonymous note spread on a desk under a lamp, next to a sketch of a prefect taking a coin purse from a first-year.\ntext: An anonymous tip: Bram Hollis has been taking bribes from first-years to skip their demerits. You have a witness. Nessa wants your byline on it. \"This is real journalism,\" she says, eyes shining.\n> left: Kill the story\n  rel nessa -small\n  rel bram +small\n  say: Nessa is disappointed in you. Somehow, Bram finds out you buried it. He nods at you in the corridor. You feel slightly dirty.\n> right: Publish it\n  renown +medium\n  rel nessa +medium\n  rel bram -large\n  gain watched\n  if rel(bram) < -18:\n    bond bram nemesis\n  say: The story runs. Bram is stripped of his sash for a month. He gets it back. And now he knows exactly who you are.\n\n@event ledger_deadline\ntitle: Deadline Night\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrack: lumen_ledger\nactor: nessa\nweight: 12\nart: candle\ntags: club\nscene: The newspaper office at 3am, crumpled drafts on the floor, two students slumped over typesetting trays.\ntext: The press runs at dawn and half the paper is blank. Nessa is chewing her quill. \"I need you tonight. All night. I'll owe you.\"\n> left: You have an exam tomorrow\n  study +tiny\n  rel nessa -tiny\n  say: You sleep. The paper comes out with a large drawing of a duck on page four. Nessa doesn't speak to you for a day.\n> right: Pull an all-nighter\n  health -small\n  study -tiny\n  rel nessa +medium\n  say: You write three articles and a horoscope. At dawn Nessa falls asleep on your shoulder, ink on her nose.\n\n# ---------------- Verdant Circle ----------------\n\n@event garden_mandrakes\ntitle: The Mandrake Choir\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrack: verdant\nweight: 14\nart: leaf\ntags: club nature\nscene: Rows of potted mandrakes in a steamy greenhouse, their little leafy heads opened in song.\ntext: The greenhouse mandrakes have started singing again. Off-key and enthusiastic. The earmuffs are on the hook by the door. Tobias isn't wearing his; he's harmonising.\n> left: Earmuffs on\n  alchemy +tiny\n  say: You repot six mandrakes in blissful silence. Good honest work.\n> right: Sing along\n  mood +small\n  chance 30:\n    health -tiny\n    say: You sing along and promptly faint. Mandrake song is strong stuff. You wake up very relaxed.\n  else:\n    say: The mandrakes adore you. The greenhouse sounds like a pub at closing time.\n\n@event garden_moonflower\ntitle: The Moonflower\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nwhen: met(tobias)\ntrack: verdant\nactor: tobias\nweight: 12\nart: flower\ntags: club romance nature\nscene: A single silver flower opening in the dark of a glass greenhouse, two faces lit by its glow.\ntext: \"The moonflower's going to bloom tonight,\" Tobias says, a little breathless. \"Once every eleven years. I'm staying up to see it. You... could stay too. If you want.\"\n> left: You need sleep\n  rel tobias -tiny\n  say: \"Course. Yeah.\" He tells you about it at breakfast, in detail, with gestures.\n> right: Stay up with him\n  health -tiny\n  rel tobias +medium\n  spark tobias +small\n  mood +small\n  say: At 2am the flower opens and fills the greenhouse with silver light. Tobias doesn't look at the flower. He looks at you.\n\n@event garden_maud_secret\ntitle: The Secret Garden\nclass: windfall\ntone: light\nwhen: member(verdant) or (month >= 7 and month <= 9)\nactor: maud\nweight: 5\nart: mushroom\ntags: club nature\nscene: An old woman in a battered straw hat holding open a hidden door in an ivy-covered wall; beyond, impossible flowers glow.\ntext: The old groundskeeper beckons you behind the potting shed, to a door in the wall you've walked past a hundred times without seeing. \"You've got the hands for it,\" Old Maud says. \"Want to see what grows where nobody's looking?\"\n> left: Thank her and decline\n  meet maud\n  rel maud +tiny\n  say: \"Suit yourself.\" The door is gone when you look back.\n> right: Follow her in\n  meet maud\n  rel maud +medium\n  alchemy +small\n  gain blessed\n  say: Inside: glowing ferns, silver bees, a pear tree that hums. Maud tucks a sprig of rosemary into your pocket. \"For luck. Don't lose it.\"\n\n# ---------------- Stargazers ----------------\n\n@event stars_cocoa_night\ntime: evening night\ntitle: Cocoa on the Roof\nclass: windfall\ntone: light\nplace: observatory\nweight: 14\nart: cup\ntags: club social\nscene: Students wrapped in blankets on a flat roof, steaming mugs in hand, telescopes pointed at a velvet sky.\ntext: Stargazers meet on the roof with blankets, telescopes and an illegal quantity of cocoa. Tonight is cloudy. Nobody cares.\n> left: Talk until dawn\n  mood +medium\n  charm +tiny\n  say: You talk about home, about the future, about whether stars are lonely. Someone falls asleep on your shoulder.\n> right: Do your homework by starlight\n  lore +small\n  study +tiny\n  say: You finish your essay by the light of the cocoa-warmer charm. Productive. A little lonely.\n\n@event stars_meteor_bet\ntime: evening night\ntitle: The Meteor Wager\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nplace: observatory\nweight: 12\nart: dice\ntags: club money\nscene: A chalkboard on an observatory wall covered in tally marks and names, a pile of coins on a stool beneath it.\ntext: The club is running a betting pool on tonight's meteor count. The current favourite is 40. The charts suggest something else. Or do they?\n> left: Bet on the charts\n  challenge research trivial:\n    win:\n      gold +medium\n      say: Fifty-three meteors. You called fifty-two. Close enough to win the pot.\n    lose:\n      gold -small\n      say: The charts were wrong, or you read them wrong. Eight crowns gone.\n> right: Stay out of it\n  mood +tiny\n  say: You watch the meteors for their own sake. Turns out that's the best seat in the house.\n\n@event stars_constellation\ntitle: An Unnamed Shape\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\nplace: observatory\nweight: 12\nart: star\ntags: club mystery\nscene: A star chart spread on a table, a student's finger tracing a new pattern between unmarked stars.\ntext: Plotting the autumn sky, you notice seven faint stars that form a ring around nothing. The charts call that patch of sky \"empty\". It doesn't look empty.\n> left: Ignore it\n  say: You plot the usual constellations. Your chart gets top marks.\n  study +tiny\n> right: Investigate\n  lore +medium\n  flag saw_hollow_star\n  say: You spend a week in the archives. Three hundred years ago, the patch was called the Hollow. Then someone scraped the name off every chart.\n\n# ---------------- Midnight Cartographers ----------------\n\n@event carto_invite\ntime: evening night\ntitle: An Invitation at Midnight\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nwhen: met(corvin) and rel(corvin) >= 6 and not member(cartographers) and turn >= 4\nactor: corvin\nweight: 20\nart: map\ntags: club mystery\nscene: A slip of black paper pushed under a dormitory door, a silver ink map on it that rearranges itself as you watch.\ntext: A black card under your door. On it, a map in silver ink that redraws itself while you watch. Corvin's handwriting: \"Midnight. Under the third gargoyle. Bring a candle. Tell no one.\"\n> left: Ignore it\n  rel corvin -tiny\n  say: The card blanks itself the next morning. Corvin doesn't mention it, but he's a little cooler for a week.\n> right: Go\n  health -tiny\n  join cartographers\n  rel corvin +medium\n  guile +tiny\n  say: Under the gargoyle: six students, a stack of maps and a passage you'd never seen. \"Welcome,\" says Corvin, \"to the club that doesn't exist.\"\n\n@event carto_thursday_stairs\ntitle: The Thursday Staircase\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\ntrack: cartographers\nweight: 14\nart: map\ntags: club mystery\nscene: A spiral staircase emerging from a blank wall in moonlight, its steps unfolding one by one.\ntext: The Cartographers have heard of a staircase that only exists on Thursdays between midnight and one. Tonight is Thursday. It is 11:58.\n> left: Map it carefully\n  health -tiny\n  lore +small\n  guile +tiny\n  say: You chart forty-one steps and a landing with a window onto a sky that isn't this one. The map is the club's pride.\n> right: Climb it to the top\n  challenge deceive trivial:\n    win:\n      gold +large\n      say: At the top, a forgotten storeroom. Inside: a chest of old academy coins. The club splits it. Corvin cries a little.\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      say: At one o'clock the staircase stops existing. You were on it. You land in a laundry chute.\n\n@event carto_lake_tunnel\ntitle: The Tunnel Under the Lake\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\nwhen: not flag(found_lake_tunnel)\ntrack: cartographers\nweight: 12\nart: door\ntags: club mystery hollowstar\nchain: hollowstar\nscene: A narrow brick tunnel dripping with water, lit by a single candle, ending at a round door carved with a black star.\ntext: A loose stone in the boathouse cellar opens on a tunnel running out under the lake. The bricks drip. At the far end, a round door carved with a black star. It is warm to the touch.\n> left: Mark it and leave\n  flag found_lake_tunnel\n  lore +tiny\n  say: You add the tunnel to the club map with a skull beside it. Corvin adds a second skull, to be safe.\n> right: Press your palm to the star\n  flag found_lake_tunnel\n  alignment -small\n  arcana +small\n  mood -tiny\n  gain hollow_dreams\n  say: The star drinks the warmth out of your hand. Behind the door, something very large shifts in its sleep.\n"},{"path":"content/events/crisis.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Crises: queued by resources hitting zero (see core/rules.grim)\n#  and by debt. These are the \"game over\" gates — each gives the\n#  player one way out, at a price, before ending the run.\n# ============================================================\n\n@event crisis_collapse\ntitle: Collapse\nclass: crisis\ntone: grim\ntrigger: queued\npriority: 90\nart: cross\ntags: crisis\nscene: A narrow infirmary bed under a high arched window, a healer's lamp floating beside it.\ntext: The world tilts. When it rights itself you are in the infirmary, and Sister Amaranth is looking at you over her spectacles. \"You have been running your body like a borrowed broom,\" she says.\n> left: Pay for the full cure (30 gold)\n  if gold >= 30:\n    gold -large\n    health +huge\n    say: Three foul tonics and two days' sleep later, you feel almost human.\n  elif collapses >= 1:\n    end ending_sent_home\n  else:\n    health +huge\n    study -large\n    collapses += 1\n    say: You can't pay. She heals you anyway, slowly, and you miss a fortnight of classes.\n> right: Ask to rest instead\n  if collapses >= 1:\n    end ending_sent_home\n  else:\n    health +huge\n    study -large\n    mood -small\n    collapses += 1\n    lose sick\n    say: A long, dull fortnight in bed. Sister Amaranth warns you: \"Next time, I write to your family.\"\n\n@event crisis_despair\ntitle: The Grey\nclass: crisis\ntone: grim\ntrigger: queued\npriority: 90\nart: rain\ntags: crisis\nscene: A student sitting on the edge of a dormitory bed, rain on the window, a packed trunk by the door.\ntext: Everything has gone grey. You have packed your trunk twice this week and unpacked it once. It would be so easy to take the next barge and simply stop.\n> left: Reach out to someone\n  if bonds(friend) + bonds(close) + bonds(partner) > 0:\n    mood +huge\n    rest\n    say: You knock on a friend's door. They don't ask questions. They just make tea, and stay.\n  elif met(maud):\n    mood +huge\n    rel maud +medium\n    say: Old Maud finds you in the gardens and puts a trowel in your hand. \"Dig,\" she says. You dig. It helps more than it should.\n  elif despairs >= 1:\n    end ending_quiet_departure\n  else:\n    mood +large\n    despairs += 1\n    say: You talk to the night porter, of all people. He tells you about his own first year. It's enough, for now.\n> right: Take the next barge\n  if despairs >= 1:\n    end ending_quiet_departure\n  else:\n    mood +large\n    despairs += 1\n    say: You get as far as the landing. The barge is late. By the time it arrives, you have decided to stay one more month.\n\n@event crisis_probation\ntitle: The Disciplinary Board\nclass: crisis\ntone: grim\ntrigger: queued\npriority: 90\nart: scales\ntags: crisis\nscene: Three faculty members behind a high bench in a dim chamber; the headmistress in the centre, hands folded.\ntext: Two terms of failing marks. You are summoned before the Disciplinary Board: three professors and the headmistress, who looks at you with an expression of deep, patient disappointment. {flag(board_reprieve): They have seen you here before.|}\n> left: Plead your case\n  if flag(board_reprieve):\n    end ending_expelled\n  else:\n    challenge negotiate medium:\n      win:\n        flag board_reprieve\n        study +huge\n        gain probation\n        say: You speak honestly and well. They grant you one reprieve. \"There will not be a second,\" says the headmistress, pleasantly.\n      lose:\n        end ending_expelled\n> right: Promise to work harder\n  if flag(board_reprieve):\n    end ending_expelled\n  else:\n    flag board_reprieve\n    study +huge\n    gain probation\n    mood -medium\n    say: \"Promises are cheap,\" says Professor Thorne. \"We'll take this one. Only this one.\" A reprieve, and a very long term ahead.\n\n# The first missed payment (M22): a reminder, with interest.\n@event grisk_reminder\ntitle: A Friendly Reminder\nclass: incident\ntone: tense\ntrigger: queued\npriority: 70\nart: letter\ntags: money\nchain: debt\nscene: A small, very neat note pinned to a dormitory door with a brass tack, the handwriting tiny and precise.\ntext: A note on your door, in a tidy goblin hand: \"A payment was missed. Mister Grisk is a patient businessman. Mister Grisk charges interest on his patience.\" Underneath, smaller: \"Pay something, or another month will be added.\"\n> left: Scrape together what you can\n  gold = 0\n  debt_missed = 0\n  mood -small\n  say: You empty your purse into an envelope and slide it under his door. It isn't twelve crowns. It is, says the note in reply, \"a gesture. Noted.\"\n> right: Put it out of your mind\n  debt_paid -= 1\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You put the note in a drawer. Another month goes on the ledger, and next month the knock on your door will be louder.\n\n@event grisk_collectors\ntitle: Grisk's Collectors\nclass: crisis\ntone: grim\ntrigger: queued\npriority: 80\nart: chain\ntags: crisis money\nchain: debt\nscene: Two large trolls in ill-fitting waistcoats looming in a dormitory doorway, one holding a ledger.\ntext: Two trolls in waistcoats are waiting outside your dormitory. \"Mister Grisk,\" says the larger one, \"is disappointed.\" The smaller one cracks his knuckles, which sounds like a rockslide.\n> left: Hand over everything you have\n  gold = 0\n  debt_missed = 0\n  mood -medium\n  if debt_paid >= 3:\n    lose in_debt\n    say: They count your purse twice and declare the debt settled. \"Pleasure,\" says the large one, meaning it.\n  else:\n    debt_paid += 1\n    renown -small\n    say: They take every coin and your good boots, in front of half the corridor. \"Partial,\" they say, and leave.\n> right: Run\n  challenge feat trivial:\n    win:\n      debt_missed = 1\n      mood -tiny\n      say: You escape across the rooftops. They'll be back, but not this month.\n    lose:\n      health -huge\n      debt_missed = 0\n      say: You do not escape. The trolls are surprisingly gentle about breaking only one rib.\n      gain injured\n"},{"path":"content/events/expansion_classes.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Expansion — one more event per elective, plus a core-class\n#  event everyone can get.\n# ============================================================\n\n@event core_flying_lesson\nslot: morning\ntitle: Compulsory Broom Flight\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nwhen: year == 1 and month <= 6\nweight: 12\nart: broom\ntags: class danger\nscene: A row of nervous first-years standing beside brooms on the lawn, one broom already rising on its own, an instructor with a whistle.\ntext: Compulsory first-year broom flight. The instructor blows her whistle. Your broom rises, hesitates, and then — clearly having taken a dislike to you — begins drifting toward the lake.\n> left: Hold on and steer it back\n  challenge feat medium:\n    win:\n      vigor +small\n      renown +small\n      say: You wrestle it round and land, wobbling, exactly on the mark. The instructor nods. The broom grudgingly accepts you.\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      say: The broom wins. You land in the shallows. Everyone sees. Everyone remembers.\n> right: Jump off while it's still low\n  mood -tiny\n  vigor +tiny\n  say: You drop four feet onto the grass. Undignified, but dry. The broom carries on to the lake alone and sinks with a sulky gurgle.\n\n@event pyro_dragon_breath\ntitle: A Sample of Dragonfire\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrack: evocation\nweight: 12\nart: dragon\ntags: class magic\nscene: A glass jar containing a single writhing flame of deep blue, held in tongs by a stern professor, students leaning back from it.\ntext: Thorne holds up a sealed jar containing one flame of genuine dragonfire, blue-white and furious. \"Who would like to try holding it?\" he asks. Nobody moves. He looks at you.\n> left: Hold it\n  challenge spell hard:\n    win:\n      arcana +medium\n      renown +small\n      rel thorne +small\n      say: It sears and struggles and then, astonishingly, settles in your palm like a small warm animal. The class stares. Thorne almost smiles.\n    lose:\n      health -large\n      say: The dragonfire disagrees. You spend a week with bandaged hands and a new respect for dragons.\n> right: Watch someone else\n  lore +tiny\n  say: A fifth-year volunteers and loses a sleeve. You take careful notes on what not to do.\n\n@event tide_water_spirit_race\ntitle: The Water Spirit Race\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrack: evocation\nweight: 12\nart: wave\ntags: class nature\nscene: Students swimming across a lake at dawn, translucent water-spirits racing alongside them like silver dolphins.\ntext: Professor Marisol's final challenge: swim the lake while the water spirits race you. \"They cheat,\" she says cheerfully. \"Ask them nicely not to.\"\n> left: Race them flat out\n  challenge feat medium:\n    win:\n      vigor +medium\n      renown +small\n      say: You touch the far shore a hand's length ahead of the fastest spirit. It sulks, then laughs like a fountain.\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      say: The spirits tow you in circles. You arrive last, but very clean.\n> right: Ask them nicely\n  challenge negotiate easy:\n    win:\n      mood +medium\n      arcana +tiny\n      say: You ask. They carry you across on their backs like a raft. Marisol says that counts. Marisol says it's the whole point.\n    lose:\n      mood -tiny\n      say: They pretend not to hear. Water spirits are terrible at manners.\n\n@event illusion_mirror_maze\ntitle: The Mirror Maze Exam\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrack: illusion\nweight: 12\nart: mirror\ntags: class magic\nscene: A maze of tall mirrors in a dark hall, each reflecting a different version of the same student, one reflection grinning.\ntext: Professor Vey's exam: a maze of enchanted mirrors. Find the exit. Some reflections lie. One of them is — she promises — the real you.\n> left: Trust your own eyes\n  challenge deceive medium:\n    win:\n      guile +medium\n      study +small\n      say: You spot the reflection that blinks late, and follow it out. Vey awards top marks and says, \"Most people trust the one that looks happiest.\"\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      say: You follow the happiest reflection for an hour. It leads you in a circle and waves goodbye.\n> right: Shatter the illusion\n  challenge spell medium:\n    win:\n      arcana +small\n      study +tiny\n      say: Every mirror goes dark at once. Exit found. Vey writes, \"Effective. Inelegant. Pass.\"\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      say: You shatter one mirror. It was real. Seven years of bad luck, the caretaker says, or three crowns for the glass.\n      gold -tiny\n\n@event alch_love_potion\ntitle: The Love Potion Assignment\nclass: windfall\ntone: warm\ntrack: alchemy\nweight: 12\nart: potion\ntags: class romance\nscene: A pink, glittering potion in a heart-shaped flask on a workbench, a student holding it up to the light with a doubtful expression.\ntext: This week's assignment: brew a love potion — \"for analytical purposes only\". Your flask glows a suspicious pink. Someone at the next bench asks if they can buy it. Several people ask.\n> left: Brew it perfectly, then pour it away\n  alchemy +medium\n  study +small\n  say: Perfect colour, perfect scent. You pour it down the sink. The pipes are affectionate for a week.\n> right: Sell it\n  alignment -small\n  gold +medium\n  chance 40:\n    gain detention\n    say: A prefect falls in love with the headmistress's portrait. It's traced back to you. Worth it, almost.\n  else:\n    say: Fifteen crowns. You never ask what it was used for. You see a lot of unusual couples at dinner.\n\n@event div_prophecy_exam\ntitle: The Prophecy Exam\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\ntrack: astromancy\nweight: 12\nart: eye\ntags: class mystery\nscene: A dim divination classroom, a single student staring into a crystal ball, the examiner's quill hovering expectantly over a blank page.\ntext: The final Divination exam: gaze into the crystal and make one prophecy about the coming month. The examiner will check. \"Vague prophecies,\" she warns, \"fail.\"\n> left: Make a bold, specific prophecy\n  chance 50:\n    study +large\n    renown +small\n    say: \"On the eighth, the kitchen cat will have kittens in the headmistress's hat.\" On the eighth, she does. You pass with distinction.\n  else:\n    study -small\n    say: \"On the eighth, it will snow indoors.\" It does not snow indoors. The examiner writes \"ambitious\".\n> right: Make a careful, safe prophecy\n  study +small\n  lore +tiny\n  say: \"Someone will be late to breakfast.\" Someone is. The examiner sighs and gives you a pass.\n\n@event art_golem_helper\ntitle: The Little Golem\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrack: artifice\nweight: 12\nart: gear\ntags: class magic\nscene: A knee-high clay golem carrying a teetering stack of books across a classroom, a student watching anxiously as it nears a staircase.\ntext: Your term project: a knee-high clay golem that carries books. It works! It works so well that it has started carrying everyone's books, whether they want it to or not.\n> left: Add a \"stop\" command\n  challenge research trivial:\n    win:\n      study +medium\n      lore +small\n      say: The golem stops. It also waits politely. The professor asks if she can borrow it for her office.\n    lose:\n      mood -tiny\n      say: Now it stops randomly, mid-staircase, holding forty books. Nobody can get past it. It looks very pleased.\n> right: Let it roam free\n  chance 70:\n    renown +small\n    mood +small\n    say: The golem becomes a beloved castle fixture, delivering books to whoever looks sad. Nobody knows who made it. You know.\n  else:\n    renown -tiny\n    say: It also delivers a Restricted Section book to a first-year. The librarian follows the clay smudges straight to your door.\n\n@event beast_hippocampus\ntitle: The Hippocampus Foal\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrack: beastcraft\nweight: 12\nart: wave\ntags: class nature\nscene: A tiny sea-horse foal with a fish's tail struggling in a rock pool on the lake shore, a student kneeling beside the water.\ntext: A hippocampus foal — half pony, half fish — has been stranded in a shrinking rock pool on the lakeshore. Professor Brask is on the other side of the grounds. The foal is panicking.\n> left: Carry it to the lake\n  challenge feat trivial:\n    win:\n      mood +medium\n      vigor +tiny\n      say: It thrashes, then trusts you. You wade out and let it go. It circles you three times before diving deep.\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      mood +small\n      say: It kicks you in the stomach with surprising force — but you get it to the water. Brask says that's how they say thank you.\n> right: Run for Professor Brask\n  lore +small\n  say: Brask arrives with a bucket and a lullaby. She makes you hold the bucket. You learn more in ten minutes than in a whole term.\n\n@event astro_eclipse\ntitle: The Eclipse\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\ntrack: astromancy\nweight: 12\nart: moon\ntags: class mystery\nscene: A black disc sliding across the sun above the observatory dome, students on the roof with smoked-glass lenses, a halo of light around the darkness.\ntext: An eclipse, the first in forty years. The astromancy class is on the roof with smoked-glass lenses. At the moment of totality, you notice something the charts don't show: a second shadow, smaller, moving the wrong way.\n> left: Record it precisely\n  lore +medium\n  find second_shadow\n  say: You chart the second shadow's path exactly. It crosses the sky and stops — directly above the lake.\n> right: Just watch the eclipse\n  mood +medium\n  say: For four minutes the world holds its breath. You'll remember the cold, and the silence, and the strange birdsong, for the rest of your life.\n\n@event heal_triage_drill\ntitle: Triage\nclass: windfall\ntone: tense\ntrack: alchemy\nweight: 12\nart: cross\ntags: class danger\nscene: A practice ward full of students acting injured, groaning theatrically on cots, a single student healer in the middle with a clipboard.\ntext: Sister Amaranth's triage drill: twelve \"injured\" students, one healer — you — and ten minutes. Some are faking worse than others. One, you realise, is genuinely hurt.\n> left: Treat the genuine injury first\n  challenge brew easy:\n    win:\n      alchemy +medium\n      study +small\n      say: You spot the real injury in the first minute. Amaranth nods, once, which is the highest honour in the infirmary.\n    lose:\n      study +tiny\n      say: You find the real injury in minute nine. Late, but not too late. Amaranth's lips thin. \"Again tomorrow.\"\n> right: Work systematically down the line\n  study +tiny\n  lore +tiny\n  say: Methodical, thorough and slow. The real patient waits seven minutes. Amaranth makes you write an essay on why.\n\n@event umbral_last_lecture\ntitle: The Last Lecture on Absence\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\nwhen: year >= 2\ntrack: umbral\nweight: 12\nart: skull\ntags: class mystery hollowstar\nchain: hollowstar\nscene: An Umbral Theory lecture hall lit only by a black candle, the professor drawing a star with a hollow centre on the board.\ntext: The Umbral professor draws a star with a hollow centre on the board. \"This is not on the syllabus,\" she says. \"Three hundred years ago this academy imprisoned an absence that thinks. I believe you should know that before you graduate.\"\n> left: Take careful notes\n  lore +medium\n  flag knows_founders_seal\n  find knows_founders_seal\n  find seven_locks\n  say: She speaks for an hour about seals and locks and the founders' fear. At the end she wipes the board clean and says, \"This lecture never happened.\"\n> right: Ask why she's telling you\n  rel isolde +tiny\n  say: \"Because something is stirring,\" she says, \"and the headmistress asked me to find out which of you would ask that question.\" She writes your name down.\n"},{"path":"content/events/expansion_faculty.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Expansion — short arcs for Professor Thorne, Old Maud and\n#  Headmistress Marrow. All `trigger: social` (need met + rel).\n# ============================================================\n\n# ---------------- Professor Thorne ----------------\n\n@event thorne_old_rival\ntitle: An Old Rival\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\ntrigger: social\nactor: thorne\nwhen: rel(thorne) >= 9 and year >= 2\nweight: 12\nart: duel\ntags: rival study\nscene: Two elderly mages facing each other across a dueling ring at dusk, one stern and grey, one flamboyant in purple, a nervous student holding a towel at the side.\ntext: A flamboyant old wizard in purple has arrived to settle \"a forty-year-old matter\" with Professor Thorne. A formal duel needs a second. Thorne looks around the courtyard and, to everyone's astonishment, points at you.\n> left: Stand as his second\n  health -tiny\n  rel thorne +medium\n  renown +small\n  arcana +tiny\n  say: The duel lasts four seconds. Thorne wins. Then both old men sit on the grass and laugh until they wheeze. You've never seen him laugh. Neither has anyone else.\n> right: Politely decline\n  rel thorne -tiny\n  say: \"Hm,\" says Thorne, and asks the caretaker instead. He wins anyway. He does not mention it again, which is somehow worse.\n\n@event thorne_roses\ntime: morning\ntitle: The Professor's Roses\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: thorne\nwhen: rel(thorne) >= 6 and month >= 7 and month <= 10\nweight: 10\nart: flower\ntags: nature\nscene: A severe old professor kneeling in a hidden rose garden at dawn, sleeves rolled up, murmuring encouragement to a single struggling rosebush.\ntext: At dawn, behind the observatory, you find Professor Thorne on his knees in a tiny walled rose garden, murmuring encouragement to a sickly bush. He sees you. Neither of you breathes.\n> left: Back away and never speak of it\n  mood -tiny\n  rel thorne +medium\n  say: You leave. The next day in class he calls on you for the hardest question — and then, very quietly, gives you full marks for a so-so answer.\n> right: Offer to help with the sick bush\n  challenge brew easy:\n    win:\n      rel thorne +large\n      alchemy +small\n      say: You know the blight; Maud taught you. Within a month the bush is covered in red roses. Thorne leaves one, without comment, on your desk.\n    lose:\n      rel thorne +small\n      say: Your remedy does nothing. But Thorne lets you kneel beside him for an hour, saying nothing, which you suspect is a great honour.\n\n@event thorne_shaking_hands\ntime: noon afternoon\ntitle: Thorne's Hands\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: thorne\nwhen: rel(thorne) >= 15 and year >= 2\nweight: 10\nart: hand\ntags: social\nscene: An old professor gripping the edge of a lectern with both hands, a dropped wand on the floor, a single student in the doorway.\ntext: After class you see Thorne's wand slip from fingers that won't stop trembling. He picks it up, slowly. \"Age,\" he says, not looking at you. \"It comes for every storm eventually. You'll say nothing.\"\n> left: Say nothing, as he asks\n  mood -tiny\n  rel thorne +medium\n  say: You never mention it. But you start arriving early to set up his demonstrations. He notices. He lets you.\n> right: Tell Sister Amaranth\n  rel thorne -medium\n  rel isolde +small\n  say: Amaranth's tonic steadies his hands. Thorne is furious with you for a week. Then he stops being furious, and starts, grudgingly, teaching you his hardest spells — \"before I can't.\"\n\n# ---------------- Old Maud ----------------\n\n@event maud_goat\ntitle: Bartholomew in the Library\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: maud\nwhen: rel(maud) >= 3\nweight: 12\nart: book\ntags: nature\nscene: A shaggy goat standing on a library table chewing a book, an apoplectic librarian below, an old woman in a straw hat hurrying through the stacks.\ntext: Maud's goat, Bartholomew, has got into the library and is eating the Restricted Section. The librarian is apoplectic. Maud, hurrying in, grabs your arm. \"He likes you. Get him out before he eats anything with teeth.\"\n> left: Lure him out with an apple\n  challenge negotiate trivial:\n    win:\n      rel maud +medium\n      say: Bartholomew follows the apple, and you, out into the sunlight. He has eaten one-third of \"Curses of the Northern Wastes\". He seems fine. Mostly.\n    lose:\n      rel maud +small\n      health -tiny\n      say: Bartholomew eats the apple, then your sleeve, then headbutts you into a shelf. Maud gets him out in the end, laughing.\n> right: Pick him up and carry him out\n  health -tiny\n  vigor +small\n  rel maud +medium\n  say: He is heavier than he looks and struggles like a sack of angry cats. You carry him all the way to the paddock. The librarian applauds.\n\n@event maud_recipe\ntitle: Maud's Soup\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\ntrigger: social\nactor: maud\nwhen: rel(maud) >= 12\nweight: 10\nart: cauldron\ntags: nature home\nscene: A small cottage kitchen, a black iron pot over the fire, an old woman guiding a student's hand as they stir.\ntext: \"I'm going to teach you my soup,\" says Maud, rolling up her sleeves. \"Cures colds, broken hearts and most curses. Don't write it down. You'll remember it if you need it.\"\n> left: Learn it properly\n  mood -tiny\n  alchemy +small\n  rel maud +medium\n  say: It takes all afternoon. Some of the ingredients are herbs; some, as far as you can tell, are just stories. You remember every step.\n> right: Mostly eat it\n  health +tiny\n  rel maud +tiny\n  say: You learn about half of it and eat three bowls. Maud declares that the most important part of the recipe anyway.\n\n@event maud_old_wand\ntitle: A Wand From Sixty Years Ago\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\ntrigger: social\nactor: maud\nwhen: rel(maud) >= 18 and flag(knows_founders_seal)\nweight: 14\nart: wand\ntags: mystery hollowstar\nchain: hollowstar\nscene: An old woman unwrapping a slender silver wand from a faded silk scarf, a photograph of three young students on the table beside it.\ntext: Maud unwraps something from a faded scarf: a slender silver wand. \"Edmund's,\" she says. \"He was the one who didn't come back. He'd have wanted it to go beneath the lake again, with someone who meant to close the door.\"\n> left: Accept it\n  arcana +medium\n  flag edmunds_wand\n  rel maud +medium\n  say: It fits your hand like it was waiting. When you hold it near the lake, it hums the same note as the drowned bell.\n> right: Tell her to keep it\n  rel maud +large\n  mood +small\n  say: \"Keep it for him,\" you say. She holds it for a long time. \"Sixty years,\" she says, \"and nobody ever said that.\" She makes tea, and you sit with her until dark.\n\n# ---------------- Headmistress Marrow ----------------\n\n@event isolde_chess\ntitle: A Game of Tower Chess\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: isolde\nwhen: rel(isolde) >= 6\nweight: 10\nart: crown\ntags: study\nscene: A chessboard of carved crystal towers on a small table by a tower window, two players, one candle, the lake silver below.\ntext: The headmistress keeps a board of crystal towers by her window. \"Do you play?\" she asks. It is clearly not a question. The pieces are already moving into position by themselves.\n> left: Play to win\n  challenge research trivial:\n    win:\n      rel isolde +medium\n      lore +small\n      say: You win. She looks at the board for a long moment, then laughs — a short, delighted sound. \"Again,\" she says. \"Thursday.\"\n    lose:\n      rel isolde +small\n      lore +tiny\n      say: She wins in nine moves and spends the next hour showing you exactly why. It is the most useful lesson you've had all year.\n> right: Play to learn how she thinks\n  mood -tiny\n  rel isolde +medium\n  guile +small\n  say: You lose, slowly, watching. She sacrifices pieces she seems to love. By the end, you understand a great deal about how the academy is run.\n\n@event isolde_portrait\ntitle: The Young Headmistress\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\ntrigger: social\nactor: isolde\nwhen: rel(isolde) >= 3\nweight: 8\nart: mirror\ntags: mystery\nscene: A portrait in a forgotten corridor of a laughing young woman in student robes with a wand behind her ear, frame dusty, eyes unmistakably the headmistress's.\ntext: In a forgotten corridor you find a portrait of a laughing girl with a wand stuck behind her ear and ink on her nose. The plaque has been scratched off. The eyes are unmistakably the headmistress's. The portrait winks.\n> left: Ask the portrait about her\n  chance 60:\n    guile +tiny\n    lore +tiny\n    say: \"Detention record of the decade,\" whispers the portrait. \"Set the observatory on fire twice. Best student this castle ever had.\" It winks again.\n  else:\n    mood -tiny\n    say: The portrait pretends to be asleep, loudly. By the time you give up, the corridor has rearranged itself, and it takes you half the evening to find your way out.\n> right: Tell the headmistress you found it\n  rel isolde +small\n  say: She is quiet for a moment. \"I thought they'd burned it.\" She almost smiles. \"Leave it where it is. Someone should remember her.\"\n\n@event isolde_judgement\ntitle: A Question of Mercy\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: isolde\nwhen: rel(isolde) >= 12 and year >= 2\nweight: 10\nart: scales\ntags: story\nscene: The headmistress at her desk with a single file open, a student seated opposite, rain on the window behind them.\ntext: The headmistress slides a file across her desk. A second-year stole from the apothecary to pay for a sick mother's medicine. The rules say expulsion. \"You're a student,\" she says. \"Tell me what a student thinks is just.\"\n> left: Argue for mercy\n  mood -tiny\n  rel isolde +medium\n  charm +tiny\n  say: You argue for an hour. She listens to all of it. The second-year is given a job in the infirmary and a stern lecture. The mother recovers.\n> right: The rules exist for a reason\n  mood -tiny\n  rel isolde +small\n  lore +tiny\n  say: \"Yes,\" she says, \"they do.\" She closes the file. You never learn what she decided, but the second-year is still in class the next week, looking grateful at nobody in particular.\n"},{"path":"content/events/expansion_life.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Expansion — more academy life, plus a short chain:\n#    timeloop: life_timeloop_1 -> life_timeloop_2 -> life_timeloop_3\n# ============================================================\n\n# ---------------- The Tuesday That Wouldn't End ----------------\n\n@event life_timeloop_1\nslot: morning\ntitle: Haven't We Done This?\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\nwhen: turn >= 5 and not flag(timeloop_started)\nweight: 6\nart: hourglass\ntags: mystery magic\nchain: timeloop\nscene: A student at breakfast staring at a plate of eggs with intense déjà vu, a clock on the wall with its hands spinning backward.\ntext: Breakfast. Eggs. Thorne trips on the third step. A first-year spills juice on a prefect. You know all of this is about to happen, because it already happened. Yesterday. Which was also Tuesday.\n> left: Ignore it — probably just tired\n  flag timeloop_started\n  queue life_timeloop_2 now\n  say: You eat the eggs. They taste exactly as they did yesterday. Thorne trips on the third step.\n> right: Warn Thorne about the step\n  flag timeloop_started\n  rel thorne +tiny\n  queue life_timeloop_2 now\n  say: \"The third step, Professor.\" He looks at you strangely and steps over it. Somewhere, a clock skips. It's still Tuesday.\n\n@event life_timeloop_2\ntitle: Tuesday, Again\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\ntrigger: queued\nart: hourglass\ntags: mystery magic\nchain: timeloop\nscene: The same breakfast scene repeated, but the student is now standing on the table, holding up a sign that reads \"IT'S STILL TUESDAY\".\ntext: It's Tuesday. Again. For the fourth time. Nobody else notices. On the bright side, you now know every answer in this afternoon's quiz. On the dark side, it's Tuesday. Forever.\n> left: Use it — ace the quiz, win every bet\n  alignment -tiny\n  study +medium\n  gold +medium\n  queue life_timeloop_3 now\n  say: You win every bet, ace the quiz, and catch the juice before it hits the prefect. On the sixth Tuesday it starts to feel hollow. Something has to give.\n> right: Find the cause\n  lore +medium\n  queue life_timeloop_3 now\n  say: You spend three Tuesdays in the library. On the fourth you find it: someone in the astronomy tower has wound an old chronometer backward. With a wand.\n\n@event life_timeloop_3\ntitle: The Broken Chronometer\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\ntrigger: queued\nart: gear\ntags: mystery magic\nchain: timeloop\nscene: An astronomy tower at night, a giant brass chronometer with its gears spinning in reverse, a sleeping first-year slumped beside it with a wand still in hand.\ntext: In the astronomy tower, a first-year is asleep beside an enormous brass chronometer, wand still stuck in its winding key. \"I just wanted one more day before my exam,\" says the note on his chest.\n> left: Fix the chronometer\n  challenge spell trivial:\n    win:\n      arcana +small\n      renown +small\n      say: You ease the wand out and the gears groan forward. Wednesday arrives like a sneeze. Nobody but you and one mortified first-year ever knows.\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      mood -tiny\n      say: The chronometer bites. It takes you two more Tuesdays and a lot of swearing. But eventually, gloriously, it's Wednesday.\n> right: Teach the first-year to fix it\n  charm +small\n  mood +small\n  say: You show him how. He fixes his own mess, cries a little, and promises never to touch a clock again. Wednesday arrives. He passes his exam.\n\n# ---------------- Around the castle ----------------\n\n@event life_portrait_escape\ntitle: The Escaped Portrait\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nweight: 10\nart: mirror\ntags: magic mystery\nscene: An empty gilded picture frame on a gallery wall, and further down the corridor a painted duchess hiding inside a landscape of a picnic.\ntext: An empty frame in the long gallery. The Duchess of Ambry has escaped her portrait and is hiding in a landscape three corridors away, refusing to come back. \"That frame,\" she declares, \"is hideous.\"\n> left: Persuade her back\n  challenge negotiate trivial:\n    win:\n      renown +small\n      say: You promise to have her frame regilded. She returns, mollified. The caretaker regilds it. You've acquired a duchess who now considers you a personal friend.\n    lose:\n      mood -tiny\n      say: She calls you a peasant and moves to a painting of a shipwreck. She's still there. She says she likes the sea air.\n> right: Leave her in the picnic\n  mood +tiny\n  say: She seems happier among the sandwiches. So does the picnic. Some arrangements simply work.\n\n@event life_library_cat\ntitle: The Library Cat\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nweight: 10\nart: cat\ntags: home\nscene: An enormous fluffy cat asleep on the exact book a student needs, on a high library shelf, one eye half-open.\ntext: The library cat — a vast, ancient beast called Footnote — is asleep on the only copy of the book you need. You have an essay due tomorrow. Footnote has no deadlines.\n> left: Move the cat\n  challenge feat trivial:\n    win:\n      study +small\n      say: You lift Footnote like a sack of warm flour. He allows it, with contempt. You get the book. He gets your chair.\n    lose:\n      health -tiny\n      say: Footnote does not consent to being moved. You now have three long scratches and still no book.\n> right: Bribe the cat\n  gold -tiny\n  mood +small\n  say: One sardine. Footnote rises like a sultan, stretches, and moves exactly one book to the left. It's enough.\n\n@event life_invent_spell\ntime: night\ntitle: A Spell of Your Own\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nwhen: arcana >= 18\nweight: 10\nart: sparkle\ntags: magic study\nscene: A student at a desk covered in crossed-out incantations, a tiny spiral of light hovering over the page, an expression of dawning triumph.\ntext: Late at night you realise there's a gap — a spell nobody has written yet, just a small one, to make ink dry instantly. You could try to invent it. Inventing spells, Thorne says, is \"how most of my former students lost their eyebrows\".\n> left: Invent it carefully\n  challenge research trivial:\n    win:\n      lore +small\n      renown +medium\n      say: Three weeks of theory and one perfect evening. The spell works. Thorne grunts and puts your name on the lecture slide for next year. Forever.\n    lose:\n      mood -tiny\n      say: Your spell dries ink instantly. It also dries the paper, the desk and your eyes. You shelve it for now.\n> right: Just try things until something works\n  chance 40:\n    arcana +medium\n    renown +small\n    say: Somehow — through chaos and luck — it works. You couldn't explain why under oath. It still counts.\n  else:\n    health -small\n    say: Your eyebrows survive. Your desk does not.\n\n@event life_cursed_umbrella\ntitle: The Cursed Umbrella\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nwhen: month >= 3 and month <= 7\nweight: 8\nart: rain\ntags: magic\nscene: A student holding a black umbrella under which a tiny private rainstorm is pouring, while the sky outside is perfectly clear.\ntext: You borrowed an umbrella from lost property. It's black, elegant and cursed: underneath it, it is always raining. The rest of the world is sunny. You are very wet.\n> left: Break the curse\n  challenge spell trivial:\n    win:\n      arcana +tiny\n      gold +tiny\n      say: The curse lifts with a soft hiss. The umbrella is now just a very nice umbrella. You sell it to Corvin, who wants it \"for drama\".\n    lose:\n      health -tiny\n      say: Now it rains sideways.\n> right: Return it to lost property\n  alignment +tiny\n  mood +tiny\n  say: The caretaker takes it back, sighs, and hangs it on a hook labelled \"DO NOT\". There are six other things on that hook.\n\n@event life_exam_dream\ntime: night\ntitle: The Exam Dream\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nwhen: month == 3 or month == 4 or month == 9 or month == 10\nweight: 10\nart: bed\ntags: home\nscene: A dream-image: a student in an exam hall in their pyjamas, the exam paper written in a language that keeps changing, a clock with no hands.\ntext: You dream you're in the exam hall in your pyjamas. The paper is in a language that keeps changing. The proctor is Bartholomew the goat. You wake at 4am, heart pounding.\n> left: Get up and revise\n  study +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: You revise until breakfast. The real exam paper is written in a normal language. You're absurdly relieved.\n> right: Go back to sleep\n  health +tiny\n  say: You dream again. This time the goat gives you full marks. You choose to take this as an omen.\n\n@event life_garden_plot\ntitle: A Patch of Earth\nclass: windfall\ntone: light\nwhen: not has(garden_plot) and turn >= 4\nweight: 5\nart: leaf\ntags: nature\nscene: A small square of freshly turned earth behind the greenhouses, a wooden stake with a student's name painted on it, a watering can beside it.\ntext: Old Maud is giving out the abandoned plots behind the greenhouses. There's one left: small, weedy and full of rocks. \"It's yours if you want it,\" she says. \"Gardens are a lot of work. Most of the work is noticing.\"\n> left: Take the plot\n  gain garden_plot\n  mood +small\n  say: You spend a whole weekend clearing rocks. By spring, you have lettuces, three violent mandrakes and a strange purple flower nobody can name.\n> right: Leave it for someone else\n  say: A shy first-year takes it and grows giant pumpkins. You feel oddly proud of them.\n\n@event life_pen_pal\ntitle: A Letter From Thornwick\nclass: windfall\ntone: light\nwhen: not has(pen_pal) and turn >= 6\nweight: 5\nart: letter\ntags: social\nscene: A letter with a green Thornwick seal open on a desk, beside a reply half-written in Lumenhold blue ink.\ntext: A letter arrives from a stranger at Thornwick Academy — the rival school. \"Dear Lumenhold student chosen at random,\" it begins. \"I'm told you're all insufferable. Prove me wrong?\"\n> left: Write back\n  gain pen_pal\n  charm +tiny\n  say: Your reply is four pages long and only slightly insufferable. The answer arrives within a week. You've made a friend in enemy territory.\n> right: Ignore it\n  say: You ignore it. A second letter arrives: \"Insufferable AND rude. Noted.\" You almost reply. Almost.\n\n@event life_three_headed_puppy\ntitle: A Very Lost Puppy\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nweight: 8\nart: paw\ntags: nature danger\nscene: A puppy with three heads sitting in a corridor, one head chewing a shoe, one licking a wall, one howling mournfully.\ntext: There is a puppy in the dormitory corridor. It has three heads. One is eating a shoe, one is licking the wall, one is howling. It is very lost and, all three heads agree, very hungry.\n> left: Feed it and find its owner\n  gold -tiny\n  mood +medium\n  say: You follow its owner's frantic shouting to Professor Brask's office. She weeps with relief. The puppy's three heads each lick you once, in strict order.\n> right: Call Professor Brask\n  rel maud +tiny\n  say: Brask arrives at a run with a lead and three biscuits. \"Bad boys,\" she says fondly. All three heads look deeply ashamed.\n\n@event life_bell_tower\ntime: night\ntitle: The Midnight Bell\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nwhen: turn >= 4\nweight: 8\nart: bell\ntags: danger social\nscene: A dark bell tower interior, ropes hanging down, a group of students at the bottom daring one of their number to climb.\ntext: Tradition: once in your time at Lumenhold, you must ring the old bell at midnight without getting caught. Your dorm has decided tonight is your night.\n> left: Climb and ring it\n  challenge deceive trivial:\n    win:\n      renown +medium\n      mood +small\n      say: BONG. The whole castle wakes. By the time the prefects reach the tower you're back in bed, innocent and smug.\n    lose:\n      gain detention\n      renown +small\n      say: BONG — and Bram is at the bottom of the stairs when you come down. Detention. But you rang it, and everybody heard.\n> right: Decline the dare\n  mood -tiny\n  say: Someone else rings it instead and becomes a legend. You'll always wonder.\n\n@event life_fog_maze\ntitle: The Fog Maze\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nwhen: month == 3 or month == 4\nweight: 8\nart: compass\ntags: nature mystery\nscene: The academy grounds swallowed by thick white fog, hedges looming like walls, a lantern glowing faintly ahead.\ntext: Fog has come up off the lake so thick you can't see your own feet. The path to the greenhouses has become a maze. Somewhere ahead, someone is calling for help, faintly.\n> left: Follow the voice\n  challenge feat trivial:\n    win:\n      renown +small\n      mood +small\n      say: You find a first-year sitting on a bench, completely lost, sixty feet from the door. You walk them in. They tell everyone you saved their life.\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      health -tiny\n      say: You walk in circles for an hour and end up exactly where you started. The voice was a very clever crow.\n> right: Cast a light and wait\n  arcana +tiny\n  say: You raise a floating light and wait. One by one, three lost students find their way to it. You feel like a lighthouse.\n\n@event life_wishing_well\ntitle: The Wishing Well\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nweight: 8\nart: coin\ntags: magic money\nscene: An old stone well in the corner of a cloister, coins glinting at the bottom, a carved inscription half-worn away.\ntext: In the old cloister there's a wishing well. The inscription says: \"ONE COIN, ONE WISH, ONE PRICE.\" The bottom glitters with three hundred years of coins.\n> left: Throw in a coin and wish\n  gold -tiny\n  chance 50:\n    mood +medium\n    say: You wish for a good month. It is, mostly. Coincidence, probably.\n  else:\n    mood -tiny\n    say: You wish. The well hiccups and spits your coin back out. Apparently, you can't afford that wish.\n> right: Fish out a few old coins\n  challenge deceive trivial:\n    win:\n      gold +medium\n      say: Fifteen crowns' worth of antique coins. The well is silent. Disapprovingly silent.\n    lose:\n      gold -tiny\n      mood -tiny\n      say: The well bites. You lose your own purse in it. ONE PRICE indeed.\n\n@event life_borrowed_broom\ntitle: The Borrowed Broom\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nweight: 8\nart: broom\ntags: danger\nscene: A student clinging to a bucking broom high above the castle, its bristles on fire, a smaller student on the ground waving frantically.\ntext: A second-year lends you their broom for an errand. Midway across the lake, you discover why they were so keen to lend it: it is extremely ticklish, and it hates you.\n> left: Wrestle it home\n  challenge feat trivial:\n    win:\n      vigor +small\n      say: You land in a heap on the lawn, triumphant and windswept. The broom sulks. The second-year owes you a favour.\n    lose:\n      health -medium\n      say: You land in the lake. The broom flies home without you, very pleased with itself.\n> right: Soothe it\n  challenge negotiate trivial:\n    win:\n      charm +tiny\n      mood +small\n      say: You talk to it gently all the way across. It purrs. Brooms shouldn't purr. Now it follows you around.\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      say: It is not soothed. It is, if anything, more ticklish.\n\n@event life_double_trouble\ntitle: Your Double Is Loose\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nwhen: (enrolled(illusion) or passed(illusion, 1)) or seen(illusion_mirror_self) > 0\nweight: 6\nart: mask\ntags: magic danger\nscene: A student staring in horror across a crowded corridor at a perfect copy of themselves, cheerfully chatting up a professor.\ntext: An illusion of you — one you thought you'd dismissed — is still wandering the castle. It has been to three of your classes, flirted with a prefect, and volunteered you for the choir.\n> left: Catch it\n  challenge deceive trivial:\n    win:\n      guile +small\n      say: You corner it in a broom cupboard. It looks at you, shrugs and pops like a bubble. The prefect, however, still expects a date.\n    lose:\n      renown +small\n      mood -tiny\n      say: It escapes, and spends a week as the most charming version of you. People are disappointed when you come back.\n> right: Let it run its course\n  renown +small\n  study +tiny\n  say: It attends your lectures and takes better notes than you do. When it fades after a week, you're almost sorry.\n\n@event life_auction\ntitle: The Lost-Property Auction\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nwhen: gold >= 10\nweight: 8\nart: dice\ntags: money\nscene: A crowded hall, a caretaker with a gavel at a lectern, tables piled with lost scarves, wands, boots and one suspiciously humming box.\ntext: Once a year the caretaker auctions off unclaimed lost property. There's a box nobody wants that hums faintly. The bidding starts at three crowns. Nobody's bidding.\n> left: Bid on the humming box\n  gold -small\n  chance 50:\n    gain inspired\n    say: Inside: a music box that plays a different song every time you open it. Each one makes you think of something new.\n  else:\n    gain hiccup_hex\n    say: Inside: a very old, very annoyed hiccup curse, which immediately transfers itself to you.\n> right: Buy a good warm scarf instead\n  gold -tiny\n  health +small\n  say: A scarf of real Northern wool, slightly singed. It's the warmest thing you've ever owned.\n\n@event life_village_busking\ntitle: Busking in the Village\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nwhen: visits_to(great_hall) >= 3 or charm >= 16\nweight: 8\nart: music\ntags: money social\nscene: A cobbled village square, a student singing beside a fountain with an upturned hat at their feet, villagers pausing to listen.\ntext: You're short on crowns, and the village square has a fountain, an empty hat and a crowd of shoppers. You do know a few songs.\n> left: Sing your heart out\n  challenge negotiate using renown trivial:\n    win:\n      gold +medium\n      renown +small\n      say: A small crowd gathers. Someone requests an encore. The hat fills with copper and one silver coin from a weeping old man.\n    lose:\n      gold +tiny\n      mood -tiny\n      say: Three crowns and a sympathetic look from a dog. Busking is harder than it looks.\n> right: Play the street-magic act instead\n  challenge spell trivial:\n    win:\n      gold +medium\n      mood +small\n      say: You make doves out of the fountain spray. Children scream with joy. The hat overflows.\n    lose:\n      gold +tiny\n      say: Your doves come out as damp sparrows. People are kind about it.\n\n@event life_moths\ntitle: A Storm of Moths\nclass: windfall\ntone: eerie\nwhen: month >= 7 and month <= 10\nweight: 5\nart: moon\ntags: nature mystery\nscene: A dormitory room filled with thousands of pale moths circling a single candle, a student standing in the doorway, astonished.\ntext: You open your door to find your room full of moths — thousands of them, pale as moonlight, circling your candle in silent spirals. It's eerie. It's also, you can't help noticing, beautiful.\n> left: Open the window and let them go\n  mood +small\n  say: They pour out into the night in a silver river. One stays behind, on your pillow, for a week. You name it Gregory.\n> right: Study them\n  lore +small\n  alchemy +tiny\n  say: They're luna moths, migrating — and drawn to something in your room. You find it: a crumb of moonpetal on your sleeve. Mystery solved. Mostly.\n"},{"path":"content/events/expansion_people.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Expansion — more character events (social slot).\n#  Three each for Lyra, Tobias, Seraphine, Corvin, Mira, Nessa,\n#  Kael; two for Bram.\n# ============================================================\n\n# ---------------- Lyra ----------------\n\n@event lyra_toast\ntitle: Lyra Cannot Cook\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: lyra\nwhen: rel(lyra) >= 9\nweight: 10\nart: flame\ntags: social\nscene: A dormitory kitchenette full of smoke, a crimson-haired girl holding a blackened slice of toast at arm's length, a fire-charm still sparking in her other hand.\ntext: Smoke pours from the kitchenette. Lyra Vaneshade, pyromancy prodigy, is holding a piece of bread that is now mostly charcoal. \"I can incinerate a target at forty paces,\" she says. \"I cannot make toast. Don't. Say. Anything.\"\n> left: Say absolutely nothing\n  rel lyra +small\n  say: You open a window, take the bread, and make two perfect slices with a toasting fork. She eats hers in dignified silence. Then she asks for another.\n> right: Teach her — gently\n  mood -tiny\n  rel lyra +medium\n  spark lyra +tiny\n  charm +tiny\n  say: \"Less fire,\" you say. \"No, less. Less than that.\" It takes eleven slices. On the twelfth, she makes perfect golden toast and looks prouder than after any duel.\n\n@event lyra_mother_visit\ntitle: Lady Vaneshade\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\ntrigger: social\nactor: lyra\nwhen: rel(lyra) >= 15 and year >= 2\nweight: 12\nart: crown\ntags: social rival\nscene: A tall woman in crimson and black walking through the courtyard like a queen inspecting troops, Lyra two steps behind her, very straight.\ntext: Lady Vaneshade has come to inspect the academy, and her daughter. She stops in front of you. \"You must be the one my daughter mentions,\" she says. \"Constantly. I'd like to know why.\"\n> left: Answer with perfect manners\n  challenge negotiate trivial:\n    win:\n      rel lyra +large\n      renown +small\n      say: You are polite, precise and unimpressed. Lady Vaneshade studies you, then says, \"Hm. Adequate.\" Lyra looks at you like you've slain a dragon.\n    lose:\n      rel lyra +small\n      mood -small\n      say: You stumble over a title. Lady Vaneshade's eyebrow says everything. Later, Lyra finds you. \"She does that to everyone,\" she says. \"You lasted longer than most.\"\n> right: Answer honestly\n  mood -tiny\n  rel lyra +medium\n  if spark(lyra) >= 15:\n    spark lyra +small\n  say: \"Because she's brilliant,\" you say, \"and nobody tells her.\" Lady Vaneshade is silent. Lyra is redder than her hair. The visit ends early.\n\n@event lyra_rival_respect\ntitle: Respect, Grudgingly\nclass: milestone\ntone: tense\ntrigger: social\nactor: lyra\nwhen: bond(lyra) == \"rival\" and rel(lyra) >= 9\nweight: 16\nart: flame\ntags: rival social\nscene: Two rivals sitting on the edge of a scorched practice ring, wands laid down between them, sharing a flask of water.\ntext: After another draw, Lyra drops onto the edge of the ring beside you. \"You're the only person here who makes me better,\" she says, as if confessing a crime. \"I hate it. I don't want it to stop.\"\n> left: \"Then let's keep it a rivalry.\"\n  rel lyra +medium\n  say: \"Good,\" she says, relieved. \"Rivalry I understand.\" She shoves you, lightly. You shove her back. The rivalry continues, warmer.\n> right: \"Or we could just be friends.\"\n  bond lyra friend\n  rel lyra +large\n  say: She considers the word like an unfamiliar spell. \"Friends,\" she says. \"Fine. But I'm still going to beat you.\" You'd be disappointed if she didn't.\n\n# ---------------- Tobias ----------------\n\n@event tobias_scarecrow\ntitle: The Scarecrow Project\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: tobias\nwhen: rel(tobias) >= 6\nweight: 10\nart: leaf\ntags: social nature\nscene: A scarecrow in a straw hat waving its arms at crows in a vegetable patch, a big farm boy and a student watching it proudly.\ntext: Tobias has built a scarecrow for the greenhouse plots and wants to enchant it to scare crows. \"Just a little life,\" he says. \"Just enough to wave. Nothing creepy.\"\n> left: Help enchant it\n  challenge spell trivial:\n    win:\n      rel tobias +medium\n      say: The scarecrow waves. It also learns to wave hello, and to tip its hat to the headmistress. The crows are terrified. The first-years adore it.\n    lose:\n      rel tobias +small\n      mood -tiny\n      say: The scarecrow comes to life, looks at you both, and walks off into the forest. You see it sometimes, at dusk, waving.\n> right: Suggest a cat instead\n  rel tobias +small\n  say: You borrow the greenhouse cat. The crows leave immediately. Tobias is a little sad about the scarecrow, so you help him make it a hat.\n\n@event tobias_dance_lessons\ntitle: Two Left Feet\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\ntrigger: social\nactor: tobias\nwhen: rel(tobias) >= 9 and (month == 4 or month == 5)\nweight: 12\nart: music\ntags: social romance\nscene: An empty classroom with desks pushed to the walls, a big boy counting steps aloud, a broom propped against the wall as his former partner.\ntext: Tobias has pushed the desks against the walls of an empty classroom. \"The Ball's next month,\" he says, mortified. \"I've been practising with a broom. The broom has complained.\"\n> left: Teach him to waltz\n  health -tiny\n  rel tobias +medium\n  spark tobias +tiny\n  charm +tiny\n  say: One-two-three, one-two-three. He steps on you exactly fifty times. On the fifty-first bar, something clicks, and for a moment he's graceful. He looks astonished.\n> right: Tell him nobody cares about footwork\n  rel tobias +tiny\n  mood +tiny\n  say: \"Just enjoy it,\" you say. He thinks about this, nods, and dances like a delighted bear. It's terrible. It's wonderful.\n\n@event tobias_storm_greenhouse\ntime: night\ntitle: Storm Over the Greenhouses\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\ntrigger: social\nactor: tobias\nwhen: rel(tobias) >= 12 and month >= 6 and month <= 8\nweight: 12\nart: bolt\ntags: nature danger\nscene: Rain lashing a glass greenhouse at night, panes cracking, a big boy holding a door shut against the wind while plants rattle inside.\ntext: A storm hits at midnight. Glass is cracking in the greenhouses. Tobias is out there in his nightshirt, trying to hold a door shut against the wind with his whole body. \"The moonflowers!\" he shouts. \"Eleven years!\"\n> left: Run out and help\n  health -small\n  rel tobias +large\n  spark tobias +tiny\n  say: You hold the panes together with every shielding charm you know until dawn. Every moonflower survives. Tobias hugs you, soaked, laughing, half crying.\n> right: Fetch Old Maud\n  health -tiny\n  rel tobias +small\n  rel maud +small\n  say: Maud arrives in her dressing gown, mutters three words at the sky, and the wind politely goes elsewhere. Tobias stares at her. \"Teach me that,\" he says.\n\n# ---------------- Seraphine ----------------\n\n@event seraphine_lost_day\ntitle: Seraphine Is Tomorrow\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\ntrigger: social\nactor: seraphine\nwhen: rel(seraphine) >= 6\nweight: 10\nart: hourglass\ntags: mystery social\nscene: A girl sitting at the wrong desk in an empty classroom, a teacup in her hands, the calendar on the wall showing the next day's date.\ntext: Seraphine is sitting in an empty classroom, waiting for a lesson that's happening tomorrow. \"I've slipped,\" she says calmly. \"I'm a day ahead. It happens. I need someone to remind me what today is.\"\n> left: Talk her back into today\n  mood -tiny\n  rel seraphine +medium\n  lore +tiny\n  say: You tell her what you had for breakfast, what the weather's like, what Thorne said this morning. Slowly her eyes stop looking through you. \"Oh,\" she says. \"Hello. Today.\"\n> right: Ask her what tomorrow holds\n  chance 50:\n    study +small\n    say: \"There's a surprise quiz in Lore,\" she says. \"Question three is a trick.\" It is. You get it right.\n  else:\n    mood -tiny\n    say: \"It rains,\" she says. \"You get wet. You'll be fine.\" You are not fine. You get very wet.\n\n@event seraphine_painting\ntitle: A Painting of Somewhere Else\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\ntrigger: social\nactor: seraphine\nwhen: rel(seraphine) >= 12\nweight: 10\nart: eye\ntags: social mystery\nscene: A small canvas on an easel showing a stranger's cottage by the sea, a girl with paint on her fingers watching someone look at it.\ntext: Seraphine has painted a small cottage by the sea, with a crooked chimney and a blue door. \"It's yours,\" she says. \"In twelve years. I thought you should see it now. In case you want a different door.\"\n> left: Keep the painting\n  chance 70:\n    mood +small\n    rel seraphine +medium\n    say: You hang it over your bed. Some nights you swear the smoke from the chimney moves. You decide, privately, that you like the blue door.\n  else:\n    rel seraphine +small\n    mood -tiny\n    say: You hang it over your bed. Some mornings the door is a different colour, and you lie there wondering which one you chose.\n> right: Ask her to paint her own future\n  mood -tiny\n  rel seraphine +medium\n  spark seraphine +tiny\n  say: She looks at the blank canvas for a long time. \"I never can,\" she says. \"Seers can't see themselves.\" Then, quietly: \"Unless someone's standing next to us.\"\n\n@event seraphine_mother\ntitle: The Oracle's Summons\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: seraphine\nwhen: rel(seraphine) >= 15 and year >= 2\nweight: 12\nart: letter\ntags: social\nscene: A girl holding a letter that glows faintly silver, standing at the top of a tower stair, looking down as if about to fall or fly.\ntext: Seraphine's mother, the Oracle of Vell, wants her home to take up the family seat. \"I'd spend my life telling kings what they want to hear,\" Seraphine says. \"But she's already seen me say yes. She always sees me say yes.\"\n> left: \"Then surprise her.\"\n  mood -tiny\n  rel seraphine +large\n  say: Seraphine writes back: no. For a whole day she's terrified, and so are you. Then she's giddy. \"I didn't see that coming,\" she keeps saying. \"I didn't see it coming!\"\n> right: \"Maybe she's right.\"\n  rel seraphine -medium\n  say: She nods slowly. She doesn't go — not yet. But she stops telling you her visions, and you miss them more than you expected.\n\n# ---------------- Corvin ----------------\n\n@event corvin_confiscation_vault\ntitle: The Confiscation Vault\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\ntrigger: social\nactor: corvin\nwhen: rel(corvin) >= 12\nweight: 12\nart: lock\ntags: social danger\nscene: A dark vault crammed with confiscated student contraband — fanged frisbees, cursed dice, a goblet of endless soup — two figures with a lantern at the door.\ntext: \"The confiscation vault,\" Corvin breathes. \"Three hundred years of everything the faculty ever took off a student. My grandfather's lucky deck is in there. I just want it back. Lookout?\"\n> left: Keep lookout\n  challenge deceive hard:\n    win:\n      rel corvin +large\n      gold +small\n      say: He comes out with the deck and a handful of confiscated coins \"for your trouble\". The deck is, it turns out, genuinely lucky. He wins at cards for a month.\n    lose:\n      gain detention\n      rel corvin +medium\n      say: The vault alarm is a very loud goblin. You both spend a week cataloguing contraband — which Corvin considers a victory.\n> right: Talk him out of it\n  rel corvin -tiny\n  challenge negotiate hard:\n    win:\n      rel corvin +medium\n      say: You convince him to just ask Thorne. Thorne, astonishingly, gives it back. \"Your grandfather cheated me with it in '41,\" he says. \"Tell him I said hello.\"\n    lose:\n      say: He goes anyway, alone. He comes back with the deck and a black eye from the goblin. Worth it, he says.\n\n@event corvin_brother\ntitle: Corvin's Brother\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: corvin\nwhen: rel(corvin) >= 15 and year >= 2\nweight: 12\nart: mask\ntags: social money\nscene: A rough-looking young man leaning on the academy gate, cap pulled low, Corvin facing him with his hands in his pockets.\ntext: There's a rough-looking man at the gates, and Corvin has gone very still. \"My brother,\" he says. \"He wants money. He always wants money. If I don't pay, he tells the headmistress where I learned to pick locks.\"\n> left: Help Corvin face him down\n  alignment +small\n  challenge negotiate trivial:\n    win:\n      rel corvin +large\n      say: You stand beside Corvin while he says no — the first time, he tells you later, that he's ever said it. His brother leaves. Corvin sits down on the steps and laughs and shakes.\n    lose:\n      rel corvin +medium\n      gold -medium\n      say: It turns ugly. In the end you pay half, just to make him leave. Corvin pays you back within a week, with a strange new seriousness.\n> right: Stay out of family business\n  rel corvin -small\n  say: Corvin pays. You see him counting out every coin he has. He's quiet for a long while after.\n\n@event corvin_cards\ntitle: How to Cheat at Cards\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: corvin\nwhen: rel(corvin) >= 6\nweight: 10\nart: cards\ntags: social money\nscene: A candlelit table in a corner of the common room, a boy fanning a deck of cards with a flourish, a student watching his hands very closely.\ntext: \"Rule one: never cheat,\" says Corvin, shuffling so fast the cards blur. \"Rule two: if you're going to cheat, never get caught. Rule three: I'm about to teach you how they cheat, so you never lose to them.\"\n> left: Learn the tricks\n  health -tiny\n  guile +small\n  rel corvin +small\n  say: By midnight you can spot a marked card, a bottom deal and a mirror charm. You will never be fleeced at Wizard's Hand again.\n> right: Learn to play honestly — and well\n  chance 60:\n    lore +tiny\n    rel corvin +small\n    gold +small\n    say: He teaches you odds and nerve instead. That weekend you win eight crowns at the common-room game without cheating. Corvin is weirdly proud.\n  else:\n    gold -tiny\n    lore +tiny\n    rel corvin +small\n    say: He teaches you odds and nerve instead. That weekend the odds take three crowns off you. \"Also a lesson,\" says Corvin.\n\n# ---------------- Mira ----------------\n\n@event mira_ornithopter\ntitle: The Test Flight\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\ntrigger: social\nactor: mira\nwhen: rel(mira) >= 9\nweight: 12\nart: gear\ntags: social danger\nscene: A rickety winged contraption balanced on the edge of a tower roof, a goggled artificer strapping into it, a friend holding a rope.\ntext: Mira has built a flying machine with canvas wings and seven propellers. It is balanced on the edge of the north tower. \"Brooms are so last century,\" they say, strapping in. \"I need someone on the rope. Probably. Possibly.\"\n> left: Hold the rope\n  challenge feat trivial:\n    win:\n      rel mira +large\n      renown +small\n      say: The machine flies — wobbly, loud, glorious — twice around the tower before landing in the lake. Mira surfaces whooping. You're both legends by dinner.\n    lose:\n      health -medium\n      rel mira +medium\n      say: The rope wins. You are dragged eight feet along the roof before letting go. The machine lands in a tree. Mira waves from it, delighted.\n> right: Insist on a safety net\n  health -tiny\n  rel mira +small\n  alchemy +tiny\n  say: You spend a day building a net. The machine flies four feet and drops into it. \"Data!\" says Mira happily. Nobody dies. It's a good day.\n\n@event mira_lab_fire\ntitle: Fire in the Workshop\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\ntrigger: social\nactor: mira\nwhen: rel(mira) >= 12\nweight: 10\nart: flame\ntags: danger\nscene: A workshop full of smoke and orange light, notebooks scattered across a burning bench, a figure in goggles coughing in the doorway.\ntext: The workshop is on fire. Properly this time. Mira is in the doorway, coughing — and trying to go back in. \"My notebooks! Three years of notes!\"\n> left: Grab the notebooks\n  challenge feat trivial:\n    win:\n      rel mira +large\n      health -small\n      say: You come out singed, smoking, with an armful of notebooks. Mira stares at you, then at the notebooks, then bursts into tears and hugs you, both of you coughing.\n    lose:\n      health -large\n      rel mira +medium\n      say: You get halfway before the smoke drives you back. You save one notebook. Mira says it's the most important one. You both know it isn't. They mean it anyway.\n> right: Drag Mira away\n  rel mira +medium\n  health -tiny\n  say: You haul Mira back from the door. The notebooks burn. Mira is silent for a week. Then they start a new notebook. The first page just says: \"Thank you.\"\n\n@event mira_name_day\ntitle: Mira's Name Day\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: mira\nwhen: rel(mira) >= 15\nweight: 10\nart: gem\ntags: social\nscene: A cluttered workshop decorated with paper chains and one small cake with a single gear-shaped candle, an artificer looking embarrassed and pleased.\ntext: It's Mira's name day. They mention it casually, as if it doesn't matter. It clearly matters. Nobody else seems to know.\n> left: Throw a surprise party\n  gold -small\n  rel mira +large\n  mood +small\n  say: Six friends, one cake and a banner you painted badly. Mira walks in, stops, and has to sit down. \"Nobody's ever...\" they start, and can't finish.\n> right: Give them a handmade gift\n  gold -tiny\n  rel mira +medium\n  spark mira +tiny\n  say: A little brass key that fits nothing. \"For whatever you build next,\" you say. Mira wears it on a chain, every day, for years.\n\n# ---------------- Nessa ----------------\n\n@event nessa_headmistress_interview\ntitle: The Impossible Interview\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: nessa\nwhen: rel(nessa) >= 9 and (met(isolde) and rel(isolde) >= 6)\nweight: 12\nart: paper\ntags: social\nscene: A newspaper editor and a student waiting outside the headmistress's tower door, the editor clutching a list of questions three pages long.\ntext: \"Nobody has interviewed the headmistress in twenty years,\" Nessa says. \"But she likes you. Probably. Get me in the door and I'll name my firstborn after you.\"\n> left: Ask the headmistress\n  rel isolde -tiny\n  challenge negotiate using renown trivial:\n    win:\n      rel nessa +large\n      renown +medium\n      say: The headmistress agrees to ten minutes. She gives Nessa forty. The interview runs on the front page and sells out three printings.\n    lose:\n      rel nessa +small\n      say: \"No,\" says the headmistress, pleasantly. \"But tell Miss Quill her piece on the kitchens was excellent.\" Nessa frames the rejection.\n> right: Suggest someone overlooked instead\n  mood -tiny\n  rel nessa +medium\n  charm +tiny\n  say: Nessa interviews the night porter instead. It's the most-read piece of the year. Half the academy cries over breakfast.\n\n@event nessa_typo\ntime: morning\ntitle: A Catastrophic Typo\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: nessa\nwhen: rel(nessa) >= 6 and met(thorne)\nweight: 10\nart: paper\ntags: social\nscene: A newspaper with a single word circled in red ink, an editor with her head in her hands, a stack of papers still warm from the press.\ntext: The Ledger has gone to print. In the headline about Professor Thorne's lecture series, a single wrong letter has turned \"Thorne's Brilliant Lectures\" into \"Thorne's Brilliant Lecturers\". Which is somehow worse. Five hundred copies.\n> left: Help her fix every copy overnight\n  health -tiny\n  rel nessa +medium\n  say: You correct five hundred copies with a scraping charm and a lot of tea. At dawn, Nessa falls asleep on the press. Thorne never knows.\n> right: Tell her to run a correction and own it\n  mood -tiny\n  rel nessa +small\n  rel thorne +small\n  say: The correction is gracious. Thorne, astonishingly, cuts it out and pins it to his door. \"Precision,\" he says in class, \"is a virtue. So is apology.\"\n\n@event nessa_burnout\ntime: evening night\ntitle: The Editor Sleeps\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: nessa\nwhen: rel(nessa) >= 12 and year >= 2\nweight: 10\nart: candle\ntags: social\nscene: A girl asleep at a desk in a newspaper office, her self-writing quill still scratching on its own, the candle burned down to a stub.\ntext: Nessa hasn't slept properly in a week. She's asleep at her desk now, and her quill — unsupervised — is still writing next week's paper. It has started inventing news.\n> left: Put her to bed and run the paper yourself\n  health -tiny\n  rel nessa +large\n  study -small\n  say: You edit, typeset and print one issue. It's adequate. When Nessa wakes she reads it, winces at your headlines, and hugs you for a long time.\n> right: Wake her and make her rest\n  mood -tiny\n  rel nessa +medium\n  say: She argues for ten minutes, then sleeps for fourteen hours. The quill's invented news about a dragon in the kitchens turns out to be true.\n\n# ---------------- Kael ----------------\n\n@event kael_northern_lights\ntime: night\ntitle: The Lights of the North\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\ntrigger: social\nactor: kael\nwhen: rel(kael) >= 12 and (month <= 5 or month >= 12)\nweight: 12\nart: star\ntags: social romance\nscene: Two figures on a frozen hillside under ribbons of green and violet light, one pointing upward, frost forming a circle around them.\ntext: Kael wakes you at midnight. \"Come,\" he says. On the hill above the lake, the sky is moving — green and violet ribbons, the lights of his home, here, for him. \"I called them,\" he says. \"I wanted you to see.\"\n> left: Watch in silence\n  health -tiny\n  rel kael +small\n  mood +medium\n  say: You watch until the lights fade. Neither of you speaks. On the walk down, he takes your hand without seeming to notice he's done it.\n> right: Ask him about home\n  health -tiny\n  rel kael +medium\n  lore +small\n  say: He tells you about ice palaces and singing glaciers and a mother who never once said she was proud. Then he says: \"This place is more home now.\" He means the hill. He means you.\n  spark kael +tiny\n\n@event kael_fever\ntitle: Kael Burns\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\ntrigger: social\nactor: kael\nwhen: rel(kael) >= 9\nweight: 10\nart: rain\ntags: danger social\nscene: A pale boy in a narrow bed, sweating, frost forming and melting on the pillow around his head in a constant cycle.\ntext: Kael has a fever — and for someone made of frost, a fever is dangerous. The infirmary's warming charms are making it worse. Sister Amaranth looks frightened. \"He needs cold,\" she says. \"Real cold. I don't know where to get it.\"\n> left: Bring him snow from the mountain\n  challenge feat trivial:\n    win:\n      rel kael +large\n      health -medium\n      say: You fly to the peaks on a borrowed broom and come back with a sack of ancient snow. By morning his fever breaks. He doesn't let go of your sleeve all day.\n    lose:\n      health -large\n      rel kael +medium\n      say: The mountain wins. You come back half-frozen and empty-handed. Kael recovers anyway, slowly — and visits you in the next bed.\n> right: Send word to Ilse\n  if met(ilse):\n    rel kael +medium\n    rel ilse +medium\n    say: Ilse arrives in a whirl of snow, puts her hand on his forehead and says something sharp in Northern. The fever breaks. Kael stares at his sister for a long time.\n  else:\n    rel kael +small\n    say: You can't find her. Kael recovers slowly, on his own, the way he's always done everything.\n\n@event kael_letter_home\ntitle: A Letter He Won't Send\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: kael\nwhen: rel(kael) >= 15\nweight: 10\nart: letter\ntags: social\nscene: A boy at a library desk, a letter half-written in sharp Northern script, a candle beside it, ink frozen in the pen.\ntext: Kael is writing a letter. He's rewritten the first line eleven times. \"To my mother,\" he says. \"I'm telling her I won't come home. I won't open the door. I'm telling her I've found...\" He stops.\n> left: \"Found what?\"\n  mood -tiny\n  rel kael +medium\n  spark kael +tiny\n  say: He looks at you for a long time. Then he writes the last word of the sentence without showing you, seals it, and sends it. He's lighter for days.\n> right: Help him find the words\n  health -tiny\n  rel kael +medium\n  charm +tiny\n  say: You sit together until the candle's gone. The letter, when it's finished, is short. It doesn't apologise once. He sends it at dawn.\n\n# ---------------- Bram ----------------\n\n@event bram_father_visit\ntitle: Bram's Father\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: bram\nwhen: flag(bram_softened) and rel(bram) >= 9\nweight: 14\nart: shield\ntags: social\nscene: A towering grey-haired man in an old head-boy's sash speaking down to a red-faced prefect in the entrance hall, students hurrying past pretending not to look.\ntext: A tall man in an old head-boy's sash is speaking to Bram in the entrance hall, quietly and terribly. Bram has shrunk two sizes. \"...never amount to anything,\" the man is saying.\n> left: Interrupt — say something good\n  challenge negotiate trivial:\n    win:\n      rel bram +large\n      say: You tell his father about the sixth-years at the boathouse. The man falls silent. Bram looks at you like you've handed him a sword.\n    lose:\n      rel bram +medium\n      say: His father looks through you as if you're furniture. But afterwards, Bram finds you and says, roughly, \"Nobody's ever stood there before. Thanks.\"\n> right: Find Bram afterwards\n  mood -tiny\n  rel bram +medium\n  say: He's behind the stables, again. You sit on the bucket next to his. You don't talk. After a while he says, \"Same time next year, probably,\" and almost laughs.\n\n@event bram_cheating\ntitle: Bram Cheats\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\ntrigger: social\nactor: bram\nwhen: met(bram) and (month == 4 or month == 10)\nweight: 10\nart: scroll\ntags: rival study\nscene: A prefect in an exam hall slipping a tiny scroll out of his sleeve, a student two desks away watching.\ntext: In the exam hall, two desks away, you see Bram Hollis — prefect, enforcer of rules — slip a tiny scroll out of his sleeve. He sees you seeing him. His face goes white.\n> left: Report him\n  rel bram -huge\n  rel isolde +small\n  renown +small\n  say: Bram loses his sash for the rest of the year. Some students cheer. You don't feel like cheering. Neither, later, does he.\n> right: Say nothing — and let him know you saw\n  alignment -tiny\n  rel bram +medium\n  guile +small\n  say: After the exam you just look at him. He nods, once. You never mention it. But his demerits stop, for you and for everyone you know.\n"},{"path":"content/events/expansion_social.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Expansion — more generic relationship events (actor: any).\n# ============================================================\n\n@event soc_lost_notes\ntitle: The Borrowed Notes\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ncast:\n  npc: any where (tagged(npc, student) or tagged(npc, prefect)) and rel(npc) >= 3\nper: npc\ntrigger: social\nrepeat: 8\nart: scroll\ntags: social study\nscene: A student holding a single soggy page of notes, the rest of the stack floating away down a stream behind them.\ntext: {npc} borrowed your Lore notes last week. {They} {are} now standing in front of you holding a single soggy page. \"There was a stream,\" {they} say{s}. \"And a duck. Mostly a duck.\"\n> left: Laugh it off\n  mood -tiny\n  rel npc +medium\n  study -tiny\n  say: You rewrite your notes from memory. {npc} brings you a cake every day for a week, by way of apology. The cakes are better than the notes were.\n> right: Make {them} rewrite them\n  rel npc -tiny\n  study +tiny\n  say: {npc} copies out forty pages from a friend's notes, grumbling. The new notes are neater than yours ever were.\n\n@event soc_birthday\ntitle: A Birthday\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ncast:\n  npc: any where tagged(npc, peer) and rel(npc) >= 9\nper: npc\ntrigger: social\nrepeat: 12\nart: gem\ntags: social festival\nscene: A small cake with a single candle in a dark common room, a group of students crouched behind a sofa, a surprised face in the doorway.\ntext: You find out — by accident — that it's {npc}'s birthday. {They} haven't told anyone. {They} {are} spending it in the library, alone, on purpose.\n> left: Organise a surprise\n  gold -small\n  rel npc +large\n  mood +small\n  say: Seven people jump out from behind a sofa. {npc} screams, then laughs, then has to sit down. \"How did you know?\" You'll never tell.\n> right: Join {them} in the library\n  rel npc +medium\n  lore +tiny\n  say: You sit down opposite {them} with your own book. Neither of you says happy birthday. At midnight {they} slide{s} a biscuit across the table, and you both grin.\n\n@event soc_bad_news\ntitle: Bad News From Home\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ncast:\n  npc: any where (tagged(npc, student) or tagged(npc, prefect)) and rel(npc) >= 12\nper: npc\ntrigger: social\nrepeat: 12\nart: letter\ntags: social\nscene: A student sitting on a stone staircase with a crumpled letter, head bowed, a friend sitting down beside them.\ntext: {npc} is sitting on the stairs with a letter in {their} lap, not moving. {Their} grandmother is ill. The barge home takes two days. \"I don't know whether to go,\" {they} say{s}.\n> left: \"Go. We'll cover your classes.\"\n  rel npc +large\n  study -tiny\n  say: {npc} catches the next barge. You take notes for two in every lesson. When {npc} comes back, {their} grandmother is better, and {they} hug{s} you on the landing.\n> right: Just sit with {them}\n  rel npc +medium\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You sit there until the stairs move under you. You don't fix anything. That's not what {they} needed.\n\n@event soc_stargazing\ntitle: Counting Stars\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\ncast:\n  npc: any where romanceable(npc) and rel(npc) >= 12 and bond(npc) != \"partner\"\nper: npc\ntrigger: social\nrepeat: 8\nart: star\ntags: social romance\nscene: Two students lying on their backs on a flat roof, close but not quite touching, a sky full of stars above.\ntext: {npc} has dragged a blanket up to the roof. \"There's a meteor shower,\" {they} say{s}. \"Supposedly.\" There's room on the blanket for two, just about.\n> left: Count meteors\n  health -tiny\n  rel npc +medium\n  mood +small\n  say: You count thirty-one. {npc} counts thirty-four and accuses you of not looking properly. You weren't, entirely.\n> right: Talk about everything else\n  health -tiny\n  spark npc +small\n  rel npc +small\n  say: You don't see a single meteor. You talk about home and fear and what you'd do if you could do anything. When you look up, the sky's gone grey with dawn.\n\n@event soc_jealous_friend\ntitle: Jealousy\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ncast:\n  npc: any where tagged(npc, peer) and (bond(npc) == \"friend\" or bond(npc) == \"close\")\nper: npc\ntrigger: social\nwhen: known() >= 4\nrepeat: 10\nart: mask\ntags: social\nscene: A student standing alone at the edge of a laughing group, arms folded, watching someone else take what used to be their seat.\ntext: {npc} has been odd all week. Finally it comes out. \"You've got all these new friends now. I just — I don't know where I fit anymore.\"\n> left: Reassure {them}\n  mood -tiny\n  rel npc +medium\n  charm +tiny\n  say: You tell {them} exactly where {they} fit: right here, first. {npc} rolls {their} eyes, but {they}'re smiling. The oddness is gone by dinner.\n> right: Tell {them} friendship isn't a contest\n  rel npc -tiny\n  lore +tiny\n  say: You're right, technically. {npc} knows it. It still lands badly. It takes a few weeks for things to feel easy again.\n\n@event soc_teach_spell\ntitle: A Trick Worth Learning\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ncast:\n  npc: any where rel(npc) >= 7\nper: npc\ntrigger: social\nrepeat: 8\nart: wand\ntags: social magic\nscene: Two students in an empty classroom, one demonstrating a wand movement, the other copying it with a tiny spark of light.\ntext: {npc} has a spell {they} invented — a little thing, half-useful, half-ridiculous — and wants to teach it to someone. \"It makes your tea exactly the right temperature,\" {they} say{s}. \"Forever.\"\n> left: Learn it properly\n  chance 75:\n    arcana +small\n    rel npc +small\n    say: It takes an hour. Your tea is now perfect, always. You'll use this spell more than anything you learn in class.\n  else:\n    health -tiny\n    rel npc +small\n    say: The first attempt freezes your tea solid, and your eyebrows with it. The second works. Your eyebrows take a day to thaw.\n> right: Teach {them} one of yours in return\n  health -tiny\n  rel npc +medium\n  charm +tiny\n  say: You trade spells like secrets. By midnight you've both got a new trick and an inside joke that will last three years.\n\n@event soc_secret_admirer\ntitle: A Secret Admirer\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\ncast:\n  npc: any where romanceable(npc) and spark(npc) >= 15 and bond(npc) != \"partner\"\nper: npc\ntrigger: social\nrepeat: 12\nart: letter\ntags: romance\nscene: A folded note tucked into a library book with a pressed flower, unsigned, the handwriting careful and slightly shaky.\ntext: Folded notes keep appearing in your library books. Unsigned. They're funny, and a little nervous, and they know things about you that only someone paying attention would know. The handwriting looks familiar.\n> left: Leave a note back\n  spark npc +small\n  say: You write back. The notes get longer. One day the last line just says: \"It's {npc}. You knew, didn't you?\" You did.\n> right: Stake out the library\n  challenge deceive trivial:\n    win:\n      spark npc +medium\n      rel npc +small\n      say: You catch {npc} red-handed, mid-tuck. {They} turn{s} the colour of a pyromancy exam. You both laugh until the librarian throws you out.\n    lose:\n      spark npc +tiny\n      say: You wait three nights and catch nobody. On the fourth morning the note says: \"Nice try.\"\n\n@event soc_injured_friend\ntitle: Carried\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\ncast:\n  npc: any where tagged(npc, peer) and rel(npc) >= 6\nper: npc\ntrigger: social\nrepeat: 10\nart: cross\ntags: social danger\nscene: A student carrying another piggyback across a muddy lawn, the injured one's ankle wrapped in a scarf.\ntext: {npc} has twisted an ankle on the far side of the grounds, a long way from the infirmary, and it's getting dark. {They} {are} trying very hard to pretend it doesn't hurt.\n> left: Carry {them} on your back\n  vigor +small\n  rel npc +medium\n  health -tiny\n  say: It's a long way. {npc} narrates the whole journey like a horse race. By the time you reach the infirmary you're both laughing too hard to breathe.\n> right: Run for help\n  alignment +tiny\n  rel npc +small\n  say: You run for Sister Amaranth, who arrives with a floating stretcher and a lot of opinions. {npc} is fine. {They} send{s} you a thank-you sweet.\n\n@event soc_rival_decency\ntitle: An Unexpected Kindness\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\ncast:\n  npc: any where tagged(npc, peer) and (bond(npc) == \"rival\" or bond(npc) == \"nemesis\" or rel(npc) <= -9)\nper: npc\ntrigger: social\nrepeat: 10\nart: hand\ntags: rival social\nscene: A student picking up a rival's scattered books from a wet floor, the rival frozen mid-step, not sure what to say.\ntext: You drop your books in the rain, and the first person to stoop and help pick them up is {npc}. {They} hand{s} them back without a word, looking as surprised as you are.\n> left: Thank {them} properly\n  mood -tiny\n  rel npc +large\n  say: \"Don't make it a thing,\" {npc} mutters. But the next day {they} nod{s} to you in the corridor. Something has thawed.\n> right: Suspect a trick\n  rel npc -tiny\n  guile +tiny\n  say: You check the books for hexes. There aren't any. {npc} saw you checking. You'll both remember that.\n\n@event soc_partner_anniversary\ntitle: An Anniversary\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\ncast:\n  npc: any where bond(npc) == \"partner\" and spark(npc) >= 40\nper: npc\ntrigger: social\nrepeat: 12\nart: ring\ntags: romance\nscene: Two people on a bench by the lake at sunset, one holding out a small wrapped box, the other's hands over their mouth.\ntext: It's been a while now — long enough that {npc} has started calling things \"ours\". Today {they} hand{s} you a small wrapped box and won't meet your eyes.\n> left: Open it now\n  gold -tiny\n  spark npc +small\n  mood +medium\n  say: Inside: a pebble from the lakeshore where you first really talked, polished to a shine. \"It's stupid,\" {npc} says. It isn't. You carry it everywhere, and spend the next day hunting for something half as good to give back.\n> right: Give {them} yours first\n  gold -small\n  spark npc +medium\n  mood +medium\n  say: You'd had the same idea. You both open your boxes at once. They're nearly the same gift. You laugh until the sun goes down.\n"},{"path":"content/events/expansion_summer.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Expansion — Summer recess (months 11–12).\n#  `has(summer_away)` = went home / travelled; otherwise you're\n#  at the near-empty academy or working in the village.\n# ============================================================\n\n@event sum_empty_castle\ntitle: The Empty Castle\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nwhen: month >= 11\nplan: castle\nweight: 16\nart: tower\ntags: mystery home\nscene: A vast empty dining hall in summer, chairs upturned on the tables, a single student eating alone in a shaft of dusty sunlight.\ntext: In summer the castle breathes differently. Corridors you've walked a thousand times are silent; doors that are always locked stand slightly open. A portrait yawns. Nobody is watching.\n> left: Explore the open doors\n  challenge deceive trivial:\n    win:\n      lore +medium\n      gold +small\n      say: Behind one door: a forgotten trophy room. Behind another: a professor's stash of very old toffee and a purse of lost-property coins nobody will miss.\n    lose:\n      mood -tiny\n      say: Behind the first door: the caretaker, in a dressing gown, very surprised. You both agree this never happened.\n> right: Enjoy the quiet\n  health +small\n  say: You read in window seats, nap on the great lawn and eat breakfast at noon. For two weeks the most famous academy in the kingdom is just a big, peaceful house.\n\n@event sum_home_chores\ntitle: Help Around the House\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nwhen: month >= 11 and not has(origin_noble)\nplan: home\nweight: 14\nart: house\ntags: home\nscene: A farmhouse kitchen in high summer, a basket of laundry, a list of chores pinned to the door, a textbook hidden under a tea towel.\ntext: Home is lovely for about three days. Then the list appears on the kitchen door: the roof, the goats, the fence, the cousin's wedding. Your summer reading list is hidden under a tea towel.\n> left: Do the chores — with magic\n  challenge spell trivial:\n    win:\n      mood +small\n      renown +small\n      say: The fence mends itself, the goats are briefly levitated, the roof is better than new. The whole street comes to watch. Your mother pretends not to be proud.\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      say: The goats are briefly levitated. Unfortunately so is the fence. Your family is very patient about it.\n> right: Do them the slow way, and talk\n  health -tiny\n  mood +small\n  vigor +small\n  say: You fix the fence by hand with your father, saying very little and then, suddenly, a lot. It's the best conversation you've had all year.\n\n@event sum_old_friend\ntitle: An Old Friend\nclass: windfall\ntone: warm\nwhen: month >= 11\nplan: home\nweight: 12\nart: group\ntags: social home\nscene: Two young people sitting on a stone wall above a village, one in academy robes, one in work clothes, a little space between them.\ntext: Your oldest friend from home is waiting at the crossroads, like always. But something's different. \"So,\" they say, not quite looking at you, \"I suppose you're too grand for us now.\"\n> left: Prove you're not\n  mood +medium\n  charm +tiny\n  say: You spend the summer exactly as you used to — orchard raids, river swims, terrible jokes. By Starfall, the space between you on the wall is gone.\n> right: Admit you've changed\n  lore +tiny\n  mood +tiny\n  say: \"I have,\" you say. \"So have you.\" They laugh, surprised. You talk all night about who you're both becoming. It's different from before. It might be better.\n\n@event sum_midsummer_fair\ntime: afternoon\ntitle: The Midsummer Fair\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nwhen: month == 11 or month == 12\nweight: 12\nart: tent\ntags: festival money\nscene: A village green full of striped tents, a carousel of enchanted wooden animals, lanterns strung between trees, a strongman lifting a cow.\ntext: The village Midsummer Fair: enchanted carousel, ring-toss, a strongman, and a booth promising \"GUESS YOUR WEIGHT IN CHEESE\". You have a few crowns in your pocket and a whole afternoon.\n> left: Win something at the ring-toss\n  challenge feat trivial:\n    win:\n      gold +medium\n      mood +small\n      say: Three rings, three hits. You win a stuffed griffon the size of a sheep and sell it to a crying child's father for fifteen crowns. Everyone wins.\n    lose:\n      gold -tiny\n      mood +tiny\n      say: Every ring bounces off. The ring-toss is definitely enchanted. You don't care; you're eating candied apples.\n> right: Ride the carousel\n  gold -tiny\n  mood +medium\n  say: Your wooden hippogriff takes off, very slightly, at the top of every turn. You ride it nine times.\n\n@event sum_letter_from_friend\ntitle: A Summer Letter\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\ncast:\n  npc: any where (tagged(npc, student) or tagged(npc, prefect)) and rel(npc) >= 12\nper: npc\nwhen: month >= 11\nart: letter\ntags: social\nscene: A thick letter with a doodle on the envelope arriving by owl at a summer window.\ntext: An owl arrives with a thick, slightly crumpled letter from {npc}. Six pages of summer news, two doodles, one pressed flower, and at the end, in smaller writing: \"I miss the castle. I miss you, actually.\"\n> left: Write a short reply\n  rel npc +small\n  say: You write a page. It's warm, if brief. {npc} keeps it anyway.\n> right: Write back for hours\n  health -tiny\n  rel npc +medium\n  mood +small\n  say: You write until the candle gutters. Twelve pages. You can't remember the last time you wrote that much that wasn't an essay.\n\n@event sum_rain_week\ntitle: A Week of Rain\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nwhen: month >= 11\nweight: 10\nart: rain\ntags: home\nscene: Rain streaming down a tall window, a student curled in an armchair with a fat novel, a teapot steaming on the sill.\ntext: It rains for a solid week. Not dramatic rain: just steady, grey, endless rain. Every plan you made for the summer is quietly drowning.\n> left: Read a very long novel\n  gold -tiny\n  mood +small\n  lore +tiny\n  say: You buy an eight-hundred-page novel about a family of sea-witches, read every word and cry at the end. It's the best week of the summer.\n> right: Practise spells indoors\n  arcana +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You practise until you can light every candle in the room with a flick. You also set fire to one curtain. It's raining; it goes out quickly.\n\n@event sum_sea_serpent\ntitle: Fishing With an Uncle\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nwhen: month >= 11\nplan: home\nweight: 8\nart: wave\ntags: nature danger\nscene: A small fishing boat on a grey-green sea, a fishing line drawn taut, something very long and scaled rising beside the hull.\ntext: Your uncle takes you fishing. Something takes the bait. Something very large. The line sings. The boat tilts. A long scaled neck rises out of the water and looks at you with polite interest.\n> left: Cut the line\n  mood +tiny\n  say: The serpent sinks back with a sigh like a disappointed whale. Your uncle tells the story for the rest of his life, and it gets bigger every time.\n> right: Try to talk to it\n  challenge negotiate trivial:\n    win:\n      lore +medium\n      renown +small\n      say: You greet it in the old courtesies Professor Brask taught you. It bows its great head, leaves a pearl on the deck and swims away. Your uncle faints.\n    lose:\n      health -medium\n      say: The serpent is not interested in conversation. It is interested in the boat. You swim home.\n\n@event sum_reading_list\ntitle: The Summer Reading List\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nwhen: month == 11\nweight: 12\nart: scroll\ntags: study\nscene: A long scroll unrolling off a table and across the floor, a list of titles in tiny handwriting, a student staring at it in despair.\ntext: The summer reading list arrives by owl. It is a scroll. It unrolls across the floor, out of the door and partway down the stairs. Thorne has added a note at the bottom: \"Optional. (It is not optional.)\"\n> left: Read all of it\n  lore +medium\n  study +medium\n  mood -small\n  say: You read everything. When term starts, Thorne asks a question about the last book and you're the only one who can answer. His eyebrow rises half a millimetre.\n> right: Read the first and last chapters of each\n  guile +tiny\n  study +tiny\n  say: A time-honoured strategy. You can talk confidently about the beginning and end of forty books. The middle remains a mystery, like most of life.\n\n@event sum_night_market\ntime: evening night\ntitle: The Night Market\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nwhen: month >= 11 and gold >= 15\nweight: 10\nart: lantern\ntags: money mystery\nscene: A market that only appears after midnight, stalls lit with coloured flames, hooded sellers, a bottle that seems to contain a small thundercloud.\ntext: A night market appears in a field outside the village — it wasn't there yesterday and won't be tomorrow. A hooded stall-keeper offers you a stoppered bottle with a tiny thundercloud inside. \"Fifteen crowns. What it does is a surprise.\"\n> left: Buy the bottle\n  gold -medium\n  chance 50:\n    gain inspired\n    arcana +small\n    say: You uncork it in your room. The little storm rains ideas on you for a fortnight. Your notebooks overflow.\n  else:\n    mood -tiny\n    gain sick\n    say: You uncork it in your room. The little storm rains on you. Just rain. For a fortnight. You catch a cold.\n> right: Just look around\n  health -tiny\n  mood +small\n  lore +tiny\n  say: You wander until dawn, buying nothing, watching everything. When the sun rises the field is empty except for a single glowing feather. You keep it.\n\n@event sum_end_of_summer\ntitle: The Last Week of Summer\nclass: windfall\ntone: warm\nwhen: month == 12\nweight: 5\nart: sun\ntags: home\nscene: A student packing a trunk in golden late-summer light, a half-finished jar of jam, a pressed flower and a new textbook on the bed.\ntext: The last week of summer has that particular light — golden, heavy, already nostalgic. Your trunk is half-packed. Part of you is desperate to get back. Part of you wants this week to last forever.\n> left: Squeeze every drop out of it\n  mood +medium\n  health +tiny\n  say: Late swims, long dinners, one final sunset from the hill. You arrive at the barge sunburnt and happy and only slightly late.\n> right: Get ready for the new year\n  study +small\n  lore +tiny\n  say: You re-read your notes, sharpen your quills and polish your wand. When term starts you are, for once, ahead. It's an unfamiliar feeling.\n"},{"path":"content/events/expansion_y3.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Expansion — Final year: careers, last times, farewells.\n#  career_* flags (set by the Career Fair) + career_accepted\n#  feed two endings: ending_teacher, ending_wanderer.\n# ============================================================\n\n@event y3_career_fair\ntitle: The Career Fair\nclass: story\ntone: light\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: final_year and month == 2\npriority: 42\ntype: menu\nart: scroll\ntags: study story\nchain: careers\nscene: The great hall lined with recruiters' booths — a Conclave mage in silver, a librarian behind a wall of books, a circus ringmaster juggling fire.\ntext: Every guild, order and institution in the kingdom has sent someone to the great hall. They are all very polite. They are all, very clearly, shopping. Where will you leave your name?\n> conclave: The Conclave of the Seven Towers\n  hint: Requires Arcana 30. Apprentice archmages.\n  when: arcana >= 30\n  locked: Needs Arcana 30.\n  flag career_conclave\n  queue y3_interview now\n> library: The Royal Library\n  hint: Requires Lore 30. Keepers of every book ever written.\n  when: lore >= 30\n  locked: Needs Lore 30.\n  flag career_library\n  queue y3_interview now\n> guild: The Guild of Alchemists\n  hint: Requires Alchemy 28. Potions, profit, explosions.\n  when: alchemy >= 28\n  locked: Needs Alchemy 28.\n  flag career_guild\n  queue y3_interview now\n> unseen: The Crown's Office of Unseen Affairs\n  hint: Requires Guile 25. They found you, actually.\n  when: guile >= 25\n  locked: Needs Guile 25.\n  flag career_unseen\n  queue y3_interview now\n> circus: The Travelling Circus of Wonders\n  hint: Requires Charm 22. Wagons, crowds, illusions.\n  when: charm >= 22\n  locked: Needs Charm 22.\n  flag career_circus\n  queue y3_interview now\n> teaching: Stay at Lumenhold as a junior teacher\n  hint: Requires a faculty member who trusts you (Thorne, Maud or the Headmistress at 25+).\n  when: rel(thorne) >= 15 or rel(maud) >= 15 or rel(isolde) >= 15\n  flag career_teaching\n  queue y3_interview now\n> none: Not yet — you'd rather not decide\n  mood +tiny\n  say: You collect a tote bag, three pamphlets and a free quill. The future can wait one more month.\n\n@event y3_interview\ntitle: The Interview\nclass: story\ntone: light\ntrigger: queued\nart: scales\ntags: study story\nchain: careers\nscene: A small panelled room, a long table, three interviewers and one empty chair facing them.\ntext: Three people behind a long table. One takes notes; one smiles too much; one says nothing at all, which is clearly the one who decides. \"Tell us,\" says the smiling one, \"why you.\"\n> left: Let your record speak\n  challenge research trivial:\n    win:\n      flag career_offered\n      queue y3_offer_letter in 2\n      renown +small\n      say: You talk about what you've learned and, more importantly, what you got wrong and fixed. The silent one writes a single word. You suspect it's \"yes\".\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      say: You recite your achievements like a shopping list. The silent one looks at the clock. \"We'll be in touch,\" says the smiling one, which means no.\n> right: Tell them a story\n  challenge negotiate trivial:\n    win:\n      flag career_offered\n      queue y3_offer_letter in 2\n      renown +small\n      say: You tell them about the night the lake froze, or the night you caught a star, or the night you were wrong. The silent one laughs. Out loud.\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      say: The story goes on too long and ends in the wrong place. Somewhere in the middle you lost them, and yourself.\n\n@event y3_offer_letter\ntitle: The Offer\nclass: story\ntone: light\ntrigger: queued\nwhen: flag(career_offered)\nart: letter\ntags: story\nchain: careers\nscene: An envelope with a heavy wax seal on a dormitory desk, unopened, a hand hovering over it.\ntext: A heavy envelope with a seal you recognise from the fair. Inside, a single page: an offer. A place. A future with a start date. It needs a signature by the end of the month.\n> left: Sign it today\n  flag career_accepted\n  mood +medium\n  say: Your signature looks strange on the page — like a grown-up's. You pin the letter above your bed and look at it every night.\n> right: Ask them to wait\n  mood -tiny\n  lore +tiny\n  say: You write back asking for time. They grant it, coolly. The future, it turns out, does not like waiting, but it will.\n\n@event y3_first_year_mentee\ntitle: Your First-Year\nclass: windfall\ntone: light\nwhen: final_year and month <= 4\nweight: 5\nart: feather\ntags: social\nscene: A tiny first-year trotting beside a fourth-year across the courtyard, firing questions, clutching an enormous timetable.\ntext: Fourth-years are assigned a first-year to mentor. Yours is called Pim, is about the size of a large cat, and has already asked forty-one questions, including \"are the stairs alive\" and \"are YOU alive\".\n> left: Answer every question honestly\n  mood +small\n  charm +small\n  say: Yes, the stairs are alive. Yes, you are alive. No, the lake is not \"friendly\". Pim takes notes. By spring, Pim is explaining things to other first-years, wrongly but with total confidence.\n> right: Teach Pim to find answers alone\n  lore +small\n  study +tiny\n  say: You hand Pim a library card and a map. Pim is furious for a week, then unstoppable. The librarian now knows Pim by name. And fears Pim, slightly.\n\n@event y3_last_lanterns\ntime: evening night\ntitle: The Last Lantern Night\nclass: windfall\ntone: light\nwhen: final_year and month == 2\nweight: 40\nart: lantern\ntags: festival\nscene: A lone student on the shore at night holding an unlit lantern, hundreds of others already drifting over the dark lake.\ntext: Your last Lantern Night. You stand on the shore with an unlit lantern and a pen and realise you have no idea what to wish for. Three years ago it was obvious. Now it isn't.\n> left: Wish for the people you'll leave behind\n  mood +medium\n  say: You write all their names. It takes the whole lantern. It rises slowly, heavily, and you watch it until it is just a star among stars.\n> right: Leave it blank\n  lore +tiny\n  mood +tiny\n  say: You let it go empty. Strangely, it rises higher than all the others. Maybe the lake likes not knowing either.\n\n@event y3_thesis\ntitle: The Final Thesis\nclass: story\ntone: light\nwhen: final_year and month >= 5 and month <= 8\nweight: 16\nart: book\ntags: study\nscene: A desk buried in drafts, a wall of pinned notes connected by string, a single candle and a single exhausted student.\ntext: Every fourth-year writes a final thesis. You could write something safe and solid — or you could take on the question nobody has answered in three hundred years: why the academy was built above a lake.\n> left: Safe and solid\n  study +medium\n  say: Two hundred pages on the thermodynamics of hovering charms. Impeccable. Unremarkable. Your examiners will sleep well.\n> right: The question nobody answered\n  challenge research trivial:\n    win:\n      study +large\n      renown +medium\n      say: Your thesis is so good that the headmistress asks for the only copy — and then, very politely, does not give it back.\n    lose:\n      study -small\n      mood -small\n      say: You chase the question until it runs out of archive. Your thesis ends mid-sentence, like the founders' ledger. The examiners are not amused.\n\n@event y3_portrait\ntitle: Sitting for the Portrait\nclass: windfall\ntone: light\nwhen: final_year and month >= 7\nweight: 5\nart: mirror\ntags: story\nscene: An old painter at an easel in a sunlit studio, a student posing stiffly in robes, half-finished portraits of past graduates watching from the walls.\ntext: Every graduate's portrait hangs in the long gallery — and every portrait, eventually, starts talking. You have one sitting to decide who will be gossiping on that wall for the next three hundred years.\n> left: Pose solemn and dignified\n  renown +small\n  say: The painter captures you looking wise and distant. Your portrait will be dignified. It will also be dreadfully bored.\n> right: Pose as yourself, mid-laugh\n  mood +medium\n  charm +tiny\n  say: Your portrait is laughing. Three days after it's hung, it starts telling the other portraits jokes. The gallery has never been louder.\n\n@event y3_letter_to_self\ntitle: A Letter to Your First-Year Self\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nwhen: final_year and month >= 4\nweight: 10\nart: letter\ntags: home\nscene: A fourth-year writing by candlelight, a box of keepsakes open beside them: a pressed flower, a ticket stub, a borrowed wand returned.\ntext: The Divination department runs a tradition: write a letter to your first-year self. They swear it will be delivered. Nobody knows how. Some fourth-years swear they received theirs.\n> left: Tell them it'll be all right\n  chance 75:\n    mood +medium\n    say: You write: \"It's going to be all right. Not easy. All right.\" The next morning the letter is gone, and you remember, very faintly, reading it once on a sky-barge.\n  else:\n    mood -tiny\n    say: You try to write something comforting, and it keeps coming out as a list of warnings. You seal it anyway. The next morning it is gone.\n> right: Warn them about everything\n  lore +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You write eleven pages of warnings. The letter is returned unopened with a note in Divination ink: \"Some things must be learned the hard way.\"\n\n@event y3_maud_seeds\ntitle: Seeds for Later\nclass: windfall\ntone: light\nwhen: final_year and month >= 9 and met(maud) and rel(maud) >= 9\nactor: maud\nweight: 5\nart: flower\ntags: nature social\nscene: An old woman pressing a small paper packet of seeds into a student's hands at the garden gate, bees drifting in the evening light.\ntext: Maud presses a paper packet into your hands. \"Seeds,\" she says. \"From the secret garden. Plant them wherever you end up. They'll grow anywhere someone is a bit kind to them.\" She sniffs. \"Like you did.\"\n> left: Hug her\n  rel maud +large\n  mood +medium\n  say: She protests for exactly one second and then hugs back, fierce and small and smelling of rosemary. \"Go on,\" she says. \"Before I get soppy.\"\n> right: Promise to come back and visit\n  rel maud +medium\n  flag promised_maud\n  say: \"Every summer,\" you promise. \"We'll see,\" she says, but she writes it on her calendar, in ink, which for Maud is a blood oath.\n\n@event y3_last_duel\ntime: morning\ntitle: One Last Duel\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nwhen: final_year and month >= 6 and (member(duelling) or flag(tournament_champion)) and met(lyra)\nactor: lyra\nweight: 16\nart: duel\ntags: rival\nscene: Two duelists bowing in an empty dueling ring at dawn, no crowd, no referee, just the mist and the lake beyond.\ntext: Dawn, the empty dueling ring, no crowd. Lyra is waiting. \"No referee,\" she says. \"No points. Just one last duel, for us. So we know.\"\n> left: Duel her with everything\n  challenge combat trivial:\n    win:\n      rel lyra +medium\n      renown +small\n      say: It lasts eleven minutes. Nobody wins. You both end up flat on your backs, laughing at the sky. \"Now we know,\" she says.\n    lose:\n      rel lyra +medium\n      health -medium\n      say: She wins, cleanly. Then she helps you up and doesn't let go of your hand for a moment longer than necessary. \"Now we know.\"\n> right: Lower your wand and just talk\n  mood -tiny\n  rel lyra +large\n  if spark(lyra) >= 20:\n    spark lyra +small\n  say: You talk until the mist burns off. About fire, and mothers, and what comes after. It turns out you didn't need to duel to know.\n\n@event y3_time_capsule\ntitle: The Time Capsule\nclass: windfall\ntone: light\nwhen: final_year and month >= 8 and bonds(friend) + bonds(close) + bonds(partner) >= 2\nweight: 5\nart: key\ntags: social\nscene: A group of students kneeling around a hole under an old oak, lowering in a battered tin box sealed with wax.\ntext: Your friends have a battered tin box, a shovel and a plan: bury a time capsule under the old oak, to be opened in twenty years. Everyone puts something in. What will you give?\n> left: Something precious\n  mood +medium\n  say: You put in the first thing you ever enchanted. It's ugly and crooked and it still hums. Someone cries, and blames the dirt.\n> right: Something ridiculous\n  mood +large\n  say: You put in a single sock, labelled \"EVIDENCE\". In twenty years, nobody will remember why. That's the point.\n\n@event y3_speech\ntitle: The Graduation Speech\nclass: story\ntone: light\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: turn == max_turns and (renown >= 40 or gpa >= 3.0)\npriority: 45\nactor: isolde\nart: crown\ntags: story\nscene: A headmistress handing a scroll to a student in an empty great hall, rows of chairs set out for a ceremony that hasn't happened yet.\ntext: The headmistress catches you in the great hall, where chairs are already being set out. \"Each year one graduate speaks for their class,\" she says. \"This year, the faculty would like it to be you.\"\n> left: Accept and write something honest\n  renown +large\n  mood +medium\n  rel isolde +small\n  say: You write about fear, and friends, and the lake, and the things you didn't know you'd miss. When you practise it alone, your voice cracks in the same place every time. You leave it in.\n> right: Decline — someone else deserves it\n  rel isolde +small\n  charm +tiny\n  say: \"Interesting,\" says the headmistress, which from her is a medal. The speech goes to a nervous first-generation student who absolutely brings the house down.\n"},{"path":"content/events/fixtures.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Fixtures (design §5.5): the people of a place or a track,\n#  promoted from the background. Lighter ladders than the core\n#  cast: an introduction where they work (or through their course\n#  or job, via `lead:`), two or three repeatable cards that come\n#  when you're there or when they look you up (rarely: `seeks:`),\n#  and one milestone. No romance; nothing touches the mystery.\n# ============================================================\n\n# ---------------- Sister Amaranth (infirmary; Alchemy & Healing) ----------------\n\n@event amaranth_intro\ntitle: No Excuses\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nplace: infirmary\nactor: amaranth\nwhen: not met(amaranth)\nweight: 30\nart: cross\ntags: social\nscene: A stern healer in a white apron holding a student's wrist up to the window light, a row of tonic bottles behind her.\ntext: You came in for a plaster. Sister Amaranth takes your hand, turns it to the light and asks how it happened. You tell her. She waits. You tell her the true version. \"Better,\" she says, and reaches for a bottle that is not a plaster.\n> left: Drink whatever it is\n  meet amaranth\n  health +small\n  mood -small\n  rel amaranth +small\n  say: It tastes of iron and regret. The hand stops throbbing before you reach the door. \"Come back if it swells,\" she calls. \"Don't come back if it doesn't.\"\n> right: Just the plaster, thanks\n  meet amaranth\n  gold -tiny\n  rel amaranth -tiny\n  say: She gives you the plaster, and a look you will remember the next time you lie to a healer. \"Three crowns,\" she says. \"The look is free.\"\n\n@event amaranth_rounds\ntime: evening night\ntitle: Evening Rounds\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\ntrigger: social\nactor: amaranth\nwhen: rel(amaranth) >= 0\nweight: 10\nrepeat: 5\nart: potion\ntags: social nature\nscene: A healer and a student walking a long infirmary ward at dusk, the student carrying a tray of small glass bottles, lamps being lit along the beds.\ntext: {Sister Amaranth catches you in the corridor and hands you a tray of tonics without a word of explanation.|Amaranth holds out the tonic tray as you pass. It seems you are on the rota now.|The tray is waiting for you on the ward table, labelled YOURS in Amaranth's square hand.} {~Six beds tonight.|A first-year with hiccups that light up.|Someone has charmed their own eyebrows off.}\n> left: Do the rounds with her\n  health -tiny\n  alchemy +small\n  rel amaranth +small\n  say: {~She tells you what each tonic does and why it tastes like that. You remember all of it, to your surprise.|You learn which patients are brave and which are loud, and that they are rarely the same ones.|At the last bed she lets you measure the dose. \"Again,\" she says. You do it again. \"Fine.\"}\n> right: You have an essay due\n  rel amaranth -tiny\n  say: {~\"So does everyone,\" she says, and takes the tray back. \"Off you go.\"|She nods, which is somehow worse than a lecture.|\"Next time,\" she says, as a fact, not a question.}\n> up[mend]: Mend the worst one yourself\n  rel amaranth +small\n  say: {~You close a split knuckle with a whisper of light. Amaranth inspects it for a long time. \"Neat,\" she says, the highest word she owns.|The sprain knits under your hands. The patient thanks Amaranth. Amaranth points at you.}\n\n@event amaranth_orders\ntitle: Doctor's Orders\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: amaranth\nwhen: rel(amaranth) >= 6 and health < 50\nweight: 14\nrepeat: 6\nart: bed\ntags: social\nscene: A healer standing in a dormitory doorway with her arms folded, a pale student halfway out of bed with a stack of books.\ntext: {Sister Amaranth has come to find you, which has never happened to anyone. \"You look like a draught of bad decisions,\" she says. \"Bed.\"|Amaranth again, at your door, arms folded. \"I can hear you coughing from the infirmary.\"|\"Bed,\" says Amaranth, before you've opened the door all the way.}\n> left: Go to bed as ordered\n  health +medium\n  mood -small\n  rel amaranth +small\n  say: {~You sleep fourteen hours. She checks on you twice and leaves a tonic on the sill both times.|Two days of broth and boredom. You come out of it a person again.|You argue for one more chapter. You lose, and sleep.}\n> right: \"I'm fine, honestly.\"\n  rel amaranth -small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {~\"Nobody who says 'honestly' is,\" she says, and leaves. You are not fine by Thursday.|She writes something on a card and walks off. It turns out to be your name, on her list.}\n\n@event amaranth_trust\ntitle: The Cupboard Key\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\ntrigger: social\nactor: amaranth\nwhen: rel(amaranth) >= 30\nweight: 20\nart: key\ntags: social magic\nscene: An infirmary storeroom at night lit by a single lamp, a healer pressing a small brass key into a student's palm, shelves of labelled bottles all around.\ntext: Sister Amaranth closes the storeroom door behind you both. \"I'm away for the solstice and the junior healer faints at blood,\" she says. \"You don't.\" She holds out the key to the tonic cupboard, and with it a page of her own notes on the mending charm.\n> left: Take the key and the notes\n  learn_spell mend\n  rel amaranth +small\n  alchemy +small\n  say: \"Mend what you can, send the rest to me, and lie to nobody,\" she says. The notes are written for someone she expects to be good at this. You read them twice and try very hard to be.\n> right: \"I'm not ready for that.\"\n  rel amaranth +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: She looks at you for a long moment. \"Good answer. Wrong, but good.\" She keeps the key. She gives you the notes anyway.\n\n# ---------------- Professor Brask (stables; Beastcraft & Flight) ----------------\n\n@event brask_shovel\ntitle: Hands Wanted\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: brask\nwhen: rel(brask) >= 0\nweight: 10\nrepeat: 5\nart: paw\ntags: nature social\nscene: A weathered professor in a leather apron holding out a shovel to a student at a stable door, a hippogriff watching suspiciously from its stall.\ntext: {Professor Brask holds out a shovel as you pass the stables. She doesn't say anything. The hippogriff behind her does, loudly.|Brask hands you the shovel before you're through the door.|\"Ah,\" says Brask, and the shovel is already in your hands.} {~The griffon chick is in a mood.|The owls have opinions about the new straw.|A hippogriff has eaten a glove. Possibly a professor's.}\n> left: Muck out the stalls\n  vigor +small\n  health -tiny\n  rel brask +small\n  say: {~An hour of straw and silence. At the end Brask says, \"The grey one likes you,\" which is more than she has said to anyone this term.|She tells you a story about a manticore, a bucket and a bishop. It ends with a bite and a moral. You miss the moral.|The hippogriff bows to you on your way out. Brask pretends not to see.}\n> right: Point out you're not in her class\n  rel brask -tiny\n  say: {~\"Nobody is,\" she says, \"until they are.\" She takes the shovel back.|\"Neither is the hippogriff,\" says Brask. \"It helps anyway.\"}\n> up[beast_speech]: Ask the stalls what they need\n  rel brask +small\n  vigor +tiny\n  say: {~The hippogriffs want the window open and the owls want it shut. You negotiate. Brask watches you with her arms folded and something almost like a smile.|The griffon chick tells you, at length, about a grievance. You pass it on. Brask nods. \"She's right, actually.\"}\n\n@event brask_night_owl\ntime: evening\ntitle: The Sick Owl\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\ntrigger: social\nactor: brask\nwhen: rel(brask) >= 10\nweight: 12\nart: owl\ntags: nature\nscene: A dim stable loft at night, a student sitting cross-legged beside a straw nest where a sick barn owl sleeps under a blanket, a lantern turned low.\ntext: Brask finds you at supper and sits down without asking, which is a first. \"The old barn owl won't eat,\" she says. \"Somebody should sit with her tonight. Somebody quiet.\" She is looking at you.\n> left: Sit up with the owl\n  health -small\n  rel brask +medium\n  vigor +tiny\n  say: You sit in the loft till dawn, dripping water onto the owl's beak. At four she takes a mouse from your fingers. In the morning Brask looks at the empty dish for a long time, then claps you once on the shoulder, hard.\n> right: Send her to Sister Amaranth\n  rel brask -tiny\n  rel amaranth +tiny\n  say: Amaranth looks at the owl, looks at you, and says she doesn't do owls. Then she sits with it all night. Brask is not sure how to feel about this, and neither are you.\n\n@event brask_trust\ntime: evening\ntitle: Old Courtesies\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\ntrigger: social\nactor: brask\nwhen: rel(brask) >= 30\nweight: 20\nart: feather\ntags: nature magic\nscene: A professor and a student at the edge of a forest at dusk, both bowing low to a great antlered stag half-hidden in the trees.\ntext: Brask takes you to the edge of the Whisperwood at dusk. \"Most people talk at animals,\" she says. \"A few talk to them. Fewer listen.\" She bows, very low, to something in the trees. \"Do as I do. I'll teach you the rest.\"\n> left: Bow, and learn\n  learn_spell beast_speech\n  rel brask +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: It takes until full dark, and something huge breathes on your neck for most of it. Then, faintly, you understand a word. It is \"rude\". Brask laughs once, loudly, the way she does once a term.\n> right: Keep your distance\n  rel brask -tiny\n  vigor +small\n  say: You stay by the path and watch. She doesn't press you. On the walk back she talks about hoof care for an hour, which you suspect is a kindness.\n\n# ---------------- Professor Vey (lecture tower; Illusion & Glamour) ----------------\n\n@event vey_pop_quiz\ntime: morning\ntitle: Is It There?\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: vey\nwhen: rel(vey) >= 0\nweight: 10\nrepeat: 5\nart: mask\ntags: magic study\nscene: A smiling professor in a violet scarf leaning against a corridor wall, a cup of tea in her hand, a second, slightly too perfect cup on the windowsill beside her.\ntext: {Professor Vey is in the corridor with two cups of tea. She offers you one. \"Only one of them is real,\" she says pleasantly.|Vey stops you on the stairs. \"There's a coin behind your ear. Or there isn't.\"|\"Good morning,\" says Vey, and the door you were about to open is, possibly, a wall.} {~She looks delighted already.|A student behind you walks into something.|She is humming.}\n> left: Look closely\n  challenge deceive medium:\n    win:\n      charm +small\n      rel vey +small\n      say: {~You pick the right one. Vey raises her cup to you. \"Insufferable,\" she says, beaming.|You catch the shimmer at the edge. \"Ah,\" says Vey. \"You've been paying attention. Dreadful habit. Keep it.\"}\n    lose:\n      mood -tiny\n      say: {~You reach for the tea and close your fingers on air. Vey enjoys this very much.|Wrong. The real one was in her hand the whole time. \"It usually is,\" she says.}\n> right: Play along and get it wrong\n  guile +small\n  rel vey +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~You reach for the false one on purpose, with feeling. Vey narrows her eyes. \"Either you're bad at this, or very good,\" she says. \"I'll find out.\"|You let her win. She knows. She lets you know she knows.}\n\n@event vey_revue\ntitle: The Faculty Revue\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: vey\nwhen: rel(vey) >= 10 and (month == 3 or month == 9)\nweight: 12\nart: music\ntags: social festival\nscene: A small stage in the Great Hall hung with violet curtains, a professor directing a nervous student holding a paper crown, chairs being set out below.\ntext: Professor Vey is producing the faculty revue, in which professors make fools of themselves for charity. \"I need someone to play a king who isn't there,\" she says. \"You'd be wonderful. You'll be terrible. Either is fine.\"\n> left: Take the part\n  challenge feat using charm medium:\n    win:\n      renown +small\n      rel vey +medium\n      say: You are a king who isn't there, magnificently. Thorne, in the front row, says \"Hm\" twice, which the faculty agree is a standing ovation.\n    lose:\n      renown +tiny\n      mood -small\n      rel vey +small\n      say: You forget your lines, invent new ones and are, by general agreement, the best thing in it for the wrong reasons. Vey keeps the paper crown on her desk.\n> right: Hide in the wings\n  rel vey -tiny\n  say: You run the curtain. The revue goes ahead without a king, which Vey says was the point all along.\n\n@event vey_trust\ntime: noon afternoon\ntitle: The Mirror Trick\nclass: milestone\ntone: eerie\ntrigger: social\nactor: vey\nwhen: rel(vey) >= 30\nweight: 20\nart: mirror\ntags: magic\nscene: An empty classroom at twilight, two identical students standing either side of a tall mirror, a professor seated on a desk between them, clapping softly.\ntext: Vey keeps you after class and tilts a tall mirror towards you. Your reflection steps out of it and sits on a desk. \"The trick isn't making a copy,\" she says. \"It's knowing which of you to send. Want to learn it?\"\n> left: Learn it\n  learn_spell mirror\n  rel vey +small\n  mood -small\n  say: It takes a week. On the last night your reflection refuses to go back. Vey talks it round over tea, then says, \"Now you know why I don't do this in public.\" You are not sure which of you walked home.\n> right: Keep your own face, thanks\n  rel vey +tiny\n  charm +small\n  say: \"Sensible,\" she says, disappointed. She teaches you instead how to tell when someone else is using it. It turns out a lot of people are.\n\n# ---------------- Professor Marisol (lakeshore; Evocation's tidecalling) ----------------\n\n@event marisol_intro\ntitle: A Dry Patch of Air\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nplace: lakeshore\nactor: marisol\nwhen: not met(marisol)\nweight: 30\nart: rain\ntags: nature magic\nscene: Heavy rain on a lake shore, a woman reading on the boathouse steps beneath a perfectly dry circle of air, raindrops sliding around it as if around glass.\ntext: It is raining on the whole lake except for one circle on the boathouse steps, where a woman in a soaked hat is reading poetry, perfectly dry. She looks up. \"It's not hard,\" says Professor Marisol. \"You just ask the water to go round. Sit, if you like.\"\n> left: Sit and ask how\n  meet marisol\n  arcana +small\n  mood -tiny\n  rel marisol +small\n  say: She explains it as a conversation. The water, apparently, is willing. By the end of the poem the rain is going round you too, for almost a minute. \"There,\" she says, and goes back to the poem.\n> right: Leave her to her book\n  meet marisol\n  health -tiny\n  say: You nod and run for cover. Behind you she reads aloud, to nobody, a poem about a storm that comes back every year to see if you've changed.\n\n@event marisol_cold_water\ntime: morning\ntitle: Cold Water Is Good for You\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: marisol\nwhen: rel(marisol) >= 0\nweight: 10\nrepeat: 5\nart: wave\ntags: nature magic\nscene: Dawn mist on a still lake, a professor standing waist-deep in the water waving cheerfully to a shivering student on the jetty.\ntext: {Professor Marisol is in the lake at dawn, waist-deep, and waves at you as though this were normal.|Marisol again, in the lake. \"It's lovely once you're in,\" she calls. It is not lovely.|\"You came!\" calls Marisol from the water. You had been walking past.} {~There is ice at the edges.|A heron is watching her with professional respect.|She has a book in one hand, held perfectly dry.}\n> left: Wade in\n  health -tiny\n  arcana +small\n  rel marisol +small\n  say: {~She teaches you to feel the lake breathing. You cannot feel your feet. Both are, she says, normal.|You ask the water to rise and it rises, a little, around your knees. Marisol whoops like a child.|By the time you climb out you've stopped shivering, which she says is a good sign and Amaranth will say is a bad one.}\n> right: Wave back from the jetty\n  rel marisol -tiny\n  say: {~She waves again, unoffended, and goes under.|\"Coward,\" she calls cheerfully. You are fine with that.}\n> up[tide]: Raise a wave to say hello\n  rel marisol +small\n  arcana +tiny\n  say: {~A small, neat wave rolls across the lake and taps her on the shoulder. She laughs so hard she sinks.|Your wave arrives slightly late and very large. Marisol rides it in to shore, delighted.}\n\n@event marisol_storm\ntitle: Weather\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\ntrigger: social\nactor: marisol\nwhen: rel(marisol) >= 12 and (season == \"autumn\" or season == \"spring\")\nweight: 12\nart: bolt\ntags: nature magic danger\nscene: A wild storm over a dark lake, a professor and a student standing on a rocky point with their arms raised, lightning splitting the sky behind them.\ntext: There's a knock at your window during the worst storm of the year. Professor Marisol is outside, hair everywhere, grinning. \"Nobody learns tides on a calm day,\" she shouts over the wind. \"Coming?\"\n> left: Go out into it\n  challenge feat using arcana medium:\n    win:\n      arcana +medium\n      rel marisol +medium\n      health -small\n      say: On the point, in the roar, you hold back one wave for three whole seconds. Marisol screams with joy. You go home soaked to the bone and lit up like a lamp.\n    lose:\n      health -medium\n      rel marisol +small\n      say: The lake throws you on your back. Marisol hauls you out by the collar, laughing and apologising in equal parts, and walks you all the way to the infirmary.\n> right: Close the window\n  rel marisol -tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: She shrugs, cheerful, and runs off into the rain. You lie awake listening to the lake and wondering what you missed.\n\n@event marisol_trust\ntitle: The Last Line\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\ntrigger: social\nactor: marisol\nwhen: rel(marisol) >= 30\nweight: 20\nart: scroll\ntags: magic\nscene: A quiet lakeside at sunset, a professor handing a student a worn book of poems with a ribbon in it, the water perfectly still before them.\ntext: Marisol presses her old book of water poems into your hands. \"I never remember the last lines,\" she says. \"You will. The tidecalling's in the margins; I wrote it there when I was your age. Read it on the shore. It works better on the shore.\"\n> left: Read it on the shore\n  learn_spell tide\n  rel marisol +small\n  mood +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: You read until dark, ankle-deep. On the last page the lake answers you, softly, all the way out to the far bank. You remember the last line. You write it in, for her.\n> right: Give it back; it's hers\n  rel marisol +medium\n  mood -tiny\n  say: She looks at the book and then at you. \"Nobody's ever given it back,\" she says, and reads you the poem herself. She forgets the last line. You tell her.\n\n# ---------------- Mabel (the tea shop) ----------------\n\n@event mabel_intro\ntitle: The Way You Open a Door\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nplace: mabels\nactor: mabel\nwhen: not met(mabel)\nweight: 30\nart: cup\ntags: social home\nscene: A cosy village tea shop with a bell over the door, a woman behind the counter already reaching for a tin of tea as a student steps in from the cold.\ntext: The bell over the door rings. The woman behind the counter looks up, considers how you came in, and reaches for a tin before you've said a word. \"Homesick,\" says Mabel, \"with a side of exam. Sit by the window, petal.\"\n> left: Sit by the window\n  meet mabel\n  gold -tiny\n  mood +small\n  rel mabel +small\n  say: The tea is exactly right and you can't say why. She brings a slice of cake you didn't order and doesn't charge for it. \"First visit,\" she says. \"After that I charge double.\"\n> right: \"Just something to go.\"\n  meet mabel\n  gold -tiny\n  say: She hands you a paper cup and looks at you over it. \"Come back when you've got time to sit,\" she says. \"You'll know when.\"\n\n@event mabel_parcel\ntitle: A Tin From Mabel\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\ntrigger: social\nactor: mabel\nwhen: rel(mabel) >= 2\nweight: 10\nrepeat: 6\nart: cup\ntags: social home\nscene: A small tin of tea wrapped in brown paper and string on a dormitory pillow, a handwritten label on top and a pressed flower tucked under the string.\ntext: {A small tin arrives on the barge with your name on it, in Mabel's round hand. The label says \"FOR THE COUGH I HEARD THROUGH THE DOOR.\"|Another tin from Mabel. The label says \"FOR WHATEVER THIS WEEK IS.\"|Mabel's tins have a shelf of their own now. Today's label says only \"YOU KNOW.\"} {~There's a pressed violet under the string.|It smells of honey and woodsmoke.|There's a biscuit taped to the lid.}\n> left: Pay her next time you're down\n  gold -tiny\n  health +small\n  rel mabel +small\n  say: {~You leave the crowns on her counter. She pushes them back. You leave them again. This goes on for some time.|You thank her in person. \"Don't,\" she says. \"Drink it.\" You do. It works.}\n> right: Send it back with a note\n  rel mabel -tiny\n  say: {~She sends it back again with a longer note. You give up and drink it.|The tin comes back on the next barge. The label now says \"STUBBORN.\"}\n\n@event mabel_cup\ntitle: Your Cup on the Hook\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\ntrigger: social\nactor: mabel\nwhen: rel(mabel) >= 25\nweight: 18\nart: cup\ntags: social home\nscene: A row of mismatched cups hanging on hooks behind a tea-shop counter, a woman hanging a new one with a student's initial painted on it.\ntext: Behind Mabel's counter is a row of hooks with cups on them, each painted with a letter. You've never asked whose they are. Today, while you watch, Mabel hangs a new one with your initial on it. \"Regulars,\" she says, as if that settled it.\n> left: Ask whose the others are\n  rel mabel +small\n  mood +medium\n  gold -tiny\n  say: She tells you, cup by cup: a fisherman, a dead headmaster, a girl who became a duchess and still writes. \"And you,\" she says. Something in your chest unknots.\n> right: Say thank you and sit down\n  mood +small\n  health +small\n  say: You sit. She brings your tea in your cup. You don't need to say anything else, and she doesn't either.\n\n# ---------------- Mr Grimsby (the bookshop) ----------------\n\n@event grimsby_intro\ntitle: Not That One\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nplace: grimsbys_books\nactor: grimsby\nwhen: not met(grimsby)\nweight: 30\nart: book\ntags: study money\nscene: A cramped bookshop with leaning shelves, an elderly bookseller on a ladder holding out a book to a student below, the student holding a list.\ntext: You ask for a textbook. An elderly man on a ladder says \"Ah!\" with enormous enthusiasm and hands you a history of lighthouses. \"Not what you asked for,\" says Mr Grimsby. \"What you'll need. You'll see.\"\n> left: Buy the lighthouse book\n  meet grimsby\n  gold -small\n  lore +small\n  rel grimsby +small\n  say: You read it on the barge home. Chapter four has the exact proof your essay was missing. When you go back to say so, he only nods. \"They always come back,\" he says. \"Lighthouses.\"\n> right: Insist on the textbook\n  meet grimsby\n  gold -tiny\n  rel grimsby -tiny\n  say: He finds it after forty minutes, under a cat. He sells it to you sadly, like a doctor handing over a prescription he doesn't believe in.\n\n@event grimsby_parcel\ntitle: The Wrong Book, Posted\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: grimsby\nwhen: rel(grimsby) >= 2\nweight: 10\nrepeat: 6\nart: book\ntags: study\nscene: A brown-paper parcel tied with string on a library desk, a student unwrapping an odd old book while a cat watches from the next chair.\ntext: {A parcel arrives from Grimsby's. Inside is a book you didn't order and a note that says \"Thought of you. Pay when convenient. A. G.\"|Another parcel from Mr Grimsby. This one is about eels.|Grimsby's parcels come monthly now, like weather.} {~It smells of rain and pipe smoke.|There's a bus ticket from another century inside the cover.|The note is written on the back of a recipe.}\n> left: Keep it and pay him\n  gold -small\n  lore +small\n  rel grimsby +small\n  say: {~It's the wrong book, exactly as promised, and somewhere in it is the right page. It always is.|You read it out of politeness and finish it out of something else.}\n> right: Post it back\n  gold -tiny\n  rel grimsby -tiny\n  say: {~The postage costs more than the book would have. He sends a cheerful note that says \"Next time, then.\"|He sends it straight back with a second, wronger book. You keep both.}\n\n@event grimsby_mind_the_shop\ntitle: Mind the Shop\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\ntrigger: social\nactor: grimsby\nwhen: rel(grimsby) >= 25\nweight: 18\nart: key\ntags: study money\nscene: A student alone behind the counter of a crowded old bookshop at dusk, a ring of keys on the counter and a customer peering in through the dusty window.\ntext: Mr Grimsby has a ladder accident (a mild one; he lands on the poetry) and needs someone to mind the shop for a week. \"You know where everything is,\" he says, which is untrue. \"Better than me,\" he adds, which is.\n> left: Mind the shop\n  lore +medium\n  gold +small\n  mood -small\n  rel grimsby +medium\n  say: You sell eleven books, all of them the wrong ones, and every customer comes back to thank you. Grimsby, on crutches, says you have the gift. He means it as a warning.\n> right: \"I can't, I'm sorry.\"\n  rel grimsby -small\n  say: He closes for the week instead, with a sign that says GONE TO LIGHTHOUSE. Nobody knows which one.\n\n# ---------------- Old Pell (Pell & Daughters; the apothecary job) ----------------\n\n@event pell_intro\ntitle: A Thousand Drawers\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nplace: pell_apothecary\nactor: pell\nwhen: not met(pell)\nweight: 30\nart: bottle\ntags: job money\nscene: An old apothecary on a library ladder in front of a wall of tiny labelled drawers, peering down over his spectacles at a student at the counter.\ntext: You ask for something for a headache. An old man on a ladder stops, counts the drawers under his breath and comes down with exactly one twist of paper. \"Willow and feverfew,\" says Old Pell. \"Three pinches. Not four. Four is for people who don't listen.\"\n> left: Ask why not four\n  meet pell\n  gold -tiny\n  alchemy +small\n  rel pell +small\n  say: He tells you, at length, with diagrams drawn in the counter dust. You leave with the cure, a headache from the lecture and the dosage for nine other things.\n> right: Pay and go\n  meet pell\n  gold -tiny\n  say: He nods at the door as you leave, as if adding you to a ledger of people who will be back. You are back within a month.\n\n@event pell_count_drops\ntitle: Count Them Aloud\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: pell\nwhen: rel(pell) >= 0 and member(apothecary_clerk)\nweight: 10\nrepeat: 5\nart: bottle\ntags: job study\nscene: An old apothecary at a wooden counter holding a glass dropper over a vial, a student beside him counting on their fingers, a wall of tiny labelled drawers behind them.\ntext: {Old Pell sets a dropper in your hand. \"Forty. Aloud.\" He watches your lips, not the vial.|\"Forty,\" says Pell, and stands behind you. You can hear him counting along under his breath.|Pell has started leaving you the hard tinctures. That is how he says he trusts you.} {~A daughter rolls her eyes at the counter.|The drawers rattle when a cart goes by.|Something in drawer three hundred is humming.}\n> left: Count, aloud, every drop\n  alchemy +small\n  mood -tiny\n  rel pell +small\n  say: {~Forty, exactly. Pell says nothing for a full minute, then \"Again.\" You are fairly sure that was praise.|You lose count at thirty-one and start over without being told. Pell grunts approvingly.}\n> right: Eyeball it; it's close enough\n  challenge brew easy:\n    win:\n      alchemy +tiny\n      say: {~It's right. Pell checks it three times and is visibly annoyed that it's right.|Close enough is, today, exactly right. \"Luck,\" says Pell. \"Don't.\"}\n    lose:\n      rel pell -small\n      perform apothecary_clerk -small\n      say: {~Forty-three. Pell pours it out without a word and hands you the dropper again. The silence lasts all shift.|Too many. A daughter fixes it before he sees. He sees.}\n\n@event pell_daughters\ntitle: Pell & Daughters\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\ntrigger: social\nactor: pell\nwhen: rel(pell) >= 20\nweight: 18\nart: scales\ntags: job home\nscene: An old apothecary and his two grown daughters arguing across a counter over a new painted shop sign, a student standing awkwardly between them.\ntext: Pell's daughters want a new sign, brighter labels and a counter that faces the door. Pell wants it to be fifty years ago. All three of them turn to you, the only person in the shop who isn't family. \"Well?\" says Pell.\n> left: Side with the daughters\n  rel pell -small\n  mood +small\n  alignment +tiny\n  say: Pell stamps into the back room. A week later the new sign goes up. Underneath it, in small old letters, it still says PELL. He shows you, gruffly, as though you'd asked.\n> right: \"The drawers have never been wrong.\"\n  rel pell +medium\n  gold +small\n  say: Pell glows. The daughters glare. Pell gives you a raise and the daughters give you all the worst stocktakes. Everyone feels this is fair.\n\n# ---------------- Grisk (the moneylender) ----------------\n\n@event grisk_pleasantries\ntitle: Pleasantries\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\ntrigger: social\nactor: grisk\nwhen: rel(grisk) >= -10 and not flag(grisk_gone)\nweight: 8\nrepeat: 6\nart: pouch\ntags: money social\nscene: A goblin in a velvet waistcoat sitting at a corner table in a low-beamed tavern, raising a small glass to a student, a fat ledger at his elbow.\ntext: {Grisk raises a small glass from the corner table of the Gilded Newt. \"My friend,\" he says. \"Do sit. I'm not working.\"|Grisk, at the corner table again. \"How is the purse?\" he asks, with what sounds like real concern.|\"Ah, my favourite non-client,\" purrs Grisk.} {~His ledger stays closed, which feels deliberate.|He is eating a single olive very slowly.|The landlady gives you a warning look.}\n> left: Sit for one drink\n  gold -tiny\n  guile +small\n  rel grisk +small\n  say: {~He talks about money the way other people talk about weather, and you learn more in an hour than in a term of Umbral Theory.|He tells you which of the village shops are in trouble and why. It feels like gossip. It is a lesson.|He pays for the drinks and writes nothing down, which is the most unsettling thing he has ever done.}\n> right: Keep walking\n  rel grisk -tiny\n  say: {~\"Another time,\" he calls after you. \"I'm very patient.\"|He raises the glass anyway. You feel it between your shoulder blades all the way out.}\n\n@event grisk_tip\ntitle: A Tip, Between Friends\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\ntrigger: social\nactor: grisk\nwhen: rel(grisk) >= 4 and not has(in_debt) and not flag(grisk_gone)\nweight: 10\nart: coin\ntags: money\nscene: A goblin leaning across a tavern table to murmur into a student's ear, sliding a single gold coin across the wood with one finger.\ntext: Grisk slides a coin across the table. \"Between friends,\" he murmurs. \"The barge company is about to buy a second barge. The ferryman's cousin will sell his share this week, cheap, because he doesn't know. You could know.\"\n> left: Buy the share\n  alignment -tiny\n  chance 60:\n    gold +large\n    rel grisk +small\n    say: The second barge arrives. The share triples. Grisk lifts his glass across the Newt. You feel rich and slightly unwell.\n  else:\n    gold -medium\n    say: The second barge sinks on its first crossing. Grisk is sympathetic. \"Risk,\" he says. \"Delicious, and sometimes poison.\"\n> right: Leave the cousin his share\n  rel grisk -tiny\n  alignment +tiny\n  say: Grisk pockets the coin. \"Honest,\" he says, as if diagnosing something. \"It'll pass. Or it won't, and you'll be poor and admired.\"\n"},{"path":"content/events/incidents.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Incidents and money sinks (design §8.2).\n#  A) forced sinks: setbacks that cost crowns or health, always\n#     with a way to soften them. Gated on gold so they drain\n#     savings rather than break poor players.\n#  B) voluntary sinks: patronage and donations for the well-off.\n#  C) year three: a term of Thornwick exchange students.\n# ============================================================\n\n# ---------------- A) Forced sinks ----------------\n\n@event inc_fever_bill\ntime: morning\ntitle: A Fever and a Bill\nclass: incident\ntone: tense\nwhen: turn >= 3 and gold >= 20 and seen(inc_fever_bill) < 2 and since(inc_fever_bill) >= 12\nweight: 6\nrepeat: 2\nart: potion\ntags: life money\nscene: A pale student in an infirmary bed, a stern nurse holding out a small blue bottle and a folded bill.\ntext: You wake in the infirmary with a fever that makes the ceiling breathe. Sister Amaranth holds up a small blue bottle. \"This clears it by morning. It is also not free,\" she says. \"Or you can sweat it out, like your ancestors.\"\n> left: Buy the tonic\n  gold -large\n  health -small\n  say: {~It tastes of tin and regret.|It tastes like a pond that has given up.} By morning the ceiling is a ceiling again, and your purse is noticeably lighter.\n> right: Sweat it out\n  health -medium\n  study -small\n  say: Three days of damp sheets and strange dreams about stairs. You miss a week of notes, and come back feeling like a wrung-out dishcloth.\n\n@event inc_broken_wand\ntitle: Snapped on the Stairs\nclass: incident\ntone: tense\nwhen: turn >= 3 and gold >= 20 and seen(inc_broken_wand) == 0\nweight: 6\nrepeat: 1\nart: wand\ntags: magic money\nscene: A student kneeling on a stone staircase, holding the two halves of a wand that still fizzes faintly at the break.\ntext: The moving stairs shift without warning. You catch yourself; your wand doesn't. It lies in two pieces on the landing, still fizzing at the break like a sulking firework.\n> left: Have it properly rebound\n  gold -large\n  say: The repairer in the village tuts, splints it with silver wire and charges you for every tut. It works better than before, which is somehow worse.\n> right: Bind it with string and hope\n  challenge spell easy:\n    win:\n      mood -tiny\n      say: It holds. It spits a little when you cast, like a cat that forgives you but wants it noted.\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      study -small\n      say: It holds for three days, then sneezes a spell backwards. Your eyebrows grow back within the week; your marks take longer.\n\n@event inc_library_fine\ntitle: The Overdue Notice\nclass: incident\ntone: light\nwhen: turn >= 3 and gold >= 20 and seen(inc_library_fine) < 2 and since(inc_library_fine) >= 12\nweight: 6\nrepeat: 2\nart: paper\ntags: study money\nscene: A prefect in a red sash holding out a long paper notice, a book with teeth marks on the desk between them.\ntext: A prefect in a red sash slides a notice across your desk. The library book you borrowed has come back late, damp and, somehow, with teeth marks. \"Damages,\" she says. \"Or you can argue. People do. It never goes well.\"\n> left: Pay the fine\n  gold -medium\n  mood -tiny\n  say: She stamps your receipt with great satisfaction. The book is reshelved in a cage, which feels like the right outcome for everyone.\n> right: Argue that the book bit first\n  challenge negotiate medium:\n    win:\n      mood +tiny\n      say: You produce the bite on your thumb as evidence. The prefect inspects it, then the book, and halves the fine to nothing. \"Fair,\" she says. \"It has a history.\"\n    lose:\n      gold -large\n      study -tiny\n      say: The prefect writes down every word you say, then doubles the fine for \"contempt of librarian\". You pay it, and a demerit besides.\n\n@event inc_letter_home\ntitle: Bad Weather at Home\nclass: incident\ntone: grim\nwhen: turn >= 3 and gold >= 30 and seen(inc_letter_home) == 0\nweight: 6\nrepeat: 1\nart: letter\ntags: home money\nscene: A student at a window reading a rain-spotted letter, a small pile of coins on the sill beside them.\ntext: A letter from home, written in a hurry. A storm took half the roof, and the repairs will cost more than anyone has. Nobody asks you for anything. That's how you know it's bad.\n> left: Send crowns home\n  gold -large\n  mood -small\n  say: You send everything you can spare and a little you can't. The reply comes a month later: a drawing of a roof, finished, and a pressed flower.\n> right: Write back with love, not money\n  mood -medium\n  say: You write four pages and keep your purse. The reply is kind. It doesn't mention the roof. That night you lie awake and listen to the rain.\n\n@event inc_stolen_purse\ntitle: Market Day Fingers\nclass: incident\ntone: tense\nwhen: turn >= 3 and gold >= 20 and seen(inc_stolen_purse) < 2 and since(inc_stolen_purse) >= 12\nweight: 6\nrepeat: 2\nart: pouch\ntags: money danger\nscene: A crowded village square on market day, a figure in a grey hood slipping away between the stalls with a small purse.\ntext: Somewhere between the cheese stall and the man selling enchanted socks, your purse stops being yours. You catch a grey hood slipping between the stalls, already three stalls ahead.\n> left: Run after the hood\n  challenge feat medium:\n    win:\n      gold -tiny\n      health -tiny\n      say: You corner the thief behind the bakery. The purse comes back a little lighter; they'd already spent some on a bun. You let them keep the bun.\n    lose:\n      gold -large\n      health -small\n      mood -small\n      say: You trip over a goose, then a second goose. When you get up, the hood is gone, and so are your crowns.\n> right: Let it go\n  gold -large\n  mood -small\n  say: You tell yourself it's only money. You tell yourself that all the way back up the hill, and it never quite takes.\n\n@event inc_hiccuping_robe\ntime: morning\ntitle: The Robe That Hiccups\nclass: incident\ntone: light\nwhen: turn >= 3 and gold >= 20 and seen(inc_hiccuping_robe) == 0\nweight: 6\nrepeat: 1\nart: cauldron\ntags: magic money\nscene: A student in potion-stained robes jolting mid-step as their robe gives a visible hiccup, classmates hiding smiles.\ntext: A cauldron two benches down boils over, and a whole batch of Draught of Unending Hiccups soaks your best robe. The stain fades by supper. The hiccups don't. Every few minutes, the robe jumps.\n> left: Buy a new robe\n  gold -medium\n  mood -tiny\n  say: The tailor in the village fits you in an afternoon and charges you for a week. The old robe now hiccups quietly in the bottom of your trunk.\n> right: Wear it with dignity\n  renown -small\n  mood -small\n  say: {~It hiccups through a lecture on silence.|It hiccups through the headmistress's announcements.} Someone starts a tally on the common-room wall. You are briefly famous for the wrong reasons.\n\n# ---------------- B) Voluntary sinks: patronage ----------------\n\n@event patron_tournament_purse\ntitle: A Patron's Purse\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nwhen: (month == 7 or month == 8) and gold >= 80\nweight: 10\nrepeat: 12\nart: trophy\ntags: festival money\nscene: A festival committee table on a lawn, a thin purse on a velvet cushion and a list of patrons with plenty of space left.\ntext: The Spring Tournament committee has a velvet cushion, a very thin prize purse and a list of patrons with a lot of room left on it. Patrons are read out before the first bout, in a loud voice, to the whole academy.\n> left: Fund the champion's purse\n  gold -large\n  renown +medium\n  say: {~Your name is read out first, and mispronounced only slightly.|Your name rings across the lawn before the first bout. Two first-years bow to you, unsure why.} The champion thanks you from the podium.\n> right: Put in a few crowns\n  gold -small\n  renown +tiny\n  say: Your name is read out somewhere near the end, between a professor's cat and \"a well-wisher\". It still counts.\n\n@event patron_ledger_print\ntitle: Paper for the Press\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nwhen: turn >= 6 and gold >= 80\nweight: 6\nrepeat: 12\nart: paper\ntags: social money\nscene: A cramped office under a staircase, a groaning printing press, and a young editor holding out an empty paper tray.\ntext: Nessa Quill corners you by the library stairs, holding an empty paper tray like evidence. \"Special edition. Biggest story of the term. No paper. You,\" she says, pointing, \"look solvent. Patrons get their name on the masthead.\"\n> left: Pay for the whole print run\n  gold -large\n  rel nessa +medium\n  renown +small\n  say: \"Headline: generous student saves free press,\" Nessa says, already writing it. The masthead lists you in tiny capitals, which she says is the most honour anyone gets.\n> right: Buy one small advertisement\n  gold -small\n  renown +small\n  say: You buy a corner of page four. Nessa writes your copy for you, and somehow it reads like a confession. People quote it at you for a week.\n\n# ---------------- B) Voluntary sinks: donations ----------------\n\n@event donate_greenhouse_glass\ntime: morning\ntitle: The Broken Panes\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nwhen: turn >= 3 and gold >= 80 and not discovered(greenhouses)\nweight: 8\nrepeat: 3\nart: leaf\ntags: nature money\nscene: A collection tin on a garden gate, broken glass glittering in the grass behind it and steam escaping from a damaged greenhouse.\ntext: Last night's hail went through the greenhouse glass like a hammer through toffee. A collection tin hangs on the gate with a hand-lettered sign: every pane paid for keeps the mandrakes from catching cold.\n> left: Pay for a whole pane\n  gold -large\n  mood +small\n  discover greenhouses\n  say: A gardener with mud to the elbows insists on showing you your pane. Inside it is warm, green and loud with opinions. You are welcome, she says, any morning.\n> right: Drop in a coin and walk on\n  gold -tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: The coin clinks into the tin. Behind the gate, something small and leafy sneezes. You feel, obscurely, that you've been judged.\n\n@event donate_duel_floor\ntitle: The Scorched Floor\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nwhen: turn >= 3 and gold >= 80 and not discovered(duel_hall)\nweight: 8\nrepeat: 3\nart: sword\ntags: club money\nscene: A sweaty duellist with a collection box at the foot of a stair, scorched floorboards stacked behind them.\ntext: A duellist with a bandaged hand rattles a box at the foot of the east stair. \"The hall floor,\" he says. \"Someone set it on fire again. Every board you buy, we carve your initials on the underside. Honour, and splinters.\"\n> left: Buy a row of floorboards\n  gold -large\n  renown +small\n  discover duel_hall\n  say: He drags you upstairs to see. The hall smells of smoke and wax, and the practice dummies glare at you. Your initials go on the underside of the board by the door.\n> right: Give him a coin for the bandage\n  gold -tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: He thanks you with exaggerated courtesy and goes back to rattling. Somewhere above, steel rings on steel, and nobody invites you up.\n\n# ---------------- C) Year three: the Thornwick exchange ----------------\n\n@event xchg_arrival\ntitle: Guests in Green\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nwhen: year == 3\nweight: 12\nrepeat: 1\nart: mask\ntags: social rival\nscene: Four students in green Thornwick robes standing in a grand entrance hall, the portraits on the walls all turned to stare at them.\ntext: Four students in Thornwick green arrive for a term, with trunks and very polite smiles. Within an hour every portrait in the entrance hall is winking at the wrong people. A prefect looks around for someone, anyone, to show them the castle.\n> left: Offer to show them round\n  challenge negotiate easy:\n    win:\n      renown +small\n      charm +small\n      say: You give the full tour, moving stairs included. By the end the boy with the sketchbook admits Lumenhold's towers are \"not as crooked as reported\".\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      renown -tiny\n      say: Halfway round, they convince you a wall is a door. It isn't. They are very sorry, and laughing too hard to say so.\n> right: Watch them from a distance\n  guile +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You watch them all week. They watch back. You learn that the quiet one counts doorways, and that they know you're watching.\n\n@event xchg_ink_cuffs\ntitle: The Ink-Stained Cuffs\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nwhen: year == 3 and seen(xchg_arrival) > 0\nweight: 12\nrepeat: 1\nart: book\ntags: study rival\nscene: A library at dusk, a student in green robes with ink-stained cuffs copying from a book chained to a desk.\ntext: Late in the library, you find the Thornwick girl with the ink-stained cuffs copying from a chained book she shouldn't have reached. She sees you see her. \"It's only history,\" she says. \"Unless you want it to be a scandal.\"\n> left: Report her to the librarian\n  renown +small\n  mood -small\n  say: The librarian thanks you, coldly. The Thornwick girl is confined to the guest wing for a week. She nods to you at dinner afterwards, as if you had both played well.\n> right: Keep quiet, for a favour\n  alignment -tiny\n  guile +small\n  renown -tiny\n  say: She tears out the page she copied and hands you the other half of her notes instead. \"We're even,\" she says. You're fairly sure you aren't.\n\n@event xchg_farewell\ntime: morning\ntitle: A Thornwick Farewell\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\nwhen: year == 3 and seen(xchg_arrival) > 0 and since(xchg_arrival) >= 3\nweight: 12\nrepeat: 1\nart: group\ntags: social rival\nscene: Students in green and blue robes on a jetty at dawn, a barge waiting, one of them holding out a striped scarf.\ntext: The Thornwick students leave on the dawn barge. The boy with the sketchbook finds you on the jetty and holds out his scarf. \"It's a custom,\" he says. \"Probably. I've just decided.\" Behind him his friends pretend not to listen.\n> left: Swap scarves\n  mood +medium\n  renown -tiny\n  say: You trade scarves on the jetty. Someone from your own house mutters \"traitor\" at breakfast. You wear it anyway, and get a letter from across the hills by the end of the month.\n> right: Challenge him to one last duel\n  challenge combat medium:\n    win:\n      renown +medium\n      say: You win, narrowly, on the wet boards. He laughs, bows, and sketches you mid-spell on the barge as it pulls away. The sketch arrives by post, flattering.\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      mood +small\n      say: He wins with an illusion of a second you. You both end up in the lake. It is, somehow, the best goodbye you've had.\n"},{"path":"content/events/jobs.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Job events — drawn while a job state is active.\n#  Jobs are taken at the Job Board (calendar.grim).\n# ============================================================\n\n# ---------------- Library Assistant ----------------\n\n@event lib_biting_book\ntitle: The Book That Bites\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrack: library_assistant\nweight: 14\nart: book\ntags: job\nscene: A thick leather book with tiny teeth along its pages snapping at a hand reaching for it on a high shelf.\ntext: Reshelving in the zoology section, a fat leather book clamps onto your finger and refuses to let go. The head librarian, without looking up, says: \"Stroke the spine.\"\n> left: Muzzle it with a belt\n  wage\n  health -tiny\n  say: You wrestle it into submission and shelve it. It growls at you every time you pass for the rest of the term.\n> right: Stroke the spine and read it\n  wage\n  lore +medium\n  health -tiny\n  say: It purrs, opens and turns out to be a fascinating treatise on carnivorous plants. It only bites twice more.\n\n@event lib_overdue_professor\ntitle: Overdue\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nwhen: met(thorne)\ntrack: library_assistant\nweight: 12\nart: hourglass\ntags: job\nscene: A severe professor towering over a library desk, a stack of books under one arm, a very small student assistant on the other side holding a fines ledger.\ntext: Professor Thorne returns a book that is eleven years overdue. The fine, by library rules, is forty crowns. He looks at you. You look at the ledger.\n> left: Waive the fine\n  wage\n  rel thorne +tiny\n  say: \"Hm,\" says Thorne. You can't tell if you've earned his respect or lost it.\n> right: Enforce the rules\n  wage\n  rel thorne +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: A long silence. Then he pays, in exact change. \"Good,\" he says, and you could swear he almost smiles.\n\n@event lib_restricted_cart\ntitle: The Restricted Cart\nclass: windfall\ntone: eerie\ntrack: library_assistant\nweight: 12\nart: lock\ntags: job mystery\nscene: A wheeled library cart chained shut, piled with black-bound books, parked alone in a candlelit aisle.\ntext: You are asked to wheel the Restricted Section returns to the vault. The cart's chain has come loose. One of the books has fallen open on a page that is writing itself.\n> left: Close it without looking\n  wage\n  study +tiny\n  say: The book snaps shut with a sound like a disappointed sigh. The vault door clangs.\n> right: Read a few lines\n  wage\n  lore +medium\n  chance 35:\n    gain umbral_curse\n    say: The words crawl off the page and up your sleeve. That night, you can't get warm.\n  else:\n    say: A spell for walking through mirrors. You will never try it. Probably.\n\n# ---------------- The Gilded Newt ----------------\n\n@event tavern_brawl\ntitle: Brawl at the Newt\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\ntrack: tavern\nweight: 14\nart: cup\ntags: job danger\nscene: A low-beamed tavern mid-brawl: a chair in mid-air, a dwarf on a table, cider sloshing everywhere.\ntext: A dwarf and a troll disagree about the price of turnips, and the Gilded Newt erupts. A chair flies past your head. The owner is hiding behind the bar, gesturing at you to do something.\n> left: Duck behind the bar too\n  wage\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You crouch beside the owner and share a bag of crisps while the room destroys itself.\n> right: Break it up\n  wage\n  challenge combat trivial:\n    win:\n      gold +medium\n      renown +small\n      say: You slam a tray on the bar and bellow. Silence. The troll apologises. The dwarf tips you fifteen crowns.\n    lose:\n      health -medium\n      say: You step in. Someone's elbow steps into your face.\n\n@event tavern_star_coin\ntitle: A Generous Stranger\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\ntrack: tavern\nweight: 12\nart: coin\ntags: job mystery hollowstar\nchain: hollowstar\nscene: A hooded figure at a corner table sliding a black coin across the wood, its surface stamped with a dark star.\ntext: A hooded stranger nurses a cider all evening and leaves a tip: a black coin stamped with a hollow star. \"For the student,\" they say. \"Tell Kael Dumont his family sends regards.\"\n> left: Refuse the coin\n  wage\n  say: The stranger shrugs and pockets it. When you look again, the corner table is empty and the cider is frozen solid.\n> right: Take it\n  wage\n  gold +tiny\n  flag star_coin\n  alignment -small\n  say: The coin is cold as ice and heavier than it should be. You find yourself turning it over in your pocket for days.\n\n@event tavern_extra_shifts\ntitle: More Shifts\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrack: tavern\nweight: 10\nart: coin\ntags: job money\nscene: A ruddy tavern owner leaning on the bar, wiping a mug, a rota pinned behind him covered in crossed-out names.\ntext: The owner is short-handed. \"Double shifts for a month,\" he says. \"Double pay. I know you've got studies, but you've also got rent.\"\n> left: Decline\n  wage\n  study +tiny\n  say: \"Fair enough,\" he says, and asks again next week.\n> right: Take the extra shifts\n  wage\n  wage overtime\n  health -medium\n  study -small\n  say: A month of late nights and early lectures. Your purse is heavy. So are your eyelids.\n\n# ---------------- Apothecary Clerk ----------------\n\n@event apoth_wrong_label\ntitle: A Mislabelled Jar\nclass: windfall\ntone: light\ntrack: apothecary_clerk\nweight: 14\nart: bottle\ntags: job\nscene: Shelves of glass jars in a narrow shop, one jar labelled SLEEPING DRAUGHT glowing an ominous green.\ntext: The jar labelled \"Sleeping Draught\" is green. Sleeping Draught is blue. You're fairly sure the green stuff is Draught of Unending Hiccups. Old Pell is very proud of his labelling.\n> left: Quietly fix the label\n  wage\n  alchemy +tiny\n  say: Nobody ever knows. Somewhere, a customer sleeps soundly instead of hiccupping for a week.\n> right: Point it out to Pell\n  wage\n  gold +tiny\n  alchemy +tiny\n  say: Pell goes red, then thanks you gruffly and slips you three crowns \"for your eyes\".\n\n@event apoth_poor_mother\ntitle: Can't Afford It\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrack: apothecary_clerk\nweight: 12\nart: potion\ntags: job\nscene: A tired woman at a shop counter counting copper coins, a small child coughing beside her.\ntext: A woman counts out coppers for fever tonic. She's fifteen crowns short. Her son coughs into her coat. Pell is in the back room.\n> left: Charge the full price\n  wage\n  alignment -small\n  gold +tiny\n  mood -small\n  say: She leaves without it. The bell on the door rings for a very long time.\n> right: Pay the difference yourself\n  wage\n  alignment +small\n  gold -medium\n  mood +medium\n  renown +small\n  say: You slip the tonic into her basket. Next week a pie appears on the counter with your name on it.\n\n@event apoth_nightshade_order\ntitle: The Nightshade Order\nclass: windfall\ntone: eerie\ntrack: apothecary_clerk\nweight: 10\nart: skull\ntags: job mystery\nscene: An order slip on a counter listing a dozen deadly reagents, signed with an elegant, illegible flourish.\ntext: A note arrives with a sealed purse: a large order of nightshade, grave-moss and powdered moonstone. Legal, technically. Ingredients for something very old and very forbidden, also technically.\n> left: Fill the order\n  wage\n  gold +medium\n  flag filled_dark_order\n  say: The courier who collects it never shows his face. The purse is very generous.\n> right: Refuse and report it\n  wage\n  rel isolde +small\n  renown +small\n  say: The headmistress listens to your report in silence. \"Thank you,\" she says. \"Tell no one else.\" She looks worried.\n\n# ---------------- Familiar Stables ----------------\n\n@event stables_hippogriff\ntitle: Bow to the Hippogriff\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nplace: stables\nweight: 14\nart: feather\ntags: job nature\nscene: A proud hippogriff in a straw-strewn stall, head lowered, regarding a stable hand who is bowing very deeply.\ntext: The new hippogriff won't let anyone near the feed trough. The stablemaster says to bow, keep eye contact and not blink. \"Whatever you do, don't blink.\"\n> left: Leave the feed and run\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You toss the feed bucket and flee. The hippogriff eats it, and the bucket.\n> right: Bow and hold its gaze\n  challenge feat trivial:\n    win:\n      vigor +small\n      mood +small\n      say: It bows back. After that it follows you around the stables like a large, feathered dog.\n    lose:\n      health -medium\n      say: You blinked.\n\n@event stables_dragon_egg\ntitle: An Egg in the Hay\nclass: windfall\ntone: eerie\nwhen: not flag(dragon_egg)\nplace: stables\nweight: 10\nart: dragon\ntags: job mystery\nscene: A speckled egg the size of a melon nestled in hay, steam rising from it, a stable hand's lantern held over it.\ntext: Mucking out the far stall, you find an egg the size of a melon, nestled in the hay and steaming gently. It's very warm. It's very definitely not a chicken's.\n> left: Report it to Professor Brask\n  renown +small\n  rel maud +tiny\n  say: Brask nearly faints. \"Smugglers! A firedrake egg!\" It's taken to the menagerie. You get a commendation.\n> right: Keep it\n  alignment -tiny\n  flag dragon_egg\n  queue egg_hatches in 3\n  say: You smuggle it back to your dormitory wrapped in a scarf. It purrs. Eggs shouldn't purr.\n\n@event egg_hatches\ntitle: It Hatches\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\ntrigger: queued\nart: dragon\ntags: danger mystery\nscene: A tiny red dragon perched on a scorched dormitory pillow, a puff of smoke rising from its nostrils, a horrified roommate in the doorway.\ntext: At 3am your pillow catches fire. The egg has hatched: a firedrake the size of a kitten, delighted with itself and setting fire to everything it loves. It loves you very much.\n> left: Hand it over to the menagerie\n  mood -tiny\n  renown +small\n  say: Brask takes it gently. \"You'll be able to visit.\" You do, every week. It always tries to set you on fire, affectionately.\n> right: Hide it as long as you can\n  challenge deceive trivial:\n    win:\n      mood +medium\n      renown +medium\n      gold -medium\n      say: You keep it secret for a month — buying fireproof sheets, raw meat, an asbestos sock — before releasing it in the mountains. It circles once, roaring.\n    lose:\n      gain detention\n      study -medium\n      health -small\n      say: The smoke alarm charm goes off in three towers at once. Discovered, singed, and in very deep trouble.\n\n@event stables_night_shift\ntitle: The Sick Pegasus\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nplace: stables\nweight: 12\nart: moon\ntags: job nature\nscene: A white pegasus lying in a moonlit stall, wings folded, a student sitting beside it with a lantern and a blanket.\ntext: The oldest pegasus in the stables is sick. Someone has to sit with her overnight, keep her warm, and make sure she drinks. It's unpaid. Old Maud says she'll do it if no one else will. Maud is eighty.\n> left: Let Maud do it\n  say: You sleep well. Maud is a little slower the next day.\n> right: Take the night shift\n  health -tiny\n  mood +small\n  rel maud +small\n  say: She drinks. You talk to her all night about home. At dawn she stands, shakes her wings, and nuzzles your hair.\n\n# ---------------- First-Year Tutor ----------------\n\n@event tutor_hopeless\ntitle: The Hopeless Case\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nplace: courtyard\nweight: 14\nart: wand\ntags: job\nscene: A tiny first-year in oversized robes pointing a wand at a feather, which is stubbornly not moving, while a tutor watches with infinite patience.\ntext: Your tutee has been trying to levitate a feather for three weeks. The feather has not moved. The tutee is starting to cry. You have one more session before their exam.\n> left: Drill the basics, again\n  study +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: Wrist, word, will. Wrist, word, will. On the exam, the feather twitches. It's enough to pass.\n> right: Try something unorthodox\n  challenge negotiate trivial:\n    win:\n      mood +medium\n      renown +small\n      say: You tell them to imagine the feather is in love with the ceiling. It shoots upward and sticks there. They pass with distinction.\n    lose:\n      mood -tiny\n      say: You tell them to imagine the feather is in love with the ceiling. They are confused. So is the feather.\n\n@event tutor_bribe\ntitle: An Indecent Proposal\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nplace: courtyard\nweight: 12\nart: pouch\ntags: job money\nscene: A rich student in tailored robes sliding a heavy coin purse across a study table, smiling smugly.\ntext: A rich second-year doesn't want tutoring. He wants an essay. \"Thirty crowns,\" he says, dropping a purse on the table. \"Nobody needs to know.\"\n> left: Refuse\n  study +tiny\n  rel isolde +tiny\n  say: He sneers and goes elsewhere. Later you see him in detention, scrubbing a cauldron.\n> right: Write the essay\n  gold +large\n  chance 35:\n    study -large\n    gain detention\n    say: His professor recognises your style. You both end up in detention. He doesn't speak to you. Fine.\n  else:\n    guile +tiny\n    say: He gets a decent mark. You get thirty crowns and a slightly sour feeling.\n\n@event tutor_prodigy\ntitle: Surpassed\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nplace: courtyard\nweight: 10\nart: sparkle\ntags: job\nscene: A small first-year casting a dazzling spell that fills a classroom with light, their tutor watching with complicated feelings.\ntext: Your newest tutee casts in a week what took you a year. Then she asks you a question you can't answer. She's eleven.\n> left: Bluff an answer\n  guile +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: She nods politely. You both know.\n> right: Admit you don't know and find out together\n  health -tiny\n  lore +small\n  mood +small\n  say: You spend an evening in the library together. She thanks you as if you've taught her something. Maybe you have.\n\n# ---------------- Broom Courier ----------------\n\n@event courier_storm\ntitle: Delivery in a Storm\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\ntrack: broom_courier\nweight: 14\nart: bolt\ntags: job danger\nscene: A courier on a broom bent low against a lightning-lit sky, a parcel strapped to their back, rain sheeting sideways.\ntext: An urgent parcel needs to cross the lake tonight. There's a storm. The dispatcher offers double pay to whoever's fool enough. Everyone looks at you.\n> left: Wait for the storm to pass\n  wage\n  gold -tiny\n  say: The parcel arrives late. The client deducts it from your wages. Rude, but survivable.\n> right: Fly through it\n  wage\n  challenge feat medium:\n    win:\n      gold +large\n      vigor +small\n      say: You thread the lightning like a needle and land soaked, laughing, with the parcel dry.\n    lose:\n      health -large\n      gain injured\n      say: Lightning strikes close enough to stand your hair on end. You lose the broom and gain a limp.\n\n@event courier_whispering_parcel\ntitle: The Whispering Parcel\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\ntrack: broom_courier\nweight: 10\nart: letter\ntags: job mystery\nscene: A brown paper parcel tied with black string on a courier's satchel, frost forming on its corners, faint whispers rising from it.\ntext: Your parcel is addressed to \"K. Dumont, Stillwater House\". It is ice cold and it whispers when you aren't looking at it.\n> left: Deliver it unopened\n  wage\n  rel kael +small\n  meet kael\n  say: Kael takes it without a word. His hands shake. The next day, he looks like he hasn't slept.\n> right: Peek inside\n  wage\n  challenge deceive easy:\n    win:\n      flag read_kael_parcel\n      alignment -tiny\n      lore +small\n      say: Inside: a letter in a cold, beautiful hand. \"The Star wakes. Come home, or open the door. Your family.\" You reseal it perfectly.\n    lose:\n      rel kael -medium\n      meet kael\n      say: The seal hisses as it breaks. Kael is waiting at the door when you arrive. He saw. He says nothing, which is worse.\n\n@event courier_sky_pirates\ntitle: Sky Pirates\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\ntrack: broom_courier\nweight: 10\nart: broom\ntags: job danger\nscene: Three ragged figures on patched brooms surrounding a courier above the clouds, cutlasses glinting.\ntext: Three figures on patched brooms drop out of a cloud and box you in. \"Toll,\" says their leader, a woman with a parrot that is also on a tiny broom. \"Eight crowns or the parcel.\"\n> left: Pay the toll\n  wage\n  gold -small\n  say: They let you pass with a salute. The parrot salutes too.\n> right: Outfly them\n  wage\n  challenge feat medium:\n    win:\n      renown +small\n      gold +tiny\n      say: You dive through a cloudbank and lose them over the lake. The client tips extra for the excitement.\n    lose:\n      health -medium\n      gold -medium\n      say: They catch you, take the toll, the parcel and your hat. The parrot wears it.\n"},{"path":"content/events/life.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Academy life — the random pool. Small moments that fill the\n#  month: weather, food, money, mischief, mishaps.\n#  Seasonal ones use `month` (1 Harvestmoon ... 12 Starfall).\n# ============================================================\n\n# ---------------- Around the castle ----------------\n\n@event life_staircase\ntitle: The Staircase Moves\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nart: tower\ntags: magic\nscene: A grand spiral staircase in mid-rotation, a student clinging to the banister as the steps swing toward a different floor.\ntext: Halfway up the west staircase, it decides to be somewhere else. You're now heading toward a floor you've never seen, at speed, and you're late for Arcana.\n> left: Hold on and see where it goes\n  lore +tiny\n  chance 50:\n    gold +small\n    say: It deposits you in a dusty forgotten classroom. In a desk: eight old crowns and a love letter from 1802.\n  else:\n    study -tiny\n    say: It takes you to the roof. Then the cellar. Then the roof again. You miss Arcana entirely.\n> right: Jump for the landing\n  challenge feat trivial:\n    win:\n      vigor +tiny\n      say: You leap and land neatly. A passing portrait applauds.\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      say: You leap and land, but not neatly. A passing portrait winces.\n\n@event life_mystery_stew\ntime: evening\ntitle: Mystery Stew\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nart: meal\ntags: home\nscene: A huge cauldron of grey-green stew in the dining hall, a ladle standing upright in it unaided.\ntext: Tonight's dinner is described on the board only as \"Stew\". It is grey-green. The ladle is standing up by itself. The cook is watching you with hope.\n> left: Eat it bravely\n  chance 60:\n    health +small\n    mood +small\n    say: It's... delicious? Rich, peppery, faintly glowing. You go back for seconds. The cook beams.\n  else:\n    health -medium\n    say: It's not delicious. It's not anything a stomach should know about. You spend the night regretting it.\n> right: Toast and jam again\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You eat toast. The cook looks hurt. You feel guilty all evening.\n\n@event life_lost_wand\ntitle: Where Is Your Wand?\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nart: wand\ntags: magic\nscene: A dormitory turned upside down, clothes everywhere, a student on hands and knees peering under a bed.\ntext: Your wand is gone. It was on your desk. Now it isn't. Classes start in twenty minutes.\n> left: Tear the room apart\n  chance 70:\n    say: It's in your boot. Why is it in your boot? You make it to class with seconds to spare.\n  else:\n    study -small\n    mood -tiny\n    say: You find it at lunch, inside the pillowcase. You've missed a whole morning.\n> right: Try a summoning charm\n  challenge spell trivial:\n    win:\n      arcana +tiny\n      say: It flies into your hand from inside the wardrobe, offended at having been lost.\n    lose:\n      mana -tiny\n      say: Every pencil in the dormitory flies at your face. The wand stays hidden out of spite.\n\n@event life_snoring_roommate\ntitle: The Snorer\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nart: bed\ntags: home\nscene: A dark dormitory, one bed shaking with snores so loud the curtains flutter, another occupant glaring with a pillow over their head.\ntext: Someone in your dormitory snores like a dragon with a head cold, and nobody will admit to it. You haven't slept properly in a week.\n> left: Brew a silencing draught\n  challenge brew trivial:\n    win:\n      health +small\n      alchemy +tiny\n      say: Perfect silence. Too perfect. The culprit, the quiet one by the window, is now silent even when awake. It wears off by Tuesday.\n    lose:\n      health -tiny\n      say: The draught finds the wrong bed. Now two people snore, in harmony.\n> right: Move to the common room sofa\n  health -tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  charm +tiny\n  say: You sleep on the sofa and meet everyone who comes down at 3am. Some of them are very interesting.\n\n@event life_portrait_gossip\ntitle: The Portraits Talk\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nart: mirror\ntags: mystery\nscene: A long gallery of gilded portraits, painted figures leaning out of their frames toward each other, whispering behind painted hands.\ntext: Two portraits in the east gallery are gossiping about a student. Then one of them says your name and they both go suspiciously quiet.\n> left: Bribe them with flattery\n  challenge negotiate trivial:\n    win:\n      guile +tiny\n      lore +tiny\n      say: Ten minutes of compliments on their brushwork, and they tell you everything. Gossip about half the faculty, too.\n    lose:\n      mood -tiny\n      say: \"Flattery,\" sniffs the duchess, \"is so common.\" They turn their backs. Literally.\n> right: Pretend you didn't hear\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You walk on. Behind you, the whispering resumes, louder.\n\n@event life_pixies\ntitle: Pixie Infestation\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nart: sparkle\ntags: danger\nscene: A dormitory full of tiny blue winged creatures swinging from curtains, stealing socks and pulling hair.\ntext: Someone left a window open and the dormitory is now full of pixies. They're stealing socks, unscrewing bedposts and laughing in very high voices.\n> left: Fight them off\n  challenge combat trivial:\n    win:\n      vigor +tiny\n      renown +small\n      say: You catch nine with a laundry bag. The dorm cheers. The pixies swear at you, colourfully.\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      say: You lose. The pixies hang you from the chandelier by your robe. It takes an hour to get down.\n> right: Bribe them with sugar\n  gold -tiny\n  mood +small\n  say: You lay a trail of sugar cubes out of the window. The pixies follow it, munching, and leave one sock as a tip.\n\n@event life_gargoyle\ntitle: A Talkative Gargoyle\nclass: windfall\ntone: light\nweight: 5\nart: tower\ntags: magic\nscene: A weathered stone gargoyle on a parapet, one eye open, mouth moving, a student sitting beside it with a sandwich.\ntext: The gargoyle above the east door clears its throat as you pass. \"You,\" it rumbles. \"You look like someone who'd listen. Nobody listens to gargoyles.\"\n> left: Hurry along\n  say: \"Typical,\" says the gargoyle. It sulks until spring.\n> right: Sit and listen\n  lore +small\n  mood +tiny\n  say: It tells you three hundred years of academy gossip, including where the founders hid the good biscuits. You find the biscuits.\n\n@event life_brownies\ntime: morning\ntitle: Help in the Night\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nart: house\ntags: magic home\nscene: Tiny bearded figures in aprons tidying a cluttered student desk by moonlight, one darning a sock.\ntext: You wake to find your desk tidied, your socks darned and your essay proofread. Brownies. They expect something in return. Traditionally: cream.\n> left: Leave out a bowl of cream\n  gold -tiny\n  mood +small\n  study +tiny\n  say: Your room stays spotless all term. Your essays have suspiciously good spelling.\n> right: Leave them nothing\n  alignment -tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: The next morning, every one of your socks is tied in knots. Brownies keep score.\n\n@event life_midnight_snack\ntime: night\ntitle: Midnight Hunger\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nart: meal\ntags: home\nscene: A student in pyjamas tiptoeing past a sleeping prefect toward a door marked KITCHENS, a candle cupped in their hand.\ntext: It's midnight and you're starving. The kitchens are three floors down, past a prefect's post.\n> left: Go back to bed hungry\n  health -tiny\n  say: You dream of sandwiches. Long, detailed, beautiful sandwiches.\n> right: Sneak down\n  challenge deceive trivial:\n    win:\n      health +small\n      mood +small\n      say: Cold pie, a pot of custard, a heel of bread. Feast of kings.\n    lose:\n      gain detention\n      say: The prefect is awake. You spend a week washing the pots you were trying to raid.\n\n@event life_insomnia\ntime: night\ntitle: The Ceiling at 3am\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nart: moon\ntags: home\nscene: A student lying awake staring at a ceiling where faint painted stars have started slowly moving.\ntext: You can't sleep. Your brain has decided to replay every embarrassing thing you've said since you were six.\n> left: Get up and study\n  lore +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: By dawn you've read three chapters and remembered one of them.\n> right: Brew chamomile and count the painted stars\n  gold -tiny\n  health +tiny\n  mood +tiny\n  say: Halfway through counting, the stars start counting back. Then you're asleep.\n\n@event life_morning_run\ntime: morning\ntitle: The Dawn Run\nclass: windfall\ntone: light\nart: sun\ntags: nature\nwhen: month <= 3 or month >= 7\nweight: 5\nscene: The lakeshore path at dawn, mist on the water, a lone runner's breath steaming, the towers catching the first gold light.\ntext: A group of students runs the lakeshore every dawn. They've invited you. It's very early. It's very cold.\n> left: Sleep in\n  mood +small\n  say: You hear them come back, glowing and smug. You are warm and unrepentant.\n> right: Run with them\n  vigor +small\n  health +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You nearly die at the first mile and feel immortal by the third. The lake shines like a coin. You feel every step of it all through your lectures.\n\n@event life_homework_mountain\ntitle: The Pile\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nart: scroll\ntags: study\nscene: A desk buried under a precarious tower of scrolls and books, a student peeking hopelessly over the top.\ntext: Three essays, a potion log, a star chart and a reading list the length of your arm. All due this week. The pile has achieved its own weather.\n> left: Grind through it\n  study +small\n  health -small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You finish at 4am on the last day. Every piece is handed in. You sleep through breakfast.\n> right: Pick what matters, skip the rest\n  renown -tiny\n  gain slacking\n  mood +small\n  say: You hand in two of five and go for a walk. The professors are not pleased. You feel great, temporarily.\n\n@event life_stolen_answers\ntitle: The Answers\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nart: scroll\ntags: study danger\nwhen: month == 3 or month == 4 or month == 9 or month == 10\nscene: A folded paper being passed under a library table, the faint glow of an examiner's seal on it.\ntext: A third-year is selling copies of next week's exam. Eight crowns. \"Guaranteed real,\" he whispers. \"Straight from the examiner's desk.\"\n> left: Walk away\n  alignment +tiny\n  mood +tiny\n  say: Two weeks later, five students who bought copies are suspended. The answers were fake.\n> right: Buy a copy\n  alignment -small\n  gold -small\n  chance 40:\n    study +large\n    guile +tiny\n    say: They're real. You ace it. You also can't quite look the examiner in the eye.\n  else:\n    study -medium\n    gain detention\n    say: They're fake, and planted. You're caught with a copy. Detention, and a very cold letter home.\n\n@event life_backfire\ntitle: Spell Backfire\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nart: sparkle\ntags: magic danger\nscene: A student mid-hiccup, a small firework bursting from their mouth, classmates ducking.\ntext: You mispronounce a minor cheering charm in the corridor. Something goes wrong. Every time you hiccup, a tiny firework comes out.\n> left: Go to the infirmary\n  gold -tiny\n  say: Sister Amaranth cures it in a minute and charges you three crowns. \"For the ceiling,\" she says.\n> right: Live with it\n  gain hiccup_hex\n  renown +small\n  say: You become briefly famous as \"the sparkler\". It's not the fame you wanted.\n\n@event life_lake_swim\ntitle: A Swim in the Lake\nclass: story\ntone: light\nart: wave\ntags: nature\nwhen: month >= 9 or month <= 1\nscene: Students leaping off a wooden jetty into a sparkling lake on a hot afternoon, something large and dark moving far beneath the surface.\ntext: It's hot and the whole year has gone down to the jetty. Something large is rumoured to live in the deep part. People are swimming anyway.\n> left: Stay on the jetty\n  mood +tiny\n  say: You dangle your feet and eat cherries. Something brushes your toe. You keep your feet up after that.\n> right: Dive in\n  health +small\n  mood +medium\n  chance 20:\n    flag lake_knows\n    say: In the deep part, you open your eyes underwater and see a round door, far below, glowing. Then you need air.\n  else:\n    say: The water's perfect. Something large and friendly swims under you, and you decide not to panic.\n\n@event life_stray_cat\ntitle: A Cat Moves In\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nart: cat\ntags: home\nscene: A scruffy black cat sitting on a dormitory pillow as if it has always lived there, one torn ear, very green eyes.\ntext: A black cat with one torn ear is sitting on your pillow. It looks at you as though you are the one trespassing.\n> left: Shoo it out\n  mood -tiny\n  say: It leaves with enormous dignity and moves in with your neighbour, who spoils it rotten.\n> right: Let it stay\n  mood +small\n  gold -tiny\n  say: It's called Professor now. It sleeps on your homework. You've never been happier.\n\n@event life_storm_night\ntitle: The Lights Go Out\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nart: bolt\ntags: mystery\nwhen: month >= 6 and month <= 8\nscene: A dark common room lit only by lightning, students huddled under blankets, one telling a ghost story.\ntext: A storm knocks out every light-crystal in the dormitory. The common room fills with students, blankets and somebody's terrible ghost story.\n> left: Tell an even scarier story\n  challenge negotiate trivial:\n    win:\n      renown +small\n      mood +small\n      say: Your story is so good that three people sleep with the lights on for a week. You're a legend.\n    lose:\n      mood +tiny\n      say: Your story is not scary. It is, however, accidentally hilarious.\n> right: Light the room with a spell\n  challenge spell trivial:\n    win:\n      renown +small\n      arcana +tiny\n      say: You fill the room with soft floating lights. Someone starts singing. It turns into the best night of term.\n    lose:\n      health -tiny\n      say: You light the sofa. Briefly. The storm seems less frightening after that.\n\n@event life_mirror_talks\ntime: morning\ntitle: The Honest Mirror\nclass: windfall\ntone: light\nweight: 5\nart: mirror\ntags: magic\nscene: A bathroom mirror with a stern painted face in its silvering, frowning at a student mid-toothbrush.\ntext: The bathroom mirror has started commenting on your appearance. \"Hair,\" it says this morning. Just: \"Hair.\"\n> left: Ask for its advice\n  charm +small\n  say: It turns out to be an excellent stylist. You look great. You hate that it's right.\n> right: Cover it with a towel\n  mood +tiny\n  say: It sulks under the towel. Muffled: \"Hair.\"\n\n@event life_prank_war\ntitle: Prank War\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nart: mask\ntags: social\nscene: A corridor ankle-deep in enchanted soap bubbles, two dormitories' worth of students hurling them at each other.\ntext: The dormitory across the corridor turned your door into a goat. The goat is fine. Honour, however, demands retaliation.\n> left: Rise above it\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You take the high road. The goat eats your homework. You let it.\n> right: Retaliate\n  challenge deceive trivial:\n    win:\n      renown +small\n      mood +small\n      say: You fill their dorm with a thousand singing bubbles. They concede by dawn.\n    lose:\n      gain detention\n      renown +tiny\n      say: You're caught carrying a crate of bubbles. Detention, but the goat seems proud of you.\n\n@event life_mail_owl\ntitle: The Wrong Letter\nclass: windfall\ntone: eerie\nweight: 5\nart: letter\ntags: mystery\nscene: A ruffled delivery owl dropping a scented pink envelope onto a breakfast plate, a student looking up in confusion.\ntext: A delivery owl drops a scented pink letter in your porridge. It's addressed to someone else. It's a love letter. It's very, very passionate.\n> left: Deliver it to its owner\n  charm +tiny\n  renown +tiny\n  say: The recipient goes crimson and thanks you. Two weeks later, they're holding hands with the sender in the dining hall.\n> right: Read it first\n  alignment -tiny\n  guile +tiny\n  mood +small\n  say: You read it. You will never be able to look the potions professor in the eye again.\n\n# ---------------- Money ----------------\n\n@event life_money_from_home\ntitle: A Parcel From Home\nclass: windfall\ntone: warm\nart: pouch\ntags: home money\nwhen: turn >= 2\nweight: 5\nscene: A brown paper parcel tied with string on a dormitory bed, a knitted scarf, a jar of jam and a small coin purse spilling out.\ntext: A parcel from home: a knitted scarf, a jar of jam, a note telling you to eat vegetables, and a small purse of coins.\n> left: Save the money\n  gold +medium\n  mood +small\n  say: You tuck it away. The jam lasts six days.\n> right: Spend it on a treat for your friends\n  gold +tiny\n  mood +medium\n  charm +tiny\n  say: Cake from the village for everyone. You're popular for an entire afternoon.\n\n@event life_found_purse\ntitle: A Purse in the Grass\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nart: pouch\ntags: money\nscene: A velvet purse lying in the long grass beside a path, coins glinting at its open mouth, initials embroidered on it.\ntext: A fat velvet purse lies in the grass by the path. Thirty crowns at least. Embroidered initials: A.T.\n> left: Return it to its owner\n  alignment +small\n  rel thorne +small\n  say: A.T. turns out to be Aldric Thorne. He takes the purse and stares at you a long moment. \"Honest,\" he says. It sounds like a diagnosis.\n> right: Keep it\n  alignment -small\n  gold +large\n  guile +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: Thirty crowns richer. A few days later, Thorne mentions in class that he lost something. He looks at no one in particular.\n\n@event life_peddler\ntitle: The Travelling Peddler\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nart: lantern\ntags: money magic\nscene: A gaudy painted wagon at the academy gates, hung with lanterns and trinkets, a peddler with a gold tooth grinning.\ntext: A peddler's wagon has parked by the gates. \"Lucky charms! Genuine article! Thirty crowns, and fortune follows you for a season!\"\n> left: Buy a lucky charm\n  when: gold >= 25\n  hint: 30 gold\n  gold -large\n  chance 50:\n    gain inspired\n    mood +small\n    say: It's a real one. Things go right all month. You start talking to it.\n  else:\n    mood -tiny\n    say: It turns your neck green. Fortune does not follow you. The peddler is long gone.\n> right: Just browse\n  mood +tiny\n  say: You buy nothing and enjoy a sugared apple and the peddler's outrageous stories.\n\n@event life_bookshop\ntitle: Sale at Grimsby's\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nart: book\ntags: money study\nscene: A crooked bookshop with books stacked to the ceiling, a hand-painted sign in the window reading HALF OFF, MOSTLY.\ntext: Grimsby's Books is having a sale. An old copy of \"Advanced Arcane Theory\" for fifteen crowns. Normally forty. It's annotated, apparently, by a famous wizard.\n> left: Buy it\n  when: gold >= 20\n  hint: 15 gold\n  gold -medium\n  lore +medium\n  arcana +small\n  say: The annotations are brilliant, rude and occasionally about the author's divorce. You learn a great deal.\n> right: Leave it\n  say: You walk away. You think about it for weeks.\n\n@event life_new_robes\ntitle: Shabby Robes\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nart: crown\ntags: money social\nwhen: renown >= 10\nscene: A student in a hall mirror holding up a fraying sleeve, a tailor's flyer in their other hand.\ntext: Your robes are fraying at every hem. Someone at dinner called them \"vintage\". It was not a compliment.\n> left: Patch them yourself\n  mood -tiny\n  charm +tiny\n  say: Your stitching is crooked but cheerful. Some people start copying it.\n> right: Buy new robes (30 gold)\n  when: gold >= 30\n  hint: 30 gold\n  gold -large\n  renown +small\n  charm +small\n  say: Midnight-blue wool, silver trim. People actually turn to look. You walk differently all week.\n\n@event life_card_game\ntime: night\ntitle: A Game of Wizard's Hand\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nart: cards\ntags: money social\nscene: A smoky corner of the common room, students around a low table with cards and coins, one card floating and changing face.\ntext: A late-night game of Wizard's Hand. The cards change faces when no one's looking. The pot is thirty crowns. The upper-years look hungry.\n> left: Play\n  challenge deceive easy:\n    win:\n      gold +large\n      say: You read every bluff and keep a straight face. Thirty crowns and a reputation.\n    lose:\n      gold -medium\n      say: You lose fifteen crowns and the upper-years' respect, in that order.\n> right: Watch and learn\n  guile +tiny\n  say: You learn more watching than you would have playing. Mostly about who cheats.\n\n@event life_essay_prize\ntitle: The Essay Prize\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nart: feather\ntags: study money\nscene: A brass plaque on a noticeboard announcing THE LUMEN ESSAY PRIZE — 50 CROWNS, a quill pinned beneath it.\ntext: The Lumen Essay Prize: thirty crowns for the best essay on \"Magic and Duty\". The deadline is Friday. You have an idea already.\n> left: Enter\n  challenge research easy:\n    win:\n      gold +large\n      renown +medium\n      say: You win. Your essay is read aloud at dinner. You don't know where to look.\n    lose:\n      lore +tiny\n      say: You don't win, but the judges' notes are glowing. Next year, maybe.\n> right: Skip it\n  say: The winner reads their essay aloud at dinner. Yours would have been better. Probably.\n\n@event life_charity\ntitle: The Winter Fund\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nart: coin\ntags: money social\nwhen: month >= 3 and month <= 5\nscene: A collection jar shaped like a lantern on the dining hall table, half full of coins, a hand-lettered sign: FOR THE VILLAGE WINTER FUND.\ntext: The village winter fund is collecting for families who can't afford fuel this winter. The jar is half full.\n> left: Donate fifteen crowns\n  when: gold >= 20\n  hint: 15 gold\n  alignment +small\n  gold -medium\n  renown +small\n  mood +small\n  say: Your name goes on a little list by the jar. You feel warm, which is the point.\n> right: Walk past\n  alignment -tiny\n  say: You walk past. The jar fills anyway. You think about it on cold nights.\n\n@event life_shady_offer\ntime: afternoon evening\ntitle: A Quiet Job\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nart: mask\ntags: money danger\nscene: A hooded figure in an alley behind the village tavern holding out a sealed envelope, a purse visible in their other hand.\ntext: A hooded figure behind the tavern: \"Deliver this envelope to the boathouse at midnight. Don't open it. Thirty crowns.\" The envelope is warm.\n> left: Refuse\n  say: The figure melts into the dark. The next morning, someone's boat is missing.\n> right: Do the job\n  gold +large\n  chance 40:\n    gain watched\n    say: You make the drop. Bram is watching from the boathouse window. He writes something down.\n  else:\n    guile +tiny\n    say: You make the drop. Nobody sees. Thirty crowns, and a slightly cold feeling in your stomach.\n\n# ---------------- Body & mind ----------------\n\n@event life_food_poisoning\ntime: evening night\ntitle: The Fish Was Off\nclass: incident\ntone: grim\nart: meal\ntags: crisis\nscene: A student curled on a dormitory bed clutching their stomach, a half-eaten fish pie on the bedside table.\ntext: The fish pie at dinner was a mistake. Your stomach is staging a revolution.\n> left: Ride it out\n  health -medium\n  mood -tiny\n  say: A miserable night. You never look at fish pie again.\n> right: Get a tonic from the infirmary\n  gold -tiny\n  health -tiny\n  say: The tonic tastes like old pennies, but it works.\n\n@event life_self_doubt\ntitle: Imposter\nclass: windfall\ntone: warm\nweight: 5\nart: mirror\ntags: home\nscene: A student sitting alone on the edge of a fountain at night, staring at their reflection broken up by ripples.\ntext: Everyone around you seems to belong here more than you do. They cast faster, learn quicker, laugh easier. Maybe the letter was a mistake.\n> left: Write down everything you've learned\n  mood +medium\n  lore +tiny\n  say: The list is longer than you expected. Much longer. You pin it above your bed.\n> right: Talk to someone about it\n  if bonds(friend) + bonds(close) + bonds(partner) > 0:\n    mood +large\n    say: Your friend laughs — not at you. \"You? Everyone thinks you're the one who belongs.\" It helps more than you can say.\n  else:\n    mood +small\n    say: You talk to the night porter. He tells you everyone feels this way. You decide to believe him.\n\n@event life_sleepwalking\ntime: night\ntitle: Sleepwalking\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\nart: moon\ntags: mystery\nscene: A student in pyjamas standing on a moonlit battlement, eyes closed, one foot raised over the edge, an owl watching.\ntext: You wake up on the battlements in your pyjamas, at the very edge, with no memory of how you got there. Your feet are wet with lake water.\n> left: Tell Sister Amaranth\n  gold -tiny\n  health +tiny\n  say: A sleeping draught and a stern warning. It doesn't happen again.\n> right: Keep it to yourself\n  mood -tiny\n  chance 40:\n    flag lake_knows\n    say: It happens twice more. Each time you're facing the lake. Each time, you're a little closer to the water.\n  else:\n    say: It doesn't happen again. You start locking your door from the inside, just in case.\n\n@event life_quiet_afternoon\nslot: afternoon\ntitle: A Quiet Afternoon\nclass: windfall\ntone: light\nweight: 5\nart: leaf\ntags: nature\nscene: A student lying in the long grass under an oak tree, a book open on their chest, clouds drifting above the towers.\ntext: For once, nothing is due, nobody needs you, and the sun is out. A whole afternoon, unclaimed.\n> left: Nap under the oak tree\n  health +medium\n  mood +small\n  say: You wake to find a bird sitting on your book. It seems content. So are you.\n> right: Practise spells in the meadow\n  arcana +small\n  mood +tiny\n  say: You make dandelion seeds dance in spirals for an hour. Nobody sees. It's perfect.\n\n@event life_injury_stairs\ntitle: The Trick Step\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nart: cross\ntags: danger\nscene: A stone staircase with one step glowing faintly, a student mid-fall, books flying everywhere.\ntext: The ninth step on the north stair is a trick step. Everyone knows. You forgot. Your leg sinks to the knee and the stair holds on.\n> left: Wait for someone to help\n  mood -tiny\n  study -tiny\n  say: Forty minutes later, a first-year pulls you out. You're late for everything.\n> right: Wrench yourself free\n  challenge feat trivial:\n    win:\n      vigor +tiny\n      say: You pull free with a pop. Your shoe stays in the stair forever.\n    lose:\n      gain injured\n      health -small\n      say: Something in your knee goes click. Not a good click.\n\n@event life_lake_fever\ntitle: Lake Fever Season\nclass: windfall\ntone: grim\nart: rain\ntags: crisis\nwhen: month >= 3 and month <= 6\nweight: 5\nscene: A dormitory where half the beds hold sniffling students with faintly blue-glowing noses, tissues everywhere.\ntext: Half your dormitory has Lake Fever. Noses are glowing blue. You feel a tickle in your throat.\n> left: Dose up on Maud's tea\n  if met(maud):\n    health +small\n    say: Maud's lemon-and-thunder tea keeps you upright through the whole outbreak.\n  else:\n    chance 50:\n      gain sick\n      say: The tea from the kitchens isn't the same. Your nose starts glowing on Thursday.\n    else:\n      say: You dodge it. Somehow.\n> right: Keep going as normal\n  chance 60:\n    gain sick\n    say: You keep going as normal for exactly two days.\n  else:\n    vigor +tiny\n    say: Iron constitution. You sail through. Everyone hates you a little.\n\n# ---------------- Seasons ----------------\n\n@event life_first_snow\ntitle: First Snow\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nart: snow\ntags: nature festival\nwhen: month == 4 or month == 5\nscene: The academy courtyard blanketed in fresh snow, students mid-snowball fight, a snowman wearing a professor's hat.\ntext: The first snow of winter falls overnight and the whole academy wakes up giddy. Classes are optional, officially. Unofficially, they're cancelled by snowball.\n> left: Join the snowball war\n  mood +medium\n  vigor +tiny\n  chance 30:\n    health -tiny\n    say: You take a snowball to the face from the headmistress herself. Her aim is terrifying.\n  else:\n    say: Your side wins. You build a snow-fort that lasts until Thawmoon.\n> right: Hot chocolate by the fire\n  gold -tiny\n  mood +small\n  health +tiny\n  say: You watch from the window, warm and smug, while the snowballs fly.\n\n@event life_spring_cleaning\ntitle: Spring Cleaning\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nart: broom\ntags: home\nwhen: month == 7 or month == 8\nscene: A dormitory with every window thrown open, brooms sweeping by themselves, dust swirling in sunbeams.\ntext: Spring cleaning. The brooms are enchanted, but the enchantment needs supervising. Your dorm's brooms have formed a union.\n> left: Negotiate with the brooms\n  challenge negotiate trivial:\n    win:\n      mood +small\n      say: You agree to one day off a week and a polish. The dorm sparkles.\n    lose:\n      health -tiny\n      say: The brooms do not accept your terms. They sweep you into a cupboard.\n> right: Clean by hand\n  health -tiny\n  vigor +tiny\n  mood +tiny\n  say: An afternoon of scrubbing. It's oddly satisfying. You find three lost quills and a sandwich from Thawmoon.\n\n@event life_summer_heat\ntitle: The Heat\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nart: sun\ntags: nature\nwhen: month == 10 or month == 11 or month == 12\nscene: Students lying flat on the cool stone floor of a shaded colloquy, fanning themselves with exam papers.\ntext: The heat is unbearable. Even the gargoyles are sweating. The lake is crowded, the library stifling, the dormitories ovens.\n> left: Cast a cooling charm on your room\n  challenge spell trivial:\n    win:\n      mood +small\n      renown +small\n      say: Your room becomes an icebox. People pay to visit. You let them for free. Mostly.\n    lose:\n      mood -tiny\n      say: You make it colder. Briefly. Then hotter. Then it rains inside.\n> right: Sleep on the roof\n  mood +tiny\n  say: You sleep under the stars with half the dormitory. It's the best idea anyone's had all year.\n\n@event life_harvest_festival\nslot: evening\ntitle: The Harvest Feast\nclass: story\ntone: light\ntrigger: auto\nrepeat: 12\nart: meal\ntags: festival\nwhen: month == 1 and turn > 1\nscene: Long tables groaning under pies and roast vegetables, candles floating above, a giant pumpkin carved like the headmistress.\ntext: The Harvest Feast: pies the size of cartwheels, cider, and a pumpkin carved to look like the headmistress, which she pretends not to notice.\n> left: Eat until you can't move\n  health +small\n  mood +medium\n  say: You eat three helpings of everything. You're carried back to the dorm by friends. Worth it.\n> right: Help in the kitchens for extra pay\n  gold +medium\n  charm +tiny\n  say: You serve pie to the entire academy. The cooks slip you coins and a whole pie of your own.\n\n@event life_rival_academy\ntitle: Visitors From Thornwick\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nart: shield\ntags: rival social\nwhen: turn >= 6\nscene: A delegation of students in green robes marching through the gates, noses raised, a Lumenhold crowd watching suspiciously.\ntext: A delegation from Thornwick Academy is visiting. Their students are smug, polished and very loudly unimpressed with Lumenhold's plumbing.\n> left: Be a gracious host\n  mood -tiny\n  charm +small\n  renown +tiny\n  say: You show them the observatory. One of them admits, grudgingly, that it's better than theirs.\n> right: Defend Lumenhold's honour\n  challenge combat trivial:\n    win:\n      renown +medium\n      say: You challenge their best to a friendly duel and win. The Thornwick students are quieter at dinner.\n    lose:\n      renown -small\n      health -small\n      say: You challenge their best to a friendly duel and lose, quite unfriendly. Thornwick will be insufferable.\n\n@event life_lost_firstyear\ntitle: A Lost First-Year\nclass: windfall\ntone: light\nart: compass\ntags: social\nwhen: month <= 3\nweight: 5\nscene: A tiny first-year in enormous robes standing in the middle of a junction of five identical corridors, clutching a timetable upside down.\ntext: A tiny first-year is standing in the middle of the corridor, clutching an upside-down timetable and trying very hard not to cry.\n> left: Point the way and hurry on\n  say: You point. They go the wrong way anyway. You hope they're all right.\n> right: Walk them to class\n  mood +small\n  study -tiny\n  charm +tiny\n  say: You walk them all the way and they talk the entire time. You're late, but they've decided you're their hero.\n\n@event life_frog_escape\ntitle: The Frogs Are Out\nclass: windfall\ntrack: alchemy\ntone: tense\nweight: 5\nart: frog\ntags: danger\nscene: A potions classroom overrun by dozens of hopping green frogs, one sitting smugly on the professor's desk.\ntext: Someone knocked over the frog crate in Potions. Thirty frogs are loose. One has climbed into the professor's hat. He has not noticed yet.\n> left: Help round them up\n  vigor +tiny\n  study +tiny\n  say: You catch eleven, a personal best. The professor finds the hat frog at dinner. You were not involved.\n> right: Quietly let a few escape out the window\n  mood +small\n  guile +tiny\n  say: Five frogs hop to freedom in the lake. You feel like a liberator.\n\n@event life_fortune_teller\ntitle: The Village Fortune-Teller\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nart: eye\ntags: mystery\nscene: A beaded doorway into a tiny dim tent, an old woman with a crystal ball and too many rings beckoning.\ntext: In the village, an old fortune-teller beckons you into her tent. \"Three crowns for your future, dearie. Eight for a good one.\"\n> left: Pay three crowns\n  gold -tiny\n  chance 50:\n    mood +small\n    say: \"You will meet a tall stranger.\" You already have. Several. It's an academy.\n  else:\n    mood -tiny\n    say: \"Beware of water.\" She won't say more. You think about the lake a lot.\n> right: Pay eight crowns\n  gold -small\n  mood +small\n  say: Your future is wonderful. She's very specific. You are fairly sure she's making it up, and you don't care.\n\n@event life_volunteer\ntitle: Clean-Up Duty\nclass: windfall\ntone: warm\nweight: 5\nart: broom\ntags: home\nscene: Students in rolled-up sleeves raking leaves on the great lawn, a groundskeeper directing them with a pitchfork.\ntext: After the storm, the grounds are a wreck. Volunteers needed to help clear branches and rebuild the hen-house. No pay. Lots of mud.\n> left: Volunteer\n  vigor +tiny\n  mood +small\n  rel maud +small\n  say: A long day of mud and laughter. An old woman in a straw hat brings everyone cocoa and remembers your name.\n> right: Stay in and study\n  study +tiny\n  say: You study through the shouting and hammering outside. The hen-house is very nice when it's done.\n"},{"path":"content/events/people_others.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Character events — Kael, Bram, faculty and staff.\n#  (Kael's main arc lives in chain_hollowstar.grim.)\n# ============================================================\n\n# ================= KAEL DUMONT =================\n\n@event kael_snowball\ntitle: Snow in Thawmoon\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: kael\nwhen: rel(kael) >= 6 and month >= 5 and month <= 7\nweight: 10\nart: snow\ntags: social\nscene: A courtyard where it is snowing in one small circle only, a boy in a fur coat standing at its centre holding a perfect snowball.\ntext: It's snowing in the courtyard. Only in a small circle, around Kael, who is holding a snowball and looking at it as though he's never been allowed to throw one.\n> left: Leave him to it\n  mood -tiny\n  say: When you pass again, the circle of snow is gone, and there's a small, lopsided snowman in its place.\n> right: Throw the first one\n  health -tiny\n  rel kael +small\n  mood +small\n  vigor +tiny\n  say: Your snowball hits him in the ear. He stares. Then he laughs — a real laugh, startled out of him — and the snow triples.\n\n@event kael_cold_hands\ntime: evening night\ntitle: Cold Hands\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\ntrigger: social\nactor: kael\nwhen: rel(kael) >= 12 and spark(kael) < 35\nweight: 10\nart: heart\ntags: social romance\nscene: Two students sitting close in a library alcove, one holding the other's frost-white hand between both of theirs.\ntext: Kael's hands are always cold. In the library tonight they're so cold the ink in his pen has frozen. He's trying to thaw it with his breath, embarrassed.\n> left: Lend him your gloves\n  health -tiny\n  rel kael +small\n  say: He wears them for the rest of the term. They're far too small. He doesn't care. Your own fingers go numb on the walk back.\n> right: Warm his hands in yours\n  health -tiny\n  rel kael +medium\n  spark kael +small\n  say: He goes very still. Then, slowly, the frost on his fingertips melts. \"Nobody's ever been warm enough,\" he says quietly. \"Before.\"\n\n@event kael_confession\ntitle: Thaw\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\ntrigger: social\nactor: kael\nwhen: spark(kael) >= 25 and rel(kael) >= 18 and bond(kael) != \"partner\"\nweight: 30\nart: snow\ntags: romance\nscene: Frost-ferns spreading across a dark window behind a pale boy who is, for once, looking directly at someone.\ntext: \"I was taught not to want things,\" Kael says. \"Wanting things is how they control you.\" Frost blooms across the window behind him. \"I want you. I'm sorry. I don't know what to do with it.\"\n> left: Gently say no\n  spark kael -large\n  rel kael +tiny\n  say: He nods, as if he expected it. \"Thank you for being honest. Nobody is.\" The frost on the window fades slowly.\n> right: Show him what to do with it\n  bond kael partner\n  spark kael +large\n  mood +large\n  say: You kiss him. Every window in the corridor frosts over at once, and then, very slowly, melts.\n\n# ================= BRAM HOLLIS =================\n\n@event bram_intro\ntitle: Running in the Halls\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nactor: bram\nwhen: not met(bram) and turn >= 2\nweight: 30\nart: scroll\ntags: rival\nscene: A burly prefect with a red sash blocking a corridor, a demerit ledger open in one hand and a quill poised.\ntext: \"Running in the halls.\" A burly prefect with a red sash steps into your path, ledger already open. \"Name?\" You weren't running. You were walking briskly.\n> left: Accept the demerit\n  meet bram\n  mood -tiny\n  study -tiny\n  say: He writes your name very slowly, enjoying it. Bram Hollis. You will learn to recognise his footsteps.\n> right: Argue\n  meet bram\n  rel bram -medium\n  renown +small\n  say: \"Walking briskly,\" you insist. Two demerits: one for running, one for \"lip\". Some students nearby clap quietly.\n  study -tiny\n\n@event bram_hall_tax\ntitle: The Hall Tax\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\ntrigger: social\nactor: bram\nwhen: rel(bram) < 6\nweight: 12\nart: coin\ntags: rival money\nscene: A prefect leaning against a stone archway, palm out, a line of nervous first-years shuffling past him.\ntext: Bram has invented a \"hall tax\" for using the east staircase. Eight crowns. \"Maintenance,\" he says. The first-years are paying. He looks at you expectantly.\n> left: Pay it\n  gold -small\n  rel bram +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: He pockets the coins. \"Pleasure.\" You take the west staircase forever after.\n> right: Refuse loudly\n  rel bram -medium\n  renown +small\n  chance 50:\n    gain watched\n    say: The first-years stop paying too. Bram is furious. He'll find a way to make you pay later. He always does.\n  else:\n    say: Your refusal spreads. Within a week, nobody pays the hall tax. Bram drops it, scowling.\n\n@event bram_sabotage\ntitle: Missing Notes\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\ntrigger: social\nactor: bram\nwhen: rel(bram) <= -12 and (month == 3 or month == 4 or month == 9 or month == 10)\nweight: 14\nart: scroll\ntags: rival study\nscene: An empty desk drawer, a few scattered pages on the floor, a red prefect's sash glimpsed disappearing around a corner.\ntext: Two days before exams, your revision notes vanish from your desk. All of them. You saw a red sash in the corridor.\n> left: Rewrite them from memory\n  challenge research easy:\n    win:\n      lore +small\n      say: You rewrite everything in one furious night. Your new notes are better than the old ones. Take that, Bram.\n    lose:\n      study -medium\n      health -tiny\n      say: You rewrite what you can. It isn't enough. Exams go worse than they should.\n> right: Search his room\n  challenge deceive easy:\n    win:\n      rel bram -medium\n      renown +small\n      say: You find your notes under his mattress, and also a teddy bear. You take both. You return the bear. Eventually.\n    lose:\n      gain detention\n      rel bram -small\n      say: He catches you in his room. That's two weeks of detention, and he gets to supervise.\n\n@event bram_crying\ntitle: Behind the Stables\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: bram\nwhen: met(bram) and turn >= 8 and not flag(bram_softened)\nweight: 8\nart: letter\ntags: social\nscene: A big prefect sitting on an upturned bucket behind the stables, a crumpled letter in his fist, shoulders shaking.\ntext: Behind the stables, you find Bram Hollis sitting on a bucket, crying into a crumpled letter. He sees you and freezes. \"If you tell anyone,\" he begins, but his voice cracks.\n> left: Walk away and never mention it\n  mood -tiny\n  rel bram +medium\n  say: You leave. You never tell anyone. Somehow, he knows you didn't. His demerits become fewer.\n> right: Sit down and ask what happened\n  mood -tiny\n  flag bram_softened\n  rel bram +huge\n  say: His father. A letter saying he's a disappointment, again. \"He was a prefect too,\" Bram says. \"Head boy. I'm just... loud.\" He wipes his face. \"Don't be nice to me. I don't know what to do with it.\"\n\n@event bram_turns\ntitle: An Unexpected Ally\nclass: milestone\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: bram\nwhen: flag(bram_softened) and rel(bram) >= 6 and bond(bram) != \"friend\"\nweight: 12\nart: shield\ntags: social\nscene: A prefect stepping between a cornered student and a pair of older bullies, arms crossed.\ntext: Two sixth-years corner you by the boathouse, looking for trouble. Then a red sash steps between you. \"Problem?\" says Bram, cracking his knuckles. They leave fast.\n> left: Thank him awkwardly\n  rel bram +medium\n  say: \"Don't make it weird,\" he says, and walks off. He's smiling, slightly.\n> right: Offer your hand\n  rel bram +large\n  bond bram friend\n  say: He stares at your hand. Then he shakes it, hard. \"Fine. Friends. But I'm still giving you demerits.\" He does. Fewer.\n\n@event bram_duel\ntitle: A Formal Challenge\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\ntrigger: social\nactor: bram\nwhen: bond(bram) == \"rival\" or bond(bram) == \"nemesis\"\nweight: 12\nart: duel\ntags: rival danger\nscene: A white glove lying on a dormitory floor, a red sash folded beside it, a crowd peering through the doorway.\ntext: Bram throws a glove at your feet in front of half the dormitory. \"Formal challenge. The dueling green, right now. Unless you're a coward.\" Everyone is watching.\n> left: Decline\n  renown -small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: \"Coward,\" he says, delighted. It sticks for a while. Then people forget. Mostly.\n> right: Accept\n  challenge combat medium:\n    win:\n      renown +large\n      rel bram +small\n      say: You disarm him in three moves. The crowd goes wild. Bram, on the grass, starts laughing. \"Fine,\" he says. \"Fine.\"\n    lose:\n      health -large\n      renown +small\n      say: He wins. But it takes him a full ten minutes, and he's limping too. He nods to you after. Grudging respect.\n\n# ================= PROFESSOR THORNE =================\n\n@event thorne_intro\ntitle: The First Question\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nactor: thorne\nwhen: not met(thorne) and turn >= 1\nweight: 16\nart: wand\ntags: study\nscene: A steep lecture theatre, a stern grey-bearded professor pointing a long finger at one student in the middle row.\ntext: First Arcana lecture. Professor Thorne surveys the room like a hawk over a field of mice. His finger lands on you. \"You. What is the first law of the Arcane?\"\n> left: Admit you don't know\n  meet thorne\n  rel thorne +small\n  say: \"Correct answer,\" he says. \"The first law is: know what you don't know. The rest of you, write that down.\"\n> right: Take a guess\n  meet thorne\n  challenge research easy:\n    win:\n      rel thorne +small\n      study +tiny\n      say: \"Energy follows intention.\" His eyebrows twitch. \"Acceptable.\" The room exhales.\n    lose:\n      rel thorne -tiny\n      mood -tiny\n      say: Your guess is so wrong the student next to you gasps. Thorne simply says, \"No,\" and moves on.\n\n@event thorne_office_hours\ntime: noon afternoon\ntitle: Office Hours\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: thorne\nwhen: rel(thorne) >= 6 and arcana >= 14\nweight: 10\nart: candle\ntags: study\nscene: A cramped office full of scorched books and a single lit candle, a stern professor leaning back in his chair, fingers steepled.\ntext: Thorne stops you after class. \"Your casting is sloppy and your instincts are good. That's an annoying combination. Thursdays. Seven o'clock. My office. Don't be late.\"\n> left: Politely decline — you're stretched thin\n  rel thorne -tiny\n  say: \"Hm.\" He seems neither surprised nor pleased. Thorne is rarely either.\n> right: Be there at seven\n  arcana +small\n  rel thorne +medium\n  health -tiny\n  say: Two hours of brutal drills. At the end, he hands you a book from his own shelf. \"Read it. Return it unburnt.\"\n\n@event thorne_storm_story\ntitle: The Storm Duel\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: thorne\nwhen: rel(thorne) >= 12\nweight: 8\nart: bolt\ntags: study\nscene: An old professor standing at a rain-lashed window, lightning illuminating a long scar along his jaw.\ntext: During a thunderstorm, Thorne stops mid-lecture and watches the lightning. \"They say I out-dueled a storm once,\" he says, to nobody in particular. \"Would you like to know what actually happened?\"\n> left: \"Yes, sir.\"\n  mood -tiny\n  gain inspired\n  rel thorne +small\n  say: He lost, he says. Badly. He spent a year learning why. \"Losing well,\" he says, \"is the only thing worth teaching.\" The class is silent.\n> right: Stay quiet\n  rel thorne +tiny\n  say: He shakes his head and returns to the lecture. You'll always wonder.\n\n@event thorne_recommendation\ntitle: A Letter of Recommendation\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: thorne\nwhen: rel(thorne) >= 18 and final_year\nweight: 16\nart: letter\ntags: study\nscene: A wax-sealed envelope on a professor's desk with a student's name written on it in sharp, angular handwriting.\ntext: Thorne calls you to his office and slides an envelope across the desk. \"The Conclave asked for my recommendations. I wrote one. I don't write many.\" He pauses. \"Don't make me regret it.\"\n> left: Thank him sincerely\n  flag thorne_letter\n  study +medium\n  rel thorne +tiny\n  mood +tiny\n  say: \"Go away,\" he says gruffly. \"I have marking.\" You see him smile at the window as you leave.\n> right: Ask why you\n  mood -tiny\n  flag thorne_letter\n  study +medium\n  rel thorne +medium\n  say: \"Because you ask questions like that,\" he says. \"Most students don't. Go.\" You leave unsure whether you were praised or dismissed.\n\n@event thorne_disappointed\ntitle: A Word in Private\nclass: routine\ntone: grim\ntrigger: social\nactor: thorne\nwhen: (turn > 4 and gpa < 2.0)\nweight: 14\nart: scales\ntags: study crisis\nscene: A professor standing by his desk with a sheaf of red-marked papers, a student standing awkwardly before him.\ntext: Thorne holds up your last three essays. All red. \"You're better than this,\" he says. \"That's not a compliment. It's an accusation.\"\n> left: Make excuses\n  rel thorne -small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: He listens to them all and says nothing. The silence is the worst grade yet.\n> right: Ask for help\n  mood -tiny\n  rel thorne +medium\n  study +medium\n  lore +tiny\n  say: He sighs and pulls out a chair. \"Sit. We'll start with the first essay, and we'll stay until it's right.\"\n\n# ================= OLD MAUD =================\n\n@event maud_chickens\ntitle: The Great Chicken Escape\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nactor: maud\nwhen: not met(maud) and turn >= 2\nweight: 12\nart: feather\ntags: nature social\nscene: Chickens fleeing across a lawn in every direction, an old woman in a straw hat waving a broom, students diving after birds.\ntext: Forty chickens are loose on the main lawn, pursued by an old woman in a straw hat. \"Don't just stand there, dearie!\" she shouts at you. \"Grab the speckled one, she's the ringleader!\"\n> left: Keep walking\n  say: You hear a crash, a squawk and a triumphant cackle behind you. The chickens, it seems, are caught.\n> right: Grab the speckled one\n  meet maud\n  challenge feat medium:\n    win:\n      rel maud +medium\n      say: You tackle the ringleader. The rest give up. \"Good hands,\" says Old Maud, pressing a warm egg into your palm. \"Come for tea.\"\n    lose:\n      rel maud +small\n      health -tiny\n      say: The ringleader escapes and pecks you, but Maud is impressed by your enthusiasm. \"Come for tea. Bring plasters.\"\n\n@event maud_honey\ntitle: Honey for a Cold\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: maud\nwhen: rel(maud) >= 3 and (has(sick) or health < 40)\nweight: 18\nart: flower\ntags: nature\nscene: A small cottage kitchen with bundles of herbs hanging from the beams, an old woman pouring golden honey into a steaming mug.\ntext: Maud spots you sniffling across the lawn and drags you into her cottage. \"Sit,\" she orders, pouring honey into something steaming that smells of lemon and thunder.\n> left: Drink it all\n  mood -tiny\n  health +medium\n  lose sick\n  rel maud +small\n  say: It tastes like summer and lightning. You sneeze sparks for the rest of the day. By morning you're cured and slightly glowing.\n> right: Drink it and help with chores\n  mood -tiny\n  health +small\n  lose sick\n  rel maud +medium\n  alchemy +tiny\n  say: You sip and sort herbs while she tells you the names of every plant in the room. You remember most of them.\n\n@event maud_old_student\ntitle: Maud Was a Student Once\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\ntrigger: social\nactor: maud\nwhen: rel(maud) >= 9 and clock(hollowstar) >= 1\nweight: 14\nart: moon\ntags: mystery hollowstar\nchain: hollowstar\nscene: An old woman sitting on a bench by the lake at dusk, looking at the water as if it might look back.\ntext: \"I was a student here, you know,\" Maud says, watching the lake. \"Sixty years ago. The lake froze one spring. Three of my friends went down to the boathouse that night. Only two came back.\" She doesn't look at you. \"Ask me what I know.\"\n> left: \"You don't have to tell me.\"\n  rel maud +medium\n  say: She pats your hand. \"Kind. But time's short, dearie.\" She tells you anyway, a little at a time, over many cups of tea.\n> right: \"What do you know?\"\n  rel maud +small\n  flag knows_founders_seal\n  find knows_founders_seal\n  find seven_locks\n  find star_key\n  say: \"Seven locks, one in each tower. The key's a star that eats light. And the thing below loves a lonely heart.\" She finally looks at you. \"Don't be lonely.\"\n\n@event maud_bees\ntitle: The Silver Bees\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: maud\nwhen: rel(maud) >= 6 and month >= 7 and month <= 10\nweight: 10\nart: flower\ntags: nature\nscene: Wooden hives in a wildflower meadow, an old woman in a veiled hat lifting a frame dripping with silver honey, bees glinting in the sun.\ntext: \"The silver bees need moving to the summer meadow,\" Maud says, handing you a veiled hat. \"They're friendly. Mostly. Don't make any sudden thoughts.\"\n> left: Help with the hives\n  challenge feat trivial:\n    win:\n      health +small\n      rel maud +medium\n      gold +small\n      say: The bees hum around you like a blessing. Maud gives you a jar of silver honey that sells for eight crowns. You eat half first.\n    lose:\n      health -medium\n      rel maud +small\n      say: You have a sudden thought. The bees know. You spend the evening with vinegar on your stings.\n> right: Watch from a safe distance\n  mood +tiny\n  say: You watch Maud move hives barehanded, singing to them. It's the calmest you've felt all month.\n\n@event maud_ill\ntitle: Maud Is Unwell\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: maud\nwhen: rel(maud) >= 12 and year >= 2 and not flag(maud_ill_done)\nweight: 12\nart: house\ntags: social\nscene: A small cottage bedroom with a patchwork quilt, an old woman propped up on pillows, a student reading aloud by candlelight.\ntext: Maud's chickens are running wild, unfed. You find her in bed, grey-faced and coughing. \"Just a chill,\" she wheezes. \"I'm eighty-one. I'm allowed.\"\n> left: Fetch Sister Amaranth\n  mood -tiny\n  flag maud_ill_done\n  rel maud +medium\n  say: Amaranth brings tonics and a stern lecture. Maud is back on her feet in a week, grumbling about fuss.\n> right: Look after her yourself for a week\n  health -tiny\n  flag maud_ill_done\n  rel maud +large\n  study -small\n  gain blessed\n  say: You feed chickens, brew teas, read her the newspaper. When she's well, she gives you a sprig of rosemary. \"You'll do,\" she says. \"You'll do.\"\n\n# ================= HEADMISTRESS ISOLDE MARROW =================\n\n@event isolde_intro\ntime: evening\ntitle: She Knows Your Name\nclass: windfall\ntone: light\nactor: isolde\nwhen: not met(isolde) and turn >= 3\nweight: 5\nart: crown\ntags: story\nscene: A tall pale woman in dark robes pausing behind a student's chair in a candlelit dining hall, a hand resting lightly on the chair back.\ntext: At dinner, a cool hand rests on the back of your chair. The headmistress. \"{player},\" she says, though you've never met. \"Your marks in Arcana are interesting. Your marks in punctuality, less so.\" She moves on.\n> left: Stammer an apology\n  meet isolde\n  rel isolde +tiny\n  say: She's already three tables away. Every student between you sits a little straighter.\n> right: \"Interesting how, Headmistress?\"\n  meet isolde\n  rel isolde +small\n  renown +small\n  say: She pauses. Turns. Almost smiles. \"Ask me again at the end of your first year,\" she says. The table stares at you.\n\n@event isolde_tea\ntitle: Tea in the Tower\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: isolde\nwhen: renown >= 25 and rel(isolde) >= 0\nweight: 10\nart: cup\ntags: social\nscene: A round tower study at twilight, two delicate teacups on a small table, a headmistress pouring tea with a perfectly steady hand.\ntext: An invitation to tea with the headmistress. Students talk about these the way soldiers talk about battles. She pours. \"Tell me, {player}: what do you want to become?\"\n> left: Give an ambitious answer\n  challenge negotiate trivial:\n    win:\n      rel isolde +medium\n      renown +small\n      say: She listens without interrupting, then says, \"Good. Ambition without arrogance. Rarer than you'd think.\"\n    lose:\n      rel isolde -tiny\n      say: She nods politely. \"Ambition is a fine servant,\" she says, \"and a poor master.\" You're dismissed shortly after.\n> right: Admit you don't know yet\n  mood -tiny\n  rel isolde +medium\n  lore +tiny\n  say: \"Neither did I, at your age,\" she says. \"I still don't, some days. That's the point of an academy.\" She refills your cup.\n\n@event isolde_warning\ntitle: A Quiet Warning\nclass: routine\ntone: grim\ntrigger: social\nactor: isolde\nwhen: has(detention) or has(watched) or has(probation)\nweight: 10\nart: scales\ntags: crisis\nscene: A headmistress walking alongside a student in a long colonnade, hands clasped behind her back, speaking without looking at them.\ntext: The headmistress falls into step beside you in the colonnade. \"I hear your name more often than I'd like,\" she says pleasantly. \"In the wrong rooms. I'd hate to hear it in mine.\"\n> left: Promise to do better\n  rel isolde +small\n  study +small\n  lose watched\n  say: \"Good.\" She stops walking. You keep going. When you look back, she's gone.\n> right: Defend yourself\n  challenge negotiate using renown trivial:\n    win:\n      rel isolde +small\n      lose watched\n      say: You lay it out plainly. She listens, then nods once. \"Fair. I'll have a word with certain prefects.\"\n    lose:\n      rel isolde -small\n      say: \"Hm,\" she says. \"Let's hope you're right.\" It doesn't feel like hope.\n"},{"path":"content/events/people_students.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Character events — fellow students.\n#  Intros are `trigger: random` (they introduce the NPC).\n#  Everything else is `trigger: social`: drawn from the monthly\n#  social slot, only once the NPC has been met.\n#  Romance arc per character: spark (generic) -> love >= 35 ->\n#  confession -> partner events. See social.grim for generic ones.\n# ============================================================\n\n# ================= LYRA VANESHADE =================\n\n@event lyra_intro\ntitle: The Last Copy\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nactor: lyra\nwhen: not met(lyra) and turn >= 2\nweight: 30\nart: book\ntags: social rival\nscene: Two hands reaching for the same red-bound book on a library shelf; one hand wears a signet ring with a flame crest.\ntext: You reach for the last copy of \"Foundations of Flame\" and a ringed hand gets there first. \"I need it more,\" says a tall girl with crimson hair, not looking at you. \"You'll only underline the wrong bits.\"\n> left: Let her have it\n  meet lyra\n  rel lyra +tiny\n  say: \"At least you know your place,\" she says, which is somehow worse than rudeness. Lyra Vaneshade. Everyone knows the name.\n> right: Argue for it\n  meet lyra\n  challenge negotiate easy:\n    win:\n      rel lyra +medium\n      say: You quote chapter three at her. She raises an eyebrow, then hands you the book. \"Fine. Return it underlined correctly.\"\n    lose:\n      rel lyra -small\n      say: She wins the argument in four sentences and leaves with the book. You think of the perfect comeback that night.\n\n@event lyra_letter\ntitle: A Letter With a Black Seal\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: lyra\nwhen: rel(lyra) >= 6\nweight: 12\nart: letter\ntags: social\nscene: A girl sitting on a window seat in an empty corridor, crushing a letter with a black wax seal in her fist, face turned away.\ntext: You find Lyra on a window seat with a crumpled letter. Black seal. Her family's. She's not crying; Vaneshades don't. But she's not quite not crying either.\n> left: Pretend you didn't see\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You walk past. Later, she's flawless again, as if nothing happened. Maybe nothing did.\n> right: Sit down beside her\n  mood -tiny\n  rel lyra +large\n  spark lyra +tiny\n  flag lyra_vulnerable\n  say: \"Second place,\" she says eventually. \"I came second in the Winter Trials. My mother wants to know who beat me.\" She almost laughs. \"It's always someone.\"\n\n@event lyra_study_rivalry\ntime: morning noon\ntitle: Top of the Class\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\ntrigger: social\nactor: lyra\nwhen: rel(lyra) > -24\nweight: 10\nrepeat: 4\nart: scroll\ntags: social study rival\nscene: Two students at opposite ends of a long library table, surrounded by towers of books, glaring over the tops of them.\ntext: The professors post this week's rankings, with an open paper this afternoon for anyone who wants to improve their place. Lyra is first. You're close enough to see the back of her head. She notices you noticing.\n> left: Suggest studying together\n  mood -tiny\n  lore +small\n  rel lyra +small\n  say: \"Together?\" She considers this like a foreign custom. Then she pulls out a chair. She's an excellent, merciless study partner.\n> right: Beat her outright\n  challenge research easy:\n    win:\n      study +medium\n      rel lyra +small\n      renown +small\n      say: The afternoon paper puts you first. Lyra stares at the list for a long time. Then she finds you and says, \"Good.\" She means it.\n    lose:\n      study +tiny\n      rel lyra -tiny\n      say: You cram through lunch and come third. Lyra is still first. She doesn't gloat. That's worse.\n\n@event lyra_family_gala\ntitle: The Vaneshade Winter Gala\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\ntrigger: social\nactor: lyra\nwhen: rel(lyra) >= 18 and month >= 3 and month <= 6\nweight: 12\nart: crown\ntags: social romance\nscene: A glittering ballroom in a noble house, candles floating in mid-air, a tall woman in crimson watching the crowd like a hawk.\ntext: \"My family's winter gala,\" Lyra says, too casually. \"I need a guest who won't embarrass me. You'll do. Probably. You'll need proper clothes.\"\n> left: Decline\n  rel lyra -tiny\n  say: \"Of course.\" A pause. \"It doesn't matter.\" It clearly does.\n> right: Go with her (15 gold for clothes)\n  gold -medium\n  charm +small\n  renown +small\n  spark lyra +small\n  rel lyra +small\n  chance 40:\n    mood -small\n    say: Lyra's mother asks who your parents are, and then asks no further questions at all. Lyra squeezes your hand under the table, hard.\n  else:\n    mood +medium\n    say: You dance, you charm a duke, you don't spill anything. In the carriage home, Lyra falls asleep on your shoulder.\n\n@event lyra_confession\ntitle: Lyra Doesn't Lose\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\ntrigger: social\nactor: lyra\nwhen: spark(lyra) >= 25 and rel(lyra) >= 18 and bond(lyra) != \"partner\"\nweight: 30\nart: flame\ntags: romance\nscene: A girl with crimson hair standing very close in a practice ring at dusk, sparks still drifting from her wand, cheeks flushed.\ntext: After practice, Lyra doesn't leave. \"I don't lose,\" she says, staring at the scorch marks. \"I don't. But I think — \" She stops. Starts again. \"I think I've lost to you. Say something before I set something on fire.\"\n> left: Let her down gently\n  spark lyra -large\n  rel lyra +tiny\n  say: She nods, sharp and quick. \"Understood.\" She practises alone for a month. Then one day she nods to you in the corridor, and it's almost all right.\n> right: Kiss her\n  bond lyra partner\n  spark lyra +large\n  mood +large\n  say: Nothing catches fire. Well. Something does, but it's only the practice dummy, and neither of you notices for a while.\n\n@event lyra_family_ultimatum\ntitle: The Ultimatum\nclass: routine\ntone: grim\ntrigger: social\nactor: lyra\nwhen: bond(lyra) == \"partner\" and turn >= 14\nweight: 18\nart: crown\ntags: romance crisis\nscene: Lyra holding a letter with a black seal, her other hand clenched around a ring she has just pulled off.\ntext: Another black-sealed letter. Lyra reads it twice and goes white. \"My mother has found out about us. End it, she says, or she ends my inheritance.\" She looks at you. \"Tell me what to do.\"\n> left: \"Choose yourself, not them.\"\n  if spark(lyra) >= 50:\n    challenge negotiate trivial:\n      win:\n        rel lyra +large\n        spark lyra +medium\n        flag lyra_free\n        say: She tears the letter in half. Then in quarters. Then she sets the quarters on fire and laughs like she hasn't in years.\n      lose:\n        rel lyra -small\n        spark lyra -medium\n        mood -medium\n        say: \"Easy for you to say,\" she snaps. She doesn't mean it. She also doesn't take it back.\n  else:\n    challenge negotiate easy:\n      win:\n        rel lyra +large\n        spark lyra +medium\n        flag lyra_free\n        say: She tears the letter in half. Then in quarters. Then she sets the quarters on fire and laughs like she hasn't in years.\n      lose:\n        rel lyra -small\n        spark lyra -medium\n        mood -medium\n        say: \"Easy for you to say,\" she snaps. She doesn't mean it. She also doesn't take it back.\n> right: Step back so she doesn't lose everything\n  bond lyra ex\n  gain heartbroken\n  rel lyra +small\n  say: You end it before she has to. She doesn't argue. That's how you know how much it costs her.\n\n@event lyra_rival_sabotage\ntitle: Sabotage in Class\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\ntrigger: social\nactor: lyra\nwhen: bond(lyra) == \"rival\" and rel(lyra) <= -6\nweight: 12\nart: potion\ntags: rival\nscene: A cauldron spitting black smoke, a crimson-haired student walking away with a tiny vial hidden in her sleeve.\ntext: Your perfect potion turns black and starts screaming. Across the room, Lyra is very carefully not looking at you.\n> left: Retaliate\n  challenge deceive trivial:\n    win:\n      rel lyra -small\n      renown +small\n      say: Next lesson, her wand produces only bubbles. Pink ones. She knows. You both know. It's war. It's sort of fun.\n    lose:\n      gain detention\n      rel lyra -medium\n      say: You're caught slipping something into her bag. She looks genuinely delighted.\n> right: Confront her directly\n  mood -tiny\n  rel lyra +medium\n  say: \"That was beneath you.\" She flushes. \"...Yes. It was.\" The next day there's a perfect replacement potion on your desk.\n\n@event lyra_burnout\ntime: morning\ntitle: Lyra Falls\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: lyra\nwhen: rel(lyra) >= 12 and year >= 2\nweight: 10\nart: cross\ntags: social\nscene: A girl collapsed across a library desk among scattered notes, quill still in hand, candle burned down to nothing.\ntext: You find Lyra face-down on a library desk at dawn, not asleep — out cold. She's been studying for three days straight. \"Don't tell anyone,\" she mumbles when she comes round. \"Mother can't know.\"\n> left: Take her to the infirmary anyway\n  rel lyra -tiny\n  spark lyra +tiny\n  say: She's furious. Then she sleeps for nineteen hours. When she wakes up, she says, quietly, \"Thank you.\"\n> right: Cover for her and make her rest\n  health -tiny\n  rel lyra +medium\n  study -tiny\n  say: You tell her professors she has a cold and sit by her door with your homework. She sleeps. You keep watch.\n\n# ================= TOBIAS FENNICK =================\n\n@event tobias_intro\ntitle: The Lost Boy\nclass: windfall\ntone: light\nactor: tobias\nwhen: not met(tobias) and turn >= 2\nweight: 30\nart: compass\ntags: social\nscene: A large, gentle-faced boy leading a crying first-year by the hand down the wrong corridor, both very lost.\ntext: A big, broad-shouldered boy is leading a crying first-year through the corridors. \"Don't worry, I know exactly where the infirmary is,\" he says, and walks confidently into a broom cupboard.\n> left: Point them the right way\n  meet tobias\n  rel tobias +small\n  say: \"Ah. Left, you say. Right. I mean, correct.\" He grins, enormous and sheepish. \"Tobias. Turnip scholarship. Thanks.\"\n> right: Walk with them\n  meet tobias\n  rel tobias +medium\n  mood +small\n  say: You end up taking the scenic route, and the first-year stops crying because Tobias is telling a very long story about a goat.\n\n@event tobias_homesick\ntitle: Homesick\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: tobias\nwhen: rel(tobias) >= 6\nweight: 12\nart: leaf\ntags: social nature\nscene: A dormitory windowsill crowded with clay pots of seedlings, a big boy watering them with a tiny can.\ntext: Tobias has gone quiet. He's planted seedlings in every cup in the common room. \"Harvest at home,\" he says. \"First one I've missed. Ma's doing it alone.\"\n> left: Cheer him up with a joke\n  rel tobias +small\n  say: Your joke is terrible. He laughs anyway, the big rumbling laugh, and that seems to help.\n> right: Help him build a window garden\n  gold -tiny\n  rel tobias +small\n  alchemy +tiny\n  mood +small\n  say: You buy seeds and a stack of pots. By the end of the week the common room smells of tomato leaves. Tobias whistles while he waters them.\n\n@event tobias_bad_harvest\ntitle: The Harvest Failed\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: tobias\nwhen: rel(tobias) >= 15 and turn >= 8 and not flag(tobias_harvest_done)\nweight: 14\nart: rain\ntags: social money\nscene: A boy packing a battered trunk in an empty dormitory, a letter with smudged handwriting open on the bed.\ntext: Tobias is packing. Blight took the harvest. His family needs money, and hands. \"Thirty crowns would get them through winter,\" he says, not asking. \"But I haven't got thirty crowns. I've got two arms.\"\n> left: Persuade him to stay and find work here\n  flag tobias_harvest_done\n  challenge negotiate trivial:\n    win:\n      rel tobias +medium\n      say: You walk him to the job board. He signs up for double shifts at the stables and sends every crown home. He stays.\n    lose:\n      rel tobias +tiny\n      mood -small\n      say: He listens, nods, and takes the barge in the morning. He's back in two months, thinner and quieter.\n> right: Give him thirty crowns\n  when: gold >= 30\n  flag tobias_harvest_done\n  gold -large\n  rel tobias +huge\n  spark tobias +tiny\n  say: He tries to refuse three times. Then he hugs you so hard your spine cracks. \"I'll pay you back. Every crown. Every one.\"\n  queue tobias_repays in 6\n\n@event tobias_repays\ntitle: Every Crown\nclass: story\ntone: light\ntrigger: queued\nactor: tobias\nart: pouch\ntags: social money\nscene: A clumsy hand-stitched pouch of coins and a jar of honey left on a dormitory pillow.\ntext: A lumpy, hand-stitched pouch on your pillow: thirty crowns exactly, in small coins. And a jar of honey from home. A note in big, careful letters: \"TOLD YOU. — T.\"\n> ok: Smile\n  gold +large\n  rel tobias +small\n  mood +small\n  say: The honey tastes of a farm you have never seen and somehow already miss.\n\n@event tobias_godparent\ntitle: The Mandrake Baby\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: tobias\nwhen: rel(tobias) >= 9\nweight: 10\nart: leaf\ntags: social nature\nscene: A tiny mandrake in a pot wearing a knitted hat, a beaming boy presenting it like a newborn.\ntext: Tobias presents a flowerpot containing a very small mandrake in a knitted hat. \"This is Gerald,\" he says. \"I'd like you to be his godparent.\" Gerald screams softly.\n> left: Politely decline\n  rel tobias -tiny\n  say: \"No pressure,\" says Tobias, visibly pressured. Gerald glares at you from under his hat.\n> right: Accept with honour\n  health -tiny\n  rel tobias +small\n  mood +small\n  alchemy +tiny\n  say: You are now Gerald's godparent. Gerald bites you. Tobias says this means Gerald loves you.\n\n@event tobias_bullied\ntitle: Tobias and the Prefect\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\ntrigger: social\nactor: tobias\nwhen: met(bram) and rel(tobias) >= 3\nweight: 10\nart: shield\ntags: social rival\nscene: A burly prefect mocking a big farm boy in a crowded corridor, students sniggering, the boy's ears bright red.\ntext: Bram is doing an impression of Tobias's accent in the corridor. People are laughing. Tobias is pretending not to hear, ears burning red.\n> left: Stay out of it\n  rel tobias -small\n  say: Tobias doesn't mention it. He doesn't sit with you at dinner, either.\n> right: Step in\n  alignment +small\n  rel tobias +large\n  rel bram -medium\n  challenge negotiate easy:\n    win:\n      renown +small\n      say: \"Funny,\" you say. \"Do your father next.\" The laughter turns on Bram. He stalks off. Tobias stares at you in awe.\n    lose:\n      gain watched\n      say: Bram turns his attention to you instead, with interest. Tobias brings you a pie later. A whole pie.\n\n@event tobias_confession\ntitle: A Confession, With Vegetables\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\ntrigger: social\nactor: tobias\nwhen: spark(tobias) >= 25 and rel(tobias) >= 18 and bond(tobias) != \"partner\"\nweight: 30\nart: heart\ntags: romance\nscene: A big boy holding out a perfect pumpkin like an offering in a greenhouse, cheeks scarlet.\ntext: Tobias corners you in the greenhouse holding a very large pumpkin. \"I grew this,\" he says. \"For you. It's the best one. I didn't know how else to — I like you. Like that. I've practised this on the pumpkin. Sorry.\"\n> left: Let him down gently\n  spark tobias -large\n  rel tobias +small\n  say: He nods a lot. \"That's all right. That's fine. You can still have the pumpkin.\" You keep it until it rots, out of loyalty.\n> right: Say yes\n  bond tobias partner\n  spark tobias +large\n  mood +large\n  say: He drops the pumpkin. It's fine; pumpkins are sturdy. So, it turns out, are his hugs.\n\n@event tobias_picnic\ntitle: A Picnic in the Orchard\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\ntrigger: social\nactor: tobias\nwhen: bond(tobias) == \"partner\" and month >= 7 and month <= 10\nweight: 14\nrepeat: 10\nart: tree\ntags: romance nature\nscene: A checked blanket under blossoming apple trees, a basket of bread and cheese, two students lying in the dappled light.\ntext: Tobias has packed a picnic: bread he baked, cheese he begged from the kitchens, apples from the orchard he has befriended. \"No exams for six hours,\" he announces. \"I checked.\"\n> left: Bring your books anyway\n  lore +small\n  spark tobias -tiny\n  say: You study on the blanket. He falls asleep with his head in your lap. It's still lovely.\n> right: Leave everything behind\n  mood +medium\n  health +small\n  spark tobias +small\n  say: The afternoon goes golden and slow. You can't remember the last time you didn't think about grades.\n\n# ================= SERAPHINE HALE =================\n\n@event seraphine_intro\ntitle: Your Left Shoe\nclass: windfall\ntone: eerie\nactor: seraphine\nwhen: not met(seraphine) and turn >= 2\nweight: 30\nart: eye\ntags: social mystery\nscene: A slight girl with silver-grey eyes and a scarf of stars passing in a corridor, pointing absently at someone's feet.\ntext: A girl drifts past you in the corridor and says, without stopping, \"You're going to lose your left shoe today. Don't worry, it's under the stairs in the east wing.\"\n> left: Ignore her\n  meet seraphine\n  say: That afternoon a staircase moves and eats your left shoe. It is, eventually, under the stairs in the east wing.\n> right: Ask how she knows\n  meet seraphine\n  rel seraphine +medium\n  say: \"I don't know things,\" she says. \"Things know me.\" Then: \"Seraphine. You'll want the shoe back by four.\" You get it back at 3:58.\n\n@event seraphine_tarot\ntitle: The Cards\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\ntrigger: social\nactor: seraphine\nwhen: rel(seraphine) >= 3\nweight: 12\nart: cards\ntags: social mystery\nscene: A tarot spread on a velvet cloth in candlelight, a girl's pale fingers turning over a card showing a lightning-struck tower.\ntext: Seraphine shuffles a battered tarot deck. \"One card,\" she says. \"For you. Only if you want it. They're quite rude sometimes.\"\n> left: Draw a card\n  chance 50:\n    mood +small\n    renown +tiny\n    say: The Star. \"Hope,\" she says, delighted. \"And someone's going to tell you a secret. Oh — it's me. I like you.\" She blushes.\n    rel seraphine +small\n  else:\n    mood -tiny\n    say: The Tower. She looks at it a long time. \"It's fine,\" she says. \"Towers fall. That's what they're for.\"\n    rel seraphine +small\n> right: Decline\n  rel seraphine +tiny\n  say: \"Good,\" she says, putting the deck away. \"Knowing is overrated.\"\n\n@event seraphine_lake_vision\ntitle: She Walks Into the Lake\nclass: story\ntone: tense\ntrigger: social\nactor: seraphine\nwhen: rel(seraphine) >= 6 and month >= 3 and month <= 9\nweight: 10\nart: wave\ntags: social danger\nscene: A girl in a nightgown wading into a misty lake at night, eyes glowing silver, arms outstretched toward something under the water.\ntext: From your window you see Seraphine walking into the lake in her nightgown, eyes shining silver, already up to her waist.\n> left: Wake a prefect\n  rel seraphine +small\n  say: The prefect drags her out, coughing. She doesn't remember anything. She looks at you strangely for days.\n> right: Run down and pull her out\n  challenge feat trivial:\n    win:\n      rel seraphine +large\n      spark seraphine +small\n      alignment +small\n      say: You reach her just as she goes under. On the shore she wakes and whispers, \"It was calling me. It called you too.\" Then she holds on very tight.\n    lose:\n      health -large\n      gain sick\n      rel seraphine +large\n      say: You both nearly drown. You both come down with Lake Fever. She brings you soup that tastes faintly of the future.\n\n@event seraphine_future\ntitle: I Saw You Old\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\ntrigger: social\nactor: seraphine\nwhen: rel(seraphine) >= 15\nweight: 10\nart: hourglass\ntags: social mystery\nscene: Two students sitting on the rim of a dry fountain, one looking up at the stars, the other looking at the first with a soft smile.\ntext: \"I saw you old,\" Seraphine says suddenly. \"Last night. You were happy. Really happy. There was someone with you.\" She pauses. \"Do you want to know who?\"\n> left: Yes, tell me\n  chance 50:\n    spark seraphine +small\n    say: She looks at you for a long time. \"I'll tell you when you're old,\" she says, and goes pink.\n  else:\n    mood +small\n    say: \"Someone kind,\" she says. \"That's all I could see. Their hands were kind.\"\n> right: No, let it be a surprise\n  mood -tiny\n  rel seraphine +medium\n  say: She smiles like you've given her a present. \"That's the right answer. Nobody ever gives the right answer.\" Not knowing nags at you for days.\n\n@event seraphine_overwhelmed\ntitle: Too Many Tomorrows\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: seraphine\nwhen: rel(seraphine) >= 9 and year >= 2\nweight: 10\nart: eye\ntags: social\nscene: A girl curled in a dark corner of an observatory with her hands over her eyes, a teacup gone cold beside her.\ntext: Seraphine hasn't come to class in a week. You find her in a dark storeroom with her hands over her eyes. \"It's too loud,\" she whispers. \"Everyone's futures. All at once.\"\n> left: Fetch Sister Amaranth\n  rel seraphine +small\n  say: Amaranth gives her a sleeping draught and a dark room. She recovers. She doesn't say much about it.\n> right: Sit with her in the dark\n  rel seraphine +large\n  spark seraphine +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You don't speak. After an hour she takes her hands from her eyes. \"Yours is quiet,\" she says. \"Your future. It's very quiet. I like it.\"\n\n@event seraphine_confession\ntitle: She Already Knew\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\ntrigger: social\nactor: seraphine\nwhen: spark(seraphine) >= 25 and rel(seraphine) >= 18 and bond(seraphine) != \"partner\"\nweight: 30\nart: star\ntags: romance\nscene: A girl with silver eyes holding out a single falling-star seed in her palm on a dark observatory balcony.\ntext: \"I've seen this conversation forty-one times,\" Seraphine says. \"In twenty-nine of them, you say yes. I've been very nervous about the other twelve.\" She holds out her hand. \"I like you. That part was always the same.\"\n> left: Gently say no\n  spark seraphine -large\n  rel seraphine +tiny\n  say: She nods. \"Twelve,\" she says softly. \"I knew it might be twelve.\" She's kind to you afterwards. Somehow that hurts more.\n> right: Take her hand\n  bond seraphine partner\n  spark seraphine +large\n  mood +large\n  say: \"Twenty-nine,\" she whispers, and laughs, and for once she's entirely in the present.\n\n# ================= CORVIN ASHBY =================\n\n@event corvin_intro\ntitle: Light Fingers\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nactor: corvin\nwhen: not met(corvin) and turn >= 2\nweight: 30\nart: mask\ntags: social\nscene: A dark-haired student with a crooked grin holding up a purse between two fingers, mid-bow.\ntext: Someone bumps you in the crowd. A moment later a dark-haired boy is bowing and holding up your purse. \"You dropped this,\" he says. \"Into my pocket. Terrible habit of yours. Corvin Ashby.\"\n> left: Snatch it back and report him\n  meet corvin\n  rel corvin -small\n  say: He's gone before the prefect arrives. Your purse is all there, plus an IOU you don't remember receiving.\n> right: Laugh\n  gold -tiny\n  meet corvin\n  rel corvin +medium\n  guile +tiny\n  say: \"Oh, I like you,\" he says, and teaches you the trick on the spot. You practise it on him. He lets you win. Probably. Later, you are three crowns short. Probably a coincidence.\n\n@event corvin_kitchen_raid\ntime: night\ntitle: The Kitchen Raid\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: corvin\nwhen: rel(corvin) >= 3\nweight: 12\nart: meal\ntags: social\nscene: Two students creeping through a cavernous castle kitchen at night, a mountain of tarts on a table, a sleeping cook snoring by the hearth.\ntext: \"Midnight,\" Corvin whispers. \"The cooks have made forty treacle tarts for the faculty dinner. Forty. Nobody needs forty.\"\n> left: Not tonight\n  rel corvin -tiny\n  say: He goes alone. The next morning there's a treacle tart on your pillow. You decide not to ask.\n> right: Lead the raid\n  challenge deceive trivial:\n    win:\n      mood +medium\n      rel corvin +medium\n      say: You get out with nine tarts and a bottle of elderflower cordial. You eat them on the roof until sunrise.\n    lose:\n      gain detention\n      rel corvin +medium\n      say: The cook wakes up. You both spend a week scrubbing pans, laughing too hard to work.\n\n@event corvin_debt\ntitle: Corvin's Little Problem\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: corvin\nwhen: rel(corvin) >= 12 and not flag(corvin_owes) and not flag(corvin_debt_refused)\nweight: 12\nart: chain\ntags: social money\nscene: A boy with a black eye sitting on the dormitory stairs, trying very hard to look casual.\ntext: Corvin has a black eye and a very casual expression. \"Small misunderstanding with some trolls,\" he says. \"Thirty crowns would clear it right up. I'll pay you back. Probably with interest. Possibly with a goat.\"\n> left: Refuse\n  flag corvin_debt_refused\n  rel corvin -medium\n  say: \"Course. No problem.\" He has another black eye the next week. He's still friendly. Mostly.\n> right: Give him thirty crowns\n  when: gold >= 30\n  gold -large\n  flag corvin_owes\n  rel corvin +large\n  queue corvin_repays in 4\n  say: He stares at the coins, then at you. \"No one's ever just... done that.\" He's quiet for a whole minute. A record.\n\n@event corvin_repays\ntitle: Interest\nclass: story\ntone: light\ntrigger: queued\nactor: corvin\nart: coin\ntags: social money\nscene: A small brass key and a fat purse left on a desk with a playing card, the jack of spades, tucked under them.\ntext: There's a fat purse on your desk and a jack of spades tucked under it. Inside the purse: more than thirty crowns. Much more.\n> left: Count it\n  chance 70:\n    gold +huge\n    rel corvin +medium\n    say: Sixty crowns. A note: \"Interest. Don't ask. — C.\" You don't ask.\n  else:\n    gold +tiny\n    rel corvin +small\n    say: Three crowns and a very convincing IOU for the other twenty-seven, with a doodle of a goat.\n> right: Find him and ask where it came from\n  rel corvin +large\n  gold +large\n  say: \"I won it,\" he says. \"Fairly. Mostly.\" He pushes the exact thirty into your hand. \"The rest's mine. I've got another debt to pay.\"\n\n@event corvin_rooftop\ntime: night\ntitle: Up on the Roof\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: corvin\nwhen: rel(corvin) >= 18\nweight: 10\nart: moon\ntags: social\nscene: Two students sitting on a steep slate roof under a full moon, legs dangling over a dizzying drop, sharing a bottle of something fizzy.\ntext: On the roof at 2am, Corvin goes quiet. \"I'm on my last warning,\" he says. \"One more detention and they send me home. And home is... not a good place.\" He laughs, badly.\n> left: Tell him to keep his head down\n  health -tiny\n  rel corvin +small\n  say: \"Head down. Right. I'll try anything once.\" He keeps his head down for almost a month.\n> right: Promise you'll cover for him\n  health -tiny\n  rel corvin +large\n  spark corvin +tiny\n  flag covering_corvin\n  say: He looks at you like nobody has ever promised him anything. \"You're an idiot,\" he says. \"Thank you.\"\n\n@event corvin_framed\ntitle: Corvin Is Framed\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\ntrigger: social\nactor: corvin\nwhen: rel(corvin) >= 9 and met(bram) and turn >= 10\nweight: 10\nart: scales\ntags: social rival\nscene: A boy standing before a disciplinary desk, a stolen silver goblet on it, a smug prefect at his shoulder.\ntext: The headmistress's silver goblet has been found in Corvin's trunk. He swears he didn't take it. Bram, who found it, looks very pleased. Corvin is one detention from expulsion.\n> left: Stay out of it\n  rel corvin -large\n  chance 50:\n    say: Corvin talks his way out, barely. He doesn't look at you for a month.\n  else:\n    rel corvin -large\n    say: Corvin is suspended for a month. When he comes back, he's different. Quieter.\n> right: Find proof he was framed\n  challenge deceive trivial:\n    win:\n      rel corvin +large\n      rel bram -large\n      renown +small\n      say: You find the bill of sale: Bram bought a fake. Bram is stripped of his sash. Corvin buys you a goat. An actual goat.\n    lose:\n      rel corvin +medium\n      gain watched\n      say: You can't prove it. But you stood up for him in front of everyone, and he'll never forget it. Neither will Bram.\n\n@event corvin_confession\ntitle: Corvin, Serious for Once\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\ntrigger: social\nactor: corvin\nwhen: spark(corvin) >= 25 and rel(corvin) >= 18 and bond(corvin) != \"partner\"\nweight: 30\nart: cards\ntags: romance\nscene: A boy holding out a playing card — the queen of hearts — with an unsteady hand, no grin for once.\ntext: Corvin holds out a playing card. The queen of hearts. His hand isn't quite steady. \"No trick,\" he says. \"No angle. I just — I like you more than I like anything, and I'm terrified, and I wanted you to have this.\"\n> left: Gently decline\n  spark corvin -large\n  rel corvin +tiny\n  say: He laughs, too quickly. \"Worth a shot.\" He keeps the card. You see it sometimes, tucked in his sleeve.\n> right: Take the card\n  bond corvin partner\n  spark corvin +large\n  mood +large\n  say: For once, Corvin Ashby has nothing to say. He just kisses you, and then grins, and then can't stop grinning.\n\n@event corvin_runaway\ntime: night\ntitle: Let's Leave Tonight\nclass: story\ntone: warm\ntrigger: social\nactor: corvin\nwhen: bond(corvin) == \"partner\" and final_year\nweight: 16\nart: map\ntags: romance danger\nscene: A small sky-barge tied to the academy dock at midnight, lanterns lit, a boy at the tiller holding out his hand.\ntext: Corvin's at your door at midnight, bag over his shoulder. \"There's a sky-barge at the dock and nobody guarding it. Let's go. Tonight. The world's huge and we're wasting it in classrooms.\"\n> left: Stay and finish what you started\n  rel corvin -small\n  spark corvin +tiny\n  say: He looks at you for a long time. Then he drops the bag. \"Fine. But after graduation, we steal a boat. A big one.\"\n> right: Take his hand\n  end ending_runaway\n\n# ================= MIRA TEN =================\n\n@event mira_intro\ntitle: An Explosion in the West Wing\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nactor: mira\nwhen: not met(mira) and turn >= 2\nweight: 30\nart: gear\ntags: social\nscene: A cloud of purple smoke billowing from a workshop door, a soot-covered student emerging with goggles and a thumbs-up.\ntext: The west wing explodes. Not badly. A figure in goggles stumbles out of the purple smoke, coughing, and gives you a thumbs-up. \"Don't worry! That was intentional! Mostly! Can you hold this?\" They hand you a ticking teapot.\n> left: Put the teapot down and back away\n  meet mira\n  rel mira +tiny\n  say: The teapot stops ticking and starts humming the academy anthem. \"Mira,\" they say. \"They/them. Thanks for not throwing it.\"\n> right: Hold the teapot steady\n  meet mira\n  challenge brew trivial:\n    win:\n      rel mira +medium\n      say: You keep it level while they twist a valve. It stops ticking. \"You've got good hands,\" Mira says. \"I'm going to need those.\"\n    lose:\n      health -tiny\n      rel mira +medium\n      say: The teapot goes off. Your eyebrows survive, mostly. Mira is apologising and laughing at the same time.\n\n@event mira_owl_gift\ntitle: A Present With Gears\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: mira\nwhen: rel(mira) >= 18 and not has(familiar_owl)\nweight: 14\nart: owl\ntags: social magic\nscene: A small brass owl with glowing amber eyes perched on a student's wrist, its clockwork feathers fluffing.\ntext: Mira presents a brass owl with amber glass eyes. It blinks, hoots the hour and says \"HOMEWORK\" in a tinny voice. \"Made it for you. It remembers deadlines. And birthdays. And grudges, slightly.\"\n> left: It's too much\n  rel mira +small\n  say: Mira looks crushed, then rallies. \"I'll give it to Thorne. He needs reminding to smile.\" The owl, you hear, refuses to work for him.\n> right: Accept it gratefully\n  mood -tiny\n  gain familiar_owl\n  rel mira +medium\n  spark mira +tiny\n  say: The owl settles on your shoulder and says, \"WELCOME.\" Mira pretends to have something in their eye. At six the next morning, the owl says \"HOMEWORK.\" It says it every morning for the rest of the year.\n\n@event mira_fair\ntitle: The Artificers' Fair\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: mira\nwhen: rel(mira) >= 9 and month >= 6 and month <= 9\nweight: 12\nart: gear\ntags: social festival\nscene: A hall crammed with bizarre inventions — walking teapots, singing clocks — and one enormous brass machine draped in a sheet.\ntext: The Artificers' Fair. Mira's entry is under a sheet and occasionally growls. \"I need an assistant to stand next to it and look confident,\" they say. \"And possibly run.\"\n> left: Watch from a safe distance\n  mood +tiny\n  rel mira +tiny\n  say: The machine turns out to be a mechanical bear that bakes bread. It wins second place. Mira is ecstatic and flour-covered.\n> right: Be the confident assistant\n  challenge negotiate trivial:\n    win:\n      renown +medium\n      rel mira +medium\n      say: You present it like a showman. The bear bakes a perfect loaf and bows. First prize. Mira kisses the trophy, then, in a fluster, your cheek.\n      spark mira +tiny\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      renown +small\n      rel mira +small\n      say: The bear eats your sleeve. The crowd loves it. Third prize and a very good story.\n\n@event mira_workshop_closed\ntitle: The Workshop Is Shut\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: mira\nwhen: rel(mira) >= 12 and year >= 2\nweight: 10\nart: lock\ntags: social\nscene: A workshop door chained shut with a faculty notice nailed to it, a forlorn student sitting on a toolbox outside.\ntext: After the third fire this term, the faculty has padlocked Mira's workshop. Mira is sitting on a toolbox outside it, looking like someone took their heart and filed it under \"hazards\".\n> left: Plead their case to Professor Thorne\n  challenge negotiate trivial:\n    win:\n      rel mira +large\n      rel thorne +tiny\n      say: Thorne agrees to reopen it — with fire buckets and a weekly inspection. Mira cries on you. Oil and all.\n    lose:\n      rel mira +small\n      rel thorne -tiny\n      say: Thorne refuses. But Mira heard you try. \"Nobody's ever argued for me before,\" they say.\n> right: Help them build a secret workshop\n  challenge deceive trivial:\n    win:\n      rel mira +large\n      guile +tiny\n      say: An abandoned classroom behind a tapestry. You bring candles; Mira brings everything else. It's perfect. It's almost entirely fireproof.\n    lose:\n      rel mira +medium\n      gain detention\n      say: Bram finds the secret workshop on day two. Worth it, says Mira, scrubbing cauldrons beside you.\n\n@event mira_dance_machine\ntitle: The Dancing Machine\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: mira\nwhen: rel(mira) >= 6\nweight: 10\nart: music\ntags: social\nscene: A spindly brass contraption with too many legs attempting a waltz in an empty classroom, a student dancing with it.\ntext: Mira has built a machine that teaches dancing. It has six legs and opinions. \"It needs a test subject,\" they say. \"It's mostly safe. It only kicked me twice.\"\n> left: Make Mira test it\n  rel mira -tiny\n  mood +small\n  say: You watch Mira and the machine waltz, badly, across the room. It kicks them twice; Mira gives you a look. It's the funniest thing you've ever seen.\n> right: Dance with the machine\n  charm +small\n  health -tiny\n  rel mira +medium\n  say: It kicks you twice too. But by the end you can waltz, and Mira is clapping with oily hands.\n\n@event mira_confession\ntitle: Blueprint\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\ntrigger: social\nactor: mira\nwhen: spark(mira) >= 25 and rel(mira) >= 18 and bond(mira) != \"partner\"\nweight: 30\nart: gear\ntags: romance\nscene: A rolled-out blueprint on a workbench showing a heart-shaped mechanism labelled with tiny careful notes, a nervous artificer beside it.\ntext: Mira unrolls a blueprint. It's a mechanical heart, labelled in tiny careful handwriting: \"Does not work without the other part.\" They clear their throat. \"I'm bad at saying things. So I drew it. You're the other part. If you want.\"\n> left: Kindly say no\n  spark mira -large\n  rel mira +small\n  say: Mira rolls up the blueprint carefully. \"Good to know. Data is data.\" They're fine. They're mostly fine.\n> right: \"I want.\"\n  bond mira partner\n  spark mira +large\n  mood +large\n  say: The heart doesn't need to work. But Mira builds it anyway, and it ticks on your windowsill, in time with your own.\n\n@event mira_family_business\ntitle: The Family Clockworks\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: mira\nwhen: rel(mira) >= 15 and year >= 2 and not flag(mira_family_done)\nweight: 10\nart: gear\ntags: social\nscene: An artificer holding a stiff letter embossed with a gear crest, a train ticket tucked inside, staring at it blankly.\ntext: A letter from Mira's parents: the family clockworks needs them home. A ticket is enclosed. \"They think this is a hobby,\" Mira says. \"This. Is. Not. A. Hobby.\"\n> left: Tell them family comes first\n  flag mira_family_done\n  rel mira -small\n  say: Mira goes home for a month and comes back with a new scar and a clearer jaw. \"They know now,\" they say. \"It's not a hobby.\"\n> right: Help them write back\n  mood -tiny\n  flag mira_family_done\n  rel mira +large\n  charm +tiny\n  say: Together you write a letter so persuasive it should be framed. Mira's mother replies: \"Fine. Win a prize.\" Mira intends to win all of them.\n\n# ================= NESSA QUILL =================\n\n@event nessa_intro\ntitle: First Impressions\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nactor: nessa\nwhen: not met(nessa) and turn >= 2\nweight: 30\nart: paper\ntags: social\nscene: A girl with ink-stained fingers and a self-writing quill floating at her shoulder, holding a notebook out expectantly.\ntext: A girl with a quill floating at her shoulder plants herself in front of you. \"Nessa Quill, Lumen Ledger. First impressions of Lumenhold, go. Be interesting, please. The last six were boring.\"\n> left: Give a safe answer\n  meet nessa\n  rel nessa +tiny\n  say: The quill writes \"SAFE\" in the margin and underlines it twice. Nessa sighs and moves on.\n> right: Say something outrageous\n  meet nessa\n  challenge negotiate easy:\n    win:\n      renown +small\n      rel nessa +medium\n      say: You're quoted on the front page. Half the academy now thinks you're hilarious. The other half thinks you're dangerous. Nessa thinks both.\n    lose:\n      renown +small\n      rel nessa +tiny\n      mood -tiny\n      say: You are quoted. Out of context. Extremely out of context.\n\n@event nessa_gossip_column\ntitle: You're in the Gossip Column\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: nessa\nwhen: renown >= 15\nweight: 12\nart: paper\ntags: social\nscene: A copy of the Lumen Ledger pinned to a noticeboard, a gossip column circled in red ink, students gathered round giggling.\ntext: This week's Ledger gossip column features you, prominently, under the headline \"WHO IS THE MYSTERY STUDENT ALWAYS IN THE LIBRARY AT MIDNIGHT?\" Nessa swears she didn't write it.\n> left: Confront Nessa\n  rel nessa -tiny\n  challenge negotiate trivial:\n    win:\n      say: She prints a correction. Sort of. It's a much more flattering paragraph about you.\n      renown +small\n    lose:\n      say: \"Freedom of the press,\" says Nessa, sweetly. You both know she wrote it.\n> right: Lean into the mystery\n  chance 70:\n    renown +small\n    mood +small\n    rel nessa +small\n    say: You start leaving cryptic notes in the library. The column runs for six more weeks. Nessa is delighted.\n  else:\n    renown +tiny\n    mood -small\n    say: The column runs for six more weeks. By the fourth it has decided you are haunting the library, and people move tables when you sit down.\n\n@event nessa_source\ntitle: The Source\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: nessa\nwhen: rel(nessa) >= 12 and (member(library_assistant) or visits_to(courtyard) >= 3 or rel(thorne) >= 9)\nweight: 10\nart: feather\ntags: social\nscene: Two students in a dark corner of the library, one whispering, the other writing furiously in a notebook.\ntext: Nessa leans in. \"You've got faculty access. The professors are planning to change the exam format and they're hiding it. I just need a quote. Anonymous. Please?\"\n> left: Refuse — that's a breach of trust\n  rel nessa -small\n  rel thorne +small\n  say: Nessa sulks for a day. Then she respects you for it, which she hates.\n> right: Give her the scoop\n  rel nessa +large\n  renown +small\n  chance 30:\n    rel thorne -large\n    say: The story runs. Thorne knows exactly who talked. His glare could curdle milk.\n  else:\n    say: The story runs. Students are grateful. Nobody knows it was you. Nessa buys you cake.\n\n@event nessa_hollow_story\ntitle: Nessa Smells a Story\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\ntrigger: social\nactor: nessa\nwhen: clues(hollowstar) >= 1 and rel(nessa) >= 6\nweight: 12\nart: paper\ntags: social mystery hollowstar\nscene: A wall in the newspaper office covered in pinned clippings and red string connecting a frozen lake, a black star and a fur-coated boy.\ntext: Nessa's office wall is covered in red string. In the middle: a sketch of a black star. \"Frozen fountains. Missing pages. A transfer from the North. Something's going on and you know about it. Spill.\"\n> left: Warn her off — it's dangerous\n  rel nessa -tiny\n  say: \"That's what people say right before the best stories.\" But she takes the string down. Mostly.\n> right: Share what you know\n  rel nessa +medium\n  chance 40:\n    gain watched\n    say: Nessa's article runs with half the facts. The man in grey reads it. So does Bram, who starts following you around.\n  else:\n    say: Nessa's notes are scarily thorough. She's found a record of three students who vanished in 1723. All on Blossom moons.\n\n@event nessa_paper_threatened\ntitle: Stop the Presses\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: nessa\nwhen: rel(nessa) >= 9 and year >= 2\nweight: 10\nart: paper\ntags: social\nscene: A printing press with a faculty seal stamped across it, a defiant student standing in front of it with arms crossed.\ntext: The headmistress has threatened to shut down the Ledger after an article about faculty salaries. Nessa is organising a protest. \"I need people on the steps. Visible people. You.\"\n> left: Stay out of it\n  rel nessa -small\n  say: The protest is small. The Ledger survives, barely. Nessa notices who wasn't there.\n> right: Stand on the steps\n  rel nessa +large\n  rel isolde -small\n  renown +medium\n  say: Forty students on the steps, chanting. The headmistress comes out and, astonishingly, listens. The Ledger stays.\n\n@event nessa_confession\ntitle: Off the Record\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\ntrigger: social\nactor: nessa\nwhen: spark(nessa) >= 25 and rel(nessa) >= 18 and bond(nessa) != \"partner\"\nweight: 30\nart: feather\ntags: romance\nscene: A girl with an inkpot in one hand, her self-writing quill hovering above a blank page that she has commanded to stay blank.\ntext: Nessa puts down her quill. Then she tells it to stop writing. It's the first time you've seen it still. \"This is off the record,\" she says. \"Completely. I like you. I've drafted this eleven times. None of the drafts were good enough.\"\n> left: Gently say no\n  spark nessa -large\n  rel nessa +tiny\n  say: \"Noted,\" she says crisply. She keeps her quill still a moment longer. Then it starts writing again, and so does she.\n> right: Kiss her\n  bond nessa partner\n  spark nessa +large\n  mood +large\n  say: Her quill, unattended, writes a single word in the margin: \"FINALLY.\"\n"},{"path":"content/events/pools/castle_halls.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Baseline pools (phase 3, step 1b): castle_halls.\n#  Repeatable routine events for map visits to the castle's\n#  public halls: courtyard, library, refectory, lecture tower,\n#  great hall, infirmary, alchemy laboratory and job board.\n#  Spec: docs/authoring/pools.md.\n# ============================================================\n\n# ---------------- The Courtyard ----------------\n\n@event courtyard_sundial_bench\ntime: afternoon\ntitle: The Slow Sundial\nplace: courtyard\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: sun\ntags: study\nscene: A stone bench beside a weathered sundial in a flagstone courtyard, a student reading with their back to the dial.\ntext: {There's a bench by the old sundial, which runs eleven minutes slow.|Someone has scratched a tiny \"+11\" by the dial's noon. A fellow sufferer.|The sundial bench is yours by custom. A first-year asked if you came with it.} {season == \"winter\": Frost fills the hour lines, and the shadow skates across them.|{~Pigeons are holding a meeting on the gnomon.|A first-year checks a watch against it, horrified.|Someone has chalked a face where noon should be.}}\n> left: Read until the shadow moves\n  lore +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {season == \"winter\": {~Your fingers go numb by page two. The chapter is worth it.|You read in gloves, turning pages with your teeth.}|{~You finish a chapter in the minutes the sundial owes you.|A pigeon reads over your shoulder, unimpressed.|The shadow crawls; you outpace it by three pages.}}\n> right: Close your eyes a while\n  mood +small\n  renown -tiny\n  say: {~You wake with a chalk moustache and no suspects.|You wake to a prefect clearing his throat an inch from your ear.|You wake rested and late, eleven minutes late by the dial.}\n\n@event courtyard_saved_seat\ntime: afternoon\ntitle: A Seat on the Rim\nplace: courtyard\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: group\ntags: social\ncast:\n  here: present(place) where tagged(here, peer) and rel(here) >= 0\nscene: Two students sitting on the dry edge of a courtyard fountain, bags between them, pigeons circling overhead.\ntext: {{here} has claimed the dry stretch of the fountain rim and waves you over.|{here} is on your favourite stretch of the fountain rim, the one the sun reaches first, and waves you over.|Your stretch of the fountain rim is known now. {here} is sitting on it and shifts over without being asked.} {~Two towers' bells are arguing about the hour.|Pigeons circle like creditors.|{season == \"winter\": Your breath hangs between you.|Someone's lunch smells better than yours.}}\n> left: Sit down and catch up\n  social here\n  if npc_is(corvin):\n    say: {~Corvin tells you three stories, one of them true, and calls you \"saint\" when you don't ask which.|Corvin spots a prefect, palms something off the rim, and goes on talking as if nothing happened.}\n  elif npc_is(nessa):\n    say: {~Nessa interviews you about the weather, the soup and your love life, in that order, and writes down all three.|Nessa reads you tomorrow's headline and asks if it's too cruel. It is. She keeps it.}\n  elif npc_is(bram):\n    say: {~Bram lists every rule broken in the courtyard while you sit. Then, gruffly, he asks how your exams went.|Bram writes a demerit for a passing first-year, then tears it up when he thinks you aren't looking.}\n  else:\n    say: {~You trade the castle's news for an hour. {here} does most of the trading.|You talk until the shadows cross the fountain. Neither of you can remember about what.}\n> right: Wave, and keep walking\n  lore +small\n  rel here -tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~You promise next time. {here} looks as if {they} {are} counting.|You spend the hour with a book instead. It is a good book; it is not much company.}\n\n@event courtyard_seasonal_game\ntitle: The Courtyard Game\nplace: courtyard\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: group\ntags: social\nscene: Students playing a rowdy game across a flagstone courtyard while others crowd the edges shouting bets.\ntext: {season == \"winter\": A snowball war between Emberhall and Stillwater has reached the treaty stage.|{season == \"spring\": A charmed ball is loose, dodging everyone who kicks at it.|{season == \"summer\": It's too hot for sport, so it's catch with a charmed apple that bites.|It's conker season. The champion conker is said to be older than the fountain.}}} {Someone shouts for one more player.|They shout for you by name.|Both sides shout for you, and start bidding.}\n> left: Join in\n  challenge duel using vigor easy:\n    win:\n      renown +small\n      mood +small\n      health -tiny\n      say: {~Your side wins, and the result is disputed until supper.|You score the deciding point and are carried three whole steps before they drop you.}\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      mood +tiny\n      say: {~You are flattened, politely, by both sides at once.|You spend the rest of the game as the thing the other side aims at.}\n> right: Hold the coats and the bets\n  chance 50:\n    gold +small\n    say: {~The favourite loses. You are the only person in the courtyard who is pleased about it.|The underdog wins by a whisker, and the coats you are holding are suddenly lighter on your conscience.}\n  else:\n    gold -small\n    say: {~The favourite wins, and so does everyone who backed them, out of your pocket.|Everyone bet the same way, and everyone was right. You pay out in a hail of small coins.}\n\n# ---------------- The Library ----------------\n\n@event library_wandering_book\ntitle: The Book That Moved\nplace: library\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: book\ntags: study\nscene: Tall library shelves leaning very slightly, a gap where one book should be, a student on a ladder peering into it.\ntext: {Yesterday your book was on the third floor. Today the third floor has never heard of it.|Your book has moved again, leaving a bookmark in the gap, like a note.|You keep a map of where the shelves hide your books. They've started hiding the map.} {~In its place is a shelf of cookery spells.|In its place are bound weather reports.|In its place is one slim volume, face out, waiting.} {final_year: Soon they'll play these games with someone else.|}\n> left: Hunt it down\n  lore +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {You find it on the sixth floor, filed under \"regret\".|You find it in the stairwell, halfway between floors, as if it had been trying to leave.|You find it at once. Either you are learning the shelves, or they are getting lazy.}\n> right: Read what's in front of you\n  lore +tiny\n  mood +small\n  renown -tiny\n  say: {~You arrive at seminar having read the wrong book with great conviction.|You learn a great deal about something nobody will ever ask you about.|The wrong book turns out to be the right book for a question nobody asks until the exam.}\n\n@event library_shared_table\ntitle: The Last Free Chair\nplace: library\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: candle\ntags: social study\ncast:\n  here: present(place) where tagged(here, peer) and rel(here) >= 0\nscene: A crowded library reading room, every table full but one, a single empty chair facing a student absorbed in a book.\ntext: {The library is full. The only free chair is opposite {here}.|The only free chair is opposite {here}. A shelf has leaned over it, as if keeping it for you.|The library knows your habits now. The free chair, opposite {here}, has a book you wanted waiting on it.} {~Rain ticks on the tall windows.|A shelf nearby is quietly rearranging itself.|Footnote is asleep in the returns basket.|{season == \"winter\": The radiators clank like a ghost with a grudge.|Dust turns gold in the light.}}\n> left: Take the chair and work\n  rel here +small\n  lore +small\n  mood -tiny\n  if npc_is(kael):\n    say: {~Kael doesn't look up. By the end of the hour there is frost on your inkwell and a tiny, careful correction in your margin.|Kael pushes a page of his notes across to you, then looks away as if it had moved on its own.}\n  elif npc_is(lyra):\n    say: {~Lyra slides the candle an inch toward your side without looking up. Neither of you mentions it.|Lyra finishes before you, and waits, and says she wasn't waiting.}\n  elif npc_is(seraphine):\n    say: {~Seraphine writes a word in your notes that you won't understand until next week.|Seraphine reads the same page for an hour, smiling at something that isn't on it.}\n  elif npc_is(bram):\n    say: {~Bram informs you that you breathe too loudly. Then he lends you his ruler.|Bram underlines every rule in his book twice. Once, he asks your opinion of one.}\n  else:\n    say: {~You work in silence for an hour, which turns out to be a kind of company.|Neither of you speaks. Both of you get further than you would have alone.}\n> right: Whisper a question\n  rel here +small\n  mood +small\n  renown -tiny\n  say: {~The conversation is worth it. The look from the front desk has a long memory.|You get three sentences in before a shush arrives from two floors up.}\n\n@event library_overdue\ntitle: Overdue\nplace: library\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: book\ntags: study money\nscene: A student holding a book behind their back near a wicker returns basket, a cat on the front desk watching them.\ntext: {The book you borrowed in a hurry is overdue, and the library knows it. The spine has started to sigh.|Another overdue book. The front desk has begun keeping a little card with your name on it.|Your overdue books sigh in chorus now, and the card at the front desk has a second side.} {~Footnote watches you approach the returns basket with professional interest.|The front desk is, for once, unattended.|A queue of first-years is paying fines in small, sad coins.}\n> left: Own up and pay the fine\n  alignment +tiny\n  gold -small\n  lore +small\n  say: {The fine is paid; the desk, mollified, lends you the sequel.|You pay, and are lent another book, which you suspect is a trap.|The desk sighs, takes your coins and hands you a book it says you'll like. It is always right, which is the annoying part.}\n> right: Slip it into the basket\n  challenge deceive easy:\n    win:\n      guile +small\n      mood +tiny\n      say: {~The book goes in without a sound. It stops sighing at once, which feels like forgiveness.|You drop it in under cover of a sneeze. Footnote saw. Footnote will keep it to himself, for a price.}\n    lose:\n      gold -small\n      renown -tiny\n      mood -tiny\n      say: {~The book announces itself with a sigh like a dying organ. Everyone turns. You pay double.|It lands with a groan. The front desk adds a mark to your card without looking up.}\n\n# ---------------- The Refectory ----------------\n\n@event refectory_late_supper\ntime: evening\ntitle: Late Supper\nplace: refectory\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: meal\ntags: home\nscene: The far end of a long refectory table at night, most benches empty, a single student with a bowl and a half-written letter.\ntext: {You come in late. The benches are empty and the cooks are wiping the far tables.|The cook leaves a lamp lit at the end of the bench now, where the draught can't find you.|Your late bowl is waiting under a plate, with a spoon laid beside it. Nobody admits to it.} {year == 1: Home feels very far away tonight.|{final_year: You count the suppers you have left, and stop.|{~The candles are down to stubs.|Someone has left a half-done puzzle on the next table.}}}\n> left: Write a letter home\n  mood +small\n  gold -tiny\n  say: {~You write two pages and post them with the last of your coppers.|You write about the food. You do not write about anything else, but they'll know.|You write a letter that is mostly questions. You post it before you can make it braver.}\n> right: Revise over the bowl\n  lore +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~You learn three dates and a spill of gravy across all of them.|The cook reads over your shoulder and corrects one of your answers. She is right.}\n\n@event refectory_house_tables\ntime: evening\ntitle: Where to Sit\nplace: refectory\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: group\ntags: social\nscene: A crowded refectory at suppertime, a student standing with a tray between a loud house table and a quieter one of strangers.\ntext: {Supper, and the question of where to sit.|Your house table has started saving a gap on the bench, roughly your width.|The gap is a named seat now, and the quiet table nods at you too.} Your house table is {~loud with a story about a goat|in the middle of a pudding treaty|singing, badly}. At the far end, {season == \"winter\": some second-years from Rootward huddle round a warming charm.|some second-years from Duskgate are arguing quietly about a map.}\n> left: Squeeze in with your house\n  mood +small\n  renown +tiny\n  health -tiny\n  say: {~You eat with an elbow in your ribs and laugh the whole way through.|Somebody's story gets better every time you hear it. So does the pudding.}\n> right: Sit with the strangers\n  charm +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~They are polite, then curious, then actually rather good company.|It is awkward for exactly one course. By pudding, they have told you their secret map route.}\n\n@event refectory_pudding_tax\ntime: evening\ntitle: The Pudding Tax\nplace: refectory\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: cup\ntags: social\ncast:\n  friend: any where rel(friend) >= 6 and (tagged(friend, student) or tagged(friend, prefect))\nscene: Two friends at a refectory table, one guarding a bowl of treacle pudding, the other holding a spoon at the ready.\ntext: {{friend} sits down opposite you and looks at your pudding the way a cat looks at a fish.|Your pudding has a reputation now. {friend} sits down opposite you with a spoon already in hand.|Word of your pudding tax has got round the refectory. Tonight {friend} comes to collect, with a little receipt.} {season == \"winter\": Tonight it's treacle sponge, steaming.|{~Tonight it's apple crumble.|Tonight it's a custard that wobbles with intent.}}\n> left: Pay the pudding tax\n  rel friend +small\n  mood -small\n  if npc_is(tobias):\n    say: {~Tobias apologises four times, eats it all, then tells you a long story about a goat who did the same. It helps, somehow.|Tobias eats half, then pushes the bowl back with a sprig of mint from his pocket on top, as an apology.}\n  elif npc_is(lyra):\n    say: {~\"Efficient,\" Lyra says, finishing it. The next day a whole pudding appears on your tray. She doesn't mention it.|Lyra eats it in four exact bites and critiques the custard. She does not give it back.}\n  elif npc_is(mira):\n    say: {~Mira eats it while explaining a pudding-defence device that would probably work. Probably.|Mira eats it with a spoon they built themselves, which hums. They say the humming is probably normal.}\n  else:\n    say: {~{friend} eats exactly half and leaves you the burnt corner.|{friend} eats it slowly, savouring your defeat.}\n> right: Defend your pudding\n  challenge deceive easy:\n    win:\n      mood +small\n      rel friend -tiny\n      say: {~You hide it under a napkin and point at the window. It works. {friend} will remember this.|You claim it's been sneezed on. {friend} retreats in disgust, and you eat in peace.}\n    lose:\n      mood -tiny\n      rel friend +tiny\n      say: {~{friend} sees straight through you and takes the whole thing as a penalty. It is hard to stay cross.|{friend} distracts you with a story and the bowl is empty before it ends.}\n\n# ---------------- The Lecture Tower ----------------\n\n@event lecture_tower_old_board\ntitle: Someone Else's Lecture\nplace: lecture_tower\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: scroll\ntags: study\nscene: An empty spiral lecture hall, a blackboard still dense with chalk diagrams from a class that has long gone.\ntext: {term: {The fourth-floor hall is empty, but someone else's lecture is still on the board.|In the empty hall, a stub of chalk waits on the ledge, as if for you.|The board has a corner fenced off in chalk, labelled \"answers here\".}|In the break the boards are wiped, except one nobody dares touch.} {~Today it's a ward diagram, labelled \"wrong, but how?\"|Today it's half a proof, ending in a thumbprint.|Today it's a list headed \"on the exam\", crossed out.}\n> left: Copy it all down\n  arcana +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~Your hand cramps. Your head, for once, does not.|You copy it all and understand most of it. The rest you'll grow into.}\n> right: Add your own answer\n  chance 60:\n    renown +small\n    say: {~Next time, someone has added \"yes!\" under your line.|Someone has answered your answer. The board has become a correspondence.|By the afternoon someone has drawn a box around your line, to keep it safe.}\n  else:\n    renown -tiny\n    mood -tiny\n    say: {~The professor whose board it was finds you holding the chalk. \"Hm,\" says the look.|Your answer is wiped by the time you reach the door, by someone who knew better.}\n\n@event lecture_tower_stair_question\ntitle: One Question a Flight\nplace: lecture_tower\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: tower\ntags: study\ncast:\n  prof: thorne\nscene: A narrow spiral stair inside a stone tower, a severe professor climbing ahead with a stack of papers, a student a step behind.\ntext: {You meet Professor Thorne on the spiral stair. He allows one question per flight, and climbs fast.|Professor Thorne on the stair. He has slowed down by about one step in seven, which is how you know he expects you.|Thorne hands you half his stack of papers at the foot of the stair, without a word, and starts climbing.} {term: |The tower is half empty for the break; your footsteps echo twice.}{final_year: There are not many of these climbs left.|}\n> left: Ask a hard question\n  challenge exam using arcana medium:\n    win:\n      arcana +small\n      rel prof +tiny\n      say: {~He answers in four words, and the four words are enough.|\"Hm,\" he says, then answers it properly, all the way to the seventh floor.}\n    lose:\n      arcana +tiny\n      mood -small\n      say: {~He answers with a harder question and leaves you on the landing.|\"Wrong question,\" he says. \"Find the right one by Thursday.\"}\n> right: Climb in silence beside him\n  rel prof +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {~He climbs faster than you. At the top he says, \"Adequate,\" and you are fairly sure he means the climbing.|Neither of you says a word for seven flights. It is oddly restful, apart from your legs.}\n\n@event lecture_tower_scorched_room\ntitle: The Scorched Room\nplace: lecture_tower\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: flame\ntags: magic danger\nscene: A practice room on a lower floor of a stone tower, walls streaked with scorch marks, a sign by the door forbidding unsupervised casting.\ntext: {The practice room on the Evocation floor is empty. A sign forbids unsupervised casting.|The scorched room is empty. Since your last visit, someone has underlined the sign.|The scorched room. The sign is underlined three times now, and signed by the headmistress.} {~A fresh scorch mark is shaped like a surprised face.|Someone has left a bucket of sand by the door.|{season == \"winter\": The walls are the only warm thing here.|The air smells faintly of singed eyebrow.}}\n> left: Practise anyway\n  mana -small\n  chance 70:\n    arcana +small\n    say: {~Your spell lands clean in the centre of the oldest scorch mark. You leave no evidence.|You practise until the flame does what you mean, not what you say.}\n  else:\n    health -small\n    renown -tiny\n    say: {~A new scorch mark joins the wall, shaped very like you.|The spell comes back at you. You leave with your eyebrows, mostly.}\n> right: Work through the theory\n  lore +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~You draw the spell on paper fifty times. Paper is very forgiving.|You learn exactly why the walls look like that, and resolve not to add to them.}\n\n# ---------------- The Great Hall ----------------\n\n@event great_hall_tomorrow_sky\ntitle: Tomorrow's Weather\nplace: great_hall\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: candle\ntags: magic\nscene: An empty great hall in the evening, floating candles drifting under a vaulted ceiling that shows a different sky from the windows.\ntext: {The great hall is empty but for the candles. The ceiling shows tomorrow.|One candle drifts over to you and hovers at reading height.|Your candle meets you at the door now and leads you to your bench.} {season == \"winter\": Tomorrow, it says, it will snow.|{season == \"spring\": Tomorrow, a storm with bad manners.|{season == \"summer\": Tomorrow is cloudless, stars and all.|Tomorrow is fog, rolling across the vaults.}}} {final_year: There are fewer tomorrows here now.|}\n> left: Lie on a bench and watch\n  mood +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {~You watch tomorrow until your neck stiffens. It is worth it.|A candle drifts down to see if you are all right, and stays.|You fall half asleep and dream tomorrow a little early.}\n> right: Read by the floating candles\n  lore +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~A candle drips wax onto the best paragraph. You read it anyway, through the wax.|The candles follow your page like curious moths. It is the best light in the castle.}\n\n@event great_hall_choir_rehearsal\ntime: evening\ntitle: The Choir Rehearses\nplace: great_hall\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: music\ntags: social\nscene: A choir rehearsing on the stage of a candlelit great hall, a few singers floating a foot off the boards, the benches mostly empty.\ntext: {The choir is rehearsing on the stage, a foot off the floor, and one voice short.|Still a voice short. The conductor has left a spare songbook on your bench.|The spare songbook has your name pencilled in it, and the alto line marked in red.} {season == \"winter\": They are learning the Midwinter music.|{~Tonight it's a round that nobody can find the end of.|Tonight it's a lament, sung far too cheerfully.}}\n> left: Listen from the back\n  mood +small\n  gold -tiny\n  say: {~The collection hat comes round. It is worth every coin.|You listen until the candles gutter. The hat comes round twice.}\n> right: Fill the missing voice\n  challenge negotiate using charm easy:\n    win:\n      renown +small\n      charm +tiny\n      say: {~You hit every note but one. The conductor says it was the best one.|You float a little off the floor on the last chord. Nobody mentions it, which is how you know.}\n    lose:\n      renown -tiny\n      mood -tiny\n      say: {~You come in confidently, in the wrong key, in the wrong song. The choir is very kind about it, which is worse.|You sing the round in a straight line and finish alone. The conductor thanks you for your enthusiasm.}\n\n@event great_hall_high_table_dare\ntitle: The High Table Dare\nplace: great_hall\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: crown\ntags: social danger\nscene: An empty high table on a dais at the end of a dark great hall, a carved chair at its centre, students daring each other from the doorway.\ntext: {Someone dares you to sit in the headmistress's chair at the empty high table.|The dare comes round again, and someone has started keeping a tally in chalk on the doorframe.|The tally on the doorframe has your column and everyone else's. First-years dare each other to dare you.} {~The chair is still warm.|A cushion embroidered with an owl sits on it.|Her teacup has been left behind, still steaming, which is worse.}\n> left: Take the dare\n  challenge deceive easy:\n    win:\n      renown +small\n      mood +small\n      say: {~You sit, cross your legs, and sip the air. The doorway erupts.|You sit for a full minute. The chair is surprisingly comfortable, and very slightly warm.}\n    lose:\n      renown -small\n      say: {~As you sit, a quiet, delighted laugh comes from the dark beyond the dais. You leave very fast.|A portrait above the table clears its throat. You have never moved so fast in your life.}\n> right: Tell them you value your life\n  renown -tiny\n  mood +tiny\n  say: {~They call you a coward, fondly. You keep your dignity, and your place at the academy.|\"Coward,\" they chant. You bow. It seems the safest thing to do in that hall.}\n\n# ---------------- The Infirmary ----------------\n\n@event infirmary_spare_bed\ntitle: The Spare Bed\nplace: infirmary\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: bed\ntags: home\nscene: A row of white infirmary beds under a high arched window, one turned down, a stern healer pointing at it.\ntext: {You only came in for a plaster.|Your name is on the slate by the end bed, in pencil.|Your name on the slate is in ink now.} {health < 40: Sister Amaranth takes one look at you and points at the spare bed.|Sister Amaranth decides, over her spectacles, that you'll live.} {~It smells of lavender and singed wool.|Two beds down, someone hiccups in a minor key.|{season == \"winter\": A warming charm hums under every blanket.|The curtains breathe.}}\n> left: Lie down for an hour\n  mood +small\n  health +small\n  gold -tiny\n  say: {~You sleep the deep, dreamless sleep of the infirmary. The tonic afterwards costs a crown and tastes of regret.|You wake under a blanket you didn't pull up. There is a tonic on the side, and a bill under it.}\n> right: Help roll bandages\n  alchemy +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~Sister Amaranth teaches you the knot she uses. You will never tie your boots any other way.|You roll bandages and listen to her opinion of duelling, which is long and specific.}\n\n@event infirmary_visiting_hour\ntitle: Visiting Hour\nplace: infirmary\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: cross\ntags: social\ncast:\n  friend: any where rel(friend) >= 6 and (tagged(friend, student) or tagged(friend, prefect))\nscene: A friend propped up in an infirmary bed by a window, a visitor on a stool beside them with a paper bag of grapes.\ntext: {Visiting hour. {friend} is in the bed by the window,|Visiting hour. Sister Amaranth waves you to the stool you used last time. Today it's {friend},|Visiting hour. The stool by the window has worn to your shape. Today it's {friend},} {~nursing a duelling bruise the colour of plums.|recovering from a draught of hiccups that hiccups back.|wrapped in three blankets against a lake chill.} {They} brighten{s} when you come in.\n> left: Sit and keep {them} company\n  social friend\n  say: {~You bring grapes and eat most of them yourself. {friend} doesn't mind.|You stay until Sister Amaranth sweeps you out. {friend} waves from the pillows.}\n> right: Smuggle in a pastry\n  chance 60:\n    rel friend +small\n    mood +tiny\n    say: {~The pastry makes it past the desk. {friend} eats it under the blanket like a crime.|You hide the pastry in a hollowed-out book. {friend} is delighted by both.}\n  else:\n    rel friend +tiny\n    renown -tiny\n    say: {~Sister Amaranth confiscates the pastry and eats it herself, slowly, while looking at you.|Sister Amaranth smells the pastry from across the ward and holds out her hand without a word.}\n\n@event infirmary_tonic_cupboard\ntitle: The Tonic Cupboard\nplace: infirmary\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: potion\ntags: danger\nscene: A tall glass-fronted cupboard of coloured tonics in an empty infirmary, its door ajar, a teacup steaming on the healer's desk.\ntext: {The tonic cupboard is open, and Sister Amaranth is in the back having her tea.|The tonic cupboard is open at teatime. A new label on the door reads \"counted daily\".|At teatime the cupboard is open, and the label now reads \"counted daily, by name\". The bright blue bottles have moved to the front.} {mana < 20: Your head aches with the dry ache of spent magic.|{~The bottles clink in the draught.|A label reads, in her handwriting, \"absolutely not\".}}\n> left: Help yourself to a tonic\n  challenge deceive easy:\n    win:\n      mana +small\n      health +tiny\n      guile +tiny\n      say: {~You drink it in the corridor. It tastes of peppermint and getting away with things.|You pocket the bottle, rinse it later, and put it back full of water. You feel slightly worse about that.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      renown -tiny\n      say: {~\"That one is for goats,\" says Sister Amaranth from the doorway. She makes you drink the right one, which is worse.|Sister Amaranth's teacup appears at your elbow. \"Put it back,\" she says, \"and sit down.\"}\n> right: Ask for one properly\n  gold -small\n  health +small\n  mana +tiny\n  say: {She charges you, lectures you, and gives you the good one.|She charges you, lectures you a little less, and gives you the good one.|\"You again,\" she says, and hands it over before you ask.}\n\n# ---------------- The Alchemy Laboratory ----------------\n\n@event alchemy_lab_spare_cauldron\ntitle: A Spare Cauldron\nplace: alchemy_lab\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: cauldron\ntags: study magic\nscene: A long laboratory bench in the afternoon light, a small cauldron free among larger ones, jars of ingredients lined up beside it.\ntext: {There is a spare cauldron on the second bench, unclaimed.|The spare cauldron has a chalk mark on its rim like your initial.|Your cauldron, everyone calls it. Someone has scratched your initial into the handle.} {~Someone has left a ladle standing upright in something.|The bench smells of mint and burnt sugar.|{season == \"summer\": The heat makes every brew twice as eager.|The windows have steamed over with other people's experiments.}}\n> left: Brew a practice batch\n  challenge brew easy:\n    win:\n      alchemy +small\n      mood +tiny\n      say: {~The brew turns the right colour at the right moment. It feels like being trusted.|It comes out clear and smells of nothing, which is exactly right.}\n    lose:\n      alchemy +tiny\n      health -tiny\n      mood -tiny\n      say: {~The brew turns a colour you have never seen. So do your eyebrows.|It foams over and sets, firmly, around your sleeve.}\n> right: Scrub the cauldrons for crowns\n  gold +tiny\n  alchemy +tiny\n  mood -small\n  say: {~You learn more about failed potions from their residue than from any lecture.|You scrub until the bench is clean. Then you scratch your own initials into it, small, under the rim.}\n\n@event alchemy_lab_third_floor\ntitle: The Third-Floor Fumes\nplace: alchemy_lab\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: bottle\ntags: social magic\nscene: A crowded upper floor of an alchemy laboratory, coloured vapour hanging under the ceiling, students working with handkerchiefs tied over their faces.\ntext: {On the third floor the ventilation charm has given up, and the fumes have taken charge.|Someone has hung a sign by the stairs giving the fumes' mood of the day.|The fumes' sign has a column for forecasts now, in several hands.} {~Everyone is speaking in rhyme and trying to hide it.|Everyone is laughing at nothing and cannot stop.|Everyone is being unusually honest about everyone else's hair.} {season == \"winter\": The windows are frozen shut.|}\n> left: Stay and work in the fug\n  alchemy +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {~You get a great deal done and agree with everyone about everything.|You leave with good notes and a headache that hums.}\n> right: Force the windows open\n  vigor +tiny\n  renown +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {season == \"winter\": {~The ice cracks. Half the floor cheers; half complains of the cold.|The frozen latch gives, and so does your thumbnail.}|{~The windows give. The fumes leave, sulking, and the floor applauds.|You wedge the window with a textbook. The fumes drift out, and nearly the textbook too.}}\n\n@event alchemy_lab_unlabelled_jar\ntitle: The Unlabelled Jar\nplace: alchemy_lab\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: potion\ntags: magic danger\nscene: A dusty shelf of ingredient jars in an alchemy laboratory, one jar with no label glowing faintly, a student reaching for it.\ntext: {Someone has left an unlabelled jar on the shared shelf. It glows, faintly.|Another unlabelled jar on the shared shelf, beside the cautious label someone wrote for the last one.|The shared shelf now has a row of careful labels, and one new jar at the end of it, waiting its turn.} {~It smells of thunder.|Something inside it turns over when you look.|It is warm to the touch.} {gpa >= 3: You could probably identify it, given time.|You have no idea what it is.}\n> left: Add a pinch to your brew\n  mana -tiny\n  chance 50:\n    mana +medium\n    alchemy +tiny\n    say: {~The brew turns gold and so do you, briefly. You feel enormous.|The brew sings a single note and leaves you humming with power.}\n  else:\n    health -small\n    say: {~The brew ignites, politely, and then impolitely.|Your cauldron sneezes. So, for the rest of the afternoon, do you.}\n> right: Label it, carefully\n  lore +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~An hour of tests later, you write a cautious label. Someone adds a skull to it.|You work out what it is. Then you wash your hands three times.}\n\n# ---------------- The Job Board ----------------\n\n@event job_board_odd_jobs\ntitle: Odd Jobs\nplace: job_board\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: paper\ntags: job money\nscene: A cork board in an entrance hall, a small notice pinned at an angle under the official job postings, a student reading it with a frown.\ntext: {Under the proper postings, someone has pinned an odd job.|Under the proper postings is an odd job, and a second pin someone has left beside it, for answers.|The odd-jobs corner has a card addressed to you by name, in a hand you're starting to recognise.} {~Someone wants a homesick portrait read to.|Someone wants a ladder held, no questions asked.|Someone wants a reluctant gargoyle walked.} {term: |In the break the board is half bare, and the odd jobs pay a little better.}\n> left: Take the job\n  gold +small\n  mood -tiny\n  health -tiny\n  say: {~It takes all afternoon and pays exactly what it said, which is rarer than it should be.|It is stranger than the notice suggested, and pays slightly less.}\n> right: Haggle first\n  challenge negotiate easy:\n    win:\n      gold +small\n      charm +tiny\n      mood -tiny\n      say: {~You haggle the fee up by half. Your employer seems to respect you more for it.|You agree a price and a biscuit. The biscuit turns out to be the better half of the deal.}\n    lose:\n      renown -tiny\n      mood -tiny\n      say: {~Your employer takes the notice down and pins up a new one, asking for \"someone less difficult\".|The job goes to a first-year who asked for nothing. They seem very happy with it.}\n\n@event job_board_lost_familiars\ntitle: Lost and Found\nplace: job_board\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: cat\ntags: social\nscene: The corner of a cork board crowded with hand-drawn posters of lost familiars and a ransom note for a sock.\ntext: {The corner of the board for lost things is busier than the jobs.|An old poster in the lost-things corner now reads \"found, thanks to a student\".|The lost-things corner has thank-you notes now. One draws the rescuer with a halo.} {~A toad called \"Sir\" is missing, last seen being brave.|A ransom note demands three biscuits for one sock.|A small owl is lost, answers to nothing, and bites.} {season == \"winter\": Half the familiars have simply gone somewhere warm.|}\n> left: Go looking for it\n  chance 50:\n    gold +small\n    mood +small\n    say: {~You find it behind a radiator in the east tower, deeply unrepentant. The reward is small and grateful.|You find it, and the owner cries, and the reward is mostly biscuits.}\n  else:\n    health -tiny\n    mood -tiny\n    say: {~You search for an hour and find only a different, much angrier creature.|You don't find it. The next day the poster says \"Found! Thanks anyway.\"}\n> right: Read every notice twice\n  guile +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~The board is the castle's diary. You learn who lost what, and who owes whom.|You learn three secrets from the small print, and who has terrible handwriting.}\n"},{"path":"content/events/pools/castle_towers.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Baseline pools: the castle towers (phase 3, step 1b).\n#  Repeatable routine events for the observatory, Mira's workshop,\n#  the Ledger office, the headmistress's tower, the Restricted\n#  Section, the third gargoyle and the seventh tower, plus the\n#  hidden-course hints for artifice, starcalling and hollow studies.\n#  No clues, no arc moves: these places stay mysterious.\n# ============================================================\n\n# ---------------- The Observatory ----------------\n\n@event observatory_night_log\ntime: evening night\ntitle: The Night Log\nplace: observatory\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: book\ntags: study magic\nscene: A brass observatory dome at night, one student writing in a huge logbook by lamplight while the great telescope swings round on its own.\ntext: {The observatory keeps a night log, and anyone may write in it.|Since your last entry, someone has drawn a moustache on the moon.|You know the log's hands by now: the careful one, the frantic one, the one who only ever writes \"cloudy\".} {season == \"winter\": Frost feathers the inside of the dome.|{~The telescope turns, unasked, towards the north.|A draught turns the pages for you.|The lamp is low on oil, as always.}}\n> left: Log what the telescope finds\n  lore +small\n  health -tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~You chart a star that wasn't there last month. The log already has it, in a hand forty years older.|Three hours, two pages and one sneeze that smudges a constellation.|The telescope settles on a quiet blue star and waits until you've written it down properly.}\n> right: Read the old entries instead\n  mood +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {~A winter entry from long ago says \"Saw nothing. Stayed anyway. Worth it.\"|A love letter written across a page of moon sightings. Very bad. Very sweet.|{final_year: You find your own first entry and barely know the hand.|A student from your year logged your star a week before you did, with a small drawn smile.}}\n\n@event observatory_one_star_each\ntime: evening night\ntitle: One Star Each\nplace: observatory\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: star\ntags: social magic\ncast:\n  here: present(place) where tagged(here, peer)\nscene: Two students at the eyepiece of a great brass telescope, one stepping aside to let the other look.\ntext: {The rule chalked on the observatory door says one star each, then pass it on. {here} has been at the eyepiece for some time.|Someone has added \"two if they're small\" under the chalk rule. {here} is at the eyepiece.|Someone is always at the telescope when you arrive; tonight it's {here}.} {season == \"summer\": The nights are short and the queue is impatient.|{~The dome is cold enough to see your breath.|The telescope creaks round to follow something.|Below, the bridges of light are going out one by one.}}\n> left: Share the eyepiece\n  rel here +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {cast_as(here, seraphine): {~\"That one,\" Seraphine says, \"is going to matter in, oh.\" She doesn't finish.|\"Left of it,\" Seraphine says. \"Next spring's left.\" You look anyway.}|{cast_as(here, kael): {~Kael steps aside and points. When your hands get cold, he stands closer.|Kael says the star's name in a Northern word, once, and doesn't explain.}|You take turns until neither of you remembers whose star it was.}}\n> right: Wait your turn properly\n  lore +small\n  mood -tiny\n  rel here -tiny\n  say: {cast_as(here, seraphine): {~Seraphine leaves mid-sentence. \"You'll want it,\" she says. \"Later. Now, I mean.\"|\"Yours is the next one,\" Seraphine says, and it is.}|{cast_as(here, kael): {~Kael yields with a formal nod and goes out onto the roof. Frost follows.|Kael waits behind you. When you turn round, he's gone.}|By your turn the star has set. The next one is nearly as good.}}\n\n@event observatory_roof_ledge\ntitle: The Roof Ledge\nplace: observatory\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: moon\ntags: life danger\nscene: A student sitting on a narrow ledge outside a brass dome, legs dangling over the castle roofs, the lake far below.\ntext: {A window in the dome opens onto the roof, and onto a ledge that everyone swears they have never sat on.|Someone else who never sits there has oiled the latch.|The ledge. You know where the loose tile is now.} {season == \"winter\": The tiles are white with frost.|{~The lake below is a second sky.|A warm wind comes up from the village.|Someone's scarf is snagged on the gutter.}}\n> left: Climb out onto the ledge\n  challenge exam using vigor easy:\n    win:\n      mood +small\n      lore +tiny\n      say: {~You sit with the whole sky to yourself until the stars begin to wheel.|The castle looks small from here, and kind, and very old.}\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      mood -tiny\n      say: {~The loose tile is loose in a new place. You slide, grab, and hang on, and climb back in with skinned palms.|A gust takes your balance and most of your dignity. The gutter keeps the rest.}\n> right: Watch through the glass\n  lore +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~You see half the sky and all of the smudges.|Through the glass the stars look like they're behind a shop window. You can look; you can't have.}\n\n@event observatory_scraped_word\ntime: evening night\ntitle: The Scraped-Out Word\nplace: observatory\nwhen: not enrolled(starcalling) and not passed(starcalling, 1)\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nrepeat: 8\nweight: 6\nart: star\ntags: study mystery\nscene: An old observatory logbook open by lamplight, the same word scraped out of line after line.\ntext: {In the night log's oldest volume, every entry by a student who had finished Advanced Astromancy uses the same word, and every time it has been scraped out.|You've worked out that the missing word was something you did to a star, not something you saw.|You know these pages now. The word is gone; the shape of the work around it is not.} {~In one margin, \"Ask. Be told.\"|A pressed flower falls out.|The lamp gutters as you turn the page.}\n> left: Trace the gaps by lamplight\n  find heard_of_starcalling\n  lore +small\n  mana -small\n  say: {~The words around the gaps say \"called\", \"named\", \"down\". Whoever taught it taught it aloud, to people who had finished Advanced Astromancy.|\"...and the light came down when I said its name...\" Then the gap. You will need someone to tell you the word.}\n> right: Ask the telescope, half joking\n  lore +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~The telescope swings to one bright star and stays there, patient as a teacher who knows you aren't ready.|It turns to a named star and holds it. You get the feeling you have to finish Advanced Astromancy before it explains.}\n\n# ---------------- Mira's Workshop ----------------\n\n@event workshop_spare_bench\ntitle: The Spare Bench\nplace: workshop\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: gear\ntags: magic life\nscene: A cluttered workshop bench under a hand-painted sign, a music box in pieces beside a jar of springs, fire buckets by the door.\ntext: {Mira keeps a spare bench for visitors, under a sign that says TOUCH THINGS (CAREFULLY).|The sign over the spare bench has grown a second line, (MORE CAREFULLY).|Your bench, as Mira has started calling it, has a jar of springs waiting.} {~Mira waves a spanner at you without looking up.|Something on a high shelf is ticking.|{season == \"winter\": The forge-lamp makes it the warmest room in the west wing.|The window is open to let the smoke out.}}\n> left: Mend a jammed music box\n  alchemy +small\n  mood -tiny\n  health -tiny\n  say: {~It plays again, a little too fast, like it's late for something.|You fix the tune and break the lid. Mira says that's probably progress.|It plays a waltz nobody has heard in years. You leave it on the sill to finish.}\n> right: Just watch Mira work\n  mood +small\n  rel mira +tiny\n  health -tiny\n  say: {~Mira explains a gear train for an hour. You follow about a third, which they say is probably a record.|You sit by the fire buckets and watch the sparks. Your eyes sting. It's a good afternoon.|Mira hands you things to hold and forgets you're holding them. It's oddly restful.}\n\n@event workshop_volunteer\ntitle: A Volunteer With Hands\nplace: workshop\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: gear\ntags: social magic\ncast:\n  maker: mira\nscene: An artificer in soot-streaked goggles holding out a strange brass contraption, a fire bucket waiting hopefully on the floor.\ntext: {\"Perfect timing! I need a volunteer and you have hands!\" {maker} holds up {~a pair of boots that walk on their own|a hat that remembers where you left things|goggles that see round corners}.|{maker} has a new invention and a familiar look. Today: {~boots that walk on their own|a hat with a memory|goggles for corners}.|You don't ask anymore. You just look at what {maker} is holding, then at the fire bucket.}\n> left: Try it on\n  chance 60:\n    alchemy +small\n    rel maker +tiny\n    mood +tiny\n    say: {~It works! For eleven glorious minutes, it works, and {maker} writes down every one of them.|It does exactly what it should. {maker} looks almost suspicious and walks round it three times.}\n  else:\n    health -small\n    rel maker +small\n    say: {~It works in the wrong direction. You spend a while upside down. {maker} apologises at length, with diagrams.|There's a bang, a smell of singed eyebrow, and {maker} saying \"Interesting!\" in a voice that means sorry.}\n> right: Hold the fire bucket\n  rel maker +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~You hold the bucket. You don't need the bucket. {maker} looks faintly disappointed.|You need the bucket. Briefly, splendidly, you need the bucket.}\n\n@event workshop_stirring_cauldron\ntitle: The Self-Stirring Cauldron\nplace: workshop\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: cauldron\ntags: magic danger\nscene: A small cauldron in a workshop corner stirring itself with a wooden spoon, a stern paper note pinned to its rim.\ntext: {The self-stirring cauldron in the corner wears a note. DO NOT ADJUST. IT KNOWS WHAT IT DID.|Someone has underlined the cauldron's note twice and added the word STILL.|The cauldron's spoon pauses when you come in, as if it remembers you.} {~It's going anticlockwise, which it isn't supposed to.|It smells of toffee and lightning.|{season == \"spring\": Someone has put flowers in it. It is stirring them.|It's stirring soup for nobody.}}\n> left: Adjust the enchantment\n  challenge enchant easy:\n    win:\n      alchemy +small\n      mood +tiny\n      say: {~It settles into a slow, contented clockwise. Mira, passing, says \"Oh!\" and then \"Huh,\" and then \"Teach me.\"|The spoon taps the rim twice, like a thank-you, and gets on with it.}\n    lose:\n      health -tiny\n      mood -small\n      say: {~It stirs your sleeve, then you, then your sleeve again.|It speeds up instead. The ceiling now has toffee on it, and so do you.}\n> right: Take notes from a safe distance\n  lore +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~You write down everything it's doing wrong. The list is long and slightly envious.|You sketch the rune on its base. The cauldron splashes your page, on purpose you think.}\n\n@event workshop_glass_cabinet\ntitle: The Glass Cabinet\nplace: workshop\nwhen: not enrolled(artifice) and not passed(artifice, 1)\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 8\nweight: 6\nart: gear\ntags: study magic\nscene: A tall glass cabinet at the back of a workshop, clockwork animals inside it, a faded card propped against the glass.\ntext: {At the back of the workshop is a glass cabinet that isn't Mira's, full of faculty machines, very still.|A clockwork mouse in the glass cabinet has moved since your last visit, and now faces the door.|The cabinet. Mira says the machines in it think, probably, and are waiting for the right student.} A card in old ink: \"Artifice. By invitation, to those who have passed Alchemy II and Evocation II.\"\n> left: Study the machines through the glass\n  alchemy +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~Nothing moves while you watch. The moment you look away, something ticks.|You count the gears in a brass beetle and lose count. Somebody made this who could make things and make them burn.}\n> right: Try the cabinet lock\n  challenge deceive easy:\n    win:\n      guile +small\n      mood +tiny\n      say: {~The lock gives. The mouse winds itself up, bows, and goes back to sleep. You close the door very quietly.|The door swings open an inch, and every machine inside turns its head to look at you. You close it again, politely.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      renown -tiny\n      say: {~The cabinet shrieks like a kettle. Mira is delighted. The corridor is not.|The lock bites your pick and keeps it. Mira says it does that, probably, to people it likes.}\n\n# ---------------- The Lumen Ledger Office ----------------\n\n@event ledger_office_unprintable\ntitle: Letters We Will Not Print\nplace: ledger_office\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: letter\ntags: life\nscene: A wicker basket overflowing with letters by the door of a cramped newspaper office, front pages papering the walls.\ntext: {By the Ledger's door sits a basket marked LETTERS WE WILL NOT PRINT. Visitors may read them.|The unprintable basket has overflowed into a second basket, which is also full.|You are a connoisseur of the unprintable basket now.} {~On top is a complaint about the soup, in verse.|One is just a drawing of a prefect, unflattering and accurate.|{season == \"winter\": Someone demands that snow be cancelled.|Someone asks who keeps moving the stairs.}}\n> left: Read the whole basket\n  mood +tiny\n  say: {~The best one is an apology to a goat.|One is from a first-year who misses home. You put it back gently.|{final_year: A letter in your own first-year hand. You don't remember sending it. You were right.|Three letters about the same broken window, all blaming different people.}}\n> right: Write one of your own\n  chance 35:\n    renown +small\n    mood +tiny\n    say: {~They print it. Two people quote it at you at breakfast; one of them disagrees with it loudly.|It runs on the back page, under your initials. You feel oddly famous for a day.}\n  else:\n    mood -tiny\n    say: {~It goes in the basket. By next week, someone is reading it aloud.|It's rejected with a note: \"Too sensible.\" You aren't sure that's a criticism.}\n\n@event ledger_office_deadline_night\ntitle: Deadline Night\nplace: ledger_office\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: paper\ntags: social\ncast:\n  here: present(place) where tagged(here, peer)\nscene: A cramped office lit by one lamp, a groaning printing press, two students folding newspapers into tall stacks.\ntext: {The press is groaning like a dragon on deadline, and {here} eyes you like a captain eyes a spare oar.|{here} hands you a stack before you've said hello, and a rag for your fingers.|You take a stack and start folding. {here} doesn't look up, which is a compliment.} {~The ink on the front page is still wet.|A headline has a spelling mistake nobody will admit to.|{final_year: You've folded this paper for years now.|The press shudders as if it's thinking of stopping.}}\n> left: Fold until the press stops\n  rel here +small\n  health -small\n  say: {cast_as(here, nessa): {~\"Good. Fast. Again,\" says Nessa. From her, that's a sonnet.|Nessa folds twice as fast as you and reads every page as she goes.}|{~By dawn your fingers are grey with ink, and you know tomorrow's news before anyone.|You fold until the stacks are taller than you. Then you fold the ones that fell over.}}\n> right: Make your excuses\n  mood +small\n  rel here -small\n  renown -tiny\n  say: {cast_as(here, nessa): {~\"Deserter,\" Nessa calls. \"Noted. Quoted. Forgiven by Thursday.\"|Nessa doesn't look up. \"Coward,\" she says, almost fondly, and writes something down.}|{~You slip away before the second stack. The press groans after you, reproachfully.|You plead an essay. Nobody believes you. Tomorrow's paper mentions a certain absent volunteer.}}\n\n@event ledger_office_drying_line\ntitle: The Drying Line\nplace: ledger_office\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: paper\ntags: life\nscene: Freshly printed pages pegged on a line across a cramped office to dry, one page hanging slightly crooked.\ntext: {Tomorrow's gossip column is pegged on a line to dry, and nobody is watching it.|The proofs hang on the line, and the peg on the gossip column is a new red one.|The drying line. You know which peg holds the gossip column.} {~One item names your house.|One item begins \"Which second-year was seen...\"|{final_year: One item is about the graduating year. Possibly you.|A whole paragraph is about a broken window.}}\n> left: Read ahead\n  challenge deceive easy:\n    win:\n      guile +small\n      mood +small\n      say: {~You read it all and hang it back exactly as crooked as it was.|You know tomorrow's scandal tonight. It is much smaller than it will look in print.}\n    lose:\n      renown -small\n      mood -tiny\n      say: {~Caught. The next column has an item beginning \"Which nosy visitor...\"|A peg snaps. The page lands face-down in the ink tray, and everyone turns round.}\n> right: Leave the line alone\n  mood -tiny\n  charm +tiny\n  say: {~You spend the night wondering. That's the price of manners.|You find out with everyone else, at breakfast. It was about you anyway.}\n\n# ---------------- The Headmistress's Tower ----------------\n\n@event headmistress_tower_stone_owl\ntime: evening\ntitle: The Stone Owl\nplace: headmistress_tower\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: owl\ntags: magic\nscene: A stone owl on the newel post of a spiral stair, one eye open, a student standing very still before it.\ntext: {The stone owl on the stair opens one eye. \"A question first,\" it says, in a voice like a door closing.|The owl's other eye is half open tonight. It has been waiting all day for someone to ask.|The owl opens both eyes for you now, which may or may not be an honour.} {~Today's question is about the founders.|Today's question is a riddle with no right answer.|{season == \"winter\": There is snow on its head, and it is not amused.|A moth has landed on its beak. It is pretending not to notice.}}\n> left: Answer the question\n  challenge research easy:\n    win:\n      lore +small\n      rel isolde +tiny\n      say: {~The owl closes its eyes and the stair is yours. Upstairs, someone laughs, short and pleased.|\"Adequate,\" says the owl, which from a stone owl is a standing ovation.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      say: {~\"No,\" says the owl, and closes both eyes. You climb anyway, feeling marked down.|The owl repeats your answer back to you, slowly. It sounds worse.}\n> right: Climb past without a word\n  guile +small\n  rel isolde -tiny\n  say: {~The owl says nothing. At the top, the door is shut. You have the strong feeling you've been noted.|You get past. The owl's eye follows you all the way up, and all the way down again.}\n\n@event headmistress_tower_tea\ntitle: Two Cups Poured\nplace: headmistress_tower\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: cup\ntags: social\ncast:\n  host: isolde\nscene: A tower study full of books, a tall pale woman pouring two cups of tea beside a board of crystal towers by the window.\ntext: {The headmistress pours two cups unasked. \"Sit. Tell me how your term is going, and then how it is really going.\"|Two cups, already poured. \"Well,\" says {host}, \"how is it really going?\"|You sit before she asks. She looks pleased, and doesn't say so.} {~The crystal towers on her board have moved.|Rain ticks on the high windows.|{final_year: \"Your last year,\" she says. \"I remember your first.\"|Footnote, the library cat, sleeps on her papers.}}\n> left: Tell her the truth\n  rel host +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {~She listens without interrupting, which is worse than any lecture and better than most comfort.|She hears all of it and says only, \"Yes. I thought so.\" Somehow that helps, later.}\n> right: Give her the polished version\n  challenge deceive hard:\n    win:\n      renown +small\n      mood +tiny\n      say: {~She laughs, short and delighted, and lets you get away with it. You aren't sure that's a good sign.|\"Very good,\" she says. \"Next time, the true one.\" She means both halves.}\n    lose:\n      rel host -tiny\n      mood -small\n      say: {~\"Mm,\" she says, and pours you more tea, and waits. You end up telling her anyway.|She lets the silence go on until it says everything you didn't.}\n\n@event headmistress_tower_window_seat\ntitle: The Window Seat\nplace: headmistress_tower\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: book\ntags: study\nscene: A deep window seat high in a tower, a student reading with the lake far below, a headmistress writing letters at a desk behind.\ntext: {The headmistress waves you to the window seat without looking up from her letters. \"Read anything. Break nothing.\"|A book has been left on the window seat, open, as if for you.|Your window seat, the headmistress calls it now, as though it always was.} {~Below, the lake is perfectly still.|Her quill scratches; the clock does not tick.|{season == \"autumn\": Leaves blow past at the height of the clouds.|The bridges of light come on one by one below.}}\n> left: Read what she left out\n  lore +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~A history of the seven towers, annotated in her hand. One note just says \"No.\"|A book of old law, with a dried rose marking a page about second chances.|A treatise on wards so dull it must be important. You get through it. She notices.}\n> right: Doze in the sun\n  mood +small\n  health +tiny\n  rel isolde -tiny\n  say: {~You wake to find a blanket over you and no comment at all, which is its own kind of comment.|You sleep through her afternoon. When you wake, she is still writing, and the light has moved.}\n\n# ---------------- The Restricted Section ----------------\n\n@event restricted_section_breathing_shelves\ntitle: The Breathing Shelves\nplace: restricted_section\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: book\ntags: study mystery\nscene: Black-bound books on tall shelves in the dark, a single candle on a reading table, the spines faintly rising and falling.\ntext: {The black-bound books breathe in the dark, slow as sleepers. You find a chair and light a candle.|You've learned to breathe with the shelves; it helps.|Your chair is where you left it. The books shift, as if making room.} {~One spine is warm to the touch.|Somewhere a page turns by itself.|{season == \"winter\": Your breath fogs. Theirs doesn't.|The candle burns low and blue.}}\n> left: Read until the candle gutters\n  lore +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {~You read about wards that were old when the towers were new. Afterwards you can't sleep, and don't quite want to.|You read a whole chapter before you notice the book has been reading along, a line ahead.}\n> right: Read only the spines\n  lore +tiny\n  mana -tiny\n  say: {~Even the titles take something out of you. One of them is just your house's name.|You walk the shelves by candlelight. The spines lean towards you, and then politely away.}\n\n@event restricted_section_lantern\ntitle: Holding the Lantern\nplace: restricted_section\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: lantern\ntags: social mystery\ncast:\n  friend: any where rel(friend) >= 6 and tagged(friend, student)\nscene: Two students in a dark library aisle, one holding a lantern high, the other reading a black book, shadows stretching behind them.\ntext: {{friend} came with you, for courage, and is holding the lantern in both hands.|You bring a second candle this time; {friend} takes the lantern without being asked.|You know which aisles creak now. {friend} holds the lantern where you point.} {~\"Did that book just sigh?\" {they} whisper{s}.|A shelf creaks behind you both.|{final_year: \"I'll miss this,\" {they} say{s}, not sounding sure.|The lantern makes the shadows walk.}}\n> left: Read while they keep watch\n  lore +small\n  rel friend -tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~You read; {friend} keeps watch and keeps count of every noise, out loud.|You lose an hour in a book. {friend} loses an hour staring at a shelf that keeps staring back.}\n> right: Take the lantern for a while\n  rel friend +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~You hold the light so {friend} can read. Something in the dark rustles the whole time, and you don't let it show.|{friend} reads aloud in a whisper. The books seem to lean in to listen.}\n\n@event restricted_section_chained_book\ntime: evening\ntitle: The Chained Book\nplace: restricted_section\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: chain\ntags: mystery danger\nscene: A heavy book chained to a library shelf, lying open on its own under a dim candle, the chain hanging slack.\ntext: {One book here is chained to its shelf, and tonight it has opened itself to a page.|The chained book lies open at a different page, and the chain is a link longer.|The chained book has been expecting you. That's the worst part.} {~The page is in a script you almost know.|The page shows a lock with no keyhole.|The page is blank until you look away.}\n> left: Read the open page\n  challenge research medium:\n    win:\n      lore +small\n      mana +small\n      say: {~The words make sense for exactly as long as you're reading them. Afterwards you only remember that they did.|You read it, and the book turns its own page, satisfied, as if you'd passed something.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      health -tiny\n      say: {~The words read you back. For a day afterwards you keep feeling underlined.|The candle goes out mid-line. In the dark, the page keeps going.}\n> right: Close it and step back\n  mood -tiny\n  mana +tiny\n  say: {~The chain rattles as the cover shuts, like a sigh. You stand with your back to it until your hands stop shaking.|You close it gently, the way you'd close a door on someone sleeping.}\n\n@event restricted_section_handleless_door\ntime: evening\ntitle: The Door Without a Handle\nplace: restricted_section\nwhen: not enrolled(hollow_studies) and not passed(hollow_studies, 1)\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nrepeat: 8\nweight: 6\nart: door\ntags: study mystery\nscene: A plain door with no handle at the back of a dark library, chalk writing on it that has not smudged in years.\ntext: {At the very back is a door with no handle and no window. On it, in chalk that never smudges, HOLLOW STUDIES, BY LEAVE OF THE HEADMISTRESS.|Tonight the door without a handle is warm, like the outer door.|You've read its chalk so often you could recite it.} Underneath, smaller: \"Umbral Theory II. Astromancy II. And know what the founders made, and why.\"\n> left: Knock\n  rel isolde +tiny\n  mood -small\n  say: {~Nobody answers. Then, very far away, a pleasant voice says, \"Not yet.\"|Your knock doesn't echo. It just stops, as if something on the other side caught it.}\n> right: Copy the chalk into your notes\n  lore +small\n  mana -small\n  say: {~The chalk makes your pencil cold. Two courses and a question, and the question is the hard part.|You copy it word for word. Back in your room, your notes have the same words, and one more underline than you made.}\n\n# ---------------- The Third Gargoyle ----------------\n\n@event gargoyle_passage_old_marks\ntitle: Marks on the Wall\nplace: gargoyle_passage\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: map\ntags: life mystery\nscene: A narrow stone passage behind a gargoyle, its walls covered in scratched initials, dates and tiny arrows, lit by a stub of candle.\ntext: {The passage walls are covered in initials, dates and tiny arrows, scratched by generations who were never here.|Among the marks on the wall is a new one, still sharp.|You know these walls now: the arrows that lie, the dates that can't be right, the initials that keep coming back.} {~The wind sings through the gargoyle's teeth.|Below, a door closes that shouldn't be there.|{season == \"winter\": Frost picks out every letter.|Moths sleep in the deeper scratches.}}\n> left: Add your own mark\n  renown +tiny\n  guile +tiny\n  health -tiny\n  say: {~You scratch your initials small and low, with a tiny arrow pointing nowhere. It feels like signing something.|{final_year: You add the year beside your old mark. It looks so much younger than you.|A knuckle pays for it. The mark is crooked and entirely yours.}}\n> right: Follow an old arrow\n  lore +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~The arrow leads to another arrow, which leads to a wall, which has a joke scratched on it.|The arrow leads to a slit window with a view of the lake no plan admits to. Your knees disagree with the route.}\n\n@event gargoyle_passage_offering_dish\ntime: evening\ntitle: The Gargoyle's Dish\nplace: gargoyle_passage\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: coin\ntags: magic life\nscene: A weathered gargoyle on a moonlit parapet with a dish worn into its stone paw, holding a button, a biscuit and a bent key.\ntext: {The third gargoyle has a dish worn into its paw, and in the dish are offerings, a button, a biscuit, a bent key.|Someone has left the gargoyle a single grape, peeled.|The gargoyle looks at you the way a landlord looks at the first of the month.} {~Tonight it looks hungry.|Tonight it looks smug.|{season == \"summer\": The stone is still warm from the day.|Rain runs out of its mouth in a steady complaint.}}\n> left: Leave a crown in the dish\n  gold -tiny\n  mood +small\n  say: {~By morning the crown is gone, and so is the biscuit. Nobody admits to either.|The crown clinks in. The gargoyle's expression doesn't change, but the wind drops.}\n> right: Borrow the biscuit\n  chance 50:\n    mood +small\n    health +tiny\n    say: {~It's a very good biscuit. Somebody's mother made it. You feel a little bad, and then less.|Shortbread, still crisp. The gargoyle allows it, this once.}\n  else:\n    mood -small\n    say: {~It's made of stone. The gargoyle's joke, and it's been waiting a long time to tell it.|The wind gets up the moment you take it, and stays up all night, rattling your window.}\n\n@event gargoyle_passage_parapet_cards\ntitle: Cards on the Parapet\nplace: gargoyle_passage\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: cards\ntags: social\ncast:\n  here: present(place) where tagged(here, peer)\nscene: Two students playing cards by lantern-light on a high castle parapet, a pot of buttons between them and the lake far below.\ntext: {On the parapet, by lantern-light, {here} is dealing cards for buttons.|{here} has a new deck, and the pot of buttons has grown.|{here} deals you in before you've sat down.} {~The wind keeps trying to take the jack.|A pigeon watches the game with too much interest.|{final_year: \"One more for the road,\" {they} say{s}.|The lake below holds the lantern perfectly still.}}\n> left: Play a hand\n  challenge deceive easy:\n    win:\n      gold +tiny\n      mood +small\n      say: {cast_as(here, corvin): {~\"Well, look at you,\" Corvin says, paying out. \"Who taught you that? Don't say me.\"|\"Remind me never to owe you money,\" Corvin tells the pigeon, and pays you in buttons.}|{~You win the pot and one actual crown that somebody bet by mistake.|You bluff with nothing and the wind turns your card over at the right moment. You take the buttons with dignity.}}\n    lose:\n      gold -small\n      mood -tiny\n      say: {cast_as(here, corvin): {~Corvin scoops the pot and winces. \"Saint, you're the worst liar I love playing.\" He gives a crown back.|\"Never bluff a man who owes three people money,\" Corvin says, kindly.}|{~You lose your buttons, then some crowns, then the will to bluff.|The wind takes your best card over the parapet. It was the only one you had.}}\n> right: Watch the lake instead\n  mood +tiny\n  health -tiny\n  rel here +tiny\n  say: {cast_as(here, corvin): {~\"Saint,\" Corvin says, not looking up, \"you'll never be rich.\" He passes you his scarf.|Corvin deals you out without a word and tosses you his scarf when the wind picks up.}|{~You sit with your back to the stone and watch the dark water while the cards slap behind you. It's cold, and peaceful.|You count the lights in the village until the game's over.}}\n\n# ---------------- The Seventh Tower ----------------\n\n@event seventh_tower_stair_count\ntitle: The Stair That Changes\nplace: seventh_tower\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: tower\ntags: mystery\nscene: A narrow spiral stair inside a dusty stone tower, a single candle climbing it, moonlight falling through slit windows.\ntext: {The seventh tower's stair never has the same number of steps twice. Scholars count it to learn how the castle bends; everyone else says it lets you up faster if you don't look.|Back on the stair that won't keep its count. You've started chalking each total on the wall; no two agree.|You've stopped expecting the stair to agree with you. You count anyway; it's a kind of manners.} {~Dust lies thick on every step but yours.|The moon follows you from window to window.|{season == \"winter\": The cold comes up through your shoes.|Your candle leans towards the top, like a dog on a lead.}}\n> left: Count every step\n  lore +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {~You lose count twice and get two answers, both certain, both wrong.|Up, one number; down, one more. You don't stay to find the extra step.|{final_year: The count matches your very first climb. You can't say why that frightens you.|You count a step that isn't there, and your foot believes you.}}\n> right: Climb without counting\n  mana +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {~You climb by feel, in the dark between windows. The stair seems to like that better.|You stop counting and the climb is suddenly short, as if the tower was in a hurry too.}\n\n@event seventh_tower_dust_room\ntime: evening\ntitle: Dust and Moonlight\nplace: seventh_tower\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: moon\ntags: mystery\nscene: A bare round room at the top of a tower, thick dust on the floor marked by footprints, moonlight slanting through one window onto a star-shaped hollow in the wall.\ntext: {At the top is one room of dust and moonlight, with a star-shaped hollow in the wall. The footprints in the dust aren't yours.|More footprints in the dust; some are yours now.|The room keeps your footprints like a guest book.} {~The moon sits in the window as if it lives here.|The hollow in the wall is darker than the night.|A moth beats at the glass from outside, trying to get in.}\n> left: Sit in the moonlight\n  mana +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {~The light is cold and kind. You sit until your shadow forgets which way to fall.|You sit where the moon is brightest. It feels like being looked at by someone very old and very tired.}\n> right: Sweep away your footprints\n  guile +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {~You sweep your way out backwards with your sleeve. The dust settles behind you, and then, you'd swear, rearranges itself.|You cough your way to the door. The other footprints you leave alone. They seem to want to stay.}\n\n@event seventh_tower_lake_window\ntitle: The Window Over the Lake\nplace: seventh_tower\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: wave\ntags: mystery danger\nscene: A small arched window high in a tower, its latch half open, the Mirror Lake lying still and silver far below.\ntext: {The tower's one window looks down on the Mirror Lake, and the latch isn't locked. They say if you open it, you can hear the lake.|The latch moves more easily than last time, as if someone else has been practising.|The window. You've stood here often enough that your hand finds the latch before you decide anything.} {~The lake is so still it looks painted.|There's a light on the water that isn't the moon's.|{season == \"winter\": The lake is grey and hard.|Mist lies on the water.}}\n> left: Open it and listen\n  challenge research using arcana easy:\n    win:\n      mana +small\n      lore +tiny\n      say: {~You hear water, and under it a sound too low to be wind. You decide it was the pipes, and don't quite believe yourself.|Nothing but wind and water. You stay until you're sure, and you're never quite sure.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      health -tiny\n      say: {~The cold comes in and won't leave. For days you hear dripping in dry rooms.|The lake is silent, entirely, the way a room goes silent when you walk in on a conversation.}\n> right: Leave it latched\n  mood -tiny\n  health +tiny\n  say: {~You leave the latch alone and go down to bed, and lie awake wondering what you didn't hear.|You put your palm flat on the cold glass instead. The lake does nothing. It's very good at that.}\n"},{"path":"content/events/pools/clubs_jobs.grim","text":"# Baseline pools (phase 3, step 1b): club practice and job shifts.\n# Clubs: duelling, lumen_ledger, verdant, cartographers. Jobs: library_assistant, tavern, apothecary_clerk, broom_courier.\n# Each track: three repeatable routines (two at rank 1, one at rank 2) and one rank-2 milestone.\n\n# ---------------- Duelling Circle (track: duelling) ----------------\n\n@event duelling_ward_line\ntitle: The Ward Line\ntrack: duelling\nrank: 1\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: shield\ntags: club duel\nscene: A row of students holding shimmering shields across a scorched hall while seniors lob sparks at them.\ntext: {Ward drill. Members hold shields in a line while the seniors lob stinging hexes down the hall.|You know by now which senior aims low, and which one apologises.|You could hold a shield in your sleep, and one night you did.} {~A dummy at the end of the line has a shield of its own, out of pity.|Someone has chalked a tally of scorched floorboards. It is long.|A first-year holds the shield upside down and does surprisingly well.}\n> left: Take the front of the line\n  challenge combat easy:\n    win:\n      perform duelling +small\n      vigor +small\n      say: {~Your shield takes everything and hums about it afterwards.|Hexes splash off your ward like rain off a good hat.|You hold until the bell. Lyra writes something down, which is either praise or a list of your faults.}\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      perform duelling +tiny\n      say: {~A hex finds the gap by your elbow. Your arm fizzes until supper.|Your ward folds like wet paper. The senior apologises, mostly to the floor.}\n> right: Hold the back row tonight\n  arcana +small\n  perform duelling -tiny\n  say: {~From the back you watch how the good shields bend instead of break. Nobody watches you.|You spend the drill studying the front row's mistakes, which feels useful and looks like hiding.|Safe at the back, you learn a great deal about the ceiling.}\n\n@event duelling_dummy_mending\ntitle: Mending the Dummies\ntrack: duelling\nrank: 1\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: duel\ntags: club\nscene: A student alone in a duelling hall at night, stitching straw back into a battered practice dummy.\ntext: {After practice someone has to mend the dummies, and tonight the rota says it's you.|Mending night. You now keep a spare spool of thread in your pocket, just for the dummies.|You have stitched every dummy here twice. They look at you with something like trust.} {~The tall dummy is missing an arm. Someone always takes the arm.|One dummy is now mostly scorch mark.|Someone has painted a new name on a dummy's chest, and a grudge beneath it.}\n> left: Stay late and stitch them up\n  mood -small\n  perform duelling +small\n  rank_xp duelling +tiny\n  say: {~By midnight every dummy is whole and you have straw in places straw should not be.|You sew one dummy's arm on slightly backwards. It looks more dangerous for it.|The hall is quiet, the needle is steady, and for an hour nobody is shouting about footwork.}\n> right: Leave them for the morning\n  mood +tiny\n  perform duelling -tiny\n  say: {~In the morning the dummies are still in pieces, and so is Lyra's patience.|You slip out early. Someone finds the dummies and puts your name on next week's rota, underlined twice.}\n\n@event duelling_refereeing_lyra\ntitle: Refereeing Lyra\ntrack: duelling\nrank: 2\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nactor: lyra\nart: duel\ntags: club duel\nscene: A student with a whistle watching a noble duellist in a chalk circle, her opponent's shield just failing.\ntext: {Officers referee, so tonight you hold the whistle while Lyra duels a second-year.|You know the circle's chalk by heart now, and Lyra's footwork better.|{rel(lyra) >= 12: Lyra no longer asks who is refereeing. She looks for you.|The whistle has a dent in it from some earlier, unrecorded argument.}} {~Her last spell landed a breath after the bell.|She stepped out of the circle by a boot's width, and knows it.|Her opponent's shield dropped first, but only if you squint.} {final_year: One of the last bouts you will call for her.|}\n> left: Call it against her\n  rel lyra -small\n  perform duelling +small\n  rank_xp duelling +small\n  say: {~\"Noted,\" Lyra says, in a voice that could chill wine. Next day her feet are perfect.|Lyra stares at you, then shakes her opponent's hand. Later a cup of tea appears on your bench.|The hall goes silent. Lyra bows to the second-year, very correctly, and to you, slightly less.}\n> right: Let it stand\n  rel lyra +tiny\n  perform duelling -tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~Lyra wins. The second-year looks at you, and you find something interesting about your whistle.|Nobody objects. Lyra does not thank you; if anything, she looks a little disappointed in you.|The bout goes in the book as hers. It sits there all week like a stone in your shoe.}\n\n@event duelling_running_practice\ntitle: Running Practice\ntrack: duelling\nrank: 2\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\nweight: 20\nart: duel\ntags: club duel\nscene: A note pinned to a duelling hall door, a crowd of students waiting inside in chalk circles.\ntext: A note is pinned to the hall door in Lyra's sharp hand: \"Called home. You are running practice. Footwork first. Do not let anyone set the ceiling on fire again.\" The whole Circle turns to see what you will say.\n> left: Run it exactly her way\n  perform duelling +small\n  rank_xp duelling +medium\n  rel lyra +small\n  say: Footwork, then shields, then footwork again. Nobody sets the ceiling on fire. Lyra reads your practice log on her return and says \"adequate\", then reads it again when she thinks you've gone.\n> right: Run it your own way\n  rank_xp duelling +medium\n  renown +small\n  mood +small\n  say: You turn the footwork drill into a game of tag with stinging hexes. The ceiling only smoulders. A week later Lyra runs the same game and calls it her idea, which is the highest praise she has.\n\n# ---------------- The Lumen Ledger (track: lumen_ledger) ----------------\n\n@event lumen_ledger_letters_box\ntitle: The Letters Box\ntrack: lumen_ledger\nrank: 1\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: letter\ntags: club\nscene: A battered wooden box under a library staircase, overflowing with folded letters and one sock.\ntext: {The letters box is full. Stringers sort it, Nessa says, having no dignity yet to lose.|Sorting day. A regular correspondent has started numbering their letters.|Under the stairs the box has burst its lid. You know the regulars by their handwriting now.} {~Porridge complaints, a poem about the lake and a sock.|A furious letter about the crossword, in a professor's hand.|An anonymous tip in lemon juice, noticed only because it smells nice.}\n> left: Print the angriest letter\n  perform lumen_ledger +small\n  renown +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~It runs on page four. By breakfast several people are angry about the angry letter, which Nessa calls \"engagement\".|The letter runs. Its author glares at you over the porridge, as if you had written it.}\n> right: Answer them all by hand\n  charm +small\n  mood -small\n  perform lumen_ledger +tiny\n  say: {~You write back to every one of them, even the ones that weren't really letters. Your hand cramps; somewhere in the castle a lonely first-year gets a reply.|A pile of replies, all polite. Nessa calls it \"a waste of a perfectly good grudge\", then borrows your phrasing.}\n\n@event lumen_ledger_courtyard_quotes\ntitle: A Word from the Courtyard\ntrack: lumen_ledger\nrank: 1\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: paper\ntags: club social\nscene: A student with a notebook stopping passers-by in a busy courtyard.\ntext: {Every issue needs a word from the courtyard. One question, many opinions.|Word has got round the courtyard that the Ledger is asking questions.|Half the courtyard now takes the long way round your notebook.} This week: {~is the soup getting thinner?|who will win the Spring Tournament?|is the lake louder lately?} {season == \"winter\": Everyone is hurrying indoors.|{~Some second-years pretend not to see you.|A passing professor looks very quotable.}}\n> left: Stop strangers and ask\n  challenge negotiate easy:\n    win:\n      perform lumen_ledger +small\n      charm +small\n      say: {~A gardener, a prefect and a very small first-year give you the best quotes of the term. The first-year's is unprintable, and perfect.|You get a quote so good Nessa moves it to the front page and pretends it was her idea to ask.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      perform lumen_ledger +tiny\n      say: {~Everyone is busy, cold or suddenly deaf. You come back with \"no comment\" in five different tones of voice.|A fourth-year answers at length, entirely about himself.}\n> right: Just quote your friends\n  mood +tiny\n  perform lumen_ledger -tiny\n  say: {~Every opinion comes from someone you had lunch with. Nessa reads them and says, \"Interesting. Your friends are very consistent.\"|It's quick. It's also the courtyard as represented by one person in several scarves.}\n\n@event lumen_ledger_red_ink\ntitle: Red Ink\ntrack: lumen_ledger\nrank: 2\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nactor: nessa\nart: scroll\ntags: club\nscene: A cramped office papered with old front pages, an editor slashing a manuscript with a red quill.\ntext: {Nessa edits your story with a red quill and no mercy.|Nessa has your draft, and this time the red quill hesitates once before it moves.|Edit night. You have learned to bring two copies, one for Nessa to kill.} \"{~Kill your darlings. Bury them. Page six.|Lead's buried. Dig it up. Headline it.|Good. Too long. Shorter. Shorter than that.}\" She has cut your favourite paragraph.\n> left: Fight for the paragraph\n  challenge negotiate vs nessa.charm medium:\n    win:\n      perform lumen_ledger +small\n      rank_xp lumen_ledger +small\n      say: {~Nessa reads it again, out loud, and puts it back. \"Fine. Stays. Don't get used to it.\"|You win the paragraph. Nessa makes you cut a different one, as a tax.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      rel nessa -tiny\n      say: {~\"No,\" says Nessa, and that is the whole argument.|She listens to your case with great interest, then cuts the paragraph anyway, plus the one you defended it with.}\n> right: Let her cut it\n  perform lumen_ledger +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {~It reads better without it. You will never forgive her.|The story runs, tighter and less yours. Nessa scribbles \"good\" in the margin, a word Nessa hands out like gold.}\n\n@event lumen_ledger_front_page\ntitle: Front Page\ntrack: lumen_ledger\nrank: 2\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\nweight: 20\nart: paper\ntags: club\nscene: A creaking printing press at dawn spitting out newspapers, a student holding the first copy up to the light.\ntext: The press groans like a dragon and spits out the dawn edition. Your story is on the front page, under your own name. Nessa hands you a tack without looking up. \"Wall,\" she says. \"That's where front pages go.\"\n> left: Pin it to the wall yourself\n  rank_xp lumen_ledger +medium\n  perform lumen_ledger +small\n  rel nessa +small\n  say: You find a gap between a scandal from before you were born and a recipe someone printed by mistake. It looks small up there, and permanent. Nessa pretends not to watch you step back and admire it.\n> right: Send the first copy home\n  rank_xp lumen_ledger +medium\n  mood +medium\n  gold -tiny\n  say: You post it home with a note that says nothing about how long it took. The reply comes within the week: they have framed it, slightly crooked, over the kitchen fire.\n\n# ---------------- Verdant Circle (track: verdant) ----------------\n\n@event verdant_watering_rota\ntitle: The Watering Rota\ntrack: verdant\nrank: 1\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: leaf\ntags: club nature\nscene: A student with a watering can and a huge chart in a steaming greenhouse, ferns leaning in hungrily.\ntext: {You're on the watering rota, a chart the size of a door.|The ferns have learned your footsteps and lean in.|You no longer need the chart, luckily, since a fern ate it.} {season == \"winter\": Frost outside the glass, drips down your collar inside.|{season == \"summer\": It's so hot the cacti look smug.|{~The biting ferns are in a mood.|Something in the far bed grew overnight.|The moonflowers do nothing, beautifully, as they will for years.}}}\n> left: Follow the chart exactly\n  perform verdant +small\n  alchemy +tiny\n  mood -small\n  say: {~Warm rainwater for the ferns, cold tea for the moss, nothing at all for the cactus that bites. It takes all afternoon.|By the chart, to the drop. Tobias says the plants look \"right pleased\", and they do, in a leafy way.}\n> right: Water by feel\n  challenge research using alchemy easy:\n    win:\n      alchemy +small\n      perform verdant +small\n      say: {~You read the leaves instead of the chart. Everything perks up, and one fern makes a sound suspiciously like purring.|You guess right on every bed. Tobias asks how you knew. You honestly don't.}\n    lose:\n      health -tiny\n      perform verdant -tiny\n      say: {~You drown a pot of sundew, and a biting fern takes a polite nip of your thumb.|The moss wanted tea, not water. It sulks visibly.}\n\n@event verdant_repotting_day\ntitle: Repotting Day\ntrack: verdant\nrank: 1\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: flower\ntags: club nature\nscene: Students in earmuffs wrestling squalling mandrakes into bigger pots on a crowded greenhouse bench.\ntext: {Repotting day. The mandrakes have outgrown their pots and have opinions about it.|Someone always takes the good earmuffs, so you bring your own now.|You know which mandrakes kick, and which only pretend to.} {~Tobias's own mandrake watches from a shelf in a knitted hat, superior.|A seed tray of something blue has started to hum.|{season == \"spring\": Everything is trying to grow at once.|The glass is fogged and the benches crowded.}}\n> left: Take on the mandrakes\n  health -tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  perform verdant +small\n  rank_xp verdant +tiny\n  say: {~One mandrake has rooted into the bench itself. It takes a trowel, a lullaby and both hands to talk it out.|Mandrake after mandrake, each into a bigger pot. Your ears ring all the way to supper.|The biggest one goes in headfirst and comes out, somehow, the right way up. You are not sure who won.}\n> right: Take the quiet seed trays\n  alchemy +small\n  mood -tiny\n  perform verdant +tiny\n  say: {~Hundreds of seedlings, pricked out one by one. Your back will remember this tomorrow.|The seed trays are peaceful and endless. By dusk you have names for most of them.}\n\n@event verdant_tobias_cuttings\ntitle: The Cuttings Question\ntrack: verdant\nrank: 2\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nactor: tobias\nart: leaf\ntags: club nature\nscene: A big gentle student cradling a tray of leggy cuttings, eyeing a full compost heap with dread.\ntext: {The benches are full and someone must choose which cuttings stay. Tobias can't. \"It's like choosing between goats,\" he says.|Bench day. Tobias hovers over a tray of cuttings, apologising to them in advance and glancing at you.|Tobias hands you the tray before he can change his mind. He is grateful in a way that looks like grief.} {season == \"autumn\": The frost will take the weakest soon anyway.|}\n> left: Choose, and compost the rest\n  perform verdant +small\n  rank_xp verdant +small\n  rel tobias -tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~You keep the strongest and compost the rest. Tobias says \"fair's fair\", then goes very quiet over the heap.|The benches breathe again. Tobias thanks you, then tells you a long story about a goat he couldn't sell. You understand it is about the cuttings.}\n> right: Find room for every one\n  rel tobias +small\n  perform verdant -tiny\n  mood +tiny\n  say: {~You find room on windowsills, under benches and in a teacup. Nothing grows well, but everything grows. Tobias laughs from the belly.|\"Ma always said a crowded field still feeds you,\" Tobias says, beaming. The benches groan. So does Old Maud, when she sees.}\n\n@event verdant_greenhouse_keys\ntitle: The Greenhouse Keys\ntrack: verdant\nrank: 2\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\nweight: 20\nactor: tobias\nart: key\ntags: club nature\nscene: A heavy ring of old iron keys handed over in a steaming glasshouse at dusk.\ntext: At dusk Tobias holds out the iron key ring, warm from his pocket. \"Ma says you give the keys to whoever shuts the gate without being asked,\" he says. \"You always shut the gate.\" From now on, opening up and locking up are yours.\n> left: Open up at dawn\n  rank_xp verdant +medium\n  perform verdant +small\n  health -tiny\n  rel tobias +small\n  say: Dawn, the glass silver with dew, every plant turning to the light you let in. You had never noticed they all turn at once. For a moment you are the only person in the world.\n> right: Walk the night round with a lamp\n  rank_xp verdant +medium\n  alchemy +small\n  mood +small\n  say: You walk the benches with a lamp, checking latches and saying goodnight, because Tobias does and it feels rude not to. Something in the far bed says it back. You decide not to mention it.\n\n# ---------------- Midnight Cartographers (track: cartographers) ----------------\n\n@event cartographers_survey_run\ntitle: A Survey Run\ntrack: cartographers\nrank: 1\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: map\ntags: club mystery\nscene: A student pacing a dark stone passage by candlelight, chalking marks on the wall.\ntext: {Initiates do the pacing. Corvin hands you chalk and a passage to measure before the castle changes its mind.|Your chalk is a stub now, so you mark the turns with notches.|Your stride is a unit of measurement on the master map now, and the castle seems to know it.} {~The passage smells of oranges, which nobody can explain.|Behind the wall, a clock ticks that no one has ever found.|Your candle leans, very slightly, toward the left-hand turning.}\n> left: Pace it properly\n  challenge research using guile easy:\n    win:\n      perform cartographers +small\n      guile +small\n      say: {~Every turning chalked and counted. The passage is exactly where it was last month, which on the master map counts as a scandal.|The passage is two strides longer from this end than from the other. Corvin is delighted.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      perform cartographers -tiny\n      say: {~Your chalk marks lead neatly into a wall that wasn't there when you started.|You lose count somewhere in the dark and have to admit it, which in this club is rarer than a map.}\n> right: Copy last month's figures\n  alignment -tiny\n  mood +tiny\n  perform cartographers -tiny\n  say: {~You copy the old figures and sit in an alcove eating toffee. The castle, as if offended, moves the passage a foot.|The figures match last month's exactly. Corvin looks at them, then at you. \"Forger,\" he says, approvingly.}\n\n@event cartographers_lantern_patrol\ntitle: The Prefect's Lantern\ntrack: cartographers\nrank: 1\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: lantern\ntags: club mystery\nscene: Students pressed into a stone alcove as a lantern's glow sweeps along a dark corridor.\ntext: {Every expedition has the moment a lantern comes round the corner. Tonight's has a red sash behind it.|A lantern, a red sash, footsteps. You know this part.|You can tell the prefects on patrol apart by their footsteps now.} {~The alcove beside you is just big enough for one.|A loose flagstone ahead clanks if you step on it.|The tapestry here already has feet showing under it, and they aren't yours.}\n> left: Freeze in the shadows\n  challenge deceive easy:\n    win:\n      perform cartographers +small\n      guile +tiny\n      say: {~The light slides over you like water off a duck. The footsteps go on.|You don't breathe for a very long time. The prefect sneezes, apologises to nobody and walks on.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      perform cartographers -tiny\n      say: {~The lantern stops on your face. You spend Saturday scrubbing pans and inventing a reason you were inside a wall.|You talk your way out with a story about a lost cat. It costs you the night's mapping and most of your dignity.}\n> right: Go back the long way\n  guile +tiny\n  health -tiny\n  perform cartographers -tiny\n  say: {~The long way is four flights of freezing stairs. You learn every one of them by heart.|You double back through the kitchens and arrive late, cold and unseen. Corvin raises a mug of milk he certainly didn't pay for.}\n\n@event cartographers_inking_night\ntitle: Inking Night\ntrack: cartographers\nrank: 2\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nactor: corvin\nart: map\ntags: club\nscene: Two students bent over a huge map drawn in silver ink that shifts as they watch, in a hidden candlelit room.\ntext: {Mappers ink the master map. Corvin unrolls it, silver lines shifting like breath, and passes you the pen.|Inking night. The map has redrawn a stair since your last visit, out of spite.|The map has started leaving a blank wherever you hesitate.} {~\"Leave the one behind the kitchens off,\" he says. \"Not for long. For me.\"|\"The stair under the clock. Don't draw it. Don't ask why.\"|He taps a corridor. \"That one stays off the map. Humour me.\"}\n> left: Ink it anyway\n  perform cartographers +small\n  rank_xp cartographers +small\n  rel corvin -small\n  say: {~You draw it, line by honest line. Corvin watches, then laughs, a little too late. \"Saint,\" he says, without the usual smile.|It goes on the map. Corvin is quiet for the rest of the night, which on him is very loud.}\n> right: Leave it off for him\n  rel corvin +small\n  perform cartographers -tiny\n  say: {~You leave a careful blank. Corvin squeezes your shoulder and doesn't make a joke, which is how you know it matters.|The map gets a gap and Corvin keeps his secret. You wonder, later, what it's for.}\n\n@event cartographers_silver_pen\ntitle: A Pen of Your Own\ntrack: cartographers\nrank: 2\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\nweight: 20\nactor: corvin\nart: map\ntags: club\nscene: A silver-nibbed pen laid on a folded map in a candlelit stone alcove.\ntext: Corvin presses a pen into your hand: silver nib, black barrel, warm from his pocket. \"Mappers carry their own,\" he says. \"And the first passage you find, you name. Club rule. I made it up, but it's a rule.\" Tonight you found one.\n> left: Name it after yourself\n  rank_xp cartographers +medium\n  guile +small\n  mood +small\n  say: Your name goes on the master map in your own silver hand, at the mouth of a passage that smells faintly of rain. The ink shivers, then settles, as if the castle had agreed.\n> right: Name it for a club joke\n  rank_xp cartographers +medium\n  rel corvin +small\n  perform cartographers +small\n  say: You name it after the night's worst joke. Corvin laughs so hard a prefect two floors down stops to listen. \"Keeper material,\" he whispers, and for once he doesn't laugh after.\n\n# ---------------- Library Assistant (track: library_assistant) ----------------\n\n@event library_assistant_wandering_shelves\ntitle: The Wandering Section\ntrack: library_assistant\nrank: 1\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: book\ntags: job study\nscene: A library aisle with an empty gap where a whole bookcase should be, a student holding a catalogue.\ntext: {The shelves rearranged themselves overnight. History is missing, and a queue is forming for it.|The shelves have wandered, this time the {~poetry|herbals|atlases}. You have brought string to mark the way.|The head librarian simply hands you the catalogue now and points upward.} {~Footnote sits where the section used to be.|A first-year swears she saw it go upstairs.|There is a faint trail in the dust, as if something heavy had tiptoed.}\n> left: Hunt it down by catalogue\n  wage\n  challenge research easy:\n    win:\n      lore +small\n      perform library_assistant +small\n      say: {~You find it on the fifth floor, wedged between the cookery books, looking innocent.|You track it by its shelfmarks to a landing that wasn't there yesterday. The queue cheers, quietly, because it's a library.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      perform library_assistant -tiny\n      say: {~You search until closing. In the morning the section is back where it started, faintly smug.|You find the wrong section, twice. The queue disperses with sighs you will hear in your sleep.}\n> right: Put up a sign and wait\n  wage\n  mood +tiny\n  perform library_assistant -tiny\n  say: {~\"Back shortly,\" says your sign. The section returns in the afternoon. The queue does not.|You sit at the desk and wait. A student asks whether the sign means you or the books.}\n\n@event library_assistant_hush_round\ntitle: The Hush Round\ntrack: library_assistant\nrank: 1\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: candle\ntags: job\nscene: A student assistant with a lamp walking between study tables in a dim library, one finger to her lips.\ntext: {Evening hush round. One lap of the reading room, a lamp, a finger to your lips.|The reading room has learned the sound of your lamp. Whispers stop a table ahead of you.|The regulars hear your step and straighten up before you reach them.} {~A table of second-years is giggling.|An old scholar snores on his notes, in a scholarly key.|Somebody's quill is scratching so loudly it echoes.} {final_year: Soon this will be someone else's lap.|}\n> left: Hush them firmly\n  wage\n  mood -tiny\n  perform library_assistant +small\n  rank_xp library_assistant +tiny\n  say: {~One look and the room falls silent. Somewhere a bookcase seems to approve.|You hush them. They glare. In the morning one of them leaves a note on the desk: \"sorry\", in very small writing.}\n> right: Let them be\n  wage\n  mood +tiny\n  perform library_assistant -tiny\n  say: {~You let it go. The noise rises, the head librarian appears from nowhere, and looks at you, not at them.|You leave them to it. For a while the murmur is the nicest sound in the building.}\n\n@event library_assistant_reference_desk\ntitle: The Reference Desk\ntrack: library_assistant\nrank: 2\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: scroll\ntags: job study\nscene: A student behind a reference desk facing a line of readers, one of them holding up a green ribbon.\ntext: {Clerks take the reference desk alone. The first question arrives before you sit down.|The desk has a worn patch now, exactly where your elbows go.|You keep a list of the strangest questions now, and it is long.} {~A professor wants \"the green book, about this thick, I read it in my youth\".|A first-year wants a book on a spell nobody has written yet.|A visiting scholar wants every book that mentions bees, by tonight.}\n> left: Dig until you find it\n  wage\n  challenge research medium:\n    win:\n      lore +small\n      perform library_assistant +small\n      rank_xp library_assistant +tiny\n      say: {~You find it in the end, in the wrong section, under a cat. The reader looks at you as if you had worked a miracle, and you have.|Three ladders, two catalogues and an educated guess. It is the right book.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      health -tiny\n      say: {~Hours later you admit defeat. The book, it turns out, was on the desk all along, propping up the inkpot.|You never find it. You dream of card catalogues all night.}\n> right: Send them off with a good guess\n  wage\n  mood +tiny\n  perform library_assistant -tiny\n  say: {~You point confidently at a staircase. They come back an hour later with a different book, which they say they liked better.|\"Try the fourth floor.\" They do. You hope the fourth floor helps.}\n\n@event library_assistant_stack_keys\ntitle: The Stack Keys\ntrack: library_assistant\nrank: 2\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\nweight: 20\nart: key\ntags: job study\nscene: An old librarian handing a brass ring of keys to a student among towering shelves at closing time.\ntext: At closing the head librarian presses a brass ring of keys into your hand: the upper stacks, the map drawers, the ladder chains. Not the Restricted Section. \"Lock up. The shelves settle after dark. Let them.\"\n> left: Do the closing round alone\n  wage\n  rank_xp library_assistant +medium\n  lore +small\n  mood +small\n  say: You walk every floor with the keys chiming. The shelves shift and creak and settle, like an old house going to sleep, and this time you are the one who gets to hear it.\n> right: Let Footnote come along\n  wage\n  rank_xp library_assistant +medium\n  mood +medium\n  say: Footnote pads ahead of you up every stair, checking each lock before you do. At the top he sits in the window and lets you scratch his ears, once. You suspect that was the real promotion.\n\n# ---------------- Server at the Gilded Newt (track: tavern) ----------------\n\n@event tavern_cider_rush\ntitle: The Cider Rush\ntrack: tavern\nrank: 1\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: cup\ntags: job money\nscene: A crowded low-beamed tavern, a server balancing a tray of steaming mugs through the press of bodies.\ntext: {The evening rush at the Gilded Newt. A dozen orders shouted at once, and a tray that holds fewer.|Half past seven, and the door won't stay shut. You have started remembering faces by their drink, which works until two people want spiced cider.|The regulars just point now, and you know what the points mean.} {~A barge crew has come in soaked and thirsty.|The card table is arguing about the rules again.|{season == \"winter\": Snow is melting off every coat in the room.|Half the village is on the step.}}\n> left: Keep every order in your head\n  wage\n  challenge research using lore easy:\n    win:\n      gold +tiny\n      perform tavern +small\n      say: {~Every mug to the right hand. The whole room tips like it's payday.|You don't miss once. The landlady, who remembers every tab to the penny, gives you a look of professional respect.}\n    lose:\n      mood -tiny\n      perform tavern -tiny\n      say: {~The spiced cider goes to the wrong table. The right table has opinions. The wrong table drinks it anyway.|You mix up two orders and three tempers.}\n> right: Write every order down\n  wage\n  perform tavern +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~Slow but sure. Nobody gets the wrong drink; nobody tips the slow server either.|Your order pad fills with a shorthand only you can read. By closing, you can't either.}\n\n@event tavern_regulars_corner\ntitle: The Regulars' Corner\ntrack: tavern\nrank: 1\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: group\ntags: job social\nscene: An old barge-man, a herb-wife from the lane and a quiet traveller at a fireside table, beckoning a server over.\ntext: {The regulars' corner by the fire wants you to sit for a minute. The fire is warm, and the minute is tempting.|The regulars' corner has saved you a stool now, with a cushion on it.|The regulars wave you over by name. They want to know how your exams went, all of them, in order.} {~The old barge-man is telling the eel story again.|The herb-wife from the lane wants her pie warmed \"exactly as before\".|A quiet traveller is listening to everything.}\n> left: Sit with them a while\n  wage\n  charm +small\n  gold +tiny\n  perform tavern -tiny\n  say: {~You hear the latest instalment of everyone's story. They tip you for listening, which is a better rate than serving.|You sit for one minute and stay for ten. The landlady clears her throat across the room like a falling tree.}\n> right: Keep the trays moving\n  wage\n  perform tavern +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~You wave and keep going. The corner toasts your back. The eel story will keep.|You work the room like weather. The corner calls you \"the brisk one\" now, which is not a compliment.}\n\n@event tavern_tab_book\ntitle: The Tab Book\ntrack: tavern\nrank: 2\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\ncast:\n  regular: present(place) where not tagged(regular, faculty)\nart: coin\ntags: job money\nscene: A ledger open on a tavern bar, a long column of figures, a hopeful customer at the far end.\ntext: {Barkeeps keep the tab book, and the tab book says {regular} owes the Newt rather a lot.|The tab book has grown a second volume, and {regular} has a page in it.|{regular} is at the end of the bar, and so, in the book, is a long column of figures.} {~{They} smile{s} at you hopefully.|{They} {are} pretending to be fascinated by the fire.|{They} {are} counting coins that clearly won't be enough.}\n> left: Call the tab in\n  wage\n  gold +tiny\n  perform tavern +small\n  rel regular -small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~{regular} pays, slowly, coin by coin, as if each one were a friend leaving.|You call it in. {regular} pays half and an apology. The landlady counts both.}\n> right: Let it ride another week\n  wage\n  rel regular +small\n  perform tavern -tiny\n  say: {~{regular} looks at you as if you had hung the moon. The landlady looks at you as if you had lost it.|Another week. The column grows, and so, oddly, does {regular}'s loyalty.}\n\n@event tavern_behind_the_bar\ntitle: Behind the Bar\ntrack: tavern\nrank: 2\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\nweight: 20\nart: cup\ntags: job\nscene: A landlady setting a heavy cellar key on a polished tavern bar, the hearth fire glowing behind her.\ntext: The landlady sets the cellar key on the bar between you. \"You pour from tonight,\" she says. \"Barkeeps feed the fire, too. It hasn't gone out in my lifetime, nor my mother's. Don't be the one.\" The hearth crackles, as if listening.\n> left: Bank the fire at closing\n  wage\n  rank_xp tavern +medium\n  perform tavern +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: You stay after the last song, banking the coals the way she showed you, slow and careful as tucking in a child. In the morning it is still burning. You check twice anyway.\n> right: Pour the staff a round\n  wage\n  rank_xp tavern +medium\n  mood +small\n  gold -tiny\n  say: You pour your first round for the cooks and the pot-boy. The landlady takes hers without a word, then taps the bar twice, which apparently means \"well poured\" in a language older than the Newt.\n\n# ---------------- Apothecary Clerk (track: apothecary_clerk) ----------------\n\n@event apothecary_clerk_counting_drops\ntitle: Counting Drops\ntrack: apothecary_clerk\nrank: 1\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: bottle\ntags: job\nscene: A student squinting at a glass dropper over a tiny vial at an apothecary counter, a wall of labelled drawers behind.\ntext: {Old Pell's first rule is to count every drop aloud. Today the counter is lined with tinctures waiting to be measured.|Drop-counting. Your tongue knows the numbers before your eyes do.|Counting drops. Old Pell says you count like a metronome now, which from him is a sonnet.} {~A customer is waiting, tapping a coin on the glass.|One of the daughters is humming, which throws off your count.|Today's tincture is the colour of a bruise and smells of violets.}\n> left: Count aloud, every drop\n  wage\n  perform apothecary_clerk +small\n  alchemy +tiny\n  mood -small\n  say: {~Every vial exact. Anyone in the shop has now heard you count higher than anyone should.|Aloud, drop by drop. Old Pell, passing, nods once without breaking stride.}\n> right: Count by eye, faster\n  wage\n  challenge brew easy:\n    win:\n      perform apothecary_clerk +small\n      gold +tiny\n      say: {~Quick and true. You clear the counter before the tea goes cold, and a satisfied customer leaves a coin.|By eye, and spot on. One of the daughters raises an eyebrow and says nothing to her father.}\n    lose:\n      perform apothecary_clerk -small\n      mood -tiny\n      say: {~One drop over. Old Pell can tell from across the shop. He tips the vial out and makes you count the next one aloud, twice.|You overshoot. The tincture turns from violet to a colour with no name, and Pell sighs like a closing door.}\n\n@event apothecary_clerk_drawer_check\ntitle: The Thousand Drawers\ntrack: apothecary_clerk\nrank: 1\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: potion\ntags: job mystery\nscene: A wall of tiny labelled wooden drawers, one of them rattling on its own.\ntext: {Each shift a clerk checks a row of drawers against the stock book. Today's row starts with one that is stuck.|A different row today, and the same feeling that something in it is awake.|Your row for the week. You have learned that the wrong drawer is never just the wrong drawer.} {~One is labelled in a hand that isn't Pell's.|One is rattling, very gently, as if politely.|One smells of snow, in {season == \"winter\": a shop this warm|the wrong season entirely}.}\n> left: Open the odd drawer\n  wage\n  challenge research using alchemy easy:\n    win:\n      alchemy +small\n      perform apothecary_clerk +small\n      say: {~Inside, a nest of dried moonpetals that belong on the top row. You move them, and the drawer stops sulking.|You coax it open and find the missing powdered newt. Pell crosses something off a list he has kept for a year.}\n    lose:\n      health -tiny\n      mood -tiny\n      say: {~The drawer opens with a sneeze of pepperwort. You sneeze until closing.|Something small and grey bolts out of the drawer and into another, and you never find out which.}\n> right: Mark the row as checked\n  wage\n  mood +tiny\n  perform apothecary_clerk -tiny\n  say: {~You tick the row and move on. Behind you a drawer rattles, as if to say it noticed.|\"Checked,\" you write. Pell reads it, opens one drawer at random, and closes it again with a look.}\n\n@event apothecary_clerk_counter_advice\ntitle: Asking the Dispenser\ntrack: apothecary_clerk\nrank: 2\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: potion\ntags: job\nscene: A worried customer leaning over an apothecary counter, a student behind it consulting a heavy recipe book.\ntext: {Dispensers advise, and today a customer asks you instead of Pell.|Pell has moved your stool to the end of the counter, where the customers come.|You know the regular coughs by sound now, before anyone opens the door.} {~A farmer has a cough that sounds like a gate.|A nervous student wants something for courage before exams.|A barge-hand has a back that hasn't straightened since autumn.}\n> left: Prescribe it yourself\n  wage\n  challenge brew medium:\n    win:\n      perform apothecary_clerk +small\n      rank_xp apothecary_clerk +small\n      say: {~You mix it, label it and explain the dose twice. A week later they come back just to say it worked.|Your remedy, your writing on the bottle. Pell reads the label, reaches for his pen, and leaves it as it is.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      perform apothecary_clerk -tiny\n      say: {~It helps a little, then not at all. They come back, and this time they ask for Pell.|Pell takes the bottle from your hand, adds one thing and gives it back. He doesn't say a word, which is worse.}\n> right: Fetch one of the daughters\n  wage\n  alchemy +small\n  perform apothecary_clerk -tiny\n  say: {~The elder daughter takes over and talks the customer down like a nervous horse. You watch and write it all down.|The younger daughter mixes it in half the time you would have. She lets you hold the funnel.}\n\n@event apothecary_clerk_labelling_pen\ntitle: The Labelling Pen\ntrack: apothecary_clerk\nrank: 2\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\nweight: 20\nart: feather\ntags: job\nscene: An old apothecary handing over a well-worn pen beside a stack of fresh blank labels.\ntext: Old Pell is very proud of his labelling. So it means something when he puts his own pen in your hand, its nib worn to his slant, and slides a stack of blank labels across the counter. \"New stock,\" he says. \"Your hand from now on. Neat, mind.\"\n> left: Label the new drawers yourself\n  wage\n  rank_xp apothecary_clerk +medium\n  alchemy +small\n  perform apothecary_clerk +small\n  say: You write every label slowly, in your best hand. Pell inspects them through his spectacles one by one and changes not a single letter. His daughters notice before you do.\n> right: Ask him to watch the first one\n  wage\n  rank_xp apothecary_clerk +medium\n  mood +medium\n  say: He watches. Your first label wobbles at the end. Pell takes the pen, adds a tiny flourish to your wobble so that it looks deliberate, and returns the pen for good.\n\n# ---------------- Broom Courier (track: broom_courier) ----------------\n\n@event broom_courier_morning_crossing\ntitle: The Morning Crossing\ntrack: broom_courier\nrank: 1\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: broom\ntags: job\nscene: A courier on a broom skimming low over a mirror-still lake at dawn, a mail sack over one shoulder.\ntext: {The dispatch bell rings. The morning sack goes to the village, and the quickest way is over the lake.|You have started timing yourself against the gulls.|The lake knows your shadow by now.} {season == \"winter\": Ice fog sits on the water like a lid.|{season == \"summer\": The water is so still you fly over your own reflection.|{~A headwind is coming off the hills.|Gulls are holding a meeting on your route.|The far shore is lost in mist.}}}\n> left: Fly straight across\n  wage\n  challenge feat easy:\n    win:\n      perform broom_courier +small\n      gold +tiny\n      say: {~Straight as a thrown stone. You're early, and the village postmistress tips early couriers.|You skim the water so low your boots leave a wake. Nobody sees, which is a shame.}\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      perform broom_courier -tiny\n      say: {~A gust drops you into the shallows. The sack stays dry; you do not.|The far shore vanishes and you fly a slow circle round a buoy. You arrive late and very damp.}\n> right: Take the long way round\n  wage\n  perform broom_courier +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~Round the shore, safe and slow. The dispatcher checks the clock and sighs.|The long way. You wave to a fisherman, who does not wave back.}\n\n@event broom_courier_broom_scramble\ntitle: The Broom Scramble\ntrack: broom_courier\nrank: 1\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: broom\ntags: job\nscene: A draughty loft rack of battered brooms, couriers jostling to grab the least battered one.\ntext: {Only one loft broom is in a good state, and every courier on shift wants it.|You have started arriving early for the brooms, and so has everyone else.|You could pick the good broom blindfold, by the smell of its varnish.} {~What's left is one that pulls left.|What's left sheds twigs when nervous.|What's left only flies if you're polite to it.} {season == \"winter\": Frost has got into all their bristles.|}\n> left: Race for the good broom\n  wage\n  perform broom_courier +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~You get there first, by a whisker and an elbow. The second-year you elbowed will remember.|The good broom is yours. It flies like a dream; the others fly like complaints.}\n> right: Take the difficult one\n  wage\n  vigor +small\n  health -tiny\n  perform broom_courier +tiny\n  say: {~It fights you the whole way across the lake. Your arms will remember this, and so will you.|It takes the whole route to come to an understanding with it. You win, narrowly.}\n\n@event broom_courier_dead_letters\ntitle: The Dead-Letter Shelf\ntrack: broom_courier\nrank: 2\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: letter\ntags: job mystery\nscene: A dusty shelf of undeliverable parcels in a courier loft, one tied with a faded ribbon.\ntext: {Couriers sort the dead letters, post with no proper address, waiting on a dusty shelf.|The dead-letter shelf has grown a second row.|You know which parcels have waited longest now, and which have stopped expecting anyone.} Today, a parcel addressed only to {~\"the tall one in the red scarf, third tower\"|\"whoever plays the flute at dawn\"|\"my brother, the one who never writes\"}.\n> left: Track down the recipient\n  wage\n  challenge research easy:\n    win:\n      perform broom_courier +small\n      rank_xp broom_courier +tiny\n      say: {~It takes three towers and a dozen questions, but the parcel reaches the right hands. They open it at once and cry a little, in a good way.|Found them. The look on their face is worth every stair.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      say: {~You knock on every door you can think of. The parcel goes back on the shelf, a little more crumpled, a little more sad.|Wrong door. Wrong tower. Wrong week.}\n> right: Leave it on the shelf\n  wage\n  mood +tiny\n  perform broom_courier -tiny\n  say: {~It goes back with the others. Some of them have been waiting longer than you have been alive.|You leave it. That night you dream of a parcel on a high tower, waiting.}\n\n@event broom_courier_own_pigeonhole\ntitle: Your Own Pigeonhole\ntrack: broom_courier\nrank: 2\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\nweight: 20\nart: bell\ntags: job\nscene: A courier loft wall of pigeonholes, one freshly painted with a student's name, a brass bell above.\ntext: The dispatcher has painted your name over a pigeonhole, in letters that will outlast the loft's roof. \"Your route from now on,\" the dispatcher says. \"Village, barge landing, the high towers. When the bell rings for you, you answer.\" The dispatch bell rings. It's for you.\n> left: Fly the route at once\n  wage\n  rank_xp broom_courier +medium\n  vigor +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: You fly your own route for the first time, every turning yours. At the barge landing someone calls you by name to hand over a letter. The wind is cold, and you don't mind at all.\n> right: Stand the runners a round\n  wage\n  rank_xp broom_courier +medium\n  mood +small\n  gold -tiny\n  say: The runners toast your pigeonhole with mugs of loft tea. Somebody chalks a little crown above your name. It is still there at the end of the year, smudged, the most permanent thing in the loft.\n"},{"path":"content/events/pools/courses_a.grim","text":"# Baseline course-morning pools, group courses_a (docs/authoring/pools.md):\n# Evocation, Astromancy & Divination, Alchemy & Healing. Repeatable routine events, three at level I and two each at II and III.\n\n# ---------------- Evocation (track: evocation, skill arcana) ----------------\n\n@event evo_snuffing_drill\ntitle: The Snuffing Drill\ntrack: evocation\nlevel: 1\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: candle\ntags: class magic\nscene: A row of iron braziers burning in a scorched lecture hall, students standing before them with their hands raised as if to calm a horse.\ntext: {Evocation I opens with a lesson nobody expected. \"Anyone can start a fire,\" says the lecturer. \"Stopping one is the course.\"|The braziers are lit before you arrive. You know which one smokes.|Your brazier goes out when you walk in now, out of habit. The lecturer relights it, unamused.} {~The front row smells of singed eyebrow.|Someone's brazier has gone out from fright.|The lecturer is timing you with an egg-timer.}\n> left: Call it back slowly\n  arcana +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~The flames shrink into embers, then into a warm ache in your palm.|It takes the whole hour. Your brazier goes out like a sigh.} {season == \"winter\": The hall is colder afterwards, and it's your fault.|The lecturer nods once.}\n> right: Snuff it in one breath\n  challenge exam using arcana easy:\n    win:\n      arcana +small\n      study evocation +tiny\n      say: {~The fire folds itself into your hand all at once.|One breath, and the brazier is dark.} The lecturer writes something down without looking surprised, which is the compliment.\n    lose:\n      health -tiny\n      mood -tiny\n      say: {~The fire goes out. It goes out up your sleeve first.|The brazier flares, then sulks, then goes out on its own terms.} You spend the rest of the hour holding your wrist in a bucket.\n\n@event evo_bucket_duty\ntitle: Bucket Duty\ntrack: evocation\nlevel: 1\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: wave\ntags: class study\nscene: A student standing at the edge of a busy lecture hall beside a bucket of sand and a bucket of water, watching sparks fly.\ntext: Every Evocation I lesson needs someone on the buckets: sand for the pyromancers, water for everyone else. {&Today it's your name on the board.|It's your turn again. The rota has a sense of humour.|Your name is on the board. You suspect the rota is personal.} {~The water bucket has a frog in it.|Someone has labelled the sand bucket \"Hope\".|The water bucket is half empty, because the tidecallers borrowed it.}\n> left: Mind the buckets, watch closely\n  study evocation +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~You put out four sleeves and a textbook, and learn more from their mistakes than from your own.|Watching from the side, you finally see what the lecturer means by \"breathe first\".} {month == 4 or month == 10: It's the best revision you've done all term.|Nobody thanks the bucket monitor.}\n> right: Swap for a turn up front\n  arcana +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {~You trade your duty for a turn at the practice line. Nobody is minding the buckets when your spark jumps the desk.|A first-year takes your buckets for a promise of help with their essay. Your practice goes well; your thumb goes pink.}\n\n@event evo_steady_cup\ntitle: The Steady Cup\ntrack: evocation\nlevel: 1\nlesson: mirror_lake\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: cup\ntags: class magic\nscene: A row of students on a stone terrace holding brimming cups at arm's length while a professor reads aloud from a slim book of poems.\ntext: {Professor Marisol's Tidecalling mornings begin with a full cup held at arm's length. Keep the water level by will alone while she reads poetry aloud.|Marisol has brought a new book of poems, thicker than the last.|You can hold the cup half asleep now, and often are.} {season == \"winter\": The water has a skin of ice, which she says is cheating.|{~The poem is very long.|The poem is about the sea, and so are your sleeves.|A gull is watching your cup with intent.}}\n> left: Hold it to the last line\n  arcana +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {~Your arm burns by the third stanza. The water doesn't move.|By the end your arm is shaking and the water isn't.} Marisol closes the book and says, \"There. The lake would approve.\"\n> right: Spill, and study her hands\n  study evocation +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~Your cup tips over in the second verse. You spend the rest watching how Marisol turns her wrist, and write it down.|You give up on the cup and watch Marisol instead. She holds a whole pond steady while turning pages.} {final_year: You'll miss her terrible poems.|Your shoes are wet.}\n\n@event evo_shaping_hour\ntitle: Shaping Hour\ntrack: evocation\nlevel: 2\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: flame\ntags: class magic\nscene: A lecture hall full of small fires shaped like birds, keys and letters, one of them visibly trying to escape through a window.\ntext: Evocation II is shaping: flame made into a sphere, a key, a bird. The lecturer keeps a ledger of every scorch mark in the hall and makes you sign for yours. {Your page is blank so far.|Your page in the ledger is filling up.|The ledger has started a second page for you.} {~Today's shape is a bird.|Today's shape is a key that turns.|Today's shape is your own initial, which is harder than it sounds.}\n> left: A hundred plain spheres\n  arcana +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {~Sphere after sphere, until your hands do it without asking you.|It's dull, honest work. Your spheres come out round, every one.} {season == \"summer\": The hall is an oven by noon.|The lecturer says nothing, which is approval.}\n> right: Try the hard shape\n  challenge exam using arcana medium:\n    win:\n      arcana +small\n      study evocation +small\n      say: {~Your bird flaps twice, circles the lectern and goes out on a perch.|Your key turns in the air, with a click you feel more than hear.} The lecturer turns the ledger round to show it hasn't moved.\n    lose:\n      health -tiny\n      mood -small\n      say: {~Your bird has opinions, and leaves through the window.|Your key comes out as a fork, and a hot one.} You sign the ledger. The lecturer blots it for you, almost kindly.\n> up[flame]: Shape it at a whisper\n  study evocation +small\n  say: {~You call the flame softly and it comes, small and obedient as a candle. The lecturer leans in to watch.|The shape comes out right first time, at a whisper. Your page in the ledger stays clean.|The flame stays the size you asked for, which is the whole lesson.}\n\n@event evo_kettle_pairs\ntitle: Fire Meets Water\ntrack: evocation\nlevel: 2\nlesson: mirror_lake\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: cauldron\ntags: class study\nscene: Pairs of students on either side of copper kettles, one with a palm of flame and one with a palm of water, steam curling between them.\ntext: {This is why Pyromancy and Tidecalling are taught together. One of you heats the kettle, one cools it, and it must simmer and never boil.|The kettles come out of the cupboard, every dent a story.|You know every kettle by its dents now. Two dents are yours.} {~Your partner is a nervous second-year from Stillwater.|Your partner is very confident, which is worrying.|Your partner yawns hugely.}\n> left: Take the fire side\n  challenge exam using arcana medium:\n    win:\n      arcana +small\n      study evocation +small\n      say: {~You and your partner find the rhythm together, and the kettle hums all hour.|Push, give, push. The kettle simmers like a contented cat.}\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      mood -tiny\n      say: {~The kettle boils, then whistles, then lifts its lid at you. You both get a faceful of steam.|You push too hard. Your partner panics and freezes the spout.}\n> right: Take the water side\n  study evocation +small\n  mana -small\n  say: {~Cooling is the patient half. You hold the kettle back all hour and your partner gets the credit.|You lend the kettle your cold for an hour and feel drained by noon.} {final_year: The kettle has been here longer than you, and will stay after you've gone.|Your fingertips stay damp until lunch.}\n\n@event evo_deep_call\ntitle: Calling From the Deep\ntrack: evocation\nlevel: 3\nlesson: mirror_lake\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: wave\ntags: class danger\nscene: Advanced students on a jetty with their arms raised, a column of dark, old lake water rising toward them out of the depths.\ntext: {Advanced Evocation calls water from the lake bed, where it is cold and old and in no hurry.|The jetty is slick at this hour.|The jetty boards know your boots by now.} The lecturer paces behind the line. The water comes up carrying things. {~Today, a bottle with no message in it.|Today, a single boot, laced.|Today, nothing at all, which is somehow worse.} {season == \"winter\": Ice grinds at the edges of the column.|Mist sits on the water.}\n> left: Reach deeper than the rest\n  challenge exam using arcana hard:\n    win:\n      arcana +medium\n      say: {~You call water up from somewhere it has been still for a century. It rises like a held breath and comes down gently.|The water obeys, then lingers, as if it wanted to talk.} The lecturer stops pacing.\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      mana -small\n      say: {~The lake pulls back. You're on your knees on the jetty, cold to the bone.|The water rises, considers you, and drops onto your head.} The lecturer pulls you up with no comment, which is a comment.\n> right: Keep to the shallows\n  study evocation +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {~You keep to safer water and log what the others bring up. It's useful and it's dull.|You hold your column at a sensible depth and watch someone else go white with the cold.} {gpa >= 3: The lecturer says you have nothing to prove. You'd like to.|You'll go deeper next time, perhaps.}\n\n@event evo_signature_flame\ntitle: A Fire of Your Own\ntrack: evocation\nlevel: 3\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: flame\ntags: class magic\nscene: A student alone at a stone bench in a dim hall, coaxing a flame of an unusual colour above their palm.\ntext: Advanced Evocation asks every student for a signature flame: its own colour, its own heat, its own temper. {Yours is a sulky orange.|Yours has turned a gold that you're quietly proud of.|Yours knows you now. It leans toward you when you come in.} {~Somebody's flame across the hall is green and smells of pine.|Somebody's flame is silver and hums.|The lecturer's own flame is lit on the lectern, blue and tiny, never discussed.}\n> left: Push it somewhere new\n  challenge exam using arcana hard:\n    win:\n      arcana +medium\n      say: {~Your flame slides into a colour nobody has a name for. The lecturer asks you to do it again, slowly.|For a moment your flame burns without smoke and without heat, just light.}\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      mood -small\n      say: {~Your flame flares white, takes a bite of your hand and goes back to orange as if nothing happened.|The new colour lasts a heartbeat. The blister lasts a week.}\n> right: Refine what it already is\n  study evocation +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~You spend the morning making it steadier, not stranger. It's a better flame by noon, and a less exciting one.|You tune the heat by a hair, then another hair.} {final_year: You'll carry this fire out of Lumenhold with you.|It's yours, at least.}\n\n# ---------------- Astromancy & Divination (track: astromancy, skill lore) ----------------\n\n@event astro_tea_rota\ntitle: Another Pot of Tea\ntrack: astromancy\nlevel: 1\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: cup\ntags: class study\nscene: A round classroom under a brass dome, every desk crowded with teacups, a huge brown teapot steaming on the lectern.\ntext: Astromancy I has a tea-leaf morning every week, and tea leaves require tea. {The class gets through three pots before ten.|The tea rota has your name on it again.|The teapot greets you like an old colleague.} {~Your cup shows something that is either a ship or a boot.|Your cup shows a shape the textbook calls the ladder. It looks like a spoon.|Your cup shows rain. Your cup always shows rain.}\n> left: Drink the pot and read\n  lore +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {~By the fourth cup your hands are shaking and the leaves are making more sense.|You read six cups in a row and see the pattern underneath the shapes.} {season == \"winter\": At least the tea is warm.|You will not sleep tonight.}\n> right: Brew the next pot\n  study astromancy +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~You take the rota and brew for the room. Watching leaves settle in a whole pot teaches you more than any one cup.|You brew it strong, the way the lecturer likes it, and learn the shapes as they sink.} {season == \"summer\": The classroom is a steam bath by ten.|Nobody thanks the brewer, but everyone notices weak tea.}\n\n@event astro_star_names\ntitle: Naming the Stars\ntrack: astromancy\nlevel: 1\nlesson: observatory\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: star\ntags: class study\nscene: Students craning their necks under a painted dome of constellations while a lecturer points upward with a very long pole.\ntext: {Astromancy I begins with names. Three hundred stars painted on the dome, to be recited on request.|The long pole comes out. The lecturer points; you name.|You dream the star names some nights now, and wake reciting.} {~Today it's the winter constellations.|Today it's the faint stars nobody can see without squinting.|Today the lecturer points at a patch of bare plaster and waits to see who invents a name.}\n> left: Recite them until perfect\n  study astromancy +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {~You chant star names under your breath all morning, like a prayer you don't quite believe in.|By noon you can name the whole southern sky backwards.} {month == 4 or month == 10: The exam is close, and the names are finally sticking.|Your neck aches from looking up.}\n> right: Volunteer to go first\n  challenge exam easy:\n    win:\n      lore +small\n      renown +tiny\n      say: {~You name every star the pole touches, including the bare plaster, which you correctly call \"nothing\".|The lecturer runs out of stars before you run out of names.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      lore +tiny\n      say: {~You call a bright star by the name of a lesser one. The lesser one's champion in the back row is offended.|You blank on the fourth star, and a whole constellation falls out of your head.}\n\n@event astro_cold_dome\ntitle: The Open Dome\ntrack: astromancy\nlevel: 1\nlesson: observatory\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: moon\ntags: class study\nscene: The observatory's brass dome cranked open to a pale dawn sky, one student at the great telescope and the rest huddled round an iron stove.\ntext: {Astromancy mornings start before the sun, under a dome cranked open to the sky.|The dome creaks open. You've learned which side of the stove is warmest.|The telescope's eyepiece has the shape of your eye by now.} {season == \"winter\": It is bitterly cold, and the stove is the most popular student in the class.|{season == \"summer\": The air is soft and the sky is already going pale.|There's dew on the telescope and on you.}} Somebody has to take the telescope.\n> left: Take the telescope turn\n  lore +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {~You track a planet across the eyepiece until your eyelashes stick to it.|The great telescope wanders, as it does. You coax it back to the sky you were assigned.} {final_year: You've looked through it more times than you can count, and you'll still miss it.|Your fingers take an hour to thaw.}\n> right: Stay by the stove with charts\n  study astromancy +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~The stove students form a little society of the warm and the guilty. You copy charts and hear every complaint.|You annotate the charts while the others shiver. The lecturer notices who is warm.}\n\n@event astro_foresight_drill\ntitle: Catch the Chalk\ntrack: astromancy\nlevel: 2\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: hourglass\ntags: class magic\nscene: A lecturer letting a stick of chalk fall from an outstretched hand while a student lunges to catch it a moment before it drops.\ntext: {Foresight at Astromancy II is practised on chalk. The lecturer drops a stick without warning; you catch it before it falls, by seeing it a heartbeat early.|The lecturer has opened a new box of chalk, which is a threat.|The lecturer's drops have got sneakier, and so have your hands.} {~The floor is white with failures.|A student from Stillwater has caught four in a row and is unbearable about it.|The lecturer is dropping two at once today.}\n> left: Catch it before it falls\n  challenge exam medium:\n    win:\n      lore +small\n      study astromancy +small\n      say: {~Your hand is there before the chalk is. The lecturer looks at it, then at you, then drops another.|You catch it a breath before the drop.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      say: {~You see it falling, clearly, half a second after it lands.|You catch a stick of chalk that wasn't dropped. The real one hits your shoe.}\n> right: Time the drops instead\n  study astromancy +small\n  mana -small\n  say: {~You keep a tally of every drop and every catch and start to see the lecturer's rhythm.|You work out that the lecturer always blinks first. It's not foresight, but it helps.} {gpa >= 3: The lecturer asks to see your tally.|Your head aches from looking ahead.}\n> up[foresight]: Let foresight do it\n  lore +small\n  say: {~You stop trying and let the spell look ahead for you. Your hand opens, and the chalk drops into it like a tame bird.|It's a two-chalk day. The spell shows you both, and you catch both.|The lecturer stops mid-drop and just looks at you. You catch it anyway.}\n\n@event astro_starlight_jars\ntitle: Bottling Starlight\ntrack: astromancy\nlevel: 2\nlesson: observatory\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: bottle\ntags: class magic\nscene: Students on the observatory roof before dawn, holding glass jars up to the sky, each jar glowing faintly with captured light.\ntext: {Starlight is gathered before dawn, one star at a time, in glass jars on the observatory roof.|The roof is slippery with frost or dew, depending.|The starlight jars. Yours has a chip in the rim you've grown fond of.} {~Most of the class is aiming for the brightest stars.|The lecturer says faint light keeps longer.|There's a slip of cloud coming in from the lake.}\n> left: Wait for the faint stars\n  challenge research medium:\n    win:\n      lore +small\n      study astromancy +small\n      say: {~Your jar fills slowly with a cool, pale light that lasts all day in your pocket.|The faint stars come one at a time.} The lecturer holds your jar up and says, \"That will keep.\"\n    lose:\n      health -tiny\n      mood -small\n      say: {~The cloud comes in before your jar is half full, and you're cold for nothing.|You wait so long the sun rises on you and burns the jar to plain glass.}\n> right: Take the bright, easy ones\n  study astromancy +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~Your jar is full in ten minutes and glows like a lamp. The lecturer calls it a lamp.|Easy light, quickly gathered and gone by noon.} {season == \"summer\": The short nights are no help.|You're back in bed before the bell.}\n\n@event astro_moving_charts\ntitle: The Charts Move\ntrack: astromancy\nlevel: 3\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: map\ntags: class study\nscene: A long table covered in star charts whose inked constellations are visibly sliding across the parchment, students pinning corners down.\ntext: {Advanced Astromancy uses charts that move. The inked stars drift to where they will be, not where they are.|The charts are livelier this morning, and one has crept off the table.|You've stopped flinching when the stars move. You still check twice.} {~Your chart keeps rearranging itself into a shape like a doorway.|Your chart is sulking in one corner.|Your chart is going faster than the others.} Pin it and read it, or read it as it goes.\n> left: Pin it and trace the drift\n  challenge research hard:\n    win:\n      lore +medium\n      say: {~You pin each star and trace its path, and for a moment you can see the whole season ahead laid out in ink.|The drift makes sense all at once.}\n    lose:\n      mana -small\n      mood -small\n      say: {~The chart slides out from under your pins and leaves ink on your sleeves.|You trace the drift for an hour and it goes back to where it started, laughing at you in its own way.}\n> right: Sketch every shape it makes\n  study astromancy +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {~You fill four pages with the shapes the chart passes through. Most are nothing. One is almost something.|You draw it as it moves, like a portrait of someone who won't sit still.} {final_year: Soon the stars will move without you watching.|The lecturer collects your pages without a word.}\n\n@event astro_far_glimpse\ntitle: Looking Too Far\ntrack: astromancy\nlevel: 3\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: eye\ntags: class magic\nscene: A student staring into a dark mirror at the centre of a curtained room, a lecturer standing close with an hourglass.\ntext: {At this level Foresight reaches a season ahead. The lecturer allows one glimpse each, timed by an hourglass. People who look longer come back strange.|The dark mirror waits behind its curtain.|The hourglass is turned before you sit down. The lecturer knows your pace.} {final_year: A season from now you'll be gone, which makes the mirror harder to look at.|{~The curtains are drawn against the morning.|Someone before you came out very quiet.}}\n> left: Look a season ahead\n  challenge exam hard:\n    win:\n      lore +small\n      study astromancy +small\n      say: {~You see a lit window and a cup going cold, and you know exactly where and when it will be. You keep it to yourself.|You see something small and true. You'll remember it when it happens.}\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      mood -small\n      say: {~The mirror shows you too much at once. You come out with a nosebleed and a headache that lasts till dinner.|You see nothing, and then you see everything.}\n> right: Look only as far as tomorrow\n  study astromancy +small\n  mana -small\n  say: {~Tomorrow, says the mirror, it will rain at noon. It does. It's not much, but it's right.|You look only one day ahead. Lunch will be soup.} {gpa >= 3: The lecturer says caution is a talent too.|It's safe, and it feels small.}\n\n# ---------------- Alchemy & Healing (track: alchemy, skill alchemy) ----------------\n\n@event alch_stir_count\ntitle: Clockwise\ntrack: alchemy\nlevel: 1\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: cauldron\ntags: class study\nscene: A laboratory of identical cauldrons, a whole class stirring in unison while the lecturer counts aloud from the front.\ntext: {Alchemy I is mostly stirring, and the stirring is counted aloud by the whole class, in unison, like a choir with spoons.|The chorus is getting good. Someone has started harmonising.|You count stirs in your sleep now, and sometimes on the stairs.} {~Today's draught is a simple tonic.|Today's draught is meant to turn blue at forty.|Today the lecturer keeps changing the count to see who's listening.}\n> left: Count every stir aloud\n  study alchemy +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~By the hundredth stir your voice is gone and your tonic is perfect.|Your count is right and your draught is dull. Both are what was asked.} {month == 4 or month == 10: The exam recipes come back to you in rhythm.|Your wrist aches.}\n> right: Stir by the colour instead\n  challenge brew easy:\n    win:\n      alchemy +small\n      say: {~You stop counting and watch the colour. It turns blue exactly when you expect it to.|You lose the count and keep the draught. The lecturer can tell, and allows it.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      health -tiny\n      say: {~You watch the colour too closely and miss it changing. The fumes make your eyes water.|Your draught turns a colour the lecturer has never seen. It's not a good sign.}\n\n@event alch_chopping_board\ntitle: Prep Work\ntrack: alchemy\nlevel: 1\nlesson: pell_and_daughters\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: leaf\ntags: class study\nscene: A long scrubbed bench heaped with roots, berries and dried beetles, students bent over chopping boards with small sharp knives.\ntext: {Half of alchemy, says the lecturer, is cutting things up correctly. The ingredients come from Pell & Daughters in the village, packed in straw, every one labelled in a careful hand.|The crate from the village is heavier today.|Your knife knows the way, and your thumb knows where not to be.} {~Today it's roots, to be sliced paper-thin.|Today it's beetles, to be crushed and not thought about.|Today it's berries, which stain everything.}\n> left: Prep for the whole bench\n  alchemy +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {~You cut enough for six. Your bench-mates bring you tea, which is fair.|You chop for an hour. The roots are perfect and your fingers smell of them till dinner.} {season == \"autumn\": The berries are fresh this time of year, and it's almost pleasant.|The crate is empty when you're done.}\n> right: Prep yours, read ahead\n  study alchemy +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {~You chop quickly and read the next recipe. You nick your thumb on the second root.|You rush the beetles and get to the reading, plus a small cut you'll be explaining all day.}\n\n@event alch_bandage_round\ntitle: Bandage Round\ntrack: alchemy\nlevel: 1\nlesson: infirmary\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: cross\ntags: class study\nscene: A sunny infirmary ward where students roll bandages at a long table while a nun in a starched apron checks their work.\ntext: {The healing half of Alchemy I is taught in the infirmary, where Sister Amaranth has the class roll bandages and watch her work.|Sister Amaranth inspects every roll, and unrolls one of yours.|Sister Amaranth hands you a roll without looking up.} {~A third-year is in with a burnt hand from Evocation.|A first-year is in with a cold and a great deal to say about it.|The ward is empty, which Sister Amaranth calls a miracle.}\n> left: Roll bandages and watch\n  study alchemy +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~You roll, and watch Sister Amaranth clean a cut in about three movements.|Your bandages pass inspection.} {season == \"winter\": The ward fills with sniffling students all morning.|She says nothing, which is high praise.}\n> right: Offer to treat a patient\n  challenge exam using alchemy easy:\n    win:\n      alchemy +small\n      say: {~You clean and bind a scraped knee neatly. The patient doesn't even wince. Sister Amaranth adjusts one knot.|You treat the burn properly. Sister Amaranth says, \"Hm.\" From her, that is a speech.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      say: {~You bandage the wrong knee. The patient is too polite to say so; Sister Amaranth is not.|Your bandage unrolls itself and the patient has to wait while you start again.}\n\n@event alch_purify_samples\ntitle: Lake Water Samples\ntrack: alchemy\nlevel: 2\nlesson: mirror_lake\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: bottle\ntags: class magic\nscene: A bench lined with jars of murky lake water, a lecturer at the end raising one now-clear jar to drink it.\ntext: {Alchemy II hands out jars of murky lake water, to be made fit to drink. The lecturer has a habit of drinking the results.|The lecturer looks thirsty, which is never a good sign.|The lecturer has brought a favourite cup for this, chipped at the rim.} {~Your jar has a small weed in it.|Your jar is suspiciously green.|Your jar has a tiny snail in it, which you set free.}\n> left: Purify it thoroughly\n  challenge brew medium:\n    win:\n      alchemy +small\n      study alchemy +small\n      say: {~Your water comes out clear and sweet. The lecturer drinks it, pauses and nods.|Clean as rainwater.} {The lecturer drinks a second glass, which nobody has seen before.|The lecturer drinks a second glass, and pretends not to.|The lecturer now holds out the cup for yours first.}\n    lose:\n      health -tiny\n      mood -small\n      say: {~You test it first, just a sip, as the lecturer always does. You spend the afternoon close to the washroom.|Your water clears, then clouds again as you lift it.}\n> right: Filter it the slow way\n  study alchemy +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {~Cloth, sand and charcoal, the old way. It takes the whole lesson.|You filter it by hand and write every step down. The lecturer drinks it and says, \"Patient.\"} {final_year: You'll be filtering water like this for the rest of your life, probably.|Your hands are grey with charcoal.}\n\n@event alch_long_simmer\ntitle: The Long Simmer\ntrack: alchemy\nlevel: 2\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: hourglass\ntags: class study\nscene: A bubbling cauldron in an empty laboratory, a single student slumped on a stool beside it with an hourglass and a book.\ntext: {Some potions simmer for hours and must be watched. Whoever is on watch when the colour turns gets the credit, or the blame.|The class takes turns at the cauldron, and the rota has come round.|Your turn at the simmer. You've brought a cushion this time.} {~The rest of the class has gone to lunch.|The potion is making a noise like a contented cat.|The lecturer has left a note that says \"Do not leave it.\"}\n> left: Sit the whole watch\n  alchemy +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {~Three hours, one cauldron, one hourglass. You see it turn and catch it at the right moment.|You read the same paragraph forty times and stir at every bubble.} {season == \"summer\": The laboratory is stifling.|By the end, you can tell its mood from the bubbles.}\n> right: Set a charm to watch it\n  challenge enchant medium:\n    win:\n      study alchemy +small\n      mood +tiny\n      say: {~Your charm rings like a bell when the colour turns. You come back from lunch in time to take it off the fire.|The charm works and you get to eat.}\n    lose:\n      alchemy +tiny\n      health -small\n      say: {~Your charm falls asleep. The potion boils over and you breathe in a lot of steam getting it off the fire.|The charm rings for no reason, three times, and the fourth time you ignore it.}\n\n@event alch_volatile_reagents\ntitle: Volatile Reagents\ntrack: alchemy\nlevel: 3\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: potion\ntags: class danger\nscene: A hushed laboratory, a student holding a glass vial of glowing liquid at arm's length, everyone else very still behind a screen.\ntext: {Advanced Alchemy handles reagents that react to being looked at the wrong way. The lecturer has one rule. One person speaks at a time, and that person is the one holding the vial.|Nobody is speaking. Somebody is breathing too loudly.|You've stopped holding your breath, mostly.} {~Today's vial is pale yellow and fizzing.|Today's vial is dark red and perfectly still, which is worse.|Today's vial is labelled in the lecturer's hand: \"Gently.\"}\n> left: Handle it yourself\n  challenge brew hard:\n    win:\n      alchemy +medium\n      say: {~You measure it drop by drop, speaking only the numbers. It settles exactly as it should.|Your hands don't shake.} The lecturer lets out a breath.\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      mood -tiny\n      say: {~The vial pops like a cork. Your eyebrows survive; your gloves do not.|A drop too many. The flask hisses and coughs out blue smoke, and you spend the hour in the infirmary.}\n> right: Pass it to the steadiest hand\n  study alchemy +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {~You hand the vial to someone with steadier hands and take careful notes. It stings a little.|You watch someone else do it right. You'll know how next time.} {gpa >= 3: The lecturer says you're allowed to be cautious.|You note every step.}\n\n@event alch_original_remedy\ntitle: A Remedy of Your Own\ntrack: alchemy\nlevel: 3\nlesson: infirmary\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: potion\ntags: class study\nscene: A student at a cluttered bench writing a recipe in a notebook, a small flask of their own remedy steaming beside it.\ntext: {Advanced Alchemy asks for an original remedy, invented, written up, and tested under Sister Amaranth's eye.|Another revision. The notebook has more crossings-out than words.|Your remedy is nearly finished. It has been nearly finished for weeks.} {~This version is for headaches.|This version is for the common cold, which has resisted remedies for centuries.|This version is for exam nerves, which you need.}\n> left: Test it on yourself\n  challenge brew hard:\n    win:\n      alchemy +medium\n      say: {~It works. Your headache lifts and your mood with it.|It works better than you expected.} {Sister Amaranth writes your recipe in her own book.|Sister Amaranth adds a note to your page in her book.|Sister Amaranth's book has a whole page of yours now.}\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      mood -small\n      say: {~It does something, but not what you wanted. Your ears are warm for the rest of the day.|It cures the headache and replaces it with hiccups.}\n> right: Ask Sister Amaranth to review it\n  study alchemy +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {~She crosses out three ingredients and one adjective.|She reads it twice, then hands it back. \"Sound,\" she says. \"Dull.\"} {final_year: You'll keep this recipe long after you leave Lumenhold.|It's better already, and you hate it a little.}\n> up[purify]: Purify the base first\n  alchemy +small\n  say: {~You draw every impurity out of the base before you start. The remedy comes out clear and gentle.|The base comes clean in one pass. Sister Amaranth smells it and says nothing, which is approval.|With a pure base, the fault in your recipe finally shows. You fix it.}\n"},{"path":"content/events/pools/courses_b.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Baseline pools (phase 3, step 1b): course mornings for\n#  Illusion & Glamour, Beastcraft & Flight and Umbral Theory.\n#  Repeatable routine events, drawn once per enrolled course a day.\n# ============================================================\n\n# ---------------- Illusion & Glamour (track: illusion) ----------------\n\n@event illu_candle_drill\ntitle: The Candle Drill\ntrack: illusion\nlevel: 1\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: candle\ntags: class magic\nscene: A row of unlit candles on a lecture bench, each crowned with a flame that casts no shadow, a professor holding her palm over one.\ntext: {Illusion I opens with a row of cold candles. Make them burn, Professor Vey says, without burning.|Vey's drawer of candles is open. Every one is older than you.|The candle drill. Some mornings you could do it asleep.} {~Vey passes her hand through each flame and says \"cold\" in the same flat voice.|Someone's flame leans in a draught only it can feel.|The student beside you has made a lovely candle and forgotten the flame.}\n> left: Hold the flame until it's right\n  study illusion +small\n  mana -small\n  say: {~Your flame steadies, then flickers in time with your breathing, which Vey says is the point.|Vey's palm hovers over your flame a moment longer than the others. \"Almost warm,\" she says. From her, that's a sonnet.|By the bell your flame has a proper blue heart. You feel oddly proud of something that isn't there.}\n> right: Make it gutter and smoke\n  challenge deceive using charm easy:\n    win:\n      charm +small\n      mood +tiny\n      say: {~You add a thread of smoke and a little gutter. The student behind you reaches to pinch it out.|Your candle drips wax that isn't there onto a desk that is. Vey watches it fall and says nothing, which means yes.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      say: {~The smoke goes up and the flame goes out, as though embarrassed by its own ambition.|Vey waves a hand through the whole performance. \"A candle doesn't try this hard,\" she says.}\n\n@event illu_one_small_change\ntitle: One Small Change\ntrack: illusion\nlevel: 1\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: mirror\ntags: class magic\nscene: Students bent over little hand mirrors, one changing the colour of her own eyes, another squinting at a nose that isn't quite his.\ntext: {Hand mirrors all round. Glamour your own face, Vey says; change one small thing and hold it.|Out come the mirrors. A glamour you can't hold on yourself, Vey says, won't hold on anyone.|Mirror practice. You know your own face too well by now.} {~Vey says people always change their eyes first.|A second-year from Rootward is giving himself a better chin, slowly.|The mirrors fog, as they do {season == \"winter\": in the cold|when the room warms}.}\n> left: Change something trivial\n  study illusion +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~You move a freckle half an inch. It takes the whole hour. Vey initials your page without looking up.|You darken one eyebrow and keep it dark. Dull, careful work, and the glamour holds.|A small scar vanishes and comes back, vanishes and comes back. By the end it stays gone until you let it.}\n> right: Change what you'd really change\n  charm +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {~It holds beautifully. You look at it longer than the exercise requires, and let it go more slowly than you'd like.|Vey glances over your shoulder. \"Ah,\" she says, kindly enough. \"That one. Everybody has one.\"|You hold it all hour. Afterwards your real face seems slightly unfamiliar, like a coat you've lent out.}\n\n@event illu_spot_the_coin\ntitle: Spot the Coin\ntrack: illusion\nlevel: 1\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: coin\ntags: class social\ncast:\n  partner: any where tagged(partner, student)\nscene: Two students at a bench with three coins between them, one coin shimmering very slightly at the edge.\ntext: {Partner work. {partner} lays out three crowns, and one of them is an illusion. Say which.|Three crowns from {partner}. One isn't there, and {they} look{s} pleased about it.|You and {partner} have started keeping score.} {~Vey walks the room flicking real coins, to confuse everyone.|The windows are bright and the illusions show it.|The next bench is arguing whether a coin can be \"partly fake\".}\n> left: Study the coins honestly\n  study illusion +small\n  mood -tiny\n  rel partner +tiny\n  say: {~It's the one with no tarnish. Illusions always come out cleaner than money does.|You stare until your eyes water and pick the middle one. Wrong. {partner} pockets the real two, laughing.|You get it on the third try, by the shine that doesn't move when the light does.}\n> right: Watch their face, not the coins\n  challenge deceive using charm easy:\n    win:\n      charm +small\n      rel partner +tiny\n      say: {~{partner} glances at the left coin a fraction too fondly. You point. {They} groan{s}.|You keep smiling until {partner} gives it away by smiling back. Vey notes that this isn't the exercise, and that it worked.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      say: {~{partner} has a better face than you'd thought. Your wrong guess buys {them} a cup of tea.|You watch {partner} so hard you miss the coin blinking out of existence entirely.}\n\n@event illu_bread_smell\ntitle: The Smell of Bread\ntrack: illusion\nlevel: 2\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: meal\ntags: class magic\nscene: A lecture bench with an illusory loaf of bread on a board, steam rising from it, and several students sniffing it doubtfully.\ntext: {Illusion II adds the other senses. Today, a loaf that looks, smells and sounds fresh when broken.|More bread. Sight is easy, Vey says; smell is where liars are caught.|The bread lesson. The floor smells of loaves that never existed.} {~Somebody's loaf smells strongly of wet dog.|One loaf crackles beautifully and smells of nothing.|{gpa >= 3: Vey asks you to go first.|Vey asks you to go last, which feels pointed.}}\n> left: Get the smell right\n  study illusion +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~You spend an hour on the smell of a crust. By the end it's nearly convincing and you're desperately hungry.|You think of a bakery on a cold street and the smell arrives with it. Vey sniffs, and nods once.|The smell is right. The sound of breaking is wrong. Vey says that's the usual order.}\n> right: Add the warmth of the oven\n  challenge enchant using charm medium:\n    win:\n      charm +small\n      renown +tiny\n      say: {~Vey picks your loaf up and puts it down hastily, as if it had burnt her. The class actually applauds.|The bench beside you swears the room got warmer. It didn't. That's the point.}\n    lose:\n      mana -small\n      mood -tiny\n      say: {~The warmth arrives and the loaf leaves. You're left holding a hot, empty board.|Your bread steams, then smokes, then smells of a cupboard. Vey writes something short.}\n\n@event illu_find_vey\ntitle: Find the Professor\ntrack: illusion\nlevel: 2\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: mask\ntags: class magic\nscene: A lecture hall where the professor's chair is empty and everyone is looking suspiciously at everyone else, including a pigeon on the sill.\ntext: {Professor Vey is never at the lectern in Illusion II. She is somewhere in the room, wearing someone else, and the first to name her wins.|Vey's chair is empty. Everyone eyes the porter with the mop.|The lectern is empty. You trust nothing in this room.} {~There's a pigeon on the sill.|A student nobody remembers has taken the back row.|One of the portraits seems unusually attentive.}\n> left: Watch everyone, all lesson\n  study illusion +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {~You don't find her, but you notice who blinks late. Vey turns out to have been the pigeon.|You learn three classmates' nervous habits and nothing about Vey, who was the chair.|At the end she steps out of the porter's coat. You'd suspected the mop. The mop was real.}\n> right: Name her early\n  challenge deceive using charm medium:\n    win:\n      charm +small\n      mood +tiny\n      say: {~\"Professor,\" you say to the new student in the back row. The glamour falls away like a dropped shawl. Vey looks almost pleased.|You name her before the bell has finished ringing. She says, \"Hm,\" which from Vey means a great deal.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      say: {~You accuse a perfectly ordinary classmate. He will remember it. So will Vey, who was standing behind you.|You point at the portrait. The portrait points back. Vey, at the lectern after all, makes a note.}\n\n@event illu_courtyard_hour\ntitle: Holding the Courtyard\ntrack: illusion\nlevel: 3\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: sparkle\ntags: class magic danger\nscene: A student standing very still in a busy courtyard, sweat on their brow, while a flock of illusory swallows circles above the crowd.\ntext: {Advanced Illusion leaves the classroom. Hold an illusion over the busy courtyard for an hour, and don't let it fray.|Another courtyard hour. The porters step round you now.|The courtyard. {final_year: One of the last times you'll hold this sky.|Your hands know the weight of it.}} {~A flock of swallows today.|Rain that doesn't wet anyone.|{season == \"winter\": Snow that never lands.|{season == \"spring\": Blossom that never lands.|{season == \"autumn\": Leaves that never land.|Thistledown that never lands.}}}}\n> left: Hold it the full hour\n  charm +small\n  health -small\n  say: {~By the end your head rings like a struck bell. Nobody looked up once, which is how you know it worked.|A first-year holds out a hand to catch something you made. You keep it falling for her until the bell.|You hold it, then sit on the fountain's edge for longer than you'd like anyone to see.}\n> right: Keep it small and steady\n  study illusion +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~One swallow, perfect, for an hour. Vey says ambition is cheap and an hour is not.|You shrink it to a patch of weather over one bench. It never frays, and nobody notices, which is the point and the pity.}\n\n@event illu_original_work\ntitle: An Illusion of Your Own\ntrack: illusion\nlevel: 3\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: eye\ntags: class magic\nscene: A darkened studio room, a student alone at the centre, and around them the half-formed image of a lit window at dusk.\ntext: {Original work, an illusion nobody taught you. Vey asks one question of each piece. \"What does it make people feel?\"|More original work. Vey's question hasn't changed; your answers get longer.|Studio morning. {final_year: Next year, Vey says, she'll ask someone else.|Vey goes round the room asking her question.}} {~A classmate has made a door that sighs.|Someone has made the smell of rain on hot stone.|Someone's illusion is humming.}\n> left: Make something true\n  study illusion +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {~You make the window of a house you grew up near, at dusk. Vey stands in front of it a long time and writes nothing down.|You make a corridor that remembers who walked down it. It's sadder than you meant. Vey says that's usually the good sign.}\n> right: Make something dazzling\n  challenge enchant using charm hard:\n    win:\n      charm +small\n      renown +small\n      say: {~A staircase of light climbs into the ceiling and keeps going. The class goes quiet. \"Yes,\" says Vey. \"But what does it feel like?\"|Your piece fills the room with lanterns, one for each person watching. Vey counts them, and finds one for herself.}\n    lose:\n      mana -small\n      mood -small\n      say: {~It's dazzling for a breath and then empty, like a fair after the fair has gone.|Vey watches it all, politely. \"Very bright,\" she says. You'd have preferred anything else.}\n\n# ---------------- Beastcraft & Flight (track: beastcraft) ----------------\n\n@event beast_mucking_out\ntitle: Mucking Out\ntrack: beastcraft\nlevel: 1\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: paw\ntags: class nature\nscene: A student with a pitchfork in a straw-strewn stable, a hippogriff watching from its stall with a look of deep judgement.\ntext: {Beastcraft I begins in the stables, with a pitchfork. Professor Brask says you don't touch a creature until you've cleaned up after it.|Pitchforks. Brask still hasn't said when that rule ends.|The stables, early. You know which stall to start with.} {~The hippogriff in the end stall is watching you.|Owls shift in the rafters, unimpressed.|{season == \"summer\": The heat doubles the smell.|A cold wind finds every gap.}}\n> left: Muck out properly\n  vigor +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {~Two stalls, a barrow and a blister. Brask inspects your work and calls the straw \"nearly decent\".|You finish before anyone else. The hippogriff treads straight through your clean straw, deliberately.|Brask says nothing, which you've learned is the best review she gives humans.}\n> right: Bow to the hippogriff first\n  challenge exam using vigor easy:\n    win:\n      study beastcraft +small\n      mood +tiny\n      say: {~You bow low and hold it. The hippogriff considers, then bows back. Brask writes your name on the board, without comment.|The hippogriff lets you near enough to see where its feathers turn to fur. You'll remember the exact line.}\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      say: {~You bow too fast. The hippogriff takes it as a challenge and accepts.|The hippogriff looks at your bow, then at your hat, and eats the hat.}\n\n@event beast_hover_line\ntitle: The Hover Line\ntrack: beastcraft\nlevel: 1\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: broom\ntags: class danger\nscene: A line of students hovering a foot above a lawn on brooms, one broom drifting out of line, a stern professor counting slowly.\ntext: {Flight is half the course. Brask lines you up on the lawn to hover a foot up and hold still while she counts.|The hover line. Brask counts slowly on purpose, you're sure.|Hover line. You and your broom have an understanding, of sorts.} {~Somebody's broom keeps edging toward the lake.|An owl has landed on the end of a broom, and its rider is pretending not to notice.|Brask says, as always, that brooms are harder to reason with than griffons.}\n> left: Hold still until the count\n  vigor +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {~You hold it until forty. Your legs remember it for a day.|The broom bucks once at thirty-one. You hold on. Brask gets to fifty and nods.|Steady all the way. Brask says it's nice to see a broom that knows who's in charge, and she means the broom.}\n> right: Study how the others fall off\n  study beastcraft +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~You stand at the end and watch. You learn a great deal about balance, and nobody lets you forget you didn't fly.|You take notes on every wobble. Brask reads them and says you should consider a career in inquests.}\n\n@event beast_feeding_round\ntitle: Feeding Round\ntrack: beastcraft\nlevel: 1\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: feather\ntags: class nature\ncast:\n  partner: any where tagged(partner, student)\nscene: Two students carrying buckets between stalls, a sulky griffon chick in a nest of straw, owls leaning down from the rafters.\ntext: {Feeding round, in pairs. You and {partner} have two buckets and a list, and the griffon chick is first on it.|Feeding round with {partner}. The griffon chick has heard the buckets.|You and {partner} could do the round blindfolded now. The chick would still sulk.} {~Brask has written \"DO NOT HURRY\" at the top of the list.|The owls lean down from the rafters to watch.|{partner} is humming to the buckets.}\n> left: Take the griffon chick\n  challenge exam using vigor easy:\n    win:\n      vigor +small\n      rel partner +tiny\n      say: {~The chick eats from the bucket, then from your hand, then tries your sleeve. {partner} applauds quietly.|It sulks, then eats, then sulks about having eaten. Brask says that's progress for a griffon.}\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      say: {~The chick bites the bucket, then the hand holding it. {partner} finishes the round alone, kindly.|It knocks the bucket over and sits in it, glaring. You spend the hour fishing out its breakfast.}\n> right: Take the owls instead\n  study beastcraft +small\n  mood -tiny\n  rel partner +tiny\n  say: {~You learn the owls' names from the tags on their perches, and what a pellet is, in detail.|Up and down the ladder for an hour. {partner} survives the chick and is grateful in a quiet way.|The owls take their mice with great ceremony. One of them seems to be counting yours.}\n\n@event beast_hare_paddock\ntitle: Running with Hares\ntrack: beastcraft\nlevel: 2\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: bolt\ntags: class magic\nscene: Students sprinting across a paddock alongside a scattering of brown hares, one student's feet suddenly much faster than the rest of them.\ntext: {Beastcraft II teaches haste the old way. Run with the hares until their speed rubs off. \"Borrow it,\" Brask says. \"Don't steal it.\"|The hare paddock. The hares remember you.|Hares. You dream in sudden right angles now.} {~The grass is wet and the hares don't care.|Brask times you with a crooked hourglass.|{season == \"summer\": It's hot and the hares are lazy.|The paddock smells of rain.}}\n> left: Run until it catches\n  vigor +small\n  health -small\n  say: {~For three strides you're faster than you've any right to be. Then you're in the hedge.|You catch it on the fourth lap, a lightness behind the knees. You pay for it with a nap you can't refuse.|The hares let you keep up for a moment, out of curiosity. It's the best moment of your week.}\n> right: Watch the hares and take notes\n  study beastcraft +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~You sketch the way a hare turns, everything at once, no warning. Brask says your sketch is better than your running.|You note how the hares rest between bursts. Brask reads it and says, \"Yes. That's the half everyone forgets.\"}\n\n@event beast_complaints\ntitle: The Complaints Hour\ntrack: beastcraft\nlevel: 2\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: owl\ntags: class nature\nscene: A student sitting on an upturned bucket in a stable, surrounded by animals who all seem to be talking at once.\ntext: {Beast-speech practice. You sit on a bucket in the stables and listen. Every creature has been waiting to complain, and the owls start with the draught.|Another hour on the bucket. The owls open, as always, with the draught.|The bucket, the owls, the draught. Brask calls it fieldwork.} {~The hippogriff wants different hay. It is very specific.|The griffon chick has a list.|{gpa >= 3: Brask leaves you alone with them, which feels like trust.|Brask stays near the door, which feels like supervision.}}\n> left: Hear every complaint out\n  study beastcraft +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {~The owls talk for forty minutes about the draught. You understand every word now, which is the trouble.|The hippogriff explains its views on hay at length. You come away fluent and exhausted.|A mouse you didn't know was there wants to be on the record. You put it on the record.}\n> right: Fix the draught yourself\n  vigor +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {~You climb into the rafters with a hammer and a plank. The owls supervise. The draught stops, and so do the complaints, briefly.|You patch the gap with straw and a board. The owls say thank you, then mention the window.}\n\n@event beast_lake_flight\ntitle: Over the Lake\ntrack: beastcraft\nlevel: 3\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nlesson: mirror_lake\nart: feather\ntags: class danger\nscene: A student on a hippogriff's back skimming low over a mirror-still lake, their reflection racing along beneath them.\ntext: {Advanced Beastcraft. A hippogriff each, over the lake, no saddle unless you ask. Brask won't think less of you for asking. She'll just remember.|The lake flight. Your hippogriff knows the way.|Over the lake. {final_year: You catch yourself memorising the view.|Your reflection keeps pace.}} {~The water is perfectly still.|A barge below, its passengers waving.|{season == \"summer\": The air is full of midges.|The wind off the water stings.}}\n> left: Ride bareback, low over the water\n  challenge exam using vigor hard:\n    win:\n      vigor +small\n      mood +small\n      say: {~You skim so low a wingtip draws a line across the lake. For one breath you and your reflection touch hands.|The hippogriff banks and you go with it, as if you'd always known how. Brask says nothing at all, then grins.}\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      mood -tiny\n      say: {~The hippogriff dips and you don't. The lake is colder than it looks. It always is.|You slide off on the turn and hang on by a fistful of feathers, all the way back to shore.}\n> right: Take the saddle and the high circuit\n  study beastcraft +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~Saddled, high and steady. You see all seven towers at once, and nobody sees you.|The safe circuit, twice. Brask remembers, as promised, and nods at you all the same.}\n\n@event beast_manticore_rounds\ntitle: Manticore Rounds\ntrack: beastcraft\nlevel: 3\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: paw\ntags: class danger\nscene: A manticore lying in straw with its eyes half-closed, one student gently holding its scorpion tail while another hums nearby.\ntext: {Advanced students check the manticore once a month. Someone holds the tail; someone hums the lullaby. Brask lets you choose.|Manticore rounds. It knows the lullaby better than you do.|The manticore yawns at you, which is either a welcome or a threat.} {~Its tail twitches in its sleep.|It has been off its food, Brask says, and is in a mood.|{final_year: You wonder who'll hold the tail after you.|Brask has brought a bucket of meat and a smaller one of patience.}}\n> left: Hold the tail\n  challenge exam using vigor hard:\n    win:\n      vigor +small\n      renown +tiny\n      say: {~You hold the tail still while Brask checks the sting. It's warm and heavy and does not, at any point, stab you.|The manticore lets you hold its tail the way a cat lets you hold a paw. Under protest, briefly, as a favour.}\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      say: {~The tail flicks. You let go just in time, mostly. Sister Amaranth asks no questions; she's seen this course before.|It rolls over, tail and all, onto you. Nothing breaks, but you're bruised in the exact shape of a manticore.}\n> right: Hum the lullaby\n  study beastcraft +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~You hum the whole lullaby twice, off-key on the high bit. The manticore sleeps through its own check-up.|You hum. A classmate holds the tail. Brask says the humming is the harder job, and nobody believes her.}\n\n# ---------------- Umbral Theory (track: umbral) ----------------\n\n@event umbral_dim_lamps\ntitle: Reading by Shadow\ntrack: umbral\nlevel: 1\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: lantern\ntags: class mystery\nscene: A dim lecture room at the top of a tower, objects hidden under cloths on a table, their shadows thrown long on the wall behind.\ntext: {Umbral Theory I, top of the tower, lamps low on purpose. Name what's under each cloth by its shadow alone.|The cloths are back. The lamps are as low as ever.|Shadow reading. The dark no longer seems strange, which the professor says is the first lesson.} {~One shadow is slightly larger than its object.|The professor moves the lamp an inch, and every answer changes.|{season == \"summer\": It's warm outside, never up here.|Rain on the windows makes the shadows shiver.}}\n> left: Read them patiently\n  study umbral +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~A teapot, a skull, a folded letter addressed to you. You open it later. It's blank.|You name every shadow but one. Under that cloth there is nothing at all. The professor doesn't explain.|Slowly, you get them all. The shadows told you, the professor says; you only had to stop interrupting.}\n> right: Slip in a false shadow\n  challenge deceive easy:\n    win:\n      guile +small\n      say: {~You add the shadow of a cat to the wall. Three classmates write \"cat\". The professor writes your name, and underlines it.|Your false shadow passes all morning. The professor only catches it when it doesn't move with the lamp.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      say: {~Your false shadow points the wrong way. The professor turns it round gently, like straightening a picture.|Your shadow-cat sits up and leaves. It wasn't meant to do that. The professor doesn't seem surprised.}\n\n@event umbral_chalk_ward\ntitle: Pale Shells\ntrack: umbral\nlevel: 1\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: shield\ntags: class magic\nscene: Students behind faint, pale bubbles of light as a professor flicks pieces of chalk at them from the front of a dim room.\ntext: {Ward drill. Each student raises a pale shell, and the professor walks the room flicking chalk at them. Her aim is excellent.|The professor has a new box of chalk.|The ward drill. Most days your shell is up before the chalk leaves her hand.} {~The front row has chalk in their hair.|Someone's ward is shaped like an apology.|{gpa >= 3: She aims at you first, out of respect.|She aims at you first, out of curiosity.}}\n> left: Hold the ward up\n  study umbral +small\n  mana -small\n  say: {~Eleven pieces of chalk, eleven small pings. Your shell holds and you feel each one in your teeth.|The chalk turns aside and lands neatly in a row. The professor nods at the row more than at you.|Your ward is pale, thin and still there at the bell. She says that's the whole of it, really.}\n> right: Duck instead\n  guile +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~You duck the first nine. The tenth bounces off your forehead. \"Avoidance is a ward,\" she says, \"of a sort.\"|You let the shell drop and simply aren't where the chalk goes. She writes something down. It might be a mark, or a warning.}\n\n@event umbral_quiet_minutes\ntitle: The Quiet Minutes\ntrack: umbral\nlevel: 1\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: candle\ntags: class mystery\nscene: A circle of students sitting perfectly still in near darkness, a single candle in the middle, everyone listening very hard.\ntext: {Each lesson opens with ten minutes of silence. \"Listen to what's hidden,\" the professor says, and puts out every lamp but one.|The quiet minutes. You can hear the tower settle, stone by stone.|Ten minutes of silence. You look forward to it, and also not.} {~Someone's stomach rumbles at the worst moment.|The candle leans toward the door, though nothing comes in.|{season == \"summer\": A bee is lost in the room somewhere.|The wind hums in the stairwell.}}\n> left: Listen properly\n  study umbral +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {~You hear the tower breathing, which it shouldn't, and a sigh on the stair that nobody makes.|You hear the lake, which is impossible from up here. The professor says that's normal for this room. It is not reassuring.|In the silence you hear someone you miss, very faintly. When the lamps come up, you don't mention it.}\n> right: Quietly finish other homework\n  challenge deceive easy:\n    win:\n      guile +small\n      say: {~You finish a whole essay by feel in the dark. The professor never notices, or never says.|Not a scratch of the quill. When the lamps come up, your essay is done and your face is innocent.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      say: {~When the lamps come up the professor is behind you, reading your essay. \"Your third paragraph is weak,\" she says.|Your quill squeaks. In that silence it sounds like a scream. Everyone turns.}\n\n@event umbral_short_distances\ntitle: Short Distances\ntrack: umbral\nlevel: 2\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: ghost\ntags: class magic danger\nscene: A student stepping into the shadow under one desk and halfway out of the shadow under another, the room's lamps burning low.\ntext: {Umbral Theory II brings the shadowstep, desk to desk. \"The shadows in between are not friendly,\" the professor says, \"so don't linger.\"|Shadowstep drill. Three desks this time, then four.|The desks. You know the way between them, and the cold that lives there.} {~Someone comes out under the wrong desk.|The lamps are lower than usual.|{gpa >= 3: The professor sets you the long way round.|The professor sets you the short way, and watches.}}\n> left: Step across\n  challenge deceive medium:\n    win:\n      guile +small\n      say: {~In, through, out. It takes no time and lasts forever. You come out under the far desk with frost on your sleeves.|Four desks, clean. Something in between brushes your ankle, curious, and lets you pass.}\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      mood -tiny\n      say: {~You come out one desk short and somewhat upside down. The cold stays in your fingers until lunch.|You linger, just for a moment. It's enough. You come back pale, and the professor sits you by the one warm lamp.}\n> right: Map the route on paper first\n  study umbral +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~You draw every shadow in the room, and where it goes at each hour. The professor keeps your map.|You work out the route in ink and walk it once, slowly, in the light. It's dull, and correct.}\n\n@event umbral_hidden_object\ntitle: Present but Unseen\ntrack: umbral\nlevel: 2\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: key\ntags: class mystery\nscene: A dim classroom being searched by several students at once while one of them sits calmly at a desk with a small brass key in plain sight.\ntext: {Hide a small key in this room, present but unseen. \"Hiding is not removal,\" the professor says. \"Removal is for amateurs.\"|The key, and somewhere to hide it. Your classmates are better at finding things.|The key exercise. Half the class pats their pockets out of habit.} {~The key is brass today.|The key is silver and very cold.|Last time a key hid in a shadow for two days.}\n> left: Hide it, and stay calm\n  challenge deceive medium:\n    win:\n      guile +small\n      mood +tiny\n      say: {~You leave the key on the desk in plain sight, in your own shadow. Nobody sees it. The professor smiles, which is rarer than a key.|You hide it in the dark behind your own ear. They search the room twice and never once look at you.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      say: {~A first-rate hiding place, found in under a minute by someone who simply asked the shadow.|You hide it so well you can't find it yourself. The professor retrieves it from the wall with a sigh.}\n> right: Search for everyone else's\n  study umbral +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~You find four keys, one in a hat, one in a hinge, one in a shadow that didn't want to let go.|You find one key and a pencil nobody has missed for years. The professor counts both.}\n\n@event umbral_stair_wards\ntitle: The Old Stair Wards\ntrack: umbral\nlevel: 3\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: shield\ntags: class mystery\nscene: A student kneeling on an ancient spiral stair, hands pressed to a faint ring of light set into the stone.\ntext: {Advanced students renew the small wards on the tower's old stair. Some are newer than the stone. Some, the professor says, are much older.|The stair wards. You know which step hums.|The stair. {final_year: Someone else will kneel here next year.|Your knees know every step.}} {~One ward is warm, as if someone's just left.|Something beyond a ward leans back when you touch it.|The stair lamps are mostly a suggestion.}\n> left: Renew them carefully\n  study umbral +small\n  mana -small\n  say: {~Step by step, ring by ring. Each ward takes a little of you and hums.|You renew eleven and pause at the twelfth. Leave that one, the professor says, and doesn't say whose it is.|At the top you're light-headed and the wards are bright. Something on the other side has gone quiet.}\n> right: Study the oldest ward instead\n  guile +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {~You trace the oldest ring with one finger. The pattern folds in on itself like a knot nobody was meant to untie, and you feel watched for noticing even that.|The old ward is cold, careful work, made by someone who was afraid. You can feel the fear still in the stone.}\n\n@event umbral_errand\ntitle: The Shadow's Errand\ntrack: umbral\nlevel: 3\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: moon\ntags: class magic mystery\nscene: A student standing in lamplight with no shadow at all, while across the room their shadow lifts a book from a shelf.\ntext: {Original work. Send your own shadow on an errand across the room, the professor says, and have it come back. \"It will want to wander. They all do.\"|Shadow errands. Yours picks its own route now.|Errand day. {final_year: Does your shadow know you're leaving soon?|Your shadow seems eager, which worries you.}} {~Someone's shadow is still under a desk from last week.|The professor's own shadow is missing today.|The lamps are low and the shadows long.}\n> left: Send it, and trust it\n  challenge deceive hard:\n    win:\n      guile +small\n      mood +tiny\n      say: {~Your shadow crosses the room, lifts a book, and brings it back open at a page you needed. You didn't ask it to.|It comes back with a feather from the rafters, which wasn't the errand. The professor counts it as initiative.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      health -tiny\n      say: {~It comes back an hour late and a little the wrong shape. For a day it lags behind you on stairs, sulking.|It doesn't come back until dusk. When it does, it smells of the lake. The professor makes you sit with the lamp.}\n> right: Keep it on a short leash\n  study umbral +small\n  mana -small\n  say: {~Your shadow goes as far as the window and no further. It looks out a long time before it comes back.|Two yards and home. Dull, safe and precise. The professor says precision is how shadow-workers get old.}\n"},{"path":"content/events/pools/courses_hidden.grim","text":"# Baseline course-morning pools, group courses_hidden (docs/authoring/pools.md):\n# the three hidden courses, Artifice, Starcalling and Hollow Studies. Repeatable routine events, two each at level I.\n\n# ---------------- Artifice (track: artifice, skills alchemy arcana; Mira's Workshop) ----------------\n\n@event art_escapement_drill\ntitle: The Ticking Hour\ntrack: artifice\nlevel: 1\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: gear\ntags: class study\nscene: A cluttered workshop bench covered in tiny brass gears, files and loupes, a row of half-built clockwork hearts ticking out of time with one another.\ntext: {Artifice begins with a heart, a fist of clockwork that must tick evenly before it is allowed to think.|Your clockwork heart is waiting where you left it, sulking at the wrong speed.|You can hear your own heart on the bench from the doorway now. Its tick is almost yours.} {~The bench beside you ticks like hail.|One heart near the door is running backwards, proudly.|The lecturer listens to each bench with eyes closed.} {season == \"winter\": The brass is cold enough to bite.|The workshop smells of solder.}\n> left: File every tooth by hand\n  alchemy +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {~Tooth by tooth, under a loupe, until your eyes water. The tick evens out by the bell.|You file all morning and burn one thumb on the solder. The heart ticks steady and slightly smug.|It's slow, fiddly work, and your hands learn it faster than your head does.}\n> right: Charm the spring to keep time\n  challenge enchant using arcana medium:\n    win:\n      arcana +small\n      study artifice +tiny\n      say: {~The charm settles into the spring like a cat into a basket. Your heart ticks like a metronome.|You whisper the rhythm into the coil and it keeps it. The lecturer opens one eye, then closes it again.}\n    lose:\n      health -tiny\n      mood -tiny\n      say: {~The spring takes the charm too literally and unwinds all at once, across the room.|Your heart ticks perfectly for a minute, then very fast, then not at all. Someone hands you a fire bucket just in case.}\n\n@event art_stirring_cauldron\ntitle: The Cauldron That Stirs\ntrack: artifice\nlevel: 1\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: cauldron\ntags: class magic\nscene: A copper cauldron on iron legs stirring itself with a long brass arm while students crowd round with notebooks and a wary distance.\ntext: {The class project is a cauldron that stirs itself. It does, mostly, in whatever direction it pleases.|The cauldron has been rebuilt again. It stirs with a new brass arm and the same old temper.|The cauldron recognises you, or behaves as if it does.} {~Today it stirs clockwise, then argues.|Today it stirs so fast the porridge in it is climbing out.|Today the arm keeps tapping the rim like an impatient guest.} {final_year: You'll miss its moods, a little.|The fire buckets by the door are full, for once.}\n> left: Recut the runes on the arm\n  study artifice +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {~You spend the morning with a graver and a magnifier, recutting the stirring runes one stroke at a time. Your fingers are nicked; the arm moves sweetly.|Fine work in brass, the whole lesson long. The arm stirs evenly, and you have solder burns in a neat little row.}\n> right: Test it on a live brew\n  challenge brew medium:\n    win:\n      alchemy +small\n      study artifice +tiny\n      say: {~The cauldron stirs your brew exactly as the recipe asks, and you take the credit for both.|The brew turns the right colour at the right minute. The cauldron seems to know it did well.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      say: {~The arm speeds up, the brew climbs the walls, and the whole class steps back as one.|The cauldron stirs your brew into something with the texture of an argument. You scrub it out until the bell.}\n\n# ---------------- Starcalling (track: starcalling, skills lore arcana; the Observatory, at night) ----------------\n\n@event star_names_in_the_dark\ntitle: Names in the Dark\ntrack: starcalling\nlevel: 1\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: star\ntags: class study\nscene: A dark brass dome open to a sky full of stars, a student at the eyepiece of a great telescope while another recites from memory, breath steaming.\ntext: {Starcalling is taught by appointment, at night, and it begins with names. \"You cannot call what you cannot name.\"|Another night appointment under the dome. The list of names is longer than it was.|The dome at night feels like yours now. You know where the draught comes in.} {~The great telescope has swung round on its own again.|A meteor scratches the sky and is gone.|An owl is sitting on the open shutter, listening.} {season == \"winter\": Your breath freezes on the eyepiece.|The night air smells of cut grass from the lawns below.}\n> left: Recite the names until perfect\n  lore +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {~You say them over and over into the cold until they stop being words and start being places.|Name after name, in order and then backwards. By the end you could find them blindfold, and nearly have to.|You stumble on the same name four times. The fifth time it comes out right, and you feel it land somewhere.}\n> right: Let the telescope choose a star\n  challenge research easy:\n    win:\n      study starcalling +small\n      say: {~The telescope settles on a star you've never heard of. You find its name in the old charts before the lecturer can tell you.|Wherever it points, you follow, and the charts agree with it. That's a whole lesson learned the other way round.}\n    lose:\n      health -tiny\n      mood -tiny\n      say: {~The telescope picks a star and then changes its mind every ten minutes. You chase it round the dome until midnight.|You spend the night at the eyepiece and come down with a stiff neck and one name you aren't sure of.}\n\n@event star_thread_of_light\ntitle: A Thread of Light\ntrack: starcalling\nlevel: 1\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: sparkle\ntags: class magic\nscene: A student in a dark observatory with hands cupped upward, a single silver thread of starlight running down through the open dome into their palms.\ntext: {Tonight you try to call down a single thread of a named star's light, no thicker than a hair.|The lesson is the same as ever: one thread, one star, held as long as you can bear it.|You've called down threads before. Tonight the lecturer wants it held longer.} {~The sky is clear and very far away.|Thin cloud keeps crossing the star you've chosen.|The other students are already standing with their hands raised.}\n> left: Call the thread down yourself\n  study starcalling +small\n  mana -small\n  say: {~The thread comes down cold and bright and pours into your palms. You hold it until your hands shake.|It takes three tries. On the third, the light drops into your hands like water from a great height.|A hair of light, the length of the whole tower. When it fades, your fingers glow for an hour.}\n> right: Keep the night log instead\n  arcana +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~You write down every thread the others call: the star, the minute, how long it held. It's dull, cold work, and you start to see the pattern.|You hold the lamp and the log while the others call. Watching from outside, you see exactly where their hands go wrong.} {season == \"winter\": Your ink keeps freezing.|The lecturer reads your log twice.}\n> up[starlight]: Hold your own starlight up to it\n  study starcalling +small\n  say: {~You light your own starlight and the thread finds it, like calling to like. It holds twice as long.|Your starlight steadies the thread, and the lecturer has you show the others how.|The thread drops straight into the light you're already holding. For a moment the dome is bright as noon.}\n\n# ---------------- Hollow Studies (track: hollow_studies, skills guile lore; the headmistress, a room without windows) ----------------\n\n@event hollow_sealed_boxes\ntitle: How It Was Sealed\ntrack: hollow_studies\nlevel: 1\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: lock\ntags: class mystery\nscene: A windowless room lined with black-bound books, a single lamp on a table, a tall calm woman setting a small locked box in front of a student.\ntext: {Each lesson, the headmistress sets a sealed thing in front of you. \"Tell me how it was closed,\" she says. \"Not what is inside. How.\"|Another sealed thing on the table. Tonight it is {~a letter with a wax seal|a small lacquered box|a jar with its lid charmed shut}.|The headmistress places tonight's sealed thing with a little smile. She has started choosing harder ones.} {~The lamp hisses.|The books on the shelves breathe slowly in the dark.|She sits with her hands folded, unhurried.}\n> left: Study the seal for the hour\n  lore +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {~You trace every line of it without touching. At the end you can name each step of the sealing, in order. She nods once.|It takes the whole hour and a headache, but you work it out backwards, layer by layer.|You get two layers right and one wrong. She tells you which, kindly, and you'll remember.}\n> right: Open it without leaving a trace\n  challenge deceive medium:\n    win:\n      guile +small\n      study hollow_studies +tiny\n      say: {~You open it and close it again, and nothing shows. She looks at it, then at you, and laughs, short and delighted.|Open, closed, not a mark. \"Good,\" she says. \"Now never do that to a door.\"}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      say: {~The seal cracks with a tiny, final sound. The headmistress writes something in her book. She remembers every mistake, and now there is one more.|It opens, and it shows. \"Everyone breaks one,\" she says pleasantly. \"Rarely so loudly.\"}\n\n@event hollow_reading_the_blank\ntitle: Reading the Blank Page\ntrack: hollow_studies\nlevel: 1\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: book\ntags: class mystery\nscene: A student bent over an apparently blank page by lamplight, faint lines of ink surfacing under their fingertips, black-bound books leaning in from the shelves.\ntext: {\"Most things that are hidden are not gone,\" the headmistress says, and gives you a blank page. \"Find what was written. Gently.\"|A new blank page tonight. It feels warmer than paper should.|You know the weight of hidden writing in a page by now, like a pocket with something in it.} {~The books on the nearest shelf seem to lean closer.|The lamp burns low and blue.|Somewhere in the stacks, a book turns its own page.} {final_year: She asks where you'll go after graduation, and listens.|She is reading while you work, and doesn't look up.}\n> left: Coax the ink up slowly\n  study hollow_studies +small\n  mana -small\n  say: {~You warm the page with a thread of power, a breath at a time. The writing surfaces slowly, safe and dull: someone's laundry list, very carefully hidden.|Line by line, the ink comes up. It's an old timetable. The lesson was in the finding.|It takes most of your strength. The page gives up a single sentence about the weather, decades ago.}\n> right: Read it bare-handed\n  guile +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {~The ink comes up fast under your fingers, and so does a chill that stays in your hand until morning.|You read it quickly and well. Your fingertips ache for an hour, and the headmistress hands you a cup of tea without comment.}\n> up[ward]: Let your ward do the reading\n  lore +small\n  say: {~Your ward settles over the page like a held breath, and the writing rises into it. The headmistress raises an eyebrow, pleased.|You read through the ward as if through glass. It costs you, but not your hands.|The hidden lines surface inside your shell, one by one, in no hurry.}\n"},{"path":"content/events/pools/courses_upper.grim","text":"# Course-morning pools, group courses_upper (docs/authoring/pools.md):\n# two more events each at levels II and III for the six core courses. Repeatable routine events.\n\n# ---------------- Evocation (track: evocation, skill arcana) ----------------\n\n@event evo_rain_barrel\ntitle: Rain on Demand\ntrack: evocation\nlevel: 2\nlesson: mirror_lake\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: rain\ntags: class magic\nscene: A row of students on a lakeside terrace, each with a small grey cloud above a wooden barrel, one cloud raining sideways onto a neighbour.\ntext: {Tidecalling at Evocation II. Pull a cloud off the lake, park it over a barrel, and fill the barrel to the chalk line. Not over, not under.|The barrels are out on the terrace again, each with a chalk line.|You know your barrel now, the one with the iron band that's slipped.} {~Professor Marisol is reading on the steps, under a perfectly dry patch of air.|The wind keeps trying to borrow the clouds.|{season == \"winter\": The clouds come off the lake as sleet.|A heron watches the barrels with professional interest.}}\n> left: Wring it out to the line\n  challenge spell medium:\n    win:\n      arcana +small\n      study evocation +tiny\n      say: {~The last drop lands on the chalk. Marisol looks up from her book and back down, which counts.|You stop the rain a breath early and let the drip finish the job. Exactly to the line.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      health -tiny\n      say: {~Your cloud overshoots, fills the barrel, and keeps going down your collar.|The cloud drifts left and rains on the next barrel, which is not yours, and on its owner, who is very not yours.}\n> right: Drizzle it in, slowly\n  arcana +small\n  mana -small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~It takes the whole lesson. Your cloud is the size of a pillow by the end, and very tired.|You fill it a thimble at a time. The others have gone in for lunch. The line is met.}\n\n@event evo_ember_stair\ntitle: Carrying the Ember\ntrack: evocation\nlevel: 2\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: flame\ntags: class magic danger\nscene: A student climbing a dim spiral stair, a single glowing ember resting in their bare open palm.\ntext: {Pyromancy at Evocation II. Carry a live ember in your bare palm from the ground floor to the top of the Lecture Tower. Keep it alight. Keep your hand.|The ember drill again. The stair hasn't got any shorter.|Ember day. You've counted the stair by now, and you know where the draught is.} {~A door on the third floor opens just as you pass.|Someone ahead of you is climbing very fast and swearing quietly.|The lamps on the stair are out, and your ember is the only light.}\n> left: Feed it, and climb fast\n  challenge feat using arcana medium:\n    win:\n      arcana +small\n      say: {~You keep the ember bright with small breaths of heat and take the stairs two at a time. It arrives fat and orange.|You reach the top with the ember glowing and your palm only warm. The lecturer tips it into a brazier without a word.}\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      say: {~The ember flares on the landing and you learn exactly how hot a palm can get. You arrive with it alight, and with a blister.|You feed it too much at the draughty window. It bites. You carry it up the last flight in your other hand.}\n> right: Shield it, and climb slow\n  study evocation +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {~You climb at a crawl with the ember cupped against the draughts. It is alight at the top, just. So are your nerves.|Step by step, a held breath at each window. Last up, but the ember's still glowing.}\n\n@event evo_firebreak\ntitle: The Reed Burn\ntrack: evocation\nlevel: 3\nlesson: mirror_lake\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: flame\ntags: class magic danger\nscene: Advanced students along a lake shore in a line, half of them calling a low wall of fire through dry reeds, half of them holding back a curtain of lake water behind it.\ntext: {Advanced Evocation does real work, the controlled burn of the dead reeds along the lake shore. Pyromancers lead the fire, tidecallers hold the line behind it.|Burn day. The shore smells of smoke for a week afterwards.|The reed burn. You can tell a fire's mood from its smoke now.} {~The wind is steady from the lake.|The wind can't decide.|The reeds are drier than anyone would like.} {season == \"summer\": The air is hot already.|Your breath steams.}\n> left: Walk at the fire's head\n  challenge spell hard:\n    win:\n      arcana +medium\n      say: {~The fire follows your hands down the shore like a dog on a lead. The lecturer lets you take it all the way to the boathouse.|You lead the fire low and even, and it goes out where you tell it. Behind you there's nothing but clean black ground.}\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      mood -small\n      say: {~A gust lifts the fire over your head. The tidecallers drop it in a wall of lake water, and you with it.|The fire jumps the line and runs. It's caught in a minute, but your eyebrows weren't.}\n> right: Hold the water line\n  study evocation +small\n  mana -small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~You stand in the shallows holding a curtain of lake water for an hour. Nothing gets past you. Nobody notices, which is the point.|Your arms ache from keeping the water up. It's the least glamorous job on the shore and the one the lecturer checks first.}\n\n@event evo_drowned_flame\ntitle: Fire Under Water\ntrack: evocation\nlevel: 3\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: candle\ntags: class magic\nscene: A glass tank of water on a scorched bench, a small blue flame burning steadily at the bottom of it.\ntext: {Original work in Advanced Evocation, a fire that burns underwater. Every student gets a tank. Most tanks get a new pane every week.|Your tank is waiting, with a fresh crack along one pane.|The tank again. You talk to the water now, and to the fire, separately.} {~Someone's tank has boiled dry and they're staring at it.|A flame at the next bench burns green beneath the water, quite calm.|The lecturer is standing well back today.}\n> left: Light it at the bottom\n  challenge spell hard:\n    win:\n      arcana +small\n      mood +tiny\n      say: {~A small blue flame takes hold under the water and stays. The bubbles go up in a thin silver line.|It catches, gutters, and settles. The water around it doesn't even warm. You don't know how you did it.}\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      say: {~The water boils in a heartbeat and the pane splits. You wear the steam on your face for the rest of the day.|The flame goes up instead of in. The tank cracks, and your sleeve is wet and scorched at once.}\n> right: Wrap it in a bubble first\n  arcana +small\n  mana -small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~You hold a skin of air around the flame and sink them both. It's half a cheat, and the lecturer says so. It burns all hour.|Your flame sits in a bubble at the bottom like a moth in a jar. Not the assignment, but not nothing.}\n\n# ---------------- Astromancy & Divination (track: astromancy, skill lore) ----------------\n\n@event astro_sealed_forecast\ntitle: Tomorrow's Weather\ntrack: astromancy\nlevel: 2\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: rain\ntags: class study\nscene: A row of sealed envelopes pinned to a corkboard in the observatory, each labelled with a student's name and tomorrow's date.\ntext: {Foresight at Astromancy II. Write down tomorrow's weather to the hour, seal it, pin it to the board. The lecturer opens the envelopes a day later, in public.|The envelopes come out again.|You've written so many forecasts you dream in drizzle.} {~Outside the dome the sky is giving nothing away.|Last week's best forecast just said \"Wet\", and was right.|{season == \"winter\": Frost on the dome, and snow somewhere in the future.|Clouds over the lake, moving with intent.}}\n> left: Commit to the hour\n  challenge research medium:\n    win:\n      lore +small\n      study astromancy +tiny\n      say: {~\"Rain at the third bell, stopping at the fourth.\" It does. The lecturer reads it out twice.|You forecast a fog to the hour. When the envelope is opened, the fog is pressing on the dome.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      say: {~\"Clear by noon.\" It rains till evening. Your envelope is read out to great enjoyment.|You predicted wind. Tomorrow turns out so still that a feather on the sill doesn't move.}\n> right: Hedge, and learn from it\n  study astromancy +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~\"Some weather, at some point.\" The lecturer reads it out flatly and moves on. But you spent the hour watching how the good forecasters work.|You write a careful forecast with three possibilities. It's right, technically. The lecturer calls it \"an umbrella of a prophecy\".}\n\n@event astro_stubborn_telescope\ntitle: The Stubborn Telescope\ntrack: astromancy\nlevel: 2\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: planet\ntags: class study\nscene: A student tugging at a huge brass telescope in a dome, while it points stubbornly at a different patch of sky.\ntext: {Astromancy II needs exact positions, and that means the great telescope. The great telescope points wherever it likes.|Your night on the great telescope. It is already pointing at something else.|The telescope swings toward you as you come in, like an old dog.} {~Tonight it wants the moon.|Tonight it's fixed on the lake, which is not in the sky.|Tonight it keeps drifting east, slowly, like a sigh.}\n> left: Argue it round\n  challenge research medium:\n    win:\n      lore +small\n      say: {~You coax it, inch by inch, and it settles on your star with a creak like a shrug.|You find the right thing to say to an old brass instrument. It points where you ask. You write the position fast.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      health -tiny\n      say: {~It spends the night pointing where it likes. So do you, in the end, cold and annoyed.|You lean on it. It leans back. You go to bed at the fourth bell with nothing written down.}\n> right: Work it out from tables\n  lore +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {~You leave the telescope to its moods and work the positions out by candlelight from the old tables. It takes until dawn.|Pages of sums by candle. The telescope watches over your shoulder, pointedly.}\n\n@event astro_long_watch\ntitle: Keeping the Light\ntrack: astromancy\nlevel: 3\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: lantern\ntags: class magic\nscene: A student on the observatory roof before dawn, a glass lantern in their lap holding a single cold point of starlight.\ntext: {Advanced Starlight. Hold one star's light in a lantern through a whole night on the roof. It wants to go home before dawn. Your job is to keep it.|The night watch on the roof again, a lantern and a blanket.|Your lantern, your star, your corner of the roof.} {~The star you've drawn is pale and blue.|The star you've drawn is small and stubborn.|The star you've drawn is very old and very faint.} {season == \"winter\": Frost on the slates.|The night is warm and full of moths.}\n> left: Keep it all night\n  lore +small\n  study astromancy +small\n  health -small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~At dawn your star is still in the lantern, blinking at the sun it was never meant to meet. You are blinking too.|You keep it till the lecturer comes up at sunrise. Your eyes are full of sand and your lantern is full of light.}\n> right: Let it go at midnight\n  lore +tiny\n  say: {~You open the lantern and the light goes up like a kite let loose. You watch it all the way home and then go to bed.|You let it go early. The lecturer's note just says \"Released at midnight.\" You suspect it's not a compliment.}\n> up[starlight]: Feed it your own starlight\n  lore +small\n  say: {~You pour a little of your own starlight into the lantern. The star brightens as if it had been lonely.|Your light and the star's together are enough to read by. You read until dawn.}\n\n@event astro_two_futures\ntitle: Both Futures\ntrack: astromancy\nlevel: 3\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: dice\ntags: class magic\nscene: A coin balanced on edge on a dark table in the observatory, with two faint ghostly outcomes hovering above it.\ntext: {Advanced Foresight. The lecturer balances a coin on its edge. Read both futures at once, the one where it falls heads and the one where it falls tails, and hold them both.|The coin on its edge again. You know how it feels to be two places at once now.|The coin. People come out of this lesson pressing their temples.} {~Someone in the front row says \"heads\" in two voices.|The lecturer is drinking tea and looking at nobody.|The candles burn straight up, very still.}\n> left: Hold both to the end\n  challenge exam hard:\n    win:\n      lore +medium\n      say: {~For a long moment you live both futures, side by side, and neither is frightening. Then the coin falls, and one of them goes out.|You hold both until the lecturer flicks the coin. One future folds away, neat as a letter.}\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      mood -small\n      say: {~The futures tangle and you get a headache that lasts till supper. For an hour you keep reaching for the wrong door handle.|You lose track of which future is which, and of which way is down. The lecturer sits you by an open window.}\n> right: Read one, then the other\n  study astromancy +small\n  mana -small\n  say: {~You read heads, then tails, carefully, one after the other. It's not the exercise, but you get both right.|One future at a time. Slower, safer, and the lecturer says you'll get there.}\n\n# ---------------- Alchemy & Healing (track: alchemy, skill alchemy) ----------------\n\n@event alch_unlabelled_vial\ntitle: The Unlabelled Vial\ntrack: alchemy\nlevel: 2\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: bottle\ntags: class study\nscene: A vial of clear liquid on a lab bench beside a strip of paper turning slowly purple, a student squinting at both.\ntext: {Alchemy II hands out an unlabelled vial each, and an hour to say what's in it. Paper strips, a candle, a nose and a notebook are allowed.|Another unlabelled vial. The lecturer enjoys these.|The vial is waiting on your bench. You've started recognising the corks.} {~Yours is perfectly clear and smells faintly of pears.|Yours is cloudy and keeps settling into layers.|Yours is warm to the touch.} {~The ventilation charm is wheezing again.|The lab is quiet except for scratching quills.}\n> left: Test it the long way\n  alchemy +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {~Strip, flame, cold, filter. By the end of the hour you've a name and three reasons for it.|Every test, in order, written up neatly. Your notes are dull and your answer is right.}\n> right: Trust your nose\n  challenge brew medium:\n    win:\n      alchemy +small\n      study alchemy +tiny\n      say: {~One sniff and you know it. You write the name and use the spare hour to check yourself.|You tip the vial under your nose and the answer arrives before the smell does. The lecturer raises an eyebrow and a tick.}\n    lose:\n      health -tiny\n      mood -small\n      say: {~One sniff and your eyes stream for the rest of the morning. You guessed wrong, too.|It's not what you thought, and it is very much up your nose. You spend the hour at the window.}\n\n@event alch_cracked_tray\ntitle: The Cracked Tray\ntrack: alchemy\nlevel: 2\nlesson: infirmary\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: heart\ntags: class magic\nscene: A tray in the infirmary laid with a cracked teacup, a cracked hand mirror and a cracked bone from a teaching skeleton, a student's hands hovering over them.\ntext: {Mend at Alchemy II. Sister Amaranth sets out a tray of cracked things, cup, mirror and bone. \"Mend those,\" she says, \"and then we'll see about people.\"|The tray again. Sister Amaranth has found new things to crack.|Sister Amaranth puts the tray down and nods at it. You know the drill.} {~A second-year is waiting on a cot with a sprained wrist from broom practice.|A third-year with a scalded hand from Evocation is waiting.|A first-year is in with a split lip and a long story about a door.}\n> left: Mend the tray, perfectly\n  challenge enchant medium:\n    win:\n      alchemy +small\n      say: {~The cracks close like seams being sewn shut. Sister Amaranth runs a thumbnail along the cup and finds nothing.|Cup, mirror, bone, whole. Sister Amaranth says \"Fine,\" which is her highest word.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      say: {~The cup mends with a ridge in it, the mirror with a ripple. Sister Amaranth sets them on the shelf of shame.|You mend the bone a little crooked. Sister Amaranth says that's how you learn and puts it back in the skeleton anyway.}\n> right: Mend the patient instead\n  study alchemy +small\n  mana -small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~You skip the tray and take the patient, with Sister Amaranth's hand on your shoulder. It goes slowly. It works.|Sister Amaranth lets you try on the patient while she watches. You're shaky and slow, and you'll never mend a cup the same way again.}\n\n@event alch_lake_fever\ntitle: Lake Fever Season\ntrack: alchemy\nlevel: 3\nlesson: infirmary\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: potion\ntags: class magic\nscene: A row of infirmary cots, students under blankets looking flushed and sorry for themselves, an advanced student drawing a faint grey haze out of one patient's brow.\ntext: {Advanced Alchemy treats real patients. The infirmary has Lake Fever cases, from swimming where they shouldn't. Sister Amaranth wants the fever drawn out, not just cooled.|Lake Fever again. Students never learn, Sister Amaranth says, and neither do lakes.|Fever duty. Sister Amaranth hands you a list without looking up.} {~Your patient says they only went in up to the knees.|Your patient is a prefect, very embarrassed.|Your patient insists they were pushed.}\n> left: Draw the fever out by hand\n  challenge enchant hard:\n    win:\n      alchemy +small\n      study alchemy +small\n      say: {~The fever comes out like a grey thread pulled from wool. Your patient sleeps. You'd like to.|You draw it out slowly and the flush leaves their face. Sister Amaranth checks your work and doesn't check twice.}\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      mood -tiny\n      say: {~You draw out half of it and the half you drew comes with you. You spend the evening shivering in the next cot.|The cure has to come from somewhere. You wake up two hours later on the floor, with a blanket on you.}\n> right: Brew the slow tonic\n  alchemy +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {~Three hours of stirring willow bark and lake moss. The tonic works by morning, which is slower and safer.|You brew the old remedy by the book. Sister Amaranth tastes it, grimaces, and approves.}\n> up[purify]: Purify it in one pass\n  study alchemy +tiny\n  health -tiny\n  say: {~Your spell pulls the fever clean in a single pass. It leaves you yawning.|One pass of Purify and the patient sits up asking for soup. The tiredness lands on you an hour later.}\n\n@event alch_third_floor_still\ntitle: The Still Cracks\ntrack: alchemy\nlevel: 3\nclass: incident\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: cauldron\ntags: class danger\nscene: A copper still on a long bench, hissing from a fresh crack, a thin green vapour creeping across the laboratory floor.\ntext: {Advanced Alchemy distils essences that would rather be gas. The ventilation charm gave up on the third floor years ago. Today your still cracks.|Distilling day, and a hiss from your still that you know.|Your still has cracked again. You're getting fast at noticing.} {~The vapour is green and smells of mint.|The vapour is pale and smells of nothing, which is worse.|The vapour is curling toward the next bench.}\n> left: Mend the crack, still hot\n  challenge enchant hard:\n    win:\n      alchemy +small\n      say: {~You close the copper under your palms while it's still singing. The hiss stops. The class breathes out.|The crack seals with a sound like a sigh, and the batch is saved. The lecturer opens a window, for form's sake.}\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      mood -small\n      say: {~The crack closes, then reopens with a pop. You breathe in a mouthful and spend the afternoon seeing double.|You burn your fingertips on the copper and the vapour gets out anyway. The lab is cleared for an hour.}\n> right: Shout, and clear the bench\n  study alchemy -tiny\n  mood -small\n  say: {~You call the warning and clear the bench. The batch is lost, nobody is hurt, and you start again next week.|Everyone is out in the corridor in a minute flat. The lecturer goes back in, a scarf over the mouth, and comes out carrying your ruined batch.}\n\n# ---------------- Illusion & Glamour (track: illusion, skill charm) ----------------\n\n@event illu_walking_double\ntitle: The Walking Double\ntrack: illusion\nlevel: 2\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: mirror\ntags: class magic\nscene: Two identical students walking side by side across a lecture hall, one of them leaving no footprints in the chalk dust.\ntext: {Mirror at Illusion II. Make a double of yourself and walk it across the room. \"Standing doubles are furniture,\" says Professor Vey. \"Make it walk.\"|Doubles day. Vey watches feet, not faces.|Your double's walk is improving. It has your slouch now.} {~The floor has been dusted with chalk, to show footprints.|Someone's double is walking backwards.|Vey is sitting in the front row, which is unsettling.}\n> left: Walk it right across\n  challenge enchant using charm medium:\n    win:\n      charm +small\n      say: {~Your double crosses the room at your pace and even scuffs the chalk. Vey watches its feet the whole way and says nothing, which is high praise.|It walks, it turns, it nearly sits down. Vey says, \"Better.\"}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      say: {~Halfway across, your double's legs forget to move and it glides. Vey says it's a ghost, not a person, and moves on.|Your double walks into the wall and keeps walking. The class watches it vanish into the plaster.}\n> right: Keep it still and perfect\n  study illusion +small\n  mana -small\n  say: {~Your double stands at your elbow, perfect to the last button. It doesn't move. Vey calls it a very good wardrobe.|You make it flawless and keep it standing. Vey taps its nose, and frowns when it holds.}\n\n@event illu_good_news\ntitle: Good News, Read Aloud\ntrack: illusion\nlevel: 2\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: mask\ntags: class magic\nscene: A student standing at a lectern reading from a dull notice while the listening class smiles as though hearing wonderful news.\ntext: {Glamour at Illusion II, and today it's the voice. Vey hands you a dull notice and asks you to read it so the class hears good news.|Another notice from Vey's drawer of very dull notices.|Vey has saved the dullest notice for you.} {~Today's is about the drains.|Today's is about a change to the laundry rota.|Today's is a list of overdue library books, in full.}\n> left: Glamour every word\n  challenge deceive using charm medium:\n    win:\n      charm +small\n      say: {~By the second line the room is beaming. Someone claps. Vey waits for the clapping to stop and asks what the notice said. Nobody knows.|You read it warm and low, and the class leans in as if you were reading their letters from home.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      say: {~Your glamour slips on a long word and the room hears exactly what it is: a notice. Someone yawns on purpose.|You overdo it. The class looks alarmed, as if the drains were about to propose.}\n> right: Read it plain, watch others\n  study illusion +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~You read it straight and sit down. Then you watch the others, and notice the best of them barely change their voices at all.|Plain reading, polite applause. You spend the hour writing down where the good ones breathe.}\n\n@event illu_false_room\ntitle: The Room That Isn't\ntrack: illusion\nlevel: 3\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: door\ntags: class magic danger\nscene: A student walking confidently into a room that is partly painted light, a real stone wall standing where the illusion shows an open doorway.\ntext: {Advanced Illusion asks for a double of a whole room. Hang it over the real one and walk a classmate through it. Vey's rule is simple. If anyone walks into a wall, it's your fault.|The room exercise. Somebody always walks into something.|You hang your room. You've learned where the real walls are.} {~Vey's volunteer today is a porter with a tray of cups.|Vey's volunteer today is Vey.|The real room has been rearranged overnight.}\n> left: Hang the whole room\n  challenge enchant using charm hard:\n    win:\n      charm +medium\n      say: {~Your walker crosses the false room and sits down in a false chair, which you hold up for them. Vey writes for a long time.|The room holds. Nobody touches a real wall. Vey walks it twice to be sure.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      health -tiny\n      say: {~Your walker steps through a door you painted onto solid stone. You take them to Sister Amaranth with an apology.|Halfway across the room flickers, and the walker freezes. So do you, and you knock over a real chair.}\n> right: Chalk the real walls first\n  study illusion +small\n  mana -small\n  say: {~You hang half a room, with the real walls marked out in chalk so nobody could miss them. Vey calls it \"a room with a safety rope\".|Safe and visible, chalk lines everywhere. Vey sighs, and then copies down how you did the window.}\n\n@event illu_peeling_layers\ntitle: Seeing Through\ntrack: illusion\nlevel: 3\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: eye\ntags: class magic\nscene: A lecture hall in layers like peeled paper: one layer with velvet curtains, one bare stone, one darker one beneath, and a student standing at its centre.\ntext: {Advanced Illusion. To make illusions you must see through them. Vey layers the hall in false rooms, one on another, and you peel them back one at a time.|Layer day. The hall looks normal. That's never a good sign.|You count the layers when you come in. You're usually wrong.} {~The top layer has velvet curtains.|The top layer is a summer garden.|The top layer is the hall itself, just slightly too tidy.}\n> left: Peel down to the stone\n  challenge exam using charm hard:\n    win:\n      charm +small\n      study illusion +tiny\n      say: {~Seven layers, then bare stone. Vey asks how many. You say seven. She smiles and says eight, and points at herself.|You reach the stone and stay there, sure of it. Vey nods slowly. \"Hold on to that,\" she says.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      health -tiny\n      say: {~You peel one too many and the stone underneath looks false. For the rest of the day you knock on walls.|After the fourth layer you stop being sure which way is up. Vey walks you to a chair, which is real.}\n> right: Stop at the third layer\n  charm +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {~Three layers and you stop, dizzy. Vey says knowing when to stop is the other half of the skill.|You get through three and sit down. The hall still looks too tidy, and you decide not to ask.}\n\n# ---------------- Beastcraft & Flight (track: beastcraft, skill vigor) ----------------\n\n@event beast_hedge_run\ntitle: The Hedge Run\ntrack: beastcraft\nlevel: 2\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: broom\ntags: class magic danger\nscene: Students on brooms skimming low over a line of hedges in a field, one broom bucking as its rider leans forward.\ntext: {Beastcraft II puts haste on a broom, low over the hedges, as fast as you dare. Professor Brask says brooms are harder to reason with than hippogriffs, and harder to apologise to.|The hedge run. Your broom remembers last time.|Hedges. You've learned to read a broom's sulk through the handle.} {~Brask is timing with a crooked hourglass.|The hedges have been trimmed, unevenly.|{season == \"autumn\": Leaves come up behind every broom.|{season == \"spring\": The hedges are full of nesting birds who object.|The ground is hard and unforgiving.}}}\n> left: Haste at the last hedge\n  challenge feat using vigor medium:\n    win:\n      vigor +small\n      study beastcraft +tiny\n      say: {~You borrow a hawk's speed for the last hedge and clear it by a hand. Brask stops the hourglass and nods.|The broom flinches, then commits. You come in fastest of the morning, with hedge in your hair.}\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      say: {~The broom bucks at the haste and you go over the hedge without it.|You're fast. The broom is faster, briefly, and then upside down. You land in a bramble.}\n> right: Fly it clean and steady\n  vigor +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~No haste, just a clean low line over every hedge. Brask says it's the best boring flying she's seen all week.|You fly it slow and tidy. The broom approves. Brask is less impressed.}\n\n@event beast_owl_post\ntitle: Teaching the Owls\ntrack: beastcraft\nlevel: 2\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: owl\ntags: class nature\nscene: A student in a stable loft with a young owl on their arm, pointing out of a high window toward a distant tower.\ntext: {Beast-speech at Beastcraft II. The young owls must learn the post routes. Brask hands you one and says, \"Explain the castle to it.\"|A new young owl, and the same castle to explain.|Another owl. You've got a speech now, and a map scratched on a beam.} {~Your owl wants to know why there are so many towers.|Your owl has strong opinions about the library.|Your owl keeps falling asleep, standing up.}\n> left: Explain it in owl\n  challenge negotiate using vigor medium:\n    win:\n      vigor +small\n      study beastcraft +tiny\n      say: {~You describe the castle in winds, perches and mice. The owl blinks, and goes and comes back with Brask's letter.|It takes a while to get \"left\" across to a bird. When it does, the owl does the route twice to show off.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      say: {~The owl listens closely, then flies to the kitchens. Every time.|The owl makes you explain \"tower\" seven times and then goes to sleep on your head.}\n> right: Walk the route with it\n  vigor +small\n  health -tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~You walk the whole post route with the owl on your arm, up every stair. Your legs ache and the owl has learned it.|Up and down the castle with an owl on your wrist. People stare. The owl stares back.}\n\n@event beast_hooves\ntitle: Hoof Day\ntrack: beastcraft\nlevel: 3\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: paw\ntags: class nature danger\nscene: A student crouched beside a hippogriff in a stall, a hoof trimming knife in hand, the hippogriff looking down with one sceptical eye.\ntext: {Advanced Beastcraft does the work nobody else wants. Today it's hooves. A hippogriff's need trimming each month, and the hippogriff disagrees.|Hoof day. The stall is the same; the hippogriff's opinion is too.|Hooves. You go in with a bucket of apples and a plan.} {~Today's hippogriff is grey and patient.|Today's is young and very sure of itself.|Today's is old and wants to tell you about it.}\n> left: Talk it into lifting a foot\n  challenge negotiate using vigor hard:\n    win:\n      vigor +small\n      study beastcraft +small\n      say: {~It listens to your case and lifts a hoof with enormous dignity. Brask watches from the door and says nothing.|You talk it through every hoof. It sighs at you between each one, like a patient uncle.}\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      mood -tiny\n      say: {~It lets you get all the way to the last hoof before stepping on your foot.|It hears you out, then sits on you. Brask lifts it off with one hand and a word.}\n> right: Rope, and Brask holding\n  vigor +small\n  health -small\n  say: {~The old way: a rope, a strong arm, Brask at its head. It's sweaty work and nobody enjoys it, including the hippogriff.|With Brask holding the head and you holding on, you finish every hoof. Your shoulders will remember it.}\n\n@event beast_night_stable\ntitle: Sitting Up With the Sick\ntrack: beastcraft\nlevel: 3\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: lantern\ntags: class nature\nscene: A student sitting in straw by lantern light, a sick creature curled beside them, the rest of the stables asleep.\ntext: {Advanced students take turns sitting up in the stables when a creature is ill. Brask leaves you a lantern, a blanket and a list.|Your turn on the night watch, and the list is shorter.|The night watch. The owls are used to you now and only mutter.} {~Tonight it's the griffon chick, off its food.|Tonight it's an old owl with a cough.|Tonight it's a hippogriff who ate something it shouldn't.} {season == \"winter\": The stable is cold enough to see your breath.|It's warm in the straw.}\n> left: Sit up all night\n  vigor +small\n  study beastcraft +small\n  health -small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~You don't sleep, and by dawn it's breathing easier. Brask finds you asleep in the straw and leaves you there.|You watch it all night, changing the water and the straw. In the morning it eats, and so do you, enormously.}\n> right: Ask it where it hurts\n  challenge negotiate using vigor hard:\n    win:\n      vigor +small\n      say: {~It tells you, in its own way, exactly where it hurts. You write it on Brask's list and both of you sleep.|It points with its whole body. You bring the right tonic and it drops off before the lantern does.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      say: {~It tells you a great deal, about its neighbours, the hay and the moon, and nothing about where it hurts.|It won't talk to you. It talks to the lantern. You stay up anyway, which is the point.}\n> up[beast_speech]: Speak with it properly\n  study beastcraft +small\n  say: {~With the spell, it's easy: it explains the pain, the food, and what it thinks of the night. You fetch the right remedy.|You speak properly, and it answers properly, slowly, like someone who's been waiting. You both feel better.}\n\n# ---------------- Umbral Theory (track: umbral, skill guile) ----------------\n\n@event umbral_ward_candle\ntitle: Ward the Candle\ntrack: umbral\nlevel: 2\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: candle\ntags: class magic social\ncast:\n  partner: any where tagged(partner, student)\nscene: Two students at a dim bench under low lamps, a candle between them inside a faint pale shell, one student trying to blow it out.\ntext: {Ward at Umbral Theory II. Ward a candle while your partner tries to put it out. Today that's {partner}.|Candle wards again, and the lamps are lower. {partner} is across the bench.|The candle exercise. You're paired with {partner}, who rolls up {their} sleeves.} {~The professor walks between the benches with a hand over the flames.|The room smells of wax and burnt thread.|Someone's ward has shrunk to the size of a thimble.}\n> left: A thick, honest shell\n  guile +small\n  mana -small\n  rel partner +tiny\n  say: {~Your ward is plain and solid. {partner} blows and blows and the candle doesn't even lean.|Strong and dull. {partner} gives up and the two of you watch the flame burn down.}\n> right: A ward that turns them aside\n  challenge deceive medium:\n    win:\n      guile +small\n      study umbral +tiny\n      say: {~Your ward turns {partner}'s breath round, and it blows {their} own hair into {their} eyes. The professor stops and says, \"Ah.\"|{partner} reaches for your candle and finds {their} hand on the wrong side of the bench. The professor approves.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      rel partner -tiny\n      say: {~Your clever ward turns aside the wrong thing, and the candle goes out with a smug little puff. {partner} smirks.|The ward bends {partner}'s breath straight into your face. You sneeze and the candle goes out.}\n\n@event umbral_dark_roll\ntitle: Who Moved\ntrack: umbral\nlevel: 2\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: moon\ntags: class magic\nscene: A pitch-dark classroom at the top of a tower, a few vague shapes of students sitting perfectly still in the gloom.\ntext: {Umbral Theory II. The professor puts out every lamp, and in the dark one student moves. Say who, and where to.|Lamps out. You've learned to hear a sleeve.|The dark. You know the room's sounds, and which floorboard is a liar.} {~Rain on the tower roof makes it harder.|It's so quiet you can hear the stair breathing.|Somebody's stomach rumbles, which helps no one.}\n> left: Listen, and name them\n  guile +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~\"Third bench, to the window.\" Right. The professor relights a lamp and moves on.|You get it on the second try, by the creak of a bench and a faint smell of soap.}\n> right: Be the one who moves\n  challenge deceive medium:\n    win:\n      guile +small\n      study umbral +tiny\n      say: {~You cross the whole room in the dark and nobody names you. When the lamps come up you're behind the professor.|Nobody hears you. The professor says, \"Someone moved,\" and seems pleased not to know who.}\n    lose:\n      health -tiny\n      mood -small\n      say: {~You catch a desk with your shin. \"Window, limping,\" says everyone at once.|Your shoe squeaks on the second step. The whole class names you, with feeling.}\n\n@event umbral_corridor_step\ntitle: Across the Corridor\ntrack: umbral\nlevel: 3\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: ghost\ntags: class magic danger\nscene: A dim stone corridor at the top of a tower, a student half stepping out of the shadow of one doorway while their own shadow lingers in another across the hall.\ntext: {Advanced Umbral Theory. Shadowstep across the corridor, door to door, the farthest the professor allows. \"Short distances only,\" the professor says. \"This is as short as long gets.\"|The corridor. The cold in between is colder than last time.|You stand at the door with your heels on the line. You know the cold that's coming.} {~The lamps are out on the landing.|A draught is moving along the floor.|Someone came back from it with frost on one shoulder.}\n> left: Step across\n  challenge deceive hard:\n    win:\n      guile +small\n      mood +tiny\n      say: {~One shadow, cold and long, and then the other. You come out on the far side shaking and grinning.|You step in by one door and out by the other, with a breath of frost on your collar. The professor writes your name, and a time.}\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      mood -small\n      say: {~The cold in between is longer than you thought. You come out in the right doorway and can't feel your hands for an hour.|You step and come out in the same doorway you went in by, with the feeling that something looked at you on the way.}\n> right: Walk it, counting shadows\n  study umbral +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~You walk the corridor and count every shadow and every lamp. The professor calls this \"mapping your road\".|You pace it out and write down each shadow's depth. Next time you'll step it. Probably.}\n> up[shadowstep]: Step with a held breath\n  guile +tiny\n  health -tiny\n  say: {~You step without thinking about it, the way the spell knows. The cold barely touches you.|The spell takes you over quick and clean. Your fingers are cold for a minute.}\n\n@event umbral_unpick_ward\ntitle: Unpicking the Box\ntrack: umbral\nlevel: 3\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: lock\ntags: class magic\nscene: A small iron-bound box on a bench under a dim lamp, faint threads of a pale ward wrapped around it, a student leaning close with a needle.\ntext: {Advanced Umbral Theory. The professor wards a small box and you unpick the ward without it noticing. \"A ward that notices you,\" the professor says, \"tells someone.\"|Box day. The professor has a new box, and a new ward on it.|The box. You've got a favourite needle for this now.} {~This ward is fine as a cobweb.|This ward is thick and patient.|This ward hums when you breathe on it.}\n> left: Thread by thread\n  guile +small\n  mana -small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~An hour with a needle and a steady breath, and the box opens. The professor checks the ward and it has no idea.|You undo it strand by strand. It takes the whole lesson, and you don't look up once.}\n> right: Cut it in one stroke\n  challenge deceive hard:\n    win:\n      guile +small\n      study umbral +small\n      say: {~You find the knot and cut it clean. The ward never knows. The professor raises a brow and opens the box.|One cut and the threads fall away. The professor spends a moment looking at the box and then at you.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      health -tiny\n      say: {~The ward notices and rings like a little bell. The professor says, \"And now someone knows.\" Everyone looks at you.|The ward snaps back at your hand and stings. The box stays shut.}\n"},{"path":"content/events/pools/grounds.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Baseline pools: the grounds (phase 3, step 1b).\n#  Repeatable routine events for map visits to the lakeshore,\n#  duelling hall, greenhouses, flight field, stables, boathouse,\n#  orchard, Maud's cottage, the secret garden, the courier loft\n#  and the Whisperwood. Spec: docs/authoring/pools.md.\n# ============================================================\n\n# ---------------- The Lakeshore ----------------\n\n@event lakeshore_shore_walk\ntitle: The Long Way Round\nplace: lakeshore\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: moon\ntags: romance nature\ncast:\n  date: any where romanceable(date) and rel(date) >= 12\nscene: Two figures walking a lakeside path at dusk, the towers reflected upside down in the still water beside them.\ntext: {You came out to walk alone. {date} finds you on the shore path and falls into step without asking.|The shore path is where you go to think. Today {date} has the same idea.|You know every stone of the shore path by now. Today {date} walks it with you.} {season == \"winter\": The lake is frozen at the edges and your breath walks ahead of you.|{season == \"summer\": The water is warm and full of sky.|{~Mist lies on the water like a sheet someone forgot to fold.|The towers stand upside down in the lake, lit window by lit window.}}}\n> left: Walk the long way round\n  spark date +small\n  rel date +tiny\n  health -small\n  say: {final_year: Halfway round, {date} says \"next year\" and stops, because there won't be one here. You walk the rest closer together.|{season == \"winter\": {~You walk until your toes go numb. {date} lends you {their} scarf and pretends not to be cold.|The ice sings under your boots. {date} takes your arm on the slippery stretch and forgets to let go.}|{~You walk until the far lamps go out. Neither of you mentions the time.|{date} points out a lit tower window and tells you who lives behind it. Half of it is invented. You don't mind which half.|You stand at the water's edge in silence, and the silence is the good kind.}}}\n> right: Talk about anything but us\n  rel date +small\n  spark date -tiny\n  health -tiny\n  say: {~You talk about exams, the cook and the weather. It's easy. It's also not what either of you came for.|You argue cheerfully about whether the lake has a bottom. It's a good walk. It's only a walk.}\n\n@event lakeshore_cold_swim\ntitle: The Cold Swim\nplace: lakeshore\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: wave\ntags: nature life\nscene: Students in towels on a lakeshore, one wading in up to the knees and shrieking, the towers reflected behind them.\ntext: {season == \"winter\": Students in towels are daring each other into the lake through a hole in the ice.|{season == \"summer\": Half the academy is in the lake; the other half pretends not to want to be.|A few hardy students are swimming out to the flat rock, shrieking.}} {The water looks inviting. It always does.|You know how cold the Mirror Lake really is. You're tempted anyway.|You and the lake have an understanding. It doesn't help.}\n> left: Brave the water\n  challenge exam using vigor easy:\n    win:\n      vigor +small\n      mood +small\n      health -tiny\n      say: {season == \"winter\": You go under, come up roaring, and are hauled out a hero.|{~You slap the flat rock, whoop, and swim back to applause from people with dry hair.|You reach the rock first. The cold reaches you second, but you were already winning.}}\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      mood -tiny\n      say: {~You come back blue to the lips and sneezing. Sister Amaranth sighs before you've even sat down.|Halfway out, the lake reminds you who is in charge. You turn back with what dignity you have left.}\n> right: Hold the towels and heckle\n  charm +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~You announce every dive like a herald. You're dry, popular and a tiny bit jealous.|You keep the towels warm and the commentary warmer. Someone throws a wet sock at you, which is fair.}\n\n# ---------------- The Duelling Hall ----------------\n\n@event duel_hall_dummies\ntitle: The Practice Dummies\nplace: duel_hall\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: shield\ntags: magic\nscene: An empty duelling hall with scorched floorboards and a row of battered straw practice dummies, one with a painted moustache.\ntext: {The near end of the hall belongs to the practice dummies, who watch you come in. The one with the painted moustache looks especially unforgiving.|The dummy with the moustache is still here, with a new scorch mark and, you'd swear, a new grudge.|The moustached dummy greets you with its usual silence. Someone has given it an eyepatch.} {~The floorboards are still warm from the last session.|A singed glove lies abandoned on the rack.|Rain drums on the high windows.}\n> left: Drill your hexes on it\n  arcana +small\n  mana -small\n  say: {~Hex after hex until your wrists ache. The dummy loses a sleeve and none of its contempt.|You land a clean one at last. A wisp of straw floats down, slowly, like applause.}\n> right: Restuff the battered ones\n  mood +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {~You haul a bale of straw over from the stables and restuff three dummies. They look almost grateful, which is unsettling.|You restuff the moustached one with special care. It still looks like it's planning something.}\n\n@event duel_hall_gallery\ntitle: From the Gallery\nplace: duel_hall\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: duel\ntags: magic social\ncast:\n  here: present(place) where tagged(here, peer)\nscene: A narrow wooden gallery above a duelling floor, one student watching another drill spells at a target below.\ntext: {From the gallery above the duelling floor you can watch whoever is practising. Today it's {here}.|You've learned to read duels from up here. Today {here} is drilling.|The gallery rail has a groove where you lean. Below, {here} is drilling.} {~Sparks drift up to the gallery rail.|Somebody's familiar is asleep on the scoreboard.|The ceiling has acquired a new scorch mark.}\n> left: Offer to spar\n  challenge combat medium:\n    win:\n      rel here +small\n      arcana +tiny\n      health -tiny\n      say: {~You take a round off {here}. {They} stare{s} at you for a long second, then ask{s} for another.|You win with a feint you stole from watching {here}. {They} notice{s}, and look{s} annoyed and pleased at once.}\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      rel here +tiny\n      say: {~{here} puts you on the floorboards, politely. From down there the ceiling's scorch marks make a sort of map.|{here} disarms you so neatly that the dummies seem to wince. Your wand skids under the gallery stairs.}\n> right: Watch and take notes\n  lore +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~You fill a page with what {here} does with {their} feet. You'll never get to try it, and you know it.|You sketch {here}'s guard from above. On paper it has a gap. On the floor it doesn't.}\n\n@event duel_hall_humming_circle\ntime: evening\ntitle: The Humming Circle\nplace: duel_hall\nwhen: slot == \"evening\"\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: rune\ntags: magic\nscene: A dark duelling hall lit only by a ring of faintly glowing runes on the floor.\ntext: {The evening bouts are over. At the near end of the hall the warding circle is still humming.|The warding circle is humming. Whoever's drilling at the far end isn't looking your way.|The circle hums. You know its tune by now.} {final_year: Soon someone else will be sneaking in here after hours.|{~A practice wand lies on the rack, still warm.|The steward's broom leans by the door, watching.|Tonight the ceiling stain looks like a dragon.}}\n> left: Step in and try a hard one\n  challenge combat hard:\n    win:\n      arcana +small\n      renown +tiny\n      mana -small\n      say: {~The spell comes out clean and the circle drinks it. Somewhere a dummy loses its head and nobody ever finds out how.|You try the thing you saw a sixth-year do. It works. You tell no one, and walk back grinning.}\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      mana -small\n      say: {~The circle hands your spell straight back to you. Your eyebrows will regrow.|The circle flares, the dummies rattle, and you leave very quickly before the steward arrives.}\n> right: Sweep up for the steward\n  gold +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~You sweep up a winter's worth of ash. The steward pays you a crown and a grunt that might be thanks.|You sweep. The circle hums at you the whole time, like a cat that wants to be let out.}\n\n# ---------------- The Greenhouses ----------------\n\n@event greenhouses_warm_glass\ntitle: The Warm Glass\nplace: greenhouses\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: leaf\ntags: nature\nscene: A student reading on an upturned pot among tall ferns inside a steamy glass greenhouse, snow on the panes outside.\ntext: {season == \"winter\": Outside it's snowing. Inside, the glass runs with warm water and everything smells of earth.|{season == \"summer\": The greenhouses are sweltering, and everyone has retreated to the far end where the vents are.|The greenhouses steam gently. Someone is singing to the mandrakes at the far end.}} {You find a dry corner by the ferns, out of everyone's way.|Someone has left a cushion in your corner by the ferns.|The ferns lean toward you when you come in, as they do for you now.}\n> left: Do your reading in the warm\n  lore +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {~You get through a whole chapter. The pages curl in the damp and you come out smelling of moss.|You read until the steam makes the ink run. What's left of the chapter sticks.}\n> right: Nap on the potting bench\n  mood +small\n  health +tiny\n  renown -tiny\n  say: {~You wake with a seed tray for a pillow and a first-year staring at you. By supper it's a story.|You wake to a mandrake humming a lullaby. You choose not to tell anyone. Someone tells everyone.}\n\n@event greenhouses_repotting\ntitle: Repotting Day\nplace: greenhouses\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: flower\ntags: nature social\ncast:\n  here: present(place)\nscene: A student elbow-deep in compost at a greenhouse bench, handing a trowel to someone arriving.\ntext: {here} is up to the elbows in compost, repotting {season == \"spring\": seedlings that have outgrown everything|{season == \"autumn\": bulbs for the winter|something with more teeth than leaves}}. {\"Hands?\" {they} ask{s}.|{They} hand{s} you a trowel before you've said hello.|There's a trowel on the hook that has come to be yours. {here} nods at it.}\n> left: Get your hands dirty\n  rel here +small\n  alchemy +tiny\n  health -tiny\n  say: {~You repot a whole row. Something nips your thumb in thanks.|You're filthy, nipped and oddly happy. {here} says you have good hands for roots.}\n> right: Ask questions, stay clean\n  lore +small\n  rel here -tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~You learn a great deal about soil. {here} learns that you're afraid of it.|You take notes. {here} gives you a look over the trowel that says notes are not hands.}\n\n@event greenhouses_cuttings\ntitle: The Cuttings Bench\nplace: greenhouses\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: potion\ntags: nature\nscene: A greenhouse bench of labelled cuttings drying in rows, a row of snapdragons with actual teeth watching from their pots.\ntext: {~A row of silver-leaf cuttings is drying on the bench|A tray of night-orchid cuttings sits by the door|A bundle of moonpetal cuttings is wrapped in damp cloth}, and Pell & Daughters pays well for that sort of thing. {Nobody is watching. The snapdragons are.|The snapdragons have seen you here before. They remember.|The snapdragons snap at you on principle now.}\n> left: Pocket a cutting\n  challenge deceive easy:\n    win:\n      gold +small\n      alignment -tiny\n      say: {~Pell's counts the leaves twice and pays without a question. You don't look at the snapdragons on your next visit.|It sells for more than you expected, which somehow makes it worse.}\n    lose:\n      renown -small\n      alignment -tiny\n      say: {~A snapdragon shrieks. Three Verdant students turn around at once. You put it back with a cough that convinces no one.|Your pocket rustles at the wrong moment. You spend the rest of the visit apologising to a fern.}\n> right: Feed the snapdragons\n  alchemy +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {~They take the food, then a little of your finger. You learn which end to hold the tongs by.|Fed, the snapdragons purr like kettles. One of them still bites you, out of habit.}\n\n# ---------------- The Flight Field ----------------\n\n@event flight_field_cheating_wind\ntitle: The Cheating Wind\nplace: flight_field\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: broom\ntags: life\nscene: A long green field with tall hoops at either end, one student wobbling on a school broom against a gusting wind.\ntext: {season == \"winter\": The flight field is white, the hoops wearing caps of snow.|{season == \"spring\": The field is soft with new grass and the wind is playful.|{~The field is empty but for the wind, practising on its own.|A lone first-year is flying very careful circles.}}} {The school brooms in the shed are, at best, opinionated.|Your usual broom is in the shed, the one that pulls left.|The broom that pulls left now pulls right, out of spite.}\n> left: Practise your landings\n  vigor +small\n  health -small\n  say: {~A dozen landings. A few of them on purpose. The grass keeps a careful record of the rest.|The wind waits until you're nearly down, then shoves. You land in a hoop instead. Technically, a goal.}\n> right: Hire a decent broom\n  gold -small\n  mood +small\n  vigor +tiny\n  say: {~The good broom goes where you look, not where it feels like. For an hour, flying is easy and the field is yours.|You fly the length of the field and back with the wind on your side for once. It costs a week's sweets.}\n\n@event flight_field_pickup_race\ntitle: The Pick-Up Race\nplace: flight_field\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: trophy\ntags: social life\nscene: Three students on brooms jostling at a crooked painted start line, a fourth holding up a scarf as a starting flag.\ntext: {A pick-up race is forming at the far hoops. Three brooms, two rules, no referee.|The pick-up racers are back. The rules have grown to four.|The pick-up race is a fixture now. Someone has painted a finish line, crookedly.} {~A second-year from Emberhall is taking bets.|A Brasswick student has bolted a lantern to their broom-tail.|A Rootward student is flying barefoot, for grip.}\n> left: Borrow a broom and race\n  challenge duel using vigor medium:\n    win:\n      renown +small\n      mood +small\n      health -tiny\n      say: {~You win by a broom's length and a lot of shouting. The second-year taking bets has to pay out and never forgives you.|You take the last hoop sideways and cross first. Someone adds a new rule, about you.}\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      mood -tiny\n      say: {~You finish last, and partly in a hedge.|The wind takes your side, then changes its mind. You walk the broom back to the start.}\n> right: Bet a crown on the outsider\n  chance 40:\n    gold +small\n    mood +tiny\n    say: {~The outsider wins on a gust nobody else saw coming. You are suddenly very good at betting.|The outsider comes in first by a nose. You toast the nose.}\n  else:\n    gold -tiny\n    mood -tiny\n    say: {~The outsider stops halfway to admire the view. Your crown goes with them.|Your flyer is disqualified for a rule invented on the spot. You don't argue. It's a good rule.}\n\n@event flight_field_long_grass\ntitle: The Long Grass\nplace: flight_field\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: pouch\ntags: life\nscene: The long grass at the edge of a flight field, scattered with things dropped from brooms, a student crouching to look.\ntext: {The long grass at the edge of the flight field catches everything that falls off a broom.|Someone has planted a little flag in the long grass, to mark where things land.|You've come to think of the long grass as the academy's real lost-and-found.} Today it's {~a velvet glove|a letter with no envelope|a single boot, laced|someone's notes for an exam}. {season == \"autumn\": The leaves are trying to bury it.|{season == \"winter\": It's half under frost.|A grasshopper is sitting on it.}}\n> left: Keep what you find\n  guile +tiny\n  mood +tiny\n  alignment -tiny\n  chance 30:\n    renown -small\n    say: {~The owner lands right beside you while you're still holding it. There's a short, very long silence.|A second-year marches over the grass shouting \"Thief!\" at the sky in general and you in particular.}\n  else:\n    say: {~Finders, keepers. The grass doesn't argue, and neither does your conscience, much.|Nobody comes looking. You tell yourself that settles it.}\n> right: Carry it to the porter's lodge\n  alignment +small\n  renown +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~It's a long walk round the field. The porter writes it in a book and gives you a look of mild surprise.|The porter thanks you with a peppermint from a jar that has clearly been open since the founders.}\n\n# ---------------- The Familiar Stables ----------------\n\n@event stables_owl_loft\ntitle: The Owl Loft\nplace: stables\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: owl\ntags: nature\nscene: A hay loft in old stables, rows of sleepy owls on the rafters above a student reading by a lantern.\ntext: {Up the ladder, above the hippogriffs, is a hay loft full of owls and quiet.|The owls open one eye each, decide you're furniture, and go back to sleep.|The owls have stopped opening their eyes for you. You've been accepted.} {season == \"winter\": The warmth of the animals rises through the boards.|{~Rain ticks on the roof.|A stray letter has fallen into the hay.|The griffon chick is complaining somewhere below.}}\n> left: Read up among the owls\n  lore +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {~You read until the light goes. The hay gets in everywhere, and the chapter gets in too.|An owl reads over your shoulder for a whole page, then hoots, pointedly, at a footnote.}\n> right: Help sort the owl post\n  charm +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~You sort a dropped bundle of post by hand. One owl seems to know which letters you're going to misfile.|You deliver three soggy letters to their owners on the way back. One of them hugs you, which you weren't ready for.}\n\n@event stables_brask_rounds\ntitle: Brask's Rounds\nplace: stables\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: paw\ntags: nature\nscene: A burly professor stroking a hippogriff's beak in a stable aisle, ignoring a student holding two feed buckets.\ntext: {Professor Brask is on his rounds, telling each hippogriff she's a very good girl. He tells you to mind the bucket.|Brask is on his rounds. He nods at you, which is more than most get.|Brask greets you by name now, somewhere after the owls.} {season == \"spring\": There are new hippogriff foals, all legs and beak.|{~A hippogriff is sulking about its feathers.|The feed is late and everyone is cross.|The stable cat is asleep in the oats.}}\n> left: Ask Brask a question\n  lore +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~He answers it at length, to the hippogriff. You take notes anyway.|\"Good question,\" he says, to the owl. You decide that counts.}\n> right: Carry the feed buckets\n  vigor +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {~Every stall, two buckets each. Brask says nothing, which is his way of saying thank you.|A hippogriff bows to you over its bucket. Brask looks almost jealous.}\n\n@event stables_griffon_sulk\ntitle: The Sulking Griffon\nplace: stables\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: feather\ntags: nature\nscene: A griffon chick with puffed-up feathers glaring from the corner of a stall, a student holding out a morsel at arm's length.\ntext: {The griffon chick is sulking in the corner stall, feathers puffed, refusing breakfast.|The griffon chick is sulking again. It's bigger this time, and so is the sulk.|The griffon is hardly a chick any more, and still sulks like one. It watches you come in.} {year == 1: The sign on the stall says not to hand-feed it, underlined twice.|{~The sign on the stall now has a drawing of a bitten hand.|The sign on the stall has been underlined until the paper tore.|Someone has added \"I mean it\" to the sign, in Brask's hand.}}\n> left: Offer it breakfast by hand\n  challenge negotiate easy:\n    win:\n      charm +small\n      mood +small\n      say: {~It considers you for a long time, then takes the morsel with great delicacy. You feel chosen.|It eats from your hand and then, briefly, leans on you. You don't move for ten minutes.}\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      mood -tiny\n      say: {~It takes the morsel and a corner of your sleeve. The sign was right.|It turns its back on you so pointedly that a passing owl looks embarrassed.}\n> right: Leave it to its sulk\n  lore +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~You read the notice about griffon moods instead. It is mostly a list of things that will not help.|You leave it alone. It looks, as you go, a little disappointed you gave up.}\n\n# ---------------- The Boathouse ----------------\n\n@event boathouse_jetty_end\ntitle: The End of the Jetty\nplace: boathouse\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: lantern\ntags: romance\ncast:\n  date: any where romanceable(date) and rel(date) >= 12\nscene: Two people sitting side by side at the end of a wooden jetty, boots over dark water, a lantern between them.\ntext: {{date} is at the end of the jetty, boots over the water. {They} move{s} over without a word.|The end of the jetty has become your place for saying things. Today {date} is already there.|You know which plank creaks. {date} doesn't, and finds it.} {season == \"winter\": The lake has frozen round the posts and creaks like an old house.|{~The rowing boats knock gently together.|A heron fishes in the shallows, pretending not to listen.|The village lamps blink on across the water.}}\n> left: Hire a boat and row out\n  gold -small\n  spark date +small\n  mood +tiny\n  say: {season == \"winter\": You can't row on ice, so you sit in a beached boat and pretend. {date} calls it a fine voyage.|{~You row badly, {date} laughs, and then neither of you is rowing at all.|Out in the middle the shore goes quiet. {date} says your name like the end of a sentence.}}\n> right: Stay on the jetty and talk\n  rel date +small\n  mood +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {final_year: You talk about where you'll both be next year. Neither of you says \"together\", and both of you hear it.|{~You talk until the lantern gutters. The jetty is cold and you don't notice until you stand.|You say one of the things you couldn't say indoors. {date} listens all the way to the end.}}\n\n@event boathouse_green_boat\ntitle: The Leaky Green Boat\nplace: boathouse\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: wave\ntags: life\nscene: Inside an old wooden boathouse, an upturned green rowing boat on trestles, initials carved into every beam above.\ntext: {The boathouse smells of tar and old rope. Its rowing boats are mostly leaks held together by paint, the green one worst of all.|Someone has patched the green boat since your last visit, badly, with what looks like a cake tin.|The boathouse knows you. The green boat still leaks.} {~Initials are carved into every beam, some so old they've gone soft.|Rain patters on the roof.|A spider has strung a web across the oars, and seems proud of it.}\n> left: Patch the green boat\n  vigor +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {~You spend an hour with tar and a mallet. The green boat floats, briefly, with an air of surprise.|Your patch holds for a whole circuit of the boathouse. You have tar in your eyebrows and a certain pride.}\n> right: Carve your initials in a beam\n  mood +small\n  gold -tiny\n  say: {final_year: You carve them next to a pair from sixty years ago. Somebody, one day, will run a thumb over yours and wonder.|{~Your penknife snaps on the last letter. The initials stay, slightly wrong, which feels right.|You find a carving that could almost be yours from long ago, and add your own beneath it.}}\n\n@event boathouse_overheard\ntitle: Things Said Outside\nplace: boathouse\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: eye\ntags: social\nscene: Two silhouettes talking quietly on a jetty at night, seen through the slats of a boathouse wall.\ntext: {Two voices out on the jetty, low and urgent. People come to the boathouse to say what they can't say indoors.|You've found the gap in the boathouse planks where every word on the jetty comes through.|Another quiet conversation on the jetty. You're starting to know the academy by its secrets.} {~It sounds like a break-up.|It sounds like an apology that isn't going well.|It sounds like someone confessing to a pudding.}\n> left: Listen a little longer\n  guile +small\n  alignment -tiny\n  chance 25:\n    renown -small\n    say: {~A plank creaks under you. The voices stop. Two very red faces come round the door, and now they know your face too.|An oar slides off its peg with a clatter. Footsteps; a hiss of \"Who's there?\"; you are, unfortunately.}\n  else:\n    say: {~You learn more than you wanted to. You'll carry it carefully, and never tell.|It ends well, as it happens. You slip away before anyone has to know you were there.}\n> right: Cough loudly and leave\n  alignment +tiny\n  charm +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~They wait for you to pass, hushed and grateful. You'll never know how it ended, and that's the price.|You make a great show of looking for an oar. By the time you've found one, they've gone.}\n\n# ---------------- The Orchard ----------------\n\n@event orchard_under_the_trees\ntitle: Under the Old Trees\nplace: orchard\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: tree\ntags: romance nature\ncast:\n  date: any where romanceable(date) and rel(date) >= 12\nscene: Two people beneath an ancient apple tree, blossom drifting down around them like snow.\ntext: {season == \"spring\": Blossom is coming down like snow, and {date} has some in {their} hair.|{season == \"autumn\": The trees are heavy with apples and wasps, and {date} is reaching for one.|{season == \"winter\": The orchard is bare and silver, and {date} is walking between the trees.|{date} has claimed the best of the orchard shade.}}} {{They} wave{s} you over.|{They} saw you coming before you saw {them}.|The old trees seem to expect you by now. {date} only half does.}\n> left: Stay until the light goes\n  spark date +small\n  health -small\n  say: {season == \"spring\": {~You stay until the blossom buries your shoes. You miss supper, and you'd do it again.|{date} picks blossom out of your hair, one petal at a time, and takes far too long about it.}|{season == \"winter\": {~Your fingers go white, and {date} takes your hands to warm them, and doesn't give them back.|You lean against the same cold trunk until the stars come out over the bare branches.}|{~You stay until the shadows outgrow the trees. You miss supper. Neither of you mentions it.|The light goes gold, then blue. {date} says something quiet you'll turn over for days.}}}\n> right: Help in the orchard together\n  rel date +small\n  vigor +tiny\n  health -tiny\n  say: {season == \"autumn\": {~You fill two baskets and earn one wasp sting each. The cook gives you a pie, which you share on the steps.|{date} holds the ladder and says nothing useful, and you don't want {them} to.}|{season == \"winter\": {~You prune dead wood side by side until your hands ache. {date} stacks it with great seriousness.|You wrap the young trees against the frost. {date} ties every knot twice.}|{season == \"spring\": {~You carry hives along the rows for the blossom. One bee follows {date} all afternoon.|You clear the grass under the blossom. {date} keeps stopping to look up.}|{~You water the young trees from a bucket chain of two. {date} is soaked by the end, and so are you.|You prop up a branch bowed with early apples. {date} holds it, and holds still, and you notice.}}}}\n\n@event orchard_windfalls\ntitle: Windfalls\nplace: orchard\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: pouch\ntags: nature life\nscene: A student gathering fallen fruit into a basket under gnarled orchard trees, older than the towers behind them.\ntext: {The orchard trees are older than the towers, and never tidy.|The oldest tree has dropped its fruit in a ring, like a table laid for guests.|You know which tree drops what by now, and which one throws.} {season == \"autumn\": Pears and apples lie bruised in the grass, attended by wasps.|{season == \"winter\": Last year's apples lie frozen, hard as stones.|{season == \"spring\": It's mostly blossom, and the odd stubborn pear.|Early apples lie in the shade, small and sharp.}}}\n> left: Gather windfalls to sell\n  gold +small\n  health -tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~The cook buys your basket for the cider vats and finds {season == \"winter\": a frozen beetle|a wasp} in it. You both pretend that's normal.|A full basket, {season == \"winter\": numb fingers|two stings} and a crick in your back. The crowns feel earned.}\n> right: Eat one under the oldest tree\n  mood +small\n  chance 30:\n    health -small\n    say: {~{season == \"winter\": It's frozen solid. Your teeth will remember this.|There was a wasp in it. There was, briefly, a wasp in you.}|It's sour enough to turn your face inside out. A passing first-year laughs, and so, eventually, do you.}\n  else:\n    health +tiny\n    say: {~It's the best thing you've eaten all month. The old tree creaks, as if pleased.|You eat it slowly. Above you, the oldest tree drops one more, gently, beside your hand.}\n\n@event orchard_cider_shed\ntitle: The Cider Shed\nplace: orchard\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: cup\ntags: social life\nscene: A tumbledown orchard shed with a wooden cider press inside, three students turning the screw and a fourth keeping watch at the door.\ntext: {In the orchard shed, a few students are running an unofficial cider press.|The lookout at the cider shed has started nodding you in.|The cider shed is a fixture now. Even the lookout seems bored.} {season == \"autumn\": The press groans with this year's apples.|The shed smells of last autumn and wet straw.} {~A goat is watching from the hedge, very carefully saying nothing.|Someone has carved a menu into the door.|A prefect's scarf hangs on a nail, unexplained.}\n> left: Buy a cup\n  gold -tiny\n  mood +small\n  chance 25:\n    renown -tiny\n    say: {~A prefect turns up as you raise the cup. Everyone is suddenly very interested in apples.|The lookout whistles too late. A prefect takes your name, then, after some thought, your cup.}\n  else:\n    say: {~It's sharp, cold and a little bit fizzy. The day goes golden at the edges.|You drink it on an upturned bucket while someone tells a story about a goose. Excellent cider. Better goose.}\n> right: Help turn the press, for a share\n  alchemy +small\n  mood -tiny\n  health -tiny\n  say: {~You turn the screw until your shoulders burn. The second-year in charge teaches you why some apples are better bruised.|You're sticky to the elbows and you've learned more about fermentation than in a term of class.}\n\n# ---------------- Old Maud's Cottage ----------------\n\n@event maud_cottage_kettle\ntitle: Tea at Maud's\nplace: maud_cottage\nwhen: slot == \"evening\"\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: cup\ntags: home\nscene: A low cottage kitchen, a kettle on the hob, an old woman pouring tea into a chipped cup, a goat's head poking through the open door.\ntext: {Old Maud's kettle is nearly boiling, as it always is. \"Sit down, dearie,\" she says. \"You look like a puddle with a worry.\"|The kettle is nearly boiling. Maud has already put out your cup, the chipped one.|Your chipped cup is waiting. Maud says it's been asking after you.} {~Bartholomew is eating a tea towel.|Rain drums on the thatch.|A hen is asleep on the windowsill.|Bees drone in the eaves.}\n> left: Stay for a second cup\n  mood +small\n  rel maud +tiny\n  health -tiny\n  say: {~Maud tells you a sad story the way other people give a recipe. You walk home late and cold, thinking about it.|\"Never trust a dry spring, dearie,\" she says. You won't understand it for a fortnight.|The second cup becomes a third. It's dark when you leave, and you've only half noticed.}\n> right: Ask her for a remedy\n  health +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~She brews you something that tastes of ditches. \"Down in one, dearie.\" You feel better at once, and resent it.|\"For what ails you,\" she says, and doesn't say what she thinks that is. It tastes of nettles and of being right.}\n\n@event maud_cottage_bartholomew\ntitle: Bartholomew Is Out\nplace: maud_cottage\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: house\ntags: home\nscene: A goat standing triumphantly on the roof of a henhouse, chickens scattering, a student approaching with an apple held out.\ntext: {Bartholomew the goat has got out.|Bartholomew is out again, and has learned to lift the gate latch with his teeth.|Bartholomew has escaped so often that the hens no longer scatter.} {slot == \"evening\": Maud doesn't even look up from her knitting.|Maud's note on the door says she's at the greenhouses, and to mind the goat.} {~He has eaten half a washing line.|He is standing on the roof of the henhouse, looking pleased.|He is in the cabbages, conducting a slow and thorough war.}\n> left: Chase him down\n  challenge exam using vigor easy:\n    win:\n      rel maud +small\n      vigor +tiny\n      say: {~You corner him by the water butt. He gives up with the air of someone who allowed it. {slot == \"evening\": \"Ta, dearie,\" calls Maud.|You tie him to the gate and leave Maud a note.}|You catch him on the second lap. He chews your sleeve all the way back, as a souvenir.}\n    lose:\n      health -tiny\n      mood -small\n      say: {~He leads you twice round the orchard and once through the hen run, then trots home on his own.|You end up in the cabbages. Bartholomew watches you from the roof, chewing, unhurried.}\n> right: Bribe him with your lunch\n  rel maud +tiny\n  mood +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {~He takes your sandwich and follows you home like a gentleman. You go hungry until supper.|Your lunch disappears in three bites. Bartholomew looks at you as if you might be worth knowing.}\n\n@event maud_cottage_top_shelf\ntitle: The Top Shelf\nplace: maud_cottage\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: bottle\ntags: magic home\nscene: A high cottage shelf crowded with stoppered jars and handwritten labels, one jar glowing faintly blue.\ntext: {{slot == \"evening\": Maud has stepped out to the hens.|Maud is up at the greenhouses.} The top shelf of jars is right there, every label in her spidery hand.|Maud has stepped out, and the top shelf has gained a jar since you were last here.|You know the top shelf by heart, except for whichever jar Maud added last.} One label reads {~\"not for you, dearie\"|\"only in emergencies\"|\"do not shake\"}, and the jar is {~glowing faintly|humming|warm to the touch}.\n> left: Open it, just a little\n  chance 50:\n    mana +small\n    mood +small\n    say: {~Something sweet and blue drifts out and settles in your chest. You feel, briefly, enormous.|A breath of summer rain escapes. You put the lid back on smiling.}\n  else:\n    health -small\n    mood -tiny\n    say: {~Your eyebrows turn the colour of the label. When Maud sees them, she says nothing whatsoever.|It sneezes. Then you sneeze, for an hour. \"Told you, dearie,\" says Maud later, not unkindly.}\n> right: Dust the shelf instead\n  alchemy +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~You dust every jar and read every label. Maud's handwriting is a whole education, if a strange one.|Maud finds a clean shelf. \"Somebody's been learning,\" she says, which isn't quite thanks.}\n\n# ---------------- The Secret Garden ----------------\n\n@event secret_garden_humming_pear\ntitle: The Humming Pear\nplace: secret_garden\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: tree\ntags: nature magic\nscene: A student lying in the grass under a pear tree in a hidden walled garden, ferns glowing softly around them.\ntext: {The pear tree in Maud's secret garden is humming to itself.|The pear tree has changed its tune since you were last here, as if it's been practising.|The pear tree hums when you come in now. You'd like to think it's for you.} {season == \"winter\": Its tune is low and slow, like something asleep.|{season == \"spring\": Its tune has gone quick and bright with blossom.|{~Its tune is one you almost know.|Its tune has a sad turn in the middle.}}}\n> left: Hum along with it\n  mana +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~You find the harmony on the third try. The tree corrects you twice, kindly.|The two of you hum until the ferns glow brighter. Afterwards you can't get the tune out of your head for days.}\n> right: Nap in its shade\n  mood +small\n  health +small\n  gold -tiny\n  say: {~You wake rested, with a crown missing from your pocket and a fresh root curled over the spot where it fell.|You sleep like a seed. A crown slips out of your pocket and the roots close over it. Rent, perhaps.}\n\n@event secret_garden_silver_bees\ntitle: The Silver Bees\nplace: secret_garden\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: sparkle\ntags: nature magic\nscene: Bees made of silver light hovering around a hive in a hollow stump, a student reaching carefully towards a dripping comb.\ntext: {The silver bees are working the ferns; their hive in the old stump drips honey the colour of moonlight.|The silver bees have built a second comb, lopsided, as if in a hurry.|The silver bees know you now, more or less.} {season == \"winter\": Even in winter they fly, slowly, like snow going the wrong way.|{~The comb is fuller than you've ever seen it.|One of them lands on your sleeve and stays.|They're dancing a direction you can't read.}}\n> left: Take a little comb\n  alchemy +small\n  chance 35:\n    health -small\n    say: {~A silver sting feels like a cold spark. Your hand glows for an hour and aches for two.|The bees file a complaint, in the form of your thumb.}\n  else:\n    say: {~You take a thumb's width of comb. It tastes like starlight and burns a little going down.|The bees let you take a little, and watch to make sure it's only a little.}\n> right: Follow a bee home\n  lore +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {~The bee leads you through brambles to a fern you've never seen, and then leaves you there, scratched and curious.|You follow it all the way round the garden and back to the stump. It seems to find this funny.}\n\n@event secret_garden_glowing_ferns\ntitle: The Glowing Ferns\nplace: secret_garden\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: leaf\ntags: nature magic\nscene: A hidden garden at dusk, waist-high ferns glowing pale green, one student crouched with fingers near a frond.\ntext: {The ferns in the secret garden glow when the light goes. One frond would light your dormitory for a month. Maud said not to pick them.|Since your last visit a new fern has unfurled by the gate, already glowing.|The ferns glow. You know exactly what a frond is worth, and what Maud said.} {~One has grown over the path, right at your hand.|A broken frond lies on the ground already, still glowing.|They're brighter than you've ever seen them.}\n> left: Pick a single frond\n  mood +small\n  alignment -tiny\n  chance 40:\n    mood -small\n    say: {~The frond goes dark before you reach the door. The garden feels a little colder to you the next time.|The frond curls up in your hand like a woodlouse and goes out. The silver bees follow you to the gate, accusingly.}\n  else:\n    say: {~It lights your dormitory green for a week. You read by it, and feel very slightly watched.|You keep it in a jar by your bed. It glows softer every night, like something being polite.}\n> right: Leave them glowing\n  lore +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~You sit and watch the glow instead, and learn the way it moves with your breath. Maud would approve, if she knew.|You leave them be. On the way out, the ferns lean after you, like a dog that wanted to come.}\n\n# ---------------- The Courier Loft ----------------\n\n@event courier_loft_letter_home\ntitle: A Letter Home\nplace: courier_loft\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: letter\ntags: home\nscene: A draughty loft full of pigeonholes, a student writing a letter on a parcel by the light of a high window.\ntext: {The courier loft will take a letter home for next to nothing. You've meant to write for weeks.|This time the letter has been half-written in your head since breakfast.|Writing home is a habit now, and the loft knows your handwriting.} {final_year: There aren't many letters left to send from here.|{season == \"winter\": Frost on the window; the pigeonholes stuffed with cards.|{~The dispatch bell rings twice while you write.|A broken broom leans against your elbow.}}}\n> left: Write it all down and send it\n  gold -tiny\n  mood +small\n  say: {~You write three pages and post them before you can cross anything out. You feel lighter, and a little homesick.|You tell them about your classes, and not about the hard parts. Then, in a postscript, you tell them about the hard parts.}\n> right: Write it, then don't send it\n  lore +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~You read it over and fold it into your pocket instead. Some letters are only for writing.|You keep this one. The courier asks if you're sure. You aren't.}\n\n@event courier_loft_dispatch_bell\ntitle: The Dispatch Bell\nplace: courier_loft\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: bell\ntags: life\nscene: A chaotic loft as a dispatch bell clangs, couriers grabbing brooms and parcels, one holding a parcel out to a visitor.\ntext: {The dispatch bell clangs and the loft explodes into brooms. A courier shoves a parcel at you. \"You've got legs. Lecture tower!\"|The couriers have started eyeing the ladder whenever someone climbs it.|The bell. A parcel. A look. You know the drill.} {~The parcel is ticking.|The parcel is addressed to \"Whoever Is On Duty\".|The parcel is very cold.}\n> left: Run the parcel across campus\n  gold +tiny\n  vigor +tiny\n  health -tiny\n  say: {~You get it there out of breath and in time. The tip is a crown and a very strange look.|You run it up four flights of stairs. The recipient signs for it without looking up, as if you were the post.}\n> right: Hand it back, politely\n  mood +tiny\n  renown -tiny\n  say: {~\"Fine, fine,\" says the courier, already on a broom. You hear about it, loudly, from the rafters.|The courier takes it back with a sigh that follows you down the ladder.}\n\n@event courier_loft_broom_rack\ntitle: The Fast Broom\nplace: courier_loft\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: broom\ntags: life\nscene: A rack of courier brooms in a loft, one sleek black broom set apart from the others with a red tag on its handle.\ntext: {At the end of the rack is the courier loft's fast broom, tagged in red. Couriers only. Nobody's watching.|The fast broom is back on the rack, red tag and all.|The red-tagged broom. You've thought about it every time you've come up here.} {season == \"winter\": The loft doors are open on a sky full of snow.|{~The loft doors stand open on a blue sky.|Someone has polished it recently.|The red tag has a fresh scorch mark.}}\n> left: Take it for one lap\n  challenge exam using vigor medium:\n    win:\n      mood +small\n      renown +tiny\n      say: {~One lap of the towers, fast enough to make your ears sing. You put it back with the bristles barely warm.|You fly it under the bridges of light and come back laughing. Nobody saw, except, possibly, everybody.}\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      gold -tiny\n      say: {~It knows you aren't a courier. It shows you the courtyard, very close up. You pay for the bristles.|It bucks you off into a haystack outside the stables. You tip the stable hand to say nothing.}\n> right: Tighten its bristles instead\n  lore +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~You spend an hour learning how a good broom is bound. It's more satisfying than you'd admit.|You retie the bristles properly. Some courier will fly a little faster tomorrow and never know why.}\n\n# ---------------- The Whisperwood ----------------\n\n@event whisperwood_sleeptalk\ntitle: The Forest Talks\nplace: whisperwood\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: tree\ntags: nature mystery\nscene: A student standing very still in a misty old forest, head tilted, as if listening to the trees.\ntext: {The Whisperwood is talking in its sleep. You stand very still to make out the words.|You've started to catch whole words in the forest's mumbling.|You've learned to stand still and let the forest talk.} Today it's {~listing trees that were felled|arguing about the rain|saying a name, over and over, that isn't yours}. {season == \"winter\": Snow muffles half of it.|{season == \"autumn\": Leaves fall with every word.|Fog moves between the trunks.}}\n> left: Listen closely\n  lore +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~You catch a whole sentence. It's about a winter long ago, and it's sad, and you walk home with it.|It notices you listening and goes quiet, the way a sleeper does. You leave feeling rude.}\n> right: Walk on, humming\n  vigor +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {~You walk the long loop humming loudly. The forest grumbles and turns over. You come back with muddy boots and a clear head.|You hum all the way round. Twice, the forest hums back.}\n\n@event whisperwood_foragers\ntitle: Foraging Season\nplace: whisperwood\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: mushroom\ntags: nature social\nscene: A handful of students with baskets crouching in a forest clearing, one holding up a mushroom for the others to inspect.\ntext: {season == \"autumn\": The Whisperwood is full of mushrooms and students with baskets.|{season == \"winter\": A few hardy foragers are scraping lichen off the oaks.|{season == \"spring\": The clearings are thick with wild garlic and foragers.|The brambles are heavy with berries, and so are the foragers.}}} {A second-year from Rootward waves you over.|The Rootward forager is back, and saves you a spot.|The foragers treat you as one of their own now.}\n> left: Forage for the apothecary\n  gold +small\n  health -small\n  say: {~Pell & Daughters buys your basket and throws out a third of it. You've still got nettle rash.|A good basket, a fair price, and brambles in places you'd rather not discuss.}\n> right: Learn the plants from the expert\n  alchemy +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~The second-year names everything, twice, with its uses and its poisons. You come away full of names and short of patience.|You learn which mushroom is supper and which is a funeral. It takes until supper, and you'll need it.}\n\n@event whisperwood_off_path\ntitle: The Path That Wasn't\nplace: whisperwood\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: compass\ntags: nature mystery\nscene: A narrow path branching off into fog between old trees, a student pausing at the fork with a hand on a marked trunk.\ntext: {A path opens to your left that wasn't there last time.|Another path that wasn't there before. The Whisperwood does this.|The wood has offered you a new path again. You're starting to think it likes you.} {~It's lined with pale stones.|It smells of woodsmoke where there's no fire.|It goes downhill, which is wrong, because you're at the bottom of the hill.}\n> left: Follow it a little way\n  challenge research medium:\n    win:\n      lore +small\n      mana +small\n      health -tiny\n      say: {~It leads to a clearing you'll never find again, full of moonpetal buds. You come back with a pocketful of quiet.|It loops you round to an old stone with words on it. You copy them down. They mean nothing yet.}\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      mood -tiny\n      say: {~You walk in circles until dusk and come out at the lake, with no idea how.|The path ends at a wall of fog. Turning back takes much longer than going in did.}\n> right: Mark a tree and turn back\n  guile +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~You carve an arrow in the bark. The next time you come, the arrow points somewhere else.|You turn back. The path watches you go, and quietly closes behind you.}\n"},{"path":"content/events/pools/plans_gigs.grim","text":"# Holiday gigs (playtest 2, M6 stub; X15 fills them): working on campus over a break, you take one of two gigs\n# open to anyone. A gig pays through its cards, better than a term job's shift; board is still paid.\n# Repeatable routine events; see docs/authoring/pools.md.\n\n@event holiday_gigs\ntitle: Holiday Work\ntrigger: queued\ntype: menu\nclass: story\ntone: warm\nart: coin\ntags: job money\nscene: A noticeboard by the castle gates in the emptying holidays, two handwritten notices pinned side by side.\ntext: Two notices are still pinned by the gates. {season == \"winter\": The snow has got under one of them.|The sun has curled the corners of both.} Old Maud wants hands for the grounds. The Refectory kitchens want someone who can peel fast and not complain about it.\n> grounds: Work the grounds for Old Maud\n  hint: Outdoor work, heavy and honest. Good pay; hard on the body.\n  gain gig_grounds\n  say: Maud looks you up and down, hands you a pair of gloves two sizes too big, and says, \"You'll grow into them.\"\n> kitchens: Work the Refectory kitchens\n  hint: Long hours by the ovens. Good pay; hard on the temper.\n  gain gig_kitchens\n  say: The head cook gives you an apron, a knife and a sack of onions, and doesn't ask your name until the sack is empty.\n\n# ---------------- The grounds (gig_grounds) ----------------\n\n@event gig_grounds_hard_ground\ntitle: Hard Ground\nplan: work\nwhen: has(gig_grounds)\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 30\nart: leaf\ntags: job nature money\nscene: A student and an old woman working a frozen garden bed at dawn, breath steaming, a wheelbarrow between them.\ntext: {season == \"winter\": The ground is frozen two fingers deep and Maud wants the beds turned anyway.|The ground is baked hard and Maud wants the beds turned anyway.} \"The soil doesn't take holidays,\" she says. {~She hands you the heavier spade.|The bees watch you from their hives with professional interest.|She is already on her third bed.}\n> left: Dig until it's done\n  gold +large\n  health -small\n  vigor +small\n  say: {~By noon your hands have blisters on their blisters. Maud counts your wages into them anyway.|You turn every bed. Maud inspects them, says \"Hm,\" and pays you a little over.}\n> right: Work at Maud's pace\n  gold +medium\n  health -tiny\n  say: {~Maud works slowly and never stops, and by evening you understand why. Less done, a smaller purse, an easier back.|You keep to her pace and her stories. She pays for the beds you turned, not the ones she did.}\n\n@event gig_grounds_storm_damage\ntitle: After the Storm\nplan: work\nwhen: has(gig_grounds)\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 30\nart: tree\ntags: job nature money\nscene: A fallen branch across an orchard path after a storm, a student with a saw, an old woman pointing at something higher up.\ntext: {season == \"winter\": Last night's storm brought down half a pear tree.|A summer storm in the night has stripped the orchard.} Maud wants it cleared before the stay-behinds go walking. A cracked limb still hangs over the path.\n> left: Climb up and cut it down\n  gold +large\n  challenge feat easy:\n    win:\n      say: {~The limb comes down where you meant it to. Maud pays you extra for not falling.|You lower it on a rope like a professional. Maud pretends not to be impressed.}\n    lose:\n      health -medium\n      say: {~The limb comes down where it wanted to. So do you, more or less. Maud pays you and sends you to the infirmary.|You get the limb and the limb gets you.}\n> right: Rope it off and clear the rest\n  gold +medium\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~You clear the path and rope off the danger. Maud nods: she'll ask someone with a ladder. The pay is for the path.|Safe and sensible, and a smaller purse for it.}\n\n@event gig_grounds_hive_check\ntitle: Checking the Hives\nplan: work\nwhen: has(gig_grounds)\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 30\nart: leaf\ntags: job nature\nscene: A student in a veiled hat leaning over an open beehive beside an old woman, who isn't wearing one.\ntext: Maud's hives sit at the end of the orchard and she checks them {season == \"winter\": by ear in winter, knocking and listening|by hand in summer, slow and bare-armed}. Today she wants you to do it. \"Tell them who you are,\" she says. \"They like manners.\"\n> left: Introduce yourself properly\n  gold +medium\n  mood +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {~You tell the bees your name and your house. Only one stings you, on principle. Maud pays you and calls you a natural.|You feel foolish for about a minute, and then you don't. The hive hums back, and one bee makes a point of it.}\n> right: Get it done quickly\n  gold +large\n  chance 50:\n    say: {~Quick and quiet. The bees let it pass this once.|You're done before the bees notice. Maud notices.}\n  else:\n    health -small\n    say: {~The bees take your hurry personally. Maud pays you and pulls out the stings with no sympathy at all.|Three stings and a lesson about manners.}\n\n# ---------------- The Refectory kitchens (gig_kitchens) ----------------\n\n@event gig_kitchens_onions\ntitle: The Onion Sack\nplan: work\nwhen: has(gig_kitchens)\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 30\nart: meal\ntags: job money\nscene: A student at a scrubbed kitchen table behind a mountain of onions, eyes streaming, a huge cook stirring a cauldron behind them.\ntext: {The holiday kitchens feed fewer mouths and work no fewer hours.|Back at the long table, back to the sack.|The head cook has started saving the worst sack for you, as a compliment.} {season == \"winter\": The Midwinter stock needs a hundred onions.|The summer stew needs a hundred onions.} Nobody has ever needed fewer.\n> left: Peel the whole sack\n  gold +large\n  mood -small\n  say: {~A hundred onions and not one tear left in you. The head cook pays you and adds a heel of fresh bread.|You finish the sack. The head cook counts the onions, then your wages, both correctly.}\n> right: Peel, then help at the stove\n  gold +medium\n  alchemy +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~Half a sack and an hour at the stove. Less pay, but now you know why the soup tastes like home.|The head cook lets you stir. Somehow the stew tastes of your grandmother's. You get paid for the onions.}\n\n@event gig_kitchens_burnt_pan\ntitle: The Burnt Pan\nplan: work\nwhen: has(gig_kitchens)\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 30\nart: meal\ntags: job money\nscene: A smoking pan on a range, a student holding it at arm's length, a cook turning round with a ladle raised.\ntext: You turn away for a moment and the porridge for the stay-behinds catches. The smell is already halfway to the dining hall. The head cook hasn't noticed. Yet.\n> left: Own up and start again\n  gold +medium\n  mood -small\n  alignment +tiny\n  say: {~The head cook sighs the sigh of thirty years in kitchens and docks you for the oats. You make it again, properly.|\"Everyone burns one,\" says the head cook. \"Once.\" You are paid, minus the oats.}\n> right: Scrape it and serve it\n  gold +large\n  chance 60:\n    say: {~Nobody notices. The stay-behinds eat it and ask for more. You don't feel great about it.|A little smoky. \"Rustic,\" says someone. You get the full day's pay.}\n  else:\n    mood -medium\n    say: {~A professor sends it back with a note. The head cook reads it aloud, slowly, to the whole kitchen.|The smell gives you away. You're paid in full and in disapproval.}\n\n@event gig_kitchens_feast_night\ntitle: Feast Night\nplan: work\nwhen: has(gig_kitchens)\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 30\nart: cup\ntags: job money social\nscene: A steaming kitchen on a feast night, trays going out in a stream, a student in an apron grinning through the chaos.\ntext: {season == \"winter\": The castle keeps a small Midwinter table for those who stayed, and the kitchens cook as if for the whole school.|The stay-behinds hold a summer supper in the courtyard, and the kitchens cook as if for the whole school.} Trays go out faster than you can fill them.\n> left: Carry trays all night\n  gold +large\n  health -small\n  say: {~You carry until your arms forget being arms. The head cook pays you double for the night, in coins and leftovers.|Up and down the stairs until midnight. Somebody toasts the kitchens and you hear it through the door.}\n> right: Stay in the back and plate\n  gold +medium\n  health -tiny\n  say: {~You plate by the ovens and listen to the feast through the wall. Smaller pay, a scorched wrist, and the best seat in the house.|Quiet hands in a hot room. The head cook gives you the last slice of pie, which is its own wage.}\n"},{"path":"content/events/pools/plans_more.grim","text":"# More break-plan pool events (castle, home, work) and the \"regular\" milestones.\n# Repeatable routine events; see docs/authoring/pools.md.\n\n# ---------------- Break: at the castle (plan: castle) ----------------\n\n@event break_castle_holiday_repairs\ntitle: Holiday Repairs\nplan: castle\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: house\ntags: home\nscene: A caretaker with a long list on a clipboard in an empty castle corridor, a student trying to look busy nearby.\ntext: {The caretaker has a list of jobs that can only be done when the castle is empty, and an eye for idle students.|The caretaker's list is out again. Your name is pencilled in at the bottom, provisionally.|The caretaker hands you the list and a mug of tea, in that order.} {season == \"winter\": Today it's snow on the battlements, heavy enough to worry the roof leads.|Today it's the dormitory mattresses, which must be carried out and beaten in the sun.}\n> left: Lend a hand\n  vigor +small\n  health -tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {season == \"winter\": {~You shovel snow off the battlements until you can't feel your ears. The view is worth one of them.|You and the caretaker clear a whole parapet in silence. At the end you're handed a biscuit, which is how the caretaker says thank you.}|{~You beat forty mattresses. Two of them contained socks, one a love letter, and one a very surprised mouse.|You carry mattresses down six flights of moving stairs. One flight moves halfway through, with you and the mattress on it.}}\n> right: Make myself scarce\n  challenge deceive easy:\n    win:\n      mood +small\n      say: {~You remember an urgent appointment with a book and vanish round a corner. The caretaker's sigh follows you a long way.|You hide in a window seat until the list has moved on to someone else.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      vigor +tiny\n      say: {~The caretaker finds you behind a tapestry and hands you a shovel without comment.|You get as far as the stairs, which choose that moment to move you straight back to the caretaker.}\n\n@event break_castle_flight_field\ntitle: The Field to Yourself\nplan: castle\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: broom\ntags: home\nscene: An empty flight field in the holidays, hoops at either end, a single student on a broom high above the grass.\ntext: {With the flying lessons over for the break, the flight field is empty and nobody has locked the broom shed.|The broom shed is unlocked again. You suspect it's on purpose now.|The flight field in the break. You know which broom in the shed pulls left.} {season == \"winter\": The hoops are rimed with frost and the wind has teeth.|The air shimmers with heat, and the wind is lazy for once.} {~A crow is using the far hoop as a perch.|Somebody's lost scarf is snagged on the near hoop.|The grass is printed with a fox's tracks.}\n> left: Fly the hoops, fast\n  challenge feat easy:\n    win:\n      vigor +small\n      mood +small\n      say: {~You thread every hoop and pull up so late that the grass brushes your boots. Nobody saw. That's almost a shame.|You lap the field until the hoops blur. Landing, you find you're grinning like a first-year.}\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      mood -tiny\n      say: {~The wind cheats, as it always does, and you meet the far hoop with your shoulder.|You land in the long grass, which has caught a great many students before you. It does not catch gently.}\n> right: Practise slow, careful turns\n  vigor +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {~Left, right, left, at walking pace, for an hour. It is deeply dull and, annoyingly, it works.|You practise the turn that always throws you until it doesn't. Your arms ache all evening.}\n\n@event break_castle_bored_stairs\ntitle: The Bored Staircases\nplan: castle\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: tower\ntags: home\nscene: A grand castle stairwell with several staircases swinging slowly through the air, a lone student waiting on a landing.\ntext: {With nobody to carry up and down, the castle's staircases have grown restless.|The staircases are bored again, and they've noticed you.|The stairs know you're one of the few left. One swings over the moment you reach the landing.} {season == \"winter\": The stairwell is cold enough to see your breath.|Warm air drifts up the stairwell with the smell of cut grass.} {~A flight is drifting past with a lost shoe on its top step.|Two staircases are sulking against the wall.|One flight keeps nudging the landing, like a dog with a stick.}\n> left: Ride whichever one comes\n  chance 60:\n    guile +small\n    mood +small\n    say: {~It carries you to a corridor you've never seen, full of portraits who are thrilled to have a visitor.|It takes you the long way, through three towers, and sets you down exactly where you wanted to be.}\n  else:\n    health -tiny\n    mood -small\n    say: {~It drops you on a landing in the east wing and refuses to come back. You walk for an hour.|It swings out over the stairwell and stops. You wait there, with nothing to read, until it gets bored again.}\n> right: Take the long way, on foot\n  vigor +small\n  mood -small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {~You take the servants' stair, all two hundred steps of it. The staircases watch you go, disappointed.|You walk round by the bridges. It takes all morning, and your legs will remember it.}\n\n@event break_castle_night_rounds\ntitle: The Lone Prefect\nplan: castle\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: lantern\ntags: home\nscene: A single lantern bobbing along a bridge between dark towers at night, a student watching it from a window.\ntext: {Only one prefect has stayed for the break, and seven towers are a lot to patrol.|The lone prefect's lantern again, bobbing across a far bridge.|You know the lone prefect's rounds by now, to the quarter-hour.} {season == \"winter\": The corridors are cold enough to frost the portraits.|The night is warm, and every window in the castle stands open.} {~A staircase shifts somewhere below with a long stone sigh.|An owl has got into the east wing.|Somewhere a door bangs in a draught.}\n> left: Wander the towers after curfew\n  challenge deceive medium:\n    win:\n      guile +small\n      mood +small\n      say: {~You have the castle to yourself for an hour: moonlit bridges, sleeping portraits, the whole lake laid out below.|You slip past the lantern twice and watch the lake from a window nobody uses. It feels like owning the place.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      guile +tiny\n      say: {~The lantern swings round and finds you. The prefect writes your name in the demerit ledger with visible relief at having something to write.|You walk straight into the prefect on a dark landing. You both scream, and then you get a demerit.}\n> right: Walk the rounds with the prefect\n  charm +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {~The prefect is glad of the company and tells you which portraits snore. You go to bed very late.|You carry the lantern while the prefect checks doors. Somewhere in the fourth tower you become friends, a little.}\n\n# ---------------- Break: at home (plan: home) ----------------\n\n@event break_home_family_recipe\ntitle: The Family Recipe\nplan: home\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: cauldron\ntags: home\nscene: A warm family kitchen, an older relative guiding a student's hands over a steaming pot, jars and bowls everywhere.\ntext: {Someone at home has decided you're old enough for the family recipe, and ties an apron on you before breakfast.|The recipe lessons resume. You're trusted with the knife now, if not the salt.|You know the first half of the recipe by heart. The second half is still guarded like a treaty.} {season == \"winter\": It's the stew for the cold months, which takes all day.|It's the summer preserve, and every fruit in the house is involved.} {~Nobody measures anything.|A pinch turns out to mean a handful.|The pot is older than you are.}\n> left: Write it all down\n  alchemy +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {~You take notes like it's a potions practical. You're corrected on every line, and then on the corrections.|Your notes are thorough, exact and, you're told, missing the point.}\n> right: Learn it by hand, the old way\n  mood +small\n  alchemy +tiny\n  health -tiny\n  say: {~You burn a finger and get a nod. In this kitchen, that's a diploma.|You get it wrong in a way that turns out to be how your grandmother made it. Everyone goes quiet, and then they eat it all.}\n\n@event break_home_own_bed\ntitle: Your Own Bed\nplan: home\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: bed\ntags: home\nscene: A narrow bed under a cottage window in early light, a student buried in blankets while the house bustles below.\ntext: {Your own bed, after a term in the dormitory. You wake at the academy's hour anyway.|The house wakes at dawn. It always has; you'd forgotten.|You know which floorboard creaks on the way down, and that everyone else learned it first.} {season == \"winter\": There's frost on the window and the blankets weigh a ton.|Sunlight is coming through the curtains, and someone is already whistling in the yard.} Downstairs, {~the kettle is going.|someone is calling your name.|the door bangs for the third time.}\n> left: Sleep until noon\n  health +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~You sleep like a stone. At lunch you're called \"the scholar\" with a new and pointed emphasis.|You wake at noon, rested and ashamed. The whole house has done a morning's work, and mentions it.}\n> right: Get up with everyone\n  vigor +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {season == \"winter\": {~You break the ice on the water butt and carry wood in before breakfast. Your hands sting all morning.|You're out in the dark with a lantern before the cockerel. The cockerel resents the competition.}|{~You're in the yard by sunrise, hauling and fetching. Breakfast, when it comes, is the best of the year.|You work the cool hours with everyone else and see why they swear by them.}}\n\n@event break_home_mending_chair\ntitle: The Mending Chair\nplan: home\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: wand\ntags: home\nscene: A chair by a cottage door piled with broken household things, a student in academy robes eyeing them with a wand.\ntext: {Word goes round the house that the scholar can fix things.|The things that need fixing have been waiting for you on a chair by the door.|The mending chair fills up between breaks, like a well.} {season == \"winter\": Today it's a stove door that won't stay shut.|Today it's a window that sticks in the heat.} {~Also a clock that strikes thirteen.|Also a doll with a missing eye.|Also a teapot nobody admits to breaking.}\n> left: Mend it with a spell\n  challenge spell easy:\n    win:\n      arcana +small\n      mood +small\n      say: {~It mends with a click and a little puff of light. The family applauds, then brings out three more things.|You fix it so well that it works better than new. Now everyone wants their things to work better than new.}\n    lose:\n      mana -small\n      mood -small\n      say: {~It's mended, technically. It also hums now, and nobody can make it stop.|The spell fixes it and then, for good measure, everything else on the chair. Most of it did not need fixing that way.}\n> right: Mend it by hand\n  vigor +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~Glue, string, a borrowed hammer and the whole morning. It holds. You don't trust it, but it holds.|You fix it the way your family always has: slowly, with a lot of opinions from the doorway.}\n\n@event break_home_the_trunk\ntitle: The Trunk in the Corner\nplan: home\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: house\ntags: home\nscene: An open travelling trunk in the corner of a small bedroom, overflowing with jars, socks and parcels in cloth.\ntext: {Your trunk stands open in the corner, and the family keeps putting things in it.|The trunk is open again, and it's filling up again.|The trunk is a family project now.} {season == \"winter\": Woollen socks, a jar of something pickled, a cake wrapped in three cloths.|Dried fruit, a straw hat nobody asked for, a cake wrapped in three cloths.} {final_year: Someone says \"for next term\", then stops.|{~The lid won't close.|Someone sits on the lid.|A note is tucked into a sock, not to be read till later.}}\n> left: Take everything\n  health +small\n  gold -tiny\n  say: {~The bargeman charges you double for the weight, but you eat well for a month.|You haul it all back across the lake. Half the corridor smells of pickles until spring.}\n> right: Sneak half of it back\n  guile +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~You smuggle the jars back into the pantry at night. In the morning they're in the trunk again, wrapped tighter.|You leave the hat on a hook by the door. You feel worse about it than you expected.}\n\n# ---------------- Break: working full-time (plan: work) ----------------\n\n@event break_work_stocktake\ntitle: Stocktaking\nplan: work\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: scales\ntags: job\nscene: A student with a ledger and pencil counting rows of stock in a quiet workplace, the owner reading over their shoulder.\ntext: {The quiet days of the break are, says the owner, the time to count everything.|Stocktaking again. You have opinions about how to count things now.|Stocktaking. You count faster than the owner, and slower than you'd like.} {member(tavern): At the Gilded Newt that means every tankard, keg and spoon.|{member(apothecary_clerk): At Pell & Daughters that means a thousand drawers.|{member(broom_courier): At the loft that means every parcel, pigeonhole and broom.|{member(library_assistant): In the library that means books that won't hold still to be counted.|That means everything, twice.}}}} The tally comes out one short.\n> left: Track down the missing one\n  challenge research using guile easy:\n    win:\n      guile +small\n      gold +tiny\n      say: {~You find it wedged behind the counter, where it has been since the last stocktake. The owner tips you for your thoroughness.|You find the mistake in the ledger itself: someone counted a box as a crate. The owner pays you a little extra and says nothing else.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      health -tiny\n      say: {~You search until dusk, on your knees in the dust. It stays missing, and the owner looks at you as if you might have eaten it.|You count everything again and get a different number. Then a third.}\n> right: Write it off and move on\n  mood +small\n  gold -tiny\n  say: {~\"Breakage,\" you write. The owner docks a coin from your pay, on principle.|You write it off, and the owner sighs and takes it from your wages.}\n\n@event break_work_complaint\ntitle: The Complaint\nplan: work\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: hand\ntags: job\nscene: An irate customer leaning over a counter, finger raised, while a young worker stands very still behind it.\ntext: {A customer has come back with a complaint and a voice built for it.|Another complaint, another raised voice. You've learned to breathe out first.|This customer has been saving the complaint all week. You can tell.} {member(tavern): The cider was too warm, or too cold, or too cider.|{member(apothecary_clerk): The tonic did nothing, which is worse, they say, than doing something.|{member(broom_courier): The parcel arrived wet, late and somehow smug.|{member(library_assistant): The book they want is out, and they want it in.|The complaint has several parts, all of them loud.}}}} The owner is out.\n> left: Talk them down\n  challenge negotiate medium:\n    win:\n      charm +small\n      gold +tiny\n      say: {~You agree with everything until there's nothing left to agree with. They leave calm, and they leave a tip.|You listen, nod and offer them a chair. By the end they're telling you about their sister.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      gold -tiny\n      say: {~It gets louder. You give them a coin of your own just to make it stop.|They demand the owner, then you, then the owner again, and you pay for the trouble out of your own pocket.}\n> right: Give them what they want\n  gold -tiny\n  mood +tiny\n  say: {~You hand over a refund out of your wages. They leave. The quiet afterwards is worth a coin.|A free one, on you. They take it without thanks, and the next customer is lovely.}\n\n@event break_work_half_hour\ntitle: Half an Hour Off\nplan: work\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: cup\ntags: job\nscene: A tired student in a work apron standing in a back doorway, looking out at the weather with a cup in hand.\ntext: {Half an hour off in the middle of a long holiday shift.|Your half hour again. You know exactly how far it goes now.|Half an hour. You spend the first minute deciding.} {season == \"winter\": Outside, the snow is coming down sideways.|Outside, the sun is bright enough to hurt.} {~The back room has one chair and one draught.|A colleague has left half a pie, possibly for sharing.|A clock somewhere strikes the wrong hour.}\n> left: Walk out into the weather\n  mood +small\n  health -tiny\n  gold -tiny\n  say: {season == \"winter\": {~You buy roast chestnuts and eat them walking, snow in your collar. You come back soaked and much happier.|You walk to the lake's edge and back. Your nose will not forgive you.}|{~You buy a cold drink and sit in the sun until your neck burns. Worth it.|You walk to the water and stand there. Half an hour is longer outside.}}\n> right: Nap in the back room\n  health +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~You sleep sitting up and wake with the shape of a crate on your cheek.|You doze off and dream of work, which seems unfair. The bell wakes you mid-shift in the dream.}\n\n@event break_work_short_wages\ntitle: A Coin Short\nplan: work\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: coin\ntags: job money\nscene: A student counting a small pile of coins on a counter for the second time, frowning, an employer pretending not to watch.\ntext: {Wages, counted out into your hand. You count them again.|Wage day. You count your coins at the counter, in front of everyone, on principle.|Wage day. The owner counts slowly, and so do you.} {~It's short by one.|It's short, and there's a button where a coin should be.|It's short by just enough to notice.} {member(tavern): The landlady remembers every tab to the penny, and has somehow forgotten this one.|{member(apothecary_clerk): Old Pell counted it himself, drop by drop.|{member(broom_courier): The dispatch ledger says otherwise, in smudged ink.|{member(library_assistant): The pay envelope says otherwise, in very small writing.|Someone's sums say otherwise.}}}}\n> left: Point it out\n  challenge negotiate easy:\n    win:\n      gold +small\n      charm +tiny\n      say: {~You show your sums. The owner checks them, coughs, and pays you the difference with a little extra for the embarrassment.|You point it out politely and firmly. The missing coin appears, with an apology and a pastry.}\n    lose:\n      gold +tiny\n      mood -small\n      say: {~The owner recounts, gets the same wrong answer, and looks hurt that you asked.|You're told it's for the breakage. You don't remember any breakage. Nobody else does either.}\n> right: Let it go this once\n  gold +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  charm +tiny\n  say: {~You pocket it without a word. The owner sees you notice, and you're given the easy shift next week.|One coin. You decide you can afford to be the bigger person, just about.}\n\n# ---------------- Regulars (one-time milestones at public places) ----------------\n\n@event refectory_cooks_taster\ntitle: The Cook's Taster\nplace: refectory\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\nweight: 30\nwhen: visits_to(refectory) >= 6\nart: meal\ntags: life\nscene: A refectory cook holding out a small spoon over the counter to a student, a new pot steaming behind her.\ntext: The cook stops you before you reach the bench and holds out a spoon. \"You're in here more than the cats,\" she says. \"So you can taste the new pot first. Regulars do.\" Behind her a dozen steaming pots wait their turn, and the other students in the queue look on with naked envy.\n> left: Give an honest verdict\n  health +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You say it wants salt and less of the grey thing. The cook frowns, tastes, and nods. From then on your bowl comes from the bottom of the pot, where the good bits are.\n> right: Praise it to the rafters\n  mood +small\n  say: You declare it the best soup in the kingdom. The cook doesn't believe a word of it, and gives you a second spoonful anyway.\n\n@event library_footnote_bag\ntitle: Footnote's Approval\nplace: library\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\nweight: 30\nwhen: visits_to(library) >= 6\nart: cat\ntags: life\nscene: A huge old library cat curled asleep on a student's satchel under a reading desk, a student standing over it, uncertain.\ntext: You come back from the shelves to find Footnote asleep on your bag, curled tight, one paw over the strap. Footnote sleeps on the reference desk and nowhere else; everybody knows that. A passing librarian stops, looks from the cat to you, and nods, as if something official has happened.\n> left: Wait for him to wake\n  mood +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: You read standing up until the lamps are lit. Footnote wakes, stretches, and walks off without a glance. Your bag smells of warm cat for a week, and you find you don't mind.\n> right: Ease the bag out, inch by inch\n  guile +small\n  say: You slide it free a fraction at a time. Footnote opens one eye, judges you, and allows it. The librarian says she's never seen him allow anything.\n\n@event mabels_your_usual\ntitle: Your Usual\nplace: mabels\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\nweight: 30\nwhen: visits_to(mabels) >= 6\nart: cup\ntags: life\nscene: A small tea shop counter with a cup already poured and a slice of cake on a plate, the shopkeeper smiling at a student in the doorway.\ntext: You haven't even reached the counter. Mabel already has your cup out, the right tea steeping and the cake you always end up choosing on a plate. \"Your usual,\" she says, and it isn't a question. On the slate behind the till, among the regulars, there's a new line in chalk.\n> left: Have the usual\n  mood +small\n  gold -tiny\n  say: It's exactly right, as it always is. Mabel watches you take the first sip and turns back to the shelves, satisfied.\n> right: Ask for something new\n  charm +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: Mabel's eyebrows rise. Then she smiles like someone handed a puzzle and reaches for a tin from the very top shelf. It's odd and smoky, and you don't like it, and you're glad you asked.\n\n@event bakery_bun_set_aside\ntitle: The Bun Set Aside\nplace: bakery\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\nweight: 30\nwhen: visits_to(bakery) >= 6\nart: meal\ntags: life\nscene: A baker's son handing a paper-wrapped bun across a flour-dusted counter, a letter written on the paper in flour.\ntext: The baker's son reaches under the counter before you've said a word and brings out a bun wrapped in paper, with your initial on it in flour. \"Saved from the first batch,\" he says. \"Regulars get the corner ones.\" Behind him, by the ovens, the oldest baker nods as if she arranged it all herself.\n> left: Insist on paying for it\n  gold -tiny\n  charm +small\n  say: He refuses twice and accepts the third time. The coin goes in a jar by the till marked, in the oldest baker's hand, \"for the post\".\n> right: Thank him and eat it warm\n  mood +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: It's still warm and far too big, and you eat it on the step in four bites and burn your mouth. You'd do it again tomorrow.\n\n@event grimsbys_right_book\ntitle: The Right Book, First Time\nplace: grimsbys_books\nclass: milestone\ntone: light\nweight: 30\nwhen: visits_to(grimsbys_books) >= 6\nart: book\ntags: life\nscene: A cramped bookshop of leaning shelves, an elderly bookseller holding out a book to a student who has only just stepped inside.\ntext: You step into Grimsby's and open your mouth, and Mr Grimsby is already holding out a book. It is exactly the one you came for. He looks as astonished as you are. \"Must be getting to know you,\" he says, and then, worried, \"Don't tell anyone.\"\n> left: Buy it before it changes\n  gold -tiny\n  mood +small\n  say: You pay at once, in case it turns into a book on beekeeping. It doesn't. You carry it all the way back to the castle, checking.\n> right: Ask what else he'd pick for me\n  charm +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: Restored to form, Mr Grimsby brings you a history of eels and a book of riddles. You buy the riddles. They're very good, and no help at all.\n\n@event courtyard_in_the_know\ntitle: In the Know\nplace: courtyard\nclass: milestone\ntone: light\nweight: 30\nwhen: visits_to(courtyard) >= 6\nart: group\ntags: social\nscene: A knot of students gathered on the rim of a courtyard fountain, leaning in, one waving a newcomer over.\ntext: The knot of students by the fountain goes quiet as you pass, and then one of them waves you over. \"You're out here every day,\" says a second-year from Duskgate. \"So. What have you heard?\" It seems you've been counted among the courtyard's regulars, and regulars are expected to bring news.\n> left: Share something harmless\n  charm +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You offer up a rumour about the refectory pudding. It's received with grave interest and is all over the castle by supper, much improved.\n> right: Say I've heard nothing\n  guile +small\n  say: They take your silence as proof that you know everything. By the end of the week, people are asking you for the news.\n"},{"path":"content/events/pools/plans_thesis.grim","text":"# Baseline pools (phase 3, step 1b): break plans, thesis and internship hosts.\n# Repeatable routine events; see docs/authoring/pools.md.\n\n# ---------------- Break: at the castle (plan: castle) ----------------\n\n@event break_castle_one_fire\ntitle: The One Lit Fire\nplan: castle\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: flame\ntags: home\nscene: A cavernous common room in the holidays, a handful of students in blankets crowded around {season == \"winter\": one roaring hearth|one open window}.\ntext: {With the houses empty, the stewards light one common room and let the rest go dark.|Someone has brought a kettle, and now there's a rota for it.|The break common room. You know everyone's blanket by now.} {season == \"winter\": The best armchair is right by the fire.|The best armchair is by the only breeze.} {~A first-year from Stillwater is asleep in it.|Footnote is in it. Footnote moves for no one.}\n> left: Talk my way into the armchair\n  challenge deceive easy:\n    win:\n      mood +small\n      guile +tiny\n      say: {~You mention that the kitchens are giving out free toffee. The chair empties. You feel only slightly bad.|You announce, with great authority, that the armchair is due to be re-stuffed. It is, now, by you.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      say: {~Nobody moves. Someone says, \"Nice try,\" without looking up from their book.|The whole room watches you fail to dislodge the armchair's occupant. It's the most entertainment anyone has had all break.}\n> right: Take the draughty corner\n  lore +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {season == \"winter\": {~The corner is cold enough to see your breath, but you read three chapters.|You read in gloves. Turning pages becomes a skill.}|{~It smells of old ink and warm dust. You read three chapters.|A wasp shares your corner. You reach an understanding.}}\n\n@event break_castle_kitchen_rota\ntitle: The Holiday Kitchen\nplan: castle\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: meal\ntags: home\nscene: A steaming castle kitchen with half its ovens cold, the cook handing aprons to two reluctant students.\ntext: {Half the kitchen staff have gone home for the break, and the cook has started drafting whoever wanders past.|The cook spots you in the corridor and holds up an apron.|The cook holds out an apron without a word. It has your name stitched on it now.} {season == \"winter\": There are puddings to steam for the few who stayed, and a goose that refuses to fit the oven.|The orchard has dropped everything at once, and every pan in the kitchen is full of jam.}\n> left: Roll up your sleeves\n  alchemy +small\n  health -tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {season == \"winter\": {~You stir puddings till your arm aches. \"Just like potion work,\" says the cook. It isn't.|You baste the goose. The goose wins, narrowly.}|{~A morning of jam teaches you more about setting points than a term of alchemy.|You label forty jars. The cook re-spells \"gooseberry\" on all of them.}}\n> right: Discover urgent business elsewhere\n  guile +tiny\n  mood +small\n  gold -tiny\n  say: {~You escape to the village and buy a pie you could have had for free. The cook sees you come back with the crumbs.|You hide in the library until lunch. Lunch, it turns out, is served by the cook, who gives you the smallest portion with a meaningful look.}\n\n@event break_castle_empty_library\ntitle: The Library on Holiday\nplan: castle\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: book\ntags: home study\nscene: A great library with no one at the desks, a cat asleep on the reference desk and a single student choosing a book.\ntext: {The librarians have gone home, and the library has been left to you and the cat.|Footnote has moved from the reference desk to your chair, and opens one eye.|Your break-morning library. One window seat is yours now.} {season == \"winter\": Frost has drawn ferns on the tall windows.|Dust turns slowly in the long summer light.} {~Every desk is free.|Someone has left half an essay on the theory of hiccups.|The reshelving cart is full, and nobody is coming for it.}\n> left: Read whatever I like\n  mood +small\n  lore +tiny\n  health -tiny\n  say: {~You read a book about the history of spoons and forget to eat lunch. It was a very good book about spoons.|You read until the light goes, then a little after. Your eyes will mention it tomorrow.}\n> right: Get ahead on next term\n  lore +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {final_year: You make notes for a term you'll only half get to use. It still feels like the right thing to do.|{~You make a neat set of notes for next term. Future you will be grateful. Present you is bored stiff.|You read ahead three chapters and find the professor's favourite mistake waiting in the fourth.}}\n\n@event break_castle_post_day\ntitle: Post for the Stay-Behinds\nplan: castle\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: letter\ntags: home\nscene: A porter sorting a thin pile of letters on a long empty table, a few students waiting hopefully.\ntext: {Post day. The porter lays the letters out on one table in the Great Hall.|The porter now sorts the stay-behinds' letters into a pile of their own.|The porter nods to you. He keeps your letters on top now.} {season == \"winter\": {~Most of it is Midwinter cards for people who went home.|One parcel is exactly the shape of a scarf.}|{~Most of it is postcards from people's travels.|A postcard says \"Wish you were here\", and nobody knows whose it is.}}\n> left: Write a long letter home\n  mood +small\n  gold -tiny\n  lore -tiny\n  say: {final_year: You write that after this year you'll be home for good, and then look at the sentence for a long time.|{~You write four pages and pay for the heavy stamp. It's the closest thing to a conversation you've had all week.|You describe the empty castle so well that you feel lonelier writing it than living it.}}\n> right: Help the porter sort it all\n  charm +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~The porter tells you who writes to whom, in strict confidence. You know far too much about the second-years now.|You sort the post into houses. There's nothing for you this time. The porter gives you a biscuit, which is nearly as good.}\n\n# ---------------- Break: at home (plan: home) ----------------\n\n@event break_home_kitchen_table\ntitle: Homework at the Kitchen Table\nplan: home\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: house\ntags: home study\nscene: A family kitchen in the morning, a student's books spread among the breakfast plates, someone reaching across them for the butter.\ntext: {You spread your books across the kitchen table, where you did your sums as a child.|Your books have been moved for the bread, and one is being used as a trivet.|The family works around your books now. Mostly.} {season == \"winter\": The stove is the only warm place, so everyone is in the kitchen.|The door is open, and everyone passing asks what the book is about.} {~A small cousin has drawn a dragon in your margin.|The kettle boils every quarter-hour, on principle.}\n> left: Study through the noise\n  lore +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {~You read the same paragraph nine times and learn it perfectly, along with the price of eggs.|You get through a chapter. Your notes now have a jam stain, a crumb and the shopping list on them.}\n> right: Give up and help with lunch\n  mood +small\n  gold -tiny\n  say: {~You're sent to the shop for the one thing everyone forgot, and you buy sweets with the change.|You peel, chop and get told about everyone in the village. Your book stays shut, and it's the best morning of the break.}\n\n@event break_home_the_box\ntitle: The Box Under the Bed\nplan: home\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: key\ntags: home\nscene: A narrow childhood bedroom with a bed far too short for its occupant, an old box of keepsakes pulled out onto the floor.\ntext: {Your old room is smaller than you remember. Under the bed is the box where you kept everything that mattered when you were nine.|Someone has dusted the box since last time and pretended not to look inside.|You've almost reached the bottom of the box.} {~This time it's a wand you made from a stick and a lot of string.|This time it's the letter you wrote to the academy at age ten, asking to be let in early.|This time it's a drawing of seven towers, each with a little flag.}\n> left: Pack it in your trunk\n  mood +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {~It comes back to Lumenhold with you. The trunk is heavier, and so, somehow, are you.|You pack it between your robes. Every time you open the trunk this term, it will be the first thing you see.}\n> right: Give it to the youngest cousin\n  charm +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~The youngest cousin takes it with enormous seriousness and announces that they're going to be a wizard too. You feel very old.|The cousin promises to keep it safe. By supper it has been traded to a neighbour's child for a frog.}\n\n@event break_home_family_debate\ntitle: The Family Debate\nplan: home\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: meal\ntags: home\nscene: A breakfast table mid-argument, several relatives pointing at a student as if they were the deciding vote.\ntext: {At breakfast the family is arguing, and someone says, \"Ask the scholar.\"|The argument has started without you. Everyone turns as you come down the stairs.|\"The scholar is up,\" someone announces. They've saved today's argument for you.} {season == \"winter\": {~Is it colder than the year of the big snow?|Can you freeze a pond by thinking at it?}|{~Do swallows sleep at the bottom of rivers?|Will it rain on the fair, and can you stop it?}}\n> left: Settle it with what I've learned\n  chance 60:\n    lore +small\n    mood +small\n    say: {~You explain it so clearly that the argument ends. There is a short, respectful silence, and then a new argument starts.|You settle it. An uncle demands to see it in writing. You write it on a napkin, and he keeps the napkin.}\n  else:\n    lore +tiny\n    mood -small\n    say: {~You explain it perfectly, and they all decide the academy has taught you nonsense.|Halfway through you realise you're wrong. So does everyone else.}\n> right: Refuse to take sides\n  charm +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~You butter your toast with great care and say, \"Hm.\" It does not stop anyone, but it does save you.|You change the subject to the neighbour's fence. It works, but now there are two arguments.}\n\n@event break_home_village_children\ntitle: The Wizard Is Home\nplan: home\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: sparkle\ntags: home\nscene: A gaggle of village children at a cottage gate in the morning, staring hopefully at a student in academy robes.\ntext: {Word has spread that you're home from the wizard school. The village children are waiting at the gate.|The children are back at the gate, with a younger sibling to show you off to.|The gate committee is in session. They've drawn up a list.} {season == \"winter\": They want more snow.|They want it to stop being so hot.} {~The smallest has a jar, in case you make something worth keeping.|One asks if you've ever met a dragon.|The tallest has a stick that might be a wand.}\n> left: Show them some sparks\n  mana -small\n  mood +small\n  arcana +tiny\n  say: {~You make a little burst of light over the gate. The children scream with delight and demand it again, and again.|You make a spark dance across the gatepost. The smallest one tries to catch it in cupped hands, or thinks they do, which is the same thing.}\n> right: Teach them something real\n  charm +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~You explain how the light gets into a spell. They listen politely and then ask for sparks.|You show them how to sit still and listen to a candle. One of them really hears it. You can tell.}\n\n# ---------------- Break: working full-time (plan: work) ----------------\n\n@event break_work_holiday_rush\ntitle: The Holiday Rush\nplan: work\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: coin\ntags: job money\nscene: A holiday rush at a busy workplace, a student in the thick of it wearing the fixed smile of someone on their fourth hour of the same question.\ntext: {The holidays bring the crowds.|This time you've learned to eat breakfast before the rush, not during it.|You can feel the rush coming an hour before it arrives.} {member(tavern): The Gilded Newt is packed to the beams.|{member(apothecary_clerk): The queue at Pell & Daughters is out of the door.|{member(broom_courier): The loft's shelves are stacked to the rafters with parcels.|{member(library_assistant): The library is full of last-minute readers.|Everyone wants something, and now.}}}} {season == \"winter\": {~Everyone wants it before Midwinter.|Wet boots, short tempers.}|{~Everyone is sunburnt.|Travellers keep asking the way.}}\n> left: Smile through it\n  charm +small\n  gold +tiny\n  mood -small\n  say: {~You smile until your face aches. Somebody tips you a coin and a peppermint.|You smile through the fourth complaint about the weather, as if it were your fault. Somehow the tips are better than ever.}\n> right: Take the heavy jobs instead\n  vigor +small\n  health -tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~You haul the heaviest loads all morning and talk to no one. Your arms complain, and nobody else does.|You take the dull, heavy work nobody wants. It's tiring and blissfully quiet.}\n\n@event break_work_opening_up\ntitle: Opening Up\nplan: work\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: key\ntags: job\nscene: A student unlocking a shop door in the half-light of early morning, breath steaming, the street empty behind them.\ntext: {In the holidays you're trusted with the keys. You open up at dawn, alone.|You've learned to lift the door as you turn the key.|You could open up in your sleep, and once you nearly did.} {season == \"winter\": It's still dark, and the stove has gone out.|The sun is up and the street smells of warm stone.} {~There's a note under the door from someone who wanted to come in at midnight.|A cat waits on the step as if it has an appointment.|The place is yours for half an hour.}\n> left: Do it all by the book\n  gold +small\n  mood -tiny\n  health -tiny\n  say: {~Swept, stocked and opened to the minute. The owner notices and slips you a little extra.|Everything is ready when the first customer arrives. Nobody notices but you. Still, the coin is good.}\n> right: Steal a quiet half hour\n  chance 70:\n    mood +small\n    say: {~You drink tea by the window and watch the street wake up. It's the best part of the job.|You read a chapter with your feet up. Nobody ever knows.}\n  else:\n    mood -tiny\n    gold -small\n    say: {~The owner arrives early and finds you with your feet up. The half hour comes out of your wages.|The first customer arrives early and finds the door locked. They leave a note. So does the owner.}\n\n@event break_work_regular\ntitle: The Holiday Regular\nplan: work\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: cup\ntags: job\nscene: An elderly customer settling in for a long chat at a counter, a student behind it glancing at the queue.\ntext: {Every holiday has its regular. This one comes every morning and wants to talk.|The regular is back. They wave to you as if you were old friends.|The regular has started calling you by your first name.} {~Today it's about their late husband's beard.|Today it's about a dragon they swear they saw as a girl.|Today it's about the year the lake froze, which may or may not have happened.} The queue behind them is getting longer.\n> left: Let them finish the story\n  charm +small\n  gold -tiny\n  say: {~You hear the whole story. The queue thins out to somewhere else. The regular leaves happy, and so, oddly, are you.|The story has an ending you didn't expect, and you'll think about it all day. The customers you lost won't.}\n> right: Hurry them gently along\n  gold +tiny\n  mood -small\n  say: {~You serve the queue and the regular leaves, mid-sentence, with a little wave. It shouldn't bother you as much as it does.|The queue is grateful. The regular says, \"Another time, then,\" and you both know they mean it.}\n\n@event break_work_new_hire\ntitle: The Holiday Hire\nplan: work\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: hand\ntags: job\nscene: A nervous new worker holding a tray at the wrong angle, a student leaning over to correct them.\ntext: {The owner has taken on a holiday hire and put them next to you.|Another holiday hire, another nervous face. You're the old hand now.|This break's new hire looks at you the way you once looked at everyone.} {~They've already dropped something.|They ask whether the job is always like this. You don't answer.|They're very keen and very fast, and very nearly right.}\n> left: Show them the ropes\n  charm +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {~By noon they know where everything is and that the owner is all bark. You've lost a morning and gained a friend.|You teach them the trick with the till. They teach you a song. You both get told off for the song.}\n> right: Let them learn the hard way\n  mood +tiny\n  gold -tiny\n  say: {~They learn. Unfortunately, they learn by breaking a jar that the owner puts on your account.|You let them flounder. It takes them all day to find the stockroom, and you end up covering their shift anyway.}\n\n# ---------------- Break: internship (plan: internship) ----------------\n\n@event break_internship_lodgings\ntitle: Breakfast at the Lodgings\nplan: internship\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: bed\ntags: home\nscene: A borrowed bed at dawn, boots by the door, a bowl of breakfast waiting and a notebook open on the blanket.\ntext: {{member(intern_expedition): Your lodging for the placement is a tent that flaps all night.|{member(intern_field_station): Your lodging is a bunk in the station cabin, under a shelf of jars.|Your lodging is one small room and a breakfast you pay for.}}|By now you can make any lodging home in an evening, a book on the sill and your boots by the door.|You've slept in enough borrowed beds to know breakfast matters more than the mattress.} {season == \"winter\": {~Your breath steams.|The water jug has frozen.}|{~It's warm before sunrise.|A bird is shouting outside.}} {~Breakfast is porridge.|Breakfast is porridge with opinions.|Breakfast is unnamed.}\n> left: Eat a proper breakfast\n  mood +small\n  gold -tiny\n  say: {~You eat slowly and listen to everyone's gossip. You arrive late enough to be noticed.|Fed, warm and five minutes late. Your supervisor looks at the clock and says nothing at all.}\n> right: Skip it and go in early\n  lore +small\n  health -tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~You're the first one in. You get through a pile of work before anyone else arrives, and your stomach growls through every meeting after.|You read the day's notes on the way. By lunchtime you're faint and well informed.}\n\n@event break_internship_briefing\ntitle: The Morning Briefing\nplan: internship\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: scroll\ntags: study\nscene: A supervisor reading a list of tasks aloud to a row of interns, one of whom has stopped writing.\ntext: {Your supervisor reads the day's tasks aloud, once, very fast.|You bring two pens now, and a spare for the intern beside you.|You've learned to read the briefing from the way your supervisor holds the list.} {~One task makes no sense at all.|One task seems to mean the opposite of what it says.|The last task is mumbled into a cup of tea.} {final_year: It's your last placement, and you want it to count.|The others all write as if they understood.}\n> left: Ask what it means\n  charm +tiny\n  lore +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {~Your supervisor explains it again, slowly, while the other interns smirk. Two of them come to you at lunch to ask what it meant.|\"Good question,\" says your supervisor, in a tone that suggests it wasn't. You get the task right, anyway.}\n> right: Guess and get on with it\n  chance 55:\n    lore +small\n    mood +tiny\n    say: {~You guess right. Your supervisor nods as if they never doubted you.|It turns out the odd task was \"take the rest of the morning off\". Lucky.}\n  else:\n    mood -small\n    say: {~You guess wrong and spend the morning carefully doing the opposite of what was wanted.|You did the right task to the wrong thing. The wrong thing will never be the same again.}\n\n@event break_internship_other_interns\ntitle: The Other Interns\nplan: internship\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: group\ntags: study\nscene: A small group of interns eating lunch together, one sitting slightly apart with a notebook.\ntext: {There are other interns here, and they've already formed a lunch table.|You've learned to find the lunch table on the first day, and which seat isn't anyone's.|By now you can tell within a morning which intern is the one to sit beside.} {~One has a cousin on the Conclave and mentions it hourly.|One is better than all of you, and very nice about it.|One has been here before and knows where the biscuits are.} {season == \"winter\": Lunch is eaten in coats.|Lunch is eaten in the sun.}\n> left: Join them for lunch\n  mood +small\n  charm +tiny\n  gold -tiny\n  say: {~You chip in for the shared food and find out which of the supervisors are kind. Useful, and fun.|You talk shop until your lunch is cold. It turns out everyone else is as lost as you are, which helps.}\n> right: Eat alone with your notes\n  lore +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {~You finish the reading for the afternoon. You hear laughter from the table, and you don't look up.|You get ahead of everyone. Nobody saves you a biscuit.}\n\n@event break_internship_stipend\ntitle: Stipend Day\nplan: internship\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: pouch\ntags: money\nscene: A small envelope of coins on a desk, a student looking at it with the expression of someone doing sums.\ntext: {Stipend day. A small envelope, your name spelled almost right.|You've learned to budget the envelope before you open it.|Stipend day. You know what it's for before it arrives, and it still feels like a gift.} {member(intern_expedition) or member(intern_field_station): {~The supply cart has come, with a pedlar riding on top.|A pedlar has walked a very long way to sell you socks.}|{season == \"winter\": {~There's a warm scarf in a shop window you pass.|A bookshop nearby has a sale on almanacs.}|{~A cart is selling lemon ices on the corner.|A bookshop nearby has put its sale out in the sun.}}}\n> left: Treat yourself\n  mood +small\n  gold -small\n  say: {member(intern_expedition) or member(intern_field_station): {~You buy the pedlar's thickest socks. Your feet have never been so grateful.|You buy a jar of honey from the pedlar and eat it with a spoon, in triumph.}|{season == \"winter\": {~You buy it. It's quite unnecessary, and the warmest you've felt all week.|You spend the lot before lunch and regret none of it.}|{~You spend it before the corner and don't regret a crown.|You take your purchase to a bench in the sun and stay until your nose burns.}}}\n> right: Buy the book your supervisor cited\n  lore +small\n  gold -small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~It's the dullest book you've ever paid for, and the most useful. You underline half of it the first night.|You send for it and read it, and the next time your supervisor mentions it you nod in the right places.}\n\n# ---------------- Thesis (track: thesis) ----------------\n\n@event thesis_margin_notes\ntitle: Notes in the Margin\ntrack: thesis\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: scroll\ntags: study\nscene: A thesis draft returned with a professor's spiky handwriting crawling up every margin.\ntext: {Professor Thorne returns your chapter. In the margin of page one he has written \"No.\"|Another chapter back. The margins say \"Why?\", \"Source?\" and, once, \"Hm.\"|Thorne's notes are getting shorter. You can't decide whether that's good.} {~Page six has a single word underlined three times.|One note just says \"See me.\"|There's a tick on page nine. You stare at it for a while.} {progress(thesis) >= 60: The argument is nearly there, and he knows it.|There's a long way to go.}\n> left: Rewrite to his notes\n  progress thesis +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {~You rewrite every page he marked. The chapter is shorter and better, and you hate that he was right.|You take every note. When he reads it again he crosses out only one thing, and you could cry.}\n> right: Defend my version\n  challenge negotiate using lore medium:\n    win:\n      progress thesis +small\n      rel thorne +tiny\n      say: {~You make your case at his office door. He listens, says \"Hm,\" and crosses out his own note.|You argue for your paragraph and he lets you keep it. \"Then make it say so,\" he says, which is almost praise.}\n    lose:\n      progress thesis -tiny\n      mood -small\n      say: {~You argue. Thorne waits until you've finished, then points at the sentence that proves him right.|He hears you out, then writes a new note in the margin. It says \"No.\" again, in smaller letters.}\n\n@event thesis_carrel_neighbour\ntitle: The Next Carrel\ntrack: thesis\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: candle\ntags: study\nscene: Two thesis carrels side by side, the partition between them covered in pinned notes and a shared candle.\ntext: {Your carrel neighbour, a final-year from Rootward, reads aloud under their breath.|Your carrel neighbour now reads your chapter titles aloud too.|You and your carrel neighbour now speak a language of sighs.} {~They're stuck on chapter three.|They've found a source that disagrees with you both.|They pass you half a sandwich over the partition.} {season == \"winter\": The thesis lab is cold enough for gloves.|Someone's notes are escaping through the window.}\n> left: Swap drafts for an hour\n  chance 70:\n    progress thesis +small\n    charm +tiny\n    mood -tiny\n    say: {~They find the hole in your argument in ten minutes. You find three in theirs. Everyone feels better and worse.|They read your chapter and say, \"Oh, I see.\" You're not sure you did, until now.}\n  else:\n    mood -small\n    say: {~They love your chapter, which helps nobody. You spend the hour fixing their commas.|They ask so many questions that you start doubting your title.}\n> right: Hunt a source in the stacks\n  lore +small\n  progress thesis +tiny\n  health -tiny\n  say: {~You find it on the bottom shelf, under a dictionary and a sleeping cat. It says exactly what you hoped, in a footnote.|Three hours of climbing ladders for one paragraph. It's a very good paragraph.}\n\n@event thesis_is_it_good\ntitle: Is It Any Good?\ntrack: thesis\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: hourglass\ntags: study\nscene: A student at a carrel in early light, holding a thick draft at arm's length as if it might bite.\ntext: {Halfway through rereading your thesis, you can't tell if it's brilliant or obvious.|The doubt has learned your timetable. It comes the morning after good work.|You know this doubt now. It reaches your carrel first.} {progress(thesis) >= 60: Most of it is written, which almost makes it worse.|So much of it is still unwritten.} {final_year and season == \"spring\": After this term there are no more terms.|{~First-years laugh in the courtyard below.|Last night's candle is a puddle.}}\n> left: Push on regardless\n  progress thesis +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {~You write a page you don't believe in. By lunch you believe in about half of it, which is a start.|You keep going. The doubt keeps you company, and gets bored by evening.}\n> right: Walk by the lake instead\n  mood +small\n  health +tiny\n  progress thesis -tiny\n  say: {~You walk around the lake and come back with no new pages and one good idea. You'll find out tomorrow if it's good.|The lake is mirror-still and has no opinion on your thesis. It's a relief.}\n\n# ---------------- The Thesis Laboratory (place: thesis_lab) ----------------\n\n@event thesis_lab_spare_carrel\ntitle: The Spare Carrel\nplace: thesis_lab\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: candle\ntags: study\nscene: A long room of wooden carrels, one at the end empty but for a candle stub and a stray page of footnotes.\ntext: {There's an empty carrel at the end of the row, with a candle stub and nobody's name on it.|The spare carrel is still unclaimed. You've started thinking of its candle stub as yours.|You know the carrels by their scratches now. The spare one has a new scratch, and you suspect it's yours.} {season == \"winter\": Everyone writes in fingerless gloves.|The windows are open, and a footnote blows off someone's desk.} {~Somewhere a quill scratches without stopping.|Someone is whispering \"however\" over and over.|A candle gutters out three carrels down, and someone groans.}\n> left: Work until the candle dies\n  lore +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {~You write until the stub drowns in its own wax. Your notes are good, and your neck will remember them.|You read one book and half of another. The room's silence does half the work for you.}\n> right: Just sit in the quiet\n  chance 70:\n    mood +small\n    say: {~You sit with your hands in your lap and listen to everyone else think. It's oddly restful.|You doze off with your cheek on someone's old footnotes. Nobody minds.}\n  else:\n    mood -tiny\n    say: {~An advisor looks into the carrel and asks what you're working on. \"Thinking,\" you say. He writes that down.|A final-year asks if you'd mind the corner. Their own candle has given out.}\n\n@event thesis_lab_footnote_war\ntitle: The Footnote War\nplace: thesis_lab\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: scroll\ntags: study\nscene: Two advisors arguing across a row of carrels, final-year students peering over the partitions to watch.\ntext: {Two advisors are arguing about a footnote across three carrels, and every final-year in the room has stopped pretending to work.|The footnote war has broken out again. Someone has drawn up sides on a scrap of paper.|By now the footnote war has rules, a referee and a running score chalked on the door.} {~Today it's about whether an old founders' letter counts as a source.|Today it's about a comma.|Today it's about a footnote to a footnote.}\n> left: Weigh in with a source\n  challenge research medium:\n    win:\n      lore +small\n      renown +tiny\n      say: {~You produce the source, open at the right page. Both advisors fall silent, and one of them asks for your name.|Your citation settles it. The loser shakes your hand and then rereads the page for an hour.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      renown -tiny\n      say: {~Your source turns out to be the one they were both arguing against. They unite, briefly, to explain why.|You cite the wrong edition. The room winces as one.}\n> right: Keep my head down and listen\n  lore +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~You learn more about sources from ten minutes of the war than from a term of lectures, and you never have to say a word.|You take notes on both sides. Neither of them would like how fair your notes are.}\n\n@event thesis_lab_borrowed_candle\ntime: evening\ntitle: Borrowing a Candle\nplace: thesis_lab\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: candle\ntags: study\nscene: A dark carrel lit by one dying candle, a full box of candles on the empty desk next door.\ntext: {The candles here give out at the worst moment. Yours just has, and the carrel next door has a full box and nobody in it.|Your candle gives out again. Next door, the usual box of spares and nobody in the chair.|The lab's candles have outlasted no one's patience tonight, least of all yours. There's a box next door.} {~A draft lies open beside the box, face up.|A note on the box says \"Help yourself\" in one hand and \"Don't\" in another.|Someone has drawn a small eye on the box.}\n> left: Borrow one and keep working\n  chance 65:\n    lore +small\n    mood -tiny\n    say: {~You light it and work until the bell. You leave a note that says \"IOU one candle\", unsigned.|One candle, two good hours. You put the stub back, which is either honest or worse.}\n  else:\n    mood -small\n    renown -tiny\n    say: {~The owner comes back for a forgotten scarf and finds you lit by their candle. They say nothing, which is worse.|By morning the whole lab knows who borrowed the candle. Someone leaves a box on your desk, with a ribbon.}\n> right: Go back in the dark\n  mood +tiny\n  health -tiny\n  say: {~You walk back across the bridges in the dark, your argument going round and round. It is better by the time you reach your bed.|You stub your toe twice on the way out. The idea you had on the stairs was worth both.}\n\n# ---------------- Internship: the Royal Library Archives ----------------\n\n@event intern_royal_library_slips\ntitle: Request Slips\ntrack: intern_royal_library\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: paper\ntags: study\nscene: A brass chute dropping paper slips into a basket at the top of a deep stairwell lined with shelves.\ntext: {Scholars upstairs send their requests down a brass chute. Your job is to fetch them from the stacks, which go down for miles.|You've worn a path in the dust of the third landing.|You can read the chute's rattle now. A long rattle means someone wants something from the very bottom.} {~One slip is in a hand so old the ink has gone brown.|One slip asks for \"the blue one\".|One slip is a love letter, sent down by mistake.}\n> left: Take the deep requests\n  perform intern_royal_library +small\n  lore +small\n  health -small\n  say: {~Four hundred steps down, the air is cold enough to keep books for ever. You come back up with the book and a lot of opinions about stairs.|You find the volume wedged behind a pipe. The scholar who asked for it nearly weeps.}\n> right: Take the near shelves\n  perform intern_royal_library -tiny\n  lore +tiny\n  mood +tiny\n  say: {~You fetch the easy ones and leave the deep slips for someone else. Someone else notices.|You're fast and pleasant, and everyone knows you've never been below the third landing.}\n\n@event intern_royal_library_mending\ntitle: The Mending Table\ntrack: intern_royal_library\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: book\ntags: study\nscene: A bright worktable under a lamp, a crumbling manuscript laid out in pieces and a student holding fine tweezers.\ntext: {Today you're at the mending table, where crumbling records are put back together one flake at a time.|The binder hands you the tweezers without a word, which counts as a greeting.|The binder trusts you with the older pages now, which are the worst.} {~The page is a royal decree on the correct length of goose necks.|The page is a recipe, half eaten by something.|The page is a list of names, one crossed out very hard.}\n> left: Mend it myself\n  challenge research easy:\n    win:\n      perform intern_royal_library +small\n      lore +small\n      say: {~Every flake goes back where it belongs. The binder holds it up to the lamp and says, \"Acceptable,\" which here is a medal.|You save the page and read it while the glue dries. It's better than you expected.}\n    lose:\n      perform intern_royal_library -small\n      mood -tiny\n      say: {~A sneeze. The binder looks at the scattered flakes and then at you. You both get the tweezers.|One flake ends up in the wrong century. Nobody can prove it was you, but everybody knows.}\n> right: Watch the binder work\n  lore +small\n  perform intern_royal_library -tiny\n  say: {~You watch for an hour and learn more than a book could tell you. The binder says you're slow. You were.|The binder works without speaking and you learn by watching. It's very peaceful and very little gets done.}\n\n# ---------------- Internship: the Conclave Laboratory ----------------\n\n@event intern_conclave_test_chamber\ntitle: The Test Chamber\ntrack: intern_conclave\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: wand\ntags: study magic\nscene: A circular chamber with scorch-marked walls, a student at the centre with a wand raised and researchers watching through thick glass.\ntext: {The Conclave's researchers need someone to cast their newest spell for the first time. The walls are thick for a reason.|Someone has chalked a new circle on the floor. The old one is scorched.|You know every scorch mark in the chamber by now, and who made each one.} {~Today's spell is meant to make a candle burn backwards.|Today's spell is meant to tidy a room. It has opinions about what tidy means.|Today's spell is unnamed. Nobody will say why.}\n> left: Step into the chamber\n  challenge enchant using arcana medium:\n    win:\n      perform intern_conclave +small\n      arcana +small\n      say: {~The spell works on the first try. Behind the glass, someone writes your name in the margin of their notes.|It works, more or less, for one glorious minute before the spell remembers itself.}\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      perform intern_conclave -tiny\n      say: {~The spell goes off sideways. Your eyebrows are fine. Your fringe has views of its own now.|The spell goes off sideways, and your robes will never fold the same way again.}\n> right: Take readings behind the glass\n  arcana +tiny\n  perform intern_conclave +tiny\n  mood -small\n  say: {~You write down numbers for three hours while someone else gets to be brave. They're very good numbers.|You note every flicker. A senior researcher reads your page and asks for your notes next time.}\n\n@event intern_conclave_minutes\ntitle: Taking the Minutes\ntrack: intern_conclave\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: scroll\ntags: study magic\nscene: A long table of elderly mages in heavy robes, all talking at once, a young intern writing furiously at the end.\ntext: {You've been asked to take the minutes of a Conclave research committee. There are nine members, and none of them ever agrees.|The committee is still arguing about last time's question, and now about the minutes too.|You could take these minutes in your sleep, and some of the committee do attend them that way.} {~Two of them are not speaking to each other, and want it recorded.|One of them asks what you think.|One of them falls asleep, and wakes up to vote.}\n> left: Offer an opinion when asked\n  chance 50:\n    perform intern_conclave +small\n    renown +tiny\n    mood -tiny\n    say: {~The table goes silent. Then the oldest mage says, \"The intern is right,\" and the rest pretend they agreed all along.|Your opinion settles the argument. It also starts two new ones.}\n  else:\n    perform intern_conclave -tiny\n    mood -small\n    say: {~Nine heads turn towards you, slowly. You say something about wands. It isn't minuted.|You give your opinion and it is recorded in full, with the note \"(intern)\".}\n> right: Keep my head down and write\n  perform intern_conclave +small\n  lore +tiny\n  mood -small\n  say: {~Your minutes are perfect and so dull that nobody reads them, which in a committee is the highest praise.|You record every word of the argument. Your hand aches, and you understand the Conclave much better than you wanted to.}\n\n# ---------------- Internship: the Guild of Alchemists' Works ----------------\n\n@event intern_alchemists_vat_watch\ntitle: Vat Watch\ntrack: intern_alchemists\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: cauldron\ntags: study\nscene: A huge copper vat in a steaming hall, a student on a gantry staring into it with a colour chart in hand.\ntext: {Your task at the works is to watch one great vat and call the foreman if it changes colour.|You've brought your own colour chart now, with notes in the margins.|You and the vat understand each other now. It's an unsettling feeling.} {~The amber is drifting, very slowly.|There's a bubble that keeps coming back, like a thought.|The vat smells faintly of cinnamon, which it never has before.} The foreman is all the way at the other end of the hall.\n> left: Adjust it myself\n  challenge brew medium:\n    win:\n      perform intern_alchemists +small\n      alchemy +small\n      say: {~A pinch of salt and a slow stir, and the vat settles back to its proper self. The foreman never knew, but the vat did.|You correct it before it goes wrong. When the foreman passes, he gives the vat a satisfied look as if he did it.}\n    lose:\n      perform intern_alchemists -small\n      health -tiny\n      say: {~The vat turns a brilliant violet and hisses. The foreman arrives at a run, and you'll be hearing about it for weeks.|You make it worse. A lot worse. The whole hall smells of cinnamon until the end of the week.}\n> right: Run for the foreman\n  perform intern_alchemists +tiny\n  alchemy +tiny\n  mood -small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {~You run the length of the hall. The foreman fixes it in a second and explains how, at length, as you catch your breath.|By the time you're back with the foreman the colour has corrected itself. He stares at you, then at the vat.}\n\n@event intern_alchemists_no_flame\ntitle: The Rule About Flames\ntrack: intern_alchemists\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: cup\ntags: study\nscene: A break room in an alchemical works, workers gathered around a kettle sitting on a glowing stone that is not, technically, on fire.\ntext: {There's a strict rule at the works about naked flames, so the break room has spent forty years inventing flameless ways to boil a kettle.|Break time. The kettle sits on its warm stone, and the workers look at you hopefully.|By now the break room asks the academy intern first, whatever the problem.} {~Today the warm stone has gone cold.|Today someone wants toast, which is a much bigger question.|Today a new inspector is touring the works.}\n> left: Help brew the flameless tea\n  chance 70:\n    alchemy +small\n    mood +small\n    say: {~You heat the kettle with a reaction you learned in your first year. The break room calls it \"the academy method\" and adopts it on the spot.|It works, though everything boiled on it comes out a little purple.}\n  else:\n    perform intern_alchemists -small\n    mood -tiny\n    say: {~Your method works so well that someone in charge notices the steam. There's a meeting about it, with you in it.|The kettle boils, and so, briefly, does the table.}\n> right: Stick to the rules\n  perform intern_alchemists +tiny\n  mood -small\n  say: {~You drink cold water while everyone else drinks tea. The foreman notes your conduct, and the break room notes it too.|You remind everyone of the rule. You are absolutely right and nobody offers you a biscuit for the rest of the week.}\n\n# ---------------- Internship: the Observatory Expedition ----------------\n\n@event intern_expedition_thin_air\ntitle: Up the Ridge\ntrack: intern_expedition\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: compass\ntags: study\nscene: A line of expedition members climbing a rocky ridge at dusk, carrying brass instruments wrapped in blankets.\ntext: {The instruments have to go up to the ridge before dark, and the air on the plateau is very thin.|Your legs know the path now. Your lungs are still negotiating.|You've carried things up this ridge so often that the goats have stopped looking at you.} {season == \"winter\": {~Snow has drifted against the path.|The wind cuts through every layer you own.}|{~The rocks are still hot from the afternoon.|The sky is clear all the way to the top of the world.}}\n> left: Carry the great lens\n  perform intern_expedition +small\n  vigor +tiny\n  health -small\n  say: {~You carry the great lens to the top without dropping it, then sit very still for a long time. The leader nods at you, once.|Your arms are numb by the top, and the lens is perfect. It's worth it when the first star shows in it.}\n> right: Do the sums in the tent\n  lore +small\n  perform intern_expedition -tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~You work the night's sums by lamplight, warm and quiet. The lamp gutters twice. Your sums don't.|You have the charts ready when they come down. Nobody thanks you, because the charts are always ready.}\n\n@event intern_expedition_chart_dispute\ntitle: A Star Out of Place\ntrack: intern_expedition\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: star\ntags: study\nscene: Two expedition members bent over a star chart by lamplight, one pointing firmly at a dot.\ntext: {Your chart and the senior astronomer's disagree about where one faint star is.|The senior astronomer's charts and yours have a running disagreement now, kept in red ink.|The senior astronomer now checks your charts first. A compliment, possibly.} {~Probably your hand shook.|Probably their eyesight.|Probably the thin air.} {season == \"winter\": The nights are long, with time to recheck everything.|The nights are short, and every hour counts.}\n> left: Recheck it all night\n  lore +small\n  perform intern_expedition +tiny\n  health -small\n  say: {~You recheck it until dawn. You were right by a hair, and nobody will ever care but you.|You were wrong by a hair. You fix it, and know the sky better than you did.}\n> right: Raise it with the astronomer\n  chance 50:\n    perform intern_expedition +small\n    charm +tiny\n    say: {~The astronomer checks, grumbles and corrects their chart. Then they buy you a cup of the camp's terrible coffee.|\"Well spotted,\" says the astronomer, through their teeth.}\n  else:\n    perform intern_expedition -small\n    mood -tiny\n    say: {~The astronomer checks and shows you, in front of everyone, exactly where you went wrong.|They don't check. They just look at you over their spectacles until you go away.}\n\n# ---------------- Internship: the Beastcraft Field Station ----------------\n\n@event intern_field_station_count\ntitle: The Dawn Count\ntrack: intern_field_station\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: feather\ntags: study nature\nscene: A student lying flat in the heather at dawn, a tally board in hand, watching nesting towers on the moor.\ntext: {Every morning someone has to lie in the heather and count what's living on the moor. Today it's you.|You've learned which tussock is the least wet, and you guard it.|You're the station's best counter now, mostly because you've stopped minding the damp.} {season == \"winter\": {~Snow hares are out, white on white.|Frost has made the heather crackle under you.}|{~The skylarks are going up one after another.|The midges have found you.}}\n> left: Count every last one\n  perform intern_field_station +small\n  vigor +tiny\n  health -small\n  say: {~You count until your fingers are numb and the tally is perfect. The warden reads it twice and pins it on the wall.|You stay out until the very last nest is counted. You come back soaked and very proud of the number.}\n> right: Make a sensible estimate\n  chance 60:\n    guile +small\n    mood +tiny\n    say: {~Your estimate is so close that nobody suspects. You spend the extra hour by the stove with a cup of tea.|The warden checks your number against his own and nods. You will never tell him.}\n  else:\n    perform intern_field_station -small\n    mood -tiny\n    say: {~The warden counted too. His number is not your number. He makes a note.|Your estimate includes a flock that left last week. The warden is very nice about it, which is worse.}\n\n@event intern_field_station_patient\ntitle: The Patient in Paddock Three\ntrack: intern_field_station\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: paw\ntags: study nature\nscene: A muddy paddock with a splinted creature glaring from a straw nest, a student holding out a bucket at arm's length.\ntext: {The station's latest patient is a moor-drake with a splinted wing and no gratitude at all.|Feeding time in paddock three. The patient sees you coming and lowers its head.|The drake in paddock three recognises you now. This hasn't made it any nicer.} {~It has eaten a glove.|It is pretending to sleep, which it does very badly.|It has learned to open the bucket by itself.} {season == \"winter\": Its breath steams in the frosty air.|It is lying in the one patch of shade.}\n> left: Feed it by hand\n  chance 65:\n    perform intern_field_station +small\n    vigor +small\n    say: {~It takes the food from your hand, very gently. Then it bites the bucket, to make a point.|It lets you check the splint, grumbling the whole time like an old man at the barber's.}\n  else:\n    health -small\n    vigor +tiny\n    say: {~It takes the food and a bit of your sleeve. Your arm is fine. Your sleeve is not.|It butts you into the mud and then looks pleased with itself.}\n> right: Use the long tongs\n  perform intern_field_station +tiny\n  mood -small\n  say: {~You feed it from a safe distance. It eats, and looks at you with deep contempt.|The tongs work perfectly. You feel you've let the patient down, somehow.}\n\n# ---------------- Internship: the Thornwick Exchange Lab ----------------\n\n@event intern_thornwick_pastries\ntitle: Thornwick Hospitality\ntrack: intern_thornwick\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: mask\ntags: study\nscene: A polished laboratory table at a rival academy, a plate of perfect pastries set out for a visiting student.\ntext: {Each morning your Thornwick hosts leave pastries on your desk, with a polite note.|The notes have grown longer and politer, which is worrying.|The pastries are a game now, and both sides know it.} {~They look perfect, which in an illusion lab is a warning.|One is steaming slightly, which it shouldn't be.|Today there's one plain biscuit, which is somehow worse.} {season == \"winter\": The pastries look very warm.|The icing hasn't melted in the heat.}\n> left: Eat one and smile\n  chance 60:\n    mood +small\n    charm +tiny\n    say: {~It's real, and very good. The Thornwick interns look almost disappointed.|It's real. You eat it slowly, in front of everyone, and thank them warmly.}\n  else:\n    mood -small\n    charm +tiny\n    say: {~It's a glamour over something dry and joyless. You eat the whole thing with a smile, and the lab respects that.|It tastes of nothing at all. You compliment the baker, and someone in the corner laughs.}\n> right: Bring my own breakfast\n  guile +small\n  perform intern_thornwick -tiny\n  say: {~You eat your own bread, very pointedly. The Thornwick students write something down.|You leave the plate untouched. Your supervisor here calls it \"a very Lumenhold choice\", and doesn't mean it nicely.}\n\n@event intern_thornwick_rivalry\ntitle: The Old Grudge\ntrack: intern_thornwick\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: duel\ntags: study\nscene: A group of rival students around a lab bench, one of them leaning in with a pointed smile towards a visitor in different robes.\ntext: {The Thornwick students have questions about Lumenhold. Most of them are about the last time the two schools met on the duelling field.|The Thornwick students have found a new angle on the old grudge.|You and the Thornwick students have a routine now. It's almost friendly.} {~Today they want to know if Lumenhold's towers still lean.|Today they ask, sweetly, if Lumenhold teaches illusion yet.|Today one of them pretends to be you, very well.}\n> left: Out-charm them\n  challenge negotiate medium:\n    win:\n      perform intern_thornwick +small\n      charm +small\n      say: {~You answer every barb with a better one. By lunch they're laughing with you rather than at you.|You make the room laugh at their joke, and then at them. Their supervisor smiles behind her notes.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      perform intern_thornwick -tiny\n      say: {~You try to be witty. It comes out as a speech about the founders. They let you finish, kindly.|You lose the exchange so badly that one of them takes a bow on your behalf.}\n> right: Let it slide\n  perform intern_thornwick +tiny\n  mood -small\n  say: {~You smile and go back to work. Your supervisor here notices, and approves, and you feel like you lost anyway.|You say nothing. It takes them all morning to realise you've won.}\n\n# ---------------- Internship: the Office of Unseen Affairs ----------------\n\n@event intern_unseen_office_coffee\ntitle: The Coffee Machine\ntrack: intern_unseen_office\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: cup\ntags: study mystery\nscene: A plain room with no windows and a gleaming coffee machine, quiet people in plain coats standing near it, not quite talking.\ntext: {Nobody at the Office talks about anything, except the coffee machine. It's very good.|A queue forms around the machine each evening, though nobody seems to join it.|The coffee queue, you've learned, is where the Office's real work gets done.} {~Two people beside you discuss the weather in a way that isn't about the weather.|A cup has a name on it that was there yesterday, under another name.|The machine hisses, and everyone falls silent until it stops.}\n> left: Linger at the machine\n  chance 60:\n    guile +small\n    perform intern_unseen_office +tiny\n    say: {~You learn three things nobody told you, and one thing nobody knows you know.|You drink your coffee slowly and listen. By the second cup you understand what nobody is saying.}\n  else:\n    mood -small\n    say: {~Someone notices you listening and asks, very kindly, how your day is going. You don't sleep that night.|You overhear something you very much wish you hadn't. It can't be unheard.}\n> right: Take my cup back to my desk\n  perform intern_unseen_office +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {~You work through the evening with your coffee. The work is dull and your supervisor approves, which is a little chilling.|Your desk is very quiet. It's the most anyone at the Office will say about you, and here that's high praise.}\n\n@event intern_unseen_office_wrong_file\ntitle: The Wrong File\ntrack: intern_unseen_office\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: eye\ntags: study mystery\nscene: A plain folder with no label lying on an empty desk under a single lamp.\ntext: {A file has been left on your desk. It isn't yours, and it isn't labelled.|A second unlabelled file. You're starting to think it's a test.|The unlabelled files keep arriving. You've stopped asking where they come from.} This one is tied shut with a red ribbon. {~It's very thin.|It's very thick, with a coffee ring on the cover.|The knot is one you don't recognise.}\n> left: Read it before returning it\n  alignment -tiny\n  challenge deceive medium:\n    win:\n      guile +small\n      perform intern_unseen_office +small\n      say: {~You read it, retie the ribbon perfectly and hand it in. Your supervisor checks the knot and smiles, very slightly.|You read every page. It's about a man who sells hats. Nothing has ever made you more nervous.}\n    lose:\n      perform intern_unseen_office -small\n      mood -tiny\n      say: {~Your supervisor takes the file back and glances at the ribbon. \"Interesting knot,\" she says.|You put the pages back in the wrong order. Somehow, everyone knows by morning.}\n> right: Return it unopened\n  alignment +tiny\n  perform intern_unseen_office +tiny\n  mood -small\n  say: {~You hand it back without looking. Your supervisor says nothing, which here is approval. You'll wonder about it for weeks.|You return it with the ribbon untouched. The next day there's a slightly better coffee waiting on your desk.}\n"},{"path":"content/events/pools/village_home.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Baseline pool: the village below the castle and the family\n#  home (break plan \"home\"). Repeatable routine events.\n# ============================================================\n\n# ---------------- The Village ----------------\n\n@event village_barge_crossing\ntitle: The Barge Across\nplace: village\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: wave\ntags: life\nscene: A flat-bottomed barge crossing a mirror-still lake, the seven towers shrinking behind it.\ntext: {The village barge is slower than walking, if you could walk on water.|The bargeman has started nodding at you like a regular.|The bargeman saves you the dry end of the bench.} {season == \"winter\": Ice ticks against the hull.|{season == \"summer\": Dragonflies ride the bow for free.|{~A first-year is being sick, very politely.|Two prefects are pretending not to hold hands.|Someone is reading aloud to a crate of chickens.}}}\n> left: Read on the crossing\n  lore +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~You finish a chapter and miss the towers turning gold behind you.|You read the same paragraph four times; the lake keeps interrupting.|You look up only when the hull bumps the quay, and feel you've skipped something.}\n> right: Chat with the bargeman\n  charm +small\n  gold -tiny\n  say: {~He tells you the lake was rougher in his father's day, and expects a tip for the history.|He has opinions about every tower and charges for none of them, except in tips.|He asks after your exams as if they were a sick relative. You tip him for the sympathy.}\n\n@event village_square_errand\ntitle: One Thing in the Square\nplace: village\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: coin\ntags: social\ncast:\n  friend: any where rel(friend) >= 6 and tagged(friend, student)\nscene: A crooked village market square, two students weaving between stalls, one already carrying too many parcels.\ntext: {friend} has come down to the village with you \"for one thing\", which turns out to be several. {First, a hat.|You've learned that \"one thing\" means at least three.|You've stopped asking what the one thing is.} {~A stallholder is selling candied chestnuts in paper twists.|A tinker is mending a cauldron with a great deal of swearing.|The market cat has chosen {friend} and will not be moved.}\n> left: Carry the parcels\n  rel friend +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {~You come back up the hill with an armful of string-tied parcels and sore wrists.|{friend} buys you a chestnut as payment. It is not enough payment.|One parcel clinks suspiciously. You decide not to ask.}\n> right: Talk {them} out of it\n  charm +small\n  rel friend -tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~You talk {friend} out of the third thing. {They} sulk{s} the whole way back to the barge.|Your argument is excellent. {friend} buys the thing anyway, and now you've been wrong in public.|You win. {friend} says you'd make a terrible shopkeeper and a worse friend, then takes your arm anyway.}\n\n@event village_last_barge\ntitle: The Last Barge\nplace: village\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: lantern\ntags: life\nscene: A lamplit village quay at dusk, the last barge pulling at its rope, a student hesitating at the edge of the square.\ntext: {The last barge back leaves at the bell, and the bell is ringing.|The last-barge bell, and you're nowhere near the quay. The bargeman knows your running by now.|The bell. Of course the bell.} {season == \"winter\": Snow is coming down thick over the square.|{~The inn has started a song with too many verses.|The lamps are only half lit.|The lake has gone the colour of pewter.}} {final_year: Not many evenings like this are left.|You could run. You could stay.}\n> left: Run for it\n  vigor +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {~You make it with a leap and one wet boot.|You make it; your scarf does not.|The bargeman holds the rope a heartbeat longer than he should. You owe him.}\n> right: Stay a little longer\n  mood +small\n  chance 50:\n    gold -small\n    say: {~A fisherman rows you back for a ruinous fare and a story you didn't ask for.|You pay the night ferryman far too much. He rows like he has somewhere better to be.}\n  else:\n    health -small\n    say: {~You sleep on a bench under the Newt's eaves and wake up with a cat on your feet.|The first barge of morning finds you yawning on the quay.}\n\n# ---------------- The Gilded Newt ----------------\n\n@event gilded_newt_fireside\ntitle: By the Newt's Fire\nplace: gilded_newt\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: flame\ntags: life\nscene: A low-beamed tavern with a roaring hearth, a student tucked into the corner seat beside it with a steaming cup.\ntext: {The Gilded Newt's fire has never gone out, and the landlady says it never will while she lives.|The landlady chalks your name on her slate before you've ordered.|Your corner by the fire is free. The regulars leave it for you now.} {season == \"winter\": Snow hisses off the boots of everyone who comes in.|{~A dog is asleep with its paws in the hearth.|Someone has hung wet socks on the fire-irons.|A farmer is explaining turnips to nobody.}}\n> left: Order a cider and stay\n  gold -tiny\n  mood +small\n  say: {~The cider is warm and spiced and costs exactly what the landlady says it does.|You stay until your cheeks are red and your worries are smaller.|The landlady brings the cider without being asked, and charges for it all the same.}\n> right: Work by the firelight\n  lore +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~You get two pages done between three toasts and a fiddle.|The noise is terrible, and somehow you concentrate better than in the library.|A regular reads over your shoulder and corrects your spelling of \"wyvern\".}\n\n@event gilded_newt_card_game\ntime: evening night\ntitle: Cards at the Newt\nplace: gilded_newt\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: cards\ntags: money\nscene: A smoky back room in a tavern, a card game around a scarred table and one empty chair.\ntext: {There's a card game in the back room of the Gilded Newt, and an empty chair.|The empty chair in the back room has a cushion on it now, as if it's been waiting.|The card players greet you by the name of your worst hand.} {~Tonight it's a tinker, a widow and a man who shuffles like a conjuror.|Tonight the stakes are copper and pride.|Tonight a second-year from Duskgate is losing very cheerfully.}\n> left: Take the empty chair\n  challenge deceive easy:\n    win:\n      gold +small\n      mood +tiny\n      say: {~You win a handful of crowns and a reputation for an honest face.|You win, and the widow asks where you learned to bluff like that.}\n    lose:\n      gold -small\n      mood -tiny\n      say: {~You lose your crowns slowly, and then all at once.|The tinker takes your last coin and buys you a drink with it, which somehow makes it worse.}\n> right: Watch for the tells\n  guile +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~You spend the evening watching. The man who shuffles like a conjuror scratches his ear when he lies.|You learn a great deal about bluffing and nothing about having fun.|Someone asks if you're playing or haunting. You leave before you have to answer.}\n\n@event gilded_newt_corner_table\ntime: evening night\ntitle: The Corner Table\nplace: gilded_newt\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: cup\ntags: social\ncast:\n  here: present(place) where met(here) and tagged(here, student)\nscene: A tavern corner table with a half-empty jug of cider, someone familiar waving a student over.\ntext: {here} is at the corner table with {a jug of cider and no plans to leave.|the house jug, which the landlady refills for whoever sits there.|the table's jug. The landlady nods at you as you come in, as if you're expected.} \"{~Sit down, you're blocking the fire.|You look like you need a drink. I look like I need company.|Don't tell anyone you saw me here.}\" {season == \"winter\": Snow is piling up against the window.|The window is open to the night.}\n> left: Sit down for one drink\n  rel here +small\n  gold -tiny\n  health -tiny\n  say: {~One drink becomes three stories and a long walk to the barge.|{here} tells you something true halfway down the jug, then pretends not to have.|You stay later than you meant to. It's worth the morning.}\n> right: Wave and keep walking\n  lore +small\n  rel here -tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~You wave and get back to your book. {here} toasts your back, a little hurt.|You plead an early lecture. It's even true.}\n\n# ---------------- Mabel's Tea Shop ----------------\n\n@event mabels_the_door\ntitle: Mabel Reads the Door\nplace: mabels\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: cup\ntags: life\nscene: A tiny tea shop crammed with tins, the shop-bell over the door still swinging, a shrewd woman behind the counter already reaching for a tin.\ntext: {Mabel looks up as you open the door and is already reaching for a tin.|Mabel watches you open the door the way a doctor watches a cough.|You've tried opening Mabel's door every way you can think of. She still knows.} {~The bell over the door rings a little flat today.|There's a queue of two, which for Mabel's is a crowd.|A cake is cooling on the counter, out of reach and smug about it.}\n> left: Let Mabel choose your tea\n  gold -tiny\n  mood +small\n  say: {~She brings you something smoky and says, \"For the week you've had.\" You hadn't told her about the week.|She brings you something with rose in it and won't explain why. It helps.|It's mint. It's always mint when you've been lying to yourself, apparently.}\n> right: Fake a cheerful entrance\n  challenge deceive easy:\n    win:\n      mood +small\n      gold -tiny\n      say: {~She brings you the cheerful tea. It's so good you actually cheer up, which may have been her plan all along.|Mabel looks fooled. Then she adds a biscuit, which means she wasn't.}\n    lose:\n      mood -tiny\n      gold -tiny\n      say: {~She brings you a pot labelled, in her own hand, \"for people pretending\".|\"Nice try, dear,\" says Mabel, and brings the strong one.}\n\n@event mabels_window_seat\ntitle: The Window Seat\nplace: mabels\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: book\ntags: study\nscene: A window seat in a tea shop looking across a still lake to seven distant towers, a teapot and open notes on the sill.\ntext: {Mabel's window seat looks out over the lake to the castle, and it's free.|Mabel has stopped asking if you're waiting for someone at the window seat.|Your window seat. Mabel puts a cushion on it when she sees you on the quay.} {season == \"winter\": Frost has drawn ferns on the glass.|{season == \"spring\": Blossom keeps landing on the sill.|{~The towers look small from here, almost friendly.|Rain streaks the glass and blurs the towers.}}}\n> left: Make one pot last all day\n  lore +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~You get through a whole chapter on one pot, under Mabel's increasingly pointed looks.|By the third hour the tea is cold and Mabel is dusting very close to your table.|You finish your notes. Mabel says nothing, which is worse.}\n> right: Order cake with it\n  mood +small\n  gold -small\n  say: {You've had two of Mabel's three cakes. Today, the third, which is lemon and worth every crown.|Mabel has started cutting the lemon cake before you sit down.|{~Mabel brings the lemon without asking. You eat it slowly while the barges come and go.|The lemon cake, the window, the lake. The work can wait.}}\n\n@event mabels_forty_teas\ntitle: Forty Kinds of Tea\nplace: mabels\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: cup\ntags: social\ncast:\n  friend: any where rel(friend) >= 6 and tagged(friend, student)\nscene: Two students at a small tea-shop table crowded with mismatched teapots, one writing in a notebook.\ntext: {friend} wants to try one of Mabel's forty teas, and Mabel insists on choosing. {Today she starts a chart of who has tried what.|Mabel's chart of your friends' teas is pinned behind the counter now; {friend}'s column gets a tick.|Mabel's chart runs to a second sheet. {friend}'s column is the shortest, and Mabel looks at it pointedly.} {~Mabel pretends not to be interested.|Mabel has opinions about the order.}\n> left: Pay for both pots\n  gold -small\n  rel friend +small\n  say: {~{friend} pronounces today's tea \"bracing\", which means awful. You drink yours anyway.|You pay. Mabel marks you on the chart as a patron of the arts.|{friend} declares it the best tea in the village, then sniffs yours to be sure.}\n> right: Order your usual\n  mood +tiny\n  gold -tiny\n  rel friend -tiny\n  say: {~{friend} calls you unadventurous. Your tea is perfect, thank you.|\"Coward,\" says {friend} fondly, over something purple.|You stick to your usual. {friend} drinks something that smells of bonfires and looks betrayed by it.}\n\n# ---------------- Grimsby's Books ----------------\n\n@event grimsbys_wrong_book\ntitle: Not the Book You Wanted\nplace: grimsbys_books\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: book\ntags: study\nscene: A bookshop so crowded the shelves lean together, an elderly bookseller on a ladder triumphantly holding up the wrong book.\ntext: {You ask Mr Grimsby for a textbook. He vanishes into the shelves and comes back with a book on beekeeping.|You ask Mr Grimsby for one thing. He brings you another, with great confidence.|Mr Grimsby sees you coming and is already halfway up a ladder, looking for the wrong book.} {~Today's is a cookery book with a whole chapter on eels.|Today's is a history of a town that may not exist.|Today's is in a language neither of you reads.}\n> left: Buy what he found\n  gold -small\n  lore +small\n  say: {~It's useless and wonderful. You read half of it on the barge.|You learn more than you meant to about something you never meant to learn.|There's a pressed flower in it, and a name you don't know on the flyleaf.}\n> right: Search the shelves yourself\n  challenge research easy:\n    win:\n      lore +small\n      gold -tiny\n      say: {~You find it behind a stack of almanacs, exactly where he said it wasn't.|You find it. Mr Grimsby looks wounded and charges you anyway.}\n    lose:\n      health -tiny\n      mood -small\n      say: {~A shelf leans on you for support. You hold it up for a long time.|An afternoon, a sneezing fit, and no book.}\n\n@event grimsbys_damp_barrow\ntitle: The Damp Barrow\nplace: grimsbys_books\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: scroll\ntags: study\nscene: A wooden barrow of damp secondhand books outside a crooked bookshop, a student reading one in the drizzle.\ntext: {Outside Grimsby's there's a barrow of secondhand books, a coin apiece, slightly damp.|The barrow has a new sign: \"NO READING WITHOUT BUYING\". Nobody obeys it.|The barrow. You know its smell now: rain, glue and other people's thoughts.} {season == \"winter\": Someone has thrown a sack over it against the sleet.|{~The top book is about knots.|A cat is asleep on the poetry.|A card in the corner says \"NOT RUDE, JUST OLD\".}}\n> left: Buy a damp book\n  gold -tiny\n  lore +small\n  say: {~It dries out wavy and full of someone else's underlinings.|The margins are better than the book. Someone argued with every page.|The last page is a shopping list in faded ink. You find it oddly moving.}\n> right: Read it standing in the rain\n  mood +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {~You read until your feet are cold and Mr Grimsby taps on the window.|You read a whole chapter in the drizzle and don't buy it. You feel like a thief and a scholar at once.|A previous owner has written \"Don't believe this bit\" in the margin. You don't.}\n\n@event grimsbys_last_copy\ntitle: The Last Copy\nplace: grimsbys_books\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: book\ntags: social\ncast:\n  friend: any where rel(friend) >= 6 and tagged(friend, student)\nscene: Two students in a cramped bookshop, both holding the same book, neither letting go.\ntext: There's one copy left of the book you need, and {friend} has {their} hand on it too. {You both laugh. Neither of you lets go.|This keeps happening to you at Grimsby's. You're starting to suspect the shop.|Mr Grimsby swears he ordered two. It never arrives as two.} {~\"I saw it first,\" {they} say{s}.|\"We could share,\" {they} offer{s}, unconvincingly.|Mr Grimsby says there might be another in the cellar. There won't.}\n> left: Let {them} have it\n  rel friend +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~{friend} promises to lend it to you \"very soon\". You know what that means.|You let go. {friend} buys you a bun on the way back, which nearly makes up for it.|{friend} reads it on the barge and tells you all the best parts, in the wrong order.}\n> right: Haggle for it\n  challenge negotiate easy:\n    win:\n      lore +small\n      gold -tiny\n      rel friend -tiny\n      say: {~Mr Grimsby takes your side for reasons of his own. {friend} won't speak to you until the quay.|You win it fair and square, and {friend} says so in a voice that means the opposite.}\n    lose:\n      gold -tiny\n      rel friend -tiny\n      say: {~{friend} outbids you, and somehow you end up paying for the tea afterwards.|You argue so long that a third-year buys it while you're both talking.}\n\n# ---------------- Pell & Daughters ----------------\n\n@event pell_apothecary_drawers\ntitle: A Thousand Drawers\nplace: pell_apothecary\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: bottle\ntags: study\nscene: An apothecary wall of a thousand tiny labelled drawers, brass handles gleaming, a student reading the labels on tiptoe.\ntext: {Pell & Daughters has a thousand drawers, each labelled in a different hand.|You find a drawer you'd swear wasn't there last time.|You've read half the labels in the shop. The other half seem to be reading you.} Today it's {~\"Moonwort. Do not hum near.\"|\"Dried courage, low quality.\"|\"Owl pellets (sorted).\"} {season == \"winter\": The shop smells of camphor and wet wool.|The shop smells of mint and something burnt.}\n> left: Buy a pinch of it\n  gold -tiny\n  alchemy +small\n  say: {~One of the daughters weighs it on scales finer than an eyelash and tells you what it's really for.|It smells of the shop all the way back across the lake.|Old Pell counts the grains himself and warns you about three things you hadn't planned to do.}\n> right: Just read the labels\n  lore +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~Old Pell watches you over his spectacles the whole time, as if labels could be stolen.|You copy a dozen labels into your notebook. A daughter asks if you're planning to open a rival shop.|You learn a great deal. Old Pell sighs every time you touch a handle.}\n\n@event pell_apothecary_queue\ntitle: Symptoms in the Queue\nplace: pell_apothecary\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: cross\ntags: social\nscene: A queue of villagers at an apothecary counter, all turning to a student in academy robes.\ntext: {The queue at Pell & Daughters notices your academy robes. A woman shows you her rash.|Word has got round the queue at Pell's that you're \"the young doctor\".|The queue parts for you as if you were a physician. You are not a physician.} {~A farmer has a cough like a door hinge.|A boy has a frog stuck to his hand, and the frog seems content.|An old man wants something for \"the usual\", and won't say what the usual is.}\n> left: Offer some advice\n  challenge brew easy:\n    win:\n      renown +small\n      mood +tiny\n      say: {~Your advice is sound. One of the daughters nods, which in this shop is a medal.|The farmer tries your remedy and comes back to thank you. Old Pell grumbles about lost trade.}\n    lose:\n      renown -tiny\n      mood -small\n      say: {~Old Pell corrects you in front of the whole queue, kindly, which is worse.|Your advice would have worked on a horse. A daughter says so.}\n> right: Study a drawer intently\n  guile +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~You pretend to be fascinated by a drawer of buttons until the queue moves on.|You say \"Hm\" like Professor Thorne and leave quickly.|You become very interested in the floor. The woman with the rash tells her friend you were rude.}\n\n@event pell_apothecary_tonic\ntitle: The Daughters' Tonic\nplace: pell_apothecary\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: potion\ntags: life\nscene: Two apothecary's daughters leaning over a counter, offering a student a small bottle of something that fizzes.\ntext: {One of Pell's daughters has a new tonic and needs someone to try it. Her father must not know.|The daughters have another tonic, and a notebook with your name at the top of a column.|The daughters see you and reach under the counter before you've said hello.} {~This one is green and fizzes.|This one smells of pears and thunder.|This one is clear, which they say is the worrying kind.}\n> left: Drink it down\n  chance 60:\n    health +small\n    mood +tiny\n    say: {~You feel wonderful, and briefly taller. The daughters take notes.|Your cold vanishes. So, for an hour, does your sense of smell.}\n  else:\n    health -small\n    say: {~You hiccup bubbles all the way back to the barge.|Your ears go warm and stay warm. The daughters take very interested notes.}\n> right: Buy a proper remedy\n  gold -small\n  health +small\n  say: {~Old Pell sells you something brown and reliable. The daughters look disappointed in you.|You buy the dull, tested thing. It works, dully.|A daughter wraps it and murmurs that you'll be sorry you missed the green one.}\n\n# ---------------- The Bakery ----------------\n\n@event bakery_bun_queue\ntitle: The Bun Queue\nplace: bakery\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: meal\ntags: life\nscene: A queue of villagers and students outside a little bakery at dawn, steam and the smell of cinnamon pouring out of the door.\ntext: {There's a queue outside the bakery before the village is properly awake, and it smells like a promise.|The baker's son counts heads in the bun queue and knows you by your scarf.|The bun queue. You know everyone in it by their order.} {season == \"winter\": Breath steams in the cold.|{season == \"autumn\": Leaves skitter round everyone's ankles.|{~The baker's son is singing off-key.|A goat has joined the queue and nobody has the heart to send it away.}}}\n> left: Buy a bag for your corridor\n  gold -small\n  renown +small\n  say: {~You come back with a warm bag and are, for one morning, the most popular person in your house.|The buns are gone before you've taken your coat off. Someone leaves you a thank-you note on a napkin.|A prefect takes one \"to check it for contraband\". It passes.}\n> right: One bun, eaten warm\n  gold -tiny\n  mood +small\n  say: {~You eat it on the quay while it's still too hot. Worth every burnt finger.|Butter, cinnamon and the lake in the morning. Nothing has ever been better.|You eat it slowly and think about nothing at all.}\n\n@event bakery_oldest_baker\ntitle: The Oldest Baker\nplace: bakery\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: letter\ntags: social\nscene: A very old woman in a flour-dusted apron sitting by warm bread ovens, looking up hopefully as the shop door opens.\ntext: The oldest of the bakers sits by the ovens, too old to knead and too stubborn to leave. {She asks, as she asks every student, whether the post at the castle is reliable these days.|She asks about the castle post. Her daughter gives you a look that says: just answer.|\"Is the post reliable?\" she asks. You've learned the answer she likes.} {~The ovens tick as they heat.|Flour hangs in the light.|The bell over the door rings, and she looks up, every time.}\n> left: Sit with her a while\n  lore +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~She tells you about her grandmother's grandmother, who waited for a letter. She doesn't say how it ended.|She tells you the family has baked here for two hundred years. She makes it sound like a promise kept.|She falls asleep mid-story. Her daughter tucks a bun into your pocket and says thank you.}\n> right: Help knead the dough\n  vigor +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {~The dough fights back. Your arms ache by the time the trays go in.|The baker shows you the turn of the wrist. You don't have it yet.|You knead until your shoulders burn. The first loaf out has your thumbprint on it.}\n\n@event bakery_cooling_rack\ntitle: The Cooling Rack\nplace: bakery\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: door\ntags: life\nscene: A tray of steaming buns cooling on the sill of an open bakery window, a crow and a student both eyeing it.\ntext: {Behind the bakery a window stands open, and on the sill a tray of buns is cooling, unwatched.|The cooling tray is back on the sill, nearer the edge this time. The bakers either trust everyone or are testing you.|The tray, the sill, the open window. It's practically an invitation.} {~A crow is eyeing it too.|Two first-years are pretending not to look at it.|Steam curls off the tray into the cold air.}\n> left: Lift one off the sill\n  challenge deceive easy:\n    win:\n      mood +small\n      alignment -tiny\n      say: {~You get away with it. It tastes slightly of guilt and mostly of cinnamon.|Clean away. The crow watches you with professional respect.}\n    lose:\n      gold -small\n      renown -tiny\n      say: {~A floury hand appears from the window, palm up. You pay double.|Caught by the baker's son, who tells everyone in the queue.}\n> right: Ask for yesterday's\n  charm +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~The baker gives you a bag of yesterday's for nothing. They're as hard as roof slates.|You get the broken ones free. You eat one and lose a little faith in bread.|\"For the student who asks,\" says the baker, and hands you a stale one with a wink.}\n\n# ---------------- Home: the family cottage ----------------\n\n@event home_cottage_old_room\ntitle: Your Old Room\nplace: home_cottage\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: bed\ntags: home\nscene: A small childhood bedroom under the eaves, a too-short bed, a window looking out over fields.\ntext: {Your old room is exactly as you left it, except smaller.|Someone has been storing apples in your old room.|Your old room has started to feel like a guest room, and you like it less for that.} {season == \"winter\": There's frost on the inside of the window, as always.|The window won't shut properly in the heat, as always.} Downstairs, the house is already awake.\n> left: Sleep until noon\n  health +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~You sleep like a stone. Downstairs, someone has done all your chores, loudly.|You wake to the smell of lunch and a pointed silence.|You sleep the clock round. You feel human, and slightly ashamed.}\n> right: Get up with the house\n  mood +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {final_year: {~You go down in the dark and realise there won't be many more mornings like this.|You memorise the kitchen without meaning to.}|{~You help with the fire. Nobody says much. Nobody needs to.|The creaky stair announces you. Someone hands you a cup before you've asked.}}\n\n@event home_cottage_list\ntitle: The List on the Table\nplace: home_cottage\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: paper\ntags: home\nscene: A kitchen table with a long handwritten list of chores weighted down by a teapot, a student in academy robes reading it with dismay.\ntext: {Home has remembered that you know where everything is.|A day home, and already a list.|The list. Your name, underlined.} {has(origin_noble): {&The steward wants the ledgers checked.|The steward wants the tenants' letters answered.}|{has(origin_merchant): {&The shop's stock needs counting.|The shop sign needs repainting.}|{has(origin_hedge): {&Your grandmother wants herbs hung to dry.|Your grandmother wants every jar labelled.}|{~The woodpile needs splitting.|The hen house door argues.}}}}\n> left: Work through the whole list\n  vigor +tiny\n  mood +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {~By supper the list is done, and someone you love says \"Well.\" It's the highest praise in the house.|It takes all day. You sleep better than you have all term.|You finish, and the list grows a second page in a different hand.}\n> right: Do it with magic\n  challenge enchant using arcana easy:\n    win:\n      arcana +small\n      mood +tiny\n      say: {~The work does itself while you supervise from a chair. The family is impressed and slightly unnerved.|It works. Your mother says it's cheating, then asks if you can do the gutters.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      health -tiny\n      say: {~The spell goes slightly wrong. Something is now upside down that wasn't before.|Soot. Everywhere. Someone laughs until they cry, then hands you a broom.}\n\n@event home_cottage_after_supper\ntitle: The Same Question\nplace: home_cottage\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: candle\ntags: home\nscene: A family kitchen after supper by candlelight, dishes drying, everyone turned towards one student.\ntext: After supper, when the dishes are done, someone always asks it. {\"So what will you do, after?\"|\"Still don't know what you'll do after?\" An aunt, this time.|By now you could ask it yourself, in any of their voices, and someone does.} {final_year: This time the answer is close enough to touch.|{~The fire pops.|Nobody pretends not to be listening.|Someone pours more tea to give you time.}}\n> left: Tell them the truth\n  charm +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {~You say you don't know. It's quiet for a moment, then someone says that's allowed.|You tell them what you're afraid of. It sounds smaller said aloud at this table.|The truth makes your mother go quiet, and then hug you, which you weren't ready for.}\n> right: Give the polished answer\n  guile +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~You give the answer you'd give a professor. They're proud. It sits oddly in your chest all night.|You make it sound like a plan. Everyone relaxes except you.|\"Very sensible,\" says an aunt. You feel as if you've told her a story.}\n\n# ---------------- Home: the fields ----------------\n\n@event home_fields_long_rows\ntitle: The Long Rows\nplace: home_fields\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: leaf\ntags: home nature\nscene: A student walking alone between long furrowed rows towards a low wall at the edge of the fields, the horizon wide and empty.\ntext: {You walk the long rows to the field's end, where you used to imagine towers.|You've seen the towers now. The horizon at the end of the rows still pulls.|The walk to the field's end is a habit now. Your feet know it by heart.} {season == \"winter\": The furrows crunch with frost.|The wheat is high and loud with insects.} {~A hare bolts.|A crow follows you at a respectful distance.|Smoke rises from a chimney you can't see.}\n> left: Walk the whole boundary\n  vigor +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {~You come back muddy to the knees and hungry as a first-year.|You walk until your legs ache and your head is finally quiet.|You sit on the low wall where you always sat, and the wind does the thinking.}\n> right: Practise where no one watches\n  arcana +small\n  mana -small\n  say: {~You set a scarecrow's hat spinning. Nobody sees. It's glorious.|You practise until your hands tingle and the crows give you a wide berth.|You try the spell you keep failing at school. Out here, with nobody watching, it nearly works.}\n\n@event home_fields_neighbours\ntitle: Neighbours' Work\nplace: home_fields\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: group\ntags: home social\nscene: Neighbours working together at the edge of a field, one waving a student in academy robes over to help.\ntext: {season == \"winter\": The neighbours are hauling wood before the next snow.|The neighbours are bringing in the hay before the weather turns.} {They wave you over without asking if you're busy.|This time somebody has saved you a pair of gloves.|They've stopped waving. They just hand you the gloves.} {~The neighbour's boy wants to be turned into a frog.|A farmer calls you \"the scholar\", mostly kindly.|Someone has brought a jug of mostly cider.}\n> left: Lend a hand\n  vigor +small\n  health -small\n  say: {~Blisters by noon, and a supper invitation from three households.|You work till dusk. Nobody mentions the academy once, which is restful.|Your back reminds you that books are lighter than anything out here.}\n> right: Bring them all a drink\n  gold -tiny\n  charm +small\n  say: {~You come back from the market with a basket and become the most popular person in the field.|They drink to the academy, then to you, then to the weather.|The neighbour's boy asks for a frog again. You give him a biscuit; it's a compromise.}\n\n@event home_fields_pond\ntitle: The Old Pond\nplace: home_fields\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: wave\ntags: home nature\nscene: A pond at the bottom of a field under an old willow, local youngsters daring each other at its edge.\ntext: {season == \"winter\": The pond at the bottom of the fields has frozen over, and the local youngsters are daring each other onto the ice.|The pond at the bottom of the fields is warm and green, and the local youngsters are daring each other off the willow.} {You used to be one of them.|This time they dare you too.|They've started daring you first.} {~A dog barks at the whole business.|Someone's little sister is keeping score.|Your old initials are still carved in the willow.}\n> left: Take the dare\n  chance 60:\n    mood +small\n    vigor +tiny\n    say: {season == \"winter\": {~You skate across and back to roaring applause from three children and a dog.|You slide the whole length on your back. It counts.}|{~You drop off the willow into the green. It's colder than memory, and better.|A perfect splash. The little sister gives it top marks.}}\n  else:\n    health -small\n    mood -tiny\n    say: {season == \"winter\": {~You go through the ice up to your knees and limp home to a lecture and a fire.|The ice cracks under you and you scramble back, soaked and laughed at.}|{~You belly-flop. The sound carries across three fields. Everyone heard.|You slip off the willow early and land in the reeds, muddy to the ears.}}\n> right: Hold the coats\n  charm +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~You hold the coats and referee the arguments. Someone has to be the grown-up now.|You keep score and are accused of bias by every side.|Holding the coats, you feel very old and quite content.}\n\n# ---------------- Home: the market town ----------------\n\n@event home_market_inn_crowd\ntitle: Outside the Inn\nplace: home_market\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: cup\ntags: home social\nscene: A knot of young locals outside a country inn, raising their cups as a student in academy robes walks up.\ntext: {The old crowd is outside the inn, exactly where you left them. \"Look who it is,\" someone says. \"The wizard.\"|The old crowd have been rehearsing the wizard joke. It has a second part now.|The old crowd outside the inn. Fewer of them every break: somebody married, somebody moved away.} {season == \"winter\": They're stamping their feet against the cold.|They're sitting on the inn steps in the long light.}\n> left: Buy a round\n  gold -small\n  mood +small\n  charm +tiny\n  say: {~They drink your health and ask if you can make the beer stronger. You can't. You're asked again.|For an hour it's as if you never left.|Someone tells the story of the time you fell in the duck pond. It has grown since you left.}\n> right: Stay for the gossip only\n  guile +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~You learn who's courting, who's feuding and whose goat ate whose washing. Somebody mutters that academy folk are tight with a coin.|You keep your crowns in your pocket and get very well informed.|They notice you didn't buy a round. They notice everything.}\n\n@event home_market_charm_seller\ntitle: The Charm Seller\nplace: home_market\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: gem\ntags: home money\nscene: A market stall hung with ribbons and trinkets under a hand-painted sign about academy blessings, a grandmother reaching for her purse.\ntext: {A stall in the market is selling \"genuine Lumenhold charms, blessed in the seven towers\".|The charm seller has new charms and a bigger sign. The claims have grown to match.|The charm seller sees your robes and starts packing up, then thinks better of it.} {has(origin_merchant): You know that markup. You were raised on it.|{~A grandmother is buying one for luck.|The charms are bent nails tied with ribbon.|One of them is, worryingly, glowing.}}\n> left: Call out the fakes\n  challenge negotiate easy:\n    win:\n      renown +small\n      mood -tiny\n      say: {~The crowd turns on him. A grandmother gets her coin back and gives you a cake. The seller gives you a look.|He folds, and offers you a share of the trade. You decline. He seems genuinely surprised.}\n    lose:\n      renown -tiny\n      mood -small\n      say: {~The crowd takes his side. \"Academy snob,\" someone says, buying two.|He out-talks you in front of everyone. You'd admire it if it were happening to someone else.}\n> right: Buy one for an aunt\n  gold -tiny\n  mood +small\n  say: {~Your aunt will love it. It's a bent nail. She will love it anyway.|You buy one. It's useless, and the old woman beside you beams at you with approval.|You buy one that glows a little. You decide not to think about why.}\n\n@event home_market_post_office\ntitle: Letters to the Castle\nplace: home_market\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: letter\ntags: home\nscene: A post office the size of a cupboard, a clerk ceremoniously weighing a stack of letters addressed to a castle on a lake.\ntext: {The town's post office is the size of a cupboard, and its clerk has never posted anything to a magic academy.|The clerk has started weighing your letters with ceremony.|The clerk reaches for the castle stamp before you speak.} {season == \"winter\": The window is fogged; it smells of sealing wax.|Flies wander in to read the notices.} {final_year: Soon you'll write to the castle from elsewhere.|You think about who at the castle you miss.}\n> left: Write to everyone\n  gold -tiny\n  mood +small\n  say: {~You write until your hand cramps. The clerk sighs at the stack.|You draw the cottage for someone who has never seen one.|You start one letter with \"I miss\" and decide to leave it that way.}\n> right: Write the essay you owe\n  lore +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~You write the essay on the clerk's counter, between parcels. It's not bad.|You post an essay to a professor. The clerk asks if it'll get you into trouble. Probably.|You finish it and post it. It's the dullest thing the clerk has ever weighed.}\n"},{"path":"content/events/slice_2a.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Phase 2a vertical slice (design §13): ten events that use every\n#  construct of the new event format — classes and tones, size\n#  tiers and presets, challenges with partial results and `vs`,\n#  spells and the swipe-up option, inline text variation, facts,\n#  memories and relations between characters — and teach two\n#  spells, Ward and Flame.\n# ============================================================\n\n@event slice_ward_lesson\ntitle: The First Ward\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\nactor: thorne\nwhen: met(thorne) and turn >= 3 and spell_level(ward) == 0\nweight: 24\nart: shield\ntags: class study magic\nscene: An office lit by one lamp, an old professor holding a pale shell of light between his palms like a soap bubble.\ntext: Professor Thorne holds up a hand and the lamplight bends around it into a pale shell. \"A ward,\" he says. \"{~It stops one blow.|One blow. That's all it ever stops.} Learn it properly or not at all.\"\n> left: Practise until it holds\n  grind arcana\n  learn_spell ward\n  rel thorne +small\n  memory: confided thorne\n  say: By midnight your shell holds for a full breath. Thorne says \"Hm,\" which from him is a standing ovation.\n> right: Ask about the theory first\n  lore +small\n  mood -tiny\n  rel thorne +tiny\n  say: He talks for an hour about lattices and intent. You understand perhaps a third of it. \"Come back when the third is a half,\" he says.\n\n@event slice_candle_flame\ntitle: Borrowed Fire\nclass: milestone\ntone: light\nactor: lyra\nwhen: met(lyra) and turn >= 4 and spell_level(flame) == 0\nweight: 24\nart: flame\ntags: class magic rival\nscene: A crimson-haired student balancing a flame on one fingertip while another student squints at a sputtering candle.\ntext: Lyra watches you fail to light a candle for the third time. \"You're asking it,\" she says. \"Fire doesn't want to be asked. {rel(lyra) >= 12: Here — watch my hand.|Watch, if you can keep up.}\"\n> left: Watch her hand\n  learn_spell flame\n  rel lyra +small\n  spark lyra +tiny\n  learn lyra.prodigy\n  say: She doesn't explain. She just does it, slowly, twice. On the third try your candle catches, and so, very briefly, does your sleeve.\n> right: Challenge her to teach you properly\n  challenge duel vs lyra.arcana medium:\n    win:\n      learn_spell flame\n      rel lyra +medium\n      renown +small\n      say: You hold your own long enough that she stops and actually explains. \"Fine,\" she says. \"You'll do.\"\n    partial:\n      learn_spell flame\n      health -small\n      say: She wins, but she shows you the trick on the way out, as if it were nothing.\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      mood -small\n      say: You end up on the floor with singed eyebrows. Lyra steps over you. \"Ask better,\" she says.\n\n@event slice_stray_cold\ntitle: The Drift\nclass: incident\ntone: eerie\nwhen: turn >= 5\nweight: 16\nart: snow\ntags: mystery danger\nscene: A corridor at night where frost spreads across the flagstones toward a student holding a candle.\ntext: Cold drifts down the corridor like a tide coming in, and the torches lean away from it. {spell_level(ward) > 0: Your ward prickles at your fingertips.|You have nothing to hold it back.}\n> left: Run for the stairs\n  health -small\n  mood -small\n  say: You make the stairs with frost in your hair. Behind you, the corridor sighs and is just a corridor again.\n> right: Stand and study it\n  challenge research hard:\n    win:\n      lore +medium\n      alignment +small\n      say: It's not weather. It's attention: something under the lake, looking. You write down everything before your hands warm up.\n    lose:\n      health -medium\n      say: You learn exactly one thing, which is how cold cold can be. Sister Amaranth wraps you in four blankets.\n> up[ward]: Raise a ward\n  mood +small\n  alignment +tiny\n  say: Your ward flares, pale and steady. The cold breaks against it and slides away, the way a wave gives up on a rock.\n\n@event slice_late_lamps\ntime: evening night\ntitle: The Last Lamp\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nwhen: turn >= 2\nweight: 14\nrepeat: 6\nart: candle\ntags: study\nscene: The library at closing time, one desk still lit, a cat asleep on an open book.\ntext: {&The library is emptying.|The library is emptying again.|The library empties around you, as usual.} Footnote, the library cat, is asleep on the book you need, and the last lamp is yours if you want it.\n> left: Keep the lamp\n  grind lore\n  say: {~You read until the words swim.|You read until Footnote leaves in protest.|You read until the night porter coughs pointedly.}\n> right: Go to bed\n  health +small\n  say: You sleep like a stone. The book will still be there. So, probably, will Footnote.\n\n@event slice_quarrel\ntitle: A Quarrel in the Snow\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nactor: kael\nwhen: met(kael) and met(lyra) and turn >= 6\nweight: 16\nart: snow\ntags: social rival\nscene: Two students facing each other in a snowy courtyard, one breathing frost, the other with sparks at her fingertips.\ntext: Kael and Lyra are facing each other in the courtyard, and the snow between them is half melted, half frozen. \"Say it again,\" Lyra says. Kael doesn't. He just looks at you.\n> left: Step between them\n  alignment +tiny\n  npc_rel lyra kael +small\n  rel kael +small\n  health -tiny\n  memory: protected kael\n  say: Lyra's sparks catch your sleeve, Kael's frost puts it out. They both stare at you. The fight, somehow, is over.\n> right: Walk past\n  npc_rel lyra kael -medium\n  rel kael -small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: Behind you, something cracks: ice, or temper. At dinner they sit at opposite ends of the hall.\n\n@event slice_burned_letter\ntitle: Ashes in the Grate\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nactor: lyra\nwhen: rel(lyra) >= 6 and not knows(lyra.mother)\nweight: 18\nart: letter\ntags: social\nscene: A fireplace where the corner of a letter with a black seal is curling into flame.\ntext: Lyra is feeding a letter into the common-room fire, a corner at a time. Black seal. She doesn't look round. \"It says 'well done',\" she says. \"It doesn't. I'm checking whether it burns better if I pretend.\"\n> left: Sit with her\n  learn lyra.mother\n  rel lyra +medium\n  mood -small\n  memory: confided lyra\n  say: You watch it burn together. \"She's never said it,\" Lyra says, to the fire. \"Not once.\" You don't say anything, and that seems to be right.\n> right: Make a joke\n  rel lyra -small\n  mood +tiny\n  say: She almost smiles. Then she puts the rest of the letter in at once and leaves you with the smoke.\n\n@event slice_hollow_whisper\ntitle: A Voice in the Water\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\nwhen: turn >= 7\nweight: 12\nart: wave\ntags: mystery hollowstar\nchain: hollowstar\nscene: A student kneeling at the edge of a still lake at night, their reflection smiling when they are not.\ntext: At the lakeshore your reflection speaks before you do. \"You're running low,\" it says, kindly. \"{mana < 15: I could fill you up.|I could make you stronger.} Just say yes. Saying yes costs nothing.\"\n> left: Say yes\n  mana +large\n  alignment -medium\n  say: Cold pours into you, generous and endless. When you stand, your reflection is a little slower to stand with you.\n> right: Walk away\n  alignment +small\n  mood -small\n  say: The reflection's smile stays on the water after you've gone. You don't look back. You can feel it not minding.\n\n@event slice_market_haggle\ntitle: The Spice Stall\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nwhen: turn >= 3\nweight: 12\nrepeat: 8\nart: coin\ntags: money\nscene: A crowded village market stall heaped with jars of glowing spices and a stall-holder with an enormous moustache.\ntext: The stall-holder in the village market wants thirty crowns for a jar of starpepper. Mabel swears by it for colds. The stall-holder swears by his prices.\n> left: Haggle\n  challenge negotiate easy:\n    win:\n      gold -small\n      health +small\n      say: Eight crowns and a compliment on your persistence. Mabel says you've been robbed, but only slightly.\n    partial:\n      gold -medium\n      health +small\n      say: Fifteen crowns. He throws in a free sneeze's worth of pepper, which you did not want.\n    lose:\n      gold -large\n      mood -tiny\n      say: You pay full price and somehow agree to a second jar. He waves you off like a nephew.\n> right: Leave it\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You walk on. You catch a cold the next week, out of spite.\n\n@event slice_wager\ntitle: The Tower Wager\nclass: windfall\ntone: light\nwhen: renown >= 3 and turn >= 4\nweight: 5\nart: dice\ntags: social\nscene: A knot of students around a table where coins and a pocket watch are being stacked as bets.\ntext: Half the common room is betting on whether you can climb the clock tower before the bell. Corvin is holding the purse, which is either reassuring or not.\n> left: Climb\n  risk renown\n  vigor +tiny\n  say: {~You make it with a heartbeat to spare.|The bell rings as your hand touches the top.} Whatever happens next, they'll tell it differently.\n> right: Decline politely\n  renown -tiny\n  say: The crowd groans. Corvin pockets the purse thoughtfully and you decide not to ask.\n\n@event slice_frost_duel\ntitle: Frost Against Fire\nclass: milestone\ntone: tense\nactor: kael\nwhen: met(kael) and rel(kael) >= 9 and turn >= 8\nweight: 20\nart: duel\ntags: duel social\nscene: A duelling ring at dawn, frost spreading from one side and a faint warmth glowing from the other.\ntext: Kael asks for a practice duel, which for him is an astonishing number of words. \"Not to win,\" he adds. \"I want to see what you do when it's cold.\"\n> left: Duel him straight\n  memory: fought kael\n  challenge combat vs kael.arcana hard:\n    win:\n      renown +small\n      rel kael +medium\n      spark kael +small\n      say: You win by a whisker, breathing steam. Kael bows, and his hands, for once, are warm when he shakes yours.\n    partial:\n      rel kael +small\n      health -small\n      say: A draw, of sorts: you're both on the ground. He laughs, a short surprised sound, and helps you up.\n    lose:\n      health -medium\n      rel kael +small\n      say: He wins, gently. \"You stopped when it got cold,\" he says. \"Don't.\"\n> right: Decline; offer him tea instead\n  rel kael +small\n  mood +tiny\n  say: He thinks about this for a long time. \"Tea,\" he says. It freezes in his cup. You make him another.\n> up[flame]: Answer with fire\n  rel kael +medium\n  spark kael +small\n  renown +small\n  memory: fought kael\n  say: Your flame meets his frost in a column of steam that fills the ring. When it clears, Kael is smiling at you, properly, for the first time.\n"},{"path":"content/events/slice_2b.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Phase 2b vertical slice (design §13): the world loop in the new\n#  format. The Evocation course (3 levels), the Duelling Circle, the\n#  Library Assistant job, five campus places, break plans, the\n#  Royal Library internship, the thesis, and a four-event arc\n#  (The Bell Under the Water). Uses cast roles, say[npc],\n#  present(place), queue within … and unqueue.\n# ============================================================\n\n# ---------------- Evocation (track: evocation) ----------------\n\n@event evo_first_spark\ntitle: The First Spark\ntrack: evocation\nlevel: 1\nlesson: lumenhold\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nart: flame\ntags: class magic\nscene: A lecture hall with scorch marks on every desk, students holding their palms up as if testing for rain.\ntext: Evocation I begins with a single instruction from the lectern: \"Make heat. Not fire. Heat.\" Half the class makes nothing. One student makes a small, apologetic fire.\n> left: Aim for heat, carefully\n  arcana +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: Your palm warms like a stone left in the sun. The lecturer nods, which is better than most get.\n> right: Aim for fire, boldly\n  challenge exam using arcana easy:\n    win:\n      arcana +medium\n      renown +tiny\n      say: A neat little flame, exactly where you meant it. The lecturer writes your name down. Approvingly, you hope.\n    lose:\n      health -tiny\n      arcana +tiny\n      say: Your sleeve learns more about fire than you do. The lecture continues around the smell.\n\n@event evo_puddle_drill\ntitle: Puddle Drill\ntrack: evocation\nlevel: 1\nlesson: mirror_lake\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nart: wave\ntags: class magic\nscene: Students crouched over puddles in a stone courtyard, trying to make the water stand up.\ntext: Tidecalling half of the course: a puddle each, and the task of making it stand up and stay up. Yours trembles, considers it, and lies back down.\n> left: Practise until it stands\n  grind arcana\n  say: By the end of the hour your puddle stands like a sulky child. It counts.\n> right: Copy the student next to you\n  alignment -tiny\n  arcana +tiny\n  guile +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You learn their trick, and also that they hum while they cast. You hum too. It works, inexplicably.\n\n@event evo_misfire\ntitle: Misfire\ntrack: evocation\nlevel: 1\nclass: incident\ntone: tense\nart: bolt\ntags: class danger\nscene: A lecture hall filling with steam after a spell meets a bucket of water at the wrong moment.\ntext: Someone's tidecalling meets someone else's flame in the middle of the room. The hall fills with scalding steam and twenty students who all have opinions.\n> left: Shield the front row\n  health -small\n  renown +small\n  say: You take the worst of it on your sleeves. The front row looks at you the way people look at lifeboats.\n> right: Get out and open the windows\n  mood -tiny\n  say: The steam pours out into the courtyard and becomes, briefly, a very small cloud with a grudge.\n\n@event evo_tide_wall\ntitle: The Tide Wall\ntrack: evocation\nlevel: 2\nlesson: mirror_lake\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nart: wave\ntags: class magic\nscene: A student holding back a wall of lake water on the shore while classmates watch from behind them.\ntext: Evocation II takes the class to the lakeshore. The exercise: raise a wall of water and hold it while a classmate walks past behind it. Your classmate looks nervous. So do you.\n> left: Hold it steady\n  challenge exam using arcana medium:\n    win:\n      arcana +medium\n      say: The wall holds. Your classmate walks past dry and amazed. You let it down gently, which is the hard part.\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      mood -small\n      say: The wall holds until it doesn't. Your classmate is very wet and very gracious about it.\n> right: Ask to go second\n  arcana +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You watch three people do it first and learn from all of their mistakes.\n\n@event evo_lattice\ntitle: The Flame Lattice\ntrack: evocation\nlevel: 2\nlesson: founders_seal\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nart: flame\ntags: class study\nscene: Interlocking threads of fire hanging in the air of a lecture hall like a glowing net.\ntext: Fire woven into a lattice, each strand holding the others up. The lecturer says the founders built wards like this. The lecturer also says you have twenty minutes.\n> left: Build it slowly\n  lore +small\n  arcana +small\n  mood -small\n  say: Your lattice is small and ugly and holds perfectly. The lecturer says \"Founder-ish,\" which you choose to take as praise.\n> right: Build it big\n  risk arcana\n  say: {~It's magnificent for nine seconds.|It holds just long enough for everyone to see it.} Then the strands find each other and the whole thing sighs into sparks.\n\n@event evo_storm_pairing\ntitle: Storm Pairing\ntrack: evocation\nlevel: 3\nclass: milestone\ntone: tense\nart: bolt\ntags: class duel\ncast:\n  partner: any where (tagged(partner, student) or tagged(partner, prefect))\nscene: Two students on a windswept tower top, one calling rain, the other calling lightning, the sky between them churning.\ntext: Advanced Evocation pairs students for a storm: one calls the rain, the other the lightning, and neither is allowed to let go first. You're paired with {partner}.\n> left: Call the lightning\n  challenge exam using arcana hard:\n    win:\n      arcana +large\n      rel partner +small\n      say: The storm answers both of you at once. {partner} laughs into the wind, soaked and astonished.\n      say[lyra]: The storm answers both of you at once. Lyra looks at you as though you've finally said something worth hearing.\n    lose:\n      health -medium\n      rel partner +tiny\n      say: The lightning goes where it likes. You both spend an afternoon in the infirmary, comparing burns.\n> right: Call the rain, and let them lead\n  arcana +medium\n  rel partner +small\n  say: You hold the rain steady while {partner} draws the lightning down. Afterwards {they} thank{s} you for not trying to steal it.\n\n@event evo_final_practical\ntitle: The Final Practical\ntrack: evocation\nlevel: 3\nclass: story\ntone: tense\nart: flame\ntags: class exam\nscene: An examination hall cleared of desks, a single student facing three examiners and a target dummy made of ice.\ntext: The last practical of Advanced Evocation: melt an ice dummy without touching the floor around it. Professor Thorne is on the panel, and he is not blinking.\n> left: Fire, precisely\n  challenge exam using arcana hard:\n    win:\n      arcana +large\n      study evocation +medium\n      rel thorne +small\n      say: The ice runs off the dummy and not a drop touches the floor. Thorne says \"Hm,\" and writes something underlined.\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      say: The dummy melts, and so does a fair part of the floor. Thorne writes something with a question mark.\n> right: Water, patiently\n  arcana +medium\n  lore +small\n  say: You draw the cold out of the ice with water instead of fire. It takes twice as long. The panel watches every minute.\n\n# ---------------- Duelling Circle (track: duelling) ----------------\n\n@event duel_first_practice\ntitle: Footwork\ntrack: duelling\nrank: 1\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nart: duel\ntags: club duel\nscene: A duelling hall with chalk circles on the floor and practice dummies with names painted on their chests.\ntext: Lyra runs practice like a general. \"Footwork,\" she says. \"If your feet are wrong, your spell is a rumour.\" She pairs you with a dummy called Gerald the Second.\n> left: Drill footwork\n  vigor +small\n  perform track +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: Two hours of stepping in circles. Gerald the Second wins every round. Your feet, however, are now correct.\n> right: Spar for real\n  challenge combat vs lyra.vigor easy:\n    win:\n      vigor +medium\n      rel lyra +small\n      say: You land a clean disarm on your partner. Across the hall, Lyra raises an eyebrow, which is her version of a standing ovation.\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      say: You spend the evening relearning which way is up. Lyra says that's the first lesson anyway.\n\n@event duel_footwork_feud\ntitle: The Footwork Feud\ntrack: duelling\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nart: duel\ntags: club rival\nscene: Two students arguing in the middle of a duelling circle while the rest of the club pretends not to listen.\ntext: Bram says the Circle's footwork is \"Emberhall nonsense\" and Lyra says Bram's footwork is \"a hazard to shipping\". Both of them look at you to settle it.\n> left: Side with Lyra\n  rel lyra +small\n  rel bram -small\n  npc_rel lyra bram -tiny\n  say: Lyra nods as if this were obvious. Bram takes a demerit book out of his pocket, looks at it, and puts it back. Progress.\n> right: Side with Bram\n  rel bram +small\n  rel lyra -small\n  say: Bram looks startled to be agreed with. Lyra looks at you like a puzzle she will solve later, with fire.\n\n@event duel_fixture\ntitle: Fixture Against Thornwick\ntrack: duelling\nrank: 2\nclass: milestone\ntone: tense\nart: trophy\ntags: club duel festival\nscene: A duelling green with rival banners, students from both academies packed onto benches.\ntext: Thornwick Academy's duelling team has crossed the hills for a fixture. The Circle needs one more duellist in the line-up, and the captain is looking at you.\n> left: Duel for the Circle\n  challenge combat hard:\n    win:\n      renown +medium\n      perform track +medium\n      say: You win your bout, and the Circle wins the fixture by a whisker. Someone carries you a few steps before remembering they don't like you.\n    partial:\n      renown +small\n      perform track +small\n      say: A draw. The Circle loses by one bout, but nobody blames you, and Lyra says \"acceptable\" twice.\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      mood -small\n      say: The Thornwick duellist is very good and very polite about it. You hate both.\n> right: Offer to be the reserve\n  perform track +tiny\n  mood +tiny\n  say: You hold coats and bring water. It turns out reserves see everything, including how Thornwick cheats.\n\n@event duel_captain_call\ntitle: The Captain's Question\ntrack: duelling\nrank: 2\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\nart: duel\ntags: club\nscene: An empty duelling hall at night, two students sitting on the edge of a chalk circle.\ntext: After practice, the captain sits beside you. \"When I graduate, someone has to run this place,\" she says. \"Someone who turns up. You turn up.\"\n> left: Say you'll think about it\n  perform track +small\n  rank_xp track +medium\n  say: \"Think fast,\" she says. \"Feet first, then spells.\" You're fairly sure that's a yes.\n> right: Say you're not the type\n  mood +tiny\n  say: \"Nobody's the type,\" she says. \"That's why it has to be someone who turns up.\"\n\n# ---------------- Library Assistant (track: library_assistant) ----------------\n\n@event lib_shelving_rush\ntitle: The Shelving Rush\ntrack: library_assistant\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nart: book\ntags: job study\nscene: A library cart overflowing with returned books, a student pushing it at a run between towering shelves.\ntext: Exam week, and every book in the library has been borrowed, read and returned at once. The cart is taller than you are. Footnote is sitting on top of it.\n> left: Shelve properly\n  wage\n  lore +small\n  perform track +small\n  mood -small\n  say: Every book in its place by closing. Footnote has moved to the top shelf to supervise.\n> right: Shelve quickly\n  wage\n  perform track -tiny\n  mood +tiny\n  say: The books are on shelves. Most of them are even on the right shelves. Someone will find out in a decade.\n\n@event lib_overdue_mage\ntitle: The Overdue Mage\ntrack: library_assistant\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nart: hourglass\ntags: job\nscene: An elderly mage at the library desk clutching a book that is sixty years overdue.\ntext: An old mage returns a book sixty years overdue and asks, with great dignity, what the fine is. The ledger says the fine is more than the library.\n> left: Waive it\n  wage\n  rel thorne +tiny\n  perform track -tiny\n  mood +small\n  say: The old mage thanks you and leaves a sweet on the desk. Later you find out he taught Professor Thorne.\n> right: Charge it, by the book\n  wage\n  gold +small\n  perform track +small\n  rel thorne -tiny\n  say: The mage pays in coins so old the bank has to look them up. The head librarian is delighted.\n\n@event lib_footnote_strike\ntitle: Footnote Goes on Strike\ntrack: library_assistant\nclass: incident\ntone: light\nart: cat\ntags: job\nscene: A large library cat lying across a reference desk, refusing to move, while students queue in front of it.\ntext: Footnote has lain down across the reference desk and will not move. Students are queueing. The cat has made its demands clear: none.\n> left: Bribe him with fish\n  wage\n  gold -tiny\n  perform track +small\n  say: Footnote accepts the fish, the apology and the chair you bring him. The queue resumes.\n> right: Work around him\n  wage\n  perform track -tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You stamp books on the far end of the desk for an hour. Footnote watches with the calm of a cat who has won.\n\n# ---------------- Places ----------------\n\n@event court_fountain_freeze\ntitle: The Frozen Fountain\nplace: courtyard\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nart: snow\ntags: mystery\nscene: A courtyard fountain frozen mid-splash, the water hanging in the air like glass.\ntext: The courtyard fountain has frozen in the middle of a splash. The water hangs in the air like glass. A first-year is trying to lick it.\n> left: Stop the first-year\n  rel kael +tiny\n  health -tiny\n  alignment +small\n  say: You pull the first-year away just as the ice cracks. For a moment you'd swear you saw a black star reflected in it.\n> right: Study the ice\n  lore +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: The ice is colder than ice should be. Your notes say \"colder than ice should be\" three times.\n\n@event court_rumour_mill\ntitle: The Rumour Mill\nplace: courtyard\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nart: group\ntags: social\ncast:\n  gossip: present(place) where tagged(gossip, peer)\nscene: Students huddled around the courtyard fountain trading whispers, one of them clearly enjoying it most.\ntext: {gossip} has news, which is to say gossip, which is to say three different stories about the same broken window. \"You'll want to hear this,\" {they} say{s}.\n> left: Listen to all of it\n  rel gossip +small\n  mood +small\n  guile -tiny\n  say: You hear all three stories. Two are true. One is about you.\n  say[nessa]: You hear all three stories. Nessa is already writing the fourth.\n> right: Make up a better one\n  guile +small\n  rel gossip +tiny\n  renown +tiny\n  alignment -tiny\n  say: Your story is better than all three. By dinner it's the official version.\n\n@event lake_skimming\ntitle: Skimming Stones\nplace: lakeshore\nclass: windfall\ntone: warm\nart: wave\ntags: nature\ncast:\n  friend: any where tagged(friend, peer) and rel(friend) >= 6\nscene: Two students skimming stones across a perfectly still lake at dusk.\ntext: {friend} is skimming stones on the Mirror Lake. The water is so still each stone leaves rings that go on forever. \"Seven,\" {they} say{s}. \"Beat that.\"\n> left: Try to beat it\n  challenge duel easy:\n    win:\n      rel friend +small\n      mood +small\n      say: Nine skips. {friend} demands a rematch and gets one. The afternoon disappears into stones.\n    lose:\n      rel friend +small\n      mood +tiny\n      say: Your stone sinks with great dignity. {friend} laughs until {they} have to sit down.\n> right: Just watch the rings\n  mood +small\n  rel friend +tiny\n  say: You sit and watch the rings spread until the light goes. Neither of you says much. It's one of the good afternoons.\n\n@event lake_reflection_bet\ntitle: The Reflection Bet\nplace: lakeshore\nclass: windfall\ntone: eerie\nart: mirror\ntags: mystery\nscene: A student staring into a still lake where their reflection appears to be holding a coin.\ntext: Your reflection in the Mirror Lake is holding a coin you don't have. It flips it. It catches it. It offers it to you.\n> left: Take the coin\n  gold +small\n  alignment -tiny\n  say: The coin is real, and cold, and you can't remember putting it in your pocket.\n> right: Shake your head\n  alignment +tiny\n  mood +tiny\n  say: Your reflection shrugs, pockets the coin and walks out of the frame before you do.\n\n@event ref_food_fight\ntitle: Pudding Diplomacy\nplace: refectory\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nart: meal\ntags: social\nscene: A refectory mid-food-fight, puddings in the air, a prefect rising slowly from behind a table.\ntext: A pudding sails across the refectory and lands in Bram's lap. The hall goes silent. Every eye turns to the table it came from, which is yours.\n> left: Own up\n  alignment +tiny\n  rel bram +small\n  renown +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: Bram writes you a demerit, then pauses. \"Good throw,\" he says, almost without wanting to.\n> right: Throw another\n  renown +small\n  rel bram -small\n  mood +small\n  say: The second pudding starts a war that the refectory will talk about for years. Historians will argue about who won. You know.\n\n@event ref_cook_secret\ntitle: The Cook's Secret\nplace: refectory\nclass: windfall\ntone: warm\nart: cup\ntags: home\nscene: A huge kitchen behind the refectory, an enormous cook stirring a pot with a spoon the size of an oar.\ntext: The cook catches you lingering by the kitchen door. \"You look like someone who misses proper soup,\" she says, and hands you a bowl before you can deny it.\n> left: Eat it slowly\n  health +small\n  mood +small\n  say: It tastes like the kitchen at home. You don't say so. She can tell anyway.\n> right: Ask for the recipe\n  alchemy +tiny\n  mood +tiny\n  say: \"Ha,\" says the cook, which means no, which means you'll have to keep coming back.\n\n@event obs_comet_watch\ntime: evening night\ntitle: The Comet Watch\nplace: observatory\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nart: planet\ntags: magic\nscene: The brass dome of an observatory open to the night, a student at the eyepiece, another beside them with a notebook.\ntext: A comet is due tonight and the observatory is full of students pretending they came for the science. The telescope has a queue. The roof has a view.\n> left: Queue for the telescope\n  lore +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: Your turn at the eyepiece lasts thirty seconds. The comet's tail is green. You'll remember it longer than most lectures.\n> right: Watch from the roof\n  mood +small\n  rel kael +tiny\n  health -tiny\n  say: The comet crosses the sky slowly, like it's looking for something. Kael is already on the roof. He moves over to make room.\n\n@event obs_star_chart\ntitle: The Missing Star\nplace: observatory\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\nart: star\ntags: mystery magic\nscene: An old star chart spread on a desk, one constellation with a careful empty circle drawn where a star should be.\ntext: An old star chart in the observatory archive has a circle drawn where a star should be, with a note in faded ink: \"It eats the light. Do not chart it.\"\n> left: Copy the chart\n  lore +small\n  say: You copy it carefully. That night you dream of a circle with nothing inside it, waiting.\n> right: Put it back\n  alignment +tiny\n  mood +tiny\n  say: You return it to the drawer. The drawer feels colder when you close it.\n\n@event green_mandrake_choir\ntitle: The Mandrake Choir\nplace: greenhouses\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nart: leaf\ntags: nature\nscene: A greenhouse bench of potted mandrakes wearing tiny earmuffs, a student conducting them with a pencil.\ntext: Tobias has taught the greenhouse mandrakes to scream in harmony. He would like your opinion. The mandrakes would like an audience.\n> left: Conduct a verse\n  rel tobias +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: You conduct one verse. Your ears ring for a day. Tobias says it's the best performance they've given.\n> right: Wear earmuffs and listen\n  rel tobias +tiny\n  mood +small\n  gold -tiny\n  say: Through the earmuffs it sounds almost beautiful. Tobias is so proud he cries a little.\n\n@event green_moonflower_sprout\ntitle: A Moonflower Seedling\nplace: greenhouses\nclass: windfall\ntone: warm\nart: flower\ntags: nature\nscene: A tiny silver-leaved seedling in a clay pot, glowing faintly in a dark greenhouse.\ntext: In a forgotten corner of the greenhouse, a moonflower seed has sprouted on its own, eleven years early. It glows faintly when you lean over it.\n> left: Pot it and look after it\n  alchemy +small\n  mood +small\n  say: You give it a pot, a label and a name. It glows a little brighter when you visit.\n> right: Tell Tobias\n  rel tobias +medium\n  say: Tobias is speechless, then very loud. The seedling gets a sign, a fence and a rota.\n\n# ---------------- Break days ----------------\n\n@event castle_empty_halls\ntitle: Empty Halls\nplan: castle\nclass: windfall\ntone: warm\nart: tower\ntags: home\nscene: A long castle corridor in the holidays, dust in sunbeams, one student walking alone.\ntext: The castle in the holidays is enormous and quiet. Your footsteps echo down corridors you've never been alone in. A door you've never noticed is standing open.\n> left: Go through the door\n  guile +small\n  mood +tiny\n  discover seventh_tower\n  say: It leads to a stair that leads to a locked door that leads, you're almost sure, to the seventh tower.\n> right: Read in the window seat\n  lore +small\n  mood +small\n  say: You read all day in a window seat with the whole lake below you. The best kind of nothing.\n\n@event castle_skeleton_crew\ntitle: The Skeleton Crew\nplan: castle\nclass: windfall\ntone: light\nart: meal\ntags: home\nscene: A handful of staff and students sharing a long table in a nearly empty great hall.\ntext: Supper in the holidays is one table in the Great Hall: you, three other students, the cook, two prefects and Old Maud, who has brought her goat.\n> left: Sit next to Maud\n  rel maud +small\n  mood +small\n  say: Maud tells a story about the frozen spring that she's never told the term-time students. Bartholomew eats your napkin.\n> right: Play cards with the prefects\n  gold +small\n  guile +tiny\n  say: The prefects play cards like they enforce rules: badly and with great confidence. You win dessert.\n\n@event home_family_supper\ntitle: Supper at Home\nplace: home_cottage\nclass: windfall\ntone: warm\nart: meal\ntags: home\nscene: A crowded kitchen table, a family passing dishes, a student back from the academy being asked too many questions.\ntext: Supper at home. Everyone wants to know what you've learned. You try to explain evocation to an aunt who thinks it's a kind of cheese.\n> left: Show them a spell\n  mood +small\n  arcana +tiny\n  say: You light every candle in the room at once. Your aunt applauds and asks if you can do it with cheese.\n> right: Just listen\n  mood +medium\n  health +tiny\n  say: You let them talk. You'd forgotten how loud and how good it is.\n\n@event home_old_friend\ntitle: An Old Friend\nplace: home_fields\nclass: windfall\ntone: warm\nart: leaf\ntags: home\nscene: Two old friends sitting on a field wall at sunset, one in academy robes, the other in work clothes.\ntext: Your oldest friend is sitting on the wall at the edge of the fields, exactly where you left them. \"So,\" they say. \"Are you a wizard now, or what?\"\n> left: Tell them everything\n  mood +medium\n  charm +tiny\n  say: You talk until the stars come out. They want to hear about the lake most of all.\n> right: Ask about their life instead\n  mood +small\n  charm +small\n  say: They've been busy too: a farm, a fiancé, a goat that bites. Your life isn't the only one that grew.\n\n@event home_market_day\ntitle: Market Day\nplace: home_market\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nart: coin\ntags: home money\nscene: A small country market, a student in academy robes being stared at by stallholders.\ntext: Market day in town. A stallholder recognises your academy robes and asks if you can do anything about his leaky roof. For a price.\n> left: Fix it with magic\n  gold +small\n  arcana +tiny\n  health -tiny\n  say: The roof is dry, the stallholder is thrilled and you are slightly scorched. He pays in apples and coins.\n> right: Just buy sweets\n  gold -tiny\n  mood +small\n  say: The sweet shop still sells your favourites. You eat them on the bridge like you used to.\n\n@event work_double_shift\ntitle: The Double Shift\nplan: work\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nart: coin\ntags: job money\nscene: A student asleep on their feet behind a counter, a clock on the wall showing a very late hour.\ntext: Holiday shifts pay double and feel triple. It's your second shift today and the evening rush hasn't started.\n> left: Push through\n  gold +medium\n  health -small\n  mood -small\n  say: You push through. The coins are heavy in your pocket and your feet are heavy everywhere else.\n> right: Swap the shift\n  gold -tiny\n  mood +small\n  say: A colleague takes it for a favour you'll owe later. You sleep for eleven hours.\n\n# ---------------- The Royal Library internship (plan: internship) ----------------\n\n@event intern_first_assignment\ntitle: The First Assignment\nplan: internship\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nart: book\ntags: study\nscene: A vast underground archive, a supervisor handing a student a list longer than their arm.\ntext: Your supervisor at the Royal Library hands you a list of forty uncatalogued boxes. \"Find out what's in them,\" she says. \"Carefully. One of them is probably alive.\"\n> left: Work through the boxes\n  lore +medium\n  mood -small\n  say: Box thirty-one is alive. It's a very small, very old dictionary that bites. You catalogue it.\n> right: Ask the other interns\n  charm +small\n  lore +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: The other interns have a system, a rota and a song. You learn all three.\n\n@event intern_index_maze\ntitle: The Index Maze\nplan: internship\nclass: milestone\ntone: tense\nart: map\ntags: study mystery\nscene: A student lost between endless shelves underground, following an index card trail by lamplight.\ntext: A cross-reference in the archive leads to another, and another, and another, deeper under the city. The last card is about the founders of Lumenhold.\n> left: Follow it to the end\n  challenge research hard:\n    win:\n      lore +large\n      renown +small\n      say: At the end is a letter from a founder about \"the seventh lock\". You copy it with shaking hands.\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      say: The trail ends at a missing card. Somebody took it. Recently.\n> right: Report it to your supervisor\n  lore +small\n  rel isolde +tiny\n  say: Your supervisor reads the card, goes pale and writes a letter to the headmistress of Lumenhold.\n\n# ---------------- Thesis (track: thesis) ----------------\n\n@event thesis_proposal\ntitle: The Proposal\ntrack: thesis\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nart: scroll\ntags: study\nscene: A desk covered in drafts, one page with a single sentence circled many times.\ntext: Your thesis proposal is due. Professor Thorne wants one sentence that says what you'll find out. You have eleven sentences and none of them is it.\n> left: Rewrite it all night\n  progress track +medium\n  health -small\n  say: At dawn you have one sentence. Thorne reads it twice. \"Yes,\" he says. \"That.\"\n> right: Hand in what you have\n  progress track +small\n  rel thorne -tiny\n  say: Thorne reads all eleven sentences and circles the fourth. \"That one,\" he says. \"Next time, only that one.\"\n\n@event thesis_lab_night\ntitle: Late in the Lab\ntrack: thesis\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nart: candle\ntags: study\nscene: A thesis laboratory at midnight, one carrel lit, a student surrounded by open books.\ntext: The thesis lab after midnight: everyone else has gone home, and your argument has a hole in it the size of the seventh tower.\n> left: Find the hole\n  progress track +medium\n  mood -small\n  say: You find it, fill it and find two more. That's progress, apparently.\n> right: Go to bed\n  rest\n  progress track -small\n  say: You sleep. In the morning the hole is still there, but you can see the edges of it.\n\n# ---------------- The Bell Under the Water (arc: the_bell) ----------------\n\n@event bell_first_ring\ntitle: A Bell Under the Water\narc: the_bell\nbeat: 1\nslot: night\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\nart: bell\ntags: mystery\nscene: A dark lakeshore at night, ripples spreading from the middle of the lake though nothing has fallen in.\ntext: In the middle of the night you hear a bell, low and slow, from the middle of the lake. Nobody else in your dormitory wakes up.\n> left: Go down to the shore\n  find bell_heard\n  health -tiny\n  say: The water is perfectly still. Then, once, a ring spreads out from the centre, as if the lake had been struck from below.\n> right: Pull the blanket over your head\n  find bell_heard\n  mood -tiny\n  say: It rings seven times. You count every one. In the morning you are the only one who remembers.\n\n@event bell_maud_rope\ntitle: Maud's Rope\narc: the_bell\nbeat: 2\nslot: night\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\nart: bell\ntags: mystery\ncast:\n  keeper: maud\nscene: An old woman in a garden shed holding up a frayed, waterlogged bell-rope.\ntext: {keeper} shows you a rope, frayed and black with lake water, that she's kept in her shed for sixty years. \"From the bell,\" she says. \"It came up the night the lake froze. Someone cut it.\"\n> left: Ask who cut it\n  find bell_rope\n  rel keeper +small\n  queue bell_answer within 1..3 with keeper\n  say: \"That,\" says {keeper}, \"is the question I've been asking for sixty years.\" She promises to tell you what she knows, when she's ready.\n> right: Leave her to her memories\n  find bell_rope\n  rel keeper +tiny\n  say: You leave her with the rope in her lap. She doesn't notice you go.\n\n@event bell_answer\ntitle: What Maud Remembers\ntrigger: queued\nactor: npc\nclass: story\ntone: warm\nart: lantern\ntags: mystery\nscene: An old woman and a student sitting by a cottage fire, a waterlogged rope coiled between them.\ntext: {npc} finds you by the boathouse. \"The rope,\" she says. \"Edmund cut it. To stop the bell ringing the thing awake. It went on ringing anyway.\"\n> ok: Listen\n  rel npc +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: She talks until the light goes. When she's finished, she seems lighter, and you seem to be carrying something.\n\n@event bell_drowned\ntitle: The Diving Bell\narc: the_bell\nbeat: 3\nslot: night\nclass: story\ntone: tense\nart: wave\ntags: mystery danger\nscene: A student in a borrowed diving helmet standing at the end of a jetty over black water.\ntext: The bell has rung every night for a week. The boathouse has an old diving helmet and a rope. The lake is very dark and very still.\n> left: Dive\n  challenge research hard:\n    win:\n      find bell_name\n      lore +medium\n      say: Far down, the bell lies on its side, and around its rim, letters: a name you don't know. You surface gasping, and you keep the name.\n    lose:\n      health -medium\n      say: The cold drives you back up before you see anything. Old Maud is waiting on the jetty with a blanket and a look.\n> right: Wait for the bell to stop\n  unqueue bell_answer\n  mood -small\n  say: You wait for a week. It doesn't stop. Something in you decides it will have to be you, next time.\n\n@event bell_silence\ntitle: The Bell Falls Silent\narc: the_bell\nbeat: 4\nslot: night\nwhen: clue(bell_name) or clue(bell_rope)\nclass: story\ntone: warm\nart: bell\ntags: mystery\nscene: The Mirror Lake at dawn, perfectly still, a student standing at the water's edge with a lantern.\ntext: {clue(bell_name): You say the name from the bell's rim out loud, over the water.|You stand at the shore with Maud's rope in your hands.} The bell rings one last time, and then the lake is just a lake.\n> ok: Let it rest\n  mood +medium\n  alignment +small\n  say: For the first time in sixty years, the lake is quiet. Somewhere in her cottage, you think, Maud sleeps through the night.\n"},{"path":"content/events/social.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Generic social events — `actor: any`.\n#  The engine binds `npc` to one met character for whom `when`\n#  is true. Use rel(npc), love(npc), bond(npc), romanceable(npc),\n#  tagged(npc, faculty) in conditions and {npc}, {they}, {them},\n#  {their}, {They} in text. These drive the relationship system:\n#  friendships form, romances spark, rivalries flare, bonds decay.\n# ============================================================\n\n# ---------------- Friendship ----------------\n\n@event soc_study_session\ntitle: Study Session\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ncast:\n  npc: any where (tagged(npc, student) or tagged(npc, prefect)) and rel(npc) >= 0\nper: npc\ntrigger: social\nrepeat: 4\nart: book\ntags: social study\nscene: Two students at a candlelit table piled with books, one explaining something with wild hand gestures.\ntext: {npc} drops into the seat across from you with an armful of books. \"Exams are coming for us all. Misery loves company. Study with me?\"\n> left: You work better alone\n  lore +tiny\n  rel npc -tiny\n  say: You get a lot done. {npc} sits at the next table and sighs loudly every few minutes.\n> right: Pull up a chair\n  mood -tiny\n  lore +small\n  study +tiny\n  rel npc +small\n  say: You get half as much done and remember twice as much of it. {npc} explains the Third Law with a biscuit diagram.\n\n@event soc_borrow_money\ntitle: A Small Loan\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ncast:\n  npc: any where tagged(npc, peer) and rel(npc) >= 3\nper: npc\ntrigger: social\nwhen: gold >= 20\nrepeat: 6\nart: pouch\ntags: social money\nscene: A student holding out an empty purse upside down with a sheepish grin.\ntext: {npc} turns out an empty purse. \"I'm fifteen crowns short for rent. I'll pay you back. I swear on my wand.\"\n> left: Sorry, you're tight too\n  rel npc -small\n  say: \"No, of course, I understand.\" {They} {are} quiet at dinner.\n> right: Lend fifteen crowns\n  gold -medium\n  rel npc +medium\n  chance 70:\n    queue soc_loan_repaid in 2 with npc\n    say: {npc} hugs you with the empty purse still in {their} hand.\n  else:\n    say: {npc} hugs you. You suspect you will never see those fifteen crowns again.\n\n@event soc_loan_repaid\ntitle: Paid Back\nclass: story\ntone: light\ntrigger: queued\nactor: npc\nart: coin\ntags: social money\nscene: A small stack of coins and a sweet bun left on a desk with a thank-you note.\ntext: {~{npc} finds you after class and presses a little stack of coins into your hand, plus a sticky bun. \"Fifteen crowns. And interest.\" The interest is the bun.|{npc} turns up at breakfast with a small purse and a very straight face. \"Fifteen crowns. I said I would.\"|{npc} slides fifteen crowns across the library table, one coin at a time. The last one has a note under it: \"Thank you. Really.\"}\n> left: Count it\n  gold +medium\n  rel npc +small\n  mood +tiny\n  say: You count it, because {they}'d be offended if you didn't. Fifteen exactly. {npc} nods, satisfied, as if a debt of honour has been settled.\n> right: Pocket it without counting\n  gold +medium\n  rel npc +small\n  mood +tiny\n  say: You put it away uncounted. {npc} looks quietly pleased to be trusted, which was worth more than the crowns.\n\n@event soc_become_friends\ntitle: Something Like Friendship\nclass: milestone\ntone: light\ncast:\n  npc: any where tagged(npc, peer) and bond(npc) == \"none\" and rel(npc) >= 18\nper: npc\ntrigger: social\nweight: 20\nart: group\ntags: social\nscene: Two students walking across the lawn at sunset, laughing at the same joke, shadows long behind them.\ntext: {npc} saves you a seat without asking now, and you save {them} one. You trade notes, complaints and the last biscuit without keeping score. Somewhere along the way, this became a friendship.\n> left: Keep a little distance\n  rel npc +tiny\n  say: You stay friendly. Just not too close. Some walls are load-bearing.\n> right: Call it what it is\n  bond npc friend\n  rel npc +medium\n  mood +small\n  say: \"So we're friends now,\" {npc} says. \"Officially.\" You shake on it, solemnly, then ruin it by laughing.\n\n@event soc_become_close\ntime: night\ntitle: The Kind of Friend You Call at 3am\nclass: milestone\ntone: light\ncast:\n  npc: any where tagged(npc, peer) and bond(npc) == \"friend\" and rel(npc) >= 36\nper: npc\ntrigger: social\nweight: 18\nart: heart\ntags: social\nscene: Two friends sitting on a dormitory floor at 3am wrapped in one blanket, empty mugs between them.\ntext: It's 3am. You can't sleep, and you've walked to {npc}'s door before you've even decided to. {They} open{s} it before you knock.\n> left: Apologise and go back to bed\n  rel npc +small\n  say: \"Don't be daft,\" {npc} says, but lets you go. In the morning there's tea outside your door.\n> right: Talk until sunrise\n  bond npc close\n  rel npc +medium\n  mood +medium\n  say: You tell {them} things you haven't told anyone. {They} tell you things back. The sun comes up and neither of you notices.\n\n@event soc_secret\ntitle: A Secret\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ncast:\n  npc: any where tagged(npc, peer) and rel(npc) >= 15\nper: npc\ntrigger: social\nrepeat: 8\nart: lock\ntags: social\nscene: Two students huddled under a staircase, one whispering into the other's ear, a finger to their lips.\ntext: {npc} pulls you under the stairs. \"I need to tell someone or I'll burst. Swear you won't tell anyone. Not even the portraits.\"\n> left: \"I'd rather not carry it.\"\n  rel npc -tiny\n  say: {npc} looks hurt, then relieved. \"Maybe that's better.\" You'll never know.\n> right: \"I swear.\"\n  mood -tiny\n  rel npc +medium\n  guile +tiny\n  say: It's not a big secret. It's the kind that's only big to the person holding it. You hold it with {them}.\n\n@event soc_argument\ntitle: Words Exchanged\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ncast:\n  npc: any where tagged(npc, peer) and rel(npc) >= 6 and bond(npc) != \"partner\"\nper: npc\ntrigger: social\nrepeat: 6\nweight: 8\nart: bolt\ntags: social\nscene: Two students facing off in an empty corridor, one with arms crossed, the other mid-gesture, both flushed.\ntext: It starts with something small — a borrowed book, a joke that landed wrong — and suddenly you and {npc} are arguing in the corridor, louder than either of you intended.\n> left: Apologise first\n  rel npc +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: \"I'm sorry.\" {npc} deflates. \"...Me too.\" It's over. It feels better than winning would have.\n> right: Stand your ground\n  rel npc -medium\n  mood -tiny\n  chance 50:\n    say: {npc} storms off. You were right. It doesn't feel as good as you'd hoped.\n  else:\n    rel npc +medium\n    say: You argue it out until you're both laughing at how ridiculous it got. Somehow you're closer after.\n\n@event soc_village_trip\ntitle: A Day in the Village\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ncast:\n  npc: any where tagged(npc, peer) and rel(npc) >= 6\nper: npc\ntrigger: social\nwhen: gold >= 5\nrepeat: 5\nart: house\ntags: social\nscene: A cobbled village street with crooked shopfronts, two students sharing a paper cone of roasted nuts.\ntext: \"Barge to the village? Tea at Mabel's, a wander round the bookshops, back before curfew?\" {npc} is already wearing {their} coat.\n> left: Not this week\n  rel npc -tiny\n  say: {They} bring{s} you back a cinnamon bun anyway.\n> right: Grab your coat\n  gold -tiny\n  mood +medium\n  rel npc +small\n  say: Tea, buns, a bookshop cat that sits on {npc}'s feet. You almost miss the last barge, laughing.\n\n@event soc_friend_sick\ntitle: Sick Friend\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ncast:\n  npc: any where tagged(npc, peer) and (bond(npc) == \"friend\" or bond(npc) == \"close\" or bond(npc) == \"partner\")\nper: npc\ntrigger: social\nrepeat: 8\nart: rain\ntags: social\nscene: A student tucked into an infirmary bed under a mountain of blankets, a friend sitting beside them with a bowl of soup.\ntext: {npc} has Lake Fever and is miserable in the infirmary. Sister Amaranth says visitors are allowed, but she won't be responsible.\n> left: Send a card\n  rel npc +tiny\n  say: You send a card with a drawing of Sister Amaranth as a dragon. {npc} keeps it on {their} bedside table.\n> right: Sit with them every evening\n  rel npc +medium\n  chance 35:\n    gain sick\n    say: You keep {them} company for a week. Then you're in the next bed. You both find this hilarious.\n  else:\n    say: You read aloud, badly, doing voices. {npc} recovers and claims it was the voices.\n\n@event soc_gift_given\ntitle: A Small Gift\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ncast:\n  npc: any where tagged(npc, peer) and rel(npc) >= 9\nper: npc\ntrigger: social\nrepeat: 7\nart: gem\ntags: social\nscene: A small wrapped parcel tied with ribbon left on a dormitory pillow, a pressed flower tucked into the bow.\ntext: There's a small parcel on your pillow. A note in {npc}'s handwriting: \"Saw this and thought of you. Don't make it weird.\"\n> left: Thank {them} later\n  mood +tiny\n  rel npc +tiny\n  say: It's a tiny brass compass that always points to the nearest teapot. You use it constantly.\n> right: Get {them} something back (8 gold)\n  gold -small\n  mood +small\n  rel npc +medium\n  say: You find {them} a book {they}'d wanted for months. {npc}'s face when {they} open{s} it is worth twice the price.\n\n@event soc_drift\ntitle: Drifting Apart\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ncast:\n  npc: any where tagged(npc, peer) and rel(npc) >= 6 and rel(npc) < 24\nper: npc\ntrigger: social\nwhen: since(soc_drift) > 3\nweight: 6\nrepeat: 6\nart: hourglass\ntags: social\nscene: A student glancing back across a busy courtyard at another who hasn't seen them, the space between them full of strangers.\ntext: You realise you haven't really talked to {npc} in weeks. You see {them} across the courtyard, laughing with people you don't know.\n> left: Let it be\n  rel npc -small\n  say: People drift. It's nobody's fault. You still smile when you pass in the halls.\n> right: Go over and say hello\n  chance 70:\n    rel npc +medium\n    mood +tiny\n    say: \"There you are!\" {npc} says, as if you'd been the one missing. Maybe you had.\n  else:\n    rel npc +tiny\n    mood -tiny\n    say: You go over. It is friendly, and short, and you both say \"we should catch up\" in a way that means you won't.\n\n@event soc_rumour\ntitle: Rumours About {npc}\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ncast:\n  npc: any where rel(npc) >= 9 and not tagged(npc, villager)\nper: npc\ntrigger: social\nrepeat: 8\nart: paper\ntags: social\nscene: A cluster of students whispering behind their hands in a corridor, glancing toward someone just out of view.\ntext: A nasty rumour about {npc} is going round the common room. It's not true. You know it's not true. People are laughing.\n> left: Let it blow over\n  rel npc -tiny\n  say: It blows over. {npc} never finds out you heard it. Probably.\n> right: Shut it down\n  challenge negotiate trivial:\n    win:\n      rel npc +medium\n      renown +small\n      say: You make the rumour-monger look so foolish that the story dies on the spot. {npc} hears about it later and just squeezes your arm.\n    lose:\n      rel npc +small\n      renown -tiny\n      say: You make a scene and now there are rumours about you too. {npc} thinks you're an idiot. A loyal idiot.\n\n@event soc_homework_help\ntitle: Desperate Homework\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ncast:\n  npc: any where (tagged(npc, student) or tagged(npc, prefect)) and rel(npc) >= 0\nper: npc\ntrigger: social\nrepeat: 5\nart: feather\ntags: social study\nscene: A student with ink on their face clutching a half-finished essay, another student leaning over to read it.\ntext: {npc} waves a half-finished essay at you. \"It's due in an hour and I've written the title twice. Help.\"\n> left: Explain the concept\n  challenge research easy:\n    win:\n      rel npc +medium\n      lore +tiny\n      say: You explain it so clearly that {npc} finishes the essay with time to spare. {They} insist{s} you're a genius.\n    lose:\n      rel npc +tiny\n      say: You explain it so confusingly that you both end up more lost. You hand it in together, two confused people against the world.\n> right: \"Copy mine.\"\n  alignment -tiny\n  rel npc +small\n  chance 30:\n    study -small\n    rel npc -tiny\n    say: The professor notices two identical essays. You both get detention, and a lecture.\n    gain detention\n  else:\n    say: {npc} hands it in and gets a better mark than you did. Unbelievable.\n\n@event soc_faculty_advice\ntitle: A Word of Advice\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ncast:\n  npc: any where tagged(npc, teacher) and rel(npc) >= 6\nper: npc\ntrigger: social\nrepeat: 6\nart: scroll\ntags: study\nscene: A professor walking with a student along a covered walkway, pointing at something in an open book.\ntext: {npc} catches you after a lecture. \"You've been working hard. Or hardly working. I can never tell with your year. Some advice, if you'll take it?\"\n> left: Politely decline\n  rel npc -tiny\n  say: \"Suit yourself.\" {They} walk{s} off. Faculty have long memories.\n> right: Listen carefully\n  mood -tiny\n  rel npc +small\n  study +small\n  lore +tiny\n  say: It's good advice. Annoyingly good. Your next essay is better for it.\n\n# ---------------- Romance ----------------\n\n@event soc_spark\ntitle: A Spark\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\ncast:\n  npc: any where romanceable(npc) and rel(npc) >= 9 and spark(npc) < 12 and bond(npc) != \"partner\"\nper: npc\ntrigger: social\nwhen: bonds(partner) == 0\nweight: 12\nrepeat: 6\nart: sparkle\ntags: social romance\nscene: Two hands brushing as they reach for the same quill, both students freezing for a heartbeat.\ntext: You reach for the same quill as {npc}. Your hands touch. Neither of you lets go immediately. It's a perfectly ordinary quill.\n> left: Laugh it off\n  rel npc +tiny\n  say: \"All yours,\" you say. {npc} laughs. The moment passes. Mostly.\n> right: Hold on a moment longer\n  chance 70:\n    spark npc +medium\n    rel npc +tiny\n    say: {npc} looks up. Something in {their} face shifts. Then {they} let{s} go, a little pink, and the quill rolls under the desk.\n  else:\n    rel npc +tiny\n    mood -tiny\n    say: {npc} pulls back as if burned, and the rest of the lesson is very, very awkward.\n\n@event soc_date\ntitle: An Invitation\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\ncast:\n  npc: any where romanceable(npc) and spark(npc) >= 12 and spark(npc) < 40 and bond(npc) != \"partner\" and rel(npc) >= 9\nper: npc\ntrigger: social\nweight: 12\nrepeat: 4\nart: heart\ntags: social romance\nscene: A student nervously holding out a hand-drawn ticket to the village's floating lantern show.\ntext: You could ask {npc} to the lantern show in the village this weekend. As friends. Or, you know. Not as friends.\n> left: Ask as friends\n  gold -tiny\n  rel npc +small\n  mood +small\n  say: It's a lovely evening. You're both very careful about elbows.\n> right: Ask as more than friends\n  if spark(npc) >= 25:\n    challenge negotiate trivial:\n      win:\n        spark npc +medium\n        mood +medium\n        say: {npc} says yes before you finish the sentence. You hold hands through the whole show and miss every lantern.\n      lose:\n        spark npc -tiny\n        mood -small\n        say: {npc} hesitates, then says {they}'d rather go as friends. It's a nice evening. It's a long evening.\n  else:\n    challenge negotiate easy:\n      win:\n        spark npc +medium\n        mood +medium\n        say: {npc} says yes before you finish the sentence. You hold hands through the whole show and miss every lantern.\n      lose:\n        spark npc -tiny\n        mood -small\n        say: {npc} hesitates, then says {they}'d rather go as friends. It's a nice evening. It's a long evening.\n\n@event soc_confess_generic\ntitle: Feelings, Spoken\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\ncast:\n  npc: any where romanceable(npc) and spark(npc) >= 30 and rel(npc) >= 18 and bond(npc) != \"partner\" and bond(npc) != \"ex\"\nper: npc\ntrigger: social\nweight: 10\nart: heart\ntags: romance\nscene: Two students standing very close on a windswept bridge between towers, one with a hand over their heart.\ntext: On the bridge between the towers, the wind snatching at your robes, you realise you can't keep it in anymore. {npc} is looking at you like {they} know what you're about to say.\n> left: Say nothing\n  spark npc -small\n  say: The moment passes. You walk back together, a careful inch apart.\n> right: Tell {them}\n  challenge negotiate trivial:\n    win:\n      bond npc partner\n      spark npc +medium\n      mood +large\n      say: \"Finally,\" {npc} says, and kisses you, and the wind steals your breath anyway.\n    lose:\n      spark npc -large\n      mood -medium\n      say: {npc} takes a long breath. \"I care about you,\" {they} say{s}. \"Just not like that. I'm sorry.\" The wind is very cold.\n\n@event soc_partner_date\ntitle: Date Night\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\ncast:\n  npc: any where bond(npc) == \"partner\"\nper: npc\ntrigger: social\nrepeat: 3\nweight: 14\nart: candle\ntags: romance\nscene: Two people at a tiny candlelit table in a window alcove of a village tea shop, snow falling outside.\ntext: {npc} has booked the window table at Mabel's. Candles. Snow outside. \"We're splitting the bill,\" {they} say{s}. \"Don't argue.\"\n> left: Talk about the future\n  gold -tiny\n  spark npc +small\n  mood +medium\n  say: You talk about where you'll be in ten years. In every version, you're both there.\n> right: Talk about absolutely nothing\n  gold -tiny\n  spark npc +tiny\n  mood +medium\n  say: You argue for an hour about whether a hotdog is a sandwich. It is the best date of your life.\n\n@event soc_partner_neglect\ntitle: \"You're Always Busy\"\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\ncast:\n  npc: any where bond(npc) == \"partner\"\nper: npc\ntrigger: social\nwhen: since(soc_partner_date) >= 5\nweight: 14\nrepeat: 5\nart: hourglass\ntags: romance\nscene: A person waiting alone at a table set for two, a candle burned low, the other chair empty.\ntext: {npc} is waiting outside your door. \"I feel like I only see you in corridors,\" {they} say{s}. \"Do you even want this?\"\n> left: \"I'm sorry. I'll make time.\"\n  spark npc +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  study -tiny\n  say: You skip a study session for a long walk by the lake. It's the right call.\n> right: \"You know how busy I am.\"\n  spark npc -medium\n  rel npc -medium\n  say: {npc} nods slowly. Something between you cools a degree.\n\n@event soc_breakup\ntitle: The End of Something\nclass: routine\ntone: grim\ncast:\n  npc: any where bond(npc) == \"partner\" and spark(npc) < 25\nper: npc\ntrigger: social\nweight: 30\nart: heartbreak\ntags: romance crisis\nscene: Two people sitting on opposite ends of a bench in a grey garden, not looking at each other, rain beginning.\ntext: {npc} sits down at the far end of the bench. \"I think we both know,\" {they} say{s} quietly. \"This isn't working anymore.\"\n> left: Fight for it\n  challenge negotiate trivial:\n    win:\n      spark npc +large\n      say: You talk for hours. It's raw and honest and it hurts. And at the end, {npc} takes your hand again.\n    lose:\n      bond npc ex\n      gain heartbroken\n      rel npc -large\n      say: You try. It only makes it harder. In the end you're both crying, and it's over anyway.\n> right: Let {them} go\n  bond npc ex\n  gain heartbroken\n  rel npc +small\n  say: You agree. It's terrible and gentle and right. {npc} kisses your forehead once before {they} leave{s}.\n\n@event soc_two_timing\ntitle: Found Out\nclass: routine\ntone: grim\ncast:\n  npc: any where bond(npc) == \"partner\"\nper: npc\ntrigger: social\nwhen: bonds(partner) >= 2\nweight: 40\nart: heartbreak\ntags: romance crisis\nscene: A person holding up two crumpled love letters in a crowded corridor, the crowd parting around them.\ntext: {npc} is holding two letters. One is to {them}. The other isn't. \"I found it in your bag,\" {they} say{s}, very quietly. Half the corridor has stopped to watch.\n> left: Beg forgiveness\n  challenge negotiate easy:\n    win:\n      spark npc -large\n      rel npc -large\n      say: {npc} doesn't forgive you. But {they} don't leave. Not yet. You're on borrowed time.\n    lose:\n      bond npc ex\n      rel npc -huge\n      renown -medium\n      gain heartbroken\n      say: {npc} tears both letters in half and walks away. Everyone knows by dinner.\n> right: Say nothing\n  bond npc ex\n  rel npc -huge\n  renown -medium\n  say: Your silence is an answer. {npc} drops the letters at your feet and leaves. The story is all over the academy.\n\n@event soc_ex_encounter\ntitle: Awkward\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\ncast:\n  npc: any where bond(npc) == \"ex\"\nper: npc\ntrigger: social\nrepeat: 6\nart: mirror\ntags: romance\nscene: Two people coming face to face in a narrow library aisle, both holding books, neither moving.\ntext: You turn into the library aisle and there's {npc}. Your ex. Holding the same book you came for. Neither of you moves.\n> left: Turn around and leave\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You find a different book in a different aisle and read the same paragraph six times.\n> right: Say hello like a person\n  rel npc +medium\n  challenge negotiate trivial:\n    win:\n      bond npc friend\n      say: It's awkward for one minute, then it isn't. You end up laughing about the time the dummy caught fire. Friends, maybe. Really.\n    lose:\n      mood -tiny\n      say: \"Hello.\" \"Hello.\" You both stand there. Then you both leave. It's a start.\n\n# ---------------- Rivalry ----------------\n\n@event soc_rival_taunt\ntime: evening\ntitle: A Public Taunt\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\ncast:\n  npc: any where tagged(npc, peer) and (bond(npc) == \"rival\" or bond(npc) == \"nemesis\" or rel(npc) <= -12)\nper: npc\ntrigger: social\nrepeat: 4\nart: mask\ntags: rival\nscene: A smirking student surrounded by a small audience, pointing at someone across a crowded dining hall.\ntext: In the middle of dinner, {npc} loudly recounts your most embarrassing moment of the term. People laugh. {They} look{s} very pleased.\n> left: Ignore {them}\n  mood -small\n  say: The laughter dies down. You eat your soup with as much dignity as soup allows.\n> right: Fire back\n  challenge negotiate trivial:\n    win:\n      renown +small\n      rel npc -small\n      say: Your comeback is so good that someone at the far table applauds. {npc} goes scarlet.\n    lose:\n      renown -small\n      rel npc -tiny\n      say: Your comeback comes out as a squeak. The laughter doubles.\n\n@event soc_make_peace\ntime: noon afternoon\ntitle: Burying the Hatchet\nclass: milestone\ntone: tense\ncast:\n  npc: any where tagged(npc, peer) and (bond(npc) == \"rival\" or bond(npc) == \"nemesis\") and rel(npc) >= 3\nper: npc\ntrigger: social\nweight: 18\nart: hand\ntags: rival social\nscene: Two former rivals shaking hands on a dueling green at sunset, wands tucked away.\ntext: {npc} finds you after class, scuffing {their} shoes. \"This whole rivalry thing. It's getting tiring. Truce?\"\n> left: Keep the edge\n  rel npc -small\n  say: \"Thought so,\" {npc} says, half-smiling. The game goes on.\n> right: Shake on it\n  bond npc friend\n  rel npc +large\n  mood +small\n  say: You shake. It's strange — you know {them} better than most of your friends. Maybe that's the point.\n\n@event soc_rival_bet\ntitle: The Wager\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\ncast:\n  npc: any where (tagged(npc, student) or tagged(npc, prefect)) and bond(npc) == \"rival\"\nper: npc\ntrigger: social\nrepeat: 6\nart: dice\ntags: rival\nscene: Two students slapping coins onto a table between them, a crowd gathering, a chalked tally on the wall.\ntext: \"Eight crowns says I outscore you in the next Arcana practical,\" {npc} says, slapping coins on the table. A crowd forms.\n> left: Decline the bet\n  rel npc -tiny\n  say: \"Thought so.\" {npc} pockets the coins. You decide not to care. You care.\n> right: Take the bet\n  challenge spell easy:\n    win:\n      gold +small\n      renown +small\n      rel npc +small\n      say: You win by a hair. {npc} pays up with a theatrical bow. \"Next time.\"\n    lose:\n      gold -small\n      rel npc +tiny\n      say: You lose by a hair. \"Next time,\" you say. {npc} grins. \"Next time.\"\n"},{"path":"content/states/states.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Persistent states. A state is something that sticks to the\n#  player across months: an origin, a class, a club, a job, an\n#  illness, a curse. States can:\n#    tick:      run every month start while active\n#    duration:  default length in months (omit = until removed)\n#    group:     only one state of the same group at a time\n#    on_add / on_expire / on_remove: effects\n#  Events check them with has(state_id) and left(state_id).\n# ============================================================\n\n# ---------------- Origins (chosen at the start) ----------------\n\n@state origin_noble\nname: Minor Nobility\nkind: origin\ngroup: origin\nart: crown\ndesc: A family name that opens doors and an allowance that arrives every month — with letters attached.\nmoney: Comfortable: savings and a monthly allowance. You will rarely need to work.\ntick:\n  gold +6\non_add:\n  gold +60\n  charm +3\n  renown +6\n\n@state origin_scholar\nname: Scholarship Student\nkind: origin\ngroup: origin\nart: scroll\ndesc: The Crown pays half your tuition, and a little more while your grades hold.\nmoney: Tight: half tuition, and a small stipend while your GPA stays at 2.0 or above. Expect to work some terms.\ntick:\n  if gpa >= 2.0:\n    gold +5\non_add:\n  lore +5\n  study +large\n\n@state origin_hedge\nname: Hedge-Witch's Grandchild\nkind: origin\ngroup: origin\nart: mushroom\ndesc: Raised among drying herbs and muttered charms. Proper magic still feels a little too proper.\nmoney: Poor: a few crowns and no allowance. You will need a job from the first term.\ntags: poor\non_add:\n  alchemy +6\n  mood +6\n  gold +15\n\n@state origin_street\nname: Street-Raised\nkind: origin\ngroup: origin\nart: mask\ndesc: You learned to read crowds before you learned to read runes.\nmoney: Poor: almost nothing in your pockets. You will need a job from the first term.\ntags: poor\non_add:\n  guile +7\n  vigor +3\n  gold +10\n\n@state origin_merchant\nname: Merchant's Child\nkind: origin\ngroup: origin\nart: scales\ndesc: Everything has a price, and you were taught to haggle it down.\nmoney: Comfortable: the largest purse, and a small monthly share from the company.\ntick:\n  gold +3\non_add:\n  gold +90\n  charm +3\n\n# ---------------- Electives (one per term) ----------------\n\n@state class_pyromancy\nname: Elective: Pyromancy\nkind: class\ngroup: elective\nart: flame\ntags: class\ndesc: Fire answers to will, not wishes. Singed eyebrows are considered a rite of passage.\ntick:\n  arcana +2\n  health -1\n\n@state class_tidecalling\nname: Elective: Tidecalling\nkind: class\ngroup: elective\nart: wave\ntags: class\ndesc: Lessons on the lake shore, bending water and patience in equal measure.\ntick:\n  arcana +1\n  vigor +1\n  mood +1\n\n@state class_illusion\nname: Elective: Illusion & Glamour\nkind: class\ngroup: elective\nart: mask\ntags: class\ndesc: The art of making people see what you want. Professor Vey insists it is not lying.\ntick:\n  guile +1\n  charm +1\n\n@state class_alchemy\nname: Elective: Practical Alchemy\nkind: class\ngroup: elective\nart: cauldron\ntags: class\ndesc: Brewing, distilling and the occasional controlled explosion.\ntick:\n  alchemy +2\n\n@state class_divination\nname: Elective: Divination\nkind: class\ngroup: elective\nart: eye\ntags: class\ndesc: Tea leaves, star charts and the Mirror of Next Tuesday.\ntick:\n  lore +1\n  if rand(1, 3) == 1:\n    mood -2\n\n@state class_artifice\nname: Elective: Artifice\nkind: class\ngroup: elective\nart: gear\ntags: class\ndesc: Enchanting objects to do what they were never meant to do.\ntick:\n  alchemy +1\n  lore +1\n\n@state class_beastcraft\nname: Elective: Beastcraft\nkind: class\ngroup: elective\nart: paw\ntags: class\ndesc: Familiars, griffon husbandry and dodging the manticore pen.\ntick:\n  vigor +1\n  mood +2\n\n@state class_astromancy\nname: Elective: Astromancy\nkind: class\ngroup: elective\nart: planet\ntags: class\ndesc: Midnight lectures on the observatory roof. You will learn the stars; you will lose sleep.\ntick:\n  lore +2\n  health -1\n\n@state class_healing\nname: Elective: Healing Arts\nkind: class\ngroup: elective\nart: cross\ntags: class\ndesc: Mending flesh with light and needle. Sister Amaranth expects perfection.\ntick:\n  alchemy +1\n  lore +1\n  health +1\n\n@state class_umbral\nname: Elective: Umbral Theory\nkind: class\ngroup: elective\nart: skull\ntags: class\ndesc: Restricted. The study of shadow, death and what lies behind both. Strictly theoretical. Mostly.\ntick:\n  arcana +2\n  lore +1\n  mood -2\n\n# ---------------- Clubs (one at a time) ----------------\n\n@state club_dueling\nname: Club: Dueling Circle\nkind: club\ngroup: club\nart: duel\ndesc: Bruises, glory and the Spring Tournament.\ntick:\n  vigor +1\n  arcana +1\n  health -1\n\n@state club_choir\nname: Club: Choir of the Spheres\nkind: club\ngroup: club\nart: music\ndesc: Harmonies that make the candles flicker in time.\ntick:\n  charm +1\n  mood +2\n\n@state club_skyrace\nname: Club: Skyrace Squad\nkind: club\ngroup: club\nart: broom\ndesc: Brooms, hoops and screaming crowds.\ntick:\n  vigor +2\n  health -1\n\n@state club_ledger\nname: Club: The Lumen Ledger\nkind: club\ngroup: club\nart: paper\ndesc: The student paper. Deadlines, scoops and enemies.\ntick:\n  guile +1\n  charm +1\n\n@state club_garden\nname: Club: Verdant Circle\nkind: club\ngroup: club\nart: leaf\ndesc: Greenhouse afternoons with mandrakes that sing off-key.\ntick:\n  alchemy +1\n  mood +1\n\n@state club_stargazers\nname: Club: Stargazers\nkind: club\ngroup: club\nart: star\ndesc: Telescopes, cocoa and constellations nobody else can name.\ntick:\n  lore +1\n  mood +1\n\n@state club_cartographers\nname: Club: Midnight Cartographers\nkind: club\ngroup: club\nart: map\ndesc: A club that officially does not exist, mapping corridors that officially do not either.\ntick:\n  guile +1\n  lore +1\n\n# ---------------- Jobs (one at a time) ----------------\n\n@state job_library\nname: Job: Library Assistant\nkind: job\ngroup: job\nart: book\ndesc: Reshelving books that bite. Quiet hours, modest pay.\ntick:\n  gold +10\n  lore +1\n  mood -1\n\n@state job_tavern\nname: Job: The Gilded Newt\nkind: job\ngroup: job\nart: cup\ndesc: Serving cider in the village tavern. Good tips, sore feet.\ntick:\n  gold +16\n  charm +1\n  health -2\n\n@state job_apothecary\nname: Job: Apothecary Clerk\nkind: job\ngroup: job\nart: bottle\ndesc: Labelling jars at Pell & Daughters. Do not lick anything.\ntick:\n  gold +12\n  alchemy +1\n\n@state job_stables\nname: Job: Familiar Stables\nkind: job\ngroup: job\nart: paw\ndesc: Feeding hippogriffs and mucking out things that breathe smoke.\ntick:\n  gold +11\n  vigor +1\n  health -1\n\n@state job_tutor\nname: Job: First-Year Tutor\nkind: job\ngroup: job\nart: feather\ndesc: Explaining the difference between a hex and a jinx, again.\ntick:\n  gold +12\n  study +tiny\n  mood -1\n\n@state job_courier\nname: Job: Broom Courier\nkind: job\ngroup: job\nart: letter\ndesc: Parcels across the lake at full speed. Pays well. Hurts often.\ntick:\n  gold +18\n  vigor +1\n  health -3\n\n# ---------------- Conditions ----------------\n\n@state hungry\nname: Hungry\nkind: debuff\nart: meal\nduration: 1\ndesc: The board went unpaid this month. Bread rolls pocketed from the refectory only go so far.\ntick:\n  health -2\n  mood -3\n\n@state sick\nname: Lake Fever\nkind: debuff\nart: rain\nduration: 2\ndesc: Shivers, sniffles and a nose that glows faintly blue.\ntick:\n  health -4\n  mood -2\n\n@state injured\nname: Injured\nkind: debuff\nart: cross\nduration: 2\ndesc: Something is bruised, sprained or bent the wrong way.\ntick:\n  health -2\n  vigor -1\n\n@state exhausted\nname: Exhausted\nkind: debuff\nart: bed\nduration: 1\ndesc: You could sleep standing up. You might have.\ntick:\n  health -3\n  mood -3\n\n@state inspired\nname: Inspired\nkind: buff\nart: sparkle\nduration: 2\ndesc: Ideas arrive faster than you can write them down.\ntick:\n  lore +1\n  arcana +1\n  mood +2\n\n@state heartbroken\nname: Heartbroken\nkind: debuff\nart: heartbreak\nduration: 3\ndesc: Songs are suddenly about you. All of them.\ntick:\n  mood -4\n\n@state slacking\nname: Skipping Classes\nkind: debuff\nart: bed\nduration: 2\ndesc: Freedom! Also: falling behind.\ntick:\n  mood +2\n\n@state detention\nname: Detention\nkind: debuff\nart: lock\nduration: 1\ndesc: Scrubbing cauldrons under the eye of a prefect.\ntick:\n  mood -3\n\n@state probation\nname: Academic Probation\nkind: debuff\nart: scales\ndesc: Your marks are below the line. Two terms under a 1.0 and the Disciplinary Board sits. A GPA of 2.0 clears it.\ntick:\n  mood -2\n  if gpa >= 2.0:\n    lose probation\n\n@state in_debt\nname: Debt to Grisk\nkind: debuff\nart: chain\ndesc: The goblin moneylender takes 12 crowns a month until the debt is cleared. Ignore a missed payment and he adds another.\n# Unpaid, the debt escalates (playtest 2, M22): the first miss brings a reminder (ignore it and a month is added),\n# the next the collectors.\ntick:\n  if gold >= 12:\n    gold -12\n    debt_paid += 1\n  else:\n    mood -4\n    debt_missed += 1\n    if debt_missed == 1:\n      queue grisk_reminder now\n  if debt_paid >= 6:\n    lose in_debt\n  if debt_missed >= 2:\n    queue grisk_collectors now\n\n@state famous\nname: Talk of the Academy\nkind: buff\nart: star\nduration: 3\ndesc: Strangers know your name. Some of them even say it nicely.\ntick:\n  renown +2\n  mood +1\n\n@state hiccup_hex\nname: Hiccup Hex\nkind: debuff\nart: sparkle\nduration: 2\ndesc: Every hiccup releases a small, harmless, extremely embarrassing firework.\ntick:\n  mood -2\n  renown +1\n\n@state blessed\nname: Maud's Blessing\nkind: buff\nart: flower\nduration: 3\ndesc: A sprig of rosemary in your pocket that never wilts.\ntick:\n  health +3\n\n@state familiar_fox\nname: Familiar: Ember\nkind: buff\nart: fox\ndesc: A fox-spirit with a smouldering tail who has decided you are hers.\ntick:\n  mood +2\n  if rand(1, 4) == 1:\n    guile +1\n\n@state familiar_owl\nname: Familiar: Clockwork Owl\nkind: buff\nart: owl\ndesc: Mira's gift. It hoots the hour, mostly correctly, and remembers your homework.\ntick:\n  study +tiny\n\n@state training\nname: Tournament Training\nkind: buff\nart: duel\nduration: 2\ndesc: Dawn drills on the dueling green.\ntick:\n  vigor +2\n  arcana +1\n  health -1\n\n@state wakeful\nname: Draught of Wakefulness\nkind: buff\nart: potion\nduration: 1\ndesc: You haven't blinked in eleven hours. Your notes are immaculate.\ntick:\n  study +small\n  health -3\n\n@state haunted\nname: Haunted Dormitory\nkind: debuff\nart: ghost\nduration: 2\ndesc: Something rearranges your socks at night.\ntick:\n  mood -2\n\n@state watched\nname: Under Bram's Eye\nkind: debuff\nart: eye\nduration: 3\ndesc: The prefect is watching you. Specifically, and with relish.\ntick:\n  mood -1\n\n@state umbral_curse\nname: Umbral Curse\nkind: debuff\nart: skull\ndesc: A cold that lives under your skin. Something in the restricted section followed you home.\ntick:\n  health -3\n  mood -1\n  arcana +1\n\n@state hollow_dreams\nname: Hollow Dreams\nkind: debuff\nart: moon\ndesc: Every night: a black star, a door beneath the lake, a voice that knows your name.\ntick:\n  mood -1\n  lore +1\n\n@state star_mark\nname: The Star-Mark\nkind: buff\nart: star\ndesc: A pale, star-shaped mark on your palm that hums near old magic.\ntick:\n  arcana +1\n\n@state summer_away\nname: Summer Away\nkind: neutral\nart: house\nduration: 2\ndesc: Away from the academy for the summer recess. No rent, no classes.\n\n@state summer_work\nname: Summer Work\nkind: job\nart: coin\nduration: 2\ndesc: A summer of honest (and occasionally dishonest) labour.\ntick:\n  gold +25\n  health -2\n\n@state frog_voice\nname: Croaking\nkind: debuff\nart: frog\nduration: 1\ndesc: A botched transfiguration left you with the voice of a bullfrog.\ntick:\n  charm -1\n  mood -2\n\n@state garden_plot\nname: Garden Plot\nkind: buff\nart: leaf\ndesc: A little square of earth behind the greenhouses that is yours. Things grow; so do you.\ntick:\n  alchemy +1\n  if rand(1, 3) == 1:\n    mood +2\n\n@state pen_pal\nname: Pen Pal in Thornwick\nkind: buff\nart: letter\ndesc: A student at the rival academy who writes you long, funny letters full of terrible gossip.\ntick:\n  mood +1\n  if rand(1, 4) == 1:\n    lore +1\n\n@state frost_ward\nname: Ilse's Frost-Ward\nkind: buff\nart: snow\ndesc: A sliver of Northern winter that Ilse sewed into your collar. Cold things think twice about you.\ntick:\n  arcana +1\n  health +1\n\n# ---------------- Break plans (design §4.3; phase 2b) ----------------\n# Chosen at the start of each break (calendar_2b.grim: break_plan). A plan lasts the break.\n\n@state plan_castle\nname: Break: at the castle\nkind: plan\ngroup: plan\nduration: 2\nart: tower\ndesc: A skeleton crew, empty corridors and secrets. Board costs 6 crowns a month.\n\n@state plan_home\nname: Break: at home\nkind: plan\ngroup: plan\nduration: 2\nart: house\ndesc: Family, old friends and free board. The days are slower here.\non_add:\n  gain summer_away 2\n\n@state gig_grounds\nname: Holiday gig: the grounds\ngroup: gig\nduration: 2\nart: leaf\ndesc: Working the grounds for Old Maud this break. Good pay, sore hands.\n\n@state gig_kitchens\nname: Holiday gig: the kitchens\ngroup: gig\nduration: 2\nart: meal\ndesc: Working the Refectory kitchens this break. Good pay, short temper.\n\n@state plan_work\nname: Break: working full-time\nkind: plan\ngroup: plan\nduration: 2\nart: coin\ndesc: Working on campus through the break: a holiday gig, and board as usual. Tiring.\n\n@state plan_internship\nname: Break: internship\nkind: plan\ngroup: plan\nduration: 2\nart: book\ntags: away\ndesc: A research placement away from the castle, with a stipend and a supervisor.\ntick: gold +6\n# One summer each (M16): a strong one brings an offer to come back; otherwise the post ends.\non_expire:\n  if (member(intern_royal_library) and performance(intern_royal_library) >= 80) or (member(intern_conclave) and performance(intern_conclave) >= 80) or (member(intern_alchemists) and performance(intern_alchemists) >= 80) or (member(intern_expedition) and performance(intern_expedition) >= 80) or (member(intern_field_station) and performance(intern_field_station) >= 80) or (member(intern_thornwick) and performance(intern_thornwick) >= 80) or (member(intern_unseen_office) and performance(intern_unseen_office) >= 80):\n    queue intern_asked_back\n  else:\n    leave internship\n"},{"path":"content/world/archetypes.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Archetypes (design §10.4): templates for side characters spawned\n#  in phase 4. Names come from per-language pools\n#  (locales/<lang>/names.lang). Every character is an adult.\n# ============================================================\n\n@archetype student\nnames: pool students\nage: 18..22\ntraits: pick 2\npronouns: any\nhouse: any\nrole: student\ndesc: A fellow student: a face in the corridor who might become a friend, a rival or a story.\n\n@archetype professor\nnames: pool professors\nage: 35..72\ntraits: pick 2\npronouns: any\nhouse: Faculty\nrole: professor\ndesc: A visiting or junior member of the faculty: an advisor, an examiner or an internship supervisor.\n\n@archetype villager\nnames: pool villagers\nage: 18..80\ntraits: pick 1\npronouns: any\nhouse: none\nrole: outsider\ndesc: Someone from the village across the lake: a shopkeeper, a bargeman, a customer at the Gilded Newt.\n\n@archetype northerner\nnames: pool northerners\nage: 18..40\ntraits: pick 2\npronouns: any\nhouse: none\nrole: outsider\ndesc: A traveller from the frozen North, with frost in their eyelashes and loyalties that are hard to read.\n"},{"path":"content/world/arcs.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Arcs (design §10.6): the main plot and character arcs as data.\n#  acts: name@year · clock: range · beats: clock@yNmM (the clock\n#  advances on schedule whatever the player does) · clues: what can\n#  be found · key_clues: needed for the finale, each with ≥ 2\n#  sources. Mechanics arrive in phase 2b; the structure in phase 3.\n# ============================================================\n\n@arc hollowstar\nname: The Hollow Star\ncanon: hard\nacts: omens@y1 investigation@y2 escalation@y3 convergence@y4\nclock: 0..12\nbeats: 1@y1m3 2@y1m9 4@y2m2 5@y2m9 7@y3m2 8@y3m7 10@y4m3 12@y4m9\nclues: founders_ledger lake_bell second_shadow seven_locks star_key kael_family ilse_truth knows_founders_seal heard_of_starcalling codex_names\nkey_clues: seven_locks star_key ilse_truth\ncast: kael ilse isolde maud seraphine\ndesc: Something that eats light sleeps beneath the Mirror Lake, behind a door the founders sealed with seven locks. Someone wants it open.\n\n@arc lyra_and_her_mother\nname: Second Place\ncanon: hard\nacts: rivals@y1 cracks@y2 choice@y3\nclock: 0..6\nclues: black_seal_letters\ncast: lyra\ndesc: Lyra Vaneshade has never once heard \"well done\". Her rivalry with you can become respect, friendship or more, and her mother will have something to say about all three.\n\n@arc kael_thaw\nname: Thaw\ncanon: hard\nacts: frost@y1 trust@y2 thaw@y3\nclock: 0..6\nclues: kael_family\ncast: kael\ndesc: Kael Dumont was sent to open a door and came to stop it. Whether he manages depends on whether anyone is warm enough.\n\n@arc ilse_dumont\nname: The Girl in White Furs\ncanon: hard\nacts: arrival@y2 the_man_in_grey@y3 north@y4\nclock: 0..6\nclues: ilse_truth\ncast: ilse kael\ndesc: Kael's sister was raised by the Hollow cult to do what he wouldn't. The man in grey wants her home. She might let him.\n\n@arc the_bell\nname: The Bell Under the Water\ncanon: soft\nacts: echo@y1 rope@y2\nclock: 0..4\nbeats: 1@y1m2 2@y1m8 3@y2m3 4@y2m9\nclues: bell_heard bell_rope bell_name\ncast: maud\ndesc: The drowned bell under the Mirror Lake rings when no one is near it. Someone wants it found.\n"},{"path":"content/world/cast.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  The cast. Each NPC has a relationship record:\n#    rel  (-100..100)  how they feel about you\n#    love (0..100)     romantic interest (romance: true only)\n#    bond             none | friend | close | rival | nemesis | partner | ex\n#  Characters are introduced with `meet <id>` (or any rel/love/bond change).\n# ============================================================\n\n@npc lyra\nname: Lyra Vaneshade\ntitle: Heir of House Vaneshade, pyromancy prodigy\npronouns: she/her\nhouse: Emberhall\ncolor: #b0383c\nromance: true\nlook: hair:7 skin:5 style:1\ntags: noble ambitious fire peer\nage: 19\nrole: student\nyear: 1\ntraits: proud ambitious lonely stubborn\ninterests: duelling fire books\nstats: arcana 34 lore 22 alchemy 8 charm 20 vigor 28 guile 12 wealth 5\nschedule: duel_hall@afternoon,evening refectory@noon library@night\nrelations: kael:-10 bram:5 nessa:-5\nvoice: Clipped and formal; sarcastic when hurt. Never says \"please\" first, never admits to being tired, apologises by doing something useful.\ndesc: Brilliant, impatient and used to winning. She treats everyone as either a rival or furniture — and is quietly lonely about it.\n\n@npc tobias\nname: Tobias Fennick\ntitle: Farmer's son on a turnip scholarship\npronouns: he/him\nhouse: Rootward\ncolor: #4f8a3c\nromance: true\nlook: hair:2 skin:0 style:2\ntags: commoner kind herbs virtuous peer\nage: 19\nrole: student\nyear: 1\ntraits: kind gentle homesick loyal\ninterests: plants animals cooking dancing\nstats: arcana 12 lore 14 alchemy 26 charm 18 vigor 30 guile 4 wealth 1\nschedule: greenhouses@morning,afternoon refectory@noon,evening stables@evening\nrelations: maud:20 bram:-15 mira:10\nvoice: Warm, slow and earnest, with farm sayings and long stories about goats. Apologises too much; laughs from the belly.\ndesc: Big hands, bigger heart. Talks to plants and, worryingly, the plants seem to answer.\n\n@npc seraphine\nname: Seraphine Hale\ntitle: Seer who never quite looks at the present\npronouns: she/her\nhouse: Stillwater\ncolor: #6a4aa8\nromance: true\nlook: hair:4 skin:3 style:4\ntags: seer quiet mystery peer\nage: 19\nrole: student\nyear: 1\ntraits: dreamy distant perceptive kind\ninterests: prophecy painting tarot stars\nstats: arcana 20 lore 32 alchemy 10 charm 16 vigor 8 guile 14 wealth 3\nschedule: observatory@evening,night lakeshore@afternoon library@morning\nrelations: kael:10 lyra:5\nvoice: Half-sentences, finished in another week. Calm, vague, then suddenly exact about something that hasn't happened yet.\ndesc: Speaks in half-sentences, as if finishing the other half in a different week. Often right. Unsettlingly often.\n\n@npc corvin\nname: Corvin Ashby\ntitle: Charming liability\npronouns: he/him\nhouse: Duskgate\ncolor: #3a3a4a\nromance: true\nlook: hair:0 skin:1 style:3\ntags: rogue guile trouble peer\nage: 20\nrole: student\nyear: 2\ntraits: charming reckless loyal secretive\ninterests: maps locks cards money\nstats: arcana 16 lore 12 alchemy 14 charm 30 vigor 18 guile 38 wealth 1\nschedule: courtyard@noon,afternoon gargoyle_passage@night,midnight gilded_newt@evening\nrelations: bram:-30 nessa:10\nvoice: Quick, teasing, deflecting with jokes; calls you \"saint\" when you're being good. When he means something, the jokes stop and so does he.\ndesc: Knows every locked door in the academy and has opinions about all of them. Owes money to at least three people.\n\n@npc mira\nname: Mira Ten\ntitle: Artificer of questionable inventions\npronouns: they/them\nhouse: Brasswick\ncolor: #2a8a8a\nromance: true\nlook: hair:5 skin:2 style:0\ntags: artificer chaotic peer\nage: 19\nrole: student\nyear: 1\ntraits: chaotic curious stubborn funny\ninterests: machines clocks flying\nstats: arcana 24 lore 20 alchemy 30 charm 14 vigor 10 guile 12 wealth 3\nschedule: workshop@afternoon,evening,night refectory@noon lecture_tower@morning\nrelations: tobias:10\nvoice: Fast, enthusiastic, full of technical tangents and exclamation marks. Talks to machines more gently than to people. Says \"probably\" a lot.\ndesc: Smells permanently of solder and ozone. Has built a clockwork owl, a self-stirring cauldron and at least one small fire.\n\n@npc kael\nname: Kael Dumont\ntitle: Transfer student from the frozen North\npronouns: he/him\nhouse: Stillwater\ncolor: #3a5a7a\nromance: true\nlook: hair:4 skin:0 style:0\ntags: mystery north hollowstar peer\nage: 20\nrole: student\nyear: 1\ntraits: guarded cold lonely protective\ninterests: stars snow books\nstats: arcana 36 lore 26 alchemy 8 charm 10 vigor 22 guile 18 wealth 2\nschedule: observatory@night,midnight library@afternoon boathouse@evening\nrelations: ilse:30 lyra:-10 seraphine:5\nvoice: Few words, quiet, as if each one costs something. Northern formality; literal-minded about feelings, startled by kindness.\ndesc: Arrived mid-term with no luggage and frost in his eyelashes. Doesn't talk about where he came from. Watches the sky at night.\n\n@npc nessa\nname: Nessa Quill\ntitle: Editor of the Lumen Ledger\npronouns: she/her\nhouse: Duskgate\ncolor: #a86a2a\nromance: true\nlook: hair:0 skin:4 style:3\ntags: gossip journalist virtuous peer\nage: 20\nrole: student\nyear: 2\ntraits: nosy idealistic overworked bold\ninterests: writing gossip justice\nstats: arcana 14 lore 26 alchemy 8 charm 30 vigor 12 guile 24 wealth 2\nschedule: ledger_office@afternoon,evening,night courtyard@noon\nrelations: bram:-20 corvin:10 lyra:-5\nvoice: Rapid, bossy, headline-shaped. Treats every conversation as an interview; goes very quiet when it's off the record.\ndesc: Runs the student paper with a quill that writes on its own. Everyone is a story; some are just not finished yet.\n\n@npc bram\nname: Bram Hollis\ntitle: Prefect, enforcer, menace\npronouns: he/him\nhouse: Emberhall\ncolor: #7a4a20\nromance: false\nstart_rel: -10\nlook: hair:3 skin:1 style:2\ntags: prefect bully rival ruthless peer\nage: 21\nrole: prefect\nyear: 3\ntraits: bullying insecure proud loyal\ninterests: rules duelling\nstats: arcana 26 lore 18 alchemy 10 charm 14 vigor 32 guile 20 wealth 4\nschedule: courtyard@morning,noon duel_hall@evening library@afternoon\nrelations: tobias:-15 corvin:-30 nessa:-20 lyra:5\nvoice: Loud, officious, rule-quoting; demerits as punctuation. Under it, his father's voice. When he's honest, he's gruff and brief.\ndesc: Upper-year prefect with a ledger of demerits and a fondness for writing your name in it.\n\n@npc thorne\nname: Professor Aldric Thorne\nshort: Professor Thorne\ntitle: Master of Arcana\npronouns: he/him\nhouse: Faculty\ncolor: #444466\nromance: false\nlook: hair:4 skin:0 style:0\ntags: faculty mentor strict teacher\nage: 68\nrole: professor\nseeks: rarely\ntraits: severe exacting dry warm\ninterests: duelling roses teaching chess\nstats: arcana 88 lore 74 alchemy 30 charm 22 vigor 40 guile 36 wealth 4\nschedule: lecture_tower@morning,afternoon great_hall@noon library@evening\nrelations: isolde:30 maud:20\nvoice: Terse, precise, allergic to flattery. \"Hm.\" is a full sentence. Praise arrives disguised as criticism, and very rarely.\ndesc: Severe, exacting, allergic to flattery. Rumoured to have once out-dueled a storm.\n\n@npc maud\nname: Old Maud\nshort: Old Maud\ntitle: Groundskeeper and hedge-witch\npronouns: she/her\nhouse: Faculty\ncolor: #6a7a3a\nromance: false\nlook: hair:4 skin:2 style:4\ntags: faculty kind herbs virtuous\nage: 81\nrole: staff\nseeks: sometimes\ntraits: kind eccentric patient warm\ninterests: plants bees soup animals history\nstats: arcana 46 lore 58 alchemy 72 charm 30 vigor 20 guile 34 wealth 2\nschedule: maud_cottage@morning,evening greenhouses@afternoon orchard@noon\nrelations: thorne:20 isolde:25 tobias:20\nvoice: Folksy and sharp; calls everyone \"dearie\". Gives advice you only understand two weeks later; tells sad stories over tea as if they were recipes.\ndesc: Keeps bees, chickens and a rather large secret garden. Gives advice you only understand two weeks later.\n\n@npc isolde\nname: Headmistress Isolde Marrow\nshort: Headmistress Marrow\ntitle: Headmistress of Lumenhold\npronouns: she/her\nhouse: Faculty\ncolor: #5a2a4a\nromance: false\nlook: hair:7 skin:5 style:1\ntags: faculty authority\nage: 57\nrole: headmistress\nseeks: rarely\ntraits: calm watchful formidable perceptive\ninterests: chess history justice\nstats: arcana 84 lore 90 alchemy 50 charm 60 vigor 30 guile 70 wealth 4\nschedule: headmistress_tower@morning,afternoon,evening great_hall@noon\nrelations: thorne:30 maud:25\nvoice: Pleasant, unhurried, every word placed like a chess piece. Never raises her voice; never needs to. A short, delighted laugh when surprised.\ndesc: Tall, pale and impossibly calm. She remembers every student's name — and every student's mistakes.\n\n@npc ilse\nname: Ilse Dumont\ntitle: Kael's younger sister, a storm in white furs\npronouns: she/her\nhouse: none\ncolor: #8aa8c8\nromance: false\nlook: hair:4 skin:5 style:1\ntags: north hollowstar ruthless peer\nage: 18\nrole: outsider\ntraits: fierce lonely proud wary\ninterests: snow duelling\nstats: arcana 40 lore 18 alchemy 6 charm 12 vigor 26 guile 24 wealth 2\nschedule: village@afternoon,evening lakeshore@night\nrelations: kael:20\nvoice: Sharp, curt, older than she is; Northern words when upset. Treats kindness like a trick she hasn't learned the counter to.\ndesc: Raised by the Hollow cult to open a door she has never seen. Sharp as an icicle, lonely as a frozen lake, and much younger than she pretends to be.\n\n# ---------------- Fixtures (design §5.5): people of a place or a track, with lighter ladders ----------------\n# They come looking for you rarely, and only once you've been coming to them (`seeks:`). No romance.\n\n@npc amaranth\nname: Sister Amaranth\nshort: Sister Amaranth\ntitle: Healer of the infirmary\npronouns: she/her\nhouse: Faculty\ncolor: #9a3b4a\nromance: false\nlook: hair:2 skin:3 style:0\ntags: faculty healer teacher virtuous\nage: 54\nrole: staff\nseeks: rarely\ntraits: severe honest patient warm\ninterests: plants soup history\nstats: arcana 40 lore 52 alchemy 80 charm 24 vigor 34 guile 30 wealth 2\nschedule: alchemy_lab@morning infirmary@afternoon,evening,night great_hall@noon\nrelations: maud:25 thorne:10 brask:15\nvoice: Short sentences, delivered while doing something else. Believes no excuse, forgives most things anyway. Praise comes as an instruction (\"Keep doing that.\").\ndesc: Runs the infirmary with warming charms, tonics and no patience for excuses. Teaches the healing half of Alchemy & Healing.\n\n@npc brask\nname: Professor Hedda Brask\nshort: Professor Brask\ntitle: Professor of Beastcraft & Flight\npronouns: she/her\nhouse: Faculty\ncolor: #7a5a2a\nromance: false\nlook: hair:3 skin:4 style:4\ntags: faculty teacher animals\nage: 61\nrole: professor\nseeks: rarely\ntraits: dry gentle stubborn brave\ninterests: animals flying history\nstats: arcana 52 lore 48 alchemy 30 charm 16 vigor 70 guile 28 wealth 2\nschedule: stables@morning,afternoon,evening great_hall@noon\nrelations: amaranth:15 maud:20 thorne:5\nvoice: Speaks to creatures in full sentences and to students in half ones. Every story ends with a bite and a moral. Laughs once a term, loudly.\ndesc: Teaches Beastcraft & Flight and is kinder to animals than to students, though the gap closes for anyone who mucks out without being asked.\n\n@npc vey\nname: Professor Ottilie Vey\nshort: Professor Vey\ntitle: Professor of Illusion & Glamour\npronouns: she/her\nhouse: Faculty\ncolor: #6a4a8a\nromance: false\nlook: hair:6 skin:1 style:1\ntags: faculty teacher theatre\nage: 46\nrole: professor\nseeks: rarely\ntraits: charming perceptive sly funny\ninterests: theatre painting cards\nstats: arcana 60 lore 50 alchemy 20 charm 78 guile 70 vigor 22 wealth 3\nschedule: lecture_tower@morning,afternoon great_hall@noon\nrelations: thorne:5 marisol:20\nvoice: Amused, theatrical, a little too observant. Asks questions she already knows the answer to, to see what you'll do. Delighted by anyone who fools her.\ndesc: Teaches Illusion & Glamour and grades on whether you fooled her. She is very hard to fool, and very generous to the few who manage it.\n\n@npc marisol\nname: Professor Ines Marisol\nshort: Professor Marisol\ntitle: Professor of Tidecalling\npronouns: she/her\nhouse: Faculty\ncolor: #2a6a8a\nromance: false\nlook: hair:1 skin:3 style:4\ntags: faculty teacher tides\nage: 39\nrole: professor\nseeks: rarely\ntraits: dreamy warm reckless curious\ninterests: tides music writing\nstats: arcana 70 lore 56 alchemy 18 charm 40 guile 20 vigor 52 wealth 2\nschedule: lakeshore@morning,evening lecture_tower@afternoon\nrelations: vey:20 thorne:15\nvoice: Soft and unhurried until there's weather; then she whoops. Quotes poems about water and forgets the last line. Believes cold water is good for everyone.\ndesc: Teaches the tidecalling half of Evocation, waist-deep in the lake at dawn. Keeps the lake calm on exam days and reads poetry under a dry patch of air when it rains.\n\n@npc mabel\nname: Mabel Thistlewood\nshort: Mabel\ntitle: Keeper of the tea shop\npronouns: she/her\nhouse: none\ncolor: #a0703a\nromance: false\nlook: hair:4 skin:2 style:4\ntags: village kind\nage: 63\nrole: villager\nseeks: rarely\ntraits: perceptive warm nosy patient\ninterests: cooking gossip music\nstats: arcana 14 lore 30 alchemy 36 charm 58 vigor 20 guile 40 wealth 3\nschedule: mabels@morning,noon,afternoon village@evening\nrelations: grimsby:20 pell:15\nvoice: Warm and brisk; finishes your order for you. Reads moods like weather and says so. Calls students \"petal\" and means something different every time.\ndesc: Forty kinds of tea, three kinds of cake, and a talent for reading your mood from the way you open her door.\n\n@npc grimsby\nname: Mr Alder Grimsby\nshort: Mr Grimsby\ntitle: Bookseller\npronouns: he/him\nhouse: none\ncolor: #5a4a2a\nromance: false\nlook: hair:4 skin:1 style:0\ntags: village books\nage: 72\nrole: villager\nseeks: rarely\ntraits: eccentric kind distant curious\ninterests: books history maps\nstats: arcana 20 lore 74 alchemy 12 charm 30 vigor 10 guile 22 wealth 2\nschedule: grimsbys_books@noon,afternoon village@evening\nrelations: mabel:20\nvoice: Vague, courteous, enthusiastic about the wrong thing. Recommends books by their smell. Remembers what you read three years ago and nothing about what you asked for today.\ndesc: Keeps a bookshop so crowded the shelves lean on each other. He will find anything except what you came in for, which is often what you needed.\n\n@npc pell\nname: Old Pell\nshort: Old Pell\ntitle: Apothecary of Pell & Daughters\npronouns: he/him\nhouse: none\ncolor: #3a5a3a\nromance: false\nlook: hair:4 skin:2 style:0\ntags: village\nage: 77\nrole: villager\nseeks: rarely\ntraits: exacting proud loyal stubborn\ninterests: plants rules money\nstats: arcana 22 lore 46 alchemy 76 charm 14 vigor 16 guile 30 wealth 3\nschedule: pell_apothecary@morning,afternoon village@evening\nrelations: mabel:15 grisk:-20\nvoice: Gruff, precise, counts aloud. Never apologises; corrects a label instead. Trusts people who measure twice, and says so once a decade.\ndesc: Taught three generations of the village to count drops. His daughters run the counter now; he runs the drawers, and the drawers are never wrong.\n\n@npc grisk\nname: Grisk\nshort: Grisk\ntitle: Moneylender, friend to the temporarily embarrassed\npronouns: he/him\nhouse: none\ncolor: #4a6a3a\nromance: false\nlook: hair:0 skin:6 style:1\ntags: village goblin money\nage: 58\nrole: villager\nseeks: sometimes\ntraits: sly charming patient guarded\ninterests: money cards gossip\nstats: arcana 18 lore 40 alchemy 20 charm 46 vigor 14 guile 82 wealth 4\nschedule: gilded_newt@evening village@afternoon\nrelations: pell:-10\nvoice: Courtly, purring, every sentence an invoice. Calls debts \"friendships\". Never threatens; describes, pleasantly, what will happen.\ndesc: A goblin in a velvet waistcoat who lends to students: sixty crowns today, twelve a month for six. He keeps a ledger, and sometimes asks for favours instead of coin.\n"},{"path":"content/world/factions.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Factions (design §3.2): who wants what. alignment: lean on the\n#  Lumen (+) ↔ Hollow (−) axis · stance: toward the player at the\n#  start of a run.\n# ============================================================\n\n@faction wardens\nname: The Wardens\nmembers: isolde thorne maud\nalignment: 60\nstance: neutral\ndesc: The headmistress's quiet circle, sworn to keep the founders' seal. They recruit students only when the seal grows thin.\n\n@faction hollow_cult\nname: The Hollow Cult\nmembers: ilse\nalignment: -80\nstance: hostile\ndesc: Northern families, the Dumonts first among them, who serve the star beneath the lake. Their messenger is the man in grey.\n\n@faction midnight_cartographers\nname: The Midnight Cartographers\nmembers: corvin\nalignment: 0\nstance: neutral\ndesc: The club that doesn't exist: students who map the castle's secret passages and share them with almost no one.\n\n@faction faculty\nname: The Faculty\nmembers: isolde thorne maud\nalignment: 30\nstance: neutral\ndesc: The professors and staff of Lumenhold: strict, eccentric, and mostly on your side whether you like it or not.\n\n@faction conclave\nname: The Conclave of the Seven Towers\nalignment: 20\nstance: neutral\ndesc: The governing body of magic in the realm, headquartered in the capital. Hands out research posts, licences and very long letters.\n\n@faction thornwick\nname: Thornwick Academy\nalignment: 0\nstance: hostile\ndesc: The rival school across the hills. Famous for illusionists, tournament grudges and spies who think they're subtle.\n"},{"path":"content/world/facts/amaranth.grim","text":"# Facts about Sister Amaranth.\n\n@fact amaranth.healer\ntier: surface\ntext: Runs the infirmary and teaches the healing half of Alchemy & Healing. Believes no excuse.\n\n@fact amaranth.truth\ntier: personal\nunlock: seen(amaranth_intro) > 0\ntext: She treats the true version of an injury faster than the story you tell her, and she always knows which one you told.\n\n@fact amaranth.rounds\ntier: personal\nunlock: seen(amaranth_rounds) > 0\ntext: She does evening rounds of every bed herself, and has never once missed one.\n\n@fact amaranth.key\ntier: personal\nunlock: seen(amaranth_trust) > 0\ntext: Only a handful of students have ever been trusted with the key to her tonic cupboard.\n"},{"path":"content/world/facts/bram.grim","text":"# Facts about Bram Hollis.\n\n@fact bram.prefect\ntier: surface\ntext: An upper-year prefect with a red sash, a ledger of demerits and a fondness for your name.\n\n@fact bram.father\ntier: secret\nunlock: seen(bram_crying) > 0 or seen(bram_father_visit) > 0\ntext: His father was head boy, and has told Bram all his life that he will never amount to anything.\n\n@fact bram.hall_tax\ntier: personal\nunlock: seen(bram_hall_tax) > 0\ntext: He once invented a \"hall tax\" for the east staircase.\n\n@fact bram.cheating\ntier: secret\nunlock: seen(bram_cheating) > 0\ntext: You saw him cheat in an exam. He knows you saw.\n\n@fact bram.ally\ntier: personal\nunlock: seen(bram_turns) > 0\ntext: Under the sash, he will step between you and trouble. Then he tells you not to make it weird.\n"},{"path":"content/world/facts/brask.grim","text":"# Facts about Professor Brask.\n\n@fact brask.beasts\ntier: surface\ntext: Teaches Beastcraft & Flight from the stables, and talks to every creature in them more kindly than to students.\n\n@fact brask.stories\ntier: personal\nunlock: seen(brask_shovel) > 0\ntext: Every one of her stories ends with a bite and a moral. She laughs out loud about once a term.\n\n@fact brask.owl\ntier: personal\nunlock: seen(brask_night_owl) > 0\ntext: The old barn owl in the stable loft has outlived three of her colleagues. She would not say which loss hurt most.\n\n@fact brask.courtesies\ntier: personal\nunlock: seen(brask_trust) > 0\ntext: She bows to the things that live in the Whisperwood, and believes it's why they have never bitten her. Much.\n"},{"path":"content/world/facts/corvin.grim","text":"# Facts about Corvin Ashby.\n\n@fact corvin.locks\ntier: surface\ntext: Knows every locked door in the academy and owes money to at least three people.\n\n@fact corvin.last_warning\ntier: personal\nunlock: seen(corvin_rooftop) > 0\ntext: He is on his last warning. One more detention and they send him home, and home is not a good place.\n\n@fact corvin.brother\ntier: secret\nunlock: seen(corvin_brother) > 0\ntext: His brother turns up for money and threatens to tell the headmistress where Corvin learned to pick locks.\n\n@fact corvin.grandfather\ntier: personal\nunlock: seen(corvin_confiscation_vault) > 0\ntext: His grandfather's lucky deck sat in the confiscation vault for decades. It really is lucky.\n\n@fact corvin.cartographers\ntier: secret\nunlock: member(cartographers)\ntext: He runs the Midnight Cartographers, the club that doesn't exist.\n\n@fact corvin.trolls\ntier: personal\nunlock: seen(corvin_debt) > 0\ntext: He once owed thirty crowns to trolls and offered to pay you back with a goat.\n"},{"path":"content/world/facts/grimsby.grim","text":"# Facts about Mr Grimsby.\n\n@fact grimsby.books\ntier: surface\ntext: Keeps the village bookshop. He will find anything except what you came in for.\n\n@fact grimsby.wrong\ntier: personal\nunlock: seen(grimsby_intro) > 0\ntext: The book he gives you instead is, more often than not, the one you needed.\n\n@fact grimsby.parcels\ntier: personal\nunlock: seen(grimsby_parcel) > 0\ntext: He posts books to students he thinks need them, on credit, and has never once chased a debt.\n\n@fact grimsby.lighthouses\ntier: personal\nunlock: seen(grimsby_mind_the_shop) > 0\ntext: He has a weakness for lighthouses, and when he closes the shop, it is usually to go and look at one.\n"},{"path":"content/world/facts/grisk.grim","text":"# Facts about Grisk.\n\n@fact grisk.lender\ntier: surface\ntext: A goblin moneylender in a velvet waistcoat: sixty crowns today, twelve a month for six months.\n\n@fact grisk.ledger\ntier: personal\nunlock: met(grisk) and seen(grisk_pleasantries) > 0\ntext: He calls debts \"friendships\", and keeps all of them in one ledger he never lets out of reach.\n\n@fact grisk.favours\ntier: personal\nunlock: seen(grisk_favour) > 0\ntext: He sometimes asks for favours instead of coin, and the favours are for clients he never names.\n"},{"path":"content/world/facts/ilse.grim","text":"# Facts about Ilse Dumont.\n\n@fact ilse.furs\ntier: surface\ntext: Kael's younger sister: white furs, a private snowstorm and coins that freeze to counters.\n\n@fact ilse.raised\ntier: personal\nunlock: rel(ilse) >= 12\ntext: The Hollow cult raised her to open a door she has never seen.\n\n@fact ilse.frost_craft\ntier: personal\nunlock: seen(ilse_frost_lessons) > 0\ntext: She knows Northern frost-craft better than anything the professors teach.\n\n@fact ilse.grey\ntier: secret\nunlock: seen(ilse_grey_returns) > 0\ntext: The man in grey came to take her home. She didn't call for help; she only looked.\n\n@fact ilse.scared\ntier: secret\nunlock: seen(ilse_kael_reunion) > 0\ntext: She admits, to her brother, that she was scared. So was he.\n"},{"path":"content/world/facts/isolde.grim","text":"# Facts about Headmistress Isolde Marrow.\n\n@fact isolde.names\ntier: surface\ntext: She remembers every student's name, and every student's mistakes.\n\n@fact isolde.portrait\ntier: secret\nunlock: seen(isolde_portrait) > 0\ntext: As a student she set the observatory on fire twice. Her portrait from those days is hidden in a forgotten corridor.\n\n@fact isolde.chess\ntier: personal\nunlock: seen(isolde_chess) > 0\ntext: She plays tower chess by her window and laughs, short and delighted, when she loses.\n\n@fact isolde.thread\ntier: secret\nunlock: flag(warden_trial) or seen(hs_isolde_summons) > 0\ntext: She has been following the same thread you have: the seal beneath the lake is weakening.\n\n@fact isolde.mercy\ntier: personal\nunlock: seen(isolde_judgement) > 0\ntext: She asks students what they think is just, and sometimes she listens.\n"},{"path":"content/world/facts/kael.grim","text":"# Facts about Kael Dumont.\n\n@fact kael.north\ntier: surface\ntext: A transfer from the frozen North who arrived mid-term with frost in his eyelashes.\n\n@fact kael.cold_hands\ntier: personal\nunlock: seen(kael_cold_hands) > 0\ntext: His hands are always cold. Nobody has ever been warm enough before.\n\n@fact kael.sky\ntier: personal\nunlock: seen(astro_kael_roof) > 0\ntext: He watches a patch of sky with no stars in it: a star that eats light.\n\n@fact kael.family\ntier: secret\nunlock: flag(kael_told)\ntext: His family serve something under the lake. They sent him to open its door; he came to stop them.\n\n@fact kael.sister\ntier: secret\nunlock: flag(met_sister) or seen(ilse_village) > 0\ntext: He has a younger sister, Ilse, raised to do what he wouldn't.\n\n@fact kael.mother\ntier: personal\nunlock: seen(kael_northern_lights) > 0\ntext: His mother never once said she was proud of him.\n\n@fact kael.wanting\ntier: secret\nunlock: seen(kael_confession) > 0\ntext: He was taught not to want things, because wanting is how they control you.\n"},{"path":"content/world/facts/lyra.grim","text":"# Facts about Lyra Vaneshade (design §10.1). Tiers: surface (known on meeting),\n# personal (closeness), secret (specific events). Shown in the People panel in phase 4.\n\n@fact lyra.prodigy\ntier: surface\ntext: The pyromancy prodigy of House Vaneshade. Everyone knows the name, and she makes sure of it.\n\n@fact lyra.toast\ntier: personal\nunlock: seen(lyra_toast) > 0\ntext: She can incinerate a target at forty paces. She cannot make toast.\n\n@fact lyra.mother\ntier: personal\nunlock: rel(lyra) >= 18\ntext: Her mother has never once told her \"well done\".\n\n@fact lyra.second_place\ntier: personal\nunlock: seen(lyra_letter) > 0\ntext: She came second in the Winter Trials once. Her mother wrote to ask who beat her.\n\n@fact lyra.burnout\ntier: secret\nunlock: seen(lyra_burnout) > 0\ntext: She studies until she collapses, and begs you not to let her mother find out.\n\n@fact lyra.rival\ntier: personal\nunlock: bond(lyra) == \"rival\"\ntext: You are the only person here who makes her better. She hates it. She doesn't want it to stop.\n\n@fact lyra.inheritance\ntier: secret\nunlock: seen(lyra_family_ultimatum) > 0\ntext: Her mother threatened her inheritance over you. She set the letter on fire.\n"},{"path":"content/world/facts/mabel.grim","text":"# Facts about Mabel.\n\n@fact mabel.teashop\ntier: surface\ntext: Keeps the tea shop in the village: forty kinds of tea, three kinds of cake.\n\n@fact mabel.door\ntier: personal\nunlock: seen(mabel_intro) > 0\ntext: She reads your mood from the way you open her door, and is rarely wrong.\n\n@fact mabel.tins\ntier: personal\nunlock: seen(mabel_parcel) > 0\ntext: She sends tins of tea up to the castle on the barge for students she's worried about, and refuses payment for them.\n\n@fact mabel.cups\ntier: personal\nunlock: seen(mabel_cup) > 0\ntext: The cups on the hooks behind her counter belong to her regulars, living and dead. She remembers every one of them.\n"},{"path":"content/world/facts/marisol.grim","text":"# Facts about Professor Marisol.\n\n@fact marisol.tides\ntier: surface\ntext: Teaches the tidecalling half of Evocation, waist-deep in the lake at dawn, and keeps the lake calm on exam days.\n\n@fact marisol.poems\ntier: personal\nunlock: seen(marisol_intro) > 0\ntext: She reads poems about water in the rain, under a dry patch of air, and never remembers their last lines.\n\n@fact marisol.storms\ntier: personal\nunlock: seen(marisol_storm) > 0\ntext: She goes out into the worst storms of the year on purpose. She says nobody learns tides on a calm day.\n\n@fact marisol.margins\ntier: personal\nunlock: seen(marisol_trust) > 0\ntext: She wrote her own tidecalling into the margins of a book of poems when she was a student, and still teaches from it.\n"},{"path":"content/world/facts/maud.grim","text":"# Facts about Old Maud.\n\n@fact maud.groundskeeper\ntier: surface\ntext: Groundskeeper and hedge-witch, keeper of bees, chickens and a goat called Bartholomew.\n\n@fact maud.student\ntier: personal\nunlock: seen(maud_old_student) > 0\ntext: She was a student here sixty years ago, the spring the lake froze.\n\n@fact maud.edmund\ntier: secret\nunlock: seen(maud_old_wand) > 0\ntext: Edmund went down beneath the lake that night and didn't come back. She kept his silver wand.\n\n@fact maud.soup\ntier: personal\nunlock: seen(maud_recipe) > 0\ntext: Her soup cures colds, broken hearts and most curses. You're not allowed to write it down.\n\n@fact maud.garden\ntier: personal\nunlock: seen(garden_maud_secret) > 0\ntext: Behind the potting shed there is a door to a garden that only exists for people she shows it to.\n\n@fact maud.age\ntier: personal\nunlock: seen(maud_ill) > 0\ntext: She is eighty-one, and says she's allowed the occasional chill.\n"},{"path":"content/world/facts/mira.grim","text":"# Facts about Mira Ten (they/them).\n\n@fact mira.artificer\ntier: surface\ntext: An artificer who smells of solder and ozone and has started at least one small fire.\n\n@fact mira.owl\ntier: personal\nunlock: seen(mira_owl_gift) > 0\ntext: Built a clockwork owl that remembers deadlines, birthdays and, slightly, grudges.\n\n@fact mira.notebooks\ntier: personal\nunlock: rel(mira) >= 12\ntext: Three years of notebooks are the most precious things they own.\n\n@fact mira.family\ntier: personal\nunlock: seen(mira_family_business) > 0\ntext: Their family runs a clockworks and thinks invention is a hobby. It is not a hobby.\n\n@fact mira.name_day\ntier: secret\nunlock: seen(mira_name_day) > 0\ntext: Nobody ever remembered their name day before.\n\n@fact mira.workshop\ntier: personal\nunlock: seen(mira_workshop_closed) > 0\ntext: After the third fire in a term, the faculty padlocked their workshop.\n"},{"path":"content/world/facts/nessa.grim","text":"# Facts about Nessa Quill.\n\n@fact nessa.editor\ntier: surface\ntext: Editor of the Lumen Ledger, with a quill that writes on its own.\n\n@fact nessa.quill\ntier: personal\nunlock: seen(nessa_burnout) > 0\ntext: Left unsupervised, her quill starts inventing the news.\n\n@fact nessa.interview\ntier: personal\nunlock: rel(nessa) >= 12\ntext: Nobody has interviewed the headmistress in twenty years. She means to be the first.\n\n@fact nessa.string\ntier: secret\nunlock: seen(nessa_hollow_story) > 0\ntext: Her office wall is covered in red string around a sketch of a black star.\n\n@fact nessa.press\ntier: personal\nunlock: seen(nessa_paper_threatened) > 0\ntext: She would chain herself to the printing press before letting anyone shut the Ledger.\n\n@fact nessa.off_record\ntier: secret\nunlock: seen(nessa_confession) > 0\ntext: The only time her quill stops is when she tells it the conversation is off the record.\n"},{"path":"content/world/facts/pell.grim","text":"# Facts about Old Pell.\n\n@fact pell.apothecary\ntier: surface\ntext: Runs the drawers at Pell & Daughters. He taught three generations of the village to count drops.\n\n@fact pell.counting\ntier: personal\nunlock: seen(pell_count_drops) > 0\ntext: He makes every new clerk count aloud. He trusts people who measure twice.\n\n@fact pell.daughters\ntier: personal\nunlock: seen(pell_daughters) > 0\ntext: His daughters run the counter and want the shop to change; he wants it to be fifty years ago. They're all secretly proud of each other.\n"},{"path":"content/world/facts/seraphine.grim","text":"# Facts about Seraphine Hale.\n\n@fact seraphine.seer\ntier: surface\ntext: A seer who speaks in half-sentences and is unsettlingly often right.\n\n@fact seraphine.slips\ntier: personal\nunlock: seen(seraphine_lost_day) > 0\ntext: Sometimes she slips a day ahead and needs someone to tell her what today is.\n\n@fact seraphine.oracle\ntier: personal\nunlock: rel(seraphine) >= 15\ntext: Her mother is the Oracle of Vell, who wants her home to tell kings what they want to hear.\n\n@fact seraphine.self\ntier: personal\nunlock: seen(seraphine_painting) > 0\ntext: Seers can't see themselves, unless someone is standing next to them.\n\n@fact seraphine.lake\ntier: secret\nunlock: seen(seraphine_lake_vision) > 0\ntext: The lake calls to her at night. She says it calls to you too.\n\n@fact seraphine.too_loud\ntier: secret\nunlock: seen(seraphine_overwhelmed) > 0\ntext: When everyone's futures get too loud she hides in the dark. Yours, she says, is quiet.\n"},{"path":"content/world/facts/thorne.grim","text":"# Facts about Professor Aldric Thorne.\n\n@fact thorne.storm\ntier: surface\ntext: Rumoured to have once out-duelled a storm.\n\n@fact thorne.lost\ntier: personal\nunlock: seen(thorne_storm_story) > 0\ntext: He lost to the storm, badly. Losing well, he says, is the only thing worth teaching.\n\n@fact thorne.roses\ntier: secret\nunlock: seen(thorne_roses) > 0\ntext: He tends a tiny walled rose garden behind the observatory at dawn and talks to the roses.\n\n@fact thorne.hands\ntier: secret\nunlock: seen(thorne_shaking_hands) > 0\ntext: His hands have started to shake. Age comes for every storm eventually.\n\n@fact thorne.rival\ntier: personal\nunlock: seen(thorne_old_rival) > 0\ntext: A flamboyant old wizard in purple has wanted a rematch with him for forty years.\n\n@fact thorne.letters\ntier: personal\nunlock: rel(thorne) >= 18\ntext: He writes very few letters of recommendation, and the Conclave reads every one.\n"},{"path":"content/world/facts/tobias.grim","text":"# Facts about Tobias Fennick.\n\n@fact tobias.scholarship\ntier: surface\ntext: A farmer's son on a turnip scholarship, built like a barn door and gentle as a lamb.\n\n@fact tobias.plants\ntier: surface\ntext: He talks to plants. Worryingly, they seem to answer.\n\n@fact tobias.ma\ntier: personal\nunlock: seen(tobias_homesick) > 0\ntext: His mother runs the farm alone at harvest. It's the first harvest he has ever missed.\n\n@fact tobias.gerald\ntier: personal\nunlock: seen(tobias_godparent) > 0\ntext: He is raising a mandrake called Gerald, who wears a knitted hat and screams softly.\n\n@fact tobias.moonflowers\ntier: personal\nunlock: rel(tobias) >= 12\ntext: The greenhouse moonflowers bloom once every eleven years. He would stand in a storm for them.\n\n@fact tobias.blight\ntier: secret\nunlock: seen(tobias_bad_harvest) > 0\ntext: Blight took his family's harvest. Thirty crowns stood between them and a hungry winter.\n\n@fact tobias.dancing\ntier: personal\nunlock: seen(tobias_dance_lessons) > 0\ntext: He practised waltzing with a broom before the Midwinter Ball. The broom complained.\n"},{"path":"content/world/facts/vey.grim","text":"# Facts about Professor Vey.\n\n@fact vey.illusion\ntier: surface\ntext: Teaches Illusion & Glamour and grades on whether you fooled her.\n\n@fact vey.tests\ntier: personal\nunlock: seen(vey_pop_quiz) > 0\ntext: She tests students in corridors, unannounced, with tea that isn't there. She keeps score.\n\n@fact vey.revue\ntier: personal\nunlock: seen(vey_revue) > 0\ntext: She produces the faculty revue every year, and has never once let Professor Thorne refuse a part.\n\n@fact vey.mirror\ntier: personal\nunlock: seen(vey_trust) > 0\ntext: She doesn't use the mirror charm in public. Once, her reflection didn't come back for a week.\n"},{"path":"content/world/lore/soft/academy.grim","text":"# Lore drafts (canon: soft): the academy and its customs.\n\n@lore lumenhold\nkeys: Lumenhold, academy, seven towers, bridges of light\ncanon: soft\ntext: Lumenhold Academy is seven towers joined by bridges of light, standing above a mirror-still lake. It has trained mages for three hundred years and has been a seal for exactly as long.\n\n@lore mirror_lake\nkeys: Mirror Lake, lake\ncanon: soft\ntext: The lake is so still it shows the towers upside down. Students swim in it in summer and catch Lake Fever when they shouldn't. Some nights it calls to seers.\n\n@lore houses\nkeys: houses, Emberhall, Rootward, Stillwater, Duskgate, Brasswick\ncanon: soft\ntext: Students belong to five houses: Emberhall (fire and ambition), Rootward (growing things), Stillwater (quiet and the far-sighted), Duskgate (shadows and secrets) and Brasswick (makers and tinkerers). Faculty belong to none.\n\n@lore crowns\nkeys: crowns, money, currency\ncanon: soft\ntext: The currency is the crown. Rent and board at the academy cost about ten crowns a month; a good meal in the village, one; a registered familiar, twenty.\n\n@lore calendar\nkeys: Harvestmoon, Emberwane, Mistveil, Frostcall, Deepwinter, Thawmoon, Stormbloom, Greenrise, Blossomtide, Highsun, Goldenhaze, Starfall\ncanon: soft\ntext: The academic year starts in autumn with Harvestmoon, then Emberwane, Mistveil, Frostcall, Deepwinter, Thawmoon, Stormbloom, Greenrise, Blossomtide, Highsun, Goldenhaze and Starfall.\n\n@lore festivals\nkeys: Harvest Feast, Lantern Night, Midwinter Ball, Spring Tournament, Starfall festival\ncanon: soft\ntext: The Harvest Feast opens the year; on Lantern Night lanterns carry wishes over the lake; the Midwinter Ball fills the Great Hall; the Spring Tournament crowns a duelling champion; and at Starfall the whole academy sleeps on the lawns to watch the meteors.\n\n@lore exams\nkeys: Winter Trials, Grand Examinations, exams\ncanon: soft\ntext: The Winter Trials test the term's work in Frostcall. The Grand Examinations close the year: three days, five examiners and a practical before a panel that includes the headmistress.\n\n@lore restricted_section\nkeys: Restricted Section, silver key\ncanon: soft\ntext: A locked section at the back of the library, opened by a silver key, where black-bound books breathe softly. Bartholomew the goat once ate a third of \"Curses of the Northern Wastes\" there.\n\n@lore confiscation_vault\nkeys: confiscation vault, contraband\ncanon: soft\ntext: Three hundred years of everything the faculty ever took off a student. Its alarm is a very loud goblin.\n\n@lore gargoyles\nkeys: gargoyle, third gargoyle, passages\ncanon: soft\ntext: The castle is riddled with passages that appear on no official plan. The way into the Midnight Cartographers' world is behind the third gargoyle on the north parapet.\n\n@lore moving_stairs\nkeys: staircases, moving stairs, east wing\ncanon: soft\ntext: Lumenhold's staircases move when they feel like it and occasionally eat shoes, which turn up under the stairs in the east wing.\n\n@lore demerits\nkeys: demerits, prefects, detention, sash\ncanon: soft\ntext: Prefects wear red sashes and keep ledgers of demerits. Detention is usually scrubbing pans or cataloguing contraband. Being stripped of a sash is the worst thing that can happen to a prefect short of expulsion.\n\n@lore familiars\nkeys: familiars, registration, Ember\ncanon: soft\ntext: Students may keep a familiar if it is registered: twenty crowns and a stamp from the registrar. Unregistered familiars are tolerated if nobody notices them, and some don't want to be noticed.\n\n@lore lumen_ledger\nkeys: Lumen Ledger, newspaper, press\ncanon: soft\ntext: The student newspaper, printed at dawn on a press that groans like a dragon. Its gossip column is feared; its editor, Nessa Quill, has a quill that writes on its own.\n\n@lore infirmary\nkeys: infirmary, Sister Amaranth, Lake Fever\ncanon: soft\ntext: Sister Amaranth runs the infirmary with warming charms, tonics and no patience for excuses. Lake Fever comes from swimming where you shouldn't; curse-cold comes from worse.\n\n@lore graduation\nkeys: graduation, speech, yearbook\ncanon: soft\ntext: Each year one graduate speaks for their class in the Great Hall, chosen by the faculty. Graduates plant something, keep something, or leave something behind.\n\n@lore fairs\nkeys: Club Fair, Job Board, Artificers' Fair, Midsummer Fair\ncanon: soft\ntext: In Emberwane the Club Fair fills the courtyard with recruiting tables; the Job Board in the entrance hall posts term-time work. The Artificers' Fair shows off students' machines, and in summer the village holds its Midsummer Fair with an enchanted carousel.\n\n@lore disciplinary_board\nkeys: Disciplinary Board, probation, expulsion\ncanon: soft\ntext: When a student's standing collapses, the Disciplinary Board meets: three professors, a long table and very little mercy. Probation comes first; expulsion second.\n\n@lore lumen_essay_prize\nkeys: Lumen Essay Prize, essay prize\ncanon: soft\ntext: An annual prize of fifty crowns for the best student essay on a set theme, judged by the faculty. Past themes include \"Magic and Duty\".\n\n@lore sorting_mirror\nkeys: Sorting Mirror, entrance hall\ncanon: soft\ntext: A tall enchanted mirror in the entrance hall. It does not sort anyone into anything; it asks, in a voice like cold water, who you were before you came.\n\n@lore spire_library\nkeys: Spire Library, the Spire\ncanon: soft\ntext: The academy's research library at the top of the tallest tower, where theses of distinction are shelved in a section students whisper about.\n"},{"path":"content/world/lore/soft/hollowstar.grim","text":"# Lore drafts (canon: soft), distilled from the existing events in phase 1.\n# Curate: review, correct, then move entries to lore/hard/ with canon: hard (design §3.6).\n# The Hollow Star and the founders' seal.\n\n@lore hollow_star\nkeys: Hollow Star, star, light-eating star, the thing under the lake\ncanon: soft\ntext: A star that is not a light but an absence: it eats light. It sleeps beneath the Mirror Lake, behind a round door. It is not asleep so much as waiting, and it learns the names of those who look for it.\n\n@lore founders_seal\nkeys: founders, seal, seven locks, seven towers\ncanon: soft\ntext: The founders built Lumenhold as a seal over the Hollow Star: seven towers, one lock in each, and a key shaped like a star that holds no light. The seal weakens over generations and must be renewed.\n\n@lore star_key\nkeys: star key, key, seventh tower\ncanon: soft\ntext: The key to the founders' seal is shaped like a star and black as a hole in the world. It was kept in a star-shaped hollow at the top of the seventh tower, behind wards that shriek.\n\n@lore seventh_tower\nkeys: seventh tower, locked stair\ncanon: soft\ntext: One of the seven towers has been locked for a century. At its top is a single room of dust and moonlight. When the star key is lifted, every candle in the academy goes out at once.\n\n@lore round_door\nkeys: door, round door, beneath the lake, tunnel\ncanon: soft\ntext: A tunnel from the boathouse runs down to a round door at the bottom of the lake. On the night the seal weakens, it stands open a crack, and the Star looks through.\n\n@lore drowned_bell\nkeys: drowned bell, bell, lake bell\ncanon: soft\ntext: A bell lies under the Mirror Lake. It tolls when the Star stirs, and once on the night the lake freezes. Edmund's silver wand hums the same note.\n\n@lore lake_freezes\nkeys: frozen lake, the Night, Blossom moon, freeze\ncanon: soft\ntext: When the seal is at its weakest, on the night of the Blossom moon, the lake freezes in a single breath in the middle of spring. Cracks glow beneath the ice and things made of frost and whispers come ashore.\n\n@lore founders_ledger\nkeys: Founders' Ledger, ledger, archive vault\ncanon: soft\ntext: The Founders' Ledger in the archive vault is three hundred years old. The entry for the founding year was cut out with a razor, but the impression of the pen remains on the page beneath. A client of Grisk's once paid to have another page removed.\n\n@lore codex_hollow_names\nkeys: Codex of Hollow Names, codex, Restricted Section\ncanon: soft\ntext: A book in the Restricted Section that finds its readers. Its first page is a list of names; the last is still being written. Reading it turns the fingertips grey.\n\n@lore star_mark\nkeys: star mark, mark, cold coin\ncanon: soft\ntext: Relics of the Star burn so cold they feel hot and can leave a pale star-shaped mark on the palm. The mark hums near the lake and makes spells come easier, which should worry its bearer more than it does.\n\n@lore hollow_dreams\nkeys: dreams, black star dream\ncanon: soft\ntext: Those the Star notices dream of the lake from underneath and wake with frost on their pillows. Lavender doesn't stop the dreams, but it makes them smell nicer.\n\n@lore man_in_grey\nkeys: man in grey, grey coat\ncanon: soft\ntext: A tall, polite man in a grey coat who serves the Star on behalf of the Northern families. Fountains freeze when he talks. He offers power to those who ask questions, and he has come for both Dumont children.\n\n@lore hollow_cult\nkeys: Hollow cult, cult, Northern families\ncanon: soft\ntext: Northern families who serve the Star beneath the lake and send their children south to open its door. Their letters carry a black-star seal.\n\n@lore dumont_family\nkeys: Dumont, Dumonts, Kael, Ilse\ncanon: soft\ntext: The Dumonts are the foremost of the Hollow families. They sent Kael to open the door; when he refused, they raised his younger sister Ilse to do it instead. Their mother has never told either child she was proud.\n\n@lore wardens\nkeys: Wardens, ward-ring, headmistress\ncanon: soft\ntext: The headmistress keeps a quiet circle sworn to the founders' seal. When the seal grows thin she recruits students she trusts and gives them silver ward-rings.\n\n@lore edmund\nkeys: Edmund, silver wand, frozen spring\ncanon: soft\ntext: Sixty years ago, the spring the lake froze, Maud and two friends went down to the boathouse. Edmund was the one who didn't come back. His slender silver wand was meant to go beneath the lake again, with someone who means to close the door.\n\n@lore starcalling\nkeys: Starcalling, named star, starlight\ncanon: soft\ntext: An old discipline of calling down the light of a named star. Light that the Hollow Star cannot swallow is one of the few weapons the founders left.\n\n@lore lumen_and_hollow\nkeys: Lumen, Hollow, light and dark, alignment\ncanon: soft\ntext: Lumenhold talks about people the way it talks about candles: some give light (Lumen), some swallow it (the Hollow). It is not a house or a club, only what people notice. Those who lean toward the Hollow find the Star noticing them back; the way out of it is slow, and usually starts with someone handing you something to do.\n\n@lore star_herald\nkeys: herald, Herald of the Hollow Star, freed\ncanon: soft\ntext: If the round door is opened by one the Star already counts as its own, it goes free instead of swallowing them, and remembers who let it out. The Northern families call such a person its herald.\n"},{"path":"content/world/lore/soft/people.grim","text":"# Lore drafts (canon: soft): minor figures and families.\n\n@lore vaneshade\nkeys: Vaneshade, Lady Vaneshade, black seal, winter gala\ncanon: soft\ntext: House Vaneshade is old pyromancer nobility. Its letters carry a black seal; its winter gala is an inspection disguised as a party. Lady Vaneshade's highest praise is \"Adequate.\"\n\n@lore oracle_of_vell\nkeys: Oracle of Vell, Vell, family seat\ncanon: soft\ntext: The Oracle of Vell, Seraphine's mother, tells kings what they want to hear and has already seen her daughter say yes to taking the family seat.\n\n@lore fennick_farm\nkeys: Fennick, farm, harvest, blight\ncanon: soft\ntext: The Fennick farm grows turnips, tomatoes and stubborn children. Tobias's mother runs it alone at harvest; one year blight nearly ruined them.\n\n@lore ten_clockworks\nkeys: Ten family, clockworks\ncanon: soft\ntext: Mira's family runs a clockworks and expects them home. After a persuasive letter, Mira's mother agreed: \"Fine. Win a prize.\"\n\n@lore ashby_family\nkeys: Ashby, Corvin's brother, Corvin's grandfather\ncanon: soft\ntext: Corvin's grandfather owned a genuinely lucky deck of cards; his brother turns up at the gates wanting money. Home, Corvin says, is not a good place.\n\n@lore hollis_family\nkeys: Hollis, Bram's father, head boy\ncanon: soft\ntext: Bram's father was a prefect and head boy at Lumenhold, and has never stopped telling his son he will not amount to anything.\n\n@lore grisk\nkeys: Grisk, goblin, moneylender, debt\ncanon: soft\ntext: A goblin in a velvet waistcoat, friend to the temporarily embarrassed: sixty crowns today, twelve a month for six months. He keeps a ledger and occasionally asks for favours instead of coin.\n\n@lore prudence_ashworth\nkeys: Prudence Ashworth, ghost, socks\ncanon: soft\ntext: A polite ghost from two hundred years ago who rearranges socks to get attention. She was in love with the baker's daughter and never delivered her letter.\n\n@lore footnote\nkeys: Footnote, library cat\ncanon: soft\ntext: The library cat, who sleeps on the reference desk and decides which books are worth reading.\n\n@lore bartholomew\nkeys: Bartholomew, goat\ncanon: soft\ntext: Maud's goat, who has eaten parts of the Restricted Section and most of a sleeve. He likes some students more than others.\n\n@lore gerald\nkeys: Gerald, mandrake\ncanon: soft\ntext: Tobias's mandrake, who wears a knitted hat, screams softly and bites the people he loves.\n\n@lore ember_fox\nkeys: Ember, fox, ember tail\ncanon: soft\ntext: A small fox with a tail that smoulders like an ember. If you free her from a snare she may come back with a ruby, or stay.\n\n@lore pim\nkeys: Pim, first-year, mentee\ncanon: soft\ntext: A nervous first-year who attaches to final-year students in need of a mentor.\n\n@lore duchess_of_ambry\nkeys: Duchess of Ambry, portrait\ncanon: soft\ntext: An escaped portrait who wanders the castle's frames, gossiping and refusing to return to her own.\n\n@lore professors\nkeys: Professor Marisol, Professor Vey, Professor Brask\ncanon: soft\ntext: Professor Marisol teaches Tidecalling and keeps the lake calm on exam days. Professor Vey teaches Illusion and grades on whether you fooled her. Professor Brask teaches Beastcraft and is kinder to animals than to students.\n\n@lore clockwork_owl\nkeys: clockwork owl, brass owl\ncanon: soft\ntext: Mira's brass owl with amber eyes: it hoots the hour, remembers deadlines and birthdays, and refuses to work for Professor Thorne.\n\n@lore thornes_rival\nkeys: wizard in purple, rematch\ncanon: soft\ntext: A flamboyant old wizard in purple who has wanted to settle a forty-year-old matter with Thorne. The rematch lasts four seconds; afterwards both old men laugh until they wheeze.\n\n@lore isolde_youth\nkeys: young headmistress, portrait, observatory fire\ncanon: soft\ntext: Before she was headmistress, Isolde Marrow was the student with the detention record of the decade, who set the observatory on fire twice.\n"},{"path":"content/world/lore/soft/places.grim","text":"# Lore drafts (canon: soft): places in and beyond the academy.\n\n@lore the_village\nkeys: village, barge, market square\ncanon: soft\ntext: The village lies across the lake, reached by barge. Its shopkeepers have seen every kind of student: the Gilded Newt, Mabel's tea shop, Grimsby's Books, Pell & Daughters and the bakery.\n\n@lore gilded_newt\nkeys: Gilded Newt, tavern\ncanon: soft\ntext: The village tavern, with low beams and a fire that never goes out. Students work shifts there and card games run late.\n\n@lore mabels\nkeys: Mabel's, tea shop\ncanon: soft\ntext: Forty kinds of tea and three kinds of cake. Mabel reads your mood from the way you open the door.\n\n@lore grimsbys\nkeys: Grimsby's Books, bookshop\ncanon: soft\ntext: A bookshop so crowded the shelves lean on each other. Mr Grimsby will find anything except what you came in for.\n\n@lore pell_and_daughters\nkeys: Pell & Daughters, apothecary, Old Pell\ncanon: soft\ntext: The village apothecary, with a thousand labelled drawers. Old Pell taught three generations to count drops.\n\n@lore bakery\nkeys: bakery, baker's daughter\ncanon: soft\ntext: The same family has baked in the village for two hundred years. A great-great-great-grandmother waited all her life for a letter from a student who died.\n\n@lore whisperwood\nkeys: Whisperwood, forest, moonpetal, shadow stag\ncanon: soft\ntext: An old forest west of the lake that talks in its sleep. Moonpetal blooms in its clearings only at midnight; a stag made of shadow guards them and steps aside only for the brave.\n\n@lore secret_garden\nkeys: secret garden, silver bees, humming pear tree\ncanon: soft\ntext: Behind the potting shed, a door in the wall leads to Maud's secret garden: glowing ferns, silver bees and a pear tree that hums. It only exists for people Maud has shown it to.\n\n@lore greenhouses\nkeys: greenhouses, moonflower\ncanon: soft\ntext: Glass houses that steam even in winter. The moonflowers there bloom once every eleven years and fill the greenhouse with silver light.\n\n@lore observatory\nkeys: observatory, telescope, east tower\ncanon: soft\ntext: A brass dome atop the east tower. The great telescope points wherever it likes. On cold nights Kael sits on the roof outside it, and frost spreads around him.\n\n@lore boathouse\nkeys: boathouse, jetty\ncanon: soft\ntext: An old boathouse with a jetty over the water, where people say the things they can't say indoors. The tunnel to the round door starts here.\n\n@lore thornwick\nkeys: Thornwick Academy, rival school\ncanon: soft\ntext: The rival academy across the hills, famous for illusionists and tournament grudges. Exchange students come and go with suspicious politeness.\n\n@lore the_north\nkeys: the North, Northern, ice palaces, singing glaciers, northern lights\ncanon: soft\ntext: The frozen North of ice palaces, singing glaciers and green-and-violet lights in the sky. Northerners touch foreheads in farewell and practise a frost-craft older than anything taught at Lumenhold.\n\n@lore port_calder\nkeys: Port Calder, coast, street origin\ncanon: soft\ntext: A rough harbour town on the coast, where students of the street origin learned their trade and where trunks get stolen only once per visit.\n\n@lore conclave\nkeys: Conclave, Conclave of the Seven Towers\ncanon: soft\ntext: The governing body of magic, in the capital. It asks professors for recommendations and runs laboratories where new spells are tested behind thick walls.\n\n@lore capital_institutions\nkeys: Royal Library, Guild of Alchemists, Office of Unseen Affairs, Travelling Circus of Wonders\ncanon: soft\ntext: Beyond the academy lie the Royal Library's archives, the Guild of Alchemists' works, the Crown's Office of Unseen Affairs, which has no sign and no address, and the Travelling Circus of Wonders, which recruits graduates who want to wander.\n"},{"path":"content/world/places.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Places (design §6.1). Positions are on the campus map\n#  (960 × 680, see docs/map-concept.svg): the castle stands on the\n#  north shore of the Mirror Lake, the village to the south-east,\n#  the Whisperwood to the west. Data only until phase 2b.\n#\n#  open:      the slots a place can be visited in\n#  discover:  when it becomes visible (omit for places open from the start)\n#  hint:      teaser shown while hinted (hinted_when) but not yet discovered\n#  draws:     tags of older events (no place: yet) that may happen here (compat, phase 2b)\n# ============================================================\n\n# ---------------- Public from the start ----------------\n\n@place courtyard\nname: The Courtyard\nregion: castle\nmap: campus 585,278\nkind: public\nopen: morning noon afternoon evening night\nart: tower\ndraws: social festival rival story\ndesc: Flagstones worn smooth by three centuries of students, a fountain that freezes when it shouldn't, and the best gossip in the castle.\n\n@place library\nname: The Library\nregion: castle\nmap: campus 380,275\nkind: public\ntrack: library_assistant\nopen: morning noon afternoon evening night\nart: book\ndraws: study\ndesc: Seven floors of shelves that rearrange themselves when nobody is looking. Footnote the cat keeps order.\n\n@place refectory\nname: The Refectory\nregion: castle\nmap: campus 470,278\nkind: public\nopen: morning noon afternoon evening\nart: meal\ndraws: social home money\ndesc: Long tables, longer benches, and soup that tastes faintly of whatever you're homesick for.\n\n@place lakeshore\nname: The Lakeshore\nregion: grounds\nmap: campus 480,505\nkind: public\nopen: morning noon afternoon evening night midnight\nart: wave\ndraws: nature mystery romance\ndesc: The Mirror Lake lies so still it shows the towers upside down. On some nights it shows other things.\n\n@place village\nname: The Village\nregion: village\nmap: campus 720,520\nkind: public\nopen: afternoon evening\nart: house\ndraws: money home\ndesc: A barge ride across the lake: crooked roofs, a market square and shopkeepers who have seen every kind of student.\n\n# ---------------- Castle ----------------\n\n@place lecture_tower\nname: The Lecture Tower\nregion: castle\nmap: campus 420,180\nkind: public\nopen: morning noon afternoon evening\nart: scroll\ndraws: class study exam\ndesc: Spiral lecture halls stacked one above another. Evocation on the lower floors, where scorch marks are expected; Umbral Theory at the top, where the lamps burn dim.\n\n@place observatory\nname: The Observatory\nregion: castle\nmap: campus 600,175\nkind: public\nopen: afternoon evening night midnight\nart: planet\ndraws: magic mystery\ndesc: A brass dome at the top of the east tower. The great telescope points wherever it likes; usually that is the right place.\n\n@place great_hall\nname: The Great Hall\nregion: castle\nmap: campus 455,215\nkind: public\nopen: noon afternoon evening\nart: crown\ndraws: festival social\ndesc: Floating candles, a vaulted ceiling that shows tomorrow's weather, and the stage where the choir rehearses and the Midwinter Ball is held.\n\n@place infirmary\nname: The Infirmary\nregion: castle\nmap: campus 530,200\nkind: public\nopen: morning noon afternoon evening night midnight\nart: potion\ndraws: crisis danger\ndesc: Clean white beds, a cupboard of tonics and Sister Amaranth, who has seen every kind of injury and believes none of the excuses.\n\n@place alchemy_lab\nname: The Alchemy Laboratory\nregion: castle\nmap: campus 555,240\nkind: public\nopen: morning afternoon evening\nart: cauldron\ndraws: class magic\ndesc: Long benches, cauldrons in every size, and a ventilation charm that has given up on the third floor.\n\n@place workshop\nname: Mira's Workshop\nregion: castle\nmap: campus 615,235\nkind: public\nopen: afternoon evening night\ndiscover: met(mira)\nhint: Something clanks and hums behind the west-wing tapestry.\nhinted_when: turn >= 2\nart: gear\ndraws: magic\ndesc: A former classroom full of springs, gears, half-finished machines and the smell of solder. Fire buckets by the door, on the faculty's orders.\n\n@place job_board\nname: The Job Board\nregion: castle\nmap: campus 520,262\nkind: public\nopen: morning noon afternoon evening\nart: paper\ndraws: job money\ndesc: A cork board in the entrance hall layered with notices: jobs, lost familiars, club recruitment and the occasional ransom note for a sock.\n\n@place ledger_office\nname: The Lumen Ledger Office\nregion: castle\nmap: campus 395,230\nkind: club\ntrack: lumen_ledger\nopen: afternoon evening night\ndiscover: member(lumen_ledger) or met(nessa)\nart: paper\ndraws: club\ndesc: A cramped room under the library stairs, walls papered with old front pages. The printing press groans like a dragon on deadline night.\n\n@place headmistress_tower\nname: The Headmistress's Tower\nregion: castle\nmap: campus 500,130\nkind: public\nopen: afternoon evening\ndiscover: rel(isolde) >= 12 or flag(warden_trial)\nhint: A spiral stair guarded by a stone owl that only opens its eyes for some students.\nhinted_when: met(isolde)\nart: crown\ndraws: story\ndesc: Books, tea and a board of crystal towers by the window. You are only here because she wants you here.\n\n@place restricted_section\nname: The Restricted Section\nregion: castle\nmap: campus 352,245\nkind: public\nopen: afternoon evening\ndiscover: seen(forbidden_section_1) > 0\nhint: A door at the back of the library that is always locked, and always slightly warm.\nhinted_when: turn >= 2\nart: lock\ndraws: mystery danger\ndesc: Black-bound books that breathe in the dark. Some of them read you back.\n\n@place gargoyle_passage\nname: The Third Gargoyle\nregion: castle\nmap: campus 418,135\nkind: club\ntrack: cartographers\nopen: afternoon evening\ndiscover: member(cartographers)\nhint: Corvin keeps glancing at a particular gargoyle.\nhinted_when: met(corvin)\nart: map\ndraws: club mystery\ndesc: Behind the third gargoyle on the north parapet, a passage that isn't on any map. Except theirs.\n\n@place seventh_tower\nname: The Seventh Tower\nregion: castle\nmap: campus 500,72\nkind: public\nopen: afternoon evening\ndiscover: clues(hollowstar) >= 3\nhint: A locked stair that leads up into a tower nobody admits is there.\nhinted_when: clock(hollowstar) >= 1\nart: key\ndraws: mystery story\ndesc: Locked for a century. At the very top, one room of dust and moonlight, and a star-shaped hollow in the wall.\n\n@place thesis_lab\nname: The Thesis Laboratory\nregion: castle\nmap: campus 565,160\nkind: thesis\ntrack: thesis\nopen: morning afternoon evening\ndiscover: year >= 4\nart: book\ndraws: study\ndesc: Rows of carrels where final-year students and their advisors argue about footnotes until the candles give out.\n\n# ---------------- Grounds ----------------\n\n@place duel_hall\nname: The Duelling Hall\nregion: grounds\nmap: campus 660,150\nkind: club\ntrack: duelling\nopen: afternoon evening\ndiscover: member(duelling) or rel(lyra) >= 9\nhint: Steel rings somewhere above the east stair.\nhinted_when: met(lyra)\nart: duel\ndraws: rival\ndesc: Scorched floorboards and a ceiling that has seen things. The practice dummies have names, and grudges.\n\n@place greenhouses\nname: The Greenhouses\nregion: grounds\nmap: campus 740,235\nkind: club\ntrack: verdant\nopen: morning afternoon evening\ndiscover: member(verdant) or met(tobias) or met(maud)\nart: leaf\ndraws: nature\ndesc: Glass houses that steam even in winter. The moonflowers bloom once every eleven years; the mandrakes have opinions every day.\n\n@place flight_field\nname: The Flight Field\nregion: grounds\nmap: campus 850,300\nkind: public\nopen: morning afternoon evening\nart: broom\ndraws: club\ndesc: A long green field with hoops at either end and a wind that cheats. Broom lessons, races and spectacular landings.\n\n@place stables\nname: The Familiar Stables\nregion: grounds\nmap: campus 845,385\nkind: public\nopen: morning afternoon evening\nart: paw\ndraws: nature\ndesc: Hippogriffs, a sulky griffon chick, owls in the rafters and Professor Brask, who talks to all of them more kindly than to students.\n\n@place boathouse\nname: The Boathouse\nregion: grounds\nmap: campus 610,440\nkind: public\nopen: afternoon evening night midnight\nart: wave\ndraws: social mystery romance\ndesc: An old wooden boathouse and a jetty out over the water. People come here to say the things they can't say indoors.\n\n@place orchard\nname: The Orchard\nregion: grounds\nmap: campus 790,165\nkind: public\nopen: morning noon afternoon evening\nart: tree\ndraws: nature romance\ndesc: Apple and pear trees older than the towers. In spring the blossom falls like snow; once, it was snow.\n\n@place maud_cottage\nname: Old Maud's Cottage\nregion: grounds\nmap: campus 700,330\nkind: public\nopen: morning noon afternoon evening\ndiscover: met(maud)\nart: house\ndraws: nature home\ndesc: A thatched cottage with chickens on the doorstep, bees in the eaves, a goat called Bartholomew and a kettle that is always nearly boiling.\n\n@place secret_garden\nname: The Secret Garden\nregion: grounds\nmap: campus 735,355\nkind: public\nopen: morning afternoon evening\ndiscover: seen(garden_maud_secret) > 0\nhint: A door in the wall behind the potting shed that you keep walking past.\nhinted_when: met(maud)\nart: flower\ndraws: nature magic\ndesc: Glowing ferns, silver bees and a pear tree that hums. It only exists for people Maud has shown it to.\n\n@place courier_loft\nname: The Courier Loft\nregion: grounds\nmap: campus 880,230\nkind: job\ntrack: broom_courier\nopen: morning afternoon evening\ndiscover: turn >= 3\nart: letter\ndraws: job\ndesc: A draughty loft full of parcels, pigeonholes and brooms in various states of repair. The dispatch bell rings at all hours.\n\n# ---------------- Village ----------------\n\n@place gilded_newt\nname: The Gilded Newt\nregion: village\nmap: campus 800,480\nkind: job\ntrack: tavern\nopen: afternoon evening night\nart: cup\ndraws: job money social\ndesc: The village tavern: low beams, a fire that never goes out, and a landlady who remembers every student's tab to the penny.\n\n@place mabels\nname: Mabel's Tea Shop\nregion: village\nmap: campus 725,565\nkind: public\nopen: morning noon afternoon evening\nart: cup\ndraws: social home\ndesc: Forty kinds of tea, three kinds of cake and Mabel, who reads your mood from the way you open the door.\n\n@place grimsbys_books\nname: Grimsby's Books\nregion: village\nmap: campus 680,600\nkind: public\nopen: noon afternoon evening\nart: book\ndraws: study money\ndesc: A bookshop so crowded the shelves lean on each other for support. Mr Grimsby will find anything except what you came in for.\n\n@place pell_apothecary\nname: Pell & Daughters\nregion: village\nmap: campus 770,600\nkind: job\ntrack: apothecary_clerk\nopen: morning afternoon evening\nart: bottle\ndraws: job money\ndesc: An apothecary of a thousand labelled drawers. Old Pell taught three generations to count drops; his daughters taught him to smile at customers.\n\n@place bakery\nname: The Bakery\nregion: village\nmap: campus 640,555\nkind: public\nopen: morning noon afternoon\nart: meal\ndraws: home money\ndesc: The same family has baked here for two hundred years. The buns are famous; so, in certain circles, is a letter that arrived very late.\n\n# ---------------- Wilds ----------------\n\n@place whisperwood\nname: The Whisperwood\nregion: wilds\nmap: campus 185,340\nkind: public\nopen: morning afternoon evening night midnight\ndiscover: met(maud) or flag(fox_met)\nhint: Fog sits on the western woods even at noon.\nhinted_when: turn >= 2\nart: tree\ndraws: nature danger mystery\ndesc: An old forest that talks in its sleep. Moonpetal grows in its clearings; so do things that are better left there.\n\n# ---------------- Internship hosts (one map each; design §7.5) ----------------\n\n@place royal_library_archives\nname: The Royal Library Archives\nregion: internship:royal_library\nmap: royal_library 480,300\nkind: internship\ntrack: intern_royal_library\nopen: morning afternoon\nart: book\ndesc: Miles of stacks beneath the capital, where the oldest records of the realm are kept in temperature-controlled silence.\n\n@place conclave_laboratory\nname: The Conclave Laboratory\nregion: internship:conclave\nmap: conclave 480,300\nkind: internship\ntrack: intern_conclave\nopen: morning afternoon evening\nart: wand\ndesc: The research laboratory of the Conclave of the Seven Towers, where new spells are tested behind very thick walls.\n\n@place alchemists_works\nname: The Guild Works\nregion: internship:alchemists\nmap: alchemists 480,300\nkind: internship\ntrack: intern_alchemists\nopen: morning afternoon\nart: cauldron\ndesc: The Guild of Alchemists' great works by the river: vats, chimneys and a strict rule about naked flames.\n\n@place observatory_camp\nname: The Expedition Camp\nregion: internship:expedition\nmap: expedition 480,300\nkind: internship\ntrack: intern_expedition\nopen: afternoon evening night\nart: planet\ndesc: Tents on a high plateau where the air is thin and the stars are close enough to argue with.\n\n@place field_station\nname: The Beastcraft Field Station\nregion: internship:field_station\nmap: field_station 480,300\nkind: internship\ntrack: intern_field_station\nopen: morning afternoon evening\nart: paw\ndesc: A cabin on the moors, ringed with paddocks and nesting towers, where injured creatures are mended and wild ones are counted.\n\n@place thornwick_lab\nname: The Thornwick Exchange Lab\nregion: internship:thornwick\nmap: thornwick 480,300\nkind: internship\ntrack: intern_thornwick\nopen: morning afternoon evening\nart: mask\ndesc: A guest laboratory at Thornwick Academy, the rival school, where the illusionists are excellent and the hospitality is suspicious.\n\n@place unseen_office\nname: The Office of Unseen Affairs\nregion: internship:unseen_office\nmap: unseen_office 480,300\nkind: internship\ntrack: intern_unseen_office\nopen: evening night\nart: eye\ndesc: The Crown's Office of Unseen Affairs. It has no sign, no address, and a very good coffee machine.\n\n# ---------------- Home (the \"go home\" break plan; map: home) ----------------\n\n@place home_cottage\nname: The Family Cottage\nregion: home\nmap: home 485,320\nkind: plan\nopen: morning noon afternoon evening night\nart: house\ndraws: home\ndesc: The kitchen table, the creaky stair, the smell of whatever is in the oven. Everyone asks how school is.\n\n@place home_fields\nname: The Fields\nregion: home\nmap: home 310,320\nkind: plan\nopen: morning afternoon evening\nart: leaf\ndraws: nature home\ndesc: Long rows, a low wall to sit on, and the same view you used to stare at when you dreamed of towers.\n\n@place home_market\nname: The Market Town\nregion: home\nmap: home 770,240\nkind: plan\nopen: noon afternoon evening\nart: coin\ndraws: money social\ndesc: Market stalls, the old crowd outside the inn, and the one shop that still sells your favourite sweets.\n"},{"path":"content/world/spells.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Spells (design §9.1): 2 per discipline, 3 levels each.\n#  cost: mana per level · power: strength per level (tunable).\n#  Data only until phase 2a adds the swipe-up spell option.\n# ============================================================\n\n@spell flame\nname: Flame\ndiscipline: evocation\ncost: 10, 9, 7\npower: 1, 2, 3\nart: flame\ndesc: Fire from the fingertips: a candle's worth at first, a bonfire's later. Burns through wards that only expect cleverness.\n\n@spell tide\nname: Tide\ndiscipline: evocation\ncost: 14, 10, 9\npower: 1, 2, 3\nart: wave\ndesc: Water called up and moved: a wave, a wall, a lake politely asked to step aside.\n\n@spell foresight\nname: Foresight\ndiscipline: astromancy\ncost: 10, 9, 7\npower: 1, 2, 3\nart: eye\ndesc: A glimpse of the next few moments. Enough to know which way the dice will fall, sometimes.\n\n@spell starlight\nname: Starlight\ndiscipline: astromancy\ncost: 18, 14, 10\npower: 1, 2, 3\nart: star\ndesc: The light of a named star, held in the hand. Darkness that eats light finds it hard to swallow.\n\n@spell mend\nname: Mend\ndiscipline: alchemy\ncost: 10, 9, 7\npower: 1, 2, 3\nart: heart\ndesc: Knits cuts, cracks and broken teacups. Hearts take longer.\n\n@spell purify\nname: Purify\ndiscipline: alchemy\ncost: 14, 10, 9\npower: 1, 2, 3\nart: potion\ndesc: Draws out poison, curse-cold and bad ingredients. The cure has to come from somewhere; it leaves you tired.\n\n@spell glamour\nname: Glamour\ndiscipline: illusion\ncost: 9, 7, 5\npower: 1, 2, 3\nart: mask\ndesc: A little more charm than you really have. People remember liking you and not quite why.\n\n@spell mirror\nname: Mirror\ndiscipline: illusion\ncost: 14, 10, 9\npower: 1, 2, 3\nart: mirror\ndesc: A double of yourself, or of anything you can see. It doesn't talk, which is its only flaw.\n\n@spell haste\nname: Haste\ndiscipline: beastcraft\ncost: 10, 9, 7\npower: 1, 2, 3\nart: bolt\ndesc: Speed borrowed from hares and hawks. You act first; you pay for it with a long nap.\n\n@spell beast_speech\nname: Beast-speech\ndiscipline: beastcraft\ncost: 10, 9, 7\npower: 1, 2, 3\nart: paw\ndesc: Talk with animals, briefly. Most of them have been waiting to complain.\n\n@spell ward\nname: Ward\ndiscipline: umbral\ncost: 14, 10, 9\npower: 1, 2, 3\nart: shield\ndesc: A pale shell that turns aside the next harm.\n\n@spell shadowstep\nname: Shadowstep\ndiscipline: umbral\ncost: 18, 14, 10\npower: 1, 2, 3\nart: ghost\ndesc: Step into one shadow and out of another. Short distances only; the shadows in between are not friendly.\n"},{"path":"content/world/timeline.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Timeline (design §3.2, §4.4). `history` entries are dated canon\n#  (years before the player's first year, as negative numbers; the\n#  first year is 0). `beat` entries are the in-run calendar of fixed\n#  beats (yNmM; m1 = Harvestmoon); in phase 2b they drive the dated\n#  `auto` events. Context for authors; not shown in the game.\n# ============================================================\n\n# ---------------- History ----------------\n\n@timeline founding\ndate: -300\nkind: history\ntext: The founders raise seven towers above the Mirror Lake as a seal over the Hollow Star, with one lock in each tower and a star-shaped key. The entry for the founding year in the Founders' Ledger is later cut out with a razor.\n\n@timeline prudence_ashworth\ndate: -200\nkind: history\ntext: Prudence Ashworth, a student, dies before she can deliver a letter to the baker's daughter in the village. Her ghost stays in the dormitories, rearranging socks.\n\n@timeline seventh_tower_locked\ndate: -100\nkind: history\ntext: The seventh tower is locked. Nobody living remembers why, and the faculty prefer it that way.\n\n@timeline the_frozen_spring\ndate: -60\nkind: history\ntext: The lake freezes one spring night. Three students go down to the boathouse; only two come back. Maud is one of them; Edmund is the one who doesn't return. His silver wand is never buried.\n\n@timeline thorne_and_the_storm\ndate: -40\nkind: history\ntext: Aldric Thorne duels a storm, loses badly, and spends a year learning why. The rumour says he won. A rival from those years still wants a rematch.\n\n@timeline isolde_appointed\ndate: -20\nkind: history\ntext: Isolde Marrow, once the student who set the observatory on fire twice, becomes headmistress. Her student portrait is hidden in a forgotten corridor.\n\n@timeline ledger_founded\ndate: -20\nkind: history\ntext: The Lumen Ledger prints its first issue. Nobody has interviewed the headmistress since she took office.\n\n@timeline kael_arrives\ndate: y1m3\nkind: beat\nevent: kael_arrives\ntext: Kael Dumont arrives mid-term from the North, with frost on the dining-hall floor behind him.\n\n# ---------------- The year's fixed beats (today's calendar; re-paced in phase 3) ----------------\n\n@timeline harvest_feast\ndate: y1m1\nkind: beat\nevent: choose_elective\ntext: The year begins: electives are chosen and the Harvest Feast is held.\n\n@timeline club_fair\ndate: y1m2\nkind: beat\nevent: club_fair\ntext: The Club Fair, and Lantern Night over the lake.\n\n@timeline lantern_night\ndate: y1m2\nkind: beat\nevent: lantern_night\ntext: Lantern Night: lanterns released over the Mirror Lake, each carrying a wish.\n\n@timeline job_board\ndate: y1m3\nkind: beat\nevent: job_board\ntext: The Job Board fills with term-time work.\n\n@timeline winter_trials\ndate: y1m4\nkind: beat\nevent: winter_trials\ntext: The Winter Trials, the first exams of the year.\n\n@timeline midwinter_ball\ndate: y1m5\nkind: beat\nevent: midwinter_ball\ntext: The Midwinter Ball in the Great Hall.\n\n@timeline spring_tournament\ndate: y1m8\nkind: beat\nevent: spring_tournament\ntext: The Spring Tournament on the duelling green.\n\n@timeline grand_examinations\ndate: y1m10\nkind: beat\nevent: grand_exams\ntext: The Grand Examinations close the academic year.\n\n@timeline summer_recess\ndate: y1m11\nkind: beat\nevent: summer_plans\ntext: Summer recess: the towers empty.\n\n@timeline starfall\ndate: y1m12\nkind: beat\nevent: starfall\ntext: Starfall, when the meteor showers come and the whole academy sleeps on the lawns.\n\n@timeline the_night\ndate: y3m9\nkind: beat\nevent: hs_the_night\ntext: The night the lake freezes. Moves to year 4 in the v0.3 calendar.\n"},{"path":"content/world/tracks.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Tracks (design §7): courses, clubs, jobs, internships and the\n#  thesis as one progression system. The reduced list of §7.1\n#  (decision D4). Data only until phase 2b; today's electives,\n#  clubs and jobs are still the `class_*`, `club_*` and `job_*`\n#  states in content/states/states.grim.\n#  legacy: the old states a track stands for (phase 2b compat: joining\n#  grants them, so their events keep firing) · opens: the first term a\n#  course is offered (default 1).\n# ============================================================\n\n# ---------------- Courses: 6 disciplines × 3 levels ----------------\n\n@track evocation\nkind: course\nname: Evocation\nskill: arcana\nlevels: 3\nlevel_names: Evocation I, Evocation II, Advanced Evocation\nplace: lecture_tower\nteaches: flame@1 flame@2 tide@2 flame@3 tide@3\nlead: thorne\ndesc: Fire and water, called up and held. Pyromancy and Tidecalling, taught together because they keep putting each other out.\n\n@track astromancy\nkind: course\nname: Astromancy & Divination\nskill: lore\nlevels: 3\nlevel_names: Astromancy I, Astromancy II, Advanced Astromancy\nplace: observatory\nteaches: foresight@1 foresight@2 starlight@2 foresight@3 starlight@3\ndesc: Reading the sky and the time that hasn't happened yet. Tea leaves in the first year; star charts that move in the third.\n\n@track alchemy\nkind: course\nname: Alchemy & Healing\nskill: alchemy\nlevels: 3\nlevel_names: Alchemy I, Alchemy II, Advanced Alchemy\nplace: alchemy_lab\nteaches: mend@1 mend@2 purify@2 mend@3 purify@3\nlead: amaranth\ndesc: Potions, remedies and the patience to stir clockwise. Half of it happens in the laboratory and half in the infirmary.\n\n@track illusion\nkind: course\nname: Illusion & Glamour\nskill: charm\nlevels: 3\nlevel_names: Illusion I, Illusion II, Advanced Illusion\nplace: lecture_tower\nteaches: glamour@1 glamour@2 mirror@2 glamour@3 mirror@3\nlead: vey\ndesc: Making people see what you choose. Professor Vey grades on whether you fooled her, and she is very hard to fool.\n\n@track beastcraft\nkind: course\nname: Beastcraft & Flight\nskill: vigor\nlevels: 3\nlevel_names: Beastcraft I, Beastcraft II, Advanced Beastcraft\nplace: stables\nteaches: haste@1 haste@2 beast_speech@2 haste@3 beast_speech@3\nopens: 2\nlead: brask\ndesc: Creatures, familiars and brooms. Professor Brask believes the first two are easier to reason with than the third.\n\n@track umbral\nkind: course\nname: Umbral Theory\nskill: guile\nlevels: 3\nlevel_names: Umbral Theory I, Umbral Theory II, Advanced Umbral Theory\nplace: lecture_tower\nteaches: ward@1 ward@2 shadowstep@2 ward@3 shadowstep@3\nopens: 2\ndesc: Shadow, wards and the magic of what is hidden. Taught at the top of the tower, where the lamps burn dim on purpose.\n\n# ---------------- Hidden courses ----------------\n\n@track artifice\nkind: course\nname: Artifice\nskill: alchemy arcana\nlevels: 1\nlevel_names: Artifice\nplace: workshop\nhidden: true\nprereq: passed(alchemy, 2) and passed(evocation, 2)\ndesc: Enchanted machinery: clockwork that thinks, cauldrons that stir themselves. Offered to students who already know how to make things and how to make them burn.\n\n@track starcalling\nkind: course\nname: Starcalling\nskill: lore arcana\nlevels: 1\nlevel_names: Starcalling\nplace: observatory\nhidden: true\nprereq: passed(astromancy, 3) and clue(heard_of_starcalling)\ndesc: Calling down the light of a named star. Not on any syllabus; taught by appointment, at night.\n\n@track hollow_studies\nkind: course\nname: Hollow Studies\nskill: guile lore\nlevels: 1\nlevel_names: Hollow Studies\nplace: restricted_section\nhidden: true\nprereq: passed(umbral, 2) and passed(astromancy, 2) and clue(knows_founders_seal)\ndesc: What the founders sealed, and how. The headmistress teaches it herself, to very few students, in a room without windows.\n\n# ---------------- Clubs (one at a time) ----------------\n\n@track duelling\nkind: club\nname: Duelling Circle\nskill: arcana vigor\nplace: duel_hall\nranks: Member, Officer, Captain\ndue: 2\nlead: lyra\ndesc: Practice bouts, the Spring Tournament and a great deal of shouting about footwork.\n\n@track lumen_ledger\nkind: club\nname: The Lumen Ledger\nskill: charm lore\nplace: ledger_office\nranks: Stringer, Reporter, Editor\ndue: 2\nlead: nessa\ndesc: The student newspaper. Scoops, deadlines, gossip columns and the occasional duty to the truth.\n\n@track verdant\nkind: club\nname: Verdant Circle\nskill: alchemy\nplace: greenhouses\nranks: Member, Gardener, Head Gardener\ndue: 2\nlead: tobias\ndesc: The greenhouse club: moonflowers, mandrakes and long talks with plants that occasionally answer.\n\n@track cartographers\nkind: club\nname: Midnight Cartographers\nskill: guile\nplace: gargoyle_passage\nhidden: true\nranks: Initiate, Mapper, Keeper of the Maps\ndue: 2\nlead: corvin\ndesc: The club that doesn't exist, mapping the passages the castle would prefer to keep. Never offered at the Club Fair; you are invited, or you aren't.\n\n# ---------------- Jobs (one at a time) ----------------\n\n@track library_assistant\nkind: job\nname: Library Assistant\nskill: lore\nplace: library\nranks: Apprentice, Clerk, Senior Clerk\npay: 12, 18, 24\ndue: 3\ndesc: Shelving books that don't want to be shelved, and keeping Footnote off the reference desk.\n\n@track tavern\nkind: job\nname: Server at the Gilded Newt\nskill: charm\nplace: gilded_newt\nranks: Server, Barkeep, Manager\npay: 14, 20, 27\ndue: 3\ndesc: Evening shifts behind the bar. Tips depend on charm; survival depends on remembering who ordered the spiced cider.\n\n@track apothecary_clerk\nkind: job\nname: Apothecary Clerk\nskill: alchemy\nplace: pell_apothecary\nranks: Clerk, Dispenser, Assistant Apothecary\npay: 12, 18, 26\ndue: 3\nlead: pell\ndesc: Counting drops and reading labels at Pell & Daughters. The wrong drawer is never just the wrong drawer.\n\n@track broom_courier\nkind: job\nname: Broom Courier\nskill: vigor\nplace: courier_loft\nranks: Runner, Courier, Chief Courier\npay: 15, 21, 27\ndue: 3\ndesc: Parcels, letters and the occasional live delivery, flown across the lake in all weathers.\n\n# ---------------- Research internships (by application; design §7.5) ----------------\n\n@track intern_royal_library\nkind: internship\nname: Royal Library Archives\nskill: lore\nplace: royal_library_archives\ndesc: Cataloguing and research in the oldest archives of the realm.\n\n@track intern_conclave\nkind: internship\nname: Conclave Laboratory\nskill: arcana\nplace: conclave_laboratory\ndesc: Spell research with the Conclave of the Seven Towers. Prestigious; a faculty recommendation is expected.\n\n@track intern_alchemists\nkind: internship\nname: Guild of Alchemists' Works\nskill: alchemy\nplace: alchemists_works\ndesc: Industrial alchemy at the Guild's great works.\n\n@track intern_expedition\nkind: internship\nname: Observatory Expedition\nskill: lore\nplace: observatory_camp\ndesc: A summer of sky charts on a high plateau.\n\n@track intern_field_station\nkind: internship\nname: Beastcraft Field Station\nskill: vigor\nplace: field_station\ndesc: Tracking, tending and counting creatures on the moors.\n\n@track intern_thornwick\nkind: internship\nname: Thornwick Exchange Lab\nskill: charm\nplace: thornwick_lab\ndesc: An exchange placement at the rival academy's illusion laboratory.\n\n@track intern_unseen_office\nkind: internship\nname: Office of Unseen Affairs\nskill: guile\nplace: unseen_office\nhidden: true\ndesc: By invitation only. Nobody applies to the Office of Unseen Affairs.\n\n# ---------------- Thesis (year 4; design §7.6) ----------------\n\n@track thesis\nkind: thesis\nname: Thesis\nplace: thesis_lab\ndue: 3\ndesc: A year-long research project with a faculty advisor, ending in a defence before a committee of three.\n"},{"path":"content/world/vocabulary.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Controlled vocabularies (design §3.1). Character traits and\n#  interests, roles, tones and memory kinds must come from these\n#  lists; lint rejects anything else. Add a value here first.\n# ============================================================\n\n@vocab traits\ndesc: Personality traits for characters and archetypes.\nvalues: proud ambitious lonely kind gentle homesick dreamy distant perceptive charming reckless loyal secretive chaotic curious stubborn guarded cold protective bold nosy idealistic overworked insecure bullying severe exacting dry warm eccentric patient calm watchful formidable fierce wary funny anxious honest sly brave shy\n\n@vocab interests\ndesc: Things characters care about; used by casting and briefs.\nvalues: duelling fire tides plants animals cooking prophecy painting tarot maps locks cards machines clocks flying stars snow writing gossip justice rules chess roses bees soup history teaching books music dancing money theatre\n\n@vocab roles\ndesc: A character's place in the academy.\nvalues: student prefect professor staff headmistress outsider villager\n\n@vocab seeks\ndesc: How readily a character comes looking for you (design §5.5): often (the default; friends and rivals), sometimes, rarely (only once you've sought them out), never.\nvalues: often sometimes rarely never\n\n@vocab tones\ndesc: The mood of an event (design §5.2 variety, §12 `tone:`).\nvalues: light warm tense grim eerie\n\n@vocab memory_kinds\ndesc: What an option can leave behind in a character's memory (design §10.2).\nvalues: helped hurt laughed fought confided kissed betrayed rescued protected abandoned\n\n@vocab regions\ndesc: Where a place is (design §6.1). Internship hosts use internship:<host>.\nvalues: castle grounds village wilds home travel internship\n\n@vocab slots\ndesc: The parts of a day (design §4.2).\nvalues: morning noon afternoon evening night midnight\n\n@vocab houses\ndesc: Student houses.\nvalues: Emberhall Rootward Stillwater Duskgate Brasswick Faculty\n\n@vocab canon_allowlist\ndesc: Proper nouns that canon lint accepts without a worldbook entry: book and essay titles, potion names, headlines and other small props. Anything that matters to the world belongs in @lore instead.\nvalues: Advanced Arcane Theory, Arcane, Curses of the Northern Wastes, Draught of Mild Courage, Draught of Unending Hiccups, Sleeping Draught, Magic and Duty, Nature of Absence, Potions, Pie-rates, Strike Again, Third Law of Correspondence, Brilliant Lectures, Brilliant Lecturers, Foundations of Flame\n"}];
window.LUMENHOLD_LOCALES = {"zh-CN":[{"path":"locales/zh-CN/core/rules.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/core/rules.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@game\n# en: Lumenhold Academy\ntitle [fceae1] = 流明堡学院\n# en: Four years. Forty-eight moons. One apprentice.\nsubtitle [e1da8c] = 四年。四十八轮月。一名学徒。\n# en: The sky-barge lurches. Below, the lake glitters; above, the seven towers of Lumenhold Academy pierce the clouds. You have a trunk, a borrowed wand and a letter of acceptance with your name spelled wrong. Four years begin now.\nintro [2dd4dc] = 天空驳船猛地一晃。脚下，湖面波光粼粼；头顶，流明堡学院的七座高塔刺破云层。你有一只行李箱、一根借来的魔杖，还有一封把你名字拼错的录取信。四年，就从此刻开始。\n# en: Harvestmoon\nmonth.1 [39637f] = 丰收月\n# en: Emberwane\nmonth.2 [293c65] = 烬落月\n# en: Mistveil\nmonth.3 [82c3ac] = 雾纱月\n# en: Frostcall\nmonth.4 [dbae91] = 霜唤月\n# en: Deepwinter\nmonth.5 [668c02] = 深冬月\n# en: Thawmoon\nmonth.6 [0fe880] = 融雪月\n# en: Stormbloom\nmonth.7 [58f0f1] = 风暴月\n# en: Greenrise\nmonth.8 [d3ee01] = 青苏月\n# en: Blossomtide\nmonth.9 [dab8ca] = 花潮月\n# en: Highsun\nmonth.10 [c6ab59] = 盛阳月\n# en: Goldenhaze\nmonth.11 [886cae] = 金霭月\n# en: Starfall\nmonth.12 [ab87b4] = 星坠月\n\n@resource health\n# en: Health\nname [6145af] = 健康\n# en: Your body. At zero you collapse.\ndesc [a87509] = 你的身体。归零时，你会倒下。\n\n@resource mood\n# en: Mood\nname [64551e] = 心情\n# en: Your spirits. At zero, despair takes hold.\ndesc [ca3154] = 你的精神。归零时，绝望会将你吞没。\n\n@resource mana\n# en: Mana\nname [78ad2a] = 法力\n# en: The magic you can spend on spells. Refills each month, faster the stronger your Arcana.\ndesc [738cd1] = 你能用来施咒的魔法。每月恢复，奥术越强恢复越快。\n\n@resource renown\n# en: Renown\nname [996fb2] = 声望\n# en: How much the academy talks about you — for better or worse. Fades a little every month.\ndesc [e64a8d] = 学院里有多少人在谈论你——无论好坏。每个月都会淡去一点。\n\n@resource alignment\n# en: Alignment\nname [6abafe] = 立场\n# en: Lumen or Hollow: the light you serve, or the dark that has noticed you. Deep in the Hollow, the way back is slow.\ndesc [6e8e70] = 光明或空洞：你所侍奉的光，或已经注意到你的黑暗。在空洞中陷得越深，回来的路就越慢。\n\n@resource gold\n# en: Crowns\nname [a78479] = 金币\n# en: Crowns in your purse. Rent is due every month.\ndesc [cf79f6] = 钱袋里的克朗。每个月都要交房租。\n\n@skill arcana\n# en: Arcana\nname [6f1c33] = 奥术\n# en: Raw spellcraft — power, control, nerve.\ndesc [0428ad] = 纯粹的施法功力——力量、控制与胆量。\n\n@skill lore\n# en: Lore\nname [d907ad] = 学识\n# en: Scholarship, theory, history, languages of the old world.\ndesc [5aadaa] = 学问、理论、历史，以及古老世界的语言。\n\n@skill alchemy\n# en: Alchemy\nname [5d01fc] = 炼金\n# en: Potions, herbs, reagents and the patience to stir clockwise.\ndesc [dbde44] = 药剂、草药、试剂，以及顺时针搅拌的耐心。\n\n@skill charm\n# en: Charm\nname [df43f0] = 魅力\n# en: Wit, warmth, persuasion. Making people want to say yes.\ndesc [e1c3b8] = 机智、温暖与说服力。让别人心甘情愿地点头。\n\n@skill vigor\n# en: Vigor\nname [6c0ee4] = 体魄\n# en: Stamina, reflexes, dueling and broom-flight.\ndesc [9f1acb] = 耐力、反应、决斗和飞帚。\n\n@skill guile\n# en: Guile\nname [987273] = 诡计\n# en: Sneaking, lying, noticing what others miss.\ndesc [fe3ade] = 潜行、撒谎，以及注意到别人忽略的东西。\n\n@hook rent\n# en: Dormitory & board\nlabel [547aba] = 宿舍与膳食\n\n@hook break_rent\n# en: Board at the castle\nlabel [0b2949] = 城堡食宿\n\n@hook tuition\n# en: Tuition\nlabel [7ee27b] = 学费\n\n@hook gpa_probation\n# en: Academic probation\nlabel [5ecefe] = 学业察看\n\n@hook coursework_evocation\n# en: Coursework\nlabel [68f6e5] = 课业\n\n@hook coursework_astromancy\n# en: Coursework\nlabel [68f6e5] = 课业\n\n@hook coursework_alchemy\n# en: Coursework\nlabel [68f6e5] = 课业\n\n@hook coursework_illusion\n# en: Coursework\nlabel [68f6e5] = 课业\n\n@hook coursework_beastcraft\n# en: Coursework\nlabel [68f6e5] = 课业\n\n@hook coursework_umbral\n# en: Coursework\nlabel [68f6e5] = 课业\n\n@hook coursework_artifice\n# en: Coursework\nlabel [68f6e5] = 课业\n\n@hook coursework_starcalling\n# en: Coursework\nlabel [68f6e5] = 课业\n\n@hook coursework_hollow_studies\n# en: Coursework\nlabel [68f6e5] = 课业\n\n@hook term_stress\n# en: Term-time pressure\nlabel [0e2de2] = 学期压力\n\n@hook exhaustion_drain\n# en: Running on empty\nlabel [069bc4] = 筋疲力尽\n\n@hook gloom_drain\n# en: Gloom\nlabel [0e658b] = 阴郁\n\n@hook natural_recovery\n# en: Rest\nlabel [e989b7] = 休养\n\n@hook fame_fades\n# en: Fading gossip\nlabel [06d4e9] = 流言消散\n\n@hook mana_regen\n# en: Mana returns\nlabel [d61671] = 法力恢复\n\n@hook exam_nerves\n# en: Exam nerves\nlabel [8b0cdb] = 考前焦虑\n\n@hook love_glow\n# en: Someone to come home to\nlabel [82093c] = 有人在等你回家\n\n@hook lonely\n# en: Loneliness\nlabel [b35139] = 孤独\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/endings.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/endings.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@ending ending_sent_home\n# en: Sent Home\ntitle [a6af26] = 被送回家\n# en: The infirmary writes to your family. A carriage comes for you on a grey morning and you watch the towers shrink in the rear window. Your body gave out before your dreams did. Perhaps, one day, you will come back.\ntext [9741e7] = 医务室给你家里写了信。一个灰蒙蒙的早晨，马车来接你，你从后窗看着高塔渐渐缩小。你的身体先于你的梦想垮掉了。也许有一天，你还会回来。\n\n@ending ending_quiet_departure\n# en: The Quiet Departure\ntitle [b17cb0] = 悄然离去\n# en: You take the dawn barge without telling anyone. The academy does not notice right away; then, one by one, people do. Some of them write. You keep the letters in a box you don't open for years. When you finally do, you are someone gentler, and you find you are not alone.\ntext [e6ed08] = 你没告诉任何人，搭上了黎明的驳船。学院起初并没察觉；后来，人们一个接一个地发现了。有些人给你写了信。你把信收进一只盒子，好多年都没打开。等你终于打开时，你已变得更温柔，而且发现自己并不孤单。\n\n@ending ending_expelled\n# en: Expelled\ntitle [a01536] = 开除学籍\n# en: The Board's decision is final. Your wand is not taken — Lumenhold is not cruel — but your name is struck from the register in ink that cannot be unwritten. You walk out of the gates with a trunk and a lesson, and the long road ahead is entirely your own.\ntext [27607d] = 委员会的决定无法更改。你的魔杖没有被收走——流明堡并不残忍——但你的名字被一种无法抹去的墨水从名册上划掉了。你提着行李箱、带着一个教训走出校门，前方的漫漫长路，完全属于你自己。\n\n@ending ending_hollow_claimed\n# en: Claimed by the Hollow Star\ntitle [83514d] = 被空洞之星吞噬\n# en: You open the door beneath the lake. The Hollow Star looks back. For a moment you understand everything, and then there is no \"you\" left to understand it. On clear nights students swear they see a new star, black against black, right above the seventh tower.\ntext [205ca2] = 你打开了湖底之门。空洞之星回望着你。有那么一瞬间，你明白了一切；随后，再也没有一个“你”能去明白什么了。晴朗的夜里，学生们发誓看见了一颗新星——黑上之黑——就在第七塔的正上方。\n\n@ending ending_runaway\n# en: Gone With the Wind\ntitle [d9dde5] = 随风而去\n# en: You and Corvin leave Lumenhold on a stolen sky-barge at midnight, laughing so hard you can barely steer. The academy will tell the story for years — usually wrong. You never graduate. You don't regret it, mostly.\ntext [c5d68b] = 午夜时分，你和科尔文驾着一艘偷来的天空驳船离开了流明堡，笑得几乎掌不稳舵。学院会把这个故事讲上好多年——而且通常讲错。你始终没有毕业。你并不后悔，大体上。\n\n@ending ending_hollow_keeper\n# en: Keeper of the Hollow Star\ntitle [3ac5e6] = 空洞之星的守护者\n# en: You graduate with a black star on your palm and a voice in the back of your mind that is not entirely yours. The Hollow Star gave you power; it will want something back. For now, the towers bow a little when you pass, and even the headmistress looks away first.\ntext [8bdf91] = 你毕业时，掌心带着一颗黑色的星，脑海深处还有一个不完全属于你的声音。空洞之星给了你力量；它总有一天会要回些什么。至于现在，你经过时高塔都会微微低头，连院长都会先移开目光。\n\n@ending ending_hollow_freed\n# en: Herald of the Hollow Star\ntitle [5e0dc6] = 空洞之星的使者\n# en: The spring the lake froze, the Star went free, and you held the door. It did not swallow you; it remembered who let it out. The Northern families write to you as one of their own now, and wherever you walk, candles burn a little lower, as if bowing. Lumenhold still hands you a diploma. Nobody at the ceremony looks you in the eye.\ntext [0ea418] = 湖面冰封的那个春天，星挣脱而去，而为它扶住门的是你。它没有吞掉你；它记得是谁放它出来的。如今北境的世家把你当作自家人来写信，你走到哪里，烛火便低下去一点，像在鞠躬。流明堡照样发给你毕业证书。典礼上没有一个人敢直视你的眼睛。\n\n@ending ending_hollow_hero\n# en: Warden of Lumenhold\ntitle [1eb489] = 流明堡的守望者\n# en: The academy will never fully know what you did beneath the lake. The headmistress does. At graduation she gives you a silver key without a lock and a seat among the Wardens, the quiet few who keep the old doors closed. Kael stands in the crowd and, for once, he is smiling.\ntext [fff990] = 学院永远不会完全知道你在湖底做了什么。院长知道。毕业典礼上，她交给你一把没有锁的银钥匙，以及守望者中的一席之地——那是守护古老之门、默默无闻的少数人。凯尔站在人群里，难得地，他在微笑。\n\n@ending ending_archmage\n# en: The Archmage's Path\ntitle [fea2fb] = 大法师之路\n# en: Graduating with highest honours and a power that makes the candles lean toward you, you are offered an apprenticeship with the Conclave of the Seven Towers. Professor Thorne shakes your hand and says, gruffly, \"Don't waste it.\" You won't.\ntext [e7e723] = 你以最高荣誉毕业，力量强到烛火都会向你倾斜。七塔议会向你发出了学徒邀约。索恩教授握着你的手，粗声说：“别浪费了。”你不会的。\n\n@ending ending_scholar\n# en: Scholar of the Spire\ntitle [02e34a] = 尖塔学者\n# en: Your thesis is shelved in the Spire Library, in the section students whisper about. The faculty offers you a junior fellowship. You will spend your life among books that bite and secrets that wait — and you could not be happier.\ntext [ea44fe] = 你的论文被收进了尖塔图书馆，就在学生们窃窃私语的那个区域。学院为你提供了初级研究员的职位。你将在会咬人的书和静静等待的秘密之间度过一生——再幸福不过了。\n\n@ending ending_beloved\n# en: Hand in Hand\ntitle [b32cc8] = 携手同行\n# en: Graduation is a blur of robes and cheers, but what you remember is a hand finding yours as the hats go up. Whatever comes next, you won't face it alone. Lumenhold taught you magic; it also taught you this.\ntext [ad5713] = 毕业典礼是长袍与欢呼交织的一片模糊，但你记得的，是帽子抛向空中时，一只手找到了你的手。无论接下来是什么，你都不会独自面对。流明堡教会了你魔法；也教会了你这个。\n\n@ending ending_beloved_lyra\n# en: The Second Flame\ntitle [c90153] = 第二簇火焰\n# en: When the hats go up, {lyra} catches yours before it lands and hands it back without a word. Later, in front of her whole family, she takes your hand and doesn't let go. For House Vaneshade, that is a declaration. For her it is simpler: she has finally found someone she doesn't need to beat.\ntext [1b430a] = 帽子抛向天空时，{lyra} 在你的帽子落地前接住了它，一言不发地递还给你。之后，当着她全家人的面，她握住你的手，再也没有松开。对凡妮希德家族来说，这是一份宣言；对她来说，事情更简单：她终于找到了一个不必去赢的人。\n\n@ending ending_beloved_tobias\n# en: Home, Wherever\ntitle [4a14ae] = 无论何处皆是家\n# en: {tobias} cries through the whole ceremony and blames the pollen. Afterwards he lifts you off your feet in front of everyone, then shows you a drawing of a farmhouse with two chairs on the porch. \"No pressure,\" he says. \"The goats have already voted.\"\ntext [f06768] = {tobias} 哭完了整场典礼，坚称是花粉过敏。之后他当着所有人的面把你抱离地面，又给你看一张画：一座农舍，门廊上摆着两把椅子。“不着急，”他说，“山羊们已经投过票了。”\n\n@ending ending_beloved_seraphine\n# en: Already Foreseen\ntitle [350c84] = 早已预见\n# en: \"I saw this,\" {seraphine} says as the hats go up. \"Years ago. You were holding my hand.\" You are. For once she smiles at the present, as if it has finally caught up with her, and asks whether you'd like to know what happens next. You tell her you'd rather find out together.\ntext [c966c4] = “我见过这一幕，”帽子抛起时 {seraphine} 说，“好几年前。你握着我的手。”你确实握着。她难得地对着此刻微笑，仿佛现在终于追上了她，然后问你想不想知道接下来会发生什么。你说你宁愿和她一起去发现。\n\n@ending ending_beloved_corvin\n# en: No More Jokes\ntitle [ffc267] = 不再说笑\n# en: {corvin} slips a key into your palm during the speeches. It opens nothing at Lumenhold; he has checked. \"It's for wherever we end up,\" he says, and for once he doesn't make a joke of it. Then he does, of course. But his hand stays in yours.\ntext [83042c] = 致辞的时候，{corvin} 把一把钥匙塞进你掌心。它在流明堡打不开任何一扇门——他检查过了。“这是给我们将来落脚的地方用的，”他说，难得没有拿它开玩笑。当然，过了一会儿他还是开了个玩笑。但他的手一直握着你的。\n\n@ending ending_beloved_mira\n# en: Built to Last\ntitle [66dc88] = 经久耐用\n# en: {mira} has rigged the hats to fly back down. Most of them do. In the cheerful chaos they press a small brass ring into your hand; it ticks faintly, like a second heart. \"It'll run for fifty years,\" they say. \"Probably. We should check.\"\ntext [c61eff] = {mira} 给帽子装了机关，让它们自己飞回来。大部分确实飞回来了。在一片欢乐的混乱里，TA 把一枚小小的黄铜戒指按进你手心：它轻轻地滴答作响，像第二颗心脏。“能走五十年，”TA 说，“大概吧。我们得一起验证一下。”\n\n@ending ending_beloved_kael\n# en: Thaw\ntitle [448405] = 解冻\n# en: {kael} doesn't throw his hat. He stands beside you in the crowd, and when the cheering is loudest he says, very quietly, \"Come north with me. Or I stay. Either.\" It is the longest sentence about feelings he has ever spoken. His hand, in yours, is warm.\ntext [edf40c] = {kael} 没有抛帽子。他站在人群里你的身边，在欢呼声最响的时候，非常轻地说：“跟我去北方吧。或者我留下。都行。”这是他说过的最长的一句关于感情的话。他握在你手里的手，是温暖的。\n\n@ending ending_beloved_nessa\n# en: Front Page\ntitle [c50df3] = 头版\n# en: The Ledger's graduation issue runs a small notice at the bottom of page six, set in {nessa}'s own type: two names, one line, no comment. All of Lumenhold reads it before breakfast. {nessa} pretends not to watch them read it, and holds your hand under the table the whole time.\ntext [ed9fec] = 《流明纪事报》的毕业特刊在第六版底部登了一则小启事，用的是 {nessa} 自己的铅字：两个名字，一行字，不加评论。全流明堡的人都在早饭前读到了它。{nessa} 假装没在看大家读它，却在桌子底下一直握着你的手。\n\n@ending ending_alchemist\n# en: Master of the Crucible\ntitle [cd1134] = 坩埚大师\n# en: Your potions are already sold under the counter in three cities. On graduation day you sign the lease on a crooked little shop by the harbour. The sign reads your name, and underneath: NO REFUNDS FOR TRANSFORMATIONS.\ntext [59bc7c] = 你的药剂早已在三座城市里私下热销。毕业那天，你在港口边签下了一间歪歪扭扭的小铺子的租约。招牌上写着你的名字，下面一行小字：变形类商品，概不退款。\n\n@ending ending_celebrity\n# en: The Name on Every Lip\ntitle [264e3e] = 人人口中的名字\n# en: The Lumen Ledger runs a special edition about you. First-years queue for your autograph. You graduate as the most talked-about student in a generation, and the world beyond the gates is already waiting to hear what you'll do next.\ntext [208c47] = 《流明纪事报》为你出了一期特刊。一年级新生排队找你签名。你作为一代人中最受热议的学生毕业，而校门外的世界，早已等着听你接下来要做什么。\n\n@ending ending_merchant\n# en: Gold in the Vaults\ntitle [2332ee] = 金库满盈\n# en: You leave Lumenhold with a diploma and a fortune. Magic, you've decided, is good, but compound interest is better. Within five years your trading house has offices in every port — and an enchanted ledger that never loses a coin.\ntext [729e03] = 你带着一纸文凭和一笔财富离开了流明堡。你认定：魔法固然好，复利更好。不出五年，你的商行在每个港口都设了分号——还有一本永远不会少一枚硬币的魔法账簿。\n\n@ending ending_shadow\n# en: The Quiet Hand\ntitle [9eb5a5] = 无声之手\n# en: No one quite knows how you graduated with those grades. No one quite remembers the favours you called in. A discreet letter offers you a position with the Crown's Office of Unseen Affairs. You accept, of course. You'd already read it before it was sent.\ntext [0900ac] = 没人说得清你是怎么凭那样的成绩毕业的。也没人记得你讨回了哪些人情。一封措辞谨慎的信，邀请你加入王室隐秘事务署。你当然接受了。信还没寄出，你就已经读过了。\n\n@ending ending_beloved_friends\n# en: The Ones Who Stayed\ntitle [5337c3] = 留下来的人\n# en: You may not leave as the brightest or the richest, but you leave surrounded. Your friends throw their hats higher than anyone's, and the photograph from that day — all of you squinting into the sun — will hang in every home you ever have.\ntext [8d3051] = 你离开时也许不是最聪明的，也不是最富有的，但你被大家簇拥着。你的朋友们把帽子抛得比谁都高；那天的合影——大家都在阳光里眯着眼——会挂在你今后住过的每一个家里。\n\n@ending ending_barely\n# en: By the Skin of Your Teeth\ntitle [434e96] = 惊险过关\n# en: Your name is called last, after a long pause and some whispering between examiners. But it is called. You graduate. You are a mage of Lumenhold, and nobody ever has to know the details.\ntext [7c93a5] = 在一段长长的停顿和考官之间的窃窃私语之后，你的名字最后一个被念到。但终究被念到了。你毕业了。你是流明堡的法师，没人需要知道其中的细节。\n\n@ending ending_bystander\n# en: Someone Else's Lantern\ntitle [93af7a] = 别人的灯笼\n# en: There was a spring frost, the year you graduated, and a night when the bell under the lake would not stop ringing. You were somewhere else: asleep, or studying, or holding a door shut for the first-years. Someone else went down with the lantern. You never learn their whole story, but every spring, when the lake thaws, you find yourself grateful to them.\ntext [737b03] = 你毕业那年下过一场春霜，还有一个夜晚，湖底的钟响个不停。那时你在别处：在睡觉，在读书，或是替一年级生死死顶住一扇门。提着灯笼下去的是别人。你始终没能知道他们的全部故事，但每年春天湖水解冻时，你总会发现自己在心里默默感谢他们。\n\n@ending ending_graduate\n# en: A Mage of Lumenhold\ntitle [691b6b] = 流明堡的法师\n# en: Robes, hats, a speech that runs long. You walk out of the gates of Lumenhold a qualified mage — not legendary, not disgraced, simply yourself, with four years of stories and a whole world to spend them in.\ntext [7b1e39] = 长袍、学位帽，一场拖得太长的致辞。你作为一名合格的法师走出流明堡的大门——不算传奇，也不丢人，只是你自己，带着四年的故事，和一整个可以挥霍它们的世界。\n\n@ending ending_teacher\n# en: The Teacher's Desk\ntitle [b21115] = 讲台之后\n# en: In the autumn you return to Lumenhold — not on the student barge, but on the faculty one. Your classroom has a window over the lake and a desk with somebody else's initials carved in it. On the first day a terrified first-year asks a question you once asked. You know exactly what to say.\ntext [dce2bc] = 秋天，你回到了流明堡——不是坐学生的驳船，而是坐教职员的那一艘。你的教室有一扇面朝湖水的窗，讲桌上刻着别人的名字缩写。开学第一天，一个吓坏了的一年级生问了一个你当年也问过的问题。你完全知道该怎么回答。\n\n@ending ending_wanderer\n# en: The Road of Wonders\ntitle [055eab] = 奇迹之路\n# en: You join the Travelling Circus of Wonders as its youngest illusionist. For years you cross the kingdoms in a painted wagon, making dragons out of moonlight for children who have never seen magic. Every winter, a letter arrives from Lumenhold. You always write back.\ntext [31dcb2] = 你加入了奇迹巡回马戏团，成了团里最年轻的幻术师。多年来，你坐着彩绘马车穿越一个又一个王国，用月光为从未见过魔法的孩子们变出巨龙。每年冬天，都会有一封来自流明堡的信。你每一封都回。\n\n@ending ending_dumonts_reunited\n# en: Two Dumonts in the Photograph\ntitle [51d393] = 合影里的两个杜蒙\n# en: In the graduation photograph, at the edge of the crowd, two pale heads lean together: Kael, and the sister who was raised to open the door he refused. Neither of them is smiling at the camera. They are smiling at each other, which is rarer, and which you helped to happen.\ntext [d79ff1] = 毕业合影里，人群边上，两颗浅色的脑袋靠在一起：凯尔，和那个被养大来打开他拒绝打开的那扇门的妹妹。他们谁都没有对着镜头笑。他们是在对彼此笑——这更难得，而你帮了这个忙。\n\n@ending ending_ilse_taken\n# en: A Chair Left Empty\ntitle [c33d8b] = 一把空着的椅子\n# en: The man in grey took Ilse north, and no letter has come. At graduation Kael keeps the seat beside him empty without saying why. You know why. Some evenings, when the lake goes very still, you still look for a girl in white furs on the far shore.\ntext [9df500] = 灰衣人把伊尔泽带去了北境，至今没有一封信。毕业典礼上，凯尔身旁的座位一直空着，他没说为什么。你知道为什么。有些傍晚，湖面静得出奇的时候，你仍会在对岸寻找一个穿白裘的女孩。\n\n@ending ending_thesis_hollowstar\n# en: The Thesis They Locked Away\ntitle [dad732] = 被锁起来的论文\n# en: Your thesis on the founders' seal earned a distinction and then disappeared into the headmistress's tower. Its catalogue card in the Spire Library reads only \"Restricted\", in her handwriting. Three people in the world have read it. One of them still writes to you with questions.\ntext [b15104] = 你关于创始者封印的毕业论文拿到了优等，随后便消失在院长塔里。尖塔图书馆里它的目录卡上只写着\"禁阅\"二字，是她的笔迹。世上读过它的只有三个人，其中一个至今还写信来向你请教。\n\n@ending ending_lumen\n# en: A Lantern for Others\ntitle [217ba6] = 为他人提灯的人\n# en: In the yearbook, under your portrait, someone has written: \"The one you went to when it mattered.\" First-years you barely remember will name their own lanterns after you. Lumenhold has a word for people like that. It is the same word as its name.\ntext [2de9cf] = 年鉴上，你的肖像下面有人写了一句：“要紧的时候，大家去找的那个人。”你几乎记不得的一年级生，日后会用你的名字给自己的灯笼命名。流明堡对这样的人有个说法——就是它自己的名字。\n\n@ending ending_hollow_touched\n# en: The Shadow at Your Heel\ntitle [b9eb7a] = 跟在脚边的影子\n# en: Your portrait in the yearbook is the only one where the candles behind you seem to have gone out. People step aside when you pass, not quite knowing why. The Hollow has learned your name, and you find, more and more often, that you answer to it.\ntext [7dd587] = 整本年鉴里，只有你的肖像身后的烛火像是熄了。人们在你经过时让开路，却说不清为什么。空洞已经知道了你的名字，而你越来越常发现，自己会应声。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/alignment.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/alignment.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event align_cold_candles\n# en: The Candles Lean Away\ntitle [1845e0] = 烛火避让\n# en: {You notice it on the east stair. As you pass, the candle flames bend away from you, all of them, like grass from a wind. A first-year on the landing notices too.|The candles lean away again. You have started to time it. It is getting faster.}\ntext [8f1ab2] = {你是在东楼梯上注意到的。你经过时，烛焰全都朝远离你的方向弯去，像风吹过草地。楼梯平台上的一个一年级生也看见了。|烛火又避开了。你开始给它计时。它越来越快了。}\n# en: A long stone corridor at dusk, every candle flame bending away from one student walking down the middle of it.\nscene [48ce03] = 黄昏时分一条长长的石廊，每一簇烛焰都朝远离走在中间那名学生的方向弯去。\n# en: Let them lean\nopt.left.label [131e40] = 由它们去吧\n# en: You walk on without looking. Behind you the first-year says nothing at all, which is its own kind of saying.\nopt.left.say.1 [93806d] = 你目不斜视地走过去。身后那个一年级生一句话也没说——这本身就是一种表态。\n# en: Stop and relight one by hand\nopt.right.label [87e0d1] = 停下，亲手点亮一盏\n# en: You cup a flame back to life with your palm. It flickers, considers you, and stays. It takes longer than it should.\nopt.right.say.1 [4fde72] = 你用掌心护着一簇火苗让它重新燃起。它摇曳着，打量着你，然后留了下来。这比本该花的时间要久。\n\n@event align_whispered_answer\n# en: A Voice Over Your Shoulder\ntitle [be6019] = 肩后的声音\n# en: Revising late, you get stuck on the same question for the third time. Then the answer is simply there, in a voice that is almost yours, from just behind your left ear. The inkwell has frosted over.\ntext [b8b636] = 深夜复习时，你第三次卡在同一道题上。然后答案就那样出现了——一个几乎是你自己的声音，从你左耳后方传来。墨水瓶上结了一层霜。\n# en: A student bent over a desk by candlelight, revision notes everywhere, and a cold patch of frost forming on the inkwell.\nscene [6d785b] = 烛光下伏案的学生，复习笔记摊得到处都是，墨水瓶上正凝出一片冰霜。\n# en: Write it down\nopt.left.label [f20ae7] = 把它写下来\n# en: It is right. Of course it is right. You write until dawn and never once get stuck, and in the morning you cannot remember learning any of it.\nopt.left.say.1 [d6cb97] = 它是对的。当然是对的。你一直写到天亮，一次也没卡住；可到了早上，你完全不记得自己学过这些。\n# en: Close the book\nopt.right.label [7a6dbd] = 合上书\n# en: You close the book and sit with your hands flat on the cover until the frost on the inkwell melts. The question stays unanswered. It's yours, at least.\nopt.right.say.1 [88c4b2] = 你合上书，双手平放在封面上，一直坐到墨水瓶上的霜化掉。那道题仍然没有答案。但至少它是你自己的。\n\n@event align_maud_way_back\n# en: Weeding, Dearie\ntitle [b9db32] = 来拔草吧，孩子\n# en: Old Maud looks at you for a long moment over her spectacles, the way she looks at a plant that's been kept in the wrong window. \"You've a cold look about you, dearie,\" she says, and holds out a trowel. \"Weeds don't pull themselves. Neither does the rest.\"\ntext [602ce6] = 老莫德从眼镜上方久久地看着你，就像在看一株被放错了窗台的植物。“你身上有股冷气，孩子，”她说着，递过来一把小铲子，“杂草不会自己拔掉。别的事也一样。”\n# en: An old woman in a straw hat kneeling in a vegetable bed, handing a trowel up to a student who hasn't asked for one.\nscene [557087] = 一位戴草帽的老妇人跪在菜畦里，把小铲子递给一个并没有开口要的学生。\n# en: Kneel and start pulling\nopt.left.label [2be082] = 跪下来开始拔\n# en: You weed until your knees ache and your hands smell of earth instead of frost. Maud hums the whole time. Nothing's fixed. Something has started.\nopt.left.say.1 [8ff3f1] = 你拔草拔到膝盖发酸，双手闻起来是泥土味而不是霜味。莫德一直在哼歌。什么都还没好起来。但有些事已经开始了。\n# en: \"I'm fine.\"\nopt.right.label [bdda04] = “我没事。”\n# en: \"Course you are,\" Maud says, and goes back to her beans. The trowel stays in the soil beside her, handle towards you, all afternoon.\nopt.right.say.1 [6bc997] = “当然没事，”莫德说着，回头去照料她的豆子。那把小铲子整个下午都插在她身边的土里，把手朝着你。\n\n@event align_reproach\n# en: \"I Don't Know You Lately\"\ntitle [5baaf9] = “我最近不认识你了”\n# en: {friend} finds you in the cloister and sits without saying hello. \"People talk,\" {they} say{s} at last. \"I keep telling them they're wrong about you. I'd like to keep being right.\"\ntext [8427e0] = {friend}在回廊里找到你，连招呼都没打就坐下了。“大家在议论，”{they}终于开口，“我一直告诉他们，他们看错你了。我希望我能一直是对的。”\n# en: Two friends on a cold bench in the cloister, one looking straight ahead, the other looking at the first.\nscene [1d2371] = 回廊里一张冰冷的长凳上坐着两个朋友，一个直视前方，另一个看着前者。\n# en: \"You are. I'll do better.\"\nopt.left.label [c698dd] = “你是对的。我会改。”\n# en: {friend} nods, slowly, and bumps {their} shoulder against yours. It is an apology and a warning at once.\nopt.left.say.1 [0320a5] = {friend}慢慢点了点头，用肩膀撞了撞你的肩。这既是和好，也是警告。\n# en: \"Mind your own business.\"\nopt.right.label [53fb82] = “少管闲事。”\n# en: {friend} gets up and goes. {They} don't look back. That's what stays with you: {they} always used to look back.\nopt.right.say.1 [d0c677] = {friend}起身走了，没有回头。让你一直记着的正是这一点：{they}以前总会回头的。\n\n@event align_ruthless_nod\n# en: Finally, Someone Who Gets It\ntitle [b51f5a] = 总算有人懂了\n# en: {other} has always looked straight through you. Today {they} nod{s} as you pass, slow and approving, the way people nod at someone who has finally learned the rules of a game.\ntext [52854c] = {other}向来对你视而不见。今天你经过时，{they}却朝你点了点头——缓慢而赞许，就像人们朝一个终于学会游戏规则的人点头那样。\n# en: A hard-faced student leaning on a pillar, giving another a slow, approving nod across a busy corridor.\nscene [8d42a3] = 一个神情冷硬的学生倚着柱子，隔着熙攘的走廊朝另一人缓缓点头示意。\n# en: Nod back\nopt.left.label [ae3c6d] = 点头回应\n# en: The nod comes back to you. It feels like being let into a room. You can't tell whether the room is warm.\nopt.left.say.1 [375363] = 点头被回敬了过来。那感觉像是被请进了一间屋子。你说不清那屋子是否温暖。\n# en: Keep walking\nopt.right.label [6ca967] = 继续往前走\n# en: You keep walking. {other} snorts, as if you'd failed a test you didn't know you were sitting.\nopt.right.say.1 [1c408e] = 你继续往前走。{other}嗤了一声，好像你没通过一场你都不知道自己在考的考试。\n\n@event align_northern_parcel\n# en: A Parcel for the North\ntitle [917cb6] = 寄往北境的包裹\n# en: {~A hooded courier stops you behind the bakery.|The hooded courier is waiting again, same alley, same black oilcloth.} \"For the boathouse, after dark,\" {~they say, and hold out the parcel and a purse.|they say. \"The families remember who is reliable.\"} The oilcloth is cold enough to ache.\ntext [79a613] = {~一个戴兜帽的信使在面包房后面拦住了你。|那个戴兜帽的信使又在等你了：同一条小巷，同一块黑油布。}“天黑后送到船屋，”{~对方说着，递来包裹和一只钱袋。|对方说，“世家记得谁靠得住。”}那块油布冷得发疼。\n# en: A hooded courier in a rain-dark alley behind the village, holding out a small parcel wrapped in black oilcloth and a purse.\nscene [ab51fb] = 村子后面一条被雨淋暗的小巷里，一个戴兜帽的信使递出一个裹着黑油布的小包裹和一只钱袋。\n# en: Carry it\nopt.left.label [f4d75d] = 替他们送去\n# en: You leave it on the boathouse step at midnight and don't look back. In the morning there's frost on the step in the shape of a hand, and the purse is heavier than it looked.\nopt.left.say.1 [f86ac1] = 你在午夜把它放在船屋的台阶上，没有回头。早上，台阶上结着一只手形的霜，而那只钱袋比看上去要沉。\n# en: Walk away\nopt.right.label [96da1a] = 转身离开\n# en: You leave the courier holding it. \"Pity,\" they say, without any pity at all, and the alley is empty when you look back.\nopt.right.say.1 [371c1f] = 你让信使自己拿着它。“可惜，”对方说，语气里没有半点可惜。你回头时，小巷已经空了。\n\n@event align_cruel_advantage\n# en: Something You Could Use\ntitle [e223de] = 可以利用的东西\n# en: {mark} has dropped a letter in the library: something private, something {they} would hate the corridor to hear. You could hand it back. You could also keep it, and {they} would owe you, quietly, for a long time.\ntext [306670] = {mark}在图书馆掉了一封信：私密的事，{they}最不愿意让整条走廊听到的事。你可以把它还回去。你也可以留着它——那样{they}就会悄悄地欠你很久。\n# en: A crumpled letter on a library floor, a student's name on the envelope, another student bending to pick it up.\nscene [8a21c3] = 图书馆地板上一封揉皱的信，信封上写着一个学生的名字，另一个学生正弯腰去捡。\n# en: Keep it\nopt.left.label [87c2f5] = 留着它\n# en: You fold it into your book. When you mention it, very lightly, {mark} goes pale and agrees to everything. It works. It keeps working.\nopt.left.say.1 [18005f] = 你把它夹进书里。当你轻描淡写地提起它时，{mark}脸色发白，什么都答应了。这招管用，而且一直管用。\n# en: Hand it back\nopt.right.label [cb62a0] = 还给对方\n# en: You hand it back unread. {mark} stares at you, then at the letter, then says \"thank you\" as if it costs something. It does, for both of you.\nopt.right.say.1 [414642] = 你没拆开就把信还了回去。{mark}盯着你，又盯着那封信，然后说了声“谢谢”，好像这要付出什么代价。确实要——对你们俩都是。\n\n@event align_frost_in_the_spell\n# en: The Cold That Helps\ntitle [7dac5f] = 有用的寒意\n# en: Practising alone, you notice that your spells come easier when you let the cold in, the cold that has been following you lately. It climbs your sleeve like ivy. The candle beside you leans away.\ntext [a3ee9f] = 独自练习时，你发现只要放那股寒意进来——最近一直跟着你的那股寒意——咒语就来得更容易。它像常春藤一样爬上你的袖子。身旁的蜡烛避开了你。\n# en: A student in an empty practice room, a spell flaring unnaturally bright in one hand, frost creeping up the sleeve to the elbow.\nscene [2b39b1] = 空荡荡的练习室里，一名学生手中的咒语亮得反常，冰霜正沿着袖子爬到手肘。\n# en: Let the cold in\nopt.left.label [13fba8] = 放寒意进来\n# en: The spell flares bright and clean, brighter than anything you've managed in daylight. It takes an hour for the feeling to come back into your fingers. You don't mind as much as you should.\nopt.left.say.1 [ddd5a7] = 咒语亮得干净利落，比你在白天施展过的任何一次都亮。手指过了一个小时才恢复知觉。你没有本该有的那么在意。\n# en: Practise the slow way\nopt.right.label [210853] = 用笨办法练\n# en: You shake the cold out of your hands and do it the slow way, badly, again and again. It is your own bad spell, at least.\nopt.right.say.1 [21c347] = 你甩掉手上的寒意，用笨办法练，练得很差，一遍又一遍。至少那是你自己的蹩脚咒语。\n\n@event align_asked_for_help\n# en: \"You're the One People Ask\"\ntitle [8a706f] = “大家都来找你”\n# en: {~A first-year hovers by your table with a torn essay and a face like a wet weekend.|A first-year you've never met asks if you're the one who helps people. Apparently you are.|Someone has written your name on the common-room board under \"ask them\".} \"Somebody said you'd know what to do,\" {~they say, as if that settles it.|they say, and then wait.}\ntext [4fe2aa] = {~一个一年级生拿着撕破的论文在你桌边徘徊，脸色像个阴雨的周末。|一个你从没见过的一年级生问你是不是那个会帮人的人。看来你是。|有人在公共休息室的告示板上“找他们问”一栏下写了你的名字。}“有人说你会知道该怎么办，”{~对方说，好像这就说明了一切。|对方说完，便等着。}\n# en: A nervous first-year with an armful of torn notes hovering at the door of a common room, looking at one particular older student.\nscene [dd1c6d] = 一个紧张的一年级生抱着一摞撕破的笔记，在公共休息室门口徘徊，眼睛望着某一位高年级学生。\n# en: Sit them down and help\nopt.left.label [d2700f] = 让对方坐下，帮忙\n# en: It takes the whole evening. They leave lighter than they came. So, oddly, do you.\nopt.left.say.1 [311f05] = 这花了整整一个晚上。对方走的时候比来时轻松了。说来奇怪，你也是。\n# en: Point them to a tutor\nopt.right.label [0b7f21] = 介绍给辅导员\n# en: You give them a name and a smile and get back to your own work. They look disappointed for a moment, then grateful anyway.\nopt.right.say.1 [9dd1bd] = 你给了对方一个名字和一个微笑，然后回去做自己的功课。对方失望了片刻，但还是很感激。\n\n@event align_warm_regard\n# en: A Good Word\ntitle [352a57] = 一句好话\n# en: {friend} pushes the last biscuit across to you. \"People talk, you know. About the kind things you do when you think nobody's looking,\" {they} say{s}. \"Don't make a face. It's good. Someone should say so.\"\ntext [6b8c11] = {friend}把最后一块饼干推到你面前。“你知道吗，大家都在说。说你以为没人看见时做的那些好事，”{they}说，“别摆那副表情。那是好事。总得有人说出来。”\n# en: Two students sharing a pot of tea in a sunny window seat, one pushing the last biscuit towards the other.\nscene [7e8fe3] = 两个学生坐在阳光照进来的窗边座位上分一壶茶，其中一人把最后一块饼干推给另一人。\n# en: Accept the biscuit, and the praise\nopt.left.label [80690f] = 收下饼干和夸奖\n# en: You eat the biscuit. {friend} looks very pleased. You buy the next pot.\nopt.left.say.1 [909198] = 你吃掉了饼干。{friend}看起来非常得意。下一壶茶你请。\n# en: Say anyone would have done the same\nopt.right.label [c1a72c] = 说换了谁都会这么做\n# en: \"Anyone would,\" you say. \"Nobody did,\" says {friend}, and lets you change the subject, but the look {they} give{s} you says it has only been postponed.\nopt.right.say.1 [af9984] = “换了谁都会的，”你说。“可没人做，”{friend}说，然后由着你换了话题，但{they}看你的眼神说明，这个话题只是暂时搁置。\n\n@event align_saint_jibe\n# en: \"Saint\"\ntitle [136318] = “圣人”\n# en: {other} blocks the doorway with one arm. \"Here comes the saint,\" {they} say{s}, loud enough for the corridor. \"Tell me, does it get heavy, being better than everyone?\"\ntext [58f004] = {other}用一只胳膊挡住门口。“圣人来了，”{they}说，声音大得整条走廊都能听见，“告诉我，比所有人都好，累不累啊？”\n# en: A smirking student blocking a doorway with one arm, another student standing in front of them with an armful of books.\nscene [1e2bb0] = 一个冷笑着的学生用一只胳膊挡住门口，另一个学生抱着一摞书站在面前。\n# en: Let it go\nopt.left.label [7a5d2f] = 算了\n# en: You wait. Eventually {other} gets bored and drops the arm. Two first-years behind you look at you like you've done something brave. You're not sure you have.\nopt.left.say.1 [657e06] = 你等着。{other}终于觉得没意思，放下了胳膊。你身后两个一年级生看着你，好像你做了什么勇敢的事。你自己可不确定。\n# en: Give as good as you get\nopt.right.label [6cf248] = 以牙还牙\n# en: Your answer is quick and much funnier than theirs. The corridor laughs. {other} goes red. It feels wonderful for about an hour.\nopt.right.say.1 [28c5ab] = 你的回嘴又快又比对方的好笑得多。走廊里一片哄笑，{other}涨红了脸。这感觉美妙了大约一个小时。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/calendar_2b.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/calendar_2b.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event enrolment\n# en: Enrolment\ntitle [cbadef] = 选课\n# en: {term_index == 1: Your first term. The registrar slides a list across the desk: four courses open to first-years.|A new term, and a new list. Courses you've passed are gone from it; new levels have appeared.} Choose two. Course fees are eight crowns.\ntext [6207d2] = {term_index == 1: 你的第一个学期。教务员把一张清单推过桌面：四门对一年级开放的课。|新学期，新清单。你已通过的课不见了，新的级别冒了出来。}选两门。课程费八克朗。\n# en: The registrar's hall at the start of term, students queueing at long tables with course lists that rewrite themselves.\nscene [6d30e9] = 开学时的教务大厅，学生们在长桌前排队，手里的课程表会自己改写。\n# en: Take no courses this term\nopt.none.label [679a29] = 这学期不选课\n# en: Allowed. Your GPA will not thank you.\nopt.none.hint [362aa4] = 可以。你的 GPA 不会感谢你。\n# en: You hand back the list blank. The registrar raises one eyebrow, which is the whole of her opinion.\nopt.none.say.1 [5046fe] = 你把空白的名单交了回去。教务员挑起一边眉毛，那就是她全部的意见。\n\n@event club_fair_v3\n# en: The Club Fair\ntitle [ce4fee] = 社团招新会\n# en: The Club Fair fills the courtyard. Every table wants you; every captain swears theirs is the one that matters. {rel(lyra) >= 6: Lyra is at the Duelling Circle's table, pretending not to watch which way you walk.|}\ntext [fa6309] = 社团招新会挤满了庭院。每张桌子都想要你，每位社长都发誓自家才是最要紧的。{rel(lyra) >= 6: 莱拉站在决斗社的桌边，假装没在看你往哪儿走。|}\n# en: The courtyard full of recruiting tables, banners snapping, a duelling demonstration drawing a crowd.\nscene [72616b] = 庭院里摆满招新桌，旗帜猎猎作响，一场决斗示范引来一圈围观者。\n# en: {member(club): Stay with your club|Not this year}\nopt.none.label [9e77c4] = {member(club): 留在你的社团|今年算了}\n# en: {member(club): Another year with the same table.|You can join at next year's fair.}\nopt.none.hint [5c873f] = {member(club): 和同一张桌子再过一年。|明年的招新会还能加入。}\n# en: {member(club): {~You stop by your own club's table to help hand out leaflets. Someone asks you if it's worth joining. You say yes and mean it.|You walk past every other table with the ease of someone already spoken for.}|You collect three leaflets and a free biscuit, and sign nothing.}\nopt.none.say.1 [b61f4a] = {member(club): {~你在自己社团的桌边帮忙发传单。有人问你值不值得加入。你说值得，而且是真心的。|你从别的桌子前走过，带着一种名花有主的从容。}|你领了三张传单和一块免费饼干，什么也没签。}\n# en: Resign from your club\nopt.quit.label [990542] = 退出你的社团\n# en: You can join again at next year's fair, as a new member.\nopt.quit.hint [5bb00e] = 明年的招新会还能以新成员身份重新加入。\n# en: {~You hand back your club pin at the table where you got it. It feels smaller than it did.|You resign. Your evenings are yours again, and nobody will expect you anywhere.}\nopt.quit.say.1 [0a0a06] = {~你在当初领徽章的那张桌子前把社团徽章交了回去。它看起来比当时小了。|你退出了。晚上又归你自己了，也没人会在哪儿等你。}\n\n@event job_board_visit\n# en: The Job Board\ntitle [377c1b] = 招工告示板\n# en: Notices for work, three layers deep: the library, the Gilded Newt, Pell & Daughters, the courier loft. Each wants someone reliable. Each says so twice.\ntext [358cb2] = 招工启事叠了三层：图书馆、镀金蝾螈酒馆、佩尔父女药铺、信使阁楼。每家都要个靠得住的人，每家都说了两遍。\n# en: A cork board crowded with notices, a student running a finger down a column of shift times.\nscene [62cc7b] = 一块挤满告示的软木板，一名学生用手指顺着一列排班时间往下划。\n# en: Just looking\nopt.look.label [4af612] = 只是看看\n# en: You read every notice twice and take none of them down.\nopt.look.say.1 [5234e4] = 你把每张告示都读了两遍，一张也没揭下来。\n# en: Hand in your notice\nopt.quit.label [e9edc6] = 递交辞呈\n# en: You can find work again later.\nopt.quit.hint [974ede] = 以后还能再找工作。\n# en: You hand in your notice. Your evenings are yours again; so is your empty purse.\nopt.quit.say.1 [3dd0ab] = 你递交了辞呈。晚上又归你自己了，空荡荡的钱袋也是。\n\n@event break_plan\n# en: The Break\ntitle [e3e693] = 假期\n# en: {month == 5: The winter break.|The summer break.} Two months without lectures. The castle empties, mostly.\ntext [78e780] = {month == 5: 冬假。|暑假。}两个月没有课。城堡几乎空了。\n# en: The academy gates thrown open, barges loading trunks, a few students lingering on the steps with nowhere they'd rather be.\nscene [003006] = 学院大门敞开，驳船装着行李箱，几个学生在台阶上逗留，哪儿也不想去。\n# en: Stay at the castle\nopt.castle.label [04365a] = 留在城堡\n# en: Quiet corridors and secrets. Board 8 crowns a month.\nopt.castle.hint [6c24c3] = 安静的走廊和秘密。每月食宿 8 克朗。\n# en: The castle in the holidays is a different building: louder echoes, kinder staff, doors you've never seen open.\nopt.castle.say.1 [f9e1bd] = 假期里的城堡是另一座建筑：回声更响，职员更和气，还有你从没见开过的门。\n# en: Go home\nopt.home.label [c93952] = 回家\n# en: Family, old friends, free board.\nopt.home.hint [1bbb35] = 家人、老朋友、免费食宿。\n# en: The barge, the coach, the last mile on foot. Home is smaller than you remember, and warmer.\nopt.home.say.1 [0b9342] = 驳船、马车，最后一里路靠双脚。家比记忆中小，也比记忆中暖。\n# en: Work on campus\nopt.work.label [2ebfbe] = 留校打工\n# en: A holiday gig that pays well. Board 8 crowns a month.\nopt.work.hint [6b010a] = 一份工钱不错的假期零工。食宿每月 8 克朗。\n# en: You trade the holidays for wages. Your feet will remember it.\nopt.work.say.1 [e054a1] = 你用假期换了工钱。你的脚会记住这一切。\n# en: Start your internship\nopt.intern.label [744abc] = 开始实习\n# en: A stipend, a supervisor and a project.\nopt.intern.hint [abd74b] = 一份津贴、一位导师和一个课题。\n# en: You pack a trunk of notebooks and take the long coach to the capital.\nopt.intern.say.1 [9f079a] = 你装了一箱笔记本，坐上去王都的长途马车。\n\n@event course_exam\n# en: Exam Day\ntitle [482384] = 考试日\n# en: Exam day for {track}. The hall is silent except for quills and somebody's knee bouncing under a desk. {~The paper is long.|The first question is one you revised.|The first question is one you didn't.|The invigilator turns the hourglass with a click you feel in your teeth.}\ntext [6e3a75] = {track}考试日。考场里鸦雀无声，只有羽毛笔的沙沙声，还有谁的膝盖在桌下抖个不停。{~试卷很长。|第一道题正好是你复习过的。|第一道题恰好是你没复习的。|监考老师把沙漏一翻，那一声咔哒你连牙齿都感觉得到。}\n# en: A silent exam hall in late light, rows of bent heads, an hourglass on the invigilator's desk.\nscene [43a564] = 傍晚光线里寂静的考场，一排排低着的头，监考桌上放着一只沙漏。\n# en: Answer what you know, carefully\nopt.left.label [7dfdbe] = 稳扎稳打，写你会的\n# en: {~You write slowly: everything you're sure of, and nothing you aren't.|You check every answer twice and hand in at the bell.}\nopt.left.say.1 [a7c6e9] = {~你写得很慢：有把握的都写上，没把握的一个字也不写。|你每道题都检查两遍，铃响才交卷。}\n# en: Go for top marks\nopt.right.label [4d6661] = 冲击高分\n# en: {~You take the hardest question first and gamble the essay on an idea you had at breakfast.|You write the answer you'd want to read, not the safe one.}\nopt.right.say.1 [b27304] = {~你先做最难的题，把整篇论述押在早饭时冒出的一个想法上。|你写下自己想读的答案，而不是稳妥的那个。}\n\n@event track_warning\n# en: A Word About Your Attendance\ntitle [6bd5a3] = 关于你的出勤\n# en: A note about {track}: your attendance and your efforts have slipped, and it has been noticed. One more term like this and there will be consequences.\ntext [2da6ae] = 一张关于{track}的便条：你的出勤和努力都在下滑，有人注意到了。再这样一个学期，就要有后果了。\n# en: A folded note on official paper, the words \"performance\" and \"concern\" underlined twice.\nscene [7cac89] = 一张折起来的公文纸，“表现”和“关切”两个词下面各划了两道线。\n# en: Promise to do better\nopt.left.label [cba502] = 保证会改进\n# en: You write back a promise in your neatest hand. Now you'll have to keep it.\nopt.left.say.1 [5983e6] = 你用最工整的字回了一份保证。现在你得兑现它了。\n# en: Let it slide\nopt.right.label [344baa] = 随它去\n# en: You put the note in a drawer. It doesn't stop existing in the drawer.\nopt.right.say.1 [7973f8] = 你把便条塞进抽屉。它在抽屉里也照样存在。\n\n@event job_term_review\n# en: Another Term?\ntitle [9e843b] = 再干一学期？\n# en: The term is over, and so is the rota for {track}. A fresh one goes up for next term, and there's a gap where your name usually goes. {~Nobody would blame you either way.|Your feet have an opinion. So does your purse.|Someone has already pencilled a question mark next to it.}\ntext [7bad94] = 学期结束了，{track}的排班表也到头了。下学期的新表会贴出来，你平常写名字的地方空着一格。{~怎么选都没人会怪你。|你的脚有意见。你的钱袋也有。|已经有人在旁边用铅笔画了个问号。}\n# en: A staff rota pinned to a door, last term's names crossed off, a blank column waiting for next term's.\nscene [2d74da] = 门上钉着一张员工排班表，上学期的名字都划掉了，空出一栏等着下学期。\n# en: Stay on next term\nopt.left.label [7e4424] = 下学期继续干\n# en: {~You write your name in the gap. Another term of shifts, another term of wages.|Your name goes back on the rota, in ink this time.}\nopt.left.say.1 [46037e] = {~你在空格里写上名字。又一学期的班，又一学期的工钱。|你的名字回到了排班表上，这次用的是墨水。}\n# en: Resign\nopt.right.label [b07941] = 辞职\n# en: {~You hand in your notice, politely. The evenings are yours again; the purse is lighter for it.|You leave the gap empty. You can always find work again, if you need to.}\nopt.right.say.1 [599e30] = {~你客客气气地递了辞呈。晚上又归你了；钱袋也因此轻了。|你让那一格空着。真需要的话，总还能再找活干。}\n\n@event track_promotion\n# en: A Step Up\ntitle [39f03f] = 更上一层\n# en: {track} wants to promote you. More responsibility, more respect, and in the right places, more pay.\ntext [a932cb] = {track}想提拔你。更多责任，更多尊重，在某些地方，还有更多薪水。\n# en: A small ceremony in a cramped room, someone pinning a badge to a student's collar while the others applaud.\nscene [c03b55] = 狭小房间里的一场小仪式，有人把徽章别在一名学生的领口上，其他人鼓掌。\n# en: Accept\nopt.left.label [b26149] = 接受\n# en: The badge is a little crooked. You don't straighten it.\nopt.left.say.1 [7c4086] = 徽章有点歪。你没去扶正它。\n# en: Not yet\nopt.right.label [8dd27c] = 还不是时候\n# en: \"When you're ready, then.\" They mean it. The offer will come again.\nopt.right.say.1 [ffa0ad] = “那就等你准备好。”他们是认真的。这个机会还会再来。\n\n@event intern_application\n# en: Applications Open\ntitle [e7df06] = 开始申请\n# en: Summer research posts are open. Six notices, six seals, six lists of requirements. Apply to as many as you like: the first letter is an evening's work, and every one after it costs another evening and the postage.\ntext [c4837d] = 夏季研究岗位开放申请了。六张告示，六枚火漆，六份要求清单。想申请几个都行：第一封信花你一个晚上，之后每多一封，就再搭上一个晚上和邮费。\n# en: A cluster of notices with wax seals pinned above all the others: summer research posts, each with its own demands.\nscene [a45b3c] = 一簇盖着火漆的告示钉在所有告示上方：夏季研究岗位，各有各的要求。\n# en: The Royal Library\nopt.royal.label [64f580] = 皇家图书馆\n# en: GPA 2.5, an interview on lore, a love of indexes.\nopt.royal.hint [fbd651] = GPA 2.5，学识面试，以及对索引的热爱。\n# en: {member(intern_royal_library): They've already asked you back.|Needs Astromancy II.}\nopt.royal.locked [c8e0b8] = {member(intern_royal_library): 他们已经请你回去了。|需要星象与占卜 II。}\n# en: {~Another letter, another evening, another stamp. Your hand aches in a very particular place.|You write it once, cross out half, write it again. The postage comes out of your own purse.}\nopt.royal.say.1 [69905a] = {~又一封信，又一个晚上，又一张邮票。你的手在一个非常特定的地方发酸。|你写了一遍，划掉一半，又写一遍。邮费从你自己的钱袋里出。}\n# en: You write the letter three times and send the best one. Probably the best one.\nopt.royal.say.2 [b67a70] = 信你写了三遍，寄出了最好的一封。大概是最好的一封。\n# en: The Conclave Laboratory\nopt.conclave.label [858e84] = 七塔议会实验室\n# en: GPA 3.0, an interview on arcana, and a word from Thorne helps.\nopt.conclave.hint [fd28c9] = GPA 3.0，奥术面试，有索恩的一句推荐会更好。\n# en: {member(intern_conclave): They've already asked you back.|Needs Evocation II.}\nopt.conclave.locked [fb2c57] = {member(intern_conclave): 他们已经请你回去了。|需要塑能术 II。}\n# en: {~Another letter, another evening, another stamp. Your hand aches in a very particular place.|You write it once, cross out half, write it again. The postage comes out of your own purse.}\nopt.conclave.say.1 [69905a] = {~又一封信，又一个晚上，又一张邮票。你的手在一个非常特定的地方发酸。|你写了一遍，划掉一半，又写一遍。邮费从你自己的钱袋里出。}\n# en: You write the letter three times and send the best one. Probably the best one.\nopt.conclave.say.2 [b67a70] = 信你写了三遍，寄出了最好的一封。大概是最好的一封。\n# en: The Guild of Alchemists' Works\nopt.alchemists.label [80cc20] = 炼金师公会工坊\n# en: GPA 2.0, an interview on alchemy, and a strong stomach.\nopt.alchemists.hint [2d4395] = GPA 2.0，炼金面试，还得有个好胃口。\n# en: {member(intern_alchemists): They've already asked you back.|Needs Alchemy II.}\nopt.alchemists.locked [e156fa] = {member(intern_alchemists): 他们已经请你回去了。|需要炼金与治愈 II。}\n# en: {~Another letter, another evening, another stamp. Your hand aches in a very particular place.|You write it once, cross out half, write it again. The postage comes out of your own purse.}\nopt.alchemists.say.1 [69905a] = {~又一封信，又一个晚上，又一张邮票。你的手在一个非常特定的地方发酸。|你写了一遍，划掉一半，又写一遍。邮费从你自己的钱袋里出。}\n# en: You write the letter three times and send the best one. Probably the best one.\nopt.alchemists.say.2 [b67a70] = 信你写了三遍，寄出了最好的一封。大概是最好的一封。\n# en: The Observatory Expedition\nopt.expedition.label [a8e59b] = 天文台远征队\n# en: An interview on vigor, and the legs for a plateau.\nopt.expedition.hint [6f139e] = 体魄面试，外加能爬上高原的双腿。\n# en: {member(intern_expedition): They've already asked you back.|Needs Astromancy II.}\nopt.expedition.locked [f35bc6] = {member(intern_expedition): 他们已经请你回去了。|需要星象与占卜 II。}\n# en: {~Another letter, another evening, another stamp. Your hand aches in a very particular place.|You write it once, cross out half, write it again. The postage comes out of your own purse.}\nopt.expedition.say.1 [69905a] = {~又一封信，又一个晚上，又一张邮票。你的手在一个非常特定的地方发酸。|你写了一遍，划掉一半，又写一遍。邮费从你自己的钱袋里出。}\n# en: You write the letter three times and send the best one. Probably the best one.\nopt.expedition.say.2 [b67a70] = 信你写了三遍，寄出了最好的一封。大概是最好的一封。\n# en: The Beastcraft Field Station\nopt.field.label [93e08b] = 驭兽野外站\n# en: An interview on vigor. Boots provided, mostly.\nopt.field.hint [c9d453] = 体魄面试。靴子基本上会发。\n# en: {member(intern_field_station): They've already asked you back.|Needs Beastcraft II.}\nopt.field.locked [5a1cba] = {member(intern_field_station): 他们已经请你回去了。|需要驭兽与飞行 II。}\n# en: {~Another letter, another evening, another stamp. Your hand aches in a very particular place.|You write it once, cross out half, write it again. The postage comes out of your own purse.}\nopt.field.say.1 [69905a] = {~又一封信，又一个晚上，又一张邮票。你的手在一个非常特定的地方发酸。|你写了一遍，划掉一半，又写一遍。邮费从你自己的钱袋里出。}\n# en: You write the letter three times and send the best one. Probably the best one.\nopt.field.say.2 [b67a70] = 信你写了三遍，寄出了最好的一封。大概是最好的一封。\n# en: The Thornwick Exchange\nopt.thornwick.label [eb19f0] = 荆木学院交换项目\n# en: GPA 2.5, an interview on charm. Bring a thick skin.\nopt.thornwick.hint [5c642d] = GPA 2.5，魅力面试。脸皮得厚一点。\n# en: {member(intern_thornwick): They've already asked you back.|Needs Illusion II.}\nopt.thornwick.locked [9eb17a] = {member(intern_thornwick): 他们已经请你回去了。|需要幻术与魅影 II。}\n# en: {~Another letter, another evening, another stamp. Your hand aches in a very particular place.|You write it once, cross out half, write it again. The postage comes out of your own purse.}\nopt.thornwick.say.1 [69905a] = {~又一封信，又一个晚上，又一张邮票。你的手在一个非常特定的地方发酸。|你写了一遍，划掉一半，又写一遍。邮费从你自己的钱袋里出。}\n# en: You write the letter three times and send the best one. Probably the best one.\nopt.thornwick.say.2 [b67a70] = 信你写了三遍，寄出了最好的一封。大概是最好的一封。\n# en: Not this year\nopt.none.label [594ecf] = 今年算了\n# en: You let the notices flap. There's always next year, until there isn't.\nopt.none.say.1 [cd2444] = 你任由告示在风里拍打。总还有明年——直到没有为止。\n\n@event intern_offer\n# en: Letters With Seals\ntitle [51ad68] = 盖着火漆的信\n# en: The spring post brings answers. {applied(internship): There are sealed letters on your desk. Which do you open first?|There are no more sealed letters.} {guile >= 35 and rel(isolde) >= 6 and not member(intern_unseen_office): There is also a plain grey envelope you never asked for.|}\ntext [7b4927] = 春天的邮件带来了回音。{applied(internship): 你的书桌上放着几封封了火漆的信。先拆哪一封？|再没有封着火漆的信了。}{guile >= 35 and rel(isolde) >= 6 and not member(intern_unseen_office): 还有一只你从没申请过的素灰色信封。|}\n# en: Heavy envelopes with wax seals on a dormitory desk, one of them plain grey with no seal at all.\nscene [28d6f5] = 宿舍书桌上放着几只封着火漆的厚信封，其中一只是素灰色的，根本没有封漆。\n# en: Letter from the Royal Library\nopt.royal.label [04415c] = 皇家图书馆的来信\n# en: They asked for a GPA of 2.5. The interview tests lore.\nopt.royal.hint [693412] = 他们要求 GPA 2.5。面试考学识。\n# en: \"We are pleased.\" You read the letter until the seal cracks. A summer in the oldest archives in the realm.\nopt.royal.say.1 [d43827] = “我们很高兴。”你把信读了一遍又一遍，直到火漆裂开。一整个夏天，待在王国最古老的档案馆里。\n# en: \"We regret.\" The interview went well, they write, and not quite well enough.\nopt.royal.say.2 [4db135] = “我们很遗憾。”信上说，你的面试表现不错，只是还差一点。\n# en: \"We regret.\" They wanted a GPA of 2.5, and they say so twice.\nopt.royal.say.3 [ee2e12] = “我们很遗憾。”他们要的是 GPA 2.5，而且说了两遍。\n# en: Letter from the Conclave\nopt.conclave.label [187867] = 七塔议会的来信\n# en: They asked for a GPA of 3.0. The interview tests arcana. A word from Thorne would help.\nopt.conclave.hint [e87947] = 他们要求 GPA 3.0。面试考奥术。有索恩的一句推荐会更好。\n# en: \"We are pleased.\" You read the letter until the seal cracks. A summer in spell research under the Seven Towers.\nopt.conclave.say.1 [b0dd08] = “我们很高兴。”你把信读了一遍又一遍，直到火漆裂开。一整个夏天，在七塔之下研究法术。\n# en: \"We regret.\" The interview went well, they write, and not quite well enough.\nopt.conclave.say.2 [4db135] = “我们很遗憾。”信上说，你的面试表现不错，只是还差一点。\n# en: \"We are pleased.\" You read the letter until the seal cracks. A summer in spell research under the Seven Towers.\nopt.conclave.say.3 [b0dd08] = “我们很高兴。”你把信读了一遍又一遍，直到火漆裂开。一整个夏天，在七塔之下研究法术。\n# en: \"We regret.\" The interview went well, they write, and not quite well enough.\nopt.conclave.say.4 [4db135] = “我们很遗憾。”信上说，你的面试表现不错，只是还差一点。\n# en: \"We regret.\" They wanted a GPA of 3.0, and they say so twice.\nopt.conclave.say.5 [fa762a] = “我们很遗憾。”他们要的是 GPA 3.0，而且说了两遍。\n# en: Letter from the Alchemists' Works\nopt.alchemists.label [3ad0f0] = 炼金师工坊的来信\n# en: They asked for a GPA of 2.0. The interview tests alchemy.\nopt.alchemists.hint [0b2080] = 他们要求 GPA 2.0。面试考炼金。\n# en: \"We are pleased.\" You read the letter until the seal cracks. A summer in the great vats of the Guild.\nopt.alchemists.say.1 [d15c64] = “我们很高兴。”你把信读了一遍又一遍，直到火漆裂开。一整个夏天，守着公会的大缸。\n# en: \"We regret.\" The interview went well, they write, and not quite well enough.\nopt.alchemists.say.2 [4db135] = “我们很遗憾。”信上说，你的面试表现不错，只是还差一点。\n# en: \"We regret.\" They wanted a GPA of 2.0, and they say so twice.\nopt.alchemists.say.3 [1f5965] = “我们很遗憾。”他们要的是 GPA 2.0，而且说了两遍。\n# en: Letter from the Expedition\nopt.expedition.label [ca58f7] = 远征队的来信\n# en: The interview tests vigor.\nopt.expedition.hint [0bd21d] = 面试考体魄。\n# en: \"We are pleased.\" You read the letter until the seal cracks. A summer of sky charts on the high plateau.\nopt.expedition.say.1 [9d271b] = “我们很高兴。”你把信读了一遍又一遍，直到火漆裂开。一整个夏天，在高原上绘制星图。\n# en: \"We regret.\" The interview went well, they write, and not quite well enough.\nopt.expedition.say.2 [4db135] = “我们很遗憾。”信上说，你的面试表现不错，只是还差一点。\n# en: Letter from the Field Station\nopt.field.label [b25fe8] = 野外站的来信\n# en: The interview tests vigor.\nopt.field.hint [0bd21d] = 面试考体魄。\n# en: \"We are pleased.\" You read the letter until the seal cracks. A summer in the moors, the heather and the creatures in them.\nopt.field.say.1 [27ceb5] = “我们很高兴。”你把信读了一遍又一遍，直到火漆裂开。一整个夏天，在荒原和石楠丛里，与那里的生灵为伴。\n# en: \"We regret.\" The interview went well, they write, and not quite well enough.\nopt.field.say.2 [4db135] = “我们很遗憾。”信上说，你的面试表现不错，只是还差一点。\n# en: Letter from Thornwick\nopt.thornwick.label [c344dc] = 荆木学院的来信\n# en: They asked for a GPA of 2.5. The interview tests charm.\nopt.thornwick.hint [5c32c3] = 他们要求 GPA 2.5。面试考魅力。\n# en: \"We are pleased.\" You read the letter until the seal cracks. A summer in the rival academy's illusion laboratory.\nopt.thornwick.say.1 [64ec58] = “我们很高兴。”你把信读了一遍又一遍，直到火漆裂开。一整个夏天，待在对手学院的幻术实验室里。\n# en: \"We regret.\" The interview went well, they write, and not quite well enough.\nopt.thornwick.say.2 [4db135] = “我们很遗憾。”信上说，你的面试表现不错，只是还差一点。\n# en: \"We regret.\" They wanted a GPA of 2.5, and they say so twice.\nopt.thornwick.say.3 [ee2e12] = “我们很遗憾。”他们要的是 GPA 2.5，而且说了两遍。\n# en: Open the plain envelope\nopt.unseen.label [ca484a] = 拆开那只素信封\n# en: Inside: a time, an address and no signature. The paper is blank again by morning. You go anyway.\nopt.unseen.say.1 [702952] = 里面写着一个时间、一个地址，没有署名。到第二天早上，纸又变成了白纸。你还是去了。\n# en: Fold the letters away\nopt.decline.label [15c522] = 把信都收起来\n# en: You fold them carefully. Next year, perhaps.\nopt.decline.say.1 [b0ac8d] = 你把信仔细折好。也许明年吧。\n\n@event intern_asked_back\n# en: Asked Back\ntitle [8aa9d6] = 再次相邀\n# en: A letter from {member(intern_royal_library): the Royal Library Archives|{member(intern_conclave): the Conclave Laboratory|{member(intern_alchemists): the Guild of Alchemists' Works|{member(intern_expedition): the Observatory Expedition|{member(intern_field_station): the Beastcraft Field Station|{member(intern_thornwick): the Thornwick Exchange|the office with no name}}}}}}. They were pleased with your summer, and the post is yours again next summer if you want it.\ntext [92bde8] = 一封来自{member(intern_royal_library): 皇家图书馆档案馆|{member(intern_conclave): 七塔议会实验室|{member(intern_alchemists): 炼金师公会工坊|{member(intern_expedition): 观星远征队|{member(intern_field_station): 驭兽术野外站|{member(intern_thornwick): 荆木学院交换项目|那间没有名字的办公室}}}}}}的信。他们对你这个夏天很满意，只要你愿意，明年夏天这个岗位还是你的。\n# en: A letter on good paper with a familiar seal, the words \"next summer\" underlined once, neatly.\nscene [522edb] = 一封用上好纸张写的信，盖着熟悉的火漆，“明年夏天”几个字下面工整地划了一道线。\n# en: Write back yes\nopt.left.label [622f58] = 回信答应\n# en: {~You write back the same evening. Next summer is spoken for, and you find you're glad.|\"Yes,\" you write, and then three more lines to make it sound less eager.}\nopt.left.say.1 [71669f] = {~你当晚就回了信。明年夏天有着落了，你发现自己挺高兴。|你写下“好的”，又添了三行，好让自己显得没那么迫不及待。}\n# en: Thank them, and decline\nopt.right.label [1b8626] = 致谢，婉拒\n# en: {~You thank them warmly and say no. Next summer is yours to spend, or to apply for something new.|A polite no. You keep the letter anyway; it's good to be asked.}\nopt.right.say.1 [a75a4d] = {~你热情地道谢，然后说不。明年夏天由你自己安排，或者去申请些新的。|一句礼貌的拒绝。信你还是留着了；被人邀请总是好事。}\n\n@event thesis_setup\n# en: Choosing a Thesis\ntitle [ed9c10] = 选定论文\n# en: The final year opens with a thesis. Before you choose this term's courses, Professor Thorne lays out three topics, at three levels of ambition.\ntext [c76290] = 最后一年以毕业论文开场。在你选定本学期的课程之前，索恩教授摆出了三个题目，野心各不相同。\n# en: A professor's study, three topic proposals laid out on the desk like cards.\nscene [82a455] = 教授的书房，三份选题提案像纸牌一样摊在桌上。\n# en: A careful survey\nopt.safe.label [d4d296] = 一篇稳妥的综述\n# en: Easier, and it will read as easier.\nopt.safe.hint [8d81c3] = 更容易，读起来也会显得更容易。\n# en: A sensible topic. Thorne says \"Sensible,\" the way other people say \"Disappointing.\"\nopt.safe.say.1 [5a3672] = 一个明智的题目。索恩说“明智”的语气，就像别人说“失望”。\n# en: An original study\nopt.solid.label [0d9f34] = 一项原创研究\n# en: The usual path.\nopt.solid.hint [8adec9] = 寻常的路。\n# en: Thorne nods. \"Good. Bring me something I don't already know.\"\nopt.solid.say.1 [9feb76] = 索恩点点头。“好。给我带来点我还不知道的东西。”\n# en: Something no one has done\nopt.ambitious.label [326da2] = 前无古人的课题\n# en: Harder, and worth more.\nopt.ambitious.hint [83c12d] = 更难，也更有分量。\n# en: Thorne is quiet for a long time. \"Hm,\" he says, and for once it sounds like hope.\nopt.ambitious.say.1 [18b887] = 索恩沉默了很久。“嗯，”他说，这一次听起来竟像是希望。\n\n@event thesis_crunch\n# en: The Crunch\ntitle [5ad669] = 最后冲刺\n# en: The defence is next month and your thesis is not ready. Your advisor's note is three words long: \"Well? Final draft.\"\ntext [db90b3] = 答辩就在下个月，你的论文还没写完。导师的便条只有短短一句：\"如何？交终稿。\"\n# en: A thesis carrel at dawn, a candle burned to a stub, drafts on the floor and a student asleep on the keyboard of a typing-quill.\nscene [d2cdf9] = 黎明时分的论文隔间，蜡烛燃成了残根，草稿散落一地，一名学生趴在打字羽毛笔的键盘上睡着了。\n# en: Work through the nights\nopt.left.label [ec1e9d] = 通宵赶工\n# en: You stop seeing daylight. The draft gets better and you get worse, which seems to be how this works.\nopt.left.say.1 [2f566c] = 你再也见不到日光。稿子越改越好，你却越来越糟，事情大概就是这样。\n# en: Hand in what you have\nopt.right.label [648c9a] = 有多少交多少\n# en: You tidy what's there and accept the gaps. Your advisor sighs, which is not the same as no.\nopt.right.say.1 [5cc846] = 你把现有的整理好，接受那些空缺。导师叹了口气——叹气不等于拒绝。\n\n@event thesis_defence\n# en: The Defence\ntitle [3a2f48] = 答辩\n# en: Three examiners, one lectern, and a year of work. {progress(thesis) >= 60: You know this material better than anyone in the room.|There are gaps, and you know where they are.}\ntext [b6b3ea] = 三位考官，一座讲台，一年的心血。{progress(thesis) >= 60: 这些内容你比屋里任何人都熟。|有漏洞，而且你知道它们在哪儿。}\n# en: A semicircular table with three examiners, a student standing at a lectern with a stack of notes.\nscene [f8606f] = 半圆形的长桌后坐着三位考官，一名学生站在讲台前，手里一摞笔记。\n# en: Defend it\nopt.left.label [0249ce] = 为它辩护\n# en: A safe topic is easier to defend and earns less; an ambitious one is harder and earns more.\nopt.left.hint [c8bbcf] = 稳妥的题目更好答辩，回报也更少；大胆的题目更难，回报也更多。\n# en: They stop asking questions and start asking for copies. Distinction.\nopt.left.say.1 [e3c797] = 他们不再提问，开始讨要副本。优等。\n# en: A pass, with one examiner's handwriting all over your conclusion.\nopt.left.say.2 [4ad614] = 通过了，只是结论部分写满了某位考官的批注。\n# en: They send you away to resubmit. It isn't the end. It feels like it.\nopt.left.say.3 [b2d4d0] = 他们让你回去重交。这不是终点。只是感觉像。\n# en: A careful survey, carefully defended. They give you a distinction and the faint air of people who wished you'd risked more.\nopt.left.say.4 [1b21e8] = 一份严谨的综述，答辩也严谨。他们给了你优等，神情里却隐约有些遗憾，好像希望你更冒险一点。\n# en: A pass. Nobody argues with a careful survey. Nobody remembers it either.\nopt.left.say.5 [19a20f] = 通过。没人会反驳一份严谨的综述，也没人会记住它。\n# en: Even a safe topic has its gaps, and they find every one. Resubmit.\nopt.left.say.6 [c336c1] = 稳妥的题目也有漏洞，他们一个不落地全找了出来。重交。\n# en: Something no one has done, and now someone has. The committee stands when you finish. One of them is crying, and pretending not to.\nopt.left.say.7 [7a1d74] = 前人从未做过的事，如今有人做到了。你讲完时，答辩委员们起立。其中一位在流泪，还假装没有。\n# en: They pass it, arguing among themselves about whether it is brilliant or mad. Possibly both.\nopt.left.say.8 [4c25e3] = 他们给了通过，彼此争论这究竟是天才还是疯狂。也许两者都是。\n# en: You reached too far, and they tell you so, at length. Resubmit. The idea was good. The proof wasn't there yet.\nopt.left.say.9 [6f1c5b] = 你走得太远了，他们这样告诉你，说了很久。重交。想法是好的，只是证明还没到位。\n\n@event lantern_night_v3\n# en: Lantern Night\ntitle [0b1f19] = 灯笼夜\n# en: Lantern Night. The whole academy gathers on the lakeshore with paper lanterns, each carrying a wish out over the water. {month == 2 and year == 1: Your first one.|Another year, another wish.}\ntext [b3014a] = 灯笼夜。全院的人带着纸灯笼聚在湖岸，每盏灯都载着一个愿望飘向水面。{month == 2 and year == 1: 这是你的第一次。|又一年，又一个愿望。}\n# en: The Mirror Lake at night, hundreds of paper lanterns drifting over the water, students crowding the shore.\nscene [cd9b4c] = 夜晚的镜湖，数百盏纸灯笼在水面上漂，学生们挤满湖岸。\n# en: Wish for something selfish\nopt.left.label [7e0393] = 许一个自私的愿望\n# en: Your lantern goes out over the water and keeps its secret. You feel lighter for having asked.\nopt.left.say.1 [0210ff] = 你的灯笼飘向湖面，守着它的秘密。问出口之后，你觉得轻松了些。\n# en: Wish for someone else\nopt.right.label [bc6686] = 为别人许愿\n# en: You don't tell anyone who. The lantern seems to know; it flies straight.\nopt.right.say.1 [00b286] = 你没告诉任何人是谁。灯笼似乎知道，它飞得笔直。\n\n@event year_four\n# en: The Final Year\ntitle [a30775] = 最后一年\n# en: The barge again, the towers again, and a thesis waiting. The first-years on the deck look at you the way you once looked at the fourth-years. At the opening feast the headmistress says, \"Fourth years, this is your last year in these halls. What you become here, you will be for a long time.\" She looks, for a moment, directly at you.\ntext [cb5447] = 又是驳船，又是高塔，还有一篇论文在等着。甲板上的一年级生看你的眼神，就像你当年看四年级生那样。开学宴上，院长说：“四年级的同学们，这是你们在这些厅堂里的最后一年。你们在这里成为什么样的人，就会长久地是那样的人。”有那么一刻，她直直地看着你。\n# en: The seven towers at dawn from the barge, the same view as the first day, but the student watching it has changed.\nscene [542188] = 从驳船上望见的黎明七塔，和第一天的景色一样，但看风景的学生已经变了。\n# en: Here we go\nopt.ok.label [e1aa95] = 走吧\n# en: You know which steps creak. You know which doors stick. One more year to learn the rest.\nopt.ok.say.1 [e1f039] = 你知道哪级台阶会响，哪扇门会卡。还有一年，去学剩下的。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/calendar.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/calendar.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event intro_arrival\n# en: The Sky-Barge\ntitle [59d00d] = 天空驳船\n# en: The barge drops through the clouds and there it is: Lumenhold, seven towers stitched together with bridges of light. A first-year beside you is quietly being sick over the side. A prefect with a ledger counts heads and frowns at yours.\ntext [402ca7] = 驳船穿过云层下降，它就在那里：流明堡，七座高塔由光之桥缝合在一起。你身旁一个一年级新生正悄悄趴在船舷边呕吐。一位拿着名册的级长在清点人头，数到你时皱了皱眉。\n# en: A wooden barge with patched sails drifting between clouds toward seven impossibly tall towers above a mirror-still lake.\nscene [45ee12] = 一艘打着补丁的木帆驳船在云间漂浮，驶向矗立在镜面般湖泊之上、高得不可思议的七座塔楼。\n# en: Step onto the landing\nopt.ok.label [2c37cd] = 踏上码头\n# en: Your boots touch stone that hums faintly underfoot. Four years. Forty-eight moons.\nopt.ok.say.1 [c9a4d2] = 你的靴子踩上了石板，脚下微微嗡鸣。四年。四十八轮月。\n\n@event choose_origin\n# en: Who Were You Before?\ntitle [3aa3fb] = 来这里之前，你是谁？\n# en: The Sorting Mirror in the entrance hall does not sort you into anything. It simply asks, in a voice like cold water, who you were before you came here. It waits.\ntext [408653] = 门厅里的“分院镜”并不会把你分到哪里去。它只是用冷水般的声音问你：来这里之前，你是谁？然后，它静静等待。\n# en: A tall enchanted mirror in the entrance hall, showing a different version of the viewer in each pane.\nscene [c49f90] = 门厅里一面高大的魔法镜，每一格镜面都映出观者不同的模样。\n# en: The younger child of a minor noble house\nopt.noble.label [53c694] = 小贵族家的小儿女\n# en: Gold, charm and a name people recognise.\nopt.noble.hint [61894b] = 金币、魅力，和一个人人认得的姓氏。\n# en: The mirror shows you a crest you have tried to outgrow. It approves, faintly.\nopt.noble.say.1 [5615e9] = 镜中出现一枚你一直想摆脱的家徽。它微微表示认可。\n# en: A scholarship student from the provinces\nopt.scholar.label [4afbdf] = 来自外省的奖学金学生\n# en: Lore and study. The stipend depends on good marks.\nopt.scholar.hint [b8b070] = 学识与学业。津贴取决于成绩。\n# en: The mirror shows you a candle burning very late in a very small room.\nopt.scholar.say.1 [e182c1] = 镜中出现一间很小的屋子，一支蜡烛燃到很晚很晚。\n# en: Raised by a hedge-witch in the marshes\nopt.hedge.label [8a7115] = 由沼泽里的乡野女巫养大\n# en: Alchemy, good spirits, an eye for herbs.\nopt.hedge.hint [3c3a98] = 炼金、好心情，还有一双识草药的眼睛。\n# en: The mirror smells, briefly, of wet moss and woodsmoke. You feel at home.\nopt.hedge.say.1 [734ca9] = 镜子里一瞬间飘出湿苔藓和柴火烟的气味。你觉得像回到了家。\n# en: Raised on the streets of Port Calder\nopt.street.label [5f489c] = 在考尔德港的街头长大\n# en: Guile and grit. Little gold.\nopt.street.hint [fefffc] = 诡计与韧劲。金币很少。\n# en: The mirror shows a pickpocket's quick hands. You check your pockets out of habit.\nopt.street.say.1 [dd71c2] = 镜中出现一双扒手的快手。你习惯性地摸了摸口袋。\n# en: The heir to a trading company\nopt.merchant.label [2fc5e4] = 一家商行的继承人\n# en: Plenty of gold, a nose for bargains.\nopt.merchant.hint [026706] = 金币充裕，对买卖嗅觉灵敏。\n# en: The mirror shows ledgers, ships and your mother's disappointed face. Still: gold.\nopt.merchant.say.1 [604ea4] = 镜中出现账簿、货船，还有你母亲失望的脸。不过：钱是真的多。\n\n@event sorting_mirror\n# en: The Sorting Mirror\ntitle [6d3a7b] = 分院镜\n# en: The Sorting Mirror in the entrance hall does not sort you into anything. It simply asks, in a voice like cold water, who you were before you came here. You tell it.\ntext [43f613] = 门厅里的“分院镜”并不会把你分到哪里去。它只是用冷水般的声音问你：来这里之前，你是谁？你告诉了它。\n# en: A tall enchanted mirror in the entrance hall, showing a different version of the viewer in each pane.\nscene [c49f90] = 门厅里一面高大的魔法镜，每一格镜面都映出观者不同的模样。\n# en: Look into the mirror\nopt.ok.label [bb5682] = 望向镜中\n# en: {has(origin_noble): The mirror shows you a crest you have tried to outgrow. It approves, faintly.|{has(origin_scholar): The mirror shows you a candle burning very late in a very small room.|{has(origin_hedge): The mirror smells, briefly, of wet moss and woodsmoke. You feel at home.|{has(origin_street): The mirror shows a pickpocket's quick hands. You check your pockets out of habit.|The mirror shows ledgers, ships and your mother's disappointed face. Still: gold.}}}}\nopt.ok.say.1 [8c73e5] = {has(origin_noble): 镜中出现一枚你一直想摆脱的家徽。它微微表示认可。|{has(origin_scholar): 镜中出现一间很小的屋子，一支蜡烛燃到很晚很晚。|{has(origin_hedge): 镜子里一瞬间飘出湿苔藓和柴火烟的气味。你觉得像回到了家。|{has(origin_street): 镜中出现一双扒手的快手。你习惯性地摸了摸口袋。|镜中出现账簿、货船，还有你母亲失望的脸。不过：钱是真的多。}}}}\n\n@event wrapup_begins\n# en: The Last Weeks\ntitle [0438f9] = 最后的几周\n# en: A notice on the great hall door: classes are over, duties are done, and final-years may stay on until graduation, board on the house. The castle is yours for one last summer.\ntext [172a68] = 大礼堂门上贴着一张告示：课程结束，职责已了，毕业生可以留到毕业典礼，食宿全免。最后一个夏天，城堡属于你。\n# en: An emptying castle in late summer light, trunks stacked in a corridor, a notice pinned to the great hall door.\nscene [1cbaa4] = 夏末的阳光里，城堡渐渐空了，走廊里堆着行李箱，大礼堂门上钉着一张告示。\n# en: Make the most of it\nopt.ok.label [793c29] = 好好享受\n# en: You walk the long way to breakfast, past every door you ever got lost behind.\nopt.ok.say.1 [725c27] = 你绕远路去吃早饭，经过每一扇你曾在后面迷过路的门。\n\n@event choose_elective\n# en: Choosing an Elective\ntitle [03e0dd] = 选择选修课\n# en: The registrar's noticeboard flaps with sign-up sheets for this term's electives. Core lectures are compulsory; your elective is yours to choose. The sheets jostle for your attention.\ntext [6ed4b3] = 教务处的告示板上，本学期选修课的报名表哗哗作响。核心课程是必修的；选修课由你自己挑。报名表们推推搡搡，争着吸引你的注意。\n# en: A long noticeboard covered in fluttering enchanted parchment, each sheet trying to shoulder the others aside.\nscene [803e43] = 一块长长的告示板，贴满了飘动的魔法羊皮纸，每一张都想用肩膀把别的挤开。\n# en: Pyromancy\nopt.pyro.label [22dcef] = 火焰术\n# en: +Arcana each month, small burns. Taught by Professor Thorne.\nopt.pyro.hint [cea401] = 每月 +奥术，偶有小烧伤。由索恩教授执教。\n# en: Tidecalling\nopt.tide.label [43908b] = 唤潮术\n# en: +Arcana, +Vigor, lakeside calm.\nopt.tide.hint [a0ea17] = +奥术，+体魄，湖畔的宁静。\n# en: Illusion & Glamour\nopt.illusion.label [5cdf61] = 幻术与魅影\n# en: +Guile, +Charm.\nopt.illusion.hint [21f570] = +诡计，+魅力。\n# en: Practical Alchemy\nopt.alchemy.label [990d4f] = 实用炼金\n# en: +Alchemy.\nopt.alchemy.hint [f7922d] = +炼金。\n# en: Divination\nopt.divination.label [61fc6c] = 占卜学\n# en: +Lore, occasional gloomy omens.\nopt.divination.hint [a946bb] = +学识，偶尔会有阴郁的预兆。\n# en: Artifice\nopt.artifice.label [de3328] = 奇械术\n# en: +Alchemy, +Lore.\nopt.artifice.hint [5dba10] = +炼金，+学识。\n# en: Beastcraft\nopt.beast.label [573a56] = 驭兽学\n# en: +Vigor, +Mood. Smells of hay.\nopt.beast.hint [2aa6d4] = +体魄，+心情。一股干草味。\n# en: Astromancy\nopt.astro.label [65a198] = 星象学\n# en: +Lore, late nights.\nopt.astro.hint [dc29a9] = +学识，经常熬夜。\n# en: Healing Arts\nopt.healing.label [e8c0bd] = 治愈术\n# en: +Alchemy, +Lore, a little health.\nopt.healing.hint [eab2fb] = +炼金，+学识，少量健康。\n# en: Umbral Theory (restricted)\nopt.umbral.label [430c05] = 暗影理论（受限）\n# en: Requires Lore 20 or second year. +Arcana, dark moods.\nopt.umbral.hint [6e81e4] = 需要学识 20 或二年级。+奥术，心情阴沉。\n# en: Needs Lore 20, or your second year.\nopt.umbral.locked [cd9acd] = 需要学识 20，或进入第二年。\n\n@event club_fair\n# en: The Club Fair\ntitle [ce4fee] = 社团招新会\n# en: The courtyard is a riot of banners. Every club wants you, or at least your membership fee. A broom whizzes overhead trailing a SKYRACE TRYOUTS banner; somewhere a choir is harmonising with a cat.\ntext [2193b8] = 庭院里旗帜招展，热闹非凡。每个社团都想要你——至少想要你的会费。一把飞帚从头顶呼啸而过，拖着“天空竞速队招新”的横幅；不知哪里，一支合唱团正在和一只猫合唱。\n# en: The great courtyard filled with banners and stalls: a duelist juggling sparks, a choir floating a foot off the ground, a girl handing out newspapers that shout headlines.\nscene [60c086] = 宏伟的庭院里满是旗帜和摊位：一名决斗者在抛接火花，一支合唱团悬浮在离地一尺的空中，一个女孩在分发会喊出头条的报纸。\n# en: Dueling Circle\nopt.duel.label [db424f] = 决斗社\n# en: +Vigor +Arcana, bruises. Home of the Spring Tournament.\nopt.duel.hint [72c44c] = +体魄 +奥术，满身淤青。春季锦标赛的大本营。\n# en: Choir of the Spheres\nopt.choir.label [41a5f0] = 天球合唱团\n# en: +Charm, +Mood.\nopt.choir.hint [ffc919] = +魅力，+心情。\n# en: Skyrace Squad\nopt.sky.label [b4b285] = 天空竞速队\n# en: +Vigor, crowd glory, crashes.\nopt.sky.hint [c4c7f0] = +体魄，观众的欢呼，以及坠机。\n# en: The Lumen Ledger\nopt.ledger.label [2606ae] = 《流明纪事报》社\n# en: +Guile +Charm. Nessa Quill's student paper.\nopt.ledger.hint [d5542a] = +诡计 +魅力。内莎·奎尔的学生报。\n# en: Verdant Circle\nopt.garden.label [7ac3fd] = 青翠社\n# en: +Alchemy +Mood. Greenhouses and gossip.\nopt.garden.hint [8c86f5] = +炼金 +心情。温室与八卦。\n# en: Stargazers\nopt.stars.label [44dc13] = 观星社\n# en: +Lore +Mood.\nopt.stars.hint [60f956] = +学识 +心情。\n# en: Keep your evenings free\nopt.none.label [418ad6] = 晚上留给自己\n# en: No club (leaves your current club, if any).\nopt.none.hint [6ad50e] = 不参加社团（若已有社团则退出）。\n\n@event job_board\n# en: The Job Board\ntitle [377c1b] = 招工告示板\n# en: Rent is ten crowns a month and your purse is a fragile thing. The board outside the bursar's office offers honest work, and some that is merely work.\ntext [185fc4] = 房租每月十克朗，而你的钱袋是个脆弱的东西。财务处门外的告示板上，有正经的工作，也有仅仅算是工作的工作。\n# en: A cork board outside the bursar's office, papered with notices; one is on fire, one is wriggling.\nscene [bbe568] = 财务处门外的一块软木告示板，贴满了启事；其中一张着了火，另一张在扭动。\n# en: Library Assistant — 10 gold/month\nopt.library.label [dece88] = 图书馆助理——每月 10 金币\n# en: +Lore, a little dull.\nopt.library.hint [1434fc] = +学识，有点无聊。\n# en: The Gilded Newt — 16 gold/month\nopt.tavern.label [13979f] = 镀金蝾螈酒馆——每月 16 金币\n# en: +Charm, tiring evenings in the village.\nopt.tavern.hint [8090e3] = +魅力，镇上累人的夜班。\n# en: Apothecary Clerk — 12 gold/month\nopt.apothecary.label [f748e6] = 药铺店员——每月 12 金币\n# en: Requires Alchemy 12. +Alchemy.\nopt.apothecary.hint [0e1e4f] = 需要炼金 12。+炼金。\n# en: Needs Alchemy 12.\nopt.apothecary.locked [784af2] = 需要炼金 12。\n# en: Familiar Stables — 11 gold/month\nopt.stables.label [8ccd8f] = 魔宠马厩——每月 11 金币\n# en: +Vigor. Things bite.\nopt.stables.hint [cf864a] = +体魄。会被咬。\n# en: First-Year Tutor — 12 gold/month\nopt.tutor.label [7403eb] = 一年级辅导员——每月 12 金币\n# en: Requires Lore 18. +Standing.\nopt.tutor.hint [9594b7] = 需要学识 18。+学业。\n# en: Needs Lore 18.\nopt.tutor.locked [ce2e73] = 需要学识 18。\n# en: Broom Courier — 18 gold/month\nopt.courier.label [142261] = 飞帚信使——每月 18 金币\n# en: Requires Vigor 14. Dangerous.\nopt.courier.hint [981b47] = 需要体魄 14。很危险。\n# en: Needs Vigor 14.\nopt.courier.locked [1f61e8] = 需要体魄 14。\n# en: No job (quit any current one)\nopt.none.label [7a08f4] = 不打工（辞去现有工作）\n\n@event lantern_night\n# en: Lantern Night\ntitle [0b1f19] = 灯笼夜\n# en: On Lantern Night every student writes a wish on a paper lantern and lets it go over the lake. Rumour says the lanterns that sink were wishes the lake wanted for itself.\ntext [608e29] = 灯笼夜里，每个学生都会在纸灯笼上写下一个愿望，让它飘过湖面。传说那些沉下去的灯笼，是湖想要据为己有的愿望。\n# en: Hundreds of paper lanterns rising off the lake at dusk, each with a wish written on it in glowing ink.\nscene [3a7795] = 黄昏时分，数百只纸灯笼从湖面升起，每一只上都用发光的墨水写着一个愿望。\n# en: Wish for something selfish\nopt.left.label [7e0393] = 许一个自私的愿望\n# en: Your lantern rises high. The next morning you find coins in your shoe. The lake is generous, and a little creepy.\nopt.left.say.1 [2f05e5] = 你的灯笼升得很高。第二天早上，你在鞋子里发现了硬币。湖很大方，也有点吓人。\n# en: Your lantern sinks with a hiss. Something under the water now knows what you want.\nopt.left.say.2 [f9d757] = 你的灯笼嘶的一声沉了下去。水下有什么东西，现在知道你想要什么了。\n# en: Wish for someone else\nopt.right.label [bc6686] = 为别人许愿\n# en: Your lantern rises and catches the wind. Across the shore, someone you have never met laughs out loud for no reason.\nopt.right.say.1 [002a2f] = 你的灯笼升起来，乘上了风。湖岸对面，一个你从未见过的人无缘无故地放声大笑。\n\n@event kael_arrives\n# en: The Boy From the North\ntitle [c3bb2e] = 来自北方的少年\n# en: Mid-term, the dining hall doors open and frost crawls across the floor. A boy in a fur coat walks in, takes the empty seat beside you, and says nothing at all. The headmistress introduces him as Kael Dumont, a transfer. She does not say from where.\ntext [fde128] = 学期过半，餐厅的大门打开，寒霜爬过地板。一个穿毛皮大衣的少年走进来，坐在你身旁的空位上，一言不发。院长介绍说他叫凯尔·杜蒙，是转学生。她没说他从哪里来。\n# en: A pale young man in a fur-lined coat standing at the door of the dining hall, frost spreading from his boots across the floor.\nscene [f60e23] = 一个面色苍白的年轻人身穿毛皮衬里的大衣站在餐厅门口，霜花从他的靴底向地板蔓延。\n# en: Leave him be\nopt.left.label [ce4663] = 别打扰他\n# en: You eat in silence. When you leave, your cup of tea has frozen solid.\nopt.left.say.1 [930b83] = 你们沉默地吃完了饭。你离开时，你那杯茶已经冻成了冰坨。\n# en: Offer him the bread\nopt.right.label [494eb6] = 把面包递给他\n# en: He looks at the bread as if no one has offered him anything in a long time. \"...Thank you.\" His voice sounds like wind over snow.\nopt.right.say.1 [6aa70f] = 他看着面包，仿佛很久没人给过他任何东西。“……谢谢。”他的声音像掠过雪原的风。\n\n@event winter_trials\n# en: The Winter Trials\ntitle [01bc52] = 冬季考核\n# en: The Winter Trials. A hundred desks, a hundred floating quills, one very old proctor who may be asleep. The first question asks you to explain the Third Law of Correspondence. The second asks you to demonstrate it.\ntext [f596dd] = 冬季考核。一百张课桌，一百支悬浮的羽毛笔，一位很老很老、可能已经睡着的监考官。第一题要你解释“第三对应定律”。第二题要你当场演示。\n# en: A vast examination hall where floating quills hover over a hundred desks and the windows are thick with frost.\nscene [f46856] = 宽阔的考场里，悬浮的羽毛笔停在一百张课桌上方，窗户上结着厚厚的霜。\n# en: Lean on theory\nopt.left.label [f71caf] = 靠理论取胜\n# en: Your answer is so thorough the quill gets a cramp. Top marks.\nopt.left.say.1 [eb0c7e] = 你的答案详尽到羽毛笔都抽筋了。满分。\n# en: You write three pages and realise halfway that you have been answering a different question.\nopt.left.say.2 [6df893] = 你写了三页，写到一半才发现自己答的是另一道题。\n# en: Lean on practice\nopt.right.label [e24f03] = 靠实践取胜\n# en: Your demonstration is clean and bright. The proctor wakes up and applauds.\nopt.right.say.1 [5963aa] = 你的演示干净利落、光彩夺目。监考官醒过来，鼓起了掌。\n# en: The demonstration backfires. Politely, but with smoke.\nopt.right.say.2 [5c503b] = 演示失败了。很有礼貌地失败了，但冒了烟。\n\n@event midwinter_ball\n# en: The Midwinter Ball\ntitle [81d1d0] = 仲冬舞会\n# en: The great hall has been turned to ice and starlight for the Midwinter Ball. Couples float above the floor. Who will you go with? You can only ask someone you know well.\ntext [e4d78e] = 为了仲冬舞会，大礼堂化作了冰雪与星光的世界。一对对舞伴悬浮在地板上方起舞。你要和谁一起去？只能邀请你熟悉的人。\n# en: The great hall transformed: an ice-crystal chandelier, couples waltzing a foot above the floor, snow falling indoors without ever landing.\nscene [84cb48] = 焕然一新的大礼堂：冰晶吊灯，离地一尺旋转华尔兹的舞伴，室内飘着永不落地的雪。\n# en: Ask Lyra\nopt.lyra.label [b5812c] = 邀请莱拉\n# en: She has had six invitations. She hasn't said yes to any.\nopt.lyra.hint [5d0f3a] = 她收到了六份邀请，一份也没答应。\n# en: \"Finally, someone who doesn't stammer.\" She dances like a duel — and wins.\nopt.lyra.say.1 [c2dd15] = “总算来了个不结巴的。”她跳舞像决斗一样——而且赢了。\n# en: \"I'm going alone. It's more efficient.\" You watch her dance alone all night, perfectly.\nopt.lyra.say.2 [ba4ab4] = “我一个人去。效率更高。”你看着她独自跳了一整晚，完美无缺。\n# en: \"Finally, someone who doesn't stammer.\" She dances like a duel — and wins.\nopt.lyra.say.3 [c2dd15] = “总算来了个不结巴的。”她跳舞像决斗一样——而且赢了。\n# en: \"I'm going alone. It's more efficient.\" You watch her dance alone all night, perfectly.\nopt.lyra.say.4 [ba4ab4] = “我一个人去。效率更高。”你看着她独自跳了一整晚，完美无缺。\n# en: Ask Tobias\nopt.tobias.label [81d042] = 邀请托比亚斯\n# en: He has been practising with a broom.\nopt.tobias.hint [b74d36] = 他一直在拿扫帚当舞伴练习。\n# en: He steps on your feet nine times and apologises ten. It's the best night of the term.\nopt.tobias.say.1 [a497bd] = 他踩了你的脚九次，道歉了十次。这是本学期最美好的一晚。\n# en: Ask Seraphine\nopt.seraphine.label [4c88b3] = 邀请瑟拉芬\n# en: She may already know you'll ask.\nopt.seraphine.hint [da4e86] = 她可能早就知道你会邀请她。\n# en: \"I said yes three weeks ago,\" she says, \"you just hadn't asked yet.\" She dances a beat ahead of the music.\nopt.seraphine.say.1 [fd7d7e] = “我三周前就答应了，”她说，“只是你那时还没开口。”她的舞步总比音乐快半拍。\n# en: Ask Corvin\nopt.corvin.label [cbffdf] = 邀请科尔文\n# en: Odds he shows up: unclear.\nopt.corvin.hint [bd223d] = 他会不会出现：不好说。\n# en: He arrives late, in a stolen jacket, and is the best dancer in the room.\nopt.corvin.say.1 [0ec70b] = 他迟到了，穿着一件偷来的外套，却是全场跳得最好的人。\n# en: He never shows. A note arrives at midnight: \"Got arrested. Long story. Save me a dance next year.\"\nopt.corvin.say.2 [877a8a] = 他始终没来。午夜时分送来一张字条：“被捕了。说来话长。明年给我留支舞。”\n# en: Ask Mira\nopt.mira.label [1a704d] = 邀请米拉\n# en: They're bringing a self-playing violin.\nopt.mira.hint [4d4230] = TA 要带一把会自己演奏的小提琴。\n# en: Mira's violin plays itself, then plays the chandelier, then plays the windows. You are asked to leave early. Worth it.\nopt.mira.say.1 [88a0f6] = 米拉的小提琴自己拉了起来，然后拉起了吊灯，然后拉起了窗户。你们被请提前离场。值了。\n# en: Ask Kael\nopt.kael.label [bf9bdb] = 邀请凯尔\n# en: He doesn't seem to feel the cold.\nopt.kael.hint [3c4856] = 他似乎感觉不到寒冷。\n# en: He holds your hand like it is something fragile. Where your fingers touch, frost-ferns bloom on the glass.\nopt.kael.say.1 [b06391] = 他握着你的手，像握着什么易碎的东西。你们指尖相触之处，玻璃上绽开了霜蕨。\n# en: Ask Nessa\nopt.nessa.label [6cfd84] = 邀请内莎\n# en: She'll be working. She's always working.\nopt.nessa.hint [698b3a] = 她会边跳边工作。她永远在工作。\n# en: She takes notes between dances. \"For the society column.\" Then she stops taking notes.\nopt.nessa.say.1 [ff1845] = 她在舞曲间隙做笔记。“写社交专栏用的。”后来，她不再做笔记了。\n# en: Go with friends\nopt.friends.label [596790] = 和朋友们一起去\n# en: You go as a pack and dance badly together and it is perfect.\nopt.friends.say.1 [4cb3f9] = 你们成群结队地去，一起跳得乱七八糟，完美极了。\n# en: Stay in and study\nopt.skip.label [6d8b57] = 留在宿舍学习\n# en: The music drifts up through the floor while you read. Somewhere, people are happy. You are productive.\nopt.skip.say.1 [c55696] = 你看书时，音乐透过地板飘上来。某个地方，人们很快乐。而你，很高效。\n\n@event spring_tournament\n# en: The Spring Tournament\ntitle [e40010] = 春季锦标赛\n# en: The Spring Tournament: dueling on the great lawn, the whole academy watching. Entry is open to anyone foolish enough.\ntext [75ecd9] = 春季锦标赛：在大草坪上决斗，全院围观。任何够傻的人都可以报名。\n# en: A ring of white stone on the lawn, crowds in the stands, two duelists bowing as their wands spark.\nscene [903c03] = 草坪上一圈白石决斗场，看台上挤满人群，两名决斗者行礼致意，魔杖迸出火花。\n# en: Watch from the stands\nopt.left.label [8d4dec] = 在看台上观战\n# en: You eat candied chestnuts and scream for strangers.\nopt.left.say.1 [8ad597] = 你吃着糖炒栗子，为素不相识的人尖叫助威。\n# en: Enter the tournament\nopt.right.label [dd70a1] = 报名参赛\n# en: First round: you win, barely, and the crowd learns your name.\nopt.right.say.1 [5dba97] = 第一轮：你赢了，险胜，观众记住了你的名字。\n# en: First round: you are knocked out of the ring in eleven seconds. A record, apparently.\nopt.right.say.2 [39b48e] = 第一轮：你在十一秒内被打出场外。据说是新纪录。\n\n@event spring_tournament_circle\n# en: The Spring Tournament\ntitle [e40010] = 春季锦标赛\n# en: The Spring Tournament: duelling on the great lawn, the whole academy watching. The Duelling Circle's names went up on the board a week ago, yours among them. The captain wants results, and says so.\ntext [ed1689] = 春季锦标赛：在大草坪上决斗，全院围观。一周前，决斗社的名单就贴上了公告板，你的名字也在其中。社长要的是成绩，而且直说了。\n# en: The great lawn ringed with banners, a chalk duelling circle at its centre and the Duelling Circle's names on a board.\nscene [0ee357] = 挂满旗帜的大草坪，中央用粉笔画着决斗圈，一块板上写着决斗社成员的名字。\n# en: Fight to make a good showing\nopt.left.label [ad260b] = 打出一场像样的比赛\n# en: You win your first bout cleanly and lose the second honourably. The captain ticks your name, which from her is a medal.\nopt.left.say.1 [53d64e] = 你干净利落地赢下第一场，又体面地输掉第二场。社长在你的名字旁打了个勾——从她那儿来说，这就是一枚奖章。\n# en: You go out in the first round, on your feet at least. The captain says you fought like someone who expected to lose.\nopt.left.say.2 [6cf312] = 你在第一轮就出局了，好歹是站着出去的。社长说你打得像个早就料到自己会输的人。\n# en: Go for the title\nopt.right.label [a6e251] = 冲击冠军\n# en: First round: you win, barely, and the crowd learns your name.\nopt.right.say.1 [5dba97] = 第一轮：你赢了，赢得很险，全场都记住了你的名字。\n# en: First round: you are knocked out of the ring in eleven seconds. A record, apparently, and not one the Circle wanted.\nopt.right.say.2 [0831c2] = 第一轮：你在十一秒内被打出了场地。据说是个纪录，可惜不是决斗社想要的那种。\n\n@event tournament_semifinal\n# en: Semifinal\ntitle [4ddbc9] = 半决赛\n# en: The semifinal. Your opponent is a fourth-year who has never lost. The crowd is louder now; you can hear your own name in it.\ntext [69650a] = 半决赛。你的对手是一位从未输过的四年级生。人群更吵了；你能在喧闹中听见自己的名字。\n# en: The same ring at sunset, scorch marks on the stone. Your opponent is grinning.\nscene [54c532] = 夕阳下的同一座决斗场，石面上满是焦痕。你的对手咧嘴笑着。\n# en: Fight clean\nopt.left.label [e5ec00] = 堂堂正正地战斗\n# en: You trade spells like questions and answers — and get the last word.\nopt.left.say.1 [a411dd] = 你们你来我往地交换咒语，像一问一答——最后一句是你说的。\n# en: A good fight. Not good enough. The crowd applauds you off the lawn.\nopt.left.say.2 [2ec6f6] = 打得很好。但还不够好。人群鼓掌送你离开草坪。\n# en: Fight dirty\nopt.right.label [2323b9] = 耍点小手段\n# en: A feint, a trip, a jinx to the shoelaces. Legal. Mostly.\nopt.right.say.1 [28eb74] = 一个虚招，一个绊子，一个冲着鞋带的小咒。合规。大体上。\n# en: The referee sees the trick. Disqualified, and booed.\nopt.right.say.2 [06fbf6] = 裁判看穿了把戏。你被取消资格，还挨了嘘声。\n\n@event tournament_final\n# en: The Final\ntitle [5a826c] = 决赛\n# en: The final. Across the ring stands {lyra}, wand raised, eyes blazing. \"I was hoping it would be you,\" she says, and she is not being friendly.\ntext [9091dd] = 决赛。决斗场对面站着{lyra}，魔杖高举，双眼燃着火光。“我就希望是你，”她说，语气可一点也不友好。\n# en: The dueling ring under floating lanterns, a silver cup on a plinth, and across from you, a familiar face.\nscene [2b21ea] = 悬浮灯笼下的决斗场，底座上放着一只银杯，对面是一张熟悉的面孔。\n# en: Give everything you have\nopt.left.label [eef34c] = 拼尽全力\n# en: Your final spell lands like a bell. Silence — then the lawn erupts. Lyra stares at you as if seeing you for the first time.\nopt.left.say.1 [3285dd] = 你最后一道咒语像钟声一样落下。一片寂静——随后草坪沸腾了。莱拉看着你，好像第一次真正看见你。\n# en: She wins, but only just. Afterwards, she offers her hand. \"Next year,\" she says. It is a promise and a threat.\nopt.left.say.2 [6d4f46] = 她赢了，但只是险胜。赛后，她向你伸出手。“明年。”她说。这既是承诺，也是威胁。\n# en: Yield with a bow\nopt.right.label [7ad851] = 鞠躬认输\n# en: The crowd boos. Lyra does not cheer her victory. Later she finds you: \"Why?\" You don't have a good answer, and she seems to like that.\nopt.right.say.1 [957fd4] = 人群发出嘘声。莱拉没有为自己的胜利欢呼。后来她找到你：“为什么？”你答不上来，而她似乎正喜欢这一点。\n\n@event grand_exams\n# en: Grand Examinations\ntitle [9cf559] = 年度大考\n# en: Year-end examinations. Three days, five examiners and a practical in front of a panel that includes the headmistress. Your standing for the year rides on this.\ntext [148501] = 学年末考试。三天，五位考官，还要在包括院长在内的评审团面前完成一项实践考核。你这一年的学业，全押在这上面了。\n# en: A circular examination chamber with a domed ceiling of moving constellations; examiners in grey robes sit behind a long table.\nscene [a8e8ed] = 一间圆形考场，穹顶上的星座缓缓移动；身着灰袍的考官们坐在长桌后。\n# en: Prepare a dazzling practical\nopt.left.label [6b5ebd] = 准备一场惊艳的实践考核\n# en: You finish your practical and the examiners actually lean forward. Distinction.\nopt.left.say.1 [1ca3b3] = 你完成实践考核时，考官们居然真的向前倾了倾身子。优秀。\n# en: The practical sputters. An examiner writes something very short.\nopt.left.say.2 [1a07cb] = 实践考核噼啪作响地熄火了。一位考官写下了很短的一句话。\n# en: Write a meticulous thesis\nopt.right.label [018303] = 写一篇严谨的论文\n# en: Your thesis is cited by one examiner in an argument with another. Distinction.\nopt.right.say.1 [dbc14d] = 一位考官在和另一位争论时引用了你的论文。优秀。\n# en: Your thesis is described, aloud, as \"ambitious\". It is not a compliment.\nopt.right.say.2 [067468] = 你的论文被当众评价为“很有野心”。这可不是夸奖。\n\n@event summer_plans\n# en: Summer Recess\ntitle [84178b] = 暑假\n# en: The towers empty for the summer. Two months of freedom. How will you spend them?\ntext [28317e] = 塔楼在夏天空了下来。两个月的自由。你打算怎么过？\n# en: The academy gates thrown open to green hills; students with trunks waving from departing sky-barges.\nscene [35fcaa] = 学院大门向青翠的山丘敞开；拖着行李箱的学生们在离港的天空驳船上挥手。\n# en: Go home\nopt.home.label [c93952] = 回家\n# en: Rest and recover. No rent while away.\nopt.home.hint [735a7b] = 休息与恢复。离校期间不交房租。\n# en: Your family hosts three dinners in your honour and asks about your marriage prospects at all of them.\nopt.home.say.1 [47c092] = 家里为你办了三场晚宴，每一场都有人问起你的婚事。\n# en: The old crowd in Port Calder treats you like a returning hero, and steals your trunk only once.\nopt.home.say.2 [c552ac] = 考尔德港的老伙计们把你当成荣归故里的英雄，只偷了你的行李箱一次。\n# en: Home is smaller than you remember, and warmer. Someone made your favourite pie.\nopt.home.say.3 [c3fe6f] = 家比你记忆中小，也更温暖。有人做了你最爱吃的馅饼。\n# en: Take summer work in the village\nopt.work.label [3f8e3c] = 在镇上打暑期工\n# en: About 50 gold over the summer. Tiring.\nopt.work.hint [6173e0] = 整个夏天约 50 金币。很累。\n# en: You haul crates, serve tables and polish brass until your hands know the shape of every coin.\nopt.work.say.1 [55acbe] = 你搬箱子、端盘子、擦铜器，直到双手熟悉每一枚硬币的形状。\n# en: Stay and study in the empty library\nopt.study.label [968f6e] = 留在空荡的图书馆学习\n# en: +Lore, +Arcana, +Standing. Lonely.\nopt.study.hint [8c75a3] = +学识，+奥术，+学业。很孤单。\n# en: Just you, the books and the dust motes. You learn things the term never had time for.\nopt.study.say.1 [fe7d5b] = 只有你、书本和浮尘。你学到了学期里永远没时间学的东西。\n# en: Travel to the coast\nopt.travel.label [78a0b3] = 去海边旅行\n# en: Costs 40 gold. Great for mood.\nopt.travel.hint [b46376] = 花费 40 金币。心情大好。\n# en: Needs 40 crowns.\nopt.travel.locked [4deed8] = 需要 40 克朗。\n# en: Salt air, shell markets, a sea serpent at sunset. You come back tanned and ten years younger.\nopt.travel.say.1 [1db6b9] = 咸咸的海风，贝壳集市，夕阳下的海蛇。你回来时晒黑了，年轻了十岁。\n# en: Join Old Maud's herb expedition\nopt.expedition.label [49633f] = 参加老莫德的采药远征\n# en: Requires knowing Maud. Adventure.\nopt.expedition.hint [362c6b] = 需要认识老莫德。冒险。\n# en: Two months in the Whisperwood with Maud and her goat. You learn which mushrooms sing and which ones lie.\nopt.expedition.say.1 [929c02] = 和莫德还有她的山羊在低语森林里待了两个月。你学会了分辨哪些蘑菇会唱歌，哪些会撒谎。\n\n@event summer_letter\n# en: A Letter From Home\ntitle [418673] = 一封家书\n# en: A letter arrives in the last month of summer, the handwriting so familiar it hurts. They want to know how you are doing. Really doing.\ntext [67658a] = 夏天的最后一个月，一封信寄到了，字迹熟悉得让人心疼。他们想知道你过得怎么样。真正过得怎么样。\n# en: A letter sealed with candle wax lying on a sunlit windowsill.\nscene [ac2d7b] = 一封用烛蜡封口的信，躺在洒满阳光的窗台上。\n# en: Write back honestly\nopt.left.label [24b451] = 如实回信\n# en: You tell them about your grades. Their reply is kind, which is worse.\nopt.left.say.1 [5e0fac] = 你告诉了他们你的成绩。他们的回信很温柔，这反而更难受。\n# en: You write six pages. It feels like setting down a heavy bag.\nopt.left.say.2 [f6372c] = 你写了六页。感觉像放下了一个沉重的包袱。\n# en: Write back that everything is perfect\nopt.right.label [979dc2] = 回信说一切都很完美\n# en: Proud of you, they write, and enclose a little money you suspect they couldn't spare.\nopt.right.say.1 [8deb66] = 他们回信说为你骄傲，还附上了一点钱——你猜他们其实拿不出来。\n# en: They reply with exclamation marks. The lie sits in your stomach all week.\nopt.right.say.2 [75bc50] = 他们的回信满是感叹号。这个谎言在你胃里堵了一整个星期。\n\n@event starfall\n# en: Starfall\ntitle [ab87b4] = 星坠节\n# en: The last night of the academic year. Everyone climbs onto the observatory roof to watch the stars fall. They say if you catch one in a jar, you get a year of luck.\ntext [6b880d] = 学年的最后一夜。大家都爬上天文台屋顶看星星坠落。据说，如果你能用罐子接住一颗，就能交一整年的好运。\n# en: Students lying on the observatory roof under a sky streaked with falling stars.\nscene [7b9deb] = 学生们躺在天文台屋顶上，头顶的夜空划满了流星。\n# en: Lie back and just watch\nopt.left.label [df7032] = 躺下来，只是看着\n# en: You count forty falling stars and then stop counting. It's enough.\nopt.left.say.1 [d2a569] = 你数了四十颗流星，然后不再数了。这就够了。\n# en: Try to catch a star\nopt.right.label [734781] = 试着接住一颗星\n# en: It lands in your jar with a hiss and glows for weeks. Students come to see it.\nopt.right.say.1 [f06369] = 它嘶的一声落进你的罐子，发光发了好几个星期。同学们都跑来看。\n# en: You lunge, miss, and nearly roll off the roof. A prefect catches you by the collar.\nopt.right.say.2 [dd228d] = 你扑了过去，没接到，差点滚下屋顶。一位级长揪住了你的衣领。\n\n@event year_two\n# en: Second Year\ntitle [c51ea2] = 第二学年\n# en: The barge brings new first-years, and you watch them gawp at the towers. Was that really you a year ago? You know which staircases move now, and which professors you can charm.\ntext [9706f9] = 驳船载来了新一届的一年级生，你看着他们目瞪口呆地仰望高塔。一年前，那真的是你吗？如今你知道哪些楼梯会动，哪些教授可以哄一哄。\n# en: The seven towers in autumn colours, the first-year barge arriving below, tiny and nervous.\nscene [aa7c3d] = 秋色中的七座高塔，下方新生的驳船正在靠岸，渺小而紧张。\n# en: Begin the second year\nopt.ok.label [338fd8] = 开始第二学年\n# en: You unpack faster than last year, and in the right order.\nopt.ok.say.1 [fc01d0] = 你收拾行李比去年快，顺序也对了。\n\n@event year_three\n# en: Third Year\ntitle [c19627] = 第三年\n# en: \"Third years,\" the headmistress says, \"you are past the middle of your time here. Next year will ask a great deal of you; spend this one finding out what you want from it.\" She looks, for a moment, directly at you.\ntext [f9a2f2] = “三年级的同学们，”院长说，“你们在这里的时光已经过半。明年会对你们提出很多要求；今年就用来弄明白，你们想从中得到什么。”有那么一刻，她直直地看着你。\n# en: The headmistress addressing a hall of third-years, every candle in the room leaning toward her.\nscene [3c61bc] = 院长向满堂的三年级生讲话，屋里的每一支蜡烛都向她倾斜。\n# en: Begin the third year\nopt.ok.label [85c8b5] = 开始第三年\n# en: You know the castle well enough now to get lost in it on purpose.\nopt.ok.say.1 [25b60e] = 如今你对城堡熟到可以故意在里面迷路了。\n\n@event graduation_eve\n# en: The Eve of Graduation\ntitle [16a29d] = 毕业前夜\n# en: Tomorrow you graduate. Your robes are laid out on the bed, slightly too long. Outside, the towers are lit gold. Four years have gone like a single held breath.\ntext [feebe6] = 明天你就要毕业了。学士袍摊在床上，稍微长了一点。窗外，高塔被点亮成金色。四年时光，像屏住的一口气，倏忽而过。\n# en: Graduation robes laid out on a dormitory bed, a single candle burning, the towers lit gold outside the window.\nscene [47f9c3] = 宿舍床上摊着毕业袍，一支蜡烛独自燃烧，窗外的塔楼灯火金黄。\n# en: Spend the night with friends\nopt.left.label [476937] = 和朋友们共度今夜\n# en: Laughter until dawn. Promises to write. Some of them, you'll keep.\nopt.left.say.1 [82accb] = 笑到天亮。约好要写信。其中一些约定，你会遵守。\n# en: Walk the halls alone one last time\nopt.right.label [9cc549] = 最后一次独自走遍厅廊\n# en: Every corridor holds a memory. At the end of the last one, the castle sighs, as if saying goodbye.\nopt.right.say.1 [fce683] = 每一条走廊都藏着回忆。走到最后一条的尽头时，城堡轻轻叹了口气，像是在道别。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/chain_hollowstar_acts.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/chain_hollowstar_acts.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event hs_silver_rings\n# en: Silver at Supper\ntitle [65d7d8] = 晚餐桌上的银戒\n# en: At supper you notice three identical silver rings at the high table: on the headmistress, on Professor Thorne and on Old Maud's earthy hand. After curfew, from your window, you see all three walking the lakeshore with lanterns, stopping at the foot of each tower in turn.\ntext [0e82e1] = 晚餐时，你注意到主宾席上有三枚一模一样的银戒指：一枚戴在院长手上，一枚在索恩教授手上，还有一枚在老莫德那双沾着泥土的手上。宵禁之后，你从窗口望见他们三人提着灯笼沿湖岸走，在每座塔脚下依次停步。\n# en: The Great Hall at supper, candlelight catching three identical silver rings on three very different hands at the high table.\nscene [7eebf1] = 晚餐时的大礼堂，烛光照亮主宾席上三只截然不同的手上三枚一模一样的银戒指。\n# en: Follow them down\nopt.left.label [088fbe] = 跟下去看看\n# en: You shadow them from tower to tower. At each one they lay a palm on the stone and wait. At the last, Maud takes her hand away and blows on her fingers as if the wall were ice.\nopt.left.say.1 [ec6d76] = 你从一座塔跟到另一座塔。每到一处，他们都把手掌贴在石墙上，静静等着。到了最后一座，莫德把手收回来，对着指尖呵气，仿佛那面墙是冰做的。\n# en: A lantern swings toward you. \"Hm,\" says Professor Thorne. \"Bed.\" He doesn't report you, which somehow feels worse.\nopt.left.say.2 [c0b820] = 一盏灯笼朝你转过来。“嗯。”索恩教授说，“睡觉去。”他没有上报你，不知为何这反而更难受。\n# en: Draw the curtains\nopt.right.label [899463] = 拉上窗帘\n# en: You sleep well, mostly. In the morning the rings are still there at breakfast, and nobody at the high table looks as if they slept at all.\nopt.right.say.1 [b6139e] = 你睡得还算不错。第二天早餐时，那几枚戒指还在，而主宾席上没有一个人看起来睡过觉。\n\n@event hs_seraphine_towers\n# en: Seven, Then Six\ntitle [257255] = 七座，然后六座\n# en: Seraphine is on the observatory balcony, counting towers on her fingers. \"Seven,\" she says. \"Seven. Six, and then… no. Seven, for now.\" She looks at you as if you had arrived a little early. \"What month is it? I lose track around the… you know.\"\ntext [a96e33] = 瑟拉芬站在观星台的阳台上，掰着手指数塔。“七座，”她说，“七座。六座，然后……不。七座，暂时。”她看着你，仿佛你来得稍微早了一点。“现在是几月？我总是在……你知道的那个地方，弄丢日子。”\n# en: A seer on the observatory balcony at night, counting the academy's towers on her fingers, one finger held back.\nscene [34ef65] = 夜里观星台的阳台上，一位预言者掰着手指数学院的塔，有一根手指迟迟没有伸出来。\n# en: \"Which one goes out?\"\nopt.left.label [a243d5] = “哪一座会熄灭？”\n# en: She points, then frowns at her own hand. \"That one. Or the one next to it. Next year, I think.\" She doesn't remember saying it the next morning, but you do.\nopt.left.say.1 [44c830] = 她指了一下，又对着自己的手皱起眉。“那一座。或者旁边那座。明年吧，我想。”第二天早上她已经不记得说过这话，但你记得。\n# en: \"It's {month}. Evening.\"\nopt.right.label [1c7b30] = “现在是{month}，傍晚。”\n# en: She lets out a long breath and sits down on the cold stone. \"Thank you. I was halfway into a winter.\" You stay until she is sure which one she's in.\nopt.right.say.1 [1f863d] = 她长长地呼出一口气，在冰凉的石头上坐了下来。“谢谢。我已经半只脚踏进冬天了。”你一直陪着她，直到她确定自己身在哪个季节。\n\n@event hs_frost_ring\n# en: A Ring of Frost\ntitle [6db639] = 一圈白霜\n# en: A warm night, and a ring of frost has formed in the middle of the lake: perfectly round, perfectly white, as wide as the Great Hall. Far below it, very faintly, a bell tolls once. The headmistress is standing alone at the waterline.\ntext [110bde] = 一个温暖的夜晚，湖心却结出了一圈白霜：浑圆，雪白，和大礼堂一样宽。在它下方很深的地方，隐约传来一声钟响。院长独自站在水边。\n# en: The Mirror Lake on a warm spring night, a perfect white ring of frost on the water, a tall woman standing at the waterline with a lantern.\nscene [34c053] = 温暖春夜里的镜湖，水面上一圈完美的白霜，一个高个子女人提着灯笼站在水边。\n# en: Watch what she does\nopt.left.label [29f82d] = 看她要做什么\n# en: She kneels and speaks to the water, slow and patient, the way you would speak to a sleeping animal. The ring shrinks, cracks, and is gone. She stays kneeling a long time afterwards.\nopt.left.say.1 [fe979a] = 她跪下来，对着湖水说话，缓慢而耐心，就像对一头熟睡的野兽说话。那圈白霜缩小、碎裂，然后消失了。她在那里跪了很久才起身。\n# en: \"Good evening,\" she says, without turning round. \"You are the third student this week. The other two asked questions.\" She tells you nothing, and remembers your name.\nopt.left.say.2 [8fce40] = “晚上好。”她头也不回地说，“你是这周第三个来的学生。另外两个至少提了问题。”她什么也没告诉你，只是记住了你的名字。\n# en: Stay inside\nopt.right.label [b9dac4] = 待在屋里\n# en: You stay inside with the shutters closed. By morning the lake is blue and glittering, and the first-years are already arguing about whether anyone saw anything at all.\nopt.right.say.1 [29cbf0] = 你关紧百叶窗待在屋里。到了早上，湖面湛蓝、波光粼粼，一年级生已经在争论到底有没有人看见过什么。\n\n@event hs_dark_tower\n# en: The Tower That Went Dark\ntitle [eb9204] = 熄灭的塔\n# en: Mid-lecture, every lamp in the Lecture Tower goes out at once, and none of them will light again. Nobody is hurt. By morning, weeks before any frost, half the lake has frozen: a sheet of ice that ends in a clean edge, as if cut. The prefects are already telling everyone to keep off it.\ntext [f6dbbf] = 讲课讲到一半，讲堂塔里所有的灯同时熄灭，再也点不亮了。没有人受伤。到了早上，离第一场霜还有好几周，半个湖却已经冻住：冰面的边缘齐整得像是刀切出来的。级长们已经在挨个叮嘱大家别上冰。\n# en: An academy tower standing black against the night sky among its lit neighbours, and below it a lake half-covered in autumn ice.\nscene [1361bd] = 一座学院塔楼在灯火通明的邻塔之间漆黑一片，塔下的湖面有一半覆着秋天的冰。\n# en: Walk out onto the ice\nopt.left.label [fe1ac8] = 走上冰面\n# en: Under your boots the ice is clear as glass. Far down, something round and dark sits on the lakebed, and a faint glow runs along its rim. Then the glow turns, slowly, as if to look up.\nopt.left.say.1 [b23a9e] = 脚下的冰清澈如玻璃。在很深的湖底，有个圆形的黑色东西，边缘流淌着微光。然后那道光缓缓转动，像是要抬头往上看。\n# en: The ice groans and a crack races past your feet. You scramble back to the shore soaked to the knee, and a prefect writes your name down very slowly.\nopt.left.say.2 [b53a93] = 冰面一声呻吟，一道裂缝从你脚边飞窜而过。你连滚带爬地退回岸上，膝盖以下全湿透了，一位级长慢条斯理地记下了你的名字。\n# en: Carry lamps for the prefects\nopt.right.label [6083a9] = 帮级长们搬灯\n# en: You spend the night carrying lamps up the Lecture Tower stairs. Every one goes out at the doorway. From her window across the courtyard, the headmistress watches you try.\nopt.right.say.1 [b28e54] = 你整夜提着灯爬讲堂塔的楼梯。每一盏灯一到门口就熄灭。庭院对面，院长站在她的窗前，看着你一次又一次地尝试。\n\n@event hs_maud_locks\n# en: Maud Has Seen Ice Before\ntitle [b78fd5] = 莫德见过这种冰\n# en: Maud is on her cottage step with two mugs of tea, as if she expected you. \"The lake doesn't freeze this time of year, dearie,\" she says. \"Last time it froze at all, I lost a friend to it.\" She pushes a mug toward you. \"Sit. Or ask. You can't really do both.\"\ntext [2065c5] = 莫德坐在小屋的台阶上，身边放着两杯茶，像是早料到你会来。“这个时节湖是不会结冰的，亲爱的。”她说，“上一次它结冰，我失去了一个朋友。”她把一杯茶推到你面前。“坐下。或者提问。两样可没法一起做。”\n# en: An old woman wrapped in a shawl on her cottage step, two mugs steaming beside her, looking down at a half-frozen lake.\nscene [f49e09] = 一位裹着披肩的老妇人坐在小屋台阶上，身边两杯茶冒着热气，俯瞰着半冻的湖面。\n# en: Sit with her\nopt.left.label [654e0a] = 陪她坐着\n# en: You drink your tea and watch the ice. She hums something old. When the mugs are empty she squeezes your hand. \"That was the right one,\" she says, and you'll only understand why much later.\nopt.left.say.1 [7b5291] = 你喝着茶，看着冰面。她哼着一支老调子。杯子空了，她捏了捏你的手。“选对了。”她说。你要过很久才明白为什么。\n# en: Ask what's holding it shut\nopt.right.label [8d8004] = 问是什么把门锁住\n# en: \"Seven towers, seven locks, one in each,\" she says. \"When a tower goes quiet, so does its lock.\" She looks at the dark tower. \"Locks can be mended. They need hands, and they need a key.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [0813e9] = “七座塔，七道锁，每座一道。”她说，“一座塔沉寂了，它的锁也就沉寂了。”她望着那座熄灭的塔。“锁是可以修的。要有人手，还要有一把钥匙。”\n\n@event hs_choosing_sides\n# en: Two Invitations\ntitle [b97cfd] = 两份邀请\n# en: Two invitations in one day. A note in the headmistress's neat hand: \"My study, after supper. Come alone. There will be chess.\" And Kael, waiting at the boathouse: \"She wants you in her circle. I know the door from the other side.\" He looks at the dark tower. \"Come with us. If you want.\"\ntext [441bf4] = 一天之内收到两份邀请。一张字条上是院长工整的字迹：“晚饭后来我书房。独自来。会有棋局。”而凯尔在船屋等你：“她想让你加入她的圈子。我从门的另一边认识那扇门。”他望向那座熄灭的塔。“跟我们来吧。如果你愿意。”\n# en: A student holding two folded notes, one sealed in silver wax and one in frost, standing between a tower and a boathouse.\nscene [0fa513] = 一个学生手里拿着两张折好的字条，一张封着银色火漆，一张封着白霜，站在一座塔和一间船屋之间。\n# en: Join the headmistress's circle\nopt.wardens.label [d0d300] = 加入院长的圈子\n# en: The Wardens: old rules, old wards.\nopt.wardens.hint [8082aa] = 守望者：古老的规矩，古老的护界。\n# en: She beats you at chess in eleven moves, then tells you about the seal while she resets the board. \"Rings are for people who have stood on the ice,\" she says. \"Until then, you have my door.\"\nopt.wardens.say.1 [1f494e] = 她用十一步棋赢了你，一边重新摆棋盘，一边给你讲封印的事。“戒指是给站上过冰面的人的。”她说，“在那之前，我的门为你开着。”\n# en: Stand with the Dumonts\nopt.dumonts.label [1cf610] = 站在杜蒙兄妹一边\n# en: Kael's way, against his own family.\nopt.dumonts.hint [3d8ab6] = 凯尔的路，与他自己的家族为敌。\n# en: \"Not my family,\" Kael says. \"Us.\" He teaches you to hold a ward in a wind that wants your fingers. His hands are cold. They are steadier than yours.\nopt.dumonts.say.1 [b5a244] = “不是我的家族，”凯尔说，“是我们。”他教你在一阵想要夺走你手指的寒风里撑住护界。他的手很冷，却比你的稳。\n# en: Go my own way\nopt.self.label [90c2bc] = 走我自己的路\n# en: Nobody's circle. Nobody's help.\nopt.self.hint [06bcd0] = 不入任何圈子，也没人帮忙。\n# en: You thank them both and keep your own notes in your own cipher. It's lonelier than you expected. Both of them, you notice, keep an eye on you anyway.\nopt.self.say.1 [559438] = 你谢过他们两人，用自己的密码记自己的笔记。这比你想象的更孤单。你发现，他们俩还是都在暗中照看着你。\n# en: Stay out of it\nopt.none.label [03d24a] = 置身事外\n# en: It isn't your door.\nopt.none.hint [4c3c5a] = 那不是你的门。\n# en: You turn both invitations down. Kael nods as if he expected it. The headmistress's note simply says, the next morning, \"Understood. Mind the ice.\"\nopt.none.say.1 [80761e] = 你婉拒了两份邀请。凯尔点点头，像是早就料到。第二天早上，院长的字条上只写着：“明白了。当心冰面。”\n\n@event hs_last_winter\n# en: The Last Winter Before\ntitle [77b708] = 最后一个冬天之前\n# en: The Lecture Tower has been dark for a year, and the lake has started freezing overnight in strange shapes: circles, mostly. Everyone who knows what's under it is getting ready in their own way. You have one winter and not enough evenings. Who do you spend them with?\ntext [22e187] = 讲堂塔已经黑了一年，湖面开始在夜里冻出古怪的形状：大多是圆圈。知道湖底有什么的人，都在用自己的方式做准备。你只有一个冬天，傍晚却不够用。你要和谁一起度过？\n# en: Lantern light in a frost-rimed window, the frozen edge of the lake beyond it, a student's desk covered in maps of seven towers.\nscene [e5b843] = 结霜的窗户里透出灯笼的光，窗外是湖面冰冻的边缘，学生的书桌上铺满了七座塔的地图。\n# en: Train with Kael on the ice\nopt.kael.label [f6da05] = 和凯尔在冰上训练\n# en: He drills you on the ice until you can hold a ward with numb hands. \"Again,\" he says, and then, quietly, \"Good.\" You will remember that he said it.\nopt.kael.say.1 [e69c38] = 他在冰上一遍遍操练你，直到你能用冻僵的手撑住护界。“再来。”他说，然后轻声补了一句：“很好。”你会记得他说过这句话。\n# en: Mend wards with Maud\nopt.maud.label [7044a1] = 和莫德修补护界\n# en: You walk the towers with her, tracing old wards back into the stone. \"Your hands are steadier than mine were,\" she says, and doesn't say whose hands she means.\nopt.maud.say.1 [9b7f55] = 你陪她走遍七塔，把古老的护界一笔一笔描回石头里。“你的手比我当年的稳。”她说，却没说她指的是谁的手。\n# en: Study with the headmistress\nopt.isolde.label [d9f337] = 跟院长学习\n# en: You lose at chess every evening and learn the founders' words between moves. Once you nearly win. Her short, delighted laugh is the warmest sound you hear all winter.\nopt.isolde.say.1 [627308] = 每个傍晚你都输棋，在落子之间学会了创始者的咒文。有一次你差点赢了。她那短促而欣喜的笑声，是你整个冬天听过最温暖的声音。\n# en: Spar with Ilse at the shore\nopt.ilse.label [b13627] = 和伊尔泽在湖边对练\n# en: She knocks you into the snow eleven times. The twelfth time you don't fall, and she looks at you as if you had done a trick she has never learned the counter to.\nopt.ilse.say.1 [86c9c1] = 她把你打进雪里十一次。第十二次你没有倒下，她看着你，仿佛你使出了一招她从没学过怎么破解的戏法。\n# en: Rest while you still can\nopt.alone.label [639301] = 趁还能休息就休息\n# en: A quiet winter. Nobody else's.\nopt.alone.hint [88a86b] = 一个安静的冬天，只属于你自己。\n# en: You sleep, you eat, you read novels with no doors in them. It's the kindest winter you've had in years, and you can feel the lake getting colder anyway.\nopt.alone.say.1 [7e93f5] = 你睡觉，吃饭，读那些里面没有门的小说。这是你多年来最温柔的一个冬天，可你还是感觉得到湖水在一天天变冷。\n\n@event hs_ilse_door\n# en: What Ilse Was Taught\ntitle [8a9ef8] = 伊尔泽学过的东西\n# en: Ilse draws a circle in the snow with her wand, then a smaller one inside it. \"This is the door,\" she says. \"I have drawn it every day since I was six. I have never seen it.\" She doesn't look up. \"Ask me what they taught me to do. Or don't. I don't care which.\"\ntext [f6aa80] = 伊尔泽用魔杖在雪地上画了一个圆，又在里面画了一个小圆。“这就是那扇门。”她说，“我从六岁起每天都画它。我从没见过它。”她没有抬头。“问我他们教了我做什么。或者别问。我无所谓。”\n# en: A young woman in white furs drawing a round door in the snow with the tip of a wand, a frozen lake behind her.\nscene [1adcfd] = 一个身披白裘的年轻女子用魔杖尖在雪地上画一扇圆门，身后是冰封的湖。\n# en: \"What did they teach you?\"\nopt.left.label [ee8d4b] = “他们教了你什么？”\n# en: She tells you, flatly, like a lesson recited: how to stand, what to say, where to put her hands. \"They raised me to open it,\" she says. \"Nobody asked me if I wanted to.\" Then, sharply: \"Now you know. Don't look at me like that.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [e300d4] = 她平板地讲给你听，像在背诵一堂课：怎么站，说什么，手放在哪里。“他们把我养大，就是为了让我打开它。”她说，“从没人问过我想不想。”然后她语气一冷：“现在你知道了。别用那种眼神看我。”\n# en: \"You don't have to tell me.\"\nopt.right.label [194cf6] = “你不必告诉我。”\n# en: She rubs the circle out with her boot. \"Kindness,\" she says, as if naming a trick. \"I haven't learned the counter to that one yet.\" She doesn't leave, though.\nopt.right.say.1 [8dd039] = 她用靴子把那个圆抹掉。“善意。”她说，像在叫出一个戏法的名字，“这一招，我还没学会怎么破解。”不过，她没有走。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/chain_hollowstar.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/chain_hollowstar.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event hs_dreams_begin\n# en: The Black Star Dream\ntitle [c0e94a] = 黑星之梦\n# en: You dream of the lake from underneath. There is a round door at the bottom, and above the water a star that is not a light but an absence. A voice says your name like it is tasting it. You wake with frost on your pillow.\ntext [aadef3] = 你梦见从水下仰望湖面。湖底有一扇圆门，水面之上有一颗星——它不是光，而是一种缺失。一个声音念出你的名字，像在品尝它。你醒来时，枕头上结着霜。\n# en: A dreamscape: a frozen lake seen from beneath, a round door glowing at the bottom, and above it a star that is a hole in the sky.\nscene [ba146d] = 梦境：从水下仰望结冰的湖面，湖底一扇发光的圆门，上方一颗如同天空破洞的星。\n# en: Write the dream down\nopt.left.label [f34e00] = 把梦记下来\n# en: Your notes fill four pages. In the morning you don't remember writing the last line: \"It is not asleep. It is waiting.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [5620a5] = 你的笔记写满了四页。早上，你不记得自己写过最后一行：“它没有沉睡。它在等待。”\n# en: Ask Seraphine what it means\nopt.right.label [37112a] = 问瑟拉芬这是什么意思\n# en: Seraphine listens, then hands you a sprig of lavender. \"It won't stop the dreams,\" she says. \"But it'll make them smell nicer.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [2b221e] = 瑟拉芬听完，递给你一枝薰衣草。“它止不住那些梦，”她说，“但能让梦闻起来香一点。”\n\n@event hs_grey_stranger\n# en: A Man in Grey\ntitle [52d66e] = 灰衣人\n# en: Late at night in the courtyard, Kael is arguing with a tall man in a grey coat. The fountain between them has frozen mid-splash. \"You will open it,\" the man says calmly, \"or your sister will.\"\ntext [aef66f] = 深夜的庭院里，凯尔正和一个穿灰色大衣的高个子男人争执。他们之间的喷泉在水花溅起的一刻冻住了。“你会打开它的，”那人平静地说，“否则你妹妹会。”\n# en: A frozen fountain in a moonlit courtyard; a tall man in a grey coat facing a younger man, frost spreading from both.\nscene [27f9c8] = 月光下庭院里一座冻结的喷泉；一个穿灰大衣的高个男人与一个年轻人对峙，霜从两人脚下蔓延。\n# en: Slip away unseen\nopt.left.label [0c4d41] = 悄悄溜走\n# en: You back into the shadows. The man in grey turns his head and looks straight at where you're hiding, then smiles and walks away.\nopt.left.say.1 [c8494a] = 你退回阴影里。灰衣人转过头，直直地看向你藏身的地方，然后笑了笑，走开了。\n# en: Step out and interrupt\nopt.right.label [ec8045] = 走出去打断他们\n# en: \"Everything all right?\" The man in grey studies you, nods politely and leaves. Kael's hands are shaking. \"You shouldn't have done that,\" he says. \"...Thank you.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [e50d62] = “没事吧？”灰衣人打量了你一番，礼貌地点点头，走了。凯尔的手在发抖。“你不该这么做的，”他说，“……谢谢。”\n\n@event hs_founders_records\n# en: The Founders' Ledger\ntitle [92688a] = 创始者账簿\n# en: In the archive vault you find the Founders' Ledger, three hundred years old. The entry for the academy's founding year has been cut out with a razor. But the page beneath still holds the impression of the pen.\ntext [8885d9] = 你在档案库里找到了三百年前的《创始者账簿》。记载学院创立那一年的条目被人用刀片割掉了。但下面那一页上，仍留着笔尖的压痕。\n# en: A dusty archive vault, a giant ledger open on a lectern, one page carefully razored out.\nscene [f6e330] = 积满灰尘的档案库，讲台上摊开一本巨大的账簿，其中一页被小心地割掉了。\n# en: Leave it — this feels dangerous\nopt.left.label [7ed54a] = 算了——这感觉很危险\n# en: You close the ledger. The dust settles. Some doors are better left alone. Probably.\nopt.left.say.1 [cce53b] = 你合上账簿。灰尘落定。有些门最好别去碰。大概吧。\n# en: Rub charcoal over the page\nopt.right.label [3eeead] = 用炭笔拓印那一页\n# en: Ghostly letters emerge: \"Seven towers, seven locks. The Star sleeps beneath the water. The key is a star that holds no light.\" The founders built the academy as a seal.\nopt.right.say.1 [8ceb50] = 幽灵般的字迹浮现出来：“七塔七锁。星眠于水下。钥匙是一颗不盛光的星。”创始者建起这座学院，正是为了作一道封印。\n# en: The impression is too faint. You make out only three words: \"...beneath the water...\"\nopt.right.say.2 [bee5f6] = 压痕太浅了。你只辨认出四个字：“……于水下……”\n\n@event hs_kael_truth\n# en: What Kael Is Running From\ntitle [5fe24d] = 凯尔在逃避什么\n# en: Kael sits with you on the boathouse jetty. \"My family serve something under this lake,\" he says at last. \"They sent me here to open its door. I came to stop them. Some nights I'm not sure which I'll actually do.\"\ntext [01cf3e] = 凯尔和你一起坐在船屋的栈桥上。“我的家族侍奉着这湖底的某样东西，”他终于开口，“他们派我来打开它的门。我来是为了阻止他们。有些夜里，我也不确定自己最后会做哪一件。”\n# en: Two students sitting on the edge of the frozen boathouse jetty at night, one with frost in his hair, both looking down at the black water.\nscene [ede058] = 夜里两个学生坐在结冰的船屋栈桥边缘，其中一人发间挂着霜，两人都低头看着漆黑的湖水。\n# en: \"Then we stop them together.\"\nopt.left.label [72f262] = “那我们一起阻止他们。”\n# en: He looks at you for a long moment, and the frost on the jetty melts a little. \"Together,\" he repeats, as if trying out a word from another language.\nopt.left.say.1 [734531] = 他久久地看着你，栈桥上的霜融化了一点。“一起。”他重复道，像在尝试一个外语单词。\n# en: Tell the headmistress\nopt.right.label [bcf093] = 告诉院长\n# en: The headmistress thanks you gravely. The next day Kael is moved to a room in the headmistress's tower. He doesn't look at you anymore.\nopt.right.say.1 [07e3f1] = 院长郑重地感谢了你。第二天，凯尔被搬进了院长塔楼里的一个房间。他再也不看你了。\n\n@event hs_star_mark\n# en: The Cold Coin Burns\ntitle [2ace55] = 冰冷的硬币在灼烧\n# en: The black-starred relic you've been carrying burns suddenly, so cold it feels hot. When you drop it, there's a pale star-shaped mark on your palm. It hums when you're near the lake.\ntext [32d3ff] = 你一直带在身上的那件黑星遗物突然灼烧起来，冷到像是滚烫。你把它扔下时，掌心多了一枚苍白的星形印记。靠近湖边时，它会嗡嗡作响。\n# en: A student's open palm, a pale star-shaped mark glowing on it, a blackened coin smoking on the floor beside them.\nscene [be9e03] = 一个学生摊开的掌心，上面闪着苍白的星形印记，一枚发黑的硬币在旁边的地上冒着烟。\n# en: Throw the relic into the lake\nopt.left.label [9e07e3] = 把遗物扔进湖里\n# en: It sinks without a ripple. The mark on your hand fades — almost entirely. Something under the water sighs.\nopt.left.say.1 [f57ba3] = 它沉了下去，没起一丝涟漪。你手上的印记褪去了——几乎完全褪去。水下有什么东西叹了口气。\n# en: Keep it. Feel the power.\nopt.right.label [9c18f9] = 留着它。感受这份力量。\n# en: The mark pulses with every heartbeat. Spells come easier now. Much easier. That should probably worry you more than it does.\nopt.right.say.1 [49d379] = 印记随着每一次心跳搏动。现在施法容易多了。容易太多了。这本该比现在更让你担心才对。\n\n@event hs_grey_offer\n# en: An Offer in Grey\ntitle [72fb9a] = 灰色的提议\n# en: The man in grey is waiting at your library table. \"You've been asking questions about the Star,\" he says pleasantly. \"It's noticed you. It could make you extraordinary. All it wants is for someone to open a door.\"\ntext [8e2f80] = 灰衣人正等在你的图书馆座位上。“你一直在打听那颗星，”他和颜悦色地说，“它注意到你了。它能让你非同凡响。它想要的，只是有人去打开一扇门。”\n# en: A grey-coated man sitting uninvited at a library table, a black velvet box open in front of him, the candles around him burning blue.\nscene [252bba] = 一个穿灰大衣的男人不请自来地坐在图书馆桌旁，面前打开着一只黑丝绒盒子，周围的蜡烛燃着蓝色火焰。\n# en: \"Get out.\"\nopt.left.label [f967c3] = “滚出去。”\n# en: He inclines his head and leaves. The candles return to yellow. You realise your hands are trembling.\nopt.left.say.1 [0a3723] = 他欠了欠身，离开了。蜡烛恢复了黄色。你这才发现自己的手在发抖。\n# en: \"...What would it give me?\"\nopt.right.label [d466af] = “……它能给我什么？”\n# en: He shows you, just for a moment. Power, endless and cold. When he's gone, you can still taste it.\nopt.right.say.1 [5c20e2] = 他给你看了，只有一瞬间。力量，无穷无尽，冰冷彻骨。他走后，你仍能尝到那股滋味。\n\n@event hs_grey_invitation\n# en: A Seat at the Northern Table\ntitle [c52641] = 北境之席\n# en: There is a letter on your pillow, sealed with a black star. The handwriting is beautiful. \"The families have watched you with great interest,\" it says. \"There is a seat for you at our table, when the door is opened. Come to the boathouse at midnight, or burn this. Either is an answer.\"\ntext [13f2ac] = 你的枕头上放着一封信，用一枚黑色的星封着蜡。字迹很漂亮。“世家怀着极大的兴趣关注着你，”信上写道，“门打开之时，我们的席上会有你一个座位。午夜到船屋来，或者烧了这封信。两者都是答复。”\n# en: A black-edged letter on a dormitory pillow, its wax seal pressed with a star that has no light in it.\nscene [f56301] = 宿舍枕头上一封镶黑边的信，封蜡上压着一颗不发光的星。\n# en: Go to the boathouse\nopt.left.label [526aa1] = 去船屋\n# en: The man in grey is waiting with two cups of something hot. He talks to you as an equal for an hour. You come back with frost in your hair and the feeling of having been measured, and found to fit.\nopt.left.say.1 [c84e12] = 灰衣人端着两杯热饮在等你。他像对待平辈一样和你谈了一个小时。你回来时头发上沾着霜，觉得自己被丈量过，而且尺寸正合适。\n# en: Burn it\nopt.right.label [162285] = 烧掉它\n# en: The paper burns blue and slow, and smells of the lake. It takes a long time to go out. Climbing back from where you've been is slower still, but this is a step.\nopt.right.say.1 [dfcc08] = 信纸燃起蓝色的火，烧得很慢，散发着湖水的气味。它很久才烧尽。从你走到的地方爬回来还要更慢，但这是一步。\n\n@event hs_grey_tests\n# en: The Good Argument\ntitle [e22d78] = 好听的道理\n# en: The man in grey falls into step beside you outside the infirmary. \"You help people,\" he says pleasantly. \"I admire it. But think how many more you could help with real power. The Star doesn't care what it's used for. Only that someone uses it.\"\ntext [9adfef] = 灰衣人在医务室外与你并肩而行。“你帮助别人，”他和气地说，“我很欣赏。可想想看，若有真正的力量，你能多帮多少人。星不在乎它被用来做什么，只在乎有人用它。”\n# en: A grey-coated man standing beside a student at the infirmary window, both looking down at a first-year with a bandaged arm.\nscene [86ebbc] = 一个穿灰外套的男人和一名学生并肩站在医务室窗前，一起低头看着一个手臂缠着绷带的一年级生。\n# en: \"No.\"\nopt.left.label [371a7a] = “不。”\n# en: \"No,\" you say. He smiles as if you'd said something charming, and leaves. The first-year in the window waves at you. You wave back, and your hand is shaking.\nopt.left.say.1 [ce8c7e] = “不，”你说。他笑了笑，仿佛你说了句讨人喜欢的话，然后离开了。窗边的一年级生朝你挥手。你也挥手，手在发抖。\n# en: \"...How many more?\"\nopt.right.label [faa864] = “……能多帮多少？”\n# en: He tells you. The numbers are very large and very cold. That night, you dream of the lake from underneath, and you are not frightened in the dream.\nopt.right.say.1 [3239c8] = 他告诉了你。那些数字非常大，也非常冷。那天夜里，你梦见从湖底往上看，而梦里的你并不害怕。\n\n@event hs_wardens_notice\n# en: The Headmistress Takes Note\ntitle [f7baa8] = 院长记下了你\n# en: On the grand stair the headmistress stops beside you. \"Several people have told me about you,\" she says, \"and not one of them was complaining. That is rare.\" Her silver ring catches the light. \"I keep a list of students I would trust with a door. Don't let it go to your head.\"\ntext [aef554] = 在大楼梯上，院长在你身旁停下。“好几个人跟我提起过你，”她说，“没有一个是来告状的。这很少见。”她的银戒指映着光。“我有一份名单，上面是我愿意托付一扇门的学生。别因此得意忘形。”\n# en: A tall woman in dark robes pausing on a stair to look down at one student among many, a silver ring glinting on her hand.\nscene [25a5bf] = 一位身着深色长袍的高个女人在楼梯上停步，低头看着众多学生中的一个，手上的银戒指闪着光。\n# en: \"I'd be honoured.\"\nopt.left.label [bec864] = “我深感荣幸。”\n# en: \"Good,\" she says. \"Honour is a heavy thing to carry. Practise.\" She walks on. You stand on the stair for some time.\nopt.left.say.1 [e74b53] = “很好，”她说，“荣誉是件沉重的东西。多练练。”她继续往前走。你在楼梯上站了好一会儿。\n# en: \"What kind of door?\"\nopt.right.label [ee5679] = “什么样的门？”\n# en: Her short, delighted laugh. \"The kind that ought to stay shut,\" she says. \"Keep asking questions like that. Just not on the stairs.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [c568f6] = 她发出那声短促而愉快的笑。“应该一直关着的那种，”她说，“继续问这样的问题吧。只是别在楼梯上问。”\n\n@event hs_seventh_tower\n# en: The Seventh Tower\ntitle [44b8dc] = 第七塔\n# en: The seventh tower has been locked for a century. You find a way in. At the very top, a single room: dust, moonlight, and a star-shaped hollow in the wall, holding something that does not reflect any light.\ntext [9081b5] = 第七塔已经锁了一个世纪。你找到了进去的路。塔顶只有一个房间：灰尘、月光，墙上一个星形的凹槽，里面放着某样不反射任何光的东西。\n# en: The top of a narrow, abandoned tower, cobwebbed and moonlit, with a wall bearing a star-shaped recess.\nscene [c775ba] = 一座狭窄废塔的顶层，蛛网密布，月光洒入，墙上有一个星形凹槽。\n# en: Take it quietly\nopt.left.label [2a0b10] = 悄悄拿走\n# en: A key shaped like a star, black as a hole in the world. The tower shudders as you lift it, then is still.\nopt.left.say.1 [3a088a] = 一把星形的钥匙，黑得像世界上的一个洞。你拿起它时，塔身一震，随即归于寂静。\n# en: A ward shrieks. A prefect finds you on the stairs. You get detention and a very long look from the headmistress.\nopt.left.say.2 [ca0973] = 防护咒尖叫起来。一位级长在楼梯上逮住了你。你被关了禁闭，还挨了院长很长很长的一眼。\n# en: Take it boldly\nopt.right.label [f86c9d] = 强行拿走\n# en: You tear through the ward with brute force. The key comes free. Every candle in the academy goes out for one second.\nopt.right.say.1 [3d7abb] = 你凭蛮力撕开了防护咒。钥匙脱落下来。学院里每一支蜡烛都熄灭了一秒钟。\n# en: The ward throws you down two flights of stairs.\nopt.right.say.2 [1014e6] = 防护咒把你甩下了两层楼梯。\n\n@event hs_isolde_summons\n# en: The Headmistress Knows\ntitle [214bdc] = 院长知道了\n# en: The headmistress summons you to her tower. \"You have been following the same thread I have,\" she says. \"On the night of the Blossom moon, the seal will weaken. I need people I can trust beneath the lake. Or you can walk away. No one would blame you.\"\ntext [aa0d92] = 院长把你召进她的塔楼。“你一直在追寻和我同一条线索，”她说，“花潮之月那一夜，封印会减弱。我需要可以信任的人到湖底去。你也可以选择离开。没有人会怪你。”\n# en: A circular study at the top of a tower, walls lined with sleeping portraits, the headmistress standing by a window that looks down on the lake.\nscene [8d69ed] = 塔顶的圆形书房，墙上挂满沉睡的肖像，院长站在俯瞰湖面的窗前。\n# en: Walk away\nopt.left.label [96da1a] = 离开\n# en: \"Wise,\" she says, and you can't tell if she means it. \"Then stay above, and keep the first-years indoors.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [00df9c] = “明智。”她说，你分不清她是不是真心的。“那就留在上面，让一年级生待在屋里。”\n# en: \"I'll be there.\"\nopt.right.label [d06338] = “我会去的。”\n# en: She presses something cold into your hand: a silver ward-ring. \"Then learn quickly. You have until Blossomtide.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [c9289c] = 她把一样冰凉的东西按进你手心：一枚银色的守护戒指。“那就学快点。你只有到花潮月为止的时间。”\n\n@event hs_the_night\n# en: The Night the Lake Froze\ntitle [6f7fcc] = 湖面冰封之夜\n# en: In the middle of spring, the lake freezes in a single breath. Cracks glow beneath the ice. The bell under the water is tolling, and every candle in the academy leans toward the shore.\ntext [9423a3] = 正值春天，湖面却在一口气之间冻结了。冰下的裂缝发着光。湖底的钟在敲响，学院里每一支蜡烛都朝着湖岸倾斜。\n# en: The whole lake frozen white in the middle of spring, cracks glowing from beneath, students crowding the towers' windows.\nscene [b58de9] = 春日里整个湖面冻得雪白，冰下裂缝透出光芒，学生们挤在塔楼的窗前。\n# en: Go down beneath the lake\nopt.left.label [983634] = 潜入湖底\n# en: You run for the boathouse. Behind you, someone is shouting your name. You don't stop.\nopt.left.say.1 [28f348] = 你朝船屋奔去。身后有人在喊你的名字。你没有停下。\n# en: Stay above and protect the students\nopt.right.label [3d4500] = 留在上面保护学生\n# en: You hold the doors of the first-year tower all night against things made of frost and whispers. By dawn the lake thaws. Something below is still asleep — for now.\nopt.right.say.1 [cee4e5] = 你整夜守着一年级塔楼的大门，抵挡由冰霜与低语构成的东西。黎明时湖面解冻了。下面的那个东西仍在沉睡——暂时。\n\n@event hs_beneath\n# en: The Door Beneath the Lake\ntitle [76c2d3] = 湖底之门\n# en: The tunnel ends at the round door. It stands open a crack. Through it, the Hollow Star looks at you — and it is beautiful, and it is hungry, and it knows your name.{=flag(kael_ally) and \" Kael is beside you, wand raised, frost spreading from his feet.\" or \"\"}\ntext [21fa28] = 隧道的尽头是那扇圆门。它开着一道缝。透过门缝，空洞之星在看着你——它很美，它很饥饿，它知道你的名字。{=flag(kael_ally) and \"凯尔就在你身旁，魔杖高举，冰霜从他脚下蔓延开来。\" or \"\"}\n# en: A vast domed cavern under the lake, a round door of black stone standing open a crack, and through it a star that is pure absence.\nscene [d3244b] = 湖底一座巨大的穹形洞窟，一扇黑石圆门开着一道缝，门后是一颗纯粹由“缺失”构成的星。\n# en: Seal the door\nopt.seal.label [b5b84d] = 封印这扇门\n# en: Needs the star key or the founders' secret. Arcana; preparation makes it easier.\nopt.seal.hint [24b520] = 需要星钥或创始者的秘密。奥术；准备越充分越容易。\n# en: You speak the founders' words and the door grinds shut. The Star screams without sound. Above, the ice cracks and the lake breathes again.\nopt.seal.say.1 [059bd9] = 你念出创始者的咒文，门轰隆隆地合上了。星无声地尖叫。上方，冰层碎裂，湖重新开始呼吸。\n# en: Your seal falters — and Kael throws his own strength into it, every ounce of frost in him. The door closes. He doesn't wake for three days.\nopt.seal.say.2 [ba547d] = 你的封印动摇了——凯尔把他自己的力量投了进去，把体内每一分寒冰都押上。门关上了。他昏迷了三天才醒。\n# en: The seal holds, barely, and takes most of you with it. You wake in the infirmary a week later.\nopt.seal.say.3 [c252f4] = 封印勉强守住了，也几乎带走了你的全部。一周后，你在医务室里醒来。\n# en: You speak the founders' words and the door grinds shut. The Star screams without sound. Above, the ice cracks and the lake breathes again.\nopt.seal.say.4 [059bd9] = 你念出创始者的咒文，门轰隆隆地合上了。星无声地尖叫。上方，冰层碎裂，湖重新开始呼吸。\n# en: Your seal falters — and Kael throws his own strength into it, every ounce of frost in him. The door closes. He doesn't wake for three days.\nopt.seal.say.5 [ba547d] = 你的封印动摇了——凯尔把他自己的力量投了进去，把体内每一分寒冰都押上。门关上了。他昏迷了三天才醒。\n# en: The seal holds, barely, and takes most of you with it. You wake in the infirmary a week later.\nopt.seal.say.6 [c252f4] = 封印勉强守住了，也几乎带走了你的全部。一周后，你在医务室里醒来。\n# en: You speak the founders' words and the door grinds shut. The Star screams without sound. Above, the ice cracks and the lake breathes again.\nopt.seal.say.7 [059bd9] = 你念出创始者的咒文，门轰隆隆地合上了。星无声地尖叫。上方，冰层碎裂，湖重新开始呼吸。\n# en: Your seal falters — and Kael throws his own strength into it, every ounce of frost in him. The door closes. He doesn't wake for three days.\nopt.seal.say.8 [ba547d] = 你的封印动摇了——凯尔把他自己的力量投了进去，把体内每一分寒冰都押上。门关上了。他昏迷了三天才醒。\n# en: The seal holds, barely, and takes most of you with it. You wake in the infirmary a week later.\nopt.seal.say.9 [c252f4] = 封印勉强守住了，也几乎带走了你的全部。一周后，你在医务室里醒来。\n# en: Accept the Star's bargain\nopt.bargain.label [3a1372] = 接受星的交易\n# en: Power beyond measure. A voice in your head forever. Only for those it has already tempted.\nopt.bargain.hint [8c7575] = 无法估量的力量。脑海里永远多一个声音。只给已经被它诱惑过的人。\n# en: You take what it offers, and it feels like coming home. The door closes on its own, for now. The Star settles into the back of your mind like a cat finding a warm lap.\nopt.bargain.say.1 [a6cf5a] = 你收下了它给的东西，那感觉像是回到了家。门自己关上了——暂时。星在你脑海深处安顿下来，像一只猫找到了温暖的膝头。\n# en: You take what it offers. The door closes on its own, for now. The Star settles into the back of your mind like a cat finding a warm lap, and you lie awake for a week.\nopt.bargain.say.2 [3945ab] = 你收下了它给的东西。门自己关上了——暂时。星在你脑海深处安顿下来，像一只猫找到了温暖的膝头，而你一连一周都睡不着。\n# en: Open the door\nopt.open.label [6f1706] = 打开这扇门\n# en: It wants out. Only one already deep in the Hollow could walk through beside it.\nopt.open.hint [55783a] = 它想出来。只有早已深陷空洞的人，才能与它并肩走过这扇门。\n# en: You set your palm on the black stone and push. The door swings wide. The Star rises past you like a breath held for three hundred years, and on its way out it touches your shoulder, the way a lord touches a sword. Above, every candle in Lumenhold goes out, and does not come back on until dawn.\nopt.open.say.1 [35b189] = 你把手掌按在黑石上，用力一推。门豁然洞开。星从你身边升起，像一口憋了三百年的气；它离开时碰了碰你的肩，如同领主以剑轻触骑士。在上方，流明堡的每一支蜡烛都熄灭了，直到天亮才重新亮起。\n# en: Run\nopt.flee.label [57e66a] = 逃跑\n# en: You run until the tunnel spits you out onto the shore. Behind you, the door is still ajar. In the morning, the headmistress's hair has gone white.\nopt.flee.say.1 [3778d7] = 你一路狂奔，直到隧道把你吐在岸边。身后，那扇门仍半开着。第二天早上，院长的头发全白了。\n\n@event hs_aftermath\n# en: After the Thaw\ntitle [a5fec6] = 解冻之后\n# en: The lake glitters as if nothing happened. Most of the academy believes there was a strange spring frost. The headmistress knows better. So does Kael, who finds you on the jetty with two cups of tea.\ntext [983d15] = 湖面波光粼粼，仿佛什么都没发生过。学院里大多数人以为那只是一场奇怪的春霜。院长知道真相。凯尔也知道——他端着两杯茶，在栈桥上找到了你。\n# en: The lake glittering in spring sunshine, students skimming stones, a bandaged figure sitting on the jetty.\nscene [c9174b] = 春日阳光下波光粼粼的湖面，学生们在打水漂，一个缠着绷带的身影坐在栈桥上。\n# en: Sit with him in silence\nopt.left.label [ed0b65] = 和他静静坐着\n# en: You watch the water. It's just water, now. Kael's tea, for once, doesn't freeze.\nopt.left.say.1 [c6b7cf] = 你们看着湖水。现在，它只是水而已。凯尔的茶，难得地没有结冰。\n# en: Tell him how you feel\nopt.right.label [cb477f] = 告诉他你的心意\n# en: He kisses you, and for the first time since you've known him, his hands are warm.\nopt.right.say.1 [b9bd38] = 他吻了你。认识他以来第一次，他的手是温暖的。\n# en: \"I owe you everything,\" he says. \"I'm not good at owing people. I'll learn.\"\nopt.right.say.2 [f06ead] = “我欠你一切，”他说，“我不擅长欠人情。我会学的。”\n\n@event hs_kael_sister\n# en: The Sister\ntitle [e9e230] = 妹妹\n# en: A girl in white furs is waiting at the gates in a private snowstorm. Kael's younger sister, Ilse. \"If my brother won't open it,\" she says, \"I will. Unless you can give me a reason not to.\"\ntext [f83d6e] = 一个身穿白色毛皮的女孩等在校门口，一场只属于她的暴风雪围着她下。凯尔的妹妹，伊尔泽。“如果我哥哥不肯打开它，”她说，“我来。除非你能给我一个不这么做的理由。”\n# en: A girl in white furs standing at the academy gates in a snowstorm that is falling only on her.\nscene [695730] = 一个身穿白色毛皮的女孩站在学院大门前，一场只落在她身上的暴风雪。\n# en: Convince her\nopt.left.label [089b27] = 说服她\n# en: You talk for an hour. Then she's crying, and Kael is holding her, and the snow stops. She stays in the village to be near him.\nopt.left.say.1 [43c937] = 你们谈了一个小时。然后她哭了，凯尔抱住了她，雪停了。她留在镇上，好离哥哥近一些。\n# en: She listens politely and leaves. \"We'll see each other again,\" she says. \"Beneath the water.\"\nopt.left.say.2 [905bac] = 她礼貌地听完，走了。“我们还会再见的，”她说，“在水下。”\n# en: Warn the headmistress\nopt.right.label [084aca] = 警告院长\n# en: Ilse is escorted from the grounds. Kael understands. He says he understands. He's quiet for a long time.\nopt.right.say.1 [1fbc19] = 伊尔泽被护送出了校园。凯尔理解。他说他理解。他沉默了很久。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/chain_ilse.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/chain_ilse.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event ilse_village\n# en: The Girl at the Bakery\ntitle [a7ff00] = 面包店里的女孩\n# en: In the village bakery a girl in white furs is trying to pay for a bun with a coin so cold it has frozen to the counter. The baker is losing patience. You recognise those eyes: they're Kael's. \"Ilse,\" she says, before you ask. \"Don't.\"\ntext [7c5df9] = 村里的面包店里，一个穿白色毛皮的女孩正想用一枚硬币买个小面包——那硬币冷得已经冻在了柜台上。面包师快没耐心了。你认得那双眼睛：是凯尔的。“伊尔泽，”不等你开口她就说，“别问。”\n# en: A village bakery counter, a pale girl in white furs pushing a coin that is visibly frosted across the wood, a baffled baker.\nscene [1cd5c0] = 村里面包店的柜台前，一个穿白色毛皮的苍白女孩把一枚明显结了霜的硬币推过木台，面包师一脸困惑。\n# en: Pay for her bun\nopt.left.label [31d334] = 替她付面包钱\n# en: She stares at the bun, then at you, as if kindness is a trick she hasn't learned the counter to yet. \"Fine,\" she says. \"Thank you. Fine.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [7b1308] = 她盯着面包，又盯着你，好像善意是一种她还没学会破解的把戏。“好吧，”她说，“谢谢。好吧。”\n# en: Leave her to it\nopt.right.label [d94459] = 随她去\n# en: You leave. Through the window you see the coin crack, and the baker's breath freeze in the air. Ilse walks out with the bun. Nobody stops her.\nopt.right.say.1 [1e970f] = 你走了。透过窗户，你看见那枚硬币裂开，面包师呼出的气在空中结成了冰。伊尔泽拿着面包走了出来。没人拦她。\n\n@event ilse_frost_lessons\n# en: A Trade in Winter\ntitle [9ac35e] = 冬日的交易\n# en: Ilse finds you by the frozen pond. \"My brother won't talk to me. You will. Tell me how he really is, and I'll teach you Northern frost-craft. It's better than anything your professors know.\"\ntext [5847bc] = 伊尔泽在结冰的池塘边找到你。“我哥哥不跟我说话。你会。告诉我他现在到底过得怎么样，我就教你北境霜术。比你那些教授懂的都强。”\n# en: A frozen pond at the edge of the woods, a girl in white furs drawing a perfect hexagon of frost in the air, a student copying her clumsily.\nscene [4361bc] = 树林边结冰的池塘，一个穿白色毛皮的女孩在空中画出一个完美的六角霜花，一名学生笨拙地跟着模仿。\n# en: Tell her the truth about Kael\nopt.left.label [63ce17] = 把凯尔的实情告诉她\n# en: You tell her about the cold hands, the roof at three in the morning, the bread. She listens without moving. Then she teaches you to freeze a falling leaf mid-air.\nopt.left.say.1 [1bc603] = 你告诉她那双冰冷的手、凌晨三点的屋顶、那些面包。她一动不动地听着。然后她教你让一片飘落的叶子在半空中冻住。\n# en: Protect Kael's privacy\nopt.right.label [4b5f67] = 守住凯尔的隐私\n# en: \"He can tell you himself,\" you say. Ilse's eyes go flat. But the next day, she's at the pond again. \"Fine. I'll teach you anyway. You'll need it.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [91c9be] = “他可以自己告诉你，”你说。伊尔泽的眼神冷了下来。但第二天，她又出现在池塘边。“行吧。我还是教你。你会用得上的。”\n\n@event ilse_grey_returns\n# en: The Man in Grey Comes for Ilse\ntitle [4eacc9] = 灰衣人来带走伊尔泽\n# en: Late at night, a lane behind the bakery. The man in grey has one hand on Ilse's shoulder. \"Home,\" he is saying softly. \"Your family is waiting. The door is waiting.\" Ilse sees you. She doesn't call out. She just looks.\ntext [4c620a] = 深夜，面包店后面的小巷。灰衣人一只手搭在伊尔泽肩上。“回家吧，”他轻声说，“你的家人在等你。那扇门在等你。”伊尔泽看见了你。她没有呼救，只是看着你。\n# en: A moonlit village lane, a man in a grey coat with one hand on a girl's shoulder, frost and shadow coiling around them both.\nscene [fb6082] = 月光下的村中小巷，一个穿灰色外套的男人一只手搭在女孩肩上，霜与阴影缠绕在两人周围。\n# en: Step between them\nopt.left.label [f242e3] = 挡在他们之间\n# en: Your spell breaks his grip. He looks at his frosted hand with mild surprise, bows, and walks into the dark. Ilse is shaking. \"Nobody's ever — \" she says, and stops.\nopt.left.say.1 [f2b4a9] = 你的咒语打断了他的手。他略带惊讶地看着自己结霜的手，鞠了一躬，走进了黑暗。伊尔泽在发抖。“从来没有人——”她说，然后停住了。\n# en: He swats your spell aside like a moth. When you get up, the lane is empty except for a single white glove in the snow.\nopt.left.say.2 [eaa8cd] = 他像拍飞蛾一样拍开了你的咒语。等你爬起来，巷子里已空无一人，只有雪地上的一只白手套。\n# en: Run for Kael\nopt.right.label [839eee] = 跑去找凯尔\n# en: Kael arrives like a blizzard. The man in grey takes one look at him and leaves without a word. Brother and sister stand in the snow, not touching, not speaking.\nopt.right.say.1 [69e63e] = 凯尔像一场暴风雪般赶到。灰衣人只看了他一眼，便一言不发地离开了。兄妹俩站在雪里，没有碰触，没有说话。\n# en: By the time you're back with help, the lane is empty. There's frost on the cobbles in the shape of a closed door.\nopt.right.say.2 [8c5d56] = 等你带着帮手回来，巷子已经空了。鹅卵石上的霜结成了一扇紧闭的门的形状。\n\n@event ilse_kael_reunion\n# en: Brother and Sister\ntitle [2c92a4] = 兄与妹\n# en: Kael and Ilse have been in the same village for weeks and have spoken eleven words to each other. You have three cups of tea, a frozen jetty and a very bad idea.\ntext [210c93] = 凯尔和伊尔泽在同一个村子里待了好几个星期，一共只对彼此说了十一个字。你有三杯茶、一座结冰的栈桥，和一个非常糟糕的主意。\n# en: Two pale siblings on opposite ends of a frozen jetty, a third person between them holding three cups of tea.\nscene [d059c4] = 结冰栈桥的两端各坐着一个苍白的兄妹，中间一个人端着三杯茶。\n# en: Sit them both down\nopt.left.label [05e582] = 让他俩坐下来谈\n# en: It takes an hour and two cups of tea are frozen solid. Then Ilse says \"I was scared,\" and Kael says \"So was I,\" and you quietly leave them to it.\nopt.left.say.1 [75d491] = 花了一个钟头，有两杯茶冻成了冰坨。然后伊尔泽说：“我很害怕。”凯尔说：“我也是。”你悄悄离开，把时间留给他们。\n# en: They argue in a language you don't speak, at a temperature you can't endure. But they argue — which, you suspect, is a start.\nopt.left.say.2 [299f28] = 他们用一种你听不懂的语言、在一种你受不了的温度里吵架。但他们在吵——你猜，这算是个开始。\n# en: Let them find their own way\nopt.right.label [a54eb8] = 让他们自己找到路\n# en: You leave the tea on the jetty. A week later you see them walking by the lake together, a careful arm's length apart. Then less.\nopt.right.say.1 [79a710] = 你把茶留在栈桥上。一星期后，你看见他们一起沿着湖边散步，小心地隔着一臂远。后来就没那么远了。\n\n@event ilse_northern_letter\n# en: A Summons From the North\ntitle [2572c4] = 来自北方的召唤\n# en: A letter with a black-star seal. The Dumont family summons Ilse home. \"If I don't go, they'll send someone worse than the man in grey,\" she says. \"If I do go, I could tell them to their faces that it's over. Or they could keep me.\"\ntext [97b031] = 一封盖着黑星封印的信。杜蒙家族召伊尔泽回家。“如果我不去，他们会派一个比灰衣人更糟的人来，”她说，“如果我去了，我可以当面告诉他们一切都结束了。或者，他们会把我留下。”\n# en: A girl holding a letter sealed with a black star, standing at a window that looks north toward far white mountains.\nscene [4e2efd] = 一个女孩手握盖着黑星封印的信，站在一扇朝北的窗前，远处是白茫茫的群山。\n# en: \"Go. Tell them it's over.\"\nopt.left.label [6759bb] = “去吧，告诉他们结束了。”\n# en: She leaves on the dawn barge, chin up. Before she goes, she presses her forehead to yours, a Northern farewell. \"If I'm not back by graduation,\" she says, \"come and get me.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [fae690] = 她坐黎明的驳船走了，下巴抬得高高的。走之前，她把额头贴在你额头上——北境的告别礼。“如果毕业前我没回来，”她说，“就来接我。”\n# en: \"Stay. Let them come.\"\nopt.right.label [ef10a1] = “留下，让他们来。”\n# en: She stays. She sews a sliver of winter into your collar \"in case they do come\". Nobody does. Not yet.\nopt.right.say.1 [3114b6] = 她留了下来。她把一缕冬天缝进你的衣领，“万一他们真的来了”。没有人来。至少现在还没有。\n\n@event ilse_farewell\n# en: Snow in Blossomtide\ntitle [224b90] = 花潮月的雪\n# en: It's spring, the orchard is in full blossom, and it is snowing — gently, over one tree. Ilse is underneath it. \"Graduation's soon,\" she says. \"Everyone's leaving. I wanted to give you something before you forgot me.\"\ntext [ccd97c] = 春天，果园里繁花盛开，却下起了雪——轻轻地，只落在一棵树上。伊尔泽就站在树下。“快毕业了，”她说，“大家都要走了。我想在你忘记我之前送你点东西。”\n# en: A single snowflake falling in a spring orchard full of blossom, a girl in white furs catching it on her glove and holding it out.\nscene [63dad1] = 繁花盛开的春日果园里飘落一片雪花，一个穿白色毛皮的女孩用手套接住它，递了过来。\n# en: \"I won't forget you.\"\nopt.left.label [6d1b23] = “我不会忘记你的。”\n# en: She doesn't believe you, but she smiles anyway — the first real smile you've seen on her. She gives you a snowflake that never melts.\nopt.left.say.1 [48bf7c] = 她不相信你，但还是笑了——你第一次在她脸上看到真正的笑容。她送给你一片永不融化的雪花。\n# en: Invite her to the graduation\nopt.right.label [d72a91] = 邀请她来参加毕业典礼\n# en: \"Me? At a graduation?\" She looks horrified, and then, slowly, delighted. On the day, she sits next to Kael in the second row and cries more than anyone.\nopt.right.say.1 [55ef3a] = “我？去毕业典礼？”她一脸惊恐，然后，慢慢地，变成了欣喜。那天，她坐在第二排凯尔身边，哭得比谁都厉害。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/chains_minor.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/chains_minor.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event forbidden_section_1\n# en: The Silver Key\ntitle [f6e05e] = 银钥匙\n# en: Midnight. The silver key fits the Restricted Section door perfectly. Beyond it, shelves of black-bound books breathe softly in the dark. Somewhere, a prefect's footsteps.\ntext [2c9862] = 午夜。银钥匙严丝合缝地插进了禁书区的门锁。门后，一排排黑皮书在黑暗中轻轻呼吸。不知哪里，传来级长的脚步声。\n# en: A heavy iron door at the end of a library aisle, a silver key turning in its lock, a candle held low.\nscene [f9b84e] = 图书馆过道尽头一扇沉重的铁门，一把银钥匙在锁孔里转动，一支蜡烛压得很低。\n# en: Turn back\nopt.left.label [2f9f2d] = 回头\n# en: You lock the door again and hang the key on a nail in your wardrobe. Some nights you hear it rattling.\nopt.left.say.1 [9742c9] = 你把门重新锁好，把钥匙挂在衣柜里的钉子上。有些夜里，你能听见它在哗啦作响。\n# en: Slip inside\nopt.right.label [316541] = 溜进去\n# en: You spend two hours reading by candle-stub. You leave with a head full of things no first-year should know.\nopt.right.say.1 [89e72d] = 你借着蜡烛头读了两个小时。离开时，脑子里装满了一年级生不该知道的东西。\n# en: Bram Hollis is waiting by the door when you come out. He is smiling. It's the worst smile you've ever seen.\nopt.right.say.2 [84c17d] = 你出来时，布拉姆·霍利斯正等在门边。他在笑。那是你见过最糟糕的笑容。\n\n@event forbidden_section_2\n# en: The Codex of Hollow Names\ntitle [b71797] = 《空洞名录》\n# en: On your third visit, a book finds you. It falls from a high shelf and lands open at your feet: the Codex of Hollow Names. The first page is a list of names. The last one is still being written. It's yours.\ntext [ced2cf] = 第三次来时，一本书找上了你。它从高高的书架上掉下来，摊开落在你脚边：《空洞名录》。第一页是一串名字。最后一个名字还在书写中。是你的名字。\n# en: A book bound in something that is not leather, open on a reading stand, its letters slowly rearranging themselves.\nscene [a07d02] = 一本用某种不是皮革的东西装订的书摊在阅读架上，字母正缓缓重新排列。\n# en: Put it back\nopt.left.label [862ed2] = 把它放回去\n# en: You shelve it. When you look back, it has fallen again, open at the same page.\nopt.left.say.1 [5dc723] = 你把它放回书架。回头一看，它又掉了下来，摊开在同一页。\n# en: Read it\nopt.right.label [fb8482] = 读它\n# en: You read until dawn. You learn about the thing under the lake. When you finally close the book, your fingertips are grey.\nopt.right.say.1 [f6835c] = 你一直读到天亮。你了解了湖底的那个东西。终于合上书时，你的指尖变成了灰色。\n# en: You read until dawn and close the book with shaking hands. Some things you can't unread.\nopt.right.say.2 [c47a6e] = 你一直读到天亮，用颤抖的手合上了书。有些东西，读过就再也忘不掉。\n\n@event forbidden_section_3\n# en: Caught\ntitle [63a445] = 被抓了\n# en: A lantern blazes in the doorway. Not a prefect this time: the headmistress herself. She looks at the book in your hands, then at you, for a very long time. \"Most students who find that book,\" she says, \"don't put it down.\"\ntext [cff27d] = 门口亮起一盏灯笼。这次不是级长：是院长本人。她看了看你手里的书，又看了看你，看了很久很久。“大多数找到那本书的学生，”她说，“都放不下它。”\n# en: A tall silhouette in the doorway of the Restricted Section, holding a lantern that casts long shadows over a startled student.\nscene [21af3f] = 禁书区门口一个高大的剪影，手中提灯在受惊的学生身上投下长长的影子。\n# en: Apologise and accept punishment\nopt.left.label [17d414] = 道歉，接受惩罚\n# en: She takes the key. \"Detention. And come to my tower on Thursdays. If you're going to learn dangerous things, you'll learn them properly.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [8e592f] = 她收走了钥匙。“禁闭。还有，每周四到我的塔楼来。既然你要学危险的东西，就该学得像样。”\n# en: Ask her what it means\nopt.right.label [a565a0] = 问她这意味着什么\n# en: A long pause. \"It means something is waking, and it is looking for help. Make sure it doesn't find yours.\" She takes the key and leaves you the candle.\nopt.right.say.1 [e927be] = 长久的沉默。“意味着有东西在苏醒，而且它在寻找帮手。别让它找到你的帮助。”她拿走了钥匙，把蜡烛留给了你。\n\n@event curse_cure_1\n# en: A Cold That Won't Leave\ntitle [a74f1f] = 驱不散的寒意\n# en: The cold under your skin is getting worse. Sister Amaranth's tonics do nothing. She looks troubled. \"This isn't an illness. It's a hunger.\" She lowers her voice. \"Old Maud might know something. Don't tell anyone I said so.\"\ntext [362396] = 皮肤下的寒意越来越重了。阿玛兰斯修女的药剂毫无作用。她面露忧色。“这不是病。是一种饥饿。”她压低声音，“老莫德也许知道些什么。别说是我说的。”\n# en: A student wrapped in three blankets beside a roaring fire, breath still fogging the air, fingertips grey.\nscene [ecf450] = 一个学生裹着三层毯子坐在熊熊炉火旁，呼出的气仍凝成白雾，指尖发灰。\n# en: Endure it\nopt.left.label [9e8205] = 忍着\n# en: You pile on blankets and keep going. The cold whispers sometimes. You pretend not to hear.\nopt.left.say.1 [cbcba6] = 你堆上毯子，继续过日子。寒意有时会低语。你假装没听见。\n# en: Go to Old Maud\nopt.right.label [fb88bc] = 去找老莫德\n# en: Maud takes one look at your fingertips and puts the kettle on. \"Moonpetal,\" she says. \"Grows in the Whisperwood. Only blooms at midnight. You'll fetch it yourself — the cure has to come from the cursed.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [fa96e3] = 莫德看了一眼你的指尖，就把水壶放上了炉子。“月瓣花，”她说，“长在低语森林里。只在午夜开。你得自己去采——解药必须来自被诅咒的人。”\n\n@event curse_cure_2\n# en: Moonpetal\ntitle [676f04] = 月瓣花\n# en: The Whisperwood after dark. Things move between the trees. In a clearing, a single silver flower is opening, and between you and it stands a stag made of shadow.\ntext [b47cd6] = 天黑后的低语森林。树林间有东西在移动。一片空地上，一朵银色的花正在绽放，而你和它之间，站着一头由阴影构成的雄鹿。\n# en: A dark forest at midnight, a single silver flower glowing in a clearing, eyes watching from between the trees.\nscene [17f216] = 午夜漆黑的森林，空地上一朵银花发着光，树影间有眼睛在窥视。\n# en: Sneak around it\nopt.left.label [efbf19] = 绕过它\n# en: You creep through the bracken and pluck the moonpetal. Maud's brew tastes like moonlight and burnt sugar. The cold is gone.\nopt.left.say.1 [1fb52a] = 你悄悄穿过蕨丛，摘下了月瓣花。莫德熬的药尝起来像月光和焦糖。寒意消失了。\n# en: The stag sees you. You run. No moonpetal tonight; you'll have to come back.\nopt.left.say.2 [f03c88] = 雄鹿看见了你。你拔腿就跑。今晚没有月瓣花了；你得再来一趟。\n# en: Face the stag\nopt.right.label [c629ba] = 直面雄鹿\n# en: You stand your ground. The stag lowers its head — and steps aside. Maud's brew drives the cold out of your bones.\nopt.right.say.1 [eecd0a] = 你站定不动。雄鹿低下头——然后让开了路。莫德的药把寒意从你骨子里驱了出去。\n# en: The stag charges. You wake up in the bracken at dawn. Maud bandages you. \"Another night,\" she says.\nopt.right.say.2 [056977] = 雄鹿冲了过来。黎明时你在蕨丛里醒来。莫德给你包扎。“改天晚上再来。”她说。\n\n@event grisk_offer\n# en: A Helpful Goblin\ntitle [6c54bd] = 乐于助人的地精\n# en: A goblin in a velvet waistcoat falls into step beside you. \"Grisk,\" he says, \"friend to the temporarily embarrassed. Sixty crowns, today. Twelve a month for six months. Very reasonable.\"\ntext [266d6d] = 一个穿天鹅绒马甲的地精走到你身边，和你并肩同行。“格里斯克，”他说，“暂时手头紧的人的朋友。今天给你六十克朗。每月还十二，还六个月。非常合理。”\n# en: A small goblin in a velvet waistcoat and pince-nez, sitting on a crate in an alley, a ledger in one hand and a quill behind his ear.\nscene [a36b2a] = 一个穿天鹅绒马甲、戴夹鼻眼镜的小地精坐在小巷的木箱上，一手拿着账簿，耳后夹着羽毛笔。\n# en: No thank you\nopt.left.label [6c52d1] = 不用了，谢谢\n# en: \"My door is always open,\" Grisk says. You suspect it is actually always locked.\nopt.left.say.1 [1a3e29] = “我的门永远为你敞开。”格里斯克说。你怀疑它其实永远锁着。\n# en: Take the loan\nopt.right.label [e30491] = 接受贷款\n# en: He counts out sixty crowns, writes your name in the ledger and smiles with a great many teeth.\nopt.right.say.1 [35d10d] = 他数出六十克朗，把你的名字写进账簿，笑着露出了非常多的牙齿。\n\n@event grisk_board_debt\n# en: Settled, With Interest\ntitle [3033c4] = 已结清，外加利息\n# en: The bursar's office has stopped sending reminders. Instead, Grisk is waiting outside it with a receipt. \"Two months' board, paid in full,\" he beams. \"By me. A loan, naturally. Sixty crowns, twelve a month for six months. You can sign here.\"\ntext [32b737] = 财务处已经不再寄催缴单了。取而代之的是，格里斯克拿着一张收据等在门外。“两个月的伙食费，全额付清，”他笑容满面，“我付的。当然是借款。六十克朗，每月十二，还六个月。在这儿签字就行。”\n# en: A goblin in a velvet waistcoat waiting outside the bursar's office, waving a receipt like a small flag.\nscene [c90a78] = 一个穿天鹅绒马甲的地精等在财务处门外，像挥小旗一样挥着一张收据。\n# en: Sign his ledger\nopt.left.label [d542c5] = 在他的账本上签字\n# en: You sign. He counts the change into your palm and writes your name in the ledger with a flourish that has teeth in it.\nopt.left.say.1 [6f886e] = 你签了字。他把找零数进你的掌心，在账本上龙飞凤舞地写下你的名字——那一笔带着獠牙。\n# en: Beg the bursar for time\nopt.right.label [ba6715] = 去求财务处宽限\n# en: You spend an afternoon on a hard bench outside the bursar's office and leave with a month's grace and the feeling of having been looked at over spectacles for a very long time.\nopt.right.say.1 [1267b3] = 你在财务处门外的硬板凳上坐了一下午，最后换来一个月的宽限，以及被人从眼镜上方久久打量的感觉。\n\n@event grisk_favour\n# en: Grisk's Proposition\ntitle [5cdd5b] = 格里斯克的提议\n# en: Grisk finds you in the library. \"I could cancel your debt,\" he murmurs. \"A small favour. There's a book in the archive — the Founders' Ledger. A client wants a page. Just one page.\"\ntext [816cbf] = 格里斯克在图书馆找到了你。“我可以免了你的债，”他低声说，“帮个小忙就行。档案库里有本书——《创始者账簿》。有位客户想要其中一页。就一页。”\n# en: The goblin moneylender leaning close in a library alcove, pushing a slip of paper across a table.\nscene [64ffac] = 地精放贷人在图书馆的壁龛里凑近，把一张纸条推过桌面。\n# en: Refuse\nopt.left.label [cada35] = 拒绝\n# en: \"Pity.\" He makes a note in his ledger. It looks expensive.\nopt.left.say.1 [84fdf0] = “可惜。”他在账簿上记了一笔。看起来代价不小。\n# en: Do the favour\nopt.right.label [c95c1c] = 帮这个忙\n# en: You razor a page from the oldest ledger in the archive. Grisk tears up your debt. You notice the page had a black star on it.\nopt.right.say.1 [3798eb] = 你从档案库最古老的账簿上割下了一页。格里斯克撕掉了你的欠条。你注意到那一页上有一颗黑星。\n# en: The archivist catches you with a razor in your hand. Detention. Grisk is nowhere to be found, and the debt stands.\nopt.right.say.2 [17b625] = 档案管理员撞见你手里拿着刀片。禁闭。格里斯克不见踪影，债务照旧。\n\n@event grisk_smuggling\n# en: One More Job\ntitle [5823eb] = 再干一票\n# en: Grisk again. \"You were so good last time. A crate needs moving across the lake tonight. Don't open it. Thirty crowns.\"\ntext [5d6393] = 又是格里斯克。“上次你干得真漂亮。今晚有个箱子要运过湖。别打开。三十克朗。”\n# en: A crate of clinking bottles hidden under a tarp on a dark boathouse dock, a small goblin silhouetted against the lake.\nscene [539e69] = 漆黑的船屋码头上，一只装满叮当作响的瓶子的箱子藏在油布下，湖面映出一个小地精的剪影。\n# en: You're done with him\nopt.left.label [71da7e] = 你和他到此为止了\n# en: \"Everyone's done with me eventually,\" Grisk says, without rancour. You never see him again.\nopt.left.say.1 [288fa5] = “大家最后都会跟我到此为止的。”格里斯克毫无怨气地说。你再也没见过他。\n# en: Move the crate\nopt.right.label [f1cc88] = 运箱子\n# en: The crate clinks. It is also, faintly, singing. You deliver it without looking inside. Thirty crowns.\nopt.right.say.1 [a32e7f] = 箱子叮当作响。还隐约在唱歌。你没往里看就送到了。三十克朗。\n# en: Bram stops you on the dock. The crate is full of contraband charm-wine. You lose the crate, keep eight crowns and gain a detention.\nopt.right.say.2 [e81800] = 布拉姆在码头上拦住了你。箱子里全是违禁的魅惑酒。你丢了箱子，留下八克朗，换来一次禁闭。\n\n@event fox_injured\n# en: Smoke in the Woods\ntitle [bf8cb4] = 林间青烟\n# en: At the edge of the forest, a thin plume of smoke. A small fox is caught in a rusted snare, its tail smouldering like an ember. It snarls at you, then whimpers.\ntext [b3132b] = 森林边缘，升起一缕细细的烟。一只小狐狸被生锈的捕兽夹夹住了，尾巴像余烬一样冒着火星。它冲你龇牙，然后呜咽起来。\n# en: A small red fox lying in the snow at the forest edge, its tail smouldering like a dying campfire, one paw caught in a rusted snare.\nscene [b579d6] = 森林边缘的雪地里躺着一只小红狐，尾巴像将熄的篝火般闷燃，一只爪子卡在生锈的捕兽夹里。\n# en: Leave it — it's wild\nopt.left.label [d9cfe4] = 别管——它是野的\n# en: You walk away. That night you dream of smoke.\nopt.left.say.1 [6bc432] = 你走开了。那天夜里，你梦见了烟。\n# en: Free it and bandage the paw\nopt.right.label [8a0394] = 放了它，包扎爪子\n# en: It bites you, of course. But it lets you wrap the paw. Then it limps into the trees, glancing back once with eyes like embers.\nopt.right.say.1 [113ea7] = 它当然咬了你一口。但它让你把爪子包扎好了。然后它一瘸一拐地走进树林，回头看了你一眼，眼睛像余烬。\n\n@event fox_returns\n# en: A Visitor at the Window\ntitle [8da400] = 窗边的访客\n# en: Two months later, a fox with a glowing tail is sitting on your windowsill. Its paw has healed. At its feet: a small, dusty ruby.\ntext [635017] = 两个月后，一只尾巴发光的狐狸坐在你的窗台上。它的爪子已经好了。它脚边放着一颗小小的、蒙尘的红宝石。\n# en: A fox with a softly glowing tail sitting on a stone windowsill at dawn, a ruby glinting at its feet.\nscene [41ab76] = 黎明时分，一只尾巴柔柔发光的狐狸坐在石砌窗台上，脚边闪着一颗红宝石。\n# en: Shoo it away\nopt.left.label [01018d] = 把它赶走\n# en: It leaves the ruby and vanishes. You sell the ruby. Somehow you feel you've failed a test.\nopt.left.say.1 [4959d7] = 它留下红宝石就消失了。你卖掉了红宝石。不知为何，你觉得自己没通过某种考验。\n# en: Open the window\nopt.right.label [15dfe6] = 打开窗户\n# en: It hops in, inspects your room thoroughly and falls asleep on your pillow. Your pillow is now slightly scorched. You don't mind.\nopt.right.say.1 [d3c253] = 它跳进来，把你的房间仔仔细细检查了一遍，然后在你的枕头上睡着了。你的枕头现在有点焦了。你不介意。\n\n@event fox_bond\n# en: Ember\ntitle [c95de4] = 余烬\n# en: The fox has decided. {term: It follows you to class|It follows you through the half-empty corridors}, sleeps on your feet and growls at Bram. Students aren't allowed unregistered familiars. Registering one costs fifteen crowns.\ntext [ccfb40] = 狐狸已经做了决定。{term: 它跟着你去上课|它跟着你穿过半空的走廊}，睡在你脚上，还冲布拉姆低吼。学生不允许拥有未登记的魔宠。登记一只要十五克朗。\n# en: A student and a small fire-tailed fox nose to nose, a soft golden light connecting them.\nscene [f6fbd2] = 一个学生和一只火尾小狐狸鼻尖相对，一道柔和的金光把他们连在一起。\n# en: Hide her\nopt.left.label [ce99de] = 把她藏起来\n# en: You name her Ember. Nobody notices a fox that doesn't want to be noticed.\nopt.left.say.1 [9f2aee] = 你给她取名“余烬”。一只不想被注意的狐狸，没人会注意到。\n# en: A professor spots her at once. You pay the fee — and Ember is yours, officially.\nopt.left.say.2 [30e3a9] = 一位教授一眼就发现了她。你交了费用——余烬正式成了你的魔宠。\n# en: Register her properly\nopt.right.label [9506c8] = 正式登记\n# en: The registrar stamps a form. \"Name?\" \"Ember,\" you say. The fox looks smug.\nopt.right.say.1 [c579b1] = 登记员在表格上盖了章。“名字？”“余烬。”你说。狐狸一脸得意。\n\n@event fox_helps\n# en: Ember Finds Something\ntitle [020478] = 余烬找到了东西\n# en: Ember drags you, by the sleeve, through three corridors and a hedge. She's found something. She's very proud of it.\ntext [f8700d] = 余烬叼着你的袖子，拖你穿过三条走廊和一道树篱。她找到了东西。她非常骄傲。\n# en: A fox nosing through dry leaves under a hedge and emerging with a purse in its jaws.\nscene [fa286c] = 一只狐狸在树篱下的枯叶里拱来拱去，叼出一只钱袋。\n# en: Let her keep it\nopt.left.label [dacce7] = 让她留着吧\n# en: It's a sock. Ember is thrilled. You are thrilled for her.\nopt.left.say.1 [afeb11] = 是一只袜子。余烬高兴坏了。你也替她高兴。\n# en: See what it is\nopt.right.label [c3d611] = 看看是什么\n# en: A lost purse. You return it to its owner, who insists you keep the reward.\nopt.right.say.1 [8c1c68] = 一只丢失的钱袋。你把它还给了失主，失主坚持要你收下酬谢。\n# en: A lost page from a professor's lecture notes. Enlightening.\nopt.right.say.2 [3f4db6] = 一页遗失的教授讲义。很有启发。\n\n@event ghost_socks\n# en: Rearranged Socks\ntitle [c1194d] = 被整理的袜子\n# en: Every morning, your socks have been rearranged. By colour. Then alphabetically by colour. Last night they spelled out \"HELP\".\ntext [79fec3] = 每天早上，你的袜子都被重新整理过。按颜色。然后按颜色的首字母排序。昨晚，它们拼出了“救命”。\n# en: A dormitory drawer open in candlelight, socks arranged in a careful spiral, a faint shimmer in the air above them.\nscene [c7a0d1] = 烛光中一只打开的宿舍抽屉，袜子被整齐地排成螺旋，上方的空气里有一丝微光。\n# en: Ignore it\nopt.left.label [e2ae0c] = 不去管它\n# en: You buy new socks. The next morning they're arranged by mood.\nopt.left.say.1 [57e0f1] = 你买了新袜子。第二天早上，它们按心情排好了。\n# en: Stay up and watch\nopt.right.label [03406b] = 熬夜守着\n# en: At three in the morning, the drawer slides open by itself.\nopt.right.say.1 [69a0e4] = 凌晨三点，抽屉自己滑开了。\n\n@event ghost_reveal\n# en: Prudence\ntitle [a6d175] = 普鲁登丝\n# en: A girl in old-fashioned robes, translucent and very polite. \"Prudence Ashworth, class of 1793. Terribly sorry about the socks. I never finished my letter. Could you deliver it? The village. The bakery. Please.\"\ntext [fd675e] = 一个穿着老式长袍的女孩，半透明，非常有礼貌。“普鲁登丝·阿什沃斯，1793 届。袜子的事实在抱歉。我的信一直没写完。你能帮我送去吗？镇上。面包店。拜托了。”\n# en: A translucent girl in old-fashioned school robes, hovering by a window, holding a faded letter.\nscene [2de1ba] = 一个身穿老式校袍的半透明女孩悬浮在窗边，手里拿着一封褪色的信。\n# en: Exorcise her\nopt.left.label [eb2bc2] = 驱散她\n# en: You speak the banishing rite. She fades with a small, hurt look. Your socks stay put after that. You feel a bit rotten.\nopt.left.say.1 [940567] = 你念出驱逐仪式的咒文。她带着一点受伤的神情消散了。从那以后你的袜子再没动过。你觉得自己有点混蛋。\n# en: Promise to deliver it\nopt.right.label [32f8b3] = 答应帮她送信\n# en: She claps her translucent hands. \"Oh, thank you! It's only taken two hundred years.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [e468ed] = 她拍着半透明的双手。“噢，谢谢你！才等了两百年而已。”\n\n@event ghost_letter\n# en: A Letter Two Hundred Years Late\ntitle [4de394] = 迟到两百年的信\n# en: The bakery has been in the same family for two hundred years. The old baker reads Prudence's letter at the counter. \"My great-great-great-grandmother waited for this,\" she says. \"She never stopped.\"\ntext [07ef08] = 这家面包店两百年来一直由同一个家族经营。老面包师在柜台前读着普鲁登丝的信。“我的高高高祖母一直在等这封信，”她说，“她从没停止过等待。”\n# en: A cosy village bakery, an old baker reading a yellowed letter with tears on her cheeks, a ghostly figure smiling in the doorway.\nscene [481d5e] = 温馨的小镇面包店，一位老面包师读着一封泛黄的信，泪流满面，门口一个幽灵般的身影在微笑。\n# en: Stay and listen to the story\nopt.left.label [7f246b] = 留下来听这个故事\n# en: Prudence was in love with the baker's daughter. When you return, the ghost is waiting, glowing like a sunrise. Then she's gone, at peace.\nopt.left.say.1 [623354] = 原来普鲁登丝爱着面包师的女儿。你回去时，幽灵正等着你，像日出一样发着光。然后她走了，安详地。\n# en: Leave quietly\nopt.right.label [69ae5f] = 悄悄离开\n# en: The baker presses a free loaf into your hands. That night, your socks are arranged into a heart. Then never again.\nopt.right.say.1 [104d89] = 面包师把一条面包塞进你手里，不收钱。那天夜里，你的袜子被摆成了一颗心。之后再也没有过。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/classes.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/classes.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event pyro_candle\n# en: Light the Candle\ntitle [b66d59] = 点燃蜡烛\n# en: Professor Thorne places a candle at the far end of the hall. \"Light it. From there.\" The class holds its breath. Last week a boy lit his own sleeve.\ntext [249ae3] = 索恩教授把一支蜡烛放在大厅的尽头。“点燃它。就站在那儿。”全班屏住了呼吸。上周有个男生点着了自己的袖子。\n# en: A dark classroom, a single candle on a stool at the far end, Professor Thorne's silhouette with folded arms.\nscene [b8447a] = 昏暗的教室，远处凳子上立着一支蜡烛，索恩教授抱着双臂的剪影。\n# en: A careful spark\nopt.left.label [1455cd] = 一点小心的火花\n# en: The wick catches with a polite little flame. Thorne grunts. From him, that is applause.\nopt.left.say.1 [e34a27] = 烛芯燃起一朵彬彬有礼的小火苗。索恩哼了一声。从他嘴里出来，这就算掌声了。\n# en: Nothing. Then a wisp of smoke from your fingertips. A few people giggle.\nopt.left.say.2 [266366] = 什么也没有。然后你的指尖冒出一缕青烟。几个人咯咯笑了起来。\n# en: Show them real fire\nopt.right.label [c2dc32] = 让他们见识真正的火\n# en: The candle erupts in a pillar of white flame and then settles, obedient. The room goes quiet.\nopt.right.say.1 [d02a9d] = 蜡烛喷出一道白色火柱，随即乖乖地平息下来。教室里鸦雀无声。\n# en: The candle lights. So do the curtains. And, briefly, your eyebrows.\nopt.right.say.2 [ed4c08] = 蜡烛点着了。窗帘也点着了。还有——短暂地——你的眉毛。\n\n@event pyro_ember_heart\n# en: The Ember Within\ntitle [819842] = 心中余烬\n# en: Today's exercise: hold a spark inside your chest and let it grow. \"Fire is fed by will,\" says Thorne. \"And by you, if you are careless.\" It burns. It is supposed to burn.\ntext [9171f7] = 今天的练习：把一点火星含在胸中，让它慢慢长大。“火以意志为食，”索恩说，“如果你不小心，也以你为食。”很烫。本来就该烫。\n# en: Students sitting cross-legged in a stone circle, each with a faint glow behind their ribs.\nscene [c54174] = 学生们盘腿坐在石圈里，每个人的肋骨后都透出微弱的光。\n# en: Stop when it hurts\nopt.left.label [d5afc2] = 痛了就停\n# en: You let the spark gutter out. Thorne says nothing. The silence is a grade.\nopt.left.say.1 [189b77] = 你让火星熄灭了。索恩一言不发。这份沉默就是分数。\n# en: Hold it longer than anyone\nopt.right.label [a7c8d5] = 坚持得比谁都久\n# en: Your vision goes white at the edges. When you open your eyes, your breath is steaming and the stones around you are warm.\nopt.right.say.1 [4859e1] = 你的视野边缘泛起一片白。睁开眼时，你呼出的气在冒白烟，周围的石头都是温热的。\n\n@event pyro_lyra_spar\n# en: A Challenge in Class\ntitle [68aa64] = 课堂上的挑战\n# en: \"Professor, I'd like a demonstration partner.\" Lyra Vaneshade points her wand at you without looking. \"That one.\"\ntext [c4c320] = “教授，我想要一个演示搭档。”莱拉·凡妮希德看都没看，就把魔杖指向了你。“就那个。”\n# en: Two students facing each other across a scorched practice ring; one is a tall girl with crimson hair, flames curling around her wrists.\nscene [377b0a] = 两名学生隔着烧焦的练习圈对峙；其中一个是高挑的红发女孩，火焰缠绕在她的手腕上。\n# en: Politely decline\nopt.left.label [8a601d] = 礼貌地拒绝\n# en: \"As expected,\" Lyra says, and turns away. It stings worse than a burn would.\nopt.left.say.1 [6071b1] = “果然。”莱拉说完便转过身去。这比被火烫还疼。\n# en: Step into the ring\nopt.right.label [bf566a] = 走进圈内\n# en: Your fire meets hers and holds. When Thorne calls time, Lyra is breathing hard. \"Again. Next week,\" she says.\nopt.right.say.1 [4d681f] = 你的火焰迎上她的，稳稳顶住。索恩喊停时，莱拉气喘吁吁。“再来。下周。”她说。\n# en: She wins in under a minute — but she looks at you afterwards like she's filing you away for later.\nopt.right.say.2 [ab3e00] = 她不到一分钟就赢了——但她事后看你的眼神，像是把你归档留待日后处理。\n\n@event tide_lake_lesson\n# en: The Lake Answers\ntitle [146cec] = 湖水的回应\n# en: Waist-deep in the freezing lake at dawn, Professor Marisol tells you to ask the water to rise. \"Ask,\" she says. \"Never demand. The lake remembers rudeness.\"\ntext [781568] = 黎明时分，你站在齐腰深的冰冷湖水里，玛丽索尔教授让你请求湖水升起。“是请求，”她说，“永远别命令。湖会记仇。”\n# en: Students standing waist-deep in a silver lake at dawn, ripples spiralling out from their outstretched hands.\nscene [398563] = 黎明时分，学生们站在齐腰深的银色湖水中，涟漪从他们伸出的手掌间旋转着荡开。\n# en: Command it anyway\nopt.left.label [400388] = 偏要命令它\n# en: The water rises in a shivering column. Marisol raises an eyebrow. \"Effective. Rude.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [4cfa1b] = 湖水升起一道颤抖的水柱。玛丽索尔挑了挑眉。“有效。无礼。”\n# en: The lake declines, and expresses its displeasure by knocking you flat.\nopt.left.say.2 [412991] = 湖拒绝了，并用一个浪头把你拍倒来表达不满。\n# en: Listen first\nopt.right.label [c35e18] = 先倾听\n# en: You stand still until the cold stops hurting. The lake murmurs — and for a moment you could swear it said your name.\nopt.right.say.1 [17e74d] = 你一动不动，直到寒冷不再刺痛。湖水低声絮语——有一瞬间，你敢发誓它叫了你的名字。\n\n@event tide_storm_drill\n# en: Storm Drill\ntitle [cb32cb] = 风暴演练\n# en: A storm rolls over the lake and Marisol is delighted. \"Perfect conditions! Hold back the rain for as long as you can.\" Most of the class has already fled.\ntext [1b19ce] = 一场风暴卷过湖面，玛丽索尔高兴坏了。“完美的条件！尽可能久地挡住雨！”班上大部分人早已逃之夭夭。\n# en: A rocky shore under black clouds, a few figures in soaked robes holding back a curtain of rain with raised hands.\nscene [04bfa1] = 乌云下的岩石湖岸，几个浑身湿透的身影举起双手，挡住一道雨幕。\n# en: Shelter with the sensible ones\nopt.left.label [897439] = 和明智的人一起躲雨\n# en: You watch from the boathouse, dry and slightly ashamed.\nopt.left.say.1 [061ff3] = 你在船屋里观看，身上干爽，心里略感惭愧。\n# en: Stay out in it\nopt.right.label [f1db6c] = 留在雨里\n# en: You hold the rain back for almost a minute. You also catch Lake Fever.\nopt.right.say.1 [28d2b9] = 你挡住雨将近一分钟。你也染上了湖热病。\n# en: For a full minute, the rain parts around you. Marisol whoops like a child.\nopt.right.say.2 [814a26] = 整整一分钟，雨水在你周围分开。玛丽索尔像个孩子一样欢呼。\n\n@event tide_drowned_bell\n# en: The Drowned Bell\ntitle [5e6210] = 沉没的钟\n# en: During a breathing-charm lesson you hear it: a bell, deep under the lake, tolling slow and soft. Nobody else seems to notice.\ntext [159540] = 在一堂水下呼吸咒的课上，你听见了：湖底深处有一口钟，缓慢而轻柔地敲响。似乎没有别人注意到。\n# en: Murky green water, a shaft of light catching the bronze curve of an enormous bell half-buried in silt.\nscene [7fdf05] = 浑浊的绿色湖水中，一束光照亮了一口半埋在淤泥里的巨钟的青铜弧面。\n# en: Mention it to Professor Marisol\nopt.left.label [dfb6fd] = 告诉玛丽索尔教授\n# en: Her face goes very still. \"There is no bell,\" she says. \"There hasn't been a bell for three hundred years.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [084e01] = 她的表情一下子僵住了。“没有什么钟，”她说，“三百年来，那里都没有钟。”\n# en: Dive down to find it\nopt.right.label [86c496] = 潜下去找它\n# en: Deep in the silt you find a broken piece of bronze, warm to the touch, engraved with a black star.\nopt.right.say.1 [6a378c] = 在深深的淤泥里，你找到一块破碎的青铜，摸上去是温的，上面刻着一颗黑色的星。\n# en: The water closes over you, dark and cold. You surface gasping, empty-handed, with the bell still ringing in your teeth.\nopt.right.say.2 [d6f863] = 湖水在你头顶合拢，黑暗而冰冷。你两手空空地浮上水面，大口喘气，那钟声还在你牙齿间回荡。\n\n@event illusion_mirror_self\n# en: A Second You\ntitle [77533b] = 另一个你\n# en: Assignment: conjure a convincing illusion of yourself. Professor Vey circles the room. \"Most students make themselves taller. Interesting, what people choose.\"\ntext [a41437] = 作业：变出一个以假乱真的自己。维伊教授在教室里踱步。“大多数学生会把自己变高一点。人们的选择，真有意思。”\n# en: A student facing a shimmering duplicate of themselves; the duplicate's smile is a little too wide.\nscene [a36cd8] = 一个学生面对着自己闪闪发光的分身；分身的笑容咧得有点太开了。\n# en: Make it flattering\nopt.left.label [90461e] = 美化一下\n# en: Your double is radiant, confident, slightly better-looking. Vey writes something down and smiles thinly.\nopt.left.say.1 [07e402] = 你的分身容光焕发、自信满满，长得还比你好看一点。维伊记下了什么，淡淡一笑。\n# en: Make it accurate\nopt.right.label [a663ce] = 如实呈现\n# en: Your double slouches the way you slouch and chews its lip the way you do. It is deeply unsettling. Vey awards top marks.\nopt.right.say.1 [6dfcc6] = 你的分身和你一样驼着背，和你一样咬着嘴唇。这让人非常不安。维伊给了满分。\n\n@event illusion_professor_prank\n# en: The Professor's Face\ntitle [795234] = 教授的脸\n# en: Your classmates dare you: wear a glamour of Professor Thorne and cancel tomorrow's Arcana lecture. \"Nobody will know,\" says someone who will definitely tell.\ntext [f7081e] = 同学们怂恿你：用幻术变成索恩教授的模样，取消明天的奥术课。“没人会发现的。”说这话的人一定会去告密。\n# en: A student wearing a shimmering glamour of a stern old professor, classmates stifling laughter behind a door.\nscene [6c7f98] = 一个学生顶着严厉老教授的闪烁幻象，同学们躲在门后憋着笑。\n# en: Absolutely not\nopt.left.label [c1af76] = 绝对不行\n# en: Wise. Someone else tries it the next day and spends a week scrubbing gargoyles.\nopt.left.say.1 [2654ad] = 明智。第二天另一个人试了，结果刷了一个星期的石像鬼。\n# en: Do it\nopt.right.label [f29ea1] = 干了\n# en: \"Class is cancelled,\" you announce in Thorne's gravel voice. The cheering is audible from three towers away.\nopt.right.say.1 [52cb62] = “今天停课。”你用索恩那砂砾般的嗓音宣布。欢呼声在三座塔外都听得见。\n# en: Halfway through, the glamour flickers. The real Thorne is standing in the doorway. He is not laughing.\nopt.right.say.2 [c3574a] = 演到一半，幻术闪了一下。真正的索恩就站在门口。他没有笑。\n\n@event illusion_dragon\n# en: The Courtyard Dragon\ntitle [e608ce] = 庭院里的龙\n# en: For your term project you must create an illusion that fools a crowd. You could make something small and flawless. Or you could put a dragon in the courtyard at lunchtime.\ntext [47084a] = 你的学期作业是制造一个能骗过一群人的幻象。你可以做个小而完美的东西。或者，你可以在午饭时间往庭院里放一条龙。\n# en: A vast translucent dragon coiled around the academy's central fountain, students frozen mid-step beneath it.\nscene [56332c] = 一条巨大的半透明的龙盘绕在学院中央喷泉上，下方的学生们僵在原地。\n# en: Small and flawless\nopt.left.label [1a8234] = 小而完美\n# en: A single butterfly that nobody can tell is fake. Vey taps it with her wand and it bursts into light. \"Perfect.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [b8332b] = 一只谁也看不出是假的蝴蝶。维伊用魔杖轻轻一点，它化作一团光。“完美。”\n# en: Dragon. Obviously.\nopt.right.label [602404] = 龙。当然是龙。\n# en: The dragon uncurls around the fountain and yawns. Forty students scream. Vey laughs until she cries.\nopt.right.say.1 [617c57] = 龙绕着喷泉舒展身体，打了个哈欠。四十个学生尖叫起来。维伊笑得眼泪都出来了。\n# en: The dragon is magnificent for four seconds and then sneezes into a cloud of glitter. The headmistress was eating lunch below it.\nopt.right.say.2 [f1e819] = 那条龙壮观了四秒钟，然后打了个喷嚏，化作一团亮片。院长当时正在下面吃午饭。\n\n@event alch_first_brew\n# en: Draught of Mild Courage\ntitle [da711f] = 微勇药剂\n# en: The recipe is on the board: Draught of Mild Courage. Seven ingredients, three stirs clockwise, one counter-clockwise, and never — never — add the newt eye early.\ntext [74f236] = 配方写在黑板上：微勇药剂。七种原料，顺时针搅三下，逆时针一下，而且绝不——绝不——能提前放蝾螈眼。\n# en: Rows of bubbling cauldrons in a low stone room, each giving off a different coloured steam.\nscene [88ce92] = 低矮的石室里一排排冒泡的坩埚，每一口都升起不同颜色的蒸汽。\n# en: Follow the recipe exactly\nopt.left.label [ae651f] = 严格按配方来\n# en: A clear, honey-coloured brew. The professor sniffs it and nods. Safe, solid, unremarkable.\nopt.left.say.1 [6cc5ac] = 一锅清澈的蜂蜜色药剂。教授闻了闻，点点头。安全、扎实、平平无奇。\n# en: Improvise a little\nopt.right.label [99aea4] = 稍微即兴发挥\n# en: Your variant tastes like cinnamon and makes people brave enough to ask for things. A fifth-year buys the whole flask.\nopt.right.say.1 [3f3ac4] = 你的改良版尝起来有肉桂味，能让人鼓起勇气开口要东西。一个五年级生把整瓶都买走了。\n# en: You taste-test. Mistake. For the next month, everything you say comes out as a deep, wet croak.\nopt.right.say.2 [875ffe] = 你亲口尝了一下。失误。接下来一个月，你说的每句话都变成了低沉潮湿的呱呱声。\n\n@event alch_unstable_cauldron\n# en: Unstable Cauldron\ntitle [69d2ed] = 不稳定的坩埚\n# en: The cauldron next to yours starts to shudder. Its owner, a terrified first-year, has added the newt eye early. It is going to blow.\ntext [e609c0] = 你旁边的坩埚开始颤抖。它的主人，一个吓坏了的一年级生，提前放了蝾螈眼。它要炸了。\n# en: A cauldron shuddering on its iron legs, violet bubbles swelling over the rim, a student frozen beside it.\nscene [444a20] = 一口坩埚在铁脚上颤动，紫色气泡从锅沿鼓出，旁边的学生僵住了。\n# en: Dive for cover\nopt.left.label [5f718b] = 扑倒找掩护\n# en: The explosion paints the ceiling violet. Nobody is hurt. Everybody is purple.\nopt.left.say.1 [c25dd0] = 爆炸把天花板染成了紫色。没人受伤。所有人都变紫了。\n# en: Try to neutralise it\nopt.right.label [cd26e0] = 试着中和它\n# en: A pinch of salt, a drop of moonwater, a quick counter-stir. The bubbling dies. The first-year bursts into grateful tears.\nopt.right.say.1 [21799b] = 一撮盐，一滴月光水，飞快地反向搅动。冒泡停了。那个一年级生感激得哭了出来。\n# en: The cauldron goes off in your face. You spend a week with violet eyebrows and a ringing in your ears.\nopt.right.say.2 [2f0478] = 坩埚在你脸上炸开了。你顶着紫色眉毛、耳朵嗡嗡响地过了一个星期。\n\n@event alch_corvin_market\n# en: A Side Business\ntitle [5b5ae3] = 副业\n# en: Corvin turns your leftover potions over in his hands. \"These would sell for real crowns in the village. I know a buyer. I take a third. Nobody needs to know they came from a classroom.\"\ntext [179554] = 科尔文把你剩下的药剂在手里翻来覆去地看。“这些在镇上能卖真金白银。我认识个买家。我抽三成。没人需要知道它们是从教室里出来的。”\n# en: A young man leaning on a cauldron table, holding up a vial against the light with a merchant's grin.\nscene [95a8de] = 一个年轻人倚着坩埚桌，把一只小瓶举到光下，脸上挂着商人的笑。\n# en: Decline\nopt.left.label [eaef39] = 拒绝\n# en: \"Your loss, saint.\" He tosses the vial back. You fumble it.\nopt.left.say.1 [160723] = “你的损失，圣人。”他把小瓶扔回来。你手忙脚乱地接住。\n# en: Shake on it\nopt.right.label [982713] = 一言为定\n# en: It goes well until a vial turns up in a professor's tea. You and Corvin share a detention and an alibi.\nopt.right.say.1 [f97a4d] = 一切顺利，直到一只小瓶出现在某位教授的茶里。你和科尔文共享了一次禁闭和一份不在场证明。\n# en: Thirty crowns, no questions asked. Corvin winks. \"Pleasure doing crime with you.\"\nopt.right.say.2 [d740ff] = 三十克朗，不问来路。科尔文眨眨眼。“和你一起犯罪真愉快。”\n\n@event div_tea_leaves\n# en: Reading the Leaves\ntitle [0b5484] = 解读茶叶\n# en: You read your partner's tea leaves. The shape at the bottom of the cup is unmistakably a skull. Your partner is looking at you hopefully.\ntext [724f94] = 你为搭档解读茶叶。杯底的形状明明白白是一个骷髅。你的搭档满怀希望地看着你。\n# en: A delicate teacup tipped on its saucer, the dregs forming a shape like a skull — or possibly a mushroom.\nscene [006d77] = 一只精致的茶杯倾倒在茶碟上，杯底的残渣形如骷髅——也可能是蘑菇。\n# en: Tell the truth\nopt.left.label [d8bf00] = 说实话\n# en: \"A skull?\" They laugh. \"Professor says a skull means change.\" They're relieved. You aren't sure you should be.\nopt.left.say.1 [fd9f44] = “骷髅？”对方笑了，“教授说骷髅代表改变。”对方松了口气。你却不确定该不该松这口气。\n# en: They go pale and leave early. The next day they trip down the stairs. Nothing serious. Still.\nopt.left.say.2 [e9a649] = 对方脸色煞白，提前离开了。第二天对方从楼梯上摔了一跤。不严重。但还是。\n# en: Lie kindly\nopt.right.label [542b7a] = 善意地撒谎\n# en: \"A mushroom,\" you say. \"Means good fortune.\" They beam. The professor, passing behind you, says nothing at all.\nopt.right.say.1 [739452] = “是蘑菇，”你说，“代表好运。”对方笑开了花。从你身后走过的教授什么也没说。\n\n@event div_next_tuesday\n# en: The Mirror of Next Tuesday\ntitle [765386] = 下周二之镜\n# en: The Mirror of Next Tuesday shows exactly what it promises. Students take turns looking. Some come away smiling. One comes away and goes straight to the infirmary.\ntext [3591e5] = “下周二之镜”展示的正如其名。学生们轮流去看。有人笑着离开。有一个人看完直接去了医务室。\n# en: An ornate standing mirror whose reflection shows the room on a different day — the light at a different angle, a different person sitting in your chair.\nscene [abfe7b] = 一面华丽的落地镜，镜中映出的是另一天的这间屋子——光线角度不同，你的椅子上坐着别人。\n# en: Take a look\nopt.left.label [a3cf08] = 看一眼\n# en: Next Tuesday you are sitting an Arcana quiz. You memorise the questions. Is that cheating? It's at least ambiguous.\nopt.left.say.1 [8821ae] = 下周二你在参加奥术小测。你记下了题目。这算作弊吗？至少说不清。\n# en: Next Tuesday you are crying in the rain outside the observatory. You don't know why. You spend the week dreading it.\nopt.left.say.2 [004b37] = 下周二你在天文台外的雨里哭泣。你不知道为什么。接下来一整周你都提心吊胆。\n# en: Decline\nopt.right.label [eaef39] = 不看了\n# en: \"I'd rather be surprised.\" The professor smiles as if you have passed a different test entirely.\nopt.right.say.1 [18dfd0] = “我宁愿到时候再惊喜。”教授笑了，好像你通过了另一场完全不同的考试。\n\n@event div_seraphine_vision\n# en: She Grabs Your Hand\ntitle [a002e6] = 她抓住了你的手\n# en: In the middle of crystal-gazing, Seraphine Hale seizes your wrist. Her eyes have gone silver. \"The door under the lake,\" she whispers. \"Don't let him open it. Or do. I can't see which.\"\ntext [52e443] = 正凝视水晶球时，瑟拉芬·海尔一把抓住你的手腕。她的眼睛变成了银色。“湖下的门，”她低语，“别让他打开它。或者让他打开。我看不清是哪个。”\n# en: A dreamy-eyed girl gripping a classmate's wrist across a table of crystal balls, her pupils wide and silver.\nscene [bda6a7] = 一个眼神迷离的女孩隔着摆满水晶球的桌子抓住同学的手腕，瞳孔放大，泛着银光。\n# en: Pull away\nopt.left.label [1414c0] = 抽回手\n# en: She blinks and lets go. \"Sorry. That happens.\" She goes back to her crystal as if nothing occurred.\nopt.left.say.1 [d082da] = 她眨了眨眼，松开了手。“抱歉。偶尔会这样。”她若无其事地继续看她的水晶球。\n# en: Ask who she means\nopt.right.label [301f4e] = 问她说的是谁\n# en: \"Someone cold,\" she says. \"Someone kind.\" Then she shakes her head, smiles vaguely, and asks if you'd like a biscuit.\nopt.right.say.1 [c004e1] = “一个冰冷的人，”她说，“一个温柔的人。”然后她摇摇头，迷迷糊糊地笑了笑，问你要不要来块饼干。\n\n@event art_clockwork_beetle\n# en: The Clockwork Beetle\ntitle [8b7d56] = 发条甲虫\n# en: Your project: a brass beetle that walks. The gears are in. The spring is wound. You could make it efficient, or you could give it a little personality.\ntext [982bd2] = 你的作业：一只会走路的黄铜甲虫。齿轮装好了。发条上紧了。你可以让它高效，也可以给它一点个性。\n# en: A brass beetle the size of a fist on a workbench, tiny gears visible through its translucent wing-cases.\nscene [3cbbcc] = 工作台上一只拳头大小的黄铜甲虫，透过半透明的鞘翅能看见细小的齿轮。\n# en: Make it efficient\nopt.left.label [b934bb] = 让它高效\n# en: It walks in a perfect straight line until it hits a wall. Full marks.\nopt.left.say.1 [2f4098] = 它沿着一条完美的直线前进，直到撞上墙。满分。\n# en: Give it personality\nopt.right.label [40ed9d] = 给它点个性\n# en: It escapes during the presentation and lives in the walls for a month, occasionally stealing biscuits. It becomes a minor legend.\nopt.right.say.1 [a6886f] = 它在展示时逃跑了，在墙里住了一个月，时不时偷块饼干。它成了一个小小的传说。\n# en: It refuses to walk unless you compliment it. The professor is torn between marking you down and asking how you did it.\nopt.right.say.2 [550353] = 除非你夸它，不然它就不肯走。教授在扣你分和问你怎么做到的之间左右为难。\n\n@event art_mira_collab\n# en: A Joint Project\ntitle [7a3675] = 合作项目\n# en: Mira Ten drops onto the bench beside you with a sketch of something that is either a self-stirring cauldron or a small war machine. \"Partners? Your hands, my brain. Or the other way round. Both, actually.\"\ntext [f90bdb] = 米拉·滕一屁股坐到你旁边的长凳上，拿着一张草图，上面画的东西要么是自动搅拌坩埚，要么是一台小型战争机器。“搭档吗？你的手，我的脑子。或者反过来。其实两样都要。”\n# en: A cluttered workbench covered in tools, sketches and one smoking teapot; a grinning student with goggles pushed up into wild hair.\nscene [455820] = 堆满工具和草图的工作台上还有一只冒烟的茶壶；一个咧嘴笑的学生把护目镜推到了蓬乱的头发上。\n# en: Work alone\nopt.left.label [e54a87] = 自己干\n# en: \"Fair,\" says Mira, with no hard feelings, and is immediately on fire. Not badly.\nopt.left.say.1 [e9e5f2] = “公平。”米拉毫无芥蒂地说，随即就着了火。不算严重。\n# en: Partner with Mira\nopt.right.label [1a6b92] = 和米拉搭档\n# en: The cauldron stirs itself, hums a tune and only threatens the professor once. Mira hugs you with oily hands.\nopt.right.say.1 [fc80da] = 坩埚自己搅拌，还哼着小曲，只威胁了教授一次。米拉用满是油污的手抱住了你。\n# en: The prototype explodes. Mira laughs so hard they cry. \"Again! We were so close!\" You find you're laughing too.\nopt.right.say.2 [fc8591] = 原型机爆炸了。米拉笑得眼泪直流。“再来！我们差一点就成了！”你发现自己也在笑。\n\n@event art_enchanted_quill\n# en: The Self-Writing Quill\ntitle [0d291a] = 自动羽毛笔\n# en: You've enchanted a quill to take dictation. It works beautifully. A thought occurs: what if it wrote your essays, too?\ntext [8d3e40] = 你给一支羽毛笔施了魔法，让它听写。效果好极了。你冒出一个念头：要是让它连论文也写了呢？\n# en: A quill writing busily on its own over a sheaf of paper while its owner sleeps on folded arms.\nscene [789717] = 一支羽毛笔在一沓纸上自己奋笔疾书，它的主人趴在胳膊上睡着了。\n# en: Just use it for notes\nopt.left.label [1cd0cf] = 只用它记笔记\n# en: Your notes are flawless. Even the professor's jokes are transcribed, with a small marginal comment: \"(not funny).\"\nopt.left.say.1 [04dda1] = 你的笔记无懈可击。连教授的笑话都记下来了，旁边还有一行小小的批注：“（不好笑）”。\n# en: Let it write your essays\nopt.right.label [904901] = 让它替你写论文\n# en: Your essays arrive on time, in impeccable penmanship. A little too impeccable. Nobody notices. Yet.\nopt.right.say.1 [fd9908] = 你的论文按时交上，字迹工整无比。工整得有点过分了。没人发现。暂时。\n# en: The quill develops opinions and writes a scathing review of the professor. It signs your name.\nopt.right.say.2 [7b012f] = 羽毛笔有了自己的主见，写了一篇痛批教授的檄文。署的是你的名字。\n\n@event beast_griffon_chick\n# en: Hatching Day\ntitle [e94ec5] = 破壳日\n# en: The griffon egg is cracking. Professor Brask whispers that the first face a chick sees, it trusts forever — or attacks forever. Hard to predict.\ntext [713b1c] = 狮鹫蛋正在裂开。布拉斯克教授低声说，雏鸟看见的第一张脸，它会永远信任——或者永远攻击。很难预料。\n# en: A straw nest under a heat lamp, a cracked golden egg, and a damp fluffy griffon chick blinking at the world.\nscene [561fcb] = 保温灯下的草窝里，一枚裂开的金蛋，一只湿漉漉、毛茸茸的狮鹫雏鸟正眨着眼打量世界。\n# en: Hang back\nopt.left.label [7bd914] = 往后站\n# en: The chick sees Brask first and bites her. She seems delighted.\nopt.left.say.1 [72d465] = 雏鸟第一眼看见了布拉斯克，咬了她一口。她看起来很开心。\n# en: Offer your hand\nopt.right.label [1f34dd] = 伸出手\n# en: The chick peeps, nuzzles your palm and falls asleep. You are, apparently, its mother now.\nopt.right.say.1 [dd4810] = 雏鸟啾啾叫着，蹭了蹭你的掌心，睡着了。看来，你现在是它妈妈了。\n# en: It bites. Hard. Griffon beaks are sharper than they look, and they look extremely sharp.\nopt.right.say.2 [96c90d] = 它咬了你。狠狠地。狮鹫的喙比看上去锋利，而它看上去就极其锋利。\n\n@event beast_manticore\n# en: The Manticore Is Loose\ntitle [c92a9e] = 蝎尾狮跑出来了\n# en: Someone left the manticore pen open. It's in the greenhouse, tail twitching, between you and the only exit.\ntext [1d8358] = 有人没关蝎尾狮的围栏。它在温室里，尾巴抽动着，正挡在你和唯一的出口之间。\n# en: A shadowy lion-shape with a scorpion's tail prowling between the greenhouse benches, students pressed against the glass.\nscene [5076e9] = 一个带着蝎尾的狮形黑影在温室长凳间徘徊，学生们紧贴着玻璃墙。\n# en: Back away slowly\nopt.left.label [b7e862] = 慢慢后退\n# en: You edge out. Professor Brask arrives with a bucket of meat and a lullaby and the crisis is over in minutes.\nopt.left.say.1 [92deb4] = 你一点点挪了出去。布拉斯克教授带着一桶肉和一首摇篮曲赶来，几分钟就平息了危机。\n# en: Try to calm it\nopt.right.label [469057] = 试着安抚它\n# en: You hum the lullaby Brask taught you. The manticore yawns, sits, and lets you scratch behind its ear. The whole class saw.\nopt.right.say.1 [3bf240] = 你哼起布拉斯克教的摇篮曲。蝎尾狮打了个哈欠，坐下来，让你挠它的耳后。全班都看见了。\n# en: The manticore does not like your humming. The tail is quicker than you are.\nopt.right.say.2 [422c18] = 蝎尾狮不喜欢你的哼唱。它的尾巴比你快。\n\n@event beast_familiar_call\n# en: Calling a Familiar\ntitle [b91c9f] = 召唤魔宠\n# en: Brask teaches the summoning circle. \"It doesn't summon,\" she says. \"It invites. Most of the time, nothing comes. That's fine. Familiars choose you.\"\ntext [3a2457] = 布拉斯克教授教你们召唤法阵。“它并不召唤，”她说，“它只是邀请。大多数时候什么也不会来。没关系。魔宠会自己选择你。”\n# en: A circle of salt and candles in a forest clearing at dusk, shapes of small animals watching from the edge of the trees.\nscene [e641a7] = 黄昏时林间空地上一圈盐和蜡烛，林边有小动物的身影在注视。\n# en: Just watch the others\nopt.left.label [4c0d66] = 只看别人做\n# en: A first-year is chosen by a very large toad. The toad looks smug about it.\nopt.left.say.1 [4d8722] = 一个一年级生被一只非常大的蟾蜍选中了。蟾蜍看起来很得意。\n# en: Sit in the circle\nopt.right.label [69d42b] = 坐进法阵\n# en: Nothing comes. But for a long moment, something at the edge of the trees watches you with eyes like embers. Then it is gone.\nopt.right.say.1 [9c9f2a] = 什么也没来。但有很长一段时间，林边有什么东西用余烬般的眼睛注视着你。然后它消失了。\n\n@event astro_comet\n# en: A New Comet\ntitle [6445f6] = 新彗星\n# en: A comet appears that isn't in any of the charts. The observatory is in uproar. Everyone wants to be the one to name it.\ntext [b8052f] = 一颗不在任何星图上的彗星出现了。天文台炸开了锅。人人都想当给它命名的那个人。\n# en: An observatory dome open to the night, a long-tailed comet smeared across the sky, students crowded at the telescopes.\nscene [9a815d] = 向夜空敞开的天文台穹顶，一颗长尾彗星划过天际，学生们挤在望远镜前。\n# en: Sketch it carefully\nopt.left.label [5702fd] = 仔细画下它\n# en: Your drawing is lovely. Someone else's name goes on the comet. You keep the drawing.\nopt.left.say.1 [605f74] = 你画得很美。彗星用了别人的名字。你留下了那幅画。\n# en: Calculate its path\nopt.right.label [e158e4] = 计算它的轨道\n# en: Your math is right. The comet is named after you, or at least after a slight misspelling of you.\nopt.right.say.1 [1b11c9] = 你算对了。彗星以你的名字命名——至少是你名字的一个小小的错误拼写。\n# en: Your calculation suggests the comet will hit the academy on Thursday. It does not. You are teased about it for weeks.\nopt.right.say.2 [0e494e] = 你的计算显示彗星将在周四撞上学院。它没有。你被取笑了好几个星期。\n\n@event astro_kael_roof\n# en: Three in the Morning\ntitle [c51909] = 凌晨三点\n# en: Long after the lecture ends you find Kael alone on the observatory roof. Frost has spread across the tiles around him. He is staring at a patch of sky where there are no stars at all.\ntext [71d4b1] = 讲座结束很久之后，你发现凯尔独自坐在天文台屋顶上。他周围的瓦片上结满了霜。他盯着天上一块一颗星星也没有的地方。\n# en: A young man in a fur coat sitting alone on the observatory roof, frost on the tiles around him, staring at one spot in the stars.\nscene [4d08d9] = 一个穿毛皮大衣的年轻人独自坐在天文台屋顶，身旁瓦片结霜，凝视着星空中的某一点。\n# en: Leave him be\nopt.left.label [ce4663] = 别打扰他\n# en: You go down to bed. In the morning there's a perfect circle of frost on the roof where he sat.\nopt.left.say.1 [267767] = 你下楼睡觉了。早上，屋顶上他坐过的地方留下了一个完美的霜圈。\n# en: Sit beside him\nopt.right.label [746969] = 坐到他身边\n# en: \"There,\" he says, pointing at the dark. \"Can you see it? A star that eats light.\" You look for a long time. You think you can.\nopt.right.say.1 [28b9f2] = “那里，”他指着黑暗说，“你看见了吗？一颗吞噬光的星。”你看了很久。你觉得你看见了。\n\n@event astro_sleepless\n# en: Sleepless Weeks\ntitle [848700] = 无眠的几周\n# en: Midnight lectures, dawn homework, morning classes. You have started seeing constellations in your porridge.\ntext [5695ef] = 午夜讲座，黎明作业，上午上课。你已经开始在粥里看见星座了。\n# en: A student asleep on an open star atlas at a library desk, a guttering candle beside them, dawn light in the window.\nscene [e3c887] = 一个学生趴在图书馆书桌上摊开的星图集上睡着了，蜡烛将熄，窗外透进晨光。\n# en: Skip a few lectures\nopt.left.label [659dd6] = 翘几节讲座\n# en: You sleep for fourteen hours and wake up feeling like a person.\nopt.left.say.1 [c5fbc8] = 你睡了十四个小时，醒来时终于觉得自己像个人了。\n# en: Power through\nopt.right.label [8bebcb] = 硬撑过去\n# en: You learn the names of forty stars and forget the names of two friends. Temporarily.\nopt.right.say.1 [a83f16] = 你记住了四十颗星星的名字，忘了两个朋友的名字。暂时的。\n\n@event heal_first_patient\n# en: Your First Patient\ntitle [aad265] = 你的第一位病人\n# en: A first-year stumbles into the practice ward with a wrist that bends the wrong way. Sister Amaranth is busy. The first-year looks at you with enormous, trusting eyes.\ntext [2614f1] = 一个一年级生跌跌撞撞地走进实习病房，手腕弯向了错误的方向。阿玛兰斯修女正忙着。那个一年级生用一双大大的、充满信任的眼睛看着你。\n# en: A pale first-year sitting on an infirmary cot holding a wrist at a strange angle, a nervous student healer kneeling beside them.\nscene [0b1ccb] = 一个脸色苍白的一年级生坐在医务室病床上，托着一只角度怪异的手腕，一位紧张的学生治疗师跪在旁边。\n# en: Fetch Sister Amaranth\nopt.left.label [0ebdc9] = 去叫阿玛兰斯修女\n# en: Amaranth fixes it in seconds and nods at you. \"Knowing your limits is the first lesson.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [386b03] = 阿玛兰斯几秒钟就治好了，对你点点头。“知道自己的极限，是第一课。”\n# en: Heal it yourself\nopt.right.label [6b0949] = 自己来治\n# en: The bone slides back with a soft click and a glow of warm light. The first-year tells everyone you're a genius.\nopt.right.say.1 [b682dc] = 骨头轻轻咔哒一声归位，泛起一阵温暖的光。那个一年级生逢人便说你是天才。\n# en: The wrist is now straight. The fingers, however, are pointing backwards. Amaranth sighs and fixes both.\nopt.right.say.2 [2d4421] = 手腕是直了。可手指却朝后指着。阿玛兰斯叹了口气，两样都修好了。\n\n@event heal_fever_ward\n# en: Fever in the Village\ntitle [3e73df] = 小镇里的热病\n# en: Lake Fever is spreading through the village below the academy. Sister Amaranth asks for student volunteers. It's hard, unpaid, and you might catch it.\ntext [ea106a] = 湖热病正在学院山下的小镇蔓延。阿玛兰斯修女在征集学生志愿者。辛苦、没报酬，你还可能被传染。\n# en: A crowded village hall lined with cots, lanterns hanging from the rafters, student healers moving between patients.\nscene [945032] = 一间摆满病床的拥挤镇公所，椽子上挂着灯笼，学生治疗师们在病人之间穿梭。\n# en: Decline\nopt.left.label [eaef39] = 拒绝\n# en: You watch the volunteers board the barge in the morning. You do your homework. It feels like the wrong homework.\nopt.left.say.1 [70c215] = 早上，你看着志愿者们登上驳船。你做着你的作业。感觉像是在做错的作业。\n# en: Volunteer\nopt.right.label [39a371] = 报名志愿\n# en: You save lives, and catch the fever yourself. The village sends a basket of honey cakes to your sickbed.\nopt.right.say.1 [d1e957] = 你救了人，自己也染上了热病。镇上送了一篮蜂蜜蛋糕到你的病床边。\n# en: A week of long nights and cool cloths. When you leave, the baker's daughter gives you her best loaf.\nopt.right.say.2 [61b0a3] = 一个星期的漫漫长夜和凉毛巾。你离开时，面包师的女儿送了你她烤得最好的一条面包。\n\n@event heal_tobias_sting\n# en: Stung\ntitle [1eecd2] = 被蛰了\n# en: Tobias wanders into the practice ward holding up a hand swollen like a pumpkin. \"Mandrake,\" he says. \"She didn't like my singing.\" He looks hopefully at you.\ntext [91e6f0] = 托比亚斯晃进实习病房，举着一只肿得像南瓜的手。“曼德拉草，”他说，“她不喜欢我唱歌。”他满怀希望地看着你。\n# en: A broad-shouldered boy sitting on a greenhouse bench, grimacing, holding up a hand swollen to twice its size.\nscene [37ac94] = 一个宽肩膀的男生坐在温室长凳上，龇牙咧嘴，举着一只肿成两倍大的手。\n# en: Send him to the infirmary\nopt.left.label [d6e106] = 让他去医务室\n# en: \"Right. Proper healer. Good idea.\" He goes, a little disappointed.\nopt.left.say.1 [d77544] = “对。找正经治疗师。好主意。”他走了，有点失望。\n# en: Treat him yourself\nopt.right.label [2aa3de] = 亲自为他治疗\n# en: Your hands are steady. The swelling goes down. Tobias stares at his hand, then at you. \"You're really good at this,\" he says softly.\nopt.right.say.1 [eb2831] = 你的手很稳。肿消了下去。托比亚斯盯着自己的手，又盯着你。“你真的很擅长这个。”他轻声说。\n\n@event umbral_whisper\n# en: The Shadow Speaks\ntitle [041067] = 影子开口了\n# en: In the middle of a lecture on the Nature of Absence, your shadow leans away from the lamp, toward your ear, and says your name.\ntext [a8a6b1] = 在一堂关于“缺席之本质”的讲座中途，你的影子背离了灯光，凑到你耳边，说出了你的名字。\n# en: A student at a desk in a dim lecture hall, their shadow on the wall behind them turned the wrong way, leaning close to whisper.\nscene [51c4bd] = 昏暗讲堂里的一个学生，身后墙上的影子朝向不对，正凑近低语。\n# en: Banish it\nopt.left.label [78332f] = 驱散它\n# en: You speak the warding phrase. Your shadow snaps back into place, sulking. It does not speak again. You think.\nopt.left.say.1 [9451ed] = 你念出防护咒语。影子啪地弹回原位，生着闷气。它没有再说话。你是这么想的。\n# en: Answer it\nopt.right.label [b623c8] = 回应它\n# en: It tells you something about death you can't unlearn. The cold starts the next morning.\nopt.right.say.1 [f3763b] = 它告诉了你一件关于死亡的事，你再也无法忘记。第二天早上，寒意开始了。\n# en: It tells you where a lost book is shelved in the library. It is right. You decide not to wonder how it knew.\nopt.right.say.2 [55a529] = 它告诉你图书馆里一本遗失的书放在哪里。它说对了。你决定不去想它是怎么知道的。\n\n@event umbral_bone_mouse\n# en: The Bone Mouse\ntitle [ae9175] = 骨鼠\n# en: Practical exercise: animate the skeleton of a field mouse. \"Purely educational,\" the professor says, with a smile nobody trusts.\ntext [c20b3b] = 实践练习：让一具田鼠骨架活动起来。“纯属教学目的。”教授说，露出一个没人信得过的微笑。\n# en: A tiny mouse skeleton on a silver tray, one bony paw twitching, students leaning away from it.\nscene [e2a825] = 银托盘上一具小小的老鼠骨架，一只骨爪在抽动，学生们纷纷向后躲。\n# en: Refuse on principle\nopt.left.label [d08672] = 出于原则拒绝\n# en: You write a short essay on why instead. The professor reads it with surprising attention.\nopt.left.say.1 [ceeff8] = 你改写了一篇短文说明理由。教授读得出奇地认真。\n# en: Do the exercise\nopt.right.label [eb5633] = 完成练习\n# en: The little skeleton stands up, washes its non-existent whiskers, and lies down again. Perfect control.\nopt.right.say.1 [6bb00e] = 小骨架站起来，洗了洗并不存在的胡须，又躺了回去。控制得完美无缺。\n# en: It gets up. It does not lie down again. It follows you back to your dormitory and sits on your pillow every night.\nopt.right.say.2 [6046b9] = 它站了起来。然后再也没躺回去。它跟着你回了宿舍，每晚都坐在你的枕头上。\n\n@event umbral_forbidden_notes\n# en: Forbidden Notes\ntitle [b392f3] = 禁忌笔记\n# en: A sixth-year slides a battered notebook across the table. \"My notes on the Restricted Section. I'm graduating. You look like you'd use them.\" A silver key is tucked in the spine.\ntext [5f1ba3] = 一个六年级生把一本破旧的笔记本推过桌面。“我关于禁书区的笔记。我要毕业了。你看起来用得上。”书脊里夹着一把银钥匙。\n# en: A battered notebook with a cracked leather cover being slid across a library table, a silver key tucked into its spine.\nscene [9bca00] = 一本皮面开裂的破旧笔记本被推过图书馆的桌面，书脊里夹着一把银钥匙。\n# en: Decline\nopt.left.label [eaef39] = 拒绝\n# en: The sixth-year shrugs and slides the notebook to the next student. You watch it go.\nopt.left.say.1 [ebf1d2] = 六年级生耸耸肩，把笔记本推给了下一个学生。你看着它被推走。\n# en: Take the notebook\nopt.right.label [beda2e] = 收下笔记本\n# en: The notes are brilliant, half-mad and annotated in three colours of ink. The key is cold in your pocket.\nopt.right.say.1 [7a1f6e] = 笔记精彩绝伦、半疯半癫，用三种颜色的墨水做了批注。那把钥匙在你口袋里冰凉。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/clubs.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/clubs.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event duel_practice_bout\n# en: Practice Bout\ntitle [39d05a] = 练习对决\n# en: Wednesday practice. The captain pairs you with a fourth-year who has a reputation for ending bouts quickly. \"Defence or attack,\" the captain says. \"Pick one and commit.\"\ntext [4d124e] = 周三的练习。社长把你和一个以速战速决出名的四年级生分在一组。“防守还是进攻，”社长说，“选一个，然后坚持到底。”\n# en: A chalk circle on a stone floor under high windows, two students mid-duel, a ring of members cheering.\nscene [d4da5e] = 高窗下石地板上的粉笔圈，两名学生正在对决，一圈社员在欢呼。\n# en: Hold your ground\nopt.left.label [52ce0a] = 坚守阵地\n# en: You weather everything she throws until she runs out of breath. The captain nods slowly.\nopt.left.say.1 [209cbc] = 你扛住了她的每一招，直到她气喘吁吁。社长缓缓点头。\n# en: You hold your ground very bravely, right up until you're on the floor.\nopt.left.say.2 [774fd6] = 你非常勇敢地坚守着阵地，一直坚守到躺在地上为止。\n# en: Go on the attack\nopt.right.label [0fc83d] = 主动进攻\n# en: Your opening hex catches her off guard. Point to you. The circle whoops.\nopt.right.say.1 [25eae8] = 你的开局恶咒打了她个措手不及。你得一分。社员们一片欢呼。\n# en: She sidesteps and you hex the wall. The wall wins.\nopt.right.say.2 [1d6bcd] = 她一个侧身，你的恶咒打在了墙上。墙赢了。\n\n@event duel_bram_hazing\n# en: Initiation, Prefect-Style\ntitle [d6f56a] = 级长式入社仪式\n# en: Bram Hollis, prefect and senior Circle member, has decided new members need \"toughening up\". He's made you hold a shield-charm against him for an hour. The hour is not up.\ntext [80241e] = 布拉姆·霍利斯——级长兼决斗社资深成员——认定新人需要“磨练磨练”。他让你顶着他的攻击维持护盾咒一小时。一小时还没到。\n# en: A burly upper-year in a prefect's sash blocking a corridor, wand twirling, a crowd of members watching nervously.\nscene [e90938] = 一个身披级长绶带的魁梧高年级生堵在走廊里转着魔杖，一群社员紧张地围观。\n# en: Endure it\nopt.left.label [9e8205] = 忍下去\n# en: Your arms shake. You don't drop the charm. Bram grunts, almost approvingly, and finds someone else to torment.\nopt.left.say.1 [cda0fd] = 你的手臂在发抖。你没有放下护盾。布拉姆哼了一声，几乎带着赞许，转身去折磨别人了。\n# en: Drop the shield and duel him\nopt.right.label [df3e2f] = 撤掉护盾，和他决斗\n# en: You disarm him in front of everyone. His wand clatters across the floor. The silence is delicious. He will remember this.\nopt.right.say.1 [df1203] = 你当众缴了他的械。他的魔杖哐啷滚过地板。那份寂静美妙极了。他会记住这一刻的。\n# en: Bram flattens you with a single bored flick. \"Toughening up,\" he reminds the room.\nopt.right.say.2 [675677] = 布拉姆百无聊赖地一挥杖就把你拍扁了。“磨练磨练。”他提醒全场。\n\n@event duel_lyra_training\n# en: Training Partners\ntitle [6e568e] = 训练搭档\n# en: Lyra finds you after practice. \"The tournament is in two months. You're the only person in this club who doesn't bore me. Train with me. Dawn. Every day. Don't be late.\"\ntext [7cf72d] = 练习结束后莱拉找到你。“锦标赛还有两个月。你是这个社里唯一不让我觉得无聊的人。跟我一起练。黎明。每天。别迟到。”\n# en: A misty lawn at dawn, two figures practising wand forms side by side, frost on the grass.\nscene [bd3dcc] = 黎明时雾气笼罩的草坪，两个身影并肩练习杖法，草上结着霜。\n# en: Decline — you need sleep\nopt.left.label [e4f9c1] = 拒绝——你需要睡觉\n# en: \"Sleep,\" she repeats, as if it's a foreign word. \"Fine.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [ceaa69] = “睡觉。”她重复了一遍，好像那是个外语词。“随你。”\n# en: Agree\nopt.right.label [362be3] = 答应\n# en: She's a merciless coach. Somewhere around the third week, you notice she's started bringing two cups of tea.\nopt.right.say.1 [28ecad] = 她是个毫不留情的教练。大约第三周，你发现她开始带两杯茶来了。\n\n@event choir_solo\n# en: Solo Auditions\ntitle [c9c57e] = 独唱选拔\n# en: Auditions for the Midwinter solo. The choirmaster's eyebrows are famously hard to impress. Your throat is dry. Your hands are wet.\ntext [d9b167] = 仲冬独唱的选拔。合唱指挥的眉毛出了名的难以打动。你的喉咙发干。你的手心冒汗。\n# en: A circular choir loft under a painted dome, a single student standing in a pool of light, the rest of the choir floating gently above their seats.\nscene [8e9373] = 彩绘穹顶下的圆形唱诗台，一名学生独自站在一束光里，其余团员轻轻悬浮在座位上方。\n# en: Stay in the back row\nopt.left.label [1db21b] = 待在后排\n# en: You harmonise from safety, and it's lovely. Somebody else gets the solo and cries with relief.\nopt.left.say.1 [7b529b] = 你在安全的位置和声，很动听。别人拿到了独唱，如释重负地哭了。\n# en: Audition\nopt.right.label [41aa8e] = 参加选拔\n# en: You hit the high note and the candles in the loft all flare at once. The choirmaster's eyebrows rise one millimetre. You got it.\nopt.right.say.1 [93eca5] = 你唱上了高音，唱诗台上所有的蜡烛同时一亮。指挥的眉毛抬高了一毫米。你选上了。\n# en: Your voice cracks on the high note and a candle goes out in protest.\nopt.right.say.2 [dae132] = 你的高音破了，一支蜡烛抗议般地熄灭了。\n\n@event choir_levitation\n# en: Off-Key Altitude\ntitle [8a0afa] = 跑调的海拔\n# en: The Choir of the Spheres literally floats when it harmonises. You keep falling out of the air on the third verse. People are starting to notice the thumps.\ntext [8ceebd] = 天球合唱团唱和声时真的会飘起来。可你每唱到第三段就会从空中掉下来。大家开始注意到那一声声“咚”了。\n# en: A choir hovering a few feet off the floor, all in harmony — except one singer, who has dropped back to the ground with a thump.\nscene [282038] = 一支悬浮在离地几尺高处齐声合唱的合唱团——除了一名团员，他“咚”地掉回了地上。\n# en: Mime the hard bits\nopt.left.label [583519] = 难的地方假唱\n# en: Nobody notices. You float serenely, mouthing words. It's a little bit like fraud.\nopt.left.say.1 [018b6b] = 没人发现。你安详地飘着，只动嘴不出声。有点像诈骗。\n# en: Practise every night\nopt.right.label [c7a2c5] = 每晚加练\n# en: A week of late nights and you float through the third verse like a feather. The choirmaster gives you a tiny nod.\nopt.right.say.1 [56e1a4] = 熬了一周夜，你像羽毛一样飘过了第三段。指挥朝你极轻地点了点头。\n\n@event choir_concert\n# en: The Winter Concert\ntitle [428813] = 冬季音乐会\n# en: The winter concert. The great hall is packed. Somewhere in the audience, the Ledger's critic is sharpening her quill.\ntext [ba472a] = 冬季音乐会。大礼堂座无虚席。观众席某处，《纪事报》的乐评人正在削她的羽毛笔。\n# en: The great hall at night, a floating choir lit by candles, the audience below with faces tilted up.\nscene [229006] = 夜晚的大礼堂，一支烛光映照的悬浮合唱团，下方的观众仰着脸。\n# en: Sing for the audience\nopt.left.label [44bf70] = 为观众而唱\n# en: You sing and the hall sways. The review calls the choir \"unearthly\". You cut it out and keep it.\nopt.left.say.1 [0a05a2] = 你一开口，整个礼堂都随之摇曳。乐评称合唱团“不似人间”。你把它剪下来收好了。\n# en: Sing for yourself\nopt.right.label [bcd2d2] = 为自己而唱\n# en: You close your eyes and forget anyone is listening. When you open them, you are higher than everyone else.\nopt.right.say.1 [e5902a] = 你闭上眼，忘了有人在听。再睁开时，你飘得比所有人都高。\n\n@event sky_tryouts\n# en: Tryouts\ntitle [066773] = 选拔赛\n# en: Skyrace tryouts: three laps around the towers, through the hoops, without dying. The captain has a stopwatch and no visible sense of humour.\ntext [ad510d] = 天空竞速队选拔：绕塔三圈，穿过所有光环，别摔死。队长手里拿着秒表，脸上看不出任何幽默感。\n# en: Brooms streaking around the towers, hoops of light hanging in the sky, a captain with a whistle on the battlements.\nscene [465a53] = 飞帚绕着高塔疾驰，光环悬在空中，城垛上站着一个吹哨子的队长。\n# en: Fly clean and steady\nopt.left.label [596f30] = 飞得干净稳当\n# en: You're not the fastest, but you hit every hoop. \"Reserve,\" says the captain. Then: \"...no. Team.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [a95632] = 你不是最快的，但每个光环都穿过了。“替补。”队长说。随即又改口：“……不。正式队员。”\n# en: You miss two hoops and nearly a gargoyle. \"Reserves,\" says the captain. \"Keep practising.\"\nopt.left.say.2 [35b3d5] = 你漏了两个光环，还差点撞上一个石像鬼。“替补。”队长说，“继续练。”\n# en: Go full speed\nopt.right.label [58ac43] = 全速冲刺\n# en: You scream through the hoops so fast the stopwatch has to be checked. You're on the team. Obviously.\nopt.right.say.1 [c5ef95] = 你尖叫着冲过光环，快到队长得核对一下秒表。你入选了。毫无悬念。\n# en: You go full speed into the north tower. The tower is fine.\nopt.right.say.2 [c3625d] = 你全速冲进了北塔。塔没事。\n\n@event sky_crash\n# en: A Bad Landing\ntitle [cc3b0c] = 糟糕的着陆\n# en: Practice. A gust catches you mid-turn, and the ground arrives rather sooner than expected. Your ankle is making an interesting shape.\ntext [ff2751] = 训练中。一阵风在你转弯时把你掀翻，地面来得比预想的要早一些。你的脚踝正呈现出一种有趣的形状。\n# en: A broom snapped in two on the grass, a student sitting up dazed, teammates landing around them.\nscene [6a9e37] = 草地上一把折成两截的飞帚，一个学生晕乎乎地坐起来，队友们在周围降落。\n# en: Walk it off\nopt.left.label [0e45b4] = 走两步就好了\n# en: You do not walk it off. You limp it off, for a month.\nopt.left.say.1 [4fdb76] = 并没有好。你一瘸一拐了一个月。\n# en: Tough as old boots. The team cheers when you get back on your broom.\nopt.left.say.2 [f9f523] = 皮实得很。你重新骑上飞帚时，全队都在欢呼。\n# en: Straight to the infirmary\nopt.right.label [510ab8] = 直奔医务室\n# en: Sister Amaranth sets it in a minute and charges you three crowns for \"carelessness\". Fair.\nopt.right.say.1 [7345b6] = 阿玛兰斯修女一分钟就把它接好了，还收了你三克朗的“粗心费”。公道。\n\n@event sky_championship\n# en: Lumenhold vs. Thornwick\ntitle [da4be7] = 流明堡对荆木学院\n# en: The championship against Thornwick Academy. Final lap. You're neck and neck with their captain, and the golden hoop is at the top of the tallest tower.\ntext [bd74b2] = 与荆木学院的冠军赛。最后一圈。你和对方队长并驾齐驱，金色光环就在最高塔的塔顶。\n# en: Two teams of broom-riders in blue and green above a packed stadium, the golden finishing hoop glowing at the top of the highest tower.\nscene [d82217] = 座无虚席的赛场上空，蓝绿两队飞帚手交错，金色终点光环在最高塔顶闪耀。\n# en: Play it safe\nopt.left.label [882711] = 稳妥起见\n# en: Thornwick takes the hoop by a whisker. Still: second place in the region. The team carries you off the field anyway.\nopt.left.say.1 [68b920] = 荆木以毫厘之差夺得光环。但不管怎样：地区亚军。全队还是把你扛出了场。\n# en: The suicide dive\nopt.right.label [494b9a] = 玩命俯冲\n# en: You dive straight down the tower face and pull up at the last second, through the hoop. The stadium explodes.\nopt.right.say.1 [742576] = 你沿着塔壁垂直俯冲，在最后一秒拉起，穿过光环。全场沸腾了。\n# en: You dive. You miss. The crowd gasps, then cheers you anyway when you stagger up waving.\nopt.right.say.2 [c58127] = 你俯冲了。你没穿中。观众倒吸一口凉气，等你踉跄着爬起来挥手时，又为你欢呼起来。\n\n@event ledger_pie_scoop\n# en: The Case of the Missing Pies\ntitle [269d69] = 馅饼失踪案\n# en: Nessa slams a file on your desk. \"Pies. Forty-two of them, gone from the kitchens in a single night. I want the culprit, I want a photo and I want it by Friday.\"\ntext [bcd7f3] = 内莎把一份卷宗拍在你桌上。“馅饼。四十二个，一夜之间从厨房消失。我要找出犯人，要照片，周五之前要。”\n# en: A cramped newspaper office in a tower attic, a printing press clanking, a girl with an ink-stained grin pointing at a map pinned with string.\nscene [6fc23f] = 塔楼阁楼里拥挤的报社，印刷机哐当作响，一个满脸墨迹、咧嘴笑的女孩指着一张钉满细绳的地图。\n# en: Write a light-hearted piece\nopt.left.label [ffe459] = 写篇轻松的小文\n# en: \"Pie-rates Strike Again.\" Nessa groans at the headline and prints it anyway.\nopt.left.say.1 [0a7002] = 《馅饼大盗再度出手》。内莎对着标题哀叹了一声，还是印了。\n# en: Actually investigate\nopt.right.label [42eb33] = 认真调查\n# en: The culprit: a family of brownies living in the chimney. Your story runs on the front page. Nessa frames it.\nopt.right.say.1 [5dd8ed] = 犯人：住在烟囱里的一窝棕仙。你的报道登上了头版。内莎把它裱了起来。\n# en: You stake out the kitchens for three nights and catch nothing but a cold. Nessa pats your head.\nopt.right.say.2 [6e3684] = 你在厨房蹲守了三晚，除了感冒什么也没逮到。内莎拍了拍你的头。\n\n@event ledger_bram_story\n# en: The Prefect's Bribes\ntitle [609034] = 级长的贿赂\n# en: An anonymous tip: Bram Hollis has been taking bribes from first-years to skip their demerits. You have a witness. Nessa wants your byline on it. \"This is real journalism,\" she says, eyes shining.\ntext [78f450] = 一条匿名线报：布拉姆·霍利斯一直在收一年级生的贿赂，帮他们免掉记过。你有目击证人。内莎想让这篇报道署你的名字。“这才是真正的新闻，”她说，眼睛闪闪发亮。\n# en: An anonymous note spread on a desk under a lamp, next to a sketch of a prefect taking a coin purse from a first-year.\nscene [b9aaf0] = 台灯下摊开的一张匿名字条，旁边是一幅速写：一名级长正从一年级生手里接过钱袋。\n# en: Kill the story\nopt.left.label [50cd1f] = 压下这篇报道\n# en: Nessa is disappointed in you. Somehow, Bram finds out you buried it. He nods at you in the corridor. You feel slightly dirty.\nopt.left.say.1 [307410] = 内莎对你很失望。不知怎么的，布拉姆知道是你压下了它。他在走廊里朝你点点头。你觉得自己有点脏。\n# en: Publish it\nopt.right.label [d859b5] = 发表它\n# en: The story runs. Bram is stripped of his sash for a month. He gets it back. And now he knows exactly who you are.\nopt.right.say.1 [017011] = 报道刊出了。布拉姆被摘掉绶带一个月。后来他又拿回去了。而现在，他非常清楚你是谁。\n\n@event ledger_deadline\n# en: Deadline Night\ntitle [d06cb7] = 截稿之夜\n# en: The press runs at dawn and half the paper is blank. Nessa is chewing her quill. \"I need you tonight. All night. I'll owe you.\"\ntext [75d368] = 印刷机天亮开印，报纸还空着一半。内莎咬着羽毛笔。“今晚我需要你。整晚。我欠你一个人情。”\n# en: The newspaper office at 3am, crumpled drafts on the floor, two students slumped over typesetting trays.\nscene [4bbe74] = 凌晨三点的报社，地上满是揉皱的草稿，两个学生趴在排字盘上。\n# en: You have an exam tomorrow\nopt.left.label [c28ae7] = 你明天有考试\n# en: You sleep. The paper comes out with a large drawing of a duck on page four. Nessa doesn't speak to you for a day.\nopt.left.say.1 [f578ae] = 你去睡了。报纸第四版上印了一幅巨大的鸭子画。内莎一整天没理你。\n# en: Pull an all-nighter\nopt.right.label [d42cd2] = 熬个通宵\n# en: You write three articles and a horoscope. At dawn Nessa falls asleep on your shoulder, ink on her nose.\nopt.right.say.1 [99e236] = 你写了三篇文章和一份星座运势。天亮时，内莎靠在你肩上睡着了，鼻尖上沾着墨水。\n\n@event garden_mandrakes\n# en: The Mandrake Choir\ntitle [a16982] = 曼德拉草合唱团\n# en: The greenhouse mandrakes have started singing again. Off-key and enthusiastic. The earmuffs are on the hook by the door. Tobias isn't wearing his; he's harmonising.\ntext [df724d] = 温室里的曼德拉草又开始唱歌了。跑调，但热情洋溢。耳罩挂在门边的钩子上。托比亚斯没戴他的；他在跟着和声。\n# en: Rows of potted mandrakes in a steamy greenhouse, their little leafy heads opened in song.\nscene [65b35a] = 雾气蒸腾的温室里一排排盆栽曼德拉草，它们长着叶子的小脑袋张嘴高歌。\n# en: Earmuffs on\nopt.left.label [1586df] = 戴上耳罩\n# en: You repot six mandrakes in blissful silence. Good honest work.\nopt.left.say.1 [652505] = 你在幸福的寂静中给六株曼德拉草换了盆。踏踏实实的好活儿。\n# en: Sing along\nopt.right.label [91a84f] = 跟着唱\n# en: You sing along and promptly faint. Mandrake song is strong stuff. You wake up very relaxed.\nopt.right.say.1 [5d48fe] = 你跟着唱，然后立刻晕了过去。曼德拉草的歌声劲儿很大。你醒来时非常放松。\n# en: The mandrakes adore you. The greenhouse sounds like a pub at closing time.\nopt.right.say.2 [acd59a] = 曼德拉草们爱死你了。温室里听起来像打烊时的酒馆。\n\n@event garden_moonflower\n# en: The Moonflower\ntitle [924066] = 月光花\n# en: \"The moonflower's going to bloom tonight,\" Tobias says, a little breathless. \"Once every eleven years. I'm staying up to see it. You... could stay too. If you want.\"\ntext [1d0b11] = “今晚月光花要开了，”托比亚斯有点喘不过气地说，“十一年才开一次。我要熬夜看。你……也可以留下。如果你想的话。”\n# en: A single silver flower opening in the dark of a glass greenhouse, two faces lit by its glow.\nscene [ada882] = 漆黑的玻璃温室里一朵银色的花正在绽放，两张脸被它的光芒照亮。\n# en: You need sleep\nopt.left.label [145c0b] = 你得睡觉了\n# en: \"Course. Yeah.\" He tells you about it at breakfast, in detail, with gestures.\nopt.left.say.1 [a1e34d] = “当然。嗯。”早饭时他给你讲了一遍，事无巨细，手舞足蹈。\n# en: Stay up with him\nopt.right.label [4a8ffd] = 陪他熬夜\n# en: At 2am the flower opens and fills the greenhouse with silver light. Tobias doesn't look at the flower. He looks at you.\nopt.right.say.1 [7c8d48] = 凌晨两点，花开了，银光洒满温室。托比亚斯没有看花。他在看你。\n\n@event garden_maud_secret\n# en: The Secret Garden\ntitle [2193f1] = 秘密花园\n# en: The old groundskeeper beckons you behind the potting shed, to a door in the wall you've walked past a hundred times without seeing. \"You've got the hands for it,\" Old Maud says. \"Want to see what grows where nobody's looking?\"\ntext [e9e7ee] = 老园丁招手让你到盆栽棚后面，那里墙上有一扇门，你路过了上百次都没看见。“你有这双手，”老莫德说，“想看看在没人注意的地方长着什么吗？”\n# en: An old woman in a battered straw hat holding open a hidden door in an ivy-covered wall; beyond, impossible flowers glow.\nscene [044583] = 一位戴破草帽的老妇人推开爬满常春藤的墙上一扇隐秘的门；门后，不可思议的花朵闪着光。\n# en: Thank her and decline\nopt.left.label [4d277b] = 谢过她，婉拒了\n# en: \"Suit yourself.\" The door is gone when you look back.\nopt.left.say.1 [05ff78] = “随你。”你回头时，那扇门已经不见了。\n# en: Follow her in\nopt.right.label [499306] = 跟她进去\n# en: Inside: glowing ferns, silver bees, a pear tree that hums. Maud tucks a sprig of rosemary into your pocket. \"For luck. Don't lose it.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [473de8] = 里面有发光的蕨类、银色的蜜蜂，还有一棵会哼歌的梨树。莫德把一枝迷迭香塞进你口袋。“保佑你的。别弄丢了。”\n\n@event stars_cocoa_night\n# en: Cocoa on the Roof\ntitle [a1a0e0] = 屋顶上的热可可\n# en: Stargazers meet on the roof with blankets, telescopes and an illegal quantity of cocoa. Tonight is cloudy. Nobody cares.\ntext [9a36b4] = 观星社在屋顶聚会，带着毯子、望远镜和数量不太合法的热可可。今晚多云。没人在乎。\n# en: Students wrapped in blankets on a flat roof, steaming mugs in hand, telescopes pointed at a velvet sky.\nscene [c74695] = 学生们裹着毯子坐在平坦的屋顶上，手捧冒热气的杯子，望远镜对着天鹅绒般的夜空。\n# en: Talk until dawn\nopt.left.label [2f64c7] = 聊到天亮\n# en: You talk about home, about the future, about whether stars are lonely. Someone falls asleep on your shoulder.\nopt.left.say.1 [6965e5] = 你们聊家乡，聊未来，聊星星会不会孤单。有人靠在你肩上睡着了。\n# en: Do your homework by starlight\nopt.right.label [5cf04c] = 借着星光写作业\n# en: You finish your essay by the light of the cocoa-warmer charm. Productive. A little lonely.\nopt.right.say.1 [6dcd21] = 你借着暖可可咒的光写完了论文。很高效。有点孤单。\n\n@event stars_meteor_bet\n# en: The Meteor Wager\ntitle [262c9e] = 流星赌局\n# en: The club is running a betting pool on tonight's meteor count. The current favourite is 40. The charts suggest something else. Or do they?\ntext [25d6ed] = 社团在为今晚的流星数量开赌盘。目前热门是 40 颗。星图暗示的数字却不一样。真的吗？\n# en: A chalkboard on an observatory wall covered in tally marks and names, a pile of coins on a stool beneath it.\nscene [e0c74a] = 天文台墙上的黑板写满计数和名字，下面凳子上堆着一堆硬币。\n# en: Bet on the charts\nopt.left.label [b8316b] = 押星图的数字\n# en: Fifty-three meteors. You called fifty-two. Close enough to win the pot.\nopt.left.say.1 [0a5d53] = 五十三颗流星。你押的是五十二。足够接近，赢下了奖池。\n# en: The charts were wrong, or you read them wrong. Eight crowns gone.\nopt.left.say.2 [bd7c8a] = 星图错了，或者是你看错了。八克朗没了。\n# en: Stay out of it\nopt.right.label [03d24a] = 不参与\n# en: You watch the meteors for their own sake. Turns out that's the best seat in the house.\nopt.right.say.1 [8c78f1] = 你只是单纯地看流星。原来这才是最好的位置。\n\n@event stars_constellation\n# en: An Unnamed Shape\ntitle [42283b] = 无名之形\n# en: Plotting the autumn sky, you notice seven faint stars that form a ring around nothing. The charts call that patch of sky \"empty\". It doesn't look empty.\ntext [541d68] = 绘制秋季星空时，你注意到七颗暗淡的星星围成一个环，环中空无一物。星图把那片天空标为“空白”。可它看起来并不空。\n# en: A star chart spread on a table, a student's finger tracing a new pattern between unmarked stars.\nscene [cb6cc5] = 桌上摊开的星图，一名学生的手指在未标注的星星间描出新的图案。\n# en: Ignore it\nopt.left.label [e2ae0c] = 不去管它\n# en: You plot the usual constellations. Your chart gets top marks.\nopt.left.say.1 [9e48fd] = 你画了常规的星座。你的星图得了最高分。\n# en: Investigate\nopt.right.label [c3e054] = 调查一下\n# en: You spend a week in the archives. Three hundred years ago, the patch was called the Hollow. Then someone scraped the name off every chart.\nopt.right.say.1 [81c647] = 你在档案馆泡了一周。三百年前，那片天空叫作“空洞”。后来有人把这个名字从每一张星图上刮掉了。\n\n@event carto_invite\n# en: An Invitation at Midnight\ntitle [324f7e] = 午夜邀请\n# en: A black card under your door. On it, a map in silver ink that redraws itself while you watch. Corvin's handwriting: \"Midnight. Under the third gargoyle. Bring a candle. Tell no one.\"\ntext [afdfc2] = 门缝下塞进一张黑色卡片。上面是一张银墨地图，在你眼前不断重绘。科尔文的笔迹：“午夜。第三尊石像鬼下面。带上蜡烛。别告诉任何人。”\n# en: A slip of black paper pushed under a dormitory door, a silver ink map on it that rearranges itself as you watch.\nscene [f40da5] = 一张从宿舍门缝塞进来的黑纸条，上面的银墨地图在你注视下自行重组。\n# en: Ignore it\nopt.left.label [e2ae0c] = 不理它\n# en: The card blanks itself the next morning. Corvin doesn't mention it, but he's a little cooler for a week.\nopt.left.say.1 [e32163] = 第二天早上卡片变成了空白。科尔文没提这事，但接下来一周对你冷淡了些。\n# en: Go\nopt.right.label [d0c56b] = 去\n# en: Under the gargoyle: six students, a stack of maps and a passage you'd never seen. \"Welcome,\" says Corvin, \"to the club that doesn't exist.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [de031b] = 石像鬼下面：六个学生、一摞地图，还有一条你从未见过的通道。“欢迎，”科尔文说，“来到这个不存在的社团。”\n\n@event carto_thursday_stairs\n# en: The Thursday Staircase\ntitle [571623] = 周四楼梯\n# en: The Cartographers have heard of a staircase that only exists on Thursdays between midnight and one. Tonight is Thursday. It is 11:58.\ntext [95afb9] = 制图社听说有一道楼梯，只在周四午夜到一点之间存在。今晚是周四。现在是 11:58。\n# en: A spiral staircase emerging from a blank wall in moonlight, its steps unfolding one by one.\nscene [54fe61] = 月光下一道螺旋楼梯从空白的墙面里冒出来，台阶一级一级地展开。\n# en: Map it carefully\nopt.left.label [150195] = 仔细绘制它\n# en: You chart forty-one steps and a landing with a window onto a sky that isn't this one. The map is the club's pride.\nopt.left.say.1 [0210dc] = 你画下了四十一级台阶，和一个平台——平台的窗外是另一片天空。这张地图成了社团的骄傲。\n# en: Climb it to the top\nopt.right.label [fb1da8] = 一直爬到顶\n# en: At the top, a forgotten storeroom. Inside: a chest of old academy coins. The club splits it. Corvin cries a little.\nopt.right.say.1 [13aaa2] = 顶上是一间被遗忘的储藏室。里面有一箱古老的学院硬币。社团把它分了。科尔文哭了一小会儿。\n# en: At one o'clock the staircase stops existing. You were on it. You land in a laundry chute.\nopt.right.say.2 [714828] = 一点整，楼梯不再存在。而你正站在上面。你掉进了洗衣房的滑道。\n\n@event carto_lake_tunnel\n# en: The Tunnel Under the Lake\ntitle [7b054e] = 湖底隧道\n# en: A loose stone in the boathouse cellar opens on a tunnel running out under the lake. The bricks drip. At the far end, a round door carved with a black star. It is warm to the touch.\ntext [ea3eb1] = 船屋地窖里一块松动的石头后面，是一条延伸到湖底的隧道。砖墙滴着水。尽头是一扇刻着黑色星星的圆门。摸上去是温的。\n# en: A narrow brick tunnel dripping with water, lit by a single candle, ending at a round door carved with a black star.\nscene [1d60a2] = 一条滴水的狭窄砖砌隧道，只有一支蜡烛照明，尽头是一扇刻着黑星的圆门。\n# en: Mark it and leave\nopt.left.label [98295f] = 做个标记就离开\n# en: You add the tunnel to the club map with a skull beside it. Corvin adds a second skull, to be safe.\nopt.left.say.1 [837f6b] = 你把隧道添到社团地图上，旁边画了个骷髅。科尔文又加了一个骷髅，以防万一。\n# en: Press your palm to the star\nopt.right.label [f4d16b] = 把手掌按在星上\n# en: The star drinks the warmth out of your hand. Behind the door, something very large shifts in its sleep.\nopt.right.say.1 [d08b1b] = 星星吸走了你掌心的温度。门后，有什么非常庞大的东西在睡梦中翻了个身。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/crisis.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/crisis.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event crisis_collapse\n# en: Collapse\ntitle [59ccfe] = 倒下\n# en: The world tilts. When it rights itself you are in the infirmary, and Sister Amaranth is looking at you over her spectacles. \"You have been running your body like a borrowed broom,\" she says.\ntext [f87609] = 世界倾斜了。等它恢复正常时，你已经躺在医务室里，阿玛兰斯修女正从眼镜上方看着你。“你对待自己的身体，就像对待一把借来的飞帚。”她说。\n# en: A narrow infirmary bed under a high arched window, a healer's lamp floating beside it.\nscene [27d146] = 高高的拱窗下一张窄窄的病床，旁边悬浮着一盏治疗师的灯。\n# en: Pay for the full cure (30 gold)\nopt.left.label [97a3e9] = 付钱彻底治好（30 金币）\n# en: Three foul tonics and two days' sleep later, you feel almost human.\nopt.left.say.1 [ecd52f] = 三剂难喝的药和两天的睡眠之后，你感觉差不多像个人了。\n# en: You can't pay. She heals you anyway, slowly, and you miss a fortnight of classes.\nopt.left.say.2 [a936d1] = 你付不起。她还是给你治了，只是慢一些，你缺了两周的课。\n# en: Ask to rest instead\nopt.right.label [8eabcd] = 请求静养\n# en: A long, dull fortnight in bed. Sister Amaranth warns you: \"Next time, I write to your family.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [b20134] = 在床上躺了漫长而无聊的两周。阿玛兰斯修女警告你：“下次，我就写信给你家里。”\n\n@event crisis_despair\n# en: The Grey\ntitle [9c424f] = 灰色\n# en: Everything has gone grey. You have packed your trunk twice this week and unpacked it once. It would be so easy to take the next barge and simply stop.\ntext [02373b] = 一切都变成了灰色。这周你收拾了两次行李，又拆开了一次。坐上下一班驳船，就此停下，是多么容易。\n# en: A student sitting on the edge of a dormitory bed, rain on the window, a packed trunk by the door.\nscene [dc74cf] = 一个学生坐在宿舍床沿，窗上有雨，门边放着收拾好的行李箱。\n# en: Reach out to someone\nopt.left.label [e2c5e9] = 向别人伸出手\n# en: You knock on a friend's door. They don't ask questions. They just make tea, and stay.\nopt.left.say.1 [5f5838] = 你敲响了朋友的门。对方什么也没问。只是泡了茶，陪着你。\n# en: Old Maud finds you in the gardens and puts a trowel in your hand. \"Dig,\" she says. You dig. It helps more than it should.\nopt.left.say.2 [a27142] = 老莫德在花园里找到你，把一把小铲子塞进你手里。“挖。”她说。你挖了。这比想象中更有用。\n# en: You talk to the night porter, of all people. He tells you about his own first year. It's enough, for now.\nopt.left.say.3 [26f9c5] = 你竟然和守夜门房聊了起来。他给你讲了他自己的一年级。暂时，这就够了。\n# en: Take the next barge\nopt.right.label [c52bdf] = 坐下一班驳船\n# en: You get as far as the landing. The barge is late. By the time it arrives, you have decided to stay one more month.\nopt.right.say.1 [d9e779] = 你走到了码头。驳船晚点了。等它到的时候，你决定再留一个月。\n\n@event crisis_probation\n# en: The Disciplinary Board\ntitle [f764c3] = 纪律委员会\n# en: Two terms of failing marks. You are summoned before the Disciplinary Board: three professors and the headmistress, who looks at you with an expression of deep, patient disappointment. {flag(board_reprieve): They have seen you here before.|}\ntext [db6aa2] = 连续两个学期挂科。你被传唤到纪律委员会面前：三位教授和院长，院长看着你的表情里满是深沉而耐心的失望。{flag(board_reprieve): 他们以前在这里见过你。|}\n# en: Three faculty members behind a high bench in a dim chamber; the headmistress in the centre, hands folded.\nscene [1c27f2] = 昏暗的厅堂里，三位教职员坐在高高的长桌后；院长居中，双手交握。\n# en: Plead your case\nopt.left.label [bb8fcc] = 为自己辩护\n# en: You speak honestly and well. They grant you one reprieve. \"There will not be a second,\" says the headmistress, pleasantly.\nopt.left.say.1 [4159dd] = 你说得诚恳而得体。他们给了你一次宽限。“不会有第二次了，”院长和气地说。\n# en: Promise to work harder\nopt.right.label [a78172] = 保证会更努力\n# en: \"Promises are cheap,\" says Professor Thorne. \"We'll take this one. Only this one.\" A reprieve, and a very long term ahead.\nopt.right.say.1 [8a82c9] = “承诺不值钱，”索恩教授说，“这一次我们收下。只此一次。”一次宽限，以及一个漫长的学期。\n\n@event grisk_reminder\n# en: A Friendly Reminder\ntitle [6aaed3] = 友情提醒\n# en: A note on your door, in a tidy goblin hand: \"A payment was missed. Mister Grisk is a patient businessman. Mister Grisk charges interest on his patience.\" Underneath, smaller: \"Pay something, or another month will be added.\"\ntext [67613b] = 你门上钉着一张字条，是工整的地精笔迹：“有一笔还款逾期了。格里斯克先生是位有耐心的生意人。格里斯克先生的耐心是要收利息的。”下面一行小字：“多少还一点，否则再加一个月。”\n# en: A small, very neat note pinned to a dormitory door with a brass tack, the handwriting tiny and precise.\nscene [309f11] = 一张小小的、非常整洁的字条用黄铜图钉钉在宿舍门上，字迹细小而一丝不苟。\n# en: Scrape together what you can\nopt.left.label [d226e0] = 能凑多少凑多少\n# en: You empty your purse into an envelope and slide it under his door. It isn't twelve crowns. It is, says the note in reply, \"a gesture. Noted.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [acdacd] = 你把钱袋倒进一个信封，塞到他门下。不到十二克朗。回信的字条上写着：“一份心意。已记录。”\n# en: Put it out of your mind\nopt.right.label [68419d] = 别去想它\n# en: You put the note in a drawer. Another month goes on the ledger, and next month the knock on your door will be louder.\nopt.right.say.1 [2bdb82] = 你把字条塞进抽屉。账本上又多了一个月，下个月的敲门声会更响。\n\n@event grisk_collectors\n# en: Grisk's Collectors\ntitle [eb1401] = 格里斯克的讨债人\n# en: Two trolls in waistcoats are waiting outside your dormitory. \"Mister Grisk,\" says the larger one, \"is disappointed.\" The smaller one cracks his knuckles, which sounds like a rockslide.\ntext [6dfc11] = 两个穿马甲的巨魔等在你的宿舍外。“格里斯克先生，”大个子说，“很失望。”小个子把指关节捏得咔咔响，听起来像山崩。\n# en: Two large trolls in ill-fitting waistcoats looming in a dormitory doorway, one holding a ledger.\nscene [8592b2] = 两个穿着不合身马甲的高大巨魔堵在宿舍门口，其中一个拿着账簿。\n# en: Hand over everything you have\nopt.left.label [eec2ea] = 交出所有的钱\n# en: They count your purse twice and declare the debt settled. \"Pleasure,\" says the large one, meaning it.\nopt.left.say.1 [377b97] = 他们把你的钱袋数了两遍，宣布债务两清。“荣幸。”大个子说，是真心的。\n# en: They take every coin and your good boots, in front of half the corridor. \"Partial,\" they say, and leave.\nopt.left.say.2 [8315e9] = 他们当着半条走廊的人，拿走了每一枚硬币和你最好的靴子。“部分还款。”他们说完就走了。\n# en: Run\nopt.right.label [57e66a] = 逃跑\n# en: You escape across the rooftops. They'll be back, but not this month.\nopt.right.say.1 [24257a] = 你从屋顶上逃走了。他们还会回来，但不是这个月。\n# en: You do not escape. The trolls are surprisingly gentle about breaking only one rib.\nopt.right.say.2 [0374eb] = 你没逃掉。巨魔们出人意料地温柔，只断了你一根肋骨。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/expansion_classes.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/expansion_classes.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event core_flying_lesson\n# en: Compulsory Broom Flight\ntitle [014809] = 必修飞帚课\n# en: Compulsory first-year broom flight. The instructor blows her whistle. Your broom rises, hesitates, and then — clearly having taken a dislike to you — begins drifting toward the lake.\ntext [d69dcf] = 一年级必修的飞帚课。教练吹响了哨子。你的扫帚升起来，犹豫了一下，然后——显然是不太喜欢你——开始往湖那边飘。\n# en: A row of nervous first-years standing beside brooms on the lawn, one broom already rising on its own, an instructor with a whistle.\nscene [40aef9] = 草坪上一排紧张的一年级生站在扫帚旁，其中一把已经自己飞了起来，教练手里拿着哨子。\n# en: Hold on and steer it back\nopt.left.label [231b0d] = 抓紧，把它拽回来\n# en: You wrestle it round and land, wobbling, exactly on the mark. The instructor nods. The broom grudgingly accepts you.\nopt.left.say.1 [9f0eb8] = 你硬是把它扳了回来，摇摇晃晃地正好落在标记上。教练点了点头。扫帚勉强认了你。\n# en: The broom wins. You land in the shallows. Everyone sees. Everyone remembers.\nopt.left.say.2 [ccfc57] = 扫帚赢了。你落进了浅水里。所有人都看见了。所有人都记住了。\n# en: Jump off while it's still low\nopt.right.label [c7b2ec] = 趁它还飞得低跳下来\n# en: You drop four feet onto the grass. Undignified, but dry. The broom carries on to the lake alone and sinks with a sulky gurgle.\nopt.right.say.1 [ce5e6e] = 你从四英尺高的地方跳到草地上。不太体面，但没湿。扫帚独自飞到湖上，咕嘟一声闷闷不乐地沉了下去。\n\n@event pyro_dragon_breath\n# en: A Sample of Dragonfire\ntitle [8d15de] = 一缕龙焰样本\n# en: Thorne holds up a sealed jar containing one flame of genuine dragonfire, blue-white and furious. \"Who would like to try holding it?\" he asks. Nobody moves. He looks at you.\ntext [d3969c] = 索恩举起一个密封的罐子，里面是一团货真价实的龙焰，蓝白色，怒气冲冲。“谁想试着捧一下？”他问。没人动。他看向了你。\n# en: A glass jar containing a single writhing flame of deep blue, held in tongs by a stern professor, students leaning back from it.\nscene [5714c4] = 一个玻璃罐里扭动着一团深蓝色的火焰，被一位严厉的教授用钳子夹着，学生们纷纷往后仰。\n# en: Hold it\nopt.left.label [bf9ba5] = 捧住它\n# en: It sears and struggles and then, astonishingly, settles in your palm like a small warm animal. The class stares. Thorne almost smiles.\nopt.left.say.1 [1f13d1] = 它灼烧着、挣扎着，然后——令人震惊地——像一只温暖的小动物一样在你掌心安顿下来。全班都看呆了。索恩差点笑了。\n# en: The dragonfire disagrees. You spend a week with bandaged hands and a new respect for dragons.\nopt.left.say.2 [77f691] = 龙焰不同意。你缠着绷带过了一个星期，对龙有了全新的敬意。\n# en: Watch someone else\nopt.right.label [1e1e35] = 看别人来\n# en: A fifth-year volunteers and loses a sleeve. You take careful notes on what not to do.\nopt.right.say.1 [ba7fb7] = 一个五年级生自告奋勇，结果烧掉了一只袖子。你仔细记下了哪些事不该做。\n\n@event tide_water_spirit_race\n# en: The Water Spirit Race\ntitle [da1049] = 水灵竞速\n# en: Professor Marisol's final challenge: swim the lake while the water spirits race you. \"They cheat,\" she says cheerfully. \"Ask them nicely not to.\"\ntext [2365f2] = 玛丽索尔教授的期末挑战：游过湖去，同时有水灵和你比赛。“它们会作弊，”她兴高采烈地说，“好好请它们别作弊。”\n# en: Students swimming across a lake at dawn, translucent water-spirits racing alongside them like silver dolphins.\nscene [561442] = 黎明时分，学生们游过湖面，半透明的水灵像银色海豚一样在他们身旁竞速。\n# en: Race them flat out\nopt.left.label [2cf84d] = 全力和它们比\n# en: You touch the far shore a hand's length ahead of the fastest spirit. It sulks, then laughs like a fountain.\nopt.left.say.1 [5f4a18] = 你比最快的那个水灵早一掌的距离摸到对岸。它生了会儿闷气，然后像喷泉一样笑了起来。\n# en: The spirits tow you in circles. You arrive last, but very clean.\nopt.left.say.2 [320621] = 水灵拖着你转圈圈。你最后一个到，不过洗得非常干净。\n# en: Ask them nicely\nopt.right.label [a2f34a] = 好好请求它们\n# en: You ask. They carry you across on their backs like a raft. Marisol says that counts. Marisol says it's the whole point.\nopt.right.say.1 [c7d0ee] = 你开口请求。它们像木筏一样把你驮了过去。玛丽索尔说这算数。玛丽索尔说这才是重点。\n# en: They pretend not to hear. Water spirits are terrible at manners.\nopt.right.say.2 [eac302] = 它们假装没听见。水灵最不讲礼貌了。\n\n@event illusion_mirror_maze\n# en: The Mirror Maze Exam\ntitle [55c841] = 镜之迷宫考试\n# en: Professor Vey's exam: a maze of enchanted mirrors. Find the exit. Some reflections lie. One of them is — she promises — the real you.\ntext [0aec9f] = 维伊教授的考试：一座魔法镜子组成的迷宫。找到出口。有些倒影会说谎。其中一个——她保证——是真正的你。\n# en: A maze of tall mirrors in a dark hall, each reflecting a different version of the same student, one reflection grinning.\nscene [b9068c] = 黑暗大厅里的高镜迷宫，每面镜子都映出同一个学生的不同模样，其中一个倒影在咧嘴笑。\n# en: Trust your own eyes\nopt.left.label [ccacee] = 相信自己的眼睛\n# en: You spot the reflection that blinks late, and follow it out. Vey awards top marks and says, \"Most people trust the one that looks happiest.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [f97c79] = 你发现了那个眨眼慢半拍的倒影，跟着它走了出去。维伊给了你最高分，说：“大多数人都相信看起来最开心的那个。”\n# en: You follow the happiest reflection for an hour. It leads you in a circle and waves goodbye.\nopt.left.say.2 [21a1ac] = 你跟着最开心的那个倒影走了一个钟头。它领你绕了一圈，然后挥手道别。\n# en: Shatter the illusion\nopt.right.label [aa2560] = 击碎幻象\n# en: Every mirror goes dark at once. Exit found. Vey writes, \"Effective. Inelegant. Pass.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [137f22] = 所有镜子同时暗了下去。出口找到了。维伊写道：“有效。不优雅。及格。”\n# en: You shatter one mirror. It was real. Seven years of bad luck, the caretaker says, or three crowns for the glass.\nopt.right.say.2 [a46c96] = 你击碎了一面镜子。它是真的。管理员说，要么倒霉七年，要么赔三克朗玻璃钱。\n\n@event alch_love_potion\n# en: The Love Potion Assignment\ntitle [d13e1c] = 爱情药水作业\n# en: This week's assignment: brew a love potion — \"for analytical purposes only\". Your flask glows a suspicious pink. Someone at the next bench asks if they can buy it. Several people ask.\ntext [3c39e1] = 本周作业：调制一剂爱情药水——“仅供分析之用”。你的烧瓶泛着可疑的粉光。隔壁桌有人问能不能买下来。好几个人都问了。\n# en: A pink, glittering potion in a heart-shaped flask on a workbench, a student holding it up to the light with a doubtful expression.\nscene [1a4005] = 工作台上一个心形烧瓶里装着闪亮的粉色药水，一名学生把它举到光下，满脸怀疑。\n# en: Brew it perfectly, then pour it away\nopt.left.label [8fa0a2] = 完美调好，然后倒掉\n# en: Perfect colour, perfect scent. You pour it down the sink. The pipes are affectionate for a week.\nopt.left.say.1 [d421ab] = 完美的颜色，完美的香气。你把它倒进了水槽。水管一整个星期都含情脉脉的。\n# en: Sell it\nopt.right.label [4ac442] = 卖掉它\n# en: A prefect falls in love with the headmistress's portrait. It's traced back to you. Worth it, almost.\nopt.right.say.1 [1f1132] = 一位级长爱上了院长的画像。追查下来找到了你。差不多值了。\n# en: Fifteen crowns. You never ask what it was used for. You see a lot of unusual couples at dinner.\nopt.right.say.2 [9fdb68] = 十五克朗。你从没问过它被用来干什么。你在晚餐时看到了很多不寻常的情侣。\n\n@event div_prophecy_exam\n# en: The Prophecy Exam\ntitle [fa5039] = 预言考试\n# en: The final Divination exam: gaze into the crystal and make one prophecy about the coming month. The examiner will check. \"Vague prophecies,\" she warns, \"fail.\"\ntext [891acd] = 占卜学期末考试：凝视水晶球，对下个月做出一条预言。考官会核实。“含糊的预言，”她警告道，“不及格。”\n# en: A dim divination classroom, a single student staring into a crystal ball, the examiner's quill hovering expectantly over a blank page.\nscene [91fdd6] = 昏暗的占卜教室，一名学生独自凝视着水晶球，考官的羽毛笔悬在空白的纸上，满怀期待。\n# en: Make a bold, specific prophecy\nopt.left.label [d1a2b7] = 做一个大胆而具体的预言\n# en: \"On the eighth, the kitchen cat will have kittens in the headmistress's hat.\" On the eighth, she does. You pass with distinction.\nopt.left.say.1 [014f80] = “八号那天，厨房的猫会在院长的帽子里生小猫。”八号那天，它真的生了。你以优异成绩通过。\n# en: \"On the eighth, it will snow indoors.\" It does not snow indoors. The examiner writes \"ambitious\".\nopt.left.say.2 [fc7167] = “八号那天，室内会下雪。”室内并没有下雪。考官写下“有野心”。\n# en: Make a careful, safe prophecy\nopt.right.label [e30df1] = 做一个谨慎稳妥的预言\n# en: \"Someone will be late to breakfast.\" Someone is. The examiner sighs and gives you a pass.\nopt.right.say.1 [c07160] = “有人早饭会迟到。”确实有人迟到了。考官叹了口气，给你打了及格。\n\n@event art_golem_helper\n# en: The Little Golem\ntitle [6f3f24] = 小魔像\n# en: Your term project: a knee-high clay golem that carries books. It works! It works so well that it has started carrying everyone's books, whether they want it to or not.\ntext [677080] = 你的学期作业：一个及膝高的黏土魔像，专门搬书。它成功了！成功得过了头，开始搬所有人的书，不管人家愿不愿意。\n# en: A knee-high clay golem carrying a teetering stack of books across a classroom, a student watching anxiously as it nears a staircase.\nscene [4ac493] = 一个及膝高的黏土魔像抱着一摞摇摇欲坠的书穿过教室，一名学生紧张地看着它走近楼梯。\n# en: Add a \"stop\" command\nopt.left.label [3c6ffc] = 加一个“停下”指令\n# en: The golem stops. It also waits politely. The professor asks if she can borrow it for her office.\nopt.left.say.1 [6ad2e4] = 魔像停下了，还会礼貌地等待。教授问能不能借去她的办公室用。\n# en: Now it stops randomly, mid-staircase, holding forty books. Nobody can get past it. It looks very pleased.\nopt.left.say.2 [d28e81] = 现在它会随机停下——在楼梯中间，抱着四十本书。谁也过不去。它看起来非常得意。\n# en: Let it roam free\nopt.right.label [d4c542] = 让它自由游荡\n# en: The golem becomes a beloved castle fixture, delivering books to whoever looks sad. Nobody knows who made it. You know.\nopt.right.say.1 [2577fe] = 魔像成了城堡里人见人爱的一景，专给看起来难过的人送书。没人知道是谁造的。你知道。\n# en: It also delivers a Restricted Section book to a first-year. The librarian follows the clay smudges straight to your door.\nopt.right.say.2 [94ea9a] = 它还把一本禁书区的书送到了一个一年级生手上。图书管理员顺着泥印一路找到了你的门口。\n\n@event beast_hippocampus\n# en: The Hippocampus Foal\ntitle [21cc8d] = 小海马驹\n# en: A hippocampus foal — half pony, half fish — has been stranded in a shrinking rock pool on the lakeshore. Professor Brask is on the other side of the grounds. The foal is panicking.\ntext [4890ea] = 一只海马驹——半是小马，半是鱼——被困在湖边一个不断缩小的石潭里。布拉斯克教授在校园另一头。小马驹惊慌失措。\n# en: A tiny sea-horse foal with a fish's tail struggling in a rock pool on the lake shore, a student kneeling beside the water.\nscene [205918] = 湖边石潭里，一只长着鱼尾的小海马驹在挣扎，一名学生跪在水边。\n# en: Carry it to the lake\nopt.left.label [d9060c] = 把它抱回湖里\n# en: It thrashes, then trusts you. You wade out and let it go. It circles you three times before diving deep.\nopt.left.say.1 [4d71d5] = 它扑腾了一阵，然后信任了你。你蹚进水里放开它。它绕着你转了三圈，然后潜入深处。\n# en: It kicks you in the stomach with surprising force — but you get it to the water. Brask says that's how they say thank you.\nopt.left.say.2 [7f1482] = 它力气大得惊人地踢中了你的肚子——但你还是把它送回了水里。布拉斯克说，它们就是这样道谢的。\n# en: Run for Professor Brask\nopt.right.label [cb87c9] = 跑去找布拉斯克教授\n# en: Brask arrives with a bucket and a lullaby. She makes you hold the bucket. You learn more in ten minutes than in a whole term.\nopt.right.say.1 [07b643] = 布拉斯克带着一只水桶和一首摇篮曲赶来了。她让你拎着水桶。你在十分钟里学到的比一整个学期还多。\n\n@event astro_eclipse\n# en: The Eclipse\ntitle [deb525] = 日食\n# en: An eclipse, the first in forty years. The astromancy class is on the roof with smoked-glass lenses. At the moment of totality, you notice something the charts don't show: a second shadow, smaller, moving the wrong way.\ntext [1ca60e] = 一场日食，四十年来的第一次。星象学课的学生们拿着烟熏玻璃镜片站在屋顶上。全食的那一刻，你注意到了星图上没有的东西：第二道阴影，更小，朝着相反的方向移动。\n# en: A black disc sliding across the sun above the observatory dome, students on the roof with smoked-glass lenses, a halo of light around the darkness.\nscene [5b724a] = 天文台穹顶上方，一个黑色圆盘滑过太阳，屋顶上的学生们举着烟熏玻璃镜片，黑暗周围一圈光晕。\n# en: Record it precisely\nopt.left.label [ee9627] = 精确记录下来\n# en: You chart the second shadow's path exactly. It crosses the sky and stops — directly above the lake.\nopt.left.say.1 [07b204] = 你精确地画出了第二道阴影的轨迹。它划过天空，停了下来——正好停在湖的上方。\n# en: Just watch the eclipse\nopt.right.label [b5b48a] = 只是看日食\n# en: For four minutes the world holds its breath. You'll remember the cold, and the silence, and the strange birdsong, for the rest of your life.\nopt.right.say.1 [3ab28b] = 有四分钟，整个世界屏住了呼吸。你这辈子都会记得那份寒冷、那份寂静，还有那奇怪的鸟鸣。\n\n@event heal_triage_drill\n# en: Triage\ntitle [090621] = 伤员分诊\n# en: Sister Amaranth's triage drill: twelve \"injured\" students, one healer — you — and ten minutes. Some are faking worse than others. One, you realise, is genuinely hurt.\ntext [c92712] = 阿玛兰斯修女的分诊演练：十二个“受伤”的学生，一个治疗师——就是你——还有十分钟。有些人装得比别人更夸张。而有一个，你意识到，是真的受伤了。\n# en: A practice ward full of students acting injured, groaning theatrically on cots, a single student healer in the middle with a clipboard.\nscene [5bb9f0] = 一间练习病房里满是假装受伤的学生，在病床上夸张地呻吟，一名学生治疗师拿着记录板站在中间。\n# en: Treat the genuine injury first\nopt.left.label [ab25ba] = 先处理真正的伤\n# en: You spot the real injury in the first minute. Amaranth nods, once, which is the highest honour in the infirmary.\nopt.left.say.1 [ac5815] = 你在第一分钟就发现了真正的伤员。阿玛兰斯点了一下头——这是医务室里的最高荣誉。\n# en: You find the real injury in minute nine. Late, but not too late. Amaranth's lips thin. \"Again tomorrow.\"\nopt.left.say.2 [da92d2] = 你在第九分钟才找到真正的伤员。晚了，但还不算太晚。阿玛兰斯抿紧了嘴唇。“明天再来。”\n# en: Work systematically down the line\nopt.right.label [7c7311] = 按顺序一个个处理\n# en: Methodical, thorough and slow. The real patient waits seven minutes. Amaranth makes you write an essay on why.\nopt.right.say.1 [503f17] = 有条不紊、细致而缓慢。真正的病人等了七分钟。阿玛兰斯让你写一篇文章解释为什么。\n\n@event umbral_last_lecture\n# en: The Last Lecture on Absence\ntitle [9b0f64] = 关于虚无的最后一课\n# en: The Umbral professor draws a star with a hollow centre on the board. \"This is not on the syllabus,\" she says. \"Three hundred years ago this academy imprisoned an absence that thinks. I believe you should know that before you graduate.\"\ntext [3f5b86] = 暗影理论教授在黑板上画了一颗中心空洞的星星。“这不在教学大纲里，”她说，“三百年前，这所学院囚禁了一个会思考的虚无。我认为你们在毕业前应该知道这件事。”\n# en: An Umbral Theory lecture hall lit only by a black candle, the professor drawing a star with a hollow centre on the board.\nscene [59edcc] = 暗影理论的讲堂只点着一支黑色蜡烛，教授在黑板上画着一颗中心空洞的星星。\n# en: Take careful notes\nopt.left.label [4d95af] = 认真记笔记\n# en: She speaks for an hour about seals and locks and the founders' fear. At the end she wipes the board clean and says, \"This lecture never happened.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [4517c0] = 她讲了一个钟头，关于封印、锁和创始者的恐惧。最后她把黑板擦干净，说：“这堂课从没发生过。”\n# en: Ask why she's telling you\nopt.right.label [16a97a] = 问她为什么告诉你们\n# en: \"Because something is stirring,\" she says, \"and the headmistress asked me to find out which of you would ask that question.\" She writes your name down.\nopt.right.say.1 [9e5f65] = “因为有什么东西正在苏醒，”她说，“院长让我看看你们当中谁会问这个问题。”她写下了你的名字。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/expansion_faculty.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/expansion_faculty.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event thorne_old_rival\n# en: An Old Rival\ntitle [357013] = 宿敌老友\n# en: A flamboyant old wizard in purple has arrived to settle \"a forty-year-old matter\" with Professor Thorne. A formal duel needs a second. Thorne looks around the courtyard and, to everyone's astonishment, points at you.\ntext [067b32] = 一位身着紫袍、浮夸的老巫师来了，要和索恩教授了结“一桩四十年前的旧事”。正式决斗需要一名副手。索恩环顾庭院，然后——令所有人震惊地——指向了你。\n# en: Two elderly mages facing each other across a dueling ring at dusk, one stern and grey, one flamboyant in purple, a nervous student holding a towel at the side.\nscene [40cb18] = 黄昏，两位年迈的法师在决斗场两端对峙，一位严肃灰白，一位紫袍浮夸，一名紧张的学生拿着毛巾站在一旁。\n# en: Stand as his second\nopt.left.label [e6354b] = 当他的副手\n# en: The duel lasts four seconds. Thorne wins. Then both old men sit on the grass and laugh until they wheeze. You've never seen him laugh. Neither has anyone else.\nopt.left.say.1 [f8d733] = 决斗只持续了四秒。索恩赢了。然后两个老头坐在草地上，一直笑到喘不过气。你从没见过他笑。别人也没见过。\n# en: Politely decline\nopt.right.label [8a601d] = 礼貌地拒绝\n# en: \"Hm,\" says Thorne, and asks the caretaker instead. He wins anyway. He does not mention it again, which is somehow worse.\nopt.right.say.1 [ddf880] = “嗯，”索恩说，转而请了管理员。他照样赢了。他再也没提起这件事，这不知怎么反而更糟。\n\n@event thorne_roses\n# en: The Professor's Roses\ntitle [ce1627] = 教授的玫瑰\n# en: At dawn, behind the observatory, you find Professor Thorne on his knees in a tiny walled rose garden, murmuring encouragement to a sickly bush. He sees you. Neither of you breathes.\ntext [163ce7] = 黎明时分，在天文台后面，你发现索恩教授跪在一个围墙环绕的小玫瑰园里，低声鼓励着一株病恹恹的玫瑰。他看见了你。你们俩都屏住了呼吸。\n# en: A severe old professor kneeling in a hidden rose garden at dawn, sleeves rolled up, murmuring encouragement to a single struggling rosebush.\nscene [0c6758] = 黎明，一位严厉的老教授跪在隐秘的玫瑰园里，袖子卷起，低声鼓励着一株挣扎求生的玫瑰。\n# en: Back away and never speak of it\nopt.left.label [225e23] = 悄悄退开，永不提起\n# en: You leave. The next day in class he calls on you for the hardest question — and then, very quietly, gives you full marks for a so-so answer.\nopt.left.say.1 [f662e1] = 你离开了。第二天上课时，他点你回答最难的问题——然后，非常小声地，给你那个马马虎虎的答案打了满分。\n# en: Offer to help with the sick bush\nopt.right.label [aa5c3c] = 主动帮忙救那株玫瑰\n# en: You know the blight; Maud taught you. Within a month the bush is covered in red roses. Thorne leaves one, without comment, on your desk.\nopt.right.say.1 [4837e1] = 你认得这种枯萎病，是莫德教你的。一个月内，那株玫瑰开满了红花。索恩在你桌上留下一朵，什么也没说。\n# en: Your remedy does nothing. But Thorne lets you kneel beside him for an hour, saying nothing, which you suspect is a great honour.\nopt.right.say.2 [629b27] = 你的药方毫无效果。但索恩让你在他身边跪了一个钟头，一言不发——你猜这是莫大的荣幸。\n\n@event thorne_shaking_hands\n# en: Thorne's Hands\ntitle [aad4f1] = 索恩的手\n# en: After class you see Thorne's wand slip from fingers that won't stop trembling. He picks it up, slowly. \"Age,\" he says, not looking at you. \"It comes for every storm eventually. You'll say nothing.\"\ntext [b146e2] = 下课后，你看见索恩的魔杖从一双抖个不停的手指间滑落。他慢慢地把它捡起来。“年纪，”他说，没有看你，“每一场风暴终有平息之时。你什么也不会说。”\n# en: An old professor gripping the edge of a lectern with both hands, a dropped wand on the floor, a single student in the doorway.\nscene [d913fc] = 一位老教授双手紧抓讲台边缘，一根魔杖掉在地上，门口站着一名学生。\n# en: Say nothing, as he asks\nopt.left.label [08876a] = 照他说的，什么也不说\n# en: You never mention it. But you start arriving early to set up his demonstrations. He notices. He lets you.\nopt.left.say.1 [5d89d2] = 你从没提起这件事。但你开始提早到教室帮他布置演示。他注意到了。他默许了。\n# en: Tell Sister Amaranth\nopt.right.label [3f4d80] = 告诉阿玛兰斯修女\n# en: Amaranth's tonic steadies his hands. Thorne is furious with you for a week. Then he stops being furious, and starts, grudgingly, teaching you his hardest spells — \"before I can't.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [f813ac] = 阿玛兰斯的药剂让他的手稳了下来。索恩对你大发雷霆了一个星期。然后他不再生气，开始勉为其难地教你他最难的咒语——“趁我还能教。”\n\n@event maud_goat\n# en: Bartholomew in the Library\ntitle [07ab64] = 图书馆里的巴塞洛缪\n# en: Maud's goat, Bartholomew, has got into the library and is eating the Restricted Section. The librarian is apoplectic. Maud, hurrying in, grabs your arm. \"He likes you. Get him out before he eats anything with teeth.\"\ntext [ff1025] = 莫德的山羊巴塞洛缪闯进了图书馆，正在吃禁书区。图书管理员气得快中风了。莫德匆匆赶来，一把抓住你的胳膊。“它喜欢你。在它吃下什么长牙的书之前，把它弄出去。”\n# en: A shaggy goat standing on a library table chewing a book, an apoplectic librarian below, an old woman in a straw hat hurrying through the stacks.\nscene [579e73] = 一只毛蓬蓬的山羊站在图书馆的桌子上嚼书，下面是气急败坏的图书管理员，一位戴草帽的老妇人匆匆穿过书架。\n# en: Lure him out with an apple\nopt.left.label [89957a] = 用苹果把它引出来\n# en: Bartholomew follows the apple, and you, out into the sunlight. He has eaten one-third of \"Curses of the Northern Wastes\". He seems fine. Mostly.\nopt.left.say.1 [2ec444] = 巴塞洛缪跟着苹果——也跟着你——走进了阳光里。它吃掉了三分之一本《北方荒原诅咒大全》。它看起来没事。大体上没事。\n# en: Bartholomew eats the apple, then your sleeve, then headbutts you into a shelf. Maud gets him out in the end, laughing.\nopt.left.say.2 [cef45a] = 巴塞洛缪吃了苹果，然后吃了你的袖子，然后一头把你顶进了书架。最后是莫德笑着把它弄出去的。\n# en: Pick him up and carry him out\nopt.right.label [5273c3] = 把它抱起来扛出去\n# en: He is heavier than he looks and struggles like a sack of angry cats. You carry him all the way to the paddock. The librarian applauds.\nopt.right.say.1 [d608cc] = 它比看上去重，挣扎起来像一麻袋愤怒的猫。你一路把它扛到了围场。图书管理员鼓起了掌。\n\n@event maud_recipe\n# en: Maud's Soup\ntitle [913763] = 莫德的汤\n# en: \"I'm going to teach you my soup,\" says Maud, rolling up her sleeves. \"Cures colds, broken hearts and most curses. Don't write it down. You'll remember it if you need it.\"\ntext [dcac02] = “我要教你我的汤，”莫德卷起袖子说，“治感冒、治心碎、治大多数诅咒。别写下来。需要的时候你自然会记得。”\n# en: A small cottage kitchen, a black iron pot over the fire, an old woman guiding a student's hand as they stir.\nscene [a5474e] = 一间小小的农舍厨房，炉火上架着一口黑铁锅，一位老妇人握着学生的手教他搅拌。\n# en: Learn it properly\nopt.left.label [9c42bb] = 认真学会它\n# en: It takes all afternoon. Some of the ingredients are herbs; some, as far as you can tell, are just stories. You remember every step.\nopt.left.say.1 [d6eaaf] = 花了一整个下午。有些材料是草药；有些，在你看来，只是故事。你记住了每一步。\n# en: Mostly eat it\nopt.right.label [390278] = 主要负责吃\n# en: You learn about half of it and eat three bowls. Maud declares that the most important part of the recipe anyway.\nopt.right.say.1 [51d561] = 你学会了大约一半，喝了三碗。莫德宣布，这反正是这道菜谱里最重要的部分。\n\n@event maud_old_wand\n# en: A Wand From Sixty Years Ago\ntitle [017dae] = 六十年前的魔杖\n# en: Maud unwraps something from a faded scarf: a slender silver wand. \"Edmund's,\" she says. \"He was the one who didn't come back. He'd have wanted it to go beneath the lake again, with someone who meant to close the door.\"\ntext [8bc08b] = 莫德从一条褪色的围巾里解开一样东西：一根纤细的银魔杖。“埃德蒙的，”她说，“他就是那个没回来的人。他会希望它再到湖底去一次，跟着一个真心想关上那扇门的人。”\n# en: An old woman unwrapping a slender silver wand from a faded silk scarf, a photograph of three young students on the table beside it.\nscene [b81801] = 一位老妇人从一条褪色的丝巾里解出一根纤细的银魔杖，旁边桌上放着一张三个年轻学生的照片。\n# en: Accept it\nopt.left.label [5003f6] = 接受它\n# en: It fits your hand like it was waiting. When you hold it near the lake, it hums the same note as the drowned bell.\nopt.left.say.1 [fe42ab] = 它在你手里服帖得好像一直在等你。你把它举到湖边时，它和那口沉没的钟发出同一个音。\n# en: Tell her to keep it\nopt.right.label [680674] = 请她留着\n# en: \"Keep it for him,\" you say. She holds it for a long time. \"Sixty years,\" she says, \"and nobody ever said that.\" She makes tea, and you sit with her until dark.\nopt.right.say.1 [da80fd] = “替他留着吧，”你说。她握着它很久很久。“六十年了，”她说，“从来没有人这么说过。”她泡了茶，你陪她坐到天黑。\n\n@event isolde_chess\n# en: A Game of Tower Chess\ntitle [b9eeea] = 一局高塔棋\n# en: The headmistress keeps a board of crystal towers by her window. \"Do you play?\" she asks. It is clearly not a question. The pieces are already moving into position by themselves.\ntext [c9c818] = 院长在窗边放着一副水晶塔棋盘。“你下棋吗？”她问。这显然不是一个问题。棋子已经自己走到了位置上。\n# en: A chessboard of carved crystal towers on a small table by a tower window, two players, one candle, the lake silver below.\nscene [8c661f] = 塔楼窗边的小桌上放着一副雕刻水晶塔的棋盘，两位棋手，一支蜡烛，窗下的湖泛着银光。\n# en: Play to win\nopt.left.label [6c2256] = 为赢而下\n# en: You win. She looks at the board for a long moment, then laughs — a short, delighted sound. \"Again,\" she says. \"Thursday.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [ad5261] = 你赢了。她盯着棋盘看了很久，然后笑了——短促而开心的一声。“再来，”她说，“星期四。”\n# en: She wins in nine moves and spends the next hour showing you exactly why. It is the most useful lesson you've had all year.\nopt.left.say.2 [a26db6] = 她九步就赢了，然后花了一个钟头告诉你究竟为什么。这是你一整年里最有用的一课。\n# en: Play to learn how she thinks\nopt.right.label [51a7ef] = 为了解她的想法而下\n# en: You lose, slowly, watching. She sacrifices pieces she seems to love. By the end, you understand a great deal about how the academy is run.\nopt.right.say.1 [21753e] = 你输了，输得很慢，一边输一边看。她会牺牲那些她似乎很珍爱的棋子。到最后，你对这所学院是怎么运转的明白了许多。\n\n@event isolde_portrait\n# en: The Young Headmistress\ntitle [9f225c] = 年轻的院长\n# en: In a forgotten corridor you find a portrait of a laughing girl with a wand stuck behind her ear and ink on her nose. The plaque has been scratched off. The eyes are unmistakably the headmistress's. The portrait winks.\ntext [2b1741] = 在一条被遗忘的走廊里，你发现了一幅画像：一个笑着的女孩，耳后别着魔杖，鼻尖沾着墨水。铭牌被刮掉了。那双眼睛无疑是院长的。画像眨了眨眼。\n# en: A portrait in a forgotten corridor of a laughing young woman in student robes with a wand behind her ear, frame dusty, eyes unmistakably the headmistress's.\nscene [b07fba] = 被遗忘的走廊里一幅画像：一位身穿学生长袍、耳后别着魔杖、笑着的年轻女子，画框积满灰尘，眼睛无疑是院长的。\n# en: Ask the portrait about her\nopt.left.label [38d936] = 向画像打听她\n# en: \"Detention record of the decade,\" whispers the portrait. \"Set the observatory on fire twice. Best student this castle ever had.\" It winks again.\nopt.left.say.1 [a6720c] = “十年来禁闭记录最多的人，”画像低声说，“把天文台烧了两次。这座城堡有过的最好的学生。”它又眨了眨眼。\n# en: The portrait pretends to be asleep, loudly. By the time you give up, the corridor has rearranged itself, and it takes you half the evening to find your way out.\nopt.left.say.2 [b05711] = 肖像大声地装睡。等你放弃时，走廊已经自己换了样子，你花了半个晚上才找到出去的路。\n# en: Tell the headmistress you found it\nopt.right.label [966699] = 告诉院长你发现了它\n# en: She is quiet for a moment. \"I thought they'd burned it.\" She almost smiles. \"Leave it where it is. Someone should remember her.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [1bb647] = 她沉默了片刻。“我还以为他们把它烧了。”她几乎笑了。“就让它留在那儿吧。总该有人记得她。”\n\n@event isolde_judgement\n# en: A Question of Mercy\ntitle [a10d01] = 关于仁慈的问题\n# en: The headmistress slides a file across her desk. A second-year stole from the apothecary to pay for a sick mother's medicine. The rules say expulsion. \"You're a student,\" she says. \"Tell me what a student thinks is just.\"\ntext [050856] = 院长把一份卷宗推过桌面。一个二年级生为了给生病的母亲买药，从药铺偷了东西。按校规应当开除。“你是学生，”她说，“告诉我，学生认为什么才算公正。”\n# en: The headmistress at her desk with a single file open, a student seated opposite, rain on the window behind them.\nscene [d36bff] = 院长坐在书桌后，面前摊开一份卷宗，一名学生坐在对面，身后的窗上落着雨。\n# en: Argue for mercy\nopt.left.label [a3a062] = 为仁慈辩护\n# en: You argue for an hour. She listens to all of it. The second-year is given a job in the infirmary and a stern lecture. The mother recovers.\nopt.left.say.1 [eb948e] = 你辩了一个钟头。她全都听完了。那个二年级生被安排到医务室打工，外加一顿严厉的训诫。那位母亲康复了。\n# en: The rules exist for a reason\nopt.right.label [194310] = 校规自有其道理\n# en: \"Yes,\" she says, \"they do.\" She closes the file. You never learn what she decided, but the second-year is still in class the next week, looking grateful at nobody in particular.\nopt.right.say.1 [2c1752] = “是的，”她说，“它有。”她合上卷宗。你始终不知道她最后怎么决定的，但下个星期那个二年级生还在上课，对着不特定的某个人一脸感激。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/expansion_life.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/expansion_life.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event life_timeloop_1\n# en: Haven't We Done This?\ntitle [f7db95] = 这事是不是发生过？\n# en: Breakfast. Eggs. Thorne trips on the third step. A first-year spills juice on a prefect. You know all of this is about to happen, because it already happened. Yesterday. Which was also Tuesday.\ntext [8f145a] = 早餐。鸡蛋。索恩在第三级台阶上绊了一下。一个一年级生把果汁洒在了级长身上。你知道这一切马上就要发生，因为它已经发生过了。昨天。昨天也是星期二。\n# en: A student at breakfast staring at a plate of eggs with intense déjà vu, a clock on the wall with its hands spinning backward.\nscene [f64ed4] = 一名学生在早餐桌前盯着一盘鸡蛋，强烈的既视感涌上心头，墙上的钟指针在倒转。\n# en: Ignore it — probably just tired\nopt.left.label [d0cdc9] = 不管了——大概只是累了\n# en: You eat the eggs. They taste exactly as they did yesterday. Thorne trips on the third step.\nopt.left.say.1 [e481ca] = 你吃了鸡蛋。味道和昨天一模一样。索恩在第三级台阶上绊了一下。\n# en: Warn Thorne about the step\nopt.right.label [eb6211] = 提醒索恩小心台阶\n# en: \"The third step, Professor.\" He looks at you strangely and steps over it. Somewhere, a clock skips. It's still Tuesday.\nopt.right.say.1 [8ba6ad] = “第三级台阶，教授。”他奇怪地看了你一眼，跨了过去。某处，一座钟跳了一格。还是星期二。\n\n@event life_timeloop_2\n# en: Tuesday, Again\ntitle [d04f00] = 又是星期二\n# en: It's Tuesday. Again. For the fourth time. Nobody else notices. On the bright side, you now know every answer in this afternoon's quiz. On the dark side, it's Tuesday. Forever.\ntext [b8ccb9] = 星期二。又是。第四次了。没有别人注意到。往好处想，你现在知道今天下午小测的每一道答案。往坏处想，今天是星期二。永远是。\n# en: The same breakfast scene repeated, but the student is now standing on the table, holding up a sign that reads \"IT'S STILL TUESDAY\".\nscene [443eec] = 同样的早餐场景再次上演，但这一次学生站在了桌子上，举着一块牌子：“现在还是星期二”。\n# en: Use it — ace the quiz, win every bet\nopt.left.label [c29420] = 利用它——考满分，赌必赢\n# en: You win every bet, ace the quiz, and catch the juice before it hits the prefect. On the sixth Tuesday it starts to feel hollow. Something has to give.\nopt.left.say.1 [fbd1e9] = 你赢了每一场赌局，小测拿了满分，还在果汁泼到级长之前接住了它。到第六个星期二，这一切开始变得空洞。总得有什么东西松动才行。\n# en: Find the cause\nopt.right.label [12f556] = 找出原因\n# en: You spend three Tuesdays in the library. On the fourth you find it: someone in the astronomy tower has wound an old chronometer backward. With a wand.\nopt.right.say.1 [8ee226] = 你在图书馆里度过了三个星期二。第四个星期二，你找到了：天文塔里有人用魔杖把一台古老的计时仪倒着上了发条。\n\n@event life_timeloop_3\n# en: The Broken Chronometer\ntitle [d82d49] = 坏掉的计时仪\n# en: In the astronomy tower, a first-year is asleep beside an enormous brass chronometer, wand still stuck in its winding key. \"I just wanted one more day before my exam,\" says the note on his chest.\ntext [94f829] = 天文塔里，一个一年级生睡在一台巨大的黄铜计时仪旁边，魔杖还卡在发条钥匙里。他胸前的字条上写着：“我只是想在考试前多要一天。”\n# en: An astronomy tower at night, a giant brass chronometer with its gears spinning in reverse, a sleeping first-year slumped beside it with a wand still in hand.\nscene [4ad62e] = 夜晚的天文塔，一台巨型黄铜计时仪的齿轮在倒转，一个睡着的一年级生瘫在旁边，手里还握着魔杖。\n# en: Fix the chronometer\nopt.left.label [bbdcd3] = 修好计时仪\n# en: You ease the wand out and the gears groan forward. Wednesday arrives like a sneeze. Nobody but you and one mortified first-year ever knows.\nopt.left.say.1 [a7de56] = 你轻轻抽出魔杖，齿轮吱嘎着向前转动。星期三像一个喷嚏一样到来了。除了你和一个羞愧万分的一年级生，没人知道这件事。\n# en: The chronometer bites. It takes you two more Tuesdays and a lot of swearing. But eventually, gloriously, it's Wednesday.\nopt.left.say.2 [276d7c] = 计时仪咬人。你又花了两个星期二、骂了不少脏话。但最终，光荣地，星期三来了。\n# en: Teach the first-year to fix it\nopt.right.label [eb58e0] = 教那个一年级生自己修\n# en: You show him how. He fixes his own mess, cries a little, and promises never to touch a clock again. Wednesday arrives. He passes his exam.\nopt.right.say.1 [c72a99] = 你教他怎么做。他亲手收拾了自己的烂摊子，哭了一小会儿，发誓再也不碰钟了。星期三来了。他考试及格了。\n\n@event life_portrait_escape\n# en: The Escaped Portrait\ntitle [2a676c] = 出逃的画像\n# en: An empty frame in the long gallery. The Duchess of Ambry has escaped her portrait and is hiding in a landscape three corridors away, refusing to come back. \"That frame,\" she declares, \"is hideous.\"\ntext [a18812] = 长廊里一个空画框。安布里公爵夫人逃出了她的画像，躲进了三条走廊外的一幅风景画里，拒绝回来。“那个画框，”她宣布，“丑死了。”\n# en: An empty gilded picture frame on a gallery wall, and further down the corridor a painted duchess hiding inside a landscape of a picnic.\nscene [805579] = 长廊墙上一个空空的镀金画框，走廊尽头，一位画中的公爵夫人正躲在一幅野餐风景画里。\n# en: Persuade her back\nopt.left.label [fd187a] = 劝她回来\n# en: You promise to have her frame regilded. She returns, mollified. The caretaker regilds it. You've acquired a duchess who now considers you a personal friend.\nopt.left.say.1 [af9e67] = 你答应给她的画框重新镀金。她回来了，气消了。管理员重新镀了金。你收获了一位公爵夫人，她现在把你当作私人朋友。\n# en: She calls you a peasant and moves to a painting of a shipwreck. She's still there. She says she likes the sea air.\nopt.left.say.2 [7c07eb] = 她骂你是个乡巴佬，搬进了一幅沉船图里。她至今还在那儿。她说她喜欢海风。\n# en: Leave her in the picnic\nopt.right.label [f13545] = 让她留在野餐里\n# en: She seems happier among the sandwiches. So does the picnic. Some arrangements simply work.\nopt.right.say.1 [09beab] = 她在三明治之间似乎更快乐。野餐也是。有些安排就是刚刚好。\n\n@event life_library_cat\n# en: The Library Cat\ntitle [d3cfff] = 图书馆的猫\n# en: The library cat — a vast, ancient beast called Footnote — is asleep on the only copy of the book you need. You have an essay due tomorrow. Footnote has no deadlines.\ntext [968aad] = 图书馆的猫——一只名叫“脚注”的巨大古老生物——正睡在你需要的那本书上，而那是唯一的一本。你的论文明天就要交。脚注没有截止日期。\n# en: An enormous fluffy cat asleep on the exact book a student needs, on a high library shelf, one eye half-open.\nscene [8ac48f] = 一只巨大的蓬松猫咪睡在图书馆高处书架上，身下正是学生需要的那本书，一只眼睛半睁着。\n# en: Move the cat\nopt.left.label [967e4f] = 把猫挪开\n# en: You lift Footnote like a sack of warm flour. He allows it, with contempt. You get the book. He gets your chair.\nopt.left.say.1 [52e940] = 你像抱一袋温热的面粉一样抱起脚注。它允许了，带着鄙夷。你拿到了书。它拿到了你的椅子。\n# en: Footnote does not consent to being moved. You now have three long scratches and still no book.\nopt.left.say.2 [6230a8] = 脚注不同意被挪动。你现在多了三道长长的抓痕，书还是没拿到。\n# en: Bribe the cat\nopt.right.label [011f22] = 贿赂这只猫\n# en: One sardine. Footnote rises like a sultan, stretches, and moves exactly one book to the left. It's enough.\nopt.right.say.1 [b290a1] = 一条沙丁鱼。脚注像苏丹一样起身，伸了个懒腰，正好往左挪了一本书的距离。够了。\n\n@event life_invent_spell\n# en: A Spell of Your Own\ntitle [9d35aa] = 属于你的咒语\n# en: Late at night you realise there's a gap — a spell nobody has written yet, just a small one, to make ink dry instantly. You could try to invent it. Inventing spells, Thorne says, is \"how most of my former students lost their eyebrows\".\ntext [40816d] = 深夜，你意识到有个空白——一个还没人写过的咒语，只是个小咒语，能让墨水瞬间变干。你可以试着发明它。索恩说，发明咒语是“我大多数前学生失去眉毛的方式”。\n# en: A student at a desk covered in crossed-out incantations, a tiny spiral of light hovering over the page, an expression of dawning triumph.\nscene [c075ca] = 一名学生坐在写满划掉的咒文的书桌前，一小团螺旋状的光悬浮在纸上，脸上渐渐露出胜利的神情。\n# en: Invent it carefully\nopt.left.label [6adb19] = 小心地发明它\n# en: Three weeks of theory and one perfect evening. The spell works. Thorne grunts and puts your name on the lecture slide for next year. Forever.\nopt.left.say.1 [5b2d62] = 三个星期的理论，加上一个完美的夜晚。咒语成功了。索恩哼了一声，把你的名字放上了明年的讲义。永远。\n# en: Your spell dries ink instantly. It also dries the paper, the desk and your eyes. You shelve it for now.\nopt.left.say.2 [20cce9] = 你的咒语能瞬间弄干墨水。也能弄干纸、书桌和你的眼睛。你暂时把它搁置了。\n# en: Just try things until something works\nopt.right.label [847e81] = 乱试一通，直到有效\n# en: Somehow — through chaos and luck — it works. You couldn't explain why under oath. It still counts.\nopt.right.say.1 [c2baba] = 不知怎么的——靠着混乱和运气——它成功了。就算发誓你也解释不了为什么。但它照样算数。\n# en: Your eyebrows survive. Your desk does not.\nopt.right.say.2 [182ef8] = 你的眉毛幸存了。你的书桌没有。\n\n@event life_cursed_umbrella\n# en: The Cursed Umbrella\ntitle [8c3b3e] = 被诅咒的伞\n# en: You borrowed an umbrella from lost property. It's black, elegant and cursed: underneath it, it is always raining. The rest of the world is sunny. You are very wet.\ntext [b42225] = 你从失物招领处借了一把伞。它是黑色的，很优雅，还被诅咒了：伞底下永远在下雨。外面整个世界阳光明媚。你浑身湿透。\n# en: A student holding a black umbrella under which a tiny private rainstorm is pouring, while the sky outside is perfectly clear.\nscene [3ed21b] = 一名学生撑着一把黑伞，伞下正下着一场私人小暴雨，而外面的天空晴朗无云。\n# en: Break the curse\nopt.left.label [bbf151] = 解除诅咒\n# en: The curse lifts with a soft hiss. The umbrella is now just a very nice umbrella. You sell it to Corvin, who wants it \"for drama\".\nopt.left.say.1 [037847] = 诅咒轻轻嘶的一声解除了。现在它只是一把非常好看的伞。你把它卖给了科尔文，他说要拿来“营造戏剧效果”。\n# en: Now it rains sideways.\nopt.left.say.2 [1a72a4] = 现在雨是横着下的。\n# en: Return it to lost property\nopt.right.label [5ea06a] = 还给失物招领处\n# en: The caretaker takes it back, sighs, and hangs it on a hook labelled \"DO NOT\". There are six other things on that hook.\nopt.right.say.1 [c90b55] = 管理员收了回去，叹了口气，把它挂在一个标着“别碰”的钩子上。那个钩子上已经挂了六样别的东西。\n\n@event life_exam_dream\n# en: The Exam Dream\ntitle [e7738c] = 考试噩梦\n# en: You dream you're in the exam hall in your pyjamas. The paper is in a language that keeps changing. The proctor is Bartholomew the goat. You wake at 4am, heart pounding.\ntext [4ad83e] = 你梦见自己穿着睡衣坐在考场里。试卷上的语言不停地变来变去。监考官是山羊巴塞洛缪。凌晨四点你惊醒了，心怦怦直跳。\n# en: A dream-image: a student in an exam hall in their pyjamas, the exam paper written in a language that keeps changing, a clock with no hands.\nscene [e45f4e] = 梦境画面：一名学生穿着睡衣坐在考场里，试卷上的文字不停变换，一座没有指针的钟。\n# en: Get up and revise\nopt.left.label [02956b] = 起床复习\n# en: You revise until breakfast. The real exam paper is written in a normal language. You're absurdly relieved.\nopt.left.say.1 [ba0aba] = 你一直复习到早餐。真正的试卷是用正常语言写的。你如释重负得有点荒唐。\n# en: Go back to sleep\nopt.right.label [097b30] = 接着睡\n# en: You dream again. This time the goat gives you full marks. You choose to take this as an omen.\nopt.right.say.1 [c9ff1e] = 你又做梦了。这次山羊给了你满分。你决定把这当作一个好兆头。\n\n@event life_garden_plot\n# en: A Patch of Earth\ntitle [675687] = 一方泥土\n# en: Old Maud is giving out the abandoned plots behind the greenhouses. There's one left: small, weedy and full of rocks. \"It's yours if you want it,\" she says. \"Gardens are a lot of work. Most of the work is noticing.\"\ntext [492379] = 老莫德正在分发温室后面那些废弃的小块地。还剩一块：小小的，杂草丛生，满是石头。“想要的话就归你，”她说，“种园子很费功夫。大部分功夫是用心去看。”\n# en: A small square of freshly turned earth behind the greenhouses, a wooden stake with a student's name painted on it, a watering can beside it.\nscene [ae502d] = 温室后面一小块刚翻过的泥土，插着一根写有学生名字的木桩，旁边放着一只水壶。\n# en: Take the plot\nopt.left.label [a249fc] = 接下这块地\n# en: You spend a whole weekend clearing rocks. By spring, you have lettuces, three violent mandrakes and a strange purple flower nobody can name.\nopt.left.say.1 [205e70] = 你花了整整一个周末清理石头。到了春天，你有了生菜、三棵暴躁的曼德拉草，和一朵谁也叫不出名字的奇怪紫花。\n# en: Leave it for someone else\nopt.right.label [43df3f] = 留给别人\n# en: A shy first-year takes it and grows giant pumpkins. You feel oddly proud of them.\nopt.right.say.1 [a9e348] = 一个害羞的一年级生接手了它，种出了巨大的南瓜。你莫名为它们感到骄傲。\n\n@event life_pen_pal\n# en: A Letter From Thornwick\ntitle [64e8f7] = 荆木来信\n# en: A letter arrives from a stranger at Thornwick Academy — the rival school. \"Dear Lumenhold student chosen at random,\" it begins. \"I'm told you're all insufferable. Prove me wrong?\"\ntext [a313a2] = 一封信来自对手学校荆木学院的一个陌生人。“亲爱的、随机抽中的流明堡学生，”信的开头写道，“听说你们全都让人受不了。能证明我错了吗？”\n# en: A letter with a green Thornwick seal open on a desk, beside a reply half-written in Lumenhold blue ink.\nscene [72beee] = 书桌上摊开一封盖着荆木绿色封印的信，旁边是一封用流明堡蓝墨水写了一半的回信。\n# en: Write back\nopt.left.label [ae4ebf] = 回信\n# en: Your reply is four pages long and only slightly insufferable. The answer arrives within a week. You've made a friend in enemy territory.\nopt.left.say.1 [3f96bc] = 你的回信写了四页，只是稍微有点让人受不了。不到一星期回信就到了。你在敌方领土交到了一个朋友。\n# en: Ignore it\nopt.right.label [e2ae0c] = 不理它\n# en: You ignore it. A second letter arrives: \"Insufferable AND rude. Noted.\" You almost reply. Almost.\nopt.right.say.1 [a47716] = 你没理它。第二封信来了：“让人受不了，而且没礼貌。记下了。”你差点就回信了。差一点。\n\n@event life_three_headed_puppy\n# en: A Very Lost Puppy\ntitle [b1e6d0] = 一只迷路的小狗\n# en: There is a puppy in the dormitory corridor. It has three heads. One is eating a shoe, one is licking the wall, one is howling. It is very lost and, all three heads agree, very hungry.\ntext [01f554] = 宿舍走廊里有一只小狗。它有三个头。一个在啃鞋，一个在舔墙，一个在嚎叫。它彻底迷路了，而且——三个头一致认为——非常饿。\n# en: A puppy with three heads sitting in a corridor, one head chewing a shoe, one licking a wall, one howling mournfully.\nscene [9614f5] = 一只三头小狗坐在走廊里，一个头在嚼鞋子，一个在舔墙，一个在悲伤地嚎叫。\n# en: Feed it and find its owner\nopt.left.label [f395df] = 喂它，然后找它的主人\n# en: You follow its owner's frantic shouting to Professor Brask's office. She weeps with relief. The puppy's three heads each lick you once, in strict order.\nopt.left.say.1 [29bcbc] = 你循着主人焦急的呼喊找到了布拉斯克教授的办公室。她松了一口气，哭了出来。小狗的三个头严格按顺序各舔了你一下。\n# en: Call Professor Brask\nopt.right.label [a54d9d] = 叫布拉斯克教授来\n# en: Brask arrives at a run with a lead and three biscuits. \"Bad boys,\" she says fondly. All three heads look deeply ashamed.\nopt.right.say.1 [b589e2] = 布拉斯克拿着牵绳和三块饼干一路跑来。“坏孩子们，”她宠溺地说。三个头看起来都羞愧极了。\n\n@event life_bell_tower\n# en: The Midnight Bell\ntitle [2cdb21] = 午夜钟声\n# en: Tradition: once in your time at Lumenhold, you must ring the old bell at midnight without getting caught. Your dorm has decided tonight is your night.\ntext [97f4cd] = 传统：在流明堡的日子里，你必须有一次在午夜敲响古钟而不被抓到。你的宿舍认定今晚就是你的夜晚。\n# en: A dark bell tower interior, ropes hanging down, a group of students at the bottom daring one of their number to climb.\nscene [33ec20] = 漆黑的钟楼内部，钟绳垂落，一群学生站在底下怂恿其中一人爬上去。\n# en: Climb and ring it\nopt.left.label [5bb0b4] = 爬上去敲钟\n# en: BONG. The whole castle wakes. By the time the prefects reach the tower you're back in bed, innocent and smug.\nopt.left.say.1 [37e43a] = 当——整座城堡都醒了。等级长们赶到钟楼时，你已经回到床上，一脸无辜又得意。\n# en: BONG — and Bram is at the bottom of the stairs when you come down. Detention. But you rang it, and everybody heard.\nopt.left.say.2 [0df3f9] = 当——而你下来时，布拉姆就站在楼梯底下。禁闭。但你敲响了它，所有人都听见了。\n# en: Decline the dare\nopt.right.label [78dc24] = 拒绝这个挑战\n# en: Someone else rings it instead and becomes a legend. You'll always wonder.\nopt.right.say.1 [a936f5] = 别人替你敲响了钟，成了传奇。你会一直好奇的。\n\n@event life_fog_maze\n# en: The Fog Maze\ntitle [a0d483] = 雾中迷宫\n# en: Fog has come up off the lake so thick you can't see your own feet. The path to the greenhouses has become a maze. Somewhere ahead, someone is calling for help, faintly.\ntext [ee15c0] = 湖上升起的雾浓得你连自己的脚都看不见。通往温室的小路变成了一座迷宫。前方某处，有人在微弱地呼救。\n# en: The academy grounds swallowed by thick white fog, hedges looming like walls, a lantern glowing faintly ahead.\nscene [666c24] = 学院的庭院被浓密的白雾吞没，树篱像墙一样隐约耸立，前方一盏灯笼发着微光。\n# en: Follow the voice\nopt.left.label [f27ba5] = 循着声音走\n# en: You find a first-year sitting on a bench, completely lost, sixty feet from the door. You walk them in. They tell everyone you saved their life.\nopt.left.say.1 [2c5755] = 你找到一个坐在长椅上、彻底迷路的一年级生，离门只有六十英尺。你领着对方进去。对方逢人便说你救了自己一命。\n# en: You walk in circles for an hour and end up exactly where you started. The voice was a very clever crow.\nopt.left.say.2 [fc0168] = 你绕了一个钟头的圈子，最后回到了起点。那声音是一只非常聪明的乌鸦。\n# en: Cast a light and wait\nopt.right.label [988921] = 点亮光芒，原地等待\n# en: You raise a floating light and wait. One by one, three lost students find their way to it. You feel like a lighthouse.\nopt.right.say.1 [380b7f] = 你升起一团漂浮的光，静静等待。三个迷路的学生一个接一个地找到了它。你觉得自己像一座灯塔。\n\n@event life_wishing_well\n# en: The Wishing Well\ntitle [8cab57] = 许愿井\n# en: In the old cloister there's a wishing well. The inscription says: \"ONE COIN, ONE WISH, ONE PRICE.\" The bottom glitters with three hundred years of coins.\ntext [0ae5ea] = 古老的回廊里有一口许愿井。铭文写着：“一枚硬币，一个愿望，一个代价。”井底闪烁着三百年来的硬币。\n# en: An old stone well in the corner of a cloister, coins glinting at the bottom, a carved inscription half-worn away.\nscene [9ea5b9] = 回廊角落里一口古老的石井，井底硬币闪闪发光，一段刻字已经磨损了一半。\n# en: Throw in a coin and wish\nopt.left.label [ce57aa] = 投一枚硬币许愿\n# en: You wish for a good month. It is, mostly. Coincidence, probably.\nopt.left.say.1 [4c845e] = 你许愿这个月过得顺利。大体上确实如此。大概是巧合。\n# en: You wish. The well hiccups and spits your coin back out. Apparently, you can't afford that wish.\nopt.left.say.2 [3fc324] = 你许了愿。井打了个嗝，把你的硬币吐了出来。看来这个愿望你付不起。\n# en: Fish out a few old coins\nopt.right.label [dbef4d] = 捞几枚古币出来\n# en: Fifteen crowns' worth of antique coins. The well is silent. Disapprovingly silent.\nopt.right.say.1 [f67aeb] = 价值十五克朗的古董硬币。井沉默着。不以为然地沉默着。\n# en: The well bites. You lose your own purse in it. ONE PRICE indeed.\nopt.right.say.2 [708beb] = 井咬人。你把自己的钱袋掉了进去。果然是“一个代价”。\n\n@event life_borrowed_broom\n# en: The Borrowed Broom\ntitle [c73e99] = 借来的扫帚\n# en: A second-year lends you their broom for an errand. Midway across the lake, you discover why they were so keen to lend it: it is extremely ticklish, and it hates you.\ntext [ca31e2] = 一个二年级生把扫帚借给你去跑腿。飞到湖中央时，你才发现对方为什么那么乐意借给你：它极其怕痒，而且讨厌你。\n# en: A student clinging to a bucking broom high above the castle, its bristles on fire, a smaller student on the ground waving frantically.\nscene [c7a1ed] = 一名学生紧紧抓着一把在城堡上空乱蹦的扫帚，帚尾着了火，地上一个小个子学生在拼命挥手。\n# en: Wrestle it home\nopt.left.label [745cb7] = 硬把它拽回去\n# en: You land in a heap on the lawn, triumphant and windswept. The broom sulks. The second-year owes you a favour.\nopt.left.say.1 [90315b] = 你一头栽在草坪上，得意洋洋、头发凌乱。扫帚生着闷气。那个二年级生欠你一个人情。\n# en: You land in the lake. The broom flies home without you, very pleased with itself.\nopt.left.say.2 [a5f5be] = 你掉进了湖里。扫帚自己飞回了家，非常得意。\n# en: Soothe it\nopt.right.label [52ec4c] = 安抚它\n# en: You talk to it gently all the way across. It purrs. Brooms shouldn't purr. Now it follows you around.\nopt.right.say.1 [542704] = 你一路温柔地跟它说话。它发出了呼噜声。扫帚不该会呼噜的。现在它到哪儿都跟着你。\n# en: It is not soothed. It is, if anything, more ticklish.\nopt.right.say.2 [1a061b] = 它没有被安抚。要说有什么变化，那就是更怕痒了。\n\n@event life_double_trouble\n# en: Your Double Is Loose\ntitle [0b749f] = 你的分身跑了\n# en: An illusion of you — one you thought you'd dismissed — is still wandering the castle. It has been to three of your classes, flirted with a prefect, and volunteered you for the choir.\ntext [e2deca] = 一个你以为早就解除了的分身幻象，还在城堡里到处游荡。它去上了你的三节课，和一位级长调情，还替你报名了合唱团。\n# en: A student staring in horror across a crowded corridor at a perfect copy of themselves, cheerfully chatting up a professor.\nscene [f7b09f] = 一名学生惊恐地隔着拥挤的走廊望着一个和自己一模一样的复制品，那复制品正兴高采烈地和一位教授攀谈。\n# en: Catch it\nopt.left.label [6ffc79] = 抓住它\n# en: You corner it in a broom cupboard. It looks at you, shrugs and pops like a bubble. The prefect, however, still expects a date.\nopt.left.say.1 [75bb7a] = 你把它堵在扫帚间里。它看着你，耸了耸肩，像泡泡一样“啪”地消失了。不过，那位级长仍然在等着约会。\n# en: It escapes, and spends a week as the most charming version of you. People are disappointed when you come back.\nopt.left.say.2 [4bfacb] = 它溜走了，当了一个星期最有魅力的你。你回来的时候，大家都有点失望。\n# en: Let it run its course\nopt.right.label [0407c9] = 随它去吧\n# en: It attends your lectures and takes better notes than you do. When it fades after a week, you're almost sorry.\nopt.right.say.1 [c344b5] = 它替你上课，笔记记得比你还好。一星期后它消散时，你几乎有点舍不得。\n\n@event life_auction\n# en: The Lost-Property Auction\ntitle [a95e5d] = 失物拍卖会\n# en: Once a year the caretaker auctions off unclaimed lost property. There's a box nobody wants that hums faintly. The bidding starts at three crowns. Nobody's bidding.\ntext [78d822] = 每年一次，管理员会拍卖无人认领的失物。有一个没人要的盒子在微微嗡鸣。起拍价三克朗。没人出价。\n# en: A crowded hall, a caretaker with a gavel at a lectern, tables piled with lost scarves, wands, boots and one suspiciously humming box.\nscene [c3957c] = 拥挤的大厅，管理员手持木槌站在讲台后，桌上堆满了丢失的围巾、魔杖、靴子，还有一个可疑地嗡嗡作响的盒子。\n# en: Bid on the humming box\nopt.left.label [fb0360] = 竞拍那个嗡鸣的盒子\n# en: Inside: a music box that plays a different song every time you open it. Each one makes you think of something new.\nopt.left.say.1 [2acaf5] = 里面是一个音乐盒，每次打开都会放一首不同的曲子。每一首都让你想到新的东西。\n# en: Inside: a very old, very annoyed hiccup curse, which immediately transfers itself to you.\nopt.left.say.2 [04b453] = 里面是一个非常古老、非常恼火的打嗝诅咒，它立刻转移到了你身上。\n# en: Buy a good warm scarf instead\nopt.right.label [98578d] = 改买一条暖和的好围巾\n# en: A scarf of real Northern wool, slightly singed. It's the warmest thing you've ever owned.\nopt.right.say.1 [e163d0] = 一条真正的北境羊毛围巾，有点烧焦。这是你拥有过的最暖和的东西。\n\n@event life_village_busking\n# en: Busking in the Village\ntitle [2797e6] = 小镇卖艺\n# en: You're short on crowns, and the village square has a fountain, an empty hat and a crowd of shoppers. You do know a few songs.\ntext [f6178e] = 你手头缺克朗，而小镇广场上有一座喷泉、一顶空帽子和一群购物的人。你确实会几首歌。\n# en: A cobbled village square, a student singing beside a fountain with an upturned hat at their feet, villagers pausing to listen.\nscene [96e354] = 铺着鹅卵石的小镇广场，一名学生在喷泉边唱歌，脚边放着一顶翻过来的帽子，镇民们驻足聆听。\n# en: Sing your heart out\nopt.left.label [ebf8e1] = 尽情放声歌唱\n# en: A small crowd gathers. Someone requests an encore. The hat fills with copper and one silver coin from a weeping old man.\nopt.left.say.1 [f60a68] = 一小群人围了过来。有人要求返场。帽子里装满了铜币，还有一位泪流满面的老人投下的一枚银币。\n# en: Three crowns and a sympathetic look from a dog. Busking is harder than it looks.\nopt.left.say.2 [06183f] = 三克朗，外加一条狗同情的眼神。卖艺比看起来难多了。\n# en: Play the street-magic act instead\nopt.right.label [1f6f47] = 改演街头魔术\n# en: You make doves out of the fountain spray. Children scream with joy. The hat overflows.\nopt.right.say.1 [747727] = 你用喷泉的水雾变出鸽子。孩子们开心得尖叫。帽子都装不下了。\n# en: Your doves come out as damp sparrows. People are kind about it.\nopt.right.say.2 [def817] = 你变出来的鸽子成了湿漉漉的麻雀。大家都很包容。\n\n@event life_moths\n# en: A Storm of Moths\ntitle [34c527] = 飞蛾风暴\n# en: You open your door to find your room full of moths — thousands of them, pale as moonlight, circling your candle in silent spirals. It's eerie. It's also, you can't help noticing, beautiful.\ntext [b3079c] = 你推开门，发现房间里满是飞蛾——成千上万只，白得像月光，绕着你的蜡烛无声地盘旋。很诡异。你也不得不承认，很美。\n# en: A dormitory room filled with thousands of pale moths circling a single candle, a student standing in the doorway, astonished.\nscene [ba25b5] = 宿舍房间里成千上万只苍白的飞蛾绕着一支蜡烛盘旋，一名学生站在门口，惊呆了。\n# en: Open the window and let them go\nopt.left.label [09f522] = 打开窗放它们走\n# en: They pour out into the night in a silver river. One stays behind, on your pillow, for a week. You name it Gregory.\nopt.left.say.1 [582ae8] = 它们像一条银色的河流涌入夜色。有一只留了下来，在你的枕头上待了一个星期。你给它起名叫格雷戈里。\n# en: Study them\nopt.right.label [3a81ec] = 研究它们\n# en: They're luna moths, migrating — and drawn to something in your room. You find it: a crumb of moonpetal on your sleeve. Mystery solved. Mostly.\nopt.right.say.1 [eb5366] = 它们是迁徙中的月形天蚕蛾——被你房间里的什么东西吸引了过来。你找到了：袖子上一点月瓣花的碎屑。谜团解开了。大体上。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/expansion_people.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/expansion_people.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event lyra_toast\n# en: Lyra Cannot Cook\ntitle [96d00a] = 莱拉不会做饭\n# en: Smoke pours from the kitchenette. Lyra Vaneshade, pyromancy prodigy, is holding a piece of bread that is now mostly charcoal. \"I can incinerate a target at forty paces,\" she says. \"I cannot make toast. Don't. Say. Anything.\"\ntext [0b5742] = 浓烟从小厨房里涌出来。莱拉·凡妮希德，火焰术天才，手里拿着一片如今基本是木炭的面包。“我能在四十步外把目标烧成灰，”她说，“我不会烤面包。别。说。话。”\n# en: A dormitory kitchenette full of smoke, a crimson-haired girl holding a blackened slice of toast at arm's length, a fire-charm still sparking in her other hand.\nscene [60f547] = 宿舍小厨房里满是浓烟，一个红发女孩把一片烤焦的面包举得远远的，另一只手上的火焰咒还在噼啪作响。\n# en: Say absolutely nothing\nopt.left.label [274efb] = 一个字也不说\n# en: You open a window, take the bread, and make two perfect slices with a toasting fork. She eats hers in dignified silence. Then she asks for another.\nopt.left.say.1 [daffed] = 你打开窗，接过面包，用烤叉烤出两片完美的吐司。她庄严地默默吃完了自己那片。然后又要了一片。\n# en: Teach her — gently\nopt.right.label [13a212] = 教她——温柔地\n# en: \"Less fire,\" you say. \"No, less. Less than that.\" It takes eleven slices. On the twelfth, she makes perfect golden toast and looks prouder than after any duel.\nopt.right.say.1 [84dd3f] = “火小一点，”你说，“不，再小。比那还小。”烤了十一片。到第十二片，她烤出了完美的金黄吐司，看起来比赢下任何一场决斗都骄傲。\n\n@event lyra_mother_visit\n# en: Lady Vaneshade\ntitle [6393ec] = 凡妮希德夫人\n# en: Lady Vaneshade has come to inspect the academy, and her daughter. She stops in front of you. \"You must be the one my daughter mentions,\" she says. \"Constantly. I'd like to know why.\"\ntext [b4cf90] = 凡妮希德夫人来视察学院，也来视察她的女儿。她在你面前停下。“你一定就是我女儿提起的那个人，”她说，“一直提。我想知道为什么。”\n# en: A tall woman in crimson and black walking through the courtyard like a queen inspecting troops, Lyra two steps behind her, very straight.\nscene [cce72a] = 一位身着红黑长裙的高个女人像女王检阅军队一样走过庭院，莱拉跟在她身后两步远，站得笔直。\n# en: Answer with perfect manners\nopt.left.label [d8a5a2] = 礼数周全地回答\n# en: You are polite, precise and unimpressed. Lady Vaneshade studies you, then says, \"Hm. Adequate.\" Lyra looks at you like you've slain a dragon.\nopt.left.say.1 [304a6e] = 你礼貌、精准，而且不卑不亢。凡妮希德夫人打量了你一番，说：“嗯。还行。”莱拉看你的眼神就像你屠了一条龙。\n# en: You stumble over a title. Lady Vaneshade's eyebrow says everything. Later, Lyra finds you. \"She does that to everyone,\" she says. \"You lasted longer than most.\"\nopt.left.say.2 [04ac64] = 你把一个头衔说结巴了。凡妮希德夫人的眉毛说明了一切。后来莱拉找到你。“她对谁都这样，”她说，“你撑得比大多数人久。”\n# en: Answer honestly\nopt.right.label [d41011] = 老实回答\n# en: \"Because she's brilliant,\" you say, \"and nobody tells her.\" Lady Vaneshade is silent. Lyra is redder than her hair. The visit ends early.\nopt.right.say.1 [756ede] = “因为她很出色，”你说，“而从没有人告诉她。”凡妮希德夫人沉默了。莱拉的脸比她的头发还红。视察提前结束了。\n\n@event lyra_rival_respect\n# en: Respect, Grudgingly\ntitle [7c61e5] = 不情愿的敬意\n# en: After another draw, Lyra drops onto the edge of the ring beside you. \"You're the only person here who makes me better,\" she says, as if confessing a crime. \"I hate it. I don't want it to stop.\"\ntext [f4816d] = 又一次平局之后，莱拉在你身边的场边坐下。“你是这里唯一能让我变得更强的人，”她说，像在坦白罪行，“我讨厌这样。我又不想它停下。”\n# en: Two rivals sitting on the edge of a scorched practice ring, wands laid down between them, sharing a flask of water.\nscene [da0f66] = 两个对手坐在一片焦黑的练习场边上，魔杖放在两人之间，分享着一壶水。\n# en: \"Then let's keep it a rivalry.\"\nopt.left.label [d4b161] = “那就继续当对手。”\n# en: \"Good,\" she says, relieved. \"Rivalry I understand.\" She shoves you, lightly. You shove her back. The rivalry continues, warmer.\nopt.left.say.1 [ceccda] = “好，”她松了一口气，“对手我懂。”她轻轻推了你一把。你推了回去。竞争还在继续，只是更暖了。\n# en: \"Or we could just be friends.\"\nopt.right.label [f2a2ab] = “或者我们可以只是朋友。”\n# en: She considers the word like an unfamiliar spell. \"Friends,\" she says. \"Fine. But I'm still going to beat you.\" You'd be disappointed if she didn't.\nopt.right.say.1 [988725] = 她像琢磨一个陌生咒语一样琢磨着这个词。“朋友，”她说，“行。但我还是要打败你。”她要是不这么说，你反而会失望。\n\n@event tobias_scarecrow\n# en: The Scarecrow Project\ntitle [2e1da2] = 稻草人计划\n# en: Tobias has built a scarecrow for the greenhouse plots and wants to enchant it to scare crows. \"Just a little life,\" he says. \"Just enough to wave. Nothing creepy.\"\ntext [3fc6d7] = 托比亚斯给温室的菜地做了一个稻草人，想给它施魔法来吓乌鸦。“就一点点生命，”他说，“刚好够它挥手。一点都不吓人。”\n# en: A scarecrow in a straw hat waving its arms at crows in a vegetable patch, a big farm boy and a student watching it proudly.\nscene [9ba2d5] = 菜地里一个戴草帽的稻草人朝乌鸦挥舞手臂，一个高大的农家男孩和一名学生自豪地看着它。\n# en: Help enchant it\nopt.left.label [d67ea8] = 帮忙施法\n# en: The scarecrow waves. It also learns to wave hello, and to tip its hat to the headmistress. The crows are terrified. The first-years adore it.\nopt.left.say.1 [0e53a1] = 稻草人挥手了。它还学会了挥手打招呼，以及向院长脱帽致意。乌鸦们吓坏了。一年级生们爱死它了。\n# en: The scarecrow comes to life, looks at you both, and walks off into the forest. You see it sometimes, at dusk, waving.\nopt.left.say.2 [96ecab] = 稻草人活了过来，看了看你们俩，然后走进了森林。你有时会在黄昏看见它，在挥手。\n# en: Suggest a cat instead\nopt.right.label [0efd5c] = 建议改用一只猫\n# en: You borrow the greenhouse cat. The crows leave immediately. Tobias is a little sad about the scarecrow, so you help him make it a hat.\nopt.right.say.1 [481aac] = 你借来了温室的猫。乌鸦立刻飞走了。托比亚斯为稻草人有点难过，于是你帮他给它做了一顶帽子。\n\n@event tobias_dance_lessons\n# en: Two Left Feet\ntitle [ed37de] = 两只左脚\n# en: Tobias has pushed the desks against the walls of an empty classroom. \"The Ball's next month,\" he says, mortified. \"I've been practising with a broom. The broom has complained.\"\ntext [ec1af4] = 托比亚斯把一间空教室的桌子都推到了墙边。“舞会就在下个月，”他窘得要命，“我一直在和一把扫帚练习。扫帚投诉了。”\n# en: An empty classroom with desks pushed to the walls, a big boy counting steps aloud, a broom propped against the wall as his former partner.\nscene [592891] = 一间空教室，桌子推到墙边，一个高大的男孩大声数着舞步，一把扫帚靠在墙边——他的前舞伴。\n# en: Teach him to waltz\nopt.left.label [97e7a3] = 教他跳华尔兹\n# en: One-two-three, one-two-three. He steps on you exactly fifty times. On the fifty-first bar, something clicks, and for a moment he's graceful. He looks astonished.\nopt.left.say.1 [4f58a0] = 一二三，一二三。他不多不少踩了你五十脚。到第五十一个小节，突然开了窍，有那么一会儿，他跳得很优雅。他自己都惊呆了。\n# en: Tell him nobody cares about footwork\nopt.right.label [22afe9] = 告诉他没人在乎舞步\n# en: \"Just enjoy it,\" you say. He thinks about this, nods, and dances like a delighted bear. It's terrible. It's wonderful.\nopt.right.say.1 [4eca0a] = “开心就好，”你说。他想了想，点点头，然后像一头快乐的熊一样跳了起来。跳得糟透了。也美妙极了。\n\n@event tobias_storm_greenhouse\n# en: Storm Over the Greenhouses\ntitle [3fabc5] = 温室上空的风暴\n# en: A storm hits at midnight. Glass is cracking in the greenhouses. Tobias is out there in his nightshirt, trying to hold a door shut against the wind with his whole body. \"The moonflowers!\" he shouts. \"Eleven years!\"\ntext [fb15f6] = 午夜，暴风雨来袭。温室的玻璃在开裂。托比亚斯穿着睡衣站在外面，用整个身体顶着门不让风吹开。“月光花！”他大喊，“十一年了！”\n# en: Rain lashing a glass greenhouse at night, panes cracking, a big boy holding a door shut against the wind while plants rattle inside.\nscene [f9b9b9] = 夜里，暴雨抽打着玻璃温室，窗格开裂，一个高大的男孩顶着狂风抵住门，里面的植物哗哗作响。\n# en: Run out and help\nopt.left.label [007212] = 冲出去帮忙\n# en: You hold the panes together with every shielding charm you know until dawn. Every moonflower survives. Tobias hugs you, soaked, laughing, half crying.\nopt.left.say.1 [d599ab] = 你用你会的每一个护盾咒把玻璃撑住，一直撑到黎明。每一朵月光花都活了下来。托比亚斯浑身湿透地抱住你，又笑又像要哭。\n# en: Fetch Old Maud\nopt.right.label [263929] = 去叫老莫德\n# en: Maud arrives in her dressing gown, mutters three words at the sky, and the wind politely goes elsewhere. Tobias stares at her. \"Teach me that,\" he says.\nopt.right.say.1 [efe395] = 莫德穿着睡袍赶来，对着天空嘟囔了三个词，风就礼貌地去了别处。托比亚斯盯着她。“教我这个，”他说。\n\n@event seraphine_lost_day\n# en: Seraphine Is Tomorrow\ntitle [d55aad] = 瑟拉芬活在明天\n# en: Seraphine is sitting in an empty classroom, waiting for a lesson that's happening tomorrow. \"I've slipped,\" she says calmly. \"I'm a day ahead. It happens. I need someone to remind me what today is.\"\ntext [2839c8] = 瑟拉芬坐在一间空教室里，等一堂明天才上的课。“我滑过头了，”她平静地说，“我提前了一天。这种事会发生。我需要有人提醒我今天是哪天。”\n# en: A girl sitting at the wrong desk in an empty classroom, a teacup in her hands, the calendar on the wall showing the next day's date.\nscene [5de877] = 一个女孩坐在空教室里不该坐的座位上，手捧茶杯，墙上的日历显示着第二天的日期。\n# en: Talk her back into today\nopt.left.label [cd1172] = 把她拉回今天\n# en: You tell her what you had for breakfast, what the weather's like, what Thorne said this morning. Slowly her eyes stop looking through you. \"Oh,\" she says. \"Hello. Today.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [de7259] = 你告诉她你早饭吃了什么、天气怎么样、索恩今早说了什么。她的目光慢慢不再穿过你。“哦，”她说，“你好。今天。”\n# en: Ask her what tomorrow holds\nopt.right.label [b4a8a2] = 问她明天会发生什么\n# en: \"There's a surprise quiz in Lore,\" she says. \"Question three is a trick.\" It is. You get it right.\nopt.right.say.1 [9b632e] = “学识课会有突击小测，”她说，“第三题是陷阱。”确实是。你答对了。\n# en: \"It rains,\" she says. \"You get wet. You'll be fine.\" You are not fine. You get very wet.\nopt.right.say.2 [111bc0] = “会下雨，”她说，“你会淋湿。你会没事的。”你并没事。你淋得透湿。\n\n@event seraphine_painting\n# en: A Painting of Somewhere Else\ntitle [e371b7] = 别处的风景\n# en: Seraphine has painted a small cottage by the sea, with a crooked chimney and a blue door. \"It's yours,\" she says. \"In twelve years. I thought you should see it now. In case you want a different door.\"\ntext [63e289] = 瑟拉芬画了一座海边的小屋，烟囱歪歪的，门是蓝色的。“这是你的，”她说，“十二年后。我觉得你应该现在就看看。万一你想要一扇不一样的门。”\n# en: A small canvas on an easel showing a stranger's cottage by the sea, a girl with paint on her fingers watching someone look at it.\nscene [88e8e6] = 画架上一幅小画布，画着一座陌生的海边小屋，一个手指沾着颜料的女孩看着别人端详它。\n# en: Keep the painting\nopt.left.label [93b1f9] = 收下这幅画\n# en: You hang it over your bed. Some nights you swear the smoke from the chimney moves. You decide, privately, that you like the blue door.\nopt.left.say.1 [c384bc] = 你把它挂在床头。有些夜晚，你发誓烟囱里的烟在动。你暗自决定，你喜欢那扇蓝色的门。\n# en: You hang it over your bed. Some mornings the door is a different colour, and you lie there wondering which one you chose.\nopt.left.say.2 [7a0c78] = 你把它挂在床头。有些早晨，那扇门换了颜色，你躺在床上琢磨自己选的是哪一扇。\n# en: Ask her to paint her own future\nopt.right.label [ab2144] = 请她画画自己的未来\n# en: She looks at the blank canvas for a long time. \"I never can,\" she says. \"Seers can't see themselves.\" Then, quietly: \"Unless someone's standing next to us.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [685acd] = 她对着空白的画布看了很久。“我画不出来，”她说，“先知看不见自己。”然后，轻声地：“除非有人站在我们身边。”\n\n@event seraphine_mother\n# en: The Oracle's Summons\ntitle [ef26c8] = 神谕的召唤\n# en: Seraphine's mother, the Oracle of Vell, wants her home to take up the family seat. \"I'd spend my life telling kings what they want to hear,\" Seraphine says. \"But she's already seen me say yes. She always sees me say yes.\"\ntext [bdd88c] = 瑟拉芬的母亲——维尔神谕——要她回家继承家族的席位。“我这辈子都得对国王们说他们想听的话，”瑟拉芬说，“可她已经看见我答应了。她总是看见我答应。”\n# en: A girl holding a letter that glows faintly silver, standing at the top of a tower stair, looking down as if about to fall or fly.\nscene [02abdd] = 一个女孩站在塔楼楼梯顶端，手握一封泛着微弱银光的信，俯视下方，仿佛就要坠落或飞起。\n# en: \"Then surprise her.\"\nopt.left.label [7766b6] = “那就给她个惊喜。”\n# en: Seraphine writes back: no. For a whole day she's terrified, and so are you. Then she's giddy. \"I didn't see that coming,\" she keeps saying. \"I didn't see it coming!\"\nopt.left.say.1 [913a47] = 瑟拉芬回信：不。整整一天她都吓坏了，你也一样。然后她兴奋得晕乎乎的。“我没预见到，”她一遍遍地说，“我没预见到！”\n# en: \"Maybe she's right.\"\nopt.right.label [0cb3e9] = “也许她是对的。”\n# en: She nods slowly. She doesn't go — not yet. But she stops telling you her visions, and you miss them more than you expected.\nopt.right.say.1 [382f15] = 她慢慢点了点头。她没有回去——暂时还没有。但她不再告诉你她的预见了，而你比自己想象的更想念那些预见。\n\n@event corvin_confiscation_vault\n# en: The Confiscation Vault\ntitle [404008] = 没收品库\n# en: \"The confiscation vault,\" Corvin breathes. \"Three hundred years of everything the faculty ever took off a student. My grandfather's lucky deck is in there. I just want it back. Lookout?\"\ntext [724ca5] = “没收品库，”科尔文屏息道，“三百年来教职员从学生手里没收的所有东西。我祖父的幸运纸牌就在里面。我只想把它拿回来。帮我望风？”\n# en: A dark vault crammed with confiscated student contraband — fanged frisbees, cursed dice, a goblet of endless soup — two figures with a lantern at the door.\nscene [efcf9c] = 一间塞满学生违禁品的黑暗库房——长着獠牙的飞盘、被诅咒的骰子、一只永远盛满汤的高脚杯——门口两个提着灯笼的身影。\n# en: Keep lookout\nopt.left.label [89becd] = 帮他望风\n# en: He comes out with the deck and a handful of confiscated coins \"for your trouble\". The deck is, it turns out, genuinely lucky. He wins at cards for a month.\nopt.left.say.1 [2b5759] = 他拿着纸牌出来了，还有一把没收来的硬币，“算你的辛苦费”。结果那副牌真的很幸运。他打牌赢了一个月。\n# en: The vault alarm is a very loud goblin. You both spend a week cataloguing contraband — which Corvin considers a victory.\nopt.left.say.2 [4c5953] = 库房的警报是一只嗓门非常大的地精。你们俩花了一星期给违禁品编目——科尔文认为这算胜利。\n# en: Talk him out of it\nopt.right.label [3af407] = 劝他打消念头\n# en: You convince him to just ask Thorne. Thorne, astonishingly, gives it back. \"Your grandfather cheated me with it in '41,\" he says. \"Tell him I said hello.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [df43d8] = 你说服他直接去问索恩。令人震惊的是，索恩把牌还给了他。“你祖父四一年用它出老千坑过我，”他说，“替我向他问好。”\n# en: He goes anyway, alone. He comes back with the deck and a black eye from the goblin. Worth it, he says.\nopt.right.say.2 [8846c7] = 他还是去了，一个人。他带着纸牌和被地精打出的一只黑眼圈回来了。他说值了。\n\n@event corvin_brother\n# en: Corvin's Brother\ntitle [b626d0] = 科尔文的哥哥\n# en: There's a rough-looking man at the gates, and Corvin has gone very still. \"My brother,\" he says. \"He wants money. He always wants money. If I don't pay, he tells the headmistress where I learned to pick locks.\"\ntext [45c115] = 校门口站着一个看起来很粗野的男人，科尔文整个人僵住了。“我哥哥，”他说，“他要钱。他永远都要钱。我要是不给，他就去告诉院长我是在哪儿学会撬锁的。”\n# en: A rough-looking young man leaning on the academy gate, cap pulled low, Corvin facing him with his hands in his pockets.\nscene [a1863b] = 一个看起来粗野的年轻人倚在学院大门上，帽子压得很低，科尔文双手插兜面对着他。\n# en: Help Corvin face him down\nopt.left.label [cb6fb2] = 帮科尔文硬顶回去\n# en: You stand beside Corvin while he says no — the first time, he tells you later, that he's ever said it. His brother leaves. Corvin sits down on the steps and laughs and shakes.\nopt.left.say.1 [8f14e6] = 你站在科尔文身边，听他说“不”——他后来告诉你，这是他第一次说出这个字。他哥哥走了。科尔文在台阶上坐下，笑着，浑身发抖。\n# en: It turns ugly. In the end you pay half, just to make him leave. Corvin pays you back within a week, with a strange new seriousness.\nopt.left.say.2 [22e67e] = 场面变得很难看。最后你付了一半，只为让他走人。科尔文一个星期内就还了你钱，带着一种陌生的郑重。\n# en: Stay out of family business\nopt.right.label [fef823] = 不掺和别人的家事\n# en: Corvin pays. You see him counting out every coin he has. He's quiet for a long while after.\nopt.right.say.1 [9f72cb] = 科尔文付了钱。你看见他把身上每一枚硬币都数了出来。之后他沉默了很久。\n\n@event corvin_cards\n# en: How to Cheat at Cards\ntitle [1de29d] = 怎样打牌出老千\n# en: \"Rule one: never cheat,\" says Corvin, shuffling so fast the cards blur. \"Rule two: if you're going to cheat, never get caught. Rule three: I'm about to teach you how they cheat, so you never lose to them.\"\ntext [e191e5] = “规则一：永远不要出千，”科尔文说着洗牌，快得纸牌都成了虚影，“规则二：如果要出千，永远别被抓到。规则三：我现在要教你他们是怎么出千的，这样你就永远不会输给他们。”\n# en: A candlelit table in a corner of the common room, a boy fanning a deck of cards with a flourish, a student watching his hands very closely.\nscene [373538] = 公共休息室角落一张烛光摇曳的桌子，一个男孩花哨地把一副牌摊成扇形，一名学生紧盯着他的手。\n# en: Learn the tricks\nopt.left.label [f47312] = 学会这些把戏\n# en: By midnight you can spot a marked card, a bottom deal and a mirror charm. You will never be fleeced at Wizard's Hand again.\nopt.left.say.1 [4eb4c5] = 到午夜，你已经能识破记号牌、底牌发牌和镜像咒。你再也不会在“巫师之手”牌局上被人宰了。\n# en: Learn to play honestly — and well\nopt.right.label [e8fdeb] = 学会诚实地打——还要打得好\n# en: He teaches you odds and nerve instead. That weekend you win eight crowns at the common-room game without cheating. Corvin is weirdly proud.\nopt.right.say.1 [ff72c2] = 他转而教你算概率和稳住心态。那个周末，你在休息室牌局上一把千都没出就赢了八克朗。科尔文骄傲得有点莫名其妙。\n# en: He teaches you odds and nerve instead. That weekend the odds take three crowns off you. \"Also a lesson,\" says Corvin.\nopt.right.say.2 [27b9a8] = 他改教你概率和胆量。那个周末，概率从你身上拿走了三克朗。“这也是一课，”科尔文说。\n\n@event mira_ornithopter\n# en: The Test Flight\ntitle [5ac4c6] = 试飞\n# en: Mira has built a flying machine with canvas wings and seven propellers. It is balanced on the edge of the north tower. \"Brooms are so last century,\" they say, strapping in. \"I need someone on the rope. Probably. Possibly.\"\ntext [53ad04] = 米拉造了一台飞行器，有帆布翅膀和七个螺旋桨，正架在北塔的边缘。“扫帚太上个世纪了，”TA一边系安全带一边说，“我需要有人拉绳子。大概吧。也许。”\n# en: A rickety winged contraption balanced on the edge of a tower roof, a goggled artificer strapping into it, a friend holding a rope.\nscene [846151] = 一台摇摇晃晃的带翼装置架在塔顶边缘，一个戴护目镜的奇械师正把自己绑进去，一个朋友抓着一根绳子。\n# en: Hold the rope\nopt.left.label [710f01] = 拉住绳子\n# en: The machine flies — wobbly, loud, glorious — twice around the tower before landing in the lake. Mira surfaces whooping. You're both legends by dinner.\nopt.left.say.1 [b1bd36] = 机器飞起来了——摇摇晃晃、吵吵闹闹、辉煌灿烂——绕塔飞了两圈，然后落进了湖里。米拉欢呼着冒出水面。到晚饭时，你们俩都成了传奇。\n# en: The rope wins. You are dragged eight feet along the roof before letting go. The machine lands in a tree. Mira waves from it, delighted.\nopt.left.say.2 [e309f7] = 绳子赢了。你被拖着在屋顶上滑了八英尺才松手。机器落在了一棵树上。米拉在树上开心地挥手。\n# en: Insist on a safety net\nopt.right.label [31e7de] = 坚持要装安全网\n# en: You spend a day building a net. The machine flies four feet and drops into it. \"Data!\" says Mira happily. Nobody dies. It's a good day.\nopt.right.say.1 [21ff86] = 你花了一天搭网。机器飞了四英尺，掉进了网里。“数据！”米拉开心地说。没人丧命。是美好的一天。\n\n@event mira_lab_fire\n# en: Fire in the Workshop\ntitle [d8f498] = 工坊失火\n# en: The workshop is on fire. Properly this time. Mira is in the doorway, coughing — and trying to go back in. \"My notebooks! Three years of notes!\"\ntext [81533b] = 工坊着火了。这次是真的着火了。米拉站在门口咳嗽着——还想往里冲。“我的笔记本！三年的笔记！”\n# en: A workshop full of smoke and orange light, notebooks scattered across a burning bench, a figure in goggles coughing in the doorway.\nscene [1abf1a] = 满是浓烟和橙色火光的工坊，笔记本散落在燃烧的工作台上，一个戴护目镜的身影在门口咳嗽。\n# en: Grab the notebooks\nopt.left.label [6162da] = 去抢笔记本\n# en: You come out singed, smoking, with an armful of notebooks. Mira stares at you, then at the notebooks, then bursts into tears and hugs you, both of you coughing.\nopt.left.say.1 [8b32a4] = 你出来时衣服烧焦、浑身冒烟，怀里抱着一摞笔记本。米拉盯着你，又盯着笔记本，然后哭着抱住了你，你们俩都在咳嗽。\n# en: You get halfway before the smoke drives you back. You save one notebook. Mira says it's the most important one. You both know it isn't. They mean it anyway.\nopt.left.say.2 [585041] = 你冲到一半就被浓烟逼了回来。你救出了一本。米拉说那是最重要的一本。你们都知道不是。但TA是真心的。\n# en: Drag Mira away\nopt.right.label [3d1894] = 把米拉拖走\n# en: You haul Mira back from the door. The notebooks burn. Mira is silent for a week. Then they start a new notebook. The first page just says: \"Thank you.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [fc0ba5] = 你把米拉从门口拽了回来。笔记本烧光了。米拉沉默了一个星期。然后TA开始了一本新笔记。第一页只写着：“谢谢你。”\n\n@event mira_name_day\n# en: Mira's Name Day\ntitle [631f91] = 米拉的命名日\n# en: It's Mira's name day. They mention it casually, as if it doesn't matter. It clearly matters. Nobody else seems to know.\ntext [42d76c] = 今天是米拉的命名日。TA随口提了一句，好像无所谓似的。显然很有所谓。其他人似乎都不知道。\n# en: A cluttered workshop decorated with paper chains and one small cake with a single gear-shaped candle, an artificer looking embarrassed and pleased.\nscene [7e06f4] = 一间杂乱的工坊挂着纸链装饰，一个小蛋糕上插着一根齿轮形状的蜡烛，奇械师看起来又难为情又开心。\n# en: Throw a surprise party\nopt.left.label [7bc69f] = 办一场惊喜派对\n# en: Six friends, one cake and a banner you painted badly. Mira walks in, stops, and has to sit down. \"Nobody's ever...\" they start, and can't finish.\nopt.left.say.1 [9db9ed] = 六个朋友、一个蛋糕，还有一条你画得很烂的横幅。米拉走进来，愣住了，不得不坐下。“从来没有人……”TA开口，却说不下去。\n# en: Give them a handmade gift\nopt.right.label [36112f] = 送TA一件手工礼物\n# en: A little brass key that fits nothing. \"For whatever you build next,\" you say. Mira wears it on a chain, every day, for years.\nopt.right.say.1 [c8052e] = 一把什么锁也开不了的小铜钥匙。“送给你接下来要造的东西，”你说。米拉把它挂在链子上，天天戴着，戴了好多年。\n\n@event nessa_headmistress_interview\n# en: The Impossible Interview\ntitle [4748ca] = 不可能的采访\n# en: \"Nobody has interviewed the headmistress in twenty years,\" Nessa says. \"But she likes you. Probably. Get me in the door and I'll name my firstborn after you.\"\ntext [f4a9d2] = “二十年来没人采访过院长，”内莎说，“但她喜欢你。大概吧。帮我进那扇门，我就用你的名字给我的头一个孩子取名。”\n# en: A newspaper editor and a student waiting outside the headmistress's tower door, the editor clutching a list of questions three pages long.\nscene [af2317] = 一位报社编辑和一名学生等在院长塔楼门外，编辑手里攥着一张三页长的问题清单。\n# en: Ask the headmistress\nopt.left.label [733de3] = 去问院长\n# en: The headmistress agrees to ten minutes. She gives Nessa forty. The interview runs on the front page and sells out three printings.\nopt.left.say.1 [00612c] = 院长答应给十分钟。结果给了内莎四十分钟。采访登上头版，加印了三次都卖光了。\n# en: \"No,\" says the headmistress, pleasantly. \"But tell Miss Quill her piece on the kitchens was excellent.\" Nessa frames the rejection.\nopt.left.say.2 [951b1e] = “不行，”院长和颜悦色地说，“不过请转告奎尔小姐，她写厨房的那篇非常出色。”内莎把这次拒绝裱了起来。\n# en: Suggest someone overlooked instead\nopt.right.label [d4c0f5] = 建议改采访一位被忽视的人\n# en: Nessa interviews the night porter instead. It's the most-read piece of the year. Half the academy cries over breakfast.\nopt.right.say.1 [c59390] = 内莎改为采访了守夜的门房。那成了全年阅读量最高的文章。半个学院的人在早餐时哭了。\n\n@event nessa_typo\n# en: A Catastrophic Typo\ntitle [f9820b] = 灾难性的错字\n# en: The Ledger has gone to print. In the headline about Professor Thorne's lecture series, a single wrong letter has turned \"Thorne's Brilliant Lectures\" into \"Thorne's Brilliant Lecturers\". Which is somehow worse. Five hundred copies.\ntext [807b2d] = 《流明纪事报》已经付印。在关于索恩教授系列讲座的标题里，一个错字把“索恩的精彩讲座”变成了“索恩的精彩讲师”。不知怎么反而更糟。五百份。\n# en: A newspaper with a single word circled in red ink, an editor with her head in her hands, a stack of papers still warm from the press.\nscene [224cf5] = 一份报纸上有一个词被红墨水圈了出来，编辑双手抱头，一摞刚印好的报纸还带着余温。\n# en: Help her fix every copy overnight\nopt.left.label [5d1986] = 帮她连夜改好每一份\n# en: You correct five hundred copies with a scraping charm and a lot of tea. At dawn, Nessa falls asleep on the press. Thorne never knows.\nopt.left.say.1 [63580e] = 你用刮除咒和大量的茶改好了五百份。黎明时，内莎趴在印刷机上睡着了。索恩永远不会知道。\n# en: Tell her to run a correction and own it\nopt.right.label [f52406] = 让她登更正启事，坦然认错\n# en: The correction is gracious. Thorne, astonishingly, cuts it out and pins it to his door. \"Precision,\" he says in class, \"is a virtue. So is apology.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [02e473] = 更正启事写得很得体。令人震惊的是，索恩把它剪下来钉在了自己门上。“精确，”他在课上说，“是一种美德。道歉也是。”\n\n@event nessa_burnout\n# en: The Editor Sleeps\ntitle [7126ab] = 编辑睡着了\n# en: Nessa hasn't slept properly in a week. She's asleep at her desk now, and her quill — unsupervised — is still writing next week's paper. It has started inventing news.\ntext [b075ee] = 内莎已经一个星期没好好睡觉了。此刻她趴在桌上睡着了，而她的羽毛笔——无人看管——还在写下周的报纸。它已经开始编造新闻了。\n# en: A girl asleep at a desk in a newspaper office, her self-writing quill still scratching on its own, the candle burned down to a stub.\nscene [6ef002] = 报社办公室里一个女孩趴在桌上睡着了，她的自动羽毛笔还在自己沙沙书写，蜡烛已经烧成了一小截。\n# en: Put her to bed and run the paper yourself\nopt.left.label [9b9579] = 送她上床，你来办报\n# en: You edit, typeset and print one issue. It's adequate. When Nessa wakes she reads it, winces at your headlines, and hugs you for a long time.\nopt.left.say.1 [959dc9] = 你编辑、排版、印出了一期。还过得去。内莎醒来读了，对你起的标题皱了皱眉，然后抱了你很久。\n# en: Wake her and make her rest\nopt.right.label [c44266] = 叫醒她，逼她休息\n# en: She argues for ten minutes, then sleeps for fourteen hours. The quill's invented news about a dragon in the kitchens turns out to be true.\nopt.right.say.1 [46fb0e] = 她争了十分钟，然后睡了十四个小时。羽毛笔编造的“厨房里有条龙”的新闻，结果是真的。\n\n@event kael_northern_lights\n# en: The Lights of the North\ntitle [8eda52] = 北方之光\n# en: Kael wakes you at midnight. \"Come,\" he says. On the hill above the lake, the sky is moving — green and violet ribbons, the lights of his home, here, for him. \"I called them,\" he says. \"I wanted you to see.\"\ntext [874ab7] = 凯尔在午夜叫醒了你。“来，”他说。湖上方的山坡上，天空在流动——绿色与紫色的光带，那是他家乡的极光，在这里，为他而来。“是我召来的，”他说，“我想让你看看。”\n# en: Two figures on a frozen hillside under ribbons of green and violet light, one pointing upward, frost forming a circle around them.\nscene [e2a410] = 两个身影站在结冰的山坡上，头顶是绿紫色的光带，其中一人指向天空，霜在他们周围结成一个圆。\n# en: Watch in silence\nopt.left.label [3bfadc] = 静静地看\n# en: You watch until the lights fade. Neither of you speaks. On the walk down, he takes your hand without seeming to notice he's done it.\nopt.left.say.1 [f93df4] = 你们一直看到极光消散。谁也没说话。下山时，他牵起了你的手，似乎自己都没察觉。\n# en: Ask him about home\nopt.right.label [946458] = 问他关于家乡的事\n# en: He tells you about ice palaces and singing glaciers and a mother who never once said she was proud. Then he says: \"This place is more home now.\" He means the hill. He means you.\nopt.right.say.1 [d63dfe] = 他给你讲冰之宫殿、会唱歌的冰川，还有一位从没说过一次为他骄傲的母亲。然后他说：“现在这里更像家了。”他说的是这座山坡。他说的是你。\n\n@event kael_fever\n# en: Kael Burns\ntitle [19ecba] = 凯尔在发烧\n# en: Kael has a fever — and for someone made of frost, a fever is dangerous. The infirmary's warming charms are making it worse. Sister Amaranth looks frightened. \"He needs cold,\" she says. \"Real cold. I don't know where to get it.\"\ntext [0e9394] = 凯尔发烧了——对一个由霜构成的人来说，发烧很危险。医务室的温暖咒只会让情况更糟。阿玛兰斯修女看起来很害怕。“他需要寒冷，”她说，“真正的寒冷。我不知道去哪儿弄。”\n# en: A pale boy in a narrow bed, sweating, frost forming and melting on the pillow around his head in a constant cycle.\nscene [44bb31] = 一张窄床上躺着一个苍白的男孩，满身是汗，他头边枕头上的霜不断凝结又融化。\n# en: Bring him snow from the mountain\nopt.left.label [395e96] = 去山上给他取雪\n# en: You fly to the peaks on a borrowed broom and come back with a sack of ancient snow. By morning his fever breaks. He doesn't let go of your sleeve all day.\nopt.left.say.1 [9f1fbf] = 你骑着借来的扫帚飞上山巅，带回一麻袋远古积雪。到早上，他的烧退了。他一整天都没松开你的袖子。\n# en: The mountain wins. You come back half-frozen and empty-handed. Kael recovers anyway, slowly — and visits you in the next bed.\nopt.left.say.2 [c8178a] = 大山赢了。你冻得半死、两手空空地回来。凯尔还是慢慢好了起来——还去隔壁病床探望了你。\n# en: Send word to Ilse\nopt.right.label [18fc37] = 给伊尔泽捎信\n# en: Ilse arrives in a whirl of snow, puts her hand on his forehead and says something sharp in Northern. The fever breaks. Kael stares at his sister for a long time.\nopt.right.say.1 [8cf203] = 伊尔泽裹着一阵飞雪赶到，把手放在他额头上，用北境语厉声说了句什么。烧退了。凯尔盯着他的妹妹看了很久。\n# en: You can't find her. Kael recovers slowly, on his own, the way he's always done everything.\nopt.right.say.2 [efbddf] = 你找不到她。凯尔靠自己慢慢好了起来，就像他一向独自扛下所有事那样。\n\n@event kael_letter_home\n# en: A Letter He Won't Send\ntitle [ff94b6] = 一封他不会寄出的信\n# en: Kael is writing a letter. He's rewritten the first line eleven times. \"To my mother,\" he says. \"I'm telling her I won't come home. I won't open the door. I'm telling her I've found...\" He stops.\ntext [71254e] = 凯尔在写信。第一行他已经重写了十一遍。“写给我母亲，”他说，“告诉她我不会回家了。我不会打开那扇门。告诉她我找到了……”他停住了。\n# en: A boy at a library desk, a letter half-written in sharp Northern script, a candle beside it, ink frozen in the pen.\nscene [fc0e7c] = 一个男孩坐在图书馆的书桌前，一封用凌厉的北境字体写了一半的信，旁边一支蜡烛，笔里的墨水冻住了。\n# en: \"Found what?\"\nopt.left.label [10295e] = “找到了什么？”\n# en: He looks at you for a long time. Then he writes the last word of the sentence without showing you, seals it, and sends it. He's lighter for days.\nopt.left.say.1 [cdad8b] = 他看了你很久。然后他没让你看，写下了那句话的最后一个词，封好信，寄了出去。之后好几天他都轻松了许多。\n# en: Help him find the words\nopt.right.label [a0a115] = 帮他找到合适的话\n# en: You sit together until the candle's gone. The letter, when it's finished, is short. It doesn't apologise once. He sends it at dawn.\nopt.right.say.1 [c25f80] = 你们一起坐到蜡烛燃尽。写完的信很短，一次也没道歉。他在黎明时寄了出去。\n\n@event bram_father_visit\n# en: Bram's Father\ntitle [26834d] = 布拉姆的父亲\n# en: A tall man in an old head-boy's sash is speaking to Bram in the entrance hall, quietly and terribly. Bram has shrunk two sizes. \"...never amount to anything,\" the man is saying.\ntext [1a56a1] = 一个披着旧式男生代表绶带的高个男人在门厅里和布拉姆说话，声音很轻，却很可怕。布拉姆缩小了两圈。“……永远不会有出息，”那人说。\n# en: A towering grey-haired man in an old head-boy's sash speaking down to a red-faced prefect in the entrance hall, students hurrying past pretending not to look.\nscene [d8766d] = 门厅里，一个身材高大、头发灰白、披着旧式男生代表绶带的男人居高临下地对一个满脸通红的级长说话，学生们匆匆走过，假装没在看。\n# en: Interrupt — say something good\nopt.left.label [720c7c] = 插话——说些好话\n# en: You tell his father about the sixth-years at the boathouse. The man falls silent. Bram looks at you like you've handed him a sword.\nopt.left.say.1 [e37892] = 你跟他父亲讲了船屋那几个六年级生的事。那人沉默了。布拉姆看你的眼神，就像你递给了他一把剑。\n# en: His father looks through you as if you're furniture. But afterwards, Bram finds you and says, roughly, \"Nobody's ever stood there before. Thanks.\"\nopt.left.say.2 [bb9dcb] = 他父亲看你就像看一件家具。但事后布拉姆找到你，粗声粗气地说：“以前从没有人站在那儿过。谢了。”\n# en: Find Bram afterwards\nopt.right.label [46a529] = 事后去找布拉姆\n# en: He's behind the stables, again. You sit on the bucket next to his. You don't talk. After a while he says, \"Same time next year, probably,\" and almost laughs.\nopt.right.say.1 [7e1535] = 他又在马厩后面。你在他旁边的水桶上坐下。你们不说话。过了一会儿他说：“明年大概还是这个时候，”然后差点笑了出来。\n\n@event bram_cheating\n# en: Bram Cheats\ntitle [cf8d51] = 布拉姆作弊\n# en: In the exam hall, two desks away, you see Bram Hollis — prefect, enforcer of rules — slip a tiny scroll out of his sleeve. He sees you seeing him. His face goes white.\ntext [5bf337] = 考场上，隔着两张桌子，你看见布拉姆·霍利斯——级长、校规的执行者——从袖子里抽出一卷小纸条。他看见你看见了他。他的脸一下子白了。\n# en: A prefect in an exam hall slipping a tiny scroll out of his sleeve, a student two desks away watching.\nscene [6230cf] = 考场里一位级长正从袖子里抽出一卷小纸条，隔两张桌子的一名学生看在眼里。\n# en: Report him\nopt.left.label [794001] = 举报他\n# en: Bram loses his sash for the rest of the year. Some students cheer. You don't feel like cheering. Neither, later, does he.\nopt.left.say.1 [bf4a08] = 布拉姆被摘掉了绶带，直到学年结束。有些学生欢呼。你并不想欢呼。后来，他也一样。\n# en: Say nothing — and let him know you saw\nopt.right.label [5c554c] = 什么也不说——但让他知道你看见了\n# en: After the exam you just look at him. He nods, once. You never mention it. But his demerits stop, for you and for everyone you know.\nopt.right.say.1 [88ddd1] = 考完后你只是看着他。他点了一下头。你从没提起这件事。但他的记过停了——对你，也对你认识的每一个人。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/expansion_social.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/expansion_social.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event soc_lost_notes\n# en: The Borrowed Notes\ntitle [f46501] = 借走的笔记\n# en: {npc} borrowed your Lore notes last week. {They} {are} now standing in front of you holding a single soggy page. \"There was a stream,\" {they} say{s}. \"And a duck. Mostly a duck.\"\ntext [5dbc83] = {npc}上星期借走了你的学识课笔记。现在{they}站在你面前，手里只剩一页湿透的纸。“有条小溪，”{they}说，“还有一只鸭子。主要是鸭子。”\n# en: A student holding a single soggy page of notes, the rest of the stack floating away down a stream behind them.\nscene [daf951] = 一名学生手里攥着一页湿透的笔记，身后剩下的那叠正顺着小溪漂走。\n# en: Laugh it off\nopt.left.label [29c02a] = 一笑了之\n# en: You rewrite your notes from memory. {npc} brings you a cake every day for a week, by way of apology. The cakes are better than the notes were.\nopt.left.say.1 [37846c] = 你凭记忆重写了笔记。{npc}一连一星期每天给你带一块蛋糕赔罪。蛋糕比笔记还好。\n# en: Make {them} rewrite them\nopt.right.label [53cd0f] = 让{them}重抄一份\n# en: {npc} copies out forty pages from a friend's notes, grumbling. The new notes are neater than yours ever were.\nopt.right.say.1 [a8c805] = {npc}一边抱怨一边从朋友那儿抄了四十页。新笔记比你自己写的还工整。\n\n@event soc_birthday\n# en: A Birthday\ntitle [492d55] = 生日\n# en: You find out — by accident — that it's {npc}'s birthday. {They} haven't told anyone. {They} {are} spending it in the library, alone, on purpose.\ntext [184bc7] = 你偶然发现今天是{npc}的生日。{they}谁也没告诉，正一个人待在图书馆里——故意的。\n# en: A small cake with a single candle in a dark common room, a group of students crouched behind a sofa, a surprised face in the doorway.\nscene [85796d] = 漆黑的公共休息室里一个插着一根蜡烛的小蛋糕，一群学生蹲在沙发后面，门口一张惊讶的脸。\n# en: Organise a surprise\nopt.left.label [c1b395] = 策划一场惊喜\n# en: Seven people jump out from behind a sofa. {npc} screams, then laughs, then has to sit down. \"How did you know?\" You'll never tell.\nopt.left.say.1 [7240f2] = 七个人从沙发后面跳出来。{npc}尖叫，然后大笑，然后不得不坐下。“你们怎么知道的？”你永远不会说。\n# en: Join {them} in the library\nopt.right.label [d7c810] = 去图书馆陪{them}\n# en: You sit down opposite {them} with your own book. Neither of you says happy birthday. At midnight {they} slide{s} a biscuit across the table, and you both grin.\nopt.right.say.1 [6d7231] = 你带着自己的书在{them}对面坐下。谁也没说生日快乐。午夜时，{they}把一块饼干推过桌面，你们相视一笑。\n\n@event soc_bad_news\n# en: Bad News From Home\ntitle [9ab55c] = 家里来的坏消息\n# en: {npc} is sitting on the stairs with a letter in {their} lap, not moving. {Their} grandmother is ill. The barge home takes two days. \"I don't know whether to go,\" {they} say{s}.\ntext [c5229c] = {npc}坐在楼梯上，膝上放着一封信，一动不动。{their}祖母病了。坐驳船回家要两天。“我不知道该不该回去，”{they}说。\n# en: A student sitting on a stone staircase with a crumpled letter, head bowed, a friend sitting down beside them.\nscene [e888fc] = 一名学生坐在石阶上，手里攥着一封揉皱的信，低着头，一个朋友在旁边坐下。\n# en: \"Go. We'll cover your classes.\"\nopt.left.label [6868e0] = “去吧，课我们帮你顶着。”\n# en: {npc} catches the next barge. You take notes for two in every lesson. When {npc} comes back, {their} grandmother is better, and {they} hug{s} you on the landing.\nopt.left.say.1 [551a90] = {npc}赶上了下一班驳船。你每节课都记两份笔记。{npc}回来时，{their}祖母好些了，{they}在楼梯平台上抱住了你。\n# en: Just sit with {them}\nopt.right.label [75d0fd] = 只是陪{them}坐着\n# en: You sit there until the stairs move under you. You don't fix anything. That's not what {they} needed.\nopt.right.say.1 [439080] = 你们一直坐到楼梯在身下挪动。你什么也没解决。那本来就不是{they}需要的。\n\n@event soc_stargazing\n# en: Counting Stars\ntitle [559b85] = 数星星\n# en: {npc} has dragged a blanket up to the roof. \"There's a meteor shower,\" {they} say{s}. \"Supposedly.\" There's room on the blanket for two, just about.\ntext [9f0616] = {npc}把一条毯子拖上了屋顶。“有流星雨，”{they}说，“据说。”毯子上刚好够两个人躺。\n# en: Two students lying on their backs on a flat roof, close but not quite touching, a sky full of stars above.\nscene [6f5d93] = 两名学生仰面躺在平屋顶上，挨得很近却又没碰到，头顶是满天繁星。\n# en: Count meteors\nopt.left.label [b22e25] = 数流星\n# en: You count thirty-one. {npc} counts thirty-four and accuses you of not looking properly. You weren't, entirely.\nopt.left.say.1 [4409c0] = 你数到三十一颗。{npc}数到三十四颗，指责你没认真看。你确实没完全在看。\n# en: Talk about everything else\nopt.right.label [a3c084] = 聊些别的一切\n# en: You don't see a single meteor. You talk about home and fear and what you'd do if you could do anything. When you look up, the sky's gone grey with dawn.\nopt.right.say.1 [88b087] = 你一颗流星也没看到。你们聊家乡、聊恐惧、聊如果什么都能做会去做什么。等你抬起头，天已经泛起了黎明的灰白。\n\n@event soc_jealous_friend\n# en: Jealousy\ntitle [bace8d] = 嫉妒\n# en: {npc} has been odd all week. Finally it comes out. \"You've got all these new friends now. I just — I don't know where I fit anymore.\"\ntext [090680] = {npc}一整个星期都怪怪的。终于说出来了：“你现在有那么多新朋友了。我就是——不知道自己还算什么了。”\n# en: A student standing alone at the edge of a laughing group, arms folded, watching someone else take what used to be their seat.\nscene [560bd3] = 一名学生抱着胳膊独自站在一群欢笑的人边上，看着别人坐上了曾经属于自己的位置。\n# en: Reassure {them}\nopt.left.label [c0cad1] = 让{them}安心\n# en: You tell {them} exactly where {they} fit: right here, first. {npc} rolls {their} eyes, but {they}'re smiling. The oddness is gone by dinner.\nopt.left.say.1 [944299] = 你明明白白地告诉{them}：{they}的位置就在这里，排第一。{npc}翻了个白眼，但在笑。晚饭前那股别扭劲就没了。\n# en: Tell {them} friendship isn't a contest\nopt.right.label [147a3c] = 告诉{them}友情不是比赛\n# en: You're right, technically. {npc} knows it. It still lands badly. It takes a few weeks for things to feel easy again.\nopt.right.say.1 [1dcb8f] = 严格来说你是对的。{npc}也知道。但这话还是说得不太好听。过了好几个星期，你们之间才重新自在起来。\n\n@event soc_teach_spell\n# en: A Trick Worth Learning\ntitle [8a5e67] = 值得一学的小把戏\n# en: {npc} has a spell {they} invented — a little thing, half-useful, half-ridiculous — and wants to teach it to someone. \"It makes your tea exactly the right temperature,\" {they} say{s}. \"Forever.\"\ntext [1a48bd] = {npc}有一个自己发明的咒语——一个小东西，一半有用，一半荒唐——想教给别人。“它能让你的茶永远保持刚刚好的温度，”{they}说，“永远。”\n# en: Two students in an empty classroom, one demonstrating a wand movement, the other copying it with a tiny spark of light.\nscene [bfffc4] = 空教室里两名学生，一个在示范魔杖的动作，另一个跟着模仿，杖尖冒出一点小火花。\n# en: Learn it properly\nopt.left.label [9c42bb] = 认真学会\n# en: It takes an hour. Your tea is now perfect, always. You'll use this spell more than anything you learn in class.\nopt.left.say.1 [9f0fc3] = 花了一个钟头。你的茶现在永远是完美的。这个咒语你用得会比课上学的任何东西都多。\n# en: The first attempt freezes your tea solid, and your eyebrows with it. The second works. Your eyebrows take a day to thaw.\nopt.left.say.2 [1057e1] = 第一次尝试把你的茶冻成了冰坨，连眉毛也一起冻上了。第二次成功了。你的眉毛花了一天才解冻。\n# en: Teach {them} one of yours in return\nopt.right.label [a24082] = 也教{them}一个你的\n# en: You trade spells like secrets. By midnight you've both got a new trick and an inside joke that will last three years.\nopt.right.say.1 [42f01a] = 你们像交换秘密一样交换咒语。到午夜时，你们都多了一个新把戏，还有一个能笑上三年的内部笑话。\n\n@event soc_secret_admirer\n# en: A Secret Admirer\ntitle [6041b8] = 神秘的仰慕者\n# en: Folded notes keep appearing in your library books. Unsigned. They're funny, and a little nervous, and they know things about you that only someone paying attention would know. The handwriting looks familiar.\ntext [a4cea9] = 你的图书馆借书里不断出现折好的字条。没有署名。它们很有趣，有点紧张，而且知道一些只有一直留意你的人才会知道的事。那笔迹看着有点眼熟。\n# en: A folded note tucked into a library book with a pressed flower, unsigned, the handwriting careful and slightly shaky.\nscene [469c2b] = 一张夹在图书馆书里的折叠字条，附着一朵压花，没有署名，字迹小心又微微发抖。\n# en: Leave a note back\nopt.left.label [ad88dc] = 回一张字条\n# en: You write back. The notes get longer. One day the last line just says: \"It's {npc}. You knew, didn't you?\" You did.\nopt.left.say.1 [5ee7cc] = 你回了信。字条越写越长。有一天，最后一行只写着：“是{npc}。你早就知道了，对吧？”你确实知道。\n# en: Stake out the library\nopt.right.label [010099] = 在图书馆蹲守\n# en: You catch {npc} red-handed, mid-tuck. {They} turn{s} the colour of a pyromancy exam. You both laugh until the librarian throws you out.\nopt.right.say.1 [a59ad0] = 你当场抓住了正往书里塞字条的{npc}。{they}的脸红得像火焰术考试。你们俩笑到被图书管理员赶了出去。\n# en: You wait three nights and catch nobody. On the fourth morning the note says: \"Nice try.\"\nopt.right.say.2 [ec1e48] = 你守了三个晚上，谁也没抓到。第四天早上，字条上写着：“想得美。”\n\n@event soc_injured_friend\n# en: Carried\ntitle [c0fda1] = 背回来\n# en: {npc} has twisted an ankle on the far side of the grounds, a long way from the infirmary, and it's getting dark. {They} {are} trying very hard to pretend it doesn't hurt.\ntext [f03529] = {npc}在校园的另一头扭伤了脚踝，离医务室很远，天也快黑了。{they}正非常努力地假装一点也不疼。\n# en: A student carrying another piggyback across a muddy lawn, the injured one's ankle wrapped in a scarf.\nscene [042d6a] = 一名学生背着另一名学生穿过泥泞的草坪，受伤那位的脚踝用围巾裹着。\n# en: Carry {them} on your back\nopt.left.label [374549] = 把{them}背回去\n# en: It's a long way. {npc} narrates the whole journey like a horse race. By the time you reach the infirmary you're both laughing too hard to breathe.\nopt.left.say.1 [6e8e08] = 路很长。{npc}一路像解说赛马一样解说着。等你们到医务室时，两个人都笑得喘不上气。\n# en: Run for help\nopt.right.label [689bdc] = 跑去找人帮忙\n# en: You run for Sister Amaranth, who arrives with a floating stretcher and a lot of opinions. {npc} is fine. {They} send{s} you a thank-you sweet.\nopt.right.say.1 [b2b6c2] = 你跑去找阿玛兰斯修女，她带着一副漂浮担架和一肚子意见赶来了。{npc}没事。{they}送了你一块谢礼糖。\n\n@event soc_rival_decency\n# en: An Unexpected Kindness\ntitle [10ec52] = 意外的善意\n# en: You drop your books in the rain, and the first person to stoop and help pick them up is {npc}. {They} hand{s} them back without a word, looking as surprised as you are.\ntext [76c0d9] = 你的书在雨里掉了一地，第一个弯腰帮你捡的人竟是{npc}。{they}一言不发地把书递还给你，看上去和你一样惊讶。\n# en: A student picking up a rival's scattered books from a wet floor, the rival frozen mid-step, not sure what to say.\nscene [b9f110] = 一名学生从湿漉漉的地上捡起对手散落的书，对手僵在半步之中，不知该说什么。\n# en: Thank {them} properly\nopt.left.label [023f60] = 好好谢谢{them}\n# en: \"Don't make it a thing,\" {npc} mutters. But the next day {they} nod{s} to you in the corridor. Something has thawed.\nopt.left.say.1 [563f24] = “别弄得像回事似的，”{npc}嘟囔道。但第二天，{they}在走廊里冲你点了点头。有什么东西解冻了。\n# en: Suspect a trick\nopt.right.label [5792ea] = 怀疑这是个圈套\n# en: You check the books for hexes. There aren't any. {npc} saw you checking. You'll both remember that.\nopt.right.say.1 [e359de] = 你检查书上有没有恶咒。没有。{npc}看见你在检查。你们俩都会记住这件事。\n\n@event soc_partner_anniversary\n# en: An Anniversary\ntitle [a63670] = 纪念日\n# en: It's been a while now — long enough that {npc} has started calling things \"ours\". Today {they} hand{s} you a small wrapped box and won't meet your eyes.\ntext [7539ef] = 已经有一阵子了——久到{npc}开始把东西叫作“我们的”。今天{they}递给你一个包好的小盒子，不敢看你的眼睛。\n# en: Two people on a bench by the lake at sunset, one holding out a small wrapped box, the other's hands over their mouth.\nscene [3c4e6d] = 日落时分，两个人坐在湖边的长椅上，一个递出一个包好的小盒子，另一个双手捂着嘴。\n# en: Open it now\nopt.left.label [a90872] = 现在就打开\n# en: Inside: a pebble from the lakeshore where you first really talked, polished to a shine. \"It's stupid,\" {npc} says. It isn't. You carry it everywhere, and spend the next day hunting for something half as good to give back.\nopt.left.say.1 [920de2] = 里面是一颗湖边的鹅卵石，来自你们第一次真正谈心的那片湖岸，被打磨得闪闪发亮。“这很傻，”{npc}说。一点也不傻。你走到哪儿都带着它，第二天又跑遍各处，想找一件哪怕有它一半好的回礼。\n# en: Give {them} yours first\nopt.right.label [64f5f8] = 先把你的送给{them}\n# en: You'd had the same idea. You both open your boxes at once. They're nearly the same gift. You laugh until the sun goes down.\nopt.right.say.1 [7c250d] = 你们想到一块儿去了。你们同时打开盒子。两份礼物几乎一模一样。你们一直笑到太阳落山。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/expansion_summer.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/expansion_summer.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event sum_empty_castle\n# en: The Empty Castle\ntitle [38b303] = 空荡荡的城堡\n# en: In summer the castle breathes differently. Corridors you've walked a thousand times are silent; doors that are always locked stand slightly open. A portrait yawns. Nobody is watching.\ntext [b8e5ca] = 夏天，城堡的呼吸都不一样了。你走过上千次的走廊寂静无声；那些永远上锁的门微微敞开着。一幅画像打了个哈欠。没有人在看。\n# en: A vast empty dining hall in summer, chairs upturned on the tables, a single student eating alone in a shaft of dusty sunlight.\nscene [06e080] = 夏日里一间空旷的大餐厅，椅子倒扣在桌上，一名学生独自坐在满是浮尘的阳光里吃饭。\n# en: Explore the open doors\nopt.left.label [476a88] = 探索那些敞开的门\n# en: Behind one door: a forgotten trophy room. Behind another: a professor's stash of very old toffee and a purse of lost-property coins nobody will miss.\nopt.left.say.1 [a2c1c1] = 一扇门后：一间被遗忘的奖杯室。另一扇门后：某位教授私藏的陈年太妃糖，还有一袋没人会惦记的失物招领硬币。\n# en: Behind the first door: the caretaker, in a dressing gown, very surprised. You both agree this never happened.\nopt.left.say.2 [eeeeef] = 第一扇门后：穿着睡袍、大吃一惊的管理员。你们俩一致同意，这事从没发生过。\n# en: Enjoy the quiet\nopt.right.label [293287] = 享受这份安静\n# en: You read in window seats, nap on the great lawn and eat breakfast at noon. For two weeks the most famous academy in the kingdom is just a big, peaceful house.\nopt.right.say.1 [08d11a] = 你在窗台上看书，在大草坪上打盹，中午才吃早饭。整整两个星期，王国最有名的学院只是一座又大又安宁的房子。\n\n@event sum_home_chores\n# en: Help Around the House\ntitle [da7efc] = 帮家里干活\n# en: Home is lovely for about three days. Then the list appears on the kitchen door: the roof, the goats, the fence, the cousin's wedding. Your summer reading list is hidden under a tea towel.\ntext [e1f4e8] = 家的美好大概能维持三天。然后清单就贴上了厨房门：屋顶、山羊、篱笆、表亲的婚礼。你的暑期书单被藏在一块茶巾底下。\n# en: A farmhouse kitchen in high summer, a basket of laundry, a list of chores pinned to the door, a textbook hidden under a tea towel.\nscene [c81350] = 盛夏的农舍厨房，一篮待洗的衣物，门上钉着家务清单，一本课本藏在茶巾底下。\n# en: Do the chores — with magic\nopt.left.label [2b5166] = 干家务——用魔法\n# en: The fence mends itself, the goats are briefly levitated, the roof is better than new. The whole street comes to watch. Your mother pretends not to be proud.\nopt.left.say.1 [6cae25] = 篱笆自己补好了，山羊短暂地浮了起来，屋顶比新的还好。整条街的人都跑来看。你妈妈假装一点也不骄傲。\n# en: The goats are briefly levitated. Unfortunately so is the fence. Your family is very patient about it.\nopt.left.say.2 [4bdcaf] = 山羊短暂地浮了起来。不幸的是，篱笆也浮起来了。你的家人对此非常有耐心。\n# en: Do them the slow way, and talk\nopt.right.label [b2c300] = 慢慢干，顺便聊聊\n# en: You fix the fence by hand with your father, saying very little and then, suddenly, a lot. It's the best conversation you've had all year.\nopt.right.say.1 [775d96] = 你和爸爸一起亲手修篱笆，一开始几乎不说话，后来突然说了很多。这是你一整年里最好的一次谈话。\n\n@event sum_old_friend\n# en: An Old Friend\ntitle [8809c5] = 老朋友\n# en: Your oldest friend from home is waiting at the crossroads, like always. But something's different. \"So,\" they say, not quite looking at you, \"I suppose you're too grand for us now.\"\ntext [66198b] = 你在家乡最老的朋友照例等在十字路口。但有些东西不一样了。“所以，”对方说，眼睛没怎么看你，“我猜你现在看不上我们了吧。”\n# en: Two young people sitting on a stone wall above a village, one in academy robes, one in work clothes, a little space between them.\nscene [b48b9c] = 两个年轻人坐在村子上方的石墙上，一个穿着学院长袍，一个穿着干活的衣服，中间隔着一点距离。\n# en: Prove you're not\nopt.left.label [4cb06b] = 证明你没有\n# en: You spend the summer exactly as you used to — orchard raids, river swims, terrible jokes. By Starfall, the space between you on the wall is gone.\nopt.left.say.1 [8019a4] = 你像从前一样度过这个夏天——偷果园、下河游泳、讲烂笑话。到了星坠月，石墙上你们之间的那点距离不见了。\n# en: Admit you've changed\nopt.right.label [11cd37] = 承认你变了\n# en: \"I have,\" you say. \"So have you.\" They laugh, surprised. You talk all night about who you're both becoming. It's different from before. It might be better.\nopt.right.say.1 [84ee4c] = “我是变了，”你说，“你也变了。”对方惊讶地笑了。你们聊了一整夜，聊你们各自正在变成什么样的人。和从前不一样了。也许会更好。\n\n@event sum_midsummer_fair\n# en: The Midsummer Fair\ntitle [fa85d7] = 仲夏集市\n# en: The village Midsummer Fair: enchanted carousel, ring-toss, a strongman, and a booth promising \"GUESS YOUR WEIGHT IN CHEESE\". You have a few crowns in your pocket and a whole afternoon.\ntext [d583e2] = 村里的仲夏集市：魔法旋转木马、套圈、大力士，还有一个摊位承诺“猜猜你的体重等于多少奶酪”。你兜里有几个克朗，还有一整个下午。\n# en: A village green full of striped tents, a carousel of enchanted wooden animals, lanterns strung between trees, a strongman lifting a cow.\nscene [b2cfbf] = 村子草地上满是条纹帐篷，一座由施了魔法的木头动物组成的旋转木马，树间挂着灯笼，一个大力士正举起一头牛。\n# en: Win something at the ring-toss\nopt.left.label [1e7b9b] = 套圈赢个奖\n# en: Three rings, three hits. You win a stuffed griffon the size of a sheep and sell it to a crying child's father for fifteen crowns. Everyone wins.\nopt.left.say.1 [b0aa3b] = 三个圈，三个全中。你赢了一只绵羊那么大的狮鹫玩偶，又以十五克朗卖给了一个哭闹孩子的爸爸。皆大欢喜。\n# en: Every ring bounces off. The ring-toss is definitely enchanted. You don't care; you're eating candied apples.\nopt.left.say.2 [09da43] = 每个圈都弹开了。这套圈摊绝对施了魔法。你不在乎，你正在吃糖苹果。\n# en: Ride the carousel\nopt.right.label [a83496] = 坐旋转木马\n# en: Your wooden hippogriff takes off, very slightly, at the top of every turn. You ride it nine times.\nopt.right.say.1 [8ddedf] = 你的木头鹰马每转到最高处都会轻轻地飞起来一点点。你坐了九次。\n\n@event sum_letter_from_friend\n# en: A Summer Letter\ntitle [947c1f] = 夏日来信\n# en: An owl arrives with a thick, slightly crumpled letter from {npc}. Six pages of summer news, two doodles, one pressed flower, and at the end, in smaller writing: \"I miss the castle. I miss you, actually.\"\ntext [915df1] = 一只猫头鹰送来了{npc}一封厚厚的、有点皱的信。六页夏日见闻，两幅涂鸦，一朵压花，最后用小一号的字写着：“我想念城堡。其实，我想你。”\n# en: A thick letter with a doodle on the envelope arriving by owl at a summer window.\nscene [c7230f] = 一封信封上画着涂鸦的厚信，由猫头鹰送到夏日的窗前。\n# en: Write a short reply\nopt.left.label [2e26d1] = 写一封简短的回信\n# en: You write a page. It's warm, if brief. {npc} keeps it anyway.\nopt.left.say.1 [ae145d] = 你写了一页。温暖，虽然简短。{npc}还是把它收了起来。\n# en: Write back for hours\nopt.right.label [c44657] = 回信写上几个钟头\n# en: You write until the candle gutters. Twelve pages. You can't remember the last time you wrote that much that wasn't an essay.\nopt.right.say.1 [d776a8] = 你一直写到蜡烛快灭。十二页。你都不记得上次写这么多不是论文的东西是什么时候了。\n\n@event sum_rain_week\n# en: A Week of Rain\ntitle [fa6851] = 一星期的雨\n# en: It rains for a solid week. Not dramatic rain: just steady, grey, endless rain. Every plan you made for the summer is quietly drowning.\ntext [8b734b] = 雨整整下了一个星期。不是戏剧性的雨，只是稳定、灰暗、没完没了的雨。你为这个夏天做的每一个计划都在悄悄被淹没。\n# en: Rain streaming down a tall window, a student curled in an armchair with a fat novel, a teapot steaming on the sill.\nscene [ea0fc1] = 雨水顺着高窗流下，一名学生蜷在扶手椅里捧着一本厚小说，窗台上的茶壶冒着热气。\n# en: Read a very long novel\nopt.left.label [fc9cb2] = 读一本很长的小说\n# en: You buy an eight-hundred-page novel about a family of sea-witches, read every word and cry at the end. It's the best week of the summer.\nopt.left.say.1 [f8199f] = 你买了一本八百页的小说，讲一个海巫家族的故事，一字不落地读完，读到结尾哭了。这是整个夏天最好的一周。\n# en: Practise spells indoors\nopt.right.label [8f2ff7] = 在室内练咒语\n# en: You practise until you can light every candle in the room with a flick. You also set fire to one curtain. It's raining; it goes out quickly.\nopt.right.say.1 [f19727] = 你一直练到手指一弹就能点亮房间里每一支蜡烛。你还烧着了一块窗帘。外面在下雨，火很快就灭了。\n\n@event sum_sea_serpent\n# en: Fishing With an Uncle\ntitle [340021] = 和叔叔去钓鱼\n# en: Your uncle takes you fishing. Something takes the bait. Something very large. The line sings. The boat tilts. A long scaled neck rises out of the water and looks at you with polite interest.\ntext [fbf017] = 叔叔带你去钓鱼。有东西咬钩了。非常大的东西。鱼线嗡嗡作响，船身倾斜。一条长满鳞片的长脖子从水里升起，带着礼貌的好奇看着你。\n# en: A small fishing boat on a grey-green sea, a fishing line drawn taut, something very long and scaled rising beside the hull.\nscene [035dc5] = 灰绿色海面上的一条小渔船，鱼线绷得紧紧的，一个很长、长满鳞片的东西正从船边升起。\n# en: Cut the line\nopt.left.label [c4df32] = 剪断鱼线\n# en: The serpent sinks back with a sigh like a disappointed whale. Your uncle tells the story for the rest of his life, and it gets bigger every time.\nopt.left.say.1 [f3a3c3] = 海蛇叹了口气沉了回去，像一头失望的鲸鱼。你叔叔后半辈子都在讲这个故事，而且每讲一次就更大一点。\n# en: Try to talk to it\nopt.right.label [2c2211] = 试着和它说话\n# en: You greet it in the old courtesies Professor Brask taught you. It bows its great head, leaves a pearl on the deck and swims away. Your uncle faints.\nopt.right.say.1 [02a4d5] = 你用布拉斯克教授教过的古老礼节向它问好。它低下巨大的头，在甲板上留下一颗珍珠，然后游走了。你叔叔晕了过去。\n# en: The serpent is not interested in conversation. It is interested in the boat. You swim home.\nopt.right.say.2 [c01ca7] = 海蛇对谈话没兴趣。它对船有兴趣。你是游回家的。\n\n@event sum_reading_list\n# en: The Summer Reading List\ntitle [2a9b43] = 暑期书单\n# en: The summer reading list arrives by owl. It is a scroll. It unrolls across the floor, out of the door and partway down the stairs. Thorne has added a note at the bottom: \"Optional. (It is not optional.)\"\ntext [56544a] = 暑期书单由猫头鹰送达。是一卷羊皮纸。它在地板上展开，滚出门外，一直滚到楼梯中间。索恩在最下面加了一句：“选读。（并不是选读。）”\n# en: A long scroll unrolling off a table and across the floor, a list of titles in tiny handwriting, a student staring at it in despair.\nscene [593aae] = 一卷长长的羊皮纸从桌上滚落、铺满地板，上面用极小的字写满书名，一名学生绝望地盯着它。\n# en: Read all of it\nopt.left.label [281ec8] = 全部读完\n# en: You read everything. When term starts, Thorne asks a question about the last book and you're the only one who can answer. His eyebrow rises half a millimetre.\nopt.left.say.1 [0c1e02] = 你全读完了。开学时，索恩问了一个关于最后一本书的问题，只有你答得上来。他的眉毛抬高了半毫米。\n# en: Read the first and last chapters of each\nopt.right.label [2423ad] = 每本只读第一章和最后一章\n# en: A time-honoured strategy. You can talk confidently about the beginning and end of forty books. The middle remains a mystery, like most of life.\nopt.right.say.1 [bca605] = 一个历史悠久的策略。你能自信地谈论四十本书的开头和结尾。中间部分仍然是个谜，就像人生的大部分一样。\n\n@event sum_night_market\n# en: The Night Market\ntitle [8fbd8e] = 夜市\n# en: A night market appears in a field outside the village — it wasn't there yesterday and won't be tomorrow. A hooded stall-keeper offers you a stoppered bottle with a tiny thundercloud inside. \"Fifteen crowns. What it does is a surprise.\"\ntext [8af6b0] = 村外的田野里出现了一个夜市——昨天还没有，明天也不会在。一个戴兜帽的摊主递给你一个塞着瓶塞的瓶子，里面有一小朵雷雨云。“十五克朗。它有什么用？那是个惊喜。”\n# en: A market that only appears after midnight, stalls lit with coloured flames, hooded sellers, a bottle that seems to contain a small thundercloud.\nscene [ad8492] = 一个只在午夜后出现的集市，摊位亮着彩色火焰，戴兜帽的卖家，一个瓶子里似乎装着一小朵雷雨云。\n# en: Buy the bottle\nopt.left.label [19c560] = 买下瓶子\n# en: You uncork it in your room. The little storm rains ideas on you for a fortnight. Your notebooks overflow.\nopt.left.say.1 [3066ff] = 你在房间里拔开瓶塞。那场小风暴往你身上下了两星期的灵感雨。你的笔记本都写满了。\n# en: You uncork it in your room. The little storm rains on you. Just rain. For a fortnight. You catch a cold.\nopt.left.say.2 [07314d] = 你在房间里拔开瓶塞。那场小风暴往你身上下雨。就是雨。下了两星期。你感冒了。\n# en: Just look around\nopt.right.label [1c076f] = 只是逛逛\n# en: You wander until dawn, buying nothing, watching everything. When the sun rises the field is empty except for a single glowing feather. You keep it.\nopt.right.say.1 [a046d1] = 你一直逛到黎明，什么也没买，什么都看了。太阳升起时，田野里空空荡荡，只剩一根发光的羽毛。你把它留了下来。\n\n@event sum_end_of_summer\n# en: The Last Week of Summer\ntitle [64767a] = 夏天的最后一周\n# en: The last week of summer has that particular light — golden, heavy, already nostalgic. Your trunk is half-packed. Part of you is desperate to get back. Part of you wants this week to last forever.\ntext [fa47ef] = 夏天的最后一周有一种特别的光——金黄、沉甸甸的，已经带着怀念。你的箱子收拾了一半。一部分的你迫不及待想回去，一部分的你希望这一周永远不要结束。\n# en: A student packing a trunk in golden late-summer light, a half-finished jar of jam, a pressed flower and a new textbook on the bed.\nscene [79a65d] = 一名学生在夏末的金色光线里收拾箱子，床上放着半罐果酱、一朵压花和一本新课本。\n# en: Squeeze every drop out of it\nopt.left.label [660c83] = 榨干它的每一滴\n# en: Late swims, long dinners, one final sunset from the hill. You arrive at the barge sunburnt and happy and only slightly late.\nopt.left.say.1 [3d2155] = 很晚的游泳、很长的晚餐、山顶上最后一次日落。你到达驳船时晒伤了、很开心，只迟到了一点点。\n# en: Get ready for the new year\nopt.right.label [50fe4f] = 为新学年做准备\n# en: You re-read your notes, sharpen your quills and polish your wand. When term starts you are, for once, ahead. It's an unfamiliar feeling.\nopt.right.say.1 [fbc642] = 你重读笔记，削好羽毛笔，擦亮魔杖。开学时你难得地领先了一步。这是一种陌生的感觉。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/expansion_y3.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/expansion_y3.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event y3_career_fair\n# en: The Career Fair\ntitle [a6ba30] = 职业招聘会\n# en: Every guild, order and institution in the kingdom has sent someone to the great hall. They are all very polite. They are all, very clearly, shopping. Where will you leave your name?\ntext [5b87fc] = 王国里每个公会、教团和机构都派了人到大礼堂来。他们都非常客气。他们也都非常明显地在挑人。你要把名字留在哪里？\n# en: The great hall lined with recruiters' booths — a Conclave mage in silver, a librarian behind a wall of books, a circus ringmaster juggling fire.\nscene [7e3280] = 大礼堂里摆满了招聘摊位——一位身着银袍的议会法师、一位坐在书墙后的图书管理员、一位抛着火把的马戏团团长。\n# en: The Conclave of the Seven Towers\nopt.conclave.label [c832a4] = 七塔议会\n# en: Requires Arcana 30. Apprentice archmages.\nopt.conclave.hint [58c200] = 需要奥术 30。大法师学徒。\n# en: Needs Arcana 30.\nopt.conclave.locked [22971b] = 需要奥术 30。\n# en: The Royal Library\nopt.library.label [64f580] = 皇家图书馆\n# en: Requires Lore 30. Keepers of every book ever written.\nopt.library.hint [248afb] = 需要学识 30。世上每一本书的守护者。\n# en: Needs Lore 30.\nopt.library.locked [b354e1] = 需要学识 30。\n# en: The Guild of Alchemists\nopt.guild.label [8a8f99] = 炼金师公会\n# en: Requires Alchemy 28. Potions, profit, explosions.\nopt.guild.hint [30134a] = 需要炼金 28。药水、利润、爆炸。\n# en: Needs Alchemy 28.\nopt.guild.locked [bcd089] = 需要炼金 28。\n# en: The Crown's Office of Unseen Affairs\nopt.unseen.label [a4101a] = 王室隐秘事务署\n# en: Requires Guile 25. They found you, actually.\nopt.unseen.hint [f8b8f6] = 需要诡计 25。其实是他们先找到你的。\n# en: Needs Guile 25.\nopt.unseen.locked [2a708b] = 需要诡计 25。\n# en: The Travelling Circus of Wonders\nopt.circus.label [f3ec0c] = 奇迹巡回马戏团\n# en: Requires Charm 22. Wagons, crowds, illusions.\nopt.circus.hint [8443a1] = 需要魅力 22。马车、人群、幻术。\n# en: Needs Charm 22.\nopt.circus.locked [04b5cb] = 需要魅力 22。\n# en: Stay at Lumenhold as a junior teacher\nopt.teaching.label [27030b] = 留在流明堡当见习教师\n# en: Requires a faculty member who trusts you (Thorne, Maud or the Headmistress at 25+).\nopt.teaching.hint [42c7ab] = 需要一位信任你的教职员（索恩、老莫德或院长，关系 25 以上）。\n# en: Not yet — you'd rather not decide\nopt.none.label [0a96fd] = 还不急——你暂时不想决定\n# en: You collect a tote bag, three pamphlets and a free quill. The future can wait one more month.\nopt.none.say.1 [b7ef72] = 你领了一个帆布袋、三本小册子和一支免费的羽毛笔。未来可以再等一个月。\n\n@event y3_interview\n# en: The Interview\ntitle [7d775f] = 面试\n# en: Three people behind a long table. One takes notes; one smiles too much; one says nothing at all, which is clearly the one who decides. \"Tell us,\" says the smiling one, \"why you.\"\ntext [06648e] = 长桌后坐着三个人。一个做笔记；一个笑得太多；一个一言不发——显然，做决定的就是这一位。“说说看，”爱笑的那位开口，“为什么是你？”\n# en: A small panelled room, a long table, three interviewers and one empty chair facing them.\nscene [05e47c] = 一间镶着护墙板的小房间，一张长桌，三位面试官，对面一把空椅子。\n# en: Let your record speak\nopt.left.label [1ea228] = 让履历说话\n# en: You talk about what you've learned and, more importantly, what you got wrong and fixed. The silent one writes a single word. You suspect it's \"yes\".\nopt.left.say.1 [c8179c] = 你讲你学到了什么，更重要的是，你搞错过什么、又是怎么改正的。沉默的那位只写下一个词。你猜那是“行”。\n# en: You recite your achievements like a shopping list. The silent one looks at the clock. \"We'll be in touch,\" says the smiling one, which means no.\nopt.left.say.2 [450fc5] = 你像念购物清单一样背诵自己的成就。沉默的那位看了一眼钟。“我们会再联系你的，”爱笑的那位说。意思是不行。\n# en: Tell them a story\nopt.right.label [a082be] = 给他们讲个故事\n# en: You tell them about the night the lake froze, or the night you caught a star, or the night you were wrong. The silent one laughs. Out loud.\nopt.right.say.1 [1b6366] = 你讲起湖面结冰的那一夜，或者你接住一颗星星的那一夜，或者你犯错的那一夜。沉默的那位笑了。出声地笑了。\n# en: The story goes on too long and ends in the wrong place. Somewhere in the middle you lost them, and yourself.\nopt.right.say.2 [c75116] = 故事讲得太长，结尾也落错了地方。讲到一半，你就把他们弄丢了，也把自己弄丢了。\n\n@event y3_offer_letter\n# en: The Offer\ntitle [d66d52] = 录用通知\n# en: A heavy envelope with a seal you recognise from the fair. Inside, a single page: an offer. A place. A future with a start date. It needs a signature by the end of the month.\ntext [c26f14] = 一个厚重的信封，封蜡上的纹章你在招聘会上见过。里面只有一页纸：一份录用。一个位置。一个有开始日期的未来。月底前需要签字。\n# en: An envelope with a heavy wax seal on a dormitory desk, unopened, a hand hovering over it.\nscene [fc8326] = 宿舍书桌上一个盖着厚重火漆的信封，还没拆开，一只手悬在上面。\n# en: Sign it today\nopt.left.label [f7ce3c] = 今天就签\n# en: Your signature looks strange on the page — like a grown-up's. You pin the letter above your bed and look at it every night.\nopt.left.say.1 [e65e25] = 你的签名在纸上看起来很陌生——像个大人的签名。你把信钉在床头，每晚都要看一眼。\n# en: Ask them to wait\nopt.right.label [3eda22] = 请他们再等等\n# en: You write back asking for time. They grant it, coolly. The future, it turns out, does not like waiting, but it will.\nopt.right.say.1 [1ecd4d] = 你回信请求多给些时间。对方同意了，语气冷淡。原来未来不喜欢等待——但它会等的。\n\n@event y3_first_year_mentee\n# en: Your First-Year\ntitle [891c46] = 你的一年级生\n# en: Fourth-years are assigned a first-year to mentor. Yours is called Pim, is about the size of a large cat, and has already asked forty-one questions, including \"are the stairs alive\" and \"are YOU alive\".\ntext [0665b1] = 四年级生都会分配到一个一年级生来带。你的那个叫皮姆，个头和一只大猫差不多，已经问了四十一个问题，包括“楼梯是活的吗”和“你是活的吗”。\n# en: A tiny first-year trotting beside a fourth-year across the courtyard, firing questions, clutching an enormous timetable.\nscene [7ece82] = 一个小小的一年级生小跑着跟在四年级生身边穿过庭院，连珠炮似的发问，怀里紧抱着一张巨大的课表。\n# en: Answer every question honestly\nopt.left.label [54cf3c] = 老老实实回答每个问题\n# en: Yes, the stairs are alive. Yes, you are alive. No, the lake is not \"friendly\". Pim takes notes. By spring, Pim is explaining things to other first-years, wrongly but with total confidence.\nopt.left.say.1 [e5db2e] = 是的，楼梯是活的。是的，你是活的。不，湖并不“友好”。皮姆一一记下。到了春天，皮姆已经在给别的一年级生讲解了——讲错了，但无比自信。\n# en: Teach Pim to find answers alone\nopt.right.label [9c42d4] = 教皮姆自己找答案\n# en: You hand Pim a library card and a map. Pim is furious for a week, then unstoppable. The librarian now knows Pim by name. And fears Pim, slightly.\nopt.right.say.1 [515043] = 你递给皮姆一张借书证和一张地图。皮姆气了一个星期，然后就势不可挡了。图书管理员现在叫得出皮姆的名字。而且有一点怕皮姆。\n\n@event y3_last_lanterns\n# en: The Last Lantern Night\ntitle [781522] = 最后一个灯笼夜\n# en: Your last Lantern Night. You stand on the shore with an unlit lantern and a pen and realise you have no idea what to wish for. Three years ago it was obvious. Now it isn't.\ntext [a78390] = 你的最后一个灯笼夜。你站在湖岸上，手里是一盏没点的灯笼和一支笔，忽然发现自己完全不知道该许什么愿。三年前答案再明显不过。现在不是了。\n# en: A lone student on the shore at night holding an unlit lantern, hundreds of others already drifting over the dark lake.\nscene [2d5995] = 夜里，一名学生独自站在岸边，手捧一盏未点亮的灯笼，数百盏灯笼已经飘向漆黑的湖面。\n# en: Wish for the people you'll leave behind\nopt.left.label [af62f4] = 为要留下的人们许愿\n# en: You write all their names. It takes the whole lantern. It rises slowly, heavily, and you watch it until it is just a star among stars.\nopt.left.say.1 [a49d3f] = 你写下了他们所有人的名字，写满了整盏灯笼。它缓缓地、沉甸甸地升起，你一直望着它，直到它变成群星中的一颗。\n# en: Leave it blank\nopt.right.label [54fbdb] = 留白\n# en: You let it go empty. Strangely, it rises higher than all the others. Maybe the lake likes not knowing either.\nopt.right.say.1 [154e37] = 你放走了一盏空灯笼。奇怪的是，它比其他所有灯笼都升得更高。也许湖也喜欢不知道答案。\n\n@event y3_thesis\n# en: The Final Thesis\ntitle [9c05e4] = 毕业论文\n# en: Every fourth-year writes a final thesis. You could write something safe and solid — or you could take on the question nobody has answered in three hundred years: why the academy was built above a lake.\ntext [5dde65] = 每个四年级生都要写毕业论文。你可以写个稳妥扎实的题目——也可以挑战那个三百年来无人解答的问题：学院为什么要建在湖上？\n# en: A desk buried in drafts, a wall of pinned notes connected by string, a single candle and a single exhausted student.\nscene [df58af] = 一张被草稿淹没的书桌，一面钉满笔记、用细绳相连的墙，一支蜡烛，一个筋疲力尽的学生。\n# en: Safe and solid\nopt.left.label [f81e8e] = 稳妥扎实\n# en: Two hundred pages on the thermodynamics of hovering charms. Impeccable. Unremarkable. Your examiners will sleep well.\nopt.left.say.1 [3e85d1] = 两百页论悬浮咒的热力学。无可挑剔，平平无奇。你的考官们会睡得很香。\n# en: The question nobody answered\nopt.right.label [e94f06] = 那个无人解答的问题\n# en: Your thesis is so good that the headmistress asks for the only copy — and then, very politely, does not give it back.\nopt.right.say.1 [124737] = 你的论文好到院长亲自索要唯一的一份——然后，非常客气地，没有还回来。\n# en: You chase the question until it runs out of archive. Your thesis ends mid-sentence, like the founders' ledger. The examiners are not amused.\nopt.right.say.2 [3f229d] = 你追着这个问题一直追到档案的尽头。你的论文在一句话中间戛然而止，就像创始者的账簿。考官们一点也不觉得有趣。\n\n@event y3_portrait\n# en: Sitting for the Portrait\ntitle [a99086] = 坐着画像\n# en: Every graduate's portrait hangs in the long gallery — and every portrait, eventually, starts talking. You have one sitting to decide who will be gossiping on that wall for the next three hundred years.\ntext [d9377f] = 每位毕业生的画像都会挂在长廊里——而每幅画像迟早都会开口说话。你只有一次坐下来的机会，来决定接下来三百年在那面墙上说闲话的会是什么样的人。\n# en: An old painter at an easel in a sunlit studio, a student posing stiffly in robes, half-finished portraits of past graduates watching from the walls.\nscene [2a1426] = 阳光充足的画室里，一位老画家站在画架前，一名身着长袍的学生僵硬地摆着姿势，往届毕业生未完成的画像从墙上注视着他们。\n# en: Pose solemn and dignified\nopt.left.label [f39072] = 庄重得体地摆姿势\n# en: The painter captures you looking wise and distant. Your portrait will be dignified. It will also be dreadfully bored.\nopt.left.say.1 [af471d] = 画家画出了你睿智而疏远的神情。你的画像会很体面。它也会无聊得要命。\n# en: Pose as yourself, mid-laugh\nopt.right.label [e9bd51] = 做你自己，笑到一半\n# en: Your portrait is laughing. Three days after it's hung, it starts telling the other portraits jokes. The gallery has never been louder.\nopt.right.say.1 [c325e4] = 你的画像在笑。挂上去三天后，它开始给其他画像讲笑话。长廊从来没有这么吵过。\n\n@event y3_letter_to_self\n# en: A Letter to Your First-Year Self\ntitle [64655e] = 写给一年级的自己\n# en: The Divination department runs a tradition: write a letter to your first-year self. They swear it will be delivered. Nobody knows how. Some fourth-years swear they received theirs.\ntext [eb06b6] = 占卜系有个传统：给一年级的自己写一封信。他们发誓信一定会送到，没人知道是怎么送的。有些四年级生发誓自己收到过。\n# en: A fourth-year writing by candlelight, a box of keepsakes open beside them: a pressed flower, a ticket stub, a borrowed wand returned.\nscene [61521a] = 一个四年级生在烛光下写信，身旁打开着一个纪念品盒子：一朵压花、一张票根、一根借来又还掉的魔杖。\n# en: Tell them it'll be all right\nopt.left.label [c88d16] = 告诉自己一切都会好的\n# en: You write: \"It's going to be all right. Not easy. All right.\" The next morning the letter is gone, and you remember, very faintly, reading it once on a sky-barge.\nopt.left.say.1 [1f6e42] = 你写道：“会好起来的。不会容易，但会好起来。”第二天早上信不见了，而你隐约记起，自己曾在一艘天空驳船上读过它。\n# en: You try to write something comforting, and it keeps coming out as a list of warnings. You seal it anyway. The next morning it is gone.\nopt.left.say.2 [8efc83] = 你想写些安慰的话，可写出来总像一张警告清单。你还是把信封好了。第二天早上，它不见了。\n# en: Warn them about everything\nopt.right.label [065681] = 把所有事都警告一遍\n# en: You write eleven pages of warnings. The letter is returned unopened with a note in Divination ink: \"Some things must be learned the hard way.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [4ed2e4] = 你写了十一页的警告。信原封未动地退了回来，附着一张用占卜墨水写的字条：“有些事必须吃过苦头才学得会。”\n\n@event y3_maud_seeds\n# en: Seeds for Later\ntitle [6b0198] = 留给以后的种子\n# en: Maud presses a paper packet into your hands. \"Seeds,\" she says. \"From the secret garden. Plant them wherever you end up. They'll grow anywhere someone is a bit kind to them.\" She sniffs. \"Like you did.\"\ntext [59075b] = 莫德把一个纸包塞进你手里。“种子，”她说，“秘密花园里的。不管你最后去了哪儿，都种下去。只要有人对它们好一点，它们在哪儿都能长。”她吸了吸鼻子。“就像你一样。”\n# en: An old woman pressing a small paper packet of seeds into a student's hands at the garden gate, bees drifting in the evening light.\nscene [f66df9] = 花园门口，一位老妇人把一小包种子按进学生手里，暮色中蜜蜂缓缓飞舞。\n# en: Hug her\nopt.left.label [042130] = 拥抱她\n# en: She protests for exactly one second and then hugs back, fierce and small and smelling of rosemary. \"Go on,\" she says. \"Before I get soppy.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [51b153] = 她抗议了整整一秒钟，然后用力回抱你，又小又结实，带着迷迭香的味道。“去吧，”她说，“趁我还没变得肉麻。”\n# en: Promise to come back and visit\nopt.right.label [17c08e] = 答应回来看她\n# en: \"Every summer,\" you promise. \"We'll see,\" she says, but she writes it on her calendar, in ink, which for Maud is a blood oath.\nopt.right.say.1 [96e47e] = “每年夏天都来，”你保证。“走着瞧，”她说，却把这件事写在了日历上——用墨水写的，对莫德来说这等于血誓。\n\n@event y3_last_duel\n# en: One Last Duel\ntitle [15df69] = 最后一场决斗\n# en: Dawn, the empty dueling ring, no crowd. Lyra is waiting. \"No referee,\" she says. \"No points. Just one last duel, for us. So we know.\"\ntext [5b0db9] = 黎明，空荡荡的决斗场，没有观众。莱拉在等你。“没有裁判，”她说，“不计分。就最后一场决斗，为了我们自己。这样我们就知道了。”\n# en: Two duelists bowing in an empty dueling ring at dawn, no crowd, no referee, just the mist and the lake beyond.\nscene [b6c6d5] = 黎明时分，两名决斗者在空无一人的决斗场里互相鞠躬，没有观众，没有裁判，只有薄雾和远处的湖。\n# en: Duel her with everything\nopt.left.label [9ed8a1] = 全力以赴和她决斗\n# en: It lasts eleven minutes. Nobody wins. You both end up flat on your backs, laughing at the sky. \"Now we know,\" she says.\nopt.left.say.1 [e4826f] = 打了十一分钟。没有人赢。最后你们俩都仰面躺在地上，对着天空大笑。“现在我们知道了，”她说。\n# en: She wins, cleanly. Then she helps you up and doesn't let go of your hand for a moment longer than necessary. \"Now we know.\"\nopt.left.say.2 [cc0906] = 她干净利落地赢了。然后她把你拉起来，握着你的手比必要的多停了一会儿。“现在我们知道了。”\n# en: Lower your wand and just talk\nopt.right.label [075cd4] = 放下魔杖，只是聊聊\n# en: You talk until the mist burns off. About fire, and mothers, and what comes after. It turns out you didn't need to duel to know.\nopt.right.say.1 [86c93a] = 你们一直聊到薄雾散尽。聊火焰，聊母亲，聊以后的事。原来不需要决斗也能知道。\n\n@event y3_time_capsule\n# en: The Time Capsule\ntitle [f63962] = 时间胶囊\n# en: Your friends have a battered tin box, a shovel and a plan: bury a time capsule under the old oak, to be opened in twenty years. Everyone puts something in. What will you give?\ntext [950958] = 你的朋友们带来了一个破旧的铁皮盒、一把铲子和一个计划：在老橡树下埋一个时间胶囊，二十年后再打开。每个人都要放点东西进去。你放什么？\n# en: A group of students kneeling around a hole under an old oak, lowering in a battered tin box sealed with wax.\nscene [cd24b8] = 一群学生跪在老橡树下的坑边，把一个用蜡封好的破铁皮盒放进去。\n# en: Something precious\nopt.left.label [3a8593] = 放一件珍贵的东西\n# en: You put in the first thing you ever enchanted. It's ugly and crooked and it still hums. Someone cries, and blames the dirt.\nopt.left.say.1 [1fe988] = 你放进了你施过魔法的第一件东西。它又丑又歪，至今还在嗡嗡作响。有人哭了，然后怪是土进了眼睛。\n# en: Something ridiculous\nopt.right.label [957c3e] = 放一件荒唐的东西\n# en: You put in a single sock, labelled \"EVIDENCE\". In twenty years, nobody will remember why. That's the point.\nopt.right.say.1 [fa92c3] = 你放进了一只袜子，上面贴着标签：“证物”。二十年后，没人会记得为什么。这正是意义所在。\n\n@event y3_speech\n# en: The Graduation Speech\ntitle [ffc856] = 毕业致辞\n# en: The headmistress catches you in the great hall, where chairs are already being set out. \"Each year one graduate speaks for their class,\" she says. \"This year, the faculty would like it to be you.\"\ntext [eebc6f] = 院长在大礼堂里叫住了你，那里已经开始摆椅子了。“每年都由一位毕业生代表全班致辞，”她说，“今年，教职员们希望是你。”\n# en: A headmistress handing a scroll to a student in an empty great hall, rows of chairs set out for a ceremony that hasn't happened yet.\nscene [9ef184] = 空荡荡的大礼堂里，一位院长把一卷纸递给一名学生，一排排椅子已为尚未举行的典礼摆好。\n# en: Accept and write something honest\nopt.left.label [d1eed2] = 接受，写些真心话\n# en: You write about fear, and friends, and the lake, and the things you didn't know you'd miss. When you practise it alone, your voice cracks in the same place every time. You leave it in.\nopt.left.say.1 [35d90b] = 你写恐惧，写朋友，写湖，写那些你从不知道自己会怀念的东西。独自练习时，你的声音每次都在同一个地方哽住。你把它留在了稿子里。\n# en: Decline — someone else deserves it\nopt.right.label [121bee] = 婉拒——值得的另有其人\n# en: \"Interesting,\" says the headmistress, which from her is a medal. The speech goes to a nervous first-generation student who absolutely brings the house down.\nopt.right.say.1 [9ddc3d] = “有意思，”院长说——从她嘴里说出来，这就是一枚勋章。致辞的机会给了一位紧张的、家里第一代上学的学生，那位学生把全场都点燃了。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/fixtures.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/fixtures.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event amaranth_intro\n# en: No Excuses\ntitle [8daa28] = 不许找借口\n# en: You came in for a plaster. Sister Amaranth takes your hand, turns it to the light and asks how it happened. You tell her. She waits. You tell her the true version. \"Better,\" she says, and reaches for a bottle that is not a plaster.\ntext [2645db] = 你本来只是来要块膏药。阿玛兰斯修女拉过你的手，对着光转了转，问你怎么弄的。你告诉了她。她等着。你又把真实的经过讲了一遍。“这还差不多，”她说着，伸手去拿一个瓶子——不是膏药。\n# en: A stern healer in a white apron holding a student's wrist up to the window light, a row of tonic bottles behind her.\nscene [fc4802] = 一位系着白围裙的严厉治疗师，把一名学生的手腕举到窗前的光里，身后摆着一排补剂瓶。\n# en: Drink whatever it is\nopt.left.label [6cbe5e] = 不管是什么，喝了\n# en: It tastes of iron and regret. The hand stops throbbing before you reach the door. \"Come back if it swells,\" she calls. \"Don't come back if it doesn't.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [9fbd34] = 一股铁锈和后悔的味道。还没走到门口，手就不疼了。“肿了再来，”她在身后喊，“不肿就别来。”\n# en: Just the plaster, thanks\nopt.right.label [1353de] = 给块膏药就行，谢谢\n# en: She gives you the plaster, and a look you will remember the next time you lie to a healer. \"Three crowns,\" she says. \"The look is free.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [606886] = 她给了你膏药，外加一个眼神——下次你再想对治疗师撒谎时，一定会想起它。“三克朗，”她说，“眼神免费。”\n\n@event amaranth_rounds\n# en: Evening Rounds\ntitle [ee9b30] = 晚间查房\n# en: {Sister Amaranth catches you in the corridor and hands you a tray of tonics without a word of explanation.|Amaranth holds out the tonic tray as you pass. It seems you are on the rota now.|The tray is waiting for you on the ward table, labelled YOURS in Amaranth's square hand.} {~Six beds tonight.|A first-year with hiccups that light up.|Someone has charmed their own eyebrows off.}\ntext [a4df7d] = {阿玛兰斯修女在走廊里拦住你，一言不发地把一盘补剂塞到你手里。|你路过时，阿玛兰斯把补剂托盘递了过来。看来你已经上了值班表。|托盘在病房桌上等着你，上面贴着阿玛兰斯方方正正的字：“你的”。} {~今晚六张床。|一个一年级生，打嗝会发光。|有人把自己的眉毛给咒没了。}\n# en: A healer and a student walking a long infirmary ward at dusk, the student carrying a tray of small glass bottles, lamps being lit along the beds.\nscene [9f326b] = 黄昏时分，一位治疗师和一名学生走在长长的病房里，学生端着一盘小玻璃瓶，病床边的灯一盏盏点亮。\n# en: Do the rounds with her\nopt.left.label [640527] = 跟她一起查房\n# en: {~She tells you what each tonic does and why it tastes like that. You remember all of it, to your surprise.|You learn which patients are brave and which are loud, and that they are rarely the same ones.|At the last bed she lets you measure the dose. \"Again,\" she says. You do it again. \"Fine.\"}\nopt.left.say.1 [e26c4d] = {~她告诉你每种补剂的用处，以及为什么是这个味道。出乎你自己意料，你全记住了。|你弄明白了哪些病人勇敢、哪些病人吵闹，而这两种人很少是同一批。|到最后一张床，她让你量剂量。“再来，”她说。你又量了一遍。“行。”}\n# en: You have an essay due\nopt.right.label [427f10] = 你还有篇论文要交\n# en: {~\"So does everyone,\" she says, and takes the tray back. \"Off you go.\"|She nods, which is somehow worse than a lecture.|\"Next time,\" she says, as a fact, not a question.}\nopt.right.say.1 [dadce6] = {~“谁不是呢，”她说着把托盘拿了回去，“走吧。”|她点点头，这比训你一顿还难受。|“下次，”她说——是陈述，不是询问。}\n# en: Mend the worst one yourself\nopt.up.label [0b5686] = 亲手治好最重的那个\n# en: {~You close a split knuckle with a whisper of light. Amaranth inspects it for a long time. \"Neat,\" she says, the highest word she owns.|The sprain knits under your hands. The patient thanks Amaranth. Amaranth points at you.}\nopt.up.say.1 [18df77] = {~你轻声一念，一道微光合上了裂开的指节。阿玛兰斯端详了很久。“利落，”她说——这是她嘴里最高的评价。|扭伤在你手下愈合。病人向阿玛兰斯道谢，阿玛兰斯指了指你。}\n\n@event amaranth_orders\n# en: Doctor's Orders\ntitle [abe905] = 医嘱\n# en: {Sister Amaranth has come to find you, which has never happened to anyone. \"You look like a draught of bad decisions,\" she says. \"Bed.\"|Amaranth again, at your door, arms folded. \"I can hear you coughing from the infirmary.\"|\"Bed,\" says Amaranth, before you've opened the door all the way.}\ntext [321559] = {阿玛兰斯修女竟然主动来找你了，这种事从没发生在任何人身上。“你看起来像一剂馊主意，”她说，“上床。”|又是阿玛兰斯，抱着胳膊站在你门口。“我在医务室都听得见你咳嗽。”|门还没完全打开，阿玛兰斯就说：“上床。”}\n# en: A healer standing in a dormitory doorway with her arms folded, a pale student halfway out of bed with a stack of books.\nscene [a0e5b7] = 一位治疗师抱着胳膊站在宿舍门口，一名脸色苍白的学生正抱着一摞书，半个身子下了床。\n# en: Go to bed as ordered\nopt.left.label [c01d90] = 遵医嘱，上床\n# en: {~You sleep fourteen hours. She checks on you twice and leaves a tonic on the sill both times.|Two days of broth and boredom. You come out of it a person again.|You argue for one more chapter. You lose, and sleep.}\nopt.left.say.1 [f4d326] = {~你睡了十四个小时。她来看了你两次，两次都在窗台上留了一瓶补剂。|两天的肉汤和无聊。熬过去，你又像个人了。|你争取再读一章。你输了，睡觉。}\n# en: \"I'm fine, honestly.\"\nopt.right.label [9142ac] = “我没事，真的。”\n# en: {~\"Nobody who says 'honestly' is,\" she says, and leaves. You are not fine by Thursday.|She writes something on a card and walks off. It turns out to be your name, on her list.}\nopt.right.say.1 [6a3989] = {~“说‘真的’的人都有事，”她说完就走了。到了周四，你果然有事。|她在一张卡片上写了点什么就走了。后来发现那是你的名字，写在她的名单上。}\n\n@event amaranth_trust\n# en: The Cupboard Key\ntitle [11b16f] = 柜子钥匙\n# en: Sister Amaranth closes the storeroom door behind you both. \"I'm away for the solstice and the junior healer faints at blood,\" she says. \"You don't.\" She holds out the key to the tonic cupboard, and with it a page of her own notes on the mending charm.\ntext [8d3c0e] = 阿玛兰斯修女随手关上储藏室的门。“冬至那几天我不在，见习治疗师一见血就晕，”她说，“你不会。”她递来补剂柜的钥匙，还有一页她自己写的愈合咒笔记。\n# en: An infirmary storeroom at night lit by a single lamp, a healer pressing a small brass key into a student's palm, shelves of labelled bottles all around.\nscene [f064f3] = 夜里的医务室储藏室，只点着一盏灯，一位治疗师把一把小铜钥匙按进学生掌心，四周的架子上全是贴好标签的瓶子。\n# en: Take the key and the notes\nopt.left.label [bdfda9] = 收下钥匙和笔记\n# en: \"Mend what you can, send the rest to me, and lie to nobody,\" she says. The notes are written for someone she expects to be good at this. You read them twice and try very hard to be.\nopt.left.say.1 [f5ce15] = “能治的治，治不了的送来给我，对谁都别撒谎，”她说。那页笔记是写给一个她认定会擅长此道的人的。你读了两遍，拼命想成为那样的人。\n# en: \"I'm not ready for that.\"\nopt.right.label [5088da] = “我还没准备好。”\n# en: She looks at you for a long moment. \"Good answer. Wrong, but good.\" She keeps the key. She gives you the notes anyway.\nopt.right.say.1 [a29e05] = 她看了你好一会儿。“好回答。错了，但是好回答。”钥匙她收回去了，笔记还是给了你。\n\n@event brask_shovel\n# en: Hands Wanted\ntitle [82086c] = 缺人手\n# en: {Professor Brask holds out a shovel as you pass the stables. She doesn't say anything. The hippogriff behind her does, loudly.|Brask hands you the shovel before you're through the door.|\"Ah,\" says Brask, and the shovel is already in your hands.} {~The griffon chick is in a mood.|The owls have opinions about the new straw.|A hippogriff has eaten a glove. Possibly a professor's.}\ntext [20789a] = {你路过马厩时，布拉斯克教授递过来一把铲子。她一句话没说。她身后的鹰头马身有翼兽倒是说了，而且很大声。|你还没进门，布拉斯克就把铲子递了过来。|“啊，”布拉斯克说，铲子已经在你手里了。} {~狮鹫幼崽在闹脾气。|猫头鹰们对新铺的稻草颇有意见。|一只鹰头马身有翼兽吃了一只手套，可能是某位教授的。}\n# en: A weathered professor in a leather apron holding out a shovel to a student at a stable door, a hippogriff watching suspiciously from its stall.\nscene [b7d66c] = 一位饱经风霜、系着皮围裙的教授在马厩门口把铲子递给一名学生，一只鹰头马身有翼兽在隔栏里狐疑地看着。\n# en: Muck out the stalls\nopt.left.label [6ef185] = 清理马厩\n# en: {~An hour of straw and silence. At the end Brask says, \"The grey one likes you,\" which is more than she has said to anyone this term.|She tells you a story about a manticore, a bucket and a bishop. It ends with a bite and a moral. You miss the moral.|The hippogriff bows to you on your way out. Brask pretends not to see.}\nopt.left.say.1 [bc4a33] = {~一个小时的稻草和沉默。最后布拉斯克说：“那只灰的喜欢你。”这比她这学期对任何人说的都多。|她给你讲了一个关于蝎尾狮、一只水桶和一位主教的故事。结尾是被咬了一口和一个道理。道理你没听清。|你走时，那只鹰头马身有翼兽向你鞠了一躬。布拉斯克假装没看见。}\n# en: Point out you're not in her class\nopt.right.label [956311] = 提醒她你不上她的课\n# en: {~\"Nobody is,\" she says, \"until they are.\" She takes the shovel back.|\"Neither is the hippogriff,\" says Brask. \"It helps anyway.\"}\nopt.right.say.1 [9a4664] = {~“谁都不上，”她说，“直到上了为止。”她把铲子拿了回去。|“那只鹰头马身有翼兽也不上，”布拉斯克说，“可它照样帮忙。”}\n# en: Ask the stalls what they need\nopt.up.label [30eec0] = 问问它们需要什么\n# en: {~The hippogriffs want the window open and the owls want it shut. You negotiate. Brask watches you with her arms folded and something almost like a smile.|The griffon chick tells you, at length, about a grievance. You pass it on. Brask nods. \"She's right, actually.\"}\nopt.up.say.1 [7b112d] = {~鹰头马身有翼兽们要开窗，猫头鹰们要关窗。你从中斡旋。布拉斯克抱着胳膊看着你，脸上几乎有点笑意。|狮鹫幼崽向你絮絮叨叨地诉了一大通委屈。你转告了。布拉斯克点点头：“其实它说得对。”}\n\n@event brask_night_owl\n# en: The Sick Owl\ntitle [a56a50] = 生病的猫头鹰\n# en: Brask finds you at supper and sits down without asking, which is a first. \"The old barn owl won't eat,\" she says. \"Somebody should sit with her tonight. Somebody quiet.\" She is looking at you.\ntext [f084a8] = 布拉斯克在晚饭时找到你，招呼也不打就坐下了，这还是头一回。“那只老仓鸮不肯吃东西，”她说，“今晚得有人陪着她。得是个安静的人。”她看着你。\n# en: A dim stable loft at night, a student sitting cross-legged beside a straw nest where a sick barn owl sleeps under a blanket, a lantern turned low.\nscene [939266] = 夜里昏暗的马厩阁楼，一名学生盘腿坐在草窝旁，一只生病的仓鸮盖着毯子睡着，提灯拧得很暗。\n# en: Sit up with the owl\nopt.left.label [2fd221] = 守着猫头鹰熬夜\n# en: You sit in the loft till dawn, dripping water onto the owl's beak. At four she takes a mouse from your fingers. In the morning Brask looks at the empty dish for a long time, then claps you once on the shoulder, hard.\nopt.left.say.1 [bae058] = 你在阁楼里坐到天亮，一滴一滴往猫头鹰喙里喂水。凌晨四点，她从你指间叼走了一只老鼠。早上，布拉斯克盯着空碗看了很久，然后在你肩上狠狠拍了一下。\n# en: Send her to Sister Amaranth\nopt.right.label [53c847] = 让她去找阿玛兰斯修女\n# en: Amaranth looks at the owl, looks at you, and says she doesn't do owls. Then she sits with it all night. Brask is not sure how to feel about this, and neither are you.\nopt.right.say.1 [4f82b4] = 阿玛兰斯看看猫头鹰，又看看你，说她不治猫头鹰。然后她陪了它一整夜。布拉斯克不知该作何感想，你也一样。\n\n@event brask_trust\n# en: Old Courtesies\ntitle [39af84] = 古老的礼节\n# en: Brask takes you to the edge of the Whisperwood at dusk. \"Most people talk at animals,\" she says. \"A few talk to them. Fewer listen.\" She bows, very low, to something in the trees. \"Do as I do. I'll teach you the rest.\"\ntext [1c1df6] = 黄昏时，布拉斯克带你到低语森林边。“大多数人对动物说话，”她说，“少数人和它们说话。更少的人肯听。”她朝树林里的什么东西深深鞠了一躬。“照我做。剩下的我来教。”\n# en: A professor and a student at the edge of a forest at dusk, both bowing low to a great antlered stag half-hidden in the trees.\nscene [5eaec1] = 黄昏的森林边缘，一位教授和一名学生向半隐在树间的一头巨大雄鹿深深鞠躬。\n# en: Bow, and learn\nopt.left.label [80ee0e] = 鞠躬，然后学\n# en: It takes until full dark, and something huge breathes on your neck for most of it. Then, faintly, you understand a word. It is \"rude\". Brask laughs once, loudly, the way she does once a term.\nopt.left.say.1 [f664c3] = 一直学到天全黑，大部分时间都有个庞然大物在你脖子后面喘气。然后，隐约地，你听懂了一个词：“没礼貌”。布拉斯克大笑了一声——就是她一学期一次的那种。\n# en: Keep your distance\nopt.right.label [0141a6] = 保持距离\n# en: You stay by the path and watch. She doesn't press you. On the walk back she talks about hoof care for an hour, which you suspect is a kindness.\nopt.right.say.1 [2e5c6a] = 你站在小路边看着。她没有勉强你。回去的路上她讲了一个小时的蹄子护理，你怀疑这是一种体贴。\n\n@event vey_pop_quiz\n# en: Is It There?\ntitle [5bb9f5] = 它在那儿吗？\n# en: {Professor Vey is in the corridor with two cups of tea. She offers you one. \"Only one of them is real,\" she says pleasantly.|Vey stops you on the stairs. \"There's a coin behind your ear. Or there isn't.\"|\"Good morning,\" says Vey, and the door you were about to open is, possibly, a wall.} {~She looks delighted already.|A student behind you walks into something.|She is humming.}\ntext [e5a7b4] = {维伊教授端着两杯茶站在走廊里，递给你一杯。“只有一杯是真的，”她和颜悦色地说。|维伊在楼梯上拦住你。“你耳朵后面有枚硬币。也可能没有。”|“早上好，”维伊说，而你正要推开的那扇门，可能是一堵墙。} {~她看上去已经很高兴了。|你身后有个学生撞上了什么东西。|她在哼歌。}\n# en: A smiling professor in a violet scarf leaning against a corridor wall, a cup of tea in her hand, a second, slightly too perfect cup on the windowsill beside her.\nscene [ad6db0] = 一位系着紫罗兰色围巾的教授笑着倚在走廊墙边，手里端着一杯茶，旁边窗台上还有一杯，完美得有点过分。\n# en: Look closely\nopt.left.label [66d2dd] = 仔细看\n# en: {~You pick the right one. Vey raises her cup to you. \"Insufferable,\" she says, beaming.|You catch the shimmer at the edge. \"Ah,\" says Vey. \"You've been paying attention. Dreadful habit. Keep it.\"}\nopt.left.say.1 [34e5b5] = {~你挑对了。维伊朝你举了举杯。“讨厌鬼，”她笑逐颜开地说。|你捕捉到了边缘那一丝微光。“啊，”维伊说，“你一直在留心。坏习惯。保持住。”}\n# en: {~You reach for the tea and close your fingers on air. Vey enjoys this very much.|Wrong. The real one was in her hand the whole time. \"It usually is,\" she says.}\nopt.left.say.2 [a7ce9a] = {~你伸手去拿茶，手指却合在了空气上。维伊对此相当享受。|错了。真的那杯一直在她手里。“通常都是，”她说。}\n# en: Play along and get it wrong\nopt.right.label [e10069] = 配合她，故意猜错\n# en: {~You reach for the false one on purpose, with feeling. Vey narrows her eyes. \"Either you're bad at this, or very good,\" she says. \"I'll find out.\"|You let her win. She knows. She lets you know she knows.}\nopt.right.say.1 [e45298] = {~你故意、而且声情并茂地去拿那杯假的。维伊眯起眼睛。“要么你很不擅长这个，要么你非常擅长，”她说，“我会弄清楚的。”|你让她赢了。她知道。她也让你知道她知道。}\n\n@event vey_revue\n# en: The Faculty Revue\ntitle [22db23] = 教职员汇演\n# en: Professor Vey is producing the faculty revue, in which professors make fools of themselves for charity. \"I need someone to play a king who isn't there,\" she says. \"You'd be wonderful. You'll be terrible. Either is fine.\"\ntext [551685] = 维伊教授在排演教职员汇演——教授们为慈善出丑的那种。“我需要一个人演一位并不在场的国王，”她说，“你会很出色。你会演砸。哪样都行。”\n# en: A small stage in the Great Hall hung with violet curtains, a professor directing a nervous student holding a paper crown, chairs being set out below.\nscene [44137b] = 大礼堂里一座挂着紫罗兰色幕布的小舞台，一位教授在指导一名拿着纸王冠的紧张学生，台下正在摆椅子。\n# en: Take the part\nopt.left.label [e6818e] = 接下这个角色\n# en: You are a king who isn't there, magnificently. Thorne, in the front row, says \"Hm\" twice, which the faculty agree is a standing ovation.\nopt.left.say.1 [d507d2] = 你把一位不在场的国王演得气势恢宏。前排的索恩说了两声“嗯”，教职员们一致认为这相当于起立鼓掌。\n# en: You forget your lines, invent new ones and are, by general agreement, the best thing in it for the wrong reasons. Vey keeps the paper crown on her desk.\nopt.left.say.2 [cd7f8a] = 你忘了台词，现编了新的，结果大家一致认为你是整场最精彩的部分——只是原因不太对。维伊把那顶纸王冠留在了她的书桌上。\n# en: Hide in the wings\nopt.right.label [f0270b] = 躲到侧幕\n# en: You run the curtain. The revue goes ahead without a king, which Vey says was the point all along.\nopt.right.say.1 [d2705a] = 你负责拉幕。汇演在没有国王的情况下照常进行，维伊说这正是重点所在。\n\n@event vey_trust\n# en: The Mirror Trick\ntitle [2b6124] = 镜像戏法\n# en: Vey keeps you after class and tilts a tall mirror towards you. Your reflection steps out of it and sits on a desk. \"The trick isn't making a copy,\" she says. \"It's knowing which of you to send. Want to learn it?\"\ntext [a3e14c] = 维伊课后留下你，把一面高高的镜子朝你倾过来。你的倒影从镜子里走出来，坐到了一张桌子上。“诀窍不在于造一个复制品，”她说，“而在于知道该派哪个你出去。想学吗？”\n# en: An empty classroom at twilight, two identical students standing either side of a tall mirror, a professor seated on a desk between them, clapping softly.\nscene [30e560] = 黄昏时空荡荡的教室，一面高大的镜子两侧站着两个一模一样的学生，一位教授坐在中间的桌子上轻轻鼓掌。\n# en: Learn it\nopt.left.label [267092] = 学\n# en: It takes a week. On the last night your reflection refuses to go back. Vey talks it round over tea, then says, \"Now you know why I don't do this in public.\" You are not sure which of you walked home.\nopt.left.say.1 [9e2416] = 学了一周。最后一晚，你的倒影不肯回去了。维伊边喝茶边把它劝了回去，然后说：“现在你知道我为什么不在人前用这个了。”走回去的是你们中的哪一个，你也说不准。\n# en: Keep your own face, thanks\nopt.right.label [78e875] = 谢了，我还是用自己的脸\n# en: \"Sensible,\" she says, disappointed. She teaches you instead how to tell when someone else is using it. It turns out a lot of people are.\nopt.right.say.1 [28577d] = “明智，”她说，有点失望。她转而教你怎么识破别人在用这招。结果发现，用的人还真不少。\n\n@event marisol_intro\n# en: A Dry Patch of Air\ntitle [7b3a88] = 一小片干燥的空气\n# en: It is raining on the whole lake except for one circle on the boathouse steps, where a woman in a soaked hat is reading poetry, perfectly dry. She looks up. \"It's not hard,\" says Professor Marisol. \"You just ask the water to go round. Sit, if you like.\"\ntext [86e87f] = 整片湖都在下雨，只有船屋台阶上的一个圆圈例外：一个戴着湿透帽子的女人在那里读诗，浑身干爽。她抬起头。“不难，”玛丽索尔教授说，“只要请水绕开就行。想坐的话就坐吧。”\n# en: Heavy rain on a lake shore, a woman reading on the boathouse steps beneath a perfectly dry circle of air, raindrops sliding around it as if around glass.\nscene [765fe2] = 湖岸大雨，一个女人坐在船屋台阶上读书，头顶是一圈完全干燥的空气，雨滴像绕过玻璃一样从四周滑落。\n# en: Sit and ask how\nopt.left.label [9059dd] = 坐下，问她怎么做到的\n# en: She explains it as a conversation. The water, apparently, is willing. By the end of the poem the rain is going round you too, for almost a minute. \"There,\" she says, and goes back to the poem.\nopt.left.say.1 [c4fcbb] = 她把这解释成一场对话。水显然是愿意的。一首诗读完时，雨也绕着你走了，差不多一分钟。“好了，”她说，接着读她的诗。\n# en: Leave her to her book\nopt.right.label [1f57ac] = 别打扰她读书\n# en: You nod and run for cover. Behind you she reads aloud, to nobody, a poem about a storm that comes back every year to see if you've changed.\nopt.right.say.1 [b3911a] = 你点点头，跑去躲雨。身后，她对着空无一人的湖岸朗读一首诗，写的是一场每年都回来看看你变了没有的暴风雨。\n\n@event marisol_cold_water\n# en: Cold Water Is Good for You\ntitle [c84271] = 冷水对你有好处\n# en: {Professor Marisol is in the lake at dawn, waist-deep, and waves at you as though this were normal.|Marisol again, in the lake. \"It's lovely once you're in,\" she calls. It is not lovely.|\"You came!\" calls Marisol from the water. You had been walking past.} {~There is ice at the edges.|A heron is watching her with professional respect.|She has a book in one hand, held perfectly dry.}\ntext [2b82e3] = {黎明时，玛丽索尔教授站在齐腰深的湖里，朝你挥手，仿佛这再正常不过。|又是玛丽索尔，又在湖里。“下来就舒服了！”她喊。一点也不舒服。|“你来啦！”玛丽索尔在水里喊。你本来只是路过。} {~湖边结了冰。|一只苍鹭正以同行的敬意看着她。|她一只手举着书，书一点没湿。}\n# en: Dawn mist on a still lake, a professor standing waist-deep in the water waving cheerfully to a shivering student on the jetty.\nscene [2bea30] = 黎明的薄雾笼罩着平静的湖面，一位教授站在齐腰深的水里，朝码头上冻得发抖的学生快活地挥手。\n# en: Wade in\nopt.left.label [f7f4b9] = 蹚下水\n# en: {~She teaches you to feel the lake breathing. You cannot feel your feet. Both are, she says, normal.|You ask the water to rise and it rises, a little, around your knees. Marisol whoops like a child.|By the time you climb out you've stopped shivering, which she says is a good sign and Amaranth will say is a bad one.}\nopt.left.say.1 [343a37] = {~她教你感受湖的呼吸。你感受不到自己的脚。她说两者都很正常。|你请水升起来，它真的在你膝边升起了一点。玛丽索尔像个孩子一样欢呼。|等你爬上岸，你已经不发抖了。她说这是好兆头，阿玛兰斯会说这是坏兆头。}\n# en: Wave back from the jetty\nopt.right.label [c55e26] = 在码头上朝她挥手\n# en: {~She waves again, unoffended, and goes under.|\"Coward,\" she calls cheerfully. You are fine with that.}\nopt.right.say.1 [1ad33e] = {~她毫不介意地又挥了挥手，沉了下去。|“胆小鬼，”她快活地喊。你觉得这样挺好。}\n# en: Raise a wave to say hello\nopt.up.label [4bb1cd] = 掀个浪打声招呼\n# en: {~A small, neat wave rolls across the lake and taps her on the shoulder. She laughs so hard she sinks.|Your wave arrives slightly late and very large. Marisol rides it in to shore, delighted.}\nopt.up.say.1 [174830] = {~一道小小的、整齐的浪越过湖面，拍了拍她的肩。她笑得沉了下去。|你的浪来得有点晚，也有点大。玛丽索尔乘着它冲上了岸，高兴极了。}\n\n@event marisol_storm\n# en: Weather\ntitle [eab633] = 天气\n# en: There's a knock at your window during the worst storm of the year. Professor Marisol is outside, hair everywhere, grinning. \"Nobody learns tides on a calm day,\" she shouts over the wind. \"Coming?\"\ntext [837719] = 一年里最猛的暴风雨中，有人敲你的窗。玛丽索尔教授站在外面，头发乱飞，满脸笑容。“风平浪静的日子里谁也学不会潮汐，”她在风里大喊，“来不来？”\n# en: A wild storm over a dark lake, a professor and a student standing on a rocky point with their arms raised, lightning splitting the sky behind them.\nscene [69a9a9] = 漆黑湖面上的狂暴风雨，一位教授和一名学生站在岩石岬角上高举双臂，身后闪电劈开天空。\n# en: Go out into it\nopt.left.label [d9d690] = 冲进风雨里\n# en: On the point, in the roar, you hold back one wave for three whole seconds. Marisol screams with joy. You go home soaked to the bone and lit up like a lamp.\nopt.left.say.1 [b3da39] = 在岬角上、在轰鸣声中，你把一道浪整整挡住了三秒。玛丽索尔开心得尖叫。你浑身湿透地回家，却像一盏点亮的灯。\n# en: The lake throws you on your back. Marisol hauls you out by the collar, laughing and apologising in equal parts, and walks you all the way to the infirmary.\nopt.left.say.2 [b1eb0b] = 湖水把你掀翻在地。玛丽索尔揪着你的领子把你拖出来，一半在笑一半在道歉，一路把你送进了医务室。\n# en: Close the window\nopt.right.label [486e14] = 关上窗\n# en: She shrugs, cheerful, and runs off into the rain. You lie awake listening to the lake and wondering what you missed.\nopt.right.say.1 [457fde] = 她耸耸肩，毫不在意地跑进雨里。你躺着听了一夜湖水，想知道自己错过了什么。\n\n@event marisol_trust\n# en: The Last Line\ntitle [66ff3e] = 最后一行\n# en: Marisol presses her old book of water poems into your hands. \"I never remember the last lines,\" she says. \"You will. The tidecalling's in the margins; I wrote it there when I was your age. Read it on the shore. It works better on the shore.\"\ntext [dc1bc8] = 玛丽索尔把她那本旧的咏水诗集塞到你手里。“我总记不住最后一行，”她说，“你会记住的。唤潮术写在页边，是我在你这个年纪时写的。去湖边读。在湖边读效果更好。”\n# en: A quiet lakeside at sunset, a professor handing a student a worn book of poems with a ribbon in it, the water perfectly still before them.\nscene [41e145] = 日落时宁静的湖边，一位教授把一本夹着丝带的旧诗集递给学生，眼前的湖水平静如镜。\n# en: Read it on the shore\nopt.left.label [0950d9] = 去湖边读\n# en: You read until dark, ankle-deep. On the last page the lake answers you, softly, all the way out to the far bank. You remember the last line. You write it in, for her.\nopt.left.say.1 [2dd6f7] = 你站在没过脚踝的水里一直读到天黑。翻到最后一页时，湖水轻轻地回应了你，一直传到对岸。你记住了最后一行。你替她把它写了上去。\n# en: Give it back; it's hers\nopt.right.label [404c00] = 还给她，这是她的\n# en: She looks at the book and then at you. \"Nobody's ever given it back,\" she says, and reads you the poem herself. She forgets the last line. You tell her.\nopt.right.say.1 [fcc45a] = 她看看书，又看看你。“从来没人还回来过，”她说，然后亲自给你读了那首诗。她忘了最后一行。你告诉了她。\n\n@event mabel_intro\n# en: The Way You Open a Door\ntitle [3e82a5] = 你推门的样子\n# en: The bell over the door rings. The woman behind the counter looks up, considers how you came in, and reaches for a tin before you've said a word. \"Homesick,\" says Mabel, \"with a side of exam. Sit by the window, petal.\"\ntext [0c0a87] = 门上的铃铛响了。柜台后的女人抬起头，打量了一下你进门的样子，你还没开口，她已经伸手去拿一罐茶了。“想家，”梅布尔说，“外加一份考试。坐窗边吧，小宝贝。”\n# en: A cosy village tea shop with a bell over the door, a woman behind the counter already reaching for a tin of tea as a student steps in from the cold.\nscene [436b7b] = 温馨的村庄茶馆，门上挂着铃铛，一名学生从寒风中走进来，柜台后的女人已经伸手去拿一罐茶。\n# en: Sit by the window\nopt.left.label [8f510d] = 坐到窗边\n# en: The tea is exactly right and you can't say why. She brings a slice of cake you didn't order and doesn't charge for it. \"First visit,\" she says. \"After that I charge double.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [e7981f] = 茶恰到好处，你说不出为什么。她端来一块你没点的蛋糕，没收钱。“第一次来，”她说，“以后收双倍。”\n# en: \"Just something to go.\"\nopt.right.label [48a21e] = “外带就好。”\n# en: She hands you a paper cup and looks at you over it. \"Come back when you've got time to sit,\" she says. \"You'll know when.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [79a3ee] = 她递给你一个纸杯，从杯沿上方看着你。“等你有空坐下了再来，”她说，“到时候你自己会知道。”\n\n@event mabel_parcel\n# en: A Tin From Mabel\ntitle [89fe3c] = 梅布尔寄来的茶罐\n# en: {A small tin arrives on the barge with your name on it, in Mabel's round hand. The label says \"FOR THE COUGH I HEARD THROUGH THE DOOR.\"|Another tin from Mabel. The label says \"FOR WHATEVER THIS WEEK IS.\"|Mabel's tins have a shelf of their own now. Today's label says only \"YOU KNOW.\"} {~There's a pressed violet under the string.|It smells of honey and woodsmoke.|There's a biscuit taped to the lid.}\ntext [a8dae8] = {驳船送来一个小罐子，上面是梅布尔圆圆的字迹写着你的名字。标签上写着“治那声隔着门我都听见了的咳嗽”。|梅布尔又寄来一罐。标签上写着“治这一周不管是什么的那些事”。|梅布尔的茶罐已经占了你一整层架子。今天的标签只写着“你懂的”。} {~绳子下面压着一朵干紫罗兰。|闻起来有蜂蜜和柴烟的味道。|盖子上用胶带粘着一块饼干。}\n# en: A small tin of tea wrapped in brown paper and string on a dormitory pillow, a handwritten label on top and a pressed flower tucked under the string.\nscene [7e98e5] = 宿舍枕头上放着一小罐用牛皮纸和细绳包好的茶，上面贴着手写标签，绳子下压着一朵干花。\n# en: Pay her next time you're down\nopt.left.label [c4d933] = 下次去时付她钱\n# en: {~You leave the crowns on her counter. She pushes them back. You leave them again. This goes on for some time.|You thank her in person. \"Don't,\" she says. \"Drink it.\" You do. It works.}\nopt.left.say.1 [d29fd6] = {~你把克朗留在她柜台上。她推回来。你又留下。这样来回了好一阵。|你当面谢她。“别谢，”她说，“喝。”你喝了。管用。}\n# en: Send it back with a note\nopt.right.label [7dbd32] = 附张字条寄回去\n# en: {~She sends it back again with a longer note. You give up and drink it.|The tin comes back on the next barge. The label now says \"STUBBORN.\"}\nopt.right.say.1 [71b3b9] = {~她又寄了回来，附上一张更长的字条。你放弃抵抗，喝了。|茶罐搭下一班驳船回来了。标签现在写着“犟”。}\n\n@event mabel_cup\n# en: Your Cup on the Hook\ntitle [8777bb] = 挂钩上你的杯子\n# en: Behind Mabel's counter is a row of hooks with cups on them, each painted with a letter. You've never asked whose they are. Today, while you watch, Mabel hangs a new one with your initial on it. \"Regulars,\" she says, as if that settled it.\ntext [2202c5] = 梅布尔的柜台后面有一排挂钩，挂着杯子，每只都画着一个字母。你从没问过它们是谁的。今天，你看着梅布尔挂上一只新杯子，上面画着你名字的首字母。“常客，”她说，好像这就说明了一切。\n# en: A row of mismatched cups hanging on hooks behind a tea-shop counter, a woman hanging a new one with a student's initial painted on it.\nscene [ea24f5] = 茶馆柜台后挂钩上一排不成套的杯子，一个女人正挂上一只新杯子，上面画着一名学生名字的首字母。\n# en: Ask whose the others are\nopt.left.label [858fea] = 问问其他杯子是谁的\n# en: She tells you, cup by cup: a fisherman, a dead headmaster, a girl who became a duchess and still writes. \"And you,\" she says. Something in your chest unknots.\nopt.left.say.1 [8a1266] = 她一只一只告诉你：一个渔夫，一位已故的校长，一个后来成了公爵夫人、至今仍写信来的姑娘。“还有你，”她说。你胸口有什么东西松开了。\n# en: Say thank you and sit down\nopt.right.label [d9af96] = 道声谢，坐下\n# en: You sit. She brings your tea in your cup. You don't need to say anything else, and she doesn't either.\nopt.right.say.1 [77da72] = 你坐下。她用你的杯子给你端来茶。你不需要再说什么，她也一样。\n\n@event grimsby_intro\n# en: Not That One\ntitle [40767f] = 不是那本\n# en: You ask for a textbook. An elderly man on a ladder says \"Ah!\" with enormous enthusiasm and hands you a history of lighthouses. \"Not what you asked for,\" says Mr Grimsby. \"What you'll need. You'll see.\"\ntext [cb47fb] = 你要一本教科书。梯子上的老人热情洋溢地“啊！”了一声，递给你一本灯塔史。“不是你要的，”格林斯比先生说，“是你需要的。你会明白的。”\n# en: A cramped bookshop with leaning shelves, an elderly bookseller on a ladder holding out a book to a student below, the student holding a list.\nscene [ee1fec] = 拥挤的书店里书架东倒西歪，一位年迈的书商站在梯子上把一本书递给下面的学生，学生手里捏着一张书单。\n# en: Buy the lighthouse book\nopt.left.label [aed25d] = 买下那本灯塔书\n# en: You read it on the barge home. Chapter four has the exact proof your essay was missing. When you go back to say so, he only nods. \"They always come back,\" he says. \"Lighthouses.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [ae1dbc] = 你在回程的驳船上读了它。第四章恰好有你论文缺的那个证明。你回去告诉他，他只是点点头。“它们总会回来的，”他说，“灯塔。”\n# en: Insist on the textbook\nopt.right.label [ffd585] = 坚持要那本教科书\n# en: He finds it after forty minutes, under a cat. He sells it to you sadly, like a doctor handing over a prescription he doesn't believe in.\nopt.right.say.1 [ac8074] = 四十分钟后，他在一只猫身子底下找到了它。他卖给你时满脸遗憾，就像一个医生开出一张他自己都不信的处方。\n\n@event grimsby_parcel\n# en: The Wrong Book, Posted\ntitle [da6a85] = 寄来的错书\n# en: {A parcel arrives from Grimsby's. Inside is a book you didn't order and a note that says \"Thought of you. Pay when convenient. A. G.\"|Another parcel from Mr Grimsby. This one is about eels.|Grimsby's parcels come monthly now, like weather.} {~It smells of rain and pipe smoke.|There's a bus ticket from another century inside the cover.|The note is written on the back of a recipe.}\ntext [88daec] = {格林斯比书店寄来一个包裹。里面是一本你没订的书，附着一张字条，写着“想到了你。方便时再付。A.G.”|格林斯比先生又寄来一个包裹。这本讲的是鳗鱼。|格林斯比的包裹现在每月都来，像天气一样。} {~闻起来有雨水和烟斗的味道。|封面里夹着一张上个世纪的车票。|字条写在一张菜谱的背面。}\n# en: A brown-paper parcel tied with string on a library desk, a student unwrapping an odd old book while a cat watches from the next chair.\nscene [443ef4] = 图书馆书桌上放着一个用牛皮纸和细绳捆着的包裹，一名学生正拆开一本古怪的旧书，邻座的猫在一旁看着。\n# en: Keep it and pay him\nopt.left.label [37a868] = 留下，付钱给他\n# en: {~It's the wrong book, exactly as promised, and somewhere in it is the right page. It always is.|You read it out of politeness and finish it out of something else.}\nopt.left.say.1 [b8bd60] = {~果然是本错书，正如承诺；而书里某处，正是你要的那一页。向来如此。|你出于礼貌开始读，读完却是出于别的原因。}\n# en: Post it back\nopt.right.label [0a6eab] = 寄回去\n# en: {~The postage costs more than the book would have. He sends a cheerful note that says \"Next time, then.\"|He sends it straight back with a second, wronger book. You keep both.}\nopt.right.say.1 [0e2a54] = {~邮费比书还贵。他寄来一张欢快的字条，写着“那就下次吧”。|他立刻把它寄了回来，外加一本更错的书。两本你都留下了。}\n\n@event grimsby_mind_the_shop\n# en: Mind the Shop\ntitle [2eaf46] = 照看书店\n# en: Mr Grimsby has a ladder accident (a mild one; he lands on the poetry) and needs someone to mind the shop for a week. \"You know where everything is,\" he says, which is untrue. \"Better than me,\" he adds, which is.\ntext [355c82] = 格林斯比先生从梯子上摔了下来（不严重，落在了诗歌区），需要有人替他照看书店一周。“你知道东西都在哪儿，”他说，这不是真的。“比我清楚，”他补了一句，这倒是真的。\n# en: A student alone behind the counter of a crowded old bookshop at dusk, a ring of keys on the counter and a customer peering in through the dusty window.\nscene [5a432f] = 黄昏时一名学生独自站在拥挤的旧书店柜台后，柜台上放着一串钥匙，一位顾客透过满是灰尘的橱窗往里张望。\n# en: Mind the shop\nopt.left.label [d31c26] = 照看书店\n# en: You sell eleven books, all of them the wrong ones, and every customer comes back to thank you. Grimsby, on crutches, says you have the gift. He means it as a warning.\nopt.left.say.1 [60e74c] = 你卖出了十一本书，全是错的，而每位顾客都回来道谢。拄着拐杖的格林斯比说你有天分。他是当作警告说的。\n# en: \"I can't, I'm sorry.\"\nopt.right.label [c71ad9] = “我做不到，抱歉。”\n# en: He closes for the week instead, with a sign that says GONE TO LIGHTHOUSE. Nobody knows which one.\nopt.right.say.1 [c09b7c] = 他干脆歇业一周，门上挂了块牌子：“看灯塔去了”。没人知道是哪一座。\n\n@event pell_intro\n# en: A Thousand Drawers\ntitle [e5d6dc] = 一千个抽屉\n# en: You ask for something for a headache. An old man on a ladder stops, counts the drawers under his breath and comes down with exactly one twist of paper. \"Willow and feverfew,\" says Old Pell. \"Three pinches. Not four. Four is for people who don't listen.\"\ntext [b95382] = 你想买点治头痛的药。梯子上的老人停下来，低声数了数抽屉，下来时手里恰好拿着一小包纸。“柳树皮加小白菊，”老佩尔说，“三撮。不是四撮。四撮是给不听话的人的。”\n# en: An old apothecary on a library ladder in front of a wall of tiny labelled drawers, peering down over his spectacles at a student at the counter.\nscene [7dc69c] = 一位老药剂师站在一面贴满标签的小抽屉墙前的梯子上，从眼镜上方俯视着柜台前的学生。\n# en: Ask why not four\nopt.left.label [3eee3f] = 问他为什么不是四撮\n# en: He tells you, at length, with diagrams drawn in the counter dust. You leave with the cure, a headache from the lecture and the dosage for nine other things.\nopt.left.say.1 [82fac2] = 他讲了很久，还在柜台的灰上画了图。你带着药、一场讲座带来的新头痛，以及另外九种东西的剂量离开了。\n# en: Pay and go\nopt.right.label [9ac792] = 付钱走人\n# en: He nods at the door as you leave, as if adding you to a ledger of people who will be back. You are back within a month.\nopt.right.say.1 [99e6c0] = 你走时他朝门口点点头，仿佛把你记进了一本“会回来的人”的账。不到一个月，你果然回来了。\n\n@event pell_count_drops\n# en: Count Them Aloud\ntitle [6a0261] = 大声数出来\n# en: {Old Pell sets a dropper in your hand. \"Forty. Aloud.\" He watches your lips, not the vial.|\"Forty,\" says Pell, and stands behind you. You can hear him counting along under his breath.|Pell has started leaving you the hard tinctures. That is how he says he trusts you.} {~A daughter rolls her eyes at the counter.|The drawers rattle when a cart goes by.|Something in drawer three hundred is humming.}\ntext [12c0ae] = {老佩尔把一支滴管放进你手里。“四十滴。数出声。”他盯着的是你的嘴唇，不是药瓶。|“四十，”佩尔说，然后站到你身后。你能听见他在低声跟着数。|佩尔开始把难配的酊剂留给你了。这就是他表达信任的方式。} {~一个女儿在柜台那边翻了个白眼。|有马车经过时，抽屉们咔嗒作响。|三百号抽屉里有什么东西在嗡嗡响。}\n# en: An old apothecary at a wooden counter holding a glass dropper over a vial, a student beside him counting on their fingers, a wall of tiny labelled drawers behind them.\nscene [6dff31] = 一位老药剂师站在木柜台前，把一支玻璃滴管悬在药瓶上方，身旁的学生掰着手指数数，身后是一整面贴着标签的小抽屉。\n# en: Count, aloud, every drop\nopt.left.label [f6db0f] = 一滴一滴大声数\n# en: {~Forty, exactly. Pell says nothing for a full minute, then \"Again.\" You are fairly sure that was praise.|You lose count at thirty-one and start over without being told. Pell grunts approvingly.}\nopt.left.say.1 [3e78a1] = {~正好四十滴。佩尔整整一分钟没说话，然后说“再来”。你相当确定那是在夸你。|数到三十一你乱了，没等人说就从头再来。佩尔赞许地哼了一声。}\n# en: Eyeball it; it's close enough\nopt.right.label [e3a574] = 目测一下，差不多就行\n# en: {~It's right. Pell checks it three times and is visibly annoyed that it's right.|Close enough is, today, exactly right. \"Luck,\" says Pell. \"Don't.\"}\nopt.right.say.1 [cec3f1] = {~对了。佩尔查了三遍，对它居然是对的这件事明显很恼火。|差不多，今天恰好就是刚好。“运气，”佩尔说，“别指望。”}\n# en: {~Forty-three. Pell pours it out without a word and hands you the dropper again. The silence lasts all shift.|Too many. A daughter fixes it before he sees. He sees.}\nopt.right.say.2 [c2009e] = {~四十三滴。佩尔一言不发地倒掉，把滴管又递给你。这份沉默持续了整个班。|多了。一个女儿赶在他看见前改好了。他还是看见了。}\n\n@event pell_daughters\n# en: Pell & Daughters\ntitle [e047f1] = 佩尔父女\n# en: Pell's daughters want a new sign, brighter labels and a counter that faces the door. Pell wants it to be fifty years ago. All three of them turn to you, the only person in the shop who isn't family. \"Well?\" says Pell.\ntext [5b6af3] = 佩尔的女儿们想要一块新招牌、更醒目的标签，还想把柜台转过来朝着门。佩尔想要五十年前的样子。三个人一齐转向你——铺子里唯一不是家里人的那个。“怎么说？”佩尔问。\n# en: An old apothecary and his two grown daughters arguing across a counter over a new painted shop sign, a student standing awkwardly between them.\nscene [d34c2c] = 一位老药剂师和两个成年的女儿隔着柜台为一块新漆的招牌争吵，一名学生尴尬地站在中间。\n# en: Side with the daughters\nopt.left.label [3b974c] = 站在女儿们一边\n# en: Pell stamps into the back room. A week later the new sign goes up. Underneath it, in small old letters, it still says PELL. He shows you, gruffly, as though you'd asked.\nopt.left.say.1 [fb675a] = 佩尔跺着脚进了里屋。一周后，新招牌挂了上去。招牌下面，用小小的旧字，依然写着“佩尔”。他板着脸指给你看，好像是你问起似的。\n# en: \"The drawers have never been wrong.\"\nopt.right.label [d09cc9] = “抽屉从来没错过。”\n# en: Pell glows. The daughters glare. Pell gives you a raise and the daughters give you all the worst stocktakes. Everyone feels this is fair.\nopt.right.say.1 [770e74] = 佩尔容光焕发。女儿们怒目而视。佩尔给你加了工钱，女儿们把最糟的盘点全派给了你。大家都觉得这很公平。\n\n@event grisk_pleasantries\n# en: Pleasantries\ntitle [3f3d10] = 寒暄\n# en: {Grisk raises a small glass from the corner table of the Gilded Newt. \"My friend,\" he says. \"Do sit. I'm not working.\"|Grisk, at the corner table again. \"How is the purse?\" he asks, with what sounds like real concern.|\"Ah, my favourite non-client,\" purrs Grisk.} {~His ledger stays closed, which feels deliberate.|He is eating a single olive very slowly.|The landlady gives you a warning look.}\ntext [25b72d] = {格里斯克在镀金蝾螈酒馆的角落桌边举起一只小酒杯。“我的朋友，”他说，“请坐。我今天不营业。”|格里斯克又坐在角落那张桌子。“钱袋还好吗？”他问，听起来像是真心关切。|“啊，我最喜欢的非客户，”格里斯克轻声说。} {~他的账簿一直合着，显然是故意的。|他正非常缓慢地吃着一颗橄榄。|老板娘朝你投来警告的一瞥。}\n# en: A goblin in a velvet waistcoat sitting at a corner table in a low-beamed tavern, raising a small glass to a student, a fat ledger at his elbow.\nscene [83bdbd] = 低矮横梁的酒馆角落，一个穿天鹅绒马甲的地精向一名学生举起一只小酒杯，手肘边放着一本厚厚的账簿。\n# en: Sit for one drink\nopt.left.label [b539a6] = 坐下喝一杯\n# en: {~He talks about money the way other people talk about weather, and you learn more in an hour than in a term of Umbral Theory.|He tells you which of the village shops are in trouble and why. It feels like gossip. It is a lesson.|He pays for the drinks and writes nothing down, which is the most unsettling thing he has ever done.}\nopt.left.say.1 [ff7388] = {~他谈起钱来就像别人谈天气，你在一小时里学到的比一学期暗影理论还多。|他告诉你村里哪些铺子遇到了麻烦，以及原因。听着像闲话，其实是一堂课。|他付了酒钱，什么也没记——这是他做过最让人不安的事。}\n# en: Keep walking\nopt.right.label [6ca967] = 继续走\n# en: {~\"Another time,\" he calls after you. \"I'm very patient.\"|He raises the glass anyway. You feel it between your shoulder blades all the way out.}\nopt.right.say.1 [4d4af1] = {~“改天，”他在你身后喊，“我很有耐心。”|他照样举了杯。直到走出门，你都觉得那目光落在你两肩之间。}\n\n@event grisk_tip\n# en: A Tip, Between Friends\ntitle [576ce2] = 朋友之间的消息\n# en: Grisk slides a coin across the table. \"Between friends,\" he murmurs. \"The barge company is about to buy a second barge. The ferryman's cousin will sell his share this week, cheap, because he doesn't know. You could know.\"\ntext [127226] = 格里斯克把一枚硬币推过桌面。“朋友之间，”他低声说，“驳船公司要买第二条驳船了。船夫的表亲这周会贱卖他的股份，因为他不知道。你可以知道。”\n# en: A goblin leaning across a tavern table to murmur into a student's ear, sliding a single gold coin across the wood with one finger.\nscene [95b950] = 一个地精隔着酒馆桌子凑到学生耳边低语，用一根手指把一枚金币推过木桌。\n# en: Buy the share\nopt.left.label [cddc5b] = 买下那份股份\n# en: The second barge arrives. The share triples. Grisk lifts his glass across the Newt. You feel rich and slightly unwell.\nopt.left.say.1 [d08cfb] = 第二条驳船到了。股份翻了三倍。格里斯克隔着蝾螈酒馆朝你举杯。你觉得自己很有钱，又有点不舒服。\n# en: The second barge sinks on its first crossing. Grisk is sympathetic. \"Risk,\" he says. \"Delicious, and sometimes poison.\"\nopt.left.say.2 [086af1] = 第二条驳船第一次渡湖就沉了。格里斯克很同情。“风险，”他说，“美味，有时也是毒药。”\n# en: Leave the cousin his share\nopt.right.label [f20d63] = 把股份留给那位表亲\n# en: Grisk pockets the coin. \"Honest,\" he says, as if diagnosing something. \"It'll pass. Or it won't, and you'll be poor and admired.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [89bb79] = 格里斯克收起硬币。“老实，”他说，像在诊断什么病，“会好的。要么好不了，那你就会又穷又受人敬重。”\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/incidents.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/incidents.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event inc_fever_bill\n# en: A Fever and a Bill\ntitle [a86a8d] = 一场高烧，一张账单\n# en: You wake in the infirmary with a fever that makes the ceiling breathe. Sister Amaranth holds up a small blue bottle. \"This clears it by morning. It is also not free,\" she says. \"Or you can sweat it out, like your ancestors.\"\ntext [9f097a] = 你在医务室醒来，烧得连天花板都像在呼吸。阿玛兰斯修女举起一只蓝色小瓶。“这个能让你明早退烧。它也不是免费的，”她说，“要不然你就像你的祖先那样，硬扛过去。”\n# en: A pale student in an infirmary bed, a stern nurse holding out a small blue bottle and a folded bill.\nscene [b357c5] = 医务室病床上一个脸色苍白的学生，一位严厉的修女递来一只蓝色小瓶和一张折好的账单。\n# en: Buy the tonic\nopt.left.label [424c55] = 买下药剂\n# en: {~It tastes of tin and regret.|It tastes like a pond that has given up.} By morning the ceiling is a ceiling again, and your purse is noticeably lighter.\nopt.left.say.1 [b9ca12] = {~它尝起来像锡和悔恨。|它尝起来像一潭放弃了的池水。}到了早上，天花板又只是天花板了，你的钱袋却明显轻了一截。\n# en: Sweat it out\nopt.right.label [cf0e70] = 硬扛过去\n# en: Three days of damp sheets and strange dreams about stairs. You miss a week of notes, and come back feeling like a wrung-out dishcloth.\nopt.right.say.1 [fe076d] = 三天的湿床单，还有一堆关于楼梯的怪梦。你漏了一整周的笔记，回来时整个人像一块拧干的抹布。\n\n@event inc_broken_wand\n# en: Snapped on the Stairs\ntitle [fbb3ae] = 断在楼梯上\n# en: The moving stairs shift without warning. You catch yourself; your wand doesn't. It lies in two pieces on the landing, still fizzing at the break like a sulking firework.\ntext [e32b26] = 移动楼梯毫无预兆地挪了位置。你稳住了自己，你的魔杖却没有。它断成两截躺在平台上，断口还在嘶嘶冒火花，像一支赌气的烟花。\n# en: A student kneeling on a stone staircase, holding the two halves of a wand that still fizzes faintly at the break.\nscene [8c70b7] = 一个学生跪在石阶上，手里捧着断成两截的魔杖，断口仍微微冒着火花。\n# en: Have it properly rebound\nopt.left.label [97789e] = 找人好好修\n# en: The repairer in the village tuts, splints it with silver wire and charges you for every tut. It works better than before, which is somehow worse.\nopt.left.say.1 [d64a94] = 山下小镇的修理匠啧啧连声，用银丝把它绑好，每啧一声都算了钱。它比以前更好用了，这反倒更让人憋屈。\n# en: Bind it with string and hope\nopt.right.label [1dbf63] = 拿绳子绑上，听天由命\n# en: It holds. It spits a little when you cast, like a cat that forgives you but wants it noted.\nopt.right.say.1 [75ab3f] = 它撑住了。施法时会稍微啐一下，像一只原谅了你、但要你记住这笔账的猫。\n# en: It holds for three days, then sneezes a spell backwards. Your eyebrows grow back within the week; your marks take longer.\nopt.right.say.2 [76926c] = 它撑了三天，然后打了个喷嚏，把一道咒语倒着放了出去。你的眉毛一周内就长回来了，你的成绩可没那么快。\n\n@event inc_library_fine\n# en: The Overdue Notice\ntitle [716b08] = 逾期通知\n# en: A prefect in a red sash slides a notice across your desk. The library book you borrowed has come back late, damp and, somehow, with teeth marks. \"Damages,\" she says. \"Or you can argue. People do. It never goes well.\"\ntext [14a576] = 一位系着红绶带的级长把一张通知推到你桌上。你借的那本图书馆的书逾期归还，湿漉漉的，还不知怎么多了一圈牙印。“赔偿，”她说，“或者你也可以争辩。有人这么干过。从来没好结果。”\n# en: A prefect in a red sash holding out a long paper notice, a book with teeth marks on the desk between them.\nscene [618e44] = 一位系红绶带的级长举着一张长长的纸质通知，两人之间的桌上放着一本带牙印的书。\n# en: Pay the fine\nopt.left.label [4e47fa] = 交罚款\n# en: She stamps your receipt with great satisfaction. The book is reshelved in a cage, which feels like the right outcome for everyone.\nopt.left.say.1 [197c1b] = 她心满意足地在你的收据上盖了章。那本书被重新上架——关进了笼子里，这对大家来说似乎都是最好的结局。\n# en: Argue that the book bit first\nopt.right.label [ab548f] = 坚称是书先咬的\n# en: You produce the bite on your thumb as evidence. The prefect inspects it, then the book, and halves the fine to nothing. \"Fair,\" she says. \"It has a history.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [672cfa] = 你亮出拇指上的咬痕作为证据。级长检查了它，又检查了那本书，把罚款一笔勾销。“说得通，”她说，“它有前科。”\n# en: The prefect writes down every word you say, then doubles the fine for \"contempt of librarian\". You pay it, and a demerit besides.\nopt.right.say.2 [a6947d] = 级长把你说的每个字都记了下来，然后以“藐视图书管理员”为由把罚款翻倍。你交了钱，还顺带吃了一个记过。\n\n@event inc_letter_home\n# en: Bad Weather at Home\ntitle [ae05d8] = 家里遭了风雨\n# en: A letter from home, written in a hurry. A storm took half the roof, and the repairs will cost more than anyone has. Nobody asks you for anything. That's how you know it's bad.\ntext [0fccb4] = 一封家信，字迹潦草。一场暴风雨掀掉了半边屋顶，修缮的花费比谁手头的钱都多。没有人向你开口要任何东西。正因如此，你知道情况很糟。\n# en: A student at a window reading a rain-spotted letter, a small pile of coins on the sill beside them.\nscene [e9924d] = 一个学生站在窗边读一封落了雨点的信，窗台上放着一小堆硬币。\n# en: Send crowns home\nopt.left.label [ca82d0] = 往家里寄克朗\n# en: You send everything you can spare and a little you can't. The reply comes a month later: a drawing of a roof, finished, and a pressed flower.\nopt.left.say.1 [bf78ca] = 你寄出了所有能省下的钱，还多寄了一点省不下的。一个月后回信来了：一张画着修好的屋顶的画，还有一朵压干的花。\n# en: Write back with love, not money\nopt.right.label [f26600] = 回信寄去爱，不寄钱\n# en: You write four pages and keep your purse. The reply is kind. It doesn't mention the roof. That night you lie awake and listen to the rain.\nopt.right.say.1 [75b549] = 你写了四页信，钱袋没动。回信很温柔，只字未提屋顶。那晚你躺着睡不着，听了一夜的雨。\n\n@event inc_stolen_purse\n# en: Market Day Fingers\ntitle [597745] = 集市日的快手\n# en: Somewhere between the cheese stall and the man selling enchanted socks, your purse stops being yours. You catch a grey hood slipping between the stalls, already three stalls ahead.\ntext [2d2565] = 在奶酪摊和那个卖魔法袜子的男人之间的某处，你的钱袋不再属于你了。你瞥见一顶灰色兜帽在摊位间溜走，已经领先你三个摊位。\n# en: A crowded village square on market day, a figure in a grey hood slipping away between the stalls with a small purse.\nscene [50e0d7] = 集市日拥挤的小镇广场上，一个戴灰兜帽的身影拿着一只小钱袋从摊位间溜走。\n# en: Run after the hood\nopt.left.label [3e6b9b] = 追那顶兜帽\n# en: You corner the thief behind the bakery. The purse comes back a little lighter; they'd already spent some on a bun. You let them keep the bun.\nopt.left.say.1 [8a1779] = 你在面包房后面堵住了小偷。钱袋还回来时轻了一点——他们已经拿去买了个面包。你让他们留着那个面包。\n# en: You trip over a goose, then a second goose. When you get up, the hood is gone, and so are your crowns.\nopt.left.say.2 [52dac0] = 你被一只鹅绊倒，接着又是第二只鹅。等你爬起来，兜帽不见了，你的克朗也不见了。\n# en: Let it go\nopt.right.label [7a5d2f] = 算了吧\n# en: You tell yourself it's only money. You tell yourself that all the way back up the hill, and it never quite takes.\nopt.right.say.1 [78bd08] = 你告诉自己，不过是钱而已。你一路走回山上都这么告诉自己，可始终没能真的说服自己。\n\n@event inc_hiccuping_robe\n# en: The Robe That Hiccups\ntitle [2955d6] = 会打嗝的长袍\n# en: A cauldron two benches down boils over, and a whole batch of Draught of Unending Hiccups soaks your best robe. The stain fades by supper. The hiccups don't. Every few minutes, the robe jumps.\ntext [291843] = 隔着两张实验台的一口坩埚沸溢了，一整锅“无尽打嗝药剂”泼透了你最好的长袍。污渍到晚饭时就褪了，打嗝却没停。每隔几分钟，长袍就要跳一下。\n# en: A student in potion-stained robes jolting mid-step as their robe gives a visible hiccup, classmates hiding smiles.\nscene [26b228] = 一个穿着沾满药渍长袍的学生走到一半猛地一颤，长袍明显打了个嗝，同学们憋着笑。\n# en: Buy a new robe\nopt.left.label [ce85a0] = 买件新长袍\n# en: The tailor in the village fits you in an afternoon and charges you for a week. The old robe now hiccups quietly in the bottom of your trunk.\nopt.left.say.1 [0564c8] = 山下小镇的裁缝一个下午就给你量好做好，却收了一周的工钱。旧长袍如今在你箱底悄悄地打着嗝。\n# en: Wear it with dignity\nopt.right.label [1a8893] = 体面地穿着它\n# en: {~It hiccups through a lecture on silence.|It hiccups through the headmistress's announcements.} Someone starts a tally on the common-room wall. You are briefly famous for the wrong reasons.\nopt.right.say.1 [20fdad] = {~它在一堂讲沉默的课上一路打嗝。|它在院长讲话时一路打嗝。}有人在公共休息室的墙上开始记次数。你短暂地出了名，原因却不太对。\n\n@event patron_tournament_purse\n# en: A Patron's Purse\ntitle [21cf49] = 赞助人的奖金袋\n# en: The Spring Tournament committee has a velvet cushion, a very thin prize purse and a list of patrons with a lot of room left on it. Patrons are read out before the first bout, in a loud voice, to the whole academy.\ntext [eebe25] = 春季锦标赛筹委会有一块天鹅绒垫子、一只瘪瘪的奖金袋，还有一份空着大片位置的赞助人名单。第一场比赛前，赞助人的名字会被高声念给全学院听。\n# en: A festival committee table on a lawn, a thin purse on a velvet cushion and a list of patrons with plenty of space left.\nscene [614979] = 草坪上一张节庆筹委会的桌子，天鹅绒垫子上放着一只瘪瘪的钱袋，旁边一份还空着很多位置的赞助人名单。\n# en: Fund the champion's purse\nopt.left.label [151eb5] = 包下冠军奖金\n# en: {~Your name is read out first, and mispronounced only slightly.|Your name rings across the lawn before the first bout. Two first-years bow to you, unsure why.} The champion thanks you from the podium.\nopt.left.say.1 [ce25e1] = {~你的名字第一个被念出来，只念错了一点点。|第一场比赛前，你的名字响彻草坪。两个一年级生朝你鞠躬，也不知为什么。}冠军在领奖台上向你致谢。\n# en: Put in a few crowns\nopt.right.label [eb6938] = 投几枚克朗\n# en: Your name is read out somewhere near the end, between a professor's cat and \"a well-wisher\". It still counts.\nopt.right.say.1 [5b4500] = 你的名字在快结束时被念到，夹在一位教授的猫和“一位好心人”之间。但也算数。\n\n@event patron_ledger_print\n# en: Paper for the Press\ntitle [f32e02] = 印刷机要纸\n# en: Nessa Quill corners you by the library stairs, holding an empty paper tray like evidence. \"Special edition. Biggest story of the term. No paper. You,\" she says, pointing, \"look solvent. Patrons get their name on the masthead.\"\ntext [2bc86d] = 内莎·奎尔在图书馆楼梯边把你堵住，举着一只空纸盘，像举着证物。“特刊。本学期最大新闻。没纸了。你，”她指着你说，“看起来有钱。赞助人能上报头。”\n# en: A cramped office under a staircase, a groaning printing press, and a young editor holding out an empty paper tray.\nscene [a206ca] = 楼梯下一间狭小的办公室，一台呻吟着的印刷机，一位年轻的编辑举着一只空纸盘。\n# en: Pay for the whole print run\nopt.left.label [451de8] = 包下整期印刷\n# en: \"Headline: generous student saves free press,\" Nessa says, already writing it. The masthead lists you in tiny capitals, which she says is the most honour anyone gets.\nopt.left.say.1 [079497] = “头条：慷慨学生拯救自由报业，”内莎说着，已经开始写了。报头上用极小的字印着你的名字，她说这已是任何人能得到的最高荣誉。\n# en: Buy one small advertisement\nopt.right.label [ebad59] = 买一小块广告\n# en: You buy a corner of page four. Nessa writes your copy for you, and somehow it reads like a confession. People quote it at you for a week.\nopt.right.say.1 [933b10] = 你买下第四版的一个角落。内莎替你写了广告词，不知怎么读起来像一份供词。整整一周都有人对着你念它。\n\n@event donate_greenhouse_glass\n# en: The Broken Panes\ntitle [cd7452] = 碎掉的玻璃\n# en: Last night's hail went through the greenhouse glass like a hammer through toffee. A collection tin hangs on the gate with a hand-lettered sign: every pane paid for keeps the mandrakes from catching cold.\ntext [fa105f] = 昨夜的冰雹砸穿温室的玻璃，就像锤子砸进太妃糖。大门上挂着一只募捐罐和一块手写的牌子：每捐一块玻璃，曼德拉草就少一分着凉的危险。\n# en: A collection tin on a garden gate, broken glass glittering in the grass behind it and steam escaping from a damaged greenhouse.\nscene [45c440] = 花园大门上挂着一只募捐罐，后面草地上碎玻璃闪闪发光，一座破损的温室正往外冒着热气。\n# en: Pay for a whole pane\nopt.left.label [14b4c4] = 捐一整块玻璃\n# en: A gardener with mud to the elbows insists on showing you your pane. Inside it is warm, green and loud with opinions. You are welcome, she says, any morning.\nopt.left.say.1 [96b7a3] = 一位泥巴糊到手肘的园丁非要带你去看“你的”那块玻璃。里面温暖、葱绿，满是各种意见。她说，你哪天早上来都欢迎。\n# en: Drop in a coin and walk on\nopt.right.label [6d6a0e] = 投枚硬币就走\n# en: The coin clinks into the tin. Behind the gate, something small and leafy sneezes. You feel, obscurely, that you've been judged.\nopt.right.say.1 [e09171] = 硬币叮当落进罐里。大门后，有个小小的、长着叶子的东西打了个喷嚏。你隐约觉得自己被评判了。\n\n@event donate_duel_floor\n# en: The Scorched Floor\ntitle [47b123] = 烧焦的地板\n# en: A duellist with a bandaged hand rattles a box at the foot of the east stair. \"The hall floor,\" he says. \"Someone set it on fire again. Every board you buy, we carve your initials on the underside. Honour, and splinters.\"\ntext [d8070e] = 一个手上缠着绷带的决斗者在东楼梯脚下摇着一只箱子。“大厅的地板，”他说，“又有人把它点着了。你买一块板，我们就把你的姓名缩写刻在背面。荣誉，外加木刺。”\n# en: A sweaty duellist with a collection box at the foot of a stair, scorched floorboards stacked behind them.\nscene [ea8375] = 一个满头大汗的决斗者在楼梯脚下捧着募捐箱，身后堆着烧焦的地板。\n# en: Buy a row of floorboards\nopt.left.label [a3aa26] = 买下一整排地板\n# en: He drags you upstairs to see. The hall smells of smoke and wax, and the practice dummies glare at you. Your initials go on the underside of the board by the door.\nopt.left.say.1 [58bb9f] = 他拽着你上楼去看。大厅里一股烟和蜡的味道，练习假人们瞪着你。你的姓名缩写刻在了门边那块板的背面。\n# en: Give him a coin for the bandage\nopt.right.label [849d48] = 给他一枚硬币买绷带\n# en: He thanks you with exaggerated courtesy and goes back to rattling. Somewhere above, steel rings on steel, and nobody invites you up.\nopt.right.say.1 [c52343] = 他夸张地向你行礼致谢，又回去接着摇箱子。楼上某处传来钢铁相击的声音，没有人邀请你上去。\n\n@event xchg_arrival\n# en: Guests in Green\ntitle [d9ca04] = 身着绿袍的客人\n# en: Four students in Thornwick green arrive for a term, with trunks and very polite smiles. Within an hour every portrait in the entrance hall is winking at the wrong people. A prefect looks around for someone, anyone, to show them the castle.\ntext [5855c8] = 四个穿着荆木绿袍的学生来这里待一个学期，带着箱子和非常客气的笑容。不到一个小时，门厅里每一幅肖像都在冲错的人眨眼。一位级长四处张望，想找个人——随便谁——带他们参观城堡。\n# en: Four students in green Thornwick robes standing in a grand entrance hall, the portraits on the walls all turned to stare at them.\nscene [4447a8] = 四个穿绿色荆木长袍的学生站在宏伟的门厅里，墙上的肖像全都转过头盯着他们。\n# en: Offer to show them round\nopt.left.label [52352b] = 主动带他们参观\n# en: You give the full tour, moving stairs included. By the end the boy with the sketchbook admits Lumenhold's towers are \"not as crooked as reported\".\nopt.left.say.1 [110d38] = 你带他们走完了全程，移动楼梯也算在内。最后那个拿速写本的男生承认，流明堡的塔“没有传闻中那么歪”。\n# en: Halfway round, they convince you a wall is a door. It isn't. They are very sorry, and laughing too hard to say so.\nopt.left.say.2 [5cd71f] = 走到一半，他们让你相信一面墙是一扇门。它不是。他们非常抱歉，只是笑得太厉害，说不出口。\n# en: Watch them from a distance\nopt.right.label [e05dd0] = 远远地观察他们\n# en: You watch them all week. They watch back. You learn that the quiet one counts doorways, and that they know you're watching.\nopt.right.say.1 [175ece] = 你观察了他们整整一周。他们也观察你。你发现那个安静的人会数门洞，也发现他们知道你在看。\n\n@event xchg_ink_cuffs\n# en: The Ink-Stained Cuffs\ntitle [a6af14] = 沾墨的袖口\n# en: Late in the library, you find the Thornwick girl with the ink-stained cuffs copying from a chained book she shouldn't have reached. She sees you see her. \"It's only history,\" she says. \"Unless you want it to be a scandal.\"\ntext [2833d3] = 深夜的图书馆里，你撞见那个袖口沾着墨迹的荆木女生正在抄一本她本不该够得着的锁链书。她看见你看见了她。“只是历史而已，”她说，“除非你想让它变成丑闻。”\n# en: A library at dusk, a student in green robes with ink-stained cuffs copying from a book chained to a desk.\nscene [00e6b0] = 黄昏的图书馆，一个袖口沾墨的绿袍学生正从一本锁在桌上的书里抄写。\n# en: Report her to the librarian\nopt.left.label [c73db6] = 向管理员举报她\n# en: The librarian thanks you, coldly. The Thornwick girl is confined to the guest wing for a week. She nods to you at dinner afterwards, as if you had both played well.\nopt.left.say.1 [66098b] = 图书管理员冷冷地向你道了谢。那个荆木女生被禁足在客房区一周。之后的晚饭上她朝你点点头，好像你们俩都下了一手好棋。\n# en: Keep quiet, for a favour\nopt.right.label [cca079] = 保持沉默，换个人情\n# en: She tears out the page she copied and hands you the other half of her notes instead. \"We're even,\" she says. You're fairly sure you aren't.\nopt.right.say.1 [ee5c42] = 她撕下抄好的那页，转而把自己笔记的另一半递给你。“我们扯平了，”她说。你很确定并没有。\n\n@event xchg_farewell\n# en: A Thornwick Farewell\ntitle [7e24b5] = 荆木的告别\n# en: The Thornwick students leave on the dawn barge. The boy with the sketchbook finds you on the jetty and holds out his scarf. \"It's a custom,\" he says. \"Probably. I've just decided.\" Behind him his friends pretend not to listen.\ntext [2522b5] = 荆木的学生们要乘黎明的驳船离开。拿速写本的男生在栈桥上找到你，递出他的围巾。“这是个习俗，”他说，“大概吧。我刚决定的。”他身后的朋友们假装没在听。\n# en: Students in green and blue robes on a jetty at dawn, a barge waiting, one of them holding out a striped scarf.\nscene [75f77e] = 黎明时分的栈桥上，穿绿袍和蓝袍的学生们站着，一艘驳船在等，其中一人递出一条条纹围巾。\n# en: Swap scarves\nopt.left.label [1fdbff] = 交换围巾\n# en: You trade scarves on the jetty. Someone from your own house mutters \"traitor\" at breakfast. You wear it anyway, and get a letter from across the hills by the end of the month.\nopt.left.say.1 [5d5b9e] = 你们在栈桥上交换了围巾。早饭时，你自己学院的某人嘀咕了一句“叛徒”。你照样围着它，月底前还收到了一封翻山而来的信。\n# en: Challenge him to one last duel\nopt.right.label [07d107] = 邀他最后决斗一场\n# en: You win, narrowly, on the wet boards. He laughs, bows, and sketches you mid-spell on the barge as it pulls away. The sketch arrives by post, flattering.\nopt.right.say.1 [debf60] = 你在湿漉漉的木板上险胜。他大笑着鞠了一躬，驳船离岸时还在给施法中的你画速写。那张速写后来寄到了，画得相当美化。\n# en: He wins with an illusion of a second you. You both end up in the lake. It is, somehow, the best goodbye you've had.\nopt.right.say.2 [cbf2ca] = 他用一个“第二个你”的幻象赢了。你们俩最后都掉进了湖里。不知怎么，这竟是你经历过最好的一次告别。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/jobs.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/jobs.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event lib_biting_book\n# en: The Book That Bites\ntitle [e0be45] = 会咬人的书\n# en: Reshelving in the zoology section, a fat leather book clamps onto your finger and refuses to let go. The head librarian, without looking up, says: \"Stroke the spine.\"\ntext [68fee7] = 你在动物学区上架时，一本厚厚的皮面书咬住了你的手指，死活不松口。图书馆馆长头也不抬地说：“摸摸它的书脊。”\n# en: A thick leather book with tiny teeth along its pages snapping at a hand reaching for it on a high shelf.\nscene [b648b2] = 一本书页边缘长着细牙的厚皮书，正冲着伸向高处书架的手咬去。\n# en: Muzzle it with a belt\nopt.left.label [c9d229] = 用皮带把它的嘴捆上\n# en: You wrestle it into submission and shelve it. It growls at you every time you pass for the rest of the term.\nopt.left.say.1 [91c33d] = 你把它摁服了，放回书架。接下来整个学期，你每次经过它都会冲你低吼。\n# en: Stroke the spine and read it\nopt.right.label [b279ad] = 摸摸书脊，读一读\n# en: It purrs, opens and turns out to be a fascinating treatise on carnivorous plants. It only bites twice more.\nopt.right.say.1 [527de2] = 它发出呼噜声，自己翻开了——原来是一部关于食肉植物的精彩论著。它只又咬了你两次。\n\n@event lib_overdue_professor\n# en: Overdue\ntitle [40f62b] = 逾期未还\n# en: Professor Thorne returns a book that is eleven years overdue. The fine, by library rules, is forty crowns. He looks at you. You look at the ledger.\ntext [f16fb3] = 索恩教授还了一本逾期十一年的书。按照图书馆规定，罚款是四十克朗。他看着你。你看着罚款簿。\n# en: A severe professor towering over a library desk, a stack of books under one arm, a very small student assistant on the other side holding a fines ledger.\nscene [9af617] = 一位严厉的教授高高站在图书馆柜台前，胳膊下夹着一摞书，柜台另一边一个小小的学生助理捧着罚款簿。\n# en: Waive the fine\nopt.left.label [20e10a] = 免了罚款\n# en: \"Hm,\" says Thorne. You can't tell if you've earned his respect or lost it.\nopt.left.say.1 [30eaf0] = “嗯。”索恩说。你说不清自己是赢得了他的尊重，还是失去了它。\n# en: Enforce the rules\nopt.right.label [32a605] = 照章办事\n# en: A long silence. Then he pays, in exact change. \"Good,\" he says, and you could swear he almost smiles.\nopt.right.say.1 [a7c7e3] = 漫长的沉默。然后他付了钱，分文不差。“很好。”他说，你敢发誓他差点笑了。\n\n@event lib_restricted_cart\n# en: The Restricted Cart\ntitle [1e31b9] = 禁书推车\n# en: You are asked to wheel the Restricted Section returns to the vault. The cart's chain has come loose. One of the books has fallen open on a page that is writing itself.\ntext [e83ef7] = 你被派去把禁书区归还的书推回书库。推车的锁链松了。其中一本书摊开在一页正在自己书写的纸上。\n# en: A wheeled library cart chained shut, piled with black-bound books, parked alone in a candlelit aisle.\nscene [e3b4b3] = 一辆用锁链锁着的图书推车，堆满黑皮书，孤零零地停在烛光摇曳的过道里。\n# en: Close it without looking\nopt.left.label [69f4b5] = 不看，直接合上\n# en: The book snaps shut with a sound like a disappointed sigh. The vault door clangs.\nopt.left.say.1 [568ca6] = 书啪地合上，发出一声像是失望的叹息。书库的门哐当关上了。\n# en: Read a few lines\nopt.right.label [40815b] = 读上几行\n# en: The words crawl off the page and up your sleeve. That night, you can't get warm.\nopt.right.say.1 [166cd2] = 文字从书页上爬出来，钻进了你的袖子。那天夜里，你怎么也暖和不起来。\n# en: A spell for walking through mirrors. You will never try it. Probably.\nopt.right.say.2 [ee6c9d] = 一个穿过镜子的咒语。你永远不会去试的。大概吧。\n\n@event tavern_brawl\n# en: Brawl at the Newt\ntitle [6ed513] = 蝾螈酒馆斗殴\n# en: A dwarf and a troll disagree about the price of turnips, and the Gilded Newt erupts. A chair flies past your head. The owner is hiding behind the bar, gesturing at you to do something.\ntext [f434ad] = 一个矮人和一个巨魔为芜菁的价格吵了起来，镀金蝾螈酒馆瞬间炸了锅。一把椅子从你头顶飞过。老板躲在吧台后面，比划着让你想想办法。\n# en: A low-beamed tavern mid-brawl: a chair in mid-air, a dwarf on a table, cider sloshing everywhere.\nscene [d9c407] = 一间低梁小酒馆正在混战：一把椅子悬在半空，一个矮人站在桌上，苹果酒洒得到处都是。\n# en: Duck behind the bar too\nopt.left.label [56a05c] = 也躲到吧台后面\n# en: You crouch beside the owner and share a bag of crisps while the room destroys itself.\nopt.left.say.1 [87132f] = 你蹲在老板旁边，和他分吃一袋薯片，看着整个屋子自我毁灭。\n# en: Break it up\nopt.right.label [12966a] = 上去劝架\n# en: You slam a tray on the bar and bellow. Silence. The troll apologises. The dwarf tips you fifteen crowns.\nopt.right.say.1 [b417fb] = 你把托盘往吧台上一拍，大吼一声。全场安静。巨魔道了歉。矮人给了你十五克朗小费。\n# en: You step in. Someone's elbow steps into your face.\nopt.right.say.2 [763c5b] = 你上前一步。不知谁的胳膊肘也上前一步，到了你脸上。\n\n@event tavern_star_coin\n# en: A Generous Stranger\ntitle [678324] = 慷慨的陌生人\n# en: A hooded stranger nurses a cider all evening and leaves a tip: a black coin stamped with a hollow star. \"For the student,\" they say. \"Tell Kael Dumont his family sends regards.\"\ntext [b2c4b8] = 一个戴兜帽的陌生人一整晚只喝了一杯苹果酒，留下的小费是一枚印着空心星的黑色硬币。“给这个学生的，”对方说，“转告凯尔·杜蒙，他的家人向他问好。”\n# en: A hooded figure at a corner table sliding a black coin across the wood, its surface stamped with a dark star.\nscene [fe800a] = 角落桌旁一个戴兜帽的身影把一枚黑色硬币推过木桌，币面上印着一颗暗星。\n# en: Refuse the coin\nopt.left.label [999c57] = 拒绝这枚硬币\n# en: The stranger shrugs and pockets it. When you look again, the corner table is empty and the cider is frozen solid.\nopt.left.say.1 [53629a] = 陌生人耸耸肩，把它收回口袋。你再看时，角落的桌子空了，那杯苹果酒冻成了冰。\n# en: Take it\nopt.right.label [18a43f] = 收下它\n# en: The coin is cold as ice and heavier than it should be. You find yourself turning it over in your pocket for days.\nopt.right.say.1 [2b7ed8] = 硬币冷得像冰，比看上去要重。接下来好几天，你发现自己总在口袋里摩挲它。\n\n@event tavern_extra_shifts\n# en: More Shifts\ntitle [bc17d5] = 加班\n# en: The owner is short-handed. \"Double shifts for a month,\" he says. \"Double pay. I know you've got studies, but you've also got rent.\"\ntext [daff5a] = 老板人手不够。“一个月双班，”他说，“双倍工钱。我知道你还要上学，但你也还要交房租。”\n# en: A ruddy tavern owner leaning on the bar, wiping a mug, a rota pinned behind him covered in crossed-out names.\nscene [205014] = 一个红光满面的酒馆老板靠在吧台上擦杯子，身后钉着的排班表上划掉了一个又一个名字。\n# en: Decline\nopt.left.label [eaef39] = 拒绝\n# en: \"Fair enough,\" he says, and asks again next week.\nopt.left.say.1 [2ad5ce] = “行吧。”他说，然后下周又来问。\n# en: Take the extra shifts\nopt.right.label [124a34] = 接下加班\n# en: A month of late nights and early lectures. Your purse is heavy. So are your eyelids.\nopt.right.say.1 [bef75a] = 一个月的深夜打工和清早上课。你的钱袋沉甸甸的。眼皮也是。\n\n@event apoth_wrong_label\n# en: A Mislabelled Jar\ntitle [2e5055] = 贴错的标签\n# en: The jar labelled \"Sleeping Draught\" is green. Sleeping Draught is blue. You're fairly sure the green stuff is Draught of Unending Hiccups. Old Pell is very proud of his labelling.\ntext [90816e] = 标着“安眠药剂”的罐子是绿色的。安眠药剂是蓝色的。你很确定这绿色的东西是“无尽打嗝药剂”。老佩尔对自己的标签工作非常自豪。\n# en: Shelves of glass jars in a narrow shop, one jar labelled SLEEPING DRAUGHT glowing an ominous green.\nscene [da4c60] = 狭窄店铺里一排排玻璃罐，其中一只标着“安眠药剂”的罐子发出不祥的绿光。\n# en: Quietly fix the label\nopt.left.label [d3ca76] = 悄悄改掉标签\n# en: Nobody ever knows. Somewhere, a customer sleeps soundly instead of hiccupping for a week.\nopt.left.say.1 [3b7685] = 永远没人知道。某个地方，一位顾客安然入睡，而不是打一星期的嗝。\n# en: Point it out to Pell\nopt.right.label [4225c6] = 告诉佩尔\n# en: Pell goes red, then thanks you gruffly and slips you three crowns \"for your eyes\".\nopt.right.say.1 [cdda86] = 佩尔脸一红，粗声道了谢，塞给你三克朗，“算你眼尖”。\n\n@event apoth_poor_mother\n# en: Can't Afford It\ntitle [f4bd61] = 买不起\n# en: A woman counts out coppers for fever tonic. She's fifteen crowns short. Her son coughs into her coat. Pell is in the back room.\ntext [1d93f2] = 一位妇人一枚枚数着铜板买退烧药。还差十五克朗。她的儿子靠在她外套上咳嗽。佩尔在后屋。\n# en: A tired woman at a shop counter counting copper coins, a small child coughing beside her.\nscene [6ddd5b] = 一位疲惫的妇人在柜台前数着铜币，身边一个小孩在咳嗽。\n# en: Charge the full price\nopt.left.label [9ea316] = 照价收钱\n# en: She leaves without it. The bell on the door rings for a very long time.\nopt.left.say.1 [b36f54] = 她没买成就走了。门上的铃铛响了很久很久。\n# en: Pay the difference yourself\nopt.right.label [28226a] = 自己垫上差价\n# en: You slip the tonic into her basket. Next week a pie appears on the counter with your name on it.\nopt.right.say.1 [8762e7] = 你把药悄悄放进她的篮子。下周，柜台上出现了一个写着你名字的馅饼。\n\n@event apoth_nightshade_order\n# en: The Nightshade Order\ntitle [d8aa3b] = 颠茄订单\n# en: A note arrives with a sealed purse: a large order of nightshade, grave-moss and powdered moonstone. Legal, technically. Ingredients for something very old and very forbidden, also technically.\ntext [7f5f4d] = 一张便条随一只封好的钱袋送来：大量的颠茄、墓苔和月长石粉。严格来说，合法。严格来说，也是某种非常古老、非常禁忌之物的原料。\n# en: An order slip on a counter listing a dozen deadly reagents, signed with an elegant, illegible flourish.\nscene [fe014b] = 柜台上一张订货单，列着十几种致命试剂，签名是一个优雅却无法辨认的花体字。\n# en: Fill the order\nopt.left.label [c3e9db] = 照单配货\n# en: The courier who collects it never shows his face. The purse is very generous.\nopt.left.say.1 [c33bee] = 来取货的信使始终没有露脸。钱袋非常丰厚。\n# en: Refuse and report it\nopt.right.label [ce8f5b] = 拒绝并上报\n# en: The headmistress listens to your report in silence. \"Thank you,\" she says. \"Tell no one else.\" She looks worried.\nopt.right.say.1 [5c685a] = 院长沉默地听完你的报告。“谢谢你，”她说，“别再告诉任何人。”她看起来很担忧。\n\n@event stables_hippogriff\n# en: Bow to the Hippogriff\ntitle [0d01e9] = 向鹰头马身有翼兽鞠躬\n# en: The new hippogriff won't let anyone near the feed trough. The stablemaster says to bow, keep eye contact and not blink. \"Whatever you do, don't blink.\"\ntext [e114f7] = 新来的鹰头马身有翼兽不让任何人靠近食槽。马厩总管说要鞠躬、保持对视、不许眨眼。“无论如何，别眨眼。”\n# en: A proud hippogriff in a straw-strewn stall, head lowered, regarding a stable hand who is bowing very deeply.\nscene [3828d0] = 铺满稻草的马厩里，一头高傲的鹰头马身有翼兽低着头，打量着一个深深鞠躬的马厩工。\n# en: Leave the feed and run\nopt.left.label [4fb2e9] = 扔下饲料就跑\n# en: You toss the feed bucket and flee. The hippogriff eats it, and the bucket.\nopt.left.say.1 [e88a40] = 你扔下饲料桶就逃。它把饲料吃了，连桶一起。\n# en: Bow and hold its gaze\nopt.right.label [d95cb6] = 鞠躬，与它对视\n# en: It bows back. After that it follows you around the stables like a large, feathered dog.\nopt.right.say.1 [9dbab4] = 它回了一个礼。从那以后，它在马厩里跟着你转，像一只长了羽毛的大狗。\n# en: You blinked.\nopt.right.say.2 [b3e9ff] = 你眨眼了。\n\n@event stables_dragon_egg\n# en: An Egg in the Hay\ntitle [b1a78f] = 草堆里的蛋\n# en: Mucking out the far stall, you find an egg the size of a melon, nestled in the hay and steaming gently. It's very warm. It's very definitely not a chicken's.\ntext [067bf4] = 清扫最里面的隔间时，你在草堆里发现一枚西瓜大的蛋，正轻轻冒着热气。它很烫。它绝对不是鸡蛋。\n# en: A speckled egg the size of a melon nestled in hay, steam rising from it, a stable hand's lantern held over it.\nscene [bb4b8b] = 一枚西瓜大小、带斑点的蛋窝在干草里冒着热气，马厩工的提灯举在它上方。\n# en: Report it to Professor Brask\nopt.left.label [983969] = 报告布拉斯克教授\n# en: Brask nearly faints. \"Smugglers! A firedrake egg!\" It's taken to the menagerie. You get a commendation.\nopt.left.say.1 [95ded5] = 布拉斯克差点晕过去。“走私犯！火龙蛋！”蛋被送进了动物园。你得到了一份嘉奖。\n# en: Keep it\nopt.right.label [87c2f5] = 留下它\n# en: You smuggle it back to your dormitory wrapped in a scarf. It purrs. Eggs shouldn't purr.\nopt.right.say.1 [8852b1] = 你用围巾把它裹好，偷偷带回了宿舍。它在打呼噜。蛋不该会打呼噜的。\n\n@event egg_hatches\n# en: It Hatches\ntitle [8c317e] = 破壳了\n# en: At 3am your pillow catches fire. The egg has hatched: a firedrake the size of a kitten, delighted with itself and setting fire to everything it loves. It loves you very much.\ntext [c42d90] = 凌晨三点，你的枕头着火了。蛋孵出来了：一条小猫大的火龙，对自己满意极了，见到喜欢的东西就点着。它非常非常喜欢你。\n# en: A tiny red dragon perched on a scorched dormitory pillow, a puff of smoke rising from its nostrils, a horrified roommate in the doorway.\nscene [41e73c] = 一条小红龙蹲在烧焦的宿舍枕头上，鼻孔冒出一缕烟，门口的室友吓呆了。\n# en: Hand it over to the menagerie\nopt.left.label [141f8c] = 交给动物园\n# en: Brask takes it gently. \"You'll be able to visit.\" You do, every week. It always tries to set you on fire, affectionately.\nopt.left.say.1 [695354] = 布拉斯克轻轻接过它。“你可以来探望。”你每周都去。它总是试图把你点着，满怀爱意地。\n# en: Hide it as long as you can\nopt.right.label [7752e9] = 能藏多久藏多久\n# en: You keep it secret for a month — buying fireproof sheets, raw meat, an asbestos sock — before releasing it in the mountains. It circles once, roaring.\nopt.right.say.1 [40bff7] = 你把它藏了一个月——买防火床单、生肉、一只石棉袜子——然后在山里放了它。它盘旋一圈，长啸一声。\n# en: The smoke alarm charm goes off in three towers at once. Discovered, singed, and in very deep trouble.\nopt.right.say.2 [25dba6] = 三座塔的烟雾警报咒同时响起。被发现了，被烧焦了，而且麻烦大了。\n\n@event stables_night_shift\n# en: The Sick Pegasus\ntitle [651cda] = 生病的天马\n# en: The oldest pegasus in the stables is sick. Someone has to sit with her overnight, keep her warm, and make sure she drinks. It's unpaid. Old Maud says she'll do it if no one else will. Maud is eighty.\ntext [e3c4b6] = 马厩里最老的天马病了。得有人整夜守着她、给她保暖、确保她喝水。没有报酬。老莫德说没人去的话她就去。莫德八十岁了。\n# en: A white pegasus lying in a moonlit stall, wings folded, a student sitting beside it with a lantern and a blanket.\nscene [e7765d] = 一匹白色天马收着翅膀卧在月光照亮的马厩里，一个学生提着灯、抱着毯子坐在它身旁。\n# en: Let Maud do it\nopt.left.label [e408cf] = 让莫德去\n# en: You sleep well. Maud is a little slower the next day.\nopt.left.say.1 [d77695] = 你睡得很好。第二天，莫德的动作慢了一些。\n# en: Take the night shift\nopt.right.label [ead26b] = 你来值夜班\n# en: She drinks. You talk to her all night about home. At dawn she stands, shakes her wings, and nuzzles your hair.\nopt.right.say.1 [e093f9] = 她喝了水。你整夜跟她聊家乡。黎明时，她站起来抖了抖翅膀，用鼻子蹭了蹭你的头发。\n\n@event tutor_hopeless\n# en: The Hopeless Case\ntitle [323c15] = 没救的学生\n# en: Your tutee has been trying to levitate a feather for three weeks. The feather has not moved. The tutee is starting to cry. You have one more session before their exam.\ntext [04a494] = 你辅导的学生已经尝试让一根羽毛悬浮三个星期了。羽毛一动没动。学生快哭了。考试前你们只剩一次课。\n# en: A tiny first-year in oversized robes pointing a wand at a feather, which is stubbornly not moving, while a tutor watches with infinite patience.\nscene [29897d] = 一个穿着过大长袍的小个子一年级生用魔杖指着一根纹丝不动的羽毛，辅导员以无限的耐心在旁边看着。\n# en: Drill the basics, again\nopt.left.label [652518] = 再练一遍基础\n# en: Wrist, word, will. Wrist, word, will. On the exam, the feather twitches. It's enough to pass.\nopt.left.say.1 [a0fdfe] = 手腕、咒语、意志。手腕、咒语、意志。考试时，羽毛抽动了一下。够及格了。\n# en: Try something unorthodox\nopt.right.label [b16c64] = 试点不寻常的办法\n# en: You tell them to imagine the feather is in love with the ceiling. It shoots upward and sticks there. They pass with distinction.\nopt.right.say.1 [e57332] = 你让对方想象羽毛爱上了天花板。羽毛嗖地升上去，粘在了那里。对方以优异成绩通过。\n# en: You tell them to imagine the feather is in love with the ceiling. They are confused. So is the feather.\nopt.right.say.2 [79cec2] = 你让对方想象羽毛爱上了天花板。对方很困惑。羽毛也很困惑。\n\n@event tutor_bribe\n# en: An Indecent Proposal\ntitle [e847fe] = 不体面的提议\n# en: A rich second-year doesn't want tutoring. He wants an essay. \"Thirty crowns,\" he says, dropping a purse on the table. \"Nobody needs to know.\"\ntext [5e864c] = 一个富家二年级生不想要辅导。他想要一篇论文。“三十克朗，”他说着把钱袋扔在桌上，“没人需要知道。”\n# en: A rich student in tailored robes sliding a heavy coin purse across a study table, smiling smugly.\nscene [188c2e] = 一个穿着定制长袍的富家学生把一只沉甸甸的钱袋推过书桌，得意地笑着。\n# en: Refuse\nopt.left.label [cada35] = 拒绝\n# en: He sneers and goes elsewhere. Later you see him in detention, scrubbing a cauldron.\nopt.left.say.1 [c00d68] = 他冷笑一声，另寻他人去了。后来你看见他在禁闭室里刷坩埚。\n# en: Write the essay\nopt.right.label [b2934c] = 替他写\n# en: His professor recognises your style. You both end up in detention. He doesn't speak to you. Fine.\nopt.right.say.1 [955908] = 他的教授认出了你的文风。你们俩都被关了禁闭。他不再理你。正好。\n# en: He gets a decent mark. You get thirty crowns and a slightly sour feeling.\nopt.right.say.2 [b854de] = 他拿到了不错的分数。你拿到了三十克朗，和一丝酸溜溜的感觉。\n\n@event tutor_prodigy\n# en: Surpassed\ntitle [679359] = 青出于蓝\n# en: Your newest tutee casts in a week what took you a year. Then she asks you a question you can't answer. She's eleven.\ntext [e46a59] = 你新带的学生一周就学会了你花一年才学会的咒语。然后她问了你一个你答不上来的问题。她才十一岁。\n# en: A small first-year casting a dazzling spell that fills a classroom with light, their tutor watching with complicated feelings.\nscene [9e6498] = 一个小个子一年级生施展出一道耀眼的咒语，满屋光芒，辅导员在一旁神情复杂。\n# en: Bluff an answer\nopt.left.label [86ba79] = 硬编一个答案\n# en: She nods politely. You both know.\nopt.left.say.1 [7e3422] = 她礼貌地点点头。你们俩心里都明白。\n# en: Admit you don't know and find out together\nopt.right.label [b4d010] = 承认不知道，一起去查\n# en: You spend an evening in the library together. She thanks you as if you've taught her something. Maybe you have.\nopt.right.say.1 [92c9bb] = 你们在图书馆待了一晚上。她向你道谢，好像你教了她什么。也许你真的教了。\n\n@event courier_storm\n# en: Delivery in a Storm\ntitle [d6be36] = 风暴中送件\n# en: An urgent parcel needs to cross the lake tonight. There's a storm. The dispatcher offers double pay to whoever's fool enough. Everyone looks at you.\ntext [7b10ad] = 一件急件今晚必须送过湖。外面有风暴。调度员给愿意去的傻瓜开双倍工钱。所有人都看着你。\n# en: A courier on a broom bent low against a lightning-lit sky, a parcel strapped to their back, rain sheeting sideways.\nscene [eccc6d] = 一名信使伏低身子骑着飞帚冲进闪电照亮的夜空，背上绑着包裹，雨水横扫。\n# en: Wait for the storm to pass\nopt.left.label [fd0d0f] = 等风暴过去\n# en: The parcel arrives late. The client deducts it from your wages. Rude, but survivable.\nopt.left.say.1 [c849f6] = 包裹迟到了。客户从你的工钱里扣了钱。无礼，但还活得下去。\n# en: Fly through it\nopt.right.label [d49b80] = 穿过风暴\n# en: You thread the lightning like a needle and land soaked, laughing, with the parcel dry.\nopt.right.say.1 [419505] = 你像穿针一样穿过闪电，浑身湿透地落地，大笑着，包裹却是干的。\n# en: Lightning strikes close enough to stand your hair on end. You lose the broom and gain a limp.\nopt.right.say.2 [1620b9] = 一道闪电劈得太近，你的头发都竖了起来。你丢了飞帚，得了一条瘸腿。\n\n@event courier_whispering_parcel\n# en: The Whispering Parcel\ntitle [00dbb7] = 会低语的包裹\n# en: Your parcel is addressed to \"K. Dumont, Stillwater House\". It is ice cold and it whispers when you aren't looking at it.\ntext [a7f392] = 你的包裹寄给“静水院，K. 杜蒙”。它冷得像冰，你不看它的时候，它会低语。\n# en: A brown paper parcel tied with black string on a courier's satchel, frost forming on its corners, faint whispers rising from it.\nscene [cef7c7] = 信使挎包上一个用黑绳捆着的牛皮纸包裹，边角结着霜，隐隐传出低语声。\n# en: Deliver it unopened\nopt.left.label [edbe83] = 原封不动地送达\n# en: Kael takes it without a word. His hands shake. The next day, he looks like he hasn't slept.\nopt.left.say.1 [6ad92e] = 凯尔一言不发地接过包裹。他的手在抖。第二天，他看起来像一夜没睡。\n# en: Peek inside\nopt.right.label [95f6de] = 偷看一眼\n# en: Inside: a letter in a cold, beautiful hand. \"The Star wakes. Come home, or open the door. Your family.\" You reseal it perfectly.\nopt.right.say.1 [49350a] = 里面是一封信，字迹冰冷而优美：“星辰苏醒了。回家，或者打开那扇门。——你的家人。”你把它完美地重新封好。\n# en: The seal hisses as it breaks. Kael is waiting at the door when you arrive. He saw. He says nothing, which is worse.\nopt.right.say.2 [1d94cc] = 封印裂开时发出嘶嘶声。你到的时候，凯尔已经等在门口了。他看见了。他什么也没说，这更糟。\n\n@event courier_sky_pirates\n# en: Sky Pirates\ntitle [933c94] = 空中海盗\n# en: Three figures on patched brooms drop out of a cloud and box you in. \"Toll,\" says their leader, a woman with a parrot that is also on a tiny broom. \"Eight crowns or the parcel.\"\ntext [0892d1] = 三个骑着补丁飞帚的身影从云里冲出来围住了你。“过路费，”领头的女人说，她的鹦鹉也骑着一把小飞帚，“八克朗，或者包裹。”\n# en: Three ragged figures on patched brooms surrounding a courier above the clouds, cutlasses glinting.\nscene [5f6143] = 云层之上，三个衣衫褴褛、骑着补丁飞帚的身影围住一名信使，弯刀闪闪发亮。\n# en: Pay the toll\nopt.left.label [a1a28f] = 交过路费\n# en: They let you pass with a salute. The parrot salutes too.\nopt.left.say.1 [de94eb] = 他们敬了个礼放你过去。鹦鹉也敬了个礼。\n# en: Outfly them\nopt.right.label [a811f6] = 甩掉他们\n# en: You dive through a cloudbank and lose them over the lake. The client tips extra for the excitement.\nopt.right.say.1 [b17031] = 你俯冲穿过云堤，在湖面上空甩掉了他们。客户因为这份刺激多给了小费。\n# en: They catch you, take the toll, the parcel and your hat. The parrot wears it.\nopt.right.say.2 [8905f7] = 他们追上了你，拿走了过路费、包裹，还有你的帽子。鹦鹉戴上了它。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/life.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/life.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event life_staircase\n# en: The Staircase Moves\ntitle [1e1113] = 楼梯动了\n# en: Halfway up the west staircase, it decides to be somewhere else. You're now heading toward a floor you've never seen, at speed, and you're late for Arcana.\ntext [b49591] = 西楼梯爬到一半，它决定去别的地方。你正被高速送往一层你从没见过的楼，而你的奥术课要迟到了。\n# en: A grand spiral staircase in mid-rotation, a student clinging to the banister as the steps swing toward a different floor.\nscene [684196] = 一道宏伟的螺旋楼梯正在旋转，一个学生紧抓扶手，台阶正转向另一层楼。\n# en: Hold on and see where it goes\nopt.left.label [441219] = 抓紧，看它去哪儿\n# en: It deposits you in a dusty forgotten classroom. In a desk: eight old crowns and a love letter from 1802.\nopt.left.say.1 [0aecae] = 它把你放在一间落满灰尘、被遗忘的教室里。课桌里有八枚旧克朗和一封 1802 年的情书。\n# en: It takes you to the roof. Then the cellar. Then the roof again. You miss Arcana entirely.\nopt.left.say.2 [30a40f] = 它把你送上屋顶。然后是地窖。然后又是屋顶。你完美错过了整节奥术课。\n# en: Jump for the landing\nopt.right.label [fc3ab6] = 跳向平台\n# en: You leap and land neatly. A passing portrait applauds.\nopt.right.say.1 [e697a6] = 你一跃而起，稳稳落地。路过的一幅肖像画鼓起了掌。\n# en: You leap and land, but not neatly. A passing portrait winces.\nopt.right.say.2 [ff31a3] = 你一跃而起，落地了，但不太稳。路过的一幅肖像画皱了皱眉。\n\n@event life_mystery_stew\n# en: Mystery Stew\ntitle [afd265] = 神秘炖菜\n# en: Tonight's dinner is described on the board only as \"Stew\". It is grey-green. The ladle is standing up by itself. The cook is watching you with hope.\ntext [70292b] = 今晚的晚餐在菜单板上只写着“炖菜”。它是灰绿色的。汤勺自己立在里面。厨师满怀希望地看着你。\n# en: A huge cauldron of grey-green stew in the dining hall, a ladle standing upright in it unaided.\nscene [1a7e62] = 餐厅里一大锅灰绿色的炖菜，一把汤勺自己笔直地立在锅中。\n# en: Eat it bravely\nopt.left.label [b372d7] = 勇敢地吃下去\n# en: It's... delicious? Rich, peppery, faintly glowing. You go back for seconds. The cook beams.\nopt.left.say.1 [768d2a] = 它……很好吃？浓郁、带着胡椒味，还微微发光。你又去盛了一碗。厨师笑开了花。\n# en: It's not delicious. It's not anything a stomach should know about. You spend the night regretting it.\nopt.left.say.2 [995531] = 不好吃。它不是任何胃应该认识的东西。你后悔了一整晚。\n# en: Toast and jam again\nopt.right.label [31a8e9] = 还是吐司配果酱\n# en: You eat toast. The cook looks hurt. You feel guilty all evening.\nopt.right.say.1 [e74e29] = 你吃了吐司。厨师一脸受伤。你内疚了一整晚。\n\n@event life_lost_wand\n# en: Where Is Your Wand?\ntitle [e9e5f4] = 你的魔杖呢？\n# en: Your wand is gone. It was on your desk. Now it isn't. Classes start in twenty minutes.\ntext [006487] = 你的魔杖不见了。它刚才还在桌上。现在没了。二十分钟后就要上课了。\n# en: A dormitory turned upside down, clothes everywhere, a student on hands and knees peering under a bed.\nscene [9d31f7] = 一间被翻得底朝天的宿舍，衣服扔得到处都是，一个学生趴在地上往床底下看。\n# en: Tear the room apart\nopt.left.label [a2a2bf] = 把房间翻个底朝天\n# en: It's in your boot. Why is it in your boot? You make it to class with seconds to spare.\nopt.left.say.1 [f6b100] = 它在你的靴子里。它为什么会在你靴子里？你在最后几秒赶到了教室。\n# en: You find it at lunch, inside the pillowcase. You've missed a whole morning.\nopt.left.say.2 [2b5185] = 午饭时你在枕套里找到了它。你错过了整整一上午。\n# en: Try a summoning charm\nopt.right.label [18e9e7] = 试试召唤咒\n# en: It flies into your hand from inside the wardrobe, offended at having been lost.\nopt.right.say.1 [d8a6af] = 它从衣柜里飞进你手里，对自己被弄丢一事颇为不满。\n# en: Every pencil in the dormitory flies at your face. The wand stays hidden out of spite.\nopt.right.say.2 [1fe091] = 宿舍里每一支铅笔都朝你脸上飞来。魔杖出于怨气，继续藏着。\n\n@event life_snoring_roommate\n# en: The Snorer\ntitle [b3c66f] = 打呼噜的人\n# en: Someone in your dormitory snores like a dragon with a head cold, and nobody will admit to it. You haven't slept properly in a week.\ntext [ab60f0] = 宿舍里有人打起呼噜来像一条感冒的龙，而且没人承认。你已经一个星期没睡好了。\n# en: A dark dormitory, one bed shaking with snores so loud the curtains flutter, another occupant glaring with a pillow over their head.\nscene [a2130d] = 漆黑的宿舍里，一张床随着震天的呼噜声摇晃，窗帘都在飘，另一个人用枕头捂着头怒目而视。\n# en: Brew a silencing draught\nopt.left.label [716d57] = 熬一剂静音药剂\n# en: Perfect silence. Too perfect. The culprit, the quiet one by the window, is now silent even when awake. It wears off by Tuesday.\nopt.left.say.1 [ab759f] = 完美的寂静。太完美了。罪魁祸首——靠窗那个安静的家伙——现在醒着也发不出声音。到周二才失效。\n# en: The draught finds the wrong bed. Now two people snore, in harmony.\nopt.left.say.2 [80815d] = 药剂找错了床。现在有两个人在打呼噜，还是和声。\n# en: Move to the common room sofa\nopt.right.label [65d659] = 搬到公共休息室的沙发上\n# en: You sleep on the sofa and meet everyone who comes down at 3am. Some of them are very interesting.\nopt.right.say.1 [168a41] = 你睡在沙发上，结识了每一个凌晨三点下楼的人。其中有些人非常有意思。\n\n@event life_portrait_gossip\n# en: The Portraits Talk\ntitle [b767ea] = 肖像画在说话\n# en: Two portraits in the east gallery are gossiping about a student. Then one of them says your name and they both go suspiciously quiet.\ntext [feda64] = 东画廊里两幅肖像画正在八卦某个学生。然后其中一幅说出了你的名字，两幅画都可疑地安静了下来。\n# en: A long gallery of gilded portraits, painted figures leaning out of their frames toward each other, whispering behind painted hands.\nscene [58f7f0] = 长长的画廊里挂满镀金肖像，画中人探出画框凑在一起，用画出来的手捂着嘴窃窃私语。\n# en: Bribe them with flattery\nopt.left.label [729d7c] = 用奉承贿赂它们\n# en: Ten minutes of compliments on their brushwork, and they tell you everything. Gossip about half the faculty, too.\nopt.left.say.1 [b45427] = 夸了十分钟它们的笔触，它们就什么都告诉你了。还附送了半个教职员团队的八卦。\n# en: \"Flattery,\" sniffs the duchess, \"is so common.\" They turn their backs. Literally.\nopt.left.say.2 [20a283] = “奉承，”公爵夫人嗤之以鼻，“太俗气了。”它们背过身去。字面意义上的。\n# en: Pretend you didn't hear\nopt.right.label [e071a6] = 假装没听见\n# en: You walk on. Behind you, the whispering resumes, louder.\nopt.right.say.1 [546c2f] = 你继续往前走。身后，窃窃私语又响了起来，更大声了。\n\n@event life_pixies\n# en: Pixie Infestation\ntitle [12ac66] = 小精灵泛滥\n# en: Someone left a window open and the dormitory is now full of pixies. They're stealing socks, unscrewing bedposts and laughing in very high voices.\ntext [53332f] = 有人没关窗户，宿舍里现在满是小精灵。它们在偷袜子、拧床柱，还用尖细的嗓音大笑。\n# en: A dormitory full of tiny blue winged creatures swinging from curtains, stealing socks and pulling hair.\nscene [9a096c] = 宿舍里到处是蓝色带翅膀的小生物，荡在窗帘上，偷袜子，扯头发。\n# en: Fight them off\nopt.left.label [a5f0b8] = 把它们打跑\n# en: You catch nine with a laundry bag. The dorm cheers. The pixies swear at you, colourfully.\nopt.left.say.1 [db8849] = 你用洗衣袋抓住了九只。宿舍一片欢呼。小精灵们花样百出地骂你。\n# en: You lose. The pixies hang you from the chandelier by your robe. It takes an hour to get down.\nopt.left.say.2 [87d5ba] = 你输了。小精灵把你的长袍挂在吊灯上，连人一起。你花了一个小时才下来。\n# en: Bribe them with sugar\nopt.right.label [8661f5] = 用糖贿赂它们\n# en: You lay a trail of sugar cubes out of the window. The pixies follow it, munching, and leave one sock as a tip.\nopt.right.say.1 [ace496] = 你在窗外铺了一路方糖。小精灵们边吃边跟着走了，还留下一只袜子当小费。\n\n@event life_gargoyle\n# en: A Talkative Gargoyle\ntitle [7093dd] = 话痨石像鬼\n# en: The gargoyle above the east door clears its throat as you pass. \"You,\" it rumbles. \"You look like someone who'd listen. Nobody listens to gargoyles.\"\ntext [4162d1] = 你经过时，东门上方的石像鬼清了清嗓子。“你，”它低沉地说，“你看起来像会听人说话的人。没人愿意听石像鬼说话。”\n# en: A weathered stone gargoyle on a parapet, one eye open, mouth moving, a student sitting beside it with a sandwich.\nscene [2eb36a] = 护墙上一尊饱经风霜的石像鬼睁着一只眼，嘴在动，一个学生拿着三明治坐在它旁边。\n# en: Hurry along\nopt.left.label [1a6798] = 赶紧走开\n# en: \"Typical,\" says the gargoyle. It sulks until spring.\nopt.left.say.1 [852a4c] = “典型。”石像鬼说。它一直闷闷不乐到了春天。\n# en: Sit and listen\nopt.right.label [1a3cf5] = 坐下来听\n# en: It tells you three hundred years of academy gossip, including where the founders hid the good biscuits. You find the biscuits.\nopt.right.say.1 [3415ec] = 它给你讲了三百年的学院八卦，包括创始者把好饼干藏在了哪里。你找到了那些饼干。\n\n@event life_brownies\n# en: Help in the Night\ntitle [fae0e4] = 夜里的帮手\n# en: You wake to find your desk tidied, your socks darned and your essay proofread. Brownies. They expect something in return. Traditionally: cream.\ntext [bfda31] = 你醒来发现书桌被收拾整齐、袜子被补好、论文被校对过了。是棕仙。它们期待回报。按照传统：奶油。\n# en: Tiny bearded figures in aprons tidying a cluttered student desk by moonlight, one darning a sock.\nscene [bb94a0] = 月光下，系着围裙的长胡子小人在整理一张凌乱的书桌，其中一个在补袜子。\n# en: Leave out a bowl of cream\nopt.left.label [a73198] = 放一碗奶油\n# en: Your room stays spotless all term. Your essays have suspiciously good spelling.\nopt.left.say.1 [f0b341] = 整个学期你的房间都一尘不染。你的论文拼写好得可疑。\n# en: Leave them nothing\nopt.right.label [760b5f] = 什么都不留\n# en: The next morning, every one of your socks is tied in knots. Brownies keep score.\nopt.right.say.1 [ac042b] = 第二天早上，你的每一只袜子都被打了结。棕仙是记账的。\n\n@event life_midnight_snack\n# en: Midnight Hunger\ntitle [5673a0] = 午夜饥饿\n# en: It's midnight and you're starving. The kitchens are three floors down, past a prefect's post.\ntext [d97eb5] = 午夜了，你饿得要命。厨房在三层楼下，要经过一个级长的岗哨。\n# en: A student in pyjamas tiptoeing past a sleeping prefect toward a door marked KITCHENS, a candle cupped in their hand.\nscene [6a7e01] = 一个穿睡衣的学生踮着脚经过打盹的级长，走向一扇写着“厨房”的门，双手护着一支蜡烛。\n# en: Go back to bed hungry\nopt.left.label [6a67a5] = 饿着回去睡\n# en: You dream of sandwiches. Long, detailed, beautiful sandwiches.\nopt.left.say.1 [d77265] = 你梦见了三明治。长长的、细节丰富的、美丽的三明治。\n# en: Sneak down\nopt.right.label [a9ae15] = 偷偷溜下去\n# en: Cold pie, a pot of custard, a heel of bread. Feast of kings.\nopt.right.say.1 [2a2136] = 冷馅饼、一罐蛋奶冻、一块面包头。帝王盛宴。\n# en: The prefect is awake. You spend a week washing the pots you were trying to raid.\nopt.right.say.2 [151ad8] = 级长醒着。你洗了一星期你本想去偷吃的那些锅。\n\n@event life_insomnia\n# en: The Ceiling at 3am\ntitle [bbad03] = 凌晨三点的天花板\n# en: You can't sleep. Your brain has decided to replay every embarrassing thing you've said since you were six.\ntext [0cce8a] = 你睡不着。你的大脑决定把你六岁以来说过的每一句丢人的话都重播一遍。\n# en: A student lying awake staring at a ceiling where faint painted stars have started slowly moving.\nscene [eda828] = 一个学生睁眼躺着，盯着天花板，上面画着的淡淡星星开始缓缓移动。\n# en: Get up and study\nopt.left.label [dd9384] = 起床学习\n# en: By dawn you've read three chapters and remembered one of them.\nopt.left.say.1 [d35323] = 到天亮时你读了三章，记住了其中一章。\n# en: Brew chamomile and count the painted stars\nopt.right.label [d3506b] = 泡杯洋甘菊茶，数天花板上的星星\n# en: Halfway through counting, the stars start counting back. Then you're asleep.\nopt.right.say.1 [297bdf] = 数到一半，星星开始反过来数你。然后你就睡着了。\n\n@event life_morning_run\n# en: The Dawn Run\ntitle [97a783] = 黎明晨跑\n# en: A group of students runs the lakeshore every dawn. They've invited you. It's very early. It's very cold.\ntext [b909a7] = 有一群学生每天黎明沿着湖岸跑步。他们邀请了你。很早。很冷。\n# en: The lakeshore path at dawn, mist on the water, a lone runner's breath steaming, the towers catching the first gold light.\nscene [0d10ee] = 黎明的湖岸小路，水面上浮着薄雾，一个孤独的跑者呼着白气，塔楼映着第一缕金光。\n# en: Sleep in\nopt.left.label [de2af7] = 睡懒觉\n# en: You hear them come back, glowing and smug. You are warm and unrepentant.\nopt.left.say.1 [58ff7c] = 你听见他们回来了，容光焕发，一脸得意。你暖暖和和，毫无悔意。\n# en: Run with them\nopt.right.label [8e7ee8] = 和他们一起跑\n# en: You nearly die at the first mile and feel immortal by the third. The lake shines like a coin. You feel every step of it all through your lectures.\nopt.right.say.1 [2e95a0] = 第一英里你差点死掉，到第三英里你觉得自己长生不老。湖面亮得像一枚硬币。整堂课上，你都能感觉到每一步的后劲。\n\n@event life_homework_mountain\n# en: The Pile\ntitle [654764] = 作业山\n# en: Three essays, a potion log, a star chart and a reading list the length of your arm. All due this week. The pile has achieved its own weather.\ntext [68b440] = 三篇论文、一份药剂日志、一张星图，还有一份和你胳膊一样长的阅读书单。全部本周截止。这座作业山已经形成了自己的小气候。\n# en: A desk buried under a precarious tower of scrolls and books, a student peeking hopelessly over the top.\nscene [7c65a4] = 书桌被一座摇摇欲坠的卷轴和书本之塔淹没，一个学生绝望地从塔顶探出头来。\n# en: Grind through it\nopt.left.label [6c90f1] = 埋头苦干\n# en: You finish at 4am on the last day. Every piece is handed in. You sleep through breakfast.\nopt.left.say.1 [2f2b04] = 最后一天凌晨四点你全部完成。每一份都交上去了。你睡过了早饭。\n# en: Pick what matters, skip the rest\nopt.right.label [321412] = 挑重要的做，其余的跳过\n# en: You hand in two of five and go for a walk. The professors are not pleased. You feel great, temporarily.\nopt.right.say.1 [c142df] = 你交了五份中的两份，然后出去散步了。教授们不太高兴。你感觉好极了，暂时。\n\n@event life_stolen_answers\n# en: The Answers\ntitle [48b36f] = 答案\n# en: A third-year is selling copies of next week's exam. Eight crowns. \"Guaranteed real,\" he whispers. \"Straight from the examiner's desk.\"\ntext [3aac85] = 一个三年级生在卖下周考试的复印件。八克朗。“保真，”他低声说，“直接从考官桌上拿的。”\n# en: A folded paper being passed under a library table, the faint glow of an examiner's seal on it.\nscene [fab1a5] = 一张折好的纸从图书馆桌下传过，上面隐隐闪着考官的印章。\n# en: Walk away\nopt.left.label [96da1a] = 走开\n# en: Two weeks later, five students who bought copies are suspended. The answers were fake.\nopt.left.say.1 [882a6f] = 两周后，买了复印件的五个学生被停学了。那些答案是假的。\n# en: Buy a copy\nopt.right.label [cae18d] = 买一份\n# en: They're real. You ace it. You also can't quite look the examiner in the eye.\nopt.right.say.1 [80fd8d] = 是真的。你考得很好。你也没法直视考官的眼睛了。\n# en: They're fake, and planted. You're caught with a copy. Detention, and a very cold letter home.\nopt.right.say.2 [aaad15] = 是假的，而且是个圈套。你被抓到持有复印件。禁闭，外加一封非常冰冷的家书。\n\n@event life_backfire\n# en: Spell Backfire\ntitle [195f68] = 咒语反噬\n# en: You mispronounce a minor cheering charm in the corridor. Something goes wrong. Every time you hiccup, a tiny firework comes out.\ntext [4df613] = 你在走廊里把一个小小的振奋咒念错了。出了点问题。现在你每打一个嗝，就会冒出一朵小烟花。\n# en: A student mid-hiccup, a small firework bursting from their mouth, classmates ducking.\nscene [89a5a5] = 一个学生正在打嗝，一朵小烟花从嘴里炸开，同学们纷纷闪躲。\n# en: Go to the infirmary\nopt.left.label [f09b9c] = 去医务室\n# en: Sister Amaranth cures it in a minute and charges you three crowns. \"For the ceiling,\" she says.\nopt.left.say.1 [4e5350] = 阿玛兰斯修女一分钟就治好了，收了你三克朗。“赔天花板的。”她说。\n# en: Live with it\nopt.right.label [a97cce] = 就这么过吧\n# en: You become briefly famous as \"the sparkler\". It's not the fame you wanted.\nopt.right.say.1 [01b18e] = 你作为“烟花人”短暂地出了名。这不是你想要的那种出名。\n\n@event life_lake_swim\n# en: A Swim in the Lake\ntitle [939267] = 湖中游泳\n# en: It's hot and the whole year has gone down to the jetty. Something large is rumoured to live in the deep part. People are swimming anyway.\ntext [9f8141] = 天很热，整个年级都跑到栈桥边去了。传说湖的深处住着什么大东西。大家还是照游不误。\n# en: Students leaping off a wooden jetty into a sparkling lake on a hot afternoon, something large and dark moving far beneath the surface.\nscene [36073b] = 炎热的午后，学生们从木栈桥跳进波光粼粼的湖里，水面下极深处有个巨大的黑影在移动。\n# en: Stay on the jetty\nopt.left.label [a30932] = 待在栈桥上\n# en: You dangle your feet and eat cherries. Something brushes your toe. You keep your feet up after that.\nopt.left.say.1 [5776ea] = 你晃着脚丫吃樱桃。有什么东西擦过你的脚趾。从那以后你一直把脚抬着。\n# en: Dive in\nopt.right.label [eb830e] = 跳下去\n# en: In the deep part, you open your eyes underwater and see a round door, far below, glowing. Then you need air.\nopt.right.say.1 [0ca583] = 在深水区，你在水下睁开眼，看见很深很深的地方有一扇圆门在发光。然后你需要换气了。\n# en: The water's perfect. Something large and friendly swims under you, and you decide not to panic.\nopt.right.say.2 [0e8868] = 水温完美。有个友善的大家伙从你身下游过，你决定不慌。\n\n@event life_stray_cat\n# en: A Cat Moves In\ntitle [9441a9] = 猫搬进来了\n# en: A black cat with one torn ear is sitting on your pillow. It looks at you as though you are the one trespassing.\ntext [f24184] = 一只耳朵缺了一角的黑猫正坐在你的枕头上。它看着你，好像闯进来的人是你。\n# en: A scruffy black cat sitting on a dormitory pillow as if it has always lived there, one torn ear, very green eyes.\nscene [2adf64] = 一只邋遢的黑猫坐在宿舍枕头上，仿佛一直住在这里，一只耳朵破了，眼睛绿得发亮。\n# en: Shoo it out\nopt.left.label [bd0ff5] = 把它赶出去\n# en: It leaves with enormous dignity and moves in with your neighbour, who spoils it rotten.\nopt.left.say.1 [73a8df] = 它极其庄重地离开了，搬去和你的邻居住，邻居把它宠上了天。\n# en: Let it stay\nopt.right.label [b09a3c] = 让它留下\n# en: It's called Professor now. It sleeps on your homework. You've never been happier.\nopt.right.say.1 [7e205d] = 它现在叫“教授”。它睡在你的作业上。你从没这么幸福过。\n\n@event life_storm_night\n# en: The Lights Go Out\ntitle [3af5ad] = 灯灭了\n# en: A storm knocks out every light-crystal in the dormitory. The common room fills with students, blankets and somebody's terrible ghost story.\ntext [cb21f0] = 一场风暴让宿舍里所有的光晶石都熄灭了。公共休息室里挤满了学生、毯子，和某人讲的烂透了的鬼故事。\n# en: A dark common room lit only by lightning, students huddled under blankets, one telling a ghost story.\nscene [09a304] = 漆黑的公共休息室只被闪电照亮，学生们裹着毯子挤在一起，有人在讲鬼故事。\n# en: Tell an even scarier story\nopt.left.label [36f701] = 讲个更吓人的故事\n# en: Your story is so good that three people sleep with the lights on for a week. You're a legend.\nopt.left.say.1 [d941b3] = 你的故事太好了，有三个人开着灯睡了一个星期。你成了传奇。\n# en: Your story is not scary. It is, however, accidentally hilarious.\nopt.left.say.2 [226bee] = 你的故事不吓人。不过，意外地非常好笑。\n# en: Light the room with a spell\nopt.right.label [928a24] = 用咒语照亮房间\n# en: You fill the room with soft floating lights. Someone starts singing. It turns into the best night of term.\nopt.right.say.1 [a9ec71] = 你让屋里飘满柔和的光点。有人唱起了歌。那成了本学期最美好的一夜。\n# en: You light the sofa. Briefly. The storm seems less frightening after that.\nopt.right.say.2 [725129] = 你点着了沙发。短暂地。那之后，风暴似乎也没那么可怕了。\n\n@event life_mirror_talks\n# en: The Honest Mirror\ntitle [11488a] = 诚实的镜子\n# en: The bathroom mirror has started commenting on your appearance. \"Hair,\" it says this morning. Just: \"Hair.\"\ntext [197720] = 浴室的镜子开始点评你的外表了。今天早上它说：“头发。”就两个字：“头发。”\n# en: A bathroom mirror with a stern painted face in its silvering, frowning at a student mid-toothbrush.\nscene [bed5d4] = 一面浴室镜子的镀银里嵌着一张严肃的画中脸，正对着刷牙刷到一半的学生皱眉。\n# en: Ask for its advice\nopt.left.label [97bf15] = 向它请教\n# en: It turns out to be an excellent stylist. You look great. You hate that it's right.\nopt.left.say.1 [88d699] = 原来它是个出色的造型师。你看起来棒极了。你讨厌它说得对。\n# en: Cover it with a towel\nopt.right.label [719e1f] = 用毛巾盖住它\n# en: It sulks under the towel. Muffled: \"Hair.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [2b3d43] = 它在毛巾下面生闷气。闷声闷气地：“头发。”\n\n@event life_prank_war\n# en: Prank War\ntitle [562813] = 恶作剧大战\n# en: The dormitory across the corridor turned your door into a goat. The goat is fine. Honour, however, demands retaliation.\ntext [a19079] = 走廊对面的宿舍把你的门变成了一只山羊。山羊没事。然而荣誉要求你报复。\n# en: A corridor ankle-deep in enchanted soap bubbles, two dormitories' worth of students hurling them at each other.\nscene [9791ed] = 走廊里没过脚踝的魔法肥皂泡，两个宿舍的学生互相扔着泡泡。\n# en: Rise above it\nopt.left.label [fe2376] = 不跟他们计较\n# en: You take the high road. The goat eats your homework. You let it.\nopt.left.say.1 [f7115b] = 你选择了高尚。山羊吃了你的作业。你随它去了。\n# en: Retaliate\nopt.right.label [5cb1e8] = 报复回去\n# en: You fill their dorm with a thousand singing bubbles. They concede by dawn.\nopt.right.say.1 [59c97e] = 你让他们宿舍里塞满了一千个会唱歌的泡泡。天亮前他们就认输了。\n# en: You're caught carrying a crate of bubbles. Detention, but the goat seems proud of you.\nopt.right.say.2 [aee56b] = 你抱着一箱泡泡时被逮住了。禁闭，但那只山羊似乎为你骄傲。\n\n@event life_mail_owl\n# en: The Wrong Letter\ntitle [38e547] = 送错的信\n# en: A delivery owl drops a scented pink letter in your porridge. It's addressed to someone else. It's a love letter. It's very, very passionate.\ntext [6296cf] = 一只送信猫头鹰把一封带香味的粉色信扔进了你的粥里。收件人不是你。是封情书。非常、非常热烈的情书。\n# en: A ruffled delivery owl dropping a scented pink envelope onto a breakfast plate, a student looking up in confusion.\nscene [2c78c3] = 一只羽毛凌乱的送信猫头鹰把一只带香味的粉色信封扔到早餐盘上，一个学生困惑地抬起头。\n# en: Deliver it to its owner\nopt.left.label [6dedfd] = 把它送给收信人\n# en: The recipient goes crimson and thanks you. Two weeks later, they're holding hands with the sender in the dining hall.\nopt.left.say.1 [364f07] = 收信人满脸通红地向你道谢。两周后，他们和写信人在餐厅里手牵着手。\n# en: Read it first\nopt.right.label [e728e4] = 先读一读\n# en: You read it. You will never be able to look the potions professor in the eye again.\nopt.right.say.1 [038e70] = 你读了。你再也无法直视魔药教授的眼睛了。\n\n@event life_money_from_home\n# en: A Parcel From Home\ntitle [0fa1f2] = 家里寄来的包裹\n# en: A parcel from home: a knitted scarf, a jar of jam, a note telling you to eat vegetables, and a small purse of coins.\ntext [e2ed27] = 一个家里寄来的包裹：一条织好的围巾、一罐果酱、一张叮嘱你多吃蔬菜的字条，还有一小袋硬币。\n# en: A brown paper parcel tied with string on a dormitory bed, a knitted scarf, a jar of jam and a small coin purse spilling out.\nscene [632059] = 宿舍床上一个用绳子捆着的牛皮纸包裹，露出一条针织围巾、一罐果酱和一只小钱袋。\n# en: Save the money\nopt.left.label [59078b] = 把钱存起来\n# en: You tuck it away. The jam lasts six days.\nopt.left.say.1 [c9f65f] = 你把钱收好。果酱吃了六天。\n# en: Spend it on a treat for your friends\nopt.right.label [c9ff29] = 请朋友们吃点好的\n# en: Cake from the village for everyone. You're popular for an entire afternoon.\nopt.right.say.1 [182e55] = 从镇上买了蛋糕分给大家。你受欢迎了整整一个下午。\n\n@event life_found_purse\n# en: A Purse in the Grass\ntitle [558b87] = 草丛里的钱袋\n# en: A fat velvet purse lies in the grass by the path. Thirty crowns at least. Embroidered initials: A.T.\ntext [bc1f08] = 小路边的草丛里躺着一只鼓鼓的天鹅绒钱袋。至少三十克朗。上面绣着缩写：A.T.\n# en: A velvet purse lying in the long grass beside a path, coins glinting at its open mouth, initials embroidered on it.\nscene [b64283] = 小路旁长草间躺着一只天鹅绒钱袋，袋口露出闪亮的硬币，上面绣着姓名缩写。\n# en: Return it to its owner\nopt.left.label [c3ae44] = 物归原主\n# en: A.T. turns out to be Aldric Thorne. He takes the purse and stares at you a long moment. \"Honest,\" he says. It sounds like a diagnosis.\nopt.left.say.1 [ccc630] = A.T. 原来是奥尔德里克·索恩。他接过钱袋，盯着你看了很久。“诚实。”他说。听起来像是诊断结果。\n# en: Keep it\nopt.right.label [87c2f5] = 据为己有\n# en: Thirty crowns richer. A few days later, Thorne mentions in class that he lost something. He looks at no one in particular.\nopt.right.say.1 [3a88fb] = 你多了三十克朗。几天后，索恩在课上提到他丢了东西。他没有特意看向任何人。\n\n@event life_peddler\n# en: The Travelling Peddler\ntitle [c295f6] = 行脚商人\n# en: A peddler's wagon has parked by the gates. \"Lucky charms! Genuine article! Thirty crowns, and fortune follows you for a season!\"\ntext [985dd5] = 一辆货郎车停在了校门边。“幸运护身符！货真价实！三十克朗，好运伴你一整季！”\n# en: A gaudy painted wagon at the academy gates, hung with lanterns and trinkets, a peddler with a gold tooth grinning.\nscene [aca905] = 学院门口一辆花哨的彩绘货车，挂满灯笼和小玩意儿，一个镶金牙的货郎咧嘴笑着。\n# en: Buy a lucky charm\nopt.left.label [00deab] = 买个幸运护身符\n# en: 30 gold\nopt.left.hint [54caf0] = 30 金币\n# en: It's a real one. Things go right all month. You start talking to it.\nopt.left.say.1 [eb2634] = 是真货。整个月诸事顺遂。你开始跟它说话了。\n# en: It turns your neck green. Fortune does not follow you. The peddler is long gone.\nopt.left.say.2 [a11a74] = 它把你的脖子染绿了。好运并没有跟着你。货郎早就跑没影了。\n# en: Just browse\nopt.right.label [dbbc5f] = 随便看看\n# en: You buy nothing and enjoy a sugared apple and the peddler's outrageous stories.\nopt.right.say.1 [017402] = 你什么都没买，享受了一只糖苹果和货郎天花乱坠的故事。\n\n@event life_bookshop\n# en: Sale at Grimsby's\ntitle [89ece4] = 格林斯比书店大促\n# en: Grimsby's Books is having a sale. An old copy of \"Advanced Arcane Theory\" for fifteen crowns. Normally forty. It's annotated, apparently, by a famous wizard.\ntext [e4e3d1] = 格林斯比书店在打折。一本旧版《高等奥术理论》只要十五克朗。平时要四十。据说上面有一位著名巫师的批注。\n# en: A crooked bookshop with books stacked to the ceiling, a hand-painted sign in the window reading HALF OFF, MOSTLY.\nscene [3daef9] = 一家歪歪扭扭的书店，书一直堆到天花板，橱窗上手绘的招牌写着：半价，大部分。\n# en: Buy it\nopt.left.label [0aa234] = 买下\n# en: 15 gold\nopt.left.hint [05537f] = 15 金币\n# en: The annotations are brilliant, rude and occasionally about the author's divorce. You learn a great deal.\nopt.left.say.1 [0f1da3] = 批注精彩、刻薄，偶尔还聊到作者的离婚。你学到了很多。\n# en: Leave it\nopt.right.label [3e2fed] = 算了\n# en: You walk away. You think about it for weeks.\nopt.right.say.1 [08ccd0] = 你走开了。接下来好几周你都在惦记它。\n\n@event life_new_robes\n# en: Shabby Robes\ntitle [a4fae7] = 破旧的长袍\n# en: Your robes are fraying at every hem. Someone at dinner called them \"vintage\". It was not a compliment.\ntext [afc5fd] = 你的长袍每一处下摆都磨毛了。晚饭时有人说它们很“复古”。那可不是夸奖。\n# en: A student in a hall mirror holding up a fraying sleeve, a tailor's flyer in their other hand.\nscene [c82b72] = 一个学生站在大厅的镜子前，举起磨毛的袖子，另一只手里拿着裁缝铺的传单。\n# en: Patch them yourself\nopt.left.label [475422] = 自己缝补\n# en: Your stitching is crooked but cheerful. Some people start copying it.\nopt.left.say.1 [019c7e] = 你的针脚歪歪扭扭，却很欢快。有些人开始模仿了。\n# en: Buy new robes (30 gold)\nopt.right.label [ed7264] = 买新长袍（30 金币）\n# en: 30 gold\nopt.right.hint [54caf0] = 30 金币\n# en: Midnight-blue wool, silver trim. People actually turn to look. You walk differently all week.\nopt.right.say.1 [2c96cc] = 午夜蓝的羊毛，银色镶边。人们真的会回头看。你一整周走路的姿势都不一样了。\n\n@event life_card_game\n# en: A Game of Wizard's Hand\ntitle [7c9c91] = 一局巫师牌\n# en: A late-night game of Wizard's Hand. The cards change faces when no one's looking. The pot is thirty crowns. The upper-years look hungry.\ntext [0d1104] = 深夜的一局巫师牌。没人看时，牌面会自己变。奖池三十克朗。高年级生们看起来很饿。\n# en: A smoky corner of the common room, students around a low table with cards and coins, one card floating and changing face.\nscene [404f4d] = 公共休息室烟雾缭绕的角落，学生们围着矮桌打牌，桌上堆着硬币，一张牌浮在空中变换牌面。\n# en: Play\nopt.left.label [ab6f83] = 下场\n# en: You read every bluff and keep a straight face. Thirty crowns and a reputation.\nopt.left.say.1 [36d8bf] = 你识破了每一次虚张声势，始终面不改色。三十克朗，外加名声。\n# en: You lose fifteen crowns and the upper-years' respect, in that order.\nopt.left.say.2 [47f34e] = 你输掉了十五克朗和高年级生的尊重，按这个顺序。\n# en: Watch and learn\nopt.right.label [37e8ef] = 看着学\n# en: You learn more watching than you would have playing. Mostly about who cheats.\nopt.right.say.1 [11df36] = 你看到的比下场玩学到的还多。主要是谁在出老千。\n\n@event life_essay_prize\n# en: The Essay Prize\ntitle [f26093] = 论文奖\n# en: The Lumen Essay Prize: thirty crowns for the best essay on \"Magic and Duty\". The deadline is Friday. You have an idea already.\ntext [04145b] = 流明论文奖：五十克朗，奖给以“魔法与责任”为题的最佳论文。周五截止。你已经有了一个想法。\n# en: A brass plaque on a noticeboard announcing THE LUMEN ESSAY PRIZE — 50 CROWNS, a quill pinned beneath it.\nscene [a84625] = 布告栏上一块黄铜牌宣布“流明论文奖——50 克朗”，下面钉着一支羽毛笔。\n# en: Enter\nopt.left.label [f863ad] = 参赛\n# en: You win. Your essay is read aloud at dinner. You don't know where to look.\nopt.left.say.1 [1ea809] = 你赢了。晚饭时你的论文被当众朗读。你不知道眼睛该往哪儿看。\n# en: You don't win, but the judges' notes are glowing. Next year, maybe.\nopt.left.say.2 [f33231] = 你没赢，但评委的点评赞不绝口。也许明年吧。\n# en: Skip it\nopt.right.label [9909cb] = 不参加\n# en: The winner reads their essay aloud at dinner. Yours would have been better. Probably.\nopt.right.say.1 [f95dff] = 晚饭时获奖者朗读了自己的论文。你的本来会更好。大概吧。\n\n@event life_charity\n# en: The Winter Fund\ntitle [04edf0] = 冬季基金\n# en: The village winter fund is collecting for families who can't afford fuel this winter. The jar is half full.\ntext [cf1ae3] = 小镇冬季基金正在为今冬买不起柴火的家庭募捐。募捐罐已经满了一半。\n# en: A collection jar shaped like a lantern on the dining hall table, half full of coins, a hand-lettered sign: FOR THE VILLAGE WINTER FUND.\nscene [cb02b0] = 餐厅桌上一只灯笼形状的募捐罐，半满的硬币，手写的牌子：小镇冬季基金。\n# en: Donate fifteen crowns\nopt.left.label [645481] = 捐十五克朗\n# en: 15 gold\nopt.left.hint [05537f] = 15 金币\n# en: Your name goes on a little list by the jar. You feel warm, which is the point.\nopt.left.say.1 [e202cc] = 你的名字被写进了罐子旁边的小名单。你觉得心里暖暖的，这正是意义所在。\n# en: Walk past\nopt.right.label [d5359c] = 走过去\n# en: You walk past. The jar fills anyway. You think about it on cold nights.\nopt.right.say.1 [2eee4f] = 你走了过去。罐子还是被装满了。寒冷的夜里，你会想起这件事。\n\n@event life_shady_offer\n# en: A Quiet Job\ntitle [c6d337] = 一份安静的差事\n# en: A hooded figure behind the tavern: \"Deliver this envelope to the boathouse at midnight. Don't open it. Thirty crowns.\" The envelope is warm.\ntext [0048f1] = 酒馆后巷里一个戴兜帽的人：“午夜把这封信送到船屋。别拆开。三十克朗。”信封是温的。\n# en: A hooded figure in an alley behind the village tavern holding out a sealed envelope, a purse visible in their other hand.\nscene [7d377f] = 镇上酒馆后的小巷里，一个戴兜帽的身影递出一封封好的信，另一只手里露出钱袋。\n# en: Refuse\nopt.left.label [cada35] = 拒绝\n# en: The figure melts into the dark. The next morning, someone's boat is missing.\nopt.left.say.1 [9eb4b8] = 那人融进了黑暗。第二天早上，有人的船不见了。\n# en: Do the job\nopt.right.label [573204] = 接下这份差事\n# en: You make the drop. Bram is watching from the boathouse window. He writes something down.\nopt.right.say.1 [e54d21] = 你送到了。布拉姆正从船屋的窗口看着。他记下了什么。\n# en: You make the drop. Nobody sees. Thirty crowns, and a slightly cold feeling in your stomach.\nopt.right.say.2 [43913a] = 你送到了。没人看见。三十克朗，外加胃里一点凉飕飕的感觉。\n\n@event life_food_poisoning\n# en: The Fish Was Off\ntitle [e4f066] = 鱼坏了\n# en: The fish pie at dinner was a mistake. Your stomach is staging a revolution.\ntext [cd4a4a] = 晚饭的鱼肉馅饼是个错误。你的胃正在闹革命。\n# en: A student curled on a dormitory bed clutching their stomach, a half-eaten fish pie on the bedside table.\nscene [4844ba] = 一个学生蜷缩在宿舍床上捂着肚子，床头柜上放着吃了一半的鱼肉馅饼。\n# en: Ride it out\nopt.left.label [43478c] = 硬扛过去\n# en: A miserable night. You never look at fish pie again.\nopt.left.say.1 [6ff02f] = 悲惨的一夜。你再也不看鱼肉馅饼一眼了。\n# en: Get a tonic from the infirmary\nopt.right.label [16a4f3] = 去医务室拿药\n# en: The tonic tastes like old pennies, but it works.\nopt.right.say.1 [f9e7fb] = 药尝起来像旧铜板，但是管用。\n\n@event life_self_doubt\n# en: Imposter\ntitle [ee3a4e] = 冒牌货\n# en: Everyone around you seems to belong here more than you do. They cast faster, learn quicker, laugh easier. Maybe the letter was a mistake.\ntext [5f044e] = 你身边的每个人似乎都比你更属于这里。他们施法更快、学得更快、笑得更自在。也许那封录取信是个错误。\n# en: A student sitting alone on the edge of a fountain at night, staring at their reflection broken up by ripples.\nscene [1842d0] = 夜里一个学生独自坐在喷泉边沿，盯着被涟漪打碎的倒影。\n# en: Write down everything you've learned\nopt.left.label [19e441] = 写下你学到的一切\n# en: The list is longer than you expected. Much longer. You pin it above your bed.\nopt.left.say.1 [c065df] = 清单比你想象的长。长得多。你把它钉在了床头。\n# en: Talk to someone about it\nopt.right.label [9fbd4a] = 找人聊聊\n# en: Your friend laughs — not at you. \"You? Everyone thinks you're the one who belongs.\" It helps more than you can say.\nopt.right.say.1 [eac20a] = 你的朋友笑了——不是在笑你。“你？大家都觉得你才是那个真正属于这里的人。”这比你能说出口的还要管用。\n# en: You talk to the night porter. He tells you everyone feels this way. You decide to believe him.\nopt.right.say.2 [bebf73] = 你和守夜门房聊了聊。他告诉你每个人都有这种感觉。你决定相信他。\n\n@event life_sleepwalking\n# en: Sleepwalking\ntitle [8fc4f7] = 梦游\n# en: You wake up on the battlements in your pyjamas, at the very edge, with no memory of how you got there. Your feet are wet with lake water.\ntext [8d1ce1] = 你醒来时穿着睡衣站在城垛上，就在最边缘，完全不记得自己是怎么上来的。你的脚被湖水打湿了。\n# en: A student in pyjamas standing on a moonlit battlement, eyes closed, one foot raised over the edge, an owl watching.\nscene [ee10e3] = 月光下一个穿睡衣的学生站在城垛上，双眼紧闭，一只脚悬在边缘外，一只猫头鹰在旁观。\n# en: Tell Sister Amaranth\nopt.left.label [3f4d80] = 告诉阿玛兰斯修女\n# en: A sleeping draught and a stern warning. It doesn't happen again.\nopt.left.say.1 [c0917f] = 一剂安眠药和一番严厉的警告。再也没发生过。\n# en: Keep it to yourself\nopt.right.label [09de43] = 自己藏着\n# en: It happens twice more. Each time you're facing the lake. Each time, you're a little closer to the water.\nopt.right.say.1 [bd1bd8] = 又发生了两次。每一次你都面朝湖水。每一次，你都离水更近一点。\n# en: It doesn't happen again. You start locking your door from the inside, just in case.\nopt.right.say.2 [3c9aa9] = 再也没发生过。你开始从里面反锁房门，以防万一。\n\n@event life_quiet_afternoon\n# en: A Quiet Afternoon\ntitle [4b76f8] = 安静的午后\n# en: For once, nothing is due, nobody needs you, and the sun is out. A whole afternoon, unclaimed.\ntext [135c6b] = 难得一次，没有作业要交，没有人需要你，太阳也出来了。一整个午后，无人认领。\n# en: A student lying in the long grass under an oak tree, a book open on their chest, clouds drifting above the towers.\nscene [9574c3] = 一个学生躺在橡树下的长草里，一本书摊在胸口，云朵飘过塔楼上空。\n# en: Nap under the oak tree\nopt.left.label [320d04] = 在橡树下打个盹\n# en: You wake to find a bird sitting on your book. It seems content. So are you.\nopt.left.say.1 [6fb673] = 你醒来时发现一只鸟落在你的书上。它看起来很满足。你也是。\n# en: Practise spells in the meadow\nopt.right.label [2c28fa] = 在草地上练习咒语\n# en: You make dandelion seeds dance in spirals for an hour. Nobody sees. It's perfect.\nopt.right.say.1 [2cbd71] = 你让蒲公英的种子跳了一个小时的螺旋舞。没人看见。完美。\n\n@event life_injury_stairs\n# en: The Trick Step\ntitle [cc11b3] = 陷阱台阶\n# en: The ninth step on the north stair is a trick step. Everyone knows. You forgot. Your leg sinks to the knee and the stair holds on.\ntext [9cc7ba] = 北楼梯的第九级是陷阱台阶。人人都知道。你忘了。你的腿陷到了膝盖，台阶紧紧咬住不放。\n# en: A stone staircase with one step glowing faintly, a student mid-fall, books flying everywhere.\nscene [02874d] = 一道石阶上有一级微微发光，一个学生正往下摔，书本飞得到处都是。\n# en: Wait for someone to help\nopt.left.label [8e8e6b] = 等人来帮忙\n# en: Forty minutes later, a first-year pulls you out. You're late for everything.\nopt.left.say.1 [be4901] = 四十分钟后，一个一年级生把你拔了出来。你什么都迟到了。\n# en: Wrench yourself free\nopt.right.label [ec2221] = 硬把腿拔出来\n# en: You pull free with a pop. Your shoe stays in the stair forever.\nopt.right.say.1 [652719] = 你“啵”的一声挣脱了。你的鞋永远留在了台阶里。\n# en: Something in your knee goes click. Not a good click.\nopt.right.say.2 [17703b] = 你膝盖里有什么东西咔哒响了一声。不是好的那种咔哒。\n\n@event life_lake_fever\n# en: Lake Fever Season\ntitle [5e2ca1] = 湖热病季节\n# en: Half your dormitory has Lake Fever. Noses are glowing blue. You feel a tickle in your throat.\ntext [77a4bc] = 你宿舍里一半的人得了湖热病。鼻子都泛着蓝光。你觉得喉咙有点痒。\n# en: A dormitory where half the beds hold sniffling students with faintly blue-glowing noses, tissues everywhere.\nscene [87b40c] = 宿舍里半数床位躺着吸鼻子的学生，鼻头泛着淡淡的蓝光，纸巾扔得到处都是。\n# en: Dose up on Maud's tea\nopt.left.label [f3b875] = 猛灌莫德的茶\n# en: Maud's lemon-and-thunder tea keeps you upright through the whole outbreak.\nopt.left.say.1 [b6b4d0] = 莫德那杯柠檬加雷声的茶让你挺过了整场流行病。\n# en: The tea from the kitchens isn't the same. Your nose starts glowing on Thursday.\nopt.left.say.2 [9b0859] = 厨房的茶不是一回事。周四你的鼻子开始发光了。\n# en: You dodge it. Somehow.\nopt.left.say.3 [61efce] = 你躲过去了。不知怎么做到的。\n# en: Keep going as normal\nopt.right.label [5303bd] = 照常生活\n# en: You keep going as normal for exactly two days.\nopt.right.say.1 [68c5c6] = 你照常生活了整整两天。\n# en: Iron constitution. You sail through. Everyone hates you a little.\nopt.right.say.2 [7e0fc1] = 铁打的体质。你安然度过。大家都有点恨你。\n\n@event life_first_snow\n# en: First Snow\ntitle [16a8b8] = 初雪\n# en: The first snow of winter falls overnight and the whole academy wakes up giddy. Classes are optional, officially. Unofficially, they're cancelled by snowball.\ntext [59861c] = 冬天的第一场雪在夜里落下，整个学院醒来时都兴奋不已。官方说法是课程自愿参加。非官方说法是课程被雪球取消了。\n# en: The academy courtyard blanketed in fresh snow, students mid-snowball fight, a snowman wearing a professor's hat.\nscene [c59afe] = 学院庭院铺满新雪，学生们正在打雪仗，一个雪人戴着教授的帽子。\n# en: Join the snowball war\nopt.left.label [dd3116] = 加入雪仗\n# en: You take a snowball to the face from the headmistress herself. Her aim is terrifying.\nopt.left.say.1 [286cf4] = 你脸上挨了一个雪球——是院长本人扔的。她的准头吓死人。\n# en: Your side wins. You build a snow-fort that lasts until Thawmoon.\nopt.left.say.2 [d4a0aa] = 你们这边赢了。你堆的雪堡一直立到了融雪月。\n# en: Hot chocolate by the fire\nopt.right.label [cba09c] = 在炉边喝热可可\n# en: You watch from the window, warm and smug, while the snowballs fly.\nopt.right.say.1 [da3e9c] = 你在窗边看着雪球横飞，暖和又得意。\n\n@event life_spring_cleaning\n# en: Spring Cleaning\ntitle [757d73] = 春季大扫除\n# en: Spring cleaning. The brooms are enchanted, but the enchantment needs supervising. Your dorm's brooms have formed a union.\ntext [cd3829] = 春季大扫除。扫帚都施了魔法，但魔法需要监督。你们宿舍的扫帚成立了工会。\n# en: A dormitory with every window thrown open, brooms sweeping by themselves, dust swirling in sunbeams.\nscene [d4aefd] = 宿舍里所有窗户都敞开着，扫帚自己扫着地，灰尘在阳光里打转。\n# en: Negotiate with the brooms\nopt.left.label [086b0c] = 和扫帚谈判\n# en: You agree to one day off a week and a polish. The dorm sparkles.\nopt.left.say.1 [627f3a] = 你同意每周给它们放一天假，外加一次抛光。宿舍焕然一新。\n# en: The brooms do not accept your terms. They sweep you into a cupboard.\nopt.left.say.2 [56e3e0] = 扫帚们不接受你的条件。它们把你扫进了柜子里。\n# en: Clean by hand\nopt.right.label [7a78f4] = 亲手打扫\n# en: An afternoon of scrubbing. It's oddly satisfying. You find three lost quills and a sandwich from Thawmoon.\nopt.right.say.1 [ffa004] = 擦洗了一个下午。出奇地让人满足。你找到了三支丢失的羽毛笔和一个融雪月的三明治。\n\n@event life_summer_heat\n# en: The Heat\ntitle [24ddd6] = 酷暑\n# en: The heat is unbearable. Even the gargoyles are sweating. The lake is crowded, the library stifling, the dormitories ovens.\ntext [b86aba] = 热得让人受不了。连石像鬼都在冒汗。湖边挤满了人，图书馆闷得透不过气，宿舍就是烤炉。\n# en: Students lying flat on the cool stone floor of a shaded colloquy, fanning themselves with exam papers.\nscene [ecf9e7] = 学生们平躺在阴凉讲堂冰凉的石地板上，用考卷扇着风。\n# en: Cast a cooling charm on your room\nopt.left.label [71928c] = 给房间施个降温咒\n# en: Your room becomes an icebox. People pay to visit. You let them for free. Mostly.\nopt.left.say.1 [4b7a2e] = 你的房间变成了冰窖。有人付钱来参观。你让他们免费进来。大部分人。\n# en: You make it colder. Briefly. Then hotter. Then it rains inside.\nopt.left.say.2 [fdb685] = 你让它变冷了。短暂地。然后变得更热了。然后屋里下起了雨。\n# en: Sleep on the roof\nopt.right.label [3e5cc0] = 睡在屋顶上\n# en: You sleep under the stars with half the dormitory. It's the best idea anyone's had all year.\nopt.right.say.1 [d71668] = 你和半个宿舍的人一起睡在星空下。这是今年所有人想出来的最好的主意。\n\n@event life_harvest_festival\n# en: The Harvest Feast\ntitle [d8f2a6] = 丰收宴\n# en: The Harvest Feast: pies the size of cartwheels, cider, and a pumpkin carved to look like the headmistress, which she pretends not to notice.\ntext [e532a4] = 丰收宴：车轮那么大的馅饼，苹果酒，还有一个刻成院长模样的南瓜——她假装没注意到。\n# en: Long tables groaning under pies and roast vegetables, candles floating above, a giant pumpkin carved like the headmistress.\nscene [749624] = 长桌上堆满了馅饼和烤蔬菜，蜡烛浮在上方，一个巨大的南瓜被刻成了院长的模样。\n# en: Eat until you can't move\nopt.left.label [36bf14] = 吃到动不了为止\n# en: You eat three helpings of everything. You're carried back to the dorm by friends. Worth it.\nopt.left.say.1 [0f7497] = 每样东西你都吃了三份。朋友们把你抬回了宿舍。值了。\n# en: Help in the kitchens for extra pay\nopt.right.label [9e76c8] = 去厨房帮忙赚外快\n# en: You serve pie to the entire academy. The cooks slip you coins and a whole pie of your own.\nopt.right.say.1 [05b6e2] = 你给全院的人端馅饼。厨师们悄悄塞给你一些硬币，还有一整个属于你的馅饼。\n\n@event life_rival_academy\n# en: Visitors From Thornwick\ntitle [ac57b9] = 荆木来客\n# en: A delegation from Thornwick Academy is visiting. Their students are smug, polished and very loudly unimpressed with Lumenhold's plumbing.\ntext [6ee3f7] = 荆木学院的代表团来访。他们的学生傲慢、体面，而且非常大声地对流明堡的水管表示不屑。\n# en: A delegation of students in green robes marching through the gates, noses raised, a Lumenhold crowd watching suspiciously.\nscene [78c6a2] = 一队身穿绿袍的学生昂首阔步地走进校门，流明堡的人群怀疑地注视着他们。\n# en: Be a gracious host\nopt.left.label [071642] = 做个大方的东道主\n# en: You show them the observatory. One of them admits, grudgingly, that it's better than theirs.\nopt.left.say.1 [a32053] = 你带他们参观了天文台。其中一人不情不愿地承认，这比他们的好。\n# en: Defend Lumenhold's honour\nopt.right.label [cfa83c] = 捍卫流明堡的荣誉\n# en: You challenge their best to a friendly duel and win. The Thornwick students are quieter at dinner.\nopt.right.say.1 [291905] = 你向他们最强的人发起友谊决斗，并且赢了。晚饭时荆木的学生安静多了。\n# en: You challenge their best to a friendly duel and lose, quite unfriendly. Thornwick will be insufferable.\nopt.right.say.2 [ad3104] = 你向他们最强的人发起友谊决斗，然后输得一点也不友谊。荆木的人会嚣张得让人受不了了。\n\n@event life_lost_firstyear\n# en: A Lost First-Year\ntitle [ed808e] = 迷路的一年级生\n# en: A tiny first-year is standing in the middle of the corridor, clutching an upside-down timetable and trying very hard not to cry.\ntext [a1cbbc] = 一个小个子一年级生站在走廊中间，攥着一张拿倒了的课表，拼命忍着不哭。\n# en: A tiny first-year in enormous robes standing in the middle of a junction of five identical corridors, clutching a timetable upside down.\nscene [6a5ef7] = 一个穿着宽大长袍的小个子一年级生站在五条一模一样的走廊交汇处，倒拿着课表。\n# en: Point the way and hurry on\nopt.left.label [ee0d43] = 指个路就赶紧走\n# en: You point. They go the wrong way anyway. You hope they're all right.\nopt.left.say.1 [f7d5b2] = 你指了路。对方还是走错了。你希望对方没事。\n# en: Walk them to class\nopt.right.label [e53c7b] = 送对方去教室\n# en: You walk them all the way and they talk the entire time. You're late, but they've decided you're their hero.\nopt.right.say.1 [344963] = 你一路把对方送到，对方一路说个不停。你迟到了，但对方已经认定你是自己的英雄。\n\n@event life_frog_escape\n# en: The Frogs Are Out\ntitle [abdd0d] = 青蛙跑出来了\n# en: Someone knocked over the frog crate in Potions. Thirty frogs are loose. One has climbed into the professor's hat. He has not noticed yet.\ntext [0c896d] = 有人在魔药课上打翻了青蛙箱。三十只青蛙跑了出来。一只爬进了教授的帽子里。他还没发现。\n# en: A potions classroom overrun by dozens of hopping green frogs, one sitting smugly on the professor's desk.\nscene [8a2633] = 魔药教室里到处是蹦跳的绿青蛙，一只得意地蹲在教授的讲台上。\n# en: Help round them up\nopt.left.label [d47151] = 帮忙把它们抓回来\n# en: You catch eleven, a personal best. The professor finds the hat frog at dinner. You were not involved.\nopt.left.say.1 [87225e] = 你抓了十一只，个人最佳纪录。教授在晚饭时发现了帽子里的那只。与你无关。\n# en: Quietly let a few escape out the window\nopt.right.label [73e9bc] = 悄悄放几只从窗户逃走\n# en: Five frogs hop to freedom in the lake. You feel like a liberator.\nopt.right.say.1 [076f02] = 五只青蛙跳进湖里重获自由。你觉得自己像个解放者。\n\n@event life_fortune_teller\n# en: The Village Fortune-Teller\ntitle [203728] = 镇上的算命婆\n# en: In the village, an old fortune-teller beckons you into her tent. \"Three crowns for your future, dearie. Eight for a good one.\"\ntext [b16d5c] = 在镇上，一个算命的老婆婆招手让你进她的帐篷。“三克朗算你的未来，孩子。八克朗算个好的。”\n# en: A beaded doorway into a tiny dim tent, an old woman with a crystal ball and too many rings beckoning.\nscene [0e06ff] = 一顶昏暗小帐篷的珠帘门口，一个戴着太多戒指、捧着水晶球的老妇人在招手。\n# en: Pay three crowns\nopt.left.label [557fdf] = 付三克朗\n# en: \"You will meet a tall stranger.\" You already have. Several. It's an academy.\nopt.left.say.1 [74a51c] = “你会遇到一个高个子陌生人。”你已经遇到了。好几个。这是所学院。\n# en: \"Beware of water.\" She won't say more. You think about the lake a lot.\nopt.left.say.2 [6db734] = “小心水。”她不肯再多说。你常常想起那片湖。\n# en: Pay eight crowns\nopt.right.label [196642] = 付八克朗\n# en: Your future is wonderful. She's very specific. You are fairly sure she's making it up, and you don't care.\nopt.right.say.1 [7d6032] = 你的未来美好极了。她说得非常具体。你很确定她是在瞎编，而你不在乎。\n\n@event life_volunteer\n# en: Clean-Up Duty\ntitle [2e9a08] = 清理值日\n# en: After the storm, the grounds are a wreck. Volunteers needed to help clear branches and rebuild the hen-house. No pay. Lots of mud.\ntext [540f81] = 风暴过后，校园一片狼藉。需要志愿者帮忙清理断枝、重建鸡舍。没有报酬。满地泥巴。\n# en: Students in rolled-up sleeves raking leaves on the great lawn, a groundskeeper directing them with a pitchfork.\nscene [5de831] = 卷起袖子的学生们在大草坪上耙落叶，一位园丁拿着草叉指挥。\n# en: Volunteer\nopt.left.label [39a371] = 报名志愿\n# en: A long day of mud and laughter. An old woman in a straw hat brings everyone cocoa and remembers your name.\nopt.left.say.1 [e39352] = 漫长的一天，满身泥巴和笑声。一位戴草帽的老妇人给每个人送来了热可可，还记住了你的名字。\n# en: Stay in and study\nopt.right.label [6d8b57] = 留在屋里学习\n# en: You study through the shouting and hammering outside. The hen-house is very nice when it's done.\nopt.right.say.1 [1e9737] = 你在外面的喧闹和敲打声中学习。鸡舍建好后很漂亮。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/people_others.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/people_others.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event kael_snowball\n# en: Snow in Thawmoon\ntitle [1b8dde] = 融雪月里的雪\n# en: It's snowing in the courtyard. Only in a small circle, around Kael, who is holding a snowball and looking at it as though he's never been allowed to throw one.\ntext [540758] = 庭院里在下雪。只在一小圈范围里，围着凯尔下。他手里捧着一个雪球，盯着它看，好像从来没被允许扔过雪球。\n# en: A courtyard where it is snowing in one small circle only, a boy in a fur coat standing at its centre holding a perfect snowball.\nscene [8ebdb4] = 庭院里只有一小圈地方在下雪，一个穿毛皮大衣的少年站在中央，捧着一个完美的雪球。\n# en: Leave him to it\nopt.left.label [b84f2c] = 让他自己待着\n# en: When you pass again, the circle of snow is gone, and there's a small, lopsided snowman in its place.\nopt.left.say.1 [631d77] = 你再经过时，那圈雪已经不见了，原地立着一个歪歪扭扭的小雪人。\n# en: Throw the first one\nopt.right.label [c2945e] = 先扔出第一个\n# en: Your snowball hits him in the ear. He stares. Then he laughs — a real laugh, startled out of him — and the snow triples.\nopt.right.say.1 [5c45fb] = 你的雪球砸中了他的耳朵。他愣愣地看着你。然后他笑了——一声真正的、被吓出来的笑——雪下得大了三倍。\n\n@event kael_cold_hands\n# en: Cold Hands\ntitle [a4a2d1] = 冰冷的手\n# en: Kael's hands are always cold. In the library tonight they're so cold the ink in his pen has frozen. He's trying to thaw it with his breath, embarrassed.\ntext [2a76e5] = 凯尔的手总是冰的。今晚在图书馆里，冷到他笔里的墨水都冻住了。他正难为情地往笔上哈气，想把它化开。\n# en: Two students sitting close in a library alcove, one holding the other's frost-white hand between both of theirs.\nscene [d7ab82] = 图书馆的壁龛里，两个学生挨坐在一起，一人用双手捧着另一人霜白的手。\n# en: Lend him your gloves\nopt.left.label [7bd15d] = 把手套借给他\n# en: He wears them for the rest of the term. They're far too small. He doesn't care. Your own fingers go numb on the walk back.\nopt.left.say.1 [6436b9] = 他戴了一整个学期。手套小得离谱。他不在乎。回去的路上，你自己的手指冻得发麻。\n# en: Warm his hands in yours\nopt.right.label [e4839d] = 用你的手暖他的手\n# en: He goes very still. Then, slowly, the frost on his fingertips melts. \"Nobody's ever been warm enough,\" he says quietly. \"Before.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [79ec72] = 他一动不动。然后，慢慢地，他指尖的霜融化了。“从来没有人足够温暖，”他轻声说，“在你之前。”\n\n@event kael_confession\n# en: Thaw\ntitle [448405] = 解冻\n# en: \"I was taught not to want things,\" Kael says. \"Wanting things is how they control you.\" Frost blooms across the window behind him. \"I want you. I'm sorry. I don't know what to do with it.\"\ntext [6c0866] = “我被教导不要想要任何东西，”凯尔说，“想要东西，他们就能控制你。”他身后的窗户上绽开了霜花。“我想要你。对不起。我不知道该拿它怎么办。”\n# en: Frost-ferns spreading across a dark window behind a pale boy who is, for once, looking directly at someone.\nscene [992624] = 一个苍白的少年身后，黑暗的窗上蔓延着霜蕨；这一次，他直视着某个人。\n# en: Gently say no\nopt.left.label [f46b92] = 温柔地拒绝\n# en: He nods, as if he expected it. \"Thank you for being honest. Nobody is.\" The frost on the window fades slowly.\nopt.left.say.1 [b81eae] = 他点点头，像是早有预料。“谢谢你的诚实。没有人诚实过。”窗上的霜慢慢褪去了。\n# en: Show him what to do with it\nopt.right.label [6ebbe7] = 告诉他该怎么办\n# en: You kiss him. Every window in the corridor frosts over at once, and then, very slowly, melts.\nopt.right.say.1 [775fed] = 你吻了他。走廊里所有的窗户一下子都结满了霜，然后，非常非常缓慢地，融化了。\n\n@event bram_intro\n# en: Running in the Halls\ntitle [b6d158] = 走廊奔跑\n# en: \"Running in the halls.\" A burly prefect with a red sash steps into your path, ledger already open. \"Name?\" You weren't running. You were walking briskly.\ntext [db3db6] = “在走廊里奔跑。”一个系着红绶带的魁梧级长挡住你的去路，记过簿已经翻开了。“名字？”你没在跑。你只是走得比较快。\n# en: A burly prefect with a red sash blocking a corridor, a demerit ledger open in one hand and a quill poised.\nscene [de700d] = 一个系红绶带的魁梧级长堵在走廊里，一手翻开记过簿，羽毛笔蓄势待发。\n# en: Accept the demerit\nopt.left.label [758a3a] = 接受记过\n# en: He writes your name very slowly, enjoying it. Bram Hollis. You will learn to recognise his footsteps.\nopt.left.say.1 [67c2a7] = 他非常缓慢地写下你的名字，乐在其中。布拉姆·霍利斯。你会学会辨认他的脚步声的。\n# en: Argue\nopt.right.label [2f9da9] = 争辩\n# en: \"Walking briskly,\" you insist. Two demerits: one for running, one for \"lip\". Some students nearby clap quietly.\nopt.right.say.1 [4c2d1e] = “是快走。”你坚持道。记两次过：一次因为奔跑，一次因为“顶嘴”。附近几个学生悄悄鼓起了掌。\n\n@event bram_hall_tax\n# en: The Hall Tax\ntitle [86f712] = 走廊税\n# en: Bram has invented a \"hall tax\" for using the east staircase. Eight crowns. \"Maintenance,\" he says. The first-years are paying. He looks at you expectantly.\ntext [7f337c] = 布拉姆发明了一种使用东楼梯的“走廊税”。八克朗。“维护费。”他说。一年级生们都在交。他满怀期待地看着你。\n# en: A prefect leaning against a stone archway, palm out, a line of nervous first-years shuffling past him.\nscene [77c192] = 一名级长靠在石拱门上，摊着手掌，一排紧张的一年级生从他身边挪过。\n# en: Pay it\nopt.left.label [e4d5d6] = 交钱\n# en: He pockets the coins. \"Pleasure.\" You take the west staircase forever after.\nopt.left.say.1 [ed6293] = 他把硬币揣进兜里。“荣幸。”从此以后你永远走西楼梯。\n# en: Refuse loudly\nopt.right.label [c7392c] = 大声拒绝\n# en: The first-years stop paying too. Bram is furious. He'll find a way to make you pay later. He always does.\nopt.right.say.1 [9b89a4] = 一年级生们也不交了。布拉姆气坏了。他迟早会想办法让你付出代价。他总会的。\n# en: Your refusal spreads. Within a week, nobody pays the hall tax. Bram drops it, scowling.\nopt.right.say.2 [7da052] = 你的拒绝传开了。不出一周，再也没人交走廊税。布拉姆黑着脸取消了它。\n\n@event bram_sabotage\n# en: Missing Notes\ntitle [a08f2a] = 消失的笔记\n# en: Two days before exams, your revision notes vanish from your desk. All of them. You saw a red sash in the corridor.\ntext [bb41e4] = 考试前两天，你的复习笔记从桌上消失了。全部。你在走廊里看见了一抹红绶带。\n# en: An empty desk drawer, a few scattered pages on the floor, a red prefect's sash glimpsed disappearing around a corner.\nscene [a3c002] = 一只空荡荡的抽屉，地上散落着几页纸，一条红色的级长绶带消失在拐角。\n# en: Rewrite them from memory\nopt.left.label [429e64] = 凭记忆重写\n# en: You rewrite everything in one furious night. Your new notes are better than the old ones. Take that, Bram.\nopt.left.say.1 [2171a3] = 你愤怒地熬了一夜，把一切重写了一遍。新笔记比旧的还好。吃瘪吧，布拉姆。\n# en: You rewrite what you can. It isn't enough. Exams go worse than they should.\nopt.left.say.2 [0f1b73] = 你重写了能记住的部分。不够。考试考得比该有的差。\n# en: Search his room\nopt.right.label [8fc51c] = 搜他的房间\n# en: You find your notes under his mattress, and also a teddy bear. You take both. You return the bear. Eventually.\nopt.right.say.1 [7aa561] = 你在他床垫下找到了你的笔记，还有一只泰迪熊。你两样都拿走了。熊你还回去了。最终。\n# en: He catches you in his room. That's two weeks of detention, and he gets to supervise.\nopt.right.say.2 [297e67] = 他在自己房间里逮住了你。两周禁闭，由他亲自监督。\n\n@event bram_crying\n# en: Behind the Stables\ntitle [321daa] = 马厩后面\n# en: Behind the stables, you find Bram Hollis sitting on a bucket, crying into a crumpled letter. He sees you and freezes. \"If you tell anyone,\" he begins, but his voice cracks.\ntext [b7fc13] = 在马厩后面，你发现布拉姆·霍利斯坐在一只水桶上，对着一封揉皱的信哭。他看见你，僵住了。“你要是敢告诉别人——”他开口，声音却哑了。\n# en: A big prefect sitting on an upturned bucket behind the stables, a crumpled letter in his fist, shoulders shaking.\nscene [a696df] = 一个高大的级长坐在马厩后倒扣的水桶上，拳头里攥着揉皱的信，肩膀在发抖。\n# en: Walk away and never mention it\nopt.left.label [6b7a64] = 走开，永远不提\n# en: You leave. You never tell anyone. Somehow, he knows you didn't. His demerits become fewer.\nopt.left.say.1 [27fbd6] = 你走开了。你从没告诉任何人。不知怎么的，他知道你没说。他给你记的过变少了。\n# en: Sit down and ask what happened\nopt.right.label [bf652a] = 坐下来，问他出了什么事\n# en: His father. A letter saying he's a disappointment, again. \"He was a prefect too,\" Bram says. \"Head boy. I'm just... loud.\" He wipes his face. \"Don't be nice to me. I don't know what to do with it.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [0fc8cf] = 是他父亲。又一封说他令人失望的信。“他以前也是级长，”布拉姆说，“学生会主席。我只是……嗓门大。”他擦了擦脸。“别对我好。我不知道该拿它怎么办。”\n\n@event bram_turns\n# en: An Unexpected Ally\ntitle [fb9811] = 意外的盟友\n# en: Two sixth-years corner you by the boathouse, looking for trouble. Then a red sash steps between you. \"Problem?\" says Bram, cracking his knuckles. They leave fast.\ntext [68c10e] = 两个六年级生在船屋边把你堵住，想找茬。这时一条红绶带插到了你们中间。“有问题吗？”布拉姆捏着指关节说。那两人很快就走了。\n# en: A prefect stepping between a cornered student and a pair of older bullies, arms crossed.\nscene [b5e93a] = 一名级长抱着双臂挡在被逼到角落的学生和两个高年级恶霸之间。\n# en: Thank him awkwardly\nopt.left.label [a37131] = 别扭地道谢\n# en: \"Don't make it weird,\" he says, and walks off. He's smiling, slightly.\nopt.left.say.1 [bd632c] = “别搞得这么肉麻。”他说完就走了。他在笑，有一点。\n# en: Offer your hand\nopt.right.label [1f34dd] = 伸出手\n# en: He stares at your hand. Then he shakes it, hard. \"Fine. Friends. But I'm still giving you demerits.\" He does. Fewer.\nopt.right.say.1 [5c04b4] = 他盯着你的手。然后用力握住。“行吧。朋友。但我照样给你记过。”他照样记。少了些。\n\n@event bram_duel\n# en: A Formal Challenge\ntitle [0903a6] = 正式挑战\n# en: Bram throws a glove at your feet in front of half the dormitory. \"Formal challenge. The dueling green, right now. Unless you're a coward.\" Everyone is watching.\ntext [28f81c] = 布拉姆当着半个宿舍的人把一只手套扔在你脚下。“正式挑战。决斗草坪，现在就去。除非你是个懦夫。”所有人都在看。\n# en: A white glove lying on a dormitory floor, a red sash folded beside it, a crowd peering through the doorway.\nscene [c0de53] = 宿舍地板上一只白手套，旁边叠着一条红绶带，门口挤满了张望的人。\n# en: Decline\nopt.left.label [eaef39] = 拒绝\n# en: \"Coward,\" he says, delighted. It sticks for a while. Then people forget. Mostly.\nopt.left.say.1 [821d12] = “懦夫。”他兴高采烈地说。这个外号跟了你一阵子。后来大家就忘了。大体上。\n# en: Accept\nopt.right.label [b26149] = 接受\n# en: You disarm him in three moves. The crowd goes wild. Bram, on the grass, starts laughing. \"Fine,\" he says. \"Fine.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [5700d2] = 你三招就缴了他的械。人群沸腾了。躺在草地上的布拉姆笑了起来。“行，”他说，“行。”\n# en: He wins. But it takes him a full ten minutes, and he's limping too. He nods to you after. Grudging respect.\nopt.right.say.2 [a5c4a7] = 他赢了。但足足花了十分钟，而且他也一瘸一拐的。事后他朝你点了点头。不情不愿的尊重。\n\n@event thorne_intro\n# en: The First Question\ntitle [7b3e5e] = 第一个问题\n# en: First Arcana lecture. Professor Thorne surveys the room like a hawk over a field of mice. His finger lands on you. \"You. What is the first law of the Arcane?\"\ntext [969362] = 第一堂奥术课。索恩教授扫视教室，像一只盯着田鼠的鹰。他的手指落在你身上。“你。奥术第一定律是什么？”\n# en: A steep lecture theatre, a stern grey-bearded professor pointing a long finger at one student in the middle row.\nscene [662f2a] = 陡峭的阶梯讲堂，一位严厉的灰胡子教授用修长的手指指着中间一排的一个学生。\n# en: Admit you don't know\nopt.left.label [a10b0e] = 承认你不知道\n# en: \"Correct answer,\" he says. \"The first law is: know what you don't know. The rest of you, write that down.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [7cfbd1] = “正确答案，”他说，“第一定律是：知道自己不知道什么。其余的人，记下来。”\n# en: Take a guess\nopt.right.label [b3c370] = 猜一个\n# en: \"Energy follows intention.\" His eyebrows twitch. \"Acceptable.\" The room exhales.\nopt.right.say.1 [a4daea] = “能量追随意念。”他的眉毛抽动了一下。“可以接受。”全班松了口气。\n# en: Your guess is so wrong the student next to you gasps. Thorne simply says, \"No,\" and moves on.\nopt.right.say.2 [0a5012] = 你猜得错得离谱，旁边的同学倒吸一口冷气。索恩只说了一句“不对”，就继续讲下去了。\n\n@event thorne_office_hours\n# en: Office Hours\ntitle [2f554a] = 答疑时间\n# en: Thorne stops you after class. \"Your casting is sloppy and your instincts are good. That's an annoying combination. Thursdays. Seven o'clock. My office. Don't be late.\"\ntext [e4245b] = 下课后索恩叫住了你。“你的施法很马虎，直觉却很好。这是个恼人的组合。周四。七点。我的办公室。别迟到。”\n# en: A cramped office full of scorched books and a single lit candle, a stern professor leaning back in his chair, fingers steepled.\nscene [78fbf0] = 一间堆满烧焦书本、只点着一支蜡烛的狭小办公室，严厉的教授靠在椅背上，十指相抵。\n# en: Politely decline — you're stretched thin\nopt.left.label [d714b1] = 礼貌地拒绝——你已经分身乏术\n# en: \"Hm.\" He seems neither surprised nor pleased. Thorne is rarely either.\nopt.left.say.1 [c8eaad] = “嗯。”他看起来既不意外，也不高兴。索恩很少有这两种情绪。\n# en: Be there at seven\nopt.right.label [5fe202] = 七点准时到\n# en: Two hours of brutal drills. At the end, he hands you a book from his own shelf. \"Read it. Return it unburnt.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [80a8e2] = 两个小时残酷的操练。结束时，他从自己的书架上抽出一本书递给你。“读完。还的时候别烧着。”\n\n@event thorne_storm_story\n# en: The Storm Duel\ntitle [c9a5ed] = 风暴决斗\n# en: During a thunderstorm, Thorne stops mid-lecture and watches the lightning. \"They say I out-dueled a storm once,\" he says, to nobody in particular. \"Would you like to know what actually happened?\"\ntext [1d58d7] = 雷雨中，索恩讲到一半停了下来，望着窗外的闪电。“他们说我曾经在决斗中赢过一场风暴，”他说，不知是对谁说的，“想知道真正发生了什么吗？”\n# en: An old professor standing at a rain-lashed window, lightning illuminating a long scar along his jaw.\nscene [3aa02a] = 一位年迈的教授站在被雨水抽打的窗前，闪电照亮了他下颌上一道长长的伤疤。\n# en: \"Yes, sir.\"\nopt.left.label [ad622a] = “想，教授。”\n# en: He lost, he says. Badly. He spent a year learning why. \"Losing well,\" he says, \"is the only thing worth teaching.\" The class is silent.\nopt.left.say.1 [bd0455] = 他说，他输了。输得很惨。他花了一年才弄明白为什么。“输得漂亮，”他说，“是唯一值得教的东西。”全班鸦雀无声。\n# en: Stay quiet\nopt.right.label [9adbd8] = 保持沉默\n# en: He shakes his head and returns to the lecture. You'll always wonder.\nopt.right.say.1 [90167f] = 他摇摇头，继续讲课。你会一直好奇下去。\n\n@event thorne_recommendation\n# en: A Letter of Recommendation\ntitle [c8fd60] = 推荐信\n# en: Thorne calls you to his office and slides an envelope across the desk. \"The Conclave asked for my recommendations. I wrote one. I don't write many.\" He pauses. \"Don't make me regret it.\"\ntext [126063] = 索恩把你叫到办公室，把一个信封推过桌面。“议会请我写推荐信。我写了一封。我很少写。”他顿了顿，“别让我后悔。”\n# en: A wax-sealed envelope on a professor's desk with a student's name written on it in sharp, angular handwriting.\nscene [96717b] = 教授桌上一个封着火漆的信封，上面用锋利棱角的字迹写着一个学生的名字。\n# en: Thank him sincerely\nopt.left.label [028201] = 真诚地道谢\n# en: \"Go away,\" he says gruffly. \"I have marking.\" You see him smile at the window as you leave.\nopt.left.say.1 [91553f] = “走吧，”他粗声说，“我还要批作业。”你离开时，看见他对着窗户笑了。\n# en: Ask why you\nopt.right.label [ca1833] = 问为什么是你\n# en: \"Because you ask questions like that,\" he says. \"Most students don't. Go.\" You leave unsure whether you were praised or dismissed.\nopt.right.say.1 [52bd7f] = “因为你会问这种问题，”他说，“大多数学生不会。走吧。”你离开时也说不清自己是被夸了还是被打发了。\n\n@event thorne_disappointed\n# en: A Word in Private\ntitle [ff3088] = 私下谈谈\n# en: Thorne holds up your last three essays. All red. \"You're better than this,\" he says. \"That's not a compliment. It's an accusation.\"\ntext [cc601d] = 索恩举起你最近的三篇论文。全是红的。“你不止这个水平，”他说，“这不是夸奖。是指控。”\n# en: A professor standing by his desk with a sheaf of red-marked papers, a student standing awkwardly before him.\nscene [9d745e] = 一位教授站在桌旁，手持一沓满是红批的论文，一个学生尴尬地站在他面前。\n# en: Make excuses\nopt.left.label [271029] = 找借口\n# en: He listens to them all and says nothing. The silence is the worst grade yet.\nopt.left.say.1 [f08449] = 他听完了你所有的借口，一言不发。这份沉默是你得过最差的分数。\n# en: Ask for help\nopt.right.label [e11804] = 请求帮助\n# en: He sighs and pulls out a chair. \"Sit. We'll start with the first essay, and we'll stay until it's right.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [211a10] = 他叹了口气，拉出一把椅子。“坐。我们从第一篇开始，改到对为止。”\n\n@event maud_chickens\n# en: The Great Chicken Escape\ntitle [c0698d] = 鸡群大逃亡\n# en: Forty chickens are loose on the main lawn, pursued by an old woman in a straw hat. \"Don't just stand there, dearie!\" she shouts at you. \"Grab the speckled one, she's the ringleader!\"\ntext [c7632d] = 四十只鸡在大草坪上四处逃窜，一位戴草帽的老妇人在后面追。“别光站着呀，孩子！”她冲你喊，“抓那只带斑点的，她是头儿！”\n# en: Chickens fleeing across a lawn in every direction, an old woman in a straw hat waving a broom, students diving after birds.\nscene [f345a3] = 鸡群在草坪上四散奔逃，戴草帽的老妇人挥着扫帚，学生们扑向鸡群。\n# en: Keep walking\nopt.left.label [6ca967] = 继续走你的路\n# en: You hear a crash, a squawk and a triumphant cackle behind you. The chickens, it seems, are caught.\nopt.left.say.1 [62bfac] = 你听见身后一声巨响、一声鸡叫和一阵得意的笑。看来鸡都被抓住了。\n# en: Grab the speckled one\nopt.right.label [5c1b61] = 抓住那只带斑点的\n# en: You tackle the ringleader. The rest give up. \"Good hands,\" says Old Maud, pressing a warm egg into your palm. \"Come for tea.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [a339b6] = 你扑倒了头儿。其余的都放弃了。“好身手。”老莫德说着把一枚温热的鸡蛋塞进你手心，“来喝茶吧。”\n# en: The ringleader escapes and pecks you, but Maud is impressed by your enthusiasm. \"Come for tea. Bring plasters.\"\nopt.right.say.2 [bfdfbf] = 头儿逃走了还啄了你一口，但莫德很欣赏你的热情。“来喝茶吧。带上创可贴。”\n\n@event maud_honey\n# en: Honey for a Cold\ntitle [b7cd42] = 治感冒的蜂蜜\n# en: Maud spots you sniffling across the lawn and drags you into her cottage. \"Sit,\" she orders, pouring honey into something steaming that smells of lemon and thunder.\ntext [7490d5] = 莫德隔着草坪看见你在吸鼻子，把你拽进了她的小屋。“坐下。”她命令道，把蜂蜜倒进一杯冒着热气、闻起来像柠檬和雷声的东西里。\n# en: A small cottage kitchen with bundles of herbs hanging from the beams, an old woman pouring golden honey into a steaming mug.\nscene [eeaeed] = 小屋厨房的房梁上挂着一捆捆草药，老妇人正把金色的蜂蜜倒进一只冒热气的杯子。\n# en: Drink it all\nopt.left.label [06ea01] = 一饮而尽\n# en: It tastes like summer and lightning. You sneeze sparks for the rest of the day. By morning you're cured and slightly glowing.\nopt.left.say.1 [2d602b] = 它尝起来像夏天和闪电。你一整天都在打喷嚏，喷出小火星。第二天早上你就好了，身上还微微发光。\n# en: Drink it and help with chores\nopt.right.label [cbf3a4] = 边喝边帮她干活\n# en: You sip and sort herbs while she tells you the names of every plant in the room. You remember most of them.\nopt.right.say.1 [d8a82c] = 你一边小口喝着，一边分拣草药，她把屋里每一株植物的名字都告诉了你。你记住了大半。\n\n@event maud_old_student\n# en: Maud Was a Student Once\ntitle [bad37c] = 莫德曾经也是学生\n# en: \"I was a student here, you know,\" Maud says, watching the lake. \"Sixty years ago. The lake froze one spring. Three of my friends went down to the boathouse that night. Only two came back.\" She doesn't look at you. \"Ask me what I know.\"\ntext [eef642] = “你知道吗，我以前也是这里的学生，”莫德望着湖面说，“六十年前。有一年春天，湖结冰了。那天夜里，我的三个朋友下到了船屋。只回来了两个。”她没有看你。“问我知道些什么吧。”\n# en: An old woman sitting on a bench by the lake at dusk, looking at the water as if it might look back.\nscene [89e70b] = 黄昏时分，一位老妇人坐在湖边长椅上，望着湖水，仿佛湖水也会回望她。\n# en: \"You don't have to tell me.\"\nopt.left.label [194cf6] = “您不必告诉我的。”\n# en: She pats your hand. \"Kind. But time's short, dearie.\" She tells you anyway, a little at a time, over many cups of tea.\nopt.left.say.1 [9b1d22] = 她拍了拍你的手。“好孩子。可时间不多了。”她还是告诉了你，一点一点地，就着许多杯茶。\n# en: \"What do you know?\"\nopt.right.label [099f17] = “您知道些什么？”\n# en: \"Seven locks, one in each tower. The key's a star that eats light. And the thing below loves a lonely heart.\" She finally looks at you. \"Don't be lonely.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [737ddb] = “七把锁，每座塔里一把。钥匙是一颗吞噬光的星。而下面那个东西，最爱孤独的心。”她终于看向你。“别孤单。”\n\n@event maud_bees\n# en: The Silver Bees\ntitle [1eab66] = 银蜂\n# en: \"The silver bees need moving to the summer meadow,\" Maud says, handing you a veiled hat. \"They're friendly. Mostly. Don't make any sudden thoughts.\"\ntext [30a86a] = “银蜂要搬去夏季草场了，”莫德说着递给你一顶带面纱的帽子，“它们很友好。大体上。别突然冒出什么念头就行。”\n# en: Wooden hives in a wildflower meadow, an old woman in a veiled hat lifting a frame dripping with silver honey, bees glinting in the sun.\nscene [53eac5] = 野花草场上的木蜂箱，一位戴面纱帽的老妇人举起一框滴着银色蜂蜜的巢脾，蜜蜂在阳光下闪闪发亮。\n# en: Help with the hives\nopt.left.label [05ba5e] = 帮忙搬蜂箱\n# en: The bees hum around you like a blessing. Maud gives you a jar of silver honey that sells for eight crowns. You eat half first.\nopt.left.say.1 [6e9b07] = 蜜蜂围着你嗡嗡作响，像一种祝福。莫德给了你一罐能卖八克朗的银蜜。你先吃了一半。\n# en: You have a sudden thought. The bees know. You spend the evening with vinegar on your stings.\nopt.left.say.2 [8b74be] = 你突然冒出了一个念头。蜜蜂知道了。你整晚都在往蜇伤上抹醋。\n# en: Watch from a safe distance\nopt.right.label [5c0ef7] = 在安全距离外观看\n# en: You watch Maud move hives barehanded, singing to them. It's the calmest you've felt all month.\nopt.right.say.1 [fca1ee] = 你看着莫德赤手搬动蜂箱，一边对它们唱歌。这是你这个月最平静的时刻。\n\n@event maud_ill\n# en: Maud Is Unwell\ntitle [1570b9] = 莫德病了\n# en: Maud's chickens are running wild, unfed. You find her in bed, grey-faced and coughing. \"Just a chill,\" she wheezes. \"I'm eighty-one. I'm allowed.\"\ntext [d16f4b] = 莫德的鸡没人喂，满地乱跑。你发现她躺在床上，脸色灰白，咳个不停。“只是着了点凉，”她喘着气说，“我八十一了。有这个资格。”\n# en: A small cottage bedroom with a patchwork quilt, an old woman propped up on pillows, a student reading aloud by candlelight.\nscene [a2af84] = 小屋卧室里铺着拼布被，一位老妇人靠在枕头上，一个学生在烛光下为她读报。\n# en: Fetch Sister Amaranth\nopt.left.label [0ebdc9] = 去叫阿玛兰斯修女\n# en: Amaranth brings tonics and a stern lecture. Maud is back on her feet in a week, grumbling about fuss.\nopt.left.say.1 [79a7ad] = 阿玛兰斯带来了药剂和一通严厉的训话。一周后莫德就下了床，嘟囔着大家小题大做。\n# en: Look after her yourself for a week\nopt.right.label [c0447c] = 亲自照顾她一周\n# en: You feed chickens, brew teas, read her the newspaper. When she's well, she gives you a sprig of rosemary. \"You'll do,\" she says. \"You'll do.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [4fecac] = 你喂鸡、煮茶、给她读报纸。她康复后，给了你一枝迷迭香。“你行，”她说，“你行的。”\n\n@event isolde_intro\n# en: She Knows Your Name\ntitle [9a7a2b] = 她知道你的名字\n# en: At dinner, a cool hand rests on the back of your chair. The headmistress. \"{player},\" she says, though you've never met. \"Your marks in Arcana are interesting. Your marks in punctuality, less so.\" She moves on.\ntext [0b5a32] = 晚餐时，一只微凉的手搭在你的椅背上。是院长。“{player}，”她说，尽管你们从未见过，“你的奥术成绩很有意思。你的守时成绩，就没那么有意思了。”她继续往前走了。\n# en: A tall pale woman in dark robes pausing behind a student's chair in a candlelit dining hall, a hand resting lightly on the chair back.\nscene [b2fa1a] = 烛光摇曳的餐厅里，一位身着深色长袍的高挑苍白女子在一名学生的椅子后驻足，一只手轻轻搭在椅背上。\n# en: Stammer an apology\nopt.left.label [d97a4c] = 结结巴巴地道歉\n# en: She's already three tables away. Every student between you sits a little straighter.\nopt.left.say.1 [e1b364] = 她已经走出三张桌子远了。你们之间的每个学生都坐直了一点。\n# en: \"Interesting how, Headmistress?\"\nopt.right.label [836bca] = “怎么个有意思法，院长？”\n# en: She pauses. Turns. Almost smiles. \"Ask me again at the end of your first year,\" she says. The table stares at you.\nopt.right.say.1 [912689] = 她停下脚步。转身。几乎笑了。“等你一年级结束时再问我一次。”她说。整桌人都盯着你看。\n\n@event isolde_tea\n# en: Tea in the Tower\ntitle [b7137e] = 塔楼下午茶\n# en: An invitation to tea with the headmistress. Students talk about these the way soldiers talk about battles. She pours. \"Tell me, {player}: what do you want to become?\"\ntext [9726d0] = 一份与院长共进下午茶的邀请。学生们谈论这种事，就像士兵谈论战役。她倒着茶。“告诉我，{player}：你想成为什么样的人？”\n# en: A round tower study at twilight, two delicate teacups on a small table, a headmistress pouring tea with a perfectly steady hand.\nscene [71ceab] = 暮色中的圆形塔楼书房，小桌上两只精致的茶杯，院长倒茶的手稳得纹丝不动。\n# en: Give an ambitious answer\nopt.left.label [aa3b70] = 给一个雄心勃勃的回答\n# en: She listens without interrupting, then says, \"Good. Ambition without arrogance. Rarer than you'd think.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [7c5144] = 她一句也没打断地听完，然后说：“很好。有雄心而不傲慢。比你想象的要少见。”\n# en: She nods politely. \"Ambition is a fine servant,\" she says, \"and a poor master.\" You're dismissed shortly after.\nopt.left.say.2 [7dba0b] = 她礼貌地点点头。“雄心是个好仆人，”她说，“却是个坏主人。”没过多久你就被请走了。\n# en: Admit you don't know yet\nopt.right.label [0c2530] = 承认你还不知道\n# en: \"Neither did I, at your age,\" she says. \"I still don't, some days. That's the point of an academy.\" She refills your cup.\nopt.right.say.1 [80f029] = “我在你这个年纪时也不知道，”她说，“有些日子我现在也不知道。这正是学院存在的意义。”她为你续上了茶。\n\n@event isolde_warning\n# en: A Quiet Warning\ntitle [a5c708] = 一句轻声的警告\n# en: The headmistress falls into step beside you in the colonnade. \"I hear your name more often than I'd like,\" she says pleasantly. \"In the wrong rooms. I'd hate to hear it in mine.\"\ntext [01291a] = 院长在柱廊里走到你身边，与你并肩而行。“我听到你名字的次数比我希望的要多，”她和气地说，“在不该听到的房间里。我可不想在我的房间里听到它。”\n# en: A headmistress walking alongside a student in a long colonnade, hands clasped behind her back, speaking without looking at them.\nscene [052fbe] = 长长的柱廊里，院长背着手与一名学生并肩而行，说话时并不看对方。\n# en: Promise to do better\nopt.left.label [cba502] = 保证会改\n# en: \"Good.\" She stops walking. You keep going. When you look back, she's gone.\nopt.left.say.1 [b48ece] = “很好。”她停下脚步。你继续往前走。回头时，她已经不见了。\n# en: Defend yourself\nopt.right.label [b9d2ca] = 为自己辩护\n# en: You lay it out plainly. She listens, then nods once. \"Fair. I'll have a word with certain prefects.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [72ff59] = 你把事情原原本本讲清楚。她听完，点了一下头。“公道。我会找某些级长谈谈。”\n# en: \"Hm,\" she says. \"Let's hope you're right.\" It doesn't feel like hope.\nopt.right.say.2 [d3a208] = “嗯，”她说，“但愿你是对的。”这听起来可不像但愿。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/people_students.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/people_students.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event lyra_intro\n# en: The Last Copy\ntitle [370a21] = 最后一本\n# en: You reach for the last copy of \"Foundations of Flame\" and a ringed hand gets there first. \"I need it more,\" says a tall girl with crimson hair, not looking at you. \"You'll only underline the wrong bits.\"\ntext [81419e] = 你伸手去拿最后一本《火焰基础》，一只戴戒指的手抢先了一步。“我更需要它，”一个高挑的红发女孩说，看都没看你，“你只会划错重点。”\n# en: Two hands reaching for the same red-bound book on a library shelf; one hand wears a signet ring with a flame crest.\nscene [396ae6] = 两只手伸向图书馆书架上同一本红皮书；其中一只手上戴着刻有火焰家徽的印章戒指。\n# en: Let her have it\nopt.left.label [6e7c4e] = 让给她\n# en: \"At least you know your place,\" she says, which is somehow worse than rudeness. Lyra Vaneshade. Everyone knows the name.\nopt.left.say.1 [69ebd2] = “至少你有自知之明。”她说，这不知怎么比无礼更伤人。莱拉·凡妮希德。人人都知道这个名字。\n# en: Argue for it\nopt.right.label [503eb7] = 据理力争\n# en: You quote chapter three at her. She raises an eyebrow, then hands you the book. \"Fine. Return it underlined correctly.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [bbdc0c] = 你给她背了一段第三章。她挑起眉毛，然后把书递给了你。“行。还的时候把重点划对。”\n# en: She wins the argument in four sentences and leaves with the book. You think of the perfect comeback that night.\nopt.right.say.2 [b0bbf8] = 她四句话就赢了这场争论，拿着书走了。那天晚上你才想到完美的回嘴。\n\n@event lyra_letter\n# en: A Letter With a Black Seal\ntitle [47420d] = 黑色封印的信\n# en: You find Lyra on a window seat with a crumpled letter. Black seal. Her family's. She's not crying; Vaneshades don't. But she's not quite not crying either.\ntext [4caeea] = 你发现莱拉坐在窗台上，手里攥着一封揉皱的信。黑色封印。她家族的。她没有哭；凡妮希德家的人不哭。但她也不完全是没在哭。\n# en: A girl sitting on a window seat in an empty corridor, crushing a letter with a black wax seal in her fist, face turned away.\nscene [ed4b56] = 空荡荡的走廊里，一个女孩坐在窗台上，把一封黑蜡封口的信攥在拳中，脸转向一边。\n# en: Pretend you didn't see\nopt.left.label [25b345] = 假装没看见\n# en: You walk past. Later, she's flawless again, as if nothing happened. Maybe nothing did.\nopt.left.say.1 [8cb435] = 你走了过去。后来，她又是那个无懈可击的她了，仿佛什么都没发生。也许真的什么都没发生。\n# en: Sit down beside her\nopt.right.label [e6df6e] = 在她身边坐下\n# en: \"Second place,\" she says eventually. \"I came second in the Winter Trials. My mother wants to know who beat me.\" She almost laughs. \"It's always someone.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [2ea3fc] = “第二名，”她终于开口，“我在冬季考核里拿了第二。我母亲想知道是谁赢了我。”她几乎笑了出来。“总会有那么一个人。”\n\n@event lyra_study_rivalry\n# en: Top of the Class\ntitle [76842e] = 全班第一\n# en: The professors post this week's rankings, with an open paper this afternoon for anyone who wants to improve their place. Lyra is first. You're close enough to see the back of her head. She notices you noticing.\ntext [938804] = 教授们公布了本周排名，还说今天下午有一场公开考试，想提高名次的人都可以参加。莱拉第一。你离她近到能看见她的后脑勺。她注意到了你在注意她。\n# en: Two students at opposite ends of a long library table, surrounded by towers of books, glaring over the tops of them.\nscene [e05ebb] = 长长的图书馆桌两端各坐一个学生，被一摞摞书包围着，隔着书堆互相瞪视。\n# en: Suggest studying together\nopt.left.label [d07df2] = 提议一起学习\n# en: \"Together?\" She considers this like a foreign custom. Then she pulls out a chair. She's an excellent, merciless study partner.\nopt.left.say.1 [4cb67d] = “一起？”她像在考虑一种外国习俗。然后她拉出了一把椅子。她是个出色而无情的学习搭档。\n# en: Beat her outright\nopt.right.label [7983cc] = 正面击败她\n# en: The afternoon paper puts you first. Lyra stares at the list for a long time. Then she finds you and says, \"Good.\" She means it.\nopt.right.say.1 [8e7fb5] = 下午的考试让你排到了第一。莱拉盯着名单看了很久。然后她找到你，说：“很好。”她是认真的。\n# en: You cram through lunch and come third. Lyra is still first. She doesn't gloat. That's worse.\nopt.right.say.2 [17788c] = 你午饭时间都在临阵磨枪，结果是第三。莱拉还是第一。她没有得意。这更糟。\n\n@event lyra_family_gala\n# en: The Vaneshade Winter Gala\ntitle [cc6875] = 凡妮希德冬季晚宴\n# en: \"My family's winter gala,\" Lyra says, too casually. \"I need a guest who won't embarrass me. You'll do. Probably. You'll need proper clothes.\"\ntext [f5575c] = “我家的冬季晚宴，”莱拉说，语气太过随意，“我需要一个不会让我丢脸的客人。你还行。大概。你得穿得像样点。”\n# en: A glittering ballroom in a noble house, candles floating in mid-air, a tall woman in crimson watching the crowd like a hawk.\nscene [bf8dd3] = 贵族宅邸里金碧辉煌的舞厅，蜡烛悬浮在空中，一位身着深红衣裙的高挑女人像鹰一样注视着人群。\n# en: Decline\nopt.left.label [eaef39] = 拒绝\n# en: \"Of course.\" A pause. \"It doesn't matter.\" It clearly does.\nopt.left.say.1 [1fe8ea] = “当然。”停顿。“无所谓。”显然有所谓。\n# en: Go with her (15 gold for clothes)\nopt.right.label [e8a9e8] = 陪她去（置装花费 15 金币）\n# en: Lyra's mother asks who your parents are, and then asks no further questions at all. Lyra squeezes your hand under the table, hard.\nopt.right.say.1 [3ae20f] = 莱拉的母亲问起你父母是谁，然后就再也没有问别的了。莱拉在桌下用力握住了你的手。\n# en: You dance, you charm a duke, you don't spill anything. In the carriage home, Lyra falls asleep on your shoulder.\nopt.right.say.2 [db666b] = 你跳舞，你迷倒了一位公爵，你什么都没洒。回家的马车上，莱拉靠在你肩上睡着了。\n\n@event lyra_confession\n# en: Lyra Doesn't Lose\ntitle [4e22c6] = 莱拉从不认输\n# en: After practice, Lyra doesn't leave. \"I don't lose,\" she says, staring at the scorch marks. \"I don't. But I think — \" She stops. Starts again. \"I think I've lost to you. Say something before I set something on fire.\"\ntext [0b2d38] = 练习结束后，莱拉没有走。“我不会输，”她盯着地上的焦痕说，“我不会。但我觉得——”她停住了。又重新开口。“我觉得我输给你了。快说点什么，不然我要烧东西了。”\n# en: A girl with crimson hair standing very close in a practice ring at dusk, sparks still drifting from her wand, cheeks flushed.\nscene [fa1888] = 黄昏的练习圈里，一个红发女孩站得很近，魔杖上还飘着火星，脸颊泛红。\n# en: Let her down gently\nopt.left.label [c62f10] = 温柔地拒绝她\n# en: She nods, sharp and quick. \"Understood.\" She practises alone for a month. Then one day she nods to you in the corridor, and it's almost all right.\nopt.left.say.1 [d294fb] = 她干脆利落地点了点头。“明白了。”她独自练了一个月。后来有一天她在走廊里朝你点点头，一切差不多都好了。\n# en: Kiss her\nopt.right.label [5fa79c] = 吻她\n# en: Nothing catches fire. Well. Something does, but it's only the practice dummy, and neither of you notices for a while.\nopt.right.say.1 [90be4c] = 什么都没着火。好吧。有东西着了，但只是练习用的假人，而且你们好一阵子都没注意到。\n\n@event lyra_family_ultimatum\n# en: The Ultimatum\ntitle [f0d1f2] = 最后通牒\n# en: Another black-sealed letter. Lyra reads it twice and goes white. \"My mother has found out about us. End it, she says, or she ends my inheritance.\" She looks at you. \"Tell me what to do.\"\ntext [fa5d3c] = 又一封黑色封印的信。莱拉读了两遍，脸色发白。“我母亲知道我们的事了。她说，要么断了，要么她断了我的继承权。”她看着你。“告诉我该怎么办。”\n# en: Lyra holding a letter with a black seal, her other hand clenched around a ring she has just pulled off.\nscene [8c7e83] = 莱拉手持一封黑色封印的信，另一只手紧紧攥着一枚刚摘下的戒指。\n# en: \"Choose yourself, not them.\"\nopt.left.label [1f5be4] = “选择你自己，而不是他们。”\n# en: She tears the letter in half. Then in quarters. Then she sets the quarters on fire and laughs like she hasn't in years.\nopt.left.say.1 [3f7f4b] = 她把信撕成两半。再撕成四片。然后把碎片点着，笑得像好多年没这样笑过。\n# en: \"Easy for you to say,\" she snaps. She doesn't mean it. She also doesn't take it back.\nopt.left.say.2 [66097c] = “说得轻巧。”她冲你吼道。她不是真心的。但她也没有收回这句话。\n# en: She tears the letter in half. Then in quarters. Then she sets the quarters on fire and laughs like she hasn't in years.\nopt.left.say.3 [3f7f4b] = 她把信撕成两半。再撕成四片。然后把碎片点着，笑得像好多年没这样笑过。\n# en: \"Easy for you to say,\" she snaps. She doesn't mean it. She also doesn't take it back.\nopt.left.say.4 [66097c] = “说得轻巧。”她冲你吼道。她不是真心的。但她也没有收回这句话。\n# en: Step back so she doesn't lose everything\nopt.right.label [9c8862] = 退一步，让她不至于失去一切\n# en: You end it before she has to. She doesn't argue. That's how you know how much it costs her.\nopt.right.say.1 [27b1b0] = 在她不得不开口之前，你先结束了这段感情。她没有争辩。正因如此，你才知道这对她代价有多大。\n\n@event lyra_rival_sabotage\n# en: Sabotage in Class\ntitle [1db464] = 课堂上的暗算\n# en: Your perfect potion turns black and starts screaming. Across the room, Lyra is very carefully not looking at you.\ntext [8b133c] = 你那锅完美的药剂突然变黑，开始尖叫。教室另一头，莱拉非常小心地没往你这边看。\n# en: A cauldron spitting black smoke, a crimson-haired student walking away with a tiny vial hidden in her sleeve.\nscene [f64dbb] = 一口坩埚喷出黑烟，一个红发学生走开，袖子里藏着一只小瓶。\n# en: Retaliate\nopt.left.label [5cb1e8] = 报复回去\n# en: Next lesson, her wand produces only bubbles. Pink ones. She knows. You both know. It's war. It's sort of fun.\nopt.left.say.1 [6d8609] = 下节课，她的魔杖只能冒出泡泡。粉红色的。她知道。你们俩都知道。开战了。还挺好玩的。\n# en: You're caught slipping something into her bag. She looks genuinely delighted.\nopt.left.say.2 [7dfe71] = 你往她包里塞东西时被逮了个正着。她看起来真心高兴。\n# en: Confront her directly\nopt.right.label [eed285] = 当面质问她\n# en: \"That was beneath you.\" She flushes. \"...Yes. It was.\" The next day there's a perfect replacement potion on your desk.\nopt.right.say.1 [3c2fd2] = “这有失你的身份。”她脸红了。“……是。是有失身份。”第二天，你桌上放着一份完美的替代药剂。\n\n@event lyra_burnout\n# en: Lyra Falls\ntitle [d0abf9] = 莱拉倒下了\n# en: You find Lyra face-down on a library desk at dawn, not asleep — out cold. She's been studying for three days straight. \"Don't tell anyone,\" she mumbles when she comes round. \"Mother can't know.\"\ntext [794170] = 黎明时分你发现莱拉趴在图书馆的桌上，不是睡着了——是昏过去了。她已经连续学了三天。“别告诉任何人，”她醒过来时含糊地说，“不能让母亲知道。”\n# en: A girl collapsed across a library desk among scattered notes, quill still in hand, candle burned down to nothing.\nscene [91fdd5] = 一个女孩瘫倒在图书馆书桌上，周围散落着笔记，手里还握着羽毛笔，蜡烛已经燃尽。\n# en: Take her to the infirmary anyway\nopt.left.label [26e217] = 还是把她送去医务室\n# en: She's furious. Then she sleeps for nineteen hours. When she wakes up, she says, quietly, \"Thank you.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [0d4dbd] = 她气坏了。然后她睡了十九个小时。醒来时，她轻声说：“谢谢你。”\n# en: Cover for her and make her rest\nopt.right.label [bfbbb0] = 替她打掩护，逼她休息\n# en: You tell her professors she has a cold and sit by her door with your homework. She sleeps. You keep watch.\nopt.right.say.1 [1b1d64] = 你告诉她的教授们她感冒了，然后带着作业守在她门口。她睡了。你守着。\n\n@event tobias_intro\n# en: The Lost Boy\ntitle [3f2dc0] = 迷路的男孩\n# en: A big, broad-shouldered boy is leading a crying first-year through the corridors. \"Don't worry, I know exactly where the infirmary is,\" he says, and walks confidently into a broom cupboard.\ntext [03f851] = 一个高大宽肩的男生正领着一个哭鼻子的一年级生穿过走廊。“别担心，我很清楚医务室在哪儿。”他说着，满怀信心地走进了扫帚间。\n# en: A large, gentle-faced boy leading a crying first-year by the hand down the wrong corridor, both very lost.\nscene [0ae971] = 一个面容温和的大个子男生牵着一个哭泣的一年级生走在错误的走廊里，两人都迷路了。\n# en: Point them the right way\nopt.left.label [f53c4f] = 给他们指路\n# en: \"Ah. Left, you say. Right. I mean, correct.\" He grins, enormous and sheepish. \"Tobias. Turnip scholarship. Thanks.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [74c134] = “啊。你说左边。对。我是说，没错。”他咧嘴一笑，笑容大大的，有点不好意思。“托比亚斯。芜菁奖学金。谢啦。”\n# en: Walk with them\nopt.right.label [799c3a] = 陪他们一起走\n# en: You end up taking the scenic route, and the first-year stops crying because Tobias is telling a very long story about a goat.\nopt.right.say.1 [c5721c] = 你们最后绕了一条风景路线，一年级生不哭了，因为托比亚斯在讲一个关于山羊的超长故事。\n\n@event tobias_homesick\n# en: Homesick\ntitle [a133e0] = 想家\n# en: Tobias has gone quiet. He's planted seedlings in every cup in the common room. \"Harvest at home,\" he says. \"First one I've missed. Ma's doing it alone.\"\ntext [fd42a8] = 托比亚斯变得沉默了。他在公共休息室的每个杯子里都种了幼苗。“家里在收庄稼，”他说，“这是我第一次错过。我妈一个人在干。”\n# en: A dormitory windowsill crowded with clay pots of seedlings, a big boy watering them with a tiny can.\nscene [433f64] = 宿舍窗台上挤满了种着幼苗的陶盆，一个大个子男生正用一只小水壶浇水。\n# en: Cheer him up with a joke\nopt.left.label [c33487] = 讲个笑话逗他开心\n# en: Your joke is terrible. He laughs anyway, the big rumbling laugh, and that seems to help.\nopt.left.say.1 [e60401] = 你的笑话烂透了。他还是笑了，那种轰隆隆的大笑，好像真的管用。\n# en: Help him build a window garden\nopt.right.label [8aab52] = 帮他建一个窗台菜园\n# en: You buy seeds and a stack of pots. By the end of the week the common room smells of tomato leaves. Tobias whistles while he waters them.\nopt.right.say.1 [4f8881] = 你买来种子和一摞花盆。到周末，公共休息室里满是番茄叶的味道。托比亚斯浇水时吹着口哨。\n\n@event tobias_bad_harvest\n# en: The Harvest Failed\ntitle [aab2ca] = 歉收\n# en: Tobias is packing. Blight took the harvest. His family needs money, and hands. \"Thirty crowns would get them through winter,\" he says, not asking. \"But I haven't got thirty crowns. I've got two arms.\"\ntext [b3ac08] = 托比亚斯在收拾行李。枯萎病毁了收成。他家需要钱，也需要人手。“三十克朗就能让他们熬过冬天，”他说，并不是在开口要，“可我没有三十克朗。我只有两条胳膊。”\n# en: A boy packing a battered trunk in an empty dormitory, a letter with smudged handwriting open on the bed.\nscene [7cc0f7] = 空荡荡的宿舍里，一个男生在收拾一只破旧的行李箱，床上摊着一封字迹模糊的信。\n# en: Persuade him to stay and find work here\nopt.left.label [4e1985] = 劝他留下，在这里找工作\n# en: You walk him to the job board. He signs up for double shifts at the stables and sends every crown home. He stays.\nopt.left.say.1 [3cac05] = 你陪他去了招工告示板。他报了马厩的双班，把每一枚克朗都寄回了家。他留下了。\n# en: He listens, nods, and takes the barge in the morning. He's back in two months, thinner and quieter.\nopt.left.say.2 [373ae1] = 他听着，点点头，第二天早上坐上了驳船。两个月后他回来了，瘦了，也更沉默了。\n# en: Give him thirty crowns\nopt.right.label [902ad2] = 给他三十克朗\n# en: He tries to refuse three times. Then he hugs you so hard your spine cracks. \"I'll pay you back. Every crown. Every one.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [2214f7] = 他推辞了三次。然后抱住你，抱得你脊椎咔咔响。“我会还你的。每一枚克朗。每一枚。”\n\n@event tobias_repays\n# en: Every Crown\ntitle [807b67] = 每一枚克朗\n# en: A lumpy, hand-stitched pouch on your pillow: thirty crowns exactly, in small coins. And a jar of honey from home. A note in big, careful letters: \"TOLD YOU. — T.\"\ntext [150790] = 你枕头上放着一只鼓鼓囊囊、手工缝制的小袋子：正好三十克朗，全是零钱。还有一罐家里的蜂蜜。一张字条，字写得又大又认真：“说到做到。——托”\n# en: A clumsy hand-stitched pouch of coins and a jar of honey left on a dormitory pillow.\nscene [1e69a7] = 宿舍枕头上一只笨拙的手缝钱袋和一罐蜂蜜。\n# en: Smile\nopt.ok.label [c2972f] = 笑一笑\n# en: The honey tastes of a farm you have never seen and somehow already miss.\nopt.ok.say.1 [8639ca] = 那罐蜂蜜尝起来像一座你从没见过、却已经开始想念的农场。\n\n@event tobias_godparent\n# en: The Mandrake Baby\ntitle [5adaaf] = 曼德拉草宝宝\n# en: Tobias presents a flowerpot containing a very small mandrake in a knitted hat. \"This is Gerald,\" he says. \"I'd like you to be his godparent.\" Gerald screams softly.\ntext [287127] = 托比亚斯捧出一个花盆，里面是一株戴着针织帽的小小曼德拉草。“这是杰拉尔德，”他说，“我想请你当他的教父母。”杰拉尔德轻声尖叫。\n# en: A tiny mandrake in a pot wearing a knitted hat, a beaming boy presenting it like a newborn.\nscene [39f8e3] = 一株戴着针织帽的小曼德拉草种在盆里，一个笑容灿烂的男生像捧着新生儿一样把它捧出来。\n# en: Politely decline\nopt.left.label [8a601d] = 礼貌地拒绝\n# en: \"No pressure,\" says Tobias, visibly pressured. Gerald glares at you from under his hat.\nopt.left.say.1 [00f5a4] = “没压力的。”托比亚斯说，明显压力很大。杰拉尔德从帽子底下瞪着你。\n# en: Accept with honour\nopt.right.label [395d80] = 荣幸地接受\n# en: You are now Gerald's godparent. Gerald bites you. Tobias says this means Gerald loves you.\nopt.right.say.1 [95091f] = 你现在是杰拉尔德的教父母了。杰拉尔德咬了你一口。托比亚斯说这代表杰拉尔德爱你。\n\n@event tobias_bullied\n# en: Tobias and the Prefect\ntitle [0d0b40] = 托比亚斯与级长\n# en: Bram is doing an impression of Tobias's accent in the corridor. People are laughing. Tobias is pretending not to hear, ears burning red.\ntext [cf84da] = 布拉姆在走廊里模仿托比亚斯的口音。大家都在笑。托比亚斯假装没听见，耳朵红得发烫。\n# en: A burly prefect mocking a big farm boy in a crowded corridor, students sniggering, the boy's ears bright red.\nscene [d6b2fe] = 拥挤的走廊里，一个魁梧的级长在嘲笑一个高大的农家男孩，学生们在窃笑，男孩的耳朵通红。\n# en: Stay out of it\nopt.left.label [03d24a] = 不掺和\n# en: Tobias doesn't mention it. He doesn't sit with you at dinner, either.\nopt.left.say.1 [be0b18] = 托比亚斯没提这事。晚饭时他也没和你坐一起。\n# en: Step in\nopt.right.label [db9576] = 站出来\n# en: \"Funny,\" you say. \"Do your father next.\" The laughter turns on Bram. He stalks off. Tobias stares at you in awe.\nopt.right.say.1 [26c55a] = “真好笑，”你说，“下一个学你爸吧。”笑声转向了布拉姆。他气冲冲地走了。托比亚斯敬畏地看着你。\n# en: Bram turns his attention to you instead, with interest. Tobias brings you a pie later. A whole pie.\nopt.right.say.2 [a956b8] = 布拉姆转而饶有兴趣地盯上了你。后来托比亚斯给你送来一个馅饼。一整个。\n\n@event tobias_confession\n# en: A Confession, With Vegetables\ntitle [a19fc5] = 一场带着蔬菜的告白\n# en: Tobias corners you in the greenhouse holding a very large pumpkin. \"I grew this,\" he says. \"For you. It's the best one. I didn't know how else to — I like you. Like that. I've practised this on the pumpkin. Sorry.\"\ntext [157367] = 托比亚斯抱着一个很大的南瓜在温室里拦住了你。“这是我种的，”他说，“送你的。是最好的那个。我不知道还能怎么——我喜欢你。是那种喜欢。我对着南瓜练习过了。对不起。”\n# en: A big boy holding out a perfect pumpkin like an offering in a greenhouse, cheeks scarlet.\nscene [563b87] = 温室里一个大个子男生像献礼一样捧出一个完美的南瓜，脸颊通红。\n# en: Let him down gently\nopt.left.label [b009f9] = 温柔地拒绝他\n# en: He nods a lot. \"That's all right. That's fine. You can still have the pumpkin.\" You keep it until it rots, out of loyalty.\nopt.left.say.1 [f837ce] = 他拼命点头。“没关系。没事的。南瓜你还是可以留着。”出于情义，你一直留到它烂掉。\n# en: Say yes\nopt.right.label [c73ccb] = 答应他\n# en: He drops the pumpkin. It's fine; pumpkins are sturdy. So, it turns out, are his hugs.\nopt.right.say.1 [3f29ab] = 他手里的南瓜掉了。没事；南瓜很结实。事实证明，他的拥抱也是。\n\n@event tobias_picnic\n# en: A Picnic in the Orchard\ntitle [2dff91] = 果园野餐\n# en: Tobias has packed a picnic: bread he baked, cheese he begged from the kitchens, apples from the orchard he has befriended. \"No exams for six hours,\" he announces. \"I checked.\"\ntext [b4eb18] = 托比亚斯准备了野餐：他自己烤的面包，从厨房软磨硬泡来的奶酪，还有他“交了朋友”的果园里的苹果。“六个小时内没有考试，”他宣布，“我查过了。”\n# en: A checked blanket under blossoming apple trees, a basket of bread and cheese, two students lying in the dappled light.\nscene [66c097] = 开花的苹果树下铺着格子毯，一篮面包和奶酪，两个学生躺在斑驳的光影里。\n# en: Bring your books anyway\nopt.left.label [b8ab05] = 还是带上书\n# en: You study on the blanket. He falls asleep with his head in your lap. It's still lovely.\nopt.left.say.1 [a7f7ec] = 你在毯子上学习。他枕着你的腿睡着了。依然很美好。\n# en: Leave everything behind\nopt.right.label [1ca23f] = 把一切都抛在脑后\n# en: The afternoon goes golden and slow. You can't remember the last time you didn't think about grades.\nopt.right.say.1 [5c67d2] = 午后变得金黄而缓慢。你已经记不起上一次不去想成绩是什么时候了。\n\n@event seraphine_intro\n# en: Your Left Shoe\ntitle [0404ae] = 你的左脚鞋\n# en: A girl drifts past you in the corridor and says, without stopping, \"You're going to lose your left shoe today. Don't worry, it's under the stairs in the east wing.\"\ntext [01fffe] = 一个女孩从你身边飘过，头也不回地说：“你今天会丢一只左脚的鞋。别担心，它在东翼的楼梯底下。”\n# en: A slight girl with silver-grey eyes and a scarf of stars passing in a corridor, pointing absently at someone's feet.\nscene [c9cd41] = 一个纤瘦的女孩围着星星图案的围巾，长着银灰色的眼睛，在走廊里经过时漫不经心地指着某人的脚。\n# en: Ignore her\nopt.left.label [44f360] = 不理她\n# en: That afternoon a staircase moves and eats your left shoe. It is, eventually, under the stairs in the east wing.\nopt.left.say.1 [b13d26] = 那天下午，一道楼梯挪了位置，吃掉了你的左脚鞋。最终，它确实在东翼的楼梯底下。\n# en: Ask how she knows\nopt.right.label [48f36e] = 问她怎么知道的\n# en: \"I don't know things,\" she says. \"Things know me.\" Then: \"Seraphine. You'll want the shoe back by four.\" You get it back at 3:58.\nopt.right.say.1 [ef5d82] = “我不知道事情，”她说，“是事情知道我。”然后又说：“瑟拉芬。你会想在四点前把鞋拿回来的。”你在三点五十八分拿了回来。\n\n@event seraphine_tarot\n# en: The Cards\ntitle [c5101b] = 牌\n# en: Seraphine shuffles a battered tarot deck. \"One card,\" she says. \"For you. Only if you want it. They're quite rude sometimes.\"\ntext [95625a] = 瑟拉芬洗着一副破旧的塔罗牌。“抽一张，”她说，“给你的。只要你想。它们有时候挺没礼貌的。”\n# en: A tarot spread on a velvet cloth in candlelight, a girl's pale fingers turning over a card showing a lightning-struck tower.\nscene [8a2f94] = 烛光下天鹅绒布上摊开的塔罗牌阵，一个女孩苍白的手指翻开一张画着雷击高塔的牌。\n# en: Draw a card\nopt.left.label [a80dc6] = 抽一张\n# en: The Star. \"Hope,\" she says, delighted. \"And someone's going to tell you a secret. Oh — it's me. I like you.\" She blushes.\nopt.left.say.1 [bfb1c6] = 星星。“希望，”她高兴地说，“还有人要告诉你一个秘密。噢——是我。我喜欢你。”她脸红了。\n# en: The Tower. She looks at it a long time. \"It's fine,\" she says. \"Towers fall. That's what they're for.\"\nopt.left.say.2 [ecbdc5] = 高塔。她看了很久。“没关系，”她说，“塔会倒。塔就是用来倒的。”\n# en: Decline\nopt.right.label [eaef39] = 不抽\n# en: \"Good,\" she says, putting the deck away. \"Knowing is overrated.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [1ff9e2] = “很好。”她把牌收起来，“知道太多被高估了。”\n\n@event seraphine_lake_vision\n# en: She Walks Into the Lake\ntitle [7cd453] = 她走进了湖里\n# en: From your window you see Seraphine walking into the lake in her nightgown, eyes shining silver, already up to her waist.\ntext [89cff3] = 你从窗口看见瑟拉芬穿着睡袍走进湖里，眼睛泛着银光，水已经没到了腰。\n# en: A girl in a nightgown wading into a misty lake at night, eyes glowing silver, arms outstretched toward something under the water.\nscene [21af5a] = 夜里一个穿睡袍的女孩涉入雾气笼罩的湖中，双眼泛着银光，双臂伸向水下的某样东西。\n# en: Wake a prefect\nopt.left.label [9162cd] = 叫醒一位级长\n# en: The prefect drags her out, coughing. She doesn't remember anything. She looks at you strangely for days.\nopt.left.say.1 [f25863] = 级长把她拖了出来，她咳个不停。她什么都不记得了。接下来几天，她看你的眼神都怪怪的。\n# en: Run down and pull her out\nopt.right.label [4787f1] = 冲下去把她拉出来\n# en: You reach her just as she goes under. On the shore she wakes and whispers, \"It was calling me. It called you too.\" Then she holds on very tight.\nopt.right.say.1 [d29a81] = 她沉下去的那一刻你抓住了她。在岸上，她醒过来低声说：“它在呼唤我。它也呼唤了你。”然后她紧紧抱住了你。\n# en: You both nearly drown. You both come down with Lake Fever. She brings you soup that tastes faintly of the future.\nopt.right.say.2 [571f07] = 你们俩都差点淹死。你们俩都得了湖热病。她给你送来的汤，隐约有未来的味道。\n\n@event seraphine_future\n# en: I Saw You Old\ntitle [9b881b] = 我看见你老了\n# en: \"I saw you old,\" Seraphine says suddenly. \"Last night. You were happy. Really happy. There was someone with you.\" She pauses. \"Do you want to know who?\"\ntext [c5d67f] = “我看见你老了，”瑟拉芬突然说，“昨晚。你很幸福。真的很幸福。有人陪着你。”她顿了顿，“你想知道是谁吗？”\n# en: Two students sitting on the rim of a dry fountain, one looking up at the stars, the other looking at the first with a soft smile.\nscene [027150] = 两个学生坐在干涸的喷泉边沿，一个仰望星空，另一个温柔地笑着看向前者。\n# en: Yes, tell me\nopt.left.label [f7e607] = 想，告诉我\n# en: She looks at you for a long time. \"I'll tell you when you're old,\" she says, and goes pink.\nopt.left.say.1 [4bb771] = 她看了你很久。“等你老了我再告诉你。”她说，脸红了。\n# en: \"Someone kind,\" she says. \"That's all I could see. Their hands were kind.\"\nopt.left.say.2 [19f673] = “一个温柔的人，”她说，“我只看得到这么多。那双手很温柔。”\n# en: No, let it be a surprise\nopt.right.label [97a4ad] = 不了，留个惊喜吧\n# en: She smiles like you've given her a present. \"That's the right answer. Nobody ever gives the right answer.\" Not knowing nags at you for days.\nopt.right.say.1 [ce9281] = 她笑了，像你送了她一份礼物。“这是正确答案。从来没人给出过正确答案。”不知道答案这件事，让你心痒了好几天。\n\n@event seraphine_overwhelmed\n# en: Too Many Tomorrows\ntitle [409210] = 太多个明天\n# en: Seraphine hasn't come to class in a week. You find her in a dark storeroom with her hands over her eyes. \"It's too loud,\" she whispers. \"Everyone's futures. All at once.\"\ntext [92ab7f] = 瑟拉芬已经一周没来上课了。你在一间黑漆漆的储藏室里找到了她，她用双手捂着眼睛。“太吵了，”她低声说，“每个人的未来。一下子全涌过来。”\n# en: A girl curled in a dark corner of an observatory with her hands over her eyes, a teacup gone cold beside her.\nscene [7f0e93] = 天文台黑暗的角落里蜷缩着一个女孩，双手捂着眼睛，身旁的茶早已凉透。\n# en: Fetch Sister Amaranth\nopt.left.label [0ebdc9] = 去叫阿玛兰斯修女\n# en: Amaranth gives her a sleeping draught and a dark room. She recovers. She doesn't say much about it.\nopt.left.say.1 [42cef1] = 阿玛兰斯给了她一剂安眠药和一间暗室。她恢复了。她对此不怎么提起。\n# en: Sit with her in the dark\nopt.right.label [30debe] = 在黑暗中陪着她\n# en: You don't speak. After an hour she takes her hands from her eyes. \"Yours is quiet,\" she says. \"Your future. It's very quiet. I like it.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [44b951] = 你没有说话。一个小时后，她把手从眼睛上拿开。“你的很安静，”她说，“你的未来。非常安静。我喜欢。”\n\n@event seraphine_confession\n# en: She Already Knew\ntitle [e91972] = 她早就知道\n# en: \"I've seen this conversation forty-one times,\" Seraphine says. \"In twenty-nine of them, you say yes. I've been very nervous about the other twelve.\" She holds out her hand. \"I like you. That part was always the same.\"\ntext [94995b] = “这段对话我已经看过四十一次了，”瑟拉芬说，“其中二十九次你答应了。另外十二次让我很紧张。”她伸出手。“我喜欢你。这一部分每次都一样。”\n# en: A girl with silver eyes holding out a single falling-star seed in her palm on a dark observatory balcony.\nscene [966292] = 漆黑的天文台阳台上，一个银色眼睛的女孩掌心托着一粒流星的种子递过来。\n# en: Gently say no\nopt.left.label [f46b92] = 温柔地拒绝\n# en: She nods. \"Twelve,\" she says softly. \"I knew it might be twelve.\" She's kind to you afterwards. Somehow that hurts more.\nopt.left.say.1 [4e2d00] = 她点点头。“十二，”她轻声说，“我就知道可能是十二。”之后她对你依然很温柔。不知为何，这更让人难过。\n# en: Take her hand\nopt.right.label [4658a2] = 握住她的手\n# en: \"Twenty-nine,\" she whispers, and laughs, and for once she's entirely in the present.\nopt.right.say.1 [cd7951] = “二十九。”她低声说，笑了起来，难得地，她完完全全活在当下。\n\n@event corvin_intro\n# en: Light Fingers\ntitle [fc7fa5] = 手快\n# en: Someone bumps you in the crowd. A moment later a dark-haired boy is bowing and holding up your purse. \"You dropped this,\" he says. \"Into my pocket. Terrible habit of yours. Corvin Ashby.\"\ntext [88b693] = 人群里有人撞了你一下。片刻之后，一个黑发男生朝你鞠躬，手里举着你的钱袋。“你掉了这个，”他说，“掉进了我的口袋。你这习惯可真糟。科尔文·阿什比。”\n# en: A dark-haired student with a crooked grin holding up a purse between two fingers, mid-bow.\nscene [77be60] = 一个嘴角歪歪笑着的黑发学生，两指夹着一只钱袋，正弯腰行礼。\n# en: Snatch it back and report him\nopt.left.label [69c705] = 一把夺回来，举报他\n# en: He's gone before the prefect arrives. Your purse is all there, plus an IOU you don't remember receiving.\nopt.left.say.1 [2b1b37] = 级长来之前他就不见了。你的钱袋一分不少，只是多了一张你不记得收过的欠条。\n# en: Laugh\nopt.right.label [52d15e] = 笑出声\n# en: \"Oh, I like you,\" he says, and teaches you the trick on the spot. You practise it on him. He lets you win. Probably. Later, you are three crowns short. Probably a coincidence.\nopt.right.say.1 [0c1a5b] = “噢，我喜欢你。”他说，当场就把这招教给了你。你拿他练手。他让你赢了。大概吧。后来你发现少了三克朗。大概是巧合。\n\n@event corvin_kitchen_raid\n# en: The Kitchen Raid\ntitle [0e6a0c] = 突袭厨房\n# en: \"Midnight,\" Corvin whispers. \"The cooks have made forty treacle tarts for the faculty dinner. Forty. Nobody needs forty.\"\ntext [ccccc4] = “午夜，”科尔文低声说，“厨师们给教职员晚宴做了四十个糖浆馅饼。四十个。没人需要四十个。”\n# en: Two students creeping through a cavernous castle kitchen at night, a mountain of tarts on a table, a sleeping cook snoring by the hearth.\nscene [c71678] = 夜里两个学生蹑手蹑脚地穿过城堡里洞穴般的大厨房，桌上堆着小山一样的馅饼，炉边的厨师打着呼噜。\n# en: Not tonight\nopt.left.label [4bf72b] = 今晚不行\n# en: He goes alone. The next morning there's a treacle tart on your pillow. You decide not to ask.\nopt.left.say.1 [4aa606] = 他自己去了。第二天早上你的枕头上放着一个糖浆馅饼。你决定不问。\n# en: Lead the raid\nopt.right.label [a419e4] = 带头突袭\n# en: You get out with nine tarts and a bottle of elderflower cordial. You eat them on the roof until sunrise.\nopt.right.say.1 [f86666] = 你们带着九个馅饼和一瓶接骨木花露溜了出来。在屋顶上一直吃到日出。\n# en: The cook wakes up. You both spend a week scrubbing pans, laughing too hard to work.\nopt.right.say.2 [eb0bd4] = 厨师醒了。你们俩刷了一星期的锅，笑得根本干不动活。\n\n@event corvin_debt\n# en: Corvin's Little Problem\ntitle [7d9f5d] = 科尔文的小麻烦\n# en: Corvin has a black eye and a very casual expression. \"Small misunderstanding with some trolls,\" he says. \"Thirty crowns would clear it right up. I'll pay you back. Probably with interest. Possibly with a goat.\"\ntext [6c578d] = 科尔文顶着一只黑眼圈，表情非常随意。“和几个巨魔有点小误会，”他说，“三十克朗就能摆平。我会还你的。大概还带利息。也可能带一只山羊。”\n# en: A boy with a black eye sitting on the dormitory stairs, trying very hard to look casual.\nscene [720349] = 一个顶着黑眼圈的男生坐在宿舍楼梯上，非常努力地装作若无其事。\n# en: Refuse\nopt.left.label [cada35] = 拒绝\n# en: \"Course. No problem.\" He has another black eye the next week. He's still friendly. Mostly.\nopt.left.say.1 [71b32c] = “当然。没问题。”下周他又多了一只黑眼圈。他对你依然友好。大体上。\n# en: Give him thirty crowns\nopt.right.label [902ad2] = 给他三十克朗\n# en: He stares at the coins, then at you. \"No one's ever just... done that.\" He's quiet for a whole minute. A record.\nopt.right.say.1 [9d7807] = 他盯着硬币，又盯着你。“从来没有人就这么……给过。”他安静了整整一分钟。破纪录了。\n\n@event corvin_repays\n# en: Interest\ntitle [8f88f3] = 利息\n# en: There's a fat purse on your desk and a jack of spades tucked under it. Inside the purse: more than thirty crowns. Much more.\ntext [4a2b6a] = 你桌上放着一只鼓鼓的钱袋，下面压着一张黑桃 J。钱袋里：不止三十克朗。多得多。\n# en: A small brass key and a fat purse left on a desk with a playing card, the jack of spades, tucked under them.\nscene [476a3b] = 桌上放着一把黄铜小钥匙和一只鼓鼓的钱袋，下面压着一张扑克牌——黑桃 J。\n# en: Count it\nopt.left.label [5fce81] = 数一数\n# en: Sixty crowns. A note: \"Interest. Don't ask. — C.\" You don't ask.\nopt.left.say.1 [f9e2eb] = 六十克朗。一张字条：“利息。别问。——科”你没问。\n# en: Three crowns and a very convincing IOU for the other twenty-seven, with a doodle of a goat.\nopt.left.say.2 [8ec290] = 三克朗，外加一张写着剩下二十七克朗、非常有说服力的欠条，上面还画了只山羊。\n# en: Find him and ask where it came from\nopt.right.label [397662] = 找到他，问钱哪儿来的\n# en: \"I won it,\" he says. \"Fairly. Mostly.\" He pushes the exact thirty into your hand. \"The rest's mine. I've got another debt to pay.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [efb600] = “赢来的，”他说，“公平地。大体上。”他把正好三十克朗塞进你手里。“剩下的是我的。我还有另一笔债要还。”\n\n@event corvin_rooftop\n# en: Up on the Roof\ntitle [6ebee8] = 屋顶上\n# en: On the roof at 2am, Corvin goes quiet. \"I'm on my last warning,\" he says. \"One more detention and they send me home. And home is... not a good place.\" He laughs, badly.\ntext [dc6f28] = 凌晨两点的屋顶上，科尔文沉默了下来。“我已经是最后一次警告了，”他说，“再关一次禁闭，他们就送我回家。而家……不是个好地方。”他笑了，笑得很难看。\n# en: Two students sitting on a steep slate roof under a full moon, legs dangling over a dizzying drop, sharing a bottle of something fizzy.\nscene [802031] = 满月下两个学生坐在陡峭的石板屋顶上，双腿悬在令人眩晕的高处，分享着一瓶冒泡的饮料。\n# en: Tell him to keep his head down\nopt.left.label [b7afec] = 叫他低调点\n# en: \"Head down. Right. I'll try anything once.\" He keeps his head down for almost a month.\nopt.left.say.1 [199c3a] = “低调。好。什么我都愿意试一次。”他低调了将近一个月。\n# en: Promise you'll cover for him\nopt.right.label [f74b9c] = 保证会帮他打掩护\n# en: He looks at you like nobody has ever promised him anything. \"You're an idiot,\" he says. \"Thank you.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [34b1d4] = 他看着你，好像从没有人给过他任何承诺。“你是个傻瓜，”他说，“谢谢你。”\n\n@event corvin_framed\n# en: Corvin Is Framed\ntitle [bd8e3b] = 科尔文被陷害了\n# en: The headmistress's silver goblet has been found in Corvin's trunk. He swears he didn't take it. Bram, who found it, looks very pleased. Corvin is one detention from expulsion.\ntext [7254e4] = 院长的银高脚杯在科尔文的行李箱里被找到了。他发誓不是他拿的。发现它的布拉姆看起来非常得意。科尔文离被开除只差一次禁闭。\n# en: A boy standing before a disciplinary desk, a stolen silver goblet on it, a smug prefect at his shoulder.\nscene [c0253d] = 一个男生站在纪律桌前，桌上放着一只失窃的银高脚杯，一个得意的级长站在他肩旁。\n# en: Stay out of it\nopt.left.label [03d24a] = 不掺和\n# en: Corvin talks his way out, barely. He doesn't look at you for a month.\nopt.left.say.1 [90a1f1] = 科尔文勉强靠嘴皮子脱了身。他一个月没正眼看你。\n# en: Corvin is suspended for a month. When he comes back, he's different. Quieter.\nopt.left.say.2 [506649] = 科尔文被停学一个月。回来时，他变了。更沉默了。\n# en: Find proof he was framed\nopt.right.label [0665b1] = 找出他被陷害的证据\n# en: You find the bill of sale: Bram bought a fake. Bram is stripped of his sash. Corvin buys you a goat. An actual goat.\nopt.right.say.1 [7307a6] = 你找到了那张收据：是布拉姆买了个赝品。布拉姆被摘掉了绶带。科尔文送了你一只山羊。真的山羊。\n# en: You can't prove it. But you stood up for him in front of everyone, and he'll never forget it. Neither will Bram.\nopt.right.say.2 [1b083f] = 你证明不了。但你当着所有人的面为他站了出来，他永远不会忘记。布拉姆也不会。\n\n@event corvin_confession\n# en: Corvin, Serious for Once\ntitle [4a31ce] = 难得认真的科尔文\n# en: Corvin holds out a playing card. The queen of hearts. His hand isn't quite steady. \"No trick,\" he says. \"No angle. I just — I like you more than I like anything, and I'm terrified, and I wanted you to have this.\"\ntext [87fdba] = 科尔文递过来一张扑克牌。红桃 Q。他的手有点不稳。“没有把戏，”他说，“没有算计。我只是——我喜欢你，胜过喜欢任何东西，我怕得要命，而且我想把这个给你。”\n# en: A boy holding out a playing card — the queen of hearts — with an unsteady hand, no grin for once.\nscene [e450dd] = 一个男生用不太稳的手递出一张扑克牌——红桃 Q——这一次他没有笑。\n# en: Gently decline\nopt.left.label [975956] = 温柔地拒绝\n# en: He laughs, too quickly. \"Worth a shot.\" He keeps the card. You see it sometimes, tucked in his sleeve.\nopt.left.say.1 [cd0293] = 他笑了，笑得太快。“值得一试。”他留着那张牌。你有时会看见它藏在他的袖子里。\n# en: Take the card\nopt.right.label [e7f785] = 接过那张牌\n# en: For once, Corvin Ashby has nothing to say. He just kisses you, and then grins, and then can't stop grinning.\nopt.right.say.1 [108f24] = 科尔文·阿什比难得地无话可说。他只是吻了你，然后咧嘴笑，然后再也停不下来。\n\n@event corvin_runaway\n# en: Let's Leave Tonight\ntitle [03c686] = 今晚就走吧\n# en: Corvin's at your door at midnight, bag over his shoulder. \"There's a sky-barge at the dock and nobody guarding it. Let's go. Tonight. The world's huge and we're wasting it in classrooms.\"\ntext [c8d466] = 午夜，科尔文背着包站在你门口。“码头上停着一艘天空驳船，没人看守。走吧。今晚。世界那么大，我们却在教室里浪费它。”\n# en: A small sky-barge tied to the academy dock at midnight, lanterns lit, a boy at the tiller holding out his hand.\nscene [6e8955] = 午夜时分，一艘小天空驳船系在学院码头上，灯笼亮着，一个男生站在舵边向你伸出手。\n# en: Stay and finish what you started\nopt.left.label [6b504c] = 留下来，把开始的事做完\n# en: He looks at you for a long time. Then he drops the bag. \"Fine. But after graduation, we steal a boat. A big one.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [d46286] = 他看了你很久。然后放下了包。“行。但毕业以后，我们去偷条船。一条大的。”\n# en: Take his hand\nopt.right.label [0c017b] = 握住他的手\n\n@event mira_intro\n# en: An Explosion in the West Wing\ntitle [dbebf9] = 西翼爆炸\n# en: The west wing explodes. Not badly. A figure in goggles stumbles out of the purple smoke, coughing, and gives you a thumbs-up. \"Don't worry! That was intentional! Mostly! Can you hold this?\" They hand you a ticking teapot.\ntext [61d334] = 西翼炸了。不严重。一个戴护目镜的身影咳嗽着从紫烟里踉跄出来，冲你竖起大拇指。“别担心！是故意的！大体上！能帮我拿一下这个吗？”TA递给你一只滴答作响的茶壶。\n# en: A cloud of purple smoke billowing from a workshop door, a soot-covered student emerging with goggles and a thumbs-up.\nscene [6592e9] = 一团紫烟从工坊门口滚滚涌出，一个满身煤灰、戴着护目镜的学生走出来竖起大拇指。\n# en: Put the teapot down and back away\nopt.left.label [0e8c08] = 放下茶壶，往后退\n# en: The teapot stops ticking and starts humming the academy anthem. \"Mira,\" they say. \"They/them. Thanks for not throwing it.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [3b2c89] = 茶壶停止了滴答，开始哼起院歌。“米拉，”TA说，“代词用 TA。谢谢你没把它扔出去。”\n# en: Hold the teapot steady\nopt.right.label [74455a] = 稳稳端住茶壶\n# en: You keep it level while they twist a valve. It stops ticking. \"You've got good hands,\" Mira says. \"I'm going to need those.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [d96a80] = 你端平茶壶，TA拧了拧一个阀门。滴答声停了。“你的手很稳，”米拉说，“我会需要这双手的。”\n# en: The teapot goes off. Your eyebrows survive, mostly. Mira is apologising and laughing at the same time.\nopt.right.say.2 [281601] = 茶壶炸了。你的眉毛基本保住了。米拉一边道歉一边笑。\n\n@event mira_owl_gift\n# en: A Present With Gears\ntitle [940c6d] = 带齿轮的礼物\n# en: Mira presents a brass owl with amber glass eyes. It blinks, hoots the hour and says \"HOMEWORK\" in a tinny voice. \"Made it for you. It remembers deadlines. And birthdays. And grudges, slightly.\"\ntext [448979] = 米拉拿出一只长着琥珀玻璃眼睛的黄铜猫头鹰。它眨眨眼，报了时，然后用细细的声音说：“作业。”“给你做的。它会记住截止日期。还有生日。还有一点点仇。”\n# en: A small brass owl with glowing amber eyes perched on a student's wrist, its clockwork feathers fluffing.\nscene [0ec99d] = 一只长着发光琥珀眼睛的小黄铜猫头鹰栖在学生的手腕上，发条羽毛蓬松起来。\n# en: It's too much\nopt.left.label [8c1449] = 这太贵重了\n# en: Mira looks crushed, then rallies. \"I'll give it to Thorne. He needs reminding to smile.\" The owl, you hear, refuses to work for him.\nopt.left.say.1 [f8ffdc] = 米拉一脸受伤，随即振作起来。“那我送给索恩。他需要有人提醒他笑一笑。”你听说，那只猫头鹰拒绝为他工作。\n# en: Accept it gratefully\nopt.right.label [0fb4bf] = 感激地收下\n# en: The owl settles on your shoulder and says, \"WELCOME.\" Mira pretends to have something in their eye. At six the next morning, the owl says \"HOMEWORK.\" It says it every morning for the rest of the year.\nopt.right.say.1 [3b26e8] = 猫头鹰落在你肩上，说：“欢迎。”米拉假装眼睛里进了东西。第二天早上六点，猫头鹰说：“作业。”接下来的一整年，它每天早上都这么说。\n\n@event mira_fair\n# en: The Artificers' Fair\ntitle [e5694f] = 奇械博览会\n# en: The Artificers' Fair. Mira's entry is under a sheet and occasionally growls. \"I need an assistant to stand next to it and look confident,\" they say. \"And possibly run.\"\ntext [3cec26] = 奇械博览会。米拉的参赛作品盖在一块布下，时不时发出低吼。“我需要一个助手站在它旁边，看起来很自信，”TA说，“可能还需要跑。”\n# en: A hall crammed with bizarre inventions — walking teapots, singing clocks — and one enormous brass machine draped in a sheet.\nscene [63de4d] = 大厅里挤满了千奇百怪的发明——会走路的茶壶、会唱歌的钟——还有一台盖着布的巨大黄铜机器。\n# en: Watch from a safe distance\nopt.left.label [5c0ef7] = 在安全距离外观看\n# en: The machine turns out to be a mechanical bear that bakes bread. It wins second place. Mira is ecstatic and flour-covered.\nopt.left.say.1 [1e39fa] = 那台机器原来是一只会烤面包的机械熊。它拿了二等奖。米拉欣喜若狂，满身面粉。\n# en: Be the confident assistant\nopt.right.label [bfb41b] = 当那个自信的助手\n# en: You present it like a showman. The bear bakes a perfect loaf and bows. First prize. Mira kisses the trophy, then, in a fluster, your cheek.\nopt.right.say.1 [455bf9] = 你像个表演家一样介绍它。熊烤出一条完美的面包，还鞠了一躬。一等奖。米拉亲了一下奖杯，然后慌慌张张地，亲了一下你的脸颊。\n# en: The bear eats your sleeve. The crowd loves it. Third prize and a very good story.\nopt.right.say.2 [8603c7] = 熊吃了你的袖子。观众爱死了。三等奖，外加一个特别好的故事。\n\n@event mira_workshop_closed\n# en: The Workshop Is Shut\ntitle [61b17b] = 工坊被封了\n# en: After the third fire this term, the faculty has padlocked Mira's workshop. Mira is sitting on a toolbox outside it, looking like someone took their heart and filed it under \"hazards\".\ntext [ec6009] = 这学期第三场火灾之后，学院给米拉的工坊上了锁。米拉坐在门外的工具箱上，看起来像有人把TA的心拿走，归档到了“危险品”一栏。\n# en: A workshop door chained shut with a faculty notice nailed to it, a forlorn student sitting on a toolbox outside.\nscene [d57061] = 一扇被铁链锁住的工坊门，上面钉着学院告示，一个失魂落魄的学生坐在门外的工具箱上。\n# en: Plead their case to Professor Thorne\nopt.left.label [fa15a7] = 向索恩教授求情\n# en: Thorne agrees to reopen it — with fire buckets and a weekly inspection. Mira cries on you. Oil and all.\nopt.left.say.1 [f41802] = 索恩同意重新开放——条件是配备消防桶并每周检查。米拉抱着你哭了。连油带泪。\n# en: Thorne refuses. But Mira heard you try. \"Nobody's ever argued for me before,\" they say.\nopt.left.say.2 [6d185c] = 索恩拒绝了。但米拉听到了你的争取。“从来没有人替我争过。”TA说。\n# en: Help them build a secret workshop\nopt.right.label [4db846] = 帮TA建一个秘密工坊\n# en: An abandoned classroom behind a tapestry. You bring candles; Mira brings everything else. It's perfect. It's almost entirely fireproof.\nopt.right.say.1 [609d97] = 挂毯后面一间废弃的教室。你带来了蜡烛；米拉带来了其他所有东西。完美。而且几乎完全防火。\n# en: Bram finds the secret workshop on day two. Worth it, says Mira, scrubbing cauldrons beside you.\nopt.right.say.2 [59c19b] = 第二天布拉姆就发现了秘密工坊。值了，米拉一边在你旁边刷坩埚一边说。\n\n@event mira_dance_machine\n# en: The Dancing Machine\ntitle [88d879] = 跳舞机\n# en: Mira has built a machine that teaches dancing. It has six legs and opinions. \"It needs a test subject,\" they say. \"It's mostly safe. It only kicked me twice.\"\ntext [64dcfa] = 米拉造了一台教人跳舞的机器。它有六条腿，还有自己的主见。“它需要一个测试对象，”TA说，“基本上是安全的。它只踢了我两次。”\n# en: A spindly brass contraption with too many legs attempting a waltz in an empty classroom, a student dancing with it.\nscene [76ab76] = 空荡荡的教室里，一台腿太多的细长黄铜装置正试图跳华尔兹，一个学生在和它共舞。\n# en: Make Mira test it\nopt.left.label [eef903] = 让米拉自己测\n# en: You watch Mira and the machine waltz, badly, across the room. It kicks them twice; Mira gives you a look. It's the funniest thing you've ever seen.\nopt.left.say.1 [14d6a5] = 你看着米拉和机器笨拙地在教室里跳华尔兹。它踢了米拉两脚；米拉瞪了你一眼。这是你见过最好笑的事。\n# en: Dance with the machine\nopt.right.label [39f27e] = 和机器跳舞\n# en: It kicks you twice too. But by the end you can waltz, and Mira is clapping with oily hands.\nopt.right.say.1 [b15018] = 它也踢了你两次。但最后你学会了华尔兹，米拉用满是油污的手鼓着掌。\n\n@event mira_confession\n# en: Blueprint\ntitle [680200] = 蓝图\n# en: Mira unrolls a blueprint. It's a mechanical heart, labelled in tiny careful handwriting: \"Does not work without the other part.\" They clear their throat. \"I'm bad at saying things. So I drew it. You're the other part. If you want.\"\ntext [0dae43] = 米拉展开一张蓝图。上面是一颗机械心脏，用细小工整的字迹标注着：“缺少另一个部件时无法运转。”TA清了清嗓子。“我不擅长说话。所以我画了出来。你就是那个部件。如果你愿意的话。”\n# en: A rolled-out blueprint on a workbench showing a heart-shaped mechanism labelled with tiny careful notes, a nervous artificer beside it.\nscene [4f0a67] = 工作台上展开一张蓝图，画着心形机械装置并标满细小工整的注释，旁边站着一个紧张的奇械师。\n# en: Kindly say no\nopt.left.label [304162] = 委婉地拒绝\n# en: Mira rolls up the blueprint carefully. \"Good to know. Data is data.\" They're fine. They're mostly fine.\nopt.left.say.1 [f124f5] = 米拉小心地卷起蓝图。“知道了。数据就是数据。”TA没事。TA基本上没事。\n# en: \"I want.\"\nopt.right.label [12191a] = “我愿意。”\n# en: The heart doesn't need to work. But Mira builds it anyway, and it ticks on your windowsill, in time with your own.\nopt.right.say.1 [719a2e] = 那颗心不需要真的运转。但米拉还是把它造了出来，它在你的窗台上滴答作响，与你的心跳同拍。\n\n@event mira_family_business\n# en: The Family Clockworks\ntitle [a29c6c] = 家族钟表行\n# en: A letter from Mira's parents: the family clockworks needs them home. A ticket is enclosed. \"They think this is a hobby,\" Mira says. \"This. Is. Not. A. Hobby.\"\ntext [811e81] = 米拉父母来信：家族钟表行需要TA回家。信里附了一张车票。“他们以为这是兴趣爱好，”米拉说，“这。不是。兴趣。爱好。”\n# en: An artificer holding a stiff letter embossed with a gear crest, a train ticket tucked inside, staring at it blankly.\nscene [2991ee] = 一个奇械师拿着一封印有齿轮家徽的硬挺信件，里面夹着一张车票，茫然地盯着它。\n# en: Tell them family comes first\nopt.left.label [170ef7] = 告诉TA家人最重要\n# en: Mira goes home for a month and comes back with a new scar and a clearer jaw. \"They know now,\" they say. \"It's not a hobby.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [9235e9] = 米拉回家待了一个月，回来时多了一道新疤，下颌线也更坚定了。“他们现在知道了，”TA说，“这不是兴趣爱好。”\n# en: Help them write back\nopt.right.label [7d4c22] = 帮TA写回信\n# en: Together you write a letter so persuasive it should be framed. Mira's mother replies: \"Fine. Win a prize.\" Mira intends to win all of them.\nopt.right.say.1 [be0fc3] = 你们一起写了一封有说服力到该裱起来的信。米拉的母亲回信：“好吧。拿个奖回来。”米拉打算把所有奖都拿回来。\n\n@event nessa_intro\n# en: First Impressions\ntitle [eee0a7] = 第一印象\n# en: A girl with a quill floating at her shoulder plants herself in front of you. \"Nessa Quill, Lumen Ledger. First impressions of Lumenhold, go. Be interesting, please. The last six were boring.\"\ntext [569607] = 一个肩头飘着羽毛笔的女孩往你面前一站。“内莎·奎尔，《流明纪事报》。对流明堡的第一印象，说吧。拜托说点有意思的。前六个都很无聊。”\n# en: A girl with ink-stained fingers and a self-writing quill floating at her shoulder, holding a notebook out expectantly.\nscene [e133f9] = 一个手指沾满墨迹的女孩，肩头飘着一支自动羽毛笔，满怀期待地递出笔记本。\n# en: Give a safe answer\nopt.left.label [6b761a] = 给个稳妥的回答\n# en: The quill writes \"SAFE\" in the margin and underlines it twice. Nessa sighs and moves on.\nopt.left.say.1 [9dd6db] = 羽毛笔在页边写下“稳妥”，还划了两道线。内莎叹了口气，走向下一个人。\n# en: Say something outrageous\nopt.right.label [9ecd42] = 说点惊人之语\n# en: You're quoted on the front page. Half the academy now thinks you're hilarious. The other half thinks you're dangerous. Nessa thinks both.\nopt.right.say.1 [83d022] = 你上了头版。现在半个学院觉得你很搞笑。另一半觉得你很危险。内莎觉得两者都是。\n# en: You are quoted. Out of context. Extremely out of context.\nopt.right.say.2 [8af99f] = 你被引用了。断章取义了。极其断章取义。\n\n@event nessa_gossip_column\n# en: You're in the Gossip Column\ntitle [22e6ad] = 你上了八卦专栏\n# en: This week's Ledger gossip column features you, prominently, under the headline \"WHO IS THE MYSTERY STUDENT ALWAYS IN THE LIBRARY AT MIDNIGHT?\" Nessa swears she didn't write it.\ntext [6890e5] = 本周《纪事报》的八卦专栏以醒目的标题刊登了你：“总在午夜出现在图书馆的神秘学生是谁？”内莎发誓不是她写的。\n# en: A copy of the Lumen Ledger pinned to a noticeboard, a gossip column circled in red ink, students gathered round giggling.\nscene [ab1073] = 布告栏上钉着一份《流明纪事报》，八卦专栏被红墨水圈了出来，学生们围在一起咯咯笑。\n# en: Confront Nessa\nopt.left.label [e29ecc] = 找内莎对质\n# en: She prints a correction. Sort of. It's a much more flattering paragraph about you.\nopt.left.say.1 [4756b3] = 她登了一则更正。算是吧。是一段把你夸得天花乱坠的文字。\n# en: \"Freedom of the press,\" says Nessa, sweetly. You both know she wrote it.\nopt.left.say.2 [8b1a52] = “新闻自由。”内莎甜甜地说。你们都知道是她写的。\n# en: Lean into the mystery\nopt.right.label [0d58af] = 顺水推舟，故弄玄虚\n# en: You start leaving cryptic notes in the library. The column runs for six more weeks. Nessa is delighted.\nopt.right.say.1 [ddffb0] = 你开始在图书馆里留下神秘的字条。这个专栏又连载了六周。内莎乐坏了。\n# en: The column runs for six more weeks. By the fourth it has decided you are haunting the library, and people move tables when you sit down.\nopt.right.say.2 [524d7e] = 专栏又连载了六周。到第四周，它认定你在图书馆闹鬼，你一坐下，别人就换桌子。\n\n@event nessa_source\n# en: The Source\ntitle [86f411] = 线人\n# en: Nessa leans in. \"You've got faculty access. The professors are planning to change the exam format and they're hiding it. I just need a quote. Anonymous. Please?\"\ntext [9e9d3b] = 内莎凑过来。“你能接触到教职员。教授们打算改考试形式，还瞒着大家。我只需要一句引述。匿名的。拜托？”\n# en: Two students in a dark corner of the library, one whispering, the other writing furiously in a notebook.\nscene [7686b3] = 图书馆昏暗的角落里两个学生，一个在低语，另一个在笔记本上奋笔疾书。\n# en: Refuse — that's a breach of trust\nopt.left.label [a7260f] = 拒绝——这是背信\n# en: Nessa sulks for a day. Then she respects you for it, which she hates.\nopt.left.say.1 [5304b2] = 内莎生了一天闷气。然后她因此尊重你，这让她很恼火。\n# en: Give her the scoop\nopt.right.label [3204b4] = 把独家消息给她\n# en: The story runs. Thorne knows exactly who talked. His glare could curdle milk.\nopt.right.say.1 [019448] = 报道刊出了。索恩完全清楚是谁说出去的。他的瞪视能让牛奶结块。\n# en: The story runs. Students are grateful. Nobody knows it was you. Nessa buys you cake.\nopt.right.say.2 [baa867] = 报道刊出了。学生们心怀感激。没人知道是你。内莎请你吃了蛋糕。\n\n@event nessa_hollow_story\n# en: Nessa Smells a Story\ntitle [9fa969] = 内莎嗅到了新闻\n# en: Nessa's office wall is covered in red string. In the middle: a sketch of a black star. \"Frozen fountains. Missing pages. A transfer from the North. Something's going on and you know about it. Spill.\"\ntext [f90ecc] = 内莎办公室的墙上缠满了红线。正中间：一幅黑星的速写。“结冰的喷泉。失踪的书页。一个从北方来的转学生。有什么事正在发生，而你知道内情。说吧。”\n# en: A wall in the newspaper office covered in pinned clippings and red string connecting a frozen lake, a black star and a fur-coated boy.\nscene [320fce] = 报社墙上钉满剪报，红线连接着冰封的湖、一颗黑星和一个穿毛皮大衣的少年。\n# en: Warn her off — it's dangerous\nopt.left.label [deabae] = 劝她收手——太危险了\n# en: \"That's what people say right before the best stories.\" But she takes the string down. Mostly.\nopt.left.say.1 [448d70] = “大家都是在最好的新闻出现之前这么说的。”但她还是把红线拆了。大部分。\n# en: Share what you know\nopt.right.label [6ba460] = 告诉她你知道的\n# en: Nessa's article runs with half the facts. The man in grey reads it. So does Bram, who starts following you around.\nopt.right.say.1 [84698a] = 内莎的文章只登出了一半事实。灰衣人读到了。布拉姆也读到了，他开始到处跟着你。\n# en: Nessa's notes are scarily thorough. She's found a record of three students who vanished in 1723. All on Blossom moons.\nopt.right.say.2 [ab1477] = 内莎的笔记详尽得吓人。她找到了一份记录：1723 年有三个学生失踪。全都在花潮之月。\n\n@event nessa_paper_threatened\n# en: Stop the Presses\ntitle [447c01] = 停印\n# en: The headmistress has threatened to shut down the Ledger after an article about faculty salaries. Nessa is organising a protest. \"I need people on the steps. Visible people. You.\"\ntext [2ab439] = 一篇关于教职员薪水的文章刊出后，院长威胁要关停《纪事报》。内莎正在组织抗议。“我需要有人站在台阶上。显眼的人。你。”\n# en: A printing press with a faculty seal stamped across it, a defiant student standing in front of it with arms crossed.\nscene [94267d] = 一台被盖上学院封印的印刷机，一个倔强的学生抱着双臂站在它前面。\n# en: Stay out of it\nopt.left.label [03d24a] = 不掺和\n# en: The protest is small. The Ledger survives, barely. Nessa notices who wasn't there.\nopt.left.say.1 [98928f] = 抗议规模很小。《纪事报》勉强保住了。内莎记住了谁没来。\n# en: Stand on the steps\nopt.right.label [1c328c] = 站上台阶\n# en: Forty students on the steps, chanting. The headmistress comes out and, astonishingly, listens. The Ledger stays.\nopt.right.say.1 [2a040b] = 四十个学生站在台阶上高呼口号。院长走了出来，出人意料地，她听了。《纪事报》保住了。\n\n@event nessa_confession\n# en: Off the Record\ntitle [a5d3b6] = 不予记录\n# en: Nessa puts down her quill. Then she tells it to stop writing. It's the first time you've seen it still. \"This is off the record,\" she says. \"Completely. I like you. I've drafted this eleven times. None of the drafts were good enough.\"\ntext [b22faa] = 内莎放下了羽毛笔。然后命令它停止书写。这是你第一次看见它静止不动。“这段不予记录，”她说，“完全不记。我喜欢你。这段话我打了十一遍草稿。没有一稿够好。”\n# en: A girl with an inkpot in one hand, her self-writing quill hovering above a blank page that she has commanded to stay blank.\nscene [b44415] = 一个女孩一手拿着墨水瓶，她的自动羽毛笔悬在一张空白的纸上方——她命令那张纸保持空白。\n# en: Gently say no\nopt.left.label [f46b92] = 温柔地拒绝\n# en: \"Noted,\" she says crisply. She keeps her quill still a moment longer. Then it starts writing again, and so does she.\nopt.left.say.1 [3ad266] = “记下了。”她干脆地说。她让羽毛笔多静止了一会儿。然后它重新开始书写，她也是。\n# en: Kiss her\nopt.right.label [5fa79c] = 吻她\n# en: Her quill, unattended, writes a single word in the margin: \"FINALLY.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [995169] = 无人看管的羽毛笔在页边写下了两个字：“终于。”\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/pools/castle_halls.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/pools/castle_halls.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event courtyard_sundial_bench\n# en: The Slow Sundial\ntitle [eef7f9] = 慢吞吞的日晷\n# en: {There's a bench by the old sundial, which runs eleven minutes slow.|Someone has scratched a tiny \"+11\" by the dial's noon. A fellow sufferer.|The sundial bench is yours by custom. A first-year asked if you came with it.} {season == \"winter\": Frost fills the hour lines, and the shadow skates across them.|{~Pigeons are holding a meeting on the gnomon.|A first-year checks a watch against it, horrified.|Someone has chalked a face where noon should be.}}\ntext [dcbbbc] = {老日晷旁有一张长椅。这日晷慢十一分钟。|有人在晷面正午旁刻了个小小的“+11”。同病相怜的人。|日晷长椅按惯例归你了。有个一年级生问过，你是不是和它配套的。} {season == \"winter\": 霜填满了时刻刻线，影子在上面滑冰。|{~一群鸽子正在晷针上开会。|一个一年级生拿怀表对它，吓坏了。|有人在正午的位置用粉笔画了张脸。}}\n# en: A stone bench beside a weathered sundial in a flagstone courtyard, a student reading with their back to the dial.\nscene [549ef0] = 石板庭院里，一张石凳挨着一座饱经风霜的日晷，一个学生背对晷面读书。\n# en: Read until the shadow moves\nopt.left.label [7b2740] = 读到影子挪动为止\n# en: {season == \"winter\": {~Your fingers go numb by page two. The chapter is worth it.|You read in gloves, turning pages with your teeth.}|{~You finish a chapter in the minutes the sundial owes you.|A pigeon reads over your shoulder, unimpressed.|The shadow crawls; you outpace it by three pages.}}\nopt.left.say.1 [5a10ee] = {season == \"winter\": {~读到第二页手指就冻麻了。这一章值得。|你戴着手套读，用牙齿翻页。}|{~日晷欠你的那几分钟，够你读完一章。|一只鸽子凑在你肩头一起读，不以为然。|影子慢慢爬，你领先它三页。}}\n# en: Close your eyes a while\nopt.right.label [64f7ca] = 闭眼歇一会儿\n# en: {~You wake with a chalk moustache and no suspects.|You wake to a prefect clearing his throat an inch from your ear.|You wake rested and late, eleven minutes late by the dial.}\nopt.right.say.1 [5659ef] = {~你醒来时脸上多了粉笔胡子，却找不到嫌疑人。|你醒来时，一位级长正贴着你耳边清嗓子。|你醒来时神清气爽，也迟到了——按日晷算，正好迟到十一分钟。}\n\n@event courtyard_saved_seat\n# en: A Seat on the Rim\ntitle [c87795] = 喷泉边的座位\n# en: {{here} has claimed the dry stretch of the fountain rim and waves you over.|{here} is on your favourite stretch of the fountain rim, the one the sun reaches first, and waves you over.|Your stretch of the fountain rim is known now. {here} is sitting on it and shifts over without being asked.} {~Two towers' bells are arguing about the hour.|Pigeons circle like creditors.|{season == \"winter\": Your breath hangs between you.|Someone's lunch smells better than yours.}}\ntext [0fa2f5] = {{here}占了喷泉边沿上干燥的一段，朝你招手。|{here}坐在你最喜欢的那段喷泉边沿上——太阳最先照到的那段——朝你招手。|喷泉边沿上你的那一段如今人尽皆知。{here}正坐在上面，不等你开口就挪了挪。} {~两座塔的钟正为几点钟争执不休。|鸽子像债主一样盘旋。|{season == \"winter\": 你们之间呵出的白气悬在半空。|别人的午饭闻起来比你的香。}}\n# en: Two students sitting on the dry edge of a courtyard fountain, bags between them, pigeons circling overhead.\nscene [9547e3] = 两个学生坐在庭院喷泉干燥的边沿上，书包放在中间，鸽子在头顶盘旋。\n# en: Sit down and catch up\nopt.left.label [ebe7f0] = 坐下聊聊\n# en: {~Corvin tells you three stories, one of them true, and calls you \"saint\" when you don't ask which.|Corvin spots a prefect, palms something off the rim, and goes on talking as if nothing happened.}\nopt.left.say.1 [3afeb8] = {~科尔文给你讲了三个故事，只有一个是真的；你没问是哪个，他便叫你“圣人”。|科尔文瞥见一位级长，从边沿上顺走了什么，接着若无其事地往下说。}\n# en: {~Nessa interviews you about the weather, the soup and your love life, in that order, and writes down all three.|Nessa reads you tomorrow's headline and asks if it's too cruel. It is. She keeps it.}\nopt.left.say.2 [d2957f] = {~内莎就天气、汤和你的感情生活依次采访了你，三样都记了下来。|内莎给你念明天的头条，问是不是太刻薄了。是的。她决定照登。}\n# en: {~Bram lists every rule broken in the courtyard while you sit. Then, gruffly, he asks how your exams went.|Bram writes a demerit for a passing first-year, then tears it up when he thinks you aren't looking.}\nopt.left.say.3 [397168] = {~你坐着的工夫，布拉姆把庭院里违反的校规一条条数了一遍。然后，他粗声粗气地问你考得怎么样。|布拉姆给路过的一年级生记了一次过，以为你没看见时又把单子撕了。}\n# en: {~You trade the castle's news for an hour. {here} does most of the trading.|You talk until the shadows cross the fountain. Neither of you can remember about what.}\nopt.left.say.4 [1508f0] = {~你们交换了一小时城堡新闻，大多是{here}在说。|你们一直聊到影子爬过喷泉，谁也想不起聊了些什么。}\n# en: Wave, and keep walking\nopt.right.label [ff1be9] = 挥挥手，继续走\n# en: {~You promise next time. {here} looks as if {they} {are} counting.|You spend the hour with a book instead. It is a good book; it is not much company.}\nopt.right.say.1 [7519a0] = {~你说下次一定。{here}看上去像是在记账。|你改和一本书待了一小时。书很好，只是不太能陪人。}\n\n@event courtyard_seasonal_game\n# en: The Courtyard Game\ntitle [bfcc17] = 庭院游戏\n# en: {season == \"winter\": A snowball war between Emberhall and Stillwater has reached the treaty stage.|{season == \"spring\": A charmed ball is loose, dodging everyone who kicks at it.|{season == \"summer\": It's too hot for sport, so it's catch with a charmed apple that bites.|It's conker season. The champion conker is said to be older than the fountain.}}} {Someone shouts for one more player.|They shout for you by name.|Both sides shout for you, and start bidding.}\ntext [d0e0af] = {season == \"winter\": 烬厅和静水之间的雪仗已经打到了签订和约的阶段。|{season == \"spring\": 一只施了魔法的球在庭院里乱窜，谁去踢就躲谁。|{season == \"summer\": 天太热，没法运动，于是改玩抛接一只会咬人的魔法苹果。|七叶树果大战的季节到了。据说那颗冠军果比喷泉还老。}}} {有人喊着还差一个人。|这回他们直接喊你的名字。|两边都在喊你，还开始竞价。}\n# en: Students playing a rowdy game across a flagstone courtyard while others crowd the edges shouting bets.\nscene [9846ca] = 学生们在石板庭院里玩得热火朝天，旁边围着的人大声下注。\n# en: Join in\nopt.left.label [a87be8] = 加入\n# en: {~Your side wins, and the result is disputed until supper.|You score the deciding point and are carried three whole steps before they drop you.}\nopt.left.say.1 [4a86ad] = {~你们这边赢了，比分一直争到晚饭。|你拿下了制胜一分，被大家抬着走了整整三步，然后摔了下来。}\n# en: {~You are flattened, politely, by both sides at once.|You spend the rest of the game as the thing the other side aims at.}\nopt.left.say.2 [af1171] = {~你被两队人马同时、礼貌地压扁了。|余下的比赛里，你成了对方瞄准的那个东西。}\n# en: Hold the coats and the bets\nopt.right.label [175f1b] = 帮忙看外套和赌注\n# en: {~The favourite loses. You are the only person in the courtyard who is pleased about it.|The underdog wins by a whisker, and the coats you are holding are suddenly lighter on your conscience.}\nopt.right.say.1 [445a49] = {~大热门输了。整个庭院里只有你一个人高兴。|冷门险胜，你手里抱着的外套忽然让你良心轻了不少。}\n# en: {~The favourite wins, and so does everyone who backed them, out of your pocket.|Everyone bet the same way, and everyone was right. You pay out in a hail of small coins.}\nopt.right.say.2 [68429d] = {~大热门赢了，押它的人也都赢了——钱从你兜里出。|大家都押了同一边，也都押对了。你在一阵小硬币的冰雹里赔了钱。}\n\n@event library_wandering_book\n# en: The Book That Moved\ntitle [fe710d] = 会挪窝的书\n# en: {Yesterday your book was on the third floor. Today the third floor has never heard of it.|Your book has moved again, leaving a bookmark in the gap, like a note.|You keep a map of where the shelves hide your books. They've started hiding the map.} {~In its place is a shelf of cookery spells.|In its place are bound weather reports.|In its place is one slim volume, face out, waiting.} {final_year: Soon they'll play these games with someone else.|}\ntext [4356c6] = {昨天你要的书还在三楼。今天三楼说从没听说过它。|你的书又挪走了，空位上留了一张书签，像一张字条。|你画了一张书架藏书的地图。书架开始藏那张地图了。} {~原位上是一架烹饪咒语。|原位上是一排装订好的天气报告。|原位上只有一本薄薄的书，封面朝外，像在等你。} {final_year: 很快，它们就要和别人玩这种游戏了。|}\n# en: Tall library shelves leaning very slightly, a gap where one book should be, a student on a ladder peering into it.\nscene [d131b1] = 高高的书架微微倾斜，本该放一本书的地方空着，一个学生站在梯子上往缝里瞧。\n# en: Hunt it down\nopt.left.label [e1addf] = 把它找出来\n# en: {You find it on the sixth floor, filed under \"regret\".|You find it in the stairwell, halfway between floors, as if it had been trying to leave.|You find it at once. Either you are learning the shelves, or they are getting lazy.}\nopt.left.say.1 [05a6d5] = {你在六楼找到了它，归在“悔恨”类下。|你在楼梯间找到了它，卡在两层之间，好像它正打算出走。|你一下子就找到了。要么是你摸透了书架，要么是书架变懒了。}\n# en: Read what's in front of you\nopt.right.label [5bdb99] = 读手边这本\n# en: {~You arrive at seminar having read the wrong book with great conviction.|You learn a great deal about something nobody will ever ask you about.|The wrong book turns out to be the right book for a question nobody asks until the exam.}\nopt.right.say.1 [f419ac] = {~你满怀信心地带着读错的书去上研讨课。|你学到了大量永远不会有人问起的知识。|读错的书恰好是对的书——对一道直到考试才有人问的题。}\n\n@event library_shared_table\n# en: The Last Free Chair\ntitle [694ef7] = 最后一把空椅子\n# en: {The library is full. The only free chair is opposite {here}.|The only free chair is opposite {here}. A shelf has leaned over it, as if keeping it for you.|The library knows your habits now. The free chair, opposite {here}, has a book you wanted waiting on it.} {~Rain ticks on the tall windows.|A shelf nearby is quietly rearranging itself.|Footnote is asleep in the returns basket.|{season == \"winter\": The radiators clank like a ghost with a grudge.|Dust turns gold in the light.}}\ntext [85baec] = {图书馆满了。唯一的空椅子在{here}对面。|唯一的空椅子在{here}对面。一个书架斜靠在椅子上方，像在替你占座。|图书馆已经摸清了你的习惯。{here}对面那把空椅子上，放着一本你想看的书在等你。} {~雨点敲着高窗。|附近的书架正悄悄自己重新排列。|“脚注”睡在还书筐里。|{season == \"winter\": 暖气片哐哐作响，像个怀恨在心的幽灵。|灰尘在光里变成金色。}}\n# en: A crowded library reading room, every table full but one, a single empty chair facing a student absorbed in a book.\nscene [b58e03] = 拥挤的图书馆阅览室，所有桌子都坐满了，只有一把空椅子，对面是个埋头读书的学生。\n# en: Take the chair and work\nopt.left.label [4d48da] = 坐下来用功\n# en: {~Kael doesn't look up. By the end of the hour there is frost on your inkwell and a tiny, careful correction in your margin.|Kael pushes a page of his notes across to you, then looks away as if it had moved on its own.}\nopt.left.say.1 [8926ef] = {~凯尔没有抬头。一小时后，你的墨水瓶上结了霜，页边多了一处细小工整的订正。|凯尔把一页笔记推到你面前，然后移开视线，仿佛那页纸是自己挪过去的。}\n# en: {~Lyra slides the candle an inch toward your side without looking up. Neither of you mentions it.|Lyra finishes before you, and waits, and says she wasn't waiting.}\nopt.left.say.2 [38a29a] = {~莱拉头也不抬，把蜡烛往你这边推了一寸。你们谁也没提。|莱拉比你先写完，却等着你，还说她没在等。}\n# en: {~Seraphine writes a word in your notes that you won't understand until next week.|Seraphine reads the same page for an hour, smiling at something that isn't on it.}\nopt.left.say.3 [395056] = {~瑟拉芬在你的笔记上写了一个词，你要到下周才会明白。|瑟拉芬对着同一页读了一小时，冲着一件不在书上的事微笑。}\n# en: {~Bram informs you that you breathe too loudly. Then he lends you his ruler.|Bram underlines every rule in his book twice. Once, he asks your opinion of one.}\nopt.left.say.4 [f53329] = {~布拉姆通知你：你呼吸太大声了。然后他把尺子借给了你。|布拉姆把书里每一条规则都划了两道线。有一次，他问你对其中一条怎么看。}\n# en: {~You work in silence for an hour, which turns out to be a kind of company.|Neither of you speaks. Both of you get further than you would have alone.}\nopt.left.say.5 [279cd0] = {~你们默默用功了一小时，原来这也算一种陪伴。|你们谁也没说话，却都比一个人时走得更远。}\n# en: Whisper a question\nopt.right.label [1952d6] = 小声问个问题\n# en: {~The conversation is worth it. The look from the front desk has a long memory.|You get three sentences in before a shush arrives from two floors up.}\nopt.right.say.1 [fa0472] = {~这番交谈很值。只是前台投来的那一眼，记性很长。|你刚说了三句，两层楼上就传来一声“嘘”。}\n\n@event library_overdue\n# en: Overdue\ntitle [40f62b] = 逾期\n# en: {The book you borrowed in a hurry is overdue, and the library knows it. The spine has started to sigh.|Another overdue book. The front desk has begun keeping a little card with your name on it.|Your overdue books sigh in chorus now, and the card at the front desk has a second side.} {~Footnote watches you approach the returns basket with professional interest.|The front desk is, for once, unattended.|A queue of first-years is paying fines in small, sad coins.}\ntext [6edf21] = {你匆忙借的那本书逾期了，图书馆心知肚明。书脊开始叹气。|又一本逾期的书。前台开始为你单独留一张写着你名字的小卡片。|你的逾期书现在齐声叹气，前台那张卡片已经写到了背面。} {~“脚注”以专业的兴趣看着你走近还书筐。|前台难得没人。|一队一年级生正用小小的、可怜的硬币交罚款。}\n# en: A student holding a book behind their back near a wicker returns basket, a cat on the front desk watching them.\nscene [9136c2] = 一个学生把书藏在身后，靠近一只柳条还书筐，前台上的猫盯着他们。\n# en: Own up and pay the fine\nopt.left.label [1ffaa0] = 认错交罚款\n# en: {The fine is paid; the desk, mollified, lends you the sequel.|You pay, and are lent another book, which you suspect is a trap.|The desk sighs, takes your coins and hands you a book it says you'll like. It is always right, which is the annoying part.}\nopt.left.say.1 [e1d0b0] = {罚款交了，前台消了气，把续集借给了你。|你交了钱，又被借了一本书，你怀疑这是个圈套。|前台叹口气，收下硬币，递给你一本说你会喜欢的书。它总是对的，这才让人恼火。}\n# en: Slip it into the basket\nopt.right.label [aa0b65] = 偷偷塞进还书筐\n# en: {~The book goes in without a sound. It stops sighing at once, which feels like forgiveness.|You drop it in under cover of a sneeze. Footnote saw. Footnote will keep it to himself, for a price.}\nopt.right.say.1 [bc9c6d] = {~书悄无声息地滑了进去，立刻不再叹气，像是原谅了你。|你借着一个喷嚏把书丢了进去。“脚注”看见了。“脚注”会替你保密——有代价的。}\n# en: {~The book announces itself with a sigh like a dying organ. Everyone turns. You pay double.|It lands with a groan. The front desk adds a mark to your card without looking up.}\nopt.right.say.2 [71c1f3] = {~那本书发出一声垂死管风琴般的叹息，宣告了自己的到来。所有人都回过头。你交了双倍罚款。|书落进筐里，呻吟了一声。前台头也不抬，在你的卡片上又记了一笔。}\n\n@event refectory_late_supper\n# en: Late Supper\ntitle [095a3c] = 晚来的晚饭\n# en: {You come in late. The benches are empty and the cooks are wiping the far tables.|The cook leaves a lamp lit at the end of the bench now, where the draught can't find you.|Your late bowl is waiting under a plate, with a spoon laid beside it. Nobody admits to it.} {year == 1: Home feels very far away tonight.|{final_year: You count the suppers you have left, and stop.|{~The candles are down to stubs.|Someone has left a half-done puzzle on the next table.}}}\ntext [9c5052] = {你来晚了。长凳都空着，厨工们在擦远处的桌子。|厨娘现在会在长凳尽头、穿堂风找不到的地方给你留一盏灯。|你那碗晚饭扣在盘子下等着你，旁边摆好了勺子。没人承认是谁放的。} {year == 1: 今晚，家显得格外遥远。|{final_year: 你数了数还剩几顿这样的晚饭，然后不数了。|{~蜡烛快烧到底了。|有人在隔壁桌上留了一幅拼了一半的拼图。}}}\n# en: The far end of a long refectory table at night, most benches empty, a single student with a bowl and a half-written letter.\nscene [0600ae] = 深夜膳堂长桌的尽头，大部分长凳空着，一个学生面前放着一只碗和一封写了一半的信。\n# en: Write a letter home\nopt.left.label [67bcd4] = 给家里写信\n# en: {~You write two pages and post them with the last of your coppers.|You write about the food. You do not write about anything else, but they'll know.|You write a letter that is mostly questions. You post it before you can make it braver.}\nopt.left.say.1 [988bf2] = {~你写了两页，用最后几枚铜板寄了出去。|你写了饭菜，别的什么都没写，但他们会懂的。|你写了一封几乎全是问题的信，趁自己还没想把它写得更勇敢之前寄了出去。}\n# en: Revise over the bowl\nopt.right.label [5fbeee] = 边吃边复习\n# en: {~You learn three dates and a spill of gravy across all of them.|The cook reads over your shoulder and corrects one of your answers. She is right.}\nopt.right.say.1 [1b9554] = {~你记住了三个年份，还有一摊洒在上面的肉汁。|厨娘从你肩后看了一眼，改正了你的一个答案。她是对的。}\n\n@event refectory_house_tables\n# en: Where to Sit\ntitle [3b38cd] = 坐哪儿\n# en: {Supper, and the question of where to sit.|Your house table has started saving a gap on the bench, roughly your width.|The gap is a named seat now, and the quiet table nods at you too.} Your house table is {~loud with a story about a goat|in the middle of a pudding treaty|singing, badly}. At the far end, {season == \"winter\": some second-years from Rootward huddle round a warming charm.|some second-years from Duskgate are arguing quietly about a map.}\ntext [386628] = {晚饭时间，又是那个问题：坐哪儿。|你们学院那桌开始在长凳上留一个空位，差不多你那么宽。|那个空位现在有了名字，安静的那桌也会朝你点头了。} 你们学院那桌{~正在大声讲一个关于山羊的故事|正在谈判一份布丁和约|正在唱歌，唱得很难听}。远处那头，{season == \"winter\": 几个根庐的二年级生围着一个取暖咒挤成一团。|几个暮门的二年级生正低声争论一张地图。}\n# en: A crowded refectory at suppertime, a student standing with a tray between a loud house table and a quieter one of strangers.\nscene [6196b3] = 晚饭时拥挤的膳堂，一个学生端着托盘站在一张喧闹的学院长桌和一张陌生人的安静桌子之间。\n# en: Squeeze in with your house\nopt.left.label [956401] = 挤进自己学院那桌\n# en: {~You eat with an elbow in your ribs and laugh the whole way through.|Somebody's story gets better every time you hear it. So does the pudding.}\nopt.left.say.1 [9bee35] = {~你肋下顶着别人的胳膊肘吃完了饭，从头笑到尾。|有人的故事每听一遍就更精彩一点，布丁也是。}\n# en: Sit with the strangers\nopt.right.label [88cc69] = 和陌生人坐一起\n# en: {~They are polite, then curious, then actually rather good company.|It is awkward for exactly one course. By pudding, they have told you their secret map route.}\nopt.right.say.1 [457b18] = {~他们先是客气，然后好奇，然后竟成了相当不错的伙伴。|尴尬只持续了一道菜。到布丁时，他们已经把秘密地图路线告诉了你。}\n\n@event refectory_pudding_tax\n# en: The Pudding Tax\ntitle [03d210] = 布丁税\n# en: {{friend} sits down opposite you and looks at your pudding the way a cat looks at a fish.|Your pudding has a reputation now. {friend} sits down opposite you with a spoon already in hand.|Word of your pudding tax has got round the refectory. Tonight {friend} comes to collect, with a little receipt.} {season == \"winter\": Tonight it's treacle sponge, steaming.|{~Tonight it's apple crumble.|Tonight it's a custard that wobbles with intent.}}\ntext [c994f2] = {{friend}在你对面坐下，看你的布丁就像猫看鱼。|你的布丁如今声名在外。{friend}在你对面坐下，勺子已经握在手里。|你的布丁税已经在膳堂传开了。今晚{friend}来收税，还带着一张小收据。} {season == \"winter\": 今晚是热气腾腾的糖浆海绵布丁。|{~今晚是苹果酥。|今晚是一份意图不明地颤动着的蛋奶冻。}}\n# en: Two friends at a refectory table, one guarding a bowl of treacle pudding, the other holding a spoon at the ready.\nscene [2f960a] = 膳堂桌边的两个朋友，一个护着一碗糖浆布丁，另一个举着勺子随时准备出手。\n# en: Pay the pudding tax\nopt.left.label [7c4562] = 交布丁税\n# en: {~Tobias apologises four times, eats it all, then tells you a long story about a goat who did the same. It helps, somehow.|Tobias eats half, then pushes the bowl back with a sprig of mint from his pocket on top, as an apology.}\nopt.left.say.1 [20994e] = {~托比亚斯道了四次歉，把它吃得一干二净，然后给你讲了个长长的故事，说一只山羊也干过这种事。不知怎么，这让你好受了些。|托比亚斯吃了一半，把碗推回来，上面放着一枝从口袋里掏出的薄荷，算是赔礼。}\n# en: {~\"Efficient,\" Lyra says, finishing it. The next day a whole pudding appears on your tray. She doesn't mention it.|Lyra eats it in four exact bites and critiques the custard. She does not give it back.}\nopt.left.say.2 [672475] = {~“高效。”莱拉吃完说道。第二天，你的托盘上出现了一整份布丁。她只字未提。|莱拉精确地四口吃完，还点评了蛋奶酱。她没还给你。}\n# en: {~Mira eats it while explaining a pudding-defence device that would probably work. Probably.|Mira eats it with a spoon they built themselves, which hums. They say the humming is probably normal.}\nopt.left.say.3 [f8d0e8] = {~米拉边吃边讲解一种防布丁被偷的装置，说大概能用。大概。|米拉用自己做的勺子吃，那勺子嗡嗡作响。TA说嗡嗡响大概是正常的。}\n# en: {~{friend} eats exactly half and leaves you the burnt corner.|{friend} eats it slowly, savouring your defeat.}\nopt.left.say.4 [565aa6] = {~{friend}正好吃了一半，把烤焦的那角留给了你。|{friend}慢慢地吃，细细品味你的失败。}\n# en: Defend your pudding\nopt.right.label [c73449] = 捍卫你的布丁\n# en: {~You hide it under a napkin and point at the window. It works. {friend} will remember this.|You claim it's been sneezed on. {friend} retreats in disgust, and you eat in peace.}\nopt.right.say.1 [38d242] = {~你把它藏在餐巾下，指着窗外。成功了。{friend}会记住这一笔的。|你声称有人对着它打过喷嚏。{friend}嫌恶地退却，你安心吃完。}\n# en: {~{friend} sees straight through you and takes the whole thing as a penalty. It is hard to stay cross.|{friend} distracts you with a story and the bowl is empty before it ends.}\nopt.right.say.2 [03e22f] = {~{friend}一眼看穿了你，把整份没收作为罚款。很难一直生气。|{friend}用一个故事分散你的注意力，故事还没讲完碗就空了。}\n\n@event lecture_tower_old_board\n# en: Someone Else's Lecture\ntitle [108850] = 别人的课\n# en: {term: {The fourth-floor hall is empty, but someone else's lecture is still on the board.|In the empty hall, a stub of chalk waits on the ledge, as if for you.|The board has a corner fenced off in chalk, labelled \"answers here\".}|In the break the boards are wiped, except one nobody dares touch.} {~Today it's a ward diagram, labelled \"wrong, but how?\"|Today it's half a proof, ending in a thumbprint.|Today it's a list headed \"on the exam\", crossed out.}\ntext [e6e2e7] = {term: {四楼的讲堂空着，黑板上却还留着别人的课。|空讲堂里，一截粉笔头在槽里等着，像是留给你的。|黑板角上用粉笔圈出一块，标着“答案写这里”。}|假期里黑板都擦干净了，只有一块没人敢碰。} {~今天是一幅护界图，标注着“错了，但错在哪？”|今天是半个证明，结尾是一枚指纹。|今天是一张题为“会考的内容”的清单，被划掉了。}\n# en: An empty spiral lecture hall, a blackboard still dense with chalk diagrams from a class that has long gone.\nscene [b21ec8] = 空荡荡的螺旋讲堂，黑板上还密密麻麻留着一堂早已散场的课的粉笔图解。\n# en: Copy it all down\nopt.left.label [71d09a] = 全部抄下来\n# en: {~Your hand cramps. Your head, for once, does not.|You copy it all and understand most of it. The rest you'll grow into.}\nopt.left.say.1 [182650] = {~你的手抽筋了。你的脑子却难得没有。|你全抄了下来，大部分都懂。剩下的，你会慢慢长进去。}\n# en: Add your own answer\nopt.right.label [b05ba7] = 写上你的答案\n# en: {~Next time, someone has added \"yes!\" under your line.|Someone has answered your answer. The board has become a correspondence.|By the afternoon someone has drawn a box around your line, to keep it safe.}\nopt.right.say.1 [8250cf] = {~下次再来，有人在你那行下面加了个“对！”|有人回答了你的回答。黑板成了一场书信往来。|到了下午，有人在你那行字周围画了个框，好好护着。}\n# en: {~The professor whose board it was finds you holding the chalk. \"Hm,\" says the look.|Your answer is wiped by the time you reach the door, by someone who knew better.}\nopt.right.say.2 [f25454] = {~那块黑板的主人——一位教授——撞见你手里拿着粉笔。那一眼在说：“嗯。”|你还没走到门口，你的答案就被一个更懂行的人擦掉了。}\n\n@event lecture_tower_stair_question\n# en: One Question a Flight\ntitle [d64af4] = 一层楼一个问题\n# en: {You meet Professor Thorne on the spiral stair. He allows one question per flight, and climbs fast.|Professor Thorne on the stair. He has slowed down by about one step in seven, which is how you know he expects you.|Thorne hands you half his stack of papers at the foot of the stair, without a word, and starts climbing.} {term: |The tower is half empty for the break; your footsteps echo twice.}{final_year: There are not many of these climbs left.|}\ntext [fa2b42] = {你在螺旋楼梯上遇见索恩教授。他允许每层楼问一个问题，而且爬得很快。|楼梯上的索恩教授。他每七级台阶慢下一级，你由此知道他在等你。|索恩在楼梯口一言不发地把一半文件塞给你，然后开始往上爬。} {term: |假期里塔楼空了一半，你的脚步声有两重回音。}{final_year: 这样的攀爬已经不剩几次了。|}\n# en: A narrow spiral stair inside a stone tower, a severe professor climbing ahead with a stack of papers, a student a step behind.\nscene [0a9608] = 石塔里狭窄的螺旋楼梯，一位严厉的教授抱着一摞文件走在前面，一个学生落后一级台阶。\n# en: Ask a hard question\nopt.left.label [7531ee] = 问个难题\n# en: {~He answers in four words, and the four words are enough.|\"Hm,\" he says, then answers it properly, all the way to the seventh floor.}\nopt.left.say.1 [0b7fe2] = {~他只用四个字回答，而这四个字就够了。|“嗯。”他说，然后认真地回答了，一直讲到七楼。}\n# en: {~He answers with a harder question and leaves you on the landing.|\"Wrong question,\" he says. \"Find the right one by Thursday.\"}\nopt.left.say.2 [0cca52] = {~他用一个更难的问题作答，把你留在了楼梯平台上。|“问错了。”他说，“星期四之前找到对的问题。”}\n# en: Climb in silence beside him\nopt.right.label [f05732] = 默默陪他爬楼\n# en: {~He climbs faster than you. At the top he says, \"Adequate,\" and you are fairly sure he means the climbing.|Neither of you says a word for seven flights. It is oddly restful, apart from your legs.}\nopt.right.say.1 [4ed2b3] = {~他爬得比你快。到了顶上，他说：“尚可。”你相当确定他指的是爬楼。|七层楼里你们一句话也没说。除了腿，这出奇地让人放松。}\n\n@event lecture_tower_scorched_room\n# en: The Scorched Room\ntitle [b134c8] = 烧焦的练习室\n# en: {The practice room on the Evocation floor is empty. A sign forbids unsupervised casting.|The scorched room is empty. Since your last visit, someone has underlined the sign.|The scorched room. The sign is underlined three times now, and signed by the headmistress.} {~A fresh scorch mark is shaped like a surprised face.|Someone has left a bucket of sand by the door.|{season == \"winter\": The walls are the only warm thing here.|The air smells faintly of singed eyebrow.}}\ntext [efe1ca] = {塑能术那层的练习室空着。告示禁止无人监督施法。|烧焦的练习室空着。自你上次来过，有人在告示下划了线。|烧焦的练习室。告示上现在划了三道线，还有院长的签名。} {~一道新焦痕的形状像一张吃惊的脸。|有人在门边放了一桶沙子。|{season == \"winter\": 这里只有墙是暖的。|空气里隐约有烧焦眉毛的味道。}}\n# en: A practice room on a lower floor of a stone tower, walls streaked with scorch marks, a sign by the door forbidding unsupervised casting.\nscene [5b6deb] = 石塔低层的一间练习室，墙上满是焦痕，门边的告示禁止无人监督施法。\n# en: Practise anyway\nopt.left.label [5b8fa1] = 照练不误\n# en: {~Your spell lands clean in the centre of the oldest scorch mark. You leave no evidence.|You practise until the flame does what you mean, not what you say.}\nopt.left.say.1 [b1fe32] = {~你的咒语正中最古老那道焦痕的中心，没留下任何证据。|你一直练到火焰照你的意思行事，而不是照你的话。}\n# en: {~A new scorch mark joins the wall, shaped very like you.|The spell comes back at you. You leave with your eyebrows, mostly.}\nopt.left.say.2 [5f86e8] = {~墙上添了一道新焦痕，形状很像你。|咒语反弹到你身上。你离开时眉毛还在——大部分。}\n# en: Work through the theory\nopt.right.label [0d1f5b] = 钻研理论\n# en: {~You draw the spell on paper fifty times. Paper is very forgiving.|You learn exactly why the walls look like that, and resolve not to add to them.}\nopt.right.say.1 [d62ca2] = {~你在纸上把咒语画了五十遍。纸很宽容。|你彻底弄懂了墙为什么是这副模样，并决心不再给它添彩。}\n\n@event great_hall_tomorrow_sky\n# en: Tomorrow's Weather\ntitle [fdb53c] = 明天的天气\n# en: {The great hall is empty but for the candles. The ceiling shows tomorrow.|One candle drifts over to you and hovers at reading height.|Your candle meets you at the door now and leads you to your bench.} {season == \"winter\": Tomorrow, it says, it will snow.|{season == \"spring\": Tomorrow, a storm with bad manners.|{season == \"summer\": Tomorrow is cloudless, stars and all.|Tomorrow is fog, rolling across the vaults.}}} {final_year: There are fewer tomorrows here now.|}\ntext [e5b36c] = {大礼堂里空无一人，只有蜡烛。天花板在放映明天。|一支蜡烛飘到你身边，停在适合读书的高度。|你的蜡烛现在会在门口迎你，把你领到你的长凳。} {season == \"winter\": 天花板说，明天要下雪。|{season == \"spring\": 明天，一场不讲礼貌的暴风雨。|{season == \"summer\": 明天万里无云，满天星斗。|明天是雾，在拱顶间缓缓滚过。}}} {final_year: 这里剩下的明天不多了。|}\n# en: An empty great hall in the evening, floating candles drifting under a vaulted ceiling that shows a different sky from the windows.\nscene [85f031] = 傍晚空荡的大礼堂，漂浮的蜡烛在拱顶下飘移，天花板上的天空与窗外不同。\n# en: Lie on a bench and watch\nopt.left.label [e7d231] = 躺在长凳上看\n# en: {~You watch tomorrow until your neck stiffens. It is worth it.|A candle drifts down to see if you are all right, and stays.|You fall half asleep and dream tomorrow a little early.}\nopt.left.say.1 [d8d820] = {~你看着明天，直到脖子发僵。值得。|一支蜡烛飘下来看你是否安好，然后就留下了。|你半睡半醒，提前梦见了一点明天。}\n# en: Read by the floating candles\nopt.right.label [e66c83] = 借烛光读书\n# en: {~A candle drips wax onto the best paragraph. You read it anyway, through the wax.|The candles follow your page like curious moths. It is the best light in the castle.}\nopt.right.say.1 [841a19] = {~一支蜡烛把蜡滴在了最好的那段上。你隔着蜡照读不误。|蜡烛像好奇的飞蛾一样跟着你的书页。这是城堡里最好的光。}\n\n@event great_hall_choir_rehearsal\n# en: The Choir Rehearses\ntitle [2fc949] = 合唱团排练\n# en: {The choir is rehearsing on the stage, a foot off the floor, and one voice short.|Still a voice short. The conductor has left a spare songbook on your bench.|The spare songbook has your name pencilled in it, and the alto line marked in red.} {season == \"winter\": They are learning the Midwinter music.|{~Tonight it's a round that nobody can find the end of.|Tonight it's a lament, sung far too cheerfully.}}\ntext [b0d458] = {合唱团在舞台上排练，离地一尺，还差一个声部。|还是差一个声部。指挥在你的长凳上放了一本备用歌本。|那本备用歌本上用铅笔写了你的名字，女低音声部用红笔标了出来。} {season == \"winter\": 他们在学仲冬的曲子。|{~今晚是一首没人找得到结尾的轮唱。|今晚是一首挽歌，唱得未免太欢快了。}}\n# en: A choir rehearsing on the stage of a candlelit great hall, a few singers floating a foot off the boards, the benches mostly empty.\nscene [f55935] = 烛光摇曳的大礼堂舞台上，合唱团正在排练，几位歌手离地漂浮一尺，长凳上几乎空着。\n# en: Listen from the back\nopt.left.label [73c47c] = 在后排听\n# en: {~The collection hat comes round. It is worth every coin.|You listen until the candles gutter. The hat comes round twice.}\nopt.left.say.1 [5759b2] = {~募捐的帽子传了过来。每一枚硬币都值。|你一直听到蜡烛摇摇欲灭。帽子传了两回。}\n# en: Fill the missing voice\nopt.right.label [2ec625] = 补上那个声部\n# en: {~You hit every note but one. The conductor says it was the best one.|You float a little off the floor on the last chord. Nobody mentions it, which is how you know.}\nopt.right.say.1 [5817c2] = {~你只错了一个音。指挥说那是最好的一个。|最后一个和弦时你微微离了地。没人提起，你就知道了。}\n# en: {~You come in confidently, in the wrong key, in the wrong song. The choir is very kind about it, which is worse.|You sing the round in a straight line and finish alone. The conductor thanks you for your enthusiasm.}\nopt.right.say.2 [ccf4f2] = {~你信心满满地进了拍——调不对，歌也不对。合唱团对此非常宽容，这更糟。|你把轮唱唱成了一条直线，最后一个人唱完。指挥感谢你的热情。}\n\n@event great_hall_high_table_dare\n# en: The High Table Dare\ntitle [09ad02] = 高桌之赌\n# en: {Someone dares you to sit in the headmistress's chair at the empty high table.|The dare comes round again, and someone has started keeping a tally in chalk on the doorframe.|The tally on the doorframe has your column and everyone else's. First-years dare each other to dare you.} {~The chair is still warm.|A cushion embroidered with an owl sits on it.|Her teacup has been left behind, still steaming, which is worse.}\ntext [3e340f] = {有人赌你不敢坐到空着的高桌上，坐院长的椅子。|这个赌又来了，有人开始在门框上用粉笔记分。|门框上的记分表里有你的一栏和其他所有人的。一年级生们互相怂恿着来赌你。} {~椅子还是温的。|椅子上放着一个绣着猫头鹰的坐垫。|她的茶杯落在那儿，还冒着热气，这更糟。}\n# en: An empty high table on a dais at the end of a dark great hall, a carved chair at its centre, students daring each other from the doorway.\nscene [788e3e] = 昏暗大礼堂尽头的高台上，一张空着的高桌，中央是一把雕花椅子，学生们在门口互相起哄。\n# en: Take the dare\nopt.left.label [6cb2fd] = 接下赌约\n# en: {~You sit, cross your legs, and sip the air. The doorway erupts.|You sit for a full minute. The chair is surprisingly comfortable, and very slightly warm.}\nopt.left.say.1 [5ee522] = {~你坐下，跷起腿，对着空气啜了一口。门口炸开了锅。|你坐了整整一分钟。椅子出奇地舒服，还微微有点暖。}\n# en: {~As you sit, a quiet, delighted laugh comes from the dark beyond the dais. You leave very fast.|A portrait above the table clears its throat. You have never moved so fast in your life.}\nopt.left.say.2 [a971e9] = {~你刚坐下，高台后的黑暗里传来一声轻轻的、愉快的笑。你飞快地离开了。|高桌上方的一幅肖像清了清嗓子。你这辈子从没跑得这么快过。}\n# en: Tell them you value your life\nopt.right.label [bbde75] = 说你还想活命\n# en: {~They call you a coward, fondly. You keep your dignity, and your place at the academy.|\"Coward,\" they chant. You bow. It seems the safest thing to do in that hall.}\nopt.right.say.1 [b3254c] = {~他们亲切地骂你胆小鬼。你保住了尊严，也保住了学籍。|“胆小鬼！”他们齐声喊。你鞠了一躬。在那间礼堂里，这似乎是最安全的做法。}\n\n@event infirmary_spare_bed\n# en: The Spare Bed\ntitle [f2835c] = 空床位\n# en: {You only came in for a plaster.|Your name is on the slate by the end bed, in pencil.|Your name on the slate is in ink now.} {health < 40: Sister Amaranth takes one look at you and points at the spare bed.|Sister Amaranth decides, over her spectacles, that you'll live.} {~It smells of lavender and singed wool.|Two beds down, someone hiccups in a minor key.|{season == \"winter\": A warming charm hums under every blanket.|The curtains breathe.}}\ntext [4c9497] = {你只是来要块膏药的。|尽头那张床边的石板上，用铅笔写着你的名字。|石板上你的名字现在是用墨水写的了。} {health < 40: 阿玛兰斯修女看了你一眼，指了指那张空床。|阿玛兰斯修女从眼镜上方打量你，判定你死不了。} {~屋里有薰衣草和烧焦羊毛的味道。|隔两张床，有人在用小调打嗝。|{season == \"winter\": 每条毯子底下都嗡嗡响着取暖咒。|窗帘在呼吸。}}\n# en: A row of white infirmary beds under a high arched window, one turned down, a stern healer pointing at it.\nscene [6baaff] = 高拱窗下一排白色病床，其中一张已铺开，一位严厉的医护指着它。\n# en: Lie down for an hour\nopt.left.label [a6694f] = 躺一个钟头\n# en: {~You sleep the deep, dreamless sleep of the infirmary. The tonic afterwards costs a crown and tastes of regret.|You wake under a blanket you didn't pull up. There is a tonic on the side, and a bill under it.}\nopt.left.say.1 [20c199] = {~你睡了一场医务室式的、无梦的深眠。醒来后的补药要一个克朗，味道像悔恨。|你醒来时身上盖着一条不是你拉的毯子。床头有一瓶补药，下面压着账单。}\n# en: Help roll bandages\nopt.right.label [a9dc7c] = 帮忙卷绷带\n# en: {~Sister Amaranth teaches you the knot she uses. You will never tie your boots any other way.|You roll bandages and listen to her opinion of duelling, which is long and specific.}\nopt.right.say.1 [1b955e] = {~阿玛兰斯修女教你她打的那种结。从此你系鞋带再也不用别的结。|你一边卷绷带，一边听她对决斗的看法——又长又具体。}\n\n@event infirmary_visiting_hour\n# en: Visiting Hour\ntitle [d29dca] = 探视时间\n# en: {Visiting hour. {friend} is in the bed by the window,|Visiting hour. Sister Amaranth waves you to the stool you used last time. Today it's {friend},|Visiting hour. The stool by the window has worn to your shape. Today it's {friend},} {~nursing a duelling bruise the colour of plums.|recovering from a draught of hiccups that hiccups back.|wrapped in three blankets against a lake chill.} {They} brighten{s} when you come in.\ntext [cefb8d] = {探视时间。{friend}躺在靠窗的床上，|探视时间。阿玛兰斯修女朝你上回坐的凳子招招手。今天是{friend}，|探视时间。窗边那只凳子已经磨成了你的形状。今天是{friend}，} {~正护着一块李子色的决斗淤青。|正从一剂会反过来打嗝的打嗝药里恢复。|正裹着三条毯子抵御湖上的寒气。} 看见你进来，{they}眼睛一亮。\n# en: A friend propped up in an infirmary bed by a window, a visitor on a stool beside them with a paper bag of grapes.\nscene [3e7207] = 一位朋友靠坐在窗边的病床上，探视者坐在旁边的凳子上，手里拿着一纸袋葡萄。\n# en: Sit and keep {them} company\nopt.left.label [036ca8] = 坐下陪陪{them}\n# en: {~You bring grapes and eat most of them yourself. {friend} doesn't mind.|You stay until Sister Amaranth sweeps you out. {friend} waves from the pillows.}\nopt.left.say.1 [d696d5] = {~你带了葡萄，自己吃了大半。{friend}并不介意。|你一直待到阿玛兰斯修女把你扫地出门。{friend}在枕头间朝你挥手。}\n# en: Smuggle in a pastry\nopt.right.label [1dcbe0] = 偷带一块点心\n# en: {~The pastry makes it past the desk. {friend} eats it under the blanket like a crime.|You hide the pastry in a hollowed-out book. {friend} is delighted by both.}\nopt.right.say.1 [5fa4dc] = {~点心顺利混过了前台。{friend}躲在毯子底下吃，像在作案。|你把点心藏在一本挖空的书里。{friend}对两样都很开心。}\n# en: {~Sister Amaranth confiscates the pastry and eats it herself, slowly, while looking at you.|Sister Amaranth smells the pastry from across the ward and holds out her hand without a word.}\nopt.right.say.2 [47d6e3] = {~阿玛兰斯修女没收了点心，然后看着你，慢慢地自己吃掉了。|阿玛兰斯修女隔着整个病房就闻到了点心味，一言不发地伸出手。}\n\n@event infirmary_tonic_cupboard\n# en: The Tonic Cupboard\ntitle [f80f9b] = 补药柜\n# en: {The tonic cupboard is open, and Sister Amaranth is in the back having her tea.|The tonic cupboard is open at teatime. A new label on the door reads \"counted daily\".|At teatime the cupboard is open, and the label now reads \"counted daily, by name\". The bright blue bottles have moved to the front.} {mana < 20: Your head aches with the dry ache of spent magic.|{~The bottles clink in the draught.|A label reads, in her handwriting, \"absolutely not\".}}\ntext [55052c] = {补药柜开着，阿玛兰斯修女在里屋喝茶。|下午茶时间，补药柜开着。门上新贴了一张标签：“每日清点”。|下午茶时间，柜门开着，标签现在写着“每日清点，按人登记”。亮蓝色的瓶子都挪到了前排。} {mana < 20: 你的头隐隐作痛，是法力耗尽后那种干涩的疼。|{~瓶子在穿堂风里叮当作响。|一张标签上是她的字迹：“绝对不行”。}}\n# en: A tall glass-fronted cupboard of coloured tonics in an empty infirmary, its door ajar, a teacup steaming on the healer's desk.\nscene [e6e707] = 空荡荡的医务室里，一个装满彩色补药的高玻璃柜，柜门半开，医护的桌上一杯茶正冒着热气。\n# en: Help yourself to a tonic\nopt.left.label [3899be] = 自己拿一瓶\n# en: {~You drink it in the corridor. It tastes of peppermint and getting away with things.|You pocket the bottle, rinse it later, and put it back full of water. You feel slightly worse about that.}\nopt.left.say.1 [758b11] = {~你在走廊里喝了它。薄荷味，外加侥幸得手的味道。|你把瓶子揣进兜里，后来洗净，灌满清水放了回去。这让你心里更过意不去了一点。}\n# en: {~\"That one is for goats,\" says Sister Amaranth from the doorway. She makes you drink the right one, which is worse.|Sister Amaranth's teacup appears at your elbow. \"Put it back,\" she says, \"and sit down.\"}\nopt.left.say.2 [2814ae] = {~“那瓶是给山羊的。”阿玛兰斯修女在门口说。她让你喝下对的那瓶，这更糟。|阿玛兰斯修女的茶杯出现在你手肘边。“放回去，”她说，“然后坐下。”}\n# en: Ask for one properly\nopt.right.label [8a2146] = 好好开口要\n# en: {She charges you, lectures you, and gives you the good one.|She charges you, lectures you a little less, and gives you the good one.|\"You again,\" she says, and hands it over before you ask.}\nopt.right.say.1 [381f3d] = {她收了钱，训了你一顿，给了你好的那瓶。|她收了钱，训得少了些，给了你好的那瓶。|“又是你。”她说，没等你开口就递了过来。}\n\n@event alchemy_lab_spare_cauldron\n# en: A Spare Cauldron\ntitle [9b8e1b] = 一口空坩埚\n# en: {There is a spare cauldron on the second bench, unclaimed.|The spare cauldron has a chalk mark on its rim like your initial.|Your cauldron, everyone calls it. Someone has scratched your initial into the handle.} {~Someone has left a ladle standing upright in something.|The bench smells of mint and burnt sugar.|{season == \"summer\": The heat makes every brew twice as eager.|The windows have steamed over with other people's experiments.}}\ntext [bbcf91] = {第二张工作台上有一口空坩埚，没人认领。|那口空坩埚的边沿上有个粉笔记号，像你名字的首字母。|大家都管它叫“你的坩埚”。有人把你的首字母正经刻在了把手上。} {~有人把一把长勺竖着插在某种东西里。|工作台上有薄荷和焦糖的味道。|{season == \"summer\": 炎热让每锅药剂都格外急躁。|窗户被别人的实验蒸得一片模糊。}}\n# en: A long laboratory bench in the afternoon light, a small cauldron free among larger ones, jars of ingredients lined up beside it.\nscene [eac0fa] = 午后阳光里一张长长的实验台，大坩埚之间空着一口小坩埚，旁边排着一列药材罐。\n# en: Brew a practice batch\nopt.left.label [7eec57] = 熬一锅练手\n# en: {~The brew turns the right colour at the right moment. It feels like being trusted.|It comes out clear and smells of nothing, which is exactly right.}\nopt.left.say.1 [2a573b] = {~药剂在恰好的时刻变成了恰好的颜色。那感觉像是被信任。|成品清澈、无味，正是该有的样子。}\n# en: {~The brew turns a colour you have never seen. So do your eyebrows.|It foams over and sets, firmly, around your sleeve.}\nopt.left.say.2 [c4de09] = {~药剂变成了一种你从没见过的颜色，你的眉毛也是。|它沸溢出来，牢牢地凝固在你的袖子上。}\n# en: Scrub the cauldrons for crowns\nopt.right.label [cb89c5] = 刷坩埚挣几个钱\n# en: {~You learn more about failed potions from their residue than from any lecture.|You scrub until the bench is clean. Then you scratch your own initials into it, small, under the rim.}\nopt.right.say.1 [dcd00e] = {~从失败药剂的残渣里，你学到的比任何一堂课都多。|你把工作台刷得干干净净，然后在边沿下面小小地刻上了自己的名字缩写。}\n\n@event alchemy_lab_third_floor\n# en: The Third-Floor Fumes\ntitle [fa28aa] = 三楼的烟雾\n# en: {On the third floor the ventilation charm has given up, and the fumes have taken charge.|Someone has hung a sign by the stairs giving the fumes' mood of the day.|The fumes' sign has a column for forecasts now, in several hands.} {~Everyone is speaking in rhyme and trying to hide it.|Everyone is laughing at nothing and cannot stop.|Everyone is being unusually honest about everyone else's hair.} {season == \"winter\": The windows are frozen shut.|}\ntext [e293ea] = {三楼的通风咒已经放弃了，烟雾接管了这里。|有人在楼梯口挂了块牌子，写着烟雾今天的心情。|烟雾的牌子现在多了一栏预报，好几种笔迹。} {~所有人都在用押韵说话，还想掩饰。|所有人都在莫名其妙地笑，停不下来。|所有人都对别人的发型异常坦诚。} {season == \"winter\": 窗户冻死了。|}\n# en: A crowded upper floor of an alchemy laboratory, coloured vapour hanging under the ceiling, students working with handkerchiefs tied over their faces.\nscene [51e9da] = 炼金实验室拥挤的楼上，彩色的蒸气悬在天花板下，学生们脸上蒙着手帕干活。\n# en: Stay and work in the fug\nopt.left.label [d14428] = 留在烟里干活\n# en: {~You get a great deal done and agree with everyone about everything.|You leave with good notes and a headache that hums.}\nopt.left.say.1 [5db6b3] = {~你干了一大堆活，而且对所有人的所有话都表示赞同。|你带着一份好笔记和一阵嗡嗡响的头疼离开。}\n# en: Force the windows open\nopt.right.label [0d2916] = 把窗户撬开\n# en: {season == \"winter\": {~The ice cracks. Half the floor cheers; half complains of the cold.|The frozen latch gives, and so does your thumbnail.}|{~The windows give. The fumes leave, sulking, and the floor applauds.|You wedge the window with a textbook. The fumes drift out, and nearly the textbook too.}}\nopt.right.say.1 [d12ef6] = {season == \"winter\": {~冰裂开了。半层楼欢呼，另一半抱怨冷。|冻住的插销开了，你的指甲也裂了。}|{~窗户开了。烟雾悻悻离去，整层楼鼓掌。|你用一本课本卡住窗户。烟雾飘了出去，课本也差点跟着出去。}}\n\n@event alchemy_lab_unlabelled_jar\n# en: The Unlabelled Jar\ntitle [a3c74d] = 没贴标签的罐子\n# en: {Someone has left an unlabelled jar on the shared shelf. It glows, faintly.|Another unlabelled jar on the shared shelf, beside the cautious label someone wrote for the last one.|The shared shelf now has a row of careful labels, and one new jar at the end of it, waiting its turn.} {~It smells of thunder.|Something inside it turns over when you look.|It is warm to the touch.} {gpa >= 3: You could probably identify it, given time.|You have no idea what it is.}\ntext [c01c59] = {有人在公用架上落下一只没贴标签的罐子。它微微发光。|公用架上又一只没贴标签的罐子，旁边是有人给上一只写的谨慎标签。|公用架上现在排着一列仔细写好的标签，末尾一只新罐子在排队等着。} {~它闻起来像雷声。|你一看它，里面就有东西翻了个身。|摸上去是温的。} {gpa >= 3: 给你点时间，你大概能认出它。|你完全不知道它是什么。}\n# en: A dusty shelf of ingredient jars in an alchemy laboratory, one jar with no label glowing faintly, a student reaching for it.\nscene [328708] = 炼金实验室里一排积灰的药材罐，其中一只没有标签、微微发光，一个学生正伸手去拿。\n# en: Add a pinch to your brew\nopt.left.label [163e45] = 往药里加一撮\n# en: {~The brew turns gold and so do you, briefly. You feel enormous.|The brew sings a single note and leaves you humming with power.}\nopt.left.say.1 [6ecb37] = {~药剂变成金色，你也短暂地变成了金色。你觉得自己无比庞大。|药剂唱出一个音，让你浑身嗡嗡作响，充满力量。}\n# en: {~The brew ignites, politely, and then impolitely.|Your cauldron sneezes. So, for the rest of the afternoon, do you.}\nopt.left.say.2 [2a14a9] = {~药剂先是礼貌地、随后很不礼貌地烧了起来。|你的坩埚打了个喷嚏。整个下午，你也跟着打喷嚏。}\n# en: Label it, carefully\nopt.right.label [0222a1] = 小心地贴上标签\n# en: {~An hour of tests later, you write a cautious label. Someone adds a skull to it.|You work out what it is. Then you wash your hands three times.}\nopt.right.say.1 [f78b5f] = {~一小时的测试之后，你写下一张谨慎的标签。有人在上面加了个骷髅。|你弄清了它是什么。然后把手洗了三遍。}\n\n@event job_board_odd_jobs\n# en: Odd Jobs\ntitle [99e6aa] = 零活\n# en: {Under the proper postings, someone has pinned an odd job.|Under the proper postings is an odd job, and a second pin someone has left beside it, for answers.|The odd-jobs corner has a card addressed to you by name, in a hand you're starting to recognise.} {~Someone wants a homesick portrait read to.|Someone wants a ladder held, no questions asked.|Someone wants a reluctant gargoyle walked.} {term: |In the break the board is half bare, and the odd jobs pay a little better.}\ntext [876911] = {正式招工启事底下，有人钉了一张零活。|正式启事底下有一张零活，旁边还多钉了一枚图钉，留着给人回复。|零活角有一张写着你名字的卡片，笔迹你开始认得了。} {~有人想找人给一幅想家的肖像画念书。|有人想找人扶梯子，不许问问题。|有人想找人遛一只不情愿的石像鬼。} {term: |假期里告示板空了一半，零活的工钱也高了一点。}\n# en: A cork board in an entrance hall, a small notice pinned at an angle under the official job postings, a student reading it with a frown.\nscene [b55309] = 门厅里的软木告示板，一张小启事歪钉在正式招工启事下面，一个学生皱着眉读它。\n# en: Take the job\nopt.left.label [a7359a] = 接下这活\n# en: {~It takes all afternoon and pays exactly what it said, which is rarer than it should be.|It is stranger than the notice suggested, and pays slightly less.}\nopt.left.say.1 [6233c9] = {~花了一整个下午，报酬和说好的分毫不差——这比应有的要少见。|比启事上写的更古怪，工钱也少了一点。}\n# en: Haggle first\nopt.right.label [789545] = 先讲讲价\n# en: {~You haggle the fee up by half. Your employer seems to respect you more for it.|You agree a price and a biscuit. The biscuit turns out to be the better half of the deal.}\nopt.right.say.1 [362f3c] = {~你把价钱抬高了一半。雇主似乎因此更尊重你了。|你们谈定了价钱外加一块饼干。结果饼干才是这笔买卖里更划算的那一半。}\n# en: {~Your employer takes the notice down and pins up a new one, asking for \"someone less difficult\".|The job goes to a first-year who asked for nothing. They seem very happy with it.}\nopt.right.say.2 [d201b3] = {~雇主把启事撕下来，换上一张新的，要找个“好说话一点的人”。|活儿给了一个什么都没要求的一年级生。那孩子看起来非常高兴。}\n\n@event job_board_lost_familiars\n# en: Lost and Found\ntitle [7ffffa] = 失物招领\n# en: {The corner of the board for lost things is busier than the jobs.|An old poster in the lost-things corner now reads \"found, thanks to a student\".|The lost-things corner has thank-you notes now. One draws the rescuer with a halo.} {~A toad called \"Sir\" is missing, last seen being brave.|A ransom note demands three biscuits for one sock.|A small owl is lost, answers to nothing, and bites.} {season == \"winter\": Half the familiars have simply gone somewhere warm.|}\ntext [8617a3] = {告示板上的失物角比招工那边还热闹。|失物角里一张旧启事上现在写着“已找到，多亏一位学生”。|失物角如今贴着感谢信。有一张把救星画上了光环。} {~一只叫“爵士”的蟾蜍走失了，最后一次被人看见时正在逞英雄。|一封勒索信要求三块饼干，换回一只袜子。|一只小猫头鹰走丢了，叫什么都不应，还咬人。} {season == \"winter\": 一半的魔宠只是自己跑去暖和地方了。|}\n# en: The corner of a cork board crowded with hand-drawn posters of lost familiars and a ransom note for a sock.\nscene [8f714b] = 软木告示板的一角，贴满了手绘的走失魔宠启事，还有一封索要一只袜子赎金的勒索信。\n# en: Go looking for it\nopt.left.label [24390e] = 去找找看\n# en: {~You find it behind a radiator in the east tower, deeply unrepentant. The reward is small and grateful.|You find it, and the owner cries, and the reward is mostly biscuits.}\nopt.left.say.1 [db63a7] = {~你在东塔的暖气片后面找到了它，毫无悔意。酬金不多，谢意满满。|你找到了它，失主哭了，酬金大部分是饼干。}\n# en: {~You search for an hour and find only a different, much angrier creature.|You don't find it. The next day the poster says \"Found! Thanks anyway.\"}\nopt.left.say.2 [473240] = {~你找了一小时，只找到另一只脾气暴躁得多的生物。|你没找到。第二天，启事上写着：“找到了！还是谢谢。”}\n# en: Read every notice twice\nopt.right.label [778bc2] = 每张启事读两遍\n# en: {~The board is the castle's diary. You learn who lost what, and who owes whom.|You learn three secrets from the small print, and who has terrible handwriting.}\nopt.right.say.1 [cd911d] = {~告示板是城堡的日记。你知道了谁丢了什么，谁欠谁的。|你从小字里读出了三个秘密，还知道了谁的字写得最难看。}\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/pools/castle_towers.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/pools/castle_towers.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event observatory_night_log\n# en: The Night Log\ntitle [9e1784] = 观星日志\n# en: {The observatory keeps a night log, and anyone may write in it.|Since your last entry, someone has drawn a moustache on the moon.|You know the log's hands by now: the careful one, the frantic one, the one who only ever writes \"cloudy\".} {season == \"winter\": Frost feathers the inside of the dome.|{~The telescope turns, unasked, towards the north.|A draught turns the pages for you.|The lamp is low on oil, as always.}}\ntext [5cd3f8] = {观星台有一本观星日志，谁都可以往里写。|自你上次写过之后，有人给月亮画了两撇胡子。|日志里的笔迹你都认得了：工整的那位，潦草的那位，还有永远只写“多云”的那位。} {season == \"winter\": 霜花在穹顶内侧结成羽毛。|{~望远镜没人碰，自己转向了北方。|一阵穿堂风替你翻开了书页。|灯油照例快烧干了。}}\n# en: A brass observatory dome at night, one student writing in a huge logbook by lamplight while the great telescope swings round on its own.\nscene [59d5ae] = 夜晚的黄铜穹顶下，一名学生借灯光在一本巨大的日志上书写，大望远镜自顾自地转动。\n# en: Log what the telescope finds\nopt.left.label [8d5562] = 记下望远镜所见\n# en: {~You chart a star that wasn't there last month. The log already has it, in a hand forty years older.|Three hours, two pages and one sneeze that smudges a constellation.|The telescope settles on a quiet blue star and waits until you've written it down properly.}\nopt.left.say.1 [08228c] = {~你标出一颗上个月还不存在的星。日志里早有记载，笔迹比你老四十年。|三个小时，两页纸，外加一个喷嚏糊掉了一整个星座。|望远镜停在一颗安静的蓝星上，一直等到你把它好好写下来。}\n# en: Read the old entries instead\nopt.right.label [c05d01] = 改读旧日的记录\n# en: {~A winter entry from long ago says \"Saw nothing. Stayed anyway. Worth it.\"|A love letter written across a page of moon sightings. Very bad. Very sweet.|{final_year: You find your own first entry and barely know the hand.|A student from your year logged your star a week before you did, with a small drawn smile.}}\nopt.right.say.1 [e270bd] = {~很久以前的一个冬夜，有人写道：“什么也没看到。还是留下了。值得。”|一封情书，横跨一整页的月相记录。写得很烂。也很甜。|{final_year: 你找到了自己的第一条记录，几乎认不出那笔迹。|你同届的一个学生比你早一周记下了你那颗星，旁边还画了个小笑脸。}}\n\n@event observatory_one_star_each\n# en: One Star Each\ntitle [0eea9a] = 每人一颗星\n# en: {The rule chalked on the observatory door says one star each, then pass it on. {here} has been at the eyepiece for some time.|Someone has added \"two if they're small\" under the chalk rule. {here} is at the eyepiece.|Someone is always at the telescope when you arrive; tonight it's {here}.} {season == \"summer\": The nights are short and the queue is impatient.|{~The dome is cold enough to see your breath.|The telescope creaks round to follow something.|Below, the bridges of light are going out one by one.}}\ntext [882180] = {观星台门上用粉笔写着规矩：每人一颗星，看完就让。{here}已经在目镜前待了好一阵。|有人在粉笔规矩下面添了一句“小的可以看两颗”。{here}正守着目镜。|你每次来，望远镜前总有人；今晚是{here}。} {season == \"summer\": 夏夜短，排队的人都很急。|{~穹顶里冷得能看见呼出的白气。|望远镜吱呀一声，转去追什么东西。|下方，光之桥一座接一座熄灭。}}\n# en: Two students at the eyepiece of a great brass telescope, one stepping aside to let the other look.\nscene [7fe37e] = 两名学生在一架巨大的黄铜望远镜目镜旁，一人侧身让另一人观看。\n# en: Share the eyepiece\nopt.left.label [b40888] = 一起用目镜\n# en: {cast_as(here, seraphine): {~\"That one,\" Seraphine says, \"is going to matter in, oh.\" She doesn't finish.|\"Left of it,\" Seraphine says. \"Next spring's left.\" You look anyway.}|{cast_as(here, kael): {~Kael steps aside and points. When your hands get cold, he stands closer.|Kael says the star's name in a Northern word, once, and doesn't explain.}|You take turns until neither of you remembers whose star it was.}}\nopt.left.say.1 [f85bdc] = {cast_as(here, seraphine): {~“那一颗，”瑟拉芬说，“将来会很要紧，大概在——噢。”她没说完。|“它左边，”瑟拉芬说，“明年春天的左边。”你还是看了。}|{cast_as(here, kael): {~凯尔让开，伸手指给你看。你的手冻僵时，他站得近了些。|凯尔用一个北境的词念出那颗星的名字，只念一次，不作解释。}|你们轮流看，看到谁也记不清那颗星是谁先找到的。}}\n# en: Wait your turn properly\nopt.right.label [da2ba7] = 规规矩矩排队\n# en: {cast_as(here, seraphine): {~Seraphine leaves mid-sentence. \"You'll want it,\" she says. \"Later. Now, I mean.\"|\"Yours is the next one,\" Seraphine says, and it is.}|{cast_as(here, kael): {~Kael yields with a formal nod and goes out onto the roof. Frost follows.|Kael waits behind you. When you turn round, he's gone.}|By your turn the star has set. The next one is nearly as good.}}\nopt.right.say.1 [2b6dbe] = {cast_as(here, seraphine): {~瑟拉芬话说到一半就走了。“你会用得上的，”她说，“以后。我是说现在。”|“下一颗是你的。”瑟拉芬说。果然如此。}|{cast_as(here, kael): {~凯尔郑重地点头让开，走到屋顶上去。霜跟着他。|凯尔在你身后等着。你回头时，他已经不在了。}|轮到你时，那颗星已经落下了。下一颗也差不多好。}}\n\n@event observatory_roof_ledge\n# en: The Roof Ledge\ntitle [9c29ff] = 屋顶的窄檐\n# en: {A window in the dome opens onto the roof, and onto a ledge that everyone swears they have never sat on.|Someone else who never sits there has oiled the latch.|The ledge. You know where the loose tile is now.} {season == \"winter\": The tiles are white with frost.|{~The lake below is a second sky.|A warm wind comes up from the village.|Someone's scarf is snagged on the gutter.}}\ntext [da0046] = {穹顶上有扇窗通向屋顶，窗外有一道窄檐——人人都发誓自己从没坐过。|另一位“从没坐过”的人给窗闩上了油。|那道窄檐。你已经知道哪块瓦是松的了。} {season == \"winter\": 瓦片上白霜一片。|{~下方的湖是第二片天空。|一阵暖风从山下小镇吹上来。|不知谁的围巾挂在了檐沟上。}}\n# en: A student sitting on a narrow ledge outside a brass dome, legs dangling over the castle roofs, the lake far below.\nscene [00d358] = 一名学生坐在黄铜穹顶外的窄檐上，双腿悬在城堡屋顶之上，湖在远远的下方。\n# en: Climb out onto the ledge\nopt.left.label [3e2750] = 爬到窄檐上去\n# en: {~You sit with the whole sky to yourself until the stars begin to wheel.|The castle looks small from here, and kind, and very old.}\nopt.left.say.1 [b70ff1] = {~整片天空归你一个人，你一直坐到群星开始旋转。|从这里看，城堡显得很小，很温柔，也很老。}\n# en: {~The loose tile is loose in a new place. You slide, grab, and hang on, and climb back in with skinned palms.|A gust takes your balance and most of your dignity. The gutter keeps the rest.}\nopt.left.say.2 [4cff5a] = {~那块松瓦这回在别处松了。你一滑，一抓，挂住了，爬回来时手掌都磨破了皮。|一阵风夺走了你的平衡和大半体面。剩下的归檐沟。}\n# en: Watch through the glass\nopt.right.label [8e53e6] = 隔着玻璃看\n# en: {~You see half the sky and all of the smudges.|Through the glass the stars look like they're behind a shop window. You can look; you can't have.}\nopt.right.say.1 [a5f56a] = {~你看到了半边天空，和玻璃上所有的污渍。|隔着玻璃，星星像摆在橱窗里。能看，不能拿。}\n\n@event observatory_scraped_word\n# en: The Scraped-Out Word\ntitle [5f3087] = 被刮掉的词\n# en: {In the night log's oldest volume, every entry by a student who had finished Advanced Astromancy uses the same word, and every time it has been scraped out.|You've worked out that the missing word was something you did to a star, not something you saw.|You know these pages now. The word is gone; the shape of the work around it is not.} {~In one margin, \"Ask. Be told.\"|A pressed flower falls out.|The lamp gutters as you turn the page.}\ntext [fd4713] = {日志最老的那一卷里，凡是修完高等星象学的学生写下的记录，都用到同一个词，而每一处都被刮掉了。|你已经弄明白，那个缺失的词是你对星星做的事，而不是你看见的东西。|这些书页你已经很熟了。那个词不在了，可它周围那份功课的轮廓还在。} {~页边写着：“去问。会有人告诉你。”|一朵压干的花掉了出来。|你翻页时，灯焰摇曳了一下。}\n# en: An old observatory logbook open by lamplight, the same word scraped out of line after line.\nscene [cccc67] = 灯下摊开一本古老的观星日志，同一个词被一行又一行地刮掉。\n# en: Trace the gaps by lamplight\nopt.left.label [5f1275] = 借灯光描摹空缺\n# en: {~The words around the gaps say \"called\", \"named\", \"down\". Whoever taught it taught it aloud, to people who had finished Advanced Astromancy.|\"...and the light came down when I said its name...\" Then the gap. You will need someone to tell you the word.}\nopt.left.say.1 [61a642] = {~空缺周围的词是“唤”“名”“降下”。不管是谁教的，都是口头教的，教给已经修完高等星象学的人。|“……当我念出它的名字，光便降了下来……”然后是空缺。你得有人把这个词告诉你。}\n# en: Ask the telescope, half joking\nopt.right.label [de5250] = 半开玩笑地问望远镜\n# en: {~The telescope swings to one bright star and stays there, patient as a teacher who knows you aren't ready.|It turns to a named star and holds it. You get the feeling you have to finish Advanced Astromancy before it explains.}\nopt.right.say.1 [2cfe48] = {~望远镜转向一颗明亮的星，停住不动，耐心得像个知道你还没准备好的老师。|它转向一颗有名字的星，稳稳对准。你觉得，得先修完高等星象学，它才肯解释。}\n\n@event workshop_spare_bench\n# en: The Spare Bench\ntitle [b782e9] = 备用工作台\n# en: {Mira keeps a spare bench for visitors, under a sign that says TOUCH THINGS (CAREFULLY).|The sign over the spare bench has grown a second line, (MORE CAREFULLY).|Your bench, as Mira has started calling it, has a jar of springs waiting.} {~Mira waves a spanner at you without looking up.|Something on a high shelf is ticking.|{season == \"winter\": The forge-lamp makes it the warmest room in the west wing.|The window is open to let the smoke out.}}\ntext [245353] = {米拉给访客留了一张备用工作台，上方挂着牌子：随便摸（小心点）。|备用工作台上方的牌子多了一行：（再小心点）。|“你的工作台”——米拉开始这么叫它了——上面放着一罐弹簧等你。} {~米拉头也不抬，冲你挥了挥扳手。|高处的架子上有什么在滴答作响。|{season == \"winter\": 锻炉灯让这里成了西翼最暖和的房间。|窗开着，好放烟出去。}}\n# en: A cluttered workshop bench under a hand-painted sign, a music box in pieces beside a jar of springs, fire buckets by the door.\nscene [d9570d] = 杂乱的工坊工作台上方挂着手绘的牌子，一只拆散的音乐盒旁放着一罐弹簧，门边摆着灭火水桶。\n# en: Mend a jammed music box\nopt.left.label [84f39e] = 修一只卡住的音乐盒\n# en: {~It plays again, a little too fast, like it's late for something.|You fix the tune and break the lid. Mira says that's probably progress.|It plays a waltz nobody has heard in years. You leave it on the sill to finish.}\nopt.left.say.1 [18ddc0] = {~它又响了，稍微快了点，像是赶着去哪儿。|曲子修好了，盒盖弄坏了。米拉说这大概也算进步。|它奏起一支多年没人听过的华尔兹。你把它放在窗台上，让它自己奏完。}\n# en: Just watch Mira work\nopt.right.label [c1f7cc] = 就看米拉干活\n# en: {~Mira explains a gear train for an hour. You follow about a third, which they say is probably a record.|You sit by the fire buckets and watch the sparks. Your eyes sting. It's a good afternoon.|Mira hands you things to hold and forgets you're holding them. It's oddly restful.}\nopt.right.say.1 [898b12] = {~米拉讲了一个钟头的齿轮传动。你听懂了三分之一，TA说这大概是纪录。|你坐在灭火水桶旁看火花。眼睛被熏得发酸。是个好下午。|米拉把东西递给你拿着，然后忘了你还拿着。出奇地让人放松。}\n\n@event workshop_volunteer\n# en: A Volunteer With Hands\ntitle [1b4b0c] = 有手的志愿者\n# en: {\"Perfect timing! I need a volunteer and you have hands!\" {maker} holds up {~a pair of boots that walk on their own|a hat that remembers where you left things|goggles that see round corners}.|{maker} has a new invention and a familiar look. Today: {~boots that walk on their own|a hat with a memory|goggles for corners}.|You don't ask anymore. You just look at what {maker} is holding, then at the fire bucket.}\ntext [33d2e9] = {“来得正好！我需要一个志愿者，而你有手！”{maker}举起{~一双会自己走路的靴子|一顶记得你把东西放在哪儿的帽子|一副能看见拐角后面的护目镜}。|{maker}有了新发明，还有那熟悉的眼神。今天是：{~自己走路的靴子|有记性的帽子|看拐角的护目镜}。|你已经不问了。你只是看看{maker}手里的东西，再看看灭火水桶。}\n# en: An artificer in soot-streaked goggles holding out a strange brass contraption, a fire bucket waiting hopefully on the floor.\nscene [204031] = 一位戴着沾满煤灰护目镜的奇械师举着一件古怪的黄铜装置，地上一只灭火水桶满怀期待地候着。\n# en: Try it on\nopt.left.label [fdeee0] = 试戴一下\n# en: {~It works! For eleven glorious minutes, it works, and {maker} writes down every one of them.|It does exactly what it should. {maker} looks almost suspicious and walks round it three times.}\nopt.left.say.1 [d3d661] = {~成功了！整整十一分钟的辉煌，它一直在运转，{maker}把每一分钟都记了下来。|它完全按设计运转。{maker}几乎起了疑心，围着它转了三圈。}\n# en: {~It works in the wrong direction. You spend a while upside down. {maker} apologises at length, with diagrams.|There's a bang, a smell of singed eyebrow, and {maker} saying \"Interesting!\" in a voice that means sorry.}\nopt.left.say.2 [bc1978] = {~它朝反方向运转了。你倒立了好一阵。{maker}长篇大论地道歉，还附了图解。|砰的一声，一股烧焦眉毛的味道，{maker}说着“有意思！”——那语气其实是“对不起”。}\n# en: Hold the fire bucket\nopt.right.label [f76215] = 帮忙拎水桶\n# en: {~You hold the bucket. You don't need the bucket. {maker} looks faintly disappointed.|You need the bucket. Briefly, splendidly, you need the bucket.}\nopt.right.say.1 [131706] = {~你拎着水桶。水桶没派上用场。{maker}看起来有点失望。|水桶派上用场了。短暂而壮丽地派上了用场。}\n\n@event workshop_stirring_cauldron\n# en: The Self-Stirring Cauldron\ntitle [0d87b7] = 自动搅拌坩埚\n# en: {The self-stirring cauldron in the corner wears a note. DO NOT ADJUST. IT KNOWS WHAT IT DID.|Someone has underlined the cauldron's note twice and added the word STILL.|The cauldron's spoon pauses when you come in, as if it remembers you.} {~It's going anticlockwise, which it isn't supposed to.|It smells of toffee and lightning.|{season == \"spring\": Someone has put flowers in it. It is stirring them.|It's stirring soup for nobody.}}\ntext [32ccf8] = {角落里那口自动搅拌的坩埚上贴着纸条。禁止调整。它知道自己干了什么。|有人在坩埚的纸条下划了两道线，又添了两个字：仍然。|你一进门，坩埚的勺子就停了一下，好像认得你。} {~它在逆时针搅，按理不该这样。|它闻起来像太妃糖和闪电。|{season == \"spring\": 有人往里面放了花。它正在搅花。|它在给谁也不是的人搅汤。}}\n# en: A small cauldron in a workshop corner stirring itself with a wooden spoon, a stern paper note pinned to its rim.\nscene [2055ca] = 工坊角落里一口小坩埚正用木勺自己搅拌，锅沿上别着一张严厉的纸条。\n# en: Adjust the enchantment\nopt.left.label [826eac] = 调整附魔\n# en: {~It settles into a slow, contented clockwise. Mira, passing, says \"Oh!\" and then \"Huh,\" and then \"Teach me.\"|The spoon taps the rim twice, like a thank-you, and gets on with it.}\nopt.left.say.1 [2ede91] = {~它慢下来，心满意足地顺时针转。米拉路过，先说“噢！”，再说“嗯？”，然后说“教我。”|勺子在锅沿上敲了两下，像在道谢，然后继续干活。}\n# en: {~It stirs your sleeve, then you, then your sleeve again.|It speeds up instead. The ceiling now has toffee on it, and so do you.}\nopt.left.say.2 [cb2784] = {~它搅了你的袖子，然后搅你，然后又搅袖子。|它反而转得更快了。天花板上现在有太妃糖，你身上也有。}\n# en: Take notes from a safe distance\nopt.right.label [cca44e] = 远远地做笔记\n# en: {~You write down everything it's doing wrong. The list is long and slightly envious.|You sketch the rune on its base. The cauldron splashes your page, on purpose you think.}\nopt.right.say.1 [bd5552] = {~你把它做错的地方全记了下来。清单很长，还带着点羡慕。|你描下它底座上的符文。坩埚溅湿了你的纸——你觉得是故意的。}\n\n@event workshop_glass_cabinet\n# en: The Glass Cabinet\ntitle [331550] = 玻璃柜\n# en: {At the back of the workshop is a glass cabinet that isn't Mira's, full of faculty machines, very still.|A clockwork mouse in the glass cabinet has moved since your last visit, and now faces the door.|The cabinet. Mira says the machines in it think, probably, and are waiting for the right student.} A card in old ink: \"Artifice. By invitation, to those who have passed Alchemy II and Evocation II.\"\ntext [dbe876] = {工坊最里面有一只不属于米拉的玻璃柜，装满了教职员做的机器，一动不动。|玻璃柜里那只发条老鼠比你上次来时挪了位置，现在正对着门。|那只柜子。米拉说里面的机器会思考，大概吧，它们在等合适的学生。} 一张旧墨水写的卡片：“奇械术。仅限受邀，须已通过炼金学 II 与塑能术 II。”\n# en: A tall glass cabinet at the back of a workshop, clockwork animals inside it, a faded card propped against the glass.\nscene [beb857] = 工坊后方一只高高的玻璃柜，里面摆着发条动物，一张褪色的卡片斜靠在玻璃上。\n# en: Study the machines through the glass\nopt.left.label [4744ef] = 隔着玻璃研究机器\n# en: {~Nothing moves while you watch. The moment you look away, something ticks.|You count the gears in a brass beetle and lose count. Somebody made this who could make things and make them burn.}\nopt.left.say.1 [3d2223] = {~你盯着看时，什么都不动。你一移开目光，就有东西滴答一声。|你数一只黄铜甲虫身上的齿轮，数乱了。造它的人，既会造东西，也会让东西烧起来。}\n# en: Try the cabinet lock\nopt.right.label [c81d94] = 试试柜子的锁\n# en: {~The lock gives. The mouse winds itself up, bows, and goes back to sleep. You close the door very quietly.|The door swings open an inch, and every machine inside turns its head to look at you. You close it again, politely.}\nopt.right.say.1 [04d562] = {~锁开了。老鼠给自己上紧发条，鞠了一躬，又睡了。你轻轻地关上柜门。|柜门开了一寸，里面每一台机器都转过头来看你。你又礼貌地把门关上了。}\n# en: {~The cabinet shrieks like a kettle. Mira is delighted. The corridor is not.|The lock bites your pick and keeps it. Mira says it does that, probably, to people it likes.}\nopt.right.say.2 [03ed70] = {~柜子像水壶一样尖叫起来。米拉乐坏了。走廊里的人可没有。|锁咬住了你的撬片，不还了。米拉说，它大概对喜欢的人都这样。}\n\n@event ledger_office_unprintable\n# en: Letters We Will Not Print\ntitle [ae0100] = 不予刊登的来信\n# en: {By the Ledger's door sits a basket marked LETTERS WE WILL NOT PRINT. Visitors may read them.|The unprintable basket has overflowed into a second basket, which is also full.|You are a connoisseur of the unprintable basket now.} {~On top is a complaint about the soup, in verse.|One is just a drawing of a prefect, unflattering and accurate.|{season == \"winter\": Someone demands that snow be cancelled.|Someone asks who keeps moving the stairs.}}\ntext [dba839] = {《流明纪事报》编辑部门口放着一只篮子，标着“不予刊登的来信”。访客可以随便读。|不予刊登的来信溢到了第二只篮子里，第二只也满了。|你现在是这只篮子的资深鉴赏家了。} {~最上面是一封用韵文写的、投诉膳堂汤的信。|有一封只画了一位级长，不好看，但很像。|{season == \"winter\": 有人要求取消下雪。|有人问到底是谁老在挪楼梯。}}\n# en: A wicker basket overflowing with letters by the door of a cramped newspaper office, front pages papering the walls.\nscene [276fb2] = 狭小的报社编辑部门口，一只柳条篮里塞满了信，墙上贴满了旧头版。\n# en: Read the whole basket\nopt.left.label [7b9421] = 把整篮都读完\n# en: {~The best one is an apology to a goat.|One is from a first-year who misses home. You put it back gently.|{final_year: A letter in your own first-year hand. You don't remember sending it. You were right.|Three letters about the same broken window, all blaming different people.}}\nopt.left.say.1 [3542b8] = {~最好的一封，是写给一只山羊的道歉信。|有一封来自一个想家的一年级生。你轻轻把它放了回去。|{final_year: 一封你自己一年级时的笔迹。你不记得寄过它。你当时说得没错。|三封信写的是同一扇碎窗户，各怪各的人。}}\n# en: Write one of your own\nopt.right.label [fec258] = 自己也写一封\n# en: {~They print it. Two people quote it at you at breakfast; one of them disagrees with it loudly.|It runs on the back page, under your initials. You feel oddly famous for a day.}\nopt.right.say.1 [07eb19] = {~他们登了。早饭时有两个人向你引用了它，其中一个大声表示反对。|它登在了末版，署着你的缩写。你莫名其妙地出了一天名。}\n# en: {~It goes in the basket. By next week, someone is reading it aloud.|It's rejected with a note: \"Too sensible.\" You aren't sure that's a criticism.}\nopt.right.say.2 [47bec5] = {~它进了篮子。下个星期，就有人在大声朗读它。|退稿附了一张字条：“太有道理了。”你不确定这算不算批评。}\n\n@event ledger_office_deadline_night\n# en: Deadline Night\ntitle [d06cb7] = 截稿之夜\n# en: {The press is groaning like a dragon on deadline, and {here} eyes you like a captain eyes a spare oar.|{here} hands you a stack before you've said hello, and a rag for your fingers.|You take a stack and start folding. {here} doesn't look up, which is a compliment.} {~The ink on the front page is still wet.|A headline has a spelling mistake nobody will admit to.|{final_year: You've folded this paper for years now.|The press shudders as if it's thinking of stopping.}}\ntext [6df153] = {印刷机像截稿夜的龙一样呻吟，{here}打量你的眼神，就像船长打量一支备用的桨。|你还没打招呼，{here}就递来一摞报纸，外加一块擦手的布。|你拿起一摞就开始折。{here}头都不抬——这已经是夸奖了。} {~头版的墨迹还没干。|有个标题拼错了，谁也不肯认。|{final_year: 你已经折了好几年这份报纸。|印刷机一阵哆嗦，像在考虑罢工。}}\n# en: A cramped office lit by one lamp, a groaning printing press, two students folding newspapers into tall stacks.\nscene [241781] = 一盏灯照亮狭小的编辑部，印刷机轰鸣呻吟，两名学生把报纸折成高高的几摞。\n# en: Fold until the press stops\nopt.left.label [b5b377] = 折到印刷机停下\n# en: {cast_as(here, nessa): {~\"Good. Fast. Again,\" says Nessa. From her, that's a sonnet.|Nessa folds twice as fast as you and reads every page as she goes.}|{~By dawn your fingers are grey with ink, and you know tomorrow's news before anyone.|You fold until the stacks are taller than you. Then you fold the ones that fell over.}}\nopt.left.say.1 [847d78] = {cast_as(here, nessa): {~“好。快。再来。”内莎说。从她嘴里出来，这就是一首十四行诗。|内莎折得比你快一倍，还边折边把每一页读完。}|{~天亮时你的手指被墨染成灰色，明天的新闻你比谁都先知道。|你折到报纸堆得比你还高。然后再把倒下来的那些重新折好。}}\n# en: Make your excuses\nopt.right.label [1694c0] = 找个借口溜走\n# en: {cast_as(here, nessa): {~\"Deserter,\" Nessa calls. \"Noted. Quoted. Forgiven by Thursday.\"|Nessa doesn't look up. \"Coward,\" she says, almost fondly, and writes something down.}|{~You slip away before the second stack. The press groans after you, reproachfully.|You plead an essay. Nobody believes you. Tomorrow's paper mentions a certain absent volunteer.}}\nopt.right.say.1 [5d4553] = {cast_as(here, nessa): {~“逃兵，”内莎喊，“已记录。已引用。周四前原谅你。”|内莎头也不抬。“胆小鬼。”她说，语气近乎亲昵，然后记下了点什么。}|{~第二摞还没折，你就溜了。印刷机在你身后埋怨地呻吟。|你说要赶论文。没人信。第二天的报上提到了某位缺席的志愿者。}}\n\n@event ledger_office_drying_line\n# en: The Drying Line\ntitle [7b0073] = 晾报绳\n# en: {Tomorrow's gossip column is pegged on a line to dry, and nobody is watching it.|The proofs hang on the line, and the peg on the gossip column is a new red one.|The drying line. You know which peg holds the gossip column.} {~One item names your house.|One item begins \"Which second-year was seen...\"|{final_year: One item is about the graduating year. Possibly you.|A whole paragraph is about a broken window.}}\ntext [30bd74] = {明天的八卦专栏夹在绳子上晾着，没人看着。|校样挂在绳上，夹八卦专栏的换成了一个新的红夹子。|那根晾报绳。你知道哪个夹子夹着八卦专栏。} {~有一条提到了你的学院。|有一条开头是“哪位二年级生被人看见……”|{final_year: 有一条写的是毕业班。也许就是你。|整整一段都在说一扇碎窗户。}}\n# en: Freshly printed pages pegged on a line across a cramped office to dry, one page hanging slightly crooked.\nscene [125d66] = 刚印好的报纸夹在横贯狭小编辑部的绳子上晾干，其中一页挂得有点歪。\n# en: Read ahead\nopt.left.label [f50674] = 先睹为快\n# en: {~You read it all and hang it back exactly as crooked as it was.|You know tomorrow's scandal tonight. It is much smaller than it will look in print.}\nopt.left.say.1 [2f2c95] = {~你全读完了，又把它原样挂回去，歪得一模一样。|今晚你就知道了明天的丑闻。它远没有印出来时看着那么大。}\n# en: {~Caught. The next column has an item beginning \"Which nosy visitor...\"|A peg snaps. The page lands face-down in the ink tray, and everyone turns round.}\nopt.left.say.2 [7904fe] = {~被抓了。下一期专栏里有一条，开头是“哪位好奇的访客……”|一个夹子崩断了。那页纸正面朝下掉进了墨盘，所有人都转过头来。}\n# en: Leave the line alone\nopt.right.label [1d8c18] = 别碰那根绳子\n# en: {~You spend the night wondering. That's the price of manners.|You find out with everyone else, at breakfast. It was about you anyway.}\nopt.right.say.1 [f85a21] = {~你琢磨了一整晚。这就是讲礼貌的代价。|第二天早饭时，你和大家一起知道了。反正写的就是你。}\n\n@event headmistress_tower_stone_owl\n# en: The Stone Owl\ntitle [6ea1cf] = 石雕猫头鹰\n# en: {The stone owl on the stair opens one eye. \"A question first,\" it says, in a voice like a door closing.|The owl's other eye is half open tonight. It has been waiting all day for someone to ask.|The owl opens both eyes for you now, which may or may not be an honour.} {~Today's question is about the founders.|Today's question is a riddle with no right answer.|{season == \"winter\": There is snow on its head, and it is not amused.|A moth has landed on its beak. It is pretending not to notice.}}\ntext [7f1a6e] = {楼梯上的石雕猫头鹰睁开一只眼。“先回答一个问题。”它说，声音像一扇正在合上的门。|今晚猫头鹰的另一只眼也半睁着。它等了一整天，就盼着有人来问。|猫头鹰现在会为你睁开两只眼了，这也许是种荣幸，也许不是。} {~今天的问题关于创始者。|今天的问题是个没有正确答案的谜语。|{season == \"winter\": 它头上积了雪，很不高兴。|一只飞蛾停在它的喙上。它假装没注意到。}}\n# en: A stone owl on the newel post of a spiral stair, one eye open, a student standing very still before it.\nscene [770d5b] = 螺旋楼梯的立柱上蹲着一只石雕猫头鹰，睁着一只眼，一名学生在它面前一动不敢动。\n# en: Answer the question\nopt.left.label [65b450] = 回答问题\n# en: {~The owl closes its eyes and the stair is yours. Upstairs, someone laughs, short and pleased.|\"Adequate,\" says the owl, which from a stone owl is a standing ovation.}\nopt.left.say.1 [0a5b41] = {~猫头鹰闭上眼，楼梯归你了。楼上有人笑了一声，短促而愉快。|“尚可。”猫头鹰说。从一只石头猫头鹰嘴里说出来，这等于全场起立鼓掌。}\n# en: {~\"No,\" says the owl, and closes both eyes. You climb anyway, feeling marked down.|The owl repeats your answer back to you, slowly. It sounds worse.}\nopt.left.say.2 [06aab3] = {~“不对。”猫头鹰说着，闭上了两只眼。你还是爬了上去，感觉被扣了分。|猫头鹰把你的答案慢慢复述了一遍。听起来更糟了。}\n# en: Climb past without a word\nopt.right.label [ef7de3] = 一言不发地走过去\n# en: {~The owl says nothing. At the top, the door is shut. You have the strong feeling you've been noted.|You get past. The owl's eye follows you all the way up, and all the way down again.}\nopt.right.say.1 [ab3525] = {~猫头鹰什么也没说。到了顶上，门关着。你强烈地感觉自己被记了一笔。|你过去了。猫头鹰的眼睛一路跟着你上楼，又一路跟着你下楼。}\n\n@event headmistress_tower_tea\n# en: Two Cups Poured\ntitle [91cc0d] = 两杯已斟好\n# en: {The headmistress pours two cups unasked. \"Sit. Tell me how your term is going, and then how it is really going.\"|Two cups, already poured. \"Well,\" says {host}, \"how is it really going?\"|You sit before she asks. She looks pleased, and doesn't say so.} {~The crystal towers on her board have moved.|Rain ticks on the high windows.|{final_year: \"Your last year,\" she says. \"I remember your first.\"|Footnote, the library cat, sleeps on her papers.}}\ntext [52e6a3] = {院长没问你要不要，就斟了两杯茶。“坐。说说你这学期过得怎么样，再说说真正过得怎么样。”|两杯茶，已经斟好。“那么，”{host}说，“真正过得怎么样？”|她还没开口，你就坐下了。她看上去很满意，但没说出来。} {~她棋盘上的水晶塔挪了位置。|雨点轻敲着高窗。|{final_year: “你的最后一年了，”她说，“我还记得你的第一年。”|图书馆的猫脚注睡在她的文件上。}}\n# en: A tower study full of books, a tall pale woman pouring two cups of tea beside a board of crystal towers by the window.\nscene [56714a] = 堆满书的塔楼书房里，一位高挑苍白的女士在窗边的水晶塔棋盘旁斟着两杯茶。\n# en: Tell her the truth\nopt.left.label [bfb1ef] = 跟她说实话\n# en: {~She listens without interrupting, which is worse than any lecture and better than most comfort.|She hears all of it and says only, \"Yes. I thought so.\" Somehow that helps, later.}\nopt.left.say.1 [22870c] = {~她从头听到尾，一句也不打断。这比任何训话都难熬，又比大多数安慰都管用。|她全听完了，只说：“是的。我也这么想。”不知怎么，过后这句话很有用。}\n# en: Give her the polished version\nopt.right.label [02edb8] = 给她一个漂亮的版本\n# en: {~She laughs, short and delighted, and lets you get away with it. You aren't sure that's a good sign.|\"Very good,\" she says. \"Next time, the true one.\" She means both halves.}\nopt.right.say.1 [12ebb6] = {~她笑了一声，短促而愉快，放你过了关。你拿不准这是不是好兆头。|“很好，”她说，“下次讲真的那个。”两句她都是认真的。}\n# en: {~\"Mm,\" she says, and pours you more tea, and waits. You end up telling her anyway.|She lets the silence go on until it says everything you didn't.}\nopt.right.say.2 [7ca99a] = {~“嗯。”她说着，又给你添了茶，然后等着。最后你还是全说了。|她任由沉默延续，直到沉默把你没说的都说了。}\n\n@event headmistress_tower_window_seat\n# en: The Window Seat\ntitle [15c249] = 窗边座位\n# en: {The headmistress waves you to the window seat without looking up from her letters. \"Read anything. Break nothing.\"|A book has been left on the window seat, open, as if for you.|Your window seat, the headmistress calls it now, as though it always was.} {~Below, the lake is perfectly still.|Her quill scratches; the clock does not tick.|{season == \"autumn\": Leaves blow past at the height of the clouds.|The bridges of light come on one by one below.}}\ntext [9a1c21] = {院长头也不抬地朝窗边座位挥了挥手，继续写信。“随便读。别弄坏东西。”|窗边座位上摊着一本书，像是给你留的。|院长现在管它叫“你的窗边座位”，好像一直如此。} {~下方的湖面纹丝不动。|她的笔沙沙作响；钟却不走。|{season == \"autumn\": 落叶在云的高度飘过。|下方的光之桥一座座亮起来。}}\n# en: A deep window seat high in a tower, a student reading with the lake far below, a headmistress writing letters at a desk behind.\nscene [830655] = 高塔上一个深深的窗边座位，一名学生在读书，湖在远远的下方，院长在身后的书桌前写信。\n# en: Read what she left out\nopt.left.label [95fb60] = 读她摊开的书\n# en: {~A history of the seven towers, annotated in her hand. One note just says \"No.\"|A book of old law, with a dried rose marking a page about second chances.|A treatise on wards so dull it must be important. You get through it. She notices.}\nopt.left.say.1 [1b1fe8] = {~一本七塔史，页边有她的批注。有一条只写着“不”。|一本古老的律法书，一朵干玫瑰夹在讲第二次机会的那一页。|一本讲护界的论著，枯燥得一定很重要。你读完了。她注意到了。}\n# en: Doze in the sun\nopt.right.label [84dad1] = 在阳光里打个盹\n# en: {~You wake to find a blanket over you and no comment at all, which is its own kind of comment.|You sleep through her afternoon. When you wake, she is still writing, and the light has moved.}\nopt.right.say.1 [b945f9] = {~你醒来时身上多了条毯子，她一句评论也没有——这本身就是一种评论。|你睡掉了她的整个下午。醒来时她还在写，光已经挪了位置。}\n\n@event restricted_section_breathing_shelves\n# en: The Breathing Shelves\ntitle [e6d5c6] = 会呼吸的书架\n# en: {The black-bound books breathe in the dark, slow as sleepers. You find a chair and light a candle.|You've learned to breathe with the shelves; it helps.|Your chair is where you left it. The books shift, as if making room.} {~One spine is warm to the touch.|Somewhere a page turns by itself.|{season == \"winter\": Your breath fogs. Theirs doesn't.|The candle burns low and blue.}}\ntext [c9b4b3] = {黑色封皮的书在黑暗里呼吸，缓慢得像熟睡的人。你找了把椅子，点上蜡烛。|你学会了跟着书架一起呼吸，这有帮助。|你的椅子还在原处。书挪了挪，像在给你腾地方。} {~有一本书脊摸上去是温的。|某处有一页书自己翻了过去。|{season == \"winter\": 你呼出白气。它们没有。|烛火烧得又低又蓝。}}\n# en: Black-bound books on tall shelves in the dark, a single candle on a reading table, the spines faintly rising and falling.\nscene [4628e1] = 黑暗中高高的书架上摆满黑色封皮的书，阅读桌上只点着一支蜡烛，书脊微微起伏。\n# en: Read until the candle gutters\nopt.left.label [15382b] = 读到蜡烛燃尽\n# en: {~You read about wards that were old when the towers were new. Afterwards you can't sleep, and don't quite want to.|You read a whole chapter before you notice the book has been reading along, a line ahead.}\nopt.left.say.1 [3d503c] = {~你读到了一些护界，它们在七塔落成时就已经古老。之后你睡不着，也不太想睡。|读完一整章，你才发现那本书一直在跟着读，比你快一行。}\n# en: Read only the spines\nopt.right.label [5119aa] = 只读书脊\n# en: {~Even the titles take something out of you. One of them is just your house's name.|You walk the shelves by candlelight. The spines lean towards you, and then politely away.}\nopt.right.say.1 [cba1ea] = {~光是书名就让你耗神。其中一本只写着你学院的名字。|你借着烛光走过一排排书架。书脊朝你倾过来，又礼貌地退了回去。}\n\n@event restricted_section_lantern\n# en: Holding the Lantern\ntitle [7c19fb] = 提灯的人\n# en: {{friend} came with you, for courage, and is holding the lantern in both hands.|You bring a second candle this time; {friend} takes the lantern without being asked.|You know which aisles creak now. {friend} holds the lantern where you point.} {~\"Did that book just sigh?\" {they} whisper{s}.|A shelf creaks behind you both.|{final_year: \"I'll miss this,\" {they} say{s}, not sounding sure.|The lantern makes the shadows walk.}}\ntext [aeda72] = {{friend}陪你来壮胆，双手提着灯。|你这回多带了一支蜡烛；{friend}不等你开口就接过了灯。|哪条过道会吱呀响，你现在都知道了。{friend}把灯举到你指的地方。} {~“那本书是不是叹了口气？”{they}小声说。|你们身后有个书架吱呀了一声。|{final_year: “我会想念这个的。”{they}说，听起来没什么把握。|灯光让影子走动起来。}}\n# en: Two students in a dark library aisle, one holding a lantern high, the other reading a black book, shadows stretching behind them.\nscene [403cba] = 黑暗的图书馆过道里，一名学生高举提灯，另一名在读一本黑色的书，影子在两人身后拉长。\n# en: Read while they keep watch\nopt.left.label [9567b8] = 你读书，TA放哨\n# en: {~You read; {friend} keeps watch and keeps count of every noise, out loud.|You lose an hour in a book. {friend} loses an hour staring at a shelf that keeps staring back.}\nopt.left.say.1 [903753] = {~你读书，{friend}放哨，并且把每一声响动都大声数出来。|你在一本书里丢了一个钟头。{friend}丢了一个钟头，跟一个不停回瞪的书架对视。}\n# en: Take the lantern for a while\nopt.right.label [83afe8] = 换你来提一会儿灯\n# en: {~You hold the light so {friend} can read. Something in the dark rustles the whole time, and you don't let it show.|{friend} reads aloud in a whisper. The books seem to lean in to listen.}\nopt.right.say.1 [06e68f] = {~你提着灯让{friend}读书。黑暗里一直有什么在窸窣作响，你没露出来。|{friend}小声地朗读。书好像都凑过来听。}\n\n@event restricted_section_chained_book\n# en: The Chained Book\ntitle [1aa621] = 锁链之书\n# en: {One book here is chained to its shelf, and tonight it has opened itself to a page.|The chained book lies open at a different page, and the chain is a link longer.|The chained book has been expecting you. That's the worst part.} {~The page is in a script you almost know.|The page shows a lock with no keyhole.|The page is blank until you look away.}\ntext [359598] = {这里有一本书被锁链拴在书架上，今晚它自己翻开到了某一页。|锁链之书摊开在另一页，锁链长了一节。|锁链之书一直在等你。这才是最糟的。} {~那一页的文字你几乎认得。|那一页画着一把没有锁孔的锁。|那一页是空白的，直到你移开目光。}\n# en: A heavy book chained to a library shelf, lying open on its own under a dim candle, the chain hanging slack.\nscene [04b543] = 一本沉重的书被锁链拴在图书馆书架上，在昏暗的烛光下自己摊开，锁链松垂。\n# en: Read the open page\nopt.left.label [a03931] = 读那翻开的一页\n# en: {~The words make sense for exactly as long as you're reading them. Afterwards you only remember that they did.|You read it, and the book turns its own page, satisfied, as if you'd passed something.}\nopt.left.say.1 [12c46f] = {~那些字只在你读着的时候才有意义。读完后，你只记得它们曾经有意义。|你读完了，书满意地自己翻了一页，好像你通过了什么。}\n# en: {~The words read you back. For a day afterwards you keep feeling underlined.|The candle goes out mid-line. In the dark, the page keeps going.}\nopt.left.say.2 [7fcd6f] = {~那些字反过来读了你。之后一整天，你总觉得自己被划了线。|蜡烛在读到一半时灭了。黑暗中，那一页还在继续。}\n# en: Close it and step back\nopt.right.label [fad0c0] = 合上它，退后\n# en: {~The chain rattles as the cover shuts, like a sigh. You stand with your back to it until your hands stop shaking.|You close it gently, the way you'd close a door on someone sleeping.}\nopt.right.say.1 [9cc33f] = {~封面合上时锁链哗啦一响，像一声叹息。你背对着它站着，直到手不再发抖。|你轻轻地合上它，就像给熟睡的人关门。}\n\n@event restricted_section_handleless_door\n# en: The Door Without a Handle\ntitle [8f3217] = 没有把手的门\n# en: {At the very back is a door with no handle and no window. On it, in chalk that never smudges, HOLLOW STUDIES, BY LEAVE OF THE HEADMISTRESS.|Tonight the door without a handle is warm, like the outer door.|You've read its chalk so often you could recite it.} Underneath, smaller: \"Umbral Theory II. Astromancy II. And know what the founders made, and why.\"\ntext [9b1c7d] = {最深处有一扇没有把手也没有窗的门。门上用永不褪色的粉笔写着：空洞研究，须经院长准许。|今晚，那扇没有把手的门是温的，和外面那扇门一样。|门上的粉笔字你读得太多，都能背下来了。} 下面一行小字：“暗影理论 II。星象学 II。并且知道创始者造了什么，又为何而造。”\n# en: A plain door with no handle at the back of a dark library, chalk writing on it that has not smudged in years.\nscene [2de116] = 漆黑图书馆的最深处，一扇没有把手的朴素木门，门上的粉笔字多年未曾模糊。\n# en: Knock\nopt.left.label [60f31f] = 敲门\n# en: {~Nobody answers. Then, very far away, a pleasant voice says, \"Not yet.\"|Your knock doesn't echo. It just stops, as if something on the other side caught it.}\nopt.left.say.1 [d95abe] = {~没人应。然后，在很远很远的地方，一个温和的声音说：“还不是时候。”|你的敲门声没有回音。它就那么停了，像门另一边有什么接住了它。}\n# en: Copy the chalk into your notes\nopt.right.label [d25ac8] = 把粉笔字抄进笔记\n# en: {~The chalk makes your pencil cold. Two courses and a question, and the question is the hard part.|You copy it word for word. Back in your room, your notes have the same words, and one more underline than you made.}\nopt.right.say.1 [8087b2] = {~粉笔字让你的铅笔变得冰凉。两门课和一个问题，难的是那个问题。|你一字不差地抄了下来。回到房间，笔记上还是那些字，却比你画的多了一道下划线。}\n\n@event gargoyle_passage_old_marks\n# en: Marks on the Wall\ntitle [1ff1e7] = 墙上的刻痕\n# en: {The passage walls are covered in initials, dates and tiny arrows, scratched by generations who were never here.|Among the marks on the wall is a new one, still sharp.|You know these walls now: the arrows that lie, the dates that can't be right, the initials that keep coming back.} {~The wind sings through the gargoyle's teeth.|Below, a door closes that shouldn't be there.|{season == \"winter\": Frost picks out every letter.|Moths sleep in the deeper scratches.}}\ntext [597d06] = {通道的墙上刻满了缩写、日期和小箭头，出自一代又一代“从没来过这里”的人。|墙上的刻痕里多了一道新的，还很锋利。|这些墙你已经熟了：说谎的箭头，不可能对的日期，反复出现的缩写。} {~风穿过石像鬼的牙缝唱着歌。|下方，一扇不该存在的门关上了。|{season == \"winter\": 霜把每个字母都描了出来。|飞蛾睡在较深的刻痕里。}}\n# en: A narrow stone passage behind a gargoyle, its walls covered in scratched initials, dates and tiny arrows, lit by a stub of candle.\nscene [5e6982] = 石像鬼身后一条狭窄的石头通道，墙上刻满缩写、日期和小箭头，一截蜡烛头照着它们。\n# en: Add your own mark\nopt.left.label [5a8fd8] = 刻下你自己的记号\n# en: {~You scratch your initials small and low, with a tiny arrow pointing nowhere. It feels like signing something.|{final_year: You add the year beside your old mark. It looks so much younger than you.|A knuckle pays for it. The mark is crooked and entirely yours.}}\nopt.left.say.1 [ae76c9] = {~你把缩写刻得又小又低，再加一个指向虚空的小箭头。感觉像在签什么契约。|{final_year: 你在旧记号旁添上了年份。它看起来比你年轻太多。|赔进去一个指关节。记号歪歪扭扭，完全属于你。}}\n# en: Follow an old arrow\nopt.right.label [40d53f] = 跟着旧箭头走\n# en: {~The arrow leads to another arrow, which leads to a wall, which has a joke scratched on it.|The arrow leads to a slit window with a view of the lake no plan admits to. Your knees disagree with the route.}\nopt.right.say.1 [1ef50f] = {~箭头指向另一个箭头，又指向一堵墙，墙上刻着一个笑话。|箭头通向一道窄窗，窗外的湖景没有哪张地图承认。你的膝盖不同意这条路线。}\n\n@event gargoyle_passage_offering_dish\n# en: The Gargoyle's Dish\ntitle [695172] = 石像鬼的碟子\n# en: {The third gargoyle has a dish worn into its paw, and in the dish are offerings, a button, a biscuit, a bent key.|Someone has left the gargoyle a single grape, peeled.|The gargoyle looks at you the way a landlord looks at the first of the month.} {~Tonight it looks hungry.|Tonight it looks smug.|{season == \"summer\": The stone is still warm from the day.|Rain runs out of its mouth in a steady complaint.}}\ntext [48ff6f] = {第三座石像鬼的爪子上磨出了一只碟子，碟里放着供品——一颗纽扣，一块饼干，一把弯钥匙。|有人给石像鬼留了一颗葡萄，还剥了皮。|石像鬼看你的眼神，就像房东在月初看房客。} {~今晚它看起来很饿。|今晚它看起来很得意。|{season == \"summer\": 石头还留着白天的余温。|雨水从它嘴里流出来，像一串不停的抱怨。}}\n# en: A weathered gargoyle on a moonlit parapet with a dish worn into its stone paw, holding a button, a biscuit and a bent key.\nscene [1fa1f4] = 月光下的护墙上，一座饱经风霜的石像鬼，石爪上磨出一只碟子，盛着一颗纽扣、一块饼干和一把弯钥匙。\n# en: Leave a crown in the dish\nopt.left.label [e7b1bc] = 往碟里放一枚克朗\n# en: {~By morning the crown is gone, and so is the biscuit. Nobody admits to either.|The crown clinks in. The gargoyle's expression doesn't change, but the wind drops.}\nopt.left.say.1 [053c47] = {~第二天早上，克朗不见了，饼干也不见了。谁都不承认。|克朗叮当一声落进碟里。石像鬼的表情没变，但风停了。}\n# en: Borrow the biscuit\nopt.right.label [d81344] = 借走那块饼干\n# en: {~It's a very good biscuit. Somebody's mother made it. You feel a little bad, and then less.|Shortbread, still crisp. The gargoyle allows it, this once.}\nopt.right.say.1 [7a740e] = {~是块很好的饼干。准是谁的母亲做的。你有点愧疚，然后愧疚少了些。|黄油酥饼，还是脆的。石像鬼这回准了。}\n# en: {~It's made of stone. The gargoyle's joke, and it's been waiting a long time to tell it.|The wind gets up the moment you take it, and stays up all night, rattling your window.}\nopt.right.say.2 [7ad359] = {~是石头做的。石像鬼的玩笑，它等了很久才等到有人上当。|你一拿起它，风就刮了起来，刮了一整夜，把你的窗户摇得哐哐响。}\n\n@event gargoyle_passage_parapet_cards\n# en: Cards on the Parapet\ntitle [d8d199] = 护墙上的牌局\n# en: {On the parapet, by lantern-light, {here} is dealing cards for buttons.|{here} has a new deck, and the pot of buttons has grown.|{here} deals you in before you've sat down.} {~The wind keeps trying to take the jack.|A pigeon watches the game with too much interest.|{final_year: \"One more for the road,\" {they} say{s}.|The lake below holds the lantern perfectly still.}}\ntext [d86f5b] = {护墙上，借着灯光，{here}正以纽扣为注发牌。|{here}换了一副新牌，纽扣堆也变大了。|你还没坐下，{here}就给你发了牌。} {~风一直想把那张杰克卷走。|一只鸽子对牌局关心得过了头。|{final_year: “最后再来一局。”{they}说。|下方的湖把灯影托得纹丝不动。}}\n# en: Two students playing cards by lantern-light on a high castle parapet, a pot of buttons between them and the lake far below.\nscene [68ef4e] = 两名学生借着灯光在城堡高处的护墙上打牌，中间一堆纽扣，湖在远远的下方。\n# en: Play a hand\nopt.left.label [fc6de9] = 来一手\n# en: {cast_as(here, corvin): {~\"Well, look at you,\" Corvin says, paying out. \"Who taught you that? Don't say me.\"|\"Remind me never to owe you money,\" Corvin tells the pigeon, and pays you in buttons.}|{~You win the pot and one actual crown that somebody bet by mistake.|You bluff with nothing and the wind turns your card over at the right moment. You take the buttons with dignity.}}\nopt.left.say.1 [2652b9] = {cast_as(here, corvin): {~“哟，瞧瞧你，”科尔文边付账边说，“谁教你的？别说是我。”|“提醒我永远别欠你钱。”科尔文对鸽子说，然后用纽扣付了账。}|{~你赢下了那堆纽扣，外加一枚有人押错了的真克朗。|你手里什么都没有却虚张声势，风恰好在对的时候掀开了你的牌。你体面地收下了纽扣。}}\n# en: {cast_as(here, corvin): {~Corvin scoops the pot and winces. \"Saint, you're the worst liar I love playing.\" He gives a crown back.|\"Never bluff a man who owes three people money,\" Corvin says, kindly.}|{~You lose your buttons, then some crowns, then the will to bluff.|The wind takes your best card over the parapet. It was the only one you had.}}\nopt.left.say.2 [13ccc3] = {cast_as(here, corvin): {~科尔文把赌注一扫而空，龇了龇牙。“圣人，你是我最爱的最烂骗子。”他还了你一枚克朗。|“别跟一个欠了三个人钱的人诈牌。”科尔文和气地说。}|{~你输光了纽扣，又输了几枚克朗，最后连虚张声势的心气也输了。|风把你最好的那张牌卷过了护墙。那也是你唯一一张好牌。}}\n# en: Watch the lake instead\nopt.right.label [1e5c6c] = 还是看湖吧\n# en: {cast_as(here, corvin): {~\"Saint,\" Corvin says, not looking up, \"you'll never be rich.\" He passes you his scarf.|Corvin deals you out without a word and tosses you his scarf when the wind picks up.}|{~You sit with your back to the stone and watch the dark water while the cards slap behind you. It's cold, and peaceful.|You count the lights in the village until the game's over.}}\nopt.right.say.1 [89ddc7] = {cast_as(here, corvin): {~“圣人，”科尔文头也不抬地说，“你这辈子发不了财。”他把围巾递给了你。|科尔文一言不发地不再给你发牌，风大起来时，把围巾扔给了你。}|{~你背靠石墙，看着漆黑的湖水，身后纸牌啪啪作响。很冷，也很安宁。|你数着山下小镇的灯火，一直数到牌局结束。}}\n\n@event seventh_tower_stair_count\n# en: The Stair That Changes\ntitle [cf510f] = 会变的楼梯\n# en: {The seventh tower's stair never has the same number of steps twice. Scholars count it to learn how the castle bends; everyone else says it lets you up faster if you don't look.|Back on the stair that won't keep its count. You've started chalking each total on the wall; no two agree.|You've stopped expecting the stair to agree with you. You count anyway; it's a kind of manners.} {~Dust lies thick on every step but yours.|The moon follows you from window to window.|{season == \"winter\": The cold comes up through your shoes.|Your candle leans towards the top, like a dog on a lead.}}\ntext [faba23] = {第七塔的楼梯从来不会两次有同样多的台阶。学者们数它，是为了弄懂城堡是怎么弯折的；其他人则说，你不看，它就让你上得更快。|又来到这道数不准的楼梯。你开始用粉笔把每次的总数写在墙上；没有两个是一样的。|你已经不指望楼梯跟你意见一致了。你照数不误，这算一种礼貌。} {~每级台阶都积着厚灰，只有你踩过的除外。|月亮从一扇窗跟到另一扇窗。|{season == \"winter\": 寒气从鞋底往上钻。|你的烛火朝塔顶倾斜，像一条被牵着的狗。}}\n# en: A narrow spiral stair inside a dusty stone tower, a single candle climbing it, moonlight falling through slit windows.\nscene [e0f863] = 落满灰尘的石塔里一道狭窄的螺旋楼梯，一支蜡烛向上移动，月光从细窄的窗缝洒进来。\n# en: Count every step\nopt.left.label [08b067] = 一级一级地数\n# en: {~You lose count twice and get two answers, both certain, both wrong.|Up, one number; down, one more. You don't stay to find the extra step.|{final_year: The count matches your very first climb. You can't say why that frightens you.|You count a step that isn't there, and your foot believes you.}}\nopt.left.say.1 [2eaa2d] = {~你数乱了两次，得出两个答案，都很笃定，都不对。|上楼一个数，下楼多一级。你没留下来找那多出来的一级。|{final_year: 这次的数字和你第一次爬时一模一样。你说不出为什么这让你害怕。|你数到了一级并不存在的台阶，而你的脚信了。}}\n# en: Climb without counting\nopt.right.label [c06bdf] = 不数，直接爬\n# en: {~You climb by feel, in the dark between windows. The stair seems to like that better.|You stop counting and the climb is suddenly short, as if the tower was in a hurry too.}\nopt.right.say.1 [ae886c] = {~你在窗与窗之间的黑暗里摸着爬。楼梯似乎更喜欢这样。|你一不数，路就忽然变短了，好像塔也急着赶路。}\n\n@event seventh_tower_dust_room\n# en: Dust and Moonlight\ntitle [17a713] = 尘埃与月光\n# en: {At the top is one room of dust and moonlight, with a star-shaped hollow in the wall. The footprints in the dust aren't yours.|More footprints in the dust; some are yours now.|The room keeps your footprints like a guest book.} {~The moon sits in the window as if it lives here.|The hollow in the wall is darker than the night.|A moth beats at the glass from outside, trying to get in.}\ntext [a4dbb1] = {塔顶只有一间满是尘埃与月光的房间，墙上有一个星形的凹槽。灰尘里的脚印不是你的。|灰尘里的脚印更多了；有些是你的了。|这间房像本访客簿一样收着你的脚印。} {~月亮坐在窗里，好像住在这儿。|墙上的凹槽比夜色还黑。|一只飞蛾在窗外扑打玻璃，想要进来。}\n# en: A bare round room at the top of a tower, thick dust on the floor marked by footprints, moonlight slanting through one window onto a star-shaped hollow in the wall.\nscene [e73e97] = 塔顶一间空荡的圆形房间，地上厚厚的灰尘留着脚印，月光从唯一的窗户斜照在墙上一个星形凹槽上。\n# en: Sit in the moonlight\nopt.left.label [dda8de] = 坐在月光里\n# en: {~The light is cold and kind. You sit until your shadow forgets which way to fall.|You sit where the moon is brightest. It feels like being looked at by someone very old and very tired.}\nopt.left.say.1 [d3dd63] = {~月光冷，却温柔。你一直坐到影子忘了该往哪边倒。|你坐在月光最亮的地方。感觉像被一个非常古老、非常疲倦的人注视着。}\n# en: Sweep away your footprints\nopt.right.label [6070ae] = 抹掉你的脚印\n# en: {~You sweep your way out backwards with your sleeve. The dust settles behind you, and then, you'd swear, rearranges itself.|You cough your way to the door. The other footprints you leave alone. They seem to want to stay.}\nopt.right.say.1 [0dad62] = {~你用袖子边退边扫出了门。灰尘在你身后落定，然后——你敢发誓——自己重新排列了一遍。|你一路咳着走到门口。别人的脚印你没动。它们似乎想留下。}\n\n@event seventh_tower_lake_window\n# en: The Window Over the Lake\ntitle [749eec] = 临湖的窗\n# en: {The tower's one window looks down on the Mirror Lake, and the latch isn't locked. They say if you open it, you can hear the lake.|The latch moves more easily than last time, as if someone else has been practising.|The window. You've stood here often enough that your hand finds the latch before you decide anything.} {~The lake is so still it looks painted.|There's a light on the water that isn't the moon's.|{season == \"winter\": The lake is grey and hard.|Mist lies on the water.}}\ntext [bdeebb] = {塔里唯一的窗俯瞰着镜湖，窗闩没有锁。据说打开它，就能听见湖的声音。|窗闩比上次更好拨了，好像另有人在练习。|这扇窗。你在这里站过太多次，还没拿定主意，手就已经找到了窗闩。} {~湖面静得像画上去的。|水面上有一点光，不是月亮的。|{season == \"winter\": 湖面灰而坚硬。|雾躺在水面上。}}\n# en: A small arched window high in a tower, its latch half open, the Mirror Lake lying still and silver far below.\nscene [4ee686] = 高塔上一扇小小的拱窗，窗闩半开，镜湖静静地泛着银光，躺在远远的下方。\n# en: Open it and listen\nopt.left.label [846a0a] = 打开窗听一听\n# en: {~You hear water, and under it a sound too low to be wind. You decide it was the pipes, and don't quite believe yourself.|Nothing but wind and water. You stay until you're sure, and you're never quite sure.}\nopt.left.say.1 [6728d3] = {~你听见水声，水声底下还有一种低得不像风的声音。你认定那是水管，自己却不太信。|只有风声和水声。你一直待到确定为止，可你从没真正确定过。}\n# en: {~The cold comes in and won't leave. For days you hear dripping in dry rooms.|The lake is silent, entirely, the way a room goes silent when you walk in on a conversation.}\nopt.left.say.2 [72dbd7] = {~寒气钻进来就不走了。之后好几天，你在干燥的房间里也听见滴水声。|湖彻底安静了，就像你走进一间屋子时，屋里的谈话戛然而止。}\n# en: Leave it latched\nopt.right.label [d532fe] = 让它闩着\n# en: {~You leave the latch alone and go down to bed, and lie awake wondering what you didn't hear.|You put your palm flat on the cold glass instead. The lake does nothing. It's very good at that.}\nopt.right.say.1 [8a19ba] = {~你没碰窗闩，下楼睡觉，然后醒着躺了半夜，想着你没听见的是什么。|你把手掌平贴在冰冷的玻璃上。湖什么也没做。它很擅长这个。}\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/pools/clubs_jobs.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/pools/clubs_jobs.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event duelling_ward_line\n# en: The Ward Line\ntitle [56f9d8] = 护盾阵线\n# en: {Ward drill. Members hold shields in a line while the seniors lob stinging hexes down the hall.|You know by now which senior aims low, and which one apologises.|You could hold a shield in your sleep, and one night you did.} {~A dummy at the end of the line has a shield of its own, out of pity.|Someone has chalked a tally of scorched floorboards. It is long.|A first-year holds the shield upside down and does surprisingly well.}\ntext [a148a4] = {护盾演练。社员排成一列举着护盾，高年级生从大厅那头往这边扔刺痛咒。|你早就知道哪位学长专往低处打，哪位打完还会道歉。|你闭着眼都能举盾，有一晚还真在梦里举了一整夜。} {~队尾的假人也被分到一面盾，纯属同情。|有人用粉笔记下了烧焦的地板数，那一串很长。|一个一年级生把盾举反了，居然撑得出奇地好。}\n# en: A row of students holding shimmering shields across a scorched hall while seniors lob sparks at them.\nscene [271b31] = 一排学生在焦黑的大厅里举着微光闪烁的护盾，高年级生朝他们抛掷火花。\n# en: Take the front of the line\nopt.left.label [d28df6] = 站到队伍最前面\n# en: {~Your shield takes everything and hums about it afterwards.|Hexes splash off your ward like rain off a good hat.|You hold until the bell. Lyra writes something down, which is either praise or a list of your faults.}\nopt.left.say.1 [ac5ab3] = {~你的护盾照单全收，事后还嗡嗡响了好一阵。|咒语从你的护界上溅开，像雨水打在一顶好帽子上。|你一直撑到铃响。莱拉记了点什么，要么是夸奖，要么是你的毛病清单。}\n# en: {~A hex finds the gap by your elbow. Your arm fizzes until supper.|Your ward folds like wet paper. The senior apologises, mostly to the floor.}\nopt.left.say.2 [2963e5] = {~一道咒语钻进你手肘边的空隙，整条胳膊麻到晚饭。|你的护盾像湿纸一样折了。那位学长道了歉，主要是对着地板。}\n# en: Hold the back row tonight\nopt.right.label [a28ef7] = 今晚待在后排\n# en: {~From the back you watch how the good shields bend instead of break. Nobody watches you.|You spend the drill studying the front row's mistakes, which feels useful and looks like hiding.|Safe at the back, you learn a great deal about the ceiling.}\nopt.right.say.1 [dd0d87] = {~在后排，你看清了好护盾是弯而不折的。没人看你。|你整场都在研究前排的失误，感觉很有用，看起来很像在躲。|在安全的后排，你对天花板有了深入了解。}\n\n@event duelling_dummy_mending\n# en: Mending the Dummies\ntitle [d3be80] = 缝补假人\n# en: {After practice someone has to mend the dummies, and tonight the rota says it's you.|Mending night. You now keep a spare spool of thread in your pocket, just for the dummies.|You have stitched every dummy here twice. They look at you with something like trust.} {~The tall dummy is missing an arm. Someone always takes the arm.|One dummy is now mostly scorch mark.|Someone has painted a new name on a dummy's chest, and a grudge beneath it.}\ntext [97b6af] = {练习结束后总得有人缝补假人，值日表上今晚写的是你。|缝补之夜。你现在口袋里常备一卷线，专为这些假人。|这里每个假人你都缝过两遍。它们看你的眼神有点像信任了。} {~那个高个子假人少了一条胳膊。总有人把胳膊拿走。|有个假人现在基本只剩焦痕。|有人在一个假人胸口画了新名字，下面还写着一笔旧怨。}\n# en: A student alone in a duelling hall at night, stitching straw back into a battered practice dummy.\nscene [29efd2] = 夜里，一个学生独自坐在决斗厅里，把稻草塞回一个破旧的练习假人。\n# en: Stay late and stitch them up\nopt.left.label [84da41] = 留下来把它们缝好\n# en: {~By midnight every dummy is whole and you have straw in places straw should not be.|You sew one dummy's arm on slightly backwards. It looks more dangerous for it.|The hall is quiet, the needle is steady, and for an hour nobody is shouting about footwork.}\nopt.left.say.1 [ab7892] = {~到午夜，所有假人都完好如初，你身上也多了几处不该有稻草的地方。|你把一个假人的胳膊缝反了。它看起来反倒更危险了。|大厅很安静，针脚很稳，整整一个钟头没人嚷嚷步法。}\n# en: Leave them for the morning\nopt.right.label [3c93be] = 留到明早再说\n# en: {~In the morning the dummies are still in pieces, and so is Lyra's patience.|You slip out early. Someone finds the dummies and puts your name on next week's rota, underlined twice.}\nopt.right.say.1 [d5fdbc] = {~第二天早上，假人们还是一地零件，莱拉的耐心也是。|你提前溜了。有人发现了这些假人，把你的名字写进下周值日表，还画了两道线。}\n\n@event duelling_refereeing_lyra\n# en: Refereeing Lyra\ntitle [9a9369] = 给莱拉当裁判\n# en: {Officers referee, so tonight you hold the whistle while Lyra duels a second-year.|You know the circle's chalk by heart now, and Lyra's footwork better.|{rel(lyra) >= 12: Lyra no longer asks who is refereeing. She looks for you.|The whistle has a dent in it from some earlier, unrecorded argument.}} {~Her last spell landed a breath after the bell.|She stepped out of the circle by a boot's width, and knows it.|Her opponent's shield dropped first, but only if you squint.} {final_year: One of the last bouts you will call for her.|}\ntext [5c5000] = {干部要当裁判，于是今晚你拿着哨子，看莱拉和一个二年级生对决。|决斗圈的粉笔线你已烂熟于心，莱拉的步法你更熟。|{rel(lyra) >= 12: 莱拉已经不问谁当裁判了。她直接找你。|哨子上有个凹痕，来自某次没记录在案的争执。}} {~她最后一道咒语比铃声晚了一口气。|她踏出圈外一只靴子宽，她自己也知道。|对手的盾先落下，但得眯着眼才看得出。} {final_year: 这是你最后几次为她判比赛了。|}\n# en: A student with a whistle watching a noble duellist in a chalk circle, her opponent's shield just failing.\nscene [a5fea0] = 一个拿着哨子的学生注视着圈中的贵族决斗者，她对手的护盾正要崩溃。\n# en: Call it against her\nopt.left.label [dff616] = 判她犯规\n# en: {~\"Noted,\" Lyra says, in a voice that could chill wine. Next day her feet are perfect.|Lyra stares at you, then shakes her opponent's hand. Later a cup of tea appears on your bench.|The hall goes silent. Lyra bows to the second-year, very correctly, and to you, slightly less.}\nopt.left.say.1 [fce7fb] = {~“记下了。”莱拉说，那声音能把酒冰镇。第二天，她的步法无懈可击。|莱拉盯着你看了好一会儿，然后和对手握了手。后来你的长凳上出现了一杯茶。|大厅一片寂静。莱拉向那个二年级生行礼，极其标准；向你行礼，稍逊一些。}\n# en: Let it stand\nopt.right.label [d81aa7] = 维持原判\n# en: {~Lyra wins. The second-year looks at you, and you find something interesting about your whistle.|Nobody objects. Lyra does not thank you; if anything, she looks a little disappointed in you.|The bout goes in the book as hers. It sits there all week like a stone in your shoe.}\nopt.right.say.1 [4fdc50] = {~莱拉赢了。那个二年级生看向你，你忽然觉得自己的哨子很有意思。|没人反对。莱拉没有谢你，甚至显得对你有点失望。|这场比赛作为她的胜场记进了册子，整整一周都像鞋里的一颗石子。}\n\n@event duelling_running_practice\n# en: Running Practice\ntitle [9e7aed] = 主持练习\n# en: A note is pinned to the hall door in Lyra's sharp hand: \"Called home. You are running practice. Footwork first. Do not let anyone set the ceiling on fire again.\" The whole Circle turns to see what you will say.\ntext [189cd9] = 决斗厅门上钉着一张字条，是莱拉凌厉的笔迹：“家里有事。你来主持练习。先练步法。别再让任何人把天花板点着。”整个决斗社都转过头来，看你要说什么。\n# en: A note pinned to a duelling hall door, a crowd of students waiting inside in chalk circles.\nscene [6d7864] = 决斗厅门上钉着一张字条，门内一群学生站在粉笔圈里等着。\n# en: Run it exactly her way\nopt.left.label [b06e03] = 完全照她的方式来\n# en: Footwork, then shields, then footwork again. Nobody sets the ceiling on fire. Lyra reads your practice log on her return and says \"adequate\", then reads it again when she thinks you've gone.\nopt.left.say.1 [a1b74e] = 步法，护盾，再练步法。没人把天花板点着。莱拉回来读了你的练习记录，说了句“还行”，又趁她以为你走了的时候读了一遍。\n# en: Run it your own way\nopt.right.label [68c6ad] = 按你自己的方式来\n# en: You turn the footwork drill into a game of tag with stinging hexes. The ceiling only smoulders. A week later Lyra runs the same game and calls it her idea, which is the highest praise she has.\nopt.right.say.1 [b32c5f] = 你把步法练习改成了带刺痛咒的捉人游戏。天花板只是冒了点烟。一周后莱拉照搬这个游戏，说是她自己的主意——这是她能给出的最高赞美。\n\n@event lumen_ledger_letters_box\n# en: The Letters Box\ntitle [4a9d28] = 读者来信箱\n# en: {The letters box is full. Stringers sort it, Nessa says, having no dignity yet to lose.|Sorting day. A regular correspondent has started numbering their letters.|Under the stairs the box has burst its lid. You know the regulars by their handwriting now.} {~Porridge complaints, a poem about the lake and a sock.|A furious letter about the crossword, in a professor's hand.|An anonymous tip in lemon juice, noticed only because it smells nice.}\ntext [83900f] = {读者来信箱满了。内莎说，来信由见习记者来分，因为他们还没有尊严可丢。|分信日。有位常客开始给自己的信编号了。|楼梯下，信箱被撑开了盖。如今你光看笔迹就认得出那些常客。} {~抱怨粥的信、一首写湖的诗，还有一只袜子。|一封痛骂填字游戏的信，显然是某位教授的笔迹。|一条用柠檬汁写的匿名线索，你注意到它只因为它闻起来很香。}\n# en: A battered wooden box under a library staircase, overflowing with folded letters and one sock.\nscene [83935d] = 图书馆楼梯下一只破旧的木箱，塞满了折好的信，还有一只袜子。\n# en: Print the angriest letter\nopt.left.label [321002] = 刊登最愤怒的那封\n# en: {~It runs on page four. By breakfast several people are angry about the angry letter, which Nessa calls \"engagement\".|The letter runs. Its author glares at you over the porridge, as if you had written it.}\nopt.left.say.1 [b798aa] = {~它登在第四版。到早饭时，已经有好几个人对这封愤怒的信感到愤怒，内莎管这叫“读者互动”。|信登出去了。写信人在早餐桌上瞪着你，好像信是你写的。}\n# en: Answer them all by hand\nopt.right.label [931b86] = 逐一亲笔回信\n# en: {~You write back to every one of them, even the ones that weren't really letters. Your hand cramps; somewhere in the castle a lonely first-year gets a reply.|A pile of replies, all polite. Nessa calls it \"a waste of a perfectly good grudge\", then borrows your phrasing.}\nopt.right.say.1 [4b7082] = {~你给每一封都回了信，连那些算不上信的也回了。手抽筋了，但城堡某处，一个孤单的一年级生收到了回信。|一摞回信，封封客气。内莎说这是“浪费了一份上好的怨气”，然后借用了你的措辞。}\n\n@event lumen_ledger_courtyard_quotes\n# en: A Word from the Courtyard\ntitle [2bef78] = 庭院之声\n# en: {Every issue needs a word from the courtyard. One question, many opinions.|Word has got round the courtyard that the Ledger is asking questions.|Half the courtyard now takes the long way round your notebook.} This week: {~is the soup getting thinner?|who will win the Spring Tournament?|is the lake louder lately?} {season == \"winter\": Everyone is hurrying indoors.|{~Some second-years pretend not to see you.|A passing professor looks very quotable.}}\ntext [e1bbb3] = {每期报纸都要一段“庭院之声”。一个问题，众说纷纭。|庭院里已经传开了，说《流明纪事报》在到处提问。|如今半个庭院见了你的本子都绕道走。} 本周的问题是{~汤是不是越来越稀了？|谁会赢得春季锦标赛？|湖最近是不是更吵了？} {season == \"winter\": 人人都急着往屋里走。|{~几个二年级生假装没看见你。|一位路过的教授看上去很值得引用。}}\n# en: A student with a notebook stopping passers-by in a busy courtyard.\nscene [3bd888] = 一个拿着笔记本的学生在热闹的庭院里拦住路人。\n# en: Stop strangers and ask\nopt.left.label [91c2da] = 拦住陌生人提问\n# en: {~A gardener, a prefect and a very small first-year give you the best quotes of the term. The first-year's is unprintable, and perfect.|You get a quote so good Nessa moves it to the front page and pretends it was her idea to ask.}\nopt.left.say.1 [b63d50] = {~一位园丁、一位级长和一个特别小的一年级生给了你本学期最好的引语。那个一年级生的没法登，但完美。|你拿到一句绝妙的引语，内莎把它挪上头版，还假装是她想到要去问的。}\n# en: {~Everyone is busy, cold or suddenly deaf. You come back with \"no comment\" in five different tones of voice.|A fourth-year answers at length, entirely about himself.}\nopt.left.say.2 [5554e8] = {~人人都忙、都冷、或者突然聋了。你带回了五种语气的“无可奉告”。|一个四年级生滔滔不绝地回答，内容全是他自己。}\n# en: Just quote your friends\nopt.right.label [1f9ee3] = 直接引用朋友的话\n# en: {~Every opinion comes from someone you had lunch with. Nessa reads them and says, \"Interesting. Your friends are very consistent.\"|It's quick. It's also the courtyard as represented by one person in several scarves.}\nopt.right.say.1 [dc03ca] = {~每条意见都来自和你一起吃午饭的人。内莎读完说：“有意思。你的朋友们观点很统一。”|又快又省事。只是整个庭院的意见，都由同一个人围着几条不同的围巾代表了。}\n\n@event lumen_ledger_red_ink\n# en: Red Ink\ntitle [025680] = 红墨水\n# en: {Nessa edits your story with a red quill and no mercy.|Nessa has your draft, and this time the red quill hesitates once before it moves.|Edit night. You have learned to bring two copies, one for Nessa to kill.} \"{~Kill your darlings. Bury them. Page six.|Lead's buried. Dig it up. Headline it.|Good. Too long. Shorter. Shorter than that.}\" She has cut your favourite paragraph.\ntext [06ae18] = {内莎用一支红羽毛笔毫不留情地改你的稿子。|内莎拿到了你的草稿，这一次，红笔落下前迟疑了一下。|改稿之夜。你学会了带两份稿子，一份留给内莎去杀。} “{~杀掉你的心头好。埋了。第六版。|导语埋太深了。挖出来。做标题。|好。太长。再短。比那还短。}”她删掉了你最喜欢的那一段。\n# en: A cramped office papered with old front pages, an editor slashing a manuscript with a red quill.\nscene [f3d694] = 贴满旧头版的狭小办公室里，一位编辑正用红羽毛笔大刀阔斧地删改稿件。\n# en: Fight for the paragraph\nopt.left.label [528d97] = 为那一段据理力争\n# en: {~Nessa reads it again, out loud, and puts it back. \"Fine. Stays. Don't get used to it.\"|You win the paragraph. Nessa makes you cut a different one, as a tax.}\nopt.left.say.1 [447a57] = {~内莎把那段大声念了一遍，又放了回去。“行。留着。别习惯。”|你保住了那一段。内莎让你删掉另一段，当作税。}\n# en: {~\"No,\" says Nessa, and that is the whole argument.|She listens to your case with great interest, then cuts the paragraph anyway, plus the one you defended it with.}\nopt.left.say.2 [57afe8] = {~“不行。”内莎说，这就是全部论证。|她兴致勃勃地听完你的辩护，然后照删不误，连你用来辩护的那段也删了。}\n# en: Let her cut it\nopt.right.label [8f19c2] = 让她删\n# en: {~It reads better without it. You will never forgive her.|The story runs, tighter and less yours. Nessa scribbles \"good\" in the margin, a word Nessa hands out like gold.}\nopt.right.say.1 [a3b0eb] = {~删了确实更好。你永远不会原谅她。|稿子登了，更紧凑，也更不像你的。内莎在页边写了个“好”，这个字她发得像金子一样省。}\n\n@event lumen_ledger_front_page\n# en: Front Page\ntitle [c50df3] = 头版\n# en: The press groans like a dragon and spits out the dawn edition. Your story is on the front page, under your own name. Nessa hands you a tack without looking up. \"Wall,\" she says. \"That's where front pages go.\"\ntext [911ced] = 印刷机像龙一样呻吟着，吐出了黎明版。你的报道登上头版，署着你自己的名字。内莎头也不抬地递给你一枚图钉。“墙上，”她说，“头版都钉那儿。”\n# en: A creaking printing press at dawn spitting out newspapers, a student holding the first copy up to the light.\nscene [632373] = 黎明时分，吱嘎作响的印刷机吐出报纸，一个学生把第一份举向光亮处。\n# en: Pin it to the wall yourself\nopt.left.label [ea7224] = 亲手把它钉上墙\n# en: You find a gap between a scandal from before you were born and a recipe someone printed by mistake. It looks small up there, and permanent. Nessa pretends not to watch you step back and admire it.\nopt.left.say.1 [1fa814] = 你在一桩你出生前的丑闻和一份误印的菜谱之间找到一处空隙。它在墙上显得很小，也很长久。内莎假装没看见你退后一步欣赏它。\n# en: Send the first copy home\nopt.right.label [a16d32] = 把第一份寄回家\n# en: You post it home with a note that says nothing about how long it took. The reply comes within the week: they have framed it, slightly crooked, over the kitchen fire.\nopt.right.say.1 [0f1990] = 你把它寄回家，附的字条里只字未提这花了多久。一周之内回信就到了：家里把它装了框，挂在厨房炉火上方，有点歪。\n\n@event verdant_watering_rota\n# en: The Watering Rota\ntitle [c1243f] = 浇水值日表\n# en: {You're on the watering rota, a chart the size of a door.|The ferns have learned your footsteps and lean in.|You no longer need the chart, luckily, since a fern ate it.} {season == \"winter\": Frost outside the glass, drips down your collar inside.|{season == \"summer\": It's so hot the cacti look smug.|{~The biting ferns are in a mood.|Something in the far bed grew overnight.|The moonflowers do nothing, beautifully, as they will for years.}}}\ntext [e4dcef] = {你上了浇水值日表，那是一张门板大的图表。|蕨类已经认得你的脚步，纷纷凑过来。|你已经不需要那张表了，幸好，因为它被一株蕨吃了。} {season == \"winter\": 玻璃外结着霜，玻璃内的水滴顺着你的领口往下流。|{season == \"summer\": 热得连仙人掌都一脸得意。|{~咬人蕨今天心情不好。|远处苗床里有东西一夜之间长高了。|月光花什么也不做，美美地，接下来许多年都会这样。}}}\n# en: A student with a watering can and a huge chart in a steaming greenhouse, ferns leaning in hungrily.\nscene [535c02] = 热气蒸腾的温室里，一个学生拿着水壶和一张巨大的图表，几株蕨饥渴地凑过来。\n# en: Follow the chart exactly\nopt.left.label [577433] = 严格按表来\n# en: {~Warm rainwater for the ferns, cold tea for the moss, nothing at all for the cactus that bites. It takes all afternoon.|By the chart, to the drop. Tobias says the plants look \"right pleased\", and they do, in a leafy way.}\nopt.left.say.1 [481e3e] = {~给蕨浇温雨水，给苔藓浇冷茶，那株咬人的仙人掌一滴也不给。整整花了一下午。|按表来，一滴不差。托比亚斯说植物们看着“可高兴了”，确实，以叶子的方式。}\n# en: Water by feel\nopt.right.label [36cb6b] = 凭感觉浇\n# en: {~You read the leaves instead of the chart. Everything perks up, and one fern makes a sound suspiciously like purring.|You guess right on every bed. Tobias asks how you knew. You honestly don't.}\nopt.right.say.1 [18e9c7] = {~你看叶子而不看表。一切都精神起来了，有一株蕨发出的声音很像呼噜。|每一床你都猜对了。托比亚斯问你怎么知道的。你真的不知道。}\n# en: {~You drown a pot of sundew, and a biting fern takes a polite nip of your thumb.|The moss wanted tea, not water. It sulks visibly.}\nopt.right.say.2 [59bb15] = {~你淹了一盆茅膏菜，一株咬人蕨还客客气气地啃了你的拇指一口。|苔藓要的是茶，不是水。它明显在生闷气。}\n\n@event verdant_repotting_day\n# en: Repotting Day\ntitle [5b9bc1] = 换盆日\n# en: {Repotting day. The mandrakes have outgrown their pots and have opinions about it.|Someone always takes the good earmuffs, so you bring your own now.|You know which mandrakes kick, and which only pretend to.} {~Tobias's own mandrake watches from a shelf in a knitted hat, superior.|A seed tray of something blue has started to hum.|{season == \"spring\": Everything is trying to grow at once.|The glass is fogged and the benches crowded.}}\ntext [d7445d] = {换盆日。曼德拉草长得比盆大了，而且对此很有意见。|好耳罩总被人抢走，所以你现在自己带一副。|你知道哪些曼德拉草真会踢人，哪些只是装装样子。} {~托比亚斯自己的那株曼德拉草戴着针织帽在架子上旁观，一脸高傲。|一盘蓝色的幼苗开始嗡嗡作响。|{season == \"spring\": 一切都在同时拼命生长。|玻璃上起了雾，苗床挤得满满当当。}}\n# en: Students in earmuffs wrestling squalling mandrakes into bigger pots on a crowded greenhouse bench.\nscene [24e9d7] = 戴耳罩的学生们在拥挤的温室长桌上，把尖叫的曼德拉草塞进更大的花盆。\n# en: Take on the mandrakes\nopt.left.label [4dbb5a] = 去对付曼德拉草\n# en: {~One mandrake has rooted into the bench itself. It takes a trowel, a lullaby and both hands to talk it out.|Mandrake after mandrake, each into a bigger pot. Your ears ring all the way to supper.|The biggest one goes in headfirst and comes out, somehow, the right way up. You are not sure who won.}\nopt.left.say.1 [69a429] = {~有一株曼德拉草把根扎进了长桌里。你用了一把小铲、一首摇篮曲和两只手，才把它劝出来。|一株接一株，全换进了大盆。你的耳朵一直响到晚饭。|最大的那株头朝下进了盆，不知怎么又正着出来了。你说不清是谁赢了。}\n# en: Take the quiet seed trays\nopt.right.label [62e44f] = 去弄安静的育苗盘\n# en: {~Hundreds of seedlings, pricked out one by one. Your back will remember this tomorrow.|The seed trays are peaceful and endless. By dusk you have names for most of them.}\nopt.right.say.1 [87ae6f] = {~几百株幼苗，一株一株地分栽。你的背明天会记住这件事。|育苗盘安静又没有尽头。到黄昏，你给大部分幼苗都起了名字。}\n\n@event verdant_tobias_cuttings\n# en: The Cuttings Question\ntitle [95ea7f] = 插条之难\n# en: {The benches are full and someone must choose which cuttings stay. Tobias can't. \"It's like choosing between goats,\" he says.|Bench day. Tobias hovers over a tray of cuttings, apologising to them in advance and glancing at you.|Tobias hands you the tray before he can change his mind. He is grateful in a way that looks like grief.} {season == \"autumn\": The frost will take the weakest soon anyway.|}\ntext [964812] = {苗床满了，得有人决定留下哪些插条。托比亚斯做不到。“这就像在山羊里挑一只。”他说。|整理苗床的日子。托比亚斯守着一盘插条，提前向它们道歉，一边偷偷看你。|托比亚斯赶在自己反悔之前把盘子递给你。他的感激看起来很像悲伤。} {season == \"autumn\": 反正霜冻很快就会带走最弱的那些。|}\n# en: A big gentle student cradling a tray of leggy cuttings, eyeing a full compost heap with dread.\nscene [9813c9] = 一个高大温和的学生抱着一盘细长的插条，忧心忡忡地看着堆满的堆肥堆。\n# en: Choose, and compost the rest\nopt.left.label [0d0865] = 挑好，其余堆肥\n# en: {~You keep the strongest and compost the rest. Tobias says \"fair's fair\", then goes very quiet over the heap.|The benches breathe again. Tobias thanks you, then tells you a long story about a goat he couldn't sell. You understand it is about the cuttings.}\nopt.left.say.1 [c66b9f] = {~你留下最壮的，其余的堆了肥。托比亚斯说“公平就是公平”，然后对着肥堆沉默了很久。|苗床终于松了口气。托比亚斯谢了你，又讲了个关于一只卖不掉的山羊的长故事。你明白，他说的是插条。}\n# en: Find room for every one\nopt.right.label [31bf75] = 给每一株都找地方\n# en: {~You find room on windowsills, under benches and in a teacup. Nothing grows well, but everything grows. Tobias laughs from the belly.|\"Ma always said a crowded field still feeds you,\" Tobias says, beaming. The benches groan. So does Old Maud, when she sees.}\nopt.right.say.1 [6ee11a] = {~窗台上、长桌下、一只茶杯里，你都找到了地方。哪株都长得不太好，但哪株都在长。托比亚斯笑得肚子直颤。|“俺娘说，挤一挤的地也照样养人。”托比亚斯笑得合不拢嘴。苗床在呻吟。老莫德看见了，也在呻吟。}\n\n@event verdant_greenhouse_keys\n# en: The Greenhouse Keys\ntitle [5b68d3] = 温室钥匙\n# en: At dusk Tobias holds out the iron key ring, warm from his pocket. \"Ma says you give the keys to whoever shuts the gate without being asked,\" he says. \"You always shut the gate.\" From now on, opening up and locking up are yours.\ntext [f2975e] = 黄昏时，托比亚斯递过来那串铁钥匙，还带着他口袋里的温度。“俺娘说，钥匙要交给不用人提醒就会关门的人，”他说，“你每次都关门。”从今往后，开门和锁门都归你了。\n# en: A heavy ring of old iron keys handed over in a steaming glasshouse at dusk.\nscene [0698f9] = 黄昏时热气蒸腾的玻璃温室里，一串沉重的旧铁钥匙被交到另一个人手中。\n# en: Open up at dawn\nopt.left.label [75086f] = 黎明时开门\n# en: Dawn, the glass silver with dew, every plant turning to the light you let in. You had never noticed they all turn at once. For a moment you are the only person in the world.\nopt.left.say.1 [186b94] = 黎明，玻璃上挂着银色的露水，每株植物都转向你放进来的光。你从没注意过它们是同时转过去的。那一刻，世上仿佛只有你一个人。\n# en: Walk the night round with a lamp\nopt.right.label [4c41e6] = 提灯巡夜\n# en: You walk the benches with a lamp, checking latches and saying goodnight, because Tobias does and it feels rude not to. Something in the far bed says it back. You decide not to mention it.\nopt.right.say.1 [03e898] = 你提着灯沿苗床走一圈，检查门闩，道声晚安，因为托比亚斯就这么做，不说好像不太礼貌。远处苗床里有什么东西也回了一句。你决定不提这事。\n\n@event cartographers_survey_run\n# en: A Survey Run\ntitle [4bf18f] = 测绘之行\n# en: {Initiates do the pacing. Corvin hands you chalk and a passage to measure before the castle changes its mind.|Your chalk is a stub now, so you mark the turns with notches.|Your stride is a unit of measurement on the master map now, and the castle seems to know it.} {~The passage smells of oranges, which nobody can explain.|Behind the wall, a clock ticks that no one has ever found.|Your candle leans, very slightly, toward the left-hand turning.}\ntext [788a29] = {步测是新人的活。科尔文递给你粉笔和一条要量的通道，得赶在城堡改主意之前量完。|你的粉笔只剩一小截了，于是你改用刻痕标记拐角。|你的步幅如今已是总图上的计量单位，城堡似乎也知道。} {~通道里有股橘子味，谁也解释不了。|墙后有一座钟在走，从来没人找到过它。|你的蜡烛火苗微微偏向左边的岔口。}\n# en: A student pacing a dark stone passage by candlelight, chalking marks on the wall.\nscene [04b918] = 一个学生借着烛光在黑暗的石砌通道里步测，在墙上做粉笔记号。\n# en: Pace it properly\nopt.left.label [6eb50c] = 老老实实步测\n# en: {~Every turning chalked and counted. The passage is exactly where it was last month, which on the master map counts as a scandal.|The passage is two strides longer from this end than from the other. Corvin is delighted.}\nopt.left.say.1 [a9e362] = {~每个拐角都标好数清。通道和上个月一模一样，这在总图上算得上丑闻。|这条通道从这头量比从那头量长两步。科尔文乐坏了。}\n# en: {~Your chalk marks lead neatly into a wall that wasn't there when you started.|You lose count somewhere in the dark and have to admit it, which in this club is rarer than a map.}\nopt.left.say.2 [b145a6] = {~你的粉笔记号整整齐齐地通进一堵你开始时还不存在的墙。|你在黑暗里数乱了，只好承认，这在本社比地图还稀罕。}\n# en: Copy last month's figures\nopt.right.label [47f515] = 抄上个月的数\n# en: {~You copy the old figures and sit in an alcove eating toffee. The castle, as if offended, moves the passage a foot.|The figures match last month's exactly. Corvin looks at them, then at you. \"Forger,\" he says, approvingly.}\nopt.right.say.1 [e56ab0] = {~你抄了旧数据，坐在壁龛里吃太妃糖。城堡像是被冒犯了，把通道挪了一尺。|数据和上个月分毫不差。科尔文看看数据，又看看你。“伪造犯。”他说，语气里带着赞许。}\n\n@event cartographers_lantern_patrol\n# en: The Prefect's Lantern\ntitle [709761] = 级长的提灯\n# en: {Every expedition has the moment a lantern comes round the corner. Tonight's has a red sash behind it.|A lantern, a red sash, footsteps. You know this part.|You can tell the prefects on patrol apart by their footsteps now.} {~The alcove beside you is just big enough for one.|A loose flagstone ahead clanks if you step on it.|The tapestry here already has feet showing under it, and they aren't yours.}\ntext [eaf71e] = {每次探险都有那么一刻，一盏提灯从拐角绕过来。今晚的提灯后面是一条红色绶带。|提灯、红绶带、脚步声。这一段你熟。|你现在光听脚步就分得出是哪位级长在巡夜。} {~你身边的壁龛刚好只够藏一个人。|前面有块松动的石板，一踩就响。|这里的挂毯底下已经露出了一双脚，不是你的。}\n# en: Students pressed into a stone alcove as a lantern's glow sweeps along a dark corridor.\nscene [0eba4e] = 几个学生紧贴在石砌壁龛里，提灯的光扫过黑暗的走廊。\n# en: Freeze in the shadows\nopt.left.label [df6ae3] = 在阴影里一动不动\n# en: {~The light slides over you like water off a duck. The footsteps go on.|You don't breathe for a very long time. The prefect sneezes, apologises to nobody and walks on.}\nopt.left.say.1 [602b23] = {~灯光从你身上滑过，像水从鸭子背上流走。脚步声继续往前。|你很久很久没敢呼吸。那位级长打了个喷嚏，不知向谁道了歉，走了。}\n# en: {~The lantern stops on your face. You spend Saturday scrubbing pans and inventing a reason you were inside a wall.|You talk your way out with a story about a lost cat. It costs you the night's mapping and most of your dignity.}\nopt.left.say.2 [4f929c] = {~灯光停在你脸上。你的星期六用来刷锅，顺便编一个你为什么在墙里的理由。|你编了个找丢失的猫的故事脱了身。代价是一晚的测绘和大半的尊严。}\n# en: Go back the long way\nopt.right.label [457126] = 绕远路回去\n# en: {~The long way is four flights of freezing stairs. You learn every one of them by heart.|You double back through the kitchens and arrive late, cold and unseen. Corvin raises a mug of milk he certainly didn't pay for.}\nopt.right.say.1 [749253] = {~远路是四段冰冷的楼梯。你把每一级都记在了心里。|你从厨房绕回去，到得又晚又冷，但没人看见。科尔文举起一杯他肯定没付钱的牛奶。}\n\n@event cartographers_inking_night\n# en: Inking Night\ntitle [e28233] = 上墨之夜\n# en: {Mappers ink the master map. Corvin unrolls it, silver lines shifting like breath, and passes you the pen.|Inking night. The map has redrawn a stair since your last visit, out of spite.|The map has started leaving a blank wherever you hesitate.} {~\"Leave the one behind the kitchens off,\" he says. \"Not for long. For me.\"|\"The stair under the clock. Don't draw it. Don't ask why.\"|He taps a corridor. \"That one stays off the map. Humour me.\"}\ntext [7741a7] = {绘图员负责给总图上墨。科尔文展开地图，银色线条如呼吸般起伏，把笔递给你。|上墨之夜。自你上次来过，地图重画了一段楼梯，纯属赌气。|地图开始在你每一处犹豫的地方留下空白。} {~“厨房后面那条别画上去，”他说，“不会太久。算帮我。”|“钟底下那段楼梯。别画。别问为什么。”|他点了点一条走廊。“这条不上图。就当迁就我。”}\n# en: Two students bent over a huge map drawn in silver ink that shifts as they watch, in a hidden candlelit room.\nscene [172414] = 隐秘的烛光小屋里，两个学生俯身在一张用银墨绘成、边看边变的巨大地图上。\n# en: Ink it anyway\nopt.left.label [f376f9] = 照样画上去\n# en: {~You draw it, line by honest line. Corvin watches, then laughs, a little too late. \"Saint,\" he says, without the usual smile.|It goes on the map. Corvin is quiet for the rest of the night, which on him is very loud.}\nopt.left.say.1 [f7237e] = {~你一笔一画老老实实地画了。科尔文看着，然后笑了，笑得晚了一点。“圣人。”他说，没有平常那种笑意。|它上了图。科尔文一整晚都没说话，这在他身上响亮得很。}\n# en: Leave it off for him\nopt.right.label [1f8fe9] = 为他留白\n# en: {~You leave a careful blank. Corvin squeezes your shoulder and doesn't make a joke, which is how you know it matters.|The map gets a gap and Corvin keeps his secret. You wonder, later, what it's for.}\nopt.right.say.1 [298e93] = {~你小心地留出一块空白。科尔文捏了捏你的肩膀，没开玩笑，你就知道这事要紧。|地图多了块空白，科尔文守住了他的秘密。后来你想，那到底是为了什么。}\n\n@event cartographers_silver_pen\n# en: A Pen of Your Own\ntitle [e2f8fb] = 你自己的笔\n# en: Corvin presses a pen into your hand: silver nib, black barrel, warm from his pocket. \"Mappers carry their own,\" he says. \"And the first passage you find, you name. Club rule. I made it up, but it's a rule.\" Tonight you found one.\ntext [4a3c7e] = 科尔文把一支笔按进你手里：银笔尖，黑笔杆，还带着他口袋里的温度。“绘图员都带自己的笔，”他说，“而且你找到的第一条通道，由你命名。社规。我编的，但它是社规。”今晚你找到了一条。\n# en: A silver-nibbed pen laid on a folded map in a candlelit stone alcove.\nscene [7722b8] = 烛光摇曳的石砌壁龛里，一支银尖钢笔放在一张折好的地图上。\n# en: Name it after yourself\nopt.left.label [080f1a] = 以自己的名字命名\n# en: Your name goes on the master map in your own silver hand, at the mouth of a passage that smells faintly of rain. The ink shivers, then settles, as if the castle had agreed.\nopt.left.say.1 [6ddc6c] = 你用自己的银墨把名字写在总图上，就在一条隐隐有雨味的通道口。墨迹颤了颤，又安定下来，好像城堡同意了。\n# en: Name it for a club joke\nopt.right.label [cf8658] = 用社里的笑话命名\n# en: You name it after the night's worst joke. Corvin laughs so hard a prefect two floors down stops to listen. \"Keeper material,\" he whispers, and for once he doesn't laugh after.\nopt.right.say.1 [6bb20d] = 你用当晚最烂的笑话给它命名。科尔文笑得太厉害，两层楼下的级长都停下来听。“守图人的料。”他低声说，这一次他说完没有笑。\n\n@event library_assistant_wandering_shelves\n# en: The Wandering Section\ntitle [ed4770] = 出走的书区\n# en: {The shelves rearranged themselves overnight. History is missing, and a queue is forming for it.|The shelves have wandered, this time the {~poetry|herbals|atlases}. You have brought string to mark the way.|The head librarian simply hands you the catalogue now and points upward.} {~Footnote sits where the section used to be.|A first-year swears she saw it go upstairs.|There is a faint trail in the dust, as if something heavy had tiptoed.}\ntext [28034d] = {书架一夜之间自己挪了位置。历史区不见了，找它的人排起了队。|书架又出走了，这回是{~诗歌区|草药区|地图集区}。你带了根绳子来标路。|图书馆馆长现在直接把目录递给你，再往上一指。} {~脚注坐在那个书区原来的位置上。|一个一年级生发誓说她看见它上楼了。|灰尘里有一道淡淡的痕迹，好像有什么重东西踮着脚走过。}\n# en: A library aisle with an empty gap where a whole bookcase should be, a student holding a catalogue.\nscene [e42286] = 图书馆过道里本该立着一整排书架的地方空空如也，一个学生拿着目录。\n# en: Hunt it down by catalogue\nopt.left.label [8c8f5d] = 按目录把它找回来\n# en: {~You find it on the fifth floor, wedged between the cookery books, looking innocent.|You track it by its shelfmarks to a landing that wasn't there yesterday. The queue cheers, quietly, because it's a library.}\nopt.left.say.1 [572a0d] = {~你在五楼找到它，夹在烹饪书中间，一脸无辜。|你按索书号追到一个昨天还不存在的楼梯平台。排队的人欢呼了，小声地，毕竟这是图书馆。}\n# en: {~You search until closing. In the morning the section is back where it started, faintly smug.|You find the wrong section, twice. The queue disperses with sighs you will hear in your sleep.}\nopt.left.say.2 [c7d25f] = {~你一直找到闭馆。第二天早上，那个书区回到了原处，隐隐有点得意。|你两次找错了书区。排队的人叹着气散了，那叹气声你在梦里都听得见。}\n# en: Put up a sign and wait\nopt.right.label [f19929] = 贴个告示等它回来\n# en: {~\"Back shortly,\" says your sign. The section returns in the afternoon. The queue does not.|You sit at the desk and wait. A student asks whether the sign means you or the books.}\nopt.right.say.1 [83e774] = {~你的告示写着“马上回来”。书区下午回来了，排队的人没有。|你坐在柜台后等。一个学生问，告示说的是你还是那些书。}\n\n@event library_assistant_hush_round\n# en: The Hush Round\ntitle [bd75f0] = 肃静巡视\n# en: {Evening hush round. One lap of the reading room, a lamp, a finger to your lips.|The reading room has learned the sound of your lamp. Whispers stop a table ahead of you.|The regulars hear your step and straighten up before you reach them.} {~A table of second-years is giggling.|An old scholar snores on his notes, in a scholarly key.|Somebody's quill is scratching so loudly it echoes.} {final_year: Soon this will be someone else's lap.|}\ntext [2d03a0] = {傍晚的肃静巡视。在阅览室走一圈，一盏灯，一根竖在唇边的手指。|阅览室已经认得你提灯的声音，你还隔着一张桌子，低语就停了。|常客们听到你的脚步，不等你走近就坐直了。} {~一桌二年级生笑个不停。|一位老学者趴在笔记上打鼾，鼾声都带着学术腔调。|不知谁的羽毛笔沙沙响得都有回声了。} {final_year: 很快这一圈就是别人的了。|}\n# en: A student assistant with a lamp walking between study tables in a dim library, one finger to her lips.\nscene [207931] = 昏暗的图书馆里，一个提灯的学生助理在自习桌之间走过，一根手指竖在唇边。\n# en: Hush them firmly\nopt.left.label [b21b8c] = 严厉地让他们安静\n# en: {~One look and the room falls silent. Somewhere a bookcase seems to approve.|You hush them. They glare. In the morning one of them leaves a note on the desk: \"sorry\", in very small writing.}\nopt.left.say.1 [26d4b2] = {~你一个眼神，整间屋子就安静了。某个书架似乎表示赞许。|你让他们安静。他们瞪你。第二天早上，柜台上有张字条写着“对不起”，字很小很小。}\n# en: Let them be\nopt.right.label [29efb1] = 由他们去\n# en: {~You let it go. The noise rises, the head librarian appears from nowhere, and looks at you, not at them.|You leave them to it. For a while the murmur is the nicest sound in the building.}\nopt.right.say.1 [48d593] = {~你没管。声音越来越大，图书馆馆长不知从哪儿冒了出来，看的是你，不是他们。|你随他们去。有那么一会儿，那阵低语是整栋楼里最好听的声音。}\n\n@event library_assistant_reference_desk\n# en: The Reference Desk\ntitle [6eeb1e] = 咨询台\n# en: {Clerks take the reference desk alone. The first question arrives before you sit down.|The desk has a worn patch now, exactly where your elbows go.|You keep a list of the strangest questions now, and it is long.} {~A professor wants \"the green book, about this thick, I read it in my youth\".|A first-year wants a book on a spell nobody has written yet.|A visiting scholar wants every book that mentions bees, by tonight.}\ntext [4ea61d] = {文员要独自值咨询台。你还没坐下，第一个问题就来了。|柜台上如今磨出了一块亮斑，正好是你搁手肘的地方。|你现在专门记录最古怪的问题，单子已经很长了。} {~一位教授要找“那本绿皮书，大概这么厚，我年轻时读过”。|一个一年级生要找一本讲一道还没人写出来的咒语的书。|一位来访学者要今晚之前找齐所有提到蜜蜂的书。}\n# en: A student behind a reference desk facing a line of readers, one of them holding up a green ribbon.\nscene [d1f7a3] = 一个学生坐在咨询台后，面对一队读者，其中一人举着一根绿丝带。\n# en: Dig until you find it\nopt.left.label [f45a7c] = 一直找到为止\n# en: {~You find it in the end, in the wrong section, under a cat. The reader looks at you as if you had worked a miracle, and you have.|Three ladders, two catalogues and an educated guess. It is the right book.}\nopt.left.say.1 [f32bdd] = {~你终于找到了，在错误的书区，压在一只猫底下。读者看你的眼神像你创造了奇迹，你确实创造了。|三架梯子、两本目录，外加一个有根据的猜测。就是这本。}\n# en: {~Hours later you admit defeat. The book, it turns out, was on the desk all along, propping up the inkpot.|You never find it. You dream of card catalogues all night.}\nopt.left.say.2 [b0cb71] = {~几个小时后你认输了。结果那本书一直在柜台上，垫着墨水瓶。|你始终没找到。你梦了一整夜的卡片目录。}\n# en: Send them off with a good guess\nopt.right.label [1e3693] = 随口指个方向\n# en: {~You point confidently at a staircase. They come back an hour later with a different book, which they say they liked better.|\"Try the fourth floor.\" They do. You hope the fourth floor helps.}\nopt.right.say.1 [108bd5] = {~你自信地指向一道楼梯。一小时后他们带着另一本书回来，说更喜欢这本。|“去四楼找找。”他们去了。你希望四楼帮得上忙。}\n\n@event library_assistant_stack_keys\n# en: The Stack Keys\ntitle [dd3ec0] = 书库钥匙\n# en: At closing the head librarian presses a brass ring of keys into your hand: the upper stacks, the map drawers, the ladder chains. Not the Restricted Section. \"Lock up. The shelves settle after dark. Let them.\"\ntext [3fbf9b] = 闭馆时，图书馆馆长把一串黄铜钥匙按进你手里：上层书库、地图抽屉、梯子的锁链。不包括禁书区。“锁门。天黑后书架会安顿下来。随它们去。”\n# en: An old librarian handing a brass ring of keys to a student among towering shelves at closing time.\nscene [5cab1e] = 闭馆时分，高耸的书架间，一位老馆员把一串黄铜钥匙交给一个学生。\n# en: Do the closing round alone\nopt.left.label [4df85b] = 独自巡视闭馆\n# en: You walk every floor with the keys chiming. The shelves shift and creak and settle, like an old house going to sleep, and this time you are the one who gets to hear it.\nopt.left.say.1 [185d52] = 你带着叮当作响的钥匙走遍每一层。书架挪动、吱呀、安定下来，像一座老房子入睡，而这一次，听见这一切的是你。\n# en: Let Footnote come along\nopt.right.label [7a3ccf] = 让脚注一起来\n# en: Footnote pads ahead of you up every stair, checking each lock before you do. At the top he sits in the window and lets you scratch his ears, once. You suspect that was the real promotion.\nopt.right.say.1 [dd5a65] = 脚注在你前面轻步走上每一级台阶，抢在你之前检查每一把锁。到了顶层，它坐在窗台上，让你挠了一下耳朵，就一下。你怀疑那才是真正的晋升。\n\n@event tavern_cider_rush\n# en: The Cider Rush\ntitle [9095b3] = 苹果酒高峰\n# en: {The evening rush at the Gilded Newt. A dozen orders shouted at once, and a tray that holds fewer.|Half past seven, and the door won't stay shut. You have started remembering faces by their drink, which works until two people want spiced cider.|The regulars just point now, and you know what the points mean.} {~A barge crew has come in soaked and thirsty.|The card table is arguing about the rules again.|{season == \"winter\": Snow is melting off every coat in the room.|Half the village is on the step.}}\ntext [8cff7f] = {镀金蝾螈酒馆的傍晚高峰。十几份点单同时喊过来，托盘却放不下那么多。|七点半，门怎么也关不上。你开始凭各人点的酒记住他们的脸，直到有两个人都要香料苹果酒。|常客们现在只用手指，你也都看得懂。} {~一队驳船船员浑身湿透地进来，口渴得很。|牌桌那边又在为规则吵架了。|{season == \"winter\": 满屋大衣上的雪都在化。|半个小镇的人都站在门口台阶上。}}\n# en: A crowded low-beamed tavern, a server balancing a tray of steaming mugs through the press of bodies.\nscene [3aaff4] = 低梁的酒馆里人头攒动，一个侍应托着一盘冒热气的杯子穿过人群。\n# en: Keep every order in your head\nopt.left.label [588aa1] = 全凭脑子记单\n# en: {~Every mug to the right hand. The whole room tips like it's payday.|You don't miss once. The landlady, who remembers every tab to the penny, gives you a look of professional respect.}\nopt.left.say.1 [9a4d7a] = {~每一杯都送到了对的人手里。整屋人给小费都像发了工钱。|你一次也没错。那位把每笔账记到分毫的老板娘，向你投来同行间的敬意。}\n# en: {~The spiced cider goes to the wrong table. The right table has opinions. The wrong table drinks it anyway.|You mix up two orders and three tempers.}\nopt.left.say.2 [8c8cc7] = {~香料苹果酒送错了桌。对的那桌很有意见。错的那桌照喝不误。|你弄混了两份点单和三个人的脾气。}\n# en: Write every order down\nopt.right.label [80b465] = 每单都写下来\n# en: {~Slow but sure. Nobody gets the wrong drink; nobody tips the slow server either.|Your order pad fills with a shorthand only you can read. By closing, you can't either.}\nopt.right.say.1 [019e3e] = {~慢，但稳。没人拿错酒，也没人给慢吞吞的侍应小费。|你的点单本上写满了只有你看得懂的速记。到打烊时，你也看不懂了。}\n\n@event tavern_regulars_corner\n# en: The Regulars' Corner\ntitle [98ab7f] = 常客角落\n# en: {The regulars' corner by the fire wants you to sit for a minute. The fire is warm, and the minute is tempting.|The regulars' corner has saved you a stool now, with a cushion on it.|The regulars wave you over by name. They want to know how your exams went, all of them, in order.} {~The old barge-man is telling the eel story again.|The herb-wife from the lane wants her pie warmed \"exactly as before\".|A quiet traveller is listening to everything.}\ntext [cc67bc] = {炉火边的常客角落想让你坐一会儿。炉火很暖，这一会儿很诱人。|常客角落如今给你留了张凳子，上面还垫了个软垫。|常客们喊着你的名字招手。他们想知道你的考试考得怎样，每一门，按顺序。} {~老驳船工又在讲那个鳗鱼的故事。|巷子里那位卖草药的大婶要她的馅饼“和上次一样”热一热。|一个安静的旅人什么都在听。}\n# en: An old barge-man, a herb-wife from the lane and a quiet traveller at a fireside table, beckoning a server over.\nscene [017fca] = 炉火边的桌旁，一位老驳船工、一位巷子里卖草药的大婶和一个安静的旅人正招呼侍应过去。\n# en: Sit with them a while\nopt.left.label [c29077] = 陪他们坐一会儿\n# en: {~You hear the latest instalment of everyone's story. They tip you for listening, which is a better rate than serving.|You sit for one minute and stay for ten. The landlady clears her throat across the room like a falling tree.}\nopt.left.say.1 [b6d230] = {~你听完了每个人故事的最新一集。他们为你肯听付了小费，比端盘子还划算。|你说坐一会儿，结果坐了好一阵。老板娘在屋子那头清了清嗓子，像一棵树倒下。}\n# en: Keep the trays moving\nopt.right.label [579530] = 托盘不停手\n# en: {~You wave and keep going. The corner toasts your back. The eel story will keep.|You work the room like weather. The corner calls you \"the brisk one\" now, which is not a compliment.}\nopt.right.say.1 [fd88a0] = {~你挥挥手继续忙。角落里的人朝你背影举杯。鳗鱼的故事跑不了。|你像一阵风一样忙遍全场。角落里的人现在叫你“利索的那个”，这可不是夸奖。}\n\n@event tavern_tab_book\n# en: The Tab Book\ntitle [c2811c] = 赊账簿\n# en: {Barkeeps keep the tab book, and the tab book says {regular} owes the Newt rather a lot.|The tab book has grown a second volume, and {regular} has a page in it.|{regular} is at the end of the bar, and so, in the book, is a long column of figures.} {~{They} smile{s} at you hopefully.|{They} {are} pretending to be fascinated by the fire.|{They} {are} counting coins that clearly won't be enough.}\ntext [165279] = {吧台侍应管着赊账簿，账簿上说{regular}欠了酒馆不少钱。|赊账簿已经续到第二册了，{regular}在里面也有一页。|{regular}坐在吧台尽头，账簿里也排着长长一列数字。} {~{They}满怀希望地冲你笑。|{They}假装对炉火很着迷。|{They}在数一把显然不够的硬币。}\n# en: A ledger open on a tavern bar, a long column of figures, a hopeful customer at the far end.\nscene [1a746d] = 酒馆吧台上摊开一本账簿，一长列数字，吧台尽头坐着一位满怀希望的客人。\n# en: Call the tab in\nopt.left.label [6ebe68] = 把账收回来\n# en: {~{regular} pays, slowly, coin by coin, as if each one were a friend leaving.|You call it in. {regular} pays half and an apology. The landlady counts both.}\nopt.left.say.1 [a5ea12] = {~{regular}一枚一枚慢慢付钱，好像每枚硬币都是一位离去的朋友。|你开口要账。{regular}付了一半，外加一句道歉。老板娘把两样都点了数。}\n# en: Let it ride another week\nopt.right.label [2f306c] = 再宽限一周\n# en: {~{regular} looks at you as if you had hung the moon. The landlady looks at you as if you had lost it.|Another week. The column grows, and so, oddly, does {regular}'s loyalty.}\nopt.right.say.1 [2f8672] = {~{regular}看你的眼神像是你把月亮挂上了天。老板娘看你的眼神像是你把脑子弄丢了。|又一周。那列数字更长了，{regular}的忠诚也奇怪地跟着长了。}\n\n@event tavern_behind_the_bar\n# en: Behind the Bar\ntitle [7962ab] = 吧台之后\n# en: The landlady sets the cellar key on the bar between you. \"You pour from tonight,\" she says. \"Barkeeps feed the fire, too. It hasn't gone out in my lifetime, nor my mother's. Don't be the one.\" The hearth crackles, as if listening.\ntext [8a0483] = 老板娘把地窖钥匙放在你们之间的吧台上。“从今晚起你来倒酒，”她说，“吧台的人也要添火。这火在我这辈子、我娘那辈子都没灭过。别让它灭在你手里。”炉火噼啪作响，像在听着。\n# en: A landlady setting a heavy cellar key on a polished tavern bar, the hearth fire glowing behind her.\nscene [818643] = 炉火在后方闪着光，老板娘把一把沉重的地窖钥匙放在擦得锃亮的吧台上。\n# en: Bank the fire at closing\nopt.left.label [065f76] = 打烊时封好炉火\n# en: You stay after the last song, banking the coals the way she showed you, slow and careful as tucking in a child. In the morning it is still burning. You check twice anyway.\nopt.left.say.1 [b13718] = 最后一支歌唱完，你留下来，照她教的封住炭火，又慢又小心，像给孩子掖被子。第二天早上火还燃着。你还是检查了两遍。\n# en: Pour the staff a round\nopt.right.label [82794d] = 给伙计们倒一轮\n# en: You pour your first round for the cooks and the pot-boy. The landlady takes hers without a word, then taps the bar twice, which apparently means \"well poured\" in a language older than the Newt.\nopt.right.say.1 [64fdee] = 你给厨子和洗杯小工倒了你的第一轮酒。老板娘一言不发地接过她那杯，在吧台上敲了两下——据说在一门比镀金蝾螈酒馆还老的语言里，这意思是“倒得好”。\n\n@event apothecary_clerk_counting_drops\n# en: Counting Drops\ntitle [77984e] = 数滴数\n# en: {Old Pell's first rule is to count every drop aloud. Today the counter is lined with tinctures waiting to be measured.|Drop-counting. Your tongue knows the numbers before your eyes do.|Counting drops. Old Pell says you count like a metronome now, which from him is a sonnet.} {~A customer is waiting, tapping a coin on the glass.|One of the daughters is humming, which throws off your count.|Today's tincture is the colour of a bruise and smells of violets.}\ntext [329e78] = {老佩尔的第一条规矩是每一滴都要大声数出来。今天柜台上排满了等着量的酊剂。|数滴数。你的舌头比眼睛先知道数字。|数滴数。老佩尔说你现在数得像节拍器，这话从他嘴里说出来就是一首十四行诗。} {~一位客人在等，用硬币轻敲玻璃柜台。|老佩尔的一个女儿在哼歌，打乱了你的节奏。|今天的酊剂是淤青的颜色，闻起来却是紫罗兰味。}\n# en: A student squinting at a glass dropper over a tiny vial at an apothecary counter, a wall of labelled drawers behind.\nscene [44e4e3] = 药铺柜台前，一个学生眯眼盯着小药瓶上的玻璃滴管，身后是一整墙贴着标签的抽屉。\n# en: Count aloud, every drop\nopt.left.label [2ee43b] = 一滴一滴大声数\n# en: {~Every vial exact. Anyone in the shop has now heard you count higher than anyone should.|Aloud, drop by drop. Old Pell, passing, nods once without breaking stride.}\nopt.left.say.1 [54324e] = {~每一瓶都分毫不差。铺子里谁都已经听你数到了一个谁都不该数到的数。|大声地，一滴一滴。老佩尔路过，头也不停地点了一下。}\n# en: Count by eye, faster\nopt.right.label [f2a5b1] = 目测，快一些\n# en: {~Quick and true. You clear the counter before the tea goes cold, and a satisfied customer leaves a coin.|By eye, and spot on. One of the daughters raises an eyebrow and says nothing to her father.}\nopt.right.say.1 [c6425c] = {~又快又准。茶还没凉，你就清空了柜台，一位满意的客人留下一枚硬币。|目测，正中。佩尔的一个女儿挑了挑眉，什么也没跟她父亲说。}\n# en: {~One drop over. Old Pell can tell from across the shop. He tips the vial out and makes you count the next one aloud, twice.|You overshoot. The tincture turns from violet to a colour with no name, and Pell sighs like a closing door.}\nopt.right.say.2 [9849c9] = {~多了一滴。老佩尔隔着整间铺子都看得出来。他把药瓶倒空，让你把下一瓶大声数两遍。|你倒多了。酊剂从紫罗兰色变成一种没有名字的颜色，佩尔叹了口气，像一扇门关上。}\n\n@event apothecary_clerk_drawer_check\n# en: The Thousand Drawers\ntitle [76310c] = 千屉之墙\n# en: {Each shift a clerk checks a row of drawers against the stock book. Today's row starts with one that is stuck.|A different row today, and the same feeling that something in it is awake.|Your row for the week. You have learned that the wrong drawer is never just the wrong drawer.} {~One is labelled in a hand that isn't Pell's.|One is rattling, very gently, as if politely.|One smells of snow, in {season == \"winter\": a shop this warm|the wrong season entirely}.}\ntext [0d19b4] = {每个班次，店员要对着库存簿核对一排抽屉。今天这一排的第一个就卡住了。|今天换了一排，还是那种里面有什么醒着的感觉。|这周轮到你这一排。你已经明白，拉错的抽屉从来不只是拉错了抽屉。} {~有一个抽屉的标签不是佩尔的笔迹。|有一个抽屉在轻轻地响，几乎是礼貌地。|有一个抽屉闻起来像雪，{season == \"winter\": 在这么暖和的铺子里|完全不合时节}。}\n# en: A wall of tiny labelled wooden drawers, one of them rattling on its own.\nscene [c05965] = 一整墙贴着标签的小木抽屉，其中一个自己在咔哒作响。\n# en: Open the odd drawer\nopt.left.label [7cc2f8] = 打开那个古怪的抽屉\n# en: {~Inside, a nest of dried moonpetals that belong on the top row. You move them, and the drawer stops sulking.|You coax it open and find the missing powdered newt. Pell crosses something off a list he has kept for a year.}\nopt.left.say.1 [500b7a] = {~里面是一窝干月光花瓣，本该放在最上排。你挪好了，抽屉不再赌气。|你哄开抽屉，找到了失踪的蝾螈粉。佩尔在一张他留了一年的清单上划掉了一项。}\n# en: {~The drawer opens with a sneeze of pepperwort. You sneeze until closing.|Something small and grey bolts out of the drawer and into another, and you never find out which.}\nopt.left.say.2 [e44af0] = {~抽屉一开，喷出一股胡椒草粉。你一直打喷嚏到打烊。|一个灰色的小东西从抽屉里窜出来，钻进了另一个抽屉，你始终不知道是哪个。}\n# en: Mark the row as checked\nopt.right.label [748f18] = 直接标成已核对\n# en: {~You tick the row and move on. Behind you a drawer rattles, as if to say it noticed.|\"Checked,\" you write. Pell reads it, opens one drawer at random, and closes it again with a look.}\nopt.right.say.1 [e90b5e] = {~你打了个勾，继续往下。身后一个抽屉咔哒一响，像是在说它看见了。|你写下“已核对”。佩尔看了看，随手拉开一个抽屉，又关上，看了你一眼。}\n\n@event apothecary_clerk_counter_advice\n# en: Asking the Dispenser\ntitle [23739a] = 请教配药师\n# en: {Dispensers advise, and today a customer asks you instead of Pell.|Pell has moved your stool to the end of the counter, where the customers come.|You know the regular coughs by sound now, before anyone opens the door.} {~A farmer has a cough that sounds like a gate.|A nervous student wants something for courage before exams.|A barge-hand has a back that hasn't straightened since autumn.}\ntext [064e8e] = {配药师要给人出主意，今天有位客人没找佩尔，而是来问你。|佩尔把你的凳子挪到了柜台尽头，客人们来的那一头。|如今常客的咳嗽声你一听就认得，门还没开就认得。} {~一个农夫咳得像一扇破门。|一个紧张的学生想要点考前壮胆的东西。|一个驳船工的腰从秋天起就没直过。}\n# en: A worried customer leaning over an apothecary counter, a student behind it consulting a heavy recipe book.\nscene [7ad077] = 一位忧心的客人倚在药铺柜台上，柜台后的学生正翻阅一本厚重的配方书。\n# en: Prescribe it yourself\nopt.left.label [34d97a] = 自己开方\n# en: {~You mix it, label it and explain the dose twice. A week later they come back just to say it worked.|Your remedy, your writing on the bottle. Pell reads the label, reaches for his pen, and leaves it as it is.}\nopt.left.say.1 [f9c18e] = {~你配好、贴上标签，把用量讲了两遍。一周后他们专程回来，只为说一句管用。|你的方子，瓶上是你的字。佩尔读了标签，伸手去拿笔，又原样留下了。}\n# en: {~It helps a little, then not at all. They come back, and this time they ask for Pell.|Pell takes the bottle from your hand, adds one thing and gives it back. He doesn't say a word, which is worse.}\nopt.left.say.2 [5c3c80] = {~起初有点用，后来就没用了。他们又来了，这回点名要佩尔。|佩尔从你手里接过瓶子，加了一样东西，又递还给你。他一句话也没说，这更糟。}\n# en: Fetch one of the daughters\nopt.right.label [f428e6] = 去叫佩尔家的女儿\n# en: {~The elder daughter takes over and talks the customer down like a nervous horse. You watch and write it all down.|The younger daughter mixes it in half the time you would have. She lets you hold the funnel.}\nopt.right.say.1 [fdb5ea] = {~大女儿接过手，像安抚一匹受惊的马一样把客人安抚下来。你在一旁看，全记了下来。|小女儿配药只用了你一半的时间。她让你帮忙扶漏斗。}\n\n@event apothecary_clerk_labelling_pen\n# en: The Labelling Pen\ntitle [cbd6f5] = 标签笔\n# en: Old Pell is very proud of his labelling. So it means something when he puts his own pen in your hand, its nib worn to his slant, and slides a stack of blank labels across the counter. \"New stock,\" he says. \"Your hand from now on. Neat, mind.\"\ntext [ea44d4] = 老佩尔对自己写的标签极为自豪。所以当他把自己的笔放进你手里——笔尖已经磨成他的斜度——又把一摞空白标签推过柜台时，这就意味着什么。“新货，”他说，“往后用你的字。写工整点。”\n# en: An old apothecary handing over a well-worn pen beside a stack of fresh blank labels.\nscene [5f9c82] = 一位老药剂师递出一支用旧了的笔，旁边是一摞崭新的空白标签。\n# en: Label the new drawers yourself\nopt.left.label [15781b] = 亲手给新抽屉贴标签\n# en: You write every label slowly, in your best hand. Pell inspects them through his spectacles one by one and changes not a single letter. His daughters notice before you do.\nopt.left.say.1 [a6af78] = 你用最好的字迹，一张一张慢慢写。佩尔隔着眼镜逐张检查，一个字母也没改。他的女儿们比你先察觉到这意味着什么。\n# en: Ask him to watch the first one\nopt.right.label [bd2847] = 请他看你写第一张\n# en: He watches. Your first label wobbles at the end. Pell takes the pen, adds a tiny flourish to your wobble so that it looks deliberate, and returns the pen for good.\nopt.right.say.1 [f27abb] = 他看着。你的第一张标签末尾抖了一下。佩尔拿过笔，在那一抖上添了个小小的花饰，让它看起来像是故意的，然后把笔还给你，从此归你。\n\n@event broom_courier_morning_crossing\n# en: The Morning Crossing\ntitle [d5cc3c] = 清晨过湖\n# en: {The dispatch bell rings. The morning sack goes to the village, and the quickest way is over the lake.|You have started timing yourself against the gulls.|The lake knows your shadow by now.} {season == \"winter\": Ice fog sits on the water like a lid.|{season == \"summer\": The water is so still you fly over your own reflection.|{~A headwind is coming off the hills.|Gulls are holding a meeting on your route.|The far shore is lost in mist.}}}\ntext [6a5395] = {派件铃响了。早班邮袋要送去山下小镇，最快的路是飞过湖面。|你开始和海鸥比速度了。|湖早就认得你的影子了。} {season == \"winter\": 冰雾像锅盖一样压在水面上。|{season == \"summer\": 湖水静得你像在自己的倒影上飞。|{~山那边吹来一阵逆风。|一群海鸥在你的航线上开会。|对岸隐没在雾里。}}}\n# en: A courier on a broom skimming low over a mirror-still lake at dawn, a mail sack over one shoulder.\nscene [021120] = 黎明时分，一个肩挎邮袋的信使骑着飞帚，贴着镜面般平静的湖水低飞。\n# en: Fly straight across\nopt.left.label [d53a49] = 直线飞过去\n# en: {~Straight as a thrown stone. You're early, and the village postmistress tips early couriers.|You skim the water so low your boots leave a wake. Nobody sees, which is a shame.}\nopt.left.say.1 [2f4ac2] = {~直得像扔出去的石子。你到早了，小镇的女邮政局长会给早到的信使小费。|你贴着水面飞得那么低，靴子都划出了水痕。没人看见，真可惜。}\n# en: {~A gust drops you into the shallows. The sack stays dry; you do not.|The far shore vanishes and you fly a slow circle round a buoy. You arrive late and very damp.}\nopt.left.say.2 [ad9944] = {~一阵风把你吹进了浅水。邮袋没湿，你湿透了。|对岸消失了，你绕着一个浮标慢慢兜圈子。到的时候又晚又湿。}\n# en: Take the long way round\nopt.right.label [d7b80a] = 沿湖岸绕远路\n# en: {~Round the shore, safe and slow. The dispatcher checks the clock and sighs.|The long way. You wave to a fisherman, who does not wave back.}\nopt.right.say.1 [cba1da] = {~沿着湖岸，稳稳当当，慢慢悠悠。派件员看了看钟，叹了口气。|绕远路。你朝一个渔夫挥手，他没挥回来。}\n\n@event broom_courier_broom_scramble\n# en: The Broom Scramble\ntitle [90ac74] = 抢飞帚\n# en: {Only one loft broom is in a good state, and every courier on shift wants it.|You have started arriving early for the brooms, and so has everyone else.|You could pick the good broom blindfold, by the smell of its varnish.} {~What's left is one that pulls left.|What's left sheds twigs when nervous.|What's left only flies if you're polite to it.} {season == \"winter\": Frost has got into all their bristles.|}\ntext [699753] = {阁楼里只有一把飞帚是好的，当班的信使人人都想要它。|你开始提早到场抢飞帚，别人也一样。|你闭着眼凭清漆味都能挑出那把好飞帚。} {~剩下的那把总往左偏。|剩下的那把一紧张就掉枝条。|剩下的那把只有你对它客气才肯飞。} {season == \"winter\": 霜已经钻进了所有飞帚的帚毛里。|}\n# en: A draughty loft rack of battered brooms, couriers jostling to grab the least battered one.\nscene [bf0220] = 透风的阁楼里一排破旧的飞帚，信使们推搡着去抢最不破的那一把。\n# en: Race for the good broom\nopt.left.label [4eba2c] = 抢那把好飞帚\n# en: {~You get there first, by a whisker and an elbow. The second-year you elbowed will remember.|The good broom is yours. It flies like a dream; the others fly like complaints.}\nopt.left.say.1 [295a16] = {~你抢先一步，赢在毫厘和一记手肘。被你挤开的那个二年级生会记住的。|好飞帚归你了。它飞起来像一场梦，别的飞帚飞起来像一串抱怨。}\n# en: Take the difficult one\nopt.right.label [c8d2e7] = 骑那把难缠的\n# en: {~It fights you the whole way across the lake. Your arms will remember this, and so will you.|It takes the whole route to come to an understanding with it. You win, narrowly.}\nopt.right.say.1 [648820] = {~它一路和你较劲，直到过了湖。你的胳膊会记住，你也会。|整条路线下来，你们才算达成了谅解。你赢了，险胜。}\n\n@event broom_courier_dead_letters\n# en: The Dead-Letter Shelf\ntitle [c14849] = 死信架\n# en: {Couriers sort the dead letters, post with no proper address, waiting on a dusty shelf.|The dead-letter shelf has grown a second row.|You know which parcels have waited longest now, and which have stopped expecting anyone.} Today, a parcel addressed only to {~\"the tall one in the red scarf, third tower\"|\"whoever plays the flute at dawn\"|\"my brother, the one who never writes\"}.\ntext [324560] = {信使要整理死信，就是那些地址不全、躺在落灰架子上的邮件。|死信架又多了一层。|你现在知道哪些包裹等得最久，哪些已经不再指望有人来取。} 今天有个包裹，收件人只写着{~“围红围巾的高个子，第三塔”|“黎明时吹长笛的人”|“我哥哥，从来不写信的那个”}。\n# en: A dusty shelf of undeliverable parcels in a courier loft, one tied with a faded ribbon.\nscene [dcc319] = 信使阁楼里一架落满灰尘的无法投递的包裹，其中一个系着褪色的丝带。\n# en: Track down the recipient\nopt.left.label [6456ac] = 找到收件人\n# en: {~It takes three towers and a dozen questions, but the parcel reaches the right hands. They open it at once and cry a little, in a good way.|Found them. The look on their face is worth every stair.}\nopt.left.say.1 [152bbf] = {~跑了三座塔、问了十几个问题，包裹终于送到了对的人手里。他们当场拆开，哭了一小会儿，是好的那种。|找到了。他们脸上的表情值得爬每一级楼梯。}\n# en: {~You knock on every door you can think of. The parcel goes back on the shelf, a little more crumpled, a little more sad.|Wrong door. Wrong tower. Wrong week.}\nopt.left.say.2 [19ab4f] = {~你敲遍了能想到的每一扇门。包裹回到了架子上，更皱了一点，也更难过了一点。|敲错门。找错塔。赶错周。}\n# en: Leave it on the shelf\nopt.right.label [52ecc9] = 放回架子上\n# en: {~It goes back with the others. Some of them have been waiting longer than you have been alive.|You leave it. That night you dream of a parcel on a high tower, waiting.}\nopt.right.say.1 [bc33a8] = {~它回到了其他包裹中间。有些在那儿等的时间比你活的还久。|你没管它。那晚你梦见一个包裹在高塔上，等着。}\n\n@event broom_courier_own_pigeonhole\n# en: Your Own Pigeonhole\ntitle [a87c3e] = 你自己的信格\n# en: The dispatcher has painted your name over a pigeonhole, in letters that will outlast the loft's roof. \"Your route from now on,\" the dispatcher says. \"Village, barge landing, the high towers. When the bell rings for you, you answer.\" The dispatch bell rings. It's for you.\ntext [f9338e] = 派件员在一个信格上方漆上了你的名字，那字迹会比阁楼的屋顶活得还久。“从今往后这是你的路线，”派件员说，“山下小镇、驳船码头、高塔区。铃为你响，你就去。”派件铃响了。是找你的。\n# en: A courier loft wall of pigeonholes, one freshly painted with a student's name, a brass bell above.\nscene [517d9b] = 信使阁楼里一整面墙的信格，其中一格刚漆上一个学生的名字，上方挂着一只黄铜铃。\n# en: Fly the route at once\nopt.left.label [9240b6] = 立刻飞一趟\n# en: You fly your own route for the first time, every turning yours. At the barge landing someone calls you by name to hand over a letter. The wind is cold, and you don't mind at all.\nopt.left.say.1 [b37714] = 你第一次飞自己的路线，每个拐弯都属于你。在驳船码头，有人喊着你的名字递来一封信。风很冷，你一点也不在意。\n# en: Stand the runners a round\nopt.right.label [631a00] = 请跑腿的伙计们喝一轮\n# en: The runners toast your pigeonhole with mugs of loft tea. Somebody chalks a little crown above your name. It is still there at the end of the year, smudged, the most permanent thing in the loft.\nopt.right.say.1 [0c186d] = 跑腿的伙计们端着阁楼茶，为你的信格干杯。有人用粉笔在你名字上方画了顶小王冠。到年底它还在，糊了，却是阁楼里最长久的东西。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/pools/courses_a.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/pools/courses_a.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event evo_snuffing_drill\n# en: The Snuffing Drill\ntitle [1894a9] = 灭火操练\n# en: {Evocation I opens with a lesson nobody expected. \"Anyone can start a fire,\" says the lecturer. \"Stopping one is the course.\"|The braziers are lit before you arrive. You know which one smokes.|Your brazier goes out when you walk in now, out of habit. The lecturer relights it, unamused.} {~The front row smells of singed eyebrow.|Someone's brazier has gone out from fright.|The lecturer is timing you with an egg-timer.}\ntext [0c2355] = {塑能术 I 的第一课出乎所有人意料。“谁都会点火，”讲师说，“学会灭火，才是这门课。”|你到之前火盆就已经点上了。哪一只爱冒烟，你心里有数。|如今你一走进来，你的火盆就习惯性地熄了。讲师把它重新点上，脸色不太好看。}{~前排飘着一股烧焦眉毛的味道。|有人的火盆被吓灭了。|讲师正拿着煮蛋计时器给你们计时。}\n# en: A row of iron braziers burning in a scorched lecture hall, students standing before them with their hands raised as if to calm a horse.\nscene [9cf592] = 焦痕斑斑的讲堂里，一排铁火盆燃着火，学生们站在盆前举起双手，像在安抚一匹马。\n# en: Call it back slowly\nopt.left.label [e24679] = 慢慢收回来\n# en: {~The flames shrink into embers, then into a warm ache in your palm.|It takes the whole hour. Your brazier goes out like a sigh.} {season == \"winter\": The hall is colder afterwards, and it's your fault.|The lecturer nods once.}\nopt.left.say.1 [b0010a] = {~火焰缩成余烬，又缩成你掌心里一阵温热的酸胀。|整整用了一节课。你的火盆像叹了口气似的熄了。}{season == \"winter\": 之后讲堂冷了一截，这得怪你。|讲师点了一下头。}\n# en: Snuff it in one breath\nopt.right.label [f77f0b] = 一口气掐灭\n# en: {~The fire folds itself into your hand all at once.|One breath, and the brazier is dark.} The lecturer writes something down without looking surprised, which is the compliment.\nopt.right.say.1 [4598da] = {~火一下子把自己折进了你的手心。|一口气，火盆就暗了。}讲师头也不抬地记了一笔，一点也不显得惊讶——这就是夸奖了。\n# en: {~The fire goes out. It goes out up your sleeve first.|The brazier flares, then sulks, then goes out on its own terms.} You spend the rest of the hour holding your wrist in a bucket.\nopt.right.say.2 [eefe9b] = {~火是灭了。不过它先顺着你的袖子灭了一遍。|火盆猛地一窜，接着闹起脾气，最后按它自己的意思熄了。}这节课剩下的时间，你都把手腕泡在水桶里。\n\n@event evo_bucket_duty\n# en: Bucket Duty\ntitle [af4be1] = 水桶值日\n# en: Every Evocation I lesson needs someone on the buckets: sand for the pyromancers, water for everyone else. {&Today it's your name on the board.|It's your turn again. The rota has a sense of humour.|Your name is on the board. You suspect the rota is personal.} {~The water bucket has a frog in it.|Someone has labelled the sand bucket \"Hope\".|The water bucket is half empty, because the tidecallers borrowed it.}\ntext [9e10c8] = 每节塑能术 I 都得有人守着水桶：沙桶留给火焰术的人，水桶留给其他所有人。{&今天黑板上写的是你的名字。|又轮到你了。这张值日表挺有幽默感。|黑板上又是你的名字。你怀疑这张值日表针对你。}{~水桶里有只青蛙。|有人在沙桶上贴了张标签：“希望”。|水桶只剩半桶，因为唤潮术的人借走了。}\n# en: A student standing at the edge of a busy lecture hall beside a bucket of sand and a bucket of water, watching sparks fly.\nscene [b53c8a] = 一个学生站在热闹讲堂的边上，身旁一桶沙、一桶水，看着火星四溅。\n# en: Mind the buckets, watch closely\nopt.left.label [6fe757] = 守好水桶，看仔细\n# en: {~You put out four sleeves and a textbook, and learn more from their mistakes than from your own.|Watching from the side, you finally see what the lecturer means by \"breathe first\".} {month == 4 or month == 10: It's the best revision you've done all term.|Nobody thanks the bucket monitor.}\nopt.left.say.1 [a55bf4] = {~你扑灭了四只袖子和一本课本，从别人的失误里学到的比从自己的还多。|站在一旁看着，你终于明白了讲师说的“先呼吸”是什么意思。}{month == 4 or month == 10: 这是你整个学期做过的最好的复习。|没人会感谢守桶的人。}\n# en: Swap for a turn up front\nopt.right.label [38056d] = 换个机会上前练\n# en: {~You trade your duty for a turn at the practice line. Nobody is minding the buckets when your spark jumps the desk.|A first-year takes your buckets for a promise of help with their essay. Your practice goes well; your thumb goes pink.}\nopt.right.say.1 [0094a4] = {~你用值日换了一次上前练习的机会。你的火星跳过课桌时，没人在守水桶。|一个一年级生答应替你守桶，条件是你帮他们改论文。你练得不错，拇指却红了一块。}\n\n@event evo_steady_cup\n# en: The Steady Cup\ntitle [1aa292] = 稳住杯子\n# en: {Professor Marisol's Tidecalling mornings begin with a full cup held at arm's length. Keep the water level by will alone while she reads poetry aloud.|Marisol has brought a new book of poems, thicker than the last.|You can hold the cup half asleep now, and often are.} {season == \"winter\": The water has a skin of ice, which she says is cheating.|{~The poem is very long.|The poem is about the sea, and so are your sleeves.|A gull is watching your cup with intent.}}\ntext [20d70b] = {玛丽索尔教授的唤潮术早课，从伸直手臂端一满杯水开始。她朗读诗歌时，你得单凭意志让水面保持平稳。|玛丽索尔带了本新诗集，比上一本还厚。|你现在半睡半醒也端得稳杯子，也确实常常半睡半醒。}{season == \"winter\": 水面结了一层薄冰，她说这算作弊。|{~这首诗很长。|这首诗写的是大海，你的袖子也成了大海。|一只海鸥正意味深长地盯着你的杯子。}}\n# en: A row of students on a stone terrace holding brimming cups at arm's length while a professor reads aloud from a slim book of poems.\nscene [a1e523] = 石砌露台上站着一排学生，手臂伸直端着满溢的杯子，一位教授在一旁捧着薄薄的诗集朗读。\n# en: Hold it to the last line\nopt.left.label [18e90f] = 撑到最后一行\n# en: {~Your arm burns by the third stanza. The water doesn't move.|By the end your arm is shaking and the water isn't.} Marisol closes the book and says, \"There. The lake would approve.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [771247] = {~读到第三节，你的手臂火辣辣地疼。水纹丝不动。|到最后，你的手臂在抖，水却没有。}玛丽索尔合上书：“好了。湖会满意的。”\n# en: Spill, and study her hands\nopt.right.label [9eabd6] = 洒了，转而看她的手\n# en: {~Your cup tips over in the second verse. You spend the rest watching how Marisol turns her wrist, and write it down.|You give up on the cup and watch Marisol instead. She holds a whole pond steady while turning pages.} {final_year: You'll miss her terrible poems.|Your shoes are wet.}\nopt.right.say.1 [69f01a] = {~第二段时你的杯子就翻了。剩下的时间你都在看玛丽索尔怎么转手腕，并把它记了下来。|你放弃了杯子，改看玛丽索尔。她一边翻书，一边让整整一池水纹丝不动。}{final_year: 你会想念她那些糟糕的诗的。|你的鞋湿了。}\n\n@event evo_shaping_hour\n# en: Shaping Hour\ntitle [45641d] = 塑形课\n# en: Evocation II is shaping: flame made into a sphere, a key, a bird. The lecturer keeps a ledger of every scorch mark in the hall and makes you sign for yours. {Your page is blank so far.|Your page in the ledger is filling up.|The ledger has started a second page for you.} {~Today's shape is a bird.|Today's shape is a key that turns.|Today's shape is your own initial, which is harder than it sounds.}\ntext [3f58c5] = 塑能术 II 学的是塑形：把火捏成球、钥匙、鸟。讲师有一本账簿，记着讲堂里每一处焦痕，谁烧的谁签字。{你那一页还是空白。|你在账簿上那一页渐渐写满了。|账簿已经给你开了第二页。}{~今天要捏的是一只鸟。|今天要捏的是一把能转动的钥匙。|今天要捏的是你自己名字的首字母，比听上去难。}\n# en: A lecture hall full of small fires shaped like birds, keys and letters, one of them visibly trying to escape through a window.\nscene [876135] = 讲堂里满是捏成鸟、钥匙和字母形状的小火团，其中一团明显正想从窗户溜走。\n# en: A hundred plain spheres\nopt.left.label [d097ee] = 捏一百个普通火球\n# en: {~Sphere after sphere, until your hands do it without asking you.|It's dull, honest work. Your spheres come out round, every one.} {season == \"summer\": The hall is an oven by noon.|The lecturer says nothing, which is approval.}\nopt.left.say.1 [e3fa06] = {~一个接一个，直到你的手不用问你就会做。|枯燥而老实的活儿。你捏出的每个火球都是圆的。}{season == \"summer\": 不到中午，讲堂就成了烤炉。|讲师什么也没说，这就是认可。}\n# en: Try the hard shape\nopt.right.label [183457] = 挑战难的形状\n# en: {~Your bird flaps twice, circles the lectern and goes out on a perch.|Your key turns in the air, with a click you feel more than hear.} The lecturer turns the ledger round to show it hasn't moved.\nopt.right.say.1 [20a781] = {~你的鸟扑了两下翅膀，绕讲台飞了一圈，落在栖木上熄灭了。|你的钥匙在半空中转动，那一声咔哒与其说听见，不如说感觉到。}讲师把账簿转过来给你看：一笔也没添。\n# en: {~Your bird has opinions, and leaves through the window.|Your key comes out as a fork, and a hot one.} You sign the ledger. The lecturer blots it for you, almost kindly.\nopt.right.say.2 [61ccab] = {~你的鸟很有主见，从窗户飞走了。|你的钥匙捏成了一把叉子，还是烫的。}你在账簿上签了字。讲师替你吸干了墨迹，几乎称得上温柔。\n# en: Shape it at a whisper\nopt.up.label [457949] = 轻声唤火塑形\n# en: {~You call the flame softly and it comes, small and obedient as a candle. The lecturer leans in to watch.|The shape comes out right first time, at a whisper. Your page in the ledger stays clean.|The flame stays the size you asked for, which is the whole lesson.}\nopt.up.say.1 [cbb4d4] = {~你轻轻唤火，它便来了，小小的，乖得像一支蜡烛。讲师凑近来看。|形状一次就成了，轻声细语之间。你在账簿上那一页依旧干干净净。|火焰保持着你要的大小——这门课教的就是这个。}\n\n@event evo_kettle_pairs\n# en: Fire Meets Water\ntitle [566646] = 水火相逢\n# en: {This is why Pyromancy and Tidecalling are taught together. One of you heats the kettle, one cools it, and it must simmer and never boil.|The kettles come out of the cupboard, every dent a story.|You know every kettle by its dents now. Two dents are yours.} {~Your partner is a nervous second-year from Stillwater.|Your partner is very confident, which is worrying.|Your partner yawns hugely.}\ntext [d796c3] = {火焰术和唤潮术之所以一起教，就是为了这个。你们一人加热水壶，一人给它降温，要它一直微沸，绝不能滚开。|水壶从柜子里搬了出来，每一处凹痕都有故事。|如今每把水壶你都能凭凹痕认出来。其中两处是你的杰作。}{~你的搭档是个紧张的静水院二年级生。|你的搭档信心十足，这让人担心。|你的搭档打了个大大的哈欠。}\n# en: Pairs of students on either side of copper kettles, one with a palm of flame and one with a palm of water, steam curling between them.\nscene [a6ec61] = 学生两两站在铜水壶两侧，一人掌心托火，一人掌心托水，蒸汽在两人之间袅袅升起。\n# en: Take the fire side\nopt.left.label [60e2d6] = 负责加热\n# en: {~You and your partner find the rhythm together, and the kettle hums all hour.|Push, give, push. The kettle simmers like a contented cat.}\nopt.left.say.1 [43d706] = {~你和搭档一起找到了节奏，水壶整节课都在轻轻哼唱。|推，让，再推。水壶微微沸着，像只心满意足的猫。}\n# en: {~The kettle boils, then whistles, then lifts its lid at you. You both get a faceful of steam.|You push too hard. Your partner panics and freezes the spout.}\nopt.left.say.2 [78b600] = {~水壶滚了，接着鸣笛，然后冲你掀起了盖子。你们俩都被蒸汽扑了一脸。|你推得太猛。搭档一慌，把壶嘴冻住了。}\n# en: Take the water side\nopt.right.label [9f0835] = 负责降温\n# en: {~Cooling is the patient half. You hold the kettle back all hour and your partner gets the credit.|You lend the kettle your cold for an hour and feel drained by noon.} {final_year: The kettle has been here longer than you, and will stay after you've gone.|Your fingertips stay damp until lunch.}\nopt.right.say.1 [432312] = {~降温是需要耐心的那一半。你整节课都在压着水壶，功劳却归了搭档。|你把自己的凉意借给水壶一整节课，还没到中午就觉得被掏空了。}{final_year: 这把水壶在这儿的年头比你长，你走后它也还会在。|你的指尖一直湿到午饭时分。}\n\n@event evo_deep_call\n# en: Calling From the Deep\ntitle [758834] = 唤自深处\n# en: {Advanced Evocation calls water from the lake bed, where it is cold and old and in no hurry.|The jetty is slick at this hour.|The jetty boards know your boots by now.} The lecturer paces behind the line. The water comes up carrying things. {~Today, a bottle with no message in it.|Today, a single boot, laced.|Today, nothing at all, which is somehow worse.} {season == \"winter\": Ice grinds at the edges of the column.|Mist sits on the water.}\ntext [1fc718] = {高等塑能术要从湖底唤水，那里的水又冷又老，一点也不着急。|这个时辰栈桥很滑。|栈桥的木板已经认得你的靴子了。}讲师在队伍后面来回踱步。水上来时总带着东西。{~今天是一只瓶子，里面没有信。|今天是一只靴子，鞋带还系着。|今天什么也没有，不知怎的反而更糟。}{season == \"winter\": 冰在水柱边缘咯吱作响。|水面上浮着一层雾。}\n# en: Advanced students on a jetty with their arms raised, a column of dark, old lake water rising toward them out of the depths.\nscene [285a58] = 高年级学生站在栈桥上高举双臂，一柱幽暗古老的湖水正从深处朝他们升起。\n# en: Reach deeper than the rest\nopt.left.label [3c22bf] = 探得比别人更深\n# en: {~You call water up from somewhere it has been still for a century. It rises like a held breath and comes down gently.|The water obeys, then lingers, as if it wanted to talk.} The lecturer stops pacing.\nopt.left.say.1 [17cf40] = {~你从一个静止了百年的地方唤起水来。它像一口屏住的气那样升起，又轻轻落下。|水服从了，随后又流连不去，像是想说点什么。}讲师停下了脚步。\n# en: {~The lake pulls back. You're on your knees on the jetty, cold to the bone.|The water rises, considers you, and drops onto your head.} The lecturer pulls you up with no comment, which is a comment.\nopt.left.say.2 [6ffd18] = {~湖往回一拽。你跪倒在栈桥上，冷到骨头里。|水升起来，打量了你一番，然后浇在你头上。}讲师一言不发地把你拉起来——不说话本身就是评语。\n# en: Keep to the shallows\nopt.right.label [98776f] = 守在浅水\n# en: {~You keep to safer water and log what the others bring up. It's useful and it's dull.|You hold your column at a sensible depth and watch someone else go white with the cold.} {gpa >= 3: The lecturer says you have nothing to prove. You'd like to.|You'll go deeper next time, perhaps.}\nopt.right.say.1 [4acfee] = {~你守着更安全的水域，记录别人捞上来的东西。有用，也无聊。|你把水柱维持在稳妥的深度，看着别人冻得脸色发白。}{gpa >= 3: 讲师说你没什么需要证明的。你倒想证明一下。|下次也许再探深一点。}\n\n@event evo_signature_flame\n# en: A Fire of Your Own\ntitle [aff5b8] = 属于你的火\n# en: Advanced Evocation asks every student for a signature flame: its own colour, its own heat, its own temper. {Yours is a sulky orange.|Yours has turned a gold that you're quietly proud of.|Yours knows you now. It leans toward you when you come in.} {~Somebody's flame across the hall is green and smells of pine.|Somebody's flame is silver and hums.|The lecturer's own flame is lit on the lectern, blue and tiny, never discussed.}\ntext [f6d5f1] = 高等塑能术要求每个学生有一簇自己的标志之火：自己的颜色、自己的温度、自己的脾气。{你的是一团闹别扭的橙色。|你的已经变成一种金色，你暗暗为它骄傲。|你的火认得你了。你一进门，它就朝你倾过来。}{~讲堂那头有人的火是绿的，闻起来像松林。|有人的火是银色的，还会嗡嗡响。|讲师自己的火点在讲台上，小小的，蓝色的，从不提起。}\n# en: A student alone at a stone bench in a dim hall, coaxing a flame of an unusual colour above their palm.\nscene [e0b3dd] = 昏暗的讲堂里，一个学生独自坐在石凳旁，掌心上方哄着一簇颜色不寻常的火。\n# en: Push it somewhere new\nopt.left.label [2cb28f] = 推它去新的地方\n# en: {~Your flame slides into a colour nobody has a name for. The lecturer asks you to do it again, slowly.|For a moment your flame burns without smoke and without heat, just light.}\nopt.left.say.1 [a2a29d] = {~你的火滑进了一种没人叫得出名字的颜色。讲师请你再做一遍，慢一点。|有那么一刻，你的火燃得既无烟也无热，只有光。}\n# en: {~Your flame flares white, takes a bite of your hand and goes back to orange as if nothing happened.|The new colour lasts a heartbeat. The blister lasts a week.}\nopt.left.say.2 [a61aa0] = {~你的火猛地变白，咬了你的手一口，又若无其事地变回橙色。|新颜色只维持了一次心跳。水泡却维持了一个星期。}\n# en: Refine what it already is\nopt.right.label [83d981] = 打磨它本来的样子\n# en: {~You spend the morning making it steadier, not stranger. It's a better flame by noon, and a less exciting one.|You tune the heat by a hair, then another hair.} {final_year: You'll carry this fire out of Lumenhold with you.|It's yours, at least.}\nopt.right.say.1 [43ecdf] = {~你花了一上午让它更稳，而不是更奇。到中午它是一簇更好的火，也是一簇更无趣的火。|你把温度调了一丝，又调了一丝。}{final_year: 你会带着这簇火离开流明堡。|至少，它是你的。}\n\n@event astro_tea_rota\n# en: Another Pot of Tea\ntitle [62fbae] = 又一壶茶\n# en: Astromancy I has a tea-leaf morning every week, and tea leaves require tea. {The class gets through three pots before ten.|The tea rota has your name on it again.|The teapot greets you like an old colleague.} {~Your cup shows something that is either a ship or a boot.|Your cup shows a shape the textbook calls the ladder. It looks like a spoon.|Your cup shows rain. Your cup always shows rain.}\ntext [700f1c] = 星象与占卜 I 每周都有一个读茶叶的早上，而读茶叶得先喝茶。{全班十点前就喝光了三壶。|泡茶值日表上又是你的名字。|茶壶像老同事一样跟你打招呼。}{~你的杯底要么是一艘船，要么是一只靴子。|你的杯底有个形状，课本管它叫梯子。看着像把勺子。|你的杯底显示有雨。你的杯底永远显示有雨。}\n# en: A round classroom under a brass dome, every desk crowded with teacups, a huge brown teapot steaming on the lectern.\nscene [990f2d] = 黄铜穹顶下的圆形教室，每张桌上都挤满茶杯，讲台上一把棕色大茶壶冒着热气。\n# en: Drink the pot and read\nopt.left.label [d0f9d4] = 喝完一壶再读\n# en: {~By the fourth cup your hands are shaking and the leaves are making more sense.|You read six cups in a row and see the pattern underneath the shapes.} {season == \"winter\": At least the tea is warm.|You will not sleep tonight.}\nopt.left.say.1 [5eb7a5] = {~到第四杯，你的手开始抖，茶叶却越来越说得通了。|你一连读了六杯，看出了形状底下的规律。}{season == \"winter\": 好在茶是热的。|你今晚别想睡了。}\n# en: Brew the next pot\nopt.right.label [213708] = 去泡下一壶\n# en: {~You take the rota and brew for the room. Watching leaves settle in a whole pot teaches you more than any one cup.|You brew it strong, the way the lecturer likes it, and learn the shapes as they sink.} {season == \"summer\": The classroom is a steam bath by ten.|Nobody thanks the brewer, but everyone notices weak tea.}\nopt.right.say.1 [7f1a13] = {~你接下值日，给全屋泡茶。看一整壶茶叶慢慢沉淀，比读哪一只杯子学到的都多。|你按讲师喜欢的那样泡得浓浓的，看着茶叶下沉时记住了它们的形状。}{season == \"summer\": 不到十点，教室就成了蒸笼。|没人感谢泡茶的人，可茶一淡，人人都会发现。}\n\n@event astro_star_names\n# en: Naming the Stars\ntitle [fb678b] = 群星之名\n# en: {Astromancy I begins with names. Three hundred stars painted on the dome, to be recited on request.|The long pole comes out. The lecturer points; you name.|You dream the star names some nights now, and wake reciting.} {~Today it's the winter constellations.|Today it's the faint stars nobody can see without squinting.|Today the lecturer points at a patch of bare plaster and waits to see who invents a name.}\ntext [03e62c] = {星象与占卜 I 从名字学起。穹顶上画着三百颗星，点到哪颗就得背出哪颗。|长杆又拿出来了。讲师指，你报名字。|如今有些夜里你会梦见星名，醒来时还在背。}{~今天背冬季星座。|今天背那些不眯眼就看不见的暗星。|今天讲师指着一块光秃秃的灰泥，等着看谁会编出个名字。}\n# en: Students craning their necks under a painted dome of constellations while a lecturer points upward with a very long pole.\nscene [2a227c] = 学生们仰着脖子，头顶是画满星座的穹顶，讲师拿一根很长的杆子往上指。\n# en: Recite them until perfect\nopt.left.label [d30e26] = 背到一字不差\n# en: {~You chant star names under your breath all morning, like a prayer you don't quite believe in.|By noon you can name the whole southern sky backwards.} {month == 4 or month == 10: The exam is close, and the names are finally sticking.|Your neck aches from looking up.}\nopt.left.say.1 [a33940] = {~你一上午都在低声念星名，像在念一段自己不太信的祷词。|到中午，你已经能把整片南天倒着背出来。}{month == 4 or month == 10: 考试近了，这些名字总算记牢了。|你的脖子仰得发酸。}\n# en: Volunteer to go first\nopt.right.label [391066] = 自告奋勇先背\n# en: {~You name every star the pole touches, including the bare plaster, which you correctly call \"nothing\".|The lecturer runs out of stars before you run out of names.}\nopt.right.say.1 [3dccc6] = {~长杆点到哪颗你就报出哪颗，连那块灰泥也没难倒你——你准确地称它为“什么也没有”。|讲师的星星都点完了，你的名字还没报完。}\n# en: {~You call a bright star by the name of a lesser one. The lesser one's champion in the back row is offended.|You blank on the fourth star, and a whole constellation falls out of your head.}\nopt.right.say.2 [e4f71b] = {~你把一颗亮星叫成了一颗暗星的名字。后排那颗暗星的拥护者很不高兴。|背到第四颗你脑子一空，一整个星座从你脑袋里掉了出去。}\n\n@event astro_cold_dome\n# en: The Open Dome\ntitle [06fe07] = 敞开的穹顶\n# en: {Astromancy mornings start before the sun, under a dome cranked open to the sky.|The dome creaks open. You've learned which side of the stove is warmest.|The telescope's eyepiece has the shape of your eye by now.} {season == \"winter\": It is bitterly cold, and the stove is the most popular student in the class.|{season == \"summer\": The air is soft and the sky is already going pale.|There's dew on the telescope and on you.}} Somebody has to take the telescope.\ntext [7c7b4e] = {星象学的早课在日出前开始，穹顶摇开，直对天空。|穹顶吱呀呀地打开。你已经摸清了火炉哪一边最暖。|望远镜的目镜如今已经是你眼睛的形状了。}{season == \"winter\": 冷得刺骨，火炉成了班上最受欢迎的同学。|{season == \"summer\": 空气柔和，天色已经开始泛白。|望远镜上有露水，你身上也有。}}总得有人去守望远镜。\n# en: The observatory's brass dome cranked open to a pale dawn sky, one student at the great telescope and the rest huddled round an iron stove.\nscene [66aa5f] = 观星台的黄铜穹顶摇开，露出淡淡的晨空，一个学生守在巨大的望远镜前，其余人围着一只铁炉子取暖。\n# en: Take the telescope turn\nopt.left.label [d7ad20] = 轮班守望远镜\n# en: {~You track a planet across the eyepiece until your eyelashes stick to it.|The great telescope wanders, as it does. You coax it back to the sky you were assigned.} {final_year: You've looked through it more times than you can count, and you'll still miss it.|Your fingers take an hour to thaw.}\nopt.left.say.1 [4c85c7] = {~你在目镜里追着一颗行星，直到睫毛粘在了上面。|巨大的望远镜照例四处乱转。你把它哄回分给你的那片天。}{final_year: 你已经数不清从它里面看过多少次了，可你还是会想念它。|你的手指过了一个钟头才暖过来。}\n# en: Stay by the stove with charts\nopt.right.label [d88b06] = 守着炉子看星图\n# en: {~The stove students form a little society of the warm and the guilty. You copy charts and hear every complaint.|You annotate the charts while the others shiver. The lecturer notices who is warm.}\nopt.right.say.1 [c05d33] = {~守炉子的学生自成一个小团体，又暖和又心虚。你抄着星图，听遍了每一句抱怨。|别人冻得发抖，你在给星图做批注。讲师注意到了谁是暖和的。}\n\n@event astro_foresight_drill\n# en: Catch the Chalk\ntitle [f960c8] = 接住粉笔\n# en: {Foresight at Astromancy II is practised on chalk. The lecturer drops a stick without warning; you catch it before it falls, by seeing it a heartbeat early.|The lecturer has opened a new box of chalk, which is a threat.|The lecturer's drops have got sneakier, and so have your hands.} {~The floor is white with failures.|A student from Stillwater has caught four in a row and is unbearable about it.|The lecturer is dropping two at once today.}\ntext [3e6f82] = {星象与占卜 II 用粉笔练预见。讲师毫无预兆地松手，你得在粉笔落地前接住它——靠的是提前一个心跳看见它。|讲师拆开了一盒新粉笔，这是一种威胁。|讲师的出手越来越刁钻，你的手也是。}{~地上一片白，全是失手的痕迹。|一个静水院的学生连接了四根，得意得让人受不了。|讲师今天一次扔两根。}\n# en: A lecturer letting a stick of chalk fall from an outstretched hand while a student lunges to catch it a moment before it drops.\nscene [9286f6] = 讲师伸手松开一根粉笔，一个学生扑上前，在它落下前的一瞬接住了它。\n# en: Catch it before it falls\nopt.left.label [351adb] = 落地前接住它\n# en: {~Your hand is there before the chalk is. The lecturer looks at it, then at you, then drops another.|You catch it a breath before the drop.}\nopt.left.say.1 [dd3ad3] = {~粉笔还没到，你的手已经在那儿了。讲师看看粉笔，看看你，又扔了一根。|你在它落下前一口气的工夫接住了。}\n# en: {~You see it falling, clearly, half a second after it lands.|You catch a stick of chalk that wasn't dropped. The real one hits your shoe.}\nopt.left.say.2 [a3fabf] = {~你清清楚楚地看见它在下落——在它落地半秒之后。|你接住了一根根本没扔的粉笔。真的那根砸在了你鞋上。}\n# en: Time the drops instead\nopt.right.label [873a97] = 改为计时观察\n# en: {~You keep a tally of every drop and every catch and start to see the lecturer's rhythm.|You work out that the lecturer always blinks first. It's not foresight, but it helps.} {gpa >= 3: The lecturer asks to see your tally.|Your head aches from looking ahead.}\nopt.right.say.1 [ee94ec] = {~你记下了每一次扔和每一次接，开始摸到讲师的节奏。|你发现讲师每次都先眨眼。这不是预见，但有用。}{gpa >= 3: 讲师要看你的记录。|往前看得太久，你头疼。}\n# en: Let foresight do it\nopt.up.label [b09d2f] = 交给预见术\n# en: {~You stop trying and let the spell look ahead for you. Your hand opens, and the chalk drops into it like a tame bird.|It's a two-chalk day. The spell shows you both, and you catch both.|The lecturer stops mid-drop and just looks at you. You catch it anyway.}\nopt.up.say.1 [c7fd6d] = {~你不再使劲，让咒语替你往前看。你的手一张开，粉笔就像一只驯服的鸟落了进来。|今天是两根粉笔的日子。咒语让你两根都看见了，你两根都接住了。|讲师扔到一半停了手，只是看着你。你照样接住了。}\n\n@event astro_starlight_jars\n# en: Bottling Starlight\ntitle [5ed382] = 装瓶星光\n# en: {Starlight is gathered before dawn, one star at a time, in glass jars on the observatory roof.|The roof is slippery with frost or dew, depending.|The starlight jars. Yours has a chip in the rim you've grown fond of.} {~Most of the class is aiming for the brightest stars.|The lecturer says faint light keeps longer.|There's a slip of cloud coming in from the lake.}\ntext [09dd8e] = {星光要在黎明前采集，在观星台屋顶上一颗一颗地装进玻璃罐。|屋顶上不是霜就是露，滑得很。|星光罐。你那只罐口有个缺口，你已经对它有了感情。}{~班上大多数人都瞄准最亮的星。|讲师说，暗淡的光存得更久。|一缕云正从湖上飘过来。}\n# en: Students on the observatory roof before dawn, holding glass jars up to the sky, each jar glowing faintly with captured light.\nscene [066f15] = 黎明前的观星台屋顶上，学生们把玻璃罐举向天空，每只罐里都微微亮着捕获的光。\n# en: Wait for the faint stars\nopt.left.label [ccc16b] = 等那些暗淡的星\n# en: {~Your jar fills slowly with a cool, pale light that lasts all day in your pocket.|The faint stars come one at a time.} The lecturer holds your jar up and says, \"That will keep.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [b37464] = {~你的罐子慢慢盛满了一种清凉苍白的光，在口袋里亮了一整天。|暗星一颗一颗地来了。}讲师举起你的罐子：“这个存得住。”\n# en: {~The cloud comes in before your jar is half full, and you're cold for nothing.|You wait so long the sun rises on you and burns the jar to plain glass.}\nopt.left.say.2 [b4c891] = {~罐子还没装到一半，云就来了，你白挨了一场冻。|你等得太久，太阳升起来，把罐子晒回了一只普通的玻璃瓶。}\n# en: Take the bright, easy ones\nopt.right.label [a8446a] = 收亮的、容易的\n# en: {~Your jar is full in ten minutes and glows like a lamp. The lecturer calls it a lamp.|Easy light, quickly gathered and gone by noon.} {season == \"summer\": The short nights are no help.|You're back in bed before the bell.}\nopt.right.say.1 [3e8d4a] = {~十分钟你的罐子就满了，亮得像盏灯。讲师管它叫灯。|来得容易的光，收得快，不到中午就散了。}{season == \"summer\": 夏夜太短，帮不上忙。|铃响之前你就回到了床上。}\n\n@event astro_moving_charts\n# en: The Charts Move\ntitle [1e1b5e] = 会动的星图\n# en: {Advanced Astromancy uses charts that move. The inked stars drift to where they will be, not where they are.|The charts are livelier this morning, and one has crept off the table.|You've stopped flinching when the stars move. You still check twice.} {~Your chart keeps rearranging itself into a shape like a doorway.|Your chart is sulking in one corner.|Your chart is going faster than the others.} Pin it and read it, or read it as it goes.\ntext [b5e372] = {高等星象学用的星图会动。墨画的星星会漂向它们将要在的位置，而不是现在的位置。|今早的星图格外活泼，有一张已经爬下了桌子。|星星移动时你已经不再吓一跳了。不过你还是会核对两遍。}{~你的星图总是自己排成一个门洞似的形状。|你的星图缩在一角闹脾气。|你的星图跑得比别人的都快。}是把它钉住再读，还是任它走着读？\n# en: A long table covered in star charts whose inked constellations are visibly sliding across the parchment, students pinning corners down.\nscene [042f72] = 长桌上铺满星图，墨画的星座在羊皮纸上清晰可见地滑动，学生们正按住纸角。\n# en: Pin it and trace the drift\nopt.left.label [89dcbd] = 钉住它，描出轨迹\n# en: {~You pin each star and trace its path, and for a moment you can see the whole season ahead laid out in ink.|The drift makes sense all at once.}\nopt.left.say.1 [26a1e5] = {~你把每颗星钉住，描出它的轨迹，有那么一刻，整个下一季在墨迹里铺展在你眼前。|漂移的规律一下子全通了。}\n# en: {~The chart slides out from under your pins and leaves ink on your sleeves.|You trace the drift for an hour and it goes back to where it started, laughing at you in its own way.}\nopt.left.say.2 [c7401b] = {~星图从你的图钉底下溜走，在你袖子上留了一片墨。|你描了一个钟头的轨迹，它又回到了原处，用它自己的方式嘲笑你。}\n# en: Sketch every shape it makes\nopt.right.label [25762b] = 画下它的每个形状\n# en: {~You fill four pages with the shapes the chart passes through. Most are nothing. One is almost something.|You draw it as it moves, like a portrait of someone who won't sit still.} {final_year: Soon the stars will move without you watching.|The lecturer collects your pages without a word.}\nopt.right.say.1 [6872b3] = {~你画满了四页星图经过的形状。大多什么也不是。有一个差点是点什么。|你边看边画，像给一个坐不住的人画肖像。}{final_year: 很快，星星就会在没有你注视的地方移动了。|讲师一言不发地收走了你的画稿。}\n\n@event astro_far_glimpse\n# en: Looking Too Far\ntitle [2bc51d] = 看得太远\n# en: {At this level Foresight reaches a season ahead. The lecturer allows one glimpse each, timed by an hourglass. People who look longer come back strange.|The dark mirror waits behind its curtain.|The hourglass is turned before you sit down. The lecturer knows your pace.} {final_year: A season from now you'll be gone, which makes the mirror harder to look at.|{~The curtains are drawn against the morning.|Someone before you came out very quiet.}}\ntext [aa382a] = {到了这一级，预见术能看到一季之后。讲师只许每人看一眼，用沙漏计时。看得更久的人，回来时都有些不对劲。|暗镜在帘子后面等着。|你还没坐下，沙漏就翻过来了。讲师知道你的节奏。}{final_year: 一季之后你已经不在这里了，这让那面镜子更难看下去。|{~窗帘拉着，挡住了早晨。|你前面那个人出来时一声不吭。}}\n# en: A student staring into a dark mirror at the centre of a curtained room, a lecturer standing close with an hourglass.\nscene [3ee78e] = 拉着帘子的房间中央，一个学生凝视着一面暗色的镜子，讲师拿着沙漏站在近旁。\n# en: Look a season ahead\nopt.left.label [39e11f] = 看一季之后\n# en: {~You see a lit window and a cup going cold, and you know exactly where and when it will be. You keep it to yourself.|You see something small and true. You'll remember it when it happens.}\nopt.left.say.1 [756d9b] = {~你看见一扇亮着的窗和一杯渐凉的茶，清楚地知道那会在何时何地。你没告诉任何人。|你看见一件小而真实的事。等它发生时你会想起来。}\n# en: {~The mirror shows you too much at once. You come out with a nosebleed and a headache that lasts till dinner.|You see nothing, and then you see everything.}\nopt.left.say.2 [9506d2] = {~镜子一下子给你看了太多。你出来时流着鼻血，头疼到晚饭。|你什么也没看见，然后看见了一切。}\n# en: Look only as far as tomorrow\nopt.right.label [9c5502] = 只看到明天\n# en: {~Tomorrow, says the mirror, it will rain at noon. It does. It's not much, but it's right.|You look only one day ahead. Lunch will be soup.} {gpa >= 3: The lecturer says caution is a talent too.|It's safe, and it feels small.}\nopt.right.say.1 [6015ed] = {~镜子说，明天正午会下雨。真下了。不算什么，但它是对的。|你只往前看了一天。午饭是汤。}{gpa >= 3: 讲师说谨慎也是一种天赋。|稳妥，却显得渺小。}\n\n@event alch_stir_count\n# en: Clockwise\ntitle [255eb5] = 顺时针\n# en: {Alchemy I is mostly stirring, and the stirring is counted aloud by the whole class, in unison, like a choir with spoons.|The chorus is getting good. Someone has started harmonising.|You count stirs in your sleep now, and sometimes on the stairs.} {~Today's draught is a simple tonic.|Today's draught is meant to turn blue at forty.|Today the lecturer keeps changing the count to see who's listening.}\ntext [e8bfc8] = {炼金与治愈 I 大半是搅拌，全班齐声数着搅拌的次数，像一支拿勺子的合唱团。|合唱越来越齐了，已经有人开始唱和声。|如今你睡着了都在数搅拌，有时走楼梯也在数。}{~今天的药剂是一种简单的补剂。|今天的药剂应该在第四十下时变蓝。|今天讲师不停地改拍子，看谁在认真听。}\n# en: A laboratory of identical cauldrons, a whole class stirring in unison while the lecturer counts aloud from the front.\nscene [ddbff8] = 一间摆满同样坩埚的实验室，全班整齐划一地搅拌，讲师在前面大声数数。\n# en: Count every stir aloud\nopt.left.label [363802] = 每一下都数出声\n# en: {~By the hundredth stir your voice is gone and your tonic is perfect.|Your count is right and your draught is dull. Both are what was asked.} {month == 4 or month == 10: The exam recipes come back to you in rhythm.|Your wrist aches.}\nopt.left.say.1 [5f0ad2] = {~搅到第一百下，你的嗓子哑了，补剂完美无缺。|你数得一下不差，药剂平平无奇。这两样都正是要求的。}{month == 4 or month == 10: 考试要背的配方随着节拍回到了你脑子里。|你的手腕酸了。}\n# en: Stir by the colour instead\nopt.right.label [b579ed] = 改看颜色来搅\n# en: {~You stop counting and watch the colour. It turns blue exactly when you expect it to.|You lose the count and keep the draught. The lecturer can tell, and allows it.}\nopt.right.say.1 [e21f75] = {~你不再数数，只盯着颜色。它正好在你预料的那一刻变蓝。|你数乱了，药剂却保住了。讲师看得出来，也由着你。}\n# en: {~You watch the colour too closely and miss it changing. The fumes make your eyes water.|Your draught turns a colour the lecturer has never seen. It's not a good sign.}\nopt.right.say.2 [170c75] = {~你盯颜色盯得太紧，反倒错过了它变色的那一刻。烟熏得你眼泪直流。|你的药剂变成了讲师从没见过的颜色。这可不是好兆头。}\n\n@event alch_chopping_board\n# en: Prep Work\ntitle [41ffcb] = 备料\n# en: {Half of alchemy, says the lecturer, is cutting things up correctly. The ingredients come from Pell & Daughters in the village, packed in straw, every one labelled in a careful hand.|The crate from the village is heavier today.|Your knife knows the way, and your thumb knows where not to be.} {~Today it's roots, to be sliced paper-thin.|Today it's beetles, to be crushed and not thought about.|Today it's berries, which stain everything.}\ntext [c17aac] = {讲师说，炼金有一半功夫在于把东西切对。材料来自村里的佩尔父女药铺，用稻草包着，每一样都贴着字迹工整的标签。|今天从村里运来的箱子更沉。|你的刀认得路，你的拇指也知道该躲在哪儿。}{~今天是根茎，要切得薄如纸。|今天是甲虫，要碾碎，而且别多想。|今天是浆果，什么都会被染色。}\n# en: A long scrubbed bench heaped with roots, berries and dried beetles, students bent over chopping boards with small sharp knives.\nscene [89c924] = 一条擦洗干净的长桌上堆满根茎、浆果和干甲虫，学生们俯身在砧板前，握着锋利的小刀。\n# en: Prep for the whole bench\nopt.left.label [65b98b] = 替整张桌备料\n# en: {~You cut enough for six. Your bench-mates bring you tea, which is fair.|You chop for an hour. The roots are perfect and your fingers smell of them till dinner.} {season == \"autumn\": The berries are fresh this time of year, and it's almost pleasant.|The crate is empty when you're done.}\nopt.left.say.1 [63a8e5] = {~你切了六个人的份。同桌给你端来了茶，这很公平。|你切了一个钟头。根茎切得完美，你的手指一直到晚饭都是那股味道。}{season == \"autumn\": 这个时节浆果新鲜，切起来几乎算得上愉快。|你切完时，箱子空了。}\n# en: Prep yours, read ahead\nopt.right.label [da51d3] = 切完自己的就预习\n# en: {~You chop quickly and read the next recipe. You nick your thumb on the second root.|You rush the beetles and get to the reading, plus a small cut you'll be explaining all day.}\nopt.right.say.1 [71bc2e] = {~你飞快切完，去读下一道配方。切第二根根茎时划破了拇指。|你匆匆碾完甲虫赶去预习，外加一道小伤口，得解释一整天。}\n\n@event alch_bandage_round\n# en: Bandage Round\ntitle [f4fd11] = 绷带值班\n# en: {The healing half of Alchemy I is taught in the infirmary, where Sister Amaranth has the class roll bandages and watch her work.|Sister Amaranth inspects every roll, and unrolls one of yours.|Sister Amaranth hands you a roll without looking up.} {~A third-year is in with a burnt hand from Evocation.|A first-year is in with a cold and a great deal to say about it.|The ward is empty, which Sister Amaranth calls a miracle.}\ntext [9642df] = {炼金与治愈 I 的治愈部分在医务室上，阿玛兰斯修女让全班卷绷带，看她做事。|阿玛兰斯修女每一卷都要检查，还拆开了你的一卷。|阿玛兰斯修女头也不抬地递给你一卷绷带。}{~一个三年级生上塑能术烧伤了手，正在这儿。|一个一年级生感冒了，而且对此有很多话要说。|病房空着，阿玛兰斯修女称之为奇迹。}\n# en: A sunny infirmary ward where students roll bandages at a long table while a nun in a starched apron checks their work.\nscene [fb0ff4] = 阳光明亮的医务室病房里，学生们在长桌边卷绷带，一位系着浆硬围裙的修女检查他们的活儿。\n# en: Roll bandages and watch\nopt.left.label [216947] = 卷绷带，在旁观察\n# en: {~You roll, and watch Sister Amaranth clean a cut in about three movements.|Your bandages pass inspection.} {season == \"winter\": The ward fills with sniffling students all morning.|She says nothing, which is high praise.}\nopt.left.say.1 [702c62] = {~你一边卷，一边看阿玛兰斯修女三两下就清理好一道伤口。|你卷的绷带通过了检查。}{season == \"winter\": 一上午，病房里挤满了吸鼻子的学生。|她什么也没说，这已是很高的评价。}\n# en: Offer to treat a patient\nopt.right.label [e0bf94] = 主动接诊一位病人\n# en: {~You clean and bind a scraped knee neatly. The patient doesn't even wince. Sister Amaranth adjusts one knot.|You treat the burn properly. Sister Amaranth says, \"Hm.\" From her, that is a speech.}\nopt.right.say.1 [506da4] = {~你把擦伤的膝盖清理包扎得干净利落。病人连眉头都没皱。阿玛兰斯修女只调了一个结。|你把烧伤处理得很妥当。阿玛兰斯修女说了声“嗯”。从她嘴里出来，这就算一篇演讲了。}\n# en: {~You bandage the wrong knee. The patient is too polite to say so; Sister Amaranth is not.|Your bandage unrolls itself and the patient has to wait while you start again.}\nopt.right.say.2 [51f5a0] = {~你包错了膝盖。病人太客气没说，阿玛兰斯修女可不客气。|你的绷带自己散开了，病人只好等你从头再来。}\n\n@event alch_purify_samples\n# en: Lake Water Samples\ntitle [468eaa] = 湖水样本\n# en: {Alchemy II hands out jars of murky lake water, to be made fit to drink. The lecturer has a habit of drinking the results.|The lecturer looks thirsty, which is never a good sign.|The lecturer has brought a favourite cup for this, chipped at the rim.} {~Your jar has a small weed in it.|Your jar is suspiciously green.|Your jar has a tiny snail in it, which you set free.}\ntext [f54094] = {炼金与治愈 II 发下一罐罐浑浊的湖水，要处理到能喝。讲师有个习惯，总要亲口喝下成果。|讲师看上去渴了，这从来不是好兆头。|讲师为此带来了一只心爱的杯子，杯沿有个缺口。}{~你的罐子里有一根小水草。|你的罐子绿得可疑。|你的罐子里有只小蜗牛，你把它放生了。}\n# en: A bench lined with jars of murky lake water, a lecturer at the end raising one now-clear jar to drink it.\nscene [3c5465] = 实验桌上摆着一排浑浊湖水的罐子，桌尾的讲师正举起一罐已变清澈的水要喝。\n# en: Purify it thoroughly\nopt.left.label [be5a08] = 彻底净化\n# en: {~Your water comes out clear and sweet. The lecturer drinks it, pauses and nods.|Clean as rainwater.} {The lecturer drinks a second glass, which nobody has seen before.|The lecturer drinks a second glass, and pretends not to.|The lecturer now holds out the cup for yours first.}\nopt.left.say.1 [c0d4c0] = {~你的水出来清澈甘甜。讲师喝了，顿了一下，点点头。|干净得像雨水。}{讲师又喝了第二杯，这可是谁都没见过的。|讲师喝了第二杯，还假装没有。|如今讲师总是先把杯子伸向你的那罐。}\n# en: {~You test it first, just a sip, as the lecturer always does. You spend the afternoon close to the washroom.|Your water clears, then clouds again as you lift it.}\nopt.left.say.2 [0ff9bd] = {~你学讲师的样子先抿了一口试试。整个下午你都待在离盥洗室不远的地方。|你的水清了，你一举起来它又浑了。}\n# en: Filter it the slow way\nopt.right.label [425b53] = 用老办法慢慢滤\n# en: {~Cloth, sand and charcoal, the old way. It takes the whole lesson.|You filter it by hand and write every step down. The lecturer drinks it and says, \"Patient.\"} {final_year: You'll be filtering water like this for the rest of your life, probably.|Your hands are grey with charcoal.}\nopt.right.say.1 [468558] = {~布、沙、木炭，老办法。花了整整一节课。|你亲手过滤，把每一步都记了下来。讲师喝了，说：“有耐心。”}{final_year: 这辈子你大概都会这样滤水了。|你的手被木炭染得灰扑扑的。}\n\n@event alch_long_simmer\n# en: The Long Simmer\ntitle [191077] = 漫长的文火\n# en: {Some potions simmer for hours and must be watched. Whoever is on watch when the colour turns gets the credit, or the blame.|The class takes turns at the cauldron, and the rota has come round.|Your turn at the simmer. You've brought a cushion this time.} {~The rest of the class has gone to lunch.|The potion is making a noise like a contented cat.|The lecturer has left a note that says \"Do not leave it.\"}\ntext [f9f568] = {有些药剂要文火熬上好几个钟头，得有人看着。颜色变的那一刻谁在值守，功劳或黑锅就归谁。|全班轮流守坩埚，值日表又轮回来了。|轮到你守文火了。这回你带了个坐垫。}{~其他人都去吃午饭了。|药剂发出的声音像只心满意足的猫。|讲师留了张字条，上面写着“别走开”。}\n# en: A bubbling cauldron in an empty laboratory, a single student slumped on a stool beside it with an hourglass and a book.\nscene [d188f1] = 空荡荡的实验室里，一口坩埚咕嘟冒泡，旁边一个学生瘫坐在凳子上，手边一只沙漏、一本书。\n# en: Sit the whole watch\nopt.left.label [85d1b0] = 守满整班\n# en: {~Three hours, one cauldron, one hourglass. You see it turn and catch it at the right moment.|You read the same paragraph forty times and stir at every bubble.} {season == \"summer\": The laboratory is stifling.|By the end, you can tell its mood from the bubbles.}\nopt.left.say.1 [6d77e0] = {~三个钟头，一口坩埚，一只沙漏。你看着它变色，在恰好的时刻接住了。|同一段话你读了四十遍，每冒一个泡就搅一下。}{season == \"summer\": 实验室闷得透不过气。|到最后，你能从气泡听出它的心情。}\n# en: Set a charm to watch it\nopt.right.label [5a82a4] = 设个咒语替你看\n# en: {~Your charm rings like a bell when the colour turns. You come back from lunch in time to take it off the fire.|The charm works and you get to eat.}\nopt.right.say.1 [e8ee44] = {~颜色一变，你的咒语就像铃铛一样响了。你吃完午饭回来，正好把它端下火。|咒语管用，你也吃上了饭。}\n# en: {~Your charm falls asleep. The potion boils over and you breathe in a lot of steam getting it off the fire.|The charm rings for no reason, three times, and the fourth time you ignore it.}\nopt.right.say.2 [8a8a14] = {~你的咒语睡着了。药剂溢了出来，你把它端下火时吸了一大口蒸汽。|咒语无缘无故响了三次，第四次你没理它。}\n\n@event alch_volatile_reagents\n# en: Volatile Reagents\ntitle [2e9938] = 不稳定的试剂\n# en: {Advanced Alchemy handles reagents that react to being looked at the wrong way. The lecturer has one rule. One person speaks at a time, and that person is the one holding the vial.|Nobody is speaking. Somebody is breathing too loudly.|You've stopped holding your breath, mostly.} {~Today's vial is pale yellow and fizzing.|Today's vial is dark red and perfectly still, which is worse.|Today's vial is labelled in the lecturer's hand: \"Gently.\"}\ntext [d543ef] = {高等炼金学处理的试剂，被人看错一眼都会起反应。讲师只有一条规矩：一次只许一个人说话，那个人就是拿着药瓶的人。|没人说话。有人喘气声太大了。|你已经不再屏住呼吸了，大体上。}{~今天的药瓶是淡黄色的，滋滋冒泡。|今天的药瓶是暗红色的，一动不动，这更糟。|今天的药瓶上是讲师的笔迹：“轻拿。”}\n# en: A hushed laboratory, a student holding a glass vial of glowing liquid at arm's length, everyone else very still behind a screen.\nscene [448114] = 鸦雀无声的实验室里，一个学生伸直手臂捏着一只发光的玻璃药瓶，其余人都一动不动地躲在屏风后。\n# en: Handle it yourself\nopt.left.label [733ca1] = 亲手处理\n# en: {~You measure it drop by drop, speaking only the numbers. It settles exactly as it should.|Your hands don't shake.} The lecturer lets out a breath.\nopt.left.say.1 [4f6cb5] = {~你一滴一滴地量，只开口念数字。它不偏不倚地沉静下来。|你的手一点没抖。}讲师长舒了一口气。\n# en: {~The vial pops like a cork. Your eyebrows survive; your gloves do not.|A drop too many. The flask hisses and coughs out blue smoke, and you spend the hour in the infirmary.}\nopt.left.say.2 [6049c7] = {~药瓶像软木塞一样砰地一响。你的眉毛保住了，手套没保住。|多了一滴。烧瓶嘶嘶作响，咳出一股蓝烟，你在医务室待了一个钟头。}\n# en: Pass it to the steadiest hand\nopt.right.label [699cc4] = 交给手最稳的人\n# en: {~You hand the vial to someone with steadier hands and take careful notes. It stings a little.|You watch someone else do it right. You'll know how next time.} {gpa >= 3: The lecturer says you're allowed to be cautious.|You note every step.}\nopt.right.say.1 [3566b4] = {~你把药瓶递给手更稳的人，自己仔细记笔记。心里有点刺痛。|你看着别人做对了。下次你就知道怎么做了。}{gpa >= 3: 讲师说你可以谨慎。|你记下了每一步。}\n\n@event alch_original_remedy\n# en: A Remedy of Your Own\ntitle [8095a0] = 你自己的药方\n# en: {Advanced Alchemy asks for an original remedy, invented, written up, and tested under Sister Amaranth's eye.|Another revision. The notebook has more crossings-out than words.|Your remedy is nearly finished. It has been nearly finished for weeks.} {~This version is for headaches.|This version is for the common cold, which has resisted remedies for centuries.|This version is for exam nerves, which you need.}\ntext [30102a] = {高等炼金学要求一份原创药方：自己发明、自己写成，在阿玛兰斯修女的注视下试验。|又一次修改。笔记本上划掉的比留下的还多。|你的药方快完成了。它已经“快完成了”好几个星期。}{~这一版治头疼。|这一版治普通感冒——几百年来它一直抵抗着各种药方。|这一版治考前紧张，你正需要。}\n# en: A student at a cluttered bench writing a recipe in a notebook, a small flask of their own remedy steaming beside it.\nscene [811658] = 一个学生在杂乱的实验桌前往笔记本上写配方，旁边一小瓶自制药剂冒着热气。\n# en: Test it on yourself\nopt.left.label [2dfc84] = 在自己身上试\n# en: {~It works. Your headache lifts and your mood with it.|It works better than you expected.} {Sister Amaranth writes your recipe in her own book.|Sister Amaranth adds a note to your page in her book.|Sister Amaranth's book has a whole page of yours now.}\nopt.left.say.1 [b89c43] = {~成了。头疼消了，心情也跟着好了。|效果比你预想的还好。}{阿玛兰斯修女把你的配方抄进了她自己的本子。|阿玛兰斯修女在本子里你那一页添了一条批注。|阿玛兰斯修女的本子里如今有整整一页是你的。}\n# en: {~It does something, but not what you wanted. Your ears are warm for the rest of the day.|It cures the headache and replaces it with hiccups.}\nopt.left.say.2 [d25c0f] = {~它确实起了作用，只是不是你想要的。你的耳朵热了一整天。|头疼治好了，换来了打嗝。}\n# en: Ask Sister Amaranth to review it\nopt.right.label [1828ec] = 请阿玛兰斯修女审阅\n# en: {~She crosses out three ingredients and one adjective.|She reads it twice, then hands it back. \"Sound,\" she says. \"Dull.\"} {final_year: You'll keep this recipe long after you leave Lumenhold.|It's better already, and you hate it a little.}\nopt.right.say.1 [b3c7c0] = {~她划掉了三种材料和一个形容词。|她读了两遍，递还给你。“可靠，”她说，“乏味。”}{final_year: 离开流明堡很久以后，你还会留着这张药方。|已经好多了，你却有点恨它。}\n# en: Purify the base first\nopt.up.label [568fb8] = 先净化基底\n# en: {~You draw every impurity out of the base before you start. The remedy comes out clear and gentle.|The base comes clean in one pass. Sister Amaranth smells it and says nothing, which is approval.|With a pure base, the fault in your recipe finally shows. You fix it.}\nopt.up.say.1 [8873fa] = {~动手之前，你把基底里的每一丝杂质都抽了出来。药剂清澈温和。|基底一遍就净了。阿玛兰斯修女闻了闻，什么也没说——这就是认可。|基底一纯，配方里的毛病终于显了出来。你把它改好了。}\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/pools/courses_b.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/pools/courses_b.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event illu_candle_drill\n# en: The Candle Drill\ntitle [81a84c] = 蜡烛操练\n# en: {Illusion I opens with a row of cold candles. Make them burn, Professor Vey says, without burning.|Vey's drawer of candles is open. Every one is older than you.|The candle drill. Some mornings you could do it asleep.} {~Vey passes her hand through each flame and says \"cold\" in the same flat voice.|Someone's flame leans in a draught only it can feel.|The student beside you has made a lovely candle and forgotten the flame.}\ntext [f1e680] = {幻术 I 以一排冷蜡烛开场。让它们燃烧，维伊教授说，但不许真的烧起来。|维伊那一抽屉蜡烛开着，每一根都比你年纪大。|蜡烛操练。有些早晨你闭着眼都能做。} {~维伊把手掌从每一朵火焰中穿过，用同样平板的声音说：“冷的。”|有人的火焰朝一侧倾斜，像在躲一阵只有它感觉得到的穿堂风。|你旁边的同学造出了一根漂亮的蜡烛，却忘了火焰。}\n# en: A row of unlit candles on a lecture bench, each crowned with a flame that casts no shadow, a professor holding her palm over one.\nscene [f627aa] = 讲台长桌上一排未点燃的蜡烛，每根都顶着一朵不投影子的火焰，一位教授把手掌悬在其中一朵上方。\n# en: Hold the flame until it's right\nopt.left.label [6ee431] = 守着火焰直到对为止\n# en: {~Your flame steadies, then flickers in time with your breathing, which Vey says is the point.|Vey's palm hovers over your flame a moment longer than the others. \"Almost warm,\" she says. From her, that's a sonnet.|By the bell your flame has a proper blue heart. You feel oddly proud of something that isn't there.}\nopt.left.say.1 [30d205] = {~你的火焰稳住了，随后跟着你的呼吸一起闪烁，维伊说这正是要点。|维伊的手掌在你的火焰上方比别人多停了一会儿。“几乎是暖的。”她说。出自她口，这简直是一首十四行诗。|下课铃响时，你的火焰有了一颗蓝色的芯。你为一样根本不存在的东西感到莫名骄傲。}\n# en: Make it gutter and smoke\nopt.right.label [5f6b6b] = 让它摇曳冒烟\n# en: {~You add a thread of smoke and a little gutter. The student behind you reaches to pinch it out.|Your candle drips wax that isn't there onto a desk that is. Vey watches it fall and says nothing, which means yes.}\nopt.right.say.1 [87d70b] = {~你添上一缕烟和一点摇曳。后排的同学伸手想把它掐灭。|你的蜡烛把不存在的蜡滴在真实的桌面上。维伊看着它落下，一言不发，意思是行。}\n# en: {~The smoke goes up and the flame goes out, as though embarrassed by its own ambition.|Vey waves a hand through the whole performance. \"A candle doesn't try this hard,\" she says.}\nopt.right.say.2 [da5b7c] = {~烟升了起来，火却灭了，像是为自己的野心难为情。|维伊一挥手穿过你的整场表演。“蜡烛不会这么卖力。”她说。}\n\n@event illu_one_small_change\n# en: One Small Change\ntitle [d7183c] = 一处小改动\n# en: {Hand mirrors all round. Glamour your own face, Vey says; change one small thing and hold it.|Out come the mirrors. A glamour you can't hold on yourself, Vey says, won't hold on anyone.|Mirror practice. You know your own face too well by now.} {~Vey says people always change their eyes first.|A second-year from Rootward is giving himself a better chin, slowly.|The mirrors fog, as they do {season == \"winter\": in the cold|when the room warms}.}\ntext [aba99d] = {人手一面小镜子。给自己的脸施魅影，维伊说；改一处小地方，并且维持住。|镜子拿出来了。维伊说，连自己身上都撑不住的魅影，在谁身上也撑不住。|镜子练习。到如今你对自己的脸太熟悉了。} {~维伊说，人们总是先改眼睛。|一个根庐的二年级生正在慢慢给自己换个更好看的下巴。|镜子起了雾，{season == \"winter\": 天冷时总是这样。|屋里一暖就会这样。}}\n# en: Students bent over little hand mirrors, one changing the colour of her own eyes, another squinting at a nose that isn't quite his.\nscene [274c55] = 学生们俯身对着小手镜，一个正在改变自己眼睛的颜色，另一个眯眼看着一只不太像自己的鼻子。\n# en: Change something trivial\nopt.left.label [1bc550] = 改点无关紧要的\n# en: {~You move a freckle half an inch. It takes the whole hour. Vey initials your page without looking up.|You darken one eyebrow and keep it dark. Dull, careful work, and the glamour holds.|A small scar vanishes and comes back, vanishes and comes back. By the end it stays gone until you let it.}\nopt.left.say.1 [1d866e] = {~你把一颗雀斑挪了半寸，花了整整一节课。维伊头也不抬地在你的纸上签了字。|你把一边眉毛描深，一直保持着。枯燥又细致的活儿，魅影稳住了。|一道小疤消失又出现，消失又出现。到最后，除非你放手，它一直不见。}\n# en: Change what you'd really change\nopt.right.label [e542b4] = 改你真正想改的\n# en: {~It holds beautifully. You look at it longer than the exercise requires, and let it go more slowly than you'd like.|Vey glances over your shoulder. \"Ah,\" she says, kindly enough. \"That one. Everybody has one.\"|You hold it all hour. Afterwards your real face seems slightly unfamiliar, like a coat you've lent out.}\nopt.right.say.1 [571e8c] = {~它维持得很美。你看它的时间比练习要求的长，放手时也比你愿意的慢。|维伊从你肩后瞥了一眼。“啊，”她说，语气还算温和，“那一处。每个人都有一处。”|你维持了整节课。之后你真正的脸显得有点陌生，像一件借出去又还回来的外套。}\n\n@event illu_spot_the_coin\n# en: Spot the Coin\ntitle [5eda6f] = 找出那枚硬币\n# en: {Partner work. {partner} lays out three crowns, and one of them is an illusion. Say which.|Three crowns from {partner}. One isn't there, and {they} look{s} pleased about it.|You and {partner} have started keeping score.} {~Vey walks the room flicking real coins, to confuse everyone.|The windows are bright and the illusions show it.|The next bench is arguing whether a coin can be \"partly fake\".}\ntext [19048f] = {搭档练习。{partner}摆出三枚克朗，其中一枚是幻象。说出是哪一枚。|{partner}摆出三枚克朗。有一枚并不存在，{they}看起来对此很得意。|你和{partner}已经开始记分了。} {~维伊在教室里走动，往空中弹真硬币，存心把大家搅糊涂。|窗外很亮，幻象在光里露了馅。|隔壁桌在争论一枚硬币能不能“部分是假的”。}\n# en: Two students at a bench with three coins between them, one coin shimmering very slightly at the edge.\nscene [056f6c] = 两个学生对坐在长桌旁，中间放着三枚硬币，其中一枚边缘微微闪烁。\n# en: Study the coins honestly\nopt.left.label [1297d9] = 老老实实看硬币\n# en: {~It's the one with no tarnish. Illusions always come out cleaner than money does.|You stare until your eyes water and pick the middle one. Wrong. {partner} pockets the real two, laughing.|You get it on the third try, by the shine that doesn't move when the light does.}\nopt.left.say.1 [996a5b] = {~是没有锈迹的那枚。幻象总比真钱干净。|你盯到眼睛发酸，选了中间那枚。错了。{partner}笑着把两枚真的收进口袋。|第三次你猜对了，凭的是那道不随光线移动的反光。}\n# en: Watch their face, not the coins\nopt.right.label [8b4837] = 看对方的脸，不看硬币\n# en: {~{partner} glances at the left coin a fraction too fondly. You point. {They} groan{s}.|You keep smiling until {partner} gives it away by smiling back. Vey notes that this isn't the exercise, and that it worked.}\nopt.right.say.1 [999726] = {~{partner}瞄向左边那枚的眼神稍稍太温柔了。你一指。{they}哀叹一声。|你一直笑，直到{partner}回了你一个笑，露了底。维伊指出这不是练习的内容，也指出它奏效了。}\n# en: {~{partner} has a better face than you'd thought. Your wrong guess buys {them} a cup of tea.|You watch {partner} so hard you miss the coin blinking out of existence entirely.}\nopt.right.say.2 [d89d14] = {~{partner}的表情管理比你想的好。你猜错的代价是请{them}喝一杯茶。|你盯{partner}盯得太用力，完全没看见那枚硬币凭空消失。}\n\n@event illu_bread_smell\n# en: The Smell of Bread\ntitle [9ea386] = 面包的香味\n# en: {Illusion II adds the other senses. Today, a loaf that looks, smells and sounds fresh when broken.|More bread. Sight is easy, Vey says; smell is where liars are caught.|The bread lesson. The floor smells of loaves that never existed.} {~Somebody's loaf smells strongly of wet dog.|One loaf crackles beautifully and smells of nothing.|{gpa >= 3: Vey asks you to go first.|Vey asks you to go last, which feels pointed.}}\ntext [cf29c4] = {幻术 II 加入了其他感官。今天的题目是一条看起来、闻起来、掰开时听起来都新鲜的面包。|面包课还没完。视觉容易，维伊说；骗子都栽在气味上。|面包课。整层楼都弥漫着从未存在过的面包的味道。} {~有人的面包浓浓一股湿狗味。|有一条面包咔嚓作响，却什么味道也没有。|{gpa >= 3: 维伊让你第一个来。|维伊让你最后一个来，像是意有所指。}}\n# en: A lecture bench with an illusory loaf of bread on a board, steam rising from it, and several students sniffing it doubtfully.\nscene [92b758] = 讲台长桌上，木板上放着一条幻象面包，正冒着热气，几个学生半信半疑地凑上去闻。\n# en: Get the smell right\nopt.left.label [721e15] = 把香味做对\n# en: {~You spend an hour on the smell of a crust. By the end it's nearly convincing and you're desperately hungry.|You think of a bakery on a cold street and the smell arrives with it. Vey sniffs, and nods once.|The smell is right. The sound of breaking is wrong. Vey says that's the usual order.}\nopt.left.say.1 [1d3997] = {~你花了一小时琢磨面包皮的味道。到最后几乎以假乱真，而你饿得发慌。|你想起寒冷街角的一家面包房，香味便随之而来。维伊嗅了嗅，点了一下头。|香味对了，掰开的声音不对。维伊说这是常见的顺序。}\n# en: Add the warmth of the oven\nopt.right.label [111260] = 加上烤炉的温度\n# en: {~Vey picks your loaf up and puts it down hastily, as if it had burnt her. The class actually applauds.|The bench beside you swears the room got warmer. It didn't. That's the point.}\nopt.right.say.1 [6871aa] = {~维伊拿起你的面包，又匆忙放下，像被烫着了。全班真的鼓起了掌。|旁边那桌发誓屋里变暖了。并没有。这正是重点。}\n# en: {~The warmth arrives and the loaf leaves. You're left holding a hot, empty board.|Your bread steams, then smokes, then smells of a cupboard. Vey writes something short.}\nopt.right.say.2 [ce76eb] = {~温度来了，面包走了。你手里只剩一块发烫的空木板。|你的面包冒气，然后冒烟，然后有股橱柜味。维伊写下了很短的一句评语。}\n\n@event illu_find_vey\n# en: Find the Professor\ntitle [4d9a54] = 找出教授\n# en: {Professor Vey is never at the lectern in Illusion II. She is somewhere in the room, wearing someone else, and the first to name her wins.|Vey's chair is empty. Everyone eyes the porter with the mop.|The lectern is empty. You trust nothing in this room.} {~There's a pigeon on the sill.|A student nobody remembers has taken the back row.|One of the portraits seems unusually attentive.}\ntext [2c9291] = {幻术 II 的课上，维伊教授从不站在讲台后。她就在教室某处，扮成别人，第一个叫出她的人获胜。|维伊的椅子空着。大家都盯着那个拿拖把的杂役。|讲台空着。这间屋里你什么都不信了。} {~窗台上有只鸽子。|后排坐着一个谁也不记得的学生。|有幅肖像画看起来格外专注。}\n# en: A lecture hall where the professor's chair is empty and everyone is looking suspiciously at everyone else, including a pigeon on the sill.\nscene [f6ad02] = 一间讲堂，教授的椅子空着，每个人都狐疑地打量着别人，包括窗台上的一只鸽子。\n# en: Watch everyone, all lesson\nopt.left.label [b92af8] = 整节课盯着每个人\n# en: {~You don't find her, but you notice who blinks late. Vey turns out to have been the pigeon.|You learn three classmates' nervous habits and nothing about Vey, who was the chair.|At the end she steps out of the porter's coat. You'd suspected the mop. The mop was real.}\nopt.left.say.1 [dcd4ba] = {~你没找到她，但留意到了谁眨眼慢半拍。原来维伊是那只鸽子。|你摸清了三个同学的紧张小动作，对维伊却一无所知——她是那把椅子。|下课时她从杂役的外套里走了出来。你怀疑过拖把。拖把是真的。}\n# en: Name her early\nopt.right.label [cf9752] = 抢先叫出她\n# en: {~\"Professor,\" you say to the new student in the back row. The glamour falls away like a dropped shawl. Vey looks almost pleased.|You name her before the bell has finished ringing. She says, \"Hm,\" which from Vey means a great deal.}\nopt.right.say.1 [f94377] = {~“教授。”你对后排那个新面孔说。魅影像一条滑落的披肩般褪去。维伊看上去几乎是满意的。|铃声还没响完，你就叫出了她。她说了声“嗯”，从维伊嘴里说出来，分量很重。}\n# en: {~You accuse a perfectly ordinary classmate. He will remember it. So will Vey, who was standing behind you.|You point at the portrait. The portrait points back. Vey, at the lectern after all, makes a note.}\nopt.right.say.2 [f4d393] = {~你指认了一个再普通不过的同学。他会记住这件事。维伊也会——她就站在你身后。|你指向那幅肖像。肖像也指向你。维伊原来就在讲台后，记了一笔。}\n\n@event illu_courtyard_hour\n# en: Holding the Courtyard\ntitle [9a561c] = 撑住庭院\n# en: {Advanced Illusion leaves the classroom. Hold an illusion over the busy courtyard for an hour, and don't let it fray.|Another courtyard hour. The porters step round you now.|The courtyard. {final_year: One of the last times you'll hold this sky.|Your hands know the weight of it.}} {~A flock of swallows today.|Rain that doesn't wet anyone.|{season == \"winter\": Snow that never lands.|{season == \"spring\": Blossom that never lands.|{season == \"autumn\": Leaves that never land.|Thistledown that never lands.}}}}\ntext [92dd07] = {高等幻术走出了教室。在热闹的庭院上空维持一个幻象一小时，别让它散开。|庭院一小时，照旧。杂役们现在会绕着你走。|庭院。{final_year: 这是你最后几次撑起这片天空了。|你的双手已熟悉它的分量。}} {~今天是一群燕子。|淋不湿任何人的雨。|{season == \"winter\": 永远落不了地的雪。|{season == \"spring\": 永远落不了地的花瓣。|{season == \"autumn\": 永远落不了地的落叶。|永远落不了地的蓟花绒。}}}}\n# en: A student standing very still in a busy courtyard, sweat on their brow, while a flock of illusory swallows circles above the crowd.\nscene [84d039] = 一个学生一动不动地站在热闹的庭院里，额上冒汗，一群幻象燕子在人群上方盘旋。\n# en: Hold it the full hour\nopt.left.label [f25e9f] = 撑满一整个小时\n# en: {~By the end your head rings like a struck bell. Nobody looked up once, which is how you know it worked.|A first-year holds out a hand to catch something you made. You keep it falling for her until the bell.|You hold it, then sit on the fountain's edge for longer than you'd like anyone to see.}\nopt.left.say.1 [29dab8] = {~结束时你的脑袋嗡嗡作响，像被敲过的钟。没有一个人抬头看过，这说明你成功了。|一个一年级生伸手去接你造出来的东西。你一直让它为她飘落，直到下课铃响。|你撑住了，然后在喷泉边坐了好一会儿，比你愿意让人看见的更久。}\n# en: Keep it small and steady\nopt.right.label [e4edc7] = 做小一点，求稳\n# en: {~One swallow, perfect, for an hour. Vey says ambition is cheap and an hour is not.|You shrink it to a patch of weather over one bench. It never frays, and nobody notices, which is the point and the pity.}\nopt.right.say.1 [9a1251] = {~一只燕子，完美无瑕，整整一小时。维伊说野心廉价，一小时可不廉价。|你把它缩成一张长椅上空的一小片天气。它始终没散，也没人注意到——这既是重点，也是遗憾。}\n\n@event illu_original_work\n# en: An Illusion of Your Own\ntitle [61c895] = 你自己的幻象\n# en: {Original work, an illusion nobody taught you. Vey asks one question of each piece. \"What does it make people feel?\"|More original work. Vey's question hasn't changed; your answers get longer.|Studio morning. {final_year: Next year, Vey says, she'll ask someone else.|Vey goes round the room asking her question.}} {~A classmate has made a door that sighs.|Someone has made the smell of rain on hot stone.|Someone's illusion is humming.}\ntext [1f71a3] = {原创作业，一个没人教过你的幻象。维伊对每件作品只问一个问题。“它让人感受到什么？”|原创作业继续。维伊的问题没变，你的回答越来越长。|工作室的早晨。{final_year: 明年，维伊说，她就要问别人了。|维伊在屋里挨个问她的问题。}} {~有个同学做了一扇会叹气的门。|有人做出了雨落在热石头上的气味。|有人的幻象在哼歌。}\n# en: A darkened studio room, a student alone at the centre, and around them the half-formed image of a lit window at dusk.\nscene [62326f] = 一间昏暗的工作室，一个学生独自站在中央，四周是一扇黄昏亮灯的窗户的半成形影像。\n# en: Make something true\nopt.left.label [dd57e1] = 做一件真实的东西\n# en: {~You make the window of a house you grew up near, at dusk. Vey stands in front of it a long time and writes nothing down.|You make a corridor that remembers who walked down it. It's sadder than you meant. Vey says that's usually the good sign.}\nopt.left.say.1 [3cd17f] = {~你做出了童年附近一栋房子黄昏时的窗。维伊在它前面站了很久，什么也没写。|你做了一条记得谁走过的走廊。它比你预想的更悲伤。维伊说，这通常是好兆头。}\n# en: Make something dazzling\nopt.right.label [9f8272] = 做一件耀眼的东西\n# en: {~A staircase of light climbs into the ceiling and keeps going. The class goes quiet. \"Yes,\" says Vey. \"But what does it feel like?\"|Your piece fills the room with lanterns, one for each person watching. Vey counts them, and finds one for herself.}\nopt.right.say.1 [28703f] = {~一道光之阶梯升进天花板，还在继续往上。全班安静下来。“是的，”维伊说，“可它感觉起来像什么？”|你的作品让满屋亮起灯笼，每个观看的人一盏。维伊数了数，发现有一盏是给她的。}\n# en: {~It's dazzling for a breath and then empty, like a fair after the fair has gone.|Vey watches it all, politely. \"Very bright,\" she says. You'd have preferred anything else.}\nopt.right.say.2 [34f199] = {~它耀眼了一瞬，然后空了，像集市散场后的集市。|维伊礼貌地看完了全程。“很亮。”她说。你宁愿听到任何别的评语。}\n\n@event beast_mucking_out\n# en: Mucking Out\ntitle [2efb0b] = 清理马厩\n# en: {Beastcraft I begins in the stables, with a pitchfork. Professor Brask says you don't touch a creature until you've cleaned up after it.|Pitchforks. Brask still hasn't said when that rule ends.|The stables, early. You know which stall to start with.} {~The hippogriff in the end stall is watching you.|Owls shift in the rafters, unimpressed.|{season == \"summer\": The heat doubles the smell.|A cold wind finds every gap.}}\ntext [a19b44] = {驭兽学 I 从马厩和一把干草叉开始。布拉斯克教授说，没替一只生灵收拾过，就别碰它。|干草叉。布拉斯克仍然没说这条规矩什么时候结束。|清早的马厩。你知道该从哪个隔栏开始了。} {~尽头隔栏里的鹰头马正盯着你。|猫头鹰在椽子上挪动，一脸不以为然。|{season == \"summer\": 热天让气味翻了一倍。|冷风钻进每一道缝。}}\n# en: A student with a pitchfork in a straw-strewn stable, a hippogriff watching from its stall with a look of deep judgement.\nscene [8f7a38] = 一个学生拿着干草叉站在铺满稻草的马厩里，一头鹰头马从隔栏里投来深深审视的目光。\n# en: Muck out properly\nopt.left.label [795f12] = 认真清理干净\n# en: {~Two stalls, a barrow and a blister. Brask inspects your work and calls the straw \"nearly decent\".|You finish before anyone else. The hippogriff treads straight through your clean straw, deliberately.|Brask says nothing, which you've learned is the best review she gives humans.}\nopt.left.say.1 [94cc87] = {~两个隔栏、一辆推车、一个水泡。布拉斯克检查后说这稻草“勉强像样”。|你第一个干完。鹰头马故意从你刚铺好的干净稻草上踩了过去。|布拉斯克一言不发，你已经明白这是她给人类的最高评价。}\n# en: Bow to the hippogriff first\nopt.right.label [d249bf] = 先向鹰头马鞠躬\n# en: {~You bow low and hold it. The hippogriff considers, then bows back. Brask writes your name on the board, without comment.|The hippogriff lets you near enough to see where its feathers turn to fur. You'll remember the exact line.}\nopt.right.say.1 [8dcc70] = {~你深深鞠躬并保持住。鹰头马考虑了一下，回了一礼。布拉斯克在黑板上写下你的名字，不加评语。|鹰头马让你走近到能看清它的羽毛在哪儿变成皮毛。你会记住那条分界线。}\n# en: {~You bow too fast. The hippogriff takes it as a challenge and accepts.|The hippogriff looks at your bow, then at your hat, and eats the hat.}\nopt.right.say.2 [0606d9] = {~你鞠躬太快了。鹰头马把它当成挑战，并且接受了。|鹰头马看了看你的鞠躬，又看了看你的帽子，然后吃掉了帽子。}\n\n@event beast_hover_line\n# en: The Hover Line\ntitle [253240] = 悬停队列\n# en: {Flight is half the course. Brask lines you up on the lawn to hover a foot up and hold still while she counts.|The hover line. Brask counts slowly on purpose, you're sure.|Hover line. You and your broom have an understanding, of sorts.} {~Somebody's broom keeps edging toward the lake.|An owl has landed on the end of a broom, and its rider is pretending not to notice.|Brask says, as always, that brooms are harder to reason with than griffons.}\ntext [28e657] = {飞行占这门课的一半。布拉斯克让你们在草坪上排成一行，离地一尺悬停，她数数时不许动。|悬停队列。你确信布拉斯克是故意数得这么慢。|悬停队列。你和你的扫帚已经算是达成了某种共识。} {~有人的扫帚一直往湖那边挪。|一只猫头鹰落在一把扫帚的尾端，骑手假装没注意到。|布拉斯克照例说，扫帚比狮鹫难讲道理多了。}\n# en: A line of students hovering a foot above a lawn on brooms, one broom drifting out of line, a stern professor counting slowly.\nscene [10dd11] = 一排学生骑着扫帚悬停在离草坪一尺高的地方，一把扫帚偏离了队列，一位严厉的教授正慢慢数数。\n# en: Hold still until the count\nopt.left.label [37c0d2] = 一动不动直到数完\n# en: {~You hold it until forty. Your legs remember it for a day.|The broom bucks once at thirty-one. You hold on. Brask gets to fifty and nods.|Steady all the way. Brask says it's nice to see a broom that knows who's in charge, and she means the broom.}\nopt.left.say.1 [10c9f7] = {~你撑到了四十。你的腿记了一整天。|数到三十一时扫帚猛地一颠。你抓住了。布拉斯克数到五十，点了点头。|全程稳稳当当。布拉斯克说很高兴看见一把知道谁说了算的扫帚——她指的是扫帚。}\n# en: Study how the others fall off\nopt.right.label [4c91b6] = 研究别人怎么摔下来\n# en: {~You stand at the end and watch. You learn a great deal about balance, and nobody lets you forget you didn't fly.|You take notes on every wobble. Brask reads them and says you should consider a career in inquests.}\nopt.right.say.1 [4219b3] = {~你站在队尾观察。你学到了很多关于平衡的东西，也没人让你忘记你没飞。|你把每一次摇晃都记了下来。布拉斯克读完说你该考虑去当验尸官。}\n\n@event beast_feeding_round\n# en: Feeding Round\ntitle [d6808f] = 喂食巡回\n# en: {Feeding round, in pairs. You and {partner} have two buckets and a list, and the griffon chick is first on it.|Feeding round with {partner}. The griffon chick has heard the buckets.|You and {partner} could do the round blindfolded now. The chick would still sulk.} {~Brask has written \"DO NOT HURRY\" at the top of the list.|The owls lean down from the rafters to watch.|{partner} is humming to the buckets.}\ntext [3e7e09] = {两人一组喂食。你和{partner}有两只桶和一张清单，狮鹫雏鸟排在第一个。|和{partner}一起喂食。狮鹫雏鸟已经听见了桶的声音。|你和{partner}现在蒙着眼都能走完一圈。雏鸟照样会闹脾气。} {~布拉斯克在清单顶上写着“不许着急”。|猫头鹰从椽子上探下身来看。|{partner}在对着桶哼歌。}\n# en: Two students carrying buckets between stalls, a sulky griffon chick in a nest of straw, owls leaning down from the rafters.\nscene [28f709] = 两个学生提着桶在隔栏间穿行，一只闹脾气的狮鹫雏鸟窝在草堆里，猫头鹰从椽子上探下身来。\n# en: Take the griffon chick\nopt.left.label [2348a0] = 负责狮鹫雏鸟\n# en: {~The chick eats from the bucket, then from your hand, then tries your sleeve. {partner} applauds quietly.|It sulks, then eats, then sulks about having eaten. Brask says that's progress for a griffon.}\nopt.left.say.1 [8826a6] = {~雏鸟先从桶里吃，再从你手里吃，然后尝了尝你的袖子。{partner}轻轻鼓掌。|它闹脾气，然后吃，然后为自己吃了而闹脾气。布拉斯克说对狮鹫来说这就是进步。}\n# en: {~The chick bites the bucket, then the hand holding it. {partner} finishes the round alone, kindly.|It knocks the bucket over and sits in it, glaring. You spend the hour fishing out its breakfast.}\nopt.left.say.2 [965485] = {~雏鸟咬了桶，接着咬了提桶的手。{partner}好心地独自喂完了一圈。|它把桶打翻，坐在里面怒目而视。你花了一小时把它的早饭捞出来。}\n# en: Take the owls instead\nopt.right.label [40efde] = 改去喂猫头鹰\n# en: {~You learn the owls' names from the tags on their perches, and what a pellet is, in detail.|Up and down the ladder for an hour. {partner} survives the chick and is grateful in a quiet way.|The owls take their mice with great ceremony. One of them seems to be counting yours.}\nopt.right.say.1 [71f29c] = {~你从栖木上的名牌认全了猫头鹰的名字，还详细了解了什么是食丸。|在梯子上爬上爬下一小时。{partner}熬过了雏鸟那一关，默默地心怀感激。|猫头鹰们隆重地接过各自的老鼠。其中一只似乎在数你手里的。}\n\n@event beast_hare_paddock\n# en: Running with Hares\ntitle [07bbef] = 与野兔同跑\n# en: {Beastcraft II teaches haste the old way. Run with the hares until their speed rubs off. \"Borrow it,\" Brask says. \"Don't steal it.\"|The hare paddock. The hares remember you.|Hares. You dream in sudden right angles now.} {~The grass is wet and the hares don't care.|Brask times you with a crooked hourglass.|{season == \"summer\": It's hot and the hares are lazy.|The paddock smells of rain.}}\ntext [334199] = {驭兽学 II 用老办法教疾速：跟着野兔跑，直到它们的速度沾到你身上。“借，”布拉斯克说，“别偷。”|野兔围场。野兔们记得你。|野兔。你现在连做梦都是突然的直角转弯。} {~草是湿的，野兔不在乎。|布拉斯克用一只歪斜的沙漏给你计时。|{season == \"summer\": 天很热，野兔很懒。|围场里有雨的气味。}}\n# en: Students sprinting across a paddock alongside a scattering of brown hares, one student's feet suddenly much faster than the rest of them.\nscene [853b14] = 学生们和一群棕色野兔一起冲过围场，其中一个学生的脚突然比身体其他部分快了许多。\n# en: Run until it catches\nopt.left.label [e48d98] = 跑到速度沾上身为止\n# en: {~For three strides you're faster than you've any right to be. Then you're in the hedge.|You catch it on the fourth lap, a lightness behind the knees. You pay for it with a nap you can't refuse.|The hares let you keep up for a moment, out of curiosity. It's the best moment of your week.}\nopt.left.say.1 [22e07d] = {~有三步你快得毫无道理。然后你一头扎进了树篱。|第四圈时你抓住了它，膝盖后面一阵轻盈。代价是一场你拒绝不了的午睡。|野兔们出于好奇，让你跟上了一会儿。这是你这周最好的时刻。}\n# en: Watch the hares and take notes\nopt.right.label [5a946d] = 观察野兔，记笔记\n# en: {~You sketch the way a hare turns, everything at once, no warning. Brask says your sketch is better than your running.|You note how the hares rest between bursts. Brask reads it and says, \"Yes. That's the half everyone forgets.\"}\nopt.right.say.1 [c0cb17] = {~你画下野兔转身的样子，一切同时发生，毫无预兆。布拉斯克说你画得比跑得好。|你记下野兔在两次冲刺之间如何休息。布拉斯克读完说：“对。这是大家都忘掉的那一半。”}\n\n@event beast_complaints\n# en: The Complaints Hour\ntitle [225734] = 投诉时间\n# en: {Beast-speech practice. You sit on a bucket in the stables and listen. Every creature has been waiting to complain, and the owls start with the draught.|Another hour on the bucket. The owls open, as always, with the draught.|The bucket, the owls, the draught. Brask calls it fieldwork.} {~The hippogriff wants different hay. It is very specific.|The griffon chick has a list.|{gpa >= 3: Brask leaves you alone with them, which feels like trust.|Brask stays near the door, which feels like supervision.}}\ntext [230c8b] = {兽语练习。你坐在马厩里一只倒扣的桶上倾听。每只生灵都早就等着要投诉，猫头鹰先说穿堂风。|还是那只桶，再坐一小时。猫头鹰照例先说穿堂风。|桶，猫头鹰，穿堂风。布拉斯克管这叫田野调查。} {~鹰头马想换一种干草，要求非常具体。|狮鹫雏鸟列了一张清单。|{gpa >= 3: 布拉斯克让你单独和它们待着，像是一种信任。|布拉斯克一直守在门边，像是一种监督。}}\n# en: A student sitting on an upturned bucket in a stable, surrounded by animals who all seem to be talking at once.\nscene [1a419d] = 一个学生坐在马厩里一只倒扣的桶上，周围的动物似乎都在同时说话。\n# en: Hear every complaint out\nopt.left.label [770d17] = 把每条投诉听完\n# en: {~The owls talk for forty minutes about the draught. You understand every word now, which is the trouble.|The hippogriff explains its views on hay at length. You come away fluent and exhausted.|A mouse you didn't know was there wants to be on the record. You put it on the record.}\nopt.left.say.1 [15dbc6] = {~猫头鹰就穿堂风讲了四十分钟。你现在每个字都听得懂，麻烦就在这儿。|鹰头马详尽阐述了它对干草的看法。你出来时既流利又筋疲力尽。|一只你都不知道在场的老鼠要求记录在案。你把它记录在案了。}\n# en: Fix the draught yourself\nopt.right.label [79b85b] = 亲手把漏风堵上\n# en: {~You climb into the rafters with a hammer and a plank. The owls supervise. The draught stops, and so do the complaints, briefly.|You patch the gap with straw and a board. The owls say thank you, then mention the window.}\nopt.right.say.1 [7d4c6a] = {~你带着锤子和木板爬上椽子。猫头鹰在一旁监工。风停了，投诉也短暂地停了。|你用稻草和一块木板堵上了缝。猫头鹰道了谢，然后提起了窗户。}\n\n@event beast_lake_flight\n# en: Over the Lake\ntitle [abc6c7] = 掠过湖面\n# en: {Advanced Beastcraft. A hippogriff each, over the lake, no saddle unless you ask. Brask won't think less of you for asking. She'll just remember.|The lake flight. Your hippogriff knows the way.|Over the lake. {final_year: You catch yourself memorising the view.|Your reflection keeps pace.}} {~The water is perfectly still.|A barge below, its passengers waving.|{season == \"summer\": The air is full of midges.|The wind off the water stings.}}\ntext [40b9e2] = {高等驭兽学。每人一头鹰头马，飞越湖面，除非你开口要，否则没有鞍。布拉斯克不会因为你要鞍而看轻你。她只会记住。|湖上飞行。你的鹰头马比你认路。|湖面上空。{final_year: 你发现自己在默默记住这片风景。|你的倒影一路跟着你。}} {~湖水纹丝不动。|下方有一艘驳船，乘客们在挥手。|{season == \"summer\": 空中满是蠓虫。|湖面吹来的风刺痛脸颊。}}\n# en: A student on a hippogriff's back skimming low over a mirror-still lake, their reflection racing along beneath them.\nscene [20d2f8] = 一个学生骑在鹰头马背上低低掠过镜面般平静的湖，倒影在下方飞驰。\n# en: Ride bareback, low over the water\nopt.left.label [e0de3a] = 无鞍贴着水面飞\n# en: {~You skim so low a wingtip draws a line across the lake. For one breath you and your reflection touch hands.|The hippogriff banks and you go with it, as if you'd always known how. Brask says nothing at all, then grins.}\nopt.left.say.1 [ce5922] = {~你飞得那么低，一侧翼尖在湖面划出一道线。有那么一口气的工夫，你和你的倒影碰了碰手。|鹰头马侧身转弯，你顺势而动，仿佛生来就会。布拉斯克一言不发，然后咧嘴笑了。}\n# en: {~The hippogriff dips and you don't. The lake is colder than it looks. It always is.|You slide off on the turn and hang on by a fistful of feathers, all the way back to shore.}\nopt.left.say.2 [f38fa3] = {~鹰头马往下一沉，你没有跟上。湖水比看上去冷。向来如此。|你在转弯时滑了下去，攥着一把羽毛吊了一路，直到岸边。}\n# en: Take the saddle and the high circuit\nopt.right.label [6b0bf1] = 上鞍，走高处的航线\n# en: {~Saddled, high and steady. You see all seven towers at once, and nobody sees you.|The safe circuit, twice. Brask remembers, as promised, and nods at you all the same.}\nopt.right.say.1 [d42d72] = {~上鞍，高飞，平稳。你一眼看尽七塔，却没人看见你。|安全航线，飞了两圈。布拉斯克果然记住了，但还是朝你点了点头。}\n\n@event beast_manticore_rounds\n# en: Manticore Rounds\ntitle [f18c88] = 蝎尾狮巡检\n# en: {Advanced students check the manticore once a month. Someone holds the tail; someone hums the lullaby. Brask lets you choose.|Manticore rounds. It knows the lullaby better than you do.|The manticore yawns at you, which is either a welcome or a threat.} {~Its tail twitches in its sleep.|It has been off its food, Brask says, and is in a mood.|{final_year: You wonder who'll hold the tail after you.|Brask has brought a bucket of meat and a smaller one of patience.}}\ntext [369268] = {高年级学生每月检查一次蝎尾狮。一人握住尾巴，一人哼摇篮曲。布拉斯克让你自己选。|蝎尾狮巡检。那首摇篮曲它比你还熟。|蝎尾狮冲你打了个哈欠，不知是欢迎还是威胁。} {~它的尾巴在睡梦中抽动。|布拉斯克说它最近不肯好好吃东西，正闹情绪。|{final_year: 你想知道你走后谁来握这条尾巴。|布拉斯克带来了一桶肉，和一小桶耐心。}}\n# en: A manticore lying in straw with its eyes half-closed, one student gently holding its scorpion tail while another hums nearby.\nscene [a03d57] = 一头蝎尾狮半眯着眼躺在稻草里，一个学生轻轻握着它的蝎尾，另一个在旁边哼歌。\n# en: Hold the tail\nopt.left.label [d27267] = 握住尾巴\n# en: {~You hold the tail still while Brask checks the sting. It's warm and heavy and does not, at any point, stab you.|The manticore lets you hold its tail the way a cat lets you hold a paw. Under protest, briefly, as a favour.}\nopt.left.say.1 [023479] = {~布拉斯克检查毒刺时，你稳稳握着尾巴。它温热、沉重，而且自始至终没有蜇你。|蝎尾狮让你握着它的尾巴，就像猫让你握它的爪子——不情不愿，片刻而已，算是赏脸。}\n# en: {~The tail flicks. You let go just in time, mostly. Sister Amaranth asks no questions; she's seen this course before.|It rolls over, tail and all, onto you. Nothing breaks, but you're bruised in the exact shape of a manticore.}\nopt.left.say.2 [f1c253] = {~尾巴一甩。你及时松了手，大体上及时。阿玛兰斯修女什么也没问；这门课她见多了。|它连尾巴带身子翻了过来，压在你身上。没断什么，但你身上的淤青恰好是一头蝎尾狮的形状。}\n# en: Hum the lullaby\nopt.right.label [978181] = 哼摇篮曲\n# en: {~You hum the whole lullaby twice, off-key on the high bit. The manticore sleeps through its own check-up.|You hum. A classmate holds the tail. Brask says the humming is the harder job, and nobody believes her.}\nopt.right.say.1 [daae1c] = {~你把整首摇篮曲哼了两遍，高音那段跑了调。蝎尾狮在自己的体检中一觉到底。|你哼歌，一个同学握尾巴。布拉斯克说哼歌才是更难的活儿，没人信她。}\n\n@event umbral_dim_lamps\n# en: Reading by Shadow\ntitle [1839c4] = 借影识物\n# en: {Umbral Theory I, top of the tower, lamps low on purpose. Name what's under each cloth by its shadow alone.|The cloths are back. The lamps are as low as ever.|Shadow reading. The dark no longer seems strange, which the professor says is the first lesson.} {~One shadow is slightly larger than its object.|The professor moves the lamp an inch, and every answer changes.|{season == \"summer\": It's warm outside, never up here.|Rain on the windows makes the shadows shiver.}}\ntext [cce932] = {暗影理论 I，塔顶，灯故意调得很暗。只凭影子说出每块布下面是什么。|布又盖回桌上了。灯和往常一样暗。|读影。黑暗不再显得陌生——教授说，这就是第一课。} {~有一道影子比它的物体略大一点。|教授把灯挪了一寸，每个答案都变了。|{season == \"summer\": 外面很暖，这上面从来不暖。|窗上的雨让影子瑟瑟发抖。}}\n# en: A dim lecture room at the top of a tower, objects hidden under cloths on a table, their shadows thrown long on the wall behind.\nscene [3e8e65] = 塔顶一间昏暗的教室，桌上的物件盖着布，它们的影子长长地投在身后的墙上。\n# en: Read them patiently\nopt.left.label [21c0ff] = 耐心地读影子\n# en: {~A teapot, a skull, a folded letter addressed to you. You open it later. It's blank.|You name every shadow but one. Under that cloth there is nothing at all. The professor doesn't explain.|Slowly, you get them all. The shadows told you, the professor says; you only had to stop interrupting.}\nopt.left.say.1 [878dca] = {~一把茶壶，一个头骨，一封写给你的折起来的信。你后来拆开，是空白的。|你认出了所有影子，只有一个例外。那块布下面什么也没有。教授没有解释。|慢慢地，你全认出来了。是影子告诉你的，教授说；你只是不再打断它们。}\n# en: Slip in a false shadow\nopt.right.label [131d22] = 混进一道假影子\n# en: {~You add the shadow of a cat to the wall. Three classmates write \"cat\". The professor writes your name, and underlines it.|Your false shadow passes all morning. The professor only catches it when it doesn't move with the lamp.}\nopt.right.say.1 [fbefe5] = {~你在墙上添了一道猫的影子。三个同学写下“猫”。教授写下你的名字，还划了线。|你的假影子撑过了整个上午。直到它没跟着灯动，教授才发现。}\n# en: {~Your false shadow points the wrong way. The professor turns it round gently, like straightening a picture.|Your shadow-cat sits up and leaves. It wasn't meant to do that. The professor doesn't seem surprised.}\nopt.right.say.2 [b03ff1] = {~你的假影子朝向反了。教授轻轻把它转了回来，像扶正一幅画。|你的影子猫坐起来，走了。它本不该这样。教授似乎并不意外。}\n\n@event umbral_chalk_ward\n# en: Pale Shells\ntitle [003c50] = 苍白的壳\n# en: {Ward drill. Each student raises a pale shell, and the professor walks the room flicking chalk at them. Her aim is excellent.|The professor has a new box of chalk.|The ward drill. Most days your shell is up before the chalk leaves her hand.} {~The front row has chalk in their hair.|Someone's ward is shaped like an apology.|{gpa >= 3: She aims at you first, out of respect.|She aims at you first, out of curiosity.}}\ntext [0e430a] = {护界操练。每个学生撑起一层苍白的壳，教授在教室里边走边朝你们弹粉笔。她准头极好。|教授换了一盒新粉笔。|护界操练。大多数日子，粉笔还没离手，你的壳就已撑起。} {~前排的人头发里全是粉笔灰。|有人的护界形状像一句道歉。|{gpa >= 3: 她第一个瞄准你，出于尊重。|她第一个瞄准你，出于好奇。}}\n# en: Students behind faint, pale bubbles of light as a professor flicks pieces of chalk at them from the front of a dim room.\nscene [710e30] = 学生们躲在一层层淡淡的苍白光罩后面，一位教授在昏暗的教室前方朝他们弹粉笔。\n# en: Hold the ward up\nopt.left.label [e167c0] = 撑住护界\n# en: {~Eleven pieces of chalk, eleven small pings. Your shell holds and you feel each one in your teeth.|The chalk turns aside and lands neatly in a row. The professor nods at the row more than at you.|Your ward is pale, thin and still there at the bell. She says that's the whole of it, really.}\nopt.left.say.1 [c8a818] = {~十一截粉笔，十一声轻响。你的壳撑住了，每一下你都震在牙根里。|粉笔被弹开，整整齐齐落成一排。教授朝那一排点头，多过朝你点头。|下课时你的护界苍白、单薄，但还在。她说，归根结底也就这些了。}\n# en: Duck instead\nopt.right.label [b1f622] = 干脆躲开\n# en: {~You duck the first nine. The tenth bounces off your forehead. \"Avoidance is a ward,\" she says, \"of a sort.\"|You let the shell drop and simply aren't where the chalk goes. She writes something down. It might be a mark, or a warning.}\nopt.right.say.1 [166b7a] = {~前九截你都躲过了。第十截弹在你额头上。“回避也是一种护界，”她说，“某种意义上。”|你撤掉壳，只是不站在粉笔要落的地方。她记了点什么，可能是分数，也可能是警告。}\n\n@event umbral_quiet_minutes\n# en: The Quiet Minutes\ntitle [089065] = 静默的十分钟\n# en: {Each lesson opens with ten minutes of silence. \"Listen to what's hidden,\" the professor says, and puts out every lamp but one.|The quiet minutes. You can hear the tower settle, stone by stone.|Ten minutes of silence. You look forward to it, and also not.} {~Someone's stomach rumbles at the worst moment.|The candle leans toward the door, though nothing comes in.|{season == \"summer\": A bee is lost in the room somewhere.|The wind hums in the stairwell.}}\ntext [82c31e] = {每节课都以十分钟的静默开始。“去听隐藏之物。”教授说，然后熄灭了所有灯，只留一盏。|静默的十分钟。你能听见塔一块石头一块石头地沉降。|十分钟的静默。你有点期待它，又有点不。} {~有人的肚子在最不合适的时候叫了。|烛火朝门口倾斜，尽管什么也没进来。|{season == \"summer\": 屋里不知哪儿有只迷路的蜜蜂。|风在楼梯井里低鸣。}}\n# en: A circle of students sitting perfectly still in near darkness, a single candle in the middle, everyone listening very hard.\nscene [5f3b47] = 一圈学生在近乎全黑中一动不动地坐着，中间只有一支蜡烛，每个人都在用力倾听。\n# en: Listen properly\nopt.left.label [e31a7f] = 认真倾听\n# en: {~You hear the tower breathing, which it shouldn't, and a sigh on the stair that nobody makes.|You hear the lake, which is impossible from up here. The professor says that's normal for this room. It is not reassuring.|In the silence you hear someone you miss, very faintly. When the lamps come up, you don't mention it.}\nopt.left.say.1 [58c635] = {~你听见塔在呼吸——它不该呼吸——还有楼梯上一声没人发出的叹息。|你听见了湖，从这里本不可能听见。教授说这在这间屋里很正常。这话并不让人安心。|静默里，你隐约听见一个你思念的人。灯亮起来时，你没提这件事。}\n# en: Quietly finish other homework\nopt.right.label [29deb1] = 悄悄写别的作业\n# en: {~You finish a whole essay by feel in the dark. The professor never notices, or never says.|Not a scratch of the quill. When the lamps come up, your essay is done and your face is innocent.}\nopt.right.say.1 [ab94fe] = {~你在黑暗里摸索着写完了一整篇论文。教授始终没发现，或者始终没说。|羽毛笔一点声音都没出。灯亮时，你的论文写完了，脸上一派无辜。}\n# en: {~When the lamps come up the professor is behind you, reading your essay. \"Your third paragraph is weak,\" she says.|Your quill squeaks. In that silence it sounds like a scream. Everyone turns.}\nopt.right.say.2 [dbf0cc] = {~灯亮时，教授就站在你身后读你的论文。“你的第三段很弱。”她说。|你的羽毛笔吱了一声。在那片寂静里，听起来像一声尖叫。所有人都转过头来。}\n\n@event umbral_short_distances\n# en: Short Distances\ntitle [35c701] = 短距离\n# en: {Umbral Theory II brings the shadowstep, desk to desk. \"The shadows in between are not friendly,\" the professor says, \"so don't linger.\"|Shadowstep drill. Three desks this time, then four.|The desks. You know the way between them, and the cold that lives there.} {~Someone comes out under the wrong desk.|The lamps are lower than usual.|{gpa >= 3: The professor sets you the long way round.|The professor sets you the short way, and watches.}}\ntext [10198a] = {暗影理论 II 教影步，从一张桌子到另一张。“中间那些影子可不友善，”教授说，“所以别逗留。”|影步操练。这次三张桌子，然后四张。|这些桌子。你认得它们之间的路，也认得住在那里的寒意。} {~有人从错误的桌子底下钻了出来。|灯比平时更暗。|{gpa >= 3: 教授让你绕远路。|教授让你走近路，并且盯着。}}\n# en: A student stepping into the shadow under one desk and halfway out of the shadow under another, the room's lamps burning low.\nscene [18d81b] = 一个学生踏进一张桌子下的影子，半个身子从另一张桌子下的影子里出来，屋里的灯低低燃着。\n# en: Step across\nopt.left.label [894fac] = 一步跨过去\n# en: {~In, through, out. It takes no time and lasts forever. You come out under the far desk with frost on your sleeves.|Four desks, clean. Something in between brushes your ankle, curious, and lets you pass.}\nopt.left.say.1 [d9ff17] = {~进去，穿过，出来。不花一点时间，又像过了一辈子。你从最远那张桌子下出来，袖子上结了霜。|四张桌子，干净利落。中间有什么东西好奇地擦过你的脚踝，然后放你过去了。}\n# en: {~You come out one desk short and somewhat upside down. The cold stays in your fingers until lunch.|You linger, just for a moment. It's enough. You come back pale, and the professor sits you by the one warm lamp.}\nopt.left.say.2 [e1feee] = {~你差一张桌子就出来了，而且有点头朝下。寒意在你指尖一直留到午饭。|你逗留了，只有一瞬。这就够了。你脸色苍白地回来，教授让你坐到唯一一盏暖灯旁。}\n# en: Map the route on paper first\nopt.right.label [f66070] = 先在纸上画好路线\n# en: {~You draw every shadow in the room, and where it goes at each hour. The professor keeps your map.|You work out the route in ink and walk it once, slowly, in the light. It's dull, and correct.}\nopt.right.say.1 [bb1960] = {~你画下屋里每一道影子，以及它每个钟点会移到哪里。教授留下了你的图。|你用墨水推好路线，然后在光亮里慢慢走了一遍。枯燥，但正确。}\n\n@event umbral_hidden_object\n# en: Present but Unseen\ntitle [a63725] = 在场而不可见\n# en: {Hide a small key in this room, present but unseen. \"Hiding is not removal,\" the professor says. \"Removal is for amateurs.\"|The key, and somewhere to hide it. Your classmates are better at finding things.|The key exercise. Half the class pats their pockets out of habit.} {~The key is brass today.|The key is silver and very cold.|Last time a key hid in a shadow for two days.}\ntext [94d2ef] = {把一把小钥匙藏在这间屋里，让它在场却不可见。“藏不是拿走，”教授说，“拿走是外行干的。”|钥匙，和一个藏它的地方。同学们找东西的本事见长。|钥匙练习。半个班都养成了拍口袋的习惯。} {~今天的钥匙是黄铜的。|钥匙是银的，冰凉刺骨。|上次有把钥匙在影子里躲了两天。}\n# en: A dim classroom being searched by several students at once while one of them sits calmly at a desk with a small brass key in plain sight.\nscene [e384e8] = 一间昏暗的教室里，好几个学生同时在翻找，其中一个却平静地坐在桌旁，一把小铜钥匙就摆在眼前。\n# en: Hide it, and stay calm\nopt.left.label [7d28db] = 藏好它，保持镇定\n# en: {~You leave the key on the desk in plain sight, in your own shadow. Nobody sees it. The professor smiles, which is rarer than a key.|You hide it in the dark behind your own ear. They search the room twice and never once look at you.}\nopt.left.say.1 [3e18d9] = {~你把钥匙明晃晃地放在桌上，就在你自己的影子里。没人看见。教授笑了，这比钥匙还难找。|你把它藏在自己耳后的暗处。他们把屋子搜了两遍，一次也没看你。}\n# en: {~A first-rate hiding place, found in under a minute by someone who simply asked the shadow.|You hide it so well you can't find it yourself. The professor retrieves it from the wall with a sigh.}\nopt.left.say.2 [b49b23] = {~一个一流的藏处，被人不到一分钟就找到了——对方只是问了问影子。|你藏得太好，连自己都找不到了。教授叹了口气，从墙里把它取了出来。}\n# en: Search for everyone else's\nopt.right.label [69b186] = 去找别人藏的\n# en: {~You find four keys, one in a hat, one in a hinge, one in a shadow that didn't want to let go.|You find one key and a pencil nobody has missed for years. The professor counts both.}\nopt.right.say.1 [71dd6c] = {~你找到了四把钥匙：一把在帽子里，一把在合页里，一把在一道不肯松手的影子里。|你找到了一把钥匙，和一支多年没人想起的铅笔。教授两样都算数。}\n\n@event umbral_stair_wards\n# en: The Old Stair Wards\ntitle [a4469b] = 古老的阶梯护界\n# en: {Advanced students renew the small wards on the tower's old stair. Some are newer than the stone. Some, the professor says, are much older.|The stair wards. You know which step hums.|The stair. {final_year: Someone else will kneel here next year.|Your knees know every step.}} {~One ward is warm, as if someone's just left.|Something beyond a ward leans back when you touch it.|The stair lamps are mostly a suggestion.}\ntext [41c59b] = {高年级学生负责加固塔里古老阶梯上的小护界。有些比石头新。有些，教授说，要古老得多。|阶梯护界。你知道哪一级会嗡鸣。|阶梯。{final_year: 明年会是别人跪在这里。|你的膝盖认得每一级台阶。}} {~有一道护界是温的，像刚有人离开。|你一碰，护界另一侧有什么东西往后靠了靠。|阶梯上的灯基本只是个意思。}\n# en: A student kneeling on an ancient spiral stair, hands pressed to a faint ring of light set into the stone.\nscene [c60e93] = 一个学生跪在古老的螺旋阶梯上，双手按着嵌进石头里的一圈微光。\n# en: Renew them carefully\nopt.left.label [405067] = 仔细地加固\n# en: {~Step by step, ring by ring. Each ward takes a little of you and hums.|You renew eleven and pause at the twelfth. Leave that one, the professor says, and doesn't say whose it is.|At the top you're light-headed and the wards are bright. Something on the other side has gone quiet.}\nopt.left.say.1 [e76ce9] = {~一级一级，一圈一圈。每道护界都取走你一点东西，然后嗡嗡作响。|你加固了十一道，在第十二道前停了下来。教授说那道别碰，却没说是谁的。|到了顶上你头晕目眩，护界都亮了。另一侧的什么已经安静下来。}\n# en: Study the oldest ward instead\nopt.right.label [6da660] = 转而研究最老的那道\n# en: {~You trace the oldest ring with one finger. The pattern folds in on itself like a knot nobody was meant to untie, and you feel watched for noticing even that.|The old ward is cold, careful work, made by someone who was afraid. You can feel the fear still in the stone.}\nopt.right.say.1 [1fc8a8] = {~你用一根手指描着最古老的那一圈。那图案向内层层折叠，像一个本就不该被人解开的结；仅仅察觉到这一点，你就觉得被什么盯上了。|那道古老的护界是冰冷而小心的手艺，出自一个害怕的人。那份恐惧至今还留在石头里。}\n\n@event umbral_errand\n# en: The Shadow's Errand\ntitle [febd12] = 影子的差事\n# en: {Original work. Send your own shadow on an errand across the room, the professor says, and have it come back. \"It will want to wander. They all do.\"|Shadow errands. Yours picks its own route now.|Errand day. {final_year: Does your shadow know you're leaving soon?|Your shadow seems eager, which worries you.}} {~Someone's shadow is still under a desk from last week.|The professor's own shadow is missing today.|The lamps are low and the shadows long.}\ntext [6c6456] = {原创作业。派你自己的影子到屋子另一头办件差事，教授说，然后让它回来。“它会想乱跑。它们都这样。”|影子差事。你的影子现在会自己挑路线了。|差事日。{final_year: 你的影子知道你快要离开了吗？|你的影子显得跃跃欲试，这让你担心。}} {~有人的影子从上周起还待在一张桌子底下。|教授自己的影子今天不见了。|灯很暗，影子很长。}\n# en: A student standing in lamplight with no shadow at all, while across the room their shadow lifts a book from a shelf.\nscene [fe154c] = 一个学生站在灯光下，身上没有一丝影子，而屋子另一头，他的影子正从书架上取下一本书。\n# en: Send it, and trust it\nopt.left.label [80242c] = 派它去，信任它\n# en: {~Your shadow crosses the room, lifts a book, and brings it back open at a page you needed. You didn't ask it to.|It comes back with a feather from the rafters, which wasn't the errand. The professor counts it as initiative.}\nopt.left.say.1 [da7fb7] = {~你的影子穿过屋子，取下一本书，带回来时正翻在你需要的那一页。你并没有叫它这么做。|它带回一根椽子上的羽毛，这不是差事内容。教授把它算作主动性。}\n# en: {~It comes back an hour late and a little the wrong shape. For a day it lags behind you on stairs, sulking.|It doesn't come back until dusk. When it does, it smells of the lake. The professor makes you sit with the lamp.}\nopt.left.say.2 [1a0a7f] = {~它晚了一小时才回来，形状还有点不对。接下来一整天，它在楼梯上总落在你后面，闹着别扭。|它直到黄昏才回来。回来时带着一股湖水的气味。教授让你在灯旁坐着。}\n# en: Keep it on a short leash\nopt.right.label [bfc9a2] = 把它拴短一点\n# en: {~Your shadow goes as far as the window and no further. It looks out a long time before it comes back.|Two yards and home. Dull, safe and precise. The professor says precision is how shadow-workers get old.}\nopt.right.say.1 [bd81c7] = {~你的影子走到窗边就停下了。它朝外望了很久，才回来。|两码远就回家。枯燥、安全、精确。教授说，精确就是影术师能活到老的原因。}\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/pools/courses_hidden.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/pools/courses_hidden.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event art_escapement_drill\n# en: The Ticking Hour\ntitle [0f189f] = 滴答一课\n# en: {Artifice begins with a heart, a fist of clockwork that must tick evenly before it is allowed to think.|Your clockwork heart is waiting where you left it, sulking at the wrong speed.|You can hear your own heart on the bench from the doorway now. Its tick is almost yours.} {~The bench beside you ticks like hail.|One heart near the door is running backwards, proudly.|The lecturer listens to each bench with eyes closed.} {season == \"winter\": The brass is cold enough to bite.|The workshop smells of solder.}\ntext [64e7d6] = {奇械术从一颗心开始：一团发条，必须先走得均匀，才被允许思考。|你的发条心还在你留下它的地方，用错误的速度生着闷气。|如今你在门口就能听见自己工作台上那颗心。它的滴答声几乎就是你的。} {~隔壁工作台滴答得像下冰雹。|门边有一颗心在倒着走，还挺得意。|讲师闭着眼，挨个工作台地听。} {season == \"winter\": 黄铜冷得咬手。|工坊里一股焊锡味。}\n# en: A cluttered workshop bench covered in tiny brass gears, files and loupes, a row of half-built clockwork hearts ticking out of time with one another.\nscene [2c43ab] = 杂乱的工坊长凳上堆满细小的黄铜齿轮、锉刀和放大镜，一排半成品的发条心各走各的节拍。\n# en: File every tooth by hand\nopt.left.label [93a8dc] = 一齿一齿手工锉\n# en: {~Tooth by tooth, under a loupe, until your eyes water. The tick evens out by the bell.|You file all morning and burn one thumb on the solder. The heart ticks steady and slightly smug.|It's slow, fiddly work, and your hands learn it faster than your head does.}\nopt.left.say.1 [0214af] = {~一齿接一齿，伏在放大镜下，直到眼睛发酸。下课铃响前，滴答声匀了。|你锉了一上午，还被焊锡烫了一下拇指。那颗心走得稳稳当当，还有点得意。|又慢又磨人的活儿，你的手比脑子学得快。}\n# en: Charm the spring to keep time\nopt.right.label [93d2cf] = 给发条施咒定时\n# en: {~The charm settles into the spring like a cat into a basket. Your heart ticks like a metronome.|You whisper the rhythm into the coil and it keeps it. The lecturer opens one eye, then closes it again.}\nopt.right.say.1 [8ba232] = {~咒语钻进发条，像猫钻进篮子。你的心走得像节拍器一样准。|你把节奏低声说进发条圈里，它就守住了。讲师睁开一只眼，又闭上了。}\n# en: {~The spring takes the charm too literally and unwinds all at once, across the room.|Your heart ticks perfectly for a minute, then very fast, then not at all. Someone hands you a fire bucket just in case.}\nopt.right.say.2 [1850ff] = {~发条把咒语理解得太字面，一口气全松开，飞过了整间屋子。|你的心完美地走了一分钟，然后飞快，然后彻底停了。有人递给你一只灭火桶，以防万一。}\n\n@event art_stirring_cauldron\n# en: The Cauldron That Stirs\ntitle [dc6732] = 自己搅拌的坩埚\n# en: {The class project is a cauldron that stirs itself. It does, mostly, in whatever direction it pleases.|The cauldron has been rebuilt again. It stirs with a new brass arm and the same old temper.|The cauldron recognises you, or behaves as if it does.} {~Today it stirs clockwise, then argues.|Today it stirs so fast the porridge in it is climbing out.|Today the arm keeps tapping the rim like an impatient guest.} {final_year: You'll miss its moods, a little.|The fire buckets by the door are full, for once.}\ntext [7715aa] = {这门课的项目是一口会自己搅拌的坩埚。它确实会搅，大多数时候，朝它高兴的方向。|坩埚又被重装了一遍。新的黄铜搅臂，还是老脾气。|坩埚认得你，或者至少装得像认得。} {~今天它先顺时针搅，然后开始闹别扭。|今天它搅得太快，锅里的粥都往外爬。|今天搅臂一个劲儿地敲锅沿，像个不耐烦的客人。} {final_year: 你会有点想念它的脾气。|门边的灭火桶难得都是满的。}\n# en: A copper cauldron on iron legs stirring itself with a long brass arm while students crowd round with notebooks and a wary distance.\nscene [9242ac] = 一口铁腿铜坩埚用一根长长的黄铜臂自己搅拌，学生们抱着笔记本围在一旁，小心地保持着距离。\n# en: Recut the runes on the arm\nopt.left.label [72bcaa] = 重刻搅臂上的符文\n# en: {~You spend the morning with a graver and a magnifier, recutting the stirring runes one stroke at a time. Your fingers are nicked; the arm moves sweetly.|Fine work in brass, the whole lesson long. The arm stirs evenly, and you have solder burns in a neat little row.}\nopt.left.say.1 [8322f8] = {~你拿着刻刀和放大镜过了一上午，一笔一画重刻搅拌符文。手指划了几道口子，搅臂动得顺滑了。|一整节课都在黄铜上做细活。搅臂搅得均匀了，你手上多了一排整整齐齐的焊锡烫痕。}\n# en: Test it on a live brew\nopt.right.label [4e638a] = 拿真药剂试一试\n# en: {~The cauldron stirs your brew exactly as the recipe asks, and you take the credit for both.|The brew turns the right colour at the right minute. The cauldron seems to know it did well.}\nopt.right.say.1 [83d27e] = {~坩埚完全按配方搅好了你的药剂，两份功劳你都领了。|药剂在对的那一分钟变成了对的颜色。坩埚似乎知道自己干得不错。}\n# en: {~The arm speeds up, the brew climbs the walls, and the whole class steps back as one.|The cauldron stirs your brew into something with the texture of an argument. You scrub it out until the bell.}\nopt.right.say.2 [08a398] = {~搅臂越转越快，药剂爬上锅壁，全班齐刷刷后退一步。|坩埚把你的药剂搅成了一种像吵架一样的质地。你一直刷锅刷到下课。}\n\n@event star_names_in_the_dark\n# en: Names in the Dark\ntitle [d9ffd3] = 黑暗中的名字\n# en: {Starcalling is taught by appointment, at night, and it begins with names. \"You cannot call what you cannot name.\"|Another night appointment under the dome. The list of names is longer than it was.|The dome at night feels like yours now. You know where the draught comes in.} {~The great telescope has swung round on its own again.|A meteor scratches the sky and is gone.|An owl is sitting on the open shutter, listening.} {season == \"winter\": Your breath freezes on the eyepiece.|The night air smells of cut grass from the lawns below.}\ntext [a6d744] = {唤星术只在夜里预约授课，而且从名字开始。“叫不出名字的，就唤不下来。”|又一次夜间预约，又在穹顶下。名单比上次更长了。|夜里的穹顶如今像是你的地方。你知道冷风从哪儿钻进来。} {~大望远镜又自己转了个方向。|一颗流星划过天空，转眼就不见了。|一只猫头鹰蹲在敞开的天窗上，在听。} {season == \"winter\": 你的呼气冻在了目镜上。|夜风里有下面草坪刚割过的青草味。}\n# en: A dark brass dome open to a sky full of stars, a student at the eyepiece of a great telescope while another recites from memory, breath steaming.\nscene [383740] = 黄铜穹顶在黑暗中向满天星斗敞开，一名学生凑在大望远镜的目镜前，另一名呼着白气凭记忆背诵。\n# en: Recite the names until perfect\nopt.left.label [5a0fad] = 把名字背到一字不差\n# en: {~You say them over and over into the cold until they stop being words and start being places.|Name after name, in order and then backwards. By the end you could find them blindfold, and nearly have to.|You stumble on the same name four times. The fifth time it comes out right, and you feel it land somewhere.}\nopt.left.say.1 [fa2b79] = {~你对着寒气一遍遍念，直到它们不再是词，而成了地方。|一个接一个，正着背，再倒着背。到最后你闭着眼也能找到它们，也差点真得闭着眼。|同一个名字你卡了四次。第五次终于念对，你感觉它落在了某个地方。}\n# en: Let the telescope choose a star\nopt.right.label [9ee4f3] = 让望远镜挑一颗星\n# en: {~The telescope settles on a star you've never heard of. You find its name in the old charts before the lecturer can tell you.|Wherever it points, you follow, and the charts agree with it. That's a whole lesson learned the other way round.}\nopt.right.say.1 [313b95] = {~望远镜停在一颗你从没听说过的星上。讲师还没开口，你已经在旧星图里找到了它的名字。|它指哪儿你跟哪儿，星图也都对得上。这一课是倒着学会的。}\n# en: {~The telescope picks a star and then changes its mind every ten minutes. You chase it round the dome until midnight.|You spend the night at the eyepiece and come down with a stiff neck and one name you aren't sure of.}\nopt.right.say.2 [18bc47] = {~望远镜挑了一颗星，然后每十分钟改一次主意。你绕着穹顶追它追到午夜。|你在目镜前熬了一夜，下来时脖子僵了，还有一个拿不准的名字。}\n\n@event star_thread_of_light\n# en: A Thread of Light\ntitle [af6199] = 一缕星光\n# en: {Tonight you try to call down a single thread of a named star's light, no thicker than a hair.|The lesson is the same as ever: one thread, one star, held as long as you can bear it.|You've called down threads before. Tonight the lecturer wants it held longer.} {~The sky is clear and very far away.|Thin cloud keeps crossing the star you've chosen.|The other students are already standing with their hands raised.}\ntext [c83445] = {今晚你要试着唤下一缕某颗有名之星的光，细不过一根头发。|这一课永远一样：一缕光，一颗星，能撑多久撑多久。|你以前唤下过光缕。今晚讲师要你撑得更久。} {~天空晴朗，又远得很。|薄云一次次掠过你选中的那颗星。|其他学生已经举着手站好了。}\n# en: A student in a dark observatory with hands cupped upward, a single silver thread of starlight running down through the open dome into their palms.\nscene [db7c41] = 昏暗的观星台里，一名学生双手向上合拢，一缕银色星光穿过敞开的穹顶落进掌心。\n# en: Call the thread down yourself\nopt.left.label [3c139b] = 亲手把光唤下来\n# en: {~The thread comes down cold and bright and pours into your palms. You hold it until your hands shake.|It takes three tries. On the third, the light drops into your hands like water from a great height.|A hair of light, the length of the whole tower. When it fades, your fingers glow for an hour.}\nopt.left.say.1 [3d4bf5] = {~光缕冰冷而明亮地落下，注满你的掌心。你一直撑到双手发抖。|试了三次。第三次，光像从极高处落下的水一样掉进你手里。|一根头发粗的光，有整座塔那么长。它消散后，你的指尖还亮了一个钟头。}\n# en: Keep the night log instead\nopt.right.label [314549] = 改去记夜间日志\n# en: {~You write down every thread the others call: the star, the minute, how long it held. It's dull, cold work, and you start to see the pattern.|You hold the lamp and the log while the others call. Watching from outside, you see exactly where their hands go wrong.} {season == \"winter\": Your ink keeps freezing.|The lecturer reads your log twice.}\nopt.right.say.1 [a4c016] = {~你记下别人唤下的每一缕光：哪颗星、几点几分、撑了多久。又冷又枯燥，但你开始看出规律了。|别人唤光时你举灯记录。从旁边看，你清清楚楚看到他们的手错在哪儿。} {season == \"winter\": 你的墨水一直结冰。|讲师把你的日志读了两遍。}\n# en: Hold your own starlight up to it\nopt.up.label [658616] = 举起自己的星光迎它\n# en: {~You light your own starlight and the thread finds it, like calling to like. It holds twice as long.|Your starlight steadies the thread, and the lecturer has you show the others how.|The thread drops straight into the light you're already holding. For a moment the dome is bright as noon.}\nopt.up.say.1 [c6e7d2] = {~你点亮自己的星光，那缕光循着它而来，同类相召。它撑了两倍长。|你的星光稳住了光缕，讲师让你示范给其他人看。|光缕径直落进你手中已有的光里。那一刻，穹顶亮如正午。}\n\n@event hollow_sealed_boxes\n# en: How It Was Sealed\ntitle [e1eab1] = 它是怎么封上的\n# en: {Each lesson, the headmistress sets a sealed thing in front of you. \"Tell me how it was closed,\" she says. \"Not what is inside. How.\"|Another sealed thing on the table. Tonight it is {~a letter with a wax seal|a small lacquered box|a jar with its lid charmed shut}.|The headmistress places tonight's sealed thing with a little smile. She has started choosing harder ones.} {~The lamp hisses.|The books on the shelves breathe slowly in the dark.|She sits with her hands folded, unhurried.}\ntext [41dde2] = {每一课，院长都把一样封住的东西放到你面前。“告诉我它是怎么封上的，”她说，“不是里面有什么。是怎么封的。”|桌上又是一样封住的东西。今晚是{~一封盖着蜡印的信|一只小漆盒|一个盖子被咒语封死的罐子}。|院长带着一丝微笑放下今晚的封物。她已经开始挑更难的了。} {~油灯嘶嘶作响。|书架上的书在黑暗里缓缓呼吸。|她双手交叠坐着，不慌不忙。}\n# en: A windowless room lined with black-bound books, a single lamp on a table, a tall calm woman setting a small locked box in front of a student.\nscene [8b6e1a] = 一间没有窗户的房间，四壁摆满黑色封皮的书，桌上一盏孤灯，一位高挑沉静的女士把一只上锁的小盒子放在学生面前。\n# en: Study the seal for the hour\nopt.left.label [b8b4fc] = 用整节课研究封印\n# en: {~You trace every line of it without touching. At the end you can name each step of the sealing, in order. She nods once.|It takes the whole hour and a headache, but you work it out backwards, layer by layer.|You get two layers right and one wrong. She tells you which, kindly, and you'll remember.}\nopt.left.say.1 [1a15af] = {~你不碰它，只把每一道纹路都描了一遍。最后你能按顺序说出封印的每一步。她点了一下头。|花了整整一节课和一场头痛，你一层一层倒推了出来。|你对了两层，错了一层。她温和地告诉你是哪一层，你会记住的。}\n# en: Open it without leaving a trace\nopt.right.label [ffc168] = 打开它，不留痕迹\n# en: {~You open it and close it again, and nothing shows. She looks at it, then at you, and laughs, short and delighted.|Open, closed, not a mark. \"Good,\" she says. \"Now never do that to a door.\"}\nopt.right.say.1 [ae8a4c] = {~你打开又合上，什么也看不出来。她看看它，又看看你，短促而愉快地笑了一声。|开了，合了，一丝痕迹也没有。“很好，”她说，“以后永远别对一扇门这么做。”}\n# en: {~The seal cracks with a tiny, final sound. The headmistress writes something in her book. She remembers every mistake, and now there is one more.|It opens, and it shows. \"Everyone breaks one,\" she says pleasantly. \"Rarely so loudly.\"}\nopt.right.say.2 [648f9d] = {~封印发出一声细小而决绝的脆响。院长在本子上写了点什么。她记得每一个错误，现在又多了一个。|开是开了，痕迹也露了。“每个人都会弄坏一个，”她和颜悦色地说，“很少有人弄得这么响。”}\n\n@event hollow_reading_the_blank\n# en: Reading the Blank Page\ntitle [1973a9] = 读空白的一页\n# en: {\"Most things that are hidden are not gone,\" the headmistress says, and gives you a blank page. \"Find what was written. Gently.\"|A new blank page tonight. It feels warmer than paper should.|You know the weight of hidden writing in a page by now, like a pocket with something in it.} {~The books on the nearest shelf seem to lean closer.|The lamp burns low and blue.|Somewhere in the stacks, a book turns its own page.} {final_year: She asks where you'll go after graduation, and listens.|She is reading while you work, and doesn't look up.}\ntext [72c579] = {“大多数被藏起来的东西并没有消失，”院长说着，递给你一张白纸，“找出写过的东西。轻一点。”|今晚又是一张新的白纸。它比纸该有的温度要暖。|如今你能掂出一页纸里藏着字的分量，就像口袋里装着东西。} {~最近那排书架上的书似乎凑近了些。|油灯烧得又低又蓝。|书库深处，有本书自己翻了一页。} {final_year: 她问你毕业后要去哪里，然后认真听着。|她在你干活时看书，头也不抬。}\n# en: A student bent over an apparently blank page by lamplight, faint lines of ink surfacing under their fingertips, black-bound books leaning in from the shelves.\nscene [6dbe72] = 一名学生借着灯光俯身看一张看似空白的纸，淡淡的墨迹在指尖下浮现，黑色封皮的书从书架上倾身过来。\n# en: Coax the ink up slowly\nopt.left.label [a0bd04] = 慢慢把墨迹引出来\n# en: {~You warm the page with a thread of power, a breath at a time. The writing surfaces slowly, safe and dull: someone's laundry list, very carefully hidden.|Line by line, the ink comes up. It's an old timetable. The lesson was in the finding.|It takes most of your strength. The page gives up a single sentence about the weather, decades ago.}\nopt.left.say.1 [fa0e7d] = {~你用一缕法力一口气一口气地温着纸页。字迹慢慢浮上来，安全又无聊：某人的洗衣清单，藏得非常仔细。|墨迹一行一行浮现，是一张旧课表。这一课的意义在于找到它。|这几乎耗尽了你的力气。纸页只吐出一句话，讲的是几十年前的天气。}\n# en: Read it bare-handed\nopt.right.label [de8da5] = 徒手直接读\n# en: {~The ink comes up fast under your fingers, and so does a chill that stays in your hand until morning.|You read it quickly and well. Your fingertips ache for an hour, and the headmistress hands you a cup of tea without comment.}\nopt.right.say.1 [0a052a] = {~墨迹在你指下飞快浮现，一股寒意也一起涌上来，在你手里一直待到早上。|你读得又快又好。指尖疼了一个钟头，院长一言不发地递给你一杯茶。}\n# en: Let your ward do the reading\nopt.up.label [3adba1] = 让护界替你读\n# en: {~Your ward settles over the page like a held breath, and the writing rises into it. The headmistress raises an eyebrow, pleased.|You read through the ward as if through glass. It costs you, but not your hands.|The hidden lines surface inside your shell, one by one, in no hurry.}\nopt.up.say.1 [325e87] = {~你的护界像一口屏住的气一样罩在纸页上，字迹浮进其中。院长扬起眉毛，颇为满意。|你隔着护界读，像隔着玻璃。它耗你的力，但不伤你的手。|藏起的字句在你的护壳里一行行浮现，不慌不忙。}\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/pools/courses_upper.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/pools/courses_upper.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event evo_rain_barrel\n# en: Rain on Demand\ntitle [b62937] = 呼雨即来\n# en: {Tidecalling at Evocation II. Pull a cloud off the lake, park it over a barrel, and fill the barrel to the chalk line. Not over, not under.|The barrels are out on the terrace again, each with a chalk line.|You know your barrel now, the one with the iron band that's slipped.} {~Professor Marisol is reading on the steps, under a perfectly dry patch of air.|The wind keeps trying to borrow the clouds.|{season == \"winter\": The clouds come off the lake as sleet.|A heron watches the barrels with professional interest.}}\ntext [735b00] = {塑能术 II 的唤潮课。从湖上拽一朵云，停在木桶上方，把桶灌到粉笔线为止。不许多，也不许少。|木桶又摆上了露台，每只都画着粉笔线。|如今你认得自己的桶了，就是铁箍滑下来的那只。} {~玛丽索尔教授坐在台阶上看书，头顶那一小块空气干干爽爽。|风一直想把云借走。|{season == \"winter\": 云从湖上过来，落下的是冻雨。|一只苍鹭以专业的兴趣盯着这些木桶。}}\n# en: A row of students on a lakeside terrace, each with a small grey cloud above a wooden barrel, one cloud raining sideways onto a neighbour.\nscene [c50d40] = 湖畔露台上站着一排学生，每人头顶一小朵灰云，下方是一只木桶，其中一朵云正斜着把雨下到邻座身上。\n# en: Wring it out to the line\nopt.left.label [0e4325] = 拧到粉笔线\n# en: {~The last drop lands on the chalk. Marisol looks up from her book and back down, which counts.|You stop the rain a breath early and let the drip finish the job. Exactly to the line.}\nopt.left.say.1 [d0f1a2] = {~最后一滴正好落在粉笔线上。玛丽索尔从书上抬眼看了看，又低下头去，这就算数了。|你提前一口气收住雨，让余滴把活干完。不多不少，正到线上。}\n# en: {~Your cloud overshoots, fills the barrel, and keeps going down your collar.|The cloud drifts left and rains on the next barrel, which is not yours, and on its owner, who is very not yours.}\nopt.left.say.2 [57a057] = {~你的云下过了头，灌满木桶后接着往你衣领里灌。|云往左飘，下在了旁边的桶上，那不是你的桶，也下在了桶的主人身上，那更不是你的人。}\n# en: Drizzle it in, slowly\nopt.right.label [b367cf] = 慢慢地洒进去\n# en: {~It takes the whole lesson. Your cloud is the size of a pillow by the end, and very tired.|You fill it a thimble at a time. The others have gone in for lunch. The line is met.}\nopt.right.say.1 [bb1daf] = {~这花了整整一节课。到最后你的云只剩枕头那么大，而且累坏了。|你一顶针一顶针地往里灌。别人都去吃午饭了。线终于到了。}\n\n@event evo_ember_stair\n# en: Carrying the Ember\ntitle [5d68d8] = 掌中余火\n# en: {Pyromancy at Evocation II. Carry a live ember in your bare palm from the ground floor to the top of the Lecture Tower. Keep it alight. Keep your hand.|The ember drill again. The stair hasn't got any shorter.|Ember day. You've counted the stair by now, and you know where the draught is.} {~A door on the third floor opens just as you pass.|Someone ahead of you is climbing very fast and swearing quietly.|The lamps on the stair are out, and your ember is the only light.}\ntext [e7707f] = {塑能术 II 的火焰术课。赤手托一块燃着的炭，从讲堂塔底层走到塔顶。别让它灭，也别丢了你的手。|又是托炭练习。楼梯并没有变短。|托炭日。你早数清了台阶，也知道穿堂风在哪儿。} {~你经过时，三楼的一扇门恰好打开。|你前面有人爬得飞快，嘴里小声骂着。|楼梯上的灯全灭了，你掌心的炭是唯一的光。}\n# en: A student climbing a dim spiral stair, a single glowing ember resting in their bare open palm.\nscene [0a00df] = 一名学生在昏暗的螺旋楼梯上攀登，一块发光的余炭躺在摊开的赤裸掌心里。\n# en: Feed it, and climb fast\nopt.left.label [76bdf4] = 喂着火，快步上\n# en: {~You keep the ember bright with small breaths of heat and take the stairs two at a time. It arrives fat and orange.|You reach the top with the ember glowing and your palm only warm. The lecturer tips it into a brazier without a word.}\nopt.left.say.1 [83cab6] = {~你用细细的热息让炭保持明亮，一步跨两级台阶。到顶时它又胖又红。|你到达塔顶，炭还亮着，掌心只是温热。讲师一言不发地把它倒进火盆。}\n# en: {~The ember flares on the landing and you learn exactly how hot a palm can get. You arrive with it alight, and with a blister.|You feed it too much at the draughty window. It bites. You carry it up the last flight in your other hand.}\nopt.left.say.2 [7dddc3] = {~炭在楼梯平台上猛地一窜，你终于知道掌心能有多烫。到顶时它还燃着，你也多了个水泡。|在透风的窗边你喂得太多，它咬了你一口。最后一段楼梯，你换了只手托着它。}\n# en: Shield it, and climb slow\nopt.right.label [d3a230] = 护着火，慢慢上\n# en: {~You climb at a crawl with the ember cupped against the draughts. It is alight at the top, just. So are your nerves.|Step by step, a held breath at each window. Last up, but the ember's still glowing.}\nopt.right.say.1 [94f8a8] = {~你一步一挪地往上爬，双手拢着炭挡风。到顶时它还亮着，勉强亮着。你的神经也是。|一步一步，每过一扇窗就屏一口气。你最后一个到，但炭还亮着。}\n\n@event evo_firebreak\n# en: The Reed Burn\ntitle [b50131] = 烧芦苇\n# en: {Advanced Evocation does real work, the controlled burn of the dead reeds along the lake shore. Pyromancers lead the fire, tidecallers hold the line behind it.|Burn day. The shore smells of smoke for a week afterwards.|The reed burn. You can tell a fire's mood from its smoke now.} {~The wind is steady from the lake.|The wind can't decide.|The reeds are drier than anyone would like.} {season == \"summer\": The air is hot already.|Your breath steams.}\ntext [394016] = {高等塑能术要干真活，湖岸那片枯芦苇的控制烧荒。火焰术的人领着火走，唤潮术的人在后面守线。|烧荒日。之后整整一周，湖岸都是烟味。|烧芦苇。如今你看烟就知道火的脾气。} {~风从湖上稳稳地吹来。|风拿不定主意。|芦苇干得让谁都不放心。} {season == \"summer\": 空气已经很热了。|你呼出的气都成了白雾。}\n# en: Advanced students along a lake shore in a line, half of them calling a low wall of fire through dry reeds, half of them holding back a curtain of lake water behind it.\nscene [c4e483] = 高年级学生沿湖岸站成一排，一半人引着一道低矮的火墙穿过枯芦苇，另一半人在火墙后撑起一道湖水的帘幕。\n# en: Walk at the fire's head\nopt.left.label [13e32e] = 走在火头\n# en: {~The fire follows your hands down the shore like a dog on a lead. The lecturer lets you take it all the way to the boathouse.|You lead the fire low and even, and it goes out where you tell it. Behind you there's nothing but clean black ground.}\nopt.left.say.1 [41bcb3] = {~火像牵着绳的狗，跟着你的手沿岸走。讲师让你一路把它带到船屋。|你把火领得又低又匀，它在你说停的地方熄灭。身后只剩干干净净的黑土。}\n# en: {~A gust lifts the fire over your head. The tidecallers drop it in a wall of lake water, and you with it.|The fire jumps the line and runs. It's caught in a minute, but your eyebrows weren't.}\nopt.left.say.2 [d9a4db] = {~一阵风把火掀过你头顶。唤潮术的人一道湖水墙把它压了下去，连你一起。|火越过了线，跑了起来。一分钟就被逮住了，你的眉毛却没逃掉。}\n# en: Hold the water line\nopt.right.label [efca5e] = 守住水线\n# en: {~You stand in the shallows holding a curtain of lake water for an hour. Nothing gets past you. Nobody notices, which is the point.|Your arms ache from keeping the water up. It's the least glamorous job on the shore and the one the lecturer checks first.}\nopt.right.say.1 [8aa46a] = {~你站在浅水里撑着一道湖水帘幕，撑了一个钟头。什么都没漏过去。没人注意到，这正是要点。|手臂撑水撑得发酸。这是岸上最不光彩的差事，却是讲师头一个检查的。}\n\n@event evo_drowned_flame\n# en: Fire Under Water\ntitle [29fc18] = 水下之火\n# en: {Original work in Advanced Evocation, a fire that burns underwater. Every student gets a tank. Most tanks get a new pane every week.|Your tank is waiting, with a fresh crack along one pane.|The tank again. You talk to the water now, and to the fire, separately.} {~Someone's tank has boiled dry and they're staring at it.|A flame at the next bench burns green beneath the water, quite calm.|The lecturer is standing well back today.}\ntext [9e9aa5] = {高等塑能术的原创作业：一团在水下燃烧的火。每人一只水箱。大多数水箱每周都要换块新玻璃。|你的水箱在等你，一块玻璃上新添了一道裂纹。|又是水箱。如今你会分别跟水和火说话。} {~有人的水箱烧干了，他们正盯着它发呆。|邻桌一团火在水底燃着绿光，十分平静。|讲师今天站得远远的。}\n# en: A glass tank of water on a scorched bench, a small blue flame burning steadily at the bottom of it.\nscene [c9026b] = 一张烧焦的长桌上放着一只玻璃水箱，一小团蓝色火焰在箱底稳稳燃烧。\n# en: Light it at the bottom\nopt.left.label [a339ad] = 在箱底点燃它\n# en: {~A small blue flame takes hold under the water and stays. The bubbles go up in a thin silver line.|It catches, gutters, and settles. The water around it doesn't even warm. You don't know how you did it.}\nopt.left.say.1 [04b84e] = {~一小团蓝火在水下点着了，并且留了下来。气泡排成一道细细的银线往上冒。|它点着了，摇晃了一下，安顿下来。周围的水甚至没变暖。你也不知道自己是怎么做到的。}\n# en: {~The water boils in a heartbeat and the pane splits. You wear the steam on your face for the rest of the day.|The flame goes up instead of in. The tank cracks, and your sleeve is wet and scorched at once.}\nopt.left.say.2 [42d7ce] = {~水一瞬间就沸了，玻璃裂开。一整天你脸上都带着那股蒸汽。|火没往里钻，反往上蹿。水箱裂了，你的袖子同时又湿又焦。}\n# en: Wrap it in a bubble first\nopt.right.label [69bcca] = 先裹一个气泡\n# en: {~You hold a skin of air around the flame and sink them both. It's half a cheat, and the lecturer says so. It burns all hour.|Your flame sits in a bubble at the bottom like a moth in a jar. Not the assignment, but not nothing.}\nopt.right.say.1 [fa293d] = {~你用一层空气包住火，把它们一起沉下去。这算半个作弊，讲师也这么说。但它烧了一整节课。|你的火待在箱底的气泡里，像罐子里的飞蛾。不是作业要求的，但也不是什么都没有。}\n\n@event astro_sealed_forecast\n# en: Tomorrow's Weather\ntitle [fdb53c] = 明日天气\n# en: {Foresight at Astromancy II. Write down tomorrow's weather to the hour, seal it, pin it to the board. The lecturer opens the envelopes a day later, in public.|The envelopes come out again.|You've written so many forecasts you dream in drizzle.} {~Outside the dome the sky is giving nothing away.|Last week's best forecast just said \"Wet\", and was right.|{season == \"winter\": Frost on the dome, and snow somewhere in the future.|Clouds over the lake, moving with intent.}}\ntext [49b26a] = {星象学 II 的预见课。写下明天每个时辰的天气，封好，钉到板上。讲师第二天会当众拆信。|信封又拿出来了。|你写过太多预报，连做梦都在下毛毛雨。} {~穹顶外的天空什么都不肯透露。|上周最好的预报只写了一个“湿”字，而且对了。|{season == \"winter\": 穹顶上结了霜，未来某处有雪。|湖上的云正带着目的移动。}}\n# en: A row of sealed envelopes pinned to a corkboard in the observatory, each labelled with a student's name and tomorrow's date.\nscene [63cf64] = 观星台的软木板上钉着一排封好的信封，每只都写着学生的名字和明天的日期。\n# en: Commit to the hour\nopt.left.label [1bcf44] = 精确到时辰\n# en: {~\"Rain at the third bell, stopping at the fourth.\" It does. The lecturer reads it out twice.|You forecast a fog to the hour. When the envelope is opened, the fog is pressing on the dome.}\nopt.left.say.1 [f390a2] = {~“三更钟时下雨，四更钟停。”果然如此。讲师念了两遍。|你预报了一场准点的雾。信封拆开时，雾正贴在穹顶上。}\n# en: {~\"Clear by noon.\" It rains till evening. Your envelope is read out to great enjoyment.|You predicted wind. Tomorrow turns out so still that a feather on the sill doesn't move.}\nopt.left.say.2 [ebf3dc] = {~“午前转晴。”雨一直下到傍晚。你的信封被念出来，大家听得很开心。|你预报有风。结果第二天静得连窗台上的羽毛都没动一下。}\n# en: Hedge, and learn from it\nopt.right.label [53eefc] = 留有余地，边看边学\n# en: {~\"Some weather, at some point.\" The lecturer reads it out flatly and moves on. But you spent the hour watching how the good forecasters work.|You write a careful forecast with three possibilities. It's right, technically. The lecturer calls it \"an umbrella of a prophecy\".}\nopt.right.say.1 [ea7525] = {~“某个时候会有某种天气。”讲师面无表情地念完，继续下一个。但这一节课你都在看高手们怎么做。|你写了一份周全的预报，列了三种可能。严格说它是对的。讲师称之为“一把伞似的预言”。}\n\n@event astro_stubborn_telescope\n# en: The Stubborn Telescope\ntitle [2959bb] = 倔强的望远镜\n# en: {Astromancy II needs exact positions, and that means the great telescope. The great telescope points wherever it likes.|Your night on the great telescope. It is already pointing at something else.|The telescope swings toward you as you come in, like an old dog.} {~Tonight it wants the moon.|Tonight it's fixed on the lake, which is not in the sky.|Tonight it keeps drifting east, slowly, like a sigh.}\ntext [5010f6] = {星象学 II 需要精确的星位，这就得用大望远镜。可大望远镜爱指哪儿就指哪儿。|今晚轮到你用大望远镜。它已经指着别处了。|你一进门，望远镜就朝你转过来，像条老狗。} {~今晚它想看月亮。|今晚它盯着湖面，可湖不在天上。|今晚它一直慢慢往东偏，像一声叹息。}\n# en: A student tugging at a huge brass telescope in a dome, while it points stubbornly at a different patch of sky.\nscene [6f250f] = 穹顶下，一名学生拽着一架巨大的黄铜望远镜，它却固执地指着另一片天空。\n# en: Argue it round\nopt.left.label [dba882] = 把它劝回来\n# en: {~You coax it, inch by inch, and it settles on your star with a creak like a shrug.|You find the right thing to say to an old brass instrument. It points where you ask. You write the position fast.}\nopt.left.say.1 [2d5414] = {~你一寸一寸地哄它，它终于停在你的星上，嘎吱一声，像是耸了耸肩。|你找到了对一件老黄铜仪器该说的话。它指向你要的地方。你飞快地记下星位。}\n# en: {~It spends the night pointing where it likes. So do you, in the end, cold and annoyed.|You lean on it. It leans back. You go to bed at the fourth bell with nothing written down.}\nopt.left.say.2 [bec012] = {~它整晚都指着它想指的地方。最后你也只好陪着，又冷又恼。|你靠在它身上使劲，它也靠回来。四更钟你上床时，本子上一个字都没有。}\n# en: Work it out from tables\nopt.right.label [0fbd24] = 用星表推算\n# en: {~You leave the telescope to its moods and work the positions out by candlelight from the old tables. It takes until dawn.|Pages of sums by candle. The telescope watches over your shoulder, pointedly.}\nopt.right.say.1 [277798] = {~你随望远镜闹脾气去，借着烛光用旧星表把星位算出来。一直算到天亮。|一页又一页的烛下演算。望远镜在你肩后盯着，意味深长。}\n\n@event astro_long_watch\n# en: Keeping the Light\ntitle [72610b] = 守住星光\n# en: {Advanced Starlight. Hold one star's light in a lantern through a whole night on the roof. It wants to go home before dawn. Your job is to keep it.|The night watch on the roof again, a lantern and a blanket.|Your lantern, your star, your corner of the roof.} {~The star you've drawn is pale and blue.|The star you've drawn is small and stubborn.|The star you've drawn is very old and very faint.} {season == \"winter\": Frost on the slates.|The night is warm and full of moths.}\ntext [a2bec7] = {高等星光课。把一颗星的光装进灯笼，在屋顶守上一整夜。天亮前它想回家，你的任务是留住它。|又是屋顶守夜，一盏灯笼，一条毯子。|你的灯笼，你的星，你在屋顶的那个角落。} {~你抽到的星又淡又蓝。|你抽到的星又小又倔。|你抽到的星非常古老，非常微弱。} {season == \"winter\": 石板瓦上结了霜。|夜晚温暖，满是飞蛾。}\n# en: A student on the observatory roof before dawn, a glass lantern in their lap holding a single cold point of starlight.\nscene [efcd22] = 天亮前的观星台屋顶上，一名学生膝上放着一盏玻璃灯笼，里面装着一点冷冷的星光。\n# en: Keep it all night\nopt.left.label [828910] = 守它一整夜\n# en: {~At dawn your star is still in the lantern, blinking at the sun it was never meant to meet. You are blinking too.|You keep it till the lecturer comes up at sunrise. Your eyes are full of sand and your lantern is full of light.}\nopt.left.say.1 [e858a1] = {~天亮时你的星还在灯笼里，对着它本不该遇见的太阳眨眼。你也在眨眼。|你一直守到讲师日出时上来。你眼里全是沙，灯笼里全是光。}\n# en: Let it go at midnight\nopt.right.label [d7678c] = 午夜放它走\n# en: {~You open the lantern and the light goes up like a kite let loose. You watch it all the way home and then go to bed.|You let it go early. The lecturer's note just says \"Released at midnight.\" You suspect it's not a compliment.}\nopt.right.say.1 [33303b] = {~你打开灯笼，光像脱了线的风筝一样升上去。你一路目送它回家，然后去睡觉。|你早早放了它。讲师的记录上只写着“午夜放归”。你怀疑这不是夸奖。}\n# en: Feed it your own starlight\nopt.up.label [07c1bd] = 喂它你的星光\n# en: {~You pour a little of your own starlight into the lantern. The star brightens as if it had been lonely.|Your light and the star's together are enough to read by. You read until dawn.}\nopt.up.say.1 [09c47f] = {~你往灯笼里倒了一点自己的星光。那颗星亮了起来，好像它一直很寂寞。|你的光和星的光合在一起，足够看书了。你一直读到天亮。}\n\n@event astro_two_futures\n# en: Both Futures\ntitle [c2e1aa] = 两种未来\n# en: {Advanced Foresight. The lecturer balances a coin on its edge. Read both futures at once, the one where it falls heads and the one where it falls tails, and hold them both.|The coin on its edge again. You know how it feels to be two places at once now.|The coin. People come out of this lesson pressing their temples.} {~Someone in the front row says \"heads\" in two voices.|The lecturer is drinking tea and looking at nobody.|The candles burn straight up, very still.}\ntext [0bce58] = {高等预见课。讲师把一枚硬币立在边缘上。同时读出两种未来，正面落地的那个和反面落地的那个，并把两者都握住。|又是立着的硬币。如今你知道同时身处两地是什么感觉了。|硬币。走出这节课的人都按着太阳穴。} {~前排有人用两个声音说“正面”。|讲师在喝茶，谁也不看。|烛火笔直向上，一动不动。}\n# en: A coin balanced on edge on a dark table in the observatory, with two faint ghostly outcomes hovering above it.\nscene [137628] = 观星台一张深色桌上，一枚硬币立在边缘，上方悬浮着两个淡淡的、幽灵似的结果。\n# en: Hold both to the end\nopt.left.label [acd6d2] = 两者握到最后\n# en: {~For a long moment you live both futures, side by side, and neither is frightening. Then the coin falls, and one of them goes out.|You hold both until the lecturer flicks the coin. One future folds away, neat as a letter.}\nopt.left.say.1 [d7223e] = {~有那么长长的一刻，你并肩活在两种未来里，哪一种都不可怕。然后硬币倒下，其中一种熄灭了。|你一直握着两者，直到讲师弹倒硬币。一种未来折了起来，整齐得像一封信。}\n# en: {~The futures tangle and you get a headache that lasts till supper. For an hour you keep reaching for the wrong door handle.|You lose track of which future is which, and of which way is down. The lecturer sits you by an open window.}\nopt.left.say.2 [1d186d] = {~两种未来缠在一起，你头疼到晚饭。之后一个钟头你总是去拧错的门把手。|你分不清哪个未来是哪个了，也分不清哪边是下。讲师让你坐到开着的窗边。}\n# en: Read one, then the other\nopt.right.label [e61653] = 先读一个，再读另一个\n# en: {~You read heads, then tails, carefully, one after the other. It's not the exercise, but you get both right.|One future at a time. Slower, safer, and the lecturer says you'll get there.}\nopt.right.say.1 [d51bf0] = {~你先读正面，再读反面，一个接一个，小心翼翼。这不是练习的要求，但两个你都读对了。|一次一个未来。更慢，更稳，讲师说你会做到的。}\n\n@event alch_unlabelled_vial\n# en: The Unlabelled Vial\ntitle [ec7cd4] = 没有标签的药瓶\n# en: {Alchemy II hands out an unlabelled vial each, and an hour to say what's in it. Paper strips, a candle, a nose and a notebook are allowed.|Another unlabelled vial. The lecturer enjoys these.|The vial is waiting on your bench. You've started recognising the corks.} {~Yours is perfectly clear and smells faintly of pears.|Yours is cloudy and keeps settling into layers.|Yours is warm to the touch.} {~The ventilation charm is wheezing again.|The lab is quiet except for scratching quills.}\ntext [1cf5b6] = {炼金 II 发给每人一只没有标签的药瓶，一个钟头内说出里面是什么。试纸、蜡烛、鼻子和笔记本都可以用。|又是没标签的药瓶。讲师很享受这个。|药瓶在你桌上等着。你已经开始认得那些软木塞了。} {~你的这瓶清澈透明，隐约有股梨香。|你的这瓶浑浊，总是沉淀分层。|你的这瓶摸上去是温的。} {~通风咒又在喘了。|实验室里很安静，只有羽毛笔沙沙作响。}\n# en: A vial of clear liquid on a lab bench beside a strip of paper turning slowly purple, a student squinting at both.\nscene [3b7b2e] = 实验台上放着一瓶透明液体，旁边一条试纸正慢慢变紫，一名学生眯着眼看着两者。\n# en: Test it the long way\nopt.left.label [2f891a] = 老老实实逐项测\n# en: {~Strip, flame, cold, filter. By the end of the hour you've a name and three reasons for it.|Every test, in order, written up neatly. Your notes are dull and your answer is right.}\nopt.left.say.1 [19c35c] = {~试纸、火焰、冷却、过滤。一个钟头结束时，你有了一个名字和三条理由。|每一项测试，按部就班，写得工工整整。你的笔记很无聊，答案是对的。}\n# en: Trust your nose\nopt.right.label [c495ff] = 相信你的鼻子\n# en: {~One sniff and you know it. You write the name and use the spare hour to check yourself.|You tip the vial under your nose and the answer arrives before the smell does. The lecturer raises an eyebrow and a tick.}\nopt.right.say.1 [aaf34f] = {~一闻你就知道了。你写下名字，用多出来的一个钟头检查自己。|你把瓶子凑到鼻子下，答案比气味先到。讲师挑了挑眉，打了个勾。}\n# en: {~One sniff and your eyes stream for the rest of the morning. You guessed wrong, too.|It's not what you thought, and it is very much up your nose. You spend the hour at the window.}\nopt.right.say.2 [6d34ad] = {~一闻之下，你整个上午都在流眼泪。而且你还猜错了。|它不是你以为的东西，而且结结实实冲进了你的鼻子。这一节课你都待在窗边。}\n\n@event alch_cracked_tray\n# en: The Cracked Tray\ntitle [a94d69] = 一盘裂物\n# en: {Mend at Alchemy II. Sister Amaranth sets out a tray of cracked things, cup, mirror and bone. \"Mend those,\" she says, \"and then we'll see about people.\"|The tray again. Sister Amaranth has found new things to crack.|Sister Amaranth puts the tray down and nods at it. You know the drill.} {~A second-year is waiting on a cot with a sprained wrist from broom practice.|A third-year with a scalded hand from Evocation is waiting.|A first-year is in with a split lip and a long story about a door.}\ntext [70946e] = {炼金 II 的愈合课。阿玛兰斯修女摆出一盘裂开的东西：杯子、镜子、骨头。“先把这些补好，”她说，“然后再说人。”|又是这盘东西。阿玛兰斯修女找到了新的可以弄裂的东西。|阿玛兰斯修女放下托盘，朝它点点头。你知道该怎么做。} {~一个二年级生坐在病床上等着，手腕是练飞帚时扭的。|一个在塑能课上烫伤手的三年级生在等着。|一个一年级生嘴唇裂了，正讲着一个关于门的长故事。}\n# en: A tray in the infirmary laid with a cracked teacup, a cracked hand mirror and a cracked bone from a teaching skeleton, a student's hands hovering over them.\nscene [a85abf] = 医务室里一只托盘，放着一只裂开的茶杯、一面裂开的手镜和一根教学骨架上裂开的骨头，一名学生的双手悬在它们上方。\n# en: Mend the tray, perfectly\nopt.left.label [9eeb5c] = 把托盘补得完美\n# en: {~The cracks close like seams being sewn shut. Sister Amaranth runs a thumbnail along the cup and finds nothing.|Cup, mirror, bone, whole. Sister Amaranth says \"Fine,\" which is her highest word.}\nopt.left.say.1 [78b3f8] = {~裂缝像被缝上的接缝一样合拢。阿玛兰斯修女用拇指甲沿杯沿划过去，什么也没摸到。|杯子、镜子、骨头，全都完好。阿玛兰斯修女说“还行”，这是她的最高评价。}\n# en: {~The cup mends with a ridge in it, the mirror with a ripple. Sister Amaranth sets them on the shelf of shame.|You mend the bone a little crooked. Sister Amaranth says that's how you learn and puts it back in the skeleton anyway.}\nopt.left.say.2 [f3c225] = {~杯子补好了，却留了一道棱；镜子补好了，却起了一道波纹。阿玛兰斯修女把它们放上了耻辱架。|你把骨头补得有点歪。阿玛兰斯修女说这就是学习，还是把它装回了骨架。}\n# en: Mend the patient instead\nopt.right.label [c58841] = 改去治病人\n# en: {~You skip the tray and take the patient, with Sister Amaranth's hand on your shoulder. It goes slowly. It works.|Sister Amaranth lets you try on the patient while she watches. You're shaky and slow, and you'll never mend a cup the same way again.}\nopt.right.say.1 [f141e7] = {~你跳过托盘，接手病人，阿玛兰斯修女的手搭在你肩上。进展很慢，但成了。|阿玛兰斯修女看着你在病人身上试。你手抖，动作慢，而且从此再也不会用原来的方式补杯子了。}\n\n@event alch_lake_fever\n# en: Lake Fever Season\ntitle [5e2ca1] = 湖热季\n# en: {Advanced Alchemy treats real patients. The infirmary has Lake Fever cases, from swimming where they shouldn't. Sister Amaranth wants the fever drawn out, not just cooled.|Lake Fever again. Students never learn, Sister Amaranth says, and neither do lakes.|Fever duty. Sister Amaranth hands you a list without looking up.} {~Your patient says they only went in up to the knees.|Your patient is a prefect, very embarrassed.|Your patient insists they were pushed.}\ntext [b9312a] = {高等炼金要治真正的病人。医务室里有湖热病例，都是在不该游泳的地方游了泳。阿玛兰斯修女要你把热病拔出来，而不只是降温。|又是湖热。修女说，学生从不长记性，湖也不长。|退热值班。阿玛兰斯修女头也不抬地递给你一张名单。} {~你的病人说他们只下水到膝盖。|你的病人是一位级长，非常难为情。|你的病人坚称是被人推下去的。}\n# en: A row of infirmary cots, students under blankets looking flushed and sorry for themselves, an advanced student drawing a faint grey haze out of one patient's brow.\nscene [4db536] = 一排医务室病床，学生们裹着毯子，满脸通红，可怜兮兮；一名高年级生正从一位病人额头上抽出一缕淡淡的灰雾。\n# en: Draw the fever out by hand\nopt.left.label [cf9f68] = 亲手把热拔出来\n# en: {~The fever comes out like a grey thread pulled from wool. Your patient sleeps. You'd like to.|You draw it out slowly and the flush leaves their face. Sister Amaranth checks your work and doesn't check twice.}\nopt.left.say.1 [c41138] = {~热病像从羊毛里抽出的一根灰线，被你拉了出来。病人睡着了。你也很想睡。|你慢慢把它抽出来，潮红从他们脸上退去。阿玛兰斯修女检查了你的活儿，没有检查第二遍。}\n# en: {~You draw out half of it and the half you drew comes with you. You spend the evening shivering in the next cot.|The cure has to come from somewhere. You wake up two hours later on the floor, with a blanket on you.}\nopt.left.say.2 [d17be0] = {~你只拔出了一半，而拔出来的那一半跟上了你。整个晚上你都在隔壁病床上发抖。|治愈总得从某处来。两个钟头后你在地板上醒来，身上盖着一条毯子。}\n# en: Brew the slow tonic\nopt.right.label [af7952] = 熬慢效补剂\n# en: {~Three hours of stirring willow bark and lake moss. The tonic works by morning, which is slower and safer.|You brew the old remedy by the book. Sister Amaranth tastes it, grimaces, and approves.}\nopt.right.say.1 [83c7b0] = {~三个钟头搅着柳树皮和湖苔。补剂到早上才见效，慢一些，也稳一些。|你照书熬了老方子。阿玛兰斯修女尝了一口，皱了皱脸，点头认可。}\n# en: Purify it in one pass\nopt.up.label [f808f7] = 一次净化干净\n# en: {~Your spell pulls the fever clean in a single pass. It leaves you yawning.|One pass of Purify and the patient sits up asking for soup. The tiredness lands on you an hour later.}\nopt.up.say.1 [ae2628] = {~你的咒语一遍就把热病拔得干干净净。它让你直打哈欠。|一遍净化，病人就坐起来要汤喝。一个钟头后，疲倦落到了你身上。}\n\n@event alch_third_floor_still\n# en: The Still Cracks\ntitle [7ecae5] = 蒸馏器裂了\n# en: {Advanced Alchemy distils essences that would rather be gas. The ventilation charm gave up on the third floor years ago. Today your still cracks.|Distilling day, and a hiss from your still that you know.|Your still has cracked again. You're getting fast at noticing.} {~The vapour is green and smells of mint.|The vapour is pale and smells of nothing, which is worse.|The vapour is curling toward the next bench.}\ntext [523dd8] = {高等炼金要蒸馏那些宁愿当气体的精华。三楼的通风咒多年前就放弃了。今天你的蒸馏器裂了。|蒸馏日，你的蒸馏器又发出那种你熟悉的嘶嘶声。|你的蒸馏器又裂了。你发现得越来越快了。} {~蒸汽是绿的，有薄荷味。|蒸汽颜色很淡，什么味道都没有，这更糟。|蒸汽正卷向隔壁的实验台。}\n# en: A copper still on a long bench, hissing from a fresh crack, a thin green vapour creeping across the laboratory floor.\nscene [e5be1b] = 长实验台上一只铜蒸馏器从新裂口里嘶嘶作响，一缕淡绿色的蒸汽正爬过实验室的地板。\n# en: Mend the crack, still hot\nopt.left.label [127d5d] = 趁热把裂缝补上\n# en: {~You close the copper under your palms while it's still singing. The hiss stops. The class breathes out.|The crack seals with a sound like a sigh, and the batch is saved. The lecturer opens a window, for form's sake.}\nopt.left.say.1 [691832] = {~铜还在嗡鸣，你就用掌心把它合上了。嘶声停了，全班松了口气。|裂缝合拢，发出一声叹息般的轻响，这一批保住了。讲师还是开了扇窗，走个形式。}\n# en: {~The crack closes, then reopens with a pop. You breathe in a mouthful and spend the afternoon seeing double.|You burn your fingertips on the copper and the vapour gets out anyway. The lab is cleared for an hour.}\nopt.left.say.2 [0658f2] = {~裂缝合上了，又“噗”地一声裂开。你吸进一大口，整个下午看东西都是重影。|你的指尖被铜烫伤，蒸汽还是跑了出来。实验室清场一个钟头。}\n# en: Shout, and clear the bench\nopt.right.label [17c853] = 大喊一声，清空台面\n# en: {~You call the warning and clear the bench. The batch is lost, nobody is hurt, and you start again next week.|Everyone is out in the corridor in a minute flat. The lecturer goes back in, a scarf over the mouth, and comes out carrying your ruined batch.}\nopt.right.say.1 [3808be] = {~你喊出警告，清空了台面。这一批毁了，没人受伤，你下周重来。|一分钟内所有人都到了走廊上。讲师用围巾捂着口鼻回去，把你那批报废的东西端了出来。}\n\n@event illu_walking_double\n# en: The Walking Double\ntitle [188b9c] = 会走的分身\n# en: {Mirror at Illusion II. Make a double of yourself and walk it across the room. \"Standing doubles are furniture,\" says Professor Vey. \"Make it walk.\"|Doubles day. Vey watches feet, not faces.|Your double's walk is improving. It has your slouch now.} {~The floor has been dusted with chalk, to show footprints.|Someone's double is walking backwards.|Vey is sitting in the front row, which is unsettling.}\ntext [8f6cbb] = {幻术 II 的镜像课。给自己造一个分身，让它走过教室。“站着的分身是家具，”维伊教授说，“让它走。”|分身日。维伊看脚，不看脸。|你的分身走得越来越好了。它现在连你的驼背都学会了。} {~地上撒了粉笔灰，好显出脚印。|有人的分身在倒着走。|维伊坐在第一排，这让人心里发毛。}\n# en: Two identical students walking side by side across a lecture hall, one of them leaving no footprints in the chalk dust.\nscene [45f81a] = 两个一模一样的学生并肩走过讲堂，其中一个在粉笔灰上没留下脚印。\n# en: Walk it right across\nopt.left.label [8d74d0] = 让它走完全程\n# en: {~Your double crosses the room at your pace and even scuffs the chalk. Vey watches its feet the whole way and says nothing, which is high praise.|It walks, it turns, it nearly sits down. Vey says, \"Better.\"}\nopt.left.say.1 [24aa13] = {~你的分身按你的步子穿过教室，甚至蹭花了粉笔灰。维伊一路盯着它的脚，什么也没说，这是很高的赞扬。|它走，它转身，它差点坐下。维伊说：“好些了。”}\n# en: {~Halfway across, your double's legs forget to move and it glides. Vey says it's a ghost, not a person, and moves on.|Your double walks into the wall and keeps walking. The class watches it vanish into the plaster.}\nopt.left.say.2 [3d1cad] = {~走到一半，分身的腿忘了动，开始飘。维伊说那是鬼，不是人，然后走开了。|你的分身撞上墙，还在继续走。全班看着它消失在灰泥里。}\n# en: Keep it still and perfect\nopt.right.label [7cb3fb] = 让它站着，完美无缺\n# en: {~Your double stands at your elbow, perfect to the last button. It doesn't move. Vey calls it a very good wardrobe.|You make it flawless and keep it standing. Vey taps its nose, and frowns when it holds.}\nopt.right.say.1 [ff4bc4] = {~你的分身站在你手肘边，连最后一颗纽扣都完美。它不动。维伊称它是一件很好的衣橱。|你把它做得天衣无缝，然后让它站着。维伊敲了敲它的鼻子，发现敲得到，皱起了眉。}\n\n@event illu_good_news\n# en: Good News, Read Aloud\ntitle [0d109c] = 当众念好消息\n# en: {Glamour at Illusion II, and today it's the voice. Vey hands you a dull notice and asks you to read it so the class hears good news.|Another notice from Vey's drawer of very dull notices.|Vey has saved the dullest notice for you.} {~Today's is about the drains.|Today's is about a change to the laundry rota.|Today's is a list of overdue library books, in full.}\ntext [02419d] = {幻术 II 的魅影课，今天练的是声音。维伊递给你一份枯燥的通知，要你念得让全班听成好消息。|又是一份来自维伊那抽屉超级枯燥通知的通知。|维伊把最枯燥的那份留给了你。} {~今天这份讲的是排水沟。|今天这份讲的是洗衣轮值表的调整。|今天这份是图书馆逾期书目，全文。}\n# en: A student standing at a lectern reading from a dull notice while the listening class smiles as though hearing wonderful news.\nscene [74b6a2] = 一名学生站在讲台前念着一份枯燥的通知，台下的同学们却笑容满面，像在听天大的好消息。\n# en: Glamour every word\nopt.left.label [3eefc7] = 字字施魅影\n# en: {~By the second line the room is beaming. Someone claps. Vey waits for the clapping to stop and asks what the notice said. Nobody knows.|You read it warm and low, and the class leans in as if you were reading their letters from home.}\nopt.left.say.1 [4d2fd3] = {~念到第二行，满屋子都笑开了。有人鼓掌。维伊等掌声停了，问通知说了什么。没人知道。|你念得温暖而低沉，全班都探过身来，好像你在念他们的家书。}\n# en: {~Your glamour slips on a long word and the room hears exactly what it is: a notice. Someone yawns on purpose.|You overdo it. The class looks alarmed, as if the drains were about to propose.}\nopt.left.say.2 [8eb96f] = {~魅影在一个长词上滑脱，全屋听到的正是它本来的样子：一份通知。有人故意打了个哈欠。|你用力过猛。全班一脸惊恐，好像排水沟要求婚了。}\n# en: Read it plain, watch others\nopt.right.label [c74a90] = 照念，看别人\n# en: {~You read it straight and sit down. Then you watch the others, and notice the best of them barely change their voices at all.|Plain reading, polite applause. You spend the hour writing down where the good ones breathe.}\nopt.right.say.1 [199d5f] = {~你照原样念完坐下。然后你看别人念，发现最好的那几个几乎没怎么改变嗓音。|平平地念，礼貌的掌声。这一节课你都在记高手们在哪里换气。}\n\n@event illu_false_room\n# en: The Room That Isn't\ntitle [04316d] = 不存在的房间\n# en: {Advanced Illusion asks for a double of a whole room. Hang it over the real one and walk a classmate through it. Vey's rule is simple. If anyone walks into a wall, it's your fault.|The room exercise. Somebody always walks into something.|You hang your room. You've learned where the real walls are.} {~Vey's volunteer today is a porter with a tray of cups.|Vey's volunteer today is Vey.|The real room has been rearranged overnight.}\ntext [1a6ffb] = {高等幻术要你造出一整个房间的分身，罩在真房间上，再让一个同学从中走过。维伊的规矩很简单。谁撞了墙，都算你的错。|房间练习。总有人会撞上点什么。|你把房间挂上去。你已经学会记住真墙在哪儿。} {~维伊今天的志愿者是一位端着一托盘杯子的门房。|维伊今天的志愿者是维伊。|真房间昨夜被重新布置过了。}\n# en: A student walking confidently into a room that is partly painted light, a real stone wall standing where the illusion shows an open doorway.\nscene [487838] = 一名学生自信地走进一个部分由光绘成的房间，幻象里敞开的门洞处，其实立着一堵真正的石墙。\n# en: Hang the whole room\nopt.left.label [f08ac4] = 挂上整个房间\n# en: {~Your walker crosses the false room and sits down in a false chair, which you hold up for them. Vey writes for a long time.|The room holds. Nobody touches a real wall. Vey walks it twice to be sure.}\nopt.left.say.1 [a782a4] = {~走的人穿过假房间，坐上一把假椅子，你替他们撑住了它。维伊写了很久。|房间撑住了。没人碰到一堵真墙。维伊走了两遍才确认。}\n# en: {~Your walker steps through a door you painted onto solid stone. You take them to Sister Amaranth with an apology.|Halfway across the room flickers, and the walker freezes. So do you, and you knock over a real chair.}\nopt.left.say.2 [4fe54c] = {~走的人迈进了你画在实心石头上的门。你带着歉意把他们送到阿玛兰斯修女那里。|走到一半房间闪了一下，走的人僵住了。你也僵住了，还撞翻了一把真椅子。}\n# en: Chalk the real walls first\nopt.right.label [26c22a] = 先用粉笔标出真墙\n# en: {~You hang half a room, with the real walls marked out in chalk so nobody could miss them. Vey calls it \"a room with a safety rope\".|Safe and visible, chalk lines everywhere. Vey sighs, and then copies down how you did the window.}\nopt.right.say.1 [2ca912] = {~你只挂了半个房间，真墙都用粉笔标了出来，谁也不会错过。维伊称之为“系了安全绳的房间”。|安全，显眼，到处是粉笔线。维伊叹了口气，然后抄下了你做窗户的方法。}\n\n@event illu_peeling_layers\n# en: Seeing Through\ntitle [014ea1] = 看穿\n# en: {Advanced Illusion. To make illusions you must see through them. Vey layers the hall in false rooms, one on another, and you peel them back one at a time.|Layer day. The hall looks normal. That's never a good sign.|You count the layers when you come in. You're usually wrong.} {~The top layer has velvet curtains.|The top layer is a summer garden.|The top layer is the hall itself, just slightly too tidy.}\ntext [9cbf59] = {高等幻术。要造幻象，就得先学会看穿幻象。维伊把讲堂层层罩上假房间，一层叠一层，你要一层一层揭开。|分层日。讲堂看起来很正常。这从来不是好兆头。|你一进门就数层数。你通常数错。} {~最上面一层挂着天鹅绒帘子。|最上面一层是夏日花园。|最上面一层就是讲堂本身，只是整洁得有点过分。}\n# en: A lecture hall in layers like peeled paper: one layer with velvet curtains, one bare stone, one darker one beneath, and a student standing at its centre.\nscene [ee496c] = 一间像剥开的纸一样分层的讲堂：一层挂着天鹅绒帘子，一层是裸露的石头，下面还有更暗的一层，一名学生站在中央。\n# en: Peel down to the stone\nopt.left.label [12cf64] = 一直揭到石头\n# en: {~Seven layers, then bare stone. Vey asks how many. You say seven. She smiles and says eight, and points at herself.|You reach the stone and stay there, sure of it. Vey nods slowly. \"Hold on to that,\" she says.}\nopt.left.say.1 [2363f2] = {~七层，然后是裸石。维伊问几层。你说七层。她笑着说八层，指了指自己。|你揭到石头，并且停在那里，确信无疑。维伊慢慢点头。“抓住这个感觉，”她说。}\n# en: {~You peel one too many and the stone underneath looks false. For the rest of the day you knock on walls.|After the fourth layer you stop being sure which way is up. Vey walks you to a chair, which is real.}\nopt.left.say.2 [f13805] = {~你多揭了一层，底下的石头看起来也像假的。这一整天你都在敲墙。|揭到第四层后，你再也不确定哪边是上。维伊把你领到一把椅子前，那把椅子是真的。}\n# en: Stop at the third layer\nopt.right.label [b16cdd] = 揭到第三层就停\n# en: {~Three layers and you stop, dizzy. Vey says knowing when to stop is the other half of the skill.|You get through three and sit down. The hall still looks too tidy, and you decide not to ask.}\nopt.right.say.1 [e7a29e] = {~三层，你停下来，头晕目眩。维伊说，知道何时停下是这门技艺的另一半。|你揭了三层就坐下了。讲堂看起来还是太整洁，你决定不问。}\n\n@event beast_hedge_run\n# en: The Hedge Run\ntitle [d8e2dc] = 越篱飞行\n# en: {Beastcraft II puts haste on a broom, low over the hedges, as fast as you dare. Professor Brask says brooms are harder to reason with than hippogriffs, and harder to apologise to.|The hedge run. Your broom remembers last time.|Hedges. You've learned to read a broom's sulk through the handle.} {~Brask is timing with a crooked hourglass.|The hedges have been trimmed, unevenly.|{season == \"autumn\": Leaves come up behind every broom.|{season == \"spring\": The hedges are full of nesting birds who object.|The ground is hard and unforgiving.}}}\ntext [b5da23] = {驭兽学 II 把疾速用在飞帚上，贴着树篱低飞，能多快就多快。布拉斯克教授说，飞帚比鹰头马身有翼兽更难讲道理，也更难道歉。|越篱飞行。你的飞帚记得上一次。|树篱。你已经学会从帚柄上读出飞帚的闷气。} {~布拉斯克拿着一只歪沙漏计时。|树篱修剪过了，修得高低不平。|{season == \"autumn\": 每把飞帚身后都扬起落叶。|{season == \"spring\": 树篱里满是筑巢的鸟，它们很有意见。|地面又硬又不饶人。}}}\n# en: Students on brooms skimming low over a line of hedges in a field, one broom bucking as its rider leans forward.\nscene [a6f163] = 学生们骑着飞帚贴着田里的一排树篱掠过，其中一把飞帚在骑手前倾时尥起了蹶子。\n# en: Haste at the last hedge\nopt.left.label [73bbd3] = 最后一道篱前加速\n# en: {~You borrow a hawk's speed for the last hedge and clear it by a hand. Brask stops the hourglass and nods.|The broom flinches, then commits. You come in fastest of the morning, with hedge in your hair.}\nopt.left.say.1 [10e673] = {~你借来鹰的速度飞越最后一道树篱，高出一掌。布拉斯克停了沙漏，点了点头。|飞帚一缩，然后豁出去了。你是这个早上最快的，头发里还挂着树篱。}\n# en: {~The broom bucks at the haste and you go over the hedge without it.|You're fast. The broom is faster, briefly, and then upside down. You land in a bramble.}\nopt.left.say.2 [56e7c7] = {~飞帚被疾速一惊，你独自越过了树篱，它没跟上。|你很快。飞帚短暂地比你更快，然后翻了个底朝天。你落进了荆棘丛。}\n# en: Fly it clean and steady\nopt.right.label [caf423] = 飞得干净平稳\n# en: {~No haste, just a clean low line over every hedge. Brask says it's the best boring flying she's seen all week.|You fly it slow and tidy. The broom approves. Brask is less impressed.}\nopt.right.say.1 [5440b7] = {~不用疾速，每道树篱都是一条干净的低线。布拉斯克说这是她这周见过最好的无聊飞行。|你飞得又慢又整齐。飞帚很满意。布拉斯克没那么满意。}\n\n@event beast_owl_post\n# en: Teaching the Owls\ntitle [ba75dc] = 教猫头鹰送信\n# en: {Beast-speech at Beastcraft II. The young owls must learn the post routes. Brask hands you one and says, \"Explain the castle to it.\"|A new young owl, and the same castle to explain.|Another owl. You've got a speech now, and a map scratched on a beam.} {~Your owl wants to know why there are so many towers.|Your owl has strong opinions about the library.|Your owl keeps falling asleep, standing up.}\ntext [9ed908] = {驭兽学 II 的兽语课。小猫头鹰们得学会送信的路线。布拉斯克递给你一只，说：“把城堡给它讲清楚。”|又一只小猫头鹰，又是同一座城堡要讲。|又一只猫头鹰。你如今有一套讲稿，还在梁上刻了张地图。} {~你的猫头鹰想知道为什么塔有这么多座。|你的猫头鹰对图书馆有很强烈的看法。|你的猫头鹰总是站着就睡着了。}\n# en: A student in a stable loft with a young owl on their arm, pointing out of a high window toward a distant tower.\nscene [3d64bf] = 马厩阁楼里，一名学生手臂上停着一只小猫头鹰，正指着高窗外远处的一座塔。\n# en: Explain it in owl\nopt.left.label [27c58e] = 用猫头鹰的话讲\n# en: {~You describe the castle in winds, perches and mice. The owl blinks, and goes and comes back with Brask's letter.|It takes a while to get \"left\" across to a bird. When it does, the owl does the route twice to show off.}\nopt.left.say.1 [7415ba] = {~你用风、栖木和老鼠来描述城堡。猫头鹰眨眨眼，飞出去，带回了布拉斯克的信。|要让一只鸟明白“左”可得花点工夫。等它明白了，它把路线飞了两遍来炫耀。}\n# en: {~The owl listens closely, then flies to the kitchens. Every time.|The owl makes you explain \"tower\" seven times and then goes to sleep on your head.}\nopt.left.say.2 [0a58cc] = {~猫头鹰听得很认真，然后飞去了厨房。每一次都是。|猫头鹰让你把“塔”解释了七遍，然后在你头上睡着了。}\n# en: Walk the route with it\nopt.right.label [beabeb] = 带它走一遍路线\n# en: {~You walk the whole post route with the owl on your arm, up every stair. Your legs ache and the owl has learned it.|Up and down the castle with an owl on your wrist. People stare. The owl stares back.}\nopt.right.say.1 [71dce0] = {~你让猫头鹰停在手臂上，把整条送信路线走了一遍，每段楼梯都爬了。你腿疼，它学会了。|胳膊上架着一只猫头鹰，在城堡里上上下下。人们盯着看。猫头鹰盯回去。}\n\n@event beast_hooves\n# en: Hoof Day\ntitle [dcf707] = 修蹄日\n# en: {Advanced Beastcraft does the work nobody else wants. Today it's hooves. A hippogriff's need trimming each month, and the hippogriff disagrees.|Hoof day. The stall is the same; the hippogriff's opinion is too.|Hooves. You go in with a bucket of apples and a plan.} {~Today's hippogriff is grey and patient.|Today's is young and very sure of itself.|Today's is old and wants to tell you about it.}\ntext [f259e2] = {高等驭兽学干的是没人想干的活。今天是修蹄。鹰头马身有翼兽的蹄子每月要修一次，而它们不同意。|修蹄日。马棚还是那个马棚，它的意见也还是那个意见。|修蹄。你带着一桶苹果和一个计划进去。} {~今天这只是灰色的，很有耐心。|今天这只很年轻，非常自以为是。|今天这只很老，想跟你聊聊。}\n# en: A student crouched beside a hippogriff in a stall, a hoof trimming knife in hand, the hippogriff looking down with one sceptical eye.\nscene [e931b9] = 一名学生蹲在马棚里一只鹰头马身有翼兽旁边，手里拿着修蹄刀，那只兽用一只怀疑的眼睛俯视着。\n# en: Talk it into lifting a foot\nopt.left.label [33c422] = 劝它抬起蹄子\n# en: {~It listens to your case and lifts a hoof with enormous dignity. Brask watches from the door and says nothing.|You talk it through every hoof. It sighs at you between each one, like a patient uncle.}\nopt.left.say.1 [83bd83] = {~它听完你的理由，极有尊严地抬起一只蹄子。布拉斯克在门口看着，什么也没说。|你一只一只蹄子地跟它谈。每修完一只它就冲你叹口气，像个耐心的叔叔。}\n# en: {~It lets you get all the way to the last hoof before stepping on your foot.|It hears you out, then sits on you. Brask lifts it off with one hand and a word.}\nopt.left.say.2 [dbd5ce] = {~它一直等到你修最后一只蹄子，才踩了你的脚。|它听你说完，然后坐在了你身上。布拉斯克一只手加一句话就把它挪开了。}\n# en: Rope, and Brask holding\nopt.right.label [34c6c0] = 上绳子，请布拉斯克按着\n# en: {~The old way: a rope, a strong arm, Brask at its head. It's sweaty work and nobody enjoys it, including the hippogriff.|With Brask holding the head and you holding on, you finish every hoof. Your shoulders will remember it.}\nopt.right.say.1 [ac6b75] = {~老办法：一根绳，一条有力的胳膊，布拉斯克按着它的头。又累又满身汗，谁都不喜欢，包括那只兽。|布拉斯克按着头，你死死抓着，每只蹄子都修完了。你的肩膀会记住这一天。}\n\n@event beast_night_stable\n# en: Sitting Up With the Sick\ntitle [789593] = 守着病兽\n# en: {Advanced students take turns sitting up in the stables when a creature is ill. Brask leaves you a lantern, a blanket and a list.|Your turn on the night watch, and the list is shorter.|The night watch. The owls are used to you now and only mutter.} {~Tonight it's the griffon chick, off its food.|Tonight it's an old owl with a cough.|Tonight it's a hippogriff who ate something it shouldn't.} {season == \"winter\": The stable is cold enough to see your breath.|It's warm in the straw.}\ntext [5beaf4] = {有生物生病时，高年级生轮流在马厩守夜。布拉斯克给你留了一盏灯笼、一条毯子和一张清单。|又轮到你守夜，清单短了些。|守夜。猫头鹰们已经习惯你了，只是嘀咕几声。} {~今晚是不肯吃东西的狮鹫雏鸟。|今晚是一只咳嗽的老猫头鹰。|今晚是一只吃了不该吃的东西的鹰头马身有翼兽。} {season == \"winter\": 马厩冷得能看见自己的呼气。|干草堆里很暖和。}\n# en: A student sitting in straw by lantern light, a sick creature curled beside them, the rest of the stables asleep.\nscene [88ac60] = 一名学生借着灯笼光坐在干草里，一只生病的生物蜷在身旁，马厩里其余的一切都睡着了。\n# en: Sit up all night\nopt.left.label [464a6d] = 守它一整夜\n# en: {~You don't sleep, and by dawn it's breathing easier. Brask finds you asleep in the straw and leaves you there.|You watch it all night, changing the water and the straw. In the morning it eats, and so do you, enormously.}\nopt.left.say.1 [9b8414] = {~你一夜没睡，天亮时它呼吸顺畅了些。布拉斯克发现你睡在干草里，就让你接着睡。|你看了它一整夜，换水，换干草。早上它吃东西了，你也吃了，吃了很多。}\n# en: Ask it where it hurts\nopt.right.label [b5e61b] = 问它哪里疼\n# en: {~It tells you, in its own way, exactly where it hurts. You write it on Brask's list and both of you sleep.|It points with its whole body. You bring the right tonic and it drops off before the lantern does.}\nopt.right.say.1 [0b1a09] = {~它用自己的方式准确地告诉了你哪里疼。你写在布拉斯克的清单上，你们俩都睡了。|它用整个身子指给你看。你拿来对的补剂，它比灯笼先睡着。}\n# en: {~It tells you a great deal, about its neighbours, the hay and the moon, and nothing about where it hurts.|It won't talk to you. It talks to the lantern. You stay up anyway, which is the point.}\nopt.right.say.2 [5042ff] = {~它跟你讲了很多，关于邻居、干草和月亮，就是没讲哪里疼。|它不跟你说话。它跟灯笼说话。你还是守着，这才是要点。}\n# en: Speak with it properly\nopt.up.label [70fe7d] = 好好跟它说说话\n# en: {~With the spell, it's easy: it explains the pain, the food, and what it thinks of the night. You fetch the right remedy.|You speak properly, and it answers properly, slowly, like someone who's been waiting. You both feel better.}\nopt.up.say.1 [84fa85] = {~有了咒语就容易了：它讲了疼在哪儿，吃了什么，还有它对这一夜的看法。你取来了对的药。|你好好地说，它也好好地答，慢慢的，像等了很久的人。你们俩都好受些了。}\n\n@event umbral_ward_candle\n# en: Ward the Candle\ntitle [b4ebd5] = 护住蜡烛\n# en: {Ward at Umbral Theory II. Ward a candle while your partner tries to put it out. Today that's {partner}.|Candle wards again, and the lamps are lower. {partner} is across the bench.|The candle exercise. You're paired with {partner}, who rolls up {their} sleeves.} {~The professor walks between the benches with a hand over the flames.|The room smells of wax and burnt thread.|Someone's ward has shrunk to the size of a thimble.}\ntext [1361ca] = {暗影理论 II 的护界课。给一根蜡烛设下护界，同时你的搭档设法把它弄灭。今天的搭档是{partner}。|又是蜡烛护界，灯压得更低了。{partner}坐在桌子对面。|蜡烛练习。你和{partner}一组，{they}卷起了{their}袖子。} {~教授在桌间走动，一只手从火焰上方掠过。|屋里一股蜡和烧焦线头的味道。|有人的护界缩得只有顶针大小。}\n# en: Two students at a dim bench under low lamps, a candle between them inside a faint pale shell, one student trying to blow it out.\nscene [5a828b] = 低垂的灯下，两名学生坐在昏暗的长桌前，中间一根蜡烛罩在一层淡淡的苍白外壳里，其中一人正试图把它吹灭。\n# en: A thick, honest shell\nopt.left.label [53a438] = 一层厚实老实的壳\n# en: {~Your ward is plain and solid. {partner} blows and blows and the candle doesn't even lean.|Strong and dull. {partner} gives up and the two of you watch the flame burn down.}\nopt.left.say.1 [67f7b2] = {~你的护界朴素而结实。{partner}吹了又吹，蜡烛连歪都没歪。|结实而乏味。{partner}放弃了，你们俩一起看着火焰燃尽。}\n# en: A ward that turns them aside\nopt.right.label [6b490b] = 设个把人引开的护界\n# en: {~Your ward turns {partner}'s breath round, and it blows {their} own hair into {their} eyes. The professor stops and says, \"Ah.\"|{partner} reaches for your candle and finds {their} hand on the wrong side of the bench. The professor approves.}\nopt.right.say.1 [f61054] = {~你的护界把{partner}的气息拐了个弯，吹得{their}头发糊了一脸。教授停下脚步，说：“啊。”|{partner}伸手去够你的蜡烛，却发现自己的手到了桌子另一边。教授表示赞许。}\n# en: {~Your clever ward turns aside the wrong thing, and the candle goes out with a smug little puff. {partner} smirks.|The ward bends {partner}'s breath straight into your face. You sneeze and the candle goes out.}\nopt.right.say.2 [903348] = {~你那个聪明的护界引开了错的东西，蜡烛得意地“噗”一声灭了。{partner}偷笑。|护界把{partner}的气息直直拐到你脸上。你打了个喷嚏，蜡烛灭了。}\n\n@event umbral_dark_roll\n# en: Who Moved\ntitle [133ff8] = 谁动了\n# en: {Umbral Theory II. The professor puts out every lamp, and in the dark one student moves. Say who, and where to.|Lamps out. You've learned to hear a sleeve.|The dark. You know the room's sounds, and which floorboard is a liar.} {~Rain on the tower roof makes it harder.|It's so quiet you can hear the stair breathing.|Somebody's stomach rumbles, which helps no one.}\ntext [d5133f] = {暗影理论 II。教授熄掉每一盏灯，黑暗中有一个学生会移动。说出是谁，挪到了哪里。|灯灭了。你已经学会听见一只袖子的声音。|黑暗。你熟悉这间屋子的声音，也知道哪块地板会撒谎。} {~雨打在塔顶，让这更难了。|安静得能听见楼梯在呼吸。|有人肚子咕咕叫，这对谁都没帮助。}\n# en: A pitch-dark classroom at the top of a tower, a few vague shapes of students sitting perfectly still in the gloom.\nscene [d2fcaa] = 塔顶一间漆黑的教室，几个模糊的学生身影一动不动地坐在昏暗里。\n# en: Listen, and name them\nopt.left.label [80bc08] = 听，然后说出名字\n# en: {~\"Third bench, to the window.\" Right. The professor relights a lamp and moves on.|You get it on the second try, by the creak of a bench and a faint smell of soap.}\nopt.left.say.1 [ed045d] = {~“第三排，往窗边去了。”对了。教授重新点亮一盏灯，继续下一轮。|第二次你猜中了，靠的是一张长凳的吱呀声和一丝淡淡的肥皂味。}\n# en: Be the one who moves\nopt.right.label [25e0c1] = 做那个移动的人\n# en: {~You cross the whole room in the dark and nobody names you. When the lamps come up you're behind the professor.|Nobody hears you. The professor says, \"Someone moved,\" and seems pleased not to know who.}\nopt.right.say.1 [8b1eff] = {~你在黑暗中穿过整个房间，没人叫出你的名字。灯亮起时你站在教授身后。|没人听见你。教授说：“有人动了，”似乎很高兴自己不知道是谁。}\n# en: {~You catch a desk with your shin. \"Window, limping,\" says everyone at once.|Your shoe squeaks on the second step. The whole class names you, with feeling.}\nopt.right.say.2 [b61389] = {~你的小腿撞上了桌子。“窗边，瘸着腿，”所有人异口同声。|你的鞋在第二级台阶上吱了一声。全班带着感情叫出了你的名字。}\n\n@event umbral_corridor_step\n# en: Across the Corridor\ntitle [d8d8cf] = 穿过走廊\n# en: {Advanced Umbral Theory. Shadowstep across the corridor, door to door, the farthest the professor allows. \"Short distances only,\" the professor says. \"This is as short as long gets.\"|The corridor. The cold in between is colder than last time.|You stand at the door with your heels on the line. You know the cold that's coming.} {~The lamps are out on the landing.|A draught is moving along the floor.|Someone came back from it with frost on one shoulder.}\ntext [7d6543] = {高等暗影理论。用影步穿过走廊，从这扇门到那扇门，这是教授允许的最远距离。“只能短距离，”教授说，“这已经是短里面最长的了。”|走廊。中间的寒冷比上次更冷。|你脚跟抵着线站在门口。你知道将要袭来的那股寒意。} {~楼梯平台上的灯灭了。|一股穿堂风贴着地面流动。|有人回来时一边肩膀上结着霜。}\n# en: A dim stone corridor at the top of a tower, a student half stepping out of the shadow of one doorway while their own shadow lingers in another across the hall.\nscene [e52a16] = 塔顶一条昏暗的石砌走廊，一名学生半个身子正从一扇门的阴影里跨出，而他们自己的影子还逗留在走廊对面的另一扇门里。\n# en: Step across\nopt.left.label [894fac] = 一步跨过去\n# en: {~One shadow, cold and long, and then the other. You come out on the far side shaking and grinning.|You step in by one door and out by the other, with a breath of frost on your collar. The professor writes your name, and a time.}\nopt.left.say.1 [d603ae] = {~一片影子，又冷又长，然后是另一片。你从对面出来，浑身发抖，咧着嘴笑。|你从一扇门进，从另一扇门出，衣领上带着一口霜气。教授写下你的名字和一个时间。}\n# en: {~The cold in between is longer than you thought. You come out in the right doorway and can't feel your hands for an hour.|You step and come out in the same doorway you went in by, with the feeling that something looked at you on the way.}\nopt.left.say.2 [0e59a5] = {~中间的寒冷比你想的更长。你从对的门口出来，一个钟头手都没有知觉。|你跨了一步，却从进去的那扇门出来了，总觉得路上有什么东西看了你一眼。}\n# en: Walk it, counting shadows\nopt.right.label [de4a9d] = 走过去，数着影子\n# en: {~You walk the corridor and count every shadow and every lamp. The professor calls this \"mapping your road\".|You pace it out and write down each shadow's depth. Next time you'll step it. Probably.}\nopt.right.say.1 [4ab040] = {~你走过走廊，数清每一片影子和每一盏灯。教授称之为“给你的路画地图”。|你一步步量过去，记下每片影子的深浅。下次你会用影步。大概吧。}\n# en: Step with a held breath\nopt.up.label [1d6b25] = 屏住呼吸踏影\n# en: {~You step without thinking about it, the way the spell knows. The cold barely touches you.|The spell takes you over quick and clean. Your fingers are cold for a minute.}\nopt.up.say.1 [690069] = {~你不假思索地一步跨过，就像咒语本身知道路。寒意几乎没碰到你。|咒语带着你又快又干净地过去了。你的手指冷了一分钟。}\n\n@event umbral_unpick_ward\n# en: Unpicking the Box\ntitle [be7a63] = 拆箱上的护界\n# en: {Advanced Umbral Theory. The professor wards a small box and you unpick the ward without it noticing. \"A ward that notices you,\" the professor says, \"tells someone.\"|Box day. The professor has a new box, and a new ward on it.|The box. You've got a favourite needle for this now.} {~This ward is fine as a cobweb.|This ward is thick and patient.|This ward hums when you breathe on it.}\ntext [f13c0d] = {高等暗影理论。教授给一只小箱子设下护界，你要在它察觉不到的情况下把护界拆掉。“察觉到你的护界，”教授说，“会告诉别人。”|拆箱日。教授拿来一只新箱子，上面是新的护界。|箱子。你现在有一根专门用来干这个的针了。} {~这道护界细得像蛛网。|这道护界又厚又有耐心。|你对它呼气时，这道护界会嗡嗡作响。}\n# en: A small iron-bound box on a bench under a dim lamp, faint threads of a pale ward wrapped around it, a student leaning close with a needle.\nscene [b89192] = 昏暗灯下的长桌上放着一只铁箍小箱，淡淡的苍白护界丝线缠绕着它，一名学生拿着针凑近。\n# en: Thread by thread\nopt.left.label [085b9e] = 一根一根地拆\n# en: {~An hour with a needle and a steady breath, and the box opens. The professor checks the ward and it has no idea.|You undo it strand by strand. It takes the whole lesson, and you don't look up once.}\nopt.left.say.1 [5e8853] = {~一根针，一口稳稳的气，一个钟头后箱子开了。教授检查护界，它浑然不觉。|你一缕一缕地解开它。这花了整节课，你一次头都没抬。}\n# en: Cut it in one stroke\nopt.right.label [bb2609] = 一刀切断\n# en: {~You find the knot and cut it clean. The ward never knows. The professor raises a brow and opens the box.|One cut and the threads fall away. The professor spends a moment looking at the box and then at you.}\nopt.right.say.1 [46e830] = {~你找到结，干净利落地切断。护界始终没察觉。教授挑了挑眉，打开了箱子。|一刀下去，丝线纷纷散落。教授看了箱子一会儿，又看了看你。}\n# en: {~The ward notices and rings like a little bell. The professor says, \"And now someone knows.\" Everyone looks at you.|The ward snaps back at your hand and stings. The box stays shut.}\nopt.right.say.2 [b4f0c5] = {~护界察觉了，像一只小铃铛一样响起来。教授说：“现在有人知道了。”所有人都看向你。|护界弹回来蜇了你的手。箱子还是关着。}\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/pools/grounds.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/pools/grounds.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event lakeshore_shore_walk\n# en: The Long Way Round\ntitle [250a85] = 绕远路\n# en: {You came out to walk alone. {date} finds you on the shore path and falls into step without asking.|The shore path is where you go to think. Today {date} has the same idea.|You know every stone of the shore path by now. Today {date} walks it with you.} {season == \"winter\": The lake is frozen at the edges and your breath walks ahead of you.|{season == \"summer\": The water is warm and full of sky.|{~Mist lies on the water like a sheet someone forgot to fold.|The towers stand upside down in the lake, lit window by lit window.}}}\ntext [22b725] = {你本想独自走走。{date}在湖岸小径上遇见你，没问一声就和你并肩走了起来。|湖岸小径是你想事情的地方。今天{date}也是这么想的。|湖岸小径上的每块石头你都认得了。今天{date}陪你走。} {season == \"winter\": 湖边结了冰，你的呼吸走在你前面。|{season == \"summer\": 湖水温暖，盛满了天空。|{~雾铺在水面上，像一张忘了叠的床单。|七塔倒映在湖里，一扇亮窗接着一扇亮窗。}}}\n# en: Two figures walking a lakeside path at dusk, the towers reflected upside down in the still water beside them.\nscene [33361d] = 黄昏时两个人影走在湖边小径上，身旁静水里倒映着颠倒的高塔。\n# en: Walk the long way round\nopt.left.label [793774] = 绕远路走一圈\n# en: {final_year: Halfway round, {date} says \"next year\" and stops, because there won't be one here. You walk the rest closer together.|{season == \"winter\": {~You walk until your toes go numb. {date} lends you {their} scarf and pretends not to be cold.|The ice sings under your boots. {date} takes your arm on the slippery stretch and forgets to let go.}|{~You walk until the far lamps go out. Neither of you mentions the time.|{date} points out a lit tower window and tells you who lives behind it. Half of it is invented. You don't mind which half.|You stand at the water's edge in silence, and the silence is the good kind.}}}\nopt.left.say.1 [51a336] = {final_year: 走到一半，{date}说了句“明年”，又停住了，因为在这里不会再有明年。剩下的路，你们靠得更近。|{season == \"winter\": {~你们一直走到你的脚趾冻僵。{date}把围巾借给你，假装自己不冷。|冰在你们靴子下歌唱。走到滑的那段，{date}挽住你的胳膊，忘了松开。}|{~你们一直走到对岸的灯火一盏盏熄灭。谁也没提时间。|{date}指着一扇亮着的塔窗，告诉你里面住的是谁。一半是编的。你不在乎是哪一半。|你们在水边默默站着，那是很好的那种沉默。}}}\n# en: Talk about anything but us\nopt.right.label [9c9439] = 聊什么都行，别聊我们\n# en: {~You talk about exams, the cook and the weather. It's easy. It's also not what either of you came for.|You argue cheerfully about whether the lake has a bottom. It's a good walk. It's only a walk.}\nopt.right.say.1 [a60f43] = {~你们聊考试、聊厨娘、聊天气。很轻松。也不是你们俩来这里想说的。|你们兴致勃勃地争论这湖到底有没有底。散步很愉快。也只是散步。}\n\n@event lakeshore_cold_swim\n# en: The Cold Swim\ntitle [242756] = 冷水游泳\n# en: {season == \"winter\": Students in towels are daring each other into the lake through a hole in the ice.|{season == \"summer\": Half the academy is in the lake; the other half pretends not to want to be.|A few hardy students are swimming out to the flat rock, shrieking.}} {The water looks inviting. It always does.|You know how cold the Mirror Lake really is. You're tempted anyway.|You and the lake have an understanding. It doesn't help.}\ntext [be4c03] = {season == \"winter\": 几个裹着毛巾的学生正互相怂恿，要从冰窟窿里跳进湖去。|{season == \"summer\": 半个学院都泡在湖里，另一半在假装自己不想下水。|几个不怕冷的学生正往平顶岩游去，一路尖叫。}} {湖水看上去很诱人。它一向如此。|你知道镜湖实际上有多冷。可你还是心动了。|你和这片湖之间有某种默契。这并没有用。}\n# en: Students in towels on a lakeshore, one wading in up to the knees and shrieking, the towers reflected behind them.\nscene [7cec80] = 湖岸上裹着毛巾的学生们，一个人蹚水到膝盖，正在尖叫，身后倒映着高塔。\n# en: Brave the water\nopt.left.label [5f5f4d] = 下水试试\n# en: {season == \"winter\": You go under, come up roaring, and are hauled out a hero.|{~You slap the flat rock, whoop, and swim back to applause from people with dry hair.|You reach the rock first. The cold reaches you second, but you were already winning.}}\nopt.left.say.1 [2400e3] = {season == \"winter\": 你沉下去，吼着冒出头，被当成英雄拖上了岸。|{~你一掌拍上平顶岩，欢呼一声，在一群头发干爽的人的掌声中游了回来。|你第一个碰到岩石。寒冷第二个碰到你，可你已经赢了。}}\n# en: {~You come back blue to the lips and sneezing. Sister Amaranth sighs before you've even sat down.|Halfway out, the lake reminds you who is in charge. You turn back with what dignity you have left.}\nopt.left.say.2 [a33452] = {~你回来时嘴唇发青、喷嚏连天。你还没坐下，阿玛兰斯修女就先叹了口气。|游到一半，湖水提醒你谁才是主人。你带着仅剩的体面掉头回来。}\n# en: Hold the towels and heckle\nopt.right.label [a0e0ba] = 帮忙拿毛巾，顺便起哄\n# en: {~You announce every dive like a herald. You're dry, popular and a tiny bit jealous.|You keep the towels warm and the commentary warmer. Someone throws a wet sock at you, which is fair.}\nopt.right.say.1 [c81fe9] = {~你像传令官一样播报每一次跳水。你很干爽、很受欢迎，也有一点点嫉妒。|你把毛巾捂得暖暖的，解说更热烈。有人朝你扔了一只湿袜子，这很公平。}\n\n@event duel_hall_dummies\n# en: The Practice Dummies\ntitle [01b66d] = 练习假人\n# en: {The near end of the hall belongs to the practice dummies, who watch you come in. The one with the painted moustache looks especially unforgiving.|The dummy with the moustache is still here, with a new scorch mark and, you'd swear, a new grudge.|The moustached dummy greets you with its usual silence. Someone has given it an eyepatch.} {~The floorboards are still warm from the last session.|A singed glove lies abandoned on the rack.|Rain drums on the high windows.}\ntext [6c4f46] = {大厅近端归练习假人所有，它们看着你进门。那个画了小胡子的看起来格外不肯原谅人。|画小胡子的假人还在，多了一道焦痕，你敢发誓，还多了一份怨气。|小胡子假人照例沉默地迎接你。有人给它戴了个眼罩。} {~地板还留着上一场练习的余温。|架子上丢着一只烧焦的手套。|雨点敲打着高窗。}\n# en: An empty duelling hall with scorched floorboards and a row of battered straw practice dummies, one with a painted moustache.\nscene [ddc84f] = 空荡的决斗厅，地板满是焦痕，一排破旧的草扎练习假人，其中一个画着小胡子。\n# en: Drill your hexes on it\nopt.left.label [74919a] = 拿它练咒\n# en: {~Hex after hex until your wrists ache. The dummy loses a sleeve and none of its contempt.|You land a clean one at last. A wisp of straw floats down, slowly, like applause.}\nopt.left.say.1 [74465f] = {~一道咒语接一道咒语，直到手腕发酸。假人掉了一只袖子，轻蔑却一分没少。|你终于打出一记干净利落的。一缕稻草慢慢飘落，像是掌声。}\n# en: Restuff the battered ones\nopt.right.label [218ba9] = 给破假人重新填草\n# en: {~You haul a bale of straw over from the stables and restuff three dummies. They look almost grateful, which is unsettling.|You restuff the moustached one with special care. It still looks like it's planning something.}\nopt.right.say.1 [2682f3] = {~你从马厩扛来一捆稻草，给三个假人重新填好。它们看上去几乎心怀感激，这让人不安。|你格外用心地给小胡子假人填了草。它看上去还是在盘算着什么。}\n\n@event duel_hall_gallery\n# en: From the Gallery\ntitle [f73290] = 看台之上\n# en: {From the gallery above the duelling floor you can watch whoever is practising. Today it's {here}.|You've learned to read duels from up here. Today {here} is drilling.|The gallery rail has a groove where you lean. Below, {here} is drilling.} {~Sparks drift up to the gallery rail.|Somebody's familiar is asleep on the scoreboard.|The ceiling has acquired a new scorch mark.}\ntext [e03136] = {从决斗场上方的看台，你能看到谁在练习。今天是{here}。|你已经学会从这上面看懂决斗了。今天{here}在练。|看台栏杆上有一道你常倚的凹痕。下面，{here}在练。} {~火星飘上看台的栏杆。|不知谁的使魔睡在记分板上。|天花板又添了一道新焦痕。}\n# en: A narrow wooden gallery above a duelling floor, one student watching another drill spells at a target below.\nscene [5292ba] = 决斗场上方狭窄的木制看台，一个学生看着下面另一个对着靶子练咒。\n# en: Offer to spar\nopt.left.label [ed03b2] = 提出陪练\n# en: {~You take a round off {here}. {They} stare{s} at you for a long second, then ask{s} for another.|You win with a feint you stole from watching {here}. {They} notice{s}, and look{s} annoyed and pleased at once.}\nopt.left.say.1 [4d8a7e] = {~你从{here}手里拿下一局。{They}盯着你看了好一会儿，然后要求再来一局。|你用看{here}练习时偷学的虚招赢了。{They}看出来了，又恼又高兴。}\n# en: {~{here} puts you on the floorboards, politely. From down there the ceiling's scorch marks make a sort of map.|{here} disarms you so neatly that the dummies seem to wince. Your wand skids under the gallery stairs.}\nopt.left.say.2 [496fdd] = {~{here}很有礼貌地把你放倒在地板上。从下面看，天花板上的焦痕像是一张地图。|{here}缴械缴得干净利落，连假人都像是缩了一下。你的魔杖滑进了看台楼梯底下。}\n# en: Watch and take notes\nopt.right.label [d829ad] = 看着记笔记\n# en: {~You fill a page with what {here} does with {their} feet. You'll never get to try it, and you know it.|You sketch {here}'s guard from above. On paper it has a gap. On the floor it doesn't.}\nopt.right.say.1 [7b657c] = {~你把{here}的步法记了满满一页。你永远不会有机会试，你也知道。|你从上方画下{here}的防守架势。纸上有个破绽。场上没有。}\n\n@event duel_hall_humming_circle\n# en: The Humming Circle\ntitle [1a4455] = 嗡鸣的法阵\n# en: {The evening bouts are over. At the near end of the hall the warding circle is still humming.|The warding circle is humming. Whoever's drilling at the far end isn't looking your way.|The circle hums. You know its tune by now.} {final_year: Soon someone else will be sneaking in here after hours.|{~A practice wand lies on the rack, still warm.|The steward's broom leans by the door, watching.|Tonight the ceiling stain looks like a dragon.}}\ntext [f7a0f6] = {晚上的比试结束了。大厅近端的护界法阵还在嗡嗡作响。|护界法阵在嗡鸣。远处练习的人没往你这边看。|法阵在嗡鸣。你已经熟悉它的调子了。} {final_year: 很快就会有别人在闭馆后溜进这里了。|{~架子上放着一根练习魔杖，还是温的。|管理员的扫帚靠在门边，像在盯着你。|今晚天花板上的污渍看起来像一条龙。}}\n# en: A dark duelling hall lit only by a ring of faintly glowing runes on the floor.\nscene [939412] = 漆黑的决斗厅，只有地上一圈微微发光的符文照明。\n# en: Step in and try a hard one\nopt.left.label [9c5c46] = 走进去试个难的\n# en: {~The spell comes out clean and the circle drinks it. Somewhere a dummy loses its head and nobody ever finds out how.|You try the thing you saw a sixth-year do. It works. You tell no one, and walk back grinning.}\nopt.left.say.1 [944a2a] = {~咒语干净利落地打出，法阵把它吞了下去。某处一个假人掉了脑袋，没人知道是怎么回事。|你试了一招见六年级生用过的。成功了。你谁也没告诉，一路笑着走回去。}\n# en: {~The circle hands your spell straight back to you. Your eyebrows will regrow.|The circle flares, the dummies rattle, and you leave very quickly before the steward arrives.}\nopt.left.say.2 [9ef7d7] = {~法阵把你的咒语原样还给了你。你的眉毛会长回来的。|法阵一闪，假人们哗啦作响，你赶在管理员来之前飞快地溜了。}\n# en: Sweep up for the steward\nopt.right.label [b29618] = 替管理员扫地\n# en: {~You sweep up a winter's worth of ash. The steward pays you a crown and a grunt that might be thanks.|You sweep. The circle hums at you the whole time, like a cat that wants to be let out.}\nopt.right.say.1 [f1a512] = {~你扫掉了一整个冬天的灰烬。管理员给了你一枚克朗，外加一声可能是道谢的哼哼。|你扫着地。法阵一直冲你嗡嗡叫，像一只想出门的猫。}\n\n@event greenhouses_warm_glass\n# en: The Warm Glass\ntitle [4d5441] = 温暖的玻璃\n# en: {season == \"winter\": Outside it's snowing. Inside, the glass runs with warm water and everything smells of earth.|{season == \"summer\": The greenhouses are sweltering, and everyone has retreated to the far end where the vents are.|The greenhouses steam gently. Someone is singing to the mandrakes at the far end.}} {You find a dry corner by the ferns, out of everyone's way.|Someone has left a cushion in your corner by the ferns.|The ferns lean toward you when you come in, as they do for you now.}\ntext [4ff1a0] = {season == \"winter\": 外面在下雪。里面，玻璃上淌着温热的水珠，到处是泥土的气味。|{season == \"summer\": 温室里闷热难当，大家都躲到了有通风口的那一头。|温室里轻轻蒸腾着水汽。远处有人在给曼德拉草唱歌。}} {你在蕨类旁找到一个干爽的角落，不碍任何人的事。|有人在你蕨类旁的那个角落里留了个垫子。|你一进门，蕨类就朝你探过来，如今它们一向如此。}\n# en: A student reading on an upturned pot among tall ferns inside a steamy glass greenhouse, snow on the panes outside.\nscene [9eaa5e] = 雾气蒸腾的玻璃温室里，一个学生坐在倒扣的花盆上，在高高的蕨类间读书，窗外落着雪。\n# en: Do your reading in the warm\nopt.left.label [2d902a] = 在暖和处读书\n# en: {~You get through a whole chapter. The pages curl in the damp and you come out smelling of moss.|You read until the steam makes the ink run. What's left of the chapter sticks.}\nopt.left.say.1 [cb014b] = {~你读完了整整一章。书页在潮气里卷了边，你出来时一身青苔味。|你一直读到水汽把墨迹洇开。剩下的那部分章节倒是记住了。}\n# en: Nap on the potting bench\nopt.right.label [8ac69b] = 在盆栽台上打个盹\n# en: {~You wake with a seed tray for a pillow and a first-year staring at you. By supper it's a story.|You wake to a mandrake humming a lullaby. You choose not to tell anyone. Someone tells everyone.}\nopt.right.say.1 [63901d] = {~你醒来时枕着育苗盘，一个一年级生正盯着你。到晚饭时，这已经成了一段故事。|你在曼德拉草哼的摇篮曲里醒来。你决定谁也不告诉。有人告诉了所有人。}\n\n@event greenhouses_repotting\n# en: Repotting Day\ntitle [5b9bc1] = 换盆日\n# en: {here} is up to the elbows in compost, repotting {season == \"spring\": seedlings that have outgrown everything|{season == \"autumn\": bulbs for the winter|something with more teeth than leaves}}. {\"Hands?\" {they} ask{s}.|{They} hand{s} you a trowel before you've said hello.|There's a trowel on the hook that has come to be yours. {here} nods at it.}\ntext [36e480] = {here}正把胳膊埋在堆肥里，给{season == \"spring\": 长得什么都装不下的幼苗|{season == \"autumn\": 准备过冬的球根|一株牙齿比叶子还多的东西}}换盆。{“搭把手？”{they}问。|你还没打招呼，{They}就递给你一把小铲子。|钩子上有一把小铲子，渐渐成了你的。{here}朝它点点头。}\n# en: A student elbow-deep in compost at a greenhouse bench, handing a trowel to someone arriving.\nscene [c4461e] = 温室工作台边，一个学生胳膊深埋在堆肥里，把小铲子递给刚到的人。\n# en: Get your hands dirty\nopt.left.label [f6a380] = 动手干活\n# en: {~You repot a whole row. Something nips your thumb in thanks.|You're filthy, nipped and oddly happy. {here} says you have good hands for roots.}\nopt.left.say.1 [fbc76b] = {~你给一整排换了盆。有什么东西咬了一下你的拇指，算是道谢。|你满身泥污，被咬了几口，却莫名开心。{here}说你天生一双侍弄根的手。}\n# en: Ask questions, stay clean\nopt.right.label [d36613] = 光提问，不沾手\n# en: {~You learn a great deal about soil. {here} learns that you're afraid of it.|You take notes. {here} gives you a look over the trowel that says notes are not hands.}\nopt.right.say.1 [537f6b] = {~你学到了很多关于泥土的知识。{here}则学到了你怕泥土。|你记着笔记。{here}隔着小铲子看了你一眼，意思是笔记不能代替手。}\n\n@event greenhouses_cuttings\n# en: The Cuttings Bench\ntitle [c5d8ff] = 插条台\n# en: {~A row of silver-leaf cuttings is drying on the bench|A tray of night-orchid cuttings sits by the door|A bundle of moonpetal cuttings is wrapped in damp cloth}, and Pell & Daughters pays well for that sort of thing. {Nobody is watching. The snapdragons are.|The snapdragons have seen you here before. They remember.|The snapdragons snap at you on principle now.}\ntext [46a324] = {~台子上晾着一排银叶插条|门边放着一盘夜兰插条|一捆月瓣花插条裹在湿布里}，佩尔父女药铺收这种东西出价不低。{没人在看。金鱼草在看。|金鱼草以前见过你。它们记得。|金鱼草现在出于原则见你就咬。}\n# en: A greenhouse bench of labelled cuttings drying in rows, a row of snapdragons with actual teeth watching from their pots.\nscene [4762ee] = 温室工作台上一排排晾着的插条，带标签，一排长着真牙齿的金鱼草在盆里盯着。\n# en: Pocket a cutting\nopt.left.label [44bf20] = 偷偷揣一根插条\n# en: {~Pell's counts the leaves twice and pays without a question. You don't look at the snapdragons on your next visit.|It sells for more than you expected, which somehow makes it worse.}\nopt.left.say.1 [495817] = {~佩尔家把叶子数了两遍，二话不说付了钱。下次来时你没敢看金鱼草。|它卖的价钱比你想的还高，不知怎么反而更糟了。}\n# en: {~A snapdragon shrieks. Three Verdant students turn around at once. You put it back with a cough that convinces no one.|Your pocket rustles at the wrong moment. You spend the rest of the visit apologising to a fern.}\nopt.left.say.2 [a29a83] = {~一株金鱼草尖叫起来。三个青翠社的学生同时回头。你干咳一声把它放回去，谁也没被说服。|你的口袋在最不该的时候沙沙作响。这一趟剩下的时间，你都在向一株蕨类道歉。}\n# en: Feed the snapdragons\nopt.right.label [22dfda] = 喂金鱼草\n# en: {~They take the food, then a little of your finger. You learn which end to hold the tongs by.|Fed, the snapdragons purr like kettles. One of them still bites you, out of habit.}\nopt.right.say.1 [5e2e7a] = {~它们吃了食物，又顺带吃了一点你的手指。你学会了夹子该拿哪一头。|吃饱的金鱼草像水壶一样咕噜咕噜。还是有一株咬了你，出于习惯。}\n\n@event flight_field_cheating_wind\n# en: The Cheating Wind\ntitle [c14839] = 耍赖的风\n# en: {season == \"winter\": The flight field is white, the hoops wearing caps of snow.|{season == \"spring\": The field is soft with new grass and the wind is playful.|{~The field is empty but for the wind, practising on its own.|A lone first-year is flying very careful circles.}}} {The school brooms in the shed are, at best, opinionated.|Your usual broom is in the shed, the one that pulls left.|The broom that pulls left now pulls right, out of spite.}\ntext [9f62d7] = {season == \"winter\": 飞行场一片雪白，圈门都戴上了雪帽。|{season == \"spring\": 场地铺满柔软的新草，风很调皮。|{~场上空无一人，只有风在自己练习。|一个一年级生在小心翼翼地绕圈飞。}}} {棚里的学校扫帚，往好里说，也是很有主见。|你常用的那把扫帚在棚里，就是总往左偏的那把。|那把往左偏的扫帚现在往右偏了，纯属赌气。}\n# en: A long green field with tall hoops at either end, one student wobbling on a school broom against a gusting wind.\nscene [7ab1d7] = 两端立着高高圈门的长条绿地上，一个学生骑着学校扫帚在阵风里摇摇晃晃。\n# en: Practise your landings\nopt.left.label [516481] = 练习降落\n# en: {~A dozen landings. A few of them on purpose. The grass keeps a careful record of the rest.|The wind waits until you're nearly down, then shoves. You land in a hoop instead. Technically, a goal.}\nopt.left.say.1 [a2c502] = {~降落了十几次，其中几次是故意的。草地仔细记下了其余的。|风等你快落地时才推了一把。你落进了圈门里。严格来说，算进球。}\n# en: Hire a decent broom\nopt.right.label [cee8bc] = 租一把像样的扫帚\n# en: {~The good broom goes where you look, not where it feels like. For an hour, flying is easy and the field is yours.|You fly the length of the field and back with the wind on your side for once. It costs a week's sweets.}\nopt.right.say.1 [a4e52f] = {~好扫帚会去你看的地方，而不是它想去的地方。有那么一个钟头，飞行很轻松，整个场地都是你的。|你顺着风飞了个来回，这可是头一回。代价是一周的零食钱。}\n\n@event flight_field_pickup_race\n# en: The Pick-Up Race\ntitle [22c25a] = 临时赛\n# en: {A pick-up race is forming at the far hoops. Three brooms, two rules, no referee.|The pick-up racers are back. The rules have grown to four.|The pick-up race is a fixture now. Someone has painted a finish line, crookedly.} {~A second-year from Emberhall is taking bets.|A Brasswick student has bolted a lantern to their broom-tail.|A Rootward student is flying barefoot, for grip.}\ntext [f14e6f] = {远处的圈门那边正在凑一场临时赛。三把扫帚，两条规则，没有裁判。|临时赛的人又来了。规则涨到了四条。|临时赛已经成了固定节目。有人歪歪扭扭地画了一条终点线。} {~一个烬厅的二年级生在坐庄。|一个铜芯的学生在扫帚尾巴上拴了盏灯笼。|一个根庐的学生光着脚飞，说是为了抓得牢。}\n# en: Three students on brooms jostling at a crooked painted start line, a fourth holding up a scarf as a starting flag.\nscene [66b550] = 三个骑扫帚的学生在一条歪斜的起跑线前推搡，第四个人举着围巾当发令旗。\n# en: Borrow a broom and race\nopt.left.label [18a85c] = 借把扫帚去比赛\n# en: {~You win by a broom's length and a lot of shouting. The second-year taking bets has to pay out and never forgives you.|You take the last hoop sideways and cross first. Someone adds a new rule, about you.}\nopt.left.say.1 [3ac547] = {~你以一扫帚之差、外加一片叫喊声获胜。那个坐庄的二年级生只好赔钱，从此再没原谅你。|你侧着身子穿过最后一个圈门，第一个冲线。有人加了一条新规则，是关于你的。}\n# en: {~You finish last, and partly in a hedge.|The wind takes your side, then changes its mind. You walk the broom back to the start.}\nopt.left.say.2 [7fc47f] = {~你最后一名到达，有一部分是在树篱里到达的。|风先站在你这边，然后又改了主意。你推着扫帚走回起点。}\n# en: Bet a crown on the outsider\nopt.right.label [dd9ff5] = 押一枚克朗给冷门\n# en: {~The outsider wins on a gust nobody else saw coming. You are suddenly very good at betting.|The outsider comes in first by a nose. You toast the nose.}\nopt.right.say.1 [3b5538] = {~冷门借着一阵谁也没料到的风赢了。你突然成了押注高手。|冷门以一个鼻尖的优势夺冠。你为那个鼻尖干杯。}\n# en: {~The outsider stops halfway to admire the view. Your crown goes with them.|Your flyer is disqualified for a rule invented on the spot. You don't argue. It's a good rule.}\nopt.right.say.2 [2c9413] = {~冷门飞到一半停下来欣赏风景。你的克朗也跟着去了。|你押的人因为一条当场发明的规则被取消资格。你没争辩。那是条好规则。}\n\n@event flight_field_long_grass\n# en: The Long Grass\ntitle [f6b1e4] = 长草丛\n# en: {The long grass at the edge of the flight field catches everything that falls off a broom.|Someone has planted a little flag in the long grass, to mark where things land.|You've come to think of the long grass as the academy's real lost-and-found.} Today it's {~a velvet glove|a letter with no envelope|a single boot, laced|someone's notes for an exam}. {season == \"autumn\": The leaves are trying to bury it.|{season == \"winter\": It's half under frost.|A grasshopper is sitting on it.}}\ntext [b88b97] = {飞行场边的长草丛接住了所有从扫帚上掉下来的东西。|有人在长草丛里插了面小旗，标出东西落下的地方。|你已经把长草丛当成学院真正的失物招领处了。}今天是{~一只天鹅绒手套|一封没有信封的信|一只系好鞋带的靴子|某人的考试笔记}。{season == \"autumn\": 落叶正想把它埋起来。|{season == \"winter\": 它有一半埋在霜里。|一只蚱蜢正坐在上面。}}\n# en: The long grass at the edge of a flight field, scattered with things dropped from brooms, a student crouching to look.\nscene [b64b13] = 飞行场边的长草丛里散落着从扫帚上掉下的东西，一个学生蹲下来查看。\n# en: Keep what you find\nopt.left.label [86edf0] = 捡到就归我\n# en: {~The owner lands right beside you while you're still holding it. There's a short, very long silence.|A second-year marches over the grass shouting \"Thief!\" at the sky in general and you in particular.}\nopt.left.say.1 [c13865] = {~你还拿在手里，失主就在你旁边落了地。接下来是一阵短暂而又漫长的沉默。|一个二年级生大步穿过草地，冲着天空泛泛地大喊“小偷！”，具体来说是冲着你。}\n# en: {~Finders, keepers. The grass doesn't argue, and neither does your conscience, much.|Nobody comes looking. You tell yourself that settles it.}\nopt.left.say.2 [04ff57] = {~谁捡到归谁。草丛没意见，你的良心也没多大意见。|没人来找。你告诉自己这就算了结了。}\n# en: Carry it to the porter's lodge\nopt.right.label [e8195a] = 送到门房去\n# en: {~It's a long walk round the field. The porter writes it in a book and gives you a look of mild surprise.|The porter thanks you with a peppermint from a jar that has clearly been open since the founders.}\nopt.right.say.1 [db1194] = {~绕场一圈可不近。门房把它记进本子，略带惊讶地看了你一眼。|门房从一个显然自创始者时代就开着的罐子里掏出一颗薄荷糖谢你。}\n\n@event stables_owl_loft\n# en: The Owl Loft\ntitle [2be609] = 猫头鹰阁楼\n# en: {Up the ladder, above the hippogriffs, is a hay loft full of owls and quiet.|The owls open one eye each, decide you're furniture, and go back to sleep.|The owls have stopped opening their eyes for you. You've been accepted.} {season == \"winter\": The warmth of the animals rises through the boards.|{~Rain ticks on the roof.|A stray letter has fallen into the hay.|The griffon chick is complaining somewhere below.}}\ntext [a980a2] = {爬上梯子，在鹰头马身有翼兽的上方，是一间满是猫头鹰和安静的干草阁楼。|猫头鹰们各睁开一只眼，认定你是件家具，又睡了。|猫头鹰们已经懒得为你睁眼了。你被接纳了。} {season == \"winter\": 动物们的暖意透过木板升上来。|{~雨点滴答敲着屋顶。|一封走失的信掉进了干草里。|楼下的狮鹫幼崽在什么地方抱怨。}}\n# en: A hay loft in old stables, rows of sleepy owls on the rafters above a student reading by a lantern.\nscene [d922b6] = 老马厩的干草阁楼里，椽子上一排排睡眼惺忪的猫头鹰，下面一个学生就着灯笼读书。\n# en: Read up among the owls\nopt.left.label [928968] = 在猫头鹰中间读书\n# en: {~You read until the light goes. The hay gets in everywhere, and the chapter gets in too.|An owl reads over your shoulder for a whole page, then hoots, pointedly, at a footnote.}\nopt.left.say.1 [583c7a] = {~你一直读到天黑。干草钻得到处都是，书里的内容也钻进了脑子。|一只猫头鹰趴在你肩上读了整整一页，然后意味深长地冲一条脚注叫了一声。}\n# en: Help sort the owl post\nopt.right.label [37a567] = 帮忙分拣猫头鹰邮件\n# en: {~You sort a dropped bundle of post by hand. One owl seems to know which letters you're going to misfile.|You deliver three soggy letters to their owners on the way back. One of them hugs you, which you weren't ready for.}\nopt.right.say.1 [49f814] = {~你亲手整理了一捆掉落的信件。有只猫头鹰似乎早就知道你会放错哪几封。|回去路上你把三封湿漉漉的信交给了收信人。其中一个人抱住了你，你完全没准备好。}\n\n@event stables_brask_rounds\n# en: Brask's Rounds\ntitle [11370d] = 布拉斯克巡厩\n# en: {Professor Brask is on his rounds, telling each hippogriff she's a very good girl. He tells you to mind the bucket.|Brask is on his rounds. He nods at you, which is more than most get.|Brask greets you by name now, somewhere after the owls.} {season == \"spring\": There are new hippogriff foals, all legs and beak.|{~A hippogriff is sulking about its feathers.|The feed is late and everyone is cross.|The stable cat is asleep in the oats.}}\ntext [92ff6a] = {布拉斯克教授正在巡厩，挨个告诉每只鹰头马身有翼兽它是个好姑娘。他让你当心水桶。|布拉斯克在巡厩。他冲你点了点头，这比大多数人得到的都多。|布拉斯克现在会叫你的名字了，排在猫头鹰后面。} {season == \"spring\": 新生的小兽崽全是腿和喙。|{~一只鹰头马身有翼兽在为自己的羽毛闹脾气。|饲料送晚了，大家都很暴躁。|马厩的猫睡在燕麦堆里。}}\n# en: A burly professor stroking a hippogriff's beak in a stable aisle, ignoring a student holding two feed buckets.\nscene [5fb5c2] = 魁梧的教授在马厩过道里抚摸一只鹰头马身有翼兽的喙，无视旁边拎着两桶饲料的学生。\n# en: Ask Brask a question\nopt.left.label [3880c8] = 向布拉斯克请教\n# en: {~He answers it at length, to the hippogriff. You take notes anyway.|\"Good question,\" he says, to the owl. You decide that counts.}\nopt.left.say.1 [7457c3] = {~他详详细细地回答了，对着那只鹰头马身有翼兽。你还是记了笔记。|“好问题，”他对猫头鹰说。你决定这也算数。}\n# en: Carry the feed buckets\nopt.right.label [2dfcee] = 帮忙提饲料桶\n# en: {~Every stall, two buckets each. Brask says nothing, which is his way of saying thank you.|A hippogriff bows to you over its bucket. Brask looks almost jealous.}\nopt.right.say.1 [7f00c1] = {~每个隔间两桶。布拉斯克一言不发，这就是他道谢的方式。|一只鹰头马身有翼兽隔着饲料桶向你鞠了一躬。布拉斯克看起来几乎有点嫉妒。}\n\n@event stables_griffon_sulk\n# en: The Sulking Griffon\ntitle [16bcc6] = 闹别扭的狮鹫\n# en: {The griffon chick is sulking in the corner stall, feathers puffed, refusing breakfast.|The griffon chick is sulking again. It's bigger this time, and so is the sulk.|The griffon is hardly a chick any more, and still sulks like one. It watches you come in.} {year == 1: The sign on the stall says not to hand-feed it, underlined twice.|{~The sign on the stall now has a drawing of a bitten hand.|The sign on the stall has been underlined until the paper tore.|Someone has added \"I mean it\" to the sign, in Brask's hand.}}\ntext [48dab2] = {狮鹫幼崽缩在角落的隔间里生闷气，羽毛炸着，不肯吃早饭。|狮鹫幼崽又在生闷气。它长大了些，闷气也大了些。|那只狮鹫已经算不上幼崽了，生起闷气来还和从前一样。它看着你进门。} {year == 1: 隔间上的牌子写着不许手喂，下面画了两道线。|{~隔间的牌子上现在画了一只被咬的手。|隔间牌子上的线被画到纸都破了。|有人在牌子上添了句“我是认真的”，是布拉斯克的笔迹。}}\n# en: A griffon chick with puffed-up feathers glaring from the corner of a stall, a student holding out a morsel at arm's length.\nscene [63efe8] = 一只羽毛炸起的狮鹫幼崽在隔间角落里怒视，一个学生伸直胳膊递出一小块食物。\n# en: Offer it breakfast by hand\nopt.left.label [1ec395] = 亲手喂它早饭\n# en: {~It considers you for a long time, then takes the morsel with great delicacy. You feel chosen.|It eats from your hand and then, briefly, leans on you. You don't move for ten minutes.}\nopt.left.say.1 [961541] = {~它打量了你很久，然后极其斯文地叼走了那块食物。你觉得自己被选中了。|它从你手里吃了东西，然后短暂地靠在你身上。你十分钟没敢动。}\n# en: {~It takes the morsel and a corner of your sleeve. The sign was right.|It turns its back on you so pointedly that a passing owl looks embarrassed.}\nopt.left.say.2 [19f04f] = {~它叼走了食物，连同你袖子的一角。牌子说得没错。|它转过身去背对你，刻意得连路过的猫头鹰都替你尴尬。}\n# en: Leave it to its sulk\nopt.right.label [bcf991] = 随它生闷气\n# en: {~You read the notice about griffon moods instead. It is mostly a list of things that will not help.|You leave it alone. It looks, as you go, a little disappointed you gave up.}\nopt.right.say.1 [7c7c8e] = {~你转而读起了关于狮鹫脾气的告示。上面大部分是一张“这些都没用”的清单。|你没去打扰它。你走的时候，它看上去有点失望你就这么放弃了。}\n\n@event boathouse_jetty_end\n# en: The End of the Jetty\ntitle [d7bf11] = 栈桥尽头\n# en: {{date} is at the end of the jetty, boots over the water. {They} move{s} over without a word.|The end of the jetty has become your place for saying things. Today {date} is already there.|You know which plank creaks. {date} doesn't, and finds it.} {season == \"winter\": The lake has frozen round the posts and creaks like an old house.|{~The rowing boats knock gently together.|A heron fishes in the shallows, pretending not to listen.|The village lamps blink on across the water.}}\ntext [072594] = {{date}坐在栈桥尽头，靴子悬在水面上。{They}一句话没说，挪出了位置。|栈桥尽头成了你说心里话的地方。今天{date}已经在那儿了。|你知道哪块木板会响。{date}不知道，一脚就踩上了。} {season == \"winter\": 湖水在桩子周围结了冰，像老房子一样吱嘎作响。|{~划艇轻轻地互相碰撞。|一只苍鹭在浅水里捕鱼，假装没在听。|对岸小镇的灯一盏盏亮起来。}}\n# en: Two people sitting side by side at the end of a wooden jetty, boots over dark water, a lantern between them.\nscene [738e69] = 两个人并肩坐在木栈桥尽头，靴子悬在黑色的水面上，中间放着一盏灯笼。\n# en: Hire a boat and row out\nopt.left.label [d01173] = 租条船划出去\n# en: {season == \"winter\": You can't row on ice, so you sit in a beached boat and pretend. {date} calls it a fine voyage.|{~You row badly, {date} laughs, and then neither of you is rowing at all.|Out in the middle the shore goes quiet. {date} says your name like the end of a sentence.}}\nopt.left.say.1 [074b0e] = {season == \"winter\": 冰上没法划船，你们就坐在搁浅的船里假装。{date}说这是一趟不错的航行。|{~你划得很糟，{date}笑你，然后谁也不划了。|划到湖心，岸上的声音都静了。{date}念你的名字，像在说一句话的结尾。}}\n# en: Stay on the jetty and talk\nopt.right.label [dedd57] = 留在栈桥上聊天\n# en: {final_year: You talk about where you'll both be next year. Neither of you says \"together\", and both of you hear it.|{~You talk until the lantern gutters. The jetty is cold and you don't notice until you stand.|You say one of the things you couldn't say indoors. {date} listens all the way to the end.}}\nopt.right.say.1 [24abeb] = {final_year: 你们聊起明年各自会在哪里。谁也没说“一起”，可两个人都听见了。|{~你们一直聊到灯笼的火苗摇摇欲灭。栈桥很冷，站起来时你才发觉。|你说出了一件在屋里说不出口的事。{date}一直听到了最后。}}\n\n@event boathouse_green_boat\n# en: The Leaky Green Boat\ntitle [c4a35d] = 漏水的绿船\n# en: {The boathouse smells of tar and old rope. Its rowing boats are mostly leaks held together by paint, the green one worst of all.|Someone has patched the green boat since your last visit, badly, with what looks like a cake tin.|The boathouse knows you. The green boat still leaks.} {~Initials are carved into every beam, some so old they've gone soft.|Rain patters on the roof.|A spider has strung a web across the oars, and seems proud of it.}\ntext [49095a] = {船屋里是焦油和旧绳子的气味。这里的划艇大多是用油漆粘在一起的漏洞，绿色那条最糟。|自你上次来过，有人给绿船打了补丁，打得很烂，用的像是个蛋糕模子。|船屋认得你。绿船还在漏水。} {~每根房梁上都刻着姓名缩写，有些老得已经模糊了。|雨点轻敲着屋顶。|一只蜘蛛在船桨间结了网，看样子很得意。}\n# en: Inside an old wooden boathouse, an upturned green rowing boat on trestles, initials carved into every beam above.\nscene [4e9c10] = 老旧木船屋里，一条绿色划艇倒扣在支架上，上方每根房梁都刻满了姓名缩写。\n# en: Patch the green boat\nopt.left.label [df86c5] = 给绿船打补丁\n# en: {~You spend an hour with tar and a mallet. The green boat floats, briefly, with an air of surprise.|Your patch holds for a whole circuit of the boathouse. You have tar in your eyebrows and a certain pride.}\nopt.left.say.1 [d25677] = {~你拿着焦油和木槌忙了一个钟头。绿船浮了起来，短暂地，带着一脸惊讶。|你的补丁撑过了绕船屋一整圈。你眉毛上沾着焦油，心里有几分骄傲。}\n# en: Carve your initials in a beam\nopt.right.label [383067] = 在房梁上刻名字缩写\n# en: {final_year: You carve them next to a pair from sixty years ago. Somebody, one day, will run a thumb over yours and wonder.|{~Your penknife snaps on the last letter. The initials stay, slightly wrong, which feels right.|You find a carving that could almost be yours from long ago, and add your own beneath it.}}\nopt.right.say.1 [6eeab8] = {final_year: 你把它刻在一对六十年前的缩写旁边。总有一天，会有人用拇指摸过你的，心生好奇。|{~最后一个字母刻到一半，小刀断了。缩写就这样留着，有点歪，感觉却刚好。|你找到一处很久以前的刻痕，几乎像是你的，于是把自己的刻在它下面。}}\n\n@event boathouse_overheard\n# en: Things Said Outside\ntitle [bff476] = 屋外的话\n# en: {Two voices out on the jetty, low and urgent. People come to the boathouse to say what they can't say indoors.|You've found the gap in the boathouse planks where every word on the jetty comes through.|Another quiet conversation on the jetty. You're starting to know the academy by its secrets.} {~It sounds like a break-up.|It sounds like an apology that isn't going well.|It sounds like someone confessing to a pudding.}\ntext [7fc752] = {栈桥上有两个声音，低沉而急切。人们来船屋，是为了说屋里说不出口的话。|你发现了船屋木板上的一道缝，栈桥上的每个字都从那里漏进来。|栈桥上又一段悄悄话。你开始从秘密里认识这所学院了。} {~听起来像是在分手。|听起来像是一次不太顺利的道歉。|听起来像是有人在向一块布丁告白。}\n# en: Two silhouettes talking quietly on a jetty at night, seen through the slats of a boathouse wall.\nscene [fbc1ce] = 夜里两个身影在栈桥上低声交谈，从船屋墙板的缝隙间望去。\n# en: Listen a little longer\nopt.left.label [9863e8] = 再多听一会儿\n# en: {~A plank creaks under you. The voices stop. Two very red faces come round the door, and now they know your face too.|An oar slides off its peg with a clatter. Footsteps; a hiss of \"Who's there?\"; you are, unfortunately.}\nopt.left.say.1 [e95a9c] = {~你脚下一块木板吱嘎一响。说话声停了。两张通红的脸从门边探过来，现在他们也认得你的脸了。|一支船桨哐当一声从钉子上滑落。脚步声；一声低喝“谁在那儿？”；很不幸，是你。}\n# en: {~You learn more than you wanted to. You'll carry it carefully, and never tell.|It ends well, as it happens. You slip away before anyone has to know you were there.}\nopt.left.say.2 [017e29] = {~你听到的比你想听的多。你会小心收好，永远不说出去。|结果是个好结局。你在有人发现之前悄悄溜走了。}\n# en: Cough loudly and leave\nopt.right.label [eb5a76] = 大声咳嗽，走开\n# en: {~They wait for you to pass, hushed and grateful. You'll never know how it ended, and that's the price.|You make a great show of looking for an oar. By the time you've found one, they've gone.}\nopt.right.say.1 [bba1fd] = {~他们安静地等你走过，满怀感激。你永远不会知道后来怎样了，这就是代价。|你装模作样地找船桨。等你找到时，他们已经走了。}\n\n@event orchard_under_the_trees\n# en: Under the Old Trees\ntitle [42dae6] = 老树之下\n# en: {season == \"spring\": Blossom is coming down like snow, and {date} has some in {their} hair.|{season == \"autumn\": The trees are heavy with apples and wasps, and {date} is reaching for one.|{season == \"winter\": The orchard is bare and silver, and {date} is walking between the trees.|{date} has claimed the best of the orchard shade.}}} {{They} wave{s} you over.|{They} saw you coming before you saw {them}.|The old trees seem to expect you by now. {date} only half does.}\ntext [f17cc4] = {season == \"spring\": 花瓣像雪一样落下，{date}的头发上沾了几片。|{season == \"autumn\": 树上挂满了苹果和黄蜂，{date}正伸手去够一个。|{season == \"winter\": 果园光秃秃的，一片银白，{date}在树间散步。|{date}占了果园里最好的一片树荫。}}} {{They}招手让你过去。|你还没看见{them}，{They}就先看见了你。|老树们似乎已经习惯等你了。{date}倒没那么笃定。}\n# en: Two people beneath an ancient apple tree, blossom drifting down around them like snow.\nscene [f622bf] = 两个人坐在一棵古老的苹果树下，花瓣像雪一样飘落在他们周围。\n# en: Stay until the light goes\nopt.left.label [953fd7] = 待到天黑\n# en: {season == \"spring\": {~You stay until the blossom buries your shoes. You miss supper, and you'd do it again.|{date} picks blossom out of your hair, one petal at a time, and takes far too long about it.}|{season == \"winter\": {~Your fingers go white, and {date} takes your hands to warm them, and doesn't give them back.|You lean against the same cold trunk until the stars come out over the bare branches.}|{~You stay until the shadows outgrow the trees. You miss supper. Neither of you mentions it.|The light goes gold, then blue. {date} says something quiet you'll turn over for days.}}}\nopt.left.say.1 [39aaad] = {season == \"spring\": {~你们一直待到花瓣埋住了鞋子。你错过了晚饭，下次还会这样。|{date}把花瓣从你头发里一片片挑出来，挑得太慢了。}|{season == \"winter\": {~你的手指冻得发白，{date}握住你的手替你暖着，再没还给你。|你们靠着同一根冰冷的树干，直到星星爬上光秃秃的枝头。}|{~你们一直待到影子比树还长。你错过了晚饭。谁也没提。|光先变成金色，又变成蓝色。{date}轻声说了句什么，你会反复回味好几天。}}}\n# en: Help in the orchard together\nopt.right.label [1e4a49] = 一起在果园帮忙\n# en: {season == \"autumn\": {~You fill two baskets and earn one wasp sting each. The cook gives you a pie, which you share on the steps.|{date} holds the ladder and says nothing useful, and you don't want {them} to.}|{season == \"winter\": {~You prune dead wood side by side until your hands ache. {date} stacks it with great seriousness.|You wrap the young trees against the frost. {date} ties every knot twice.}|{season == \"spring\": {~You carry hives along the rows for the blossom. One bee follows {date} all afternoon.|You clear the grass under the blossom. {date} keeps stopping to look up.}|{~You water the young trees from a bucket chain of two. {date} is soaked by the end, and so are you.|You prop up a branch bowed with early apples. {date} holds it, and holds still, and you notice.}}}}\nopt.right.say.1 [2a9d18] = {season == \"autumn\": {~你们装满了两筐，各挨了一下黄蜂蜇。厨娘给了你们一个馅饼，你们坐在台阶上分着吃了。|{date}扶着梯子，什么有用的话也没说，你也不希望{them}说。}|{season == \"winter\": {~你们并肩修剪枯枝，直到手都酸了。{date}郑重其事地把枝条码好。|你们给小树裹上防霜的草帘。{date}每个结都系两遍。}|{season == \"spring\": {~你们沿着树行搬蜂箱去授粉。一只蜜蜂跟了{date}一下午。|你们清理花下的杂草。{date}总是停下来抬头看。}|{~你们俩排成一条水桶接力，给小树浇水。最后{date}浑身湿透，你也是。|你们撑起一根被早熟苹果压弯的枝条。{date}扶着它，一动不动，你注意到了。}}}}\n\n@event orchard_windfalls\n# en: Windfalls\ntitle [03d58f] = 落果\n# en: {The orchard trees are older than the towers, and never tidy.|The oldest tree has dropped its fruit in a ring, like a table laid for guests.|You know which tree drops what by now, and which one throws.} {season == \"autumn\": Pears and apples lie bruised in the grass, attended by wasps.|{season == \"winter\": Last year's apples lie frozen, hard as stones.|{season == \"spring\": It's mostly blossom, and the odd stubborn pear.|Early apples lie in the shade, small and sharp.}}}\ntext [7efe1c] = {果园的树比七塔还老，从来不收拾干净。|最老的那棵树把果子落成一圈，像为客人摆好的餐桌。|你现在知道哪棵树掉什么，哪棵树会砸人。} {season == \"autumn\": 梨和苹果磕伤了躺在草里，黄蜂在一旁看守。|{season == \"winter\": 去年的苹果冻在地上，硬得像石头。|{season == \"spring\": 大多是落花，还有几只倔强的梨。|早熟的苹果躺在树荫里，又小又酸。}}}\n# en: A student gathering fallen fruit into a basket under gnarled orchard trees, older than the towers behind them.\nscene [0d2ab4] = 盘曲的老果树下，一个学生把落果捡进篮子，树比身后的高塔还老。\n# en: Gather windfalls to sell\nopt.left.label [0a0959] = 捡落果去卖\n# en: {~The cook buys your basket for the cider vats and finds {season == \"winter\": a frozen beetle|a wasp} in it. You both pretend that's normal.|A full basket, {season == \"winter\": numb fingers|two stings} and a crick in your back. The crowns feel earned.}\nopt.left.say.1 [4d832f] = {~厨娘买下你的篮子拿去酿苹果酒，在里面发现了{season == \"winter\": 一只冻僵的甲虫|一只黄蜂}。你们俩都假装这很正常。|满满一篮，{season == \"winter\": 冻僵的手指|两处蜇伤}，外加腰酸。这些克朗挣得踏实。}\n# en: Eat one under the oldest tree\nopt.right.label [bfdcad] = 在最老的树下吃一个\n# en: {~{season == \"winter\": It's frozen solid. Your teeth will remember this.|There was a wasp in it. There was, briefly, a wasp in you.}|It's sour enough to turn your face inside out. A passing first-year laughs, and so, eventually, do you.}\nopt.right.say.1 [ed7210] = {~{season == \"winter\": 冻得像石头。你的牙会记住这一口。|里面有只黄蜂。有那么一会儿，黄蜂在你嘴里。}|酸得你五官都皱到了一起。路过的一年级生笑了，最后你也笑了。}\n# en: {~It's the best thing you've eaten all month. The old tree creaks, as if pleased.|You eat it slowly. Above you, the oldest tree drops one more, gently, beside your hand.}\nopt.right.say.2 [005784] = {~这是你这个月吃过最好吃的东西。老树吱呀一声，像是很高兴。|你慢慢地吃。头顶上，最老的那棵树又轻轻落下一个，正落在你手边。}\n\n@event orchard_cider_shed\n# en: The Cider Shed\ntitle [b0d03f] = 苹果酒棚\n# en: {In the orchard shed, a few students are running an unofficial cider press.|The lookout at the cider shed has started nodding you in.|The cider shed is a fixture now. Even the lookout seems bored.} {season == \"autumn\": The press groans with this year's apples.|The shed smells of last autumn and wet straw.} {~A goat is watching from the hedge, very carefully saying nothing.|Someone has carved a menu into the door.|A prefect's scarf hangs on a nail, unexplained.}\ntext [cc01bb] = {果园的棚子里，几个学生在偷偷经营一台苹果酒榨机。|苹果酒棚望风的人开始点头放你进去了。|苹果酒棚已经成了固定去处。连望风的都显得无聊了。} {season == \"autumn\": 榨机被今年的苹果压得吱嘎作响。|棚里一股去年秋天和湿稻草的味道。} {~一只山羊在树篱边看着，非常小心地一言不发。|有人在门上刻了一份酒单。|钉子上挂着一条级长的围巾，来历不明。}\n# en: A tumbledown orchard shed with a wooden cider press inside, three students turning the screw and a fourth keeping watch at the door.\nscene [31b8c3] = 破旧的果园棚子里有一台木制苹果酒榨机，三个学生在拧螺杆，第四个在门口望风。\n# en: Buy a cup\nopt.left.label [e3b76c] = 买一杯\n# en: {~A prefect turns up as you raise the cup. Everyone is suddenly very interested in apples.|The lookout whistles too late. A prefect takes your name, then, after some thought, your cup.}\nopt.left.say.1 [9b2e70] = {~你刚举起杯子，一个级长就出现了。大家突然都对苹果产生了浓厚兴趣。|望风的口哨吹晚了。一个级长记下你的名字，想了想，又没收了你的杯子。}\n# en: {~It's sharp, cold and a little bit fizzy. The day goes golden at the edges.|You drink it on an upturned bucket while someone tells a story about a goose. Excellent cider. Better goose.}\nopt.left.say.2 [f516a4] = {~酒又酸又凉，还有一点点气泡。这一天的边缘都变成了金色。|你坐在倒扣的桶上喝，有人在讲一只鹅的故事。酒很好。鹅更好。}\n# en: Help turn the press, for a share\nopt.right.label [297dd0] = 帮忙榨酒，换一份\n# en: {~You turn the screw until your shoulders burn. The second-year in charge teaches you why some apples are better bruised.|You're sticky to the elbows and you've learned more about fermentation than in a term of class.}\nopt.right.say.1 [88c45d] = {~你拧螺杆拧到肩膀火辣辣地疼。主事的二年级生教你为什么有些苹果磕伤了反而更好。|你黏到了胳膊肘，学到的发酵知识比一学期的课还多。}\n\n@event maud_cottage_kettle\n# en: Tea at Maud's\ntitle [14a007] = 莫德家的茶\n# en: {Old Maud's kettle is nearly boiling, as it always is. \"Sit down, dearie,\" she says. \"You look like a puddle with a worry.\"|The kettle is nearly boiling. Maud has already put out your cup, the chipped one.|Your chipped cup is waiting. Maud says it's been asking after you.} {~Bartholomew is eating a tea towel.|Rain drums on the thatch.|A hen is asleep on the windowsill.|Bees drone in the eaves.}\ntext [5d334a] = {老莫德的水壶快要开了，一向如此。“坐吧，亲爱的，”她说，“你看着像个犯愁的水洼。”|水壶快要开了。莫德已经摆好了你的杯子，那只有缺口的。|你那只有缺口的杯子在等你。莫德说它一直在打听你。} {~巴塞洛缪在吃一条茶巾。|雨点敲着茅草屋顶。|一只母鸡睡在窗台上。|蜜蜂在屋檐下嗡嗡作响。}\n# en: A low cottage kitchen, a kettle on the hob, an old woman pouring tea into a chipped cup, a goat's head poking through the open door.\nscene [20892a] = 低矮的小屋厨房，炉上坐着水壶，一位老妇人往有缺口的杯子里倒茶，一只山羊从敞开的门外探进头来。\n# en: Stay for a second cup\nopt.left.label [0720c4] = 再喝一杯\n# en: {~Maud tells you a sad story the way other people give a recipe. You walk home late and cold, thinking about it.|\"Never trust a dry spring, dearie,\" she says. You won't understand it for a fortnight.|The second cup becomes a third. It's dark when you leave, and you've only half noticed.}\nopt.left.say.1 [289bdc] = {~莫德像别人讲菜谱那样讲了一个伤心的故事。你很晚才冒着寒气走回去，一路想着它。|“永远别信干燥的春天，亲爱的，”她说。两个星期后你才会明白。|第二杯变成了第三杯。你离开时天已经黑了，你几乎没察觉。}\n# en: Ask her for a remedy\nopt.right.label [72f493] = 向她讨个偏方\n# en: {~She brews you something that tastes of ditches. \"Down in one, dearie.\" You feel better at once, and resent it.|\"For what ails you,\" she says, and doesn't say what she thinks that is. It tastes of nettles and of being right.}\nopt.right.say.1 [3da51f] = {~她给你熬了一碗沟渠味的东西。“一口喝干，亲爱的。”你立刻好多了，并为此愤愤不平。|“治你的毛病，”她说，却没说她觉得你是什么毛病。那味道像荨麻，也像她说对了。}\n\n@event maud_cottage_bartholomew\n# en: Bartholomew Is Out\ntitle [e4bd17] = 巴塞洛缪跑了\n# en: {Bartholomew the goat has got out.|Bartholomew is out again, and has learned to lift the gate latch with his teeth.|Bartholomew has escaped so often that the hens no longer scatter.} {slot == \"evening\": Maud doesn't even look up from her knitting.|Maud's note on the door says she's at the greenhouses, and to mind the goat.} {~He has eaten half a washing line.|He is standing on the roof of the henhouse, looking pleased.|He is in the cabbages, conducting a slow and thorough war.}\ntext [0662f9] = {巴塞洛缪跑出来了。|巴塞洛缪又跑出来了，它学会了用牙齿抬门闩。|巴塞洛缪跑得太勤，母鸡们都懒得四散奔逃了。} {slot == \"evening\": 莫德连头都不从针线活上抬。|门上莫德留的字条说她去温室了，让人看好山羊。} {~它已经吃掉了半根晾衣绳。|它站在鸡舍顶上，一脸得意。|它在卷心菜地里，打一场缓慢而彻底的战争。}\n# en: A goat standing triumphantly on the roof of a henhouse, chickens scattering, a student approaching with an apple held out.\nscene [d9f8cd] = 一只山羊得意洋洋地站在鸡舍顶上，鸡群四散，一个学生举着苹果靠近。\n# en: Chase him down\nopt.left.label [f61b49] = 把它追回来\n# en: {~You corner him by the water butt. He gives up with the air of someone who allowed it. {slot == \"evening\": \"Ta, dearie,\" calls Maud.|You tie him to the gate and leave Maud a note.}|You catch him on the second lap. He chews your sleeve all the way back, as a souvenir.}\nopt.left.say.1 [c51200] = {~你在接雨水的桶边把它堵住了。它投降的样子像是在施舍你。{slot == \"evening\": “谢啦，亲爱的，”莫德喊道。|你把它拴在门上，给莫德留了张字条。}|你在第二圈抓住了它。它一路嚼着你的袖子回去，当作纪念品。}\n# en: {~He leads you twice round the orchard and once through the hen run, then trots home on his own.|You end up in the cabbages. Bartholomew watches you from the roof, chewing, unhurried.}\nopt.left.say.2 [65a8aa] = {~它领着你绕果园跑了两圈，又穿过鸡圈一回，然后自己溜达回家了。|你最后栽进了卷心菜地。巴塞洛缪站在屋顶上看着你，嚼着东西，不慌不忙。}\n# en: Bribe him with your lunch\nopt.right.label [eb4050] = 拿午饭贿赂它\n# en: {~He takes your sandwich and follows you home like a gentleman. You go hungry until supper.|Your lunch disappears in three bites. Bartholomew looks at you as if you might be worth knowing.}\nopt.right.say.1 [6e3d03] = {~它接过你的三明治，像位绅士一样跟你回家。你一直饿到晚饭。|你的午饭三口就没了。巴塞洛缪看着你，像是觉得你或许值得结交。}\n\n@event maud_cottage_top_shelf\n# en: The Top Shelf\ntitle [fd2f93] = 最高一层架子\n# en: {{slot == \"evening\": Maud has stepped out to the hens.|Maud is up at the greenhouses.} The top shelf of jars is right there, every label in her spidery hand.|Maud has stepped out, and the top shelf has gained a jar since you were last here.|You know the top shelf by heart, except for whichever jar Maud added last.} One label reads {~\"not for you, dearie\"|\"only in emergencies\"|\"do not shake\"}, and the jar is {~glowing faintly|humming|warm to the touch}.\ntext [2a1adf] = {{slot == \"evening\": 莫德出去喂鸡了。|莫德去温室了。}最高一层的罐子就在眼前，每张标签都是她那蜘蛛似的笔迹。|莫德出去了，最高一层比你上次来时多了个罐子。|最高一层你已经烂熟于心，只有莫德最近添的那个罐子除外。}其中一张标签写着{~“不是给你的，亲爱的”|“仅限紧急情况”|“切勿摇晃”}，那罐子{~发着微光|在嗡嗡作响|摸上去是温的}。\n# en: A high cottage shelf crowded with stoppered jars and handwritten labels, one jar glowing faintly blue.\nscene [c62b98] = 小屋里高高的架子上挤满了塞着瓶塞的罐子和手写标签，其中一罐发着淡淡的蓝光。\n# en: Open it, just a little\nopt.left.label [73d5dd] = 打开一点点\n# en: {~Something sweet and blue drifts out and settles in your chest. You feel, briefly, enormous.|A breath of summer rain escapes. You put the lid back on smiling.}\nopt.left.say.1 [be6cb4] = {~一缕甜甜的蓝色飘出来，落进你的胸口。有那么一会儿，你觉得自己无比庞大。|一口夏雨的气息逃了出来。你笑着把盖子盖了回去。}\n# en: {~Your eyebrows turn the colour of the label. When Maud sees them, she says nothing whatsoever.|It sneezes. Then you sneeze, for an hour. \"Told you, dearie,\" says Maud later, not unkindly.}\nopt.left.say.2 [c3daf2] = {~你的眉毛变成了标签的颜色。莫德看见后，一个字也没说。|它打了个喷嚏。然后你打了一个钟头的喷嚏。“早说过了，亲爱的，”莫德后来说，语气并不凶。}\n# en: Dust the shelf instead\nopt.right.label [cb81d8] = 改为掸掸灰\n# en: {~You dust every jar and read every label. Maud's handwriting is a whole education, if a strange one.|Maud finds a clean shelf. \"Somebody's been learning,\" she says, which isn't quite thanks.}\nopt.right.say.1 [2ecf20] = {~你掸了每个罐子，读了每张标签。莫德的笔迹本身就是一门学问，虽然古怪。|莫德发现架子干干净净。“有人在长学问了，”她说，这不太算道谢。}\n\n@event secret_garden_humming_pear\n# en: The Humming Pear\ntitle [041751] = 哼歌的梨树\n# en: {The pear tree in Maud's secret garden is humming to itself.|The pear tree has changed its tune since you were last here, as if it's been practising.|The pear tree hums when you come in now. You'd like to think it's for you.} {season == \"winter\": Its tune is low and slow, like something asleep.|{season == \"spring\": Its tune has gone quick and bright with blossom.|{~Its tune is one you almost know.|Its tune has a sad turn in the middle.}}}\ntext [6496d4] = {莫德秘密花园里的梨树在自顾自地哼歌。|自你上次来过，梨树换了个调子，像是一直在练习。|现在你一进来，梨树就开始哼歌。你愿意相信那是为你哼的。} {season == \"winter\": 它的调子又低又慢，像某种在沉睡的东西。|{season == \"spring\": 它的调子随着开花变得轻快明亮。|{~那是一支你几乎会唱的曲子。|那支曲子中间有个忧伤的转折。}}}\n# en: A student lying in the grass under a pear tree in a hidden walled garden, ferns glowing softly around them.\nscene [69e58c] = 隐蔽的围墙花园里，一个学生躺在梨树下的草地上，周围的蕨类柔柔发光。\n# en: Hum along with it\nopt.left.label [d18c15] = 跟着它一起哼\n# en: {~You find the harmony on the third try. The tree corrects you twice, kindly.|The two of you hum until the ferns glow brighter. Afterwards you can't get the tune out of your head for days.}\nopt.left.say.1 [6e09d2] = {~第三次你才找到和声。梨树温和地纠正了你两次。|你们一起哼，直到蕨类更亮了。之后好几天，那调子都在你脑子里挥之不去。}\n# en: Nap in its shade\nopt.right.label [981938] = 在树荫下打个盹\n# en: {~You wake rested, with a crown missing from your pocket and a fresh root curled over the spot where it fell.|You sleep like a seed. A crown slips out of your pocket and the roots close over it. Rent, perhaps.}\nopt.right.say.1 [342475] = {~你醒来时神清气爽，口袋里少了一枚克朗，掉落的地方盘着一条新长的根。|你睡得像一颗种子。一枚克朗从口袋滑出，树根把它合拢了。大概是房租吧。}\n\n@event secret_garden_silver_bees\n# en: The Silver Bees\ntitle [1eab66] = 银蜂\n# en: {The silver bees are working the ferns; their hive in the old stump drips honey the colour of moonlight.|The silver bees have built a second comb, lopsided, as if in a hurry.|The silver bees know you now, more or less.} {season == \"winter\": Even in winter they fly, slowly, like snow going the wrong way.|{~The comb is fuller than you've ever seen it.|One of them lands on your sleeve and stays.|They're dancing a direction you can't read.}}\ntext [af42a7] = {银蜂在蕨类间忙碌；老树桩里的蜂巢淌着月光色的蜂蜜。|银蜂又筑了第二块蜂巢，歪歪扭扭，像是赶工。|银蜂现在或多或少认得你了。} {season == \"winter\": 即使在冬天它们也在飞，慢慢的，像飘错了方向的雪。|{~蜂巢比你见过的任何时候都满。|一只落在你的袖子上，不走了。|它们跳着一个你读不懂的方向。}}\n# en: Bees made of silver light hovering around a hive in a hollow stump, a student reaching carefully towards a dripping comb.\nscene [db712c] = 银光构成的蜜蜂绕着空心树桩里的蜂巢盘旋，一个学生小心翼翼地伸手去取滴蜜的蜂巢。\n# en: Take a little comb\nopt.left.label [9d8f0c] = 取一小块蜂巢\n# en: {~A silver sting feels like a cold spark. Your hand glows for an hour and aches for two.|The bees file a complaint, in the form of your thumb.}\nopt.left.say.1 [fcb1a8] = {~被银蜂蜇一下，像一颗冰冷的火星。你的手亮了一个钟头，又疼了两个钟头。|蜜蜂提出了抗议——抗议书就是你肿起来的拇指。}\n# en: {~You take a thumb's width of comb. It tastes like starlight and burns a little going down.|The bees let you take a little, and watch to make sure it's only a little.}\nopt.left.say.2 [3fb9fc] = {~你取了拇指宽的一小块。尝起来像星光，咽下去有点烫。|蜜蜂让你取了一点，还盯着确保真的只有一点。}\n# en: Follow a bee home\nopt.right.label [3d2092] = 跟着一只蜜蜂回家\n# en: {~The bee leads you through brambles to a fern you've never seen, and then leaves you there, scratched and curious.|You follow it all the way round the garden and back to the stump. It seems to find this funny.}\nopt.right.say.1 [38c41e] = {~蜜蜂领你穿过荆棘，来到一株你从没见过的蕨类前，然后把你丢在那儿，满身划痕，满心好奇。|你跟着它绕花园一圈，又回到树桩。它似乎觉得这很好笑。}\n\n@event secret_garden_glowing_ferns\n# en: The Glowing Ferns\ntitle [725061] = 发光的蕨类\n# en: {The ferns in the secret garden glow when the light goes. One frond would light your dormitory for a month. Maud said not to pick them.|Since your last visit a new fern has unfurled by the gate, already glowing.|The ferns glow. You know exactly what a frond is worth, and what Maud said.} {~One has grown over the path, right at your hand.|A broken frond lies on the ground already, still glowing.|They're brighter than you've ever seen them.}\ntext [0a5c00] = {天色一暗，秘密花园里的蕨类就亮了。一片叶子就能把你的宿舍照亮一个月。莫德说过不许摘。|自你上次来过，门边又舒展开一株新蕨，已经亮了。|蕨类在发光。你清楚一片叶子值多少，也清楚莫德说过什么。} {~有一株长到了小路上，正在你手边。|地上已经有一片折断的叶子，还在发光。|它们比你见过的任何时候都亮。}\n# en: A hidden garden at dusk, waist-high ferns glowing pale green, one student crouched with fingers near a frond.\nscene [cac6b0] = 黄昏时的隐秘花园，齐腰高的蕨类发着淡绿色的光，一个学生蹲着，手指靠近一片叶子。\n# en: Pick a single frond\nopt.left.label [ed6b96] = 只摘一片\n# en: {~The frond goes dark before you reach the door. The garden feels a little colder to you the next time.|The frond curls up in your hand like a woodlouse and goes out. The silver bees follow you to the gate, accusingly.}\nopt.left.say.1 [6a94a0] = {~还没走到门口，那片叶子就暗了。下次来时，花园对你冷淡了一些。|叶子在你手里像鼠妇一样蜷起来，熄灭了。银蜂一路跟你到门口，满是责备。}\n# en: {~It lights your dormitory green for a week. You read by it, and feel very slightly watched.|You keep it in a jar by your bed. It glows softer every night, like something being polite.}\nopt.left.say.2 [f9103b] = {~它把你的宿舍照成绿色，亮了一个星期。你借着它读书，总觉得有一点点被注视。|你把它放在床边的罐子里。它一夜比一夜暗，像在客气。}\n# en: Leave them glowing\nopt.right.label [76d055] = 让它们继续发光\n# en: {~You sit and watch the glow instead, and learn the way it moves with your breath. Maud would approve, if she knew.|You leave them be. On the way out, the ferns lean after you, like a dog that wanted to come.}\nopt.right.say.1 [997205] = {~你坐下来看那光，发现它随着你的呼吸起伏。莫德要是知道，会赞许的。|你没去碰它们。离开时，蕨类朝你的方向倾着，像一只想跟来的狗。}\n\n@event courier_loft_letter_home\n# en: A Letter Home\ntitle [8bce33] = 一封家书\n# en: {The courier loft will take a letter home for next to nothing. You've meant to write for weeks.|This time the letter has been half-written in your head since breakfast.|Writing home is a habit now, and the loft knows your handwriting.} {final_year: There aren't many letters left to send from here.|{season == \"winter\": Frost on the window; the pigeonholes stuffed with cards.|{~The dispatch bell rings twice while you write.|A broken broom leans against your elbow.}}}\ntext [4aab95] = {信使阁楼花不了几个钱就能帮你把信送回家。你已经想写好几个星期了。|这一封从早饭起就在你脑子里写了一半。|往家里写信成了习惯，阁楼也认得你的笔迹了。} {final_year: 能从这里寄出的信不多了。|{season == \"winter\": 窗上结着霜，信格里塞满了贺卡。|{~你写信时，发件铃响了两次。|一把坏扫帚靠在你胳膊肘边。}}}\n# en: A draughty loft full of pigeonholes, a student writing a letter on a parcel by the light of a high window.\nscene [953afc] = 满是信格的透风阁楼里，一个学生借着高窗的光，在一个包裹上写信。\n# en: Write it all down and send it\nopt.left.label [2de5e0] = 全写下来寄出去\n# en: {~You write three pages and post them before you can cross anything out. You feel lighter, and a little homesick.|You tell them about your classes, and not about the hard parts. Then, in a postscript, you tell them about the hard parts.}\nopt.left.say.1 [99363f] = {~你写了三页，在来得及划掉任何一句之前就寄了出去。你觉得轻松了些，也有点想家。|你跟他们讲了你的课，没讲难处。然后，在附言里，你讲了难处。}\n# en: Write it, then don't send it\nopt.right.label [cf5f6f] = 写好了，但不寄\n# en: {~You read it over and fold it into your pocket instead. Some letters are only for writing.|You keep this one. The courier asks if you're sure. You aren't.}\nopt.right.say.1 [dc4ba8] = {~你读了一遍，折好放进口袋。有些信只是用来写的。|这封你留下了。信使问你确定吗。你并不确定。}\n\n@event courier_loft_dispatch_bell\n# en: The Dispatch Bell\ntitle [e813ff] = 发件铃\n# en: {The dispatch bell clangs and the loft explodes into brooms. A courier shoves a parcel at you. \"You've got legs. Lecture tower!\"|The couriers have started eyeing the ladder whenever someone climbs it.|The bell. A parcel. A look. You know the drill.} {~The parcel is ticking.|The parcel is addressed to \"Whoever Is On Duty\".|The parcel is very cold.}\ntext [e03a27] = {发件铃一响，阁楼里扫帚乱飞。一个信使把包裹塞给你。“你有腿。讲堂塔！”|现在每次有人爬梯子，信使们都会瞟上一眼。|铃声。包裹。一个眼神。你知道该怎么做。} {~包裹在滴答作响。|包裹上写着“寄给值班的随便谁”。|包裹冰凉冰凉的。}\n# en: A chaotic loft as a dispatch bell clangs, couriers grabbing brooms and parcels, one holding a parcel out to a visitor.\nscene [b87d28] = 发件铃大作，乱成一团的阁楼里信使们抓起扫帚和包裹，其中一个把包裹递给来客。\n# en: Run the parcel across campus\nopt.left.label [71bfc4] = 跑一趟送包裹\n# en: {~You get it there out of breath and in time. The tip is a crown and a very strange look.|You run it up four flights of stairs. The recipient signs for it without looking up, as if you were the post.}\nopt.left.say.1 [e47c5c] = {~你气喘吁吁但准时送到了。小费是一枚克朗外加一个非常奇怪的眼神。|你跑上四层楼。收件人头也不抬就签了字，好像你本来就是邮差。}\n# en: Hand it back, politely\nopt.right.label [b4d5ae] = 客气地还回去\n# en: {~\"Fine, fine,\" says the courier, already on a broom. You hear about it, loudly, from the rafters.|The courier takes it back with a sigh that follows you down the ladder.}\nopt.right.say.1 [e35daf] = {~“行吧行吧，”信使说着已经跨上了扫帚。你从椽子上方听到了响亮的抱怨。|信使叹着气接回包裹，那声叹息一路跟着你下了梯子。}\n\n@event courier_loft_broom_rack\n# en: The Fast Broom\ntitle [0775c7] = 快扫帚\n# en: {At the end of the rack is the courier loft's fast broom, tagged in red. Couriers only. Nobody's watching.|The fast broom is back on the rack, red tag and all.|The red-tagged broom. You've thought about it every time you've come up here.} {season == \"winter\": The loft doors are open on a sky full of snow.|{~The loft doors stand open on a blue sky.|Someone has polished it recently.|The red tag has a fresh scorch mark.}}\ntext [1b6ec0] = {架子尽头是信使阁楼的快扫帚，挂着红牌。仅限信使。没人在看。|快扫帚又回到了架子上，红牌也在。|那把挂红牌的扫帚。每次上来你都会想到它。} {season == \"winter\": 阁楼的门敞着，外面是漫天飞雪。|{~阁楼的门敞着，外面是蓝天。|最近有人给它上过油。|红牌上有一道新焦痕。}}\n# en: A rack of courier brooms in a loft, one sleek black broom set apart from the others with a red tag on its handle.\nscene [76169f] = 阁楼里一排信使扫帚，其中一把光滑的黑扫帚单独放着，柄上挂着红牌。\n# en: Take it for one lap\nopt.left.label [c03fc1] = 骑它飞一圈\n# en: {~One lap of the towers, fast enough to make your ears sing. You put it back with the bristles barely warm.|You fly it under the bridges of light and come back laughing. Nobody saw, except, possibly, everybody.}\nopt.left.say.1 [f2bce0] = {~绕七塔一圈，快得耳朵嗡嗡响。你放回去时，帚毛几乎没热。|你骑着它从光之桥底下穿过，笑着飞回来。没人看见，除了，也许，所有人。}\n# en: {~It knows you aren't a courier. It shows you the courtyard, very close up. You pay for the bristles.|It bucks you off into a haystack outside the stables. You tip the stable hand to say nothing.}\nopt.left.say.2 [ad18ee] = {~它知道你不是信使。它让你近距离地看了看庭院的地面。帚毛钱你来赔。|它把你甩进了马厩外的干草堆。你给马夫塞了小费，让他别声张。}\n# en: Tighten its bristles instead\nopt.right.label [8b8ed8] = 改为给它紧紧帚毛\n# en: {~You spend an hour learning how a good broom is bound. It's more satisfying than you'd admit.|You retie the bristles properly. Some courier will fly a little faster tomorrow and never know why.}\nopt.right.say.1 [f4a58a] = {~你花了一个钟头学一把好扫帚是怎么扎成的。这比你愿意承认的更让人满足。|你把帚毛重新扎好。明天会有个信使飞得快一点，却永远不知道为什么。}\n\n@event whisperwood_sleeptalk\n# en: The Forest Talks\ntitle [380652] = 森林在说话\n# en: {The Whisperwood is talking in its sleep. You stand very still to make out the words.|You've started to catch whole words in the forest's mumbling.|You've learned to stand still and let the forest talk.} Today it's {~listing trees that were felled|arguing about the rain|saying a name, over and over, that isn't yours}. {season == \"winter\": Snow muffles half of it.|{season == \"autumn\": Leaves fall with every word.|Fog moves between the trunks.}}\ntext [3f3c49] = {低语森林在说梦话。你一动不动地站着，想听清那些词。|你开始能听清森林嘟囔里的整个词了。|你已经学会静静站着，让森林说下去。}今天它在{~列举被砍倒的树|为下雨的事争吵|一遍遍念着一个不是你的名字}。{season == \"winter\": 雪捂住了一半。|{season == \"autumn\": 每说一个字就落下一片叶子。|雾在树干间游移。}}\n# en: A student standing very still in a misty old forest, head tilted, as if listening to the trees.\nscene [9d0c9e] = 雾气弥漫的古老森林里，一个学生一动不动地站着，侧着头，像是在听树说话。\n# en: Listen closely\nopt.left.label [21f0ed] = 仔细听\n# en: {~You catch a whole sentence. It's about a winter long ago, and it's sad, and you walk home with it.|It notices you listening and goes quiet, the way a sleeper does. You leave feeling rude.}\nopt.left.say.1 [d37f87] = {~你听清了完整的一句。那是关于很久以前的一个冬天，很悲伤，你带着它走回了家。|它发现你在听，便像睡着的人那样安静下来。你离开时觉得自己很失礼。}\n# en: Walk on, humming\nopt.right.label [f1a5bc] = 哼着歌继续走\n# en: {~You walk the long loop humming loudly. The forest grumbles and turns over. You come back with muddy boots and a clear head.|You hum all the way round. Twice, the forest hums back.}\nopt.right.say.1 [bd45a9] = {~你大声哼着歌走完长长的一圈。森林嘟囔着翻了个身。你带着一靴子泥和清醒的脑袋回来。|你一路哼着歌。有两次，森林也哼了回来。}\n\n@event whisperwood_foragers\n# en: Foraging Season\ntitle [067bb9] = 采集季\n# en: {season == \"autumn\": The Whisperwood is full of mushrooms and students with baskets.|{season == \"winter\": A few hardy foragers are scraping lichen off the oaks.|{season == \"spring\": The clearings are thick with wild garlic and foragers.|The brambles are heavy with berries, and so are the foragers.}}} {A second-year from Rootward waves you over.|The Rootward forager is back, and saves you a spot.|The foragers treat you as one of their own now.}\ntext [62e283] = {season == \"autumn\": 低语森林里满是蘑菇和提着篮子的学生。|{season == \"winter\": 几个不怕冷的采集者在刮橡树上的地衣。|{season == \"spring\": 林间空地长满了野蒜，也挤满了采集者。|荆棘丛上挂满了浆果，采集者身上也挂满了。}}} {一个根庐的二年级生朝你招手。|那个根庐的采集者又来了，给你留了个位置。|采集者们现在把你当自己人了。}\n# en: A handful of students with baskets crouching in a forest clearing, one holding up a mushroom for the others to inspect.\nscene [6bbb73] = 几个提篮子的学生蹲在林间空地上，其中一个举起一只蘑菇给其他人看。\n# en: Forage for the apothecary\nopt.left.label [c317dd] = 为药铺采集\n# en: {~Pell & Daughters buys your basket and throws out a third of it. You've still got nettle rash.|A good basket, a fair price, and brambles in places you'd rather not discuss.}\nopt.left.say.1 [bf43eb] = {~佩尔父女药铺收下你的篮子，扔掉了三分之一。你身上的荨麻疹还在。|一篮好货，一个公道价，还有一些你不愿细说的地方扎进了荆棘。}\n# en: Learn the plants from the expert\nopt.right.label [5629c5] = 跟行家认植物\n# en: {~The second-year names everything, twice, with its uses and its poisons. You come away full of names and short of patience.|You learn which mushroom is supper and which is a funeral. It takes until supper, and you'll need it.}\nopt.right.say.1 [77f64a] = {~那个二年级生把每样东西都说了两遍，连用途带毒性。你满脑子名字，耐心所剩无几。|你学会了分辨哪种蘑菇是晚餐，哪种是葬礼。一直学到晚饭时分，你会用得上的。}\n\n@event whisperwood_off_path\n# en: The Path That Wasn't\ntitle [916a4a] = 本不存在的小路\n# en: {A path opens to your left that wasn't there last time.|Another path that wasn't there before. The Whisperwood does this.|The wood has offered you a new path again. You're starting to think it likes you.} {~It's lined with pale stones.|It smells of woodsmoke where there's no fire.|It goes downhill, which is wrong, because you're at the bottom of the hill.}\ntext [f9bc34] = {你左手边出现了一条上次没有的小路。|又一条以前没有的小路。低语森林就是这样。|森林又给你开了一条新路。你开始觉得它喜欢你。} {~路边排着苍白的石头。|没有火，却闻得到柴烟味。|它往下坡走，这不对，因为你已经在山脚了。}\n# en: A narrow path branching off into fog between old trees, a student pausing at the fork with a hand on a marked trunk.\nscene [a1f31c] = 古树间一条窄路分岔伸进雾里，一个学生在岔口停下，手扶着一棵做了记号的树干。\n# en: Follow it a little way\nopt.left.label [03da75] = 跟着走一小段\n# en: {~It leads to a clearing you'll never find again, full of moonpetal buds. You come back with a pocketful of quiet.|It loops you round to an old stone with words on it. You copy them down. They mean nothing yet.}\nopt.left.say.1 [38bca7] = {~它通向一片你再也找不到的空地，开满了月瓣花的花苞。你带回了满满一口袋的安静。|它绕着你来到一块刻着字的老石头前。你把字抄了下来。它们暂时还没有意义。}\n# en: {~You walk in circles until dusk and come out at the lake, with no idea how.|The path ends at a wall of fog. Turning back takes much longer than going in did.}\nopt.left.say.2 [e6445e] = {~你转圈转到黄昏，出来时到了湖边，不知道是怎么走到的。|小路尽头是一堵雾墙。往回走比进来花的时间长得多。}\n# en: Mark a tree and turn back\nopt.right.label [851506] = 在树上做记号，回头\n# en: {~You carve an arrow in the bark. The next time you come, the arrow points somewhere else.|You turn back. The path watches you go, and quietly closes behind you.}\nopt.right.say.1 [6b720f] = {~你在树皮上刻了个箭头。下次来时，箭头指向了别处。|你转身回去。小路目送你离开，在你身后悄悄合上了。}\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/pools/plans_gigs.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/pools/plans_gigs.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event holiday_gigs\n# en: Holiday Work\ntitle [ec8a5c] = 假期打工\n# en: Two notices are still pinned by the gates. {season == \"winter\": The snow has got under one of them.|The sun has curled the corners of both.} Old Maud wants hands for the grounds. The Refectory kitchens want someone who can peel fast and not complain about it.\ntext [38e9ab] = 城门边还钉着两张告示。{season == \"winter\": 雪已经钻到其中一张底下了。|太阳把两张的边角都晒卷了。}老莫德要人手打理庭园。膳堂后厨要一个削皮快、还不抱怨的人。\n# en: A noticeboard by the castle gates in the emptying holidays, two handwritten notices pinned side by side.\nscene [e17fee] = 假期里渐渐空下来的城堡大门边，一块告示板上并排钉着两张手写告示。\n# en: Work the grounds for Old Maud\nopt.grounds.label [304060] = 跟老莫德打理庭园\n# en: Outdoor work, heavy and honest. Good pay; hard on the body.\nopt.grounds.hint [1918f6] = 户外活，累但实在。工钱不错；伤身体。\n# en: Maud looks you up and down, hands you a pair of gloves two sizes too big, and says, \"You'll grow into them.\"\nopt.grounds.say.1 [d53e24] = 莫德把你从头打量到脚，递给你一副大了两号的手套，说：“你会长大的。”\n# en: Work the Refectory kitchens\nopt.kitchens.label [fb1ad3] = 去膳堂后厨帮工\n# en: Long hours by the ovens. Good pay; hard on the temper.\nopt.kitchens.hint [305589] = 在炉灶边站一整天。工钱不错；磨脾气。\n# en: The head cook gives you an apron, a knife and a sack of onions, and doesn't ask your name until the sack is empty.\nopt.kitchens.say.1 [e58aab] = 大厨递给你一条围裙、一把刀和一麻袋洋葱，直到麻袋空了才问你叫什么。\n\n@event gig_grounds_hard_ground\n# en: Hard Ground\ntitle [fbbd07] = 硬土\n# en: {season == \"winter\": The ground is frozen two fingers deep and Maud wants the beds turned anyway.|The ground is baked hard and Maud wants the beds turned anyway.} \"The soil doesn't take holidays,\" she says. {~She hands you the heavier spade.|The bees watch you from their hives with professional interest.|She is already on her third bed.}\ntext [3eb87e] = {season == \"winter\": 地冻了两指深，莫德照样要你把苗床翻一遍。|地被晒得梆硬，莫德照样要你把苗床翻一遍。}“土可不放假。”她说。{~她把重的那把铁锹递给你。|蜂箱里的蜜蜂以一种专业的兴趣看着你。|她已经在翻第三块了。}\n# en: A student and an old woman working a frozen garden bed at dawn, breath steaming, a wheelbarrow between them.\nscene [2f53ea] = 黎明时分，一个学生和一位老妇人在结冻的苗床上干活，呼出白气，两人之间放着一辆手推车。\n# en: Dig until it's done\nopt.left.label [9aec2a] = 一直挖到干完\n# en: {~By noon your hands have blisters on their blisters. Maud counts your wages into them anyway.|You turn every bed. Maud inspects them, says \"Hm,\" and pays you a little over.}\nopt.left.say.1 [961929] = {~到中午，你手上的水泡又起了水泡。莫德照样把工钱数进你手里。|你翻完了每一块苗床。莫德检查一遍，说了声“嗯”，多给了你一点。}\n# en: Work at Maud's pace\nopt.right.label [1e7055] = 跟着莫德的节奏\n# en: {~Maud works slowly and never stops, and by evening you understand why. Less done, a smaller purse, an easier back.|You keep to her pace and her stories. She pays for the beds you turned, not the ones she did.}\nopt.right.say.1 [fe8c13] = {~莫德干得慢，却从不停手，到傍晚你才明白为什么。活干得少，钱袋轻些，腰也轻松些。|你跟着她的节奏，听着她的故事。她按你翻的苗床付钱，不算她翻的。}\n\n@event gig_grounds_storm_damage\n# en: After the Storm\ntitle [683893] = 风暴过后\n# en: {season == \"winter\": Last night's storm brought down half a pear tree.|A summer storm in the night has stripped the orchard.} Maud wants it cleared before the stay-behinds go walking. A cracked limb still hangs over the path.\ntext [506bdf] = {season == \"winter\": 昨夜的风暴吹倒了半棵梨树。|夜里一场夏日风暴把果园刮得七零八落。}莫德要在留校的人出来散步之前把路清出来。一根裂开的树枝还悬在小路上方。\n# en: A fallen branch across an orchard path after a storm, a student with a saw, an old woman pointing at something higher up.\nscene [a80029] = 风暴过后，一根断枝横在果园小路上，一个学生拿着锯子，一位老妇人指着更高处的什么东西。\n# en: Climb up and cut it down\nopt.left.label [d35b25] = 爬上去把它锯下来\n# en: {~The limb comes down where you meant it to. Maud pays you extra for not falling.|You lower it on a rope like a professional. Maud pretends not to be impressed.}\nopt.left.say.1 [378de5] = {~树枝正落在你想让它落的地方。莫德因为你没摔下来，多给了你钱。|你像个行家一样用绳子把它放了下来。莫德假装没被打动。}\n# en: {~The limb comes down where it wanted to. So do you, more or less. Maud pays you and sends you to the infirmary.|You get the limb and the limb gets you.}\nopt.left.say.2 [10307d] = {~树枝落在了它想落的地方。你也差不多。莫德付了钱，把你送去了医务室。|你拿下了树枝，树枝也拿下了你。}\n# en: Rope it off and clear the rest\nopt.right.label [27673a] = 拉绳隔开，清理其余\n# en: {~You clear the path and rope off the danger. Maud nods: she'll ask someone with a ladder. The pay is for the path.|Safe and sensible, and a smaller purse for it.}\nopt.right.say.1 [8ff26f] = {~你清好了小路，把危险的地方用绳子隔开。莫德点点头：她会找个有梯子的人来。工钱只算小路的。|稳妥又明智，钱袋也因此轻一些。}\n\n@event gig_grounds_hive_check\n# en: Checking the Hives\ntitle [5a5a35] = 查看蜂箱\n# en: Maud's hives sit at the end of the orchard and she checks them {season == \"winter\": by ear in winter, knocking and listening|by hand in summer, slow and bare-armed}. Today she wants you to do it. \"Tell them who you are,\" she says. \"They like manners.\"\ntext [169e49] = 莫德的蜂箱放在果园尽头，她{season == \"winter\": 冬天靠耳朵检查，敲一敲，听一听|夏天亲手检查，慢慢来，光着胳膊}。今天她要你来做。“告诉它们你是谁，”她说，“它们讲礼貌。”\n# en: A student in a veiled hat leaning over an open beehive beside an old woman, who isn't wearing one.\nscene [d6c9fc] = 一个戴着面纱帽的学生俯身在打开的蜂箱前，旁边的老妇人什么也没戴。\n# en: Introduce yourself properly\nopt.left.label [182928] = 规规矩矩地自我介绍\n# en: {~You tell the bees your name and your house. Only one stings you, on principle. Maud pays you and calls you a natural.|You feel foolish for about a minute, and then you don't. The hive hums back, and one bee makes a point of it.}\nopt.left.say.1 [5a9176] = {~你把名字和学院告诉了蜜蜂。只有一只蜇了你，出于原则。莫德付了钱，说你是天生的料。|你觉得自己傻了大约一分钟，然后就不觉得了。蜂箱嗡嗡地回应你，有一只蜜蜂特意强调了一下。}\n# en: Get it done quickly\nopt.right.label [b40e28] = 快点干完\n# en: {~Quick and quiet. The bees let it pass this once.|You're done before the bees notice. Maud notices.}\nopt.right.say.1 [0c0b37] = {~又快又安静。蜜蜂这次放过了你。|你干完了，蜜蜂还没察觉。莫德察觉了。}\n# en: {~The bees take your hurry personally. Maud pays you and pulls out the stings with no sympathy at all.|Three stings and a lesson about manners.}\nopt.right.say.2 [f4f88a] = {~蜜蜂把你的匆忙当成了冒犯。莫德付了钱，毫无同情地替你拔出蜂刺。|三根蜂刺，外加一堂礼貌课。}\n\n@event gig_kitchens_onions\n# en: The Onion Sack\ntitle [c188ed] = 洋葱麻袋\n# en: {The holiday kitchens feed fewer mouths and work no fewer hours.|Back at the long table, back to the sack.|The head cook has started saving the worst sack for you, as a compliment.} {season == \"winter\": The Midwinter stock needs a hundred onions.|The summer stew needs a hundred onions.} Nobody has ever needed fewer.\ntext [dc295a] = {假期里的后厨要喂的嘴少了，活一点没少。|回到长桌，回到麻袋。|大厨开始把最糟的那袋留给你，算是一种夸奖。}{season == \"winter\": 仲冬的高汤要一百个洋葱。|夏天的炖菜要一百个洋葱。}从来没人需要少一点。\n# en: A student at a scrubbed kitchen table behind a mountain of onions, eyes streaming, a huge cook stirring a cauldron behind them.\nscene [a0922b] = 擦洗干净的厨房长桌后，一个学生埋在洋葱堆里泪流满面，身后一个高大的厨子在搅一口大锅。\n# en: Peel the whole sack\nopt.left.label [211f4f] = 把整袋都削完\n# en: {~A hundred onions and not one tear left in you. The head cook pays you and adds a heel of fresh bread.|You finish the sack. The head cook counts the onions, then your wages, both correctly.}\nopt.left.say.1 [593e83] = {~一百个洋葱，你一滴眼泪都不剩了。大厨付了钱，又塞给你一块新鲜面包。|你削完了整袋。大厨数了洋葱，又数了你的工钱，都没数错。}\n# en: Peel, then help at the stove\nopt.right.label [f73412] = 削一半，再去灶台帮忙\n# en: {~Half a sack and an hour at the stove. Less pay, but now you know why the soup tastes like home.|The head cook lets you stir. Somehow the stew tastes of your grandmother's. You get paid for the onions.}\nopt.right.say.1 [675088] = {~半袋洋葱，加上灶台边的一个钟头。钱少些，但现在你知道这汤为什么有家的味道了。|大厨让你搅锅。不知怎么，炖菜有了你外婆的味道。你拿到了洋葱的工钱。}\n\n@event gig_kitchens_burnt_pan\n# en: The Burnt Pan\ntitle [ce0174] = 烧焦的锅\n# en: You turn away for a moment and the porridge for the stay-behinds catches. The smell is already halfway to the dining hall. The head cook hasn't noticed. Yet.\ntext [3467e5] = 你一转身，给留校的人煮的粥就糊了。焦味已经飘到半路的饭堂。大厨还没发现。暂时。\n# en: A smoking pan on a range, a student holding it at arm's length, a cook turning round with a ladle raised.\nscene [078326] = 灶台上一口冒烟的锅，一个学生伸直胳膊举着它，一个厨子举着汤勺转过身来。\n# en: Own up and start again\nopt.left.label [df7779] = 坦白，重新煮\n# en: {~The head cook sighs the sigh of thirty years in kitchens and docks you for the oats. You make it again, properly.|\"Everyone burns one,\" says the head cook. \"Once.\" You are paid, minus the oats.}\nopt.left.say.1 [d4b5ef] = {~大厨叹了一口三十年后厨的气，扣了你的燕麦钱。你重新煮了一锅，这次好好煮。|“谁都烧糊过一锅，”大厨说，“一次。”你拿到了工钱，扣掉燕麦。}\n# en: Scrape it and serve it\nopt.right.label [1bae79] = 刮一刮端出去\n# en: {~Nobody notices. The stay-behinds eat it and ask for more. You don't feel great about it.|A little smoky. \"Rustic,\" says someone. You get the full day's pay.}\nopt.right.say.1 [15e03b] = {~没人发现。留校的人吃了还要添。你心里不太舒服。|有点烟熏味。“很有乡土风味。”有人说。你拿到了全天的工钱。}\n# en: {~A professor sends it back with a note. The head cook reads it aloud, slowly, to the whole kitchen.|The smell gives you away. You're paid in full and in disapproval.}\nopt.right.say.2 [8635a7] = {~一位教授把粥退了回来，附了张字条。大厨当着整个后厨，一字一字慢慢念了出来。|焦味出卖了你。工钱一分不少，白眼也一个不少。}\n\n@event gig_kitchens_feast_night\n# en: Feast Night\ntitle [17ed66] = 宴会之夜\n# en: {season == \"winter\": The castle keeps a small Midwinter table for those who stayed, and the kitchens cook as if for the whole school.|The stay-behinds hold a summer supper in the courtyard, and the kitchens cook as if for the whole school.} Trays go out faster than you can fill them.\ntext [01e659] = {season == \"winter\": 城堡为留下来的人摆了一小桌仲冬宴，后厨却按全校的分量在做。|留校的人在庭院里办夏日晚餐，后厨却按全校的分量在做。}托盘端出去的速度比你装盘还快。\n# en: A steaming kitchen on a feast night, trays going out in a stream, a student in an apron grinning through the chaos.\nscene [53b738] = 宴会之夜热气腾腾的后厨，托盘一个接一个端出去，一个系着围裙的学生在混乱中咧嘴笑着。\n# en: Carry trays all night\nopt.left.label [9101e4] = 整晚端托盘\n# en: {~You carry until your arms forget being arms. The head cook pays you double for the night, in coins and leftovers.|Up and down the stairs until midnight. Somebody toasts the kitchens and you hear it through the door.}\nopt.left.say.1 [aecfbc] = {~你端到胳膊都忘了自己是胳膊。大厨付了你这一晚的双份工钱，一份是钱，一份是剩菜。|楼上楼下一直跑到半夜。有人为后厨举杯，你隔着门都听见了。}\n# en: Stay in the back and plate\nopt.right.label [5d859a] = 待在后面装盘\n# en: {~You plate by the ovens and listen to the feast through the wall. Smaller pay, a scorched wrist, and the best seat in the house.|Quiet hands in a hot room. The head cook gives you the last slice of pie, which is its own wage.}\nopt.right.say.1 [cae555] = {~你在烤炉边装盘，隔着墙听宴会的热闹。钱少些，手腕烫了一下，却是全场最好的位置。|热屋子里一双安静的手。大厨把最后一块派给了你，这本身就是一份工钱。}\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/pools/plans_more.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/pools/plans_more.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event break_castle_holiday_repairs\n# en: Holiday Repairs\ntitle [d8f001] = 假期修缮\n# en: {The caretaker has a list of jobs that can only be done when the castle is empty, and an eye for idle students.|The caretaker's list is out again. Your name is pencilled in at the bottom, provisionally.|The caretaker hands you the list and a mug of tea, in that order.} {season == \"winter\": Today it's snow on the battlements, heavy enough to worry the roof leads.|Today it's the dormitory mattresses, which must be carried out and beaten in the sun.}\ntext [74f49c] = {看门人有一张只能趁城堡空着时才能干的活儿清单，还有一双专盯闲人的眼睛。|看门人的清单又拿出来了。你的名字用铅笔写在最底下，暂定。|看门人递给你清单和一杯茶，顺序如上。} {season == \"winter\": 今天是城垛上的积雪，重得叫屋顶的铅皮都发愁。|今天是宿舍的床垫，得一张张搬出去，在太阳底下拍打。}\n# en: A caretaker with a long list on a clipboard in an empty castle corridor, a student trying to look busy nearby.\nscene [b4f7a1] = 空荡荡的城堡走廊里，看门人拿着夹板上的一长串清单，旁边一个学生努力装出很忙的样子。\n# en: Lend a hand\nopt.left.label [d5e12c] = 搭把手\n# en: {season == \"winter\": {~You shovel snow off the battlements until you can't feel your ears. The view is worth one of them.|You and the caretaker clear a whole parapet in silence. At the end you're handed a biscuit, which is how the caretaker says thank you.}|{~You beat forty mattresses. Two of them contained socks, one a love letter, and one a very surprised mouse.|You carry mattresses down six flights of moving stairs. One flight moves halfway through, with you and the mattress on it.}}\nopt.left.say.1 [82db30] = {season == \"winter\": {~你在城垛上铲雪，铲到耳朵没了知觉。这景色值其中一只耳朵。|你和看门人一声不吭地清完了一整段城墙。最后你得到一块饼干，这是看门人说谢谢的方式。}|{~你拍了四十张床垫。其中两张里有袜子，一张里有情书，还有一张里有一只非常吃惊的老鼠。|你扛着床垫下了六段会动的楼梯。有一段走到一半动了，连你带床垫一起。}}\n# en: Make myself scarce\nopt.right.label [8a06da] = 我先溜了\n# en: {~You remember an urgent appointment with a book and vanish round a corner. The caretaker's sigh follows you a long way.|You hide in a window seat until the list has moved on to someone else.}\nopt.right.say.1 [3ca560] = {~你想起一本书有急事等你，拐个弯就不见了。看门人的叹气声追了你好远。|你躲在窗台座里，直到清单换了别人。}\n# en: {~The caretaker finds you behind a tapestry and hands you a shovel without comment.|You get as far as the stairs, which choose that moment to move you straight back to the caretaker.}\nopt.right.say.2 [bc9719] = {~看门人在挂毯后面找到了你，一言不发地递来一把铲子。|你刚跑到楼梯口，楼梯就挑了这个时候，把你直接送回看门人跟前。}\n\n@event break_castle_flight_field\n# en: The Field to Yourself\ntitle [4a919a] = 独享飞行场\n# en: {With the flying lessons over for the break, the flight field is empty and nobody has locked the broom shed.|The broom shed is unlocked again. You suspect it's on purpose now.|The flight field in the break. You know which broom in the shed pulls left.} {season == \"winter\": The hoops are rimed with frost and the wind has teeth.|The air shimmers with heat, and the wind is lazy for once.} {~A crow is using the far hoop as a perch.|Somebody's lost scarf is snagged on the near hoop.|The grass is printed with a fox's tracks.}\ntext [653e69] = {假期飞行课停了，飞行场空无一人，也没人锁扫帚棚。|扫帚棚又没锁。你现在怀疑是故意的。|假期的飞行场。你知道棚里哪把扫帚往左偏。} {season == \"winter\": 圆环上结着白霜，风像长了牙。|空气热得发颤，风难得懒洋洋的。} {~一只乌鸦把远处的圆环当成了栖木。|近处的圆环上挂着不知谁丢的围巾。|草地上印着一串狐狸的脚印。}\n# en: An empty flight field in the holidays, hoops at either end, a single student on a broom high above the grass.\nscene [2e2a95] = 假期里空荡荡的飞行场，两端立着圆环，一个学生骑着扫帚高高掠过草地。\n# en: Fly the hoops, fast\nopt.left.label [91ec70] = 高速穿环\n# en: {~You thread every hoop and pull up so late that the grass brushes your boots. Nobody saw. That's almost a shame.|You lap the field until the hoops blur. Landing, you find you're grinning like a first-year.}\nopt.left.say.1 [f80422] = {~你穿过每一个圆环，拉起得那么晚，靴子都擦到了草尖。没人看见。这几乎有点可惜。|你绕场飞到圆环都模糊了。落地时，你发现自己笑得像个一年级生。}\n# en: {~The wind cheats, as it always does, and you meet the far hoop with your shoulder.|You land in the long grass, which has caught a great many students before you. It does not catch gently.}\nopt.left.say.2 [bc67f8] = {~风照例耍赖，你的肩膀撞上了远处的圆环。|你落进了长草丛，它接住过许多学生，但从不温柔。}\n# en: Practise slow, careful turns\nopt.right.label [5ede57] = 慢慢练转弯\n# en: {~Left, right, left, at walking pace, for an hour. It is deeply dull and, annoyingly, it works.|You practise the turn that always throws you until it doesn't. Your arms ache all evening.}\nopt.right.say.1 [524f9e] = {~左、右、左，步行的速度，整整一个钟头。无聊透顶，而且，可气的是，真有用。|你一遍遍练那个总把你甩下去的转弯，直到它不再甩你。整个傍晚胳膊都在酸。}\n\n@event break_castle_bored_stairs\n# en: The Bored Staircases\ntitle [1e7930] = 无聊的楼梯\n# en: {With nobody to carry up and down, the castle's staircases have grown restless.|The staircases are bored again, and they've noticed you.|The stairs know you're one of the few left. One swings over the moment you reach the landing.} {season == \"winter\": The stairwell is cold enough to see your breath.|Warm air drifts up the stairwell with the smell of cut grass.} {~A flight is drifting past with a lost shoe on its top step.|Two staircases are sulking against the wall.|One flight keeps nudging the landing, like a dog with a stick.}\ntext [fccdbc] = {没人可载上载下，城堡的楼梯开始坐不住了。|楼梯又无聊了，而且它们注意到了你。|楼梯知道你是少数留下的人之一。你一走到平台，就有一段荡了过来。} {season == \"winter\": 楼梯井冷得能看见自己的呼吸。|暖风带着刚割过的青草味飘上楼梯井。} {~一段楼梯飘过，最上面一级放着一只丢了的鞋。|两段楼梯靠着墙在赌气。|有一段不停地拱着平台，像叼着木棍的狗。}\n# en: A grand castle stairwell with several staircases swinging slowly through the air, a lone student waiting on a landing.\nscene [491257] = 宏伟的城堡楼梯井里，几段楼梯在半空中缓缓摇荡，一个学生独自在平台上等着。\n# en: Ride whichever one comes\nopt.left.label [b32bfb] = 来哪段坐哪段\n# en: {~It carries you to a corridor you've never seen, full of portraits who are thrilled to have a visitor.|It takes you the long way, through three towers, and sets you down exactly where you wanted to be.}\nopt.left.say.1 [04fae9] = {~它把你送到一条你从没见过的走廊，满墙的肖像见有人来，高兴坏了。|它绕了远路，穿过三座塔，最后恰好把你放在你想去的地方。}\n# en: {~It drops you on a landing in the east wing and refuses to come back. You walk for an hour.|It swings out over the stairwell and stops. You wait there, with nothing to read, until it gets bored again.}\nopt.left.say.2 [a9edca] = {~它把你丢在东翼的一个平台上，死活不肯回来。你走了一个钟头。|它荡到楼梯井半空就停住了。你在那儿干等，手边什么书也没有，直到它又觉得无聊。}\n# en: Take the long way, on foot\nopt.right.label [a78eb5] = 绕远路，走过去\n# en: {~You take the servants' stair, all two hundred steps of it. The staircases watch you go, disappointed.|You walk round by the bridges. It takes all morning, and your legs will remember it.}\nopt.right.say.1 [6c5c22] = {~你走仆人楼梯，整整两百级。楼梯们目送你离开，一脸失望。|你从廊桥绕过去，花了一整个上午，腿会记住这一趟的。}\n\n@event break_castle_night_rounds\n# en: The Lone Prefect\ntitle [5e697b] = 孤独的级长\n# en: {Only one prefect has stayed for the break, and seven towers are a lot to patrol.|The lone prefect's lantern again, bobbing across a far bridge.|You know the lone prefect's rounds by now, to the quarter-hour.} {season == \"winter\": The corridors are cold enough to frost the portraits.|The night is warm, and every window in the castle stands open.} {~A staircase shifts somewhere below with a long stone sigh.|An owl has got into the east wing.|Somewhere a door bangs in a draught.}\ntext [afcae5] = {假期只有一位级长留了下来，七座塔可不好巡。|又是那位级长的灯笼，在远处的廊桥上晃。|你现在对那位级长的巡夜路线了如指掌，精确到一刻钟。} {season == \"winter\": 走廊冷得连肖像都结了霜。|夜里很暖，城堡的每扇窗都开着。} {~楼下某处，一段楼梯长长地叹了口石头气，挪了位置。|一只猫头鹰钻进了东翼。|某处一扇门被穿堂风吹得砰砰响。}\n# en: A single lantern bobbing along a bridge between dark towers at night, a student watching it from a window.\nscene [1d3f26] = 夜里，一盏灯笼沿着两座黑塔之间的廊桥晃动，一个学生在窗边看着它。\n# en: Wander the towers after curfew\nopt.left.label [a4fb1b] = 宵禁后在塔里闲逛\n# en: {~You have the castle to yourself for an hour: moonlit bridges, sleeping portraits, the whole lake laid out below.|You slip past the lantern twice and watch the lake from a window nobody uses. It feels like owning the place.}\nopt.left.say.1 [bdbdbb] = {~整整一个钟头，城堡归你一个人：月光下的廊桥，熟睡的肖像，脚下铺开的整片湖。|你两次溜过灯笼，在一扇没人用的窗前看湖。感觉像这地方归你所有。}\n# en: {~The lantern swings round and finds you. The prefect writes your name in the demerit ledger with visible relief at having something to write.|You walk straight into the prefect on a dark landing. You both scream, and then you get a demerit.}\nopt.left.say.2 [dab387] = {~灯笼一转，照住了你。级长把你的名字记进记过簿，看得出总算有东西可记，松了口气。|你在漆黑的平台上一头撞上级长。你们俩都尖叫了，然后你被记了一过。}\n# en: Walk the rounds with the prefect\nopt.right.label [6867b8] = 陪级长巡夜\n# en: {~The prefect is glad of the company and tells you which portraits snore. You go to bed very late.|You carry the lantern while the prefect checks doors. Somewhere in the fourth tower you become friends, a little.}\nopt.right.say.1 [10568d] = {~级长很高兴有人作伴，告诉你哪些肖像会打呼。你很晚才睡。|级长查门，你提着灯笼。走到第四座塔的时候，你们成了朋友，一点点。}\n\n@event break_home_family_recipe\n# en: The Family Recipe\ntitle [3ea562] = 家传菜谱\n# en: {Someone at home has decided you're old enough for the family recipe, and ties an apron on you before breakfast.|The recipe lessons resume. You're trusted with the knife now, if not the salt.|You know the first half of the recipe by heart. The second half is still guarded like a treaty.} {season == \"winter\": It's the stew for the cold months, which takes all day.|It's the summer preserve, and every fruit in the house is involved.} {~Nobody measures anything.|A pinch turns out to mean a handful.|The pot is older than you are.}\ntext [494db6] = {家里有人认定你够大了，可以学家传菜谱了，早饭前就给你系上了围裙。|菜谱课继续。如今刀可以交给你了，盐还不行。|菜谱的前半你已经烂熟于心。后半还像条约一样严防死守。} {season == \"winter\": 是冬天的炖菜，要炖一整天。|是夏天的果酱，家里每一颗果子都得出力。} {~谁也不称分量。|“一小撮”原来是一大把。|这口锅比你年纪还大。}\n# en: A warm family kitchen, an older relative guiding a student's hands over a steaming pot, jars and bowls everywhere.\nscene [7fcbc3] = 温暖的家中厨房，一位长辈握着学生的手在冒热气的锅上方指点，四处摆满罐子和碗。\n# en: Write it all down\nopt.left.label [c13862] = 全都记下来\n# en: {~You take notes like it's a potions practical. You're corrected on every line, and then on the corrections.|Your notes are thorough, exact and, you're told, missing the point.}\nopt.left.say.1 [66920c] = {~你像上魔药实践课一样做笔记。每一行都被纠正，然后纠正又被纠正。|你的笔记详尽、精确，而且据说完全没抓住要点。}\n# en: Learn it by hand, the old way\nopt.right.label [990459] = 用手学，老办法\n# en: {~You burn a finger and get a nod. In this kitchen, that's a diploma.|You get it wrong in a way that turns out to be how your grandmother made it. Everyone goes quiet, and then they eat it all.}\nopt.right.say.1 [212327] = {~你烫了一根手指，换来一个点头。在这间厨房里，这就是毕业证书。|你做错了，错法恰好跟你祖母当年一样。大家沉默了一会儿，然后把它吃了个精光。}\n\n@event break_home_own_bed\n# en: Your Own Bed\ntitle [cf9821] = 自己的床\n# en: {Your own bed, after a term in the dormitory. You wake at the academy's hour anyway.|The house wakes at dawn. It always has; you'd forgotten.|You know which floorboard creaks on the way down, and that everyone else learned it first.} {season == \"winter\": There's frost on the window and the blankets weigh a ton.|Sunlight is coming through the curtains, and someone is already whistling in the yard.} Downstairs, {~the kettle is going.|someone is calling your name.|the door bangs for the third time.}\ntext [e0699e] = {在宿舍住了一学期，终于睡回自己的床。你还是在学院的钟点醒了。|家里天一亮就醒。一直如此，你只是忘了。|你知道下楼时哪块地板会响，也知道全家早就比你先知道。} {season == \"winter\": 窗上结着霜，被子重得像一吨。|阳光透过窗帘，院子里已经有人在吹口哨。} 楼下，{~水壶响了。|有人在喊你的名字。|门第三次砰地关上。}\n# en: A narrow bed under a cottage window in early light, a student buried in blankets while the house bustles below.\nscene [12a0dd] = 清晨微光中，小屋窗下一张窄床，一个学生埋在被子里，楼下一片忙碌。\n# en: Sleep until noon\nopt.left.label [7405ea] = 睡到中午\n# en: {~You sleep like a stone. At lunch you're called \"the scholar\" with a new and pointed emphasis.|You wake at noon, rested and ashamed. The whole house has done a morning's work, and mentions it.}\nopt.left.say.1 [653073] = {~你睡得像块石头。午饭时，大家喊你“学者”，带着一种新鲜而意味深长的重音。|你中午醒来，神清气爽又满心惭愧。全家已经干完一上午的活，还特意提了提。}\n# en: Get up with everyone\nopt.right.label [b1c969] = 跟大家一起起床\n# en: {season == \"winter\": {~You break the ice on the water butt and carry wood in before breakfast. Your hands sting all morning.|You're out in the dark with a lantern before the cockerel. The cockerel resents the competition.}|{~You're in the yard by sunrise, hauling and fetching. Breakfast, when it comes, is the best of the year.|You work the cool hours with everyone else and see why they swear by them.}}\nopt.right.say.1 [3d9b9e] = {season == \"winter\": {~早饭前你敲开水桶上的冰，把柴搬进屋。整个上午手都在刺痛。|公鸡还没叫，你就提着灯笼出门了。公鸡对这个竞争者很不满。}|{~日出时你已经在院子里搬这搬那。早饭端上来时，是一年里最香的一顿。|你跟大家一起趁凉干活，总算明白他们为什么对这几个钟头赞不绝口。}}\n\n@event break_home_mending_chair\n# en: The Mending Chair\ntitle [56e7dd] = 待修的椅子\n# en: {Word goes round the house that the scholar can fix things.|The things that need fixing have been waiting for you on a chair by the door.|The mending chair fills up between breaks, like a well.} {season == \"winter\": Today it's a stove door that won't stay shut.|Today it's a window that sticks in the heat.} {~Also a clock that strikes thirteen.|Also a doll with a missing eye.|Also a teapot nobody admits to breaking.}\ntext [64b233] = {家里传开了：学者会修东西。|要修的东西已经在门边的椅子上等着你了。|这把待修的椅子在两次假期之间就会堆满，像一口井。} {season == \"winter\": 今天是一扇关不严的炉门。|今天是一扇热天卡住的窗户。} {~还有一只会敲十三下的钟。|还有一个少了一只眼睛的布娃娃。|还有一把没人承认打碎的茶壶。}\n# en: A chair by a cottage door piled with broken household things, a student in academy robes eyeing them with a wand.\nscene [691b03] = 小屋门边一把椅子上堆满了坏掉的家什，一个穿学院长袍的学生拿着魔杖打量它们。\n# en: Mend it with a spell\nopt.left.label [16d363] = 用咒语修好\n# en: {~It mends with a click and a little puff of light. The family applauds, then brings out three more things.|You fix it so well that it works better than new. Now everyone wants their things to work better than new.}\nopt.left.say.1 [419187] = {~咔哒一声，冒出一小团光，修好了。全家鼓掌，然后又搬出三件来。|你修得比新的还好用。现在人人都想让自己的东西比新的还好用。}\n# en: {~It's mended, technically. It also hums now, and nobody can make it stop.|The spell fixes it and then, for good measure, everything else on the chair. Most of it did not need fixing that way.}\nopt.left.say.2 [f7485f] = {~严格来说，修好了。只是它现在会嗡嗡响，谁也停不下来。|咒语修好了它，顺带把椅子上别的东西也一并修了。大部分并不需要那样修。}\n# en: Mend it by hand\nopt.right.label [2c35c0] = 动手修\n# en: {~Glue, string, a borrowed hammer and the whole morning. It holds. You don't trust it, but it holds.|You fix it the way your family always has: slowly, with a lot of opinions from the doorway.}\nopt.right.say.1 [1a68c9] = {~胶水、绳子、借来的锤子，外加一整个上午。它撑住了。你不太信它，可它撑住了。|你照家里一贯的法子修：慢慢来，门口一堆人发表意见。}\n\n@event break_home_the_trunk\n# en: The Trunk in the Corner\ntitle [bc6453] = 角落里的箱子\n# en: {Your trunk stands open in the corner, and the family keeps putting things in it.|The trunk is open again, and it's filling up again.|The trunk is a family project now.} {season == \"winter\": Woollen socks, a jar of something pickled, a cake wrapped in three cloths.|Dried fruit, a straw hat nobody asked for, a cake wrapped in three cloths.} {final_year: Someone says \"for next term\", then stops.|{~The lid won't close.|Someone sits on the lid.|A note is tucked into a sock, not to be read till later.}}\ntext [ee0214] = {你的箱子敞在角落里，家里人不停地往里塞东西。|箱子又敞开了，又满起来了。|这只箱子如今是全家的工程。} {season == \"winter\": 羊毛袜、一罐不知腌了什么的东西、一块裹了三层布的蛋糕。|果干、一顶没人要的草帽、一块裹了三层布的蛋糕。} {final_year: 有人说“下学期带着”，然后停住了。|{~盖子合不上。|有人坐到了盖子上。|一只袜子里塞着一张纸条，说好以后再看。}}\n# en: An open travelling trunk in the corner of a small bedroom, overflowing with jars, socks and parcels in cloth.\nscene [cc7e9b] = 小卧室角落里一只敞开的旅行箱，罐子、袜子和布包的东西满得溢了出来。\n# en: Take everything\nopt.left.label [054bdb] = 全都带上\n# en: {~The bargeman charges you double for the weight, but you eat well for a month.|You haul it all back across the lake. Half the corridor smells of pickles until spring.}\nopt.left.say.1 [ed11ba] = {~箱子太沉，船夫收了你双倍船钱，可你一个月都吃得很好。|你把这些全拖过了湖。半条走廊一直到春天都是腌菜味。}\n# en: Sneak half of it back\nopt.right.label [4040fd] = 偷偷放回一半\n# en: {~You smuggle the jars back into the pantry at night. In the morning they're in the trunk again, wrapped tighter.|You leave the hat on a hook by the door. You feel worse about it than you expected.}\nopt.right.say.1 [353f90] = {~你夜里把罐子偷偷送回储藏室。第二天早上，它们又回到了箱子里，裹得更紧了。|你把草帽挂在门边的钩子上。你比自己预想的更过意不去。}\n\n@event break_work_stocktake\n# en: Stocktaking\ntitle [8bb9d1] = 盘点库存\n# en: {The quiet days of the break are, says the owner, the time to count everything.|Stocktaking again. You have opinions about how to count things now.|Stocktaking. You count faster than the owner, and slower than you'd like.} {member(tavern): At the Gilded Newt that means every tankard, keg and spoon.|{member(apothecary_clerk): At Pell & Daughters that means a thousand drawers.|{member(broom_courier): At the loft that means every parcel, pigeonhole and broom.|{member(library_assistant): In the library that means books that won't hold still to be counted.|That means everything, twice.}}}} The tally comes out one short.\ntext [e4cd36] = {老板说，假期的清闲日子正适合把东西全数一遍。|又盘点了。如今你对怎么数东西都有自己的见解了。|盘点。你数得比老板快，比你自己希望的慢。} {member(tavern): 在镀金蝾螈酒馆，这意味着每一只酒杯、每一桶酒、每一把勺子。|{member(apothecary_clerk): 在佩尔父女药铺，这意味着一千个抽屉。|{member(broom_courier): 在阁楼，这意味着每一个包裹、每一个信格、每一把扫帚。|{member(library_assistant): 在图书馆，这意味着那些不肯老老实实让人数的书。|这意味着所有东西，数两遍。}}}} 总数差了一个。\n# en: A student with a ledger and pencil counting rows of stock in a quiet workplace, the owner reading over their shoulder.\nscene [199b94] = 安静的工作场所里，一个学生拿着账本和铅笔清点一排排货物，老板从肩后看着。\n# en: Track down the missing one\nopt.left.label [855fc3] = 找出少的那一个\n# en: {~You find it wedged behind the counter, where it has been since the last stocktake. The owner tips you for your thoroughness.|You find the mistake in the ledger itself: someone counted a box as a crate. The owner pays you a little extra and says nothing else.}\nopt.left.say.1 [ecb532] = {~你在柜台后面找到了它，它从上次盘点起就卡在那儿了。老板为你的细心赏了点小钱。|你发现错在账本本身：有人把一箱记成了一桶。老板多付了你一点，别的什么也没说。}\n# en: {~You search until dusk, on your knees in the dust. It stays missing, and the owner looks at you as if you might have eaten it.|You count everything again and get a different number. Then a third.}\nopt.left.say.2 [8ebc14] = {~你跪在灰里找到天黑。它还是不见踪影，老板看你的眼神，好像是你把它吃了。|你又全数了一遍，得出另一个数。然后是第三个。}\n# en: Write it off and move on\nopt.right.label [71a437] = 记作损耗了事\n# en: {~\"Breakage,\" you write. The owner docks a coin from your pay, on principle.|You write it off, and the owner sighs and takes it from your wages.}\nopt.right.say.1 [5560d4] = {~你写下“损耗”。老板照规矩从你工钱里扣了一枚。|你把它记作损耗，老板叹口气，从你工钱里扣了。}\n\n@event break_work_complaint\n# en: The Complaint\ntitle [dd0cf5] = 投诉\n# en: {A customer has come back with a complaint and a voice built for it.|Another complaint, another raised voice. You've learned to breathe out first.|This customer has been saving the complaint all week. You can tell.} {member(tavern): The cider was too warm, or too cold, or too cider.|{member(apothecary_clerk): The tonic did nothing, which is worse, they say, than doing something.|{member(broom_courier): The parcel arrived wet, late and somehow smug.|{member(library_assistant): The book they want is out, and they want it in.|The complaint has several parts, all of them loud.}}}} The owner is out.\ntext [ec696e] = {一位客人带着投诉回来了，嗓门天生就是为投诉准备的。|又一桩投诉，又一个大嗓门。你已经学会先呼一口气。|这位客人攒了一整个星期的投诉。你看得出来。} {member(tavern): 苹果酒太热，或者太冷，或者太苹果酒了。|{member(apothecary_clerk): 补药毫无效果，他们说，这比有点效果还糟。|{member(broom_courier): 包裹送到时又湿又迟，还不知怎么透着一股得意。|{member(library_assistant): 他们要的书借出去了，他们要它现在就回来。|投诉分好几部分，每一部分都很响。}}}} 老板不在。\n# en: An irate customer leaning over a counter, finger raised, while a young worker stands very still behind it.\nscene [eec2a5] = 一位气冲冲的客人俯身在柜台上竖着手指，柜台后的年轻伙计一动不动。\n# en: Talk them down\nopt.left.label [dfea51] = 好言安抚\n# en: {~You agree with everything until there's nothing left to agree with. They leave calm, and they leave a tip.|You listen, nod and offer them a chair. By the end they're telling you about their sister.}\nopt.left.say.1 [46ab42] = {~你什么都附和，直到再没什么可附和。对方心平气和地走了，还留了小费。|你倾听、点头，请对方坐下。说到最后，对方在跟你讲他们的姐妹。}\n# en: {~It gets louder. You give them a coin of your own just to make it stop.|They demand the owner, then you, then the owner again, and you pay for the trouble out of your own pocket.}\nopt.left.say.2 [da9d83] = {~声音越来越大。你掏了自己的一枚钱，只求快点结束。|对方要找老板，然后找你，然后又找老板，最后这场麻烦由你自掏腰包。}\n# en: Give them what they want\nopt.right.label [876ac8] = 给他们想要的\n# en: {~You hand over a refund out of your wages. They leave. The quiet afterwards is worth a coin.|A free one, on you. They take it without thanks, and the next customer is lovely.}\nopt.right.say.1 [60d4c2] = {~你从工钱里退了款。对方走了。之后的清静值一枚钱。|这份算你请。对方连谢都没说就拿走了，下一位客人倒是可爱极了。}\n\n@event break_work_half_hour\n# en: Half an Hour Off\ntitle [647a16] = 半个钟头的休息\n# en: {Half an hour off in the middle of a long holiday shift.|Your half hour again. You know exactly how far it goes now.|Half an hour. You spend the first minute deciding.} {season == \"winter\": Outside, the snow is coming down sideways.|Outside, the sun is bright enough to hurt.} {~The back room has one chair and one draught.|A colleague has left half a pie, possibly for sharing.|A clock somewhere strikes the wrong hour.}\ntext [ef7aff] = {漫长的假期班次中间，有半个钟头休息。|又是你的半个钟头。如今你清楚它能走多远。|半个钟头。头一分钟用来做决定。} {season == \"winter\": 外面，雪横着飘。|外面，太阳亮得刺眼。} {~后屋有一把椅子和一股穿堂风。|同事留下半个馅饼，也许是给大家分的。|某处的钟敲错了钟点。}\n# en: A tired student in a work apron standing in a back doorway, looking out at the weather with a cup in hand.\nscene [defd5a] = 一个系着围裙的疲惫学生站在后门口，手里捧着杯子，望着外面的天气。\n# en: Walk out into the weather\nopt.left.label [e0bf5d] = 出门走走\n# en: {season == \"winter\": {~You buy roast chestnuts and eat them walking, snow in your collar. You come back soaked and much happier.|You walk to the lake's edge and back. Your nose will not forgive you.}|{~You buy a cold drink and sit in the sun until your neck burns. Worth it.|You walk to the water and stand there. Half an hour is longer outside.}}\nopt.left.say.1 [65f2ce] = {season == \"winter\": {~你买了烤栗子边走边吃，雪灌进领口。回来时湿透了，心情却好多了。|你走到湖边又走回来。你的鼻子不会原谅你。}|{~你买了杯冷饮，坐在太阳底下晒到脖子发红。值。|你走到水边站着。半个钟头在外面显得更长。}}\n# en: Nap in the back room\nopt.right.label [a2e9fa] = 在后屋打个盹\n# en: {~You sleep sitting up and wake with the shape of a crate on your cheek.|You doze off and dream of work, which seems unfair. The bell wakes you mid-shift in the dream.}\nopt.right.say.1 [65498b] = {~你坐着睡着了，醒来脸上印着木箱的形状。|你打了个盹，梦见在干活，这似乎不太公平。梦里干到一半，铃声把你叫醒了。}\n\n@event break_work_short_wages\n# en: A Coin Short\ntitle [996363] = 少了一枚\n# en: {Wages, counted out into your hand. You count them again.|Wage day. You count your coins at the counter, in front of everyone, on principle.|Wage day. The owner counts slowly, and so do you.} {~It's short by one.|It's short, and there's a button where a coin should be.|It's short by just enough to notice.} {member(tavern): The landlady remembers every tab to the penny, and has somehow forgotten this one.|{member(apothecary_clerk): Old Pell counted it himself, drop by drop.|{member(broom_courier): The dispatch ledger says otherwise, in smudged ink.|{member(library_assistant): The pay envelope says otherwise, in very small writing.|Someone's sums say otherwise.}}}}\ntext [8a7526] = {工钱数到你手里。你又数了一遍。|发工钱的日子。你现在当众在柜台上数钱，这是原则。|发工钱的日子。老板数得慢，你也数得慢。} {~少了一枚。|少了，本该是钱的地方放着一颗纽扣。|少的刚好够你察觉。} {member(tavern): 老板娘记得每一笔赊账，分毫不差，偏偏把这一笔忘了。|{member(apothecary_clerk): 是老佩尔亲手数的，一滴一滴地数。|{member(broom_courier): 派送账簿上可不是这么写的，墨迹还糊了。|{member(library_assistant): 工资袋上可不是这么写的，字小得很。|有人的算术可不是这么算的。}}}}\n# en: A student counting a small pile of coins on a counter for the second time, frowning, an employer pretending not to watch.\nscene [5dacbb] = 一个学生在柜台上第二次清点一小堆硬币，皱着眉，雇主假装没在看。\n# en: Point it out\nopt.left.label [d2676e] = 指出来\n# en: {~You show your sums. The owner checks them, coughs, and pays you the difference with a little extra for the embarrassment.|You point it out politely and firmly. The missing coin appears, with an apology and a pastry.}\nopt.left.say.1 [d3e741] = {~你把算式摆出来。老板核对一遍，咳了一声，补上差额，还为这份尴尬多给了一点。|你礼貌而坚定地指出来。少的那枚出现了，附带一句道歉和一块点心。}\n# en: {~The owner recounts, gets the same wrong answer, and looks hurt that you asked.|You're told it's for the breakage. You don't remember any breakage. Nobody else does either.}\nopt.left.say.2 [123ecc] = {~老板重数一遍，得出同样的错数，还一脸受伤，怪你多问。|你被告知这是赔损耗的。你不记得有什么损耗。别人也不记得。}\n# en: Let it go this once\nopt.right.label [b87d7e] = 这次就算了\n# en: {~You pocket it without a word. The owner sees you notice, and you're given the easy shift next week.|One coin. You decide you can afford to be the bigger person, just about.}\nopt.right.say.1 [9df634] = {~你一声不吭地收下了。老板看见你注意到了，下周给你排了轻松的班。|一枚而已。你决定自己大度得起，勉强吧。}\n\n@event refectory_cooks_taster\n# en: The Cook's Taster\ntitle [64d79d] = 厨娘的试吃员\n# en: The cook stops you before you reach the bench and holds out a spoon. \"You're in here more than the cats,\" she says. \"So you can taste the new pot first. Regulars do.\" Behind her a dozen steaming pots wait their turn, and the other students in the queue look on with naked envy.\ntext [6626d7] = 你还没走到长凳，厨娘就拦住你，递过来一把勺子。“你来得比猫还勤，”她说，“所以新的一锅你先尝。常客都这样。”她身后十几口锅冒着热气等着轮到，排队的学生们毫不掩饰地眼红。\n# en: A refectory cook holding out a small spoon over the counter to a student, a new pot steaming behind her.\nscene [2e4b9e] = 膳堂厨娘隔着柜台递给学生一把小勺，身后一口新锅冒着热气。\n# en: Give an honest verdict\nopt.left.label [5b70db] = 说实话\n# en: You say it wants salt and less of the grey thing. The cook frowns, tastes, and nods. From then on your bowl comes from the bottom of the pot, where the good bits are.\nopt.left.say.1 [e40fb3] = 你说得多放盐，少放那灰乎乎的东西。厨娘皱眉尝了尝，点了点头。从此你的那碗都从锅底盛，好料都在那儿。\n# en: Praise it to the rafters\nopt.right.label [7c4191] = 夸上天去\n# en: You declare it the best soup in the kingdom. The cook doesn't believe a word of it, and gives you a second spoonful anyway.\nopt.right.say.1 [c29b20] = 你宣布这是王国里最好喝的汤。厨娘一个字也不信，还是又给了你一勺。\n\n@event library_footnote_bag\n# en: Footnote's Approval\ntitle [c453a2] = 脚注的认可\n# en: You come back from the shelves to find Footnote asleep on your bag, curled tight, one paw over the strap. Footnote sleeps on the reference desk and nowhere else; everybody knows that. A passing librarian stops, looks from the cat to you, and nods, as if something official has happened.\ntext [ffc4bd] = 你从书架间回来，发现脚注睡在你的书包上，蜷得紧紧的，一只爪子搭着背带。人人都知道，脚注只睡参考台，别处一概不睡。一位路过的图书管理员停下来，看看猫又看看你，点了点头，仿佛刚发生了什么正式的事。\n# en: A huge old library cat curled asleep on a student's satchel under a reading desk, a student standing over it, uncertain.\nscene [515a63] = 阅览桌下，一只硕大的老图书馆猫蜷在学生的书包上睡觉，学生站在一旁不知所措。\n# en: Wait for him to wake\nopt.left.label [03f336] = 等他睡醒\n# en: You read standing up until the lamps are lit. Footnote wakes, stretches, and walks off without a glance. Your bag smells of warm cat for a week, and you find you don't mind.\nopt.left.say.1 [5ee19a] = 你站着看书，一直看到点灯。脚注醒了，伸个懒腰，看都不看你就走了。你的书包一个星期都带着暖烘烘的猫味，而你发现自己并不介意。\n# en: Ease the bag out, inch by inch\nopt.right.label [e23815] = 一寸寸抽出书包\n# en: You slide it free a fraction at a time. Footnote opens one eye, judges you, and allows it. The librarian says she's never seen him allow anything.\nopt.right.say.1 [2fedfc] = 你一点一点把书包抽出来。脚注睁开一只眼，审视了你一番，准了。图书管理员说她从没见他准过任何事。\n\n@event mabels_your_usual\n# en: Your Usual\ntitle [c3563c] = 老样子\n# en: You haven't even reached the counter. Mabel already has your cup out, the right tea steeping and the cake you always end up choosing on a plate. \"Your usual,\" she says, and it isn't a question. On the slate behind the till, among the regulars, there's a new line in chalk.\ntext [05c137] = 你还没走到柜台，梅布尔已经摆好了你的杯子，对的茶正在泡，盘子里放着你每次最后都会挑的那块蛋糕。“老样子，”她说，不是在问。收银台后的石板上，常客名单里多了一行新粉笔字。\n# en: A small tea shop counter with a cup already poured and a slice of cake on a plate, the shopkeeper smiling at a student in the doorway.\nscene [a81742] = 小茶馆的柜台上已经倒好一杯茶、放着一块蛋糕，店主朝门口的学生微笑。\n# en: Have the usual\nopt.left.label [de70ee] = 就要老样子\n# en: It's exactly right, as it always is. Mabel watches you take the first sip and turns back to the shelves, satisfied.\nopt.left.say.1 [ba0c49] = 恰到好处，一如既往。梅布尔看着你喝下第一口，心满意足地转回货架。\n# en: Ask for something new\nopt.right.label [35d8bb] = 来点新的\n# en: Mabel's eyebrows rise. Then she smiles like someone handed a puzzle and reaches for a tin from the very top shelf. It's odd and smoky, and you don't like it, and you're glad you asked.\nopt.right.say.1 [09aadb] = 梅布尔挑起眉毛。接着她笑得像拿到了一道谜题，伸手去够最顶层架子上的一个茶罐。那茶古怪又带烟味，你不喜欢，但你很高兴自己问了。\n\n@event bakery_bun_set_aside\n# en: The Bun Set Aside\ntitle [795ce5] = 留给你的面包\n# en: The baker's son reaches under the counter before you've said a word and brings out a bun wrapped in paper, with your initial on it in flour. \"Saved from the first batch,\" he says. \"Regulars get the corner ones.\" Behind him, by the ovens, the oldest baker nods as if she arranged it all herself.\ntext [09ad6a] = 你还没开口，面包师的儿子就从柜台下拿出一个纸包的面包，纸上用面粉写着你名字的首字母。“头一炉留的，”他说，“常客拿边角的。”他身后的炉边，最年长的面包师点点头，好像这一切都是她安排的。\n# en: A baker's son handing a paper-wrapped bun across a flour-dusted counter, a letter written on the paper in flour.\nscene [e72281] = 面包师的儿子隔着撒满面粉的柜台递过一个纸包面包，纸上用面粉写着一个字母。\n# en: Insist on paying for it\nopt.left.label [5b7a00] = 坚持付钱\n# en: He refuses twice and accepts the third time. The coin goes in a jar by the till marked, in the oldest baker's hand, \"for the post\".\nopt.left.say.1 [d333cd] = 他推辞了两次，第三次收下了。那枚钱进了收银台边的一个罐子，罐上是最年长的面包师的笔迹：“寄信用”。\n# en: Thank him and eat it warm\nopt.right.label [8942f0] = 道谢，趁热吃\n# en: It's still warm and far too big, and you eat it on the step in four bites and burn your mouth. You'd do it again tomorrow.\nopt.right.say.1 [aea84b] = 面包还热着，大得离谱，你坐在台阶上四口吃完，烫了嘴。明天你还会这么干。\n\n@event grimsbys_right_book\n# en: The Right Book, First Time\ntitle [38138c] = 一次就拿对的书\n# en: You step into Grimsby's and open your mouth, and Mr Grimsby is already holding out a book. It is exactly the one you came for. He looks as astonished as you are. \"Must be getting to know you,\" he says, and then, worried, \"Don't tell anyone.\"\ntext [c259e5] = 你走进格林斯比书店，刚张开嘴，格林斯比先生已经把一本书递了过来。正是你要找的那本。他和你一样惊讶。“大概是开始了解你了，”他说，又担心地补了一句，“别告诉别人。”\n# en: A cramped bookshop of leaning shelves, an elderly bookseller holding out a book to a student who has only just stepped inside.\nscene [83e433] = 书架东倒西歪的狭小书店里，一位年迈的书商把一本书递给刚进门的学生。\n# en: Buy it before it changes\nopt.left.label [8c5f21] = 趁它没变赶紧买\n# en: You pay at once, in case it turns into a book on beekeeping. It doesn't. You carry it all the way back to the castle, checking.\nopt.left.say.1 [e0afe4] = 你立刻付钱，以防它变成一本养蜂的书。它没变。你一路把它抱回城堡，边走边检查。\n# en: Ask what else he'd pick for me\nopt.right.label [4293db] = 问他还会给我挑啥\n# en: Restored to form, Mr Grimsby brings you a history of eels and a book of riddles. You buy the riddles. They're very good, and no help at all.\nopt.right.say.1 [8afbc7] = 格林斯比先生恢复了常态，给你拿来一本鳗鱼史和一本谜语书。你买了谜语书。谜语很好，一点用也没有。\n\n@event courtyard_in_the_know\n# en: In the Know\ntitle [143948] = 消息灵通\n# en: The knot of students by the fountain goes quiet as you pass, and then one of them waves you over. \"You're out here every day,\" says a second-year from Duskgate. \"So. What have you heard?\" It seems you've been counted among the courtyard's regulars, and regulars are expected to bring news.\ntext [f4ab24] = 你经过喷泉时，那一圈学生安静下来，接着其中一个朝你招手。“你天天在这儿，”一个暮门的二年级生说，“那么，你听到什么了？”看来你已经被算作庭院的常客了，而常客是要带消息来的。\n# en: A knot of students gathered on the rim of a courtyard fountain, leaning in, one waving a newcomer over.\nscene [ed9aa8] = 一群学生聚在庭院喷泉边沿，凑头说话，其中一人朝新来的人招手。\n# en: Share something harmless\nopt.left.label [743b67] = 说点无伤大雅的\n# en: You offer up a rumour about the refectory pudding. It's received with grave interest and is all over the castle by supper, much improved.\nopt.left.say.1 [04ca03] = 你贡献了一条关于膳堂布丁的传闻。大家郑重其事地听了，到晚饭时它已传遍城堡，还被添油加醋了不少。\n# en: Say I've heard nothing\nopt.right.label [4cfbdc] = 说我啥也没听说\n# en: They take your silence as proof that you know everything. By the end of the week, people are asking you for the news.\nopt.right.say.1 [60a72f] = 他们把你的沉默当成你什么都知道的证据。不到一个星期，就有人来找你打听消息了。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/pools/plans_thesis.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/pools/plans_thesis.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event break_castle_one_fire\n# en: The One Lit Fire\ntitle [c9e9bf] = 唯一一炉火\n# en: {With the houses empty, the stewards light one common room and let the rest go dark.|Someone has brought a kettle, and now there's a rota for it.|The break common room. You know everyone's blanket by now.} {season == \"winter\": The best armchair is right by the fire.|The best armchair is by the only breeze.} {~A first-year from Stillwater is asleep in it.|Footnote is in it. Footnote moves for no one.}\ntext [35aebe] = {宿舍楼都空了，管事们只点亮一间公共休息室，其余的任它暗着。|有人带来了水壶，现在还排了轮值表。|假期的公共休息室。现在每个人的毯子你都认得了。} {season == \"winter\": 最好的扶手椅就在炉火旁。|最好的扶手椅就在唯一有风的窗边。} {~一个静水院的一年级生在椅子上睡着了。|脚注占着那把椅子。脚注不给任何人让座。}\n# en: A cavernous common room in the holidays, a handful of students in blankets crowded around {season == \"winter\": one roaring hearth|one open window}.\nscene [24c42d] = 假期里一间空旷的公共休息室，几个裹着毯子的学生挤在{season == \"winter\": 一炉旺火|一扇敞开的窗}旁。\n# en: Talk my way into the armchair\nopt.left.label [483910] = 把扶手椅骗到手\n# en: {~You mention that the kitchens are giving out free toffee. The chair empties. You feel only slightly bad.|You announce, with great authority, that the armchair is due to be re-stuffed. It is, now, by you.}\nopt.left.say.1 [814810] = {~你随口提到厨房在免费发太妃糖。椅子空了。你只觉得有一点点愧疚。|你郑重宣布这把扶手椅要重新填絮了。现在它确实被填上了——用你。}\n# en: {~Nobody moves. Someone says, \"Nice try,\" without looking up from their book.|The whole room watches you fail to dislodge the armchair's occupant. It's the most entertainment anyone has had all break.}\nopt.left.say.2 [46c33f] = {~没人动。有人头也不抬地说：“想得美。”|全屋的人看着你没能把扶手椅上的那位挪开。这是整个假期里大家看过最好看的节目。}\n# en: Take the draughty corner\nopt.right.label [590fe4] = 去漏风的角落\n# en: {season == \"winter\": {~The corner is cold enough to see your breath, but you read three chapters.|You read in gloves. Turning pages becomes a skill.}|{~It smells of old ink and warm dust. You read three chapters.|A wasp shares your corner. You reach an understanding.}}\nopt.right.say.1 [f0f6f6] = {season == \"winter\": {~角落冷得能看见自己的呼气，但你读完了三章。|你戴着手套读书。翻页成了一门手艺。}|{~那里有旧墨水和晒暖的灰尘的气味。你读完了三章。|一只黄蜂和你共享角落。你们达成了谅解。}}\n\n@event break_castle_kitchen_rota\n# en: The Holiday Kitchen\ntitle [cef060] = 假期的厨房\n# en: {Half the kitchen staff have gone home for the break, and the cook has started drafting whoever wanders past.|The cook spots you in the corridor and holds up an apron.|The cook holds out an apron without a word. It has your name stitched on it now.} {season == \"winter\": There are puddings to steam for the few who stayed, and a goose that refuses to fit the oven.|The orchard has dropped everything at once, and every pan in the kitchen is full of jam.}\ntext [21605b] = {一半厨工回家过假期了，厨娘开始拉路过的人来帮忙。|厨娘在走廊里看见你，举起一条围裙。|厨娘一言不发地递过一条围裙。上面现在绣着你的名字。} {season == \"winter\": 要给留下的几个人蒸布丁，还有一只死活塞不进烤炉的鹅。|果园里的果子一下子全落了，厨房里每口锅都在熬果酱。}\n# en: A steaming castle kitchen with half its ovens cold, the cook handing aprons to two reluctant students.\nscene [d1a5e4] = 冒着热气的城堡厨房，一半烤炉是冷的，厨娘把围裙递给两个不情愿的学生。\n# en: Roll up your sleeves\nopt.left.label [52915f] = 挽起袖子\n# en: {season == \"winter\": {~You stir puddings till your arm aches. \"Just like potion work,\" says the cook. It isn't.|You baste the goose. The goose wins, narrowly.}|{~A morning of jam teaches you more about setting points than a term of alchemy.|You label forty jars. The cook re-spells \"gooseberry\" on all of them.}}\nopt.left.say.1 [8da2f1] = {season == \"winter\": {~你搅布丁搅到胳膊发酸。“跟熬魔药一样。”厨娘说。并不一样。|你给鹅刷油。鹅以微弱优势获胜。}|{~一上午的果酱让你对凝固点的了解超过一学期炼金课。|你给四十个罐子贴了标签。厨娘把每张上的“醋栗”都重新拼了一遍。}}\n# en: Discover urgent business elsewhere\nopt.right.label [14e943] = 想起别处有急事\n# en: {~You escape to the village and buy a pie you could have had for free. The cook sees you come back with the crumbs.|You hide in the library until lunch. Lunch, it turns out, is served by the cook, who gives you the smallest portion with a meaningful look.}\nopt.right.say.1 [7339e7] = {~你溜去山下小镇，买了一个本来可以白吃的馅饼。厨娘看见你带着饼渣回来。|你躲在图书馆直到午饭。结果午饭是厨娘盛的，她意味深长地给了你最小的一份。}\n\n@event break_castle_empty_library\n# en: The Library on Holiday\ntitle [4ce56c] = 放假的图书馆\n# en: {The librarians have gone home, and the library has been left to you and the cat.|Footnote has moved from the reference desk to your chair, and opens one eye.|Your break-morning library. One window seat is yours now.} {season == \"winter\": Frost has drawn ferns on the tall windows.|Dust turns slowly in the long summer light.} {~Every desk is free.|Someone has left half an essay on the theory of hiccups.|The reshelving cart is full, and nobody is coming for it.}\ntext [df5224] = {图书管理员们都回家了，图书馆留给了你和那只猫。|脚注从咨询台挪到了你的椅子上，睁开一只眼。|你的假期早晨图书馆。有一个靠窗的座位现在归你了。} {season == \"winter\": 霜在高窗上画满了蕨叶。|灰尘在长长的夏日光线里慢慢打转。} {~每张书桌都空着。|有人留下半篇论打嗝理论的论文。|还书车堆满了，没人会来推。}\n# en: A great library with no one at the desks, a cat asleep on the reference desk and a single student choosing a book.\nscene [e16f43] = 一座书桌空无一人的大图书馆，一只猫睡在咨询台上，一个学生在挑书。\n# en: Read whatever I like\nopt.left.label [0ceb4d] = 想读什么读什么\n# en: {~You read a book about the history of spoons and forget to eat lunch. It was a very good book about spoons.|You read until the light goes, then a little after. Your eyes will mention it tomorrow.}\nopt.left.say.1 [7d67df] = {~你读了一本讲勺子历史的书，忘了吃午饭。那是一本非常好的讲勺子的书。|你一直读到天光散尽，又多读了一会儿。明天你的眼睛会提起这件事。}\n# en: Get ahead on next term\nopt.right.label [d47dc6] = 提前预习下学期\n# en: {final_year: You make notes for a term you'll only half get to use. It still feels like the right thing to do.|{~You make a neat set of notes for next term. Future you will be grateful. Present you is bored stiff.|You read ahead three chapters and find the professor's favourite mistake waiting in the fourth.}}\nopt.right.say.1 [9f7a93] = {final_year: 你为一个只能用上一半的学期做笔记。可还是觉得该这么做。|{~你为下学期整理了一套整齐的笔记。将来的你会感激。现在的你闷得要命。|你往前读了三章，在第四章撞见了教授最爱犯的那个错误。}}\n\n@event break_castle_post_day\n# en: Post for the Stay-Behinds\ntitle [38476c] = 留守者的信件\n# en: {Post day. The porter lays the letters out on one table in the Great Hall.|The porter now sorts the stay-behinds' letters into a pile of their own.|The porter nods to you. He keeps your letters on top now.} {season == \"winter\": {~Most of it is Midwinter cards for people who went home.|One parcel is exactly the shape of a scarf.}|{~Most of it is postcards from people's travels.|A postcard says \"Wish you were here\", and nobody knows whose it is.}}\ntext [63e061] = {邮件日。门房把信摊在大礼堂的一张桌子上。|门房现在把留守者的信单独分成一小叠。|门房朝你点点头。他现在把你的信放在最上面。} {season == \"winter\": {~大多是寄给回家的人的仲冬贺卡。|有个包裹的形状和一条围巾一模一样。}|{~大多是旅行中的人寄来的明信片。|一张明信片上只写着“真希望你也在”，没人知道是给谁的。}}\n# en: A porter sorting a thin pile of letters on a long empty table, a few students waiting hopefully.\nscene [b2c195] = 门房在空荡的长桌上分拣一小叠信，几个学生满怀希望地等着。\n# en: Write a long letter home\nopt.left.label [b376a0] = 给家里写封长信\n# en: {final_year: You write that after this year you'll be home for good, and then look at the sentence for a long time.|{~You write four pages and pay for the heavy stamp. It's the closest thing to a conversation you've had all week.|You describe the empty castle so well that you feel lonelier writing it than living it.}}\nopt.left.say.1 [491cfa] = {final_year: 你写道过完这一年就会回家长住了，然后盯着这句话看了很久。|{~你写了四页，付了厚信的邮费。这是你这一周里最像聊天的一次。|你把空荡荡的城堡写得太好，写的时候比住在里面还寂寞。}}\n# en: Help the porter sort it all\nopt.right.label [329389] = 帮门房分拣\n# en: {~The porter tells you who writes to whom, in strict confidence. You know far too much about the second-years now.|You sort the post into houses. There's nothing for you this time. The porter gives you a biscuit, which is nearly as good.}\nopt.right.say.1 [93bca8] = {~门房严格保密地告诉你谁给谁写信。现在你对二年级生知道得太多了。|你把信按学院分好。这次没有你的。门房给了你一块饼干，差不多一样好。}\n\n@event break_home_kitchen_table\n# en: Homework at the Kitchen Table\ntitle [081adf] = 厨房桌上的功课\n# en: {You spread your books across the kitchen table, where you did your sums as a child.|Your books have been moved for the bread, and one is being used as a trivet.|The family works around your books now. Mostly.} {season == \"winter\": The stove is the only warm place, so everyone is in the kitchen.|The door is open, and everyone passing asks what the book is about.} {~A small cousin has drawn a dragon in your margin.|The kettle boils every quarter-hour, on principle.}\ntext [272d56] = {你把书摊在厨房桌上，小时候你就在这儿算算术。|你的书被挪开给面包腾地方，有一本被拿去垫了锅。|家里人现在会绕着你的书干活。大多数时候。} {season == \"winter\": 炉子是屋里唯一暖和的地方，所以大家都在厨房。|门朝院子开着，每个路过的人都要问这书讲什么。} {~一个小表亲在你的页边画了条龙。|水壶每一刻钟烧开一次，原则问题。}\n# en: A family kitchen in the morning, a student's books spread among the breakfast plates, someone reaching across them for the butter.\nscene [6a8ca6] = 早晨的家中厨房，学生的书摊在早餐盘子之间，有人越过书去够黄油。\n# en: Study through the noise\nopt.left.label [9218fa] = 顶着吵闹学习\n# en: {~You read the same paragraph nine times and learn it perfectly, along with the price of eggs.|You get through a chapter. Your notes now have a jam stain, a crumb and the shopping list on them.}\nopt.left.say.1 [a65fee] = {~同一段你读了九遍，背得滚瓜烂熟，连带记住了鸡蛋的价钱。|你读完了一章。你的笔记上现在有一块果酱渍、一粒面包屑和一张购物清单。}\n# en: Give up and help with lunch\nopt.right.label [91e189] = 放弃，帮忙做午饭\n# en: {~You're sent to the shop for the one thing everyone forgot, and you buy sweets with the change.|You peel, chop and get told about everyone in the village. Your book stays shut, and it's the best morning of the break.}\nopt.right.say.1 [7e9a03] = {~你被派去店里买大家都忘了的那样东西，用找零买了糖。|你削皮、切菜，听了村里每个人的近况。书一直没打开，这是假期里最好的一个上午。}\n\n@event break_home_the_box\n# en: The Box Under the Bed\ntitle [dfd52f] = 床底下的盒子\n# en: {Your old room is smaller than you remember. Under the bed is the box where you kept everything that mattered when you were nine.|Someone has dusted the box since last time and pretended not to look inside.|You've almost reached the bottom of the box.} {~This time it's a wand you made from a stick and a lot of string.|This time it's the letter you wrote to the academy at age ten, asking to be let in early.|This time it's a drawing of seven towers, each with a little flag.}\ntext [4a2c58] = {你的旧房间比记忆里小。床底下是你九岁时装一切重要东西的盒子。|上次之后有人掸过盒子上的灰，还假装没往里看。|你快翻到盒底了。} {~这次是一根你用树枝和一大堆绳子做的魔杖。|这次是你十岁时写给学院的信，请求提前入学。|这次是一幅七座塔的画，每座塔上都插着小旗。}\n# en: A narrow childhood bedroom with a bed far too short for its occupant, an old box of keepsakes pulled out onto the floor.\nscene [2fff89] = 一间窄小的儿时卧室，床对现在的主人来说短得多，一盒旧纪念品被拖到地板上。\n# en: Pack it in your trunk\nopt.left.label [60f445] = 装进行李箱\n# en: {~It comes back to Lumenhold with you. The trunk is heavier, and so, somehow, are you.|You pack it between your robes. Every time you open the trunk this term, it will be the first thing you see.}\nopt.left.say.1 [db216f] = {~它跟你一起回了流明堡。箱子更沉了，不知怎么，你也是。|你把它夹在袍子中间。这学期每次打开箱子，它都会是你第一眼看到的东西。}\n# en: Give it to the youngest cousin\nopt.right.label [5ecbd0] = 送给最小的表亲\n# en: {~The youngest cousin takes it with enormous seriousness and announces that they're going to be a wizard too. You feel very old.|The cousin promises to keep it safe. By supper it has been traded to a neighbour's child for a frog.}\nopt.right.say.1 [53894b] = {~最小的表亲无比郑重地接过去，宣布自己也要当巫师。你觉得自己老了。|表亲保证会好好收着。到晚饭时，它已经被换给邻居家的孩子，换来一只青蛙。}\n\n@event break_home_family_debate\n# en: The Family Debate\ntitle [673f25] = 家庭辩论\n# en: {At breakfast the family is arguing, and someone says, \"Ask the scholar.\"|The argument has started without you. Everyone turns as you come down the stairs.|\"The scholar is up,\" someone announces. They've saved today's argument for you.} {season == \"winter\": {~Is it colder than the year of the big snow?|Can you freeze a pond by thinking at it?}|{~Do swallows sleep at the bottom of rivers?|Will it rain on the fair, and can you stop it?}}\ntext [ae49b2] = {早饭时一家人在争论，有人说：“问问读书人。”|争论没等你就开始了。你下楼时，所有人都转过头来。|“读书人起来了。”有人宣布。他们把今天的争论留给了你。} {season == \"winter\": {~今年是不是比大雪那年还冷？|光靠想就能把池塘冻住吗？}|{~燕子是不是睡在河底？|集市那天会下雨吗，你能不能拦住？}}\n# en: A breakfast table mid-argument, several relatives pointing at a student as if they were the deciding vote.\nscene [0af97b] = 争论正酣的早餐桌，几个亲戚指着一个学生，好像那是决定性的一票。\n# en: Settle it with what I've learned\nopt.left.label [650629] = 用学到的东西解决\n# en: {~You explain it so clearly that the argument ends. There is a short, respectful silence, and then a new argument starts.|You settle it. An uncle demands to see it in writing. You write it on a napkin, and he keeps the napkin.}\nopt.left.say.1 [4a57e8] = {~你讲得清清楚楚，争论结束了。一阵短暂而尊敬的沉默，然后新的争论开始了。|你解决了。一位叔叔要你写下来。你写在餐巾上，他把餐巾收了起来。}\n# en: {~You explain it perfectly, and they all decide the academy has taught you nonsense.|Halfway through you realise you're wrong. So does everyone else.}\nopt.left.say.2 [52fa36] = {~你讲得完美无缺，结果他们一致认定学院教了你一堆胡话。|讲到一半你发现自己错了。其他人也发现了。}\n# en: Refuse to take sides\nopt.right.label [146465] = 拒绝站队\n# en: {~You butter your toast with great care and say, \"Hm.\" It does not stop anyone, but it does save you.|You change the subject to the neighbour's fence. It works, but now there are two arguments.}\nopt.right.say.1 [9a1a3b] = {~你非常仔细地给吐司抹黄油，说了声“嗯”。没能阻止任何人，但救了你自己。|你把话题转到邻居家的篱笆上。奏效了，但现在有两场争论了。}\n\n@event break_home_village_children\n# en: The Wizard Is Home\ntitle [167820] = 巫师回来了\n# en: {Word has spread that you're home from the wizard school. The village children are waiting at the gate.|The children are back at the gate, with a younger sibling to show you off to.|The gate committee is in session. They've drawn up a list.} {season == \"winter\": They want more snow.|They want it to stop being so hot.} {~The smallest has a jar, in case you make something worth keeping.|One asks if you've ever met a dragon.|The tallest has a stick that might be a wand.}\ntext [3088a4] = {消息传开了，说你从巫师学校回来了。村里的孩子们在门口等着。|孩子们又来到门口，还带了个弟弟妹妹来看你。|门口委员会又开会了。他们列了一张清单。} {season == \"winter\": 他们想要更多的雪。|他们想让天别这么热。} {~最小的那个拿着一个罐子，万一你变出什么值得留着的东西。|有个孩子问你见没见过龙。|最高的那个拿着一根棍子，说不定真是魔杖。}\n# en: A gaggle of village children at a cottage gate in the morning, staring hopefully at a student in academy robes.\nscene [53f9d2] = 早晨一群村里孩子聚在小屋门口，满怀期待地盯着一个穿学院长袍的学生。\n# en: Show them some sparks\nopt.left.label [b1cdc8] = 给他们放点火花\n# en: {~You make a little burst of light over the gate. The children scream with delight and demand it again, and again.|You make a spark dance across the gatepost. The smallest one tries to catch it in cupped hands, or thinks they do, which is the same thing.}\nopt.left.say.1 [a47bb8] = {~你在门上方变出一小团光。孩子们高兴得尖叫，要你再来，再来，再来。|你让一颗火花在门柱上跳舞。最小的那个想用手捧住它，或者以为捧住了，那也一样。}\n# en: Teach them something real\nopt.right.label [f0ec48] = 教他们点真东西\n# en: {~You explain how the light gets into a spell. They listen politely and then ask for sparks.|You show them how to sit still and listen to a candle. One of them really hears it. You can tell.}\nopt.right.say.1 [d2d889] = {~你讲光是怎么进到咒语里的。他们礼貌地听完，然后要火花。|你教他们静静坐着听一支蜡烛。其中一个真的听见了。你看得出来。}\n\n@event break_work_holiday_rush\n# en: The Holiday Rush\ntitle [c1bbea] = 假日高峰\n# en: {The holidays bring the crowds.|This time you've learned to eat breakfast before the rush, not during it.|You can feel the rush coming an hour before it arrives.} {member(tavern): The Gilded Newt is packed to the beams.|{member(apothecary_clerk): The queue at Pell & Daughters is out of the door.|{member(broom_courier): The loft's shelves are stacked to the rafters with parcels.|{member(library_assistant): The library is full of last-minute readers.|Everyone wants something, and now.}}}} {season == \"winter\": {~Everyone wants it before Midwinter.|Wet boots, short tempers.}|{~Everyone is sunburnt.|Travellers keep asking the way.}}\ntext [bf46c0] = {假期带来了人潮。|这次你学会了在高峰前吃早饭，而不是在高峰中。|高峰还有一个钟头才到，你已经感觉到了。} {member(tavern): 镀金蝾螈酒馆挤得水泄不通。|{member(apothecary_clerk): 佩尔父女药铺的队排到了门外。|{member(broom_courier): 阁楼的架子上包裹一直堆到房梁。|{member(library_assistant): 图书馆里全是临时抱佛脚的读者。|人人都想要点什么，而且现在就要。}}}} {season == \"winter\": {~人人都想在仲冬前拿到。|湿靴子，坏脾气。}|{~人人都晒得通红。|旅人不停地问路。}}\n# en: A holiday rush at a busy workplace, a student in the thick of it wearing the fixed smile of someone on their fourth hour of the same question.\nscene [0eb180] = 假期里忙碌的工作场所，一个学生身处人潮中央，挂着第四个钟头回答同一个问题的僵硬笑容。\n# en: Smile through it\nopt.left.label [35213b] = 笑着撑过去\n# en: {~You smile until your face aches. Somebody tips you a coin and a peppermint.|You smile through the fourth complaint about the weather, as if it were your fault. Somehow the tips are better than ever.}\nopt.left.say.1 [e14faf] = {~你笑到脸发酸。有人给了你一枚小费和一颗薄荷糖。|第四个人来抱怨天气，好像是你的错，你照样笑着。不知怎么，小费比哪次都多。}\n# en: Take the heavy jobs instead\nopt.right.label [7fdbc4] = 改干重活\n# en: {~You haul the heaviest loads all morning and talk to no one. Your arms complain, and nobody else does.|You take the dull, heavy work nobody wants. It's tiring and blissfully quiet.}\nopt.right.say.1 [ae49ee] = {~你搬了一上午最沉的东西，一句话没说。胳膊在抱怨，别的谁也没抱怨。|你揽下了没人想干的又闷又重的活。累，但安静得幸福。}\n\n@event break_work_opening_up\n# en: Opening Up\ntitle [307370] = 开门\n# en: {In the holidays you're trusted with the keys. You open up at dawn, alone.|You've learned to lift the door as you turn the key.|You could open up in your sleep, and once you nearly did.} {season == \"winter\": It's still dark, and the stove has gone out.|The sun is up and the street smells of warm stone.} {~There's a note under the door from someone who wanted to come in at midnight.|A cat waits on the step as if it has an appointment.|The place is yours for half an hour.}\ntext [b61388] = {假期里你被托付了钥匙。天一亮你独自开门。|你学会了转钥匙时顺手把门往上一抬。|你闭着眼都能开门，有一次差点真这么干了。} {season == \"winter\": 天还黑着，炉子夜里灭了。|太阳已经升起，街上有暖石头的气味。} {~门缝下塞着一张字条，来自半夜想进来的人。|一只猫在台阶上等着，好像有预约。|这地方有半个钟头归你一个人。}\n# en: A student unlocking a shop door in the half-light of early morning, breath steaming, the street empty behind them.\nscene [7b5489] = 清晨微光中一个学生在开店门，呼出白气，身后的街道空无一人。\n# en: Do it all by the book\nopt.left.label [433ff5] = 一切按规矩来\n# en: {~Swept, stocked and opened to the minute. The owner notices and slips you a little extra.|Everything is ready when the first customer arrives. Nobody notices but you. Still, the coin is good.}\nopt.left.say.1 [87aff6] = {~扫好、摆好、准点开门。老板注意到了，悄悄多塞给你一点。|第一位客人来时一切就绪。除了你没人注意。不过钱是实在的。}\n# en: Steal a quiet half hour\nopt.right.label [48ac16] = 偷半个钟头清闲\n# en: {~You drink tea by the window and watch the street wake up. It's the best part of the job.|You read a chapter with your feet up. Nobody ever knows.}\nopt.right.say.1 [78a2fb] = {~你在窗边喝茶，看街道醒来。这是这份工作最好的部分。|你把脚翘起来读了一章。没人知道。}\n# en: {~The owner arrives early and finds you with your feet up. The half hour comes out of your wages.|The first customer arrives early and finds the door locked. They leave a note. So does the owner.}\nopt.right.say.2 [f55bf7] = {~老板来早了，看见你翘着脚。那半个钟头从工钱里扣了。|第一位客人来早了，发现门锁着。他留了张字条。老板也留了一张。}\n\n@event break_work_regular\n# en: The Holiday Regular\ntitle [4ae6e4] = 假期常客\n# en: {Every holiday has its regular. This one comes every morning and wants to talk.|The regular is back. They wave to you as if you were old friends.|The regular has started calling you by your first name.} {~Today it's about their late husband's beard.|Today it's about a dragon they swear they saw as a girl.|Today it's about the year the lake froze, which may or may not have happened.} The queue behind them is getting longer.\ntext [4389fa] = {每个假期都有个常客。这一位每天早上都来，而且想聊天。|常客又来了。冲你挥手，像是老朋友。|常客开始直接叫你的名字了。} {~今天聊的是过世丈夫的胡子。|今天聊的是一条龙，她发誓小时候见过。|今天聊的是湖结冰的那一年，那年也许有，也许没有。} 后面的队越排越长。\n# en: An elderly customer settling in for a long chat at a counter, a student behind it glancing at the queue.\nscene [4825ff] = 一位年长的客人在柜台前坐定准备长谈，柜台后的学生瞥着排队的人。\n# en: Let them finish the story\nopt.left.label [884a81] = 让他们把故事讲完\n# en: {~You hear the whole story. The queue thins out to somewhere else. The regular leaves happy, and so, oddly, are you.|The story has an ending you didn't expect, and you'll think about it all day. The customers you lost won't.}\nopt.left.say.1 [fb7b77] = {~你听完了整个故事。排队的人散去别家了。常客高高兴兴地走了，奇怪的是，你也是。|故事的结局出乎意料，你会想一整天。流失的客人可不会。}\n# en: Hurry them gently along\nopt.right.label [7b4db3] = 温和地催一催\n# en: {~You serve the queue and the regular leaves, mid-sentence, with a little wave. It shouldn't bother you as much as it does.|The queue is grateful. The regular says, \"Another time, then,\" and you both know they mean it.}\nopt.right.say.1 [65f752] = {~你招呼了排队的人，常客话说到一半，挥挥手走了。这事不该让你这么在意。|排队的人很感激。常客说：“那下回吧。”你们都知道这话是真心的。}\n\n@event break_work_new_hire\n# en: The Holiday Hire\ntitle [ff5b6a] = 假期新人\n# en: {The owner has taken on a holiday hire and put them next to you.|Another holiday hire, another nervous face. You're the old hand now.|This break's new hire looks at you the way you once looked at everyone.} {~They've already dropped something.|They ask whether the job is always like this. You don't answer.|They're very keen and very fast, and very nearly right.}\ntext [d79564] = {老板招了个假期临时工，安排在你旁边。|又一个临时工，又一张紧张的脸。现在你是老手了。|这个假期的新人看你的眼神，就像你当初看所有人。} {~他们已经摔了个东西。|他们问这活儿是不是一直这样。你没回答。|他们很热心、很快，而且差一点就对了。}\n# en: A nervous new worker holding a tray at the wrong angle, a student leaning over to correct them.\nscene [c6ec03] = 一个紧张的新人用错误的角度端着托盘，一个学生探身过去纠正。\n# en: Show them the ropes\nopt.left.label [f00110] = 带带新人\n# en: {~By noon they know where everything is and that the owner is all bark. You've lost a morning and gained a friend.|You teach them the trick with the till. They teach you a song. You both get told off for the song.}\nopt.left.say.1 [b40c0d] = {~到中午他们知道了每样东西在哪，也知道老板只是嘴凶。你搭进一个上午，多了个朋友。|你教他们收银台的窍门。他们教你一首歌。你们俩因为那首歌一起挨了骂。}\n# en: Let them learn the hard way\nopt.right.label [c3bb81] = 让他们吃点苦头\n# en: {~They learn. Unfortunately, they learn by breaking a jar that the owner puts on your account.|You let them flounder. It takes them all day to find the stockroom, and you end up covering their shift anyway.}\nopt.right.say.1 [e7b404] = {~他们学会了。可惜是靠打碎一个罐子学会的，老板记在了你账上。|你让他们自己摸索。他们找了一整天库房，最后还是你替他们顶了班。}\n\n@event break_internship_lodgings\n# en: Breakfast at the Lodgings\ntitle [ae293d] = 寄宿处的早餐\n# en: {{member(intern_expedition): Your lodging for the placement is a tent that flaps all night.|{member(intern_field_station): Your lodging is a bunk in the station cabin, under a shelf of jars.|Your lodging is one small room and a breakfast you pay for.}}|By now you can make any lodging home in an evening, a book on the sill and your boots by the door.|You've slept in enough borrowed beds to know breakfast matters more than the mattress.} {season == \"winter\": {~Your breath steams.|The water jug has frozen.}|{~It's warm before sunrise.|A bird is shouting outside.}} {~Breakfast is porridge.|Breakfast is porridge with opinions.|Breakfast is unnamed.}\ntext [f2d575] = {{member(intern_expedition): 这次实习的住处是一顶整夜扑扑作响的帐篷。|{member(intern_field_station): 你的住处是野外站小木屋里的一张铺位，头顶一排罐子。|你的住处是一个小房间，外加一顿要付钱的早餐。}}|如今不管什么住处，你一个晚上就能住成家：窗台上一本书，门边一双靴子。|你睡过的借来的床够多了，知道早餐比床垫要紧。} {season == \"winter\": {~呼出的气冒着白烟。|水壶冻住了。}|{~太阳没出来就暖了。|外面有只鸟在大喊。}} {~早餐是粥。|早餐是有主见的粥。|早餐是一样没人叫得出名的东西。}\n# en: A borrowed bed at dawn, boots by the door, a bowl of breakfast waiting and a notebook open on the blanket.\nscene [7aadaf] = 黎明时一张借来的床，靴子放在门边，一碗早餐等着，毯子上摊着一本笔记。\n# en: Eat a proper breakfast\nopt.left.label [f0ec45] = 好好吃顿早饭\n# en: {~You eat slowly and listen to everyone's gossip. You arrive late enough to be noticed.|Fed, warm and five minutes late. Your supervisor looks at the clock and says nothing at all.}\nopt.left.say.1 [125952] = {~你慢慢吃，听着各种闲话。到得晚了，晚到会被注意。|吃饱了，暖和了，晚了五分钟。导师看了看钟，什么也没说。}\n# en: Skip it and go in early\nopt.right.label [cfe210] = 不吃了，早点去\n# en: {~You're the first one in. You get through a pile of work before anyone else arrives, and your stomach growls through every meeting after.|You read the day's notes on the way. By lunchtime you're faint and well informed.}\nopt.right.say.1 [67b2c7] = {~你第一个到。别人来之前你已经处理掉一堆活，之后的每场会上你的肚子都在叫。|你在路上读当天的笔记。到午饭时你头晕眼花，消息灵通。}\n\n@event break_internship_briefing\n# en: The Morning Briefing\ntitle [7aa6d4] = 晨间布置\n# en: {Your supervisor reads the day's tasks aloud, once, very fast.|You bring two pens now, and a spare for the intern beside you.|You've learned to read the briefing from the way your supervisor holds the list.} {~One task makes no sense at all.|One task seems to mean the opposite of what it says.|The last task is mumbled into a cup of tea.} {final_year: It's your last placement, and you want it to count.|The others all write as if they understood.}\ntext [fa84ba] = {导师把当天的任务大声念一遍，只念一次，念得飞快。|你现在带两支笔，还给旁边的实习生备一支。|你已经能从导师拿清单的姿势读出任务了。} {~有一项任务完全说不通。|有一项任务似乎和字面意思正相反。|最后一项任务是对着茶杯嘟囔出来的。} {final_year: 这是最后一次实习，你想让它有分量。|其他人都写得好像全听懂了。}\n# en: A supervisor reading a list of tasks aloud to a row of interns, one of whom has stopped writing.\nscene [ba7fb6] = 导师对着一排实习生大声念任务清单，其中一个停下了笔。\n# en: Ask what it means\nopt.left.label [5dfe5f] = 问清楚是什么意思\n# en: {~Your supervisor explains it again, slowly, while the other interns smirk. Two of them come to you at lunch to ask what it meant.|\"Good question,\" says your supervisor, in a tone that suggests it wasn't. You get the task right, anyway.}\nopt.left.say.1 [792617] = {~导师又慢慢解释了一遍，其他实习生偷笑。午饭时有两个人跑来问你那是什么意思。|“好问题。”导师说，语气表明并不是。不管怎样，你把任务做对了。}\n# en: Guess and get on with it\nopt.right.label [f42afd] = 猜一猜，先干起来\n# en: {~You guess right. Your supervisor nods as if they never doubted you.|It turns out the odd task was \"take the rest of the morning off\". Lucky.}\nopt.right.say.1 [403deb] = {~你猜对了。导师点点头，好像从没怀疑过你。|原来那项古怪的任务是“上午剩下的时间休息”。运气不错。}\n# en: {~You guess wrong and spend the morning carefully doing the opposite of what was wanted.|You did the right task to the wrong thing. The wrong thing will never be the same again.}\nopt.right.say.2 [c7aa1c] = {~你猜错了，一上午都在一丝不苟地做和要求相反的事。|你对错的东西做了对的任务。那样东西再也回不去了。}\n\n@event break_internship_other_interns\n# en: The Other Interns\ntitle [ebc4c3] = 其他实习生\n# en: {There are other interns here, and they've already formed a lunch table.|You've learned to find the lunch table on the first day, and which seat isn't anyone's.|By now you can tell within a morning which intern is the one to sit beside.} {~One has a cousin on the Conclave and mentions it hourly.|One is better than all of you, and very nice about it.|One has been here before and knows where the biscuits are.} {season == \"winter\": Lunch is eaten in coats.|Lunch is eaten in the sun.}\ntext [5f46bc] = {这里还有别的实习生，他们已经凑成了一张午饭桌。|你学会了第一天就找到午饭桌，还有哪个座位不属于任何人。|如今一上午你就能看出该坐在哪个实习生旁边。} {~有一个的表亲在七塔议会，每小时提一次。|有一个比你们都强，而且人很好。|有一个以前来过，知道好饼干藏在哪。} {season == \"winter\": 午饭是穿着外套吃的。|午饭是晒着太阳吃的。}\n# en: A small group of interns eating lunch together, one sitting slightly apart with a notebook.\nscene [72d29c] = 几个实习生一起吃午饭，一个拿着笔记本的人坐得稍远。\n# en: Join them for lunch\nopt.left.label [24ea1a] = 和他们一起吃午饭\n# en: {~You chip in for the shared food and find out which of the supervisors are kind. Useful, and fun.|You talk shop until your lunch is cold. It turns out everyone else is as lost as you are, which helps.}\nopt.left.say.1 [489e67] = {~你凑钱买了大家分的吃食，打听到哪些导师心肠好。有用，也开心。|你们聊工作聊到饭都凉了。原来大家和你一样摸不着头脑，这让人宽心。}\n# en: Eat alone with your notes\nopt.right.label [5262d8] = 独自看笔记吃饭\n# en: {~You finish the reading for the afternoon. You hear laughter from the table, and you don't look up.|You get ahead of everyone. Nobody saves you a biscuit.}\nopt.right.say.1 [eb6606] = {~你读完了下午的材料。桌子那边传来笑声，你没抬头。|你领先了所有人。没人给你留饼干。}\n\n@event break_internship_stipend\n# en: Stipend Day\ntitle [75f50a] = 发津贴的日子\n# en: {Stipend day. A small envelope, your name spelled almost right.|You've learned to budget the envelope before you open it.|Stipend day. You know what it's for before it arrives, and it still feels like a gift.} {member(intern_expedition) or member(intern_field_station): {~The supply cart has come, with a pedlar riding on top.|A pedlar has walked a very long way to sell you socks.}|{season == \"winter\": {~There's a warm scarf in a shop window you pass.|A bookshop nearby has a sale on almanacs.}|{~A cart is selling lemon ices on the corner.|A bookshop nearby has put its sale out in the sun.}}}\ntext [db989b] = {发津贴了。一个小信封，你的名字几乎拼对了。|你学会了在拆信封之前就把钱安排好。|发津贴的日子。钱还没到你就知道它的用处，可它仍然像一份礼物。} {member(intern_expedition) or member(intern_field_station): {~补给车来了，车顶上坐着一个货郎。|一个货郎走了很远的路来卖袜子给你。}|{season == \"winter\": {~你路过的橱窗里有一条暖和的围巾。|附近的书店在打折卖年历。}|{~街角有辆小车在卖柠檬冰。|附近的书店把打折书摆到了太阳底下。}}}\n# en: A small envelope of coins on a desk, a student looking at it with the expression of someone doing sums.\nscene [8956ab] = 书桌上一个装着硬币的小信封，一个学生看着它，一脸在算账的表情。\n# en: Treat yourself\nopt.left.label [ebab6c] = 犒劳一下自己\n# en: {member(intern_expedition) or member(intern_field_station): {~You buy the pedlar's thickest socks. Your feet have never been so grateful.|You buy a jar of honey from the pedlar and eat it with a spoon, in triumph.}|{season == \"winter\": {~You buy it. It's quite unnecessary, and the warmest you've felt all week.|You spend the lot before lunch and regret none of it.}|{~You spend it before the corner and don't regret a crown.|You take your purchase to a bench in the sun and stay until your nose burns.}}}\nopt.left.say.1 [4d9a99] = {member(intern_expedition) or member(intern_field_station): {~你买了货郎最厚的袜子。你的脚从没这么感激过。|你从货郎那儿买了一罐蜂蜜，用勺子吃，吃得扬眉吐气。}|{season == \"winter\": {~你买下了它。完全没必要，却是你这一周最暖和的时刻。|午饭前你就花光了，一点也不后悔。}|{~没走到街角你就花完了，一个克朗都不后悔。|你带着买来的东西坐到阳光下的长椅上，一直坐到鼻子晒伤。}}}\n# en: Buy the book your supervisor cited\nopt.right.label [d968e7] = 买导师引用的那本书\n# en: {~It's the dullest book you've ever paid for, and the most useful. You underline half of it the first night.|You send for it and read it, and the next time your supervisor mentions it you nod in the right places.}\nopt.right.say.1 [3bed7f] = {~这是你花钱买过最枯燥的书，也最有用。第一晚你就划了一半。|你托人捎来读了，下次导师提到它时，你在该点头的地方点了头。}\n\n@event thesis_margin_notes\n# en: Notes in the Margin\ntitle [6ecc14] = 页边批注\n# en: {Professor Thorne returns your chapter. In the margin of page one he has written \"No.\"|Another chapter back. The margins say \"Why?\", \"Source?\" and, once, \"Hm.\"|Thorne's notes are getting shorter. You can't decide whether that's good.} {~Page six has a single word underlined three times.|One note just says \"See me.\"|There's a tick on page nine. You stare at it for a while.} {progress(thesis) >= 60: The argument is nearly there, and he knows it.|There's a long way to go.}\ntext [4c2d2f] = {索恩教授把你的章节退了回来。第一页的页边上他写着：“不。”|又一章退回来了。页边写着“为什么？”“出处？”，还有一处：“嗯。”|索恩的批注越来越短。你拿不准这是不是好事。} {~第六页有一个词被画了三道线。|有一条批注只写着“来见我”。|第九页有一个勾。你盯着它看了好一会儿。} {progress(thesis) >= 60: 论证就快成了，他也知道。|还有很长的路要走。}\n# en: A thesis draft returned with a professor's spiky handwriting crawling up every margin.\nscene [981dda] = 一份退回的论文草稿，教授尖利的字迹爬满了每一处页边。\n# en: Rewrite to his notes\nopt.left.label [cbc3e9] = 照他的批注改\n# en: {~You rewrite every page he marked. The chapter is shorter and better, and you hate that he was right.|You take every note. When he reads it again he crosses out only one thing, and you could cry.}\nopt.left.say.1 [ed9ffe] = {~他标过的每一页你都重写了。这一章更短、更好，你恨他是对的。|你采纳了每一条批注。他再读时只划掉了一处，你差点哭出来。}\n# en: Defend my version\nopt.right.label [6497f5] = 为我的版本辩护\n# en: {~You make your case at his office door. He listens, says \"Hm,\" and crosses out his own note.|You argue for your paragraph and he lets you keep it. \"Then make it say so,\" he says, which is almost praise.}\nopt.right.say.1 [d4a78e] = {~你在他办公室门口陈述理由。他听完，说了声“嗯”，划掉了自己的批注。|你为自己那一段据理力争，他让你留下了。“那就让它把话说清楚。”他说，这几乎算是夸奖。}\n# en: {~You argue. Thorne waits until you've finished, then points at the sentence that proves him right.|He hears you out, then writes a new note in the margin. It says \"No.\" again, in smaller letters.}\nopt.right.say.2 [b7b6bf] = {~你争了。索恩等你说完，然后指着证明他是对的那一句。|他听你说完，在页边写了一条新批注。还是“不。”，字小了一号。}\n\n@event thesis_carrel_neighbour\n# en: The Next Carrel\ntitle [1fe6fe] = 隔壁隔间\n# en: {Your carrel neighbour, a final-year from Rootward, reads aloud under their breath.|Your carrel neighbour now reads your chapter titles aloud too.|You and your carrel neighbour now speak a language of sighs.} {~They're stuck on chapter three.|They've found a source that disagrees with you both.|They pass you half a sandwich over the partition.} {season == \"winter\": The thesis lab is cold enough for gloves.|Someone's notes are escaping through the window.}\ntext [97a4d0] = {你的隔间邻居是一位根庐的毕业年级生，总在小声念书。|你的隔间邻居现在连你的章节标题也念出声来。|你和隔间邻居现在用叹气交流。} {~他们卡在第三章了。|他们找到一份和你俩都唱反调的资料。|他们从隔板上方递给你半个三明治。} {season == \"winter\": 论文实验室冷得要戴手套。|有人的笔记正从窗口往外逃。}\n# en: Two thesis carrels side by side, the partition between them covered in pinned notes and a shared candle.\nscene [e1d83d] = 两个并排的论文隔间，中间的隔板上钉满了笔记，还有一支共用的蜡烛。\n# en: Swap drafts for an hour\nopt.left.label [d31846] = 交换草稿看一小时\n# en: {~They find the hole in your argument in ten minutes. You find three in theirs. Everyone feels better and worse.|They read your chapter and say, \"Oh, I see.\" You're not sure you did, until now.}\nopt.left.say.1 [1201cc] = {~他们十分钟就找到了你论证里的漏洞。你在他们的里面找到三个。大家都觉得好受了些，也难受了些。|他们读完你的章节，说：“哦，我明白了。”在这之前你都不确定自己明白。}\n# en: {~They love your chapter, which helps nobody. You spend the hour fixing their commas.|They ask so many questions that you start doubting your title.}\nopt.left.say.2 [cd87ca] = {~他们很喜欢你的章节，这对谁都没帮助。你花了一小时改他们的逗号。|他们问了太多问题，你开始怀疑起自己的标题。}\n# en: Hunt a source in the stacks\nopt.right.label [714cad] = 去书库找资料\n# en: {~You find it on the bottom shelf, under a dictionary and a sleeping cat. It says exactly what you hoped, in a footnote.|Three hours of climbing ladders for one paragraph. It's a very good paragraph.}\nopt.right.say.1 [5c9181] = {~你在最底层书架上找到了它，压在一本词典和一只睡着的猫下面。它说的正是你希望的，在一条脚注里。|爬了三个钟头梯子换来一段话。是非常好的一段话。}\n\n@event thesis_is_it_good\n# en: Is It Any Good?\ntitle [cfa126] = 写得好吗？\n# en: {Halfway through rereading your thesis, you can't tell if it's brilliant or obvious.|The doubt has learned your timetable. It comes the morning after good work.|You know this doubt now. It reaches your carrel first.} {progress(thesis) >= 60: Most of it is written, which almost makes it worse.|So much of it is still unwritten.} {final_year and season == \"spring\": After this term there are no more terms.|{~First-years laugh in the courtyard below.|Last night's candle is a puddle.}}\ntext [6118b4] = {重读论文到一半，你分不清它是精彩还是平庸。|怀疑摸清了你的作息。它总在干得不错的一夜之后的早晨出现。|你认得这种怀疑了。它比你先到隔间。} {progress(thesis) >= 60: 大部分已经写完，这几乎让事情更糟。|还有太多没写。} {final_year and season == \"spring\": 这学期过后，就再也没有学期了。|{~楼下庭院里一年级生在笑。|昨晚的蜡烛化成了一摊。}}\n# en: A student at a carrel in early light, holding a thick draft at arm's length as if it might bite.\nscene [43c23c] = 清晨的隔间里，一个学生把厚厚的草稿举得远远的，好像它会咬人。\n# en: Push on regardless\nopt.left.label [b83a2c] = 不管了，继续写\n# en: {~You write a page you don't believe in. By lunch you believe in about half of it, which is a start.|You keep going. The doubt keeps you company, and gets bored by evening.}\nopt.left.say.1 [029adc] = {~你写了一页自己都不信的东西。到午饭时你信了大约一半，算是个开始。|你接着写。怀疑一直陪着你，到傍晚它自己也腻了。}\n# en: Walk by the lake instead\nopt.right.label [ff9e77] = 改去湖边散步\n# en: {~You walk around the lake and come back with no new pages and one good idea. You'll find out tomorrow if it's good.|The lake is mirror-still and has no opinion on your thesis. It's a relief.}\nopt.right.say.1 [11fa26] = {~你绕湖走了一圈，回来时没有新页，只有一个好主意。明天就知道它好不好了。|湖面如镜，对你的论文毫无意见。真是解脱。}\n\n@event thesis_lab_spare_carrel\n# en: The Spare Carrel\ntitle [59d7e8] = 空着的隔间\n# en: {There's an empty carrel at the end of the row, with a candle stub and nobody's name on it.|The spare carrel is still unclaimed. You've started thinking of its candle stub as yours.|You know the carrels by their scratches now. The spare one has a new scratch, and you suspect it's yours.} {season == \"winter\": Everyone writes in fingerless gloves.|The windows are open, and a footnote blows off someone's desk.} {~Somewhere a quill scratches without stopping.|Someone is whispering \"however\" over and over.|A candle gutters out three carrels down, and someone groans.}\ntext [2415ea] = {这排尽头有个空隔间，只有一截蜡烛头，没写任何人的名字。|那个空隔间还是没人认领。你开始把那截蜡烛头当成自己的了。|你现在能凭划痕认出每个隔间。空着的那个多了一道新划痕，你怀疑是你划的。} {season == \"winter\": 人人都戴着露指手套写字。|窗户开着，一条脚注从别人桌上飞走了。} {~某处一支羽毛笔沙沙写个不停。|有人在一遍遍低声念着“然而”。|隔三个隔间的蜡烛灭了，有人哀叹了一声。}\n# en: A long room of wooden carrels, one at the end empty but for a candle stub and a stray page of footnotes.\nscene [9557dc] = 一长排木制研读隔间，尽头那个空着，只有一截蜡烛头和一页散落的脚注。\n# en: Work until the candle dies\nopt.left.label [6a43fb] = 写到蜡烛燃尽\n# en: {~You write until the stub drowns in its own wax. Your notes are good, and your neck will remember them.|You read one book and half of another. The room's silence does half the work for you.}\nopt.left.say.1 [b50a9a] = {~你一直写到烛头淹没在自己的蜡油里。笔记很好，脖子会记住它们。|你读完一本书，又读了半本。屋里的安静替你干了一半的活。}\n# en: Just sit in the quiet\nopt.right.label [fbb3b9] = 只是在安静里坐坐\n# en: {~You sit with your hands in your lap and listen to everyone else think. It's oddly restful.|You doze off with your cheek on someone's old footnotes. Nobody minds.}\nopt.right.say.1 [2d5943] = {~你双手放在膝上，听着别人思考。出奇地让人放松。|你脸贴着别人的旧脚注睡着了。没人介意。}\n# en: {~An advisor looks into the carrel and asks what you're working on. \"Thinking,\" you say. He writes that down.|A final-year asks if you'd mind the corner. Their own candle has given out.}\nopt.right.say.2 [ae00fe] = {~一位导师探头问你在研究什么。“思考。”你说。他把这记了下来。|一位毕业年级生问你介不介意让出角落。他们自己的蜡烛燃尽了。}\n\n@event thesis_lab_footnote_war\n# en: The Footnote War\ntitle [e57e26] = 脚注之战\n# en: {Two advisors are arguing about a footnote across three carrels, and every final-year in the room has stopped pretending to work.|The footnote war has broken out again. Someone has drawn up sides on a scrap of paper.|By now the footnote war has rules, a referee and a running score chalked on the door.} {~Today it's about whether an old founders' letter counts as a source.|Today it's about a comma.|Today it's about a footnote to a footnote.}\ntext [c4915c] = {两位导师隔着三个隔间为一条脚注争吵，屋里每个毕业年级生都不再假装干活了。|脚注之战又打响了。有人在纸片上分好了阵营。|如今脚注之战有了规则、裁判，门上还用粉笔记着比分。} {~今天争的是创始者的一封旧信算不算出处。|今天争的是一个逗号。|今天争的是一条脚注的脚注。}\n# en: Two advisors arguing across a row of carrels, final-year students peering over the partitions to watch.\nscene [0da858] = 两位导师隔着一排隔间争吵，毕业年级生们从隔板上方探头观战。\n# en: Weigh in with a source\nopt.left.label [52a73e] = 拿出处加入战局\n# en: {~You produce the source, open at the right page. Both advisors fall silent, and one of them asks for your name.|Your citation settles it. The loser shakes your hand and then rereads the page for an hour.}\nopt.left.say.1 [ebd6e8] = {~你拿出那份资料，正翻在对的那一页。两位导师都安静了，其中一位问了你的名字。|你的引文终结了争论。输的一方和你握了手，然后把那一页重读了一个钟头。}\n# en: {~Your source turns out to be the one they were both arguing against. They unite, briefly, to explain why.|You cite the wrong edition. The room winces as one.}\nopt.left.say.2 [bb8861] = {~你的资料恰好是他们俩都在反对的那份。他们短暂地联手，向你解释为什么。|你引错了版本。全屋人齐齐一缩。}\n# en: Keep my head down and listen\nopt.right.label [a9d2c8] = 低头听着\n# en: {~You learn more about sources from ten minutes of the war than from a term of lectures, and you never have to say a word.|You take notes on both sides. Neither of them would like how fair your notes are.}\nopt.right.say.1 [cfc044] = {~十分钟的战事教你的出处知识比一学期的课还多，而你一句话都不用说。|你把双方的论点都记了下来。他们谁都不会喜欢你记得这么公允。}\n\n@event thesis_lab_borrowed_candle\n# en: Borrowing a Candle\ntitle [7f13b4] = 借一支蜡烛\n# en: {The candles here give out at the worst moment. Yours just has, and the carrel next door has a full box and nobody in it.|Your candle gives out again. Next door, the usual box of spares and nobody in the chair.|The lab's candles have outlasted no one's patience tonight, least of all yours. There's a box next door.} {~A draft lies open beside the box, face up.|A note on the box says \"Help yourself\" in one hand and \"Don't\" in another.|Someone has drawn a small eye on the box.}\ntext [e6ab7e] = {这里的蜡烛总在最要命的时候燃尽。你的刚灭，隔壁隔间有一整盒，人却不在。|你的蜡烛又灭了。隔壁照例放着一盒备用的，椅子上没人。|今晚实验室的蜡烛没能比任何人的耐心撑得更久，尤其是你的。隔壁有一盒。} {~盒子旁边摊着一份草稿，正面朝上。|盒子上一张字条写着“请自取”，另一种笔迹写着“别”。|有人在盒子上画了一只小眼睛。}\n# en: A dark carrel lit by one dying candle, a full box of candles on the empty desk next door.\nscene [a34fc6] = 一支将熄的蜡烛照着昏暗的隔间，隔壁空桌上放着满满一盒蜡烛。\n# en: Borrow one and keep working\nopt.left.label [178881] = 借一支接着干\n# en: {~You light it and work until the bell. You leave a note that says \"IOU one candle\", unsigned.|One candle, two good hours. You put the stub back, which is either honest or worse.}\nopt.left.say.1 [929efb] = {~你点上它一直干到钟响。你留了张字条：“欠蜡烛一支”，没署名。|一支蜡烛，两个好钟头。你把烛头放了回去，这要么算诚实，要么更糟。}\n# en: {~The owner comes back for a forgotten scarf and finds you lit by their candle. They say nothing, which is worse.|By morning the whole lab knows who borrowed the candle. Someone leaves a box on your desk, with a ribbon.}\nopt.left.say.2 [dcbbf0] = {~主人回来取忘下的围巾，发现你正借着他们的烛光。他们什么也没说，这更糟。|到早上全实验室都知道是谁借了蜡烛。有人在你桌上放了一盒，还系了丝带。}\n# en: Go back in the dark\nopt.right.label [48d592] = 摸黑回去\n# en: {~You walk back across the bridges in the dark, your argument going round and round. It is better by the time you reach your bed.|You stub your toe twice on the way out. The idea you had on the stairs was worth both.}\nopt.right.say.1 [8370a2] = {~你摸黑走过光之桥，论证在脑子里转个不停。走到床边时它已经好多了。|出门路上你踢到两次脚趾。楼梯上冒出的那个主意值这两下。}\n\n@event intern_royal_library_slips\n# en: Request Slips\ntitle [75d659] = 借阅单\n# en: {Scholars upstairs send their requests down a brass chute. Your job is to fetch them from the stacks, which go down for miles.|You've worn a path in the dust of the third landing.|You can read the chute's rattle now. A long rattle means someone wants something from the very bottom.} {~One slip is in a hand so old the ink has gone brown.|One slip asks for \"the blue one\".|One slip is a love letter, sent down by mistake.}\ntext [2e1959] = {楼上的学者把借阅单从黄铜滑槽送下来。你的工作是去书库里取书，而书库往下延伸好几里。|你在第三层平台的灰尘里踩出了一条路。|你现在听得懂滑槽的响声了。长长的一声，意味着有人要最底层的东西。} {~有张单子的笔迹古老得墨水都变褐了。|有张单子写着“蓝色那本”。|有张单子是封情书，寄错了地方。}\n# en: A brass chute dropping paper slips into a basket at the top of a deep stairwell lined with shelves.\nscene [7b85db] = 一道黄铜滑槽把纸条投进篮子，篮子放在一口排满书架的深井楼梯顶上。\n# en: Take the deep requests\nopt.left.label [879821] = 接最深处的单子\n# en: {~Four hundred steps down, the air is cold enough to keep books for ever. You come back up with the book and a lot of opinions about stairs.|You find the volume wedged behind a pipe. The scholar who asked for it nearly weeps.}\nopt.left.say.1 [4cc82f] = {~往下四百级台阶，空气冷得书能永远保存。你带着那本书和一肚子对楼梯的意见回到上面。|你在一根管子后面找到了那本书。要它的学者差点哭了。}\n# en: Take the near shelves\nopt.right.label [ee4e7d] = 只取近处书架的\n# en: {~You fetch the easy ones and leave the deep slips for someone else. Someone else notices.|You're fast and pleasant, and everyone knows you've never been below the third landing.}\nopt.right.say.1 [eb61e9] = {~你取了容易的，把深处的单子留给别人。别人注意到了。|你又快又和气，可大家都知道你从没下过第三层平台。}\n\n@event intern_royal_library_mending\n# en: The Mending Table\ntitle [1b5cc4] = 修补台\n# en: {Today you're at the mending table, where crumbling records are put back together one flake at a time.|The binder hands you the tweezers without a word, which counts as a greeting.|The binder trusts you with the older pages now, which are the worst.} {~The page is a royal decree on the correct length of goose necks.|The page is a recipe, half eaten by something.|The page is a list of names, one crossed out very hard.}\ntext [6c0c62] = {今天你在修补台，把碎裂的档案一片一片拼回去。|装订师一言不发地把镊子递给你，这就算打招呼了。|装订师现在把更老的书页交给你了，那些最难。} {~这页是一道王室法令，规定鹅脖子的正确长度。|这页是一份食谱，被什么东西啃掉了一半。|这页是一份名单，有一个名字被狠狠划掉了。}\n# en: A bright worktable under a lamp, a crumbling manuscript laid out in pieces and a student holding fine tweezers.\nscene [cb79c7] = 灯下一张明亮的工作台，一份碎裂的手稿摊成好几块，一个学生拿着细镊子。\n# en: Mend it myself\nopt.left.label [67b19a] = 我自己来修\n# en: {~Every flake goes back where it belongs. The binder holds it up to the lamp and says, \"Acceptable,\" which here is a medal.|You save the page and read it while the glue dries. It's better than you expected.}\nopt.left.say.1 [c8e44e] = {~每一片都回到了原处。装订师把它举到灯下说：“还行。”在这里，这就是勋章。|你救下了这一页，趁胶水干的时候读了它。比你想的有意思。}\n# en: {~A sneeze. The binder looks at the scattered flakes and then at you. You both get the tweezers.|One flake ends up in the wrong century. Nobody can prove it was you, but everybody knows.}\nopt.left.say.2 [55b845] = {~一个喷嚏。装订师看看散落的碎片，又看看你。你们俩一起拿起了镊子。|有一片被放进了错误的世纪。没人能证明是你，但人人都知道。}\n# en: Watch the binder work\nopt.right.label [5b7d5a] = 看装订师干活\n# en: {~You watch for an hour and learn more than a book could tell you. The binder says you're slow. You were.|The binder works without speaking and you learn by watching. It's very peaceful and very little gets done.}\nopt.right.say.1 [559067] = {~你看了一个钟头，学到的比书上能教的多。装订师说你慢。你确实慢。|装订师一言不发地干活，你在一旁看着学。非常宁静，也几乎没干成什么。}\n\n@event intern_conclave_test_chamber\n# en: The Test Chamber\ntitle [cb5a52] = 测试室\n# en: {The Conclave's researchers need someone to cast their newest spell for the first time. The walls are thick for a reason.|Someone has chalked a new circle on the floor. The old one is scorched.|You know every scorch mark in the chamber by now, and who made each one.} {~Today's spell is meant to make a candle burn backwards.|Today's spell is meant to tidy a room. It has opinions about what tidy means.|Today's spell is unnamed. Nobody will say why.}\ntext [647dd1] = {议会的研究员们需要有人第一次施放他们最新的咒语。墙那么厚是有原因的。|有人在地上用粉笔画了个新圈。旧的那个已经焦了。|如今测试室里每道焦痕你都认得，也知道是谁留下的。} {~今天的咒语据说能让蜡烛倒着烧。|今天的咒语据说能整理房间。它对“整理”有自己的看法。|今天的咒语没有名字。没人肯说为什么。}\n# en: A circular chamber with scorch-marked walls, a student at the centre with a wand raised and researchers watching through thick glass.\nscene [033a72] = 一间墙上满是焦痕的圆形测试室，一个学生站在中央举着魔杖，研究员们隔着厚玻璃观看。\n# en: Step into the chamber\nopt.left.label [2de272] = 走进测试室\n# en: {~The spell works on the first try. Behind the glass, someone writes your name in the margin of their notes.|It works, more or less, for one glorious minute before the spell remembers itself.}\nopt.left.say.1 [680416] = {~咒语一次成功。玻璃后面有人在笔记页边写下了你的名字。|基本成功了，光辉的一分钟之后，咒语才想起自己是谁。}\n# en: {~The spell goes off sideways. Your eyebrows are fine. Your fringe has views of its own now.|The spell goes off sideways, and your robes will never fold the same way again.}\nopt.left.say.2 [e3a2a9] = {~咒语往旁边炸开了。你的眉毛没事。你的刘海从此有了自己的主见。|咒语往旁边炸开了，你的袍子再也叠不回原来的样子。}\n# en: Take readings behind the glass\nopt.right.label [531746] = 在玻璃后记读数\n# en: {~You write down numbers for three hours while someone else gets to be brave. They're very good numbers.|You note every flicker. A senior researcher reads your page and asks for your notes next time.}\nopt.right.say.1 [fd1f33] = {~别人去当勇士，你记了三个钟头数字。都是非常好的数字。|你记下了每一次闪烁。一位资深研究员读了你的记录，要你下次把笔记给他。}\n\n@event intern_conclave_minutes\n# en: Taking the Minutes\ntitle [3271ff] = 做会议记录\n# en: {You've been asked to take the minutes of a Conclave research committee. There are nine members, and none of them ever agrees.|The committee is still arguing about last time's question, and now about the minutes too.|You could take these minutes in your sleep, and some of the committee do attend them that way.} {~Two of them are not speaking to each other, and want it recorded.|One of them asks what you think.|One of them falls asleep, and wakes up to vote.}\ntext [6435e2] = {你被派去给议会的一个研究委员会做记录。委员有九位，从没有意见一致过。|委员会还在吵上次的问题，现在连上次的记录也一起吵。|这记录你闭着眼都能做，有几位委员也确实是闭着眼出席的。} {~其中两位互不说话，并要求记录在案。|其中一位问你怎么看。|其中一位睡着了，醒来正好投票。}\n# en: A long table of elderly mages in heavy robes, all talking at once, a young intern writing furiously at the end.\nscene [998689] = 一张长桌旁坐满穿厚袍的年长法师，全都在同时说话，桌尾一个年轻实习生在拼命记录。\n# en: Offer an opinion when asked\nopt.left.label [48ef1a] = 被问到时说出看法\n# en: {~The table goes silent. Then the oldest mage says, \"The intern is right,\" and the rest pretend they agreed all along.|Your opinion settles the argument. It also starts two new ones.}\nopt.left.say.1 [76b9aa] = {~桌边鸦雀无声。然后最年长的法师说：“实习生说得对。”其余人假装自己早就同意。|你的看法平息了争论。也引发了两场新的。}\n# en: {~Nine heads turn towards you, slowly. You say something about wands. It isn't minuted.|You give your opinion and it is recorded in full, with the note \"(intern)\".}\nopt.left.say.2 [9f6fdb] = {~九颗脑袋慢慢转向你。你说了点关于魔杖的话。没被记录。|你的看法被完整记录在案，后面注着“（实习生）”。}\n# en: Keep my head down and write\nopt.right.label [1678b2] = 低头只管记录\n# en: {~Your minutes are perfect and so dull that nobody reads them, which in a committee is the highest praise.|You record every word of the argument. Your hand aches, and you understand the Conclave much better than you wanted to.}\nopt.right.say.1 [55dff9] = {~你的记录完美无缺，枯燥到没人读，在委员会里这是最高的赞誉。|你记下了争论的每一个字。手腕发酸，对议会的了解远超你的本意。}\n\n@event intern_alchemists_vat_watch\n# en: Vat Watch\ntitle [6ef11f] = 看守大缸\n# en: {Your task at the works is to watch one great vat and call the foreman if it changes colour.|You've brought your own colour chart now, with notes in the margins.|You and the vat understand each other now. It's an unsettling feeling.} {~The amber is drifting, very slowly.|There's a bubble that keeps coming back, like a thought.|The vat smells faintly of cinnamon, which it never has before.} The foreman is all the way at the other end of the hall.\ntext [51575f] = {你在工坊的任务是看守一口大缸，一旦变色就去叫工头。|你现在自带色卡，页边写满了笔记。|你和大缸现在心意相通。这感觉让人不安。} {~琥珀色正在极慢地漂移。|有个气泡一直冒回来，像一个念头。|大缸有一丝肉桂味，以前从没有过。} 工头在大厅的另一头。\n# en: A huge copper vat in a steaming hall, a student on a gantry staring into it with a colour chart in hand.\nscene [e8908d] = 蒸汽弥漫的大厅里一口巨大的铜缸，一个学生站在吊台上拿着色卡盯着缸里。\n# en: Adjust it myself\nopt.left.label [76449d] = 我自己调\n# en: {~A pinch of salt and a slow stir, and the vat settles back to its proper self. The foreman never knew, but the vat did.|You correct it before it goes wrong. When the foreman passes, he gives the vat a satisfied look as if he did it.}\nopt.left.say.1 [787567] = {~一撮盐，慢慢搅动，大缸恢复了本来的样子。工头永远不会知道，但大缸知道。|你在出事之前就纠正了。工头路过时满意地看了大缸一眼，好像是他干的。}\n# en: {~The vat turns a brilliant violet and hisses. The foreman arrives at a run, and you'll be hearing about it for weeks.|You make it worse. A lot worse. The whole hall smells of cinnamon until the end of the week.}\nopt.left.say.2 [f430e1] = {~大缸变成鲜亮的紫罗兰色，嘶嘶作响。工头一路跑来，这事你要被念叨好几个星期。|你把它弄得更糟。糟得多。整座大厅到周末都是肉桂味。}\n# en: Run for the foreman\nopt.right.label [4b3ef4] = 跑去叫工头\n# en: {~You run the length of the hall. The foreman fixes it in a second and explains how, at length, as you catch your breath.|By the time you're back with the foreman the colour has corrected itself. He stares at you, then at the vat.}\nopt.right.say.1 [6678d5] = {~你跑完整个大厅。工头一秒钟就修好了，趁你喘气时详详细细地讲了怎么修。|等你带着工头回来，颜色已经自己恢复了。他盯着你，又盯着大缸。}\n\n@event intern_alchemists_no_flame\n# en: The Rule About Flames\ntitle [a32af9] = 关于明火的规定\n# en: {There's a strict rule at the works about naked flames, so the break room has spent forty years inventing flameless ways to boil a kettle.|Break time. The kettle sits on its warm stone, and the workers look at you hopefully.|By now the break room asks the academy intern first, whatever the problem.} {~Today the warm stone has gone cold.|Today someone wants toast, which is a much bigger question.|Today a new inspector is touring the works.}\ntext [d8eb15] = {工坊对明火有严格规定，所以休息室花了四十年发明各种不用火烧水的办法。|休息时间。水壶坐在温热的石头上，工人们满怀希望地看着你。|如今休息室不管遇到什么问题，都先问学院来的实习生。} {~今天那块温石凉了。|今天有人想吃烤面包，这可是大得多的问题。|今天新来的巡查员在视察工坊。}\n# en: A break room in an alchemical works, workers gathered around a kettle sitting on a glowing stone that is not, technically, on fire.\nscene [03a5ac] = 炼金工坊的休息室，工人们围着一把放在发光石头上的水壶，严格说来那并不算着火。\n# en: Help brew the flameless tea\nopt.left.label [823819] = 帮忙泡无火茶\n# en: {~You heat the kettle with a reaction you learned in your first year. The break room calls it \"the academy method\" and adopts it on the spot.|It works, though everything boiled on it comes out a little purple.}\nopt.left.say.1 [6d74a5] = {~你用一年级学的一个反应把水壶烧热了。休息室管它叫“学院法”，当场采用。|成功了，不过用它煮出来的东西都有点发紫。}\n# en: {~Your method works so well that someone in charge notices the steam. There's a meeting about it, with you in it.|The kettle boils, and so, briefly, does the table.}\nopt.left.say.2 [161dbb] = {~你的方法太好用了，有位管事的注意到了蒸汽。为此开了个会，你也在场。|水壶开了，桌子也短暂地开了。}\n# en: Stick to the rules\nopt.right.label [b9e488] = 遵守规定\n# en: {~You drink cold water while everyone else drinks tea. The foreman notes your conduct, and the break room notes it too.|You remind everyone of the rule. You are absolutely right and nobody offers you a biscuit for the rest of the week.}\nopt.right.say.1 [d6631e] = {~别人喝茶，你喝凉水。工头记下了你的表现，休息室也记下了。|你提醒大家注意规定。你完全正确，这一整周没人再分你饼干。}\n\n@event intern_expedition_thin_air\n# en: Up the Ridge\ntitle [23ba3c] = 登上山脊\n# en: {The instruments have to go up to the ridge before dark, and the air on the plateau is very thin.|Your legs know the path now. Your lungs are still negotiating.|You've carried things up this ridge so often that the goats have stopped looking at you.} {season == \"winter\": {~Snow has drifted against the path.|The wind cuts through every layer you own.}|{~The rocks are still hot from the afternoon.|The sky is clear all the way to the top of the world.}}\ntext [d6f7b7] = {天黑前仪器必须运上山脊，而高原上的空气非常稀薄。|腿已经认得路了。肺还在讨价还价。|你往这道山脊上搬过太多次东西，山羊都不再看你了。} {season == \"winter\": {~雪堆在了路边。|风穿透你身上的每一层衣服。}|{~石头还留着下午的热。|天空晴朗，一直晴到世界的顶端。}}\n# en: A line of expedition members climbing a rocky ridge at dusk, carrying brass instruments wrapped in blankets.\nscene [00b5b6] = 黄昏时一队远征队员攀上岩石山脊，抱着裹在毯子里的黄铜仪器。\n# en: Carry the great lens\nopt.left.label [e7e4f2] = 扛那块大透镜\n# en: {~You carry the great lens to the top without dropping it, then sit very still for a long time. The leader nods at you, once.|Your arms are numb by the top, and the lens is perfect. It's worth it when the first star shows in it.}\nopt.left.say.1 [5b1f0a] = {~你把大透镜扛到山顶，一点没摔，然后一动不动地坐了很久。领队朝你点了一下头。|到山顶时你的胳膊已经麻了，透镜完好无损。当第一颗星出现在镜中，一切都值了。}\n# en: Do the sums in the tent\nopt.right.label [d9b374] = 在帐篷里算数\n# en: {~You work the night's sums by lamplight, warm and quiet. The lamp gutters twice. Your sums don't.|You have the charts ready when they come down. Nobody thanks you, because the charts are always ready.}\nopt.right.say.1 [a6fd0a] = {~你借着灯光算当晚的数，温暖又安静。灯芯跳了两下，你的算式一下也没跳。|他们下来时星图已经准备好了。没人道谢，因为星图总是准备好的。}\n\n@event intern_expedition_chart_dispute\n# en: A Star Out of Place\ntitle [dbb002] = 错位的星\n# en: {Your chart and the senior astronomer's disagree about where one faint star is.|The senior astronomer's charts and yours have a running disagreement now, kept in red ink.|The senior astronomer now checks your charts first. A compliment, possibly.} {~Probably your hand shook.|Probably their eyesight.|Probably the thin air.} {season == \"winter\": The nights are long, with time to recheck everything.|The nights are short, and every hour counts.}\ntext [c739a4] = {你的星图和资深天文学家的星图，在一颗暗星的位置上不一致。|资深天文学家的星图和你的现在有一场持续的分歧，用红墨水记着。|资深天文学家现在先查你的星图。也许算是夸奖。} {~大概是你的手抖了。|大概是他们的眼神。|大概是稀薄的空气。} {season == \"winter\": 夜很长，有时间把一切都复核一遍。|夜很短，每个钟头都要紧。}\n# en: Two expedition members bent over a star chart by lamplight, one pointing firmly at a dot.\nscene [576570] = 两名远征队员借着灯光俯身看一张星图，其中一人坚定地指着一个点。\n# en: Recheck it all night\nopt.left.label [19c9f0] = 通宵复核\n# en: {~You recheck it until dawn. You were right by a hair, and nobody will ever care but you.|You were wrong by a hair. You fix it, and know the sky better than you did.}\nopt.left.say.1 [a067a2] = {~你一直核对到天亮。你对了，差之毫厘，除了你没人会在乎。|你错了，差之毫厘。你改了过来，对星空的了解又多了一点。}\n# en: Raise it with the astronomer\nopt.right.label [8f17a5] = 向天文学家提出来\n# en: {~The astronomer checks, grumbles and corrects their chart. Then they buy you a cup of the camp's terrible coffee.|\"Well spotted,\" says the astronomer, through their teeth.}\nopt.right.say.1 [fef456] = {~天文学家查了一遍，嘟囔着改了自己的星图。然后请你喝了一杯营地里难喝的咖啡。|“眼尖。”天文学家咬着牙说。}\n# en: {~The astronomer checks and shows you, in front of everyone, exactly where you went wrong.|They don't check. They just look at you over their spectacles until you go away.}\nopt.right.say.2 [e6af69] = {~天文学家查了，当着所有人的面给你指出哪里错了。|他们没查。只是从眼镜上方看着你，直到你走开。}\n\n@event intern_field_station_count\n# en: The Dawn Count\ntitle [c272a9] = 黎明清点\n# en: {Every morning someone has to lie in the heather and count what's living on the moor. Today it's you.|You've learned which tussock is the least wet, and you guard it.|You're the station's best counter now, mostly because you've stopped minding the damp.} {season == \"winter\": {~Snow hares are out, white on white.|Frost has made the heather crackle under you.}|{~The skylarks are going up one after another.|The midges have found you.}}\ntext [cdb641] = {每天早上都得有人趴在石楠丛里，清点荒原上活着的东西。今天轮到你。|你摸清了哪个草丛最不湿，并且守着它。|你现在是野外站最好的清点员，主要是因为你不再在乎潮湿了。} {season == \"winter\": {~雪兔出来了，白上加白。|霜让石楠在你身下咔咔作响。}|{~云雀一只接一只飞上天。|蠓虫找到你了。}}\n# en: A student lying flat in the heather at dawn, a tally board in hand, watching nesting towers on the moor.\nscene [94a0ee] = 黎明时一个学生平趴在石楠丛中，手拿计数板，望着荒原上的筑巢塔。\n# en: Count every last one\nopt.left.label [1a8fb1] = 一只不落地数\n# en: {~You count until your fingers are numb and the tally is perfect. The warden reads it twice and pins it on the wall.|You stay out until the very last nest is counted. You come back soaked and very proud of the number.}\nopt.left.say.1 [272659] = {~你数到手指发麻，记录完美无缺。站长读了两遍，钉在了墙上。|你一直待到最后一个巢数完。回来时浑身湿透，为这个数字非常骄傲。}\n# en: Make a sensible estimate\nopt.right.label [db92d7] = 合理估一个数\n# en: {~Your estimate is so close that nobody suspects. You spend the extra hour by the stove with a cup of tea.|The warden checks your number against his own and nods. You will never tell him.}\nopt.right.say.1 [8f5830] = {~你估得太准了，没人起疑。省下的那个钟头你在炉边喝了杯茶。|站长拿你的数和他自己的对了对，点点头。你永远不会告诉他。}\n# en: {~The warden counted too. His number is not your number. He makes a note.|Your estimate includes a flock that left last week. The warden is very nice about it, which is worse.}\nopt.right.say.2 [925258] = {~站长也数了。他的数不是你的数。他记了一笔。|你的估计里包括了一群上周就飞走的鸟。站长对此非常客气，这更糟。}\n\n@event intern_field_station_patient\n# en: The Patient in Paddock Three\ntitle [813ec2] = 三号围场的病号\n# en: {The station's latest patient is a moor-drake with a splinted wing and no gratitude at all.|Feeding time in paddock three. The patient sees you coming and lowers its head.|The drake in paddock three recognises you now. This hasn't made it any nicer.} {~It has eaten a glove.|It is pretending to sleep, which it does very badly.|It has learned to open the bucket by itself.} {season == \"winter\": Its breath steams in the frosty air.|It is lying in the one patch of shade.}\ntext [ced8b7] = {野外站最新的病号是一头荒原幼龙，翅膀上着夹板，毫无感恩之心。|三号围场喂食时间。病号看见你来，低下了头。|三号围场的幼龙现在认得你了。这并没让它变得友善。} {~它吃了一只手套。|它在装睡，装得很差。|它学会了自己打开桶。} {season == \"winter\": 它的呼吸在霜冷的空气里冒着白气。|它躺在唯一一块阴凉里。}\n# en: A muddy paddock with a splinted creature glaring from a straw nest, a student holding out a bucket at arm's length.\nscene [ddabad] = 泥泞的围场里一头上着夹板的生物在草窝里瞪着眼，一个学生伸长胳膊递出一只桶。\n# en: Feed it by hand\nopt.left.label [cc2f18] = 亲手喂它\n# en: {~It takes the food from your hand, very gently. Then it bites the bucket, to make a point.|It lets you check the splint, grumbling the whole time like an old man at the barber's.}\nopt.left.say.1 [4e99a6] = {~它从你手里非常轻地叼走食物。然后咬了一口桶，以示立场。|它让你检查了夹板，全程嘟嘟囔囔，像理发店里的老头。}\n# en: {~It takes the food and a bit of your sleeve. Your arm is fine. Your sleeve is not.|It butts you into the mud and then looks pleased with itself.}\nopt.left.say.2 [3309dd] = {~它叼走了食物，外加一截你的袖子。胳膊没事。袖子有事。|它把你拱进泥里，然后一脸得意。}\n# en: Use the long tongs\nopt.right.label [cd0b4c] = 用长夹子喂\n# en: {~You feed it from a safe distance. It eats, and looks at you with deep contempt.|The tongs work perfectly. You feel you've let the patient down, somehow.}\nopt.right.say.1 [c8b36f] = {~你隔着安全距离喂它。它吃了，用深深的鄙夷看着你。|夹子好用极了。可你总觉得自己辜负了这位病号。}\n\n@event intern_thornwick_pastries\n# en: Thornwick Hospitality\ntitle [5e9c3e] = 荆木的待客之道\n# en: {Each morning your Thornwick hosts leave pastries on your desk, with a polite note.|The notes have grown longer and politer, which is worrying.|The pastries are a game now, and both sides know it.} {~They look perfect, which in an illusion lab is a warning.|One is steaming slightly, which it shouldn't be.|Today there's one plain biscuit, which is somehow worse.} {season == \"winter\": The pastries look very warm.|The icing hasn't melted in the heat.}\ntext [a1b318] = {每天早上，荆木的东道主都在你桌上放一盘点心，附一张客气的字条。|字条越写越长、越写越客气，这让人担心。|点心现在成了一场游戏，双方都心知肚明。} {~它们看起来完美无缺，在幻术实验室这就是警告。|有一块在微微冒热气，它不该冒的。|今天只有一块普通饼干，不知怎么更可疑了。} {season == \"winter\": 点心看起来很暖。|天这么热，糖霜却没化。}\n# en: A polished laboratory table at a rival academy, a plate of perfect pastries set out for a visiting student.\nscene [7a642b] = 对手学院一张光洁的实验桌上，一盘完美的点心摆在来访学生面前。\n# en: Eat one and smile\nopt.left.label [bb9a84] = 吃一块，再笑一笑\n# en: {~It's real, and very good. The Thornwick interns look almost disappointed.|It's real. You eat it slowly, in front of everyone, and thank them warmly.}\nopt.left.say.1 [02d79a] = {~是真的，而且非常好吃。荆木的实习生看起来几乎有点失望。|是真的。你当着所有人的面慢慢吃完，热情地道谢。}\n# en: {~It's a glamour over something dry and joyless. You eat the whole thing with a smile, and the lab respects that.|It tastes of nothing at all. You compliment the baker, and someone in the corner laughs.}\nopt.left.say.2 [c412ce] = {~是套在某种又干又无趣的东西上的魅影。你笑着吃完了整块，实验室对此肃然起敬。|它什么味道都没有。你夸了夸烘焙师，角落里有人笑出了声。}\n# en: Bring my own breakfast\nopt.right.label [34615e] = 自带早饭\n# en: {~You eat your own bread, very pointedly. The Thornwick students write something down.|You leave the plate untouched. Your supervisor here calls it \"a very Lumenhold choice\", and doesn't mean it nicely.}\nopt.right.say.1 [5cdb12] = {~你意味深长地吃着自己的面包。荆木的学生记了点什么。|你没碰那盘点心。这里的导师说这是“非常流明堡的选择”，不是好话。}\n\n@event intern_thornwick_rivalry\n# en: The Old Grudge\ntitle [7f876b] = 老恩怨\n# en: {The Thornwick students have questions about Lumenhold. Most of them are about the last time the two schools met on the duelling field.|The Thornwick students have found a new angle on the old grudge.|You and the Thornwick students have a routine now. It's almost friendly.} {~Today they want to know if Lumenhold's towers still lean.|Today they ask, sweetly, if Lumenhold teaches illusion yet.|Today one of them pretends to be you, very well.}\ntext [fa1e9c] = {荆木的学生对流明堡有些问题。大多是关于两校上一次在决斗场上交手的事。|荆木的学生为这桩老恩怨找到了新角度。|你和荆木的学生现在有了固定节目。几乎算友好。} {~今天他们想知道流明堡的塔是不是还歪着。|今天他们甜甜地问，流明堡是不是终于开幻术课了。|今天他们中有人扮成了你，扮得非常像。}\n# en: A group of rival students around a lab bench, one of them leaning in with a pointed smile towards a visitor in different robes.\nscene [07d8c1] = 一群对手学院的学生围在实验台边，其中一个朝穿着不同长袍的访客探过身去，笑得意味深长。\n# en: Out-charm them\nopt.left.label [a1eb25] = 用魅力压过他们\n# en: {~You answer every barb with a better one. By lunch they're laughing with you rather than at you.|You make the room laugh at their joke, and then at them. Their supervisor smiles behind her notes.}\nopt.left.say.1 [b5641f] = {~每句挖苦你都回以更妙的一句。到午饭时，他们是在跟你一起笑，而不是笑你。|你让满屋人先笑他们的笑话，再笑他们。他们的导师在笔记后面露出微笑。}\n# en: {~You try to be witty. It comes out as a speech about the founders. They let you finish, kindly.|You lose the exchange so badly that one of them takes a bow on your behalf.}\nopt.left.say.2 [70ef48] = {~你想说点俏皮话，结果说成了一篇关于创始者的演讲。他们好心地让你讲完了。|你输得太惨，他们中有人替你鞠了一躬。}\n# en: Let it slide\nopt.right.label [344baa] = 随它去\n# en: {~You smile and go back to work. Your supervisor here notices, and approves, and you feel like you lost anyway.|You say nothing. It takes them all morning to realise you've won.}\nopt.right.say.1 [0d9b22] = {~你笑笑，回去干活。这里的导师注意到了，也赞许，可你还是觉得自己输了。|你什么也没说。他们花了一上午才意识到你赢了。}\n\n@event intern_unseen_office_coffee\n# en: The Coffee Machine\ntitle [9f1fbd] = 咖啡机\n# en: {Nobody at the Office talks about anything, except the coffee machine. It's very good.|A queue forms around the machine each evening, though nobody seems to join it.|The coffee queue, you've learned, is where the Office's real work gets done.} {~Two people beside you discuss the weather in a way that isn't about the weather.|A cup has a name on it that was there yesterday, under another name.|The machine hisses, and everyone falls silent until it stops.}\ntext [bf535e] = {署里没人谈论任何事，除了咖啡机。它非常好用。|每晚咖啡机周围都会排起队，却没人看起来是去排队的。|你已经明白，咖啡队伍才是署里真正干活的地方。} {~你旁边两个人在聊天气，聊法显然不是在聊天气。|一只杯子上写着一个名字，昨天它也在，写的是另一个名字。|机器一嘶响，所有人都停下话头，直到它响完。}\n# en: A plain room with no windows and a gleaming coffee machine, quiet people in plain coats standing near it, not quite talking.\nscene [2a796e] = 一间没有窗户的朴素房间，一台锃亮的咖啡机，几个穿着普通外套的安静的人站在旁边，不太算是在交谈。\n# en: Linger at the machine\nopt.left.label [a4aa91] = 在咖啡机边多待会儿\n# en: {~You learn three things nobody told you, and one thing nobody knows you know.|You drink your coffee slowly and listen. By the second cup you understand what nobody is saying.}\nopt.left.say.1 [2e44c1] = {~你知道了三件没人告诉你的事，还有一件没人知道你知道的事。|你慢慢喝着咖啡听着。到第二杯，你听懂了没人说出口的东西。}\n# en: {~Someone notices you listening and asks, very kindly, how your day is going. You don't sleep that night.|You overhear something you very much wish you hadn't. It can't be unheard.}\nopt.left.say.2 [3946a5] = {~有人发现你在听，非常和气地问你今天过得怎样。那晚你没睡着。|你无意中听到一件真希望没听到的事。听到了就忘不掉了。}\n# en: Take my cup back to my desk\nopt.right.label [f05b97] = 端回自己桌上\n# en: {~You work through the evening with your coffee. The work is dull and your supervisor approves, which is a little chilling.|Your desk is very quiet. It's the most anyone at the Office will say about you, and here that's high praise.}\nopt.right.say.1 [54086b] = {~你就着咖啡工作了一晚。活很枯燥，导师很满意，这有点让人发冷。|你的桌子非常安静。这是署里任何人对你说过的最多的话，在这里这是高度赞扬。}\n\n@event intern_unseen_office_wrong_file\n# en: The Wrong File\ntitle [a461ed] = 错放的卷宗\n# en: {A file has been left on your desk. It isn't yours, and it isn't labelled.|A second unlabelled file. You're starting to think it's a test.|The unlabelled files keep arriving. You've stopped asking where they come from.} This one is tied shut with a red ribbon. {~It's very thin.|It's very thick, with a coffee ring on the cover.|The knot is one you don't recognise.}\ntext [4e3acb] = {有份卷宗被留在你桌上。不是你的，也没有标签。|第二份没有标签的卷宗。你开始觉得这是一场考验。|没有标签的卷宗不停地送来。你已经不再问它们从哪来。} 这份用红丝带系着。{~它非常薄。|它非常厚，封面上有个咖啡杯印。|那个结你从没见过。}\n# en: A plain folder with no label lying on an empty desk under a single lamp.\nscene [395e41] = 一盏孤灯下的空桌上，放着一个没有标签的朴素文件夹。\n# en: Read it before returning it\nopt.left.label [6c5a88] = 先读再归还\n# en: {~You read it, retie the ribbon perfectly and hand it in. Your supervisor checks the knot and smiles, very slightly.|You read every page. It's about a man who sells hats. Nothing has ever made you more nervous.}\nopt.left.say.1 [bff383] = {~你读完了，把丝带系得一模一样，交了回去。导师检查了那个结，非常轻微地笑了笑。|你读了每一页。讲的是一个卖帽子的人。从没有什么让你这么紧张过。}\n# en: {~Your supervisor takes the file back and glances at the ribbon. \"Interesting knot,\" she says.|You put the pages back in the wrong order. Somehow, everyone knows by morning.}\nopt.left.say.2 [3ef205] = {~导师拿回卷宗，瞥了一眼丝带。“有意思的结。”她说。|你把书页放回去时顺序错了。不知怎么，第二天早上人人都知道了。}\n# en: Return it unopened\nopt.right.label [ab3e46] = 原封不动地归还\n# en: {~You hand it back without looking. Your supervisor says nothing, which here is approval. You'll wonder about it for weeks.|You return it with the ribbon untouched. The next day there's a slightly better coffee waiting on your desk.}\nopt.right.say.1 [fb7116] = {~你看都没看就交了回去。导师什么也没说，在这里这就是认可。你会为此琢磨好几个星期。|你把丝带原样未动地还了回去。第二天你桌上多了一杯稍好一点的咖啡。}\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/pools/village_home.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/pools/village_home.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event village_barge_crossing\n# en: The Barge Across\ntitle [bac81a] = 渡湖驳船\n# en: {The village barge is slower than walking, if you could walk on water.|The bargeman has started nodding at you like a regular.|The bargeman saves you the dry end of the bench.} {season == \"winter\": Ice ticks against the hull.|{season == \"summer\": Dragonflies ride the bow for free.|{~A first-year is being sick, very politely.|Two prefects are pretending not to hold hands.|Someone is reading aloud to a crate of chickens.}}}\ntext [afc63f] = {去小镇的驳船慢得很——如果你能在水上走路，走过去都比它快。|船夫开始像对待熟客那样冲你点头了。|船夫开始给你留长凳上干的那一头了。} {season == \"winter\": 薄冰一下下轻敲着船身。|{season == \"summer\": 蜻蜓停在船头，免费搭船。|{~一个一年级生正趴在船边呕吐，吐得非常有礼貌。|两个级长正假装没在牵手。|有人在给一笼鸡大声朗读。}}}\n# en: A flat-bottomed barge crossing a mirror-still lake, the seven towers shrinking behind it.\nscene [456c6d] = 一艘平底驳船驶过镜面般平静的湖，七座塔在身后渐渐变小。\n# en: Read on the crossing\nopt.left.label [467133] = 渡湖时看书\n# en: {~You finish a chapter and miss the towers turning gold behind you.|You read the same paragraph four times; the lake keeps interrupting.|You look up only when the hull bumps the quay, and feel you've skipped something.}\nopt.left.say.1 [484d14] = {~你读完一章，却错过了身后的塔楼被夕阳染成金色。|同一段你读了四遍；湖光总来打岔。|直到船身撞上码头你才抬头，总觉得错过了什么。}\n# en: Chat with the bargeman\nopt.right.label [89b231] = 跟船夫聊天\n# en: {~He tells you the lake was rougher in his father's day, and expects a tip for the history.|He has opinions about every tower and charges for none of them, except in tips.|He asks after your exams as if they were a sick relative. You tip him for the sympathy.}\nopt.right.say.1 [704add] = {~他说他父亲那时候湖上风浪大得多，并且指望你为这段历史付点小费。|他对每座塔都有看法，一概免费——小费除外。|他问起你的考试，口气像在问候一位生病的亲戚。你为这份同情付了小费。}\n\n@event village_square_errand\n# en: One Thing in the Square\ntitle [e25a84] = 广场上的“就一件事”\n# en: {friend} has come down to the village with you \"for one thing\", which turns out to be several. {First, a hat.|You've learned that \"one thing\" means at least three.|You've stopped asking what the one thing is.} {~A stallholder is selling candied chestnuts in paper twists.|A tinker is mending a cauldron with a great deal of swearing.|The market cat has chosen {friend} and will not be moved.}\ntext [10243b] = {friend}陪你下山到小镇，说是“就办一件事”，结果是好几件。{先是一顶帽子。|你已经明白，“一件事”至少意味着三件。|你已经不再问那“一件事”是什么了。}{~一个摊贩在卖纸包的糖炒栗子。|一个补锅匠正骂骂咧咧地修一口坩埚。|集市上的猫选中了{friend}，怎么也赶不走。}\n# en: A crooked village market square, two students weaving between stalls, one already carrying too many parcels.\nscene [52254a] = 歪歪扭扭的小镇集市广场，两个学生在摊位间穿行，其中一个已经抱了太多包裹。\n# en: Carry the parcels\nopt.left.label [faeb8d] = 帮忙拎包裹\n# en: {~You come back up the hill with an armful of string-tied parcels and sore wrists.|{friend} buys you a chestnut as payment. It is not enough payment.|One parcel clinks suspiciously. You decide not to ask.}\nopt.left.say.1 [5cbcb5] = {~你抱着一摞系着绳子的包裹爬回山上，手腕酸痛。|{friend}买了颗栗子当报酬。这报酬远远不够。|有个包裹叮当作响，十分可疑。你决定不问。}\n# en: Talk {them} out of it\nopt.right.label [2a8f41] = 劝{them}别买了\n# en: {~You talk {friend} out of the third thing. {They} sulk{s} the whole way back to the barge.|Your argument is excellent. {friend} buys the thing anyway, and now you've been wrong in public.|You win. {friend} says you'd make a terrible shopkeeper and a worse friend, then takes your arm anyway.}\nopt.right.say.1 [4768d2] = {~你劝{friend}放弃了第三样东西。{They}一路生着闷气回到驳船上。|你的论证无懈可击。{friend}还是买了，于是你当众输了一回。|你赢了。{friend}说你会是个糟糕的店主、更糟糕的朋友，然后照样挽起你的胳膊。}\n\n@event village_last_barge\n# en: The Last Barge\ntitle [d7085d] = 末班驳船\n# en: {The last barge back leaves at the bell, and the bell is ringing.|The last-barge bell, and you're nowhere near the quay. The bargeman knows your running by now.|The bell. Of course the bell.} {season == \"winter\": Snow is coming down thick over the square.|{~The inn has started a song with too many verses.|The lamps are only half lit.|The lake has gone the colour of pewter.}} {final_year: Not many evenings like this are left.|You could run. You could stay.}\ntext [6fe390] = {回城堡的末班驳船随钟声开船，而钟声正在响。|末班船的钟声响了，你离码头还远着呢。船夫早就熟悉你奔跑的样子了。|钟声。当然是钟声。} {season == \"winter\": 大雪正纷纷扬扬落在广场上。|{~酒馆里有人唱起一首段落多得没完的歌。|街灯才点亮一半。|湖水变成了锡镴般的灰色。}} {final_year: 这样的夜晚剩下的不多了。|你可以跑。你也可以留下。}\n# en: A lamplit village quay at dusk, the last barge pulling at its rope, a student hesitating at the edge of the square.\nscene [aa10dd] = 黄昏时灯火点点的小镇码头，末班驳船拽着缆绳，一个学生在广场边犹豫。\n# en: Run for it\nopt.left.label [38d292] = 跑去赶船\n# en: {~You make it with a leap and one wet boot.|You make it; your scarf does not.|The bargeman holds the rope a heartbeat longer than he should. You owe him.}\nopt.left.say.1 [2b9596] = {~你纵身一跃赶上了，湿了一只靴子。|你赶上了；你的围巾没赶上。|船夫多攥了一会儿缆绳，比他该攥的久一点。你欠他个人情。}\n# en: Stay a little longer\nopt.right.label [4a025a] = 再待一会儿\n# en: {~A fisherman rows you back for a ruinous fare and a story you didn't ask for.|You pay the night ferryman far too much. He rows like he has somewhere better to be.}\nopt.right.say.1 [4245d0] = {~一个渔夫划船送你回去，要价高得离谱，还附赠一个你没想听的故事。|你付给夜间摆渡人太多钱了。他划船的样子，像是有更要紧的地方要去。}\n# en: {~You sleep on a bench under the Newt's eaves and wake up with a cat on your feet.|The first barge of morning finds you yawning on the quay.}\nopt.right.say.2 [daec8c] = {~你在蝾螈酒馆的屋檐下睡了一夜长凳，醒来时脚上趴着一只猫。|清晨的头班驳船发现你正在码头上打哈欠。}\n\n@event gilded_newt_fireside\n# en: By the Newt's Fire\ntitle [5b6147] = 蝾螈的炉火边\n# en: {The Gilded Newt's fire has never gone out, and the landlady says it never will while she lives.|The landlady chalks your name on her slate before you've ordered.|Your corner by the fire is free. The regulars leave it for you now.} {season == \"winter\": Snow hisses off the boots of everyone who comes in.|{~A dog is asleep with its paws in the hearth.|Someone has hung wet socks on the fire-irons.|A farmer is explaining turnips to nobody.}}\ntext [c2f98a] = {镀金蝾螈酒馆的炉火从没熄过，老板娘说只要她还活着，就永远不会熄。|你还没点单，老板娘就已经在石板上写好了你的名字。|炉边你的那个角落空着。常客们现在都把它留给你。} {season == \"winter\": 每个进门的人靴子上的雪都在嘶嘶作响。|{~一条狗把爪子搭在炉边睡着了。|有人把湿袜子挂在了火钳上。|一个农夫正对着空气讲解芜菁。}}\n# en: A low-beamed tavern with a roaring hearth, a student tucked into the corner seat beside it with a steaming cup.\nscene [886f83] = 低矮房梁的酒馆里炉火熊熊，一个学生窝在炉边的角落座位，捧着一杯冒热气的饮料。\n# en: Order a cider and stay\nopt.left.label [fc74cf] = 点杯苹果酒坐着\n# en: {~The cider is warm and spiced and costs exactly what the landlady says it does.|You stay until your cheeks are red and your worries are smaller.|The landlady brings the cider without being asked, and charges for it all the same.}\nopt.left.say.1 [6609da] = {~苹果酒温热、带着香料味，价钱分毫不差，正如老板娘所说。|你一直坐到脸颊发红，烦恼也变小了。|老板娘没等你开口就端来了苹果酒，钱照收不误。}\n# en: Work by the firelight\nopt.right.label [01152e] = 借着炉光干活\n# en: {~You get two pages done between three toasts and a fiddle.|The noise is terrible, and somehow you concentrate better than in the library.|A regular reads over your shoulder and corrects your spelling of \"wyvern\".}\nopt.right.say.1 [d61234] = {~在三轮祝酒和一段提琴之间，你写完了两页。|吵得要命，可不知为何你比在图书馆里还专心。|一位常客从你肩后看过来，纠正了你“双足飞龙”的拼写。}\n\n@event gilded_newt_card_game\n# en: Cards at the Newt\ntitle [cae7f0] = 蝾螈的牌局\n# en: {There's a card game in the back room of the Gilded Newt, and an empty chair.|The empty chair in the back room has a cushion on it now, as if it's been waiting.|The card players greet you by the name of your worst hand.} {~Tonight it's a tinker, a widow and a man who shuffles like a conjuror.|Tonight the stakes are copper and pride.|Tonight a second-year from Duskgate is losing very cheerfully.}\ntext [82b92a] = {镀金蝾螈的后屋有一局牌，还有一把空椅子。|后屋那把空椅子上如今多了个坐垫，像是一直在等你。|牌友们用你最烂的那手牌给你起了外号来打招呼。} {~今晚是一个补锅匠、一个寡妇，还有一个洗牌像变戏法的男人。|今晚的赌注是铜板和面子。|今晚一个暮门的二年级生正输得兴高采烈。}\n# en: A smoky back room in a tavern, a card game around a scarred table and one empty chair.\nscene [939cdf] = 烟雾缭绕的酒馆后屋，一张满是划痕的桌上正在打牌，还有一把空椅子。\n# en: Take the empty chair\nopt.left.label [2c28bd] = 坐上那把空椅子\n# en: {~You win a handful of crowns and a reputation for an honest face.|You win, and the widow asks where you learned to bluff like that.}\nopt.left.say.1 [08271b] = {~你赢了一把克朗，还落了个“老实面相”的名声。|你赢了，寡妇问你从哪儿学的这手虚张声势。}\n# en: {~You lose your crowns slowly, and then all at once.|The tinker takes your last coin and buys you a drink with it, which somehow makes it worse.}\nopt.left.say.2 [8e7d17] = {~你的克朗先是慢慢地输，然后一下子全没了。|补锅匠赢走你最后一枚硬币，又用它请你喝了一杯，不知怎的更难受了。}\n# en: Watch for the tells\nopt.right.label [f1d424] = 观察他们的破绽\n# en: {~You spend the evening watching. The man who shuffles like a conjuror scratches his ear when he lies.|You learn a great deal about bluffing and nothing about having fun.|Someone asks if you're playing or haunting. You leave before you have to answer.}\nopt.right.say.1 [28ad8d] = {~你看了一整晚。那个洗牌像变戏法的男人，撒谎时会挠耳朵。|你学到了很多虚张声势的门道，却一点乐子也没有。|有人问你是来打牌的还是来闹鬼的。你没等回答就走了。}\n\n@event gilded_newt_corner_table\n# en: The Corner Table\ntitle [30043d] = 角落那张桌\n# en: {here} is at the corner table with {a jug of cider and no plans to leave.|the house jug, which the landlady refills for whoever sits there.|the table's jug. The landlady nods at you as you come in, as if you're expected.} \"{~Sit down, you're blocking the fire.|You look like you need a drink. I look like I need company.|Don't tell anyone you saw me here.}\" {season == \"winter\": Snow is piling up against the window.|The window is open to the night.}\ntext [8248cd] = {here}坐在角落那张桌旁，{面前一壶苹果酒，完全没有走的意思。|面前是店里那只专属酒壶，谁坐在那儿，老板娘就给谁续满。|面前是那张桌的酒壶。你进门时老板娘冲你点点头，好像早料到你会来。}“{~坐下吧，你挡着火了。|你看上去需要喝一杯。我看上去需要个伴。|别告诉别人你在这儿见过我。}”{season == \"winter\": 雪正堆在窗台上。|窗户朝着夜色敞开。}\n# en: A tavern corner table with a half-empty jug of cider, someone familiar waving a student over.\nscene [5c8294] = 酒馆角落的桌上放着半壶苹果酒，一个熟面孔正招手叫学生过去。\n# en: Sit down for one drink\nopt.left.label [e16ef4] = 坐下喝一杯\n# en: {~One drink becomes three stories and a long walk to the barge.|{here} tells you something true halfway down the jug, then pretends not to have.|You stay later than you meant to. It's worth the morning.}\nopt.left.say.1 [6ac2b8] = {~一杯变成了三个故事，外加一段走回驳船的长路。|喝到半壶时{here}跟你说了句真心话，然后装作没说过。|你待得比打算的晚。明早难熬，但值得。}\n# en: Wave and keep walking\nopt.right.label [11b59d] = 挥挥手继续走\n# en: {~You wave and get back to your book. {here} toasts your back, a little hurt.|You plead an early lecture. It's even true.}\nopt.right.say.1 [9c38b6] = {~你挥挥手，回去看你的书。{here}冲你的背影举杯，有点受伤。|你推说明早有课。这居然是真的。}\n\n@event mabels_the_door\n# en: Mabel Reads the Door\ntitle [d9674c] = 梅布尔看门识人\n# en: {Mabel looks up as you open the door and is already reaching for a tin.|Mabel watches you open the door the way a doctor watches a cough.|You've tried opening Mabel's door every way you can think of. She still knows.} {~The bell over the door rings a little flat today.|There's a queue of two, which for Mabel's is a crowd.|A cake is cooling on the counter, out of reach and smug about it.}\ntext [01fd58] = {你一推门，梅布尔抬起头，手已经伸向了一个茶罐。|梅布尔看你推门的样子，就像医生听人咳嗽。|你试过用各种方式推梅布尔的门。她照样看得出来。} {~今天门上的铃铛响得有点走调。|店里排着两个人的队，对梅布尔茶馆来说这已经算拥挤了。|柜台上晾着一块蛋糕，够不着，还一脸得意。}\n# en: A tiny tea shop crammed with tins, the shop-bell over the door still swinging, a shrewd woman behind the counter already reaching for a tin.\nscene [8b72a6] = 塞满茶罐的小茶馆，门上的铃铛还在摇晃，柜台后一位精明的妇人已经伸手去拿茶罐。\n# en: Let Mabel choose your tea\nopt.left.label [d2a63d] = 让梅布尔替你选\n# en: {~She brings you something smoky and says, \"For the week you've had.\" You hadn't told her about the week.|She brings you something with rose in it and won't explain why. It helps.|It's mint. It's always mint when you've been lying to yourself, apparently.}\nopt.left.say.1 [05b49e] = {~她端来一杯带烟熏味的茶，说：“给你这一周喝的。”你可没跟她提过这一周。|她端来一杯有玫瑰味的茶，也不解释为什么。挺管用。|是薄荷茶。据说你自欺欺人的时候，永远是薄荷茶。}\n# en: Fake a cheerful entrance\nopt.right.label [35b845] = 装出高兴的样子进门\n# en: {~She brings you the cheerful tea. It's so good you actually cheer up, which may have been her plan all along.|Mabel looks fooled. Then she adds a biscuit, which means she wasn't.}\nopt.right.say.1 [a472a0] = {~她端来了“开心的茶”。好喝得你真的开心起来——说不定这本来就是她的打算。|梅布尔一副被骗过去的样子。然后她多给了你一块饼干，说明她根本没被骗。}\n# en: {~She brings you a pot labelled, in her own hand, \"for people pretending\".|\"Nice try, dear,\" says Mabel, and brings the strong one.}\nopt.right.say.2 [a03282] = {~她端来一壶茶，壶上是她亲手写的标签：“给装样子的人”。|“想得美，亲爱的。”梅布尔说完，端来了浓的那种。}\n\n@event mabels_window_seat\n# en: The Window Seat\ntitle [15c249] = 靠窗的座位\n# en: {Mabel's window seat looks out over the lake to the castle, and it's free.|Mabel has stopped asking if you're waiting for someone at the window seat.|Your window seat. Mabel puts a cushion on it when she sees you on the quay.} {season == \"winter\": Frost has drawn ferns on the glass.|{season == \"spring\": Blossom keeps landing on the sill.|{~The towers look small from here, almost friendly.|Rain streaks the glass and blurs the towers.}}}\ntext [a61345] = {梅布尔茶馆的靠窗座位能望见湖对岸的城堡，而且正空着。|你坐在靠窗座位时，梅布尔已经不再问你是不是在等人了。|你的靠窗座位。梅布尔一看见你在码头，就往上放个软垫。} {season == \"winter\": 霜在玻璃上画出了蕨叶。|{season == \"spring\": 花瓣不停落在窗台上。|{~从这儿看，塔楼显得小小的，几乎有点亲切。|雨水顺着玻璃流下，把塔楼晕成一片。}}}\n# en: A window seat in a tea shop looking across a still lake to seven distant towers, a teapot and open notes on the sill.\nscene [36406e] = 茶馆的靠窗座位，隔着平静的湖望见远处七座塔，窗台上放着茶壶和摊开的笔记。\n# en: Make one pot last all day\nopt.left.label [abe18d] = 一壶茶坐一整天\n# en: {~You get through a whole chapter on one pot, under Mabel's increasingly pointed looks.|By the third hour the tea is cold and Mabel is dusting very close to your table.|You finish your notes. Mabel says nothing, which is worse.}\nopt.left.say.1 [3095f9] = {~一壶茶你读完了整整一章，梅布尔的眼神越来越意味深长。|到第三个钟头，茶凉了，梅布尔在离你桌子很近的地方掸灰。|你写完了笔记。梅布尔一句话也没说，这更可怕。}\n# en: Order cake with it\nopt.right.label [8d317c] = 再配块蛋糕\n# en: {You've had two of Mabel's three cakes. Today, the third, which is lemon and worth every crown.|Mabel has started cutting the lemon cake before you sit down.|{~Mabel brings the lemon without asking. You eat it slowly while the barges come and go.|The lemon cake, the window, the lake. The work can wait.}}\nopt.right.say.1 [a91f90] = {梅布尔的三种蛋糕你吃过两种了。今天是第三种，柠檬味的，每个克朗都花得值。|梅布尔开始在你坐下之前就切好柠檬蛋糕了。|{~梅布尔没等你开口就端来了柠檬蛋糕。你慢慢吃着，看驳船来来去去。|柠檬蛋糕、窗户、湖。功课可以等一等。}}\n\n@event mabels_forty_teas\n# en: Forty Kinds of Tea\ntitle [e3533b] = 四十种茶\n# en: {friend} wants to try one of Mabel's forty teas, and Mabel insists on choosing. {Today she starts a chart of who has tried what.|Mabel's chart of your friends' teas is pinned behind the counter now; {friend}'s column gets a tick.|Mabel's chart runs to a second sheet. {friend}'s column is the shortest, and Mabel looks at it pointedly.} {~Mabel pretends not to be interested.|Mabel has opinions about the order.}\ntext [438769] = {friend}想尝尝梅布尔的四十种茶之一，而梅布尔坚持由她来选。{今天她开始画一张表，记下谁喝过什么。|梅布尔那张记录你朋友们喝茶的表，如今钉在了柜台后面；{friend}那一栏添了一个勾。|梅布尔的表已经画到第二页了。{friend}那一栏最短，梅布尔意味深长地盯着它。}{~梅布尔假装不感兴趣。|梅布尔对喝的顺序很有意见。}\n# en: Two students at a small tea-shop table crowded with mismatched teapots, one writing in a notebook.\nscene [b50dbf] = 两个学生坐在茶馆的小桌旁，桌上挤满了不成套的茶壶，其中一人在本子上写着什么。\n# en: Pay for both pots\nopt.left.label [76b503] = 两壶都我请\n# en: {~{friend} pronounces today's tea \"bracing\", which means awful. You drink yours anyway.|You pay. Mabel marks you on the chart as a patron of the arts.|{friend} declares it the best tea in the village, then sniffs yours to be sure.}\nopt.left.say.1 [487728] = {~{friend}宣布今天的茶“提神”，意思是难喝。你还是把自己那杯喝完了。|你付了钱。梅布尔在表上把你记作“艺术赞助人”。|{friend}宣布这是全镇最好的茶，然后凑过来闻了闻你那杯，以防万一。}\n# en: Order your usual\nopt.right.label [f9feec] = 点我的老样子\n# en: {~{friend} calls you unadventurous. Your tea is perfect, thank you.|\"Coward,\" says {friend} fondly, over something purple.|You stick to your usual. {friend} drinks something that smells of bonfires and looks betrayed by it.}\nopt.right.say.1 [50470e] = {~{friend}说你不爱冒险。你的茶好得很，谢谢。|“胆小鬼。”{friend}端着一杯紫色的东西，亲昵地说。|你坚持喝老样子。{friend}喝了一杯有篝火味的茶，一脸被背叛的表情。}\n\n@event grimsbys_wrong_book\n# en: Not the Book You Wanted\ntitle [480464] = 不是你要的那本\n# en: {You ask Mr Grimsby for a textbook. He vanishes into the shelves and comes back with a book on beekeeping.|You ask Mr Grimsby for one thing. He brings you another, with great confidence.|Mr Grimsby sees you coming and is already halfway up a ladder, looking for the wrong book.} {~Today's is a cookery book with a whole chapter on eels.|Today's is a history of a town that may not exist.|Today's is in a language neither of you reads.}\ntext [2b8dca] = {你向格林斯比先生要一本教科书。他消失在书架间，拿回来一本讲养蜂的书。|你向格林斯比先生要一样东西。他信心十足地拿来另一样。|格林斯比先生一看见你进门，就已经爬上半截梯子，去找那本错的书了。} {~今天这本是菜谱，有整整一章讲鳗鱼。|今天这本是一座可能并不存在的小镇的历史。|今天这本用的语言你们俩都不认识。}\n# en: A bookshop so crowded the shelves lean together, an elderly bookseller on a ladder triumphantly holding up the wrong book.\nscene [ea591e] = 挤得书架互相倚靠的书店里，一位老书商站在梯子上，得意地举着一本错的书。\n# en: Buy what he found\nopt.left.label [8212ca] = 买下他找到的书\n# en: {~It's useless and wonderful. You read half of it on the barge.|You learn more than you meant to about something you never meant to learn.|There's a pressed flower in it, and a name you don't know on the flyleaf.}\nopt.left.say.1 [7b70a6] = {~它毫无用处，又妙不可言。你在驳船上就读了一半。|你学到了比预想更多的东西，关于一件你从没打算学的事。|书里夹着一朵压干的花，扉页上写着一个你不认识的名字。}\n# en: Search the shelves yourself\nopt.right.label [68f103] = 自己去书架上找\n# en: {~You find it behind a stack of almanacs, exactly where he said it wasn't.|You find it. Mr Grimsby looks wounded and charges you anyway.}\nopt.right.say.1 [34eb8d] = {~你在一摞年鉴后面找到了它，正是他说不在的地方。|你找到了。格林斯比先生一脸受伤，照样收了你的钱。}\n# en: {~A shelf leans on you for support. You hold it up for a long time.|An afternoon, a sneezing fit, and no book.}\nopt.right.say.2 [f7eb7b] = {~一个书架靠在你身上寻求支撑。你扶了它很久。|一个下午，一阵喷嚏，没有书。}\n\n@event grimsbys_damp_barrow\n# en: The Damp Barrow\ntitle [b887f1] = 受潮的书车\n# en: {Outside Grimsby's there's a barrow of secondhand books, a coin apiece, slightly damp.|The barrow has a new sign: \"NO READING WITHOUT BUYING\". Nobody obeys it.|The barrow. You know its smell now: rain, glue and other people's thoughts.} {season == \"winter\": Someone has thrown a sack over it against the sleet.|{~The top book is about knots.|A cat is asleep on the poetry.|A card in the corner says \"NOT RUDE, JUST OLD\".}}\ntext [63330f] = {格林斯比书店门外有一车二手书，一枚硬币一本，略微受潮。|书车上多了块新牌子：“不买不许看。”没人理会。|那辆书车。你现在认得它的味道了：雨水、胶水，还有别人的思绪。} {season == \"winter\": 有人往上面盖了只麻袋挡雨雪。|{~最上面那本讲的是打绳结。|一只猫睡在诗集上。|角落里一张卡片写着：“不是没礼貌，只是旧了。”}}\n# en: A wooden barrow of damp secondhand books outside a crooked bookshop, a student reading one in the drizzle.\nscene [908d11] = 歪斜的书店外，一辆木推车装满受潮的二手书，一个学生在细雨中读着其中一本。\n# en: Buy a damp book\nopt.left.label [428d93] = 买本受潮的书\n# en: {~It dries out wavy and full of someone else's underlinings.|The margins are better than the book. Someone argued with every page.|The last page is a shopping list in faded ink. You find it oddly moving.}\nopt.left.say.1 [2e1f2a] = {~书晾干后皱巴巴的，满是别人画的线。|页边批注比正文还精彩。有人跟每一页都吵过架。|最后一页是一张褪色的购物清单。你莫名有些感动。}\n# en: Read it standing in the rain\nopt.right.label [e908fc] = 站在雨里读\n# en: {~You read until your feet are cold and Mr Grimsby taps on the window.|You read a whole chapter in the drizzle and don't buy it. You feel like a thief and a scholar at once.|A previous owner has written \"Don't believe this bit\" in the margin. You don't.}\nopt.right.say.1 [408375] = {~你一直读到脚冻僵，格林斯比先生敲了敲窗户。|你在细雨里读完一整章，却没买。你觉得自己既像小偷又像学者。|一位前主人在页边写着“这段别信”。你没信。}\n\n@event grimsbys_last_copy\n# en: The Last Copy\ntitle [370a21] = 最后一本\n# en: There's one copy left of the book you need, and {friend} has {their} hand on it too. {You both laugh. Neither of you lets go.|This keeps happening to you at Grimsby's. You're starting to suspect the shop.|Mr Grimsby swears he ordered two. It never arrives as two.} {~\"I saw it first,\" {they} say{s}.|\"We could share,\" {they} offer{s}, unconvincingly.|Mr Grimsby says there might be another in the cellar. There won't.}\ntext [31bff6] = 你要的那本书只剩一本了，而{friend}的手也搭在上面。{你们都笑了。谁也没松手。|在格林斯比书店，这种事总落到你头上。你开始怀疑是这家店在搞鬼。|格林斯比先生发誓他订了两本。到货时从来都只有一本。}{~“我先看到的。”{they}说。|“我们可以合着看。”{they}提议，毫无说服力。|格林斯比先生说地窖里也许还有一本。不会有的。}\n# en: Two students in a cramped bookshop, both holding the same book, neither letting go.\nscene [ec8868] = 拥挤的书店里，两个学生握着同一本书，谁也不肯放手。\n# en: Let {them} have it\nopt.left.label [e68f07] = 让给{them}\n# en: {~{friend} promises to lend it to you \"very soon\". You know what that means.|You let go. {friend} buys you a bun on the way back, which nearly makes up for it.|{friend} reads it on the barge and tells you all the best parts, in the wrong order.}\nopt.left.say.1 [87b7a4] = {~{friend}答应“很快”就借给你。你知道那是什么意思。|你松了手。{friend}回去路上请你吃了个面包，差不多算扯平了。|{friend}在驳船上就读了，把最精彩的部分讲给你听，顺序全是乱的。}\n# en: Haggle for it\nopt.right.label [3e6001] = 讨价还价抢下它\n# en: {~Mr Grimsby takes your side for reasons of his own. {friend} won't speak to you until the quay.|You win it fair and square, and {friend} says so in a voice that means the opposite.}\nopt.right.say.1 [f53bc6] = {~格林斯比先生出于自己的理由站在你这边。{friend}一直到码头都没理你。|你光明正大地赢了，{friend}也这么说，只是语气意思正相反。}\n# en: {~{friend} outbids you, and somehow you end up paying for the tea afterwards.|You argue so long that a third-year buys it while you're both talking.}\nopt.right.say.2 [d8d4d0] = {~{friend}出价比你高，结果不知怎的，事后的茶钱还是你付的。|你们争了太久，一个三年级生趁你们说话时把书买走了。}\n\n@event pell_apothecary_drawers\n# en: A Thousand Drawers\ntitle [e5d6dc] = 一千个抽屉\n# en: {Pell & Daughters has a thousand drawers, each labelled in a different hand.|You find a drawer you'd swear wasn't there last time.|You've read half the labels in the shop. The other half seem to be reading you.} Today it's {~\"Moonwort. Do not hum near.\"|\"Dried courage, low quality.\"|\"Owl pellets (sorted).\"} {season == \"winter\": The shop smells of camphor and wet wool.|The shop smells of mint and something burnt.}\ntext [84e00d] = {佩尔父女药铺有一千个抽屉，每个标签的笔迹都不一样。|你找到一个抽屉，敢发誓上次还不在。|店里的标签你读了一半。另一半好像在读你。}今天是{~“月草。附近勿哼歌。”|“晒干的勇气，品质较次。”|“猫头鹰食丸（已分拣）。”}{season == \"winter\": 店里一股樟脑和湿羊毛的味道。|店里一股薄荷和什么东西烧焦的味道。}\n# en: An apothecary wall of a thousand tiny labelled drawers, brass handles gleaming, a student reading the labels on tiptoe.\nscene [2f6345] = 药铺的一整面墙是上千个贴着标签的小抽屉，黄铜把手闪闪发亮，一个学生踮着脚读标签。\n# en: Buy a pinch of it\nopt.left.label [a72b3a] = 买一小撮\n# en: {~One of the daughters weighs it on scales finer than an eyelash and tells you what it's really for.|It smells of the shop all the way back across the lake.|Old Pell counts the grains himself and warns you about three things you hadn't planned to do.}\nopt.left.say.1 [7c16ef] = {~一个女儿用比睫毛还精细的秤给你称好，告诉你它真正的用途。|回去渡湖的一路上，它都散发着药铺的气味。|老佩尔亲自数了颗粒，还提醒你三件你本来没打算做的事。}\n# en: Just read the labels\nopt.right.label [82b51d] = 只看看标签\n# en: {~Old Pell watches you over his spectacles the whole time, as if labels could be stolen.|You copy a dozen labels into your notebook. A daughter asks if you're planning to open a rival shop.|You learn a great deal. Old Pell sighs every time you touch a handle.}\nopt.right.say.1 [a83406] = {~老佩尔一直从眼镜上方盯着你，好像标签也能被偷走。|你把十几个标签抄进本子。一个女儿问你是不是打算开家对手店。|你学到了很多。你每碰一下把手，老佩尔就叹一口气。}\n\n@event pell_apothecary_queue\n# en: Symptoms in the Queue\ntitle [ab0a8c] = 排队的病症\n# en: {The queue at Pell & Daughters notices your academy robes. A woman shows you her rash.|Word has got round the queue at Pell's that you're \"the young doctor\".|The queue parts for you as if you were a physician. You are not a physician.} {~A farmer has a cough like a door hinge.|A boy has a frog stuck to his hand, and the frog seems content.|An old man wants something for \"the usual\", and won't say what the usual is.}\ntext [787236] = {佩尔药铺排队的人注意到了你的学院袍。一位妇人给你看她的疹子。|佩尔药铺的队伍里传开了，大家都叫你“小大夫”。|队伍为你让开一条路，好像你是医师。你不是医师。} {~一个农夫咳得像吱呀作响的门轴。|一个男孩手上粘着一只青蛙，青蛙看起来挺满足。|一位老人要买治“老毛病”的药，死活不说是什么毛病。}\n# en: A queue of villagers at an apothecary counter, all turning to a student in academy robes.\nscene [87f566] = 药铺柜台前排着一队镇民，全都转向一个身穿学院袍的学生。\n# en: Offer some advice\nopt.left.label [55dc45] = 给点建议\n# en: {~Your advice is sound. One of the daughters nods, which in this shop is a medal.|The farmer tries your remedy and comes back to thank you. Old Pell grumbles about lost trade.}\nopt.left.say.1 [f7c27f] = {~你的建议很靠谱。一个女儿点了点头，在这家店里这就是勋章。|农夫试了你的方子，回来向你道谢。老佩尔抱怨生意被抢了。}\n# en: {~Old Pell corrects you in front of the whole queue, kindly, which is worse.|Your advice would have worked on a horse. A daughter says so.}\nopt.left.say.2 [0fdc23] = {~老佩尔当着全队人的面纠正了你，语气很和善，这更糟。|你的建议拿去治马倒是管用。一个女儿这么说。}\n# en: Study a drawer intently\nopt.right.label [37493b] = 专心研究抽屉\n# en: {~You pretend to be fascinated by a drawer of buttons until the queue moves on.|You say \"Hm\" like Professor Thorne and leave quickly.|You become very interested in the floor. The woman with the rash tells her friend you were rude.}\nopt.right.say.1 [7283ab] = {~你假装对一抽屉纽扣着了迷，直到队伍往前挪走。|你学索恩教授说了声“嗯。”，然后迅速离开。|你突然对地板产生了浓厚兴趣。长疹子的妇人跟朋友说你没礼貌。}\n\n@event pell_apothecary_tonic\n# en: The Daughters' Tonic\ntitle [7c5d2b] = 女儿们的补药\n# en: {One of Pell's daughters has a new tonic and needs someone to try it. Her father must not know.|The daughters have another tonic, and a notebook with your name at the top of a column.|The daughters see you and reach under the counter before you've said hello.} {~This one is green and fizzes.|This one smells of pears and thunder.|This one is clear, which they say is the worrying kind.}\ntext [f8cc18] = {佩尔的一个女儿调了一种新补药，需要有人试喝。绝不能让她父亲知道。|女儿们又调了一种补药，还有个本子，其中一栏最上面写着你的名字。|女儿们一看见你，没等你打招呼就把手伸到了柜台底下。} {~这一瓶是绿色的，还冒泡。|这一瓶闻起来像梨和雷雨。|这一瓶是透明的，她们说这是最让人担心的那种。}\n# en: Two apothecary's daughters leaning over a counter, offering a student a small bottle of something that fizzes.\nscene [ecaffe] = 药铺的两个女儿趴在柜台上，递给学生一小瓶冒着泡的东西。\n# en: Drink it down\nopt.left.label [65d992] = 一口喝下\n# en: {~You feel wonderful, and briefly taller. The daughters take notes.|Your cold vanishes. So, for an hour, does your sense of smell.}\nopt.left.say.1 [45a9dc] = {~你感觉棒极了，还短暂地长高了一点。女儿们记着笔记。|你的感冒消失了。你的嗅觉也消失了一个钟头。}\n# en: {~You hiccup bubbles all the way back to the barge.|Your ears go warm and stay warm. The daughters take very interested notes.}\nopt.left.say.2 [e5b95b] = {~你一路打着冒泡的嗝回到驳船。|你的耳朵发热，而且一直热着。女儿们兴致勃勃地记着笔记。}\n# en: Buy a proper remedy\nopt.right.label [8493b6] = 买副正经药\n# en: {~Old Pell sells you something brown and reliable. The daughters look disappointed in you.|You buy the dull, tested thing. It works, dully.|A daughter wraps it and murmurs that you'll be sorry you missed the green one.}\nopt.right.say.1 [15501e] = {~老佩尔卖给你一种褐色的、靠得住的药。女儿们对你很失望。|你买了那种无聊又经过检验的药。它管用，无聊地管用。|一个女儿一边包药一边嘀咕，说你错过那瓶绿的会后悔的。}\n\n@event bakery_bun_queue\n# en: The Bun Queue\ntitle [b02932] = 买面包的队伍\n# en: {There's a queue outside the bakery before the village is properly awake, and it smells like a promise.|The baker's son counts heads in the bun queue and knows you by your scarf.|The bun queue. You know everyone in it by their order.} {season == \"winter\": Breath steams in the cold.|{season == \"autumn\": Leaves skitter round everyone's ankles.|{~The baker's son is singing off-key.|A goat has joined the queue and nobody has the heart to send it away.}}}\ntext [3390ec] = {小镇还没完全醒来，面包房门外已经排起了队，空气里的香味像一个承诺。|面包师的儿子数着排队的人头，认得你的围巾。|买面包的队伍。队里每个人你都能按他们点的东西认出来。} {season == \"winter\": 呼出的气在冷空气里冒着白雾。|{season == \"autumn\": 落叶在每个人的脚踝边打转。|{~面包师的儿子在跑调地唱歌。|一只山羊也排进了队，谁都不忍心把它赶走。}}}\n# en: A queue of villagers and students outside a little bakery at dawn, steam and the smell of cinnamon pouring out of the door.\nscene [09ed66] = 黎明时一家小面包房外排着镇民和学生，热气和肉桂香从门里涌出来。\n# en: Buy a bag for your corridor\nopt.left.label [fed2ca] = 给走廊的人买一袋\n# en: {~You come back with a warm bag and are, for one morning, the most popular person in your house.|The buns are gone before you've taken your coat off. Someone leaves you a thank-you note on a napkin.|A prefect takes one \"to check it for contraband\". It passes.}\nopt.left.say.1 [c3953d] = {~你带回一袋热面包，整整一个早上成了学院里最受欢迎的人。|你还没脱外套，面包就被抢光了。有人在餐巾上给你留了张感谢条。|一个级长拿走一个，说要“检查有没有违禁品”。检查通过。}\n# en: One bun, eaten warm\nopt.right.label [7ed830] = 趁热吃一个\n# en: {~You eat it on the quay while it's still too hot. Worth every burnt finger.|Butter, cinnamon and the lake in the morning. Nothing has ever been better.|You eat it slowly and think about nothing at all.}\nopt.right.say.1 [46c827] = {~你在码头上趁烫就吃了。烫了手指也值。|黄油、肉桂，还有清晨的湖。再没有比这更好的了。|你慢慢吃着，什么也不想。}\n\n@event bakery_oldest_baker\n# en: The Oldest Baker\ntitle [efae3a] = 最老的面包师\n# en: The oldest of the bakers sits by the ovens, too old to knead and too stubborn to leave. {She asks, as she asks every student, whether the post at the castle is reliable these days.|She asks about the castle post. Her daughter gives you a look that says: just answer.|\"Is the post reliable?\" she asks. You've learned the answer she likes.} {~The ovens tick as they heat.|Flour hangs in the light.|The bell over the door rings, and she looks up, every time.}\ntext [e29790] = 面包师里最年长的那位坐在烤炉边，老得揉不动面，又倔得不肯走。{她问你——就像她问每个学生那样——城堡如今的邮递是否靠得住。|她问起城堡的邮递。她女儿给了你一个眼神：回答就是了。|“邮递靠得住吗？”她问。你已经学会了她爱听的答案。}{~烤炉升温时发出轻轻的嗒嗒声。|面粉悬在光里。|门上的铃铛一响，她就抬头，每次都是。}\n# en: A very old woman in a flour-dusted apron sitting by warm bread ovens, looking up hopefully as the shop door opens.\nscene [3f911a] = 一位系着沾满面粉围裙的老妇人坐在温热的烤炉边，门一开，她便满怀期待地抬起头。\n# en: Sit with her a while\nopt.left.label [4b2f48] = 陪她坐一会儿\n# en: {~She tells you about her grandmother's grandmother, who waited for a letter. She doesn't say how it ended.|She tells you the family has baked here for two hundred years. She makes it sound like a promise kept.|She falls asleep mid-story. Her daughter tucks a bun into your pocket and says thank you.}\nopt.left.say.1 [ee82a6] = {~她讲起她祖母的祖母，那人一直在等一封信。她没说结局。|她说她家在这里烤了两百年面包。她说得像一个守住了的承诺。|她讲着讲着睡着了。她女儿往你口袋里塞了个面包，说了声谢谢。}\n# en: Help knead the dough\nopt.right.label [d60b9f] = 帮忙揉面\n# en: {~The dough fights back. Your arms ache by the time the trays go in.|The baker shows you the turn of the wrist. You don't have it yet.|You knead until your shoulders burn. The first loaf out has your thumbprint on it.}\nopt.right.say.1 [25609a] = {~面团会反抗。等烤盘送进炉子时，你的胳膊已经酸了。|面包师教你手腕那一转。你还没学会。|你揉到肩膀发烫。出炉的第一个面包上留着你的拇指印。}\n\n@event bakery_cooling_rack\n# en: The Cooling Rack\ntitle [5733b6] = 晾面包的窗台\n# en: {Behind the bakery a window stands open, and on the sill a tray of buns is cooling, unwatched.|The cooling tray is back on the sill, nearer the edge this time. The bakers either trust everyone or are testing you.|The tray, the sill, the open window. It's practically an invitation.} {~A crow is eyeing it too.|Two first-years are pretending not to look at it.|Steam curls off the tray into the cold air.}\ntext [343ef1] = {面包房后面有扇窗开着，窗台上晾着一盘面包，没人看着。|那盘面包又晾在窗台上了，这回离边缘更近。面包师们要么谁都信任，要么是在考验你。|托盘、窗台、开着的窗。简直就是邀请。} {~一只乌鸦也盯着它。|两个一年级生正假装没在看它。|热气从托盘上袅袅升进冷空气里。}\n# en: A tray of steaming buns cooling on the sill of an open bakery window, a crow and a student both eyeing it.\nscene [22dd92] = 面包房敞开的窗台上晾着一盘冒热气的面包，一只乌鸦和一个学生都盯着它。\n# en: Lift one off the sill\nopt.left.label [9b35c2] = 从窗台顺一个\n# en: {~You get away with it. It tastes slightly of guilt and mostly of cinnamon.|Clean away. The crow watches you with professional respect.}\nopt.left.say.1 [6a1d7f] = {~你得手了。吃起来有一点点内疚味，主要是肉桂味。|干净利落。乌鸦用同行的敬意看着你。}\n# en: {~A floury hand appears from the window, palm up. You pay double.|Caught by the baker's son, who tells everyone in the queue.}\nopt.left.say.2 [50cf95] = {~一只沾满面粉的手从窗里伸出来，掌心朝上。你付了双倍。|被面包师的儿子逮个正着，他告诉了队伍里的每一个人。}\n# en: Ask for yesterday's\nopt.right.label [eb962f] = 讨点昨天的\n# en: {~The baker gives you a bag of yesterday's for nothing. They're as hard as roof slates.|You get the broken ones free. You eat one and lose a little faith in bread.|\"For the student who asks,\" says the baker, and hands you a stale one with a wink.}\nopt.right.say.1 [b47d93] = {~面包师白送你一袋昨天的面包。硬得像屋顶的石板。|你免费拿到了碎掉的那些。吃了一个，你对面包的信仰动摇了一点。|“给肯开口问的学生。”面包师说着，眨眨眼递给你一个干硬的。}\n\n@event home_cottage_old_room\n# en: Your Old Room\ntitle [8a919e] = 你的旧房间\n# en: {Your old room is exactly as you left it, except smaller.|Someone has been storing apples in your old room.|Your old room has started to feel like a guest room, and you like it less for that.} {season == \"winter\": There's frost on the inside of the window, as always.|The window won't shut properly in the heat, as always.} Downstairs, the house is already awake.\ntext [05514b] = {你的旧房间和你离开时一模一样，只是变小了。|有人在你的旧房间里存了苹果。|你的旧房间开始像一间客房了，你因此不那么喜欢它了。} {season == \"winter\": 窗户内侧结着霜，一如往常。|天热时窗户总关不严，一如往常。}楼下，一家人早已醒了。\n# en: A small childhood bedroom under the eaves, a too-short bed, a window looking out over fields.\nscene [0981a6] = 屋檐下一间小小的儿时卧室，一张太短的床，窗外是田野。\n# en: Sleep until noon\nopt.left.label [7405ea] = 睡到中午\n# en: {~You sleep like a stone. Downstairs, someone has done all your chores, loudly.|You wake to the smell of lunch and a pointed silence.|You sleep the clock round. You feel human, and slightly ashamed.}\nopt.left.say.1 [5ed973] = {~你睡得像块石头。楼下有人把你的家务全干了，动静很大。|你在午饭的香味和意味深长的沉默中醒来。|你睡了个对时。感觉活过来了，也有点惭愧。}\n# en: Get up with the house\nopt.right.label [4c01bf] = 跟全家一起起床\n# en: {final_year: {~You go down in the dark and realise there won't be many more mornings like this.|You memorise the kitchen without meaning to.}|{~You help with the fire. Nobody says much. Nobody needs to.|The creaky stair announces you. Someone hands you a cup before you've asked.}}\nopt.right.say.1 [6b3cab] = {final_year: {~你摸黑下楼，忽然意识到这样的早晨不会再有很多了。|你不知不觉把厨房的样子记在了心里。}|{~你帮着生火。谁都没多说话。也不需要。|吱呀的楼梯报告了你的到来。你还没开口，就有人递给你一杯茶。}}\n\n@event home_cottage_list\n# en: The List on the Table\ntitle [e8c27a] = 桌上的清单\n# en: {Home has remembered that you know where everything is.|A day home, and already a list.|The list. Your name, underlined.} {has(origin_noble): {&The steward wants the ledgers checked.|The steward wants the tenants' letters answered.}|{has(origin_merchant): {&The shop's stock needs counting.|The shop sign needs repainting.}|{has(origin_hedge): {&Your grandmother wants herbs hung to dry.|Your grandmother wants every jar labelled.}|{~The woodpile needs splitting.|The hen house door argues.}}}}\ntext [4de3e8] = {家里没忘，东西放在哪儿你最清楚。|才回家一天，就有了一张清单。|那张清单。你的名字，下面画着线。} {has(origin_noble): {&管家想让你核一核账本。|管家想让你给佃户们回信。}|{has(origin_merchant): {&店里的存货要清点。|店门口的招牌要重新上漆。}|{has(origin_hedge): {&你外婆要你把草药挂起来晾干。|你外婆要你给每个罐子都贴好标签。}|{~柴堆要劈。|鸡舍的门总在跟人较劲。}}}}\n# en: A kitchen table with a long handwritten list of chores weighted down by a teapot, a student in academy robes reading it with dismay.\nscene [a8ef94] = 厨房桌上压着茶壶的一张长长的家务清单，一个穿学院袍的学生沮丧地读着它。\n# en: Work through the whole list\nopt.left.label [6b16eb] = 把清单全干完\n# en: {~By supper the list is done, and someone you love says \"Well.\" It's the highest praise in the house.|It takes all day. You sleep better than you have all term.|You finish, and the list grows a second page in a different hand.}\nopt.left.say.1 [16a1d5] = {~到晚饭时清单全做完了，一位你爱的人说了声“嗯，行。”这是家里的最高赞誉。|干了一整天。你睡得比整个学期都好。|你干完了，清单上又多出了第二页，笔迹不一样。}\n# en: Do it with magic\nopt.right.label [155e10] = 用魔法干\n# en: {~The work does itself while you supervise from a chair. The family is impressed and slightly unnerved.|It works. Your mother says it's cheating, then asks if you can do the gutters.}\nopt.right.say.1 [53274e] = {~活儿自己干完了，你坐在椅子上监工。家人既佩服又有点发毛。|成功了。你母亲说这是作弊，然后问你能不能把檐沟也弄了。}\n# en: {~The spell goes slightly wrong. Something is now upside down that wasn't before.|Soot. Everywhere. Someone laughs until they cry, then hands you a broom.}\nopt.right.say.2 [7e8230] = {~咒语出了点偏差。有样东西现在是倒着的，原来不是。|煤灰。到处都是。有人笑到流泪，然后递给你一把扫帚。}\n\n@event home_cottage_after_supper\n# en: The Same Question\ntitle [2bfa76] = 同一个问题\n# en: After supper, when the dishes are done, someone always asks it. {\"So what will you do, after?\"|\"Still don't know what you'll do after?\" An aunt, this time.|By now you could ask it yourself, in any of their voices, and someone does.} {final_year: This time the answer is close enough to touch.|{~The fire pops.|Nobody pretends not to be listening.|Someone pours more tea to give you time.}}\ntext [4db073] = 晚饭后，碗碟洗完，总有人会问那个问题。{“那你以后打算做什么？”|“还不知道以后做什么吗？”这回是一位姑妈。|如今你自己都能用他们每个人的腔调问出这句话了——而总有人真的问了。}{final_year: 这一次，答案已经近在眼前。|{~炉火噼啪一响。|没人假装不在听。|有人又倒了些茶，给你留点时间。}}\n# en: A family kitchen after supper by candlelight, dishes drying, everyone turned towards one student.\nscene [ff9628] = 晚饭后烛光下的家庭厨房，碗碟正在晾干，所有人都转向一个学生。\n# en: Tell them the truth\nopt.left.label [f3042a] = 说实话\n# en: {~You say you don't know. It's quiet for a moment, then someone says that's allowed.|You tell them what you're afraid of. It sounds smaller said aloud at this table.|The truth makes your mother go quiet, and then hug you, which you weren't ready for.}\nopt.left.say.1 [9e28c9] = {~你说你不知道。安静了一会儿，然后有人说，这也没关系。|你把你害怕的事告诉了他们。在这张桌边说出来，它显得小了些。|实话让你母亲沉默了一下，然后抱住了你，你完全没准备好。}\n# en: Give the polished answer\nopt.right.label [fcba81] = 给个体面的答案\n# en: {~You give the answer you'd give a professor. They're proud. It sits oddly in your chest all night.|You make it sound like a plan. Everyone relaxes except you.|\"Very sensible,\" says an aunt. You feel as if you've told her a story.}\nopt.right.say.1 [4ed89d] = {~你给出了对教授说的那种答案。他们很骄傲。整晚它都在你心里硌着。|你说得像个计划。所有人都松了口气，除了你。|“很稳妥。”一位姑妈说。你觉得自己像给她讲了个故事。}\n\n@event home_fields_long_rows\n# en: The Long Rows\ntitle [e5b4eb] = 长长的田垄\n# en: {You walk the long rows to the field's end, where you used to imagine towers.|You've seen the towers now. The horizon at the end of the rows still pulls.|The walk to the field's end is a habit now. Your feet know it by heart.} {season == \"winter\": The furrows crunch with frost.|The wheat is high and loud with insects.} {~A hare bolts.|A crow follows you at a respectful distance.|Smoke rises from a chimney you can't see.}\ntext [b79905] = {你沿着长长的田垄走到田地尽头，从前你就在那里想象高塔。|你如今见过那些塔了。田垄尽头的地平线依然在召唤。|走到田地尽头已成了习惯。你的脚比你的脑子更熟悉这条路。} {season == \"winter\": 垄沟结着霜，踩上去嘎吱作响。|麦子长得老高，虫鸣一片。} {~一只野兔窜了出去。|一只乌鸦隔着得体的距离跟着你。|远处看不见的烟囱里升起一缕烟。}\n# en: A student walking alone between long furrowed rows towards a low wall at the edge of the fields, the horizon wide and empty.\nscene [edb3f6] = 一个学生独自走在长长的田垄间，走向田地边缘的矮墙，地平线辽阔而空旷。\n# en: Walk the whole boundary\nopt.left.label [259a1e] = 沿田界走一整圈\n# en: {~You come back muddy to the knees and hungry as a first-year.|You walk until your legs ache and your head is finally quiet.|You sit on the low wall where you always sat, and the wind does the thinking.}\nopt.left.say.1 [31d573] = {~你回来时泥到膝盖，饿得像个一年级生。|你一直走到腿酸，脑子终于安静了。|你坐在一直坐的那段矮墙上，让风替你思考。}\n# en: Practise where no one watches\nopt.right.label [b2a1d1] = 没人时练练法术\n# en: {~You set a scarecrow's hat spinning. Nobody sees. It's glorious.|You practise until your hands tingle and the crows give you a wide berth.|You try the spell you keep failing at school. Out here, with nobody watching, it nearly works.}\nopt.right.say.1 [0ad658] = {~你让稻草人的帽子转了起来。没人看见。太棒了。|你练到双手发麻，乌鸦都绕着你飞。|你试了那个在学校总失败的咒语。在这儿，没人看着，它差点就成了。}\n\n@event home_fields_neighbours\n# en: Neighbours' Work\ntitle [332e5b] = 邻居们的活计\n# en: {season == \"winter\": The neighbours are hauling wood before the next snow.|The neighbours are bringing in the hay before the weather turns.} {They wave you over without asking if you're busy.|This time somebody has saved you a pair of gloves.|They've stopped waving. They just hand you the gloves.} {~The neighbour's boy wants to be turned into a frog.|A farmer calls you \"the scholar\", mostly kindly.|Someone has brought a jug of mostly cider.}\ntext [e37feb] = {season == \"winter\": 邻居们赶在下一场雪前运柴。|邻居们赶在变天前收干草。} {他们招手叫你过去，也不问你忙不忙。|这回有人给你留了一副手套。|他们不再招手了，直接把手套递给你。} {~邻居家的男孩想知道你能不能把他变成青蛙。|一个农夫叫你“读书人”，大体是善意的。|有人带来一壶基本算是苹果酒的东西。}\n# en: Neighbours working together at the edge of a field, one waving a student in academy robes over to help.\nscene [962599] = 邻居们在田边一起干活，其中一人招手叫一个穿学院袍的学生过来帮忙。\n# en: Lend a hand\nopt.left.label [d5e12c] = 搭把手\n# en: {~Blisters by noon, and a supper invitation from three households.|You work till dusk. Nobody mentions the academy once, which is restful.|Your back reminds you that books are lighter than anything out here.}\nopt.left.say.1 [9bf08a] = {~中午就起了水泡，还收到三户人家的晚饭邀请。|你一直干到黄昏。没人提起学院一个字，这让人很放松。|你的后背提醒你，书比这儿的任何东西都轻。}\n# en: Bring them all a drink\nopt.right.label [a87c41] = 给大家带点喝的\n# en: {~You come back from the market with a basket and become the most popular person in the field.|They drink to the academy, then to you, then to the weather.|The neighbour's boy asks for a frog again. You give him a biscuit; it's a compromise.}\nopt.right.say.1 [497c04] = {~你从集市提回一篮子东西，成了田里最受欢迎的人。|他们为学院干杯，再为你干杯，再为天气干杯。|邻居家的男孩又要变青蛙。你给了他一块饼干，算是折中。}\n\n@event home_fields_pond\n# en: The Old Pond\ntitle [ed9a38] = 老池塘\n# en: {season == \"winter\": The pond at the bottom of the fields has frozen over, and the local youngsters are daring each other onto the ice.|The pond at the bottom of the fields is warm and green, and the local youngsters are daring each other off the willow.} {You used to be one of them.|This time they dare you too.|They've started daring you first.} {~A dog barks at the whole business.|Someone's little sister is keeping score.|Your old initials are still carved in the willow.}\ntext [209ed4] = {season == \"winter\": 田地尽头的池塘结了冰，镇上的年轻人正互相激将着上冰。|田地尽头的池塘温暖碧绿，镇上的年轻人正互相激将着从柳树上跳下去。} {你从前也是他们中的一个。|这回他们也激你了。|他们现在先激你。} {~一条狗冲着这一切汪汪叫。|有人的小妹妹在记分。|你小时候刻的名字缩写还在柳树上。}\n# en: A pond at the bottom of a field under an old willow, local youngsters daring each other at its edge.\nscene [6031f0] = 田边一棵老柳树下的池塘，镇上的年轻人在岸边互相激将。\n# en: Take the dare\nopt.left.label [6cb2fd] = 接受挑战\n# en: {season == \"winter\": {~You skate across and back to roaring applause from three children and a dog.|You slide the whole length on your back. It counts.}|{~You drop off the willow into the green. It's colder than memory, and better.|A perfect splash. The little sister gives it top marks.}}\nopt.left.say.1 [4d7d33] = {season == \"winter\": {~你滑过去又滑回来，赢得三个孩子和一条狗的欢呼。|你仰面滑完了全程。这也算数。}|{~你从柳树上跳进碧水里。比记忆中更冷，也更好。|完美的水花。小妹妹给了最高分。}}\n# en: {season == \"winter\": {~You go through the ice up to your knees and limp home to a lecture and a fire.|The ice cracks under you and you scramble back, soaked and laughed at.}|{~You belly-flop. The sound carries across three fields. Everyone heard.|You slip off the willow early and land in the reeds, muddy to the ears.}}\nopt.left.say.2 [1ed50b] = {season == \"winter\": {~你踩破冰面，水没到膝盖，一瘸一拐地回家，迎接你的是一顿数落和一炉火。|冰在你脚下裂开，你手脚并用爬回岸上，浑身湿透，被笑了个够。}|{~你肚皮拍水。那声响传遍了三块田。所有人都听见了。|你还没站稳就从柳树上滑了下去，一头栽进芦苇丛，泥糊到耳朵。}}\n# en: Hold the coats\nopt.right.label [aecc6f] = 帮大家看外套\n# en: {~You hold the coats and referee the arguments. Someone has to be the grown-up now.|You keep score and are accused of bias by every side.|Holding the coats, you feel very old and quite content.}\nopt.right.say.1 [bd0b04] = {~你看着外套，调解争吵。现在总得有人当大人了。|你负责记分，结果每一方都说你偏心。|抱着一堆外套，你觉得自己老了，也挺知足。}\n\n@event home_market_inn_crowd\n# en: Outside the Inn\ntitle [d68f78] = 客栈门外\n# en: {The old crowd is outside the inn, exactly where you left them. \"Look who it is,\" someone says. \"The wizard.\"|The old crowd have been rehearsing the wizard joke. It has a second part now.|The old crowd outside the inn. Fewer of them every break: somebody married, somebody moved away.} {season == \"winter\": They're stamping their feet against the cold.|They're sitting on the inn steps in the long light.}\ntext [9c5c3d] = {老伙计们聚在客栈门外，跟你离开时一样。“看看这是谁，”有人说，“大巫师回来啦。”|老伙计们排练过“巫师”那个笑话了。现在还多了第二段。|客栈门外的老伙计们。每个假期都少几个人：有人成了家，有人搬走了。} {season == \"winter\": 他们在寒风里跺着脚。|他们坐在客栈台阶上，沐着长长的日光。}\n# en: A knot of young locals outside a country inn, raising their cups as a student in academy robes walks up.\nscene [a67c59] = 乡间客栈外聚着一群年轻的本地人，看见一个穿学院袍的学生走来，纷纷举起杯子。\n# en: Buy a round\nopt.left.label [ac2c88] = 请大家喝一轮\n# en: {~They drink your health and ask if you can make the beer stronger. You can't. You're asked again.|For an hour it's as if you never left.|Someone tells the story of the time you fell in the duck pond. It has grown since you left.}\nopt.left.say.1 [641be5] = {~他们为你的健康干杯，问你能不能把啤酒变得更烈。你不能。他们又问了一遍。|有那么一个钟头，仿佛你从未离开。|有人讲起你掉进鸭塘的那件事。你走后，这故事又长了不少。}\n# en: Stay for the gossip only\nopt.right.label [b278e5] = 只听八卦\n# en: {~You learn who's courting, who's feuding and whose goat ate whose washing. Somebody mutters that academy folk are tight with a coin.|You keep your crowns in your pocket and get very well informed.|They notice you didn't buy a round. They notice everything.}\nopt.right.say.1 [34b92e] = {~你知道了谁在恋爱、谁在结仇、谁家的山羊吃了谁家晾的衣服。有人嘀咕学院的人真抠门。|你把克朗留在兜里，消息却灵通得很。|他们注意到你没请客。他们什么都注意得到。}\n\n@event home_market_charm_seller\n# en: The Charm Seller\ntitle [dc4b46] = 卖护身符的人\n# en: {A stall in the market is selling \"genuine Lumenhold charms, blessed in the seven towers\".|The charm seller has new charms and a bigger sign. The claims have grown to match.|The charm seller sees your robes and starts packing up, then thinks better of it.} {has(origin_merchant): You know that markup. You were raised on it.|{~A grandmother is buying one for luck.|The charms are bent nails tied with ribbon.|One of them is, worryingly, glowing.}}\ntext [9b36a4] = {集市上有个摊子在卖“正宗流明堡护身符，七塔加持”。|卖护身符的人换了新货，招牌也更大了。说辞跟着一起膨胀。|卖护身符的人看见你的学院袍，开始收摊，又改了主意。} {has(origin_merchant): 你认得那种加价。你就是在这种加价里长大的。|{~一位老奶奶正买一个求好运。|那些护身符是系着丝带的弯钉子。|其中一个居然在发光，叫人不安。}}\n# en: A market stall hung with ribbons and trinkets under a hand-painted sign about academy blessings, a grandmother reaching for her purse.\nscene [2e47e6] = 挂满丝带和小饰物的集市摊位，手绘招牌上吹嘘着学院加持，一位老奶奶正伸手去掏钱袋。\n# en: Call out the fakes\nopt.left.label [2cfc0c] = 揭穿假货\n# en: {~The crowd turns on him. A grandmother gets her coin back and gives you a cake. The seller gives you a look.|He folds, and offers you a share of the trade. You decline. He seems genuinely surprised.}\nopt.left.say.1 [2188b8] = {~人群倒向了你这边。一位老奶奶拿回了钱，送你一块蛋糕。摊主瞪了你一眼。|他认输了，提出分你一份生意。你拒绝了。他似乎真心感到意外。}\n# en: {~The crowd takes his side. \"Academy snob,\" someone says, buying two.|He out-talks you in front of everyone. You'd admire it if it were happening to someone else.}\nopt.left.say.2 [a96e0a] = {~人群站在他那边。“学院来的势利眼。”有人说着，买了两个。|他当着所有人的面把你说得哑口无言。要是发生在别人身上，你会佩服的。}\n# en: Buy one for an aunt\nopt.right.label [7ab465] = 给姑妈买一个\n# en: {~Your aunt will love it. It's a bent nail. She will love it anyway.|You buy one. It's useless, and the old woman beside you beams at you with approval.|You buy one that glows a little. You decide not to think about why.}\nopt.right.say.1 [18ad4e] = {~你姑妈会喜欢的。那是根弯钉子。她照样会喜欢。|你买了一个。毫无用处，但旁边的老妇人对你赞许地笑了。|你买了一个微微发光的。你决定不去想它为什么发光。}\n\n@event home_market_post_office\n# en: Letters to the Castle\ntitle [b5b8cc] = 寄往城堡的信\n# en: {The town's post office is the size of a cupboard, and its clerk has never posted anything to a magic academy.|The clerk has started weighing your letters with ceremony.|The clerk reaches for the castle stamp before you speak.} {season == \"winter\": The window is fogged; it smells of sealing wax.|Flies wander in to read the notices.} {final_year: Soon you'll write to the castle from elsewhere.|You think about who at the castle you miss.}\ntext [69c4a9] = {镇上的邮局只有柜子那么大，邮务员从没往魔法学院寄过东西。|邮务员开始郑重其事地称你的信了，像举行仪式。|你还没开口，邮务员就去拿城堡邮戳了。} {season == \"winter\": 窗户上蒙着雾气，有股封蜡的味道。|苍蝇飞进来读告示。} {final_year: 很快，你就要从别处给城堡写信了。|你想着城堡里那些你想念的人。}\n# en: A post office the size of a cupboard, a clerk ceremoniously weighing a stack of letters addressed to a castle on a lake.\nscene [8ee65f] = 柜子大小的邮局里，邮务员郑重地称着一摞寄往湖上城堡的信。\n# en: Write to everyone\nopt.left.label [0c3aa2] = 给每个人写信\n# en: {~You write until your hand cramps. The clerk sighs at the stack.|You draw the cottage for someone who has never seen one.|You start one letter with \"I miss\" and decide to leave it that way.}\nopt.left.say.1 [5c035e] = {~你写到手抽筋。邮务员对着那一摞叹气。|你给一个从没见过乡下小屋的人画了一幅你家的小屋。|你一封信开头写了“我想念”，决定就这样留着。}\n# en: Write the essay you owe\nopt.right.label [4e919a] = 写欠下的论文\n# en: {~You write the essay on the clerk's counter, between parcels. It's not bad.|You post an essay to a professor. The clerk asks if it'll get you into trouble. Probably.|You finish it and post it. It's the dullest thing the clerk has ever weighed.}\nopt.right.say.1 [d23b12] = {~你在邮局柜台上、包裹之间写完了论文。还不赖。|你把论文寄给一位教授。邮务员问这会不会给你惹麻烦。大概会。|你写完寄出。这是邮务员称过的最无聊的东西。}\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/slice_2a.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/slice_2a.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event slice_ward_lesson\n# en: The First Ward\ntitle [bb9ea0] = 第一道护界\n# en: Professor Thorne holds up a hand and the lamplight bends around it into a pale shell. \"A ward,\" he says. \"{~It stops one blow.|One blow. That's all it ever stops.} Learn it properly or not at all.\"\ntext [df5c56] = 索恩教授抬起一只手，灯光绕着它弯成一层苍白的护壳。“护界，”他说，“{~它能挡一击。|一击。它永远只挡得住一击。}要么好好学，要么别学。”\n# en: An office lit by one lamp, an old professor holding a pale shell of light between his palms like a soap bubble.\nscene [86947c] = 一间只点着一盏灯的办公室，一位老教授在两掌之间托着一层苍白的光壳，像一个肥皂泡。\n# en: Practise until it holds\nopt.left.label [ca9f8f] = 练到它撑得住为止\n# en: By midnight your shell holds for a full breath. Thorne says \"Hm,\" which from him is a standing ovation.\nopt.left.say.1 [1c452f] = 到午夜，你的护壳能撑满一整口气。索恩说了声“嗯”——从他嘴里出来，这相当于全场起立鼓掌。\n# en: Ask about the theory first\nopt.right.label [e6ac23] = 先问问原理\n# en: He talks for an hour about lattices and intent. You understand perhaps a third of it. \"Come back when the third is a half,\" he says.\nopt.right.say.1 [75d35a] = 他讲了一个小时的晶格与意图。你大概听懂了三分之一。“等这三分之一变成一半再来，”他说。\n\n@event slice_candle_flame\n# en: Borrowed Fire\ntitle [f667df] = 借来的火\n# en: Lyra watches you fail to light a candle for the third time. \"You're asking it,\" she says. \"Fire doesn't want to be asked. {rel(lyra) >= 12: Here — watch my hand.|Watch, if you can keep up.}\"\ntext [8ed13c] = 莱拉看着你第三次没能点燃蜡烛。“你在请求它，”她说，“火不喜欢被请求。{rel(lyra) >= 12: 来——看我的手。|看好了，如果你跟得上的话。}”\n# en: A crimson-haired student balancing a flame on one fingertip while another student squints at a sputtering candle.\nscene [7237b8] = 一个绯红头发的学生指尖托着一簇火苗，另一个学生眯眼盯着一根噼啪作响的蜡烛。\n# en: Watch her hand\nopt.left.label [8f7d80] = 看她的手\n# en: She doesn't explain. She just does it, slowly, twice. On the third try your candle catches, and so, very briefly, does your sleeve.\nopt.left.say.1 [59ceef] = 她不解释，只是慢慢地做了两遍。第三次，你的蜡烛点着了——你的袖子也短暂地着了一下。\n# en: Challenge her to teach you properly\nopt.right.label [02abfd] = 激她好好教你\n# en: You hold your own long enough that she stops and actually explains. \"Fine,\" she says. \"You'll do.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [24afbf] = 你撑得够久，久到她停下来真的开始讲解。“行吧，”她说，“你还凑合。”\n# en: She wins, but she shows you the trick on the way out, as if it were nothing.\nopt.right.say.2 [101e7f] = 她赢了，但离开时顺手把诀窍演示给你看，仿佛那不值一提。\n# en: You end up on the floor with singed eyebrows. Lyra steps over you. \"Ask better,\" she says.\nopt.right.say.3 [a982db] = 你最后躺在地上，眉毛被燎焦了。莱拉从你身上跨过去。“下次问得好一点，”她说。\n\n@event slice_stray_cold\n# en: The Drift\ntitle [b9d28f] = 寒流\n# en: Cold drifts down the corridor like a tide coming in, and the torches lean away from it. {spell_level(ward) > 0: Your ward prickles at your fingertips.|You have nothing to hold it back.}\ntext [74bc38] = 寒意像涨潮一样漫过走廊，火把纷纷向一旁倾斜。{spell_level(ward) > 0: 你的护界在指尖隐隐刺痛。|你没有任何东西能挡住它。}\n# en: A corridor at night where frost spreads across the flagstones toward a student holding a candle.\nscene [e9d25c] = 夜里的一条走廊，霜正沿着石板向一个举着蜡烛的学生蔓延。\n# en: Run for the stairs\nopt.left.label [3e7200] = 往楼梯跑\n# en: You make the stairs with frost in your hair. Behind you, the corridor sighs and is just a corridor again.\nopt.left.say.1 [498ddb] = 你冲上楼梯时，头发里结满了霜。身后的走廊叹了口气，又变回了普通的走廊。\n# en: Stand and study it\nopt.right.label [0a0a0c] = 站住，研究它\n# en: It's not weather. It's attention: something under the lake, looking. You write down everything before your hands warm up.\nopt.right.say.1 [26fe26] = 这不是天气，是注视：湖底的某样东西，在看。你趁双手还没回暖，把一切都记了下来。\n# en: You learn exactly one thing, which is how cold cold can be. Sister Amaranth wraps you in four blankets.\nopt.right.say.2 [46b7a7] = 你只学到了一件事：冷能冷到什么地步。阿玛兰斯修女给你裹了四层毯子。\n# en: Raise a ward\nopt.up.label [d5a702] = 撑起护界\n# en: Your ward flares, pale and steady. The cold breaks against it and slides away, the way a wave gives up on a rock.\nopt.up.say.1 [e39cb8] = 你的护界亮起，苍白而稳定。寒流撞上它，滑开了，像浪头对一块礁石放弃了。\n\n@event slice_late_lamps\n# en: The Last Lamp\ntitle [5ba6ee] = 最后一盏灯\n# en: {&The library is emptying.|The library is emptying again.|The library empties around you, as usual.} Footnote, the library cat, is asleep on the book you need, and the last lamp is yours if you want it.\ntext [1008ad] = {&图书馆渐渐空了。|图书馆又渐渐空了。|图书馆照例在你身边空了下来。}图书馆的猫“脚注”趴在你要的那本书上睡着了，最后一盏灯，你想要就是你的。\n# en: The library at closing time, one desk still lit, a cat asleep on an open book.\nscene [6a380e] = 闭馆时的图书馆，只剩一张书桌还亮着，一只猫睡在摊开的书上。\n# en: Keep the lamp\nopt.left.label [b4ddff] = 守着这盏灯\n# en: {~You read until the words swim.|You read until Footnote leaves in protest.|You read until the night porter coughs pointedly.}\nopt.left.say.1 [76a6fa] = {~你读到字都开始游动。|你读到“脚注”愤然离席。|你读到守夜人意味深长地咳嗽。}\n# en: Go to bed\nopt.right.label [2fa817] = 回去睡觉\n# en: You sleep like a stone. The book will still be there. So, probably, will Footnote.\nopt.right.say.1 [11b132] = 你睡得像块石头。书还会在那里。“脚注”大概也是。\n\n@event slice_quarrel\n# en: A Quarrel in the Snow\ntitle [1902ab] = 雪中争执\n# en: Kael and Lyra are facing each other in the courtyard, and the snow between them is half melted, half frozen. \"Say it again,\" Lyra says. Kael doesn't. He just looks at you.\ntext [092495] = 凯尔和莱拉在庭院里对峙，两人之间的雪一半融化、一半冻结。“再说一遍，”莱拉说。凯尔没有说。他只是看向你。\n# en: Two students facing each other in a snowy courtyard, one breathing frost, the other with sparks at her fingertips.\nscene [38c0d1] = 积雪的庭院里，两个学生面对面站着，一个呼出寒霜，另一个指尖迸着火星。\n# en: Step between them\nopt.left.label [f242e3] = 站到他们中间\n# en: Lyra's sparks catch your sleeve, Kael's frost puts it out. They both stare at you. The fight, somehow, is over.\nopt.left.say.1 [5ee694] = 莱拉的火星燎到你的袖子，凯尔的霜又把它扑灭。两人都盯着你。不知怎么，这场架就这么结束了。\n# en: Walk past\nopt.right.label [d5359c] = 径直走过\n# en: Behind you, something cracks: ice, or temper. At dinner they sit at opposite ends of the hall.\nopt.right.say.1 [d025e8] = 身后有什么裂开了：是冰，还是脾气。晚饭时，他们坐在大厅的两头。\n\n@event slice_burned_letter\n# en: Ashes in the Grate\ntitle [4b34a6] = 壁炉里的灰\n# en: Lyra is feeding a letter into the common-room fire, a corner at a time. Black seal. She doesn't look round. \"It says 'well done',\" she says. \"It doesn't. I'm checking whether it burns better if I pretend.\"\ntext [847047] = 莱拉正把一封信一角一角地喂进公共休息室的壁炉。黑色封蜡。她没有回头。“信上写着‘做得好’，”她说，“其实没有。我在试试，假装有的话它会不会烧得更旺。”\n# en: A fireplace where the corner of a letter with a black seal is curling into flame.\nscene [e9282e] = 一座壁炉，一封盖着黑色封蜡的信的一角正卷进火焰里。\n# en: Sit with her\nopt.left.label [654e0a] = 陪她坐着\n# en: You watch it burn together. \"She's never said it,\" Lyra says, to the fire. \"Not once.\" You don't say anything, and that seems to be right.\nopt.left.say.1 [437064] = 你们一起看着它烧完。“她从没说过，”莱拉对着火说，“一次都没有。”你什么也没说，而这似乎正是对的。\n# en: Make a joke\nopt.right.label [5f1ec3] = 开个玩笑\n# en: She almost smiles. Then she puts the rest of the letter in at once and leaves you with the smoke.\nopt.right.say.1 [282f1d] = 她差点笑了。然后她把剩下的信一股脑儿丢进火里，留你和烟待在一起。\n\n@event slice_hollow_whisper\n# en: A Voice in the Water\ntitle [b31d19] = 水中的声音\n# en: At the lakeshore your reflection speaks before you do. \"You're running low,\" it says, kindly. \"{mana < 15: I could fill you up.|I could make you stronger.} Just say yes. Saying yes costs nothing.\"\ntext [f94671] = 在湖岸边，你的倒影先于你开口。“你快见底了，”它温和地说，“{mana < 15: 我可以把你填满。|我可以让你更强。}只要说‘好’。说‘好’什么都不花。”\n# en: A student kneeling at the edge of a still lake at night, their reflection smiling when they are not.\nscene [4bb1f8] = 夜里，一个学生跪在平静的湖边，水中的倒影在笑，而他自己并没有笑。\n# en: Say yes\nopt.left.label [c73ccb] = 说“好”\n# en: Cold pours into you, generous and endless. When you stand, your reflection is a little slower to stand with you.\nopt.left.say.1 [3ad748] = 寒意涌进你的身体，慷慨而无穷无尽。你站起来时，倒影慢了一拍才跟着站起来。\n# en: Walk away\nopt.right.label [96da1a] = 转身离开\n# en: The reflection's smile stays on the water after you've gone. You don't look back. You can feel it not minding.\nopt.right.say.1 [8bbf02] = 你走后，倒影的笑容还留在水面上。你没有回头。你能感觉到它并不在意。\n\n@event slice_market_haggle\n# en: The Spice Stall\ntitle [8682c8] = 香料摊\n# en: The stall-holder in the village market wants thirty crowns for a jar of starpepper. Mabel swears by it for colds. The stall-holder swears by his prices.\ntext [27ec12] = 小镇集市上的摊主要三十克朗一罐星辰胡椒。梅布尔发誓它治感冒有奇效。摊主则发誓他的价钱童叟无欺。\n# en: A crowded village market stall heaped with jars of glowing spices and a stall-holder with an enormous moustache.\nscene [ba55f9] = 拥挤的小镇集市摊位，堆满发光的香料罐，摊主留着一大把胡子。\n# en: Haggle\nopt.left.label [90048b] = 讨价还价\n# en: Eight crowns and a compliment on your persistence. Mabel says you've been robbed, but only slightly.\nopt.left.say.1 [ec3b10] = 八克朗，外加一句夸你有毅力。梅布尔说你被坑了，不过只坑了一点点。\n# en: Fifteen crowns. He throws in a free sneeze's worth of pepper, which you did not want.\nopt.left.say.2 [93bde1] = 十五克朗。他还白送你一个喷嚏的胡椒量——你并不想要。\n# en: You pay full price and somehow agree to a second jar. He waves you off like a nephew.\nopt.left.say.3 [43a450] = 你付了全价，还不知怎么答应再买一罐。他像送别侄子一样挥手送你走。\n# en: Leave it\nopt.right.label [3e2fed] = 算了\n# en: You walk on. You catch a cold the next week, out of spite.\nopt.right.say.1 [128e73] = 你走开了。下周你就感冒了，纯属赌气。\n\n@event slice_wager\n# en: The Tower Wager\ntitle [013b2f] = 钟楼赌约\n# en: Half the common room is betting on whether you can climb the clock tower before the bell. Corvin is holding the purse, which is either reassuring or not.\ntext [3371c6] = 半个公共休息室的人在赌你能不能在钟响前爬上钟楼。钱袋在科尔文手里——这让人放心，也可能不。\n# en: A knot of students around a table where coins and a pocket watch are being stacked as bets.\nscene [217f9f] = 一群学生围着一张桌子，桌上叠着当赌注的硬币和一块怀表。\n# en: Climb\nopt.left.label [2c7b2a] = 爬\n# en: {~You make it with a heartbeat to spare.|The bell rings as your hand touches the top.} Whatever happens next, they'll tell it differently.\nopt.left.say.1 [cc8c91] = {~你提前一个心跳的工夫登了顶。|你的手碰到塔顶的那一刻，钟声响了。}不管接下来发生什么，他们每个人讲的版本都会不一样。\n# en: Decline politely\nopt.right.label [c5820d] = 礼貌地拒绝\n# en: The crowd groans. Corvin pockets the purse thoughtfully and you decide not to ask.\nopt.right.say.1 [3f87ed] = 人群一片哀叹。科尔文若有所思地把钱袋揣进兜里，你决定不问。\n\n@event slice_frost_duel\n# en: Frost Against Fire\ntitle [63f7ba] = 霜与火\n# en: Kael asks for a practice duel, which for him is an astonishing number of words. \"Not to win,\" he adds. \"I want to see what you do when it's cold.\"\ntext [4a4fd1] = 凯尔请你和他练一场决斗——对他来说，这已经是惊人的字数。“不是为了赢，”他补充道，“我想看看天冷的时候你会怎么做。”\n# en: A duelling ring at dawn, frost spreading from one side and a faint warmth glowing from the other.\nscene [8d420a] = 黎明时分的决斗场，一边蔓延着寒霜，另一边泛着微弱的暖光。\n# en: Duel him straight\nopt.left.label [a7dfd1] = 正面和他决斗\n# en: You win by a whisker, breathing steam. Kael bows, and his hands, for once, are warm when he shakes yours.\nopt.left.say.1 [2529ff] = 你以毫厘之差获胜，呼出白汽。凯尔鞠了一躬，和你握手时，他的手难得是暖的。\n# en: A draw, of sorts: you're both on the ground. He laughs, a short surprised sound, and helps you up.\nopt.left.say.2 [a850c0] = 算是平局：你们俩都倒在地上。他笑了，短促而意外的一声，然后把你拉了起来。\n# en: He wins, gently. \"You stopped when it got cold,\" he says. \"Don't.\"\nopt.left.say.3 [9548e4] = 他赢了，赢得很温和。“天一冷，你就停下了，”他说，“别停。”\n# en: Decline; offer him tea instead\nopt.right.label [3c2e56] = 婉拒，改请他喝茶\n# en: He thinks about this for a long time. \"Tea,\" he says. It freezes in his cup. You make him another.\nopt.right.say.1 [c0affc] = 他想了很久。“茶，”他说。茶在他杯子里冻住了。你又给他泡了一杯。\n# en: Answer with fire\nopt.up.label [589d8f] = 以火回应\n# en: Your flame meets his frost in a column of steam that fills the ring. When it clears, Kael is smiling at you, properly, for the first time.\nopt.up.say.1 [62beaf] = 你的火焰迎上他的寒霜，一柱蒸汽填满了整个决斗场。散去时，凯尔正对你微笑——第一次真正地微笑。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/slice_2b.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/slice_2b.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event evo_first_spark\n# en: The First Spark\ntitle [5b0523] = 第一缕火花\n# en: Evocation I begins with a single instruction from the lectern: \"Make heat. Not fire. Heat.\" Half the class makes nothing. One student makes a small, apologetic fire.\ntext [db1b7b] = 塑能术 I 以讲台上的一句指令开场：“造热。不是火。是热。”半个班什么都没造出来。有一个学生造出了一小团满怀歉意的火。\n# en: A lecture hall with scorch marks on every desk, students holding their palms up as if testing for rain.\nscene [47f33f] = 每张课桌上都有焦痕的讲堂，学生们摊开手掌，像在试探下没下雨。\n# en: Aim for heat, carefully\nopt.left.label [ac4d80] = 小心地只求热\n# en: Your palm warms like a stone left in the sun. The lecturer nods, which is better than most get.\nopt.left.say.1 [fe739e] = 你的掌心暖得像晒过太阳的石头。讲师点了点头，这已经胜过大多数人了。\n# en: Aim for fire, boldly\nopt.right.label [639beb] = 大胆地求火\n# en: A neat little flame, exactly where you meant it. The lecturer writes your name down. Approvingly, you hope.\nopt.right.say.1 [013ce4] = 一小簇利落的火苗，正好出现在你想要的位置。讲师记下了你的名字。你希望是带着赞许的。\n# en: Your sleeve learns more about fire than you do. The lecture continues around the smell.\nopt.right.say.2 [090858] = 你的袖子比你更懂火了。课在这股焦味里继续。\n\n@event evo_puddle_drill\n# en: Puddle Drill\ntitle [3a9384] = 水洼操练\n# en: Tidecalling half of the course: a puddle each, and the task of making it stand up and stay up. Yours trembles, considers it, and lies back down.\ntext [4ca6c9] = 课程的唤潮术部分：每人一个水洼，任务是让它站起来并保持站立。你的那个颤了颤，考虑了一下，又躺了回去。\n# en: Students crouched over puddles in a stone courtyard, trying to make the water stand up.\nscene [83fa0c] = 学生们蹲在石砌庭院的水洼前，试图让水站起来。\n# en: Practise until it stands\nopt.left.label [1dfa4e] = 练到它站起来\n# en: By the end of the hour your puddle stands like a sulky child. It counts.\nopt.left.say.1 [106f8b] = 一节课快结束时，你的水洼像个赌气的孩子一样站着。算数。\n# en: Copy the student next to you\nopt.right.label [ead31d] = 照搬旁边同学的做法\n# en: You learn their trick, and also that they hum while they cast. You hum too. It works, inexplicably.\nopt.right.say.1 [c0e062] = 你学到了对方的诀窍，还发现他们施法时会哼歌。你也哼了。莫名其妙地，管用。\n\n@event evo_misfire\n# en: Misfire\ntitle [3350b4] = 失手\n# en: Someone's tidecalling meets someone else's flame in the middle of the room. The hall fills with scalding steam and twenty students who all have opinions.\ntext [e661e8] = 某人的唤潮术在房间正中撞上了另一个人的火焰。讲堂里灌满了滚烫的蒸汽，还有二十个各有意见的学生。\n# en: A lecture hall filling with steam after a spell meets a bucket of water at the wrong moment.\nscene [ffbe1f] = 一道咒语在错误的时刻撞上一桶水，讲堂里蒸汽弥漫。\n# en: Shield the front row\nopt.left.label [1e4bc0] = 护住前排\n# en: You take the worst of it on your sleeves. The front row looks at you the way people look at lifeboats.\nopt.left.say.1 [d4298c] = 最烫的那一下你用袖子挡了。前排的人看你的眼神，就像看救生艇。\n# en: Get out and open the windows\nopt.right.label [eb7ddc] = 出去开窗\n# en: The steam pours out into the courtyard and becomes, briefly, a very small cloud with a grudge.\nopt.right.say.1 [3f970d] = 蒸汽涌进庭院，短暂地变成了一朵很小、很记仇的云。\n\n@event evo_tide_wall\n# en: The Tide Wall\ntitle [f1844c] = 潮墙\n# en: Evocation II takes the class to the lakeshore. The exercise: raise a wall of water and hold it while a classmate walks past behind it. Your classmate looks nervous. So do you.\ntext [5eb5bd] = 塑能术 II 把全班带到湖岸。练习：升起一道水墙并撑住，让一位同学从墙后走过。那位同学看起来很紧张。你也是。\n# en: A student holding back a wall of lake water on the shore while classmates watch from behind them.\nscene [23ccc0] = 一名学生在岸边挡住一道湖水筑成的墙，同学们在后面看着。\n# en: Hold it steady\nopt.left.label [d6f0e9] = 稳稳撑住\n# en: The wall holds. Your classmate walks past dry and amazed. You let it down gently, which is the hard part.\nopt.left.say.1 [240cd4] = 水墙撑住了。同学干干爽爽地走过去，满脸惊叹。你把墙轻轻放下——这才是最难的部分。\n# en: The wall holds until it doesn't. Your classmate is very wet and very gracious about it.\nopt.left.say.2 [b2a1a9] = 水墙撑住了，直到撑不住。同学浑身湿透，却对此十分大度。\n# en: Ask to go second\nopt.right.label [208e6d] = 请求第二个上\n# en: You watch three people do it first and learn from all of their mistakes.\nopt.right.say.1 [2b89b8] = 你看着三个人先做，把他们的错误统统学了个遍。\n\n@event evo_lattice\n# en: The Flame Lattice\ntitle [7d30cd] = 火焰格网\n# en: Fire woven into a lattice, each strand holding the others up. The lecturer says the founders built wards like this. The lecturer also says you have twenty minutes.\ntext [954a4b] = 火被编成格网，每一股都撑着其他几股。讲师说创始者当年就是这样筑护界的。讲师还说你有二十分钟。\n# en: Interlocking threads of fire hanging in the air of a lecture hall like a glowing net.\nscene [827db1] = 交错的火线悬在讲堂半空，像一张发光的网。\n# en: Build it slowly\nopt.left.label [03433a] = 慢慢搭\n# en: Your lattice is small and ugly and holds perfectly. The lecturer says \"Founder-ish,\" which you choose to take as praise.\nopt.left.say.1 [068b5a] = 你的格网又小又丑，却撑得完美无缺。讲师说“有点创始者的味道”，你决定把它当成表扬。\n# en: Build it big\nopt.right.label [33df52] = 搭个大的\n# en: {~It's magnificent for nine seconds.|It holds just long enough for everyone to see it.} Then the strands find each other and the whole thing sighs into sparks.\nopt.right.say.1 [180491] = {~它壮丽了整整九秒。|它撑得刚好够每个人看清。}然后火线彼此找到对方，整个东西叹息一声，化作火星。\n\n@event evo_storm_pairing\n# en: Storm Pairing\ntitle [6e518e] = 风暴搭档\n# en: Advanced Evocation pairs students for a storm: one calls the rain, the other the lightning, and neither is allowed to let go first. You're paired with {partner}.\ntext [bcffa7] = 高等塑能术让学生两两搭档召唤风暴：一人唤雨，一人引雷，谁都不许先松手。你的搭档是{partner}。\n# en: Two students on a windswept tower top, one calling rain, the other calling lightning, the sky between them churning.\nscene [0f5fd7] = 狂风中的塔顶上，两名学生一个唤雨一个引雷，两人之间的天空翻涌不息。\n# en: Call the lightning\nopt.left.label [26bb28] = 引雷\n# en: The storm answers both of you at once. {partner} laughs into the wind, soaked and astonished.\nopt.left.say.1 [2fb5cc] = 风暴同时回应了你们俩。{partner}迎着风大笑，浑身湿透，满脸惊愕。\n# en: The storm answers both of you at once. Lyra looks at you as though you've finally said something worth hearing.\nopt.left.say.1@lyra [e5dc7b] = 风暴同时回应了你们俩。莱拉看着你，仿佛你终于说了句值得一听的话。\n# en: The lightning goes where it likes. You both spend an afternoon in the infirmary, comparing burns.\nopt.left.say.2 [f7819a] = 闪电想去哪儿就去哪儿。你们俩在医务室待了一下午，互相比较烧伤。\n# en: Call the rain, and let them lead\nopt.right.label [f3854c] = 唤雨，让对方主导\n# en: You hold the rain steady while {partner} draws the lightning down. Afterwards {they} thank{s} you for not trying to steal it.\nopt.right.say.1 [be557c] = 你稳住雨幕，{partner}把闪电引了下来。之后{they}谢谢你没有抢风头。\n\n@event evo_final_practical\n# en: The Final Practical\ntitle [035f37] = 期末实践考\n# en: The last practical of Advanced Evocation: melt an ice dummy without touching the floor around it. Professor Thorne is on the panel, and he is not blinking.\ntext [9b1d08] = 高等塑能术的最后一场实践考：融化一个冰人偶，不许碰到周围的地面。索恩教授在考官席上，眼都不眨。\n# en: An examination hall cleared of desks, a single student facing three examiners and a target dummy made of ice.\nscene [49ac8b] = 清空了课桌的考场，一名学生面对三位考官和一个冰做的靶子人偶。\n# en: Fire, precisely\nopt.left.label [c25e11] = 用火，精准地\n# en: The ice runs off the dummy and not a drop touches the floor. Thorne says \"Hm,\" and writes something underlined.\nopt.left.say.1 [0fdd37] = 冰从人偶上流下，没有一滴碰到地面。索恩说了声“嗯”，写下一行带下划线的字。\n# en: The dummy melts, and so does a fair part of the floor. Thorne writes something with a question mark.\nopt.left.say.2 [20fe15] = 人偶融了，地板也融了不少。索恩写下一行带问号的字。\n# en: Water, patiently\nopt.right.label [21c7ce] = 用水，耐心地\n# en: You draw the cold out of the ice with water instead of fire. It takes twice as long. The panel watches every minute.\nopt.right.say.1 [8c406a] = 你用水而不是火把冰里的寒意抽了出来。花了两倍的时间。考官们一分钟都没错过。\n\n@event duel_first_practice\n# en: Footwork\ntitle [86c7d4] = 步法\n# en: Lyra runs practice like a general. \"Footwork,\" she says. \"If your feet are wrong, your spell is a rumour.\" She pairs you with a dummy called Gerald the Second.\ntext [ef78a6] = 莱拉像将军一样主持练习。“步法，”她说，“步法错了，你的咒语就只是个传闻。”她给你配了一个叫“杰拉尔德二世”的假人。\n# en: A duelling hall with chalk circles on the floor and practice dummies with names painted on their chests.\nscene [f28b25] = 地上画着粉笔圈的决斗厅，练习假人胸口上漆着名字。\n# en: Drill footwork\nopt.left.label [7dee9f] = 练步法\n# en: Two hours of stepping in circles. Gerald the Second wins every round. Your feet, however, are now correct.\nopt.left.say.1 [9d28ca] = 绕圈走了两个小时。杰拉尔德二世赢了每一回合。不过你的脚，现在是对的了。\n# en: Spar for real\nopt.right.label [9e070e] = 真刀真枪对练\n# en: You land a clean disarm on your partner. Across the hall, Lyra raises an eyebrow, which is her version of a standing ovation.\nopt.right.say.1 [71d335] = 你干净利落地缴了对手的械。大厅另一头，莱拉挑了挑眉——这是她版本的起立鼓掌。\n# en: You spend the evening relearning which way is up. Lyra says that's the first lesson anyway.\nopt.right.say.2 [99acac] = 你一晚上都在重新学习哪边是上。莱拉说反正这本来就是第一课。\n\n@event duel_footwork_feud\n# en: The Footwork Feud\ntitle [cf293b] = 步法之争\n# en: Bram says the Circle's footwork is \"Emberhall nonsense\" and Lyra says Bram's footwork is \"a hazard to shipping\". Both of them look at you to settle it.\ntext [8b7ae0] = 布拉姆说决斗社的步法是“烬厅的胡闹”，莱拉说布拉姆的步法是“航运公害”。两人都看着你，等你裁决。\n# en: Two students arguing in the middle of a duelling circle while the rest of the club pretends not to listen.\nscene [a9dbb3] = 两名学生在决斗圈中央争吵，社里其他人假装没在听。\n# en: Side with Lyra\nopt.left.label [451590] = 站莱拉这边\n# en: Lyra nods as if this were obvious. Bram takes a demerit book out of his pocket, looks at it, and puts it back. Progress.\nopt.left.say.1 [dfa265] = 莱拉点点头，好像这是理所当然。布拉姆从口袋里掏出记过本，看了看，又放了回去。有进步。\n# en: Side with Bram\nopt.right.label [1bec38] = 站布拉姆这边\n# en: Bram looks startled to be agreed with. Lyra looks at you like a puzzle she will solve later, with fire.\nopt.right.say.1 [4720c9] = 有人赞同自己，布拉姆显得很吃惊。莱拉看你的样子，像在看一道她以后会用火来解的谜题。\n\n@event duel_fixture\n# en: Fixture Against Thornwick\ntitle [d8c992] = 对阵荆木\n# en: Thornwick Academy's duelling team has crossed the hills for a fixture. The Circle needs one more duellist in the line-up, and the captain is looking at you.\ntext [7ff7a2] = 荆木学院的决斗队翻山过来比赛。决斗社的阵容还差一名决斗者，社长正看着你。\n# en: A duelling green with rival banners, students from both academies packed onto benches.\nscene [0b0deb] = 挂着两校旗帜的决斗场，两所学院的学生挤满长凳。\n# en: Duel for the Circle\nopt.left.label [4b23b1] = 为决斗社出战\n# en: You win your bout, and the Circle wins the fixture by a whisker. Someone carries you a few steps before remembering they don't like you.\nopt.left.say.1 [8c53d7] = 你赢了自己那场，决斗社以毫厘之差赢下比赛。有人把你扛起来走了几步，才想起他们并不喜欢你。\n# en: A draw. The Circle loses by one bout, but nobody blames you, and Lyra says \"acceptable\" twice.\nopt.left.say.2 [d9fb94] = 平局。决斗社输了一场，但没人怪你，莱拉说了两遍“还行”。\n# en: The Thornwick duellist is very good and very polite about it. You hate both.\nopt.left.say.3 [929b7a] = 荆木的决斗者非常厉害，而且对此非常有礼貌。两样你都讨厌。\n# en: Offer to be the reserve\nopt.right.label [44cb08] = 主动当替补\n# en: You hold coats and bring water. It turns out reserves see everything, including how Thornwick cheats.\nopt.right.say.1 [eac3f5] = 你帮人拿外套、递水。原来替补什么都看得见，包括荆木是怎么作弊的。\n\n@event duel_captain_call\n# en: The Captain's Question\ntitle [dfe526] = 社长的问题\n# en: After practice, the captain sits beside you. \"When I graduate, someone has to run this place,\" she says. \"Someone who turns up. You turn up.\"\ntext [3fd3c8] = 练习结束后，社长在你身边坐下。“我毕业以后，总得有人管这地方，”她说，“得是个会准时出现的人。你会。”\n# en: An empty duelling hall at night, two students sitting on the edge of a chalk circle.\nscene [0d20d7] = 夜里空荡荡的决斗厅，两名学生坐在粉笔圈边上。\n# en: Say you'll think about it\nopt.left.label [b4769e] = 说你会考虑\n# en: \"Think fast,\" she says. \"Feet first, then spells.\" You're fairly sure that's a yes.\nopt.left.say.1 [a70c37] = “考虑快点，”她说，“先动脚，再施法。”你很确定这算是答应了。\n# en: Say you're not the type\nopt.right.label [9fe8f9] = 说你不是那块料\n# en: \"Nobody's the type,\" she says. \"That's why it has to be someone who turns up.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [cd23ec] = “没人是那块料，”她说，“所以才得是个会准时出现的人。”\n\n@event lib_shelving_rush\n# en: The Shelving Rush\ntitle [5625e2] = 上架高峰\n# en: Exam week, and every book in the library has been borrowed, read and returned at once. The cart is taller than you are. Footnote is sitting on top of it.\ntext [6fa1df] = 考试周，图书馆里的每本书都同时被借走、读完又还回来。书车比你还高。脚注正坐在最顶上。\n# en: A library cart overflowing with returned books, a student pushing it at a run between towering shelves.\nscene [7fe4c3] = 一辆堆满还书的图书车，一名学生推着它在高耸的书架间小跑。\n# en: Shelve properly\nopt.left.label [bac2cb] = 规规矩矩地上架\n# en: Every book in its place by closing. Footnote has moved to the top shelf to supervise.\nopt.left.say.1 [3b9e2e] = 闭馆前每本书都归了位。脚注已经挪到最高一层书架上监工了。\n# en: Shelve quickly\nopt.right.label [9d5012] = 快速上架\n# en: The books are on shelves. Most of them are even on the right shelves. Someone will find out in a decade.\nopt.right.say.1 [79d70e] = 书都在架子上了。大部分甚至在对的架子上。十年后会有人发现的。\n\n@event lib_overdue_mage\n# en: The Overdue Mage\ntitle [99bf32] = 逾期的法师\n# en: An old mage returns a book sixty years overdue and asks, with great dignity, what the fine is. The ledger says the fine is more than the library.\ntext [6a40d1] = 一位老法师归还一本逾期六十年的书，并十分庄重地询问罚金是多少。账本上的罚金比图书馆还值钱。\n# en: An elderly mage at the library desk clutching a book that is sixty years overdue.\nscene [b70648] = 一位年迈的法师站在图书馆柜台前，紧攥着一本逾期六十年的书。\n# en: Waive it\nopt.left.label [899866] = 免了吧\n# en: The old mage thanks you and leaves a sweet on the desk. Later you find out he taught Professor Thorne.\nopt.left.say.1 [ef4397] = 老法师谢过你，在柜台上留下一颗糖。后来你才知道，他教过索恩教授。\n# en: Charge it, by the book\nopt.right.label [396965] = 照章收费\n# en: The mage pays in coins so old the bank has to look them up. The head librarian is delighted.\nopt.right.say.1 [6746d8] = 法师付的硬币老到银行都得去查。馆长高兴坏了。\n\n@event lib_footnote_strike\n# en: Footnote Goes on Strike\ntitle [ec48a4] = 脚注罢工\n# en: Footnote has lain down across the reference desk and will not move. Students are queueing. The cat has made its demands clear: none.\ntext [8627c8] = 脚注横躺在参考咨询台上，一动不动。学生们在排队。这只猫已经把它的诉求表达得很清楚了：没有诉求。\n# en: A large library cat lying across a reference desk, refusing to move, while students queue in front of it.\nscene [bcfc48] = 一只大个儿的图书馆猫横躺在参考咨询台上不肯挪窝，学生们在它面前排起了队。\n# en: Bribe him with fish\nopt.left.label [ca7565] = 用鱼贿赂它\n# en: Footnote accepts the fish, the apology and the chair you bring him. The queue resumes.\nopt.left.say.1 [d9ebb3] = 脚注接受了鱼、道歉，还有你搬来的椅子。队伍恢复了流动。\n# en: Work around him\nopt.right.label [ea0c37] = 绕开它干活\n# en: You stamp books on the far end of the desk for an hour. Footnote watches with the calm of a cat who has won.\nopt.right.say.1 [40e4a9] = 你在柜台另一头盖了一小时的章。脚注以胜利者的平静注视着你。\n\n@event court_fountain_freeze\n# en: The Frozen Fountain\ntitle [f87a8e] = 冻住的喷泉\n# en: The courtyard fountain has frozen in the middle of a splash. The water hangs in the air like glass. A first-year is trying to lick it.\ntext [abc188] = 庭院的喷泉在水花溅起的一瞬冻住了。水像玻璃一样悬在半空。一个一年级生正想去舔它。\n# en: A courtyard fountain frozen mid-splash, the water hanging in the air like glass.\nscene [8a0a42] = 庭院喷泉冻在了水花飞溅的瞬间，水像玻璃一样悬在空中。\n# en: Stop the first-year\nopt.left.label [d24736] = 拦住那个一年级生\n# en: You pull the first-year away just as the ice cracks. For a moment you'd swear you saw a black star reflected in it.\nopt.left.say.1 [267623] = 冰裂开的那一刻，你正好把一年级生拉开。有一瞬间，你发誓看见冰里映着一颗黑色的星。\n# en: Study the ice\nopt.right.label [e6dcae] = 研究这块冰\n# en: The ice is colder than ice should be. Your notes say \"colder than ice should be\" three times.\nopt.right.say.1 [3744c0] = 这冰比冰该有的还冷。你的笔记里把“比冰该有的还冷”写了三遍。\n\n@event court_rumour_mill\n# en: The Rumour Mill\ntitle [b2db14] = 流言工坊\n# en: {gossip} has news, which is to say gossip, which is to say three different stories about the same broken window. \"You'll want to hear this,\" {they} say{s}.\ntext [ba7e30] = {gossip}有新闻——也就是八卦——也就是关于同一扇破窗户的三个不同版本。“你会想听的，”{they}说。\n# en: Students huddled around the courtyard fountain trading whispers, one of them clearly enjoying it most.\nscene [bc5c84] = 学生们围在庭院喷泉边交换耳语，其中一个显然最乐在其中。\n# en: Listen to all of it\nopt.left.label [71c08a] = 全都听一遍\n# en: You hear all three stories. Two are true. One is about you.\nopt.left.say.1 [1839fa] = 三个版本你都听了。两个是真的。一个是关于你的。\n# en: You hear all three stories. Nessa is already writing the fourth.\nopt.left.say.1@nessa [198fa7] = 三个版本你都听了。内莎已经在写第四个了。\n# en: Make up a better one\nopt.right.label [ed234b] = 编个更好的\n# en: Your story is better than all three. By dinner it's the official version.\nopt.right.say.1 [2aa741] = 你的版本比那三个都好。到晚饭时，它已经成了官方说法。\n\n@event lake_skimming\n# en: Skimming Stones\ntitle [a14e0e] = 打水漂\n# en: {friend} is skimming stones on the Mirror Lake. The water is so still each stone leaves rings that go on forever. \"Seven,\" {they} say{s}. \"Beat that.\"\ntext [90f788] = {friend}在镜湖上打水漂。湖面静得出奇，每块石头留下的涟漪都仿佛永远不会散。“七下，”{they}说，“来比比。”\n# en: Two students skimming stones across a perfectly still lake at dusk.\nscene [d7fdb5] = 黄昏时分，两名学生在一片静止如镜的湖面上打水漂。\n# en: Try to beat it\nopt.left.label [1284ba] = 试着赢过去\n# en: Nine skips. {friend} demands a rematch and gets one. The afternoon disappears into stones.\nopt.left.say.1 [1f9fa1] = 九下。{friend}要求再来一局，也如愿了。整个下午都消失在石子里。\n# en: Your stone sinks with great dignity. {friend} laughs until {they} have to sit down.\nopt.left.say.2 [533d65] = 你的石头带着极大的尊严沉了下去。{friend}笑得只好坐下来。\n# en: Just watch the rings\nopt.right.label [39fffc] = 就看涟漪\n# en: You sit and watch the rings spread until the light goes. Neither of you says much. It's one of the good afternoons.\nopt.right.say.1 [2ebf1f] = 你坐着看涟漪扩散，直到天光散尽。你们俩都没说什么。这是那种美好的下午之一。\n\n@event lake_reflection_bet\n# en: The Reflection Bet\ntitle [7b2fea] = 倒影的赌约\n# en: Your reflection in the Mirror Lake is holding a coin you don't have. It flips it. It catches it. It offers it to you.\ntext [ad0e65] = 你在镜湖里的倒影拿着一枚你并没有的硬币。它抛起，接住，然后递向你。\n# en: A student staring into a still lake where their reflection appears to be holding a coin.\nscene [407b4a] = 一名学生凝视着平静的湖面，水中的倒影似乎握着一枚硬币。\n# en: Take the coin\nopt.left.label [4cb026] = 接过硬币\n# en: The coin is real, and cold, and you can't remember putting it in your pocket.\nopt.left.say.1 [67628e] = 硬币是真的，冰凉，而你想不起自己是什么时候把它放进口袋的。\n# en: Shake your head\nopt.right.label [5e0346] = 摇头\n# en: Your reflection shrugs, pockets the coin and walks out of the frame before you do.\nopt.right.say.1 [3c806f] = 你的倒影耸耸肩，把硬币揣进兜里，先你一步走出了画面。\n\n@event ref_food_fight\n# en: Pudding Diplomacy\ntitle [f97acc] = 布丁外交\n# en: A pudding sails across the refectory and lands in Bram's lap. The hall goes silent. Every eye turns to the table it came from, which is yours.\ntext [e5a752] = 一块布丁飞过膳堂，落在布拉姆腿上。大厅一片死寂。所有目光都转向它飞来的那张桌子——你的那张。\n# en: A refectory mid-food-fight, puddings in the air, a prefect rising slowly from behind a table.\nscene [b7e5a2] = 膳堂里一场食物大战正酣，布丁在空中飞，一位级长从桌子后面缓缓站起。\n# en: Own up\nopt.left.label [3a0128] = 承认是我\n# en: Bram writes you a demerit, then pauses. \"Good throw,\" he says, almost without wanting to.\nopt.left.say.1 [d32724] = 布拉姆给你记了一次过，然后顿了顿。“扔得好，”他说，几乎是不情愿的。\n# en: Throw another\nopt.right.label [7417cc] = 再扔一块\n# en: The second pudding starts a war that the refectory will talk about for years. Historians will argue about who won. You know.\nopt.right.say.1 [2e4f89] = 第二块布丁引发了一场膳堂会议论好几年的战争。史学家会争论谁赢了。你知道。\n\n@event ref_cook_secret\n# en: The Cook's Secret\ntitle [c0a5b6] = 厨娘的秘密\n# en: The cook catches you lingering by the kitchen door. \"You look like someone who misses proper soup,\" she says, and hands you a bowl before you can deny it.\ntext [1cf0fd] = 厨娘逮到你在厨房门口徘徊。“你看着就像个想念正经汤的人，”她说，不等你否认就递来一碗。\n# en: A huge kitchen behind the refectory, an enormous cook stirring a pot with a spoon the size of an oar.\nscene [ea98ca] = 膳堂后面的大厨房，一位魁梧的厨娘用船桨那么大的勺子搅着锅。\n# en: Eat it slowly\nopt.left.label [3da080] = 慢慢地喝\n# en: It tastes like the kitchen at home. You don't say so. She can tell anyway.\nopt.left.say.1 [d6f79e] = 这味道像家里的厨房。你没说出口。她反正看得出来。\n# en: Ask for the recipe\nopt.right.label [604f64] = 讨要配方\n# en: \"Ha,\" says the cook, which means no, which means you'll have to keep coming back.\nopt.right.say.1 [fdb0a2] = “哈，”厨娘说。意思是不行，也就是说你得一直回来。\n\n@event obs_comet_watch\n# en: The Comet Watch\ntitle [d479ab] = 守望彗星\n# en: A comet is due tonight and the observatory is full of students pretending they came for the science. The telescope has a queue. The roof has a view.\ntext [20d293] = 今晚有彗星经过，观星台里挤满了假装是为科学而来的学生。望远镜前排着队。屋顶上有好风景。\n# en: The brass dome of an observatory open to the night, a student at the eyepiece, another beside them with a notebook.\nscene [3227ec] = 向夜空敞开的黄铜穹顶观星台，一名学生凑在目镜前，另一人拿着笔记本站在旁边。\n# en: Queue for the telescope\nopt.left.label [1a7ef0] = 排队用望远镜\n# en: Your turn at the eyepiece lasts thirty seconds. The comet's tail is green. You'll remember it longer than most lectures.\nopt.left.say.1 [54f31c] = 轮到你看目镜只有三十秒。彗尾是绿色的。你会记得它比大多数课都久。\n# en: Watch from the roof\nopt.right.label [5e0cfb] = 去屋顶看\n# en: The comet crosses the sky slowly, like it's looking for something. Kael is already on the roof. He moves over to make room.\nopt.right.say.1 [344326] = 彗星慢慢划过天空，像在找什么东西。凯尔已经在屋顶上了。他挪了挪，给你让出位置。\n\n@event obs_star_chart\n# en: The Missing Star\ntitle [f95144] = 缺失的星\n# en: An old star chart in the observatory archive has a circle drawn where a star should be, with a note in faded ink: \"It eats the light. Do not chart it.\"\ntext [f9ab12] = 观星台档案里一张旧星图上，本该是一颗星的地方画着一个圈，旁边用褪色的墨水写着：“它吞噬光。勿绘。”\n# en: An old star chart spread on a desk, one constellation with a careful empty circle drawn where a star should be.\nscene [8cbcfa] = 桌上摊开一张旧星图，某个星座里本该有星的位置被仔细地画了一个空圈。\n# en: Copy the chart\nopt.left.label [ddb5f3] = 抄下星图\n# en: You copy it carefully. That night you dream of a circle with nothing inside it, waiting.\nopt.left.say.1 [c900a1] = 你仔细地抄了下来。那晚你梦见一个空无一物的圈，在等待。\n# en: Put it back\nopt.right.label [862ed2] = 放回原处\n# en: You return it to the drawer. The drawer feels colder when you close it.\nopt.right.say.1 [ca8bc6] = 你把它放回抽屉。合上时，抽屉似乎冷了些。\n\n@event green_mandrake_choir\n# en: The Mandrake Choir\ntitle [a16982] = 曼德拉草合唱团\n# en: Tobias has taught the greenhouse mandrakes to scream in harmony. He would like your opinion. The mandrakes would like an audience.\ntext [26476f] = 托比亚斯教会了温室里的曼德拉草用和声尖叫。他想听听你的意见。曼德拉草想要观众。\n# en: A greenhouse bench of potted mandrakes wearing tiny earmuffs, a student conducting them with a pencil.\nscene [556372] = 温室长凳上一排戴着小耳罩的盆栽曼德拉草，一名学生拿铅笔指挥它们。\n# en: Conduct a verse\nopt.left.label [8d7d71] = 指挥一段\n# en: You conduct one verse. Your ears ring for a day. Tobias says it's the best performance they've given.\nopt.left.say.1 [fe14aa] = 你指挥了一段。耳朵嗡嗡响了一整天。托比亚斯说这是它们最好的一场演出。\n# en: Wear earmuffs and listen\nopt.right.label [fa6dc3] = 戴上耳罩听\n# en: Through the earmuffs it sounds almost beautiful. Tobias is so proud he cries a little.\nopt.right.say.1 [07ed14] = 隔着耳罩，听起来几乎很美。托比亚斯骄傲得哭了一小会儿。\n\n@event green_moonflower_sprout\n# en: A Moonflower Seedling\ntitle [e51a03] = 月光花幼苗\n# en: In a forgotten corner of the greenhouse, a moonflower seed has sprouted on its own, eleven years early. It glows faintly when you lean over it.\ntext [b895be] = 温室一个被遗忘的角落里，一粒月光花种子自己发了芽，早了整整十一年。你俯身靠近时，它发出微弱的光。\n# en: A tiny silver-leaved seedling in a clay pot, glowing faintly in a dark greenhouse.\nscene [1fd66d] = 陶盆里一株银叶小苗，在黑暗的温室中幽幽发光。\n# en: Pot it and look after it\nopt.left.label [aa4dfe] = 移进盆里好好照料\n# en: You give it a pot, a label and a name. It glows a little brighter when you visit.\nopt.left.say.1 [3976bb] = 你给了它一个盆、一张标签和一个名字。你来看它时，它会亮一点。\n# en: Tell Tobias\nopt.right.label [fe6832] = 告诉托比亚斯\n# en: Tobias is speechless, then very loud. The seedling gets a sign, a fence and a rota.\nopt.right.say.1 [69881e] = 托比亚斯先是说不出话，然后非常大声。幼苗得到了一块牌子、一圈篱笆和一张值班表。\n\n@event castle_empty_halls\n# en: Empty Halls\ntitle [468042] = 空荡的走廊\n# en: The castle in the holidays is enormous and quiet. Your footsteps echo down corridors you've never been alone in. A door you've never noticed is standing open.\ntext [30fae9] = 假期里的城堡巨大而安静。你的脚步声在从没独自走过的走廊里回荡。一扇你从未注意过的门敞开着。\n# en: A long castle corridor in the holidays, dust in sunbeams, one student walking alone.\nscene [c48035] = 假期里一条长长的城堡走廊，阳光里浮着灰尘，一名学生独自走着。\n# en: Go through the door\nopt.left.label [974ffb] = 穿过那扇门\n# en: It leads to a stair that leads to a locked door that leads, you're almost sure, to the seventh tower.\nopt.left.say.1 [6544d0] = 门后是一段楼梯，楼梯通向一扇锁着的门，而那扇门——你几乎可以肯定——通向第七塔。\n# en: Read in the window seat\nopt.right.label [a83ff6] = 在窗边座位上读书\n# en: You read all day in a window seat with the whole lake below you. The best kind of nothing.\nopt.right.say.1 [19769a] = 你在窗边座位上读了一整天书，整片湖就在脚下。最好的那种无所事事。\n\n@event castle_skeleton_crew\n# en: The Skeleton Crew\ntitle [6ec6d4] = 留守的人们\n# en: Supper in the holidays is one table in the Great Hall: you, three other students, the cook, two prefects and Old Maud, who has brought her goat.\ntext [a9be7b] = 假期的晚饭只在大礼堂摆一张桌子：你、另外三个学生、厨娘、两位级长，还有带着山羊来的老莫德。\n# en: A handful of staff and students sharing a long table in a nearly empty great hall.\nscene [4c6b8f] = 几乎空荡的大礼堂里，寥寥几名职员和学生共用一张长桌。\n# en: Sit next to Maud\nopt.left.label [d82b60] = 坐在莫德旁边\n# en: Maud tells a story about the frozen spring that she's never told the term-time students. Bartholomew eats your napkin.\nopt.left.say.1 [2c57bf] = 莫德讲了一个关于冰封之春的故事，那是她从没对学期里的学生讲过的。巴塞洛缪吃掉了你的餐巾。\n# en: Play cards with the prefects\nopt.right.label [a22285] = 和级长们打牌\n# en: The prefects play cards like they enforce rules: badly and with great confidence. You win dessert.\nopt.right.say.1 [3b59a8] = 级长们打牌就像执行校规：水平很烂，信心十足。你赢了甜点。\n\n@event home_family_supper\n# en: Supper at Home\ntitle [1f26c6] = 家里的晚饭\n# en: Supper at home. Everyone wants to know what you've learned. You try to explain evocation to an aunt who thinks it's a kind of cheese.\ntext [8ab73f] = 家里的晚饭。每个人都想知道你学了什么。你试着向一位以为塑能术是某种奶酪的姑妈解释它。\n# en: A crowded kitchen table, a family passing dishes, a student back from the academy being asked too many questions.\nscene [ec2a5f] = 挤满人的餐桌，一家人传着菜，一个从学院回来的学生被问了太多问题。\n# en: Show them a spell\nopt.left.label [803b4f] = 给他们露一手\n# en: You light every candle in the room at once. Your aunt applauds and asks if you can do it with cheese.\nopt.left.say.1 [5d4fe6] = 你一下点亮了屋里所有的蜡烛。姑妈鼓掌，问你能不能对奶酪也来一下。\n# en: Just listen\nopt.right.label [6a9f3e] = 就听着\n# en: You let them talk. You'd forgotten how loud and how good it is.\nopt.right.say.1 [738cc0] = 你让他们说。你都忘了这有多吵，又有多好。\n\n@event home_old_friend\n# en: An Old Friend\ntitle [8809c5] = 老朋友\n# en: Your oldest friend is sitting on the wall at the edge of the fields, exactly where you left them. \"So,\" they say. \"Are you a wizard now, or what?\"\ntext [e85e1a] = 你最老的朋友坐在田边的矮墙上，就在你离开时的那个位置。“所以，”对方说，“你现在算巫师了，还是怎样？”\n# en: Two old friends sitting on a field wall at sunset, one in academy robes, the other in work clothes.\nscene [82fd45] = 夕阳下两位老朋友坐在田边矮墙上，一个穿着学院长袍，另一个穿着干活的衣服。\n# en: Tell them everything\nopt.left.label [09f8b1] = 把一切都告诉对方\n# en: You talk until the stars come out. They want to hear about the lake most of all.\nopt.left.say.1 [5d2429] = 你们一直聊到星星出来。对方最想听的是那片湖。\n# en: Ask about their life instead\nopt.right.label [24a3a1] = 反过来问问对方的生活\n# en: They've been busy too: a farm, a fiancé, a goat that bites. Your life isn't the only one that grew.\nopt.right.say.1 [91a591] = 对方也很忙：一座农场、一位未婚夫、一只咬人的山羊。长大的不只是你的生活。\n\n@event home_market_day\n# en: Market Day\ntitle [0bcdaf] = 赶集日\n# en: Market day in town. A stallholder recognises your academy robes and asks if you can do anything about his leaky roof. For a price.\ntext [ebfea1] = 镇上的赶集日。一个摊主认出了你的学院长袍，问你能不能帮他修修漏雨的屋顶。有偿的。\n# en: A small country market, a student in academy robes being stared at by stallholders.\nscene [a7a658] = 一个乡间小集市，一名穿着学院长袍的学生被摊主们盯着看。\n# en: Fix it with magic\nopt.left.label [77d22c] = 用魔法修好它\n# en: The roof is dry, the stallholder is thrilled and you are slightly scorched. He pays in apples and coins.\nopt.left.say.1 [f09cd8] = 屋顶干了，摊主乐坏了，你被轻微烤焦了。他用苹果和硬币付账。\n# en: Just buy sweets\nopt.right.label [bbf438] = 只买糖\n# en: The sweet shop still sells your favourites. You eat them on the bridge like you used to.\nopt.right.say.1 [ea94aa] = 糖果店还卖你最爱的那几样。你像从前一样坐在桥上吃。\n\n@event work_double_shift\n# en: The Double Shift\ntitle [6df679] = 连轴转\n# en: Holiday shifts pay double and feel triple. It's your second shift today and the evening rush hasn't started.\ntext [60fdf7] = 假期的班工钱翻倍，感觉翻三倍。这是你今天的第二个班，晚高峰还没开始。\n# en: A student asleep on their feet behind a counter, a clock on the wall showing a very late hour.\nscene [6bbbbe] = 一名学生站在柜台后面睡着了，墙上的钟指着一个很晚的时间。\n# en: Push through\nopt.left.label [f6ed7c] = 咬牙撑过去\n# en: You push through. The coins are heavy in your pocket and your feet are heavy everywhere else.\nopt.left.say.1 [900c8d] = 你撑过去了。口袋里的硬币沉甸甸的，身上其他地方也沉甸甸的。\n# en: Swap the shift\nopt.right.label [dcf3f9] = 找人换班\n# en: A colleague takes it for a favour you'll owe later. You sleep for eleven hours.\nopt.right.say.1 [6106e9] = 一位同事替了你，换你以后欠一个人情。你睡了十一个小时。\n\n@event intern_first_assignment\n# en: The First Assignment\ntitle [12864b] = 第一项任务\n# en: Your supervisor at the Royal Library hands you a list of forty uncatalogued boxes. \"Find out what's in them,\" she says. \"Carefully. One of them is probably alive.\"\ntext [106ac0] = 你在皇家图书馆的导师递给你一张清单，上面是四十个未编目的箱子。“查清里面有什么，”她说，“小心点。其中有一个大概是活的。”\n# en: A vast underground archive, a supervisor handing a student a list longer than their arm.\nscene [cf395e] = 一座巨大的地下档案馆，导师递给学生一张比胳膊还长的清单。\n# en: Work through the boxes\nopt.left.label [af7cdf] = 逐个整理箱子\n# en: Box thirty-one is alive. It's a very small, very old dictionary that bites. You catalogue it.\nopt.left.say.1 [f9f573] = 第三十一号箱是活的。里面是一本很小、很老、会咬人的词典。你把它编了目。\n# en: Ask the other interns\nopt.right.label [d7f73e] = 问问其他实习生\n# en: The other interns have a system, a rota and a song. You learn all three.\nopt.right.say.1 [b61185] = 其他实习生有一套系统、一张值班表和一首歌。三样你都学会了。\n\n@event intern_index_maze\n# en: The Index Maze\ntitle [708be7] = 索引迷宫\n# en: A cross-reference in the archive leads to another, and another, and another, deeper under the city. The last card is about the founders of Lumenhold.\ntext [6ca7e0] = 档案里的一条交叉引用指向另一条，又一条，再一条，越来越深地钻进城市地下。最后一张卡片讲的是流明堡的创始者。\n# en: A student lost between endless shelves underground, following an index card trail by lamplight.\nscene [37ed2d] = 一名学生迷失在地下无尽的书架间，借着灯光追寻一串索引卡的线索。\n# en: Follow it to the end\nopt.left.label [b0b834] = 追到底\n# en: At the end is a letter from a founder about \"the seventh lock\". You copy it with shaking hands.\nopt.left.say.1 [89da61] = 尽头是一位创始者写的信，谈到“第七把锁”。你手发着抖把它抄了下来。\n# en: The trail ends at a missing card. Somebody took it. Recently.\nopt.left.say.2 [a4b290] = 线索断在一张缺失的卡片上。有人拿走了它。就在最近。\n# en: Report it to your supervisor\nopt.right.label [10c4be] = 报告给导师\n# en: Your supervisor reads the card, goes pale and writes a letter to the headmistress of Lumenhold.\nopt.right.say.1 [10b589] = 导师读了卡片，脸色发白，提笔给流明堡的院长写了封信。\n\n@event thesis_proposal\n# en: The Proposal\ntitle [0d98d2] = 开题\n# en: Your thesis proposal is due. Professor Thorne wants one sentence that says what you'll find out. You have eleven sentences and none of them is it.\ntext [6735b0] = 论文开题报告该交了。索恩教授要一句话，说清你要查明什么。你有十一句话，没有一句是。\n# en: A desk covered in drafts, one page with a single sentence circled many times.\nscene [19addc] = 一张铺满草稿的桌子，有一页上只有一句话，被圈了很多遍。\n# en: Rewrite it all night\nopt.left.label [2fa9ed] = 通宵重写\n# en: At dawn you have one sentence. Thorne reads it twice. \"Yes,\" he says. \"That.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [348567] = 黎明时你有了一句话。索恩读了两遍。“对，”他说，“就是这个。”\n# en: Hand in what you have\nopt.right.label [648c9a] = 交上手头的\n# en: Thorne reads all eleven sentences and circles the fourth. \"That one,\" he says. \"Next time, only that one.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [8b0a3c] = 索恩读完十一句话，圈出第四句。“这一句，”他说，“下次，只要这一句。”\n\n@event thesis_lab_night\n# en: Late in the Lab\ntitle [d2c7a0] = 实验室深夜\n# en: The thesis lab after midnight: everyone else has gone home, and your argument has a hole in it the size of the seventh tower.\ntext [0fcfb7] = 午夜后的论文实验室：其他人都回去了，而你的论证里有一个第七塔那么大的漏洞。\n# en: A thesis laboratory at midnight, one carrel lit, a student surrounded by open books.\nscene [9ec7f2] = 午夜的论文实验室，只有一个隔间亮着灯，一名学生被摊开的书围着。\n# en: Find the hole\nopt.left.label [35b489] = 找到漏洞\n# en: You find it, fill it and find two more. That's progress, apparently.\nopt.left.say.1 [e36cbd] = 你找到了，补上了，又发现了两个。据说这就叫进展。\n# en: Go to bed\nopt.right.label [2fa817] = 去睡觉\n# en: You sleep. In the morning the hole is still there, but you can see the edges of it.\nopt.right.say.1 [0eb578] = 你睡了。早上漏洞还在，但你能看清它的边了。\n\n@event bell_first_ring\n# en: A Bell Under the Water\ntitle [1289ef] = 水下的钟声\n# en: In the middle of the night you hear a bell, low and slow, from the middle of the lake. Nobody else in your dormitory wakes up.\ntext [cfa605] = 半夜里你听见钟声，低沉而缓慢，从湖心传来。宿舍里没有别人醒来。\n# en: A dark lakeshore at night, ripples spreading from the middle of the lake though nothing has fallen in.\nscene [38d9e1] = 夜里漆黑的湖岸，涟漪从湖心扩散开来，尽管什么都没有落进水里。\n# en: Go down to the shore\nopt.left.label [c0c878] = 下到湖边去\n# en: The water is perfectly still. Then, once, a ring spreads out from the centre, as if the lake had been struck from below.\nopt.left.say.1 [baf151] = 湖面纹丝不动。然后，只一次，一圈涟漪从中心荡开，仿佛湖被人从下面敲了一下。\n# en: Pull the blanket over your head\nopt.right.label [352adf] = 把毯子蒙过头\n# en: It rings seven times. You count every one. In the morning you are the only one who remembers.\nopt.right.say.1 [e7bc35] = 钟响了七下。你每一下都数了。到了早上，只有你一个人还记得。\n\n@event bell_maud_rope\n# en: Maud's Rope\ntitle [852d20] = 莫德的绳子\n# en: {keeper} shows you a rope, frayed and black with lake water, that she's kept in her shed for sixty years. \"From the bell,\" she says. \"It came up the night the lake froze. Someone cut it.\"\ntext [090813] = {keeper}给你看一根绳子，磨损了，被湖水浸得发黑，她在棚子里留了六十年。“钟上的，”她说，“湖冻上那晚它浮了上来。有人割断了它。”\n# en: An old woman in a garden shed holding up a frayed, waterlogged bell-rope.\nscene [609899] = 花园棚屋里，一位老妇人举着一根磨损、浸透了水的钟绳。\n# en: Ask who cut it\nopt.left.label [a6108f] = 问是谁割断的\n# en: \"That,\" says {keeper}, \"is the question I've been asking for sixty years.\" She promises to tell you what she knows, when she's ready.\nopt.left.say.1 [4bd3a1] = “这个嘛，”{keeper}说，“我问了六十年了。”她答应等她准备好，就把知道的告诉你。\n# en: Leave her to her memories\nopt.right.label [9f94d2] = 让她独自回忆\n# en: You leave her with the rope in her lap. She doesn't notice you go.\nopt.right.say.1 [10a7b7] = 你留她抱着膝上的绳子。她没注意到你离开。\n\n@event bell_answer\n# en: What Maud Remembers\ntitle [7f91ce] = 莫德记得的事\n# en: {npc} finds you by the boathouse. \"The rope,\" she says. \"Edmund cut it. To stop the bell ringing the thing awake. It went on ringing anyway.\"\ntext [b0e638] = {npc}在船屋边找到你。“那根绳子，”她说，“是埃德蒙割的。为了不让钟声把那东西敲醒。可钟还是一直在响。”\n# en: An old woman and a student sitting by a cottage fire, a waterlogged rope coiled between them.\nscene [6145b3] = 小屋炉火边坐着一位老妇人和一名学生，两人之间盘着一根浸透水的绳子。\n# en: Listen\nopt.ok.label [03cec6] = 听着\n# en: She talks until the light goes. When she's finished, she seems lighter, and you seem to be carrying something.\nopt.ok.say.1 [de8d6c] = 她一直讲到天光散尽。讲完时她似乎轻松了，而你似乎背上了什么。\n\n@event bell_drowned\n# en: The Diving Bell\ntitle [980982] = 潜水钟\n# en: The bell has rung every night for a week. The boathouse has an old diving helmet and a rope. The lake is very dark and very still.\ntext [62e2e6] = 钟已经连着响了一个星期。船屋里有一个旧潜水头盔和一根绳子。湖水非常黑，非常静。\n# en: A student in a borrowed diving helmet standing at the end of a jetty over black water.\nscene [f56404] = 一名学生戴着借来的潜水头盔，站在黑水之上的栈桥尽头。\n# en: Dive\nopt.left.label [ad77f7] = 潜下去\n# en: Far down, the bell lies on its side, and around its rim, letters: a name you don't know. You surface gasping, and you keep the name.\nopt.left.say.1 [f03244] = 在很深的地方，钟侧躺着，钟沿上刻着字：一个你不认识的名字。你喘着气浮出水面，把那个名字记在了心里。\n# en: The cold drives you back up before you see anything. Old Maud is waiting on the jetty with a blanket and a look.\nopt.left.say.2 [32863f] = 你还没看见什么，寒冷就把你逼了回去。老莫德拿着毯子和一个眼神等在栈桥上。\n# en: Wait for the bell to stop\nopt.right.label [d2920e] = 等钟声停下\n# en: You wait for a week. It doesn't stop. Something in you decides it will have to be you, next time.\nopt.right.say.1 [717419] = 你等了一个星期。它没有停。你心里有什么东西决定了：下一次，得是你去。\n\n@event bell_silence\n# en: The Bell Falls Silent\ntitle [6d5cb4] = 钟声沉寂\n# en: {clue(bell_name): You say the name from the bell's rim out loud, over the water.|You stand at the shore with Maud's rope in your hands.} The bell rings one last time, and then the lake is just a lake.\ntext [721f49] = {clue(bell_name): 你对着湖面，大声念出钟沿上的那个名字。|你站在岸边，手里握着莫德的绳子。}钟最后响了一次，然后湖就只是一片湖了。\n# en: The Mirror Lake at dawn, perfectly still, a student standing at the water's edge with a lantern.\nscene [82908a] = 黎明的镜湖，平静如镜，一名学生提着灯站在水边。\n# en: Let it rest\nopt.ok.label [657b8b] = 让它安息\n# en: For the first time in sixty years, the lake is quiet. Somewhere in her cottage, you think, Maud sleeps through the night.\nopt.ok.say.1 [e9634e] = 六十年来第一次，湖安静了下来。你想，此刻在小屋里，莫德一定一觉睡到了天亮。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/social.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/social.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event soc_study_session\n# en: Study Session\ntitle [c13d4e] = 一起自习\n# en: {npc} drops into the seat across from you with an armful of books. \"Exams are coming for us all. Misery loves company. Study with me?\"\ntext [e20c0b] = {npc}抱着一摞书一屁股坐到你对面。“考试要来收拾我们所有人了。同病相怜嘛。一起学？”\n# en: Two students at a candlelit table piled with books, one explaining something with wild hand gestures.\nscene [cb85c7] = 两个学生坐在堆满书的烛光桌前，其中一个正手舞足蹈地讲解着什么。\n# en: You work better alone\nopt.left.label [1f18da] = 你一个人效率更高\n# en: You get a lot done. {npc} sits at the next table and sighs loudly every few minutes.\nopt.left.say.1 [0d0d7f] = 你完成了很多。{npc}坐在隔壁桌，每隔几分钟就大声叹一口气。\n# en: Pull up a chair\nopt.right.label [d0a229] = 拉过一把椅子\n# en: You get half as much done and remember twice as much of it. {npc} explains the Third Law with a biscuit diagram.\nopt.right.say.1 [7cc537] = 你只完成了一半，却记住了两倍的内容。{npc}用饼干摆图讲解了第三定律。\n\n@event soc_borrow_money\n# en: A Small Loan\ntitle [cd870f] = 一笔小借款\n# en: {npc} turns out an empty purse. \"I'm fifteen crowns short for rent. I'll pay you back. I swear on my wand.\"\ntext [d700eb] = {npc}把空空的钱袋翻了过来。“我房租还差十五克朗。我会还你的。我以我的魔杖发誓。”\n# en: A student holding out an empty purse upside down with a sheepish grin.\nscene [d2da5b] = 一个学生把空钱袋倒过来递给你看，脸上挂着不好意思的笑。\n# en: Sorry, you're tight too\nopt.left.label [d94071] = 抱歉，你也手头紧\n# en: \"No, of course, I understand.\" {They} {are} quiet at dinner.\nopt.left.say.1 [e86862] = “不不，当然，我理解。”晚饭时{they}一直没怎么说话。\n# en: Lend fifteen crowns\nopt.right.label [0326cf] = 借十五克朗\n# en: {npc} hugs you with the empty purse still in {their} hand.\nopt.right.say.1 [995967] = {npc}抱住了你，手里还攥着那只空钱袋。\n# en: {npc} hugs you. You suspect you will never see those fifteen crowns again.\nopt.right.say.2 [506678] = {npc}抱住了你。你怀疑那十五克朗再也回不来了。\n\n@event soc_loan_repaid\n# en: Paid Back\ntitle [ed4502] = 还钱\n# en: {~{npc} finds you after class and presses a little stack of coins into your hand, plus a sticky bun. \"Fifteen crowns. And interest.\" The interest is the bun.|{npc} turns up at breakfast with a small purse and a very straight face. \"Fifteen crowns. I said I would.\"|{npc} slides fifteen crowns across the library table, one coin at a time. The last one has a note under it: \"Thank you. Really.\"}\ntext [d1878a] = {~下课后{npc}找到你，往你手里塞了一小摞硬币，外加一个黏糊糊的小面包。“十五克朗。还有利息。”利息就是那个面包。|{npc}在早餐时出现，拿着一个小钱袋，一脸严肃。“十五克朗。我说过会还的。”|{npc}在图书馆的桌子上把十五克朗一枚一枚推到你面前。最后一枚下面压着一张字条：“谢谢你。真的。”}\n# en: A small stack of coins and a sweet bun left on a desk with a thank-you note.\nscene [a79734] = 桌上放着一小摞硬币和一个甜面包，旁边还有一张感谢字条。\n# en: Count it\nopt.left.label [5fce81] = 数一数\n# en: You count it, because {they}'d be offended if you didn't. Fifteen exactly. {npc} nods, satisfied, as if a debt of honour has been settled.\nopt.left.say.1 [3ec7dd] = 你数了一遍，因为不数的话{they}会觉得被冒犯。正好十五。{npc}满意地点点头，好像一笔关乎荣誉的债终于了结了。\n# en: Pocket it without counting\nopt.right.label [4b0d5d] = 不数就收下\n# en: You put it away uncounted. {npc} looks quietly pleased to be trusted, which was worth more than the crowns.\nopt.right.say.1 [997bc1] = 你没数就收了起来。{npc}看起来暗自高兴被你信任——这比那些克朗值钱多了。\n\n@event soc_become_friends\n# en: Something Like Friendship\ntitle [68b938] = 类似友谊的东西\n# en: {npc} saves you a seat without asking now, and you save {them} one. You trade notes, complaints and the last biscuit without keeping score. Somewhere along the way, this became a friendship.\ntext [013159] = 现在{npc}会不声不响地给你占座，你也给{them}占。你们交换笔记、牢骚和最后一块饼干，从不记账。不知从何时起，这已经成了一段友谊。\n# en: Two students walking across the lawn at sunset, laughing at the same joke, shadows long behind them.\nscene [ea4144] = 夕阳下两个学生穿过草坪，为同一个笑话大笑，身后拖着长长的影子。\n# en: Keep a little distance\nopt.left.label [37e0f8] = 保持一点距离\n# en: You stay friendly. Just not too close. Some walls are load-bearing.\nopt.left.say.1 [4470d5] = 你们保持友好。只是不太亲近。有些墙是承重墙。\n# en: Call it what it is\nopt.right.label [9f3cf3] = 挑明了说\n# en: \"So we're friends now,\" {npc} says. \"Officially.\" You shake on it, solemnly, then ruin it by laughing.\nopt.right.say.1 [eee457] = “那我们现在是朋友了，”{npc}说，“正式的。”你们郑重地握手，然后笑场毁掉了这份庄重。\n\n@event soc_become_close\n# en: The Kind of Friend You Call at 3am\ntitle [e891f8] = 凌晨三点可以去敲门的那种朋友\n# en: It's 3am. You can't sleep, and you've walked to {npc}'s door before you've even decided to. {They} open{s} it before you knock.\ntext [216ec9] = 凌晨三点。你睡不着，还没想好就已经走到了{npc}的门前。你还没敲门，{they}就把门打开了。\n# en: Two friends sitting on a dormitory floor at 3am wrapped in one blanket, empty mugs between them.\nscene [9f5741] = 凌晨三点，两个朋友裹着同一条毯子坐在宿舍地板上，中间摆着空杯子。\n# en: Apologise and go back to bed\nopt.left.label [f9def6] = 道个歉，回去睡觉\n# en: \"Don't be daft,\" {npc} says, but lets you go. In the morning there's tea outside your door.\nopt.left.say.1 [5078b4] = “别傻了。”{npc}说，但还是让你走了。早上，你门外放着一杯茶。\n# en: Talk until sunrise\nopt.right.label [f5afa4] = 聊到日出\n# en: You tell {them} things you haven't told anyone. {They} tell you things back. The sun comes up and neither of you notices.\nopt.right.say.1 [c821d1] = 你告诉了{them}从没告诉过任何人的事。{they}也告诉了你{their}心事。太阳升起来了，你们谁都没注意到。\n\n@event soc_secret\n# en: A Secret\ntitle [880a88] = 一个秘密\n# en: {npc} pulls you under the stairs. \"I need to tell someone or I'll burst. Swear you won't tell anyone. Not even the portraits.\"\ntext [556649] = {npc}把你拉到楼梯底下。“我得告诉一个人，不然我会憋炸的。发誓你谁也不说。连肖像画都不说。”\n# en: Two students huddled under a staircase, one whispering into the other's ear, a finger to their lips.\nscene [e33a93] = 两个学生挤在楼梯下，一个凑在另一个耳边低语，手指竖在唇边。\n# en: \"I'd rather not carry it.\"\nopt.left.label [a4f5da] = “我宁愿不背这个包袱。”\n# en: {npc} looks hurt, then relieved. \"Maybe that's better.\" You'll never know.\nopt.left.say.1 [8b5a46] = {npc}先是一脸受伤，然后松了口气。“也许这样更好。”你永远不会知道了。\n# en: \"I swear.\"\nopt.right.label [381a0e] = “我发誓。”\n# en: It's not a big secret. It's the kind that's only big to the person holding it. You hold it with {them}.\nopt.right.say.1 [a21861] = 不是什么大秘密。是那种只对守着它的人来说很大的秘密。你陪{them}一起守着。\n\n@event soc_argument\n# en: Words Exchanged\ntitle [260001] = 口角\n# en: It starts with something small — a borrowed book, a joke that landed wrong — and suddenly you and {npc} are arguing in the corridor, louder than either of you intended.\ntext [4ede37] = 起因是件小事——一本借走的书，一个说错时机的玩笑——突然间你和{npc}就在走廊里吵了起来，嗓门比你们俩想的都大。\n# en: Two students facing off in an empty corridor, one with arms crossed, the other mid-gesture, both flushed.\nscene [935b50] = 两个学生在空荡的走廊里对峙，一个抱着双臂，另一个正比划着，两人都涨红了脸。\n# en: Apologise first\nopt.left.label [9f3966] = 先道歉\n# en: \"I'm sorry.\" {npc} deflates. \"...Me too.\" It's over. It feels better than winning would have.\nopt.left.say.1 [47bc55] = “对不起。”{npc}一下子泄了气。“……我也是。”结束了。这比吵赢了感觉更好。\n# en: Stand your ground\nopt.right.label [3881f5] = 坚持立场\n# en: {npc} storms off. You were right. It doesn't feel as good as you'd hoped.\nopt.right.say.1 [23d1ff] = {npc}气冲冲地走了。你是对的。可感觉并没有你希望的那么好。\n# en: You argue it out until you're both laughing at how ridiculous it got. Somehow you're closer after.\nopt.right.say.2 [16b2d4] = 你们一直吵到两个人都笑了出来，笑这事有多荒唐。不知怎么的，吵完反而更亲近了。\n\n@event soc_village_trip\n# en: A Day in the Village\ntitle [1afb52] = 镇上一日\n# en: \"Barge to the village? Tea at Mabel's, a wander round the bookshops, back before curfew?\" {npc} is already wearing {their} coat.\ntext [07e032] = “坐驳船去镇上吗？去梅布尔茶馆喝茶，逛逛书店，宵禁前回来？”{npc}已经穿好外套了。\n# en: A cobbled village street with crooked shopfronts, two students sharing a paper cone of roasted nuts.\nscene [743ea4] = 鹅卵石铺就的小镇街道，歪歪扭扭的店面，两个学生分享着一纸筒烤坚果。\n# en: Not this week\nopt.left.label [0fe3ce] = 这周不行\n# en: {They} bring{s} you back a cinnamon bun anyway.\nopt.left.say.1 [bce668] = {they}还是给你带回来一个肉桂卷。\n# en: Grab your coat\nopt.right.label [b14325] = 抓起外套\n# en: Tea, buns, a bookshop cat that sits on {npc}'s feet. You almost miss the last barge, laughing.\nopt.right.say.1 [fca757] = 喝茶，吃面包，书店的猫趴在{npc}脚上。你们笑着差点错过最后一班驳船。\n\n@event soc_friend_sick\n# en: Sick Friend\ntitle [ac406d] = 生病的朋友\n# en: {npc} has Lake Fever and is miserable in the infirmary. Sister Amaranth says visitors are allowed, but she won't be responsible.\ntext [0a1413] = {npc}得了湖热病，在医务室里难受得要命。阿玛兰斯修女说可以探视，但出了事她不负责。\n# en: A student tucked into an infirmary bed under a mountain of blankets, a friend sitting beside them with a bowl of soup.\nscene [43529f] = 一个学生被埋在医务室病床的一堆毯子底下，一位朋友端着一碗汤坐在床边。\n# en: Send a card\nopt.left.label [72cab2] = 送张卡片\n# en: You send a card with a drawing of Sister Amaranth as a dragon. {npc} keeps it on {their} bedside table.\nopt.left.say.1 [eae564] = 你送了一张卡片，上面画着变成了龙的阿玛兰斯修女。{npc}把它摆在床头柜上。\n# en: Sit with them every evening\nopt.right.label [09fda2] = 每晚都去陪着\n# en: You keep {them} company for a week. Then you're in the next bed. You both find this hilarious.\nopt.right.say.1 [8c2cc5] = 你陪了{them}一个星期。然后你住进了隔壁床。你们俩都觉得这太好笑了。\n# en: You read aloud, badly, doing voices. {npc} recovers and claims it was the voices.\nopt.right.say.2 [55eed3] = 你给{them}念书，念得很烂，还学各种腔调。{npc}康复后坚称是那些腔调治好了{them}。\n\n@event soc_gift_given\n# en: A Small Gift\ntitle [9dafb3] = 一件小礼物\n# en: There's a small parcel on your pillow. A note in {npc}'s handwriting: \"Saw this and thought of you. Don't make it weird.\"\ntext [1d6ca6] = 你的枕头上放着一个小包裹。附着一张{npc}字迹的字条：“看见这个就想到了你。别想太多。”\n# en: A small wrapped parcel tied with ribbon left on a dormitory pillow, a pressed flower tucked into the bow.\nscene [5a37d3] = 宿舍枕头上一个系着丝带的小包裹，蝴蝶结里夹着一朵压花。\n# en: Thank {them} later\nopt.left.label [a0fa69] = 回头再谢{them}\n# en: It's a tiny brass compass that always points to the nearest teapot. You use it constantly.\nopt.left.say.1 [0451b8] = 是一个小小的黄铜罗盘，永远指向最近的茶壶。你天天都在用。\n# en: Get {them} something back (8 gold)\nopt.right.label [42a27b] = 回赠一份礼物（8 金币）\n# en: You find {them} a book {they}'d wanted for months. {npc}'s face when {they} open{s} it is worth twice the price.\nopt.right.say.1 [3ade33] = 你找到了一本{they}想要了好几个月的书。{npc}打开时的表情，值两倍的价钱。\n\n@event soc_drift\n# en: Drifting Apart\ntitle [73c51a] = 渐行渐远\n# en: You realise you haven't really talked to {npc} in weeks. You see {them} across the courtyard, laughing with people you don't know.\ntext [17b0c4] = 你发现自己已经好几周没好好跟{npc}说过话了。你看见{them}在庭院那头，和一群你不认识的人说笑。\n# en: A student glancing back across a busy courtyard at another who hasn't seen them, the space between them full of strangers.\nscene [e8800d] = 一个学生回头望向熙攘庭院另一端没看见自己的人，两人之间隔满了陌生人。\n# en: Let it be\nopt.left.label [71cc4c] = 顺其自然\n# en: People drift. It's nobody's fault. You still smile when you pass in the halls.\nopt.left.say.1 [8a48fa] = 人总会渐行渐远。不是谁的错。在走廊里擦肩而过时，你们仍会微笑。\n# en: Go over and say hello\nopt.right.label [cdae69] = 走过去打个招呼\n# en: \"There you are!\" {npc} says, as if you'd been the one missing. Maybe you had.\nopt.right.say.1 [1d0cee] = “你在这儿呀！”{npc}说，好像失踪的人是你。也许确实是。\n# en: You go over. It is friendly, and short, and you both say \"we should catch up\" in a way that means you won't.\nopt.right.say.2 [46fd68] = 你走过去。气氛很友好，也很短，你们都说了句“改天聚聚”，那语气意味着不会有改天。\n\n@event soc_rumour\n# en: Rumours About {npc}\ntitle [e3d846] = 关于{npc}的流言\n# en: A nasty rumour about {npc} is going round the common room. It's not true. You know it's not true. People are laughing.\ntext [bf28b6] = 公共休息室里流传着一条关于{npc}的恶毒流言。不是真的。你知道不是真的。大家在笑。\n# en: A cluster of students whispering behind their hands in a corridor, glancing toward someone just out of view.\nscene [78a3d1] = 一群学生在走廊里捂着嘴窃窃私语，朝视线外的某个人瞟去。\n# en: Let it blow over\nopt.left.label [962dcd] = 等它自己平息\n# en: It blows over. {npc} never finds out you heard it. Probably.\nopt.left.say.1 [fd8345] = 它平息了。{npc}永远不会知道你听到过。大概吧。\n# en: Shut it down\nopt.right.label [e3e364] = 当场制止\n# en: You make the rumour-monger look so foolish that the story dies on the spot. {npc} hears about it later and just squeezes your arm.\nopt.right.say.1 [78c724] = 你让造谣的人出尽洋相，流言当场就死了。{npc}后来听说了，只是轻轻捏了捏你的胳膊。\n# en: You make a scene and now there are rumours about you too. {npc} thinks you're an idiot. A loyal idiot.\nopt.right.say.2 [ea7565] = 你大闹了一场，现在也有关于你的流言了。{npc}觉得你是个傻瓜。一个忠诚的傻瓜。\n\n@event soc_homework_help\n# en: Desperate Homework\ntitle [b925b8] = 作业告急\n# en: {npc} waves a half-finished essay at you. \"It's due in an hour and I've written the title twice. Help.\"\ntext [600d7a] = {npc}朝你挥着一篇写了一半的论文。“一小时后就要交了，我只把标题写了两遍。救命。”\n# en: A student with ink on their face clutching a half-finished essay, another student leaning over to read it.\nscene [d955d2] = 一个脸上沾着墨水的学生攥着一篇半成品论文，另一个学生凑过来看。\n# en: Explain the concept\nopt.left.label [d8e8f3] = 讲解知识点\n# en: You explain it so clearly that {npc} finishes the essay with time to spare. {They} insist{s} you're a genius.\nopt.left.say.1 [cf80f6] = 你讲得清清楚楚，{npc}提前写完了论文。{they}坚称你是个天才。\n# en: You explain it so confusingly that you both end up more lost. You hand it in together, two confused people against the world.\nopt.left.say.2 [a0a001] = 你讲得一团乱麻，两个人都更糊涂了。你们一起交了上去——两个糊涂人对抗全世界。\n# en: \"Copy mine.\"\nopt.right.label [b2d7d3] = “抄我的吧。”\n# en: The professor notices two identical essays. You both get detention, and a lecture.\nopt.right.say.1 [bd43e1] = 教授发现了两篇一模一样的论文。你们俩都被关了禁闭，还挨了一顿训。\n# en: {npc} hands it in and gets a better mark than you did. Unbelievable.\nopt.right.say.2 [c22ac9] = {npc}交了上去，分数比你还高。难以置信。\n\n@event soc_faculty_advice\n# en: A Word of Advice\ntitle [594b8b] = 一句忠告\n# en: {npc} catches you after a lecture. \"You've been working hard. Or hardly working. I can never tell with your year. Some advice, if you'll take it?\"\ntext [890d50] = 讲座结束后{npc}叫住了你。“你一直很用功。或者说一直很用‘空’。你们这一届我总是分不清。给你点建议，听不听？”\n# en: A professor walking with a student along a covered walkway, pointing at something in an open book.\nscene [41385c] = 一位教授和一名学生沿着有顶棚的步道走着，指着一本摊开的书里的某处。\n# en: Politely decline\nopt.left.label [8a601d] = 礼貌地拒绝\n# en: \"Suit yourself.\" {They} walk{s} off. Faculty have long memories.\nopt.left.say.1 [19801b] = “随你。”{they}走开了。教职员的记性可都很好。\n# en: Listen carefully\nopt.right.label [1b6b4d] = 认真听\n# en: It's good advice. Annoyingly good. Your next essay is better for it.\nopt.right.say.1 [aeda53] = 是好建议。好得让人恼火。你的下一篇论文因此进步了不少。\n\n@event soc_spark\n# en: A Spark\ntitle [21e629] = 火花\n# en: You reach for the same quill as {npc}. Your hands touch. Neither of you lets go immediately. It's a perfectly ordinary quill.\ntext [563401] = 你和{npc}同时伸手去拿同一支羽毛笔。你们的手碰到了。谁也没有马上松开。那只是一支再普通不过的羽毛笔。\n# en: Two hands brushing as they reach for the same quill, both students freezing for a heartbeat.\nscene [d87696] = 两只手在伸向同一支羽毛笔时轻轻相触，两个学生都愣了一拍。\n# en: Laugh it off\nopt.left.label [29c02a] = 一笑了之\n# en: \"All yours,\" you say. {npc} laughs. The moment passes. Mostly.\nopt.left.say.1 [134287] = “你用吧。”你说。{npc}笑了。那一刻过去了。大体上。\n# en: Hold on a moment longer\nopt.right.label [04d88b] = 多握一会儿\n# en: {npc} looks up. Something in {their} face shifts. Then {they} let{s} go, a little pink, and the quill rolls under the desk.\nopt.right.say.1 [6bf6bf] = {npc}抬起头。{their}脸上有什么东西变了。然后{they}松开了手，脸有点红，羽毛笔滚到了桌子底下。\n# en: {npc} pulls back as if burned, and the rest of the lesson is very, very awkward.\nopt.right.say.2 [cb9381] = {npc}像被烫到一样缩回了手，那节课剩下的时间尴尬得要命。\n\n@event soc_date\n# en: An Invitation\ntitle [aba1c1] = 一次邀约\n# en: You could ask {npc} to the lantern show in the village this weekend. As friends. Or, you know. Not as friends.\ntext [ec7b1b] = 你可以邀请{npc}这个周末去镇上看灯笼表演。以朋友的身份。或者，你懂的。不只是朋友。\n# en: A student nervously holding out a hand-drawn ticket to the village's floating lantern show.\nscene [57c131] = 一个学生紧张地递出一张手绘的小镇浮灯表演门票。\n# en: Ask as friends\nopt.left.label [bd7c43] = 以朋友身份邀请\n# en: It's a lovely evening. You're both very careful about elbows.\nopt.left.say.1 [692d15] = 那是个美好的夜晚。你们俩都非常小心地不碰到对方的胳膊肘。\n# en: Ask as more than friends\nopt.right.label [e73a1b] = 以不只是朋友的身份邀请\n# en: {npc} says yes before you finish the sentence. You hold hands through the whole show and miss every lantern.\nopt.right.say.1 [ccf6c4] = 你话还没说完{npc}就答应了。整场表演你们都牵着手，一盏灯笼也没看见。\n# en: {npc} hesitates, then says {they}'d rather go as friends. It's a nice evening. It's a long evening.\nopt.right.say.2 [097e94] = {npc}犹豫了一下，说还是以朋友的身份去吧。那是个不错的夜晚。也是个漫长的夜晚。\n# en: {npc} says yes before you finish the sentence. You hold hands through the whole show and miss every lantern.\nopt.right.say.3 [ccf6c4] = 你话还没说完{npc}就答应了。整场表演你们都牵着手，一盏灯笼也没看见。\n# en: {npc} hesitates, then says {they}'d rather go as friends. It's a nice evening. It's a long evening.\nopt.right.say.4 [097e94] = {npc}犹豫了一下，说还是以朋友的身份去吧。那是个不错的夜晚。也是个漫长的夜晚。\n\n@event soc_confess_generic\n# en: Feelings, Spoken\ntitle [0fdaae] = 说出口的心意\n# en: On the bridge between the towers, the wind snatching at your robes, you realise you can't keep it in anymore. {npc} is looking at you like {they} know what you're about to say.\ntext [0fb936] = 在塔楼之间的桥上，风扯着你的长袍，你意识到自己再也藏不住了。{npc}看着你，好像已经知道你要说什么。\n# en: Two students standing very close on a windswept bridge between towers, one with a hand over their heart.\nscene [80f257] = 两个学生紧挨着站在塔楼之间风大的桥上，其中一人手按在心口。\n# en: Say nothing\nopt.left.label [d1151d] = 什么也不说\n# en: The moment passes. You walk back together, a careful inch apart.\nopt.left.say.1 [8f2183] = 那一刻过去了。你们一起往回走，小心地隔着一寸距离。\n# en: Tell {them}\nopt.right.label [3c60a0] = 告诉{them}\n# en: \"Finally,\" {npc} says, and kisses you, and the wind steals your breath anyway.\nopt.right.say.1 [bdde21] = “终于说了。”{npc}说着吻了你，而风依旧夺走了你的呼吸。\n# en: {npc} takes a long breath. \"I care about you,\" {they} say{s}. \"Just not like that. I'm sorry.\" The wind is very cold.\nopt.right.say.2 [fe43d9] = {npc}深吸了一口气。“我在乎你，”{they}说，“只是不是那种在乎。对不起。”风很冷。\n\n@event soc_partner_date\n# en: Date Night\ntitle [b6d8e3] = 约会之夜\n# en: {npc} has booked the window table at Mabel's. Candles. Snow outside. \"We're splitting the bill,\" {they} say{s}. \"Don't argue.\"\ntext [cdc59b] = {npc}订了梅布尔茶馆靠窗的位子。蜡烛。窗外飘着雪。“这顿我们平摊，”{they}说，“别跟我争。”\n# en: Two people at a tiny candlelit table in a window alcove of a village tea shop, snow falling outside.\nscene [7b9157] = 小镇茶馆窗边凹室里一张小小的烛光桌，两个人对坐，窗外飘着雪。\n# en: Talk about the future\nopt.left.label [4d8f04] = 聊聊未来\n# en: You talk about where you'll be in ten years. In every version, you're both there.\nopt.left.say.1 [c75a7f] = 你们聊起十年后会在哪里。每一个版本里，你们俩都在。\n# en: Talk about absolutely nothing\nopt.right.label [75d2d9] = 聊些完全没用的事\n# en: You argue for an hour about whether a hotdog is a sandwich. It is the best date of your life.\nopt.right.say.1 [3c61e8] = 你们争论了一个小时热狗到底算不算三明治。这是你这辈子最棒的一次约会。\n\n@event soc_partner_neglect\n# en: \"You're Always Busy\"\ntitle [46e97c] = “你总是很忙”\n# en: {npc} is waiting outside your door. \"I feel like I only see you in corridors,\" {they} say{s}. \"Do you even want this?\"\ntext [0e3f61] = {npc}等在你门外。“我觉得我只能在走廊里见到你，”{they}说，“你到底还想不想要这段感情？”\n# en: A person waiting alone at a table set for two, a candle burned low, the other chair empty.\nscene [b603a7] = 一个人独自坐在摆好两人份餐具的桌前，蜡烛快燃尽了，另一把椅子空着。\n# en: \"I'm sorry. I'll make time.\"\nopt.left.label [b59fcc] = “对不起。我会挤出时间的。”\n# en: You skip a study session for a long walk by the lake. It's the right call.\nopt.left.say.1 [8a05e5] = 你翘掉一次自习，陪{them}在湖边走了很久。这个决定是对的。\n# en: \"You know how busy I am.\"\nopt.right.label [a60981] = “你知道我有多忙。”\n# en: {npc} nods slowly. Something between you cools a degree.\nopt.right.say.1 [2bc62a] = {npc}缓缓点了点头。你们之间的什么东西，冷了一度。\n\n@event soc_breakup\n# en: The End of Something\ntitle [179272] = 某件事的结束\n# en: {npc} sits down at the far end of the bench. \"I think we both know,\" {they} say{s} quietly. \"This isn't working anymore.\"\ntext [c059c0] = {npc}在长椅的另一头坐下。“我想我们都心知肚明，”{they}轻声说，“这已经行不通了。”\n# en: Two people sitting on opposite ends of a bench in a grey garden, not looking at each other, rain beginning.\nscene [4b79cd] = 灰蒙蒙的花园里，两个人坐在长椅两端，谁也不看谁，开始下雨了。\n# en: Fight for it\nopt.left.label [7ed077] = 争取一下\n# en: You talk for hours. It's raw and honest and it hurts. And at the end, {npc} takes your hand again.\nopt.left.say.1 [207908] = 你们谈了几个小时。赤裸、坦诚，而且很痛。最后，{npc}又握住了你的手。\n# en: You try. It only makes it harder. In the end you're both crying, and it's over anyway.\nopt.left.say.2 [2de21e] = 你努力了。却只让一切更难。最后你们都哭了，而它终究还是结束了。\n# en: Let {them} go\nopt.right.label [185a66] = 放{them}走\n# en: You agree. It's terrible and gentle and right. {npc} kisses your forehead once before {they} leave{s}.\nopt.right.say.1 [e0897b] = 你同意了。这很糟糕，很温柔，也很对。{npc}离开前在你额头上吻了一下。\n\n@event soc_two_timing\n# en: Found Out\ntitle [f1af67] = 东窗事发\n# en: {npc} is holding two letters. One is to {them}. The other isn't. \"I found it in your bag,\" {they} say{s}, very quietly. Half the corridor has stopped to watch.\ntext [b63ccd] = {npc}手里拿着两封信。一封是写给{them}的。另一封不是。“我在你包里找到的，”{they}说，声音很轻。半条走廊的人都停下来看着。\n# en: A person holding up two crumpled love letters in a crowded corridor, the crowd parting around them.\nscene [28e97e] = 拥挤的走廊里，一个人举着两封揉皱的情书，人群在他们周围分开。\n# en: Beg forgiveness\nopt.left.label [b42d94] = 乞求原谅\n# en: {npc} doesn't forgive you. But {they} don't leave. Not yet. You're on borrowed time.\nopt.left.say.1 [27d6d1] = {npc}没有原谅你。但{they}也没有离开。暂时没有。你在借来的时间里过日子。\n# en: {npc} tears both letters in half and walks away. Everyone knows by dinner.\nopt.left.say.2 [3f568e] = {npc}把两封信都撕成两半，转身离开。晚饭时所有人都知道了。\n# en: Say nothing\nopt.right.label [d1151d] = 一言不发\n# en: Your silence is an answer. {npc} drops the letters at your feet and leaves. The story is all over the academy.\nopt.right.say.1 [855b96] = 你的沉默就是答案。{npc}把信扔在你脚边，走了。这件事传遍了整个学院。\n\n@event soc_ex_encounter\n# en: Awkward\ntitle [8ea9c0] = 尴尬\n# en: You turn into the library aisle and there's {npc}. Your ex. Holding the same book you came for. Neither of you moves.\ntext [320159] = 你拐进图书馆的一条过道，{npc}就在那儿。你的前任。手里拿着你正要找的那本书。你们谁也没动。\n# en: Two people coming face to face in a narrow library aisle, both holding books, neither moving.\nscene [0292af] = 两个人在图书馆狭窄的过道里迎面相遇，手里都拿着书，谁也没动。\n# en: Turn around and leave\nopt.left.label [a746c5] = 转身离开\n# en: You find a different book in a different aisle and read the same paragraph six times.\nopt.left.say.1 [a75b45] = 你在另一条过道找了另一本书，把同一段读了六遍。\n# en: Say hello like a person\nopt.right.label [06a265] = 像个正常人一样打招呼\n# en: It's awkward for one minute, then it isn't. You end up laughing about the time the dummy caught fire. Friends, maybe. Really.\nopt.right.say.1 [9dcf0b] = 尴尬了一分钟，然后就不尴尬了。你们最后笑着聊起那次假人着火的事。也许能做朋友。真的。\n# en: \"Hello.\" \"Hello.\" You both stand there. Then you both leave. It's a start.\nopt.right.say.2 [f4910b] = “你好。”“你好。”你们俩站在那儿。然后各自离开。算是个开始。\n\n@event soc_rival_taunt\n# en: A Public Taunt\ntitle [0b1cd5] = 当众嘲讽\n# en: In the middle of dinner, {npc} loudly recounts your most embarrassing moment of the term. People laugh. {They} look{s} very pleased.\ntext [2c1a0a] = 晚饭吃到一半，{npc}大声讲起你这学期最丢人的一幕。大家都笑了。{they}看起来非常得意。\n# en: A smirking student surrounded by a small audience, pointing at someone across a crowded dining hall.\nscene [fc4e76] = 一个得意洋洋的学生被一小群观众围着，隔着拥挤的餐厅指向某人。\n# en: Ignore {them}\nopt.left.label [c17487] = 不理{them}\n# en: The laughter dies down. You eat your soup with as much dignity as soup allows.\nopt.left.say.1 [4234da] = 笑声渐渐平息。你以喝汤所能允许的最大尊严喝完了汤。\n# en: Fire back\nopt.right.label [edc214] = 反唇相讥\n# en: Your comeback is so good that someone at the far table applauds. {npc} goes scarlet.\nopt.right.say.1 [e2e5b5] = 你的回击太漂亮了，远处那桌有人鼓起掌来。{npc}满脸通红。\n# en: Your comeback comes out as a squeak. The laughter doubles.\nopt.right.say.2 [992f30] = 你的回击变成了一声尖叫。笑声翻了一倍。\n\n@event soc_make_peace\n# en: Burying the Hatchet\ntitle [91164f] = 握手言和\n# en: {npc} finds you after class, scuffing {their} shoes. \"This whole rivalry thing. It's getting tiring. Truce?\"\ntext [d5620e] = 下课后{npc}找到你，鞋尖蹭着地面。“这整个当对手的事儿。有点累了。休战？”\n# en: Two former rivals shaking hands on a dueling green at sunset, wands tucked away.\nscene [f9c463] = 夕阳下的决斗草坪，两个昔日对手收起魔杖握手。\n# en: Keep the edge\nopt.left.label [a15600] = 保持锋芒\n# en: \"Thought so,\" {npc} says, half-smiling. The game goes on.\nopt.left.say.1 [4e5e1c] = “我就知道。”{npc}半笑着说。较量继续。\n# en: Shake on it\nopt.right.label [982713] = 握手为定\n# en: You shake. It's strange — you know {them} better than most of your friends. Maybe that's the point.\nopt.right.say.1 [e1ff36] = 你们握了手。说来奇怪——你对{them}的了解，比对大多数朋友都深。也许这正是意义所在。\n\n@event soc_rival_bet\n# en: The Wager\ntitle [5a804e] = 赌约\n# en: \"Eight crowns says I outscore you in the next Arcana practical,\" {npc} says, slapping coins on the table. A crowd forms.\ntext [2cae36] = “八克朗，赌下次奥术实践课我分数比你高。”{npc}把硬币拍在桌上。一群人围了过来。\n# en: Two students slapping coins onto a table between them, a crowd gathering, a chalked tally on the wall.\nscene [8dd838] = 两个学生把硬币拍在中间的桌上，人群聚拢，墙上用粉笔记着比分。\n# en: Decline the bet\nopt.left.label [99b979] = 不赌\n# en: \"Thought so.\" {npc} pockets the coins. You decide not to care. You care.\nopt.left.say.1 [556d0b] = “我就知道。”{npc}收起了硬币。你决定不在乎。你很在乎。\n# en: Take the bet\nopt.right.label [8a4d52] = 接下赌约\n# en: You win by a hair. {npc} pays up with a theatrical bow. \"Next time.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [e1aac3] = 你以毫厘之差赢了。{npc}夸张地鞠了一躬，付了钱。“下次再说。”\n# en: You lose by a hair. \"Next time,\" you say. {npc} grins. \"Next time.\"\nopt.right.say.2 [8023a9] = 你以毫厘之差输了。“下次再说。”你说。{npc}咧嘴一笑。“下次再说。”\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/states/states.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/states/states.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@state origin_noble\n# en: Minor Nobility\nname [ccd408] = 小贵族出身\n# en: A family name that opens doors and an allowance that arrives every month — with letters attached.\ndesc [f16745] = 一个能敲开大门的姓氏，外加每月准时到账的零花钱——附带家书。\n# en: Comfortable: savings and a monthly allowance. You will rarely need to work.\nmoney [544741] = 宽裕：有积蓄，每月还有零用钱。你很少需要打工。\n\n@state origin_scholar\n# en: Scholarship Student\nname [036234] = 奖学金学生\n# en: The Crown pays half your tuition, and a little more while your grades hold.\ndesc [2a2f58] = 王室为你支付一半学费；成绩保持住的话，还会多给一点。\n# en: Tight: half tuition, and a small stipend while your GPA stays at 2.0 or above. Expect to work some terms.\nmoney [a7650b] = 拮据：学费减半，GPA 保持在 2.0 及以上时有一小笔津贴。有些学期需要打工。\n\n@state origin_hedge\n# en: Hedge-Witch's Grandchild\nname [b36bba] = 乡野女巫的孙辈\n# en: Raised among drying herbs and muttered charms. Proper magic still feels a little too proper.\ndesc [3e1ecc] = 在晾晒的草药和念叨的咒语间长大。正统魔法对你来说，总还是有点太“正统”了。\n# en: Poor: a few crowns and no allowance. You will need a job from the first term.\nmoney [51d12c] = 贫穷：只有几枚克朗，没有零用钱。你从第一个学期起就需要一份工作。\n\n@state origin_street\n# en: Street-Raised\nname [9b8f09] = 街头长大\n# en: You learned to read crowds before you learned to read runes.\ndesc [8551b3] = 你学会读懂人群，比学会读懂符文还早。\n# en: Poor: almost nothing in your pockets. You will need a job from the first term.\nmoney [7429cf] = 贫穷：口袋里几乎一无所有。你从第一个学期起就需要一份工作。\n\n@state origin_merchant\n# en: Merchant's Child\nname [a051a5] = 商人之子\n# en: Everything has a price, and you were taught to haggle it down.\ndesc [c5b5c4] = 万物皆有价，而你从小就学会了砍价。\n# en: Comfortable: the largest purse, and a small monthly share from the company.\nmoney [1cd550] = 宽裕：钱袋最鼓，公司每月还会分你一小份。\n\n@state class_pyromancy\n# en: Elective: Pyromancy\nname [bc2cb8] = 选修：火焰术\n# en: Fire answers to will, not wishes. Singed eyebrows are considered a rite of passage.\ndesc [f7cdfd] = 火焰回应意志，而非愿望。烧焦眉毛被视为一种成人礼。\n\n@state class_tidecalling\n# en: Elective: Tidecalling\nname [25e58c] = 选修：唤潮术\n# en: Lessons on the lake shore, bending water and patience in equal measure.\ndesc [550653] = 在湖岸上课，操纵湖水与磨练耐心，二者同等重要。\n\n@state class_illusion\n# en: Elective: Illusion & Glamour\nname [b30964] = 选修：幻术与魅影\n# en: The art of making people see what you want. Professor Vey insists it is not lying.\ndesc [7aa865] = 让人看见你想让他们看见的东西的艺术。维伊教授坚称这不算撒谎。\n\n@state class_alchemy\n# en: Elective: Practical Alchemy\nname [8a43a8] = 选修：实用炼金\n# en: Brewing, distilling and the occasional controlled explosion.\ndesc [4cfafb] = 熬煮、蒸馏，以及偶尔一场受控的爆炸。\n\n@state class_divination\n# en: Elective: Divination\nname [14ec75] = 选修：占卜学\n# en: Tea leaves, star charts and the Mirror of Next Tuesday.\ndesc [531e06] = 茶叶、星图，还有“下周二之镜”。\n\n@state class_artifice\n# en: Elective: Artifice\nname [20fedd] = 选修：奇械术\n# en: Enchanting objects to do what they were never meant to do.\ndesc [1d11f0] = 给物品施加魔法，让它们做本不该做的事。\n\n@state class_beastcraft\n# en: Elective: Beastcraft\nname [664aeb] = 选修：驭兽学\n# en: Familiars, griffon husbandry and dodging the manticore pen.\ndesc [d23187] = 魔宠、狮鹫饲养，以及绕开蝎尾狮的围栏。\n\n@state class_astromancy\n# en: Elective: Astromancy\nname [58b65d] = 选修：星象学\n# en: Midnight lectures on the observatory roof. You will learn the stars; you will lose sleep.\ndesc [9aeff9] = 天文台屋顶上的午夜讲座。你会认识星星，也会失去睡眠。\n\n@state class_healing\n# en: Elective: Healing Arts\nname [9056e4] = 选修：治愈术\n# en: Mending flesh with light and needle. Sister Amaranth expects perfection.\ndesc [1f8e64] = 以光与针缝合血肉。阿玛兰斯修女要求完美。\n\n@state class_umbral\n# en: Elective: Umbral Theory\nname [44e968] = 选修：暗影理论\n# en: Restricted. The study of shadow, death and what lies behind both. Strictly theoretical. Mostly.\ndesc [ddfba6] = 受限课程。研究阴影、死亡，以及二者背后的东西。纯属理论。大体上是。\n\n@state club_dueling\n# en: Club: Dueling Circle\nname [ba1685] = 社团：决斗社\n# en: Bruises, glory and the Spring Tournament.\ndesc [284ecb] = 淤青、荣耀，还有春季锦标赛。\n\n@state club_choir\n# en: Club: Choir of the Spheres\nname [7a5e02] = 社团：天球合唱团\n# en: Harmonies that make the candles flicker in time.\ndesc [f7c729] = 和声能让烛火随着节拍摇曳。\n\n@state club_skyrace\n# en: Club: Skyrace Squad\nname [ffb40f] = 社团：天空竞速队\n# en: Brooms, hoops and screaming crowds.\ndesc [727b30] = 飞帚、光环和尖叫的人群。\n\n@state club_ledger\n# en: Club: The Lumen Ledger\nname [c5d754] = 社团：《流明纪事报》社\n# en: The student paper. Deadlines, scoops and enemies.\ndesc [0bbdce] = 学生报纸。截稿日、独家新闻和仇家。\n\n@state club_garden\n# en: Club: Verdant Circle\nname [b7b687] = 社团：青翠社\n# en: Greenhouse afternoons with mandrakes that sing off-key.\ndesc [443e64] = 温室里的午后时光，还有唱歌跑调的曼德拉草。\n\n@state club_stargazers\n# en: Club: Stargazers\nname [82b69d] = 社团：观星社\n# en: Telescopes, cocoa and constellations nobody else can name.\ndesc [7d729f] = 望远镜、热可可，以及谁也叫不出名字的星座。\n\n@state club_cartographers\n# en: Club: Midnight Cartographers\nname [6737ac] = 社团：午夜制图社\n# en: A club that officially does not exist, mapping corridors that officially do not either.\ndesc [e99289] = 一个官方上并不存在的社团，绘制着官方上同样不存在的走廊。\n\n@state job_library\n# en: Job: Library Assistant\nname [79ff99] = 工作：图书馆助理\n# en: Reshelving books that bite. Quiet hours, modest pay.\ndesc [c1eafc] = 把会咬人的书放回书架。工时安静，薪水微薄。\n\n@state job_tavern\n# en: Job: The Gilded Newt\nname [73eefc] = 工作：镀金蝾螈酒馆\n# en: Serving cider in the village tavern. Good tips, sore feet.\ndesc [598a90] = 在镇上的酒馆端苹果酒。小费不错，脚很酸。\n\n@state job_apothecary\n# en: Job: Apothecary Clerk\nname [1e38cf] = 工作：药铺店员\n# en: Labelling jars at Pell & Daughters. Do not lick anything.\ndesc [ddcc95] = 在佩尔父女药铺给瓶瓶罐罐贴标签。什么都别舔。\n\n@state job_stables\n# en: Job: Familiar Stables\nname [ecb6b5] = 工作：魔宠马厩\n# en: Feeding hippogriffs and mucking out things that breathe smoke.\ndesc [1c9431] = 喂鹰头马身有翼兽，清扫会喷烟的东西。\n\n@state job_tutor\n# en: Job: First-Year Tutor\nname [c57f7a] = 工作：一年级辅导员\n# en: Explaining the difference between a hex and a jinx, again.\ndesc [0f630b] = 再一次解释“恶咒”和“小咒”的区别。\n\n@state job_courier\n# en: Job: Broom Courier\nname [c82174] = 工作：飞帚信使\n# en: Parcels across the lake at full speed. Pays well. Hurts often.\ndesc [216f36] = 全速飞越湖面送包裹。报酬丰厚。经常受伤。\n\n@state hungry\n# en: Hungry\nname [0ae59c] = 饥饿\n# en: The board went unpaid this month. Bread rolls pocketed from the refectory only go so far.\ndesc [67e035] = 这个月的伙食费没交上。从膳堂顺来的面包卷也只能撑这么久。\n\n@state sick\n# en: Lake Fever\nname [977d44] = 湖热病\n# en: Shivers, sniffles and a nose that glows faintly blue.\ndesc [ca2430] = 发抖、流鼻涕，鼻头还泛着淡淡的蓝光。\n\n@state injured\n# en: Injured\nname [624ada] = 受伤\n# en: Something is bruised, sprained or bent the wrong way.\ndesc [316db4] = 身上有什么地方青了、扭了，或者弯错了方向。\n\n@state exhausted\n# en: Exhausted\nname [34cdec] = 精疲力竭\n# en: You could sleep standing up. You might have.\ndesc [6c252f] = 你站着都能睡着。说不定已经睡过了。\n\n@state inspired\n# en: Inspired\nname [b91753] = 灵感迸发\n# en: Ideas arrive faster than you can write them down.\ndesc [ba8e50] = 想法涌来的速度比你记下来还快。\n\n@state heartbroken\n# en: Heartbroken\nname [a560a2] = 心碎\n# en: Songs are suddenly about you. All of them.\ndesc [dd2311] = 突然之间，所有的歌唱的都是你。每一首。\n\n@state slacking\n# en: Skipping Classes\nname [169826] = 逃课中\n# en: Freedom! Also: falling behind.\ndesc [e0a116] = 自由！以及：进度落后。\n\n@state detention\n# en: Detention\nname [49189b] = 禁闭\n# en: Scrubbing cauldrons under the eye of a prefect.\ndesc [e387b0] = 在级长的监视下刷坩埚。\n\n@state probation\n# en: Academic Probation\nname [e41d9e] = 留校察看\n# en: Your marks are below the line. Two terms under a 1.0 and the Disciplinary Board sits. A GPA of 2.0 clears it.\ndesc [9f9702] = 你的成绩跌破了底线。连续两个学期GPA 低于 1.0，纪律委员会就要开会了。GPA 回到 2.0 即可解除。\n\n@state in_debt\n# en: Debt to Grisk\nname [d38823] = 欠格里斯克的债\n# en: The goblin moneylender takes 12 crowns a month until the debt is cleared. Ignore a missed payment and he adds another.\ndesc [37f244] = 地精放贷人每月拿走 12 克朗，直到债务还清。漏还一笔却置之不理，他就再加一个月。\n\n@state famous\n# en: Talk of the Academy\nname [a60529] = 学院热议人物\n# en: Strangers know your name. Some of them even say it nicely.\ndesc [e6822e] = 陌生人都知道你的名字。其中一些人提起时甚至还挺客气。\n\n@state hiccup_hex\n# en: Hiccup Hex\nname [c18908] = 打嗝恶咒\n# en: Every hiccup releases a small, harmless, extremely embarrassing firework.\ndesc [14430d] = 每打一个嗝，就会冒出一朵小小的、无害的、极其丢人的烟花。\n\n@state blessed\n# en: Maud's Blessing\nname [d2410d] = 莫德的祝福\n# en: A sprig of rosemary in your pocket that never wilts.\ndesc [7af4e4] = 口袋里一枝永不枯萎的迷迭香。\n\n@state familiar_fox\n# en: Familiar: Ember\nname [7343af] = 魔宠：余烬\n# en: A fox-spirit with a smouldering tail who has decided you are hers.\ndesc [8b3cfa] = 一只尾巴冒着余火的狐灵，她已经认定你是她的人了。\n\n@state familiar_owl\n# en: Familiar: Clockwork Owl\nname [e993d5] = 魔宠：发条猫头鹰\n# en: Mira's gift. It hoots the hour, mostly correctly, and remembers your homework.\ndesc [f65a4c] = 米拉的礼物。它会报时（大多准确），还会记得你的作业。\n\n@state training\n# en: Tournament Training\nname [5aecec] = 锦标赛特训\n# en: Dawn drills on the dueling green.\ndesc [ba8ab4] = 在决斗草坪上的黎明操练。\n\n@state wakeful\n# en: Draught of Wakefulness\nname [e9272f] = 清醒药剂\n# en: You haven't blinked in eleven hours. Your notes are immaculate.\ndesc [8764b7] = 你已经十一个小时没眨眼了。笔记无可挑剔。\n\n@state haunted\n# en: Haunted Dormitory\nname [42efc1] = 闹鬼的宿舍\n# en: Something rearranges your socks at night.\ndesc [88b052] = 夜里有什么东西在重新整理你的袜子。\n\n@state watched\n# en: Under Bram's Eye\nname [53b10a] = 被布拉姆盯上\n# en: The prefect is watching you. Specifically, and with relish.\ndesc [03458f] = 那位级长正盯着你。专门盯着，而且乐在其中。\n\n@state umbral_curse\n# en: Umbral Curse\nname [11a418] = 暗影诅咒\n# en: A cold that lives under your skin. Something in the restricted section followed you home.\ndesc [3ffb2e] = 一股寒意住在你的皮肤下。禁书区里有什么东西跟着你回来了。\n\n@state hollow_dreams\n# en: Hollow Dreams\nname [b977da] = 空洞之梦\n# en: Every night: a black star, a door beneath the lake, a voice that knows your name.\ndesc [b0df12] = 每一夜：一颗黑色的星，一扇湖底之门，一个知道你名字的声音。\n\n@state star_mark\n# en: The Star-Mark\nname [b7b256] = 星痕\n# en: A pale, star-shaped mark on your palm that hums near old magic.\ndesc [c1678d] = 掌心一枚苍白的星形印记，靠近古老魔法时会嗡嗡作响。\n\n@state summer_away\n# en: Summer Away\nname [77bf0e] = 离校度暑\n# en: Away from the academy for the summer recess. No rent, no classes.\ndesc [abac1f] = 暑假期间离开学院。不交房租，不用上课。\n\n@state summer_work\n# en: Summer Work\nname [2d8ae9] = 暑期打工\n# en: A summer of honest (and occasionally dishonest) labour.\ndesc [4dd36c] = 一个诚实（偶尔不太诚实）劳动的夏天。\n\n@state frog_voice\n# en: Croaking\nname [5daa73] = 呱呱叫\n# en: A botched transfiguration left you with the voice of a bullfrog.\ndesc [02c975] = 一次失败的变形术，让你的嗓音变成了牛蛙。\n\n@state garden_plot\n# en: Garden Plot\nname [7ba473] = 一方菜园\n# en: A little square of earth behind the greenhouses that is yours. Things grow; so do you.\ndesc [3a1dd5] = 温室后面一小块属于你的泥土。东西在长，你也在长。\n\n@state pen_pal\n# en: Pen Pal in Thornwick\nname [7e85f7] = 荆木学院的笔友\n# en: A student at the rival academy who writes you long, funny letters full of terrible gossip.\ndesc [b0b239] = 对手学院的一名学生，常给你写又长又好笑、满是糟糕八卦的信。\n\n@state frost_ward\n# en: Ilse's Frost-Ward\nname [302a95] = 伊尔泽的霜之护符\n# en: A sliver of Northern winter that Ilse sewed into your collar. Cold things think twice about you.\ndesc [ca24da] = 伊尔泽缝进你衣领里的一缕北境寒冬。冰冷的东西靠近你之前都会犹豫一下。\n\n@state plan_castle\n# en: Break: at the castle\nname [961ce0] = 假期：留在城堡\n# en: A skeleton crew, empty corridors and secrets. Board costs 6 crowns a month.\ndesc [c6616d] = 只剩少数留守的职员、空荡的走廊和秘密。每月食宿 6 克朗。\n\n@state plan_home\n# en: Break: at home\nname [5a492e] = 假期：回家\n# en: Family, old friends and free board. The days are slower here.\ndesc [8e2284] = 家人、老朋友和免费食宿。这里的日子过得慢些。\n\n@state gig_grounds\n# en: Holiday gig: the grounds\nname [1908bb] = 假期打工：庭园\n# en: Working the grounds for Old Maud this break. Good pay, sore hands.\ndesc [c0cf6b] = 这个假期跟老莫德打理庭园。工钱不错，手会酸。\n\n@state gig_kitchens\n# en: Holiday gig: the kitchens\nname [78fcf0] = 假期打工：后厨\n# en: Working the Refectory kitchens this break. Good pay, short temper.\ndesc [b6d78e] = 这个假期在膳堂后厨帮工。工钱不错，脾气会变短。\n\n@state plan_work\n# en: Break: working full-time\nname [3237e0] = 假期：全职打工\n# en: Working on campus through the break: a holiday gig, and board as usual. Tiring.\ndesc [91b8eb] = 整个假期在校内打工：一份假期零工，食宿照常付。很累。\n\n@state plan_internship\n# en: Break: internship\nname [4871d8] = 假期：实习\n# en: A research placement away from the castle, with a stipend and a supervisor.\ndesc [f66cf3] = 离开城堡的研究实习，有津贴，也有导师。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/ui.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) interface strings.\n# Keys come from UI_EN in src/i18n.js. Keep {placeholders}; \"_html\" keys may contain <b> tags.\n# pronoun.* keys fill {they} {them} {their} {theirs} {are} {s} in content text (empty is allowed).\n\n@ui\n# en: English\nlang.name = 简体中文\n# en: Language\nlang.label = 语言\n# en: Your name\ntitle.yourName = 你的名字\n# en: Seed (optional)\ntitle.seed = 随机种子（可选）\n# en: random\ntitle.seedPlaceholder = 随机\n# en: Begin your first year\ntitle.begin = 开始你的第一学年\n# en: Who were you before?\norigin.heading = 来这里之前，你是谁？\n# en: Where you come from decides what you start with, and how hard money will be.\norigin.intro = 你的出身决定了你起步时有什么，以及钱会有多难挣。\n# en: Hard mode for money: plan on working a job most terms.\norigin.poorWarning = 手头最紧：做好大多数学期都要打工的准备。\n# en: Back\norigin.back = 返回\n# en: Continue · {info}\ntitle.continue = 继续 · {info}\n# en: {name} — {month}, Year {year}\ntitle.saveInfo = {name} — 第 {year} 年{month}\n# en: <b>Swipe</b> each card left or right (or press ← / →) to choose. Some moments open a <b>menu</b> with more options. Keep your <b>Health, Mood, Gold and Standing</b> above zero for three years — 36 months — and see who you become. Glowing dots above a meter show what a choice will touch.\ntitle.howto_html = 左右<b>滑动</b>卡牌（或按 ← / →）做出选择。某些时刻会弹出<b>菜单</b>，提供更多选项。在三年——共 36 个月——里，让你的<b>健康、心情、金币和学业</b>都保持在零以上，看看你会成为怎样的人。属性上方亮起的光点，提示这个选择会影响什么。\n# en: Wren, Ash, Rowan, Elowen, Fenn, Isla, Corin, Sable, Theo, Marlo, Juniper, Ezra\ntitle.names = 雷恩, 艾什, 罗文, 埃洛温, 芬恩, 艾拉, 科林, 塞布尔, 西奥, 马洛, 朱妮珀, 以斯拉\n# en: Wren\ntitle.defaultName = 雷恩\n# en: Character & people\ntop.people = 角色与人物\n# en: Toggle illustration notes\ntop.notes = 显示／隐藏插图说明\n# en: Save & return to title\ntop.quit = 保存并返回标题\n# en: Language\ntop.lang = 语言\n# en: YEAR {year} · MONTH {turn} OF {max}\ndate.sub = 第 {year} 年 · 第 {turn} / {max} 月\n# en: You\ntab.you = 你\n# en: People\ntab.people = 人物\n# en: Journal\ntab.journal = 日志\n# en: Conditions & commitments\nside.conditions = 状态与承诺\n# en: Nothing yet.\nside.nothing = 暂时还没有。\n# en: {n} mo\nside.monthsLeft = 剩 {n} 月\n# en: You haven't really met anyone yet.\nside.noPeople = 你还没真正认识谁。\n# en: Relationship {n}\nside.relationship = 关系 {n}\n# en: Spark {n}\nside.romance = 心动 {n}\n# en: Spells\nside.spells = 咒语\n# en: {n} mana\nside.spellCost = {n} 法力\n# en: Your story is yet unwritten.\nside.emptyJournal = 你的故事尚未落笔。\n# en: {month} · Y{year}\njournal.when = 第 {year} 年 · {month}\n# en: friend\nbond.friend = 朋友\n# en: close\nbond.close = 挚友\n# en: rival\nbond.rival = 对手\n# en: nemesis\nbond.nemesis = 宿敌\n# en: partner\nbond.partner = 恋人\n# en: ex\nbond.ex = 前任\n# en: Class\ntag.class = 课程\n# en: Club\ntag.club = 社团\n# en: Work\ntag.job = 打工\n# en: Social\ntag.social = 社交\n# en: Romance\ntag.romance = 恋爱\n# en: Study\ntag.study = 学业\n# en: Mystery\ntag.mystery = 谜团\n# en: Danger\ntag.danger = 危险\n# en: Crisis\ntag.crisis = 危机\n# en: Festival\ntag.festival = 节庆\n# en: Coin\ntag.money = 钱财\n# en: Grounds\ntag.nature = 校园\n# en: Home\ntag.home = 日常\n# en: Rivalry\ntag.rival = 较量\n# en: Exam\ntag.exam = 考试\n# en: Chronicle\ntag.story = 编年\n# en: Magic\ntag.magic = 魔法\n# en: Academy life\ntag.default = 学院生活\n# en: consequence\ncard.consequence = 后果\n# en: Illustration: {text}\ncard.illustration = 插图：{text}\n# en: Not available\ncard.notAvailable = 无法选择\n# en: {skill} check · {p}%\ncheck.button = {skill}检定 · {p}%\n# en: {skill} check ({p}%) · {result}\ncheck.chip = {skill}检定（{p}%）· {result}\n# en: success\ncheck.success = 成功\n# en: failed\ncheck.failed = 失败\n# en: {name} {delta}\nchip.rel = {name} {delta}\n# en: {track} performance {delta}\nchip.perf = {track}表现 {delta}\n# en: {track} progress {delta}\nchip.progress = {track}进度 {delta}\n# en: {track} study {delta}\nchip.study = {track}备考 {delta}\n# en: {name} (spark) {delta}\nchip.love = {name}（心动）{delta}\n# en: You met {name}.\nnote.meet = 你认识了{name}。\n# en: {name}: {status}\nnote.bond = {name}：{status}\n# en: {name} now sees you as a rival: respect with an edge, which can sit alongside friendship\nnote.bondRival = {name} 把你视为对手：是尊重，也带着锋芒，可以与友谊并存\n# en: + {state}\nnote.gain = + {state}\n# en: − {state}\nnote.lose = − {state}\n# en: New spell: {spell} {level}\nnote.spell = 新咒语：{spell} {level}\n# en: You learned something about {name}.\nnote.fact = 你对{name}多了一分了解。\n# en: {kind} ({p}%) · {result}\ncheck.challengeChip = {kind}（{p}%）· {result}\n# en: {kind} · {p}%\ncheck.challengeButton = {kind} · {p}%\n# en: Combat · {p}% · −{cost} mana\ncheck.combatButton = 战斗 · {p}% · 法力 −{cost}\n# en: Exam: {skill} · pass {p}% · A or B {top}%\ncheck.examButton = 考试：{skill} · 及格 {p}% · A 或 B {top}%\n# en: wild\nswing.wild = 起伏大\n# en: steady\nswing.steady = 稳定\n# en: Combat\nchallenge.combat = 战斗\n# en: Tests {skill}\ncourse.tests = 考查{skill}\n# en: {tier} exam, {p}% pass today\ncourse.exam = {tier}考试，按现在算及格率 {p}%\n# en: teaches {spells}\ncourse.teaches = 教授{spells}\n# en: About {gold} crowns a shift · {n} shifts a term\njob.outlook = 每班约 {gold} 克朗 · 每学期 {n} 班\n# en: Leaves {from}: your {rank} rank and {perf} performance; you start here as new\ntrack.switch = 将离开{from}：你的{rank}职位和 {perf} 表现；在这里从新人做起\n# en: Medium\ntier.medium = 中等\n# en: Hard\ntier.hard = 困难\n# en: Heroic\ntier.heroic = 英雄级\n# en: Duel\nchallenge.duel = 决斗\n# en: Negotiation\nchallenge.negotiate = 交涉\n# en: Deception\nchallenge.deceive = 欺瞒\n# en: Exam\nchallenge.exam = 考试\n# en: Research\nchallenge.research = 研究\n# en: Brewing\nchallenge.brew = 调制\n# en: Enchanting\nchallenge.enchant = 附魔\n# en: Feat\nchallenge.feat = 体能\n# en: Spellwork\nchallenge.spell = 施法\n# en: win\nchallenge.win = 胜\n# en: partial\nchallenge.partial = 小胜\n# en: lose\nchallenge.lose = 负\n# en: {spell} {level} · {cost} mana\nspell.button = {spell} {level} · {cost} 法力\n# en: Not enough mana\nspell.short = 法力不足\n# en: Something follows from this.\npreview.follows = 这会引出后续。\n# en: Your saved game was from an older version of the game and can no longer be loaded. Its run log was kept.\ntitle.oldSave = 你的存档来自旧版本游戏，已无法读取。它的游戏记录已被保留。\n# en: From an older version; can’t be loaded\nslots.tooOld = 旧版本存档，无法读取\n# en: tap or swipe to continue\noutcome.tap = 轻点或滑动继续\n# en: Continue\noutcome.continue = 继续\n# en: Year {year} · Month {turn} of {max}\nmonth.header = 第 {year} 年 · 第 {turn} / {max} 月\n# en: This month\nmonth.thisMonth = 本月\n# en: Begin {month}\nmonth.begin = 开始{month}\n# en: Continue\nmonth.continue = 继续\n# en: The leaves turn. A new term begins.\nseason.1 = 落叶纷飞，新学期开始了。\n# en: Lanterns over the lake.\nseason.2 = 灯笼飘过湖面。\n# en: Fog creeps up the stairwells.\nseason.3 = 雾气爬上了楼梯间。\n# en: Frost on the windows; exams in the air.\nseason.4 = 窗上结霜，考试的气息弥漫开来。\n# en: Deep winter. The Midwinter Ball approaches.\nseason.5 = 隆冬时节。仲冬舞会近了。\n# en: The thaw, and a new term.\nseason.6 = 冰雪消融，新学期又至。\n# en: Storms roll in off the mountains.\nseason.7 = 风暴从山那边滚滚而来。\n# en: The lawns are green again. Tournament season.\nseason.8 = 草坪又绿了。锦标赛的季节到了。\n# en: Blossom on every bough.\nseason.9 = 枝头开满了花。\n# en: High summer heat — and the Grand Examinations.\nseason.10 = 盛夏酷暑——还有年度大考。\n# en: Summer recess. The towers empty.\nseason.11 = 暑假到了。塔楼空了下来。\n# en: The last weeks of summer. Stars begin to fall.\nseason.12 = 夏天的最后几周。星星开始坠落。\n# en: Your story ends early · {month}, Year {year}\nending.early = 你的故事提前落幕 · 第 {year} 年{month}\n# en: Graduation · Ending\nending.graduation = 毕业 · 结局\n# en: The people who mattered\nending.people = 那些重要的人\n# en: Begin anew\nending.again = 重新开始\n# en: Read your journal\nending.journal = 翻阅你的日志\n# en: Back to the ending\nending.back = 回到结局\n# en: fond\nending.fond = 亲近\n# en: cold\nending.cold = 冷淡\n# en: Export this run\nending.export = 导出本局记录\n# en: The Hollow Star\nending.slot.mystery = 空洞之星\n# en: Bonds\nending.slot.relationships = 羁绊\n# en: Remembered as\nending.slot.personal = 人们记得你是\n# en: This card\nfb.this = 本卡\n# en: Last card\nfb.last = 上一张\n# en: Previous\nfb.prev = 前一张\n# en: Ending\nfb.ending = 结局\n# en: Good\nfb.good = 好\n# en: Bad\nfb.bad = 不好\n# en: Rate & comment (F)\nfb.open = 评分与评论（F）\n# en: Feedback\nfb.heading = 反馈\n# en: Rate cards and leave notes for the writers. Everything is saved with this run’s log.\nfb.intro = 为卡牌评分并给作者留言。所有内容都会随本局记录一起保存。\n# en: You chose: {choice}\nfb.chose = 你的选择：{choice}\n# en: What worked, what didn’t? Tone, typos, balance, a confusing choice…\nfb.placeholder = 哪里好，哪里不好？语气、错字、数值平衡、令人困惑的选项……\n# en: Save\nfb.save = 保存\n# en: Cancel\nfb.cancel = 取消\n# en: Nothing to rate yet.\nfb.none = 还没有可以评分的内容。\n# en: {month} · Y{year}\nfb.when = 第 {year} 年 · {month}\n# en: Playtest log: {runs} runs · {notes} notes\nfb.log = 试玩记录：{runs} 局 · {notes} 条反馈\n# en: Export\nfb.export = 导出\n# en: Morning\nslot.morning = 清晨\n# en: Noon\nslot.noon = 正午\n# en: Afternoon\nslot.afternoon = 午后\n# en: Evening\nslot.evening = 傍晚\n# en: Night\nslot.night = 夜晚\n# en: Midnight\nslot.midnight = 午夜\n# en: Term\nphase.term = 学期中\n# en: Break\nphase.break = 假期\n# en: Wrap-up\nphase.wrapup = 收尾\n# en: {month} · Year {year} · {phase}\nmap.title = 第 {year} 年 · {month} · {phase}\n# en: Where will you spend the afternoon?\nmap.question1 = 这个下午你要去哪儿？\n# en: Where will you spend the afternoon and the evening?\nmap.question2 = 这个下午和傍晚你要去哪儿？\n# en: Where will you spend the afternoon?\nmap.q.afternoon = 这个下午你要去哪儿？\n# en: Where will you spend the evening?\nmap.q.evening = 这个傍晚你要去哪儿？\n# en: Where will you go tonight?\nmap.q.midnight = 今晚你要去哪儿？\n# en: visit {n} of {of}\nmap.visitOf = 第 {n} / {of} 次拜访\n# en: Stay in\nmap.stayIn = 待在住处\n# en: {event}: at {place}, this {slot}\nmap.festivalHere = {event}：这个{slot}在{place}\n# en: choose…\nmap.choose = 选择……\n# en: Begin the day\nmap.go = 开始这一天\n# en: List view\nmap.showList = 列表视图\n# en: Map view\nmap.showMap = 地图视图\n# en: Somewhere you haven’t found yet.\nmap.undiscovered = 一个你还没发现的地方。\n# en: Both visits are picked; tap one to change it.\nmap.full = 两次拜访都选好了；点其中一个可以更换。\n# en: {name} isn’t open then (open: {hours}).\nmap.closed = {name}那时不开放（开放时间：{hours}）。\n# en: closed now\nmap.closedNow = 现在关闭\n# en: open\nmap.open = 开放\n# en: not yet found\nmap.hinted = 尚未发现\n# en: festival this {slot}\nmap.festival = 这个{slot}有节庆\n# en: here: {names}\nmap.here = 在这里：{names}\n# en: something new\nmap.new = 有新鲜事\n# en: Practice · {n} left\nmap.practiceLeft = 练习 · 还剩 {n} 次\n# en: Shift · {n} left\nmap.shiftsLeft = 班次 · 还剩 {n} 次\n# en: Session · {n} left\nmap.sessionsLeft = 课时 · 还剩 {n} 次\n# en: Confirm ({n}/{max})\nmenu.confirm = 确认（{n}/{max}）\n# en: {track}: {grade}\nnote.grade = {track}：{grade}\n# en: {track}: {grade} ({skill} {value} against an exam bar of {bar}; pass chance {p}%)\nnote.gradeWhy = {track}：{grade}（{skill} {value}，考试门槛 {bar}；及格几率 {p}%）\n# en: Enrolled: {track}\nnote.enrol = 已选课：{track}\n# en: Joined: {track}\nnote.join = 已加入：{track}\n# en: Left: {track}\nnote.leave = 已退出：{track}\n# en: {track}: done\nnote.thesisDone = {track}：已完成\n# en: Promoted in {track}\nnote.promote = 在{track}获得晋升\n# en: Dropped from {track}\nnote.dropped = 被{track}除名\n# en: {track}: missed {n}\nnote.missed = {track}：缺席 {n} 次\n# en: {track}: {n} left this term\nnote.attend = {track}：本学期还剩 {n} 次\n# en: {track}: your wages, {gold} crowns\nnote.wage = {track}：本次工钱 {gold} 克朗\n# en: {track}: an extra visit (breaks don't count toward the term)\nnote.attendBreak = {track}：额外的一次（假期不计入学期要求）\n# en: New place: {place}\nnote.discover = 新地点：{place}\n# en: A clue found\nnote.clue = 发现了一条线索\n# en: Studies & commitments\nside.studies = 学业与职责\n# en: GPA {gpa}\nside.gpa = GPA {gpa}\n# en: Enrolled this term\nside.enrolled = 本学期所选课程\n# en: Grades: {grades}\nside.grades = 成绩：{grades}\n# en: {rank} · {due} due this term\nside.rankLine = {rank} · 本学期需出席 {due} 次\n# en: {n}%\nside.perf = {n}%\n# en: Progress {n}%\nside.progress = 进度 {n}%\n# en: Applied\nside.applied = 已申请\n# en: Saved games\nslots.heading = 存档\n# en: Autosave\nslots.auto = 自动存档\n# en: Slot {n}\nslots.slot = 存档位 {n}\n# en: Empty\nslots.empty = 空\n# en: {name} — {month}, Year {year}\nslots.info = {name} — 第 {year} 年 · {month}\n# en: Save here\nslots.saveHere = 存到这里\n# en: Load\nslots.load = 读取\n# en: Saved\nslots.saved = 已保存\n# en: Close\nslots.close = 关闭\n# en: Stored on the server. Loading a slot continues it as a new run.\nslots.whereServer = 存档保存在服务器上。读取存档位后，会作为一局新的游戏继续。\n# en: Stored in this browser. Loading a slot continues it as a new run.\nslots.whereLocal = 存档保存在本浏览器中。读取存档位后，会作为一局新的游戏继续。\n# en: Connected: runs and feedback are saved on the server.\nnet.connected = 已连接：游戏记录与反馈会保存到服务器。\n# en: Admin\nnet.admin = 管理\n# en: she\npronoun.she.they = 她\n# en: her\npronoun.she.them = 她\n# en: her\npronoun.she.their = 她的\n# en: hers\npronoun.she.theirs = 她的\n# en: is\npronoun.she.are = \n# en: s\npronoun.she.s = \n# en: he\npronoun.he.they = 他\n# en: him\npronoun.he.them = 他\n# en: his\npronoun.he.their = 他的\n# en: his\npronoun.he.theirs = 他的\n# en: is\npronoun.he.are = \n# en: s\npronoun.he.s = \n# en: they\npronoun.they.they = TA\n# en: them\npronoun.they.them = TA\n# en: their\npronoun.they.their = TA的\n# en: theirs\npronoun.they.theirs = TA的\n# en: are\npronoun.they.are = \n# en: \npronoun.they.s = \n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/world/arcs.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/world/arcs.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@arc hollowstar\n# en: The Hollow Star\nname [e1bf91] = 空洞之星\n# en: Something that eats light sleeps beneath the Mirror Lake, behind a door the founders sealed with seven locks. Someone wants it open.\ndesc [30af88] = 有一种吞噬光的东西沉睡在镜湖底下，被创始人用七道锁封在一扇门后。有人想把门打开。\n\n@arc lyra_and_her_mother\n# en: Second Place\nname [604ed6] = 第二名\n# en: Lyra Vaneshade has never once heard \"well done\". Her rivalry with you can become respect, friendship or more, and her mother will have something to say about all three.\ndesc [227832] = 莱拉·凡妮希德从未听过一句“做得好”。她与你的竞争可以变成尊重、友谊，甚至更多——而她的母亲对这三者都会有话要说。\n\n@arc kael_thaw\n# en: Thaw\nname [448405] = 解冻\n# en: Kael Dumont was sent to open a door and came to stop it. Whether he manages depends on whether anyone is warm enough.\ndesc [4e1d95] = 凯尔·杜蒙被派来打开一扇门，却来阻止它。他能否做到，取决于有没有人足够温暖。\n\n@arc ilse_dumont\n# en: The Girl in White Furs\nname [a99aba] = 白裘少女\n# en: Kael's sister was raised by the Hollow cult to do what he wouldn't. The man in grey wants her home. She might let him.\ndesc [7d861b] = 凯尔的妹妹被空洞教团养大，去做他不肯做的事。灰衣人想带她回家。她也许会跟他走。\n\n@arc the_bell\n# en: The Bell Under the Water\nname [f2ca3f] = 水下的钟\n# en: The drowned bell under the Mirror Lake rings when no one is near it. Someone wants it found.\ndesc [e24896] = 镜湖底那口沉钟会在无人靠近时鸣响。有人想让它被找到。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/world/cast.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/world/cast.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@npc lyra\n# en: Lyra Vaneshade\nname [7ccd7a] = 莱拉·凡妮希德\n# en: Heir of House Vaneshade, pyromancy prodigy\ntitle [a4c6ad] = 凡妮希德家族继承人，火焰术天才\n# en: Emberhall\nhouse [6adebf] = 烬厅\n# en: Brilliant, impatient and used to winning. She treats everyone as either a rival or furniture — and is quietly lonely about it.\ndesc [b8c380] = 才华横溢，缺乏耐心，习惯了赢。在她眼里，每个人要么是对手，要么是家具——她为此默默地孤独着。\n\n@npc tobias\n# en: Tobias Fennick\nname [b5c657] = 托比亚斯·芬尼克\n# en: Farmer's son on a turnip scholarship\ntitle [ae6bc4] = 拿着芜菁奖学金的农家子弟\n# en: Rootward\nhouse [dd14d3] = 根庐\n# en: Big hands, bigger heart. Talks to plants and, worryingly, the plants seem to answer.\ndesc [325d88] = 手掌大，心更大。他会跟植物说话，而令人担忧的是，植物似乎会回答。\n\n@npc seraphine\n# en: Seraphine Hale\nname [b9b6ae] = 瑟拉芬·海尔\n# en: Seer who never quite looks at the present\ntitle [a72b9b] = 从不正眼看“现在”的预言者\n# en: Stillwater\nhouse [67e7fc] = 静水\n# en: Speaks in half-sentences, as if finishing the other half in a different week. Often right. Unsettlingly often.\ndesc [abbc16] = 说话总是只说半句，仿佛另一半要留到下个星期再说完。她常常是对的。准得让人发毛。\n\n@npc corvin\n# en: Corvin Ashby\nname [5f13bd] = 科尔文·阿什比\n# en: Charming liability\ntitle [c06ba3] = 迷人的麻烦精\n# en: Duskgate\nhouse [a1e05f] = 暮门\n# en: Knows every locked door in the academy and has opinions about all of them. Owes money to at least three people.\ndesc [3fb5db] = 认识学院里每一扇锁着的门，并对每一扇都有意见。至少欠着三个人的钱。\n\n@npc mira\n# en: Mira Ten\nname [6acc41] = 米拉·滕\n# en: Artificer of questionable inventions\ntitle [dcc640] = 发明可疑的奇械师\n# en: Brasswick\nhouse [6604c0] = 铜芯\n# en: Smells permanently of solder and ozone. Has built a clockwork owl, a self-stirring cauldron and at least one small fire.\ndesc [7b73bc] = 身上永远带着焊锡和臭氧的味道。造过一只发条猫头鹰、一口自动搅拌坩埚，以及至少一场小火灾。\n\n@npc kael\n# en: Kael Dumont\nname [18aead] = 凯尔·杜蒙\n# en: Transfer student from the frozen North\ntitle [80355d] = 来自冰封北境的转学生\n# en: Stillwater\nhouse [67e7fc] = 静水\n# en: Arrived mid-term with no luggage and frost in his eyelashes. Doesn't talk about where he came from. Watches the sky at night.\ndesc [058f2e] = 学期中途到来，没带行李，睫毛上挂着霜。从不谈起自己从哪里来。夜里总在看天。\n\n@npc nessa\n# en: Nessa Quill\nname [3ad688] = 内莎·奎尔\n# en: Editor of the Lumen Ledger\ntitle [c35a40] = 《流明纪事报》主编\n# en: Duskgate\nhouse [a1e05f] = 暮门\n# en: Runs the student paper with a quill that writes on its own. Everyone is a story; some are just not finished yet.\ndesc [2179de] = 用一支会自己写字的羽毛笔经营着学生报纸。每个人都是一个故事；有些只是还没写完。\n\n@npc bram\n# en: Bram Hollis\nname [182554] = 布拉姆·霍利斯\n# en: Prefect, enforcer, menace\ntitle [2cee1b] = 级长、执法者、祸害\n# en: Emberhall\nhouse [6adebf] = 烬厅\n# en: Upper-year prefect with a ledger of demerits and a fondness for writing your name in it.\ndesc [01de4f] = 高年级级长，揣着一本记过簿，特别喜欢在上面写你的名字。\n\n@npc thorne\n# en: Professor Aldric Thorne\nname [734bbb] = 奥尔德里克·索恩教授\n# en: Professor Thorne\nshort [fff982] = 索恩教授\n# en: Master of Arcana\ntitle [c13eee] = 奥术大师\n# en: Faculty\nhouse [078d5f] = 教职员\n# en: Severe, exacting, allergic to flattery. Rumoured to have once out-dueled a storm.\ndesc [a1a584] = 严厉、苛刻，对奉承过敏。传说他曾在决斗中赢过一场风暴。\n\n@npc maud\n# en: Old Maud\nname [9c77f5] = 老莫德\n# en: Old Maud\nshort [9c77f5] = 老莫德\n# en: Groundskeeper and hedge-witch\ntitle [e5de9f] = 园丁兼乡野女巫\n# en: Faculty\nhouse [078d5f] = 教职员\n# en: Keeps bees, chickens and a rather large secret garden. Gives advice you only understand two weeks later.\ndesc [8d1e7c] = 养蜂、养鸡，还有一座相当大的秘密花园。她的建议，你往往要过两周才听得懂。\n\n@npc isolde\n# en: Headmistress Isolde Marrow\nname [9e3659] = 伊索尔德·马罗院长\n# en: Headmistress Marrow\nshort [feeebf] = 马罗院长\n# en: Headmistress of Lumenhold\ntitle [b622da] = 流明堡院长\n# en: Faculty\nhouse [078d5f] = 教职员\n# en: Tall, pale and impossibly calm. She remembers every student's name — and every student's mistakes.\ndesc [264ae0] = 高挑、苍白，冷静得不可思议。她记得每个学生的名字——也记得每个学生犯过的错。\n\n@npc ilse\n# en: Ilse Dumont\nname [7e9501] = 伊尔泽·杜蒙\n# en: Kael's younger sister, a storm in white furs\ntitle [8c137b] = 凯尔的妹妹，一场裹着白色毛皮的风暴\n# en: none\nhouse [a7afdb] = 无\n# en: Raised by the Hollow cult to open a door she has never seen. Sharp as an icicle, lonely as a frozen lake, and much younger than she pretends to be.\ndesc [908293] = 被空洞教团养大，只为打开一扇她从未见过的门。锋利如冰锥，孤独如冰封的湖——而且比她装出来的样子年轻得多。\n\n@npc amaranth\n# en: Sister Amaranth\nname [f35ea5] = 阿玛兰斯修女\n# en: Sister Amaranth\nshort [f35ea5] = 阿玛兰斯修女\n# en: Healer of the infirmary\ntitle [6b950d] = 医务室的治疗师\n# en: Faculty\nhouse [078d5f] = 教职员\n# en: Runs the infirmary with warming charms, tonics and no patience for excuses. Teaches the healing half of Alchemy & Healing.\ndesc [8363a5] = 掌管医务室：暖身咒、补剂，以及对借口零容忍。也教炼金与治愈里治愈的那一半。\n\n@npc brask\n# en: Professor Hedda Brask\nname [dafdd1] = 赫达·布拉斯克教授\n# en: Professor Brask\nshort [a513af] = 布拉斯克教授\n# en: Professor of Beastcraft & Flight\ntitle [af9122] = 驭兽与飞行教授\n# en: Faculty\nhouse [078d5f] = 教职员\n# en: Teaches Beastcraft & Flight and is kinder to animals than to students, though the gap closes for anyone who mucks out without being asked.\ndesc [34945d] = 教驭兽与飞行，对动物比对学生和气；不过谁要是不等吩咐就去铲马厩，这差距就会缩小。\n\n@npc vey\n# en: Professor Ottilie Vey\nname [0ed5bc] = 奥蒂莉·维伊教授\n# en: Professor Vey\nshort [a16fca] = 维伊教授\n# en: Professor of Illusion & Glamour\ntitle [3b7ca9] = 幻术与魅影教授\n# en: Faculty\nhouse [078d5f] = 教职员\n# en: Teaches Illusion & Glamour and grades on whether you fooled her. She is very hard to fool, and very generous to the few who manage it.\ndesc [e5e975] = 教幻术与魅影，按你骗没骗过她来打分。她极难被骗，对少数骗过她的人却极其慷慨。\n\n@npc marisol\n# en: Professor Ines Marisol\nname [36ca1e] = 伊内丝·玛丽索尔教授\n# en: Professor Marisol\nshort [40ede1] = 玛丽索尔教授\n# en: Professor of Tidecalling\ntitle [867c83] = 唤潮术教授\n# en: Faculty\nhouse [078d5f] = 教职员\n# en: Teaches the tidecalling half of Evocation, waist-deep in the lake at dawn. Keeps the lake calm on exam days and reads poetry under a dry patch of air when it rains.\ndesc [9fa4f2] = 教塑能术里唤潮的那一半，黎明时分站在齐腰深的湖水里上课。考试那几天由她让湖面平静；下雨时，她在一小片干燥的空气下读诗。\n\n@npc mabel\n# en: Mabel Thistlewood\nname [472ddc] = 梅布尔·蓟木\n# en: Mabel\nshort [60ac42] = 梅布尔\n# en: Keeper of the tea shop\ntitle [389e2b] = 茶馆老板娘\n# en: none\nhouse [a7afdb] = 无\n# en: Forty kinds of tea, three kinds of cake, and a talent for reading your mood from the way you open her door.\ndesc [5c8604] = 四十种茶，三种蛋糕，还有一样本事：看你怎么推门，就知道你今天心情如何。\n\n@npc grimsby\n# en: Mr Alder Grimsby\nname [be7a57] = 奥尔德·格林斯比先生\n# en: Mr Grimsby\nshort [aa7b23] = 格林斯比先生\n# en: Bookseller\ntitle [c29f43] = 书商\n# en: none\nhouse [a7afdb] = 无\n# en: Keeps a bookshop so crowded the shelves lean on each other. He will find anything except what you came in for, which is often what you needed.\ndesc [bf3215] = 他的书店挤得书架互相倚靠。你要什么他都找得到，唯独找不到你进门要的那本——而那往往正是你需要的。\n\n@npc pell\n# en: Old Pell\nname [345189] = 老佩尔\n# en: Old Pell\nshort [345189] = 老佩尔\n# en: Apothecary of Pell & Daughters\ntitle [c6c952] = 佩尔父女药铺的药剂师\n# en: none\nhouse [a7afdb] = 无\n# en: Taught three generations of the village to count drops. His daughters run the counter now; he runs the drawers, and the drawers are never wrong.\ndesc [5f1bca] = 教会了村里三代人数药滴。如今柜台归他的女儿们管，他管抽屉，而抽屉从不出错。\n\n@npc grisk\n# en: Grisk\nname [9443cd] = 格里斯克\n# en: Grisk\nshort [9443cd] = 格里斯克\n# en: Moneylender, friend to the temporarily embarrassed\ntitle [ad4b4c] = 放贷人，一时手头拮据者的朋友\n# en: none\nhouse [a7afdb] = 无\n# en: A goblin in a velvet waistcoat who lends to students: sixty crowns today, twelve a month for six. He keeps a ledger, and sometimes asks for favours instead of coin.\ndesc [cc2c49] = 穿天鹅绒马甲的地精，专借钱给学生：今天六十克朗，之后六个月每月十二。他有一本账簿，偶尔要人情而不要钱。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/world/factions.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/world/factions.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@faction wardens\n# en: The Wardens\nname [2a89ea] = 守望者\n# en: The headmistress's quiet circle, sworn to keep the founders' seal. They recruit students only when the seal grows thin.\ndesc [f1e201] = 院长身边一个安静的小圈子，誓守创始人的封印。只有在封印变薄时，他们才会招募学生。\n\n@faction hollow_cult\n# en: The Hollow Cult\nname [b781ad] = 空洞教团\n# en: Northern families, the Dumonts first among them, who serve the star beneath the lake. Their messenger is the man in grey.\ndesc [25011f] = 侍奉湖底之星的北境家族，杜蒙家首当其冲。他们的信使就是灰衣人。\n\n@faction midnight_cartographers\n# en: The Midnight Cartographers\nname [40c563] = 午夜制图社\n# en: The club that doesn't exist: students who map the castle's secret passages and share them with almost no one.\ndesc [14391b] = 那个不存在的社团：一群绘制城堡秘密通道、却几乎从不与人分享的学生。\n\n@faction faculty\n# en: The Faculty\nname [5b97ac] = 教职员\n# en: The professors and staff of Lumenhold: strict, eccentric, and mostly on your side whether you like it or not.\ndesc [645f6f] = 流明堡的教授与职员：严格、古怪，而且不管你乐不乐意，大多站在你这一边。\n\n@faction conclave\n# en: The Conclave of the Seven Towers\nname [c832a4] = 七塔议会\n# en: The governing body of magic in the realm, headquartered in the capital. Hands out research posts, licences and very long letters.\ndesc [76fd4f] = 王国魔法界的管理机构，总部设在王都。负责分配研究职位、颁发执照，以及寄来非常长的信。\n\n@faction thornwick\n# en: Thornwick Academy\nname [74b242] = 荆木学院\n# en: The rival school across the hills. Famous for illusionists, tournament grudges and spies who think they're subtle.\ndesc [28e5ca] = 山那边的对手学校。以幻术师、锦标赛积怨，以及自以为很隐蔽的间谍闻名。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/world/facts/amaranth.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/world/facts/amaranth.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@fact amaranth.healer\n# en: Runs the infirmary and teaches the healing half of Alchemy & Healing. Believes no excuse.\ntext [63b5e6] = 掌管医务室，教炼金与治愈里治愈的那一半。任何借口她都不信。\n\n@fact amaranth.truth\n# en: She treats the true version of an injury faster than the story you tell her, and she always knows which one you told.\ntext [76020a] = 你说出伤的真实来历，她治得比听你编的故事更快；而你说的是哪一种，她总是知道。\n\n@fact amaranth.rounds\n# en: She does evening rounds of every bed herself, and has never once missed one.\ntext [8c9554] = 每晚的查房她都亲自走遍每张床，从未落下过一次。\n\n@fact amaranth.key\n# en: Only a handful of students have ever been trusted with the key to her tonic cupboard.\ntext [5f5b57] = 被托付过她补剂柜钥匙的学生，屈指可数。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/world/facts/bram.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/world/facts/bram.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@fact bram.prefect\n# en: An upper-year prefect with a red sash, a ledger of demerits and a fondness for your name.\ntext [3dbe57] = 高年级级长，系着红绶带，揣着一本记过簿，特别喜欢往上写你的名字。\n\n@fact bram.father\n# en: His father was head boy, and has told Bram all his life that he will never amount to anything.\ntext [414950] = 他父亲当年是男生学生会主席，一辈子都在告诉布拉姆他不会有出息。\n\n@fact bram.hall_tax\n# en: He once invented a \"hall tax\" for the east staircase.\ntext [67e9e9] = 他曾为东侧楼梯发明过一项“走廊税”。\n\n@fact bram.cheating\n# en: You saw him cheat in an exam. He knows you saw.\ntext [2e1460] = 你看见他考试作弊。他知道你看见了。\n\n@fact bram.ally\n# en: Under the sash, he will step between you and trouble. Then he tells you not to make it weird.\ntext [e7ac5a] = 在那条绶带底下，他会挡在你和麻烦之间。然后叫你别把气氛搞得奇怪。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/world/facts/brask.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/world/facts/brask.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@fact brask.beasts\n# en: Teaches Beastcraft & Flight from the stables, and talks to every creature in them more kindly than to students.\ntext [267a74] = 在马厩里教驭兽与飞行，对里面每一只生灵说话都比对学生和气。\n\n@fact brask.stories\n# en: Every one of her stories ends with a bite and a moral. She laughs out loud about once a term.\ntext [cbc9ea] = 她讲的每个故事都以被咬一口和一个道理收尾。她大约一学期大笑一次。\n\n@fact brask.owl\n# en: The old barn owl in the stable loft has outlived three of her colleagues. She would not say which loss hurt most.\ntext [88c4c3] = 马厩阁楼上那只老仓鸮，比她的三位同事都活得长。哪一次失去最让她难过，她不肯说。\n\n@fact brask.courtesies\n# en: She bows to the things that live in the Whisperwood, and believes it's why they have never bitten her. Much.\ntext [ce36dc] = 她会向住在低语森林里的东西鞠躬，并相信正因如此它们从没咬过她。基本没有。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/world/facts/corvin.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/world/facts/corvin.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@fact corvin.locks\n# en: Knows every locked door in the academy and owes money to at least three people.\ntext [8de015] = 学院里每一扇锁着的门他都知道，而且至少欠着三个人的钱。\n\n@fact corvin.last_warning\n# en: He is on his last warning. One more detention and they send him home, and home is not a good place.\ntext [b33ba6] = 他已经是最后一次警告了。再被罚一次禁闭，就会被送回家——而家，不是个好地方。\n\n@fact corvin.brother\n# en: His brother turns up for money and threatens to tell the headmistress where Corvin learned to pick locks.\ntext [bb32a8] = 他哥哥会找上门来要钱，还威胁要告诉院长科尔文是在哪儿学会撬锁的。\n\n@fact corvin.grandfather\n# en: His grandfather's lucky deck sat in the confiscation vault for decades. It really is lucky.\ntext [26a338] = 他祖父的幸运牌在没收品库里躺了几十年。那副牌是真的幸运。\n\n@fact corvin.cartographers\n# en: He runs the Midnight Cartographers, the club that doesn't exist.\ntext [d80fd1] = 他是午夜制图社的头儿——那个不存在的社团。\n\n@fact corvin.trolls\n# en: He once owed thirty crowns to trolls and offered to pay you back with a goat.\ntext [976180] = 他曾欠巨魔三十克朗，还提出用一只山羊来还你钱。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/world/facts/grimsby.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/world/facts/grimsby.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@fact grimsby.books\n# en: Keeps the village bookshop. He will find anything except what you came in for.\ntext [034041] = 开着村里的书店。你要什么他都找得到，唯独找不到你进门要的那本。\n\n@fact grimsby.wrong\n# en: The book he gives you instead is, more often than not, the one you needed.\ntext [2be71b] = 他塞给你的那本“错书”，多半正是你需要的。\n\n@fact grimsby.parcels\n# en: He posts books to students he thinks need them, on credit, and has never once chased a debt.\ntext [5362f8] = 他会把他觉得学生需要的书赊账寄过去，从没催过一次债。\n\n@fact grimsby.lighthouses\n# en: He has a weakness for lighthouses, and when he closes the shop, it is usually to go and look at one.\ntext [f1d0ca] = 他对灯塔情有独钟；他关店的时候，通常就是去看某座灯塔了。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/world/facts/grisk.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/world/facts/grisk.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@fact grisk.lender\n# en: A goblin moneylender in a velvet waistcoat: sixty crowns today, twelve a month for six months.\ntext [023206] = 穿天鹅绒马甲的地精放贷人：今天六十克朗，之后六个月每月十二。\n\n@fact grisk.ledger\n# en: He calls debts \"friendships\", and keeps all of them in one ledger he never lets out of reach.\ntext [fa5464] = 他把债叫作“交情”，全都记在一本从不离手的账簿里。\n\n@fact grisk.favours\n# en: He sometimes asks for favours instead of coin, and the favours are for clients he never names.\ntext [a745d9] = 他有时要人情而不要钱，而那些人情是替他从不透露姓名的客户讨的。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/world/facts/ilse.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/world/facts/ilse.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@fact ilse.furs\n# en: Kael's younger sister: white furs, a private snowstorm and coins that freeze to counters.\ntext [e7eab1] = 凯尔的妹妹：一身白裘，一场随身的私人小暴风雪，还有会冻在柜台上的硬币。\n\n@fact ilse.raised\n# en: The Hollow cult raised her to open a door she has never seen.\ntext [a006d7] = 空洞教团把她养大，要她去打开一扇她从没见过的门。\n\n@fact ilse.frost_craft\n# en: She knows Northern frost-craft better than anything the professors teach.\ntext [ac2fad] = 她对北境霜术的了解，胜过教授们教的任何东西。\n\n@fact ilse.grey\n# en: The man in grey came to take her home. She didn't call for help; she only looked.\ntext [f8b96d] = 灰衣人来带她回家。她没有呼救，只是看着。\n\n@fact ilse.scared\n# en: She admits, to her brother, that she was scared. So was he.\ntext [fe62fb] = 她向哥哥承认，她当时很害怕。他也是。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/world/facts/isolde.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/world/facts/isolde.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@fact isolde.names\n# en: She remembers every student's name, and every student's mistakes.\ntext [00ea88] = 她记得每个学生的名字，也记得每个学生犯过的错。\n\n@fact isolde.portrait\n# en: As a student she set the observatory on fire twice. Her portrait from those days is hidden in a forgotten corridor.\ntext [389493] = 她当学生时，两次把观星台点着了。那时的肖像被藏在一条被遗忘的走廊里。\n\n@fact isolde.chess\n# en: She plays tower chess by her window and laughs, short and delighted, when she loses.\ntext [29f019] = 她在窗边下水晶塔棋，输了的时候会短促而愉快地笑出声。\n\n@fact isolde.thread\n# en: She has been following the same thread you have: the seal beneath the lake is weakening.\ntext [db9906] = 她一直在追查和你同一条线索：湖底的封印正在变弱。\n\n@fact isolde.mercy\n# en: She asks students what they think is just, and sometimes she listens.\ntext [d24034] = 她会问学生，他们认为怎样才算公正——有时，她会听。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/world/facts/kael.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/world/facts/kael.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@fact kael.north\n# en: A transfer from the frozen North who arrived mid-term with frost in his eyelashes.\ntext [c365b9] = 来自冰封北境的转学生，学期中途抵达，睫毛上挂着霜。\n\n@fact kael.cold_hands\n# en: His hands are always cold. Nobody has ever been warm enough before.\ntext [4b3405] = 他的手总是冰凉。以前从没有人暖到足以让它们回温。\n\n@fact kael.sky\n# en: He watches a patch of sky with no stars in it: a star that eats light.\ntext [1206d5] = 他凝望着一片没有星星的夜空：一颗吞噬光的星。\n\n@fact kael.family\n# en: His family serve something under the lake. They sent him to open its door; he came to stop them.\ntext [cfe636] = 他的家族侍奉着湖底的某样东西。他们派他来打开那扇门；他来，是为了阻止他们。\n\n@fact kael.sister\n# en: He has a younger sister, Ilse, raised to do what he wouldn't.\ntext [8def5c] = 他有一个妹妹，伊尔泽，被培养去做他不肯做的事。\n\n@fact kael.mother\n# en: His mother never once said she was proud of him.\ntext [a4117c] = 他的母亲从没说过一次为他骄傲。\n\n@fact kael.wanting\n# en: He was taught not to want things, because wanting is how they control you.\ntext [34ad8d] = 他被教导不要想要任何东西，因为想要，就是他们控制你的方式。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/world/facts/lyra.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/world/facts/lyra.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@fact lyra.prodigy\n# en: The pyromancy prodigy of House Vaneshade. Everyone knows the name, and she makes sure of it.\ntext [7f3f8a] = 凡妮希德家族的火焰术天才。人人都知道这个名字，她也确保人人都知道。\n\n@fact lyra.toast\n# en: She can incinerate a target at forty paces. She cannot make toast.\ntext [8eef62] = 她能在四十步外把靶子烧成灰。她不会烤面包片。\n\n@fact lyra.mother\n# en: Her mother has never once told her \"well done\".\ntext [a56886] = 她的母亲从没对她说过一句“做得好”。\n\n@fact lyra.second_place\n# en: She came second in the Winter Trials once. Her mother wrote to ask who beat her.\ntext [c586c0] = 她在冬季试炼中得过一次第二名。她母亲写信来问是谁赢了她。\n\n@fact lyra.burnout\n# en: She studies until she collapses, and begs you not to let her mother find out.\ntext [8e25bd] = 她会一直学到昏过去，还求你别让她母亲知道。\n\n@fact lyra.rival\n# en: You are the only person here who makes her better. She hates it. She doesn't want it to stop.\ntext [049900] = 你是这里唯一能让她变得更好的人。她恨这一点。她不想让它停下。\n\n@fact lyra.inheritance\n# en: Her mother threatened her inheritance over you. She set the letter on fire.\ntext [b8b62f] = 她母亲为了你，拿她的继承权来威胁她。她把那封信烧了。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/world/facts/mabel.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/world/facts/mabel.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@fact mabel.teashop\n# en: Keeps the tea shop in the village: forty kinds of tea, three kinds of cake.\ntext [c5dd16] = 在村里开茶馆：四十种茶，三种蛋糕。\n\n@fact mabel.door\n# en: She reads your mood from the way you open her door, and is rarely wrong.\ntext [33ce2b] = 看你怎么推她的门，就知道你的心情，而且很少看错。\n\n@fact mabel.tins\n# en: She sends tins of tea up to the castle on the barge for students she's worried about, and refuses payment for them.\ntext [390792] = 她会用驳船把一罐罐茶送上城堡，给她放心不下的学生，而且坚决不收钱。\n\n@fact mabel.cups\n# en: The cups on the hooks behind her counter belong to her regulars, living and dead. She remembers every one of them.\ntext [3e39fe] = 她柜台后挂钩上的杯子属于她的常客，有活着的，也有已故的。每一位她都记得。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/world/facts/marisol.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/world/facts/marisol.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@fact marisol.tides\n# en: Teaches the tidecalling half of Evocation, waist-deep in the lake at dawn, and keeps the lake calm on exam days.\ntext [5356ef] = 教塑能术里唤潮的那一半，黎明时站在齐腰深的湖水里上课；考试那几天由她让湖面平静。\n\n@fact marisol.poems\n# en: She reads poems about water in the rain, under a dry patch of air, and never remembers their last lines.\ntext [936059] = 她在雨里、在一小片干燥的空气下读写水的诗，却从来记不住最后一行。\n\n@fact marisol.storms\n# en: She goes out into the worst storms of the year on purpose. She says nobody learns tides on a calm day.\ntext [2d8f05] = 她专挑一年里最猛的暴风雨出门。她说风平浪静的日子里，谁也学不会潮汐。\n\n@fact marisol.margins\n# en: She wrote her own tidecalling into the margins of a book of poems when she was a student, and still teaches from it.\ntext [2b7267] = 她还是学生时，把自己的唤潮术写在一本诗集的页边，至今仍照着它教。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/world/facts/maud.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/world/facts/maud.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@fact maud.groundskeeper\n# en: Groundskeeper and hedge-witch, keeper of bees, chickens and a goat called Bartholomew.\ntext [54cb93] = 园丁兼乡野女巫，养蜂、养鸡，还有一只叫巴塞洛缪的山羊。\n\n@fact maud.student\n# en: She was a student here sixty years ago, the spring the lake froze.\ntext [5b3f12] = 六十年前她也是这里的学生——就在湖面结冰的那个春天。\n\n@fact maud.edmund\n# en: Edmund went down beneath the lake that night and didn't come back. She kept his silver wand.\ntext [624c6b] = 那天夜里埃德蒙下到湖底，再没回来。她一直留着他的银魔杖。\n\n@fact maud.soup\n# en: Her soup cures colds, broken hearts and most curses. You're not allowed to write it down.\ntext [395a7c] = 她的汤能治感冒、心碎和大多数诅咒。不许写下来。\n\n@fact maud.garden\n# en: Behind the potting shed there is a door to a garden that only exists for people she shows it to.\ntext [408faa] = 盆栽棚后面有一扇门，通往一座只为她领进去的人而存在的花园。\n\n@fact maud.age\n# en: She is eighty-one, and says she's allowed the occasional chill.\ntext [601a9c] = 她八十一岁了，说自己偶尔着个凉是被允许的。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/world/facts/mira.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/world/facts/mira.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@fact mira.artificer\n# en: An artificer who smells of solder and ozone and has started at least one small fire.\ntext [6f21ba] = 一个浑身焊锡和臭氧味的奇械师，至少引发过一场小火灾。\n\n@fact mira.owl\n# en: Built a clockwork owl that remembers deadlines, birthdays and, slightly, grudges.\ntext [841167] = 造过一只发条猫头鹰，能记住截止日期、生日，还有一点点的仇。\n\n@fact mira.notebooks\n# en: Three years of notebooks are the most precious things they own.\ntext [9a3403] = 三年的笔记本是 TA 最珍贵的东西。\n\n@fact mira.family\n# en: Their family runs a clockworks and thinks invention is a hobby. It is not a hobby.\ntext [91fa32] = TA 家开着一间钟表工坊，觉得发明只是个爱好。这不是爱好。\n\n@fact mira.name_day\n# en: Nobody ever remembered their name day before.\ntext [40158a] = 以前从没有人记得 TA 的命名日。\n\n@fact mira.workshop\n# en: After the third fire in a term, the faculty padlocked their workshop.\ntext [14af34] = 一个学期里第三次失火之后，教职员给 TA 的工坊上了挂锁。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/world/facts/nessa.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/world/facts/nessa.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@fact nessa.editor\n# en: Editor of the Lumen Ledger, with a quill that writes on its own.\ntext [e5df5e] = 《流明纪事报》的主编，有一支会自己写字的羽毛笔。\n\n@fact nessa.quill\n# en: Left unsupervised, her quill starts inventing the news.\ntext [e4c012] = 一旦没人看着，她的羽毛笔就会开始编新闻。\n\n@fact nessa.interview\n# en: Nobody has interviewed the headmistress in twenty years. She means to be the first.\ntext [0599a6] = 二十年来没有人采访过院长。她打算做第一个。\n\n@fact nessa.string\n# en: Her office wall is covered in red string around a sketch of a black star.\ntext [98be58] = 她办公室的墙上缠满了红线，正中间是一颗黑色星星的草图。\n\n@fact nessa.press\n# en: She would chain herself to the printing press before letting anyone shut the Ledger.\ntext [c72444] = 谁要是想关掉《流明纪事报》，她宁可把自己锁在印刷机上。\n\n@fact nessa.off_record\n# en: The only time her quill stops is when she tells it the conversation is off the record.\ntext [9ac8f2] = 她的羽毛笔唯一停下的时候，是她告诉它这段对话不作记录。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/world/facts/pell.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/world/facts/pell.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@fact pell.apothecary\n# en: Runs the drawers at Pell & Daughters. He taught three generations of the village to count drops.\ntext [0b856c] = 管着佩尔父女药铺的抽屉。他教会了村里三代人数药滴。\n\n@fact pell.counting\n# en: He makes every new clerk count aloud. He trusts people who measure twice.\ntext [4c5e9c] = 每个新店员都得被他逼着大声数数。他信得过量两遍的人。\n\n@fact pell.daughters\n# en: His daughters run the counter and want the shop to change; he wants it to be fifty years ago. They're all secretly proud of each other.\ntext [4a27be] = 女儿们管柜台，想让铺子变一变；他却希望一切停在五十年前。其实三个人都暗暗以彼此为傲。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/world/facts/seraphine.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/world/facts/seraphine.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@fact seraphine.seer\n# en: A seer who speaks in half-sentences and is unsettlingly often right.\ntext [8fee9c] = 一个说话总说半句的先知，准得叫人心里发毛。\n\n@fact seraphine.slips\n# en: Sometimes she slips a day ahead and needs someone to tell her what today is.\ntext [6bdae8] = 有时她会滑到明天去，需要有人告诉她今天是哪一天。\n\n@fact seraphine.oracle\n# en: Her mother is the Oracle of Vell, who wants her home to tell kings what they want to hear.\ntext [e701e0] = 她的母亲是维尔神谕，想让她回家，去对国王们说他们爱听的话。\n\n@fact seraphine.self\n# en: Seers can't see themselves, unless someone is standing next to them.\ntext [90e477] = 先知看不见自己——除非有人站在他们身边。\n\n@fact seraphine.lake\n# en: The lake calls to her at night. She says it calls to you too.\ntext [37ecd3] = 夜里，湖在呼唤她。她说，它也在呼唤你。\n\n@fact seraphine.too_loud\n# en: When everyone's futures get too loud she hides in the dark. Yours, she says, is quiet.\ntext [9cdeb7] = 当所有人的未来吵得太响时，她会躲进黑暗里。她说，你的未来很安静。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/world/facts/thorne.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/world/facts/thorne.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@fact thorne.storm\n# en: Rumoured to have once out-duelled a storm.\ntext [fa9226] = 据说他曾在决斗中打败过一场风暴。\n\n@fact thorne.lost\n# en: He lost to the storm, badly. Losing well, he says, is the only thing worth teaching.\ntext [f094e4] = 他输给了那场风暴，输得很惨。他说，好好地输，是唯一值得教的东西。\n\n@fact thorne.roses\n# en: He tends a tiny walled rose garden behind the observatory at dawn and talks to the roses.\ntext [ec7ccc] = 黎明时分，他在观星台后面一座小小的围墙玫瑰园里打理花草，还对玫瑰说话。\n\n@fact thorne.hands\n# en: His hands have started to shake. Age comes for every storm eventually.\ntext [95c9a9] = 他的手开始发抖了。岁月终究会找上每一场风暴。\n\n@fact thorne.rival\n# en: A flamboyant old wizard in purple has wanted a rematch with him for forty years.\ntext [054b17] = 一个穿紫袍、作派浮夸的老巫师，四十年来一直想跟他再比一场。\n\n@fact thorne.letters\n# en: He writes very few letters of recommendation, and the Conclave reads every one.\ntext [067d68] = 他很少写推荐信，而七塔议会每一封都会读。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/world/facts/tobias.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/world/facts/tobias.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@fact tobias.scholarship\n# en: A farmer's son on a turnip scholarship, built like a barn door and gentle as a lamb.\ntext [2b6d69] = 一个拿芜菁奖学金的农家子弟，块头像谷仓门，性子像小羊羔。\n\n@fact tobias.plants\n# en: He talks to plants. Worryingly, they seem to answer.\ntext [e42ce7] = 他会跟植物说话。令人担心的是，它们似乎会回答。\n\n@fact tobias.ma\n# en: His mother runs the farm alone at harvest. It's the first harvest he has ever missed.\ntext [b4dd4f] = 收获季节，他母亲一个人打理农场。这是他第一次错过收获。\n\n@fact tobias.gerald\n# en: He is raising a mandrake called Gerald, who wears a knitted hat and screams softly.\ntext [f5ffc4] = 他在养一株叫杰拉尔德的曼德拉草，戴着一顶针织小帽，会轻声尖叫。\n\n@fact tobias.moonflowers\n# en: The greenhouse moonflowers bloom once every eleven years. He would stand in a storm for them.\ntext [047bca] = 温室里的月光花每十一年才开一次。为了它们，他愿意站在暴风雨里。\n\n@fact tobias.blight\n# en: Blight took his family's harvest. Thirty crowns stood between them and a hungry winter.\ntext [15f8ba] = 枯萎病毁了他家的收成。他们和一个挨饿的冬天之间，只隔着三十克朗。\n\n@fact tobias.dancing\n# en: He practised waltzing with a broom before the Midwinter Ball. The broom complained.\ntext [3db4ab] = 冬至舞会前，他拿一把扫帚练华尔兹。扫帚提出了抗议。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/world/facts/vey.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/world/facts/vey.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@fact vey.illusion\n# en: Teaches Illusion & Glamour and grades on whether you fooled her.\ntext [c1fb18] = 教幻术与魅影，按你骗没骗过她来打分。\n\n@fact vey.tests\n# en: She tests students in corridors, unannounced, with tea that isn't there. She keeps score.\ntext [60f4e6] = 她会在走廊里突击考学生，用并不存在的茶。她一直在记分。\n\n@fact vey.revue\n# en: She produces the faculty revue every year, and has never once let Professor Thorne refuse a part.\ntext [d5edf4] = 她每年都负责排演教职员汇演，从没让索恩教授推掉过一个角色。\n\n@fact vey.mirror\n# en: She doesn't use the mirror charm in public. Once, her reflection didn't come back for a week.\ntext [950435] = 她从不在人前用镜像咒。有一次，她的倒影整整一周没回来。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/world/places.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/world/places.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@place courtyard\n# en: The Courtyard\nname [1b8037] = 庭院\n# en: Flagstones worn smooth by three centuries of students, a fountain that freezes when it shouldn't, and the best gossip in the castle.\ndesc [d60535] = 被三百年学生踩得光滑的石板，一座总在不该结冰时结冰的喷泉，以及全城堡最好的八卦。\n\n@place library\n# en: The Library\nname [64e91f] = 图书馆\n# en: Seven floors of shelves that rearrange themselves when nobody is looking. Footnote the cat keeps order.\ndesc [9e9a17] = 七层书架，趁没人注意时自己重新排列。图书馆猫“脚注”负责维持秩序。\n\n@place refectory\n# en: The Refectory\nname [0b96c1] = 膳堂\n# en: Long tables, longer benches, and soup that tastes faintly of whatever you're homesick for.\ndesc [289d61] = 长桌，更长的长凳，还有一种汤——它尝起来隐约像你正在想念的那个地方。\n\n@place lakeshore\n# en: The Lakeshore\nname [6c0614] = 湖岸\n# en: The Mirror Lake lies so still it shows the towers upside down. On some nights it shows other things.\ndesc [733f61] = 镜湖静得能倒映出倒立的塔楼。有些夜晚，它倒映出的是别的东西。\n\n@place village\n# en: The Village\nname [973da4] = 山下小镇\n# en: A barge ride across the lake: crooked roofs, a market square and shopkeepers who have seen every kind of student.\ndesc [419536] = 乘驳船渡过湖面：歪斜的屋顶、集市广场，还有见识过各种学生的店主们。\n\n@place lecture_tower\n# en: The Lecture Tower\nname [d37b7f] = 讲堂塔\n# en: Spiral lecture halls stacked one above another. Evocation on the lower floors, where scorch marks are expected; Umbral Theory at the top, where the lamps burn dim.\ndesc [3bff12] = 一层叠一层的螺旋讲堂。塑能术在底层——那里的焦痕是意料之中的事；暗影理论在顶层，灯光昏暗。\n\n@place observatory\n# en: The Observatory\nname [202edc] = 观星台\n# en: A brass dome at the top of the east tower. The great telescope points wherever it likes; usually that is the right place.\ndesc [4813c0] = 东塔顶上的黄铜穹顶。大望远镜想指向哪儿就指向哪儿——通常，那正是该看的地方。\n\n@place great_hall\n# en: The Great Hall\nname [e97d10] = 大礼堂\n# en: Floating candles, a vaulted ceiling that shows tomorrow's weather, and the stage where the choir rehearses and the Midwinter Ball is held.\ndesc [eb7d92] = 漂浮的蜡烛，能显示明天天气的拱顶，还有合唱团排练、冬至舞会举办的舞台。\n\n@place infirmary\n# en: The Infirmary\nname [b3b383] = 医务室\n# en: Clean white beds, a cupboard of tonics and Sister Amaranth, who has seen every kind of injury and believes none of the excuses.\ndesc [80a36a] = 洁白的床铺，一柜子补药，还有阿玛兰斯修女——她见过各种伤，却一个借口都不信。\n\n@place alchemy_lab\n# en: The Alchemy Laboratory\nname [333e04] = 炼金实验室\n# en: Long benches, cauldrons in every size, and a ventilation charm that has given up on the third floor.\ndesc [9767f5] = 长条工作台，大大小小的坩埚，还有一道在三楼早已放弃抵抗的通风咒。\n\n@place workshop\n# en: Mira's Workshop\nname [0290f1] = 米拉的工坊\n# en: A former classroom full of springs, gears, half-finished machines and the smell of solder. Fire buckets by the door, on the faculty's orders.\ndesc [fed5e7] = 一间旧教室，堆满弹簧、齿轮、半成品机器和焊锡味。门边按教职员的要求摆着几只灭火桶。\n# en: Something clanks and hums behind the west-wing tapestry.\nhint [507a91] = 西翼挂毯后面，有什么东西在叮当作响、嗡嗡低鸣。\n\n@place job_board\n# en: The Job Board\nname [377c1b] = 招工告示板\n# en: A cork board in the entrance hall layered with notices: jobs, lost familiars, club recruitment and the occasional ransom note for a sock.\ndesc [4e9473] = 门厅里的一块软木板，层层叠叠贴满告示：招工、走失的使魔、社团招新，偶尔还有一封某只袜子的赎金信。\n\n@place ledger_office\n# en: The Lumen Ledger Office\nname [fdd2aa] = 《流明纪事报》编辑部\n# en: A cramped room under the library stairs, walls papered with old front pages. The printing press groans like a dragon on deadline night.\ndesc [40d726] = 图书馆楼梯下一间逼仄的小屋，墙上贴满旧头版。截稿之夜，印刷机呻吟得像一条赶工的龙。\n\n@place headmistress_tower\n# en: The Headmistress's Tower\nname [735fdf] = 院长塔\n# en: Books, tea and a board of crystal towers by the window. You are only here because she wants you here.\ndesc [a5fc77] = 书、茶，还有窗边一盘水晶塔棋。你会在这里，只因为她想让你在这里。\n# en: A spiral stair guarded by a stone owl that only opens its eyes for some students.\nhint [49a3ae] = 一道由石头猫头鹰把守的螺旋楼梯——它只为某些学生睁开眼睛。\n\n@place restricted_section\n# en: The Restricted Section\nname [b1cf8a] = 禁书区\n# en: Black-bound books that breathe in the dark. Some of them read you back.\ndesc [9d32a6] = 在黑暗中呼吸的黑皮书。有些书会反过来读你。\n# en: A door at the back of the library that is always locked, and always slightly warm.\nhint [16cc43] = 图书馆最里面有一扇门，总是锁着，而且总是微微发烫。\n\n@place gargoyle_passage\n# en: The Third Gargoyle\nname [e05e0b] = 第三座石像鬼\n# en: Behind the third gargoyle on the north parapet, a passage that isn't on any map. Except theirs.\ndesc [e1c46f] = 北侧护墙上第三座石像鬼背后，有一条任何地图上都没有的通道。除了他们的地图。\n# en: Corvin keeps glancing at a particular gargoyle.\nhint [b7dd0a] = 科尔文总忍不住瞟向某一座石像鬼。\n\n@place seventh_tower\n# en: The Seventh Tower\nname [44b8dc] = 第七塔\n# en: Locked for a century. At the very top, one room of dust and moonlight, and a star-shaped hollow in the wall.\ndesc [f3b2a5] = 已经锁了一个世纪。塔顶只有一间屋子：灰尘、月光，以及墙上一个星形的凹槽。\n# en: A locked stair that leads up into a tower nobody admits is there.\nhint [5d54eb] = 一道上锁的楼梯，通往一座没人承认存在的塔。\n\n@place thesis_lab\n# en: The Thesis Laboratory\nname [92f1a5] = 论文实验室\n# en: Rows of carrels where final-year students and their advisors argue about footnotes until the candles give out.\ndesc [136592] = 一排排研读隔间，毕业年级的学生和导师在这里为脚注争论，直到蜡烛燃尽。\n\n@place duel_hall\n# en: The Duelling Hall\nname [481ae1] = 决斗厅\n# en: Scorched floorboards and a ceiling that has seen things. The practice dummies have names, and grudges.\ndesc [5bea7b] = 烧焦的地板，和一块见过太多场面的天花板。练习用的假人都有名字，也都记仇。\n# en: Steel rings somewhere above the east stair.\nhint [96a1b0] = 东侧楼梯上方，某处传来金铁交鸣。\n\n@place greenhouses\n# en: The Greenhouses\nname [27cc8e] = 温室\n# en: Glass houses that steam even in winter. The moonflowers bloom once every eleven years; the mandrakes have opinions every day.\ndesc [b1e233] = 冬天也冒着热气的玻璃房。月光花每十一年才开一次；曼德拉草每天都有意见。\n\n@place flight_field\n# en: The Flight Field\nname [ee78ba] = 飞行场\n# en: A long green field with hoops at either end and a wind that cheats. Broom lessons, races and spectacular landings.\ndesc [1c581a] = 一片狭长的草场，两端各立着圆环，风总爱作弊。飞帚课、竞速，以及各种壮观的着陆。\n\n@place stables\n# en: The Familiar Stables\nname [151887] = 使魔马厩\n# en: Hippogriffs, a sulky griffon chick, owls in the rafters and Professor Brask, who talks to all of them more kindly than to students.\ndesc [b3d42f] = 鹰头马身兽、一只闹脾气的狮鹫幼崽、椽梁上的猫头鹰，还有布拉斯克教授——他对它们说话，比对学生温柔得多。\n\n@place boathouse\n# en: The Boathouse\nname [ba1cb2] = 船屋\n# en: An old wooden boathouse and a jetty out over the water. People come here to say the things they can't say indoors.\ndesc [cf59e4] = 一座旧木船屋，一道伸向水面的栈桥。人们来这里说那些在室内说不出口的话。\n\n@place orchard\n# en: The Orchard\nname [f7870f] = 果园\n# en: Apple and pear trees older than the towers. In spring the blossom falls like snow; once, it was snow.\ndesc [73fd80] = 比塔楼还老的苹果树和梨树。春天花落如雪；有一次，落下的真是雪。\n\n@place maud_cottage\n# en: Old Maud's Cottage\nname [37a3a6] = 老莫德的小屋\n# en: A thatched cottage with chickens on the doorstep, bees in the eaves, a goat called Bartholomew and a kettle that is always nearly boiling.\ndesc [77de96] = 一间茅草小屋：门口有鸡，屋檐有蜂，还有一只叫巴塞洛缪的山羊，以及一只永远快要烧开的水壶。\n\n@place secret_garden\n# en: The Secret Garden\nname [2193f1] = 秘密花园\n# en: Glowing ferns, silver bees and a pear tree that hums. It only exists for people Maud has shown it to.\ndesc [7a21e5] = 发光的蕨类、银色的蜜蜂，还有一棵会哼歌的梨树。它只为莫德带进来过的人存在。\n# en: A door in the wall behind the potting shed that you keep walking past.\nhint [17ccd0] = 盆栽棚后面的墙上有一扇门，你总是从它旁边走过去。\n\n@place courier_loft\n# en: The Courier Loft\nname [ef540e] = 信使阁楼\n# en: A draughty loft full of parcels, pigeonholes and brooms in various states of repair. The dispatch bell rings at all hours.\ndesc [6cb721] = 一间漏风的阁楼，堆满包裹、信格和各种状态的扫帚。派送铃不分昼夜地响。\n\n@place gilded_newt\n# en: The Gilded Newt\nname [0d7995] = 镀金蝾螈酒馆\n# en: The village tavern: low beams, a fire that never goes out, and a landlady who remembers every student's tab to the penny.\ndesc [5e1794] = 小镇的酒馆：低矮的房梁，一炉从不熄灭的火，还有一位把每个学生的欠账都记到最后一个铜子儿的老板娘。\n\n@place mabels\n# en: Mabel's Tea Shop\nname [d2018a] = 梅布尔茶馆\n# en: Forty kinds of tea, three kinds of cake and Mabel, who reads your mood from the way you open the door.\ndesc [a4c364] = 四十种茶，三种蛋糕，还有梅布尔——她能从你推门的样子读出你的心情。\n\n@place grimsbys_books\n# en: Grimsby's Books\nname [5f12ee] = 格林斯比书店\n# en: A bookshop so crowded the shelves lean on each other for support. Mr Grimsby will find anything except what you came in for.\ndesc [555651] = 一家挤得书架都要互相倚靠的书店。格林斯比先生什么都找得到，除了你进门要找的那本。\n\n@place pell_apothecary\n# en: Pell & Daughters\nname [e047f1] = 佩尔父女药铺\n# en: An apothecary of a thousand labelled drawers. Old Pell taught three generations to count drops; his daughters taught him to smile at customers.\ndesc [4bb1ef] = 一家有上千个贴着标签的抽屉的药铺。老佩尔教会了三代人数药滴；他的女儿们教会了他对客人微笑。\n\n@place bakery\n# en: The Bakery\nname [ddad08] = 面包房\n# en: The same family has baked here for two hundred years. The buns are famous; so, in certain circles, is a letter that arrived very late.\ndesc [7aadb6] = 同一家人在这里烤了两百年面包。小圆面包远近闻名；在某些圈子里，一封迟到了很久的信也是。\n\n@place whisperwood\n# en: The Whisperwood\nname [0cc2ff] = 低语森林\n# en: An old forest that talks in its sleep. Moonpetal grows in its clearings; so do things that are better left there.\ndesc [c682e5] = 一片会说梦话的古老森林。月瓣花长在林间空地上；那里也长着一些最好留在原地的东西。\n# en: Fog sits on the western woods even at noon.\nhint [a946d3] = 西边的树林里，即使正午也笼着雾。\n\n@place royal_library_archives\n# en: The Royal Library Archives\nname [c0d567] = 皇家图书馆档案馆\n# en: Miles of stacks beneath the capital, where the oldest records of the realm are kept in temperature-controlled silence.\ndesc [1c545c] = 王都地下绵延数里的书库，王国最古老的记录在恒温的寂静中保存。\n\n@place conclave_laboratory\n# en: The Conclave Laboratory\nname [858e84] = 七塔议会实验室\n# en: The research laboratory of the Conclave of the Seven Towers, where new spells are tested behind very thick walls.\ndesc [d888bf] = 七塔议会的研究实验室，新咒语在非常非常厚的墙后接受测试。\n\n@place alchemists_works\n# en: The Guild Works\nname [f658d1] = 公会工坊\n# en: The Guild of Alchemists' great works by the river: vats, chimneys and a strict rule about naked flames.\ndesc [041a92] = 炼金师公会在河边的大工坊：大缸、烟囱，以及一条关于明火的严格规定。\n\n@place observatory_camp\n# en: The Expedition Camp\nname [bdc258] = 远征营地\n# en: Tents on a high plateau where the air is thin and the stars are close enough to argue with.\ndesc [aa6f2e] = 高原上的帐篷。空气稀薄，星星近得可以和它们争论。\n\n@place field_station\n# en: The Beastcraft Field Station\nname [93e08b] = 驭兽术野外站\n# en: A cabin on the moors, ringed with paddocks and nesting towers, where injured creatures are mended and wild ones are counted.\ndesc [0090c1] = 荒原上的一座小木屋，四周是围场和筑巢塔。受伤的生灵在这里被治好，野生的则在这里被清点。\n\n@place thornwick_lab\n# en: The Thornwick Exchange Lab\nname [efa297] = 荆木交换实验室\n# en: A guest laboratory at Thornwick Academy, the rival school, where the illusionists are excellent and the hospitality is suspicious.\ndesc [8e0893] = 对手学校荆木学院的一间客座实验室：幻术师们一流，待客之道可疑。\n\n@place unseen_office\n# en: The Office of Unseen Affairs\nname [dcf6f5] = 隐秘事务署\n# en: The Crown's Office of Unseen Affairs. It has no sign, no address, and a very good coffee machine.\ndesc [fb76d0] = 王室隐秘事务署。没有招牌，没有地址，只有一台非常好用的咖啡机。\n\n@place home_cottage\n# en: The Family Cottage\nname [be9171] = 老家小屋\n# en: The kitchen table, the creaky stair, the smell of whatever is in the oven. Everyone asks how school is.\ndesc [e5a1df] = 餐桌、吱呀的楼梯、烤箱里不知什么的香味。每个人都问你学校怎么样。\n\n@place home_fields\n# en: The Fields\nname [50cb01] = 田野\n# en: Long rows, a low wall to sit on, and the same view you used to stare at when you dreamed of towers.\ndesc [5dc6c3] = 长长的田垄，一堵可以坐的矮墙，还有你从前梦想高塔时一直盯着看的那片风景。\n\n@place home_market\n# en: The Market Town\nname [28baa8] = 集镇\n# en: Market stalls, the old crowd outside the inn, and the one shop that still sells your favourite sweets.\ndesc [0cdab5] = 集市摊位、旅店门口的老面孔，还有那家仍在卖你最爱的糖果的小店。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/world/spells.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/world/spells.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@spell flame\n# en: Flame\nname [1551a0] = 火焰\n# en: Fire from the fingertips: a candle's worth at first, a bonfire's later. Burns through wards that only expect cleverness.\ndesc [500494] = 指尖生火：起初只有一根蜡烛的分量，后来是一堆篝火。能烧穿那些只防备聪明人的结界。\n\n@spell tide\n# en: Tide\nname [f63371] = 潮涌\n# en: Water called up and moved: a wave, a wall, a lake politely asked to step aside.\ndesc [dfeef2] = 召来并驱使水：一道浪，一堵水墙，或请一整片湖礼貌地让开。\n\n@spell foresight\n# en: Foresight\nname [1834e4] = 预见\n# en: A glimpse of the next few moments. Enough to know which way the dice will fall, sometimes.\ndesc [d1ae0e] = 瞥见接下来的片刻。有时，足以知道骰子会落在哪一面。\n\n@spell starlight\n# en: Starlight\nname [461e3b] = 星光\n# en: The light of a named star, held in the hand. Darkness that eats light finds it hard to swallow.\ndesc [c90510] = 一颗有名字的星星的光，握在手里。吞噬光的黑暗很难把它咽下去。\n\n@spell mend\n# en: Mend\nname [c5efbd] = 愈合\n# en: Knits cuts, cracks and broken teacups. Hearts take longer.\ndesc [fff564] = 缝合伤口、裂缝和摔碎的茶杯。心要慢一些。\n\n@spell purify\n# en: Purify\nname [3d9ade] = 净化\n# en: Draws out poison, curse-cold and bad ingredients. The cure has to come from somewhere; it leaves you tired.\ndesc [3c860c] = 拔除毒素、诅咒之寒和坏掉的药材。解药总得从某处来；它会让你很累。\n\n@spell glamour\n# en: Glamour\nname [ca8c7e] = 魅影\n# en: A little more charm than you really have. People remember liking you and not quite why.\ndesc [844244] = 比你实际拥有的多一点点魅力。人们记得喜欢你，却说不清为什么。\n\n@spell mirror\n# en: Mirror\nname [247ee2] = 镜像\n# en: A double of yourself, or of anything you can see. It doesn't talk, which is its only flaw.\ndesc [664e97] = 一个你自己的替身，或任何你看得见的东西的替身。它不会说话——这是它唯一的缺点。\n\n@spell haste\n# en: Haste\nname [2f6010] = 疾速\n# en: Speed borrowed from hares and hawks. You act first; you pay for it with a long nap.\ndesc [1f52a3] = 从野兔和鹰那里借来的速度。你先出手；代价是之后睡上很久。\n\n@spell beast_speech\n# en: Beast-speech\nname [ba402d] = 兽语\n# en: Talk with animals, briefly. Most of them have been waiting to complain.\ndesc [9298ce] = 短暂地与动物交谈。它们大多一直在等着抱怨。\n\n@spell ward\n# en: Ward\nname [a11053] = 护界\n# en: A pale shell that turns aside the next harm.\ndesc [349fd9] = 一层苍白的护壳，能挡开下一次伤害。\n\n@spell shadowstep\n# en: Shadowstep\nname [d5b5ab] = 影步\n# en: Step into one shadow and out of another. Short distances only; the shadows in between are not friendly.\ndesc [c7cfde] = 踏入一片影子，从另一片影子里走出。只限短距离；中间的那些影子并不友善。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/world/tracks.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/world/tracks.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@track evocation\n# en: Evocation\nname [ade249] = 塑能术\n# en: Evocation I, Evocation II, Advanced Evocation\nlevel_names [bc7426] = 塑能术 I、塑能术 II、高等塑能术\n# en: Fire and water, called up and held. Pyromancy and Tidecalling, taught together because they keep putting each other out.\ndesc [17db46] = 召唤并驾驭火与水。火焰术和唤潮术合在一起教，因为它们总是互相浇灭。\n\n@track astromancy\n# en: Astromancy & Divination\nname [f9e4fb] = 星象与占卜\n# en: Astromancy I, Astromancy II, Advanced Astromancy\nlevel_names [9c3a9b] = 星象学 I、星象学 II、高等星象学\n# en: Reading the sky and the time that hasn't happened yet. Tea leaves in the first year; star charts that move in the third.\ndesc [74bd21] = 解读星空，以及尚未发生的时间。一年级看茶叶；三年级的星图会自己动。\n\n@track alchemy\n# en: Alchemy & Healing\nname [dd40d4] = 炼金与治愈\n# en: Alchemy I, Alchemy II, Advanced Alchemy\nlevel_names [5ac82d] = 炼金学 I、炼金学 II、高等炼金学\n# en: Potions, remedies and the patience to stir clockwise. Half of it happens in the laboratory and half in the infirmary.\ndesc [88b971] = 药剂、疗方，以及顺时针搅拌的耐心。一半在实验室里学，一半在医务室里学。\n\n@track illusion\n# en: Illusion & Glamour\nname [5cdf61] = 幻术与魅影\n# en: Illusion I, Illusion II, Advanced Illusion\nlevel_names [b3f52d] = 幻术 I、幻术 II、高等幻术\n# en: Making people see what you choose. Professor Vey grades on whether you fooled her, and she is very hard to fool.\ndesc [fcaca3] = 让别人看见你选择的东西。维伊教授的评分标准是你有没有骗过她——而她很难被骗。\n\n@track beastcraft\n# en: Beastcraft & Flight\nname [d8160a] = 驭兽与飞行\n# en: Beastcraft I, Beastcraft II, Advanced Beastcraft\nlevel_names [ff1301] = 驭兽学 I、驭兽学 II、高等驭兽学\n# en: Creatures, familiars and brooms. Professor Brask believes the first two are easier to reason with than the third.\ndesc [4a5da1] = 生灵、使魔与扫帚。布拉斯克教授认为前两者比第三者讲道理。\n\n@track umbral\n# en: Umbral Theory\nname [97bda7] = 暗影理论\n# en: Umbral Theory I, Umbral Theory II, Advanced Umbral Theory\nlevel_names [3a8538] = 暗影理论 I、暗影理论 II、高等暗影理论\n# en: Shadow, wards and the magic of what is hidden. Taught at the top of the tower, where the lamps burn dim on purpose.\ndesc [418538] = 影、结界，以及隐藏之物的魔法。在塔顶讲授，那里的灯故意点得很暗。\n\n@track artifice\n# en: Artifice\nname [de3328] = 奇械术\n# en: Artifice\nlevel_names [de3328] = 奇械术\n# en: Enchanted machinery: clockwork that thinks, cauldrons that stir themselves. Offered to students who already know how to make things and how to make them burn.\ndesc [7cb472] = 附魔机械：会思考的发条装置，会自己搅拌的坩埚。只开放给已经懂得如何造东西、也懂得东西如何着火的学生。\n\n@track starcalling\n# en: Starcalling\nname [7a102b] = 唤星术\n# en: Starcalling\nlevel_names [7a102b] = 唤星术\n# en: Calling down the light of a named star. Not on any syllabus; taught by appointment, at night.\ndesc [73276b] = 召唤一颗有名字的星星的光。不在任何课表上；需预约，夜间授课。\n\n@track hollow_studies\n# en: Hollow Studies\nname [1138df] = 空洞研究\n# en: Hollow Studies\nlevel_names [1138df] = 空洞研究\n# en: What the founders sealed, and how. The headmistress teaches it herself, to very few students, in a room without windows.\ndesc [e6cd54] = 创始人封印了什么，又是如何封印的。院长亲自讲授，学生极少，在一间没有窗的屋子里。\n\n@track duelling\n# en: Duelling Circle\nname [d0fcd5] = 决斗社\n# en: Member, Officer, Captain\nranks [b9c401] = 社员、干事、队长\n# en: Practice bouts, the Spring Tournament and a great deal of shouting about footwork.\ndesc [cd83f9] = 练习对决、春季锦标赛，还有关于步法的大量吼叫。\n\n@track lumen_ledger\n# en: The Lumen Ledger\nname [2606ae] = 《流明纪事报》社\n# en: Stringer, Reporter, Editor\nranks [82cebb] = 通讯员、记者、主编\n# en: The student newspaper. Scoops, deadlines, gossip columns and the occasional duty to the truth.\ndesc [ab972d] = 学生报纸。独家新闻、截稿日、八卦专栏，偶尔还有对真相的责任。\n\n@track verdant\n# en: Verdant Circle\nname [7ac3fd] = 青翠社\n# en: Member, Gardener, Head Gardener\nranks [cddc83] = 社员、园丁、首席园丁\n# en: The greenhouse club: moonflowers, mandrakes and long talks with plants that occasionally answer.\ndesc [263288] = 温室社团：月光花、曼德拉草，还有和偶尔会回话的植物之间的长谈。\n\n@track cartographers\n# en: Midnight Cartographers\nname [771e36] = 午夜制图社\n# en: Initiate, Mapper, Keeper of the Maps\nranks [ddba8c] = 新人、制图员、地图守护者\n# en: The club that doesn't exist, mapping the passages the castle would prefer to keep. Never offered at the Club Fair; you are invited, or you aren't.\ndesc [f48173] = 那个不存在的社团，专门绘制城堡宁愿藏起来的通道。社团招新会上从不出现；你要么被邀请，要么没有。\n\n@track library_assistant\n# en: Library Assistant\nname [111c30] = 图书馆助理\n# en: Apprentice, Clerk, Senior Clerk\nranks [e4d902] = 学徒、馆员、资深馆员\n# en: Shelving books that don't want to be shelved, and keeping Footnote off the reference desk.\ndesc [dd4493] = 把不想被上架的书上架，并且别让“脚注”坐上参考咨询台。\n\n@track tavern\n# en: Server at the Gilded Newt\nname [e530c9] = 镀金蝾螈酒馆侍应\n# en: Server, Barkeep, Manager\nranks [a43a61] = 侍应、吧台手、经理\n# en: Evening shifts behind the bar. Tips depend on charm; survival depends on remembering who ordered the spiced cider.\ndesc [173a67] = 吧台后的晚班。小费取决于魅力；能否活下来，取决于你记不记得是谁点了香料苹果酒。\n\n@track apothecary_clerk\n# en: Apothecary Clerk\nname [7d9f6c] = 药铺店员\n# en: Clerk, Dispenser, Assistant Apothecary\nranks [53ed21] = 店员、配药员、助理药师\n# en: Counting drops and reading labels at Pell & Daughters. The wrong drawer is never just the wrong drawer.\ndesc [b1db1f] = 在佩尔父女药铺数药滴、认标签。拿错的抽屉，从来不只是拿错了抽屉。\n\n@track broom_courier\n# en: Broom Courier\nname [8250e5] = 飞帚信使\n# en: Runner, Courier, Chief Courier\nranks [1d2734] = 跑腿、信使、首席信使\n# en: Parcels, letters and the occasional live delivery, flown across the lake in all weathers.\ndesc [2e4f08] = 包裹、信件，偶尔还有活物，风雨无阻地飞越湖面送达。\n\n@track intern_royal_library\n# en: Royal Library Archives\nname [df141e] = 皇家图书馆档案馆\n# en: Cataloguing and research in the oldest archives of the realm.\ndesc [fa724c] = 在王国最古老的档案馆里编目、做研究。\n\n@track intern_conclave\n# en: Conclave Laboratory\nname [132d7b] = 七塔议会实验室\n# en: Spell research with the Conclave of the Seven Towers. Prestigious; a faculty recommendation is expected.\ndesc [256192] = 在七塔议会从事咒语研究。名额珍贵；通常需要教职员的推荐信。\n\n@track intern_alchemists\n# en: Guild of Alchemists' Works\nname [6d98e1] = 炼金师公会工坊\n# en: Industrial alchemy at the Guild's great works.\ndesc [56dec3] = 在公会的大工坊里做工业炼金。\n\n@track intern_expedition\n# en: Observatory Expedition\nname [fe57ea] = 观星远征\n# en: A summer of sky charts on a high plateau.\ndesc [03b2f6] = 在高原上与星图共度一个夏天。\n\n@track intern_field_station\n# en: Beastcraft Field Station\nname [939e0e] = 驭兽术野外站\n# en: Tracking, tending and counting creatures on the moors.\ndesc [7564b3] = 在荒原上追踪、照料、清点各种生灵。\n\n@track intern_thornwick\n# en: Thornwick Exchange Lab\nname [28923e] = 荆木交换实验室\n# en: An exchange placement at the rival academy's illusion laboratory.\ndesc [51032c] = 前往对手学院的幻术实验室交换实习。\n\n@track intern_unseen_office\n# en: Office of Unseen Affairs\nname [c20130] = 隐秘事务署\n# en: By invitation only. Nobody applies to the Office of Unseen Affairs.\ndesc [55df65] = 只凭邀请。没有人会申请隐秘事务署。\n\n@track thesis\n# en: Thesis\nname [187afd] = 毕业论文\n# en: A year-long research project with a faculty advisor, ending in a defence before a committee of three.\ndesc [a30062] = 与一位教职员导师合作、为期一年的研究课题，最后在三人委员会面前答辩。\n"}]};
